   "q": "On 1 March, Halden Ltd wrote to Corin offering to sell him a printing press for £20,000. Corin received the letter on 3 March. On 4 March, Halden posted a letter revoking the offer. On 5 March, Corin posted a properly addressed and stamped letter accepting the offer. Halden's revocation reached Corin on 6 March, and Corin's acceptance reached Halden on 7 March. The offer said nothing about how it should be accepted.\n\nWhich of the following is correct?",
   "o": [
    "A contract was formed on 5 March when Corin posted his acceptance, because the revocation had not yet been received.",
    "No contract was formed because Halden's revocation was posted on 4 March, before Corin posted his acceptance.",
    "A contract was formed on 7 March when Halden received Corin's acceptance.",
    "No contract was formed because Corin received the revocation before Halden received the acceptance.",
    "No contract was formed because the postal rule applies equally to acceptance and revocation, so the earlier posting prevails."
   ],
   "a": 0,
   "e": "**Contract formed on 5 March, when the acceptance was posted.**\n- Under the **postal rule** (**Adams v Lindsell**), an acceptance is effective when **properly posted**, provided post was a reasonable method and the offer did not require actual receipt.\n- A **revocation** is effective only when **received** (**Byrne v Van Tienhoven**). Halden's revocation reached Corin on 6 March, after the contract was formed on 5 March, so it was too late.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- The postal rule does **not** apply to revocation, so posting the revocation on 4 March achieved nothing.\n- Acceptance was not delayed until receipt on 7 March because the postal rule applied.\n- The order in which letters arrived is irrelevant once the acceptance was posted before the revocation was received."
