   "e": "**No contract: the counter-offer destroyed the original offer.**\n- Leon's reply 'I'll give you £10,500' proposed **new terms**. It was a **counter-offer** that **rejected** Tamsin's offer (**Hyde v Wrench**). The original offer could not later be accepted.\n- Tamsin's reply simply refused the counter-offer. On these facts it did not clearly make a fresh offer, so Leon's later 'acceptance' was at most a new offer, which Tamsin can refuse.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A promise to keep an offer open is not binding without consideration, and in any event the offer had been rejected.\n- A counter-offer naming a different price is not a request for information (contrast **Stevenson v McLean**).\n- The timing of email receipt does not matter because there was no live offer to accept."
