   "a": 0,
   "e": "**The challenge will fail.**\n- The **Sewel convention** says Westminster will **not normally** legislate on devolved matters without consent. **s.107(6) GoWA 2006** recognises it.\n- In **R (Miller) v Secretary of State for Exiting the EU** [2017], the Supreme Court held that putting the convention in statute did **not** turn it into a **legally enforceable** rule. It remains a **political** convention.\n- Westminster remains **sovereign** and can legislate on devolved matters (**s.107(5)**).\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- The statutory reference does not make it legally binding.\n- Harm is irrelevant; the courts do not police the convention.\n- The point is not standing; nobody can enforce it in court.\n- Westminster keeps power to legislate for Wales on any matter."
