   "a": 2,
   "e": "The correct answer is that **there is no binding contract** because it is not **signed by both parties**.\n\n- **s.2 LP(MP)A 1989** requires a contract for the sale of land to be **in writing**, to incorporate **all the expressly agreed terms**, and to be **signed by or on behalf of each party**.\n- Non-compliance means there is **no contract at all**; it is void, not merely unenforceable.\n- Writing 'agreed' without a signature does not satisfy the signature requirement.\n\nThe idea that only the party seeking enforcement must sign reflects the old s.40 LPA 1925 rule, which no longer applies. A deed is required to transfer a legal estate (s.52 LPA), not to make the contract. Specific performance needs a valid contract in the first place."
