   "e": "The correct answer is that Pavel **probably has no easement**: he needs an **implied reservation**, and these are implied only in narrow cases.\n\n- A seller who wants to keep a right over the land sold must **expressly reserve** it.\n- **Wheeldon v Burrows** and **s.62 LPA** operate in favour of the **grantee** (buyer), not the seller.\n- Reservation is implied only for **necessity** (land unusable without it) or **common intention**. Nothing suggests Pavel's house is landlocked.\n- Prescription needs use by one owner against **another** owner; Pavel owned both plots, so he could not prescribe against himself.\n\nThe facts about the track being continuous and apparent would only help a **buyer** under Wheeldon v Burrows. Long use is not the test for necessity."
