   "q": "Rhys is selling his house to Sian. The contract incorporates the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition) and contains no special conditions on insurance. After exchange but before completion, the house is badly damaged by fire. Rhys had cancelled his insurance on exchange.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?",
   "o": [
    "Sian bears the risk from exchange and must complete at the full price.",
    "Rhys retains the risk until completion, and if the house is unusable for its purpose either party may rescind.",
    "The contract is automatically frustrated and the deposit is forfeited to Rhys.",
    "Sian must complete but may claim the cost of repairs under Rhys's cancelled insurance.",
    "Rhys must repair the house at his own cost before completion takes place."
   ],
   "a": 0,
   "e": "The correct answer is that **Sian bears the risk from exchange** and must complete at the full price.\n\n- Under **SC 5.1.1** of the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edn, 2018 revision), the property is **at the risk of the buyer from the date of the contract** (exchange).\n- Under **SC 5.1.2** the seller is under **no obligation to insure** unless the contract requires it or he must insure under a lease, so Rhys was entitled to cancel his policy.\n- Sian should have insured from exchange. She must complete and pay the full price.\n\nThe option allowing either party to rescind if the house is unusable describes the old **4th edition** rule, which the 5th edition replaced; the **SCPC** (condition 8.1) also puts the risk on the buyer. A contract for the sale of land is rarely frustrated. The contract imposes no duty on Rhys to repair, and Sian has no claim on a cancelled policy."
