   "q": "Daniel telephones Abbot Solicitors and asks them to act on the sale of his 88-year-old mother's house, with the proceeds to be paid to him. He says his mother is frail and that he will pass on her instructions. He offers to pay the firm's fees.\n\nWhat is the best course of action for the firm?",
   "o": [
    "Act on Daniel's instructions, because he is paying the fees.",
    "Act on Daniel's instructions provided he confirms in writing that his mother agrees.",
    "Refuse to act, because a firm cannot act where a relative is involved.",
    "Meet the mother, ideally without Daniel present, to confirm her instructions, her capacity and that she is not under undue influence.",
    "Act on Daniel's instructions, because there is a presumption of capacity."
   ],
   "a": 3,
   "e": "The firm should **meet the mother**, ideally **alone**, to confirm her instructions and capacity and check for **undue influence**.\n\nUnder **Code para 3.1**, you only act on instructions from the **client** or someone **properly authorised**. Where a third party gives instructions, you must check directly with the client. Here there are warning signs: an elderly client and proceeds going to the person giving instructions. The firm must check her **capacity** and that she is acting freely.\n\n- Who pays the fees does not determine who the client is.\n- Written confirmation obtained through Daniel is not enough.\n- There is no rule against acting where relatives are involved.\n- The presumption of capacity relates to the mother, but it does not remove the need to take instructions from her."
