window.SQE_QUICK["ethics"] = [
  {"t":"principles-competence","q":"How many SRA Principles are there?","o":["Seven","Five","Ten","Twelve"],"e":"There are **seven** Principles, from the rule of law to the best interests of each client."},
  {"t":"principles-competence","q":"Which Principles prevail when the Principles conflict?","o":["Those protecting the wider public interest","Best interests of each client","Independence","Equality, diversity and inclusion"],"e":"Principles safeguarding the **wider public interest** take precedence over the client's interests."},
  {"t":"principles-competence","q":"A solicitor backdates a document, knowing the facts but believing it is acceptable. Ordinary decent people would see it as dishonest. Is the solicitor dishonest?","o":["Yes: honesty is judged objectively on their actual state of mind","No: the solicitor did not realise it was dishonest","No: dishonesty requires an intent to gain","Only if the client lost money"],"e":"**Dishonesty test**: find the person's **actual knowledge or belief** as to the facts, then apply the standards of **ordinary decent people**; they need not realise it is dishonest (Ivey v Genting)."},
  {"t":"principles-competence","q":"How many areas does the Statement of Solicitor Competence have?","o":["Four","Three","Six","Seven"],"e":"**Four**: ethics; technical legal practice; working with other people; managing themselves and their work."},
  {"t":"trust-fairness-undertakings","q":"Must an undertaking be in writing to be binding?","o":["No, it can be oral","Yes, always","Only if over £10,000","Only if signed by a partner"],"e":"An undertaking can be **oral or written** and need not use the word \"undertake\"."},
  {"t":"trust-fairness-undertakings","q":"Who can release a solicitor from an undertaking?","o":["The recipient","The client","The firm's COLP","The SRA"],"e":"Only the **recipient** can agree to vary or release it. The client cannot override it."},
  {"t":"trust-fairness-undertakings","q":"If no time is agreed, when must an undertaking be performed?","o":["Within a reasonable time","Within 14 days","Within 28 days","Whenever convenient"],"e":"Code **para 1.3**: within the agreed time or a **reasonable time**."},
  {"t":"trust-fairness-undertakings","q":"Which Code paragraph requires managers to challenge bullying and harassment?","o":["Para 1.5","Para 1.2","Para 3.5","Para 7.9"],"e":"**Para 1.5**: treat colleagues fairly; managers must **challenge** behaviour below this standard."},
  {"t":"duty-to-court","q":"Which Code paragraph requires drawing adverse authorities to the court's attention?","o":["Para 2.7","Para 2.4","Para 1.4","Para 6.4"],"e":"**Para 2.7**: relevant cases, statutes and procedural irregularities, even if adverse."},
  {"t":"duty-to-court","q":"Can a solicitor whose client admits guilt continue to act on a not guilty plea?","o":["Yes, but not assert innocence","No, they must withdraw","Yes, and call an alibi","Only with the court's permission"],"e":"They can **put the prosecution to proof** but must not assert innocence or call false evidence."},
  {"t":"duty-to-court","q":"What should a solicitor tell the court when withdrawing because the client will mislead it?","o":["Professional reasons","The client's admission","The client's planned evidence","Nothing, just leave"],"e":"Confidentiality means saying only it is for **professional reasons**."},
  {"t":"duty-to-court","q":"Which payment to a witness is prohibited?","o":["One dependent on the outcome","Reasonable travel expenses","Compensation for lost earnings","An expert's agreed hourly fee"],"e":"Code **para 2.3** bans benefits **dependent on the evidence or outcome**."},
  {"t":"instructions-capacity-service","q":"Which Act sets the test for mental capacity?","o":["Mental Capacity Act 2005","Mental Health Act 1983","Care Act 2014","Wills Act 1837"],"e":"The **Mental Capacity Act 2005**: presumption of capacity, decision-specific test."},
  {"t":"instructions-capacity-service","q":"Under the MCA 2005, how is capacity assessed?","o":["Decision-specific, at the time","By medical diagnosis alone","By age over 75","By the wisdom of the decision"],"e":"Capacity is **decision-specific**. A diagnosis or unwise decision does not prove lack of capacity."},
  {"t":"instructions-capacity-service","q":"On what basis can a solicitor end a retainer?","o":["Good reason and reasonable notice","Any reason, without notice","Only with client consent","Only with SRA permission"],"e":"A solicitor may end it only for **good reason** and on **reasonable notice**."},
  {"t":"instructions-capacity-service","q":"Which Code paragraph limits acting to instructions from the client or someone authorised?","o":["Para 3.1","Para 8.1","Para 6.1","Para 4.2"],"e":"**Para 3.1**: act only on instructions from the client or someone **properly authorised**."},
  {"t":"client-money","q":"How often must client account reconciliations be carried out?","o":["At least every five weeks","Every month","Every quarter","Once a year"],"e":"SRA Accounts Rules: reconciliations at least every **five weeks**."},
  {"t":"client-money","q":"When can a firm transfer its costs out of client account?","o":["After a bill or written notification","As soon as work is done","At the end of the accounting period","When the client pays the next invoice"],"e":"Costs can be taken only once a **bill or written notification** has been given."},
  {"t":"client-money","q":"What must a firm do on finding a shortfall caused by an error in client account?","o":["Replace it immediately from firm money","Wait for the client to repay","Report to LeO","Correct it at year end"],"e":"Breaches must be **corrected promptly**, and money improperly withdrawn must be **replaced immediately** from the firm's own money."},
  {"t":"client-money","q":"Within what period must an accountant's report be obtained, unless exempt?","o":["Six months of period end","Three months of period end","Twelve months of period end","One month of period end"],"e":"Within **six months** of the end of the accounting period."},
  {"t":"business-referrals-publicity","q":"In which matters are referral payments banned by the Code?","o":["Criminal proceedings","Residential conveyancing","Wills and probate","Commercial contracts"],"e":"Code **para 5.1** bans referral payments for clients in **criminal** proceedings. PI referral fees are banned by LASPO."},
  {"t":"business-referrals-publicity","q":"How must a fee-sharing agreement be made?","o":["In writing","Orally","By deed","With SRA approval"],"e":"Fee-sharing agreements must be **in writing**."},
  {"t":"business-referrals-publicity","q":"Who may a solicitor approach unsolicited to advertise legal services?","o":["Current or former clients","Accident victims in hospital","Any homeowner","People at a police station"],"e":"Para **8.9** bans unsolicited approaches to the public, except **current or former clients**."},
  {"t":"business-referrals-publicity","q":"Which statute bans referral fees in personal injury claims?","o":["LASPO 2012","Legal Services Act 2007","Solicitors Act 1974","Courts and Legal Services Act 1990"],"e":"**LASPO 2012** bans referral fees in personal injury and death claims."},
  {"t":"conflicts-of-interest","q":"Can client consent cure an own interest conflict?","o":["No, never","Yes, if in writing","Yes, with safeguards","Yes, if the client is sophisticated"],"e":"There are **no exceptions** to para 6.1."},
  {"t":"conflicts-of-interest","q":"Which situation fits the competing for the same objective exception?","o":["Two bidders for one business","Buyer and seller of a house","Joint buyers of a house","Co-founders of a company"],"e":"**Competing for the same objective**: two clients seeking the same asset, e.g. two bidders."},
  {"t":"conflicts-of-interest","q":"What should a solicitor do if a client wants to leave them a significant gift and refuses independent advice?","o":["Decline to act","Act, with a file note","Act, with a colleague as witness","Act, but reduce the gift"],"e":"A significant gift is an **own interest conflict**. If the client won't get independent advice, **don't act**."},
  {"t":"conflicts-of-interest","q":"How must consent under the para 6.2 exceptions be given?","o":["Informed, given or evidenced in writing","Orally","By deed","Implied from conduct"],"e":"All clients must give **informed consent, given or evidenced in writing**."},
  {"t":"confidentiality-disclosure","q":"Does the duty of confidentiality survive the client's death?","o":["Yes","No","Only for one year","Only if privileged"],"e":"It continues after the retainer ends and after **death**."},
  {"t":"confidentiality-disclosure","q":"Which Code paragraph sets out the duty of disclosure to the client?","o":["Para 6.4","Para 6.3","Para 6.5","Para 7.7"],"e":"**Para 6.4**: tell the client all material information, subject to four exceptions."},
  {"t":"confidentiality-disclosure","q":"A firm wants to act against a former client and holds that client's material confidential information. Without the former client's consent, when may it act behind an information barrier?","o":["Only if effective measures leave no real risk of disclosure","Whenever the former client is told","Whenever a partner signs a confidentiality undertaking","Never: acting against former clients is banned"],"e":"Para 6.5: the firm may act if **effective measures** mean there is **no real risk** of disclosure, or with informed written consent (Prince Jefri Bolkiah v KPMG)."},
  {"t":"confidentiality-disclosure","q":"Must a solicitor tell their client the contents of a privileged document sent by mistake?","o":["No","Yes, always","Only if helpful","Only if the court agrees"],"e":"Para **6.4(d)** excludes privileged documents known only through **mistaken disclosure**."},
  {"t":"cooperation-reporting","q":"Which Code paragraph requires reporting a serious breach to the SRA?","o":["Para 7.7","Para 7.3","Para 7.11","Para 8.4"],"e":"**Para 7.7**: report promptly facts reasonably believed capable of a serious breach, including your own."},
  {"t":"cooperation-reporting","q":"When does telling the COLP satisfy your reporting duty?","o":["On the understanding they will report","Always","Never","Only if in writing"],"e":"Para **7.12**: only on the understanding the COLP or COFA will pass it on."},
  {"t":"cooperation-reporting","q":"Which Code paragraph protects whistleblowers from detrimental treatment?","o":["Para 7.9","Para 7.5","Para 1.5","Para 3.5"],"e":"**Para 7.9**, whether or not the SRA investigates."},
  {"t":"cooperation-reporting","q":"Which event must a solicitor notify to the SRA under para 7.6?","o":["Being charged with a criminal offence","Changing practice area","Taking annual leave","Receiving a client complaint"],"e":"Para **7.6**: criminal charge, conviction or caution, insolvency events and material changes."},
  {"t":"complaints-legal-ombudsman","q":"Since April 2023, what is the basic Legal Ombudsman time limit from the act complained of?","o":["One year","Six years","Three years","Six months"],"e":"**One year** from the act or omission, or one year from when the complainant should have known."},
  {"t":"complaints-legal-ombudsman","q":"How long must a firm normally have to resolve a complaint before LeO steps in?","o":["Eight weeks","Four weeks","Six months","Twelve weeks"],"e":"LeO normally waits for the firm's **final response** or **eight weeks**."},
  {"t":"complaints-legal-ombudsman","q":"What is the maximum value of remedies the Legal Ombudsman can award?","o":["£50,000","£25,000","£100,000","£10,000"],"e":"The total value of remedies is capped at **£50,000**."},
  {"t":"complaints-legal-ombudsman","q":"Within what time of the firm's final response must the complaint go to LeO?","o":["Six months","Eight weeks","One year","Three months"],"e":"**Six months**, provided the final response told the client about LeO and the limit."},
  {"t":"firms-compliance-protections","q":"Which compliance officer is responsible for the Accounts Rules?","o":["COFA","COLP","MLRO","Managing partner"],"e":"The **COFA** covers the Accounts Rules; the **COLP** covers everything else."},
  {"t":"firms-compliance-protections","q":"What is the usual maximum Compensation Fund grant per claim?","o":["£2 million","£50,000","£500,000","£3 million"],"e":"The usual maximum is **£2 million**."},
  {"t":"firms-compliance-protections","q":"Who deals with negligence claims against a closed firm made after the six-year run-off period?","o":["The Solicitors Indemnity Fund, managed by the SRA","The Compensation Fund","The Legal Ombudsman","Nobody"],"e":"Post-six-year claims go to the **Solicitors Indemnity Fund (SIF)**, which the **SRA** has managed since **1 October 2023**."},
  {"t":"firms-compliance-protections","q":"How are a firm's managers responsible for its compliance?","o":["Jointly and severally","Only the senior partner","Only the COLP","Only if they caused the breach"],"e":"Under the Code for Firms, managers are **jointly and severally** responsible."},
  {"t":"ndas-slapps","q":"Since October 2025, which Act voids NDA clauses stopping crime victims reporting to police?","o":["Victims and Prisoners Act 2024","Employment Rights Act 1996","Public Interest Disclosure Act 1998","Equality Act 2010"],"e":"**s.17 Victims and Prisoners Act 2024**, in force 1 October 2025."},
  {"t":"ndas-slapps","q":"Which Act contains a statutory definition of a SLAPP?","o":["Economic Crime and Corporate Transparency Act 2023","Defamation Act 2013","Legal Services Act 2007","Human Rights Act 1998"],"e":"**ECCTA 2023 s.195**, though the SRA's expectations are wider."},
  {"t":"ndas-slapps","q":"Which label is misused in SLAPP-type letters, according to the SRA?","o":["Without prejudice","Subject to contract","Open correspondence","Draft for approval"],"e":"The SRA warns against marking letters **\"without prejudice\"** (or \"not for publication\") when the conditions aren't met."},
  {"t":"ndas-slapps","q":"What must an NDA never prevent a person from doing?","o":["Reporting misconduct to the SRA","Disclosing the settlement sum publicly","Posting about it on social media","Telling a journalist the terms"],"e":"NDAs must not stop **reporting to the SRA**, regulators or the police, or protected disclosures."}
];

;
window.SQE_TERMS = window.SQE_TERMS || {};
window.SQE_TERMS["ethics"] = [
  {t:"principles-competence", w:"Seven SRA Principles", d:"Rule of law, public trust, independence, honesty, integrity, equality and diversity, client's best interests."},
  {t:"principles-competence", w:"Public interest takes precedence", d:"When Principles conflict, those protecting the wider public interest beat the client's interests."},
  {t:"principles-competence", w:"Wingate v SRA", d:"Integrity means adherence to the profession's ethical standards; wider than honesty."},
  {t:"principles-competence", w:"Statement of Solicitor Competence", d:"Sets the threshold standard a solicitor needs: ethics, technical practice, working with others, self-management."},
  {t:"principles-competence", w:"Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal", d:"Hears serious cases and can strike off, suspend or fine without limit."},
  {t:"principles-competence", w:"SRA fine limit", d:"Up to £25,000 for individuals and traditional firms; unlimited for economic crime matters."},
  {t:"trust-fairness-undertakings", w:"Undertaking", d:"A statement, oral or written, relied on by the recipient, that you will or will not do something."},
  {t:"trust-fairness-undertakings", w:"Para 1.2 unfair advantage", d:"You must not abuse your position by taking unfair advantage of clients or others."},
  {t:"trust-fairness-undertakings", w:"Para 1.4 do not mislead", d:"Never mislead clients, the court or others, including by being complicit in a client's acts."},
  {t:"trust-fairness-undertakings", w:"Release of an undertaking", d:"An undertaking can only be varied or released with the recipient's agreement."},
  {t:"trust-fairness-undertakings", w:"Reasonable time", d:"An undertaking with no stated timescale must be performed within a reasonable time."},
  {t:"duty-to-court", w:"Para 2.7 adverse authorities", d:"Draw the court's attention to relevant law and irregularities, even if against your client."},
  {t:"duty-to-court", w:"Para 2.3 benefits to witnesses", d:"No benefit to a witness that depends on their evidence or the outcome of the case."},
  {t:"duty-to-court", w:"Putting the prosecution to proof", d:"Allowed where the client admits guilt; you may test the evidence but never assert innocence."},
  {t:"duty-to-court", w:"Withdrawing for professional reasons", d:"Cease to act without telling the court the confidential reason."},
  {t:"duty-to-court", w:"No property in a witness", d:"You may interview the other side's witnesses, taking care not to influence them."},
  {t:"duty-to-court", w:"Coaching a witness", d:"Rehearsing or shaping a witness's answers; forbidden, though explaining procedure is allowed."},
  {t:"instructions-capacity-service", w:"Para 3.1 instructions", d:"Act only on instructions from the client or someone properly authorised to give them."},
  {t:"instructions-capacity-service", w:"Undue influence", d:"Improper pressure by a third party, often one who benefits, that taints the client's instructions."},
  {t:"instructions-capacity-service", w:"Mental Capacity Act 2005", d:"Adults are presumed to have capacity; capacity is decision-specific."},
  {t:"instructions-capacity-service", w:"Understand, retain, use or weigh, communicate", d:"The four abilities a person must have to make a decision under the MCA test."},
  {t:"instructions-capacity-service", w:"Lasting power of attorney", d:"Lets an attorney instruct you for a client who has lost capacity."},
  {t:"instructions-capacity-service", w:"Para 3.5 supervision", d:"Where you supervise others, you remain accountable for their work."},
  {t:"client-money", w:"SRA Accounts Rules 2019", d:"Rules requiring client money to be kept separate in a client account and used properly."},
  {t:"client-money", w:"Banking facility (rule 3.3)", d:"Client account may only be used for payments relating to the legal services provided."},
  {t:"client-money", w:"Bill or written notification of costs", d:"Must be given before client money is transferred to pay the firm's costs."},
  {t:"client-money", w:"Five-weekly reconciliation", d:"Client accounts must be reconciled at least every five weeks."},
  {t:"client-money", w:"Mixed payment", d:"Part client, part firm money: pay it all into client account, then transfer the firm's share promptly."},
  {t:"client-money", w:"Accountant's report", d:"Required within six months of the end of the accounting period, unless exempt."},
  {t:"business-referrals-publicity", w:"Para 5.1 referrals", d:"Disclose any interest in referrals and fee sharing to clients; fee sharing must be in writing."},
  {t:"business-referrals-publicity", w:"LASPO 2012 s.56", d:"Bans referral fees in personal injury and death claims."},
  {t:"business-referrals-publicity", w:"Criminal referral payments", d:"You must not make or receive referral payments for clients in criminal proceedings."},
  {t:"business-referrals-publicity", w:"Para 8.9 unsolicited approaches", d:"No unsolicited approaches to the public to advertise services, except current or former clients."},
  {t:"business-referrals-publicity", w:"Para 5.3 separate business", d:"Refer a client to your separate business only with the client's informed consent."},
  {t:"conflicts-of-interest", w:"Own interest conflict (para 6.1)", d:"Your duty to the client conflicts with your own interests; no exceptions, consent cannot cure it."},
  {t:"conflicts-of-interest", w:"Client conflict (para 6.2)", d:"Duties to two or more clients in the same or related matter conflict, or significantly risk it."},
  {t:"conflicts-of-interest", w:"Substantially common interest", d:"Exception where clients share a clear common purpose and agree on how to achieve it."},
  {t:"conflicts-of-interest", w:"Competing for the same objective", d:"Exception where clients compete for the same asset, e.g. two bidders for one business."},
  {t:"conflicts-of-interest", w:"Significant gift from a client", d:"Creates an own interest conflict; advise independent advice or decline to act."},
  {t:"confidentiality-disclosure", w:"Para 6.3 confidentiality", d:"Keep current and former clients' affairs confidential unless the law requires or the client consents."},
  {t:"confidentiality-disclosure", w:"Para 6.4 duty of disclosure", d:"Tell the client all material information you know, subject to narrow exceptions."},
  {t:"confidentiality-disclosure", w:"Legal professional privilege", d:"Protects lawyer-client communications from disclosure to anyone, even a court; lost if furthering crime."},
  {t:"confidentiality-disclosure", w:"Para 6.5 acting against a client", d:"Cannot act adversely holding material confidential information unless effective measures or written consent."},
  {t:"confidentiality-disclosure", w:"Information barrier", d:"Ethical wall separating teams so there is no real risk of confidential information leaking."},
  {t:"confidentiality-disclosure", w:"Prince Jefri Bolkiah v KPMG", d:"An information barrier must be effective and established in advance."},
  {t:"cooperation-reporting", w:"Para 7.7 reporting duty", d:"Promptly report facts you reasonably believe are capable of amounting to a serious breach, including your own."},
  {t:"cooperation-reporting", w:"Para 7.5 not preventing reports", d:"Do not try to stop anyone giving information to the SRA, regulators or prosecutors."},
  {t:"cooperation-reporting", w:"Para 7.9 whistleblower protection", d:"Do not subject anyone to detrimental treatment for making or proposing a report."},
  {t:"cooperation-reporting", w:"Para 7.11 openness", d:"Be honest and open with clients when things go wrong, and put matters right."},
  {t:"cooperation-reporting", w:"Para 7.12 reporting via COLP", d:"Reporting to the COLP or COFA satisfies your duty if you understand they will pass it on."},
  {t:"complaints-legal-ombudsman", w:"Legal Ombudsman (LeO)", d:"Deals with complaints of poor service by authorised lawyers; the SRA handles misconduct."},
  {t:"complaints-legal-ombudsman", w:"8-week rule", d:"LeO normally accepts a complaint only after the firm's final response or 8 weeks."},
  {t:"complaints-legal-ombudsman", w:"Six months from final response", d:"Complaint must reach LeO within six months of the firm's final response."},
  {t:"complaints-legal-ombudsman", w:"One-year LeO limit", d:"Complain within one year of the act or of when the complainant should have known."},
  {t:"complaints-legal-ombudsman", w:"£50,000 LeO cap", d:"The maximum total value of remedies the Legal Ombudsman can award."},
  {t:"complaints-legal-ombudsman", w:"Micro-enterprise", d:"A small business that, like individuals and small charities, can complain to LeO."},
  {t:"firms-compliance-protections", w:"Code of Conduct for Firms", d:"Applies to authorised firms, managers and compliance officers, adding duties on systems and controls."},
  {t:"firms-compliance-protections", w:"COLP", d:"Compliance Officer for Legal Practice: ensures regulatory compliance (except accounts) and reports serious breaches."},
  {t:"firms-compliance-protections", w:"COFA", d:"Compliance Officer for Finance and Administration: ensures compliance with the Accounts Rules."},
  {t:"firms-compliance-protections", w:"Minimum Terms and Conditions", d:"Compulsory PII: at least £2 million per claim, £3 million for LLPs and companies."},
  {t:"firms-compliance-protections", w:"SRA Compensation Fund", d:"Discretionary last-resort fund for losses from dishonesty or failure to account; usual maximum £2 million."},
  {t:"firms-compliance-protections", w:"Six years' run-off cover", d:"PII cover for a firm that closes with no successor practice."},
  {t:"ndas-slapps", w:"SRA warning notice", d:"Sets out the SRA's expectations on a risk; ignoring it is likely to lead to discipline."},
  {t:"ndas-slapps", w:"Non-disclosure agreement (NDA)", d:"Lawful confidentiality clause, but it must not stop reports to the SRA, police or regulators."},
  {t:"ndas-slapps", w:"s.17 Victims and Prisoners Act 2024", d:"From 1 October 2025, NDAs are void insofar as they stop crime victims disclosing to police or lawyers."},
  {t:"ndas-slapps", w:"SLAPP", d:"Strategic lawsuit against public participation: misusing litigation to stifle lawful public scrutiny."},
  {t:"ndas-slapps", w:"s.195 ECCTA 2023", d:"Statutory definition of economic crime SLAPPs; the SRA's expectations are wider."},
  {t:"ndas-slapps", w:"Misusing without prejudice", d:"Labelling threatening letters without prejudice when not genuine settlement talks is misleading."}
];

;
window.SQE_CARDS = window.SQE_CARDS || {};
window.SQE_CARDS["ethics"] = [["Who regulates solicitors, and what is its rulebook called?","The **SRA** (Solicitors Regulation Authority). Its rulebook is the **SRA Standards and Regulations**.","principles-competence"],
["How is ethics examined in SQE1?","**Pervasively**. There is no separate paper, so an ethical issue can appear in a question on **any subject**.","principles-competence"],
["When did the SRA Standards and Regulations come into force, and what is their style?","**25 November 2019**. They are short and **principles-based**: they state the outcome to achieve. SRA **guidance** adds detail but is **not a rule**.","principles-competence"],
["Who do the SRA Principles and the two Codes apply to?","The **Principles**: everyone the SRA regulates, individuals and firms. The **Code for Solicitors, RELs, RFLs and RSLs**: **individuals**. The **Code for Firms**: **authorised firms**, their **managers** and **compliance officers**.","principles-competence"],
["What are the eight sections of the Code for Solicitors?","1 **trust and fairness**. 2 **dispute resolution and courts**. 3 **service and competence**. 4 **client money and assets**. 5 **business requirements**. 6 **conflicts, confidentiality and disclosure**. 7 **cooperation and accountability**. 8 **services to the public**.","principles-competence"],
["What are the seven SRA Principles?","You act: 1 upholding the **rule of law** and **proper administration of justice**; 2 upholding **public trust and confidence**; 3 with **independence**; 4 with **honesty**; 5 with **integrity**; 6 encouraging **equality, diversity and inclusion**; 7 in the **best interests of each client**.","principles-competence"],
["Do the Principles apply outside practice?","**Yes**, but only so far as conduct affects **public trust** in you and the profession. In practice this means mainly **Principles 2, 4 and 5**.","principles-competence"],
["Which Principles win when they conflict?","Those safeguarding the **wider public interest** (e.g. **rule of law**, **public confidence**) **take precedence over** an individual client's interests. So **Principle 7** gives way to **1, 2, 4 and 5**.","principles-competence"],
["Does acting in the client's best interests mean doing what the client says?","**No.** **Independence** (Principle 3) means giving your own professional judgement, even if unwelcome.","principles-competence"],
["May a solicitor threaten criminal prosecution to get a civil payment for a client?","**No.** That is improper and takes **unfair advantage** (para 1.2). The solicitor can send a measured letter setting out the real **civil claim**.","principles-competence"],
["What is the test for dishonesty?","**Stage 1 (subjective)**: what did the person **actually know or believe** about the facts? **Stage 2 (objective)**: was the conduct dishonest by the standards of **ordinary decent people**? (Ivey v Genting)","principles-competence"],
["Must the person realise that ordinary people would see the conduct as dishonest?","**No.** There is **no** such requirement. An unreasonable belief can still be genuine, but the more unreasonable, the less likely it was held.","principles-competence"],
["What is the usual result of a finding of dishonesty against a solicitor?","Almost always **strike-off**.","principles-competence"],
["What is integrity, and how does it differ from honesty?","Adherence to the **ethical standards of the profession**. It is **wider** than honesty: a solicitor can **lack integrity without being dishonest**, e.g. **recklessly** making misleading statements or taking **unfair advantage**.","principles-competence"],
["Is integrity a free-standing code of morality?","**No.** It is measured against the **SRA's rules and guidance**. Private conduct is engaged only where it realistically touches **professional standing**.","principles-competence"],
["Is an honest mistake a breach of integrity?","**Usually not.** An honest, good-faith mistake is normally a **competence** issue (para 3.2).","principles-competence"],
["What are the four areas of the Statement of Solicitor Competence?","**A**: ethics, professionalism and judgement. **B**: technical legal practice. **C**: working with other people. **D**: managing themselves and their own work.","principles-competence"],
["What does competence A1 require, and why does it matter for SQE1?","To **act honestly and with integrity**, in line with legal and regulatory requirements and the Code. SQE1 maps **A1** to the ethics questions.","principles-competence"],
["What is the Threshold Standard?","The level expected of a **newly qualified** (\"Day One\") solicitor. SQE1 is set at that level.","principles-competence"],
["How must solicitors maintain competence?","There are **no minimum CPD hours**. Solicitors must **reflect** on practice, address learning needs and confirm this in an **annual declaration** (Code para **3.3**).","principles-competence"],
["What sanctions can the SRA itself impose?","A **rebuke**. A **fine** up to **£25,000** for individuals and traditional firms (**unlimited** for **economic crime** matters). **Conditions** on a practising certificate or authorisation. Letters of advice and warnings.","principles-competence"],
["What can the Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal do?","**Strike off**, **suspend**, impose an **unlimited fine**, make restriction orders and order costs. Appeals go to the **High Court**.","principles-competence"],
["What is SRA intervention?","The SRA **takes control of a firm's client money and files** to protect clients, e.g. suspected dishonesty, or a sole practitioner's death or incapacity.","principles-competence"],
["What is a section 43 order?","An order controlling the **employment of non-solicitors** in law firms, e.g. a cashier who stole money.","principles-competence"],
["A solicitor misses a deadline by honest oversight. What must she do?","Put it right and **tell the client** (para 7.11). It is a **competence** issue. **Hiding** it or lying about it would be **dishonest** and likely lead to strike-off.","principles-competence"],
["What does para 1.1 of the Code prohibit?","**Unfair discrimination**: letting **personal views** affect professional relationships or the way you provide services.","trust-fairness-undertakings"],
["What does para 1.2 prohibit?","**Abusing your position** by taking **unfair advantage** of clients **or others**.","trust-fairness-undertakings"],
["What are examples of a solicitor taking unfair advantage of clients or others under para 1.2?","Exploiting an **unrepresented** person's lack of legal knowledge. Demanding sums **not legally due**. **Threatening criminal proceedings** to extract a civil payment. Oppressive **NDA** terms.","trust-fairness-undertakings"],
["Who does para 1.2 protect?","**Others**, not just clients: the other side, **unrepresented opponents**, witnesses, journalists.","trust-fairness-undertakings"],
["What does para 1.3 require?","You **perform all undertakings** within the agreed time or, if none, within a **reasonable** time.","trust-fairness-undertakings"],
["What does para 1.4 prohibit?","**Misleading** or attempting to mislead clients, the court or others, by your own acts or **omissions**, or by being **complicit** in others' (including your client's).","trust-fairness-undertakings"],
["Can a half-truth or silence breach para 1.4?","**Yes**: a **half-truth**, a deliberate **omission** or **standing by** while the client misleads. But there is **no duty to volunteer** weaknesses in your client's case, provided nothing said is misleading.","trust-fairness-undertakings"],
["What does para 1.5 require?","Treat **colleagues** fairly and with respect, with no bullying, harassment or unfair discrimination. **Managers** must **challenge** behaviour below this standard.","trust-fairness-undertakings"],
["Writing to an unrepresented opponent: what is allowed?","You may firmly assert the client's **genuine legal position**. You must **not** exaggerate the law, threaten unavailable consequences, or exploit their ignorance. You need not advise them, but nothing may mislead.","trust-fairness-undertakings"],
["What is an undertaking (SRA Glossary)?","A **statement**, **oral or written**, whether or not it uses the word \"undertake\", to someone who **reasonably relies** on it, that **you or a third party will do something** or **refrain** from doing something.","trust-fairness-undertakings"],
["Is \"I will try to send the deeds\" an undertaking?","**Probably not.** \"I will try\" or \"I expect\" is not a **promise**. \"I will send the deeds tomorrow\" to a solicitor who relies on it **is** one.","trust-fairness-undertakings"],
["What undertakings are common in conveyancing and litigation?","Conveyancing: to **redeem the seller's mortgage** on completion; Code for Completion by Post. Litigation: to **pay costs**, keep documents confidential, hold money or documents **to someone's order**.","trust-fairness-undertakings"],
["Who is bound by a solicitor's undertaking?","The solicitor **personally**, and the **firm**. An undertaking given by a **trainee or staff member** with authority also binds the firm.","trust-fairness-undertakings"],
["Is an undertaking given \"on behalf of my client\" personally binding?","**Yes**, unless personal liability is **clearly excluded** in the wording at the time.","trust-fairness-undertakings"],
["Can the client release a solicitor from an undertaking?","**No.** It must be performed even if the client **withdraws instructions** or it becomes **costly** or difficult. Only the **recipient** can agree to vary or release it.","trust-fairness-undertakings"],
["How is an ambiguous undertaking interpreted?","Generally **in favour of the recipient**, because the giver chose the words.","trust-fairness-undertakings"],
["How is an undertaking enforced?","As **conduct**: breach is misconduct under para 1.3, for the SRA or SDT. Through the **court**: solicitors are **officers of the court**, so the High Court can order performance or compensation without proof of a contract.","trust-fairness-undertakings"],
["Can the SRA award compensation for breach of an undertaking?","**No.** The recipient's route to money is the **court**.","trust-fairness-undertakings"],
["Can an undertaking from a non-solicitor be enforced the same way?","**No.** It cannot be enforced through the SRA or the court's supervisory jurisdiction over solicitors. Another regulated lawyer answers to their **own regulator**.","trust-fairness-undertakings"],
["Is a conditional undertaking binding if the condition fails?","**No.** E.g. \"subject to receipt of funds from X\" does not bind if the funds never arrive.","trust-fairness-undertakings"],
["Seller's solicitor gave a mortgage redemption undertaking; the seller now wants the proceeds paid to him. What should she do?","**Refuse and redeem the mortgage.** The undertaking binds her and only the **buyer's solicitor** could release it. Paying the seller would also breach the **Accounts Rules**.","trust-fairness-undertakings"],
["How should a solicitor give undertakings safely?","Only about matters **within your control**. Undertake to pay only if money is **in client account** or certain, or make it **conditional**. Use **clear wording**. Get the **client's authority**. Keep a **register**.","trust-fairness-undertakings"],
["What should you do if you cannot comply with an undertaking?","Tell the recipient **immediately** and try to agree a **variation**. Do not just let the deadline pass.","trust-fairness-undertakings"],
["Which duty wins if the duty to the court and the duty to the client clash?","The **duty to the court**. But **confidentiality** still applies, so the solution is usually to **withdraw** without saying why.","duty-to-court"],
["What do paras 2.1 and 2.2 prohibit?","**2.1**: misusing or **tampering with evidence**. **2.2**: seeking to **influence the substance of evidence**, e.g. generating false evidence or persuading witnesses to change it.","duty-to-court"],
["What does para 2.3 prohibit, and what payments are allowed?","No **benefit to a witness** dependent on their **evidence** or the **outcome**. You may pay **reasonable expenses**, compensation for **loss of time**, and an expert's **proper fee**.","duty-to-court"],
["Can an expert be paid a contingency fee?","**No.** A fee depending on what the witness says or whether the client wins is prohibited (para 2.3).","duty-to-court"],
["What does para 2.4 require?","Only make assertions or submissions that are **properly arguable**. You can run an argument likely to lose, but not assert a fact you **know is false** or allege **fraud** without a proper basis.","duty-to-court"],
["What do paras 2.5 and 2.6 require?","**2.5**: do not place yourself in **contempt of court**, and comply with court orders. **2.6**: do not **waste the court's time**.","duty-to-court"],
["Give examples of contempt of court.","Breaching an **injunction**. Misusing documents obtained on **disclosure** for another purpose. **Recording** proceedings without permission.","duty-to-court"],
["What does para 2.7 require?","Draw the court's attention to **relevant cases and statutory provisions**, or **procedural irregularities**, likely to have a **material effect** on the outcome. This applies even if they are **against your client** and the opponent missed them.","duty-to-court"],
["Must a solicitor reveal damaging facts to the court?","**No.** The para 2.7 duty is about **law and procedure**, not **facts**. But you must never **mislead**.","duty-to-court"],
["What must you do if you cannot continue without misleading the court?","If the client will not let you correct the position, you must **withdraw**.","duty-to-court"],
["Can you act for a client who admits guilt but pleads not guilty?","**Yes.** Everyone may require the prosecution to **prove its case**. You can continue on a limited basis.","duty-to-court"],
["What can you do for a client who has admitted guilt to you?","**Cross-examine** to test the prosecution evidence. Argue the evidence is **insufficient**. Submit **no case to answer**. Argue points of law, e.g. **inadmissibility**.","duty-to-court"],
["What can you not do for a client who has admitted guilt?","Assert or suggest they **did not commit** it. Suggest **someone else** did. Put forward an **alibi** or other false positive defence. **Call the client** to deny the offence.","duty-to-court"],
["What if the client who admitted guilt insists on giving evidence denying it?","You must **withdraw**.","duty-to-court"],
["Do inconsistent instructions amount to an admission?","**No.** You may question the client. You are not knowingly misleading unless the client actually told you the defence is **false**. Deciding guilt is for the **court**.","duty-to-court"],
["The client says before trial that they will give false evidence. What must you do?","Advise them not to and explain you **cannot act** on that basis. If they insist, **cease to act**.","duty-to-court"],
["You discover the client has already misled the court. What must you do?","Ask for the client's **consent to correct** it. If they refuse, **withdraw**, or you would be complicit (para 1.4).","duty-to-court"],
["Must you tell the court about a client's previous convictions?","**No duty** to tell the court.","duty-to-court"],
["The prosecution wrongly says the client has no previous convictions. What may you do?","You need **not correct** it. But you must **not take advantage**: no assertion of **good character** and no reliance on the mistake in mitigation.","duty-to-court"],
["The client wants to tell the court he has no convictions when he has. What must you do?","**Refuse**. If he insists, **withdraw**.","duty-to-court"],
["What if the client gives the court a false name, address or date of birth?","That **misleads the court**. You must not continue unless the client agrees to **correct** it.","duty-to-court"],
["Do the same rules apply in civil cases?","**Yes.** E.g. a client who wants to **hide a disclosable document** or **exaggerate** a claim: advise that disclosure duties override their wishes, and **withdraw** if they refuse.","duty-to-court"],
["What do you tell the court when withdrawing?","Only that you are withdrawing for **professional reasons** (\"professionally embarrassed\"). **Never** the reason, which is confidential.","duty-to-court"],
["Is confidentiality owed after withdrawing?","**Yes.** You must not tell the **new representative** or the **court** what the client said without consent.","duty-to-court"],
["Can you interview a witness who gave a statement to the other side?","**Yes**: there is **no property in a witness**. In criminal cases it is good practice to tell the **prosecution** first and offer them the chance to attend.","duty-to-court"],
["Preparing a witness v coaching: what is the line?","You **may** explain procedure, cross-examination and the court layout. You must **not** tell them what to say, **rehearse** their evidence, or discuss **other witnesses'** evidence (para 2.2).","duty-to-court"],
["Who can you take instructions from (para 3.1)?","The **client**, or someone **properly authorised** to instruct on their behalf.","instructions-capacity-service"],
["What if you suspect instructions do not reflect the client's wishes?","Do **not act** unless you have **satisfied yourself** that they do (para 3.1).","instructions-capacity-service"],
["Who is the client when you act for a company?","The **company**. You take instructions from an **authorised** director or employee, but that individual is not the client (para 8.1).","instructions-capacity-service"],
["What is the retainer?","The **contract** between solicitor and client. It defines the **scope** of the work and your authority, and is confirmed in writing, usually in the **client care letter**.","instructions-capacity-service"],
["Can you accept instructions passed on by a third party?","**Yes**, but you must **check directly with the client** that they are genuine and the third party is authorised. With an elderly or vulnerable client, **see the client alone**.","instructions-capacity-service"],
["What is undue influence, and when is the risk highest?","Improper pressure, or abuse of a relationship of **trust and confidence**, overriding the client's free will. The risk is highest where the **third party benefits**, e.g. a transfer of a parent's house to a child.","instructions-capacity-service"],
["Can you discuss the client's affairs with a relative who brought the instructions?","Only with the client's **consent** (para 6.3).","instructions-capacity-service"],
["Does paying the fees make a third party the client?","**No.** You act only in the **client's** best interests.","instructions-capacity-service"],
["A third party insists on being present or answers for the client. What should you do?","Insist on a **private meeting**. If you cannot satisfy yourself the instructions are genuine, **do not act**.","instructions-capacity-service"],
["What are the key capacity principles in s.1 MCA 2005?","Adults are **presumed to have capacity**. **All practicable steps** to help must be taken first. An **unwise decision** does not show incapacity. Acts for a person lacking capacity must be in their **best interests** and **least restrictive**.","instructions-capacity-service"],
["What is the s.2 diagnostic test for lack of capacity?","At the material time, the person cannot make the decision because of an **impairment of, or disturbance in the functioning of, the mind or brain**.","instructions-capacity-service"],
["When is a person unable to make a decision (s.3)?","If they cannot **understand** the relevant information, **retain** it, **use or weigh** it, or **communicate** the decision by any means.","instructions-capacity-service"],
["Is capacity all-or-nothing?","**No.** It is **decision-specific** and **time-specific**.","instructions-capacity-service"],
["Does a diagnosis of dementia mean the client lacks capacity?","**No.** Assess the person's ability to make **this decision now**.","instructions-capacity-service"],
["What should you do if you doubt a client's capacity?","Get a **medical opinion** (with consent) and keep a detailed **attendance note**. A medical report is **not** needed in every case: capacity is **presumed**.","instructions-capacity-service"],
["What is the \"golden rule\" for wills?","For elderly or ill testators, have a **doctor witness or approve** the will.","instructions-capacity-service"],
["Who can instruct you if the client lacks capacity?","An **attorney** under a **registered** lasting power of attorney, within its scope. A **deputy** appointed by the **Court of Protection**. Or an attorney under a registered **enduring power** made before 1 October 2007.","instructions-capacity-service"],
["Where must an LPA be registered before use?","With the **Office of the Public Guardian**.","instructions-capacity-service"],
["What happens if the client loses capacity mid-retainer with no attorney or deputy?","Your **authority to act ends**. You may need to protect the client's position, e.g. by applying to the **Court of Protection**.","instructions-capacity-service"],
["Are you free to refuse instructions?","**Generally yes**, but never for a **discriminatory** reason (para 1.1, Equality Act 2010).","instructions-capacity-service"],
["When must you refuse instructions or stop acting?","If acting would **break the law or SRA rules**. If you lack **competence or resources**. If there is a **conflict** or you hold an opponent's **confidential information**. If there is **duress or undue influence**. If the client lacks **capacity** and no one can instruct.","instructions-capacity-service"],
["How can the client end the retainer?","**At any time**, for any reason. They may still have to pay for work done.","instructions-capacity-service"],
["How can the solicitor end the retainer?","Only for **good reason** and on **reasonable notice**.","instructions-capacity-service"],
["What are good reasons for a solicitor to end the retainer?","Failure to pay **money on account** as agreed. **Breakdown of trust and confidence**. The client insists on a **breach of the rules**. A **conflict** arises. You cannot get proper instructions.","instructions-capacity-service"],
["What is reasonable notice when ending a retainer?","It depends on the circumstances. Do not stop just before a **hearing** or **limitation deadline** without giving time to find someone else, and warn the client of key dates.","instructions-capacity-service"],
["Can you keep the client's papers until your bill is paid?","**Generally yes**: a **lien**. But not where it would **seriously prejudice** the client or obstruct proceedings; the court can order handover on terms.","instructions-capacity-service"],
["What do paras 3.2 and 3.3 require?","**3.2**: a **competent** service delivered in a **timely** manner. **3.3**: **maintain your competence** and keep knowledge and skills **up to date**.","instructions-capacity-service"],
["What does para 3.4 require?","Take account of the client's **attributes, needs and circumstances** (e.g. vulnerability, language, disability, age), including **reasonable adjustments**.","instructions-capacity-service"],
["What do paras 3.5 and 3.6 require of supervisors and managers?","**3.5**: you stay **accountable** for work done through those you supervise and must **effectively supervise** it. **3.6**: ensure those you manage are **competent** and kept up to date.","instructions-capacity-service"],
["What should you do if asked to do work you are not competent to do?","Get **help** or **supervision**, **refer** the client, or **decline**. Doing it unsupervised breaches **para 3.2**.","instructions-capacity-service"],
["What are the three section 4 duties in the Code?","**4.1** **account** for financial benefits. **4.2** **safeguard** client money and assets. **4.3** do not **personally hold** client money.","client-money"],
["What does para 4.1 require about commissions?","A financial benefit received because of the client's instructions (e.g. a **commission**) must be **accounted to the client**, unless the client has **agreed otherwise** with proper information.","client-money"],
["What does para 4.2 require?","**Safeguard** money and assets entrusted by clients and others, e.g. keep original **wills, deeds and share certificates** securely.","client-money"],
["Can a solicitor personally hold client money (para 4.3)?","**No**, save as permitted. In an **authorised body** the **firm** holds it. A **freelance** solicitor may receive only limited payments and **cannot operate a client account**.","client-money"],
["What is client money (rule 2.1 Accounts Rules)?","Money for **regulated services** to a client. Money held for a **third party**. Money held as **trustee** or office holder (e.g. **attorney**, **deputy**). Money for **costs** and **unpaid disbursements** received **before a bill** or written notification.","client-money"],
["Is £5,000 \"on account of costs\" before any bill client money?","**Yes.** Money for costs received **before a bill** is **client money**.","client-money"],
["Is payment of a delivered bill that includes an unpaid disbursement client money?","**No**, it is **firm money**. But the firm must pay the third party **promptly**.","client-money"],
["Is money received as executor client money?","**Yes**, it is held as **trustee** or office holder.","client-money"],
["What is a client account?","An account at a bank or building society in **England and Wales**, in the **firm's name**, with **\"client\"** in the title. Client money must be kept **separate** from firm money.","client-money"],
["When must client money be paid into client account?","**Promptly** on receipt, unless an exception applies, e.g. the client asks **in writing** for it to be held elsewhere.","client-money"],
["When can client money be withdrawn?","Only for the **purpose** for which it is held, on the client's or third party's **instructions**, or with **SRA authorisation**.","client-money"],
["When can a firm take its costs from client money?","Only after giving the client a **bill or other written notification** of costs. Finishing the work is **not enough**.","client-money"],
["Can one client's money be used for another?","**No.** Only withdraw for a client if you hold **sufficient funds for that client**. Otherwise you create a **shortfall**.","client-money"],
["When must client money be returned?","**Promptly** once there is no longer any **proper reason** to hold it.","client-money"],
["How is a mixed payment handled?","Pay it into client account (or allocate it) and move the **firm's share out promptly**.","client-money"],
["What is the banking facility ban (rule 3.3)?","Do not use client account to provide **banking facilities**. Payments in and out must relate to the **regulated services** you deliver. E.g. refuse to hold sale proceeds to pay **household bills** or receive a client's **salary**.","client-money"],
["Must interest be paid on client money?","A **fair sum of interest**, under a written policy, unless agreed otherwise **in writing** with the client.","client-money"],
["What if client money is improperly withdrawn by an innocent mistake?","**Correct breaches promptly**. Replace the money **immediately** from the **firm's own money**, whether or not anyone was at fault.","client-money"],
["How often must client accounts be reconciled?","At least every **five weeks** (rule 8.3). The bank statement, cash book and client ledgers must agree.","client-money"],
["When is an accountant's report needed?","Annually, within **six months** of the end of the accounting period (rule 12.1). It is exempt if all client money comes from the **Legal Aid Agency**, or average balance is not over **£10,000** and maximum not over **£250,000**.","client-money"],
["Which accountant's reports must currently be sent to the SRA?","Only a **qualified** report showing client money **at risk**.","client-money"],
["Who is responsible for Accounts Rules compliance?","The **COFA** must take all reasonable steps to ensure compliance and report **serious breaches** to the SRA promptly. All **managers** are responsible too.","client-money"],
["What is a third-party managed account?","An alternative where a **regulated payment provider** holds the money, so the firm does **not hold client money** itself.","client-money"],
["Is the rule requiring every firm to send its accountant's report to the SRA in force for the exam?","**No.** Examinable law is as at **11 September 2026**. The new requirement applies from **1 April 2027**. The ban on some managers being COLP or COFA was **paused**.","client-money"],
["What is a referral fee?","A payment, or other benefit, for **introducing or referring** a client.","business-referrals-publicity"],
["What is a separate business?","A business you own, manage or work for that is **not authorised** by the SRA or another legal regulator.","business-referrals-publicity"],
["What must clients be told under para 5.1(a) and (b)?","Any **financial or other interest** you have in referring them, or an **introducer** has in referring them to you. Any relevant **fee sharing arrangement**.","business-referrals-publicity"],
["What form must a fee-sharing agreement take?","It must be **in writing** (para 5.1(c)).","business-referrals-publicity"],
["Are referral payments allowed in criminal matters?","**No.** No payments to or from introducers for clients subject to **criminal proceedings** (para 5.1(d)).","business-referrals-publicity"],
["What does para 5.1(e) require?","A referred client must **not have been acquired** in a way that would breach SRA rules if the introducer were regulated, e.g. **cold calling**.","business-referrals-publicity"],
["How does the SRA treat a payment that looks like a referral fee?","It is **treated as a referral fee unless you show otherwise**, e.g. a genuine fee for marketing (para 5.2).","business-referrals-publicity"],
["Are referral fees allowed in personal injury claims?","**No.** Paying and receiving are **banned** in **personal injury or death** claims by **LASPO 2012 s.56**. Client **consent cannot cure** the ban.","business-referrals-publicity"],
["When are referral fees allowed?","In other matters (e.g. **conveyancing**, wills, family) if **disclosed**, **in writing** where fees are shared, and **independence** is preserved.","business-referrals-publicity"],
["What does independence require in an introducer arrangement?","The introducer must not **influence your advice**. You give independent advice even if unwelcome to the introducer. You cannot enter an arrangement requiring you to act against the client's interests.","business-referrals-publicity"],
["When can you refer a client to a separate business (para 5.3)?","Only with the client's **informed consent**, including understanding that it is **not regulated by the SRA**, so SRA protections (**Compensation Fund**, **indemnity insurance**, **Legal Ombudsman**) do not apply.","business-referrals-publicity"],
["What does para 8.10(c) prohibit?","Misleading clients into thinking an **unregulated business** is **regulated by the SRA**.","business-referrals-publicity"],
["What does para 5.4 prohibit?","Being a manager, employee, member or interest holder of a business using the name \"**solicitors**\", or describing its work as done by solicitors, unless it is an **authorised body**.","business-referrals-publicity"],
["What do paras 5.5 and 5.6 require?","**5.5**: holders of a practising certificate or registration must deliver an **annual return** to the SRA. **5.6**: a solicitor doing **reserved activities** in a **non-commercial body** must ensure it has adequate **indemnity insurance**.","business-referrals-publicity"],
["What does para 8.8 require of publicity?","**Accurate**, **not misleading** information, including about **charges** and when **interest** is payable.","business-referrals-publicity"],
["Give examples of misleading publicity.","Not stating whether prices include **VAT** and **disbursements**. \"No win no fee\" without explaining **deductions**. Claiming expertise you lack. Misleading **reviews**.","business-referrals-publicity"],
["What does para 8.9 prohibit?","**Unsolicited approaches** to **members of the public** to advertise legal services. Exception: **current or former clients**.","business-referrals-publicity"],
["Which approaches breach para 8.9?","**Cold calling**, **door-stepping**, approaching people at an **accident scene** or in **hospital**, unsolicited **targeted texts**.","business-referrals-publicity"],
["Which marketing does not breach para 8.9?","General **advertising** (leaflets to every door, online and TV ads, sponsorship, social media posts). Contacting **existing or former clients**. Approaches to **businesses**, if not misleading.","business-referrals-publicity"],
["Can you use an introducer who cold-calls for you?","**No.** Para **5.1(e)** stops you getting round para 8.9 that way.","business-referrals-publicity"],
["In what order should you check a referral question?","1: Is the fee **banned** outright (personal injury: statute; criminal: Code)? 2: Is there **disclosure** and a **written** fee-sharing agreement? 3: Is your advice still **independent**?","business-referrals-publicity"],
["What is an own interest conflict?","Where your duty to act in a client's best interests **conflicts, or there is a significant risk** it may conflict, with **your own interests** in that or a related matter. Your interests include your **firm's** and those of people **close to you**.","conflicts-of-interest"],
["What is a client conflict?","Where your separate duties to **two or more clients** in the **same or related matters** conflict, or there is a **significant risk** they may.","conflicts-of-interest"],
["What is a \"significant risk\" of conflict, and when do you check?","A **real, not fanciful**, likelihood. You must consider conflicts at the start and **throughout** the matter.","conflicts-of-interest"],
["What is a related matter?","Matters **connected** in some way, e.g. the same **property**, **transaction** or **dispute**, even if not identical.","conflicts-of-interest"],
["Can a client consent to you acting despite an own interest conflict?","**No.** Para **6.1** has **no exceptions**, and consent does **not** cure it.","conflicts-of-interest"],
["What situations commonly give rise to an own interest conflict?","The client buying from or selling to you, your **family** or firm. A **financial interest** in the matter. **Loans** to or from the client. A **mistake** giving the client a **claim against you**. A **significant gift**. A **personal relationship**. **Commission** depending on your advice.","conflicts-of-interest"],
["A client wants to leave you a significant gift in her will. What should you do?","Advise her to take **independent legal advice**. If she **refuses**, **do not act** in preparing the will.","conflicts-of-interest"],
["Are small gifts from clients a problem?","**No.** **Trivial gifts**, such as chocolates or a bottle of wine at Christmas, are fine.","conflicts-of-interest"],
["Does independent advice remove every risk with a client's gift?","**No.** Still consider **undue influence**, and you may face a challenge by disappointed family members.","conflicts-of-interest"],
["When can you act despite a client conflict (para 6.2)?","Only if the clients have a **substantially common interest** or are **competing for the same objective**, **and** all three conditions are met.","conflicts-of-interest"],
["What are the three conditions in para 6.2?","1: All clients give **informed consent**, given or evidenced **in writing**. 2: **Effective safeguards** for confidential information where appropriate. 3: You are satisfied it is **reasonable** to act for all.","conflicts-of-interest"],
["What is a substantially common interest?","A clear **common purpose** and a **strong consensus** on how to achieve it; any conflict is peripheral. E.g. co-founders setting up a company on **agreed terms**, or spouses buying a house together.","conflicts-of-interest"],
["What is \"competing for the same objective\"?","Clients competing for an **asset, contract or business opportunity** which, if one gets it, is **unattainable** to the others, sought through an **insolvency** process, an **auction or tender**, or a **non-public bid or offer**. E.g. **two bidders** in a sealed-bid auction for the same business.","conflicts-of-interest"],
["What factors affect whether it is reasonable to act for both clients?","Is one client **vulnerable**, much less sophisticated or in a far **weaker bargaining position**? Can you give each the advice they need? Is the matter likely to become **contentious**? If so, you cannot act for both.","conflicts-of-interest"],
["What safeguards are usually used for a client conflict?","**Information barriers**: separate teams, IT and physical separation, staff undertakings, especially where clients are **competing**.","conflicts-of-interest"],
["What if a conflict becomes real during the matter?","You must usually **stop acting** for at least one client, and often **all**, because you hold each client's confidential information.","conflicts-of-interest"],
["Can you act for buyer and seller of the same property?","Usually **no**: their interests are opposed. Only rarely within an exception (e.g. a **family transfer** on agreed terms with no negotiation), with all conditions met.","conflicts-of-interest"],
["Can you act for borrower and lender on a residential mortgage?","Usually **yes**, if the mortgage is on the lender's **standard terms** and instructions are limited to usual requirements (e.g. the **UK Finance Mortgage Lenders' Handbook**).","conflicts-of-interest"],
["When does acting for borrower and lender become a conflict?","If terms are **individually negotiated**, or you learn the borrower is **misleading the lender** (e.g. a hidden cashback). Then **stop acting**; confidentiality may stop you telling the lender.","conflicts-of-interest"],
["Joint clients fall out. What should you do?","Usually **stop acting for both**. Continue for one only if you hold no **material confidential information** of the other, or the other **consents**.","conflicts-of-interest"],
["Can you act for a company and its director on the director's service contract?","A **conflict** is likely. Advise the director to take **separate advice**.","conflicts-of-interest"],
["Can you act for the seller and the buyer of a business?","**No.** Their interests are opposed in the same transaction and **no exception** applies.","conflicts-of-interest"],
["Is a conflict for one solicitor a conflict for the whole firm?","**Usually yes.** The **Code for Firms** applies the same rules, because the firm is the entity retained.","conflicts-of-interest"],
["What does para 6.3 require?","Keep the affairs of **current and former clients** confidential unless disclosure is **required or permitted by law** or the **client consents**.","confidentiality-disclosure"],
["What does the duty of confidentiality cover?","**All information** about the client's affairs, however learned, including the **fact that they are a client**.","confidentiality-disclosure"],
["Does confidentiality end with the retainer or the client's death?","**No.** It continues after both. After death, the right to waive passes to the **personal representatives**.","confidentiality-disclosure"],
["Who is bound by the duty of confidentiality in a firm?","**Everyone**, including support staff. The firm must protect it with its **systems**, e.g. secure IT.","confidentiality-disclosure"],
["How does confidentiality differ from legal professional privilege?","**Confidentiality** is a conduct and equitable duty covering **all** information, and the **law can override it**. **LPP** is a **legal right** to refuse disclosure, even to a court, covering only privileged communications. It is overridden very rarely, mainly by the **crime/fraud exception**.","confidentiality-disclosure"],
["What is legal advice privilege?","Confidential communications between **lawyer and client** made for the dominant purpose of giving or receiving **legal advice**, whether or not litigation is in view.","confidentiality-disclosure"],
["What is litigation privilege?","Confidential communications between lawyer or client and **third parties** (e.g. experts), made when litigation is **in progress or reasonably in prospect**, for the **dominant purpose** of that litigation.","confidentiality-disclosure"],
["What is the crime/fraud exception?","There is **no privilege** for communications made to **further a crime or fraud**.","confidentiality-disclosure"],
["When is disclosure required by law?","A **court order** or witness summons (subject to privilege). A **SAR** to the **National Crime Agency** under **POCA 2002** or the **Terrorism Act 2000**. Statutory requests (e.g. HMRC, the SRA).","confidentiality-disclosure"],
["When is disclosure permitted in exceptional cases?","To prevent a **criminal act** reasonably believed likely to cause **serious bodily harm**. To protect a **child or vulnerable adult** from abuse. Disclose only what is **necessary**, to the appropriate authority.","confidentiality-disclosure"],
["Can you share client information with counsel or an expert?","**Yes**, under **implied authority**, to carry out the client's instructions. The same applies within the firm.","confidentiality-disclosure"],
["What does para 6.4 require?","Make the client aware of **all information material to the matter** of which you have knowledge.","confidentiality-disclosure"],
["What are the exceptions to the duty of disclosure in para 6.4?","(a) **Legal restrictions** for national security or crime prevention (e.g. **tipping off**). (b) The client's **written informed consent** to non-disclosure. (c) Risk of **serious physical or mental injury**. (d) A **privileged document** you know of only through **mistaken disclosure**.","confidentiality-disclosure"],
["The other side mistakenly sends you their privileged advice. What should you do?","You need **not** tell your client its contents (6.4(d)). Good practice: **stop reading**, tell the sender, **return** it and do **not use** it.","confidentiality-disclosure"],
["You hold client A's confidential information that is material to client B. Which duty wins?","**Confidentiality**. You cannot breach A's confidence, so you usually **cannot act** for B, unless A **consents** or a 6.4 exception applies.","confidentiality-disclosure"],
["When can you not act against a current or former client (para 6.5)?","Where you hold that client's **confidential information** that is **material** to the matter and the new client's interest is **adverse** to theirs.","confidentiality-disclosure"],
["What are the two ways out under para 6.5?","(a) **Effective measures** leaving **no real risk of disclosure**. Or (b) that client's **informed consent**, given or evidenced **in writing**.","confidentiality-disclosure"],
["Can you act against a former client in an unrelated matter?","**Yes.** The restriction applies only where you hold **material** confidential information.","confidentiality-disclosure"],
["What makes an information barrier effective?","Separate teams in different locations, locked files and **IT controls**, staff **undertakings**, monitoring and no shared supervisors. It should be **established in advance**, not set up hastily.","confidentiality-disclosure"],
["Who bears the burden of showing an information barrier works?","The **firm** must show **no real risk** of disclosure. The burden is **heavy**, and harder to meet in a **small firm** or where the **same people** are involved (Bolkiah v KPMG).","confidentiality-disclosure"],
["Para 6.2 v para 6.5: how do you tell them apart?","**6.2**: two **current** clients with conflicting interests in the same or a related matter. **6.5**: acting against a current or **former** client whose **material confidential information** you hold. 6.5 has **no common interest exception**.","confidentiality-disclosure"],
["What do paras 7.1 and 7.2 require?","**7.1**: keep up to date with and **follow the law and regulation**. **7.2**: be able to **justify your decisions and actions** to show compliance, so keep good records.","cooperation-reporting"],
["What does para 7.3 require?","**Cooperate** with the SRA, other regulators, **ombudsmen** and bodies overseeing or investigating legal services.","cooperation-reporting"],
["What does para 7.4 require?","**Respond promptly** to the SRA with full and accurate explanations, information and documents. Ensure relevant information is **available for inspection**.","cooperation-reporting"],
["What does para 7.5 prohibit?","**Attempting to prevent anyone** from providing information to the SRA or any body with regulatory, investigatory or prosecutory functions. So an **NDA** must not stop reporting.","cooperation-reporting"],
["When must you notify the SRA about yourself (para 7.6)?","**Promptly** if you are subject to a **criminal charge, conviction or caution** (subject to the Rehabilitation of Offenders Act 1974). A **relevant insolvency event**. A **material change** to information given to the SRA. Information given being **false, misleading, incomplete or inaccurate**.","cooperation-reporting"],
["What counts as a relevant insolvency event?","E.g. **bankruptcy**, an **individual voluntary arrangement**, a **debt relief order**.","cooperation-reporting"],
["Must a solicitor report a drink-driving conviction?","**Yes.** It is a **conviction**, notifiable under **para 7.6**.","cooperation-reporting"],
["What does para 7.7 require?","**Report promptly** to the SRA, or another approved regulator, facts you **reasonably believe** are **capable of amounting to a serious breach** by **any person** they regulate, **including you**.","cooperation-reporting"],
["What does para 7.8 add?","Inform the SRA promptly of matters you reasonably believe it should know so it can **investigate** whether a serious breach occurred, even if you are **not sure**.","cooperation-reporting"],
["Must you be sure a serious breach has happened before reporting?","**No.** Report once you **reasonably believe** the facts are **capable of amounting** to one. Do not delay to finish your own **investigation**.","cooperation-reporting"],
["Whose breaches must be reported?","**Anyone** the SRA regulates: yourself, colleagues, your **managers**, solicitors at **other firms** and SRA-regulated firms. For a **barrister**, report to the **BSB**.","cooperation-reporting"],
["What protection does para 7.9 give whistleblowers?","No one may be subjected to **detrimental treatment** for reporting, or proposing to report, on a reasonable belief, whether or not the SRA acts.","cooperation-reporting"],
["What does para 7.10 require?","Act **promptly** to take any **remedial action** the SRA requests.","cooperation-reporting"],
["What does para 7.11 require when things go wrong?","Be **honest and open** with clients. If they suffer loss or harm, **put matters right** if possible and **explain fully and promptly** what happened and its likely impact.","cooperation-reporting"],
["Can you meet your reporting duty by telling the COLP or COFA?","**Yes** (para 7.12), but only if you reasonably expect them to **pass it on**. If the COLP is the wrongdoer or refuses, report **directly** to the SRA.","cooperation-reporting"],
["What factors make a breach serious?","**Dishonesty** or lack of **integrity** (always serious). **Harm** to clients, especially the **vulnerable**. A **pattern** or systemic failure. Deliberate or reckless **intent**. **Seniority**. **Concealment**. Impact on **public confidence**.","cooperation-reporting"],
["Must a minor, isolated breach corrected promptly be reported?","**Usually not**, but the firm must **record** it, as it keeps records of all breaches.","cooperation-reporting"],
["Does repaying misused client money remove the duty to report?","**No.** Putting the money back does **not** remove the duty.","cooperation-reporting"],
["A junior finds a partner has paid personal bills from client account. What should he do?","**Report promptly** to the SRA, directly or via the COLP if confident they will report. Do not raise it with the partner first and wait, or investigate fully first.","cooperation-reporting"],
["Your mistake may give the client a claim against you. What must you do?","Tell the client **promptly and honestly**. Advise **independent legal advice**. **Stop acting** if a significant risk of conflict remains: para 6.1 has no exceptions. **Notify your insurers** promptly.","cooperation-reporting"],
["What must you not do after a mistake that harms a client?","**Conceal** it, ask the client to **sign away rights** without advice, or **settle secretly** to avoid reporting.","cooperation-reporting"],
["What does para 8.1 require?","**Identify who you are acting for** in any matter. For a **company**, the client is the **company**. For **joint clients**, be clear you act for both and what happens if they disagree.","complaints-legal-ombudsman"],
["What do paras 8.6 and 8.7 require?","**8.6**: information clients can **understand**, so they can make **informed decisions**. **8.7**: the **best possible information** on **how the matter will be priced** and the **likely overall cost**, at the start and as the matter progresses.","complaints-legal-ombudsman"],
["What goes in the client care letter?","The **scope** of work. Who does it and who supervises. The **basis of charging**. A **costs estimate** including **disbursements** and **VAT**. **Complaints** rights.","complaints-legal-ombudsman"],
["What if a costs estimate turns out too low?","**Update** the client **promptly**.","complaints-legal-ombudsman"],
["Which services need published price and service information under the Transparency Rules?","**Residential conveyancing**, **uncontested probate**, **motoring offences**, **employment tribunal** claims, **debt recovery** (up to £100,000), **immigration** (not asylum) and business **licensing** applications.","complaints-legal-ombudsman"],
["What else must every firm publish?","Its **complaints procedure** (including LeO and the SRA) and the SRA **digital badge**.","complaints-legal-ombudsman"],
["What must clients be told in writing at the start about complaints (para 8.3)?","Their right to complain about **services and charges**. **How** and **to whom**. Any right to complain to the **Legal Ombudsman** and **when**.","complaints-legal-ombudsman"],
["What must you tell a client whose complaint is unresolved after 8 weeks (para 8.4)?","In writing: their right to complain to **LeO**, the **time frame** and contact details. If your procedure is exhausted: that you **cannot settle** it, the name and website of an approved **ADR** body, and **whether you agree** to use it.","complaints-legal-ombudsman"],
["How must complaints be dealt with (para 8.5)?","**Promptly, fairly and free of charge**. You cannot make a client **waive** LeO rights.","complaints-legal-ombudsman"],
["What does the Legal Ombudsman deal with?","Complaints about **poor service** by authorised lawyers, under **Part 6 LSA 2007**. It is **not** a disciplinary body: **misconduct** goes to the **SRA**.","complaints-legal-ombudsman"],
["On what basis does LeO decide complaints?","What is **fair and reasonable** in all the circumstances, not only strict legal liability.","complaints-legal-ombudsman"],
["Who can complain to LeO?","**Individuals**. **Micro-enterprises** (fewer than **10** employees and turnover or balance sheet up to **€2 million**). **Charities**, clubs and **trustees** under **£1 million**. **Personal representatives** and **beneficiaries** of estates.","complaints-legal-ombudsman"],
["Who cannot complain to LeO?","**Large businesses**, **public bodies**, someone complaining about the **other side's** solicitor, and authorised lawyers about their own services.","complaints-legal-ombudsman"],
["When will LeO normally accept a complaint?","Only after the firm's **final response**, or after **8 weeks** from the complaint, unless exceptional circumstances apply.","complaints-legal-ombudsman"],
["What is the six-month LeO time limit?","The complaint must reach LeO within **six months** of the firm's **final response**, if that response mentioned LeO and the six-month limit.","complaints-legal-ombudsman"],
["What is the outer time limit for LeO complaints since 1 April 2023?","**One year** from the act or omission, **or one year** from when the complainant **should reasonably have known** of cause for complaint.","complaints-legal-ombudsman"],
["Can LeO extend its time limits?","**Yes**, in **exceptional circumstances**.","complaints-legal-ombudsman"],
["Which old LeO time limits no longer apply?","The **six-year** and **three-year** limits, replaced on **1 April 2023**.","complaints-legal-ombudsman"],
["What remedies can LeO order?","An **apology**. **Refund or reduce fees**. **Compensation** for financial loss and **distress and inconvenience**. **Put right** an error at the firm's expense. **Interest**.","complaints-legal-ombudsman"],
["What is the cap on LeO remedies?","**£50,000** per complaint.","complaints-legal-ombudsman"],
["Is an ombudsman's decision binding?","If the complainant **accepts** it, it is **binding** on both and enforceable, and they cannot sue on the same matter. If they **reject** it, it is not binding and they may go to court.","complaints-legal-ombudsman"],
["Who pays LeO's case fee?","The **firm**, subject to exceptions where it is not at fault.","complaints-legal-ombudsman"],
["What are the types of authorised firm?","**Recognised sole practice**. **Recognised body** (owned and managed by lawyers). **Licensed body** (**ABS**), where **non-lawyers** own or manage, licensed under the LSA 2007.","firms-compliance-protections"],
["Who are a firm's managers?","**Partners** (partnership), **members** (LLP) or **directors** (company).","firms-compliance-protections"],
["What does section 2 of the Code for Firms require?","Effective **governance, systems and controls**. **Records** showing compliance. Ensure managers and staff comply. Monitor **financial stability**. Identify, monitor and manage **material risks**.","firms-compliance-protections"],
["What does section 3 of the Code for Firms require?","**Cooperate** with the SRA. **Notify** changes, e.g. ownership or financial difficulty. **Report serious breaches** and matters the SRA should investigate.","firms-compliance-protections"],
["Who is responsible for a firm's compliance with the Code for Firms?","The **managers**. Where managers share management responsibility, it is **joint and several**: each manager can be held responsible for the whole.","firms-compliance-protections"],
["What must a firm do about bullying and harassment?","**Challenge** bullying, harassment and discrimination. Its systems must support **reporting** without detriment.","firms-compliance-protections"],
["What is the COLP responsible for under the Code of Conduct for Firms?","Taking all reasonable steps to ensure compliance with the **terms and conditions of the firm's authorisation**, and with the **SRA's regulatory arrangements** by the firm, its managers, employees and interest holders, **except the Accounts Rules** (the COFA's job) (para **9.1**).\nThe COLP must **promptly report** to the SRA any facts reasonably believed capable of amounting to a **serious breach**.","firms-compliance-protections"],
["What is the COFA responsible for?","All reasonable steps to ensure compliance with the **SRA Accounts Rules**, and **promptly reporting** serious Accounts Rules breaches.","firms-compliance-protections"],
["Who can be a COLP or a COFA?","**COLP**: must be a **lawyer** working in the firm, approved by the SRA. **COFA**: **need not be a lawyer**, approved by the SRA. In a small firm one person can hold **both** roles.","firms-compliance-protections"],
["Can a manager be the COLP or COFA for the exam?","**Yes.** The rule barring some managers in higher-risk firms was **paused** at the end of September 2026, so the current rule applies at the **11 September 2026** date.","firms-compliance-protections"],
["What does professional indemnity insurance cover?","Civil claims for loss caused by the firm's **negligence** or other civil liability in its legal work. It is **compulsory** for every authorised firm.","firms-compliance-protections"],
["What are the PII requirements?","Cover with a **participating insurer** on the SRA's **Minimum Terms and Conditions**.","firms-compliance-protections"],
["What is the minimum PII cover?","**£2 million** for each and every claim. **£3 million** for **LLPs and companies**.","firms-compliance-protections"],
["Can the insurer refuse a client's claim because the firm breached the policy?","**No.** Under the MTC it must pay and may **recover from the firm** afterwards. **Excesses** are paid by the firm, not deducted from the client's compensation.","firms-compliance-protections"],
["What happens to PII when a firm closes with no successor?","The policy gives **six years' run-off cover**. Later claims go to the **Solicitors Indemnity Fund**, which the **SRA** has managed since **1 October 2023**.","firms-compliance-protections"],
["What is the SRA Compensation Fund?","A **discretionary** fund of **last resort**, paid for by solicitors and firms. There is **no right** to a payment.","firms-compliance-protections"],
["When does the Compensation Fund pay?","For loss caused by a solicitor's or employee's **dishonesty**, or a **failure to account** for money held, that **cannot be recovered elsewhere**.","firms-compliance-protections"],
["What are the Compensation Fund limits?","Usual maximum **£2 million** per claim. Claims normally within **12 months** of becoming aware of the loss.","firms-compliance-protections"],
["When is the Compensation Fund not available?","For services from an **unregulated** business. Nor is it a substitute for **PII** where the loss was caused by **negligence**.","firms-compliance-protections"],
["Which protection fits which loss?","**Negligence**: **PII**. **Theft or dishonesty** with no other recovery: **Compensation Fund**. **Poor service**: **Legal Ombudsman**. Negligence claim after six-year run-off: **SIF**.","firms-compliance-protections"],
["Does the SRA itself award compensation?","**No.**","firms-compliance-protections"],
["What must clients be told about regulation (paras 8.10 and 8.11)?","**8.10**: **whether and how** the services are regulated, and never present an **unauthorised** business as SRA-regulated. **8.11**: which **regulatory protections** apply (so say clearly when they do not).","firms-compliance-protections"],
["Can a solicitor in an unregulated business carry out reserved legal activities?","**No.** E.g. no **conducting litigation**, rights of audience, or reserved conveyancing and probate work there.","firms-compliance-protections"],
["What is an SRA warning notice?","The SRA's view of how the Principles and Code apply to a specific risk. It is **not a new rule**, but **failing to have regard** to it is likely to be treated as evidence of a breach.","ndas-slapps"],
["Are NDAs lawful?","**Yes**, and often legitimate, e.g. protecting **trade secrets** or **settlement terms**. But the SRA says they **should not be used routinely**.","ndas-slapps"],
["What must an NDA not prevent or deter?","Reporting to the **SRA** or another **regulator** (para 7.5). Reporting an offence to the **police** or helping a **criminal investigation**. A **protected disclosure** (whistleblowing). Disclosures **required by law** or to **legal, medical or tax advisers**.","ndas-slapps"],
["What does s.17 Victims and Prisoners Act 2024 do?","From **1 October 2025**, a confidentiality clause is **void** so far as it would stop a person who reasonably considers themselves a **victim of crime** disclosing to the **police**, a **lawyer**, a **regulator** or others listed.","ndas-slapps"],
["Which NDA drafting tactics should solicitors avoid?","Clauses known to be **unenforceable**. **Warranties**, **indemnities** or **clawback** clauses to deter permitted disclosures. **Undue pressure** or **oppressive time limits**. Discouraging **independent advice**. Refusing a **copy** of the agreement.","ndas-slapps"],
["How should NDA terms be drafted?","In **plain English**, saying clearly what can be disclosed and to whom. Take particular care with **unrepresented** or **vulnerable** parties (para 1.2).","ndas-slapps"],
["Which duties are engaged by improper NDAs?","Principles **2** (public trust) and **5** (integrity). Code paras **1.2** (unfair advantage), **1.4** (misleading) and **7.5** (preventing reporting).","ndas-slapps"],
["A client wants an NDA stopping an employee talking to the police or a regulator. What should you do?","**Do not draft it in that form.** The NDA may protect the settlement terms, but must make clear that reports to **police**, **regulators** and **protected disclosures** are not prevented.","ndas-slapps"],
["What is a SLAPP?","**Misuse** of the legal system, including **threats** of litigation, to **stifle** lawful public scrutiny or publication on matters of **public interest**. Targets are typically **journalists**, academics, campaigners and whistleblowers.","ndas-slapps"],
["How does s.195 ECCTA 2023 define a SLAPP?","For **economic crime** claims, four conditions: (a) the claimant's conduct restrains, or is intended to restrain, **freedom of speech**; (b) the information has to do with **economic crime**; (c) the disclosure is for a purpose related to the **public interest in combating economic crime**; (d) the claimant's behaviour is intended to cause **harm beyond** that in properly conducted litigation.","ndas-slapps"],
["What does s.194 ECCTA 2023 provide?","Court rules allowing an economic crime SLAPP to be **struck out early** unless the claimant shows it is **more likely than not to succeed**, with **costs protection** for the defendant. Now **CPR 3.4(2)(d)** and **CPR 44.2(9)**, in force since **18 June 2025**.","ndas-slapps"],
["Are the SRA's SLAPP expectations limited to economic crime?","**No.** They are **wider** than s.195 and cover any abusive litigation conduct.","ndas-slapps"],
["What are the SLAPP red flags?","A **publication on a matter of public importance**. A marked **imbalance of power**. A client unwilling to take advice or with unclear **motives**.","ndas-slapps"],
["What causes of action are common in SLAPPs?","**Defamation**, **privacy**, **data protection**, **breach of confidence** and **harassment**.","ndas-slapps"],
["What counts as a meritless claim in SLAPP conduct?","Threatening claims that are **not properly arguable**. **Exaggerated** remedies, damages or costs, e.g. threatening **imprisonment** in a civil claim.","ndas-slapps"],
["What counts as oppressive SLAPP conduct?","**Aggressive** letters. Disproportionate correspondence. Vague allegations to a lay person. Implying the recipient **cannot seek advice**. Unreasonably **short deadlines**, e.g. for a **right of reply**.","ndas-slapps"],
["What counts as improper purpose in SLAPP conduct?","Threats to pressure for an **unconnected outcome**, stall a process or close down enquiries, sometimes with **no intention** of suing. Targeting an **individual journalist** rather than the publisher.","ndas-slapps"],
["How can labels be misused in SLAPP letters?","\"**Without prejudice**\" on a letter that is not a genuine settlement attempt. \"**Not for publication**\" or \"**private and confidential**\" to suggest the recipient cannot publish or seek advice. Headings like \"**Legal Notice**\" falsely suggesting court authority.","ndas-slapps"],
["What should a solicitor check before sending a pre-action letter?","Take sufficient instructions and reasonable steps to check the claim is **properly arguable** (para **2.4**).","ndas-slapps"],
["Which duties are engaged by SLAPP conduct?","Principles **1**, **2**, **3** (independence) and **5**. Code paras **1.2**, **1.4**, **2.4** and **2.6**.","ndas-slapps"],
["Should solicitors report SLAPP conduct by other solicitors?","**Yes**, where it may be a **serious breach** (paras 7.7-7.9), and no one may be treated detrimentally for doing so.","ndas-slapps"],
["What is usually the best answer in an NDA or SLAPP question?","Keep the client's **legitimate aim** but remove the **misleading, oppressive or reporting-blocking** element. Both extremes are usually wrong: blindly following the client, or refusing to act at all.","ndas-slapps"]];

;
window.SQE_QA = window.SQE_QA || {};
window.SQE_QA["ethics"] = [
 {
  "t": "principles-competence",
  "q": "Paul, a solicitor, signs an attestation clause on a client's deed stating that he witnessed the client sign it. In fact the client signed it at home and Paul was not present. Paul genuinely believed this was a harmless formality that many solicitors follow, and he did not realise that anyone would regard it as dishonest.\n\nHow is Paul's conduct most likely to be judged?",
  "o": [
   "As honest, because he genuinely believed that what he did was a harmless and common formality.",
   "As honest, because he did not realise that ordinary decent people would regard his conduct as dishonest.",
   "As dishonest, as he knew he had not witnessed it and ordinary decent people would see it as dishonest.",
   "As a lack of competence only, because the client's signature on the deed was genuine.",
   "As dishonest only if the client or a third party suffered a financial loss as a result."
  ],
  "a": 2,
  "e": "The **Ivey test** asks first what Paul **actually knew or believed** about the facts: he knew he had not witnessed the signature. It then asks whether, given that knowledge, his conduct was dishonest by the standards of **ordinary decent people**. There is **no requirement** that he realised others would see it as dishonest, and loss is not an element. A finding of dishonesty almost always leads to **strike-off**."
 },
 {
  "t": "principles-competence",
  "q": "Rosa qualified as a solicitor eight months ago. She asks her training principal how many hours of continuing professional development she must complete each year to keep her practising certificate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?",
  "o": [
   "There is no minimum, but she must reflect on her learning needs and make an annual declaration.",
   "She must complete at least 16 hours of accredited training each practice year.",
   "She must complete at least 16 hours a year, of which a quarter must be on ethics and professional conduct.",
   "There is no requirement at all, because competence is assessed only at the point of qualification.",
   "She must pass an annual SRA competence assessment set at the level of the Threshold Standard."
  ],
  "a": 0,
  "e": "There is **no minimum number of CPD hours**. To meet **Code para 3.3** (maintain your competence and keep your knowledge and skills up to date), solicitors must **reflect** on their practice, identify and address their learning needs, and confirm this in an **annual declaration** when renewing their practising certificate. The Threshold Standard describes the level expected of a Day One solicitor; it is not an annual test."
 },
 {
  "t": "principles-competence",
  "q": "The SRA has investigated Gethin, a solicitor in a traditional partnership, for serious misconduct that does not involve money laundering or any other economic crime. It considers that a fine of about £40,000 would be appropriate, and that suspension might also be justified.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the matter can be dealt with?",
  "o": [
   "The SRA can impose the £40,000 fine itself, because its fining powers are unlimited for individual solicitors.",
   "The SRA can impose the fine and suspend Gethin itself, subject to an appeal to the High Court.",
   "The case must go to the Legal Ombudsman, which can fine solicitors up to £50,000.",
   "The SRA cannot impose that fine itself, so it would go to the Tribunal, which can fine without limit.",
   "The SRA can impose a fine of up to £25,000 and order Gethin's firm to pay the balance."
  ],
  "a": 3,
  "e": "The **SRA's** own fining power is limited to **£25,000** for individuals and traditional firms, except for **economic crime** matters, where it is unlimited. Serious cases go to the **Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal**, which can **strike off**, **suspend** and impose an **unlimited fine**. The Legal Ombudsman deals with poor service, not discipline, and appeals from the Tribunal go to the High Court."
 },
 {
  "t": "trust-fairness-undertakings",
  "q": "Ben, a trainee at Morton LLP, acting for the seller, Mrs Ellis, told the buyer's solicitor by email: \"We will redeem the Coastal Bank mortgage from the completion money.\" Completion takes place. Mrs Ellis then instructs the firm to pay the whole of the proceeds to her instead, saying she will deal with the bank herself.\n\nWhat should Morton LLP do?",
  "o": [
   "Follow Mrs Ellis's instructions, because the client's instructions prevail.",
   "Redeem the mortgage, because the email was an undertaking binding on the firm.",
   "Follow Mrs Ellis's instructions, because a trainee cannot give an undertaking that binds the firm.",
   "Redeem the mortgage only if the buyer's solicitor confirms they relied on the email.",
   "Pay half to Mrs Ellis and half to the bank as a compromise."
  ],
  "a": 1,
  "e": "The firm must **redeem the mortgage**.\n\nAn **undertaking** is any statement, oral or written, whether or not it uses the word \"undertake\", to someone who **reasonably relies** on it, that you **will do something**. Ben's email is a classic undertaking to redeem, and the buyer's solicitor relied on it in completing. Undertakings given by staff bind the **firm**. Under **Code para 1.3**, undertakings must be performed. A client's later instructions **cannot override** them.\n\n- Client instructions do not prevail over an undertaking.\n- Trainees and other staff can give binding undertakings.\n- Reliance is evident from completion; no further confirmation is needed.\n- A partial payment would breach the undertaking."
 },
 {
  "t": "trust-fairness-undertakings",
  "q": "Sasha, a solicitor, tells the other side's solicitor on the telephone that she will send the original share certificates to them. No date is mentioned. Three months later she has not sent them because she has been busy with other work.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Sasha's position?",
  "o": [
   "She has not breached any undertaking because nothing was put in writing or confirmed by email.",
   "She has not breached any undertaking because no time limit was agreed.",
   "She is in breach only if the other side has suffered financial loss.",
   "She is in breach only if she used the word \"undertake\".",
   "She has probably breached it, as it had to be performed within a reasonable time."
  ],
  "a": 4,
  "e": "Sasha has probably **breached her undertaking**.\n\nUndertakings can be **oral** and need not use the word \"undertake\". Under **Code para 1.3**, an undertaking must be performed within the agreed timescale or, if none was agreed, within a **reasonable time**. Three months' delay in sending share certificates because of other work is unlikely to be reasonable.\n\n- Oral undertakings are binding.\n- The absence of a time limit means a reasonable time applies, not that there is no deadline.\n- Breach does not depend on loss, although loss may affect what follows.\n- The particular words used do not matter."
 },
 {
  "t": "trust-fairness-undertakings",
  "q": "Kofi acts for a landlord. The tenant, Mrs Asante, who has no solicitor, writes asking whether she must pay for a roof repair. The lease clearly makes the landlord responsible for the roof. The landlord asks Kofi to reply that she is legally obliged to pay because she reported the leak late.\n\nWhat should Kofi do?",
  "o": [
   "Send the reply, because his duty is to act in the best interests of his client, the landlord.",
   "Refuse to say she is obliged to pay, making clear he acts only for the landlord.",
   "Send the reply, provided he also suggests that she may wish to take independent legal advice first.",
   "Advise her fully on her rights under the lease, because she is unrepresented.",
   "Refuse to reply at all, because a solicitor must never correspond with an unrepresented party."
  ],
  "a": 1,
  "e": "Kofi must not **take unfair advantage** of an unrepresented person (**para 1.2**) or **mislead** anyone (**para 1.4**), so he cannot state a legal obligation that does not exist. He is not obliged to advise Mrs Asante, as she is not his client, but anything he does say must not be misleading. The client's interests do not override these duties, and suggesting independent advice does not cure a misleading statement."
 },
 {
  "t": "duty-to-court",
  "q": "Nadia, a solicitor, acts for the claimant in a contract claim. While preparing her skeleton argument she finds a recent Court of Appeal decision that is directly against her client's case. The defendant's counsel has not mentioned it. Her client tells her not to raise it.\n\nWhat should Nadia do?",
  "o": [
   "Not mention the case, because her duty is to act in her client's best interests.",
   "Not mention the case unless the judge asks whether there are any adverse authorities.",
   "Withdraw from acting, because she cannot both follow her client's instructions and act properly.",
   "Draw the court's attention to the case, even though it is against her client.",
   "Tell the defendant's counsel about the case so that they can raise it instead."
  ],
  "a": 3,
  "e": "Nadia must **draw the court's attention** to the case.\n\nUnder **Code para 2.7**, you draw the court's attention to **relevant cases and statutory provisions** likely to have a material effect on the outcome. The duty applies even if the authority is **adverse** to your client and the other side has missed it. The duty to the court and the proper **administration of justice** (**Principle 1**) takes priority over the client's interests (**Principle 7**).\n\n- Staying silent would breach para 2.7 and risk misleading the court (para 1.4).\n- The duty does not depend on the judge asking.\n- She need not withdraw: she can comply with her duty and continue to argue the case (e.g. that the authority can be distinguished).\n- Telling the opponent is not how the duty is met."
 },
 {
  "t": "duty-to-court",
  "q": "Liam is charged with burglary. In a conference he tells his solicitor, Jess, that he did commit the burglary, but he wants to plead not guilty because he thinks the identification evidence is weak.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Jess may do?",
  "o": [
   "She may continue, test the prosecution evidence and argue it is insufficient, but not assert innocence.",
   "She must withdraw immediately, because she now knows the client is guilty.",
   "She may continue to act and call Liam to give evidence denying that he was there.",
   "She must inform the court that Liam has admitted the offence.",
   "She may continue to act and suggest to the prosecution witnesses that another person committed the burglary."
  ],
  "a": 0,
  "e": "Jess **may continue to act** and put the prosecution to proof, but must **not assert innocence**.\n\nA client who admits guilt is still entitled to require the prosecution to **prove** its case. Jess can cross-examine to test the identification evidence and make a submission of no case to answer. But she must not **mislead the court** (**Code para 1.4**) by asserting that Liam did not do it, calling evidence she knows to be false, or suggesting someone else did it.\n\n- There is no duty to withdraw just because the client has admitted guilt.\n- Calling Liam to deny presence would be knowingly putting false evidence before the court.\n- The admission is **confidential**; she must not disclose it.\n- Suggesting another person committed the offence would be misleading."
 },
 {
  "t": "duty-to-court",
  "q": "During a trial, Ahmed tells his solicitor, Grace, that he will give evidence tomorrow that he was at his sister's house at the time of the assault. Earlier, he had told Grace that he was at the scene. Grace advises him not to give false evidence, but he insists.\n\nWhat should Grace do?",
  "o": [
   "Continue to act and call Ahmed, because what a client tells his solicitor is confidential.",
   "Continue to act but tell the court of Ahmed's earlier account.",
   "Continue to act and call Ahmed, but not refer to his alibi in her closing speech.",
   "Ask the judge for an adjournment so Ahmed can find another solicitor, explaining why.",
   "Cease to act, telling the court only that she is withdrawing for professional reasons."
  ],
  "a": 4,
  "e": "Grace must **cease to act**, without revealing the reason.\n\nShe cannot be party to putting **false evidence** before the court: that would breach **Code paras 1.4 and 2.2** and **Principle 1**. Because the client insists, she must **withdraw**. Her duty of **confidentiality** (**para 6.3**) means she must not reveal why, so she tells the court she is withdrawing for **professional reasons**.\n\n- Continuing and calling him would mean knowingly allowing the court to be misled.\n- Telling the court of the earlier account breaches confidentiality.\n- Not mentioning the alibi in closing still involves her in the deception.\n- She may seek time for new representation but must not explain the reason."
 },
 {
  "t": "duty-to-court",
  "q": "At a sentencing hearing, the prosecutor tells the magistrates that Callum has no previous convictions. Callum's solicitor, Rhys, knows from Callum that he has two previous convictions for similar offences. Callum asks Rhys to emphasise his good character in mitigation.\n\nWhat should Rhys do?",
  "o": [
   "Emphasise Callum's good character, because the prosecutor's statement is not Rhys's responsibility.",
   "Tell the court that the prosecutor's information is wrong and disclose the convictions.",
   "Not rely on good character; if Callum insists that he does, Rhys must withdraw.",
   "Rely on good character but avoid saying the words \"no previous convictions\".",
   "Ask the prosecutor privately to correct the record without telling Callum."
  ],
  "a": 2,
  "e": "Rhys must **not rely on good character**, and must withdraw if Callum insists.\n\nRhys has **no duty to correct** the prosecution's error, and **confidentiality** (**para 6.3**) prevents him disclosing the convictions without Callum's consent. But he must not **mislead the court** (**para 1.4**). Relying on good character he knows to be false would be actively misleading. If Callum insists, Rhys must **cease to act**.\n\n- Emphasising good character, however phrased, would mislead the court.\n- Disclosing the convictions without consent breaches confidentiality.\n- A private word with the prosecutor is still a breach of confidentiality."
 },
 {
  "t": "duty-to-court",
  "q": "Ffion acts for a claimant in a personal injury claim. A key eyewitness says she will only attend court if she is paid £1,000 if the claimant wins. Ffion's client is happy to pay this.\n\nWhat should Ffion do?",
  "o": [
   "Agree, because the client has consented to the payment.",
   "Agree, provided the payment is disclosed to the other side and recorded in the witness statement.",
   "Refuse, but offer to reimburse the witness's reasonable expenses and loss of earnings.",
   "Agree, because witnesses are entitled to compensation for their time.",
   "Refuse and report the witness to the police for attempting to pervert the course of justice."
  ],
  "a": 2,
  "e": "Ffion must **refuse** the outcome-linked payment, but may pay **reasonable expenses** and compensation for lost time.\n\n**Code para 2.3** prohibits providing or offering any **benefit** to a witness **dependent on the nature of their evidence or the outcome** of the case. A payment conditional on winning gives the witness an interest in the outcome. Paying reasonable **expenses** and compensation for **loss of time**, not tied to the outcome, is permitted.\n\n- Client consent does not make a prohibited payment acceptable.\n- Disclosure to the other side does not cure it.\n- Compensation for time is fine only if it is **not** dependent on the result.\n- Reporting to the police is disproportionate; the witness is asking, not threatening."
 },
 {
  "t": "instructions-capacity-service",
  "q": "Daniel telephones Abbot Solicitors and asks them to act on the sale of his 88-year-old mother's house, with the proceeds to be paid to him. He says his mother is frail and that he will pass on her instructions. He offers to pay the firm's fees.\n\nWhat is the best course of action for the firm?",
  "o": [
   "Meet the mother, ideally without Daniel, to confirm her instructions, capacity and free will.",
   "Act on Daniel's instructions, because he is paying the fees.",
   "Act on Daniel's instructions provided he confirms in writing that his mother agrees with the sale.",
   "Refuse to act, because a firm cannot act where a relative is involved.",
   "Act on Daniel's instructions, because there is a presumption of capacity."
  ],
  "a": 0,
  "e": "The firm should **meet the mother**, ideally **alone**, to confirm her instructions and capacity and check for **undue influence**.\n\nUnder **Code para 3.1**, you only act on instructions from the **client** or someone **properly authorised**. Where a third party gives instructions, you must check directly with the client. Here there are warning signs: an elderly client and proceeds going to the person giving instructions. The firm must check her **capacity** and that she is acting freely.\n\n- Who pays the fees does not determine who the client is.\n- Written confirmation obtained through Daniel is not enough.\n- There is no rule against acting where relatives are involved.\n- The presumption of capacity relates to the mother, but it does not remove the need to take instructions from her."
 },
 {
  "t": "instructions-capacity-service",
  "q": "Mr Patel, aged 79, instructs Harriet to make a new will. His daughter tells Harriet that he was diagnosed with mild dementia last year. In the meeting he explains his assets and why he wants to leave more to one grandchild, and answers Harriet's questions clearly.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Harriet's position?",
  "o": [
   "She must refuse to act, because a diagnosis of dementia means that he lacks capacity to make a will.",
   "She can only act if the daughter consents to the will.",
   "She can only act once the Court of Protection has approved the will.",
   "She can act if satisfied he has capacity for this decision, keeping a careful note.",
   "She can act only if Mr Patel's will benefits all family members equally."
  ],
  "a": 3,
  "e": "Harriet **can act** if satisfied he has capacity for **this decision**, ideally with a **medical opinion** and a careful file note.\n\nAdults are **presumed** to have capacity, and capacity is **decision-specific** (as reflected in the **Mental Capacity Act 2005**). A **diagnosis** does not by itself mean lack of capacity. For a will, the question is whether he has **testamentary capacity** (the common law **Banks v Goodfellow** test): he understands that he is making a will and its effect, the extent of his property, and the claims of those he might be expected to benefit, and no disorder of the mind distorts his judgment. His clear explanation suggests he does. Given the diagnosis, it is good practice to get a medical opinion.\n\n- A diagnosis is not conclusive.\n- The daughter is not the client and her consent is irrelevant.\n- A Court of Protection statutory will is needed only if he lacks capacity.\n- An unequal or unwise will does not show lack of capacity."
 },
 {
  "t": "instructions-capacity-service",
  "q": "Under the terms of business agreed at the outset, Erin agreed to pay £2,000 on account of costs before her solicitor issues proceedings. Despite reminders she has refused to pay. Her limitation period expires in four months.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the solicitor's position?",
  "o": [
   "The solicitor must continue to act because a retainer can never be ended by the solicitor.",
   "The solicitor may end the retainer for good reason, on reasonable notice.",
   "The solicitor may stop work immediately without telling Erin.",
   "The solicitor must issue proceedings at the firm's own expense to protect the limitation period.",
   "The solicitor may end the retainer only if Erin consents."
  ],
  "a": 1,
  "e": "The solicitor **may end the retainer** for **good reason** on **reasonable notice**.\n\nA solicitor can terminate the retainer only for a **good reason** and on **reasonable notice**. Failing to pay money on account in breach of agreed terms is a good reason. Four months before limitation gives time for reasonable notice and for Erin to find other representation. The solicitor should warn her clearly about the limitation date.\n\n- Solicitors can end the retainer for good reason.\n- Stopping work without notice would harm the client and breach the duty to give reasonable notice.\n- There is no duty to fund the client's proceedings.\n- Client consent is not needed where there is good reason."
 },
 {
  "t": "client-money",
  "q": "Mr Grant has recently completed his house sale through Hollins Solicitors. The firm holds the £50,000 balance of the proceeds. He asks the firm to keep the money in client account for the next year and use it to pay his household bills and his daughter's school fees as they arise.\n\nWhat should the firm do?",
  "o": [
   "Agree, because the money is already in client account.",
   "Agree, provided it pays Mr Grant interest on the money.",
   "Agree, provided Mr Grant gives written instructions for each payment out of client account.",
   "Agree, but charge a fee for each payment made.",
   "Refuse and return the money, as client account must not be a banking facility."
  ],
  "a": 4,
  "e": "The firm must **refuse** and **return the money promptly**.\n\nUnder the **SRA Accounts Rules**, client account must not be used to provide **banking facilities** (rule 3.3). Payments into and out of client account must relate to the **legal services** the firm is providing. Once the sale is complete, there is no proper reason to hold the balance, so it must be **returned promptly**.\n\n- The money being in client account already does not justify keeping it.\n- Paying interest does not make it lawful.\n- Written instructions do not override the banking facility ban.\n- Charging a fee would make the breach worse."
 },
 {
  "t": "client-money",
  "q": "Tara holds £3,000 in client account on account of costs for her client, Mr Lowe. She has finished the work, and her costs are £2,500 plus VAT. She has not yet sent a bill.\n\nWhat must Tara do before transferring her costs from client account to business account?",
  "o": [
   "Nothing, because the client paid the money on account of costs.",
   "Give Mr Lowe a bill or other written notification of costs.",
   "Obtain Mr Lowe's written consent to each transfer from client account.",
   "Wait until the end of the accounting period.",
   "Obtain the COFA's written approval."
  ],
  "a": 1,
  "e": "Tara must give Mr Lowe a **bill or other written notification** of costs first.\n\nUnder the **SRA Accounts Rules**, client money can be withdrawn to pay the firm's costs only once a **bill of costs or other written notification** of the costs incurred has been given to the client (or paying party). Money on account remains **client money** until then. The balance must then be returned promptly.\n\n- Money paid on account is still client money until the bill.\n- Separate written consent to each transfer is not required.\n- There is no need to wait for the end of the accounting period.\n- The COFA monitors compliance but does not approve individual transfers."
 },
 {
  "t": "client-money",
  "q": "During a reconciliation, Clark & Co's cashier finds that £4,000 was paid from client account for Client A's matter using money held for Client B, because of a keying error. No one acted dishonestly.\n\nWhat must the firm do?",
  "o": [
   "Wait until Client A pays the firm and then correct the account.",
   "Ask Client B to agree to the money being treated as a loan to Client A.",
   "Write to Client A asking for repayment, and do nothing further unless Client A refuses.",
   "Replace the shortfall in Client B's ledger from the firm's own money promptly.",
   "Correct the error at the end of the accounting period."
  ],
  "a": 3,
  "e": "The firm must **replace the shortfall promptly** from its **own money**.\n\nThe **Accounts Rules** prohibit using one client's money for another and require breaches to be **corrected promptly**, including replacing money improperly withdrawn. The firm should then seek recovery from Client A, record the breach, and the COFA should consider whether it is serious enough to report.\n\n- Waiting for Client A to pay leaves Client B's money at risk.\n- Treating it as a loan between clients is improper.\n- Asking Client A for repayment does not remove the duty to replace the shortfall immediately.\n- Correction cannot wait for the end of the accounting period."
 },
 {
  "t": "business-referrals-publicity",
  "q": "Priya, a conveyancing solicitor, is offered an arrangement by a local estate agent. The agent will recommend Priya's firm to its buyers, and the firm will pay the agent £150 for each client who instructs it.\n\nWhich of the following is required for the firm to accept the arrangement?",
  "o": [
   "Tell clients of the arrangement, put it in writing and keep its advice independent.",
   "Nothing, because referral fees are banned in all residential conveyancing and personal injury work.",
   "The firm need only record the payments in its accounts.",
   "The firm must obtain the SRA's prior approval.",
   "The firm must ensure the estate agent is regulated by the SRA."
  ],
  "a": 0,
  "e": "The firm must **inform clients**, put the arrangement **in writing**, and keep its advice **independent**.\n\nUnder **Code para 5.1**, where a third party introduces business to you, clients must be informed of any **financial or other interest** in the referral, any **fee-sharing** agreement must be **in writing**, and the client must not have been acquired in breach of SRA rules. The firm must also protect its **independence** (**Principle 3**) and act in the client's best interests.\n\n- Referral fees are banned in **personal injury** and **criminal** matters, not conveyancing.\n- Recording payments alone does not meet the disclosure requirements.\n- SRA approval is not required.\n- The introducer need not be regulated."
 },
 {
  "t": "business-referrals-publicity",
  "q": "A police station referral agency offers to send criminal defence clients to Holt Solicitors in return for a payment of £200 for each client. The clients will be told about the payment.\n\nWhat is the best advice to Holt Solicitors?",
  "o": [
   "It may accept, provided the clients are told about the payment.",
   "It may accept, provided the arrangement is in writing.",
   "It may accept, provided the clients consent in writing after being told the amount of the payment.",
   "It may accept, provided the payment is called a marketing fee.",
   "It must not accept, because such payments are banned in criminal matters."
  ],
  "a": 4,
  "e": "The firm **must not accept**.\n\n**Code para 5.1** prohibits making or receiving **payments relating to a referral** for clients who are the subject of **criminal proceedings**. Disclosure, writing and consent do not make it acceptable. The SRA also looks at the substance of payments: calling it a marketing fee does not help.\n\n- Telling clients is not enough in criminal matters.\n- A written agreement does not cure the prohibition.\n- Client consent does not cure the prohibition.\n- Relabelling the payment does not change its substance."
 },
 {
  "t": "business-referrals-publicity",
  "q": "Cleo, a newly qualified solicitor, learns that a road accident victim is in the local hospital. Her firm's partner asks her to visit him on the ward, introduce herself and offer the firm's personal injury services, giving him a leaflet. The victim has never been a client of the firm.\n\nWhat should Cleo do?",
  "o": [
   "Visit as asked, because offering services in person is good client care.",
   "Visit as asked, provided she makes clear the firm offers no-win, no-fee terms on such claims.",
   "Decline, because it would be an unsolicited approach to a member of the public.",
   "Visit only if the hospital gives permission.",
   "Send the leaflet to him by post instead, because a written approach is not an unsolicited approach."
  ],
  "a": 2,
  "e": "Cleo should **decline**.\n\n**Code para 8.9** prohibits **unsolicited approaches** to **members of the public**, other than **current or former clients**, to **advertise legal services**. Approaching an accident victim in hospital who has not asked for contact is a classic example. It also risks taking **unfair advantage** of someone who may be vulnerable (**para 1.2**), and breaches Principle 2.\n\n- Personal approaches like this are what para 8.9 prohibits.\n- The fee terms do not make the approach acceptable.\n- Hospital permission does not change the Code.\n- Posting the leaflet to him personally would still be a targeted, unsolicited approach. General, untargeted advertising (such as leaflets delivered to every home in a wide area) is allowed, but singling him out is not."
 },
 {
  "t": "conflicts-of-interest",
  "q": "Lucy is a solicitor. Her long-standing client, Mr Doyle, asks her to act on the sale of his holiday cottage. Lucy's husband wants to buy it, and Mr Doyle is happy for Lucy to act for him on the sale.\n\nWhat should Lucy do?",
  "o": [
   "Act for Mr Doyle, because he has given informed consent.",
   "Act for both Mr Doyle and her husband, because they have a substantially common interest in the sale.",
   "Decline, because an own interest conflict cannot be cured by consent.",
   "Act for Mr Doyle, provided the price is supported by an independent valuation.",
   "Act for Mr Doyle, provided she puts information barriers in place."
  ],
  "a": 2,
  "e": "Lucy must **decline to act**.\n\nUnder **Code para 6.1**, you must not act if there is an **own interest conflict** or a significant risk of one. Lucy's husband's interest as buyer is effectively her own: her personal interest conflicts with her duty to get the best deal for Mr Doyle. There are **no exceptions** to para 6.1, so **consent** cannot cure it.\n\n- The consent exceptions only apply to **client** conflicts under para 6.2.\n- Seller and buyer do not have a substantially common interest.\n- An independent valuation does not remove the conflict.\n- Information barriers address confidentiality, not own interest conflicts."
 },
 {
  "t": "conflicts-of-interest",
  "q": "Mrs Ward, a widow with no close family, asks her solicitor, Owen, to draft a new will leaving him £100,000 in gratitude for years of help. Owen suggests she take independent advice, but she refuses, saying she trusts him completely.\n\nWhat should Owen do?",
  "o": [
   "Decline to draft the will, because there is an own interest conflict.",
   "Draft the will, because Mrs Ward has capacity and has made her wishes clear.",
   "Draft the will, but ask a colleague in his firm to witness it.",
   "Draft the will, provided he records her reasons in a file note.",
   "Draft the will but reduce the gift to a smaller amount."
  ],
  "a": 0,
  "e": "Owen should **decline** to draft the will.\n\nA **significant gift** to the solicitor creates an **own interest conflict** (**Code para 6.1**). His interest in receiving the legacy conflicts with his duty to advise Mrs Ward in her best interests. SRA guidance is that the client should be advised to take **independent legal advice**. If she refuses, the solicitor should **not act**.\n\n- Capacity and clear wishes do not cure an own interest conflict.\n- A colleague witnessing the will does not remove Owen's conflict.\n- A file note does not cure it.\n- Changing the amount of the gift himself is improper."
 },
 {
  "t": "conflicts-of-interest",
  "q": "Kai and Mia want to set up a new company together to run a café. They agree on the business plan, the shareholdings and the roles each will take. They ask Fern, a solicitor, to act for both of them in forming the company and preparing a shareholders' agreement.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Fern may act for both?",
  "o": [
   "She may not act, because the interests of joint clients always conflict.",
   "She may act, because there is no conflict at all between co-founders.",
   "She may act only under the competing for the same objective exception.",
   "She may act under the substantially common interest exception, with written consent, if reasonable.",
   "She may act only if they both take independent legal advice first on the shareholdings and their roles."
  ],
  "a": 3,
  "e": "Fern **may act** under the **substantially common interest** exception, if the conditions are met.\n\nThere is a **significant risk** of conflict in drafting a shareholders' agreement because terms may favour one founder. But under **Code para 6.2(a)**, you may act where the clients have a **substantially common interest**: a clear common purpose and strong consensus on how to achieve it, as here. The conditions must also be met: **informed consent given or evidenced in writing**, **effective safeguards** where appropriate, and Fern must be satisfied it is **reasonable** to act for both.\n\n- Joint clients do not always conflict.\n- It is wrong to say there is no risk of conflict at all.\n- They are not competing for the same objective.\n- Independent advice is not a requirement of the exception."
 },
 {
  "t": "conflicts-of-interest",
  "q": "Two long-standing clients of Greaves LLP, Alpha Ltd and Beta Ltd, are both bidding to buy Delta Ltd in a sealed-bid auction. Both are experienced commercial companies with in-house lawyers. Both want Greaves LLP to act.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm may act for both?",
  "o": [
   "It may not act for both in any circumstances, because their interests directly conflict.",
   "It may act for both, with written consent, effective barriers and if reasonable.",
   "It may act for both under the substantially common interest exception, because they both want to buy Delta Ltd.",
   "It may act for both only if Delta Ltd also consents.",
   "It may act for both without any conditions, because they are sophisticated clients."
  ],
  "a": 1,
  "e": "The firm **may act** under the **competing for the same objective** exception, if the conditions are met.\n\n**Code para 6.2(b)** allows acting where clients are **competing for the same objective** (here, buying Delta Ltd), provided all clients give **informed consent in writing**, **effective safeguards** protect each client's confidential information (information barriers between the teams), and the firm is satisfied it is **reasonable** to act for both. Sophisticated clients with in-house lawyers make it more likely to be reasonable.\n\n- The exception exists for exactly this situation.\n- They are competitors, not clients with a common interest.\n- The seller's consent is not required.\n- Sophistication is relevant to reasonableness but the conditions still apply."
 },
 {
  "t": "conflicts-of-interest",
  "q": "Jack and Elena, a married couple, instructed Rowe Solicitors to buy a house jointly. Before exchange they separate. Jack now wants to proceed with the purchase in his sole name, and Elena wants to withdraw and recover her deposit contribution.\n\nWhat should the firm do?",
  "o": [
   "Continue to act for both, because they were joint clients from the start.",
   "Continue to act for Jack only, because he is the one who wants to proceed.",
   "Continue to act for both if they each give written consent to the firm continuing to act.",
   "Continue to act for Elena only, because she contributed the deposit.",
   "Recognise a conflict and stop acting for at least one, and probably both."
  ],
  "a": 4,
  "e": "A **client conflict** has arisen, so the firm must **stop acting** for at least one client and probably both.\n\nUnder **Code para 6.2**, you must not act where clients' interests in the same matter conflict unless an exception applies. Jack and Elena no longer have a substantially common interest and are not competing for the same objective in the relevant sense. The firm will also hold **confidential information** about each that is material to the other (**para 6.5**), so continuing for one is possible only if the other consents or there is no real risk to their confidential information.\n\n- Their original joint instruction does not prevent a later conflict.\n- Choosing one client without considering confidentiality is wrong.\n- Consent alone does not satisfy para 6.2; an exception is needed."
 },
 {
  "t": "confidentiality-disclosure",
  "q": "Two years ago, Lane & Co acted for Orbis Ltd in negotiating a supply contract and obtained detailed confidential information about Orbis Ltd's pricing and finances. Now a new client, Zest Ltd, wants Lane & Co to sue Orbis Ltd for breach of a similar contract. The same partner would handle the claim. Orbis Ltd refuses consent.\n\nWhat is the best advice?",
  "o": [
   "Lane & Co may act, because Orbis Ltd is a former client.",
   "It should not act, as it holds material confidential information and has no consent.",
   "Lane & Co may act, provided it does not use the information unless it becomes relevant to the dispute.",
   "Lane & Co may act once it puts an information barrier around the same partner.",
   "Lane & Co may act, because the duty of confidentiality ended when the retainer ended."
  ],
  "a": 1,
  "e": "Lane & Co **should not act**.\n\nUnder **Code para 6.5**, you must not act for a client whose interests are **adverse** to a **current or former client** where you hold that client's **confidential information** that is **material** to the matter, unless **effective measures** mean there is **no real risk** of disclosure, or the client gives **informed written consent**. Orbis Ltd refuses consent, and an information barrier cannot work where the **same partner** holds the information.\n\n- The duty extends to **former** clients.\n- Promising not to use information is not an effective measure.\n- An information barrier around the person who knows the information is meaningless.\n- Confidentiality continues after the retainer ends."
 },
 {
  "t": "confidentiality-disclosure",
  "q": "During a meeting about his divorce, Carl tells his solicitor, Imogen, that he is going to his ex-wife's house tonight with a knife to \"make her pay\". Imogen believes he is serious.\n\nWhat may Imogen do?",
  "o": [
   "Nothing, because the information is confidential and privileged.",
   "Disclose the threat only if Carl consents.",
   "Disclose the threat only after obtaining a court order permitting her to make the disclosure.",
   "Disclose the threat to the police, as disclosure to prevent serious harm is permitted.",
   "Disclose the threat to Carl's ex-wife's solicitor only."
  ],
  "a": 3,
  "e": "Imogen **may disclose** the threat to the **police**.\n\nConfidentiality (**Code para 6.3**) is subject to exceptions where disclosure is **required or permitted by law**. In **exceptional circumstances**, disclosure is permitted to prevent a client or another person committing a crime likely to cause **serious harm**. A credible threat to attack someone with a knife tonight is such a case. Privilege does not protect communications made to further a crime. She should disclose only what is necessary.\n\n- Confidentiality is not absolute where serious harm is threatened.\n- Carl's consent is not needed and would not be given.\n- There is no time for a court order, and none is needed.\n- Telling only the ex-wife's solicitor may not prevent the harm."
 },
 {
  "t": "confidentiality-disclosure",
  "q": "Sophie acts for a claimant in a dispute. The defendant's solicitors mistakenly email her a copy of counsel's advice to the defendant on the weaknesses in its case. Sophie realises what it is after reading the first paragraph.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Sophie's duty to her own client?",
  "o": [
   "She need not tell her client the contents of a privileged document sent by mistake.",
   "She must tell her client the full contents, because she must disclose all material information.",
   "She must tell her client the contents only if they would help the client.",
   "She must send the advice to her client and let the client decide whether to use it.",
   "She must tell the court about the advice."
  ],
  "a": 0,
  "e": "Sophie is **not required** to tell her client the contents.\n\nUnder **Code para 6.4**, the duty to tell the client all material information does not apply where the information is in a **privileged document** that you know about **only because it was mistakenly disclosed** (para 6.4(d)). She should stop reading, tell the sender, and return or delete it. Using it could also be restrained by the court.\n\n- The disclosure duty is subject to this specific exception.\n- Whether it helps the client is irrelevant.\n- Sending it to the client would defeat the exception and probably be improper.\n- There is no duty to inform the court."
 },
 {
  "t": "cooperation-reporting",
  "q": "Adam, a solicitor at Marsh LLP, discovers that one of the partners, Hugh, has been routinely adding hours he did not work to clients' bills. Adam is worried this will damage his career if he reports it.\n\nWhat must Adam do?",
  "o": [
   "Nothing, because it is the COLP's responsibility to report breaches.",
   "Raise it with Hugh first and report it to the SRA only if Hugh refuses to stop the practice.",
   "Report it only once he has gathered conclusive evidence of dishonesty.",
   "Report it only if a client complains.",
   "Report it promptly to the SRA, directly or via the COLP if confident they will report."
  ],
  "a": 4,
  "e": "Adam must **report promptly** to the SRA, directly or via the COLP.\n\n**Code para 7.7** requires you to report promptly any facts or matters you **reasonably believe** are **capable of amounting to a serious breach** by anyone the SRA regulates. Dishonest overcharging is serious. Under **para 7.12**, telling the **COLP** satisfies the duty only **on the understanding** that the COLP will report it. **Para 7.9** protects Adam from **detrimental treatment** for reporting.\n\n- The duty is personal; it is not just the COLP's.\n- Raising it with Hugh first is not a substitute and could allow evidence to be concealed.\n- A reasonable belief is enough; conclusive proof is not required.\n- The duty does not depend on a complaint."
 },
 {
  "t": "cooperation-reporting",
  "q": "Mo, an associate, reports to the SRA that her firm's managing partner has been misusing client money. The SRA decides not to investigate. The managing partner then removes Mo from all her high-value matters and cancels her promotion review.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the managing partner's conduct?",
  "o": [
   "It is acceptable, because the SRA found nothing to investigate.",
   "It is acceptable, because management decisions are a matter for the firm.",
   "It breaches the Code, as no one may suffer detriment for reporting.",
   "It breaches the Code only if Mo's report was correct and the misuse of money is proved.",
   "It breaches the Code only if Mo first reported internally."
  ],
  "a": 2,
  "e": "The conduct **breaches the Code**.\n\n**Code para 7.9** says you do not subject any person to **detrimental treatment** for making or proposing to make a report based on a reasonably held belief, **irrespective of whether the SRA subsequently investigates** or takes action. Removing her from matters and cancelling her review is detrimental treatment linked to the report. The Code for Firms has a matching duty.\n\n- The SRA's decision not to investigate is irrelevant.\n- Firms' management freedom does not extend to punishing whistleblowers.\n- The protection applies where the belief is **reasonably held**, even if it turns out to be wrong.\n- There is no requirement to report internally first."
 },
 {
  "t": "cooperation-reporting",
  "q": "Rob, a solicitor, realises that he failed to issue proceedings for his client, Ms Hale, before the limitation period expired. Her claim is now probably statute-barred. Ms Hale does not yet know.\n\nWhat should Rob do?",
  "o": [
   "Say nothing, and try to negotiate a settlement with the defendant anyway.",
   "Issue proceedings anyway and hope the defendant does not raise limitation.",
   "Tell her promptly and honestly, advise independent advice and notify insurers.",
   "Offer Ms Hale a fee refund in return for her agreeing not to complain.",
   "Continue to act and advise Ms Hale on her claim against the firm, as she trusts him."
  ],
  "a": 2,
  "e": "Rob must **tell Ms Hale promptly and honestly**, advise her to get **independent advice**, and notify the **insurers**.\n\n**Code para 7.11** requires you to be **honest and open** with clients when things go wrong and to **explain fully and promptly** what has happened and the likely impact. Ms Hale may now have a claim against the firm, creating an **own interest conflict** (**para 6.1**), so Rob cannot advise her on it. The firm must notify its **PII insurers**, and consider whether the matter needs reporting to the SRA.\n\n- Concealing the error breaches para 7.11 and Principles 4 and 5.\n- Issuing hopelessly out of time wastes the court's time and misleads the client.\n- Buying her silence breaches the Code and the right to complain.\n- Advising on a claim against himself is an own interest conflict."
 },
 {
  "t": "complaints-legal-ombudsman",
  "q": "Ms Burke's solicitor completed her remortgage on 1 March 2025. She received the firm's final bill the same month. On 1 May 2026 she realises, for the first time, that the firm made a mistake on the charge registration, and she complains to the firm. It sends its final response on 15 June 2026, including details of the Legal Ombudsman and the six-month limit.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the Legal Ombudsman can accept her complaint?",
  "o": [
   "Yes, if within six months of the final response, as she is within a year of when she should have known.",
   "No, because more than one year has passed since the act or omission she complains about, so she is out of time.",
   "No, because she must refer it within eight weeks of complaining to the firm.",
   "Yes, because the limit is six years from the act or omission.",
   "No, because remortgage complaints fall outside the Ombudsman's jurisdiction."
  ],
  "a": 0,
  "e": "Yes: the Ombudsman **can accept** it if she refers within **six months of the final response**.\n\nUnder the Legal Ombudsman **Scheme Rules** in force since **1 April 2023**, a complaint must be referred within **one year of the act or omission**, **or** within **one year of when the complainant should reasonably have known** there was cause for complaint. She discovered the problem on 1 May 2026, so the one-year awareness period is satisfied. She must also refer within **six months** of the firm's **final response**.\n\n- The act-or-omission limit is an alternative, not the only test.\n- The eight weeks is the period after which LeO can accept a complaint if the firm has not resolved it, not a deadline for the client.\n- The six-year limit was replaced in 2023.\n- Remortgage work is within LeO's jurisdiction."
 },
 {
  "t": "complaints-legal-ombudsman",
  "q": "Holly complains to her solicitors about delay on her probate matter. Three weeks later, having heard nothing beyond an acknowledgement, she contacts the Legal Ombudsman. There are no exceptional circumstances.\n\nWhat is the Ombudsman most likely to do?",
  "o": [
   "Investigate immediately, because the firm has failed to respond.",
   "Refuse to deal with the complaint at any time, because she has gone straight to the Ombudsman.",
   "Refer the complaint to the SRA instead.",
   "Tell her it normally needs the firm's final response or eight weeks to pass.",
   "Order the firm to respond to her complaint within seven days and report back."
  ],
  "a": 3,
  "e": "The Ombudsman will tell her it **cannot normally consider** the complaint until the firm has sent its **final response** or **eight weeks** have passed.\n\nThe firm must first have the chance to resolve the complaint. Under the Scheme Rules, the Ombudsman normally only accepts a complaint once the firm's complaints procedure has finished (a **final response**) or **eight weeks** have passed since the complaint was made, unless there are **exceptional circumstances**.\n\n- Three weeks is too early to investigate.\n- She is not barred permanently; she can return after eight weeks.\n- Delay is a service issue for the Ombudsman, not a conduct issue for the SRA.\n- The Ombudsman does not order a response within seven days."
 },
 {
  "t": "complaints-legal-ombudsman",
  "q": "Mr Okafor complained to his solicitors about their handling of his house purchase. After receiving the firm's final response, he referred the complaint to the Legal Ombudsman in time. He is eligible to complain. He says the firm's poor service caused him £80,000 of loss and wants that sum paid.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the Legal Ombudsman's powers?",
  "o": [
   "It can award the full £80,000, because it decides what is fair and reasonable in all the circumstances.",
   "It can direct remedies up to £50,000, and accepting its decision ends any right to sue on it.",
   "It can only direct the firm to apologise and reduce its fees, not pay compensation for loss.",
   "It can award compensation, but only if Mr Okafor first proves negligence in the courts.",
   "It can award up to £50,000, and he can then sue the firm for the balance even if he accepts the decision."
  ],
  "a": 1,
  "e": "The **Legal Ombudsman** can direct an apology, a refund or reduction of fees, **compensation** for loss and for distress and inconvenience, putting things right, and interest. The total value of remedies is **capped at £50,000** per complaint. If the complainant **accepts** the final decision it becomes **binding** and he cannot then sue over the same matter. If he **rejects** it, he remains free to go to court."
 },
 {
  "t": "firms-compliance-protections",
  "q": "Pell & Co, a small firm, has collapsed after its cashier stole £200,000 from client account over two years. The firm has closed, its bank is not liable, and the cashier has no assets. Several clients have lost money that was held for their house purchases.\n\nWhere should the clients look for recovery of their losses?",
  "o": [
   "The firm's professional indemnity insurers, because PII covers all losses suffered by clients.",
   "The Legal Ombudsman, because it can award compensation for any loss caused by a firm.",
   "The Solicitors Indemnity Fund, because the firm has closed with no successor practice to meet the claims.",
   "The SRA, which will order the firm's former partners to compensate the clients.",
   "The SRA Compensation Fund, as the loss came from dishonesty and cannot be recovered elsewhere."
  ],
  "a": 4,
  "e": "The **Compensation Fund** is a **discretionary** fund of **last resort** that may pay where a client has lost money through a solicitor's or employee's **dishonesty**, or a **failure to account**, and the loss **cannot be recovered elsewhere**. **PII** is for **negligence** claims, and the **Legal Ombudsman** deals with poor service up to £50,000. The **Solicitors Indemnity Fund** covers negligence claims after the six-year run-off period, and the SRA itself does not award compensation."
 },
 {
  "t": "firms-compliance-protections",
  "q": "Wren Solicitors negligently failed to register a client's charge, causing her a loss of £150,000. When she claims, the firm's professional indemnity insurer says it will refuse to pay because the firm failed to disclose a previous claim when it renewed its policy.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the client's position?",
  "o": [
   "The insurer may refuse to pay, so she must apply to the SRA Compensation Fund instead.",
   "The insurer must pay under the Minimum Terms, and may recover from the firm afterwards.",
   "The insurer may refuse to pay, so her only remedy is a complaint to the Legal Ombudsman.",
   "The insurer must pay, but may deduct the firm's policy excess from the compensation that it pays to her.",
   "The insurer must pay only if the firm is an LLP, because only incorporated firms need PII."
  ],
  "a": 1,
  "e": "**Professional indemnity insurance** is **compulsory** for every authorised firm and must follow the SRA's **Minimum Terms and Conditions**. Under them, the insurer **cannot refuse** to pay a client's claim because the firm failed to disclose something or breached the policy; it can **recover from the firm** afterwards. **Excesses** are paid by the firm, not deducted from the client's compensation, and the Compensation Fund is not a substitute for PII in a **negligence** case."
 },
 {
  "t": "firms-compliance-protections",
  "q": "Hari is a solicitor who also owns an HR consultancy that is not authorised by the SRA or any other legal regulator. A client of the consultancy lost money after following negligent employment advice from one of its consultants. The client assumed she was protected in the same way as a client of a law firm.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?",
  "o": [
   "She can claim against the professional indemnity insurance of Hari's law firm, because Hari is a solicitor.",
   "She can apply to the SRA Compensation Fund, because a solicitor owns the consultancy.",
   "She can complain to the Legal Ombudsman, because the advice was legal advice given by a solicitor's business.",
   "No SRA protections apply, and Hari should have made clear the consultancy was unregulated.",
   "She can claim against the Solicitors Indemnity Fund, because the consultancy is not insured."
  ],
  "a": 3,
  "e": "Services from an **unregulated** business are **not covered** by the SRA protections: **PII**, the **Compensation Fund** and the **Legal Ombudsman**. Under **paras 8.10 and 8.11**, clients must understand **whether and how** services are regulated, and a solicitor must not let clients think an unauthorised business is SRA-regulated. Referring a law firm client to such a business also requires **informed consent** under para 5.3."
 },
 {
  "t": "ndas-slapps",
  "q": "Ivy, an employment solicitor, acts for a company settling a harassment complaint by a junior employee, Dan, who is unrepresented in negotiations. The company asks Ivy to include a clause that Dan \"will not disclose the allegations to anyone, including any regulator or the police\", with repayment of the settlement if he does.\n\nWhat should Ivy do?",
  "o": [
   "Refuse that wording and draft a clause that expressly permits reports to regulators and police.",
   "Include the clause, because the client is entitled to confidentiality as a term of any settlement it agrees.",
   "Include the clause, because Dan can take advice before signing.",
   "Include the clause but remove the repayment provision so that it is not oppressive to Dan.",
   "Refuse to act for the company in the settlement at all."
  ],
  "a": 0,
  "e": "Ivy should advise that she **cannot include** a clause blocking reports to regulators or the police, and draft one that **expressly permits** them.\n\nThe SRA **warning notice on NDAs** says NDAs must not be used to prevent or deter reporting to the **police**, a **regulator** or making **protected disclosures**, nor use **clawback** clauses to deter permitted disclosures. **s.17 Victims and Prisoners Act 2024** (in force October 2025) also makes such clauses void for victims of crime. Doing so would breach **Code paras 1.2, 1.4 and 7.5**. A confidentiality clause with the proper carve-outs is legitimate.\n\n- The client's wish does not justify a misleading or unenforceable clause.\n- Dan's ability to take advice does not cure it; he is unrepresented and especially at risk.\n- Removing only the repayment provision leaves the prohibited restriction.\n- She can act; she just cannot include that clause."
 },
 {
  "t": "ndas-slapps",
  "q": "Quentin, a wealthy businessman, instructs Ray, a solicitor, about a journalist who is about to publish an article on Quentin's business dealings. Quentin wants a letter, marked \"Private and Confidential, Not for Publication\", threatening the journalist with criminal prosecution and a seven-figure damages claim unless the article is dropped within 24 hours. Ray thinks any defamation claim would be weak.\n\nWhat should Ray do?",
  "o": [
   "Send the letter as instructed, because his duty is to act in Quentin's best interests.",
   "Send the letter but mark it \"Without Prejudice\" instead.",
   "Send the letter but extend the deadline to 48 hours.",
   "Send the letter only if Quentin confirms in writing that the allegations in the article are untrue.",
   "Refuse to send it as drafted, and advise on any properly arguable claim."
  ],
  "a": 4,
  "e": "Ray should **refuse to send the letter as drafted** and advise on any **properly arguable** claim proportionately.\n\nThe SRA **SLAPPs warning notice** identifies as improper: threatening **meritless** claims or exaggerated remedies (e.g. **criminal prosecution** in a civil matter), **oppressive** tactics against individuals, unreasonably **short deadlines**, and **misleading labels** such as \"not for publication\" or \"without prejudice\" where the conditions are not met. This would breach **Code paras 1.2 and 2.4** and **Principles 1, 2 and 3**. Independence means not simply doing what the client wants.\n\n- Best interests never justify abusive conduct.\n- \"Without prejudice\" would also be misused here.\n- A slightly longer deadline does not fix the other problems.\n- The client's assurance does not make a weak claim properly arguable."
 }
];

;
window.SQE_LEARN = window.SQE_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_LEARN["blp/business-structures"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🏪", title:"Every business needs a vehicle", body:"A business is run through a **business structure** (also called a **business vehicle**). Think of it as the legal 'shape' the business takes."},
   {t:"card", i:"🖐️", title:"Five structures on the syllabus", body:"**Sole trader**, **general partnership**, **limited liability partnership (LLP)**, **private limited company (Ltd)** and **public limited company (plc)**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧍", title:"Question 1: is it its own 'person'?", body:"**Separate legal personality** means the business is a 'person' in law, separate from its owners. Companies and LLPs: **yes**. Sole traders and general partnerships: **no**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Which two structures are NOT separate legal persons?", body:"**Sole traders** and **general partnerships**. Companies (Ltd and plc) and LLPs are."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛡️", title:"Question 2: is the owners' liability limited?", body:"If the business can't pay its **creditors** (people it owes money to), can they take the owners' savings, house and car?"},
   {t:"compare", i:"⚖️", title:"Unlimited vs limited liability", cols:["", "Sole trader / partner", "Shareholder / LLP member"], rows:[["Creditors can take personal assets?","✅ Yes","❌ No"],["Name","🔓 Unlimited liability","🔒 Limited liability"],["Most you can lose","💸 Everything","💷 What you put in or agreed to put in"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"🗺️", title:"What this topic covers (and what's elsewhere)", body:"This topic: the structures, legal personality, limited liability, when courts **pierce the corporate veil**, and choosing a structure.\n\nElsewhere: setting each one up (**Forming a company, partnership or LLP**), partners' authority and agreements (**Partnerships and LLPs**), directors' duties (**Directors**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"💡", title:"Exam tip: label the structure first", body:"Ask: '**Registered at Companies House?**' If not, it's a sole trader or partnership, so **unlimited liability**. Watch for two people 'just starting to work together': a partnership may already exist."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"If a sole trader's business fails, can creditors take their house?", body:"**Yes.** A sole trader has **unlimited liability**: business debts are their personal debts."}
 ]},
 {h:"The five structures", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🧍", title:"Sole trader: what it is", body:"One individual who owns and runs a business on their own account. They **can still employ staff**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🪞", title:"Sole trader: business = person", body:"**No separate legal personality.** Business assets and debts are the owner's personal assets and debts."},
   {t:"card", i:"💥", title:"Sole trader: unlimited liability", body:"The owner is personally liable for **every** business debt. If the business fails, they can be made **bankrupt**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚀", title:"Sole trader: no formalities", body:"Just start trading. They must register with **HMRC** (HM Revenue & Customs, the UK tax authority) for **self-assessment** (filing your own tax return)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🤫", title:"Sole trader: privacy and tax", body:"Nothing filed at **Companies House** (the public register of companies and LLPs), so accounts stay private. Profits are **trading income**: **income tax** + **Class 4 NICs** (National Insurance contributions) in the year made, taken out or not."},
   {t:"card", i:"🪧", title:"Sole trader: business name", body:"Trading under a name that isn't their own surname? Their **name and an address** must appear on business documents and at the premises (CA 2006 Part 41)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Does a sole trader file anything at Companies House?", body:"**No.** Nothing public. They only register with HMRC for self-assessment."},
   {t:"card", i:"🤝", title:"Partnership: the definition", body:"**Partnership Act 1890 (PA 1890) s.1**: the relationship between **persons carrying on a business in common with a view of profit**. It's the **aim** of profit that counts: a business that makes a **loss** can still be a partnership."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚡", title:"Partnership: it just happens", body:"It arises **automatically from the facts**. No written agreement, no registration, not even an **intention** to be partners is needed."},
   {t:"case", i:"🕯️", title:"Example: Priya and Tom's candles", facts:"They make and sell candles together, pay costs out of the takings and split the profit equally. They sign and register nothing.", rule:"They're a **general partnership** (business in common with a view of profit), so both have **unlimited liability** for the stall's debts."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔍", title:"Partnership clues (s.2)", body:"Getting a **share of the profits** is evidence of partnership, but not conclusive. Just **co-owning property** (s.2(1)) or **sharing gross returns** (takings before expenses, s.2(2)) is not enough on its own."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏷️", title:"Partnership: 'the firm' is just a label", body:"**No separate legal personality** in England and Wales. 'The firm' is the collective name for the partners (s.4). It can sue and be sued in the firm's name, but that's a court procedure rule, not legal personhood."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Two friends run a stall together and split the profit, with no paperwork. Partners?", body:"**Yes**, if they carry on a business in common with a view of profit. Sharing **profits** is evidence (not conclusive). Sharing only **gross returns** (takings before expenses) would not be enough on its own."},
   {t:"card", i:"💥", title:"Partnership: unlimited liability", body:"Each partner is personally liable for firm debts incurred while a partner (**s.9**) and for **torts** (civil wrongs) by a partner in the ordinary course of business (**ss.10 and 12**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎯", title:"One partner can be made to pay it all", body:"Liability is **joint** for the firm's debts (s.9) and **joint and several** for wrongs (s.12). Either way, a creditor can recover the **whole debt from any one partner** (suing one doesn't stop them suing the others: Civil Liability (Contribution) Act 1978 s.3). That partner then claims a share back from the others."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔚", title:"Partnership: when it ends (default)", body:"**Dissolved** when a partner **dies or becomes bankrupt** (s.33), or, in a **partnership at will** (no fixed term), when any partner **gives notice** (ss.26, 32). An agreement can say otherwise."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧾", title:"Partnership: tax", body:"The firm pays **no tax**. Each partner pays income tax + Class 4 NICs on **their own share**. The firm files a **partnership tax return** with HMRC."},
   {t:"card", i:"🪧", title:"Partnership: business name", body:"Same rule as a sole trader: if the firm trades under a name other than the partners' surnames, each **partner's name and an address** must appear on business documents and at the premises (CA 2006 Part 41)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A partner dies and there's no agreement. What happens?", body:"The partnership is **dissolved** by default (PA 1890 s.33)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📝", title:"LLP: created by registration", body:"Only exists by **registering at Companies House** under the **Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000 (LLPA 2000)**. Popular with professional firms like solicitors and accountants. Name must end '**limited liability partnership**' or '**LLP**'."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏛️", title:"LLP: a separate legal person", body:"It's a **body corporate** (s.1): owns property, makes contracts, is liable for its own debts. Each member is an **agent of the LLP**, not of the other members."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛡️", title:"LLP: limited liability", body:"Members' liability is limited to what they **agreed to contribute** (usually capital). LLP creditors can't normally pursue members personally.\n\nExceptions mirror directors: a member who **personally takes on responsibility** (e.g. gives their own negligent advice) can be sued personally, alongside the LLP, as can a member caught by statutory claims such as **wrongful trading**."},
   {t:"card", i:"👥", title:"LLP: minimum people", body:"At least **2 members** and **2 designated members** (members with extra duties, like signing and filing accounts and the **confirmation statement**, the yearly check that the public record is up to date)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔁", title:"LLP: fewer than 2 designated?", body:"If the LLP names fewer than two designated members, **every member is a designated member** (LLPA 2000 s.8(2))."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Is an LLP member an agent of the other members?", body:"**No.** Each member is an agent of the **LLP** itself."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧩", title:"LLP: flexible inside, public outside", body:"Members run management and profit-sharing however they like in a **members' agreement**. No directors or shareholders. But it files **annual accounts** and a confirmation statement, so details are **public**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🪟", title:"LLP: tax transparent", body:"Taxed **like a partnership**. The LLP pays no income or corporation tax; each member is taxed on their own share."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏢", title:"Ltd: the everyday company", body:"Formed by **registering at Companies House** under the **Companies Act 2006 (CA 2006)**. By far the most common structure for trading businesses. Name must end '**limited**' or '**Ltd**' (CA 2006 s.59)."},
   {t:"card", i:"👑", title:"Ltd: owners vs managers", body:"A **separate legal person**, owned by **shareholders** (also called **members**) and run by **directors**. One person can be the only shareholder **and** only director."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛡️", title:"Ltd: limited liability", body:"Shareholders' liability is limited to any amount **unpaid on their shares**. Once fully paid, they have nothing more to lose."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Can one person run a Ltd company alone?", body:"**Yes.** Minimum is **1 shareholder** and **1 director**, and they can be the same person."},
   {t:"card", i:"👤", title:"Ltd: minimum people", body:"At least **1 shareholder** and **1 director**. At least one director must be a **natural person** (a human, not a company). A **company secretary** is optional."},
   {t:"card", i:"🪙", title:"Ltd: no minimum share capital", body:"There is **no minimum share capital** for a private company: it can be formed with a **single share of 1p**. Only a **plc** must have allotted share capital of at least **£50,000**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚫", title:"Ltd: no public share offers", body:"A private company **cannot offer its shares to the public** (CA 2006 s.755)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📢", title:"Ltd: publicity", body:"Files annual accounts and a yearly **confirmation statement** (which updates shareholder details). Must notify changes of **directors** and **PSCs** within 14 days and file a return when it issues new shares. All **open to public inspection**."},
   {t:"card", i:"💷", title:"Ltd: tax", body:"The company pays **corporation tax** on profits. Shareholders pay income tax on **dividends** received; directors and employees pay income tax and NICs on **salaries**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Who pays tax on a Ltd company's trading profits?", body:"**The company**, via **corporation tax**. Owners are only taxed on what they take out (dividends, salary)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📈", title:"plc: may offer its shares to the public", body:"A company that **may offer its shares to the public**. Name must end 'public limited company' or 'plc'. Same personality and limited liability as a Ltd."},
   {t:"card", i:"➕", title:"plc: extra requirements", body:"At least **2 directors**, a **qualified company secretary**, and allotted share capital of at least **£50,000** (the **authorised minimum**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"¼", title:"plc: paying up shares", body:"Each share must be paid up to at least **one quarter** of its nominal (face) value, plus the whole of any **premium** (amount paid above nominal value)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎟️", title:"plc: trading certificate", body:"A company **formed** as a plc needs a **trading certificate** (CA 2006 s.761, confirming the £50,000 minimum) **before doing business or borrowing**. A Ltd that **re-registers** as a plc doesn't need one."},
   {t:"card", i:"📋", title:"plc: unlisted only", body:"The syllabus covers **unlisted** plcs: they can offer shares to the public but aren't on the **Official List** (shares traded on a regulated market like the LSE Main Market). Listing rules are off-syllabus."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"What's the minimum allotted share capital for a plc?", body:"**£50,000**, each share paid up to at least ¼ of nominal value plus all of any premium."}
 ]},
 {h:"Side-by-side comparison", cards:[
   {t:"compare", i:"🏗️", title:"How formed", cols:["", "How it's formed"], rows:[["🧍 Sole trader","Just start trading; register with HMRC"],["🤝 Partnership","Automatic when PA 1890 s.1 test met; written agreement advisable"],["🏛️ LLP","Register at Companies House (LLPA 2000)"],["🏢 Ltd","Register at Companies House (CA 2006)"],["📈 plc","Register + **trading certificate** (s.761) before trading"]]},
   {t:"compare", i:"👥", title:"Minimum people", cols:["", "Minimum"], rows:[["🧍 Sole trader","1"],["🤝 Partnership","2 partners"],["🏛️ LLP","2 members, 2 designated members"],["🏢 Ltd","1 shareholder, 1 director"],["📈 plc","1 shareholder, **2 directors** + qualified secretary"]]},
   {t:"compare", i:"⚖️", title:"Personality and liability", cols:["", "Own legal person?", "Owners' liability"], rows:[["🧍 Sole trader","❌","🔓 Unlimited"],["🤝 Partnership","❌","🔓 Unlimited (any partner can pay the whole debt)"],["🏛️ LLP","✅","🔒 Limited to agreed contribution"],["🏢 Ltd","✅","🔒 Limited to amount unpaid on shares"],["📈 plc","✅","🔒 Limited to amount unpaid on shares"]]},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"An LLP member's liability is limited to...?", body:"Their **agreed contribution**. (A shareholder's is limited to the amount **unpaid on their shares**.)"},
   {t:"compare", i:"🧭", title:"Who manages", cols:["", "Managed by"], rows:[["🧍 Sole trader","The owner"],["🤝 Partnership","All partners (by default)"],["🏛️ LLP","All members (default), or as members' agreement says"],["🏢 Ltd / 📈 plc","**Directors**; shareholders vote on key matters"]]},
   {t:"compare", i:"📢", title:"Filing and publicity", cols:["", "What's public"], rows:[["🧍 Sole trader","Nothing"],["🤝 Partnership","Nothing"],["🏛️ LLP","Accounts, confirmation statement, members"],["🏢 Ltd","Accounts, confirmation statement, officers, shareholders, constitution"],["📈 plc","As Ltd, with shorter accounts deadline"]]},
   {t:"compare", i:"🧾", title:"Tax", cols:["", "How taxed"], rows:[["🧍 Sole trader","Income tax + Class 4 NICs on all profits"],["🤝 Partnership","Each partner: income tax + Class 4 NICs on own share"],["🏛️ LLP","**Transparent**: each member taxed like a partner"],["🏢 Ltd / 📈 plc","**Corporation tax** on profits; owners taxed on salary/dividends"]]},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Which structures pay no tax themselves?", body:"**Partnerships** and **LLPs** (tax transparent). The partners/members are taxed on their shares."},
   {t:"compare", i:"💰", title:"Who owns the profits", cols:["", "Profits belong to"], rows:[["🧍 Sole trader","The owner, all of them"],["🤝 Partnership","The partners (equally by default)"],["🏛️ LLP","The LLP, shared among members as agreed (equally by default)"],["🏢 Ltd / 📈 plc","**The company**; shareholders get only dividends declared"]]},
   {t:"compare", i:"🏦", title:"Raising money", cols:["", "Options"], rows:[["🧍 Sole trader","Own money and bank loans (secured by mortgage or fixed charge); **no floating charge**"],["🤝 Partnership","Partners' capital and bank loans (secured by mortgage or fixed charge); **no floating charge**; can't issue shares"],["🏛️ LLP","Members' capital; can give a **floating charge**"],["🏢 Ltd","Loans, floating charge, sell shares privately"],["📈 plc","As Ltd, plus **offer shares to the public**"]]},
   {t:"compare", i:"♾️", title:"If an owner dies or leaves", cols:["", "What happens"], rows:[["🧍 Sole trader","Business ends with the owner"],["🤝 Partnership","Dissolved by default unless agreement says otherwise"],["🏛️ LLP","Continues"],["🏢 Ltd / 📈 plc","Continues (**perpetual succession**)"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"🌊", title:"What's a floating charge?", body:"Security over a **changing pool of assets** (like stock) that the business keeps using and selling until the charge 'crystallises'. In practice only **companies and LLPs** can grant one. More in **Debt finance and security**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📅", title:"Accounts deadlines", body:"File within **9 months** of year end for a private company or LLP; **6 months** for a plc. (Different deadlines apply to a company's first accounts.) ECCTA 2023 reforms (identity checks, registered email) are in **Forming a company**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"How long does a plc have to file its accounts?", body:"**6 months** from year end (9 months for a Ltd or LLP)."},
   {t:"compare", i:"⚠️", title:"Watch out: LLP vs limited partnership (LP)", cols:["", "LLP (LLPA 2000)", "LP (LP Act 1907)"], rows:[["Own legal person?","✅","❌ (in England and Wales)"],["All owners limited?","✅","❌ needs ≥1 **general partner** with unlimited liability"],["Lose protection by managing?","❌","✅ limited partners do"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"🚨", title:"LP trap", body:"If a question wants limited liability for **all** the owners, an **LP is wrong**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Separate legal personality", cards:[
   {t:"case", i:"👢", title:"Salomon v A Salomon & Co Ltd (1897)", facts:"Mr Salomon sold his boot business to his company, keeping 20,001 of 20,007 shares (family held 1 each). Part of the price was owed to him, secured by **debentures** (a charge over company assets). The company went insolvent.", rule:"Once validly registered, the company is a **different person** from him, even though he controlled it. He wasn't liable for its debts, and as a **secured creditor** his loan was repaid first."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧑‍⚖️", title:"The Salomon rule", body:"A validly formed company is a separate person from its shareholders and directors, **even a one-person company**. Same for an **LLP**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Unsecured creditors said the company was 'really just Mr Salomon'. Did that work?", body:"**No.** The House of Lords held the company was a separate person, and Mr Salomon was not liable for its debts."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏠", title:"Consequence 1: it owns its own property", body:"Company assets belong to the **company**, not the shareholders."},
   {t:"case", i:"🪵", title:"Macaura v Northern Assurance (1925)", facts:"Mr Macaura sold his timber to a company he owned, then insured it in his **own name**. It burned down.", rule:"No claim: the timber was the company's, so he had no **insurable interest**. A shareholder doesn't own company assets, even with **all** the shares."},
   {t:"card", i:"✍️", title:"Consequence 2: it can contract with its own people", body:"A company can make contracts with its own shareholders and directors, including employment contracts."},
   {t:"case", i:"✈️", title:"Lee v Lee's Air Farming (1961)", facts:"Mr Lee was controlling shareholder, sole director **and** pilot of a crop-spraying company. He died in a crash.", rule:"His widow could claim as widow of an **employee**: he had an employment contract with the company, a separate person."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Why couldn't Macaura claim on the insurance?", body:"The timber belonged to the **company**, not him, so he had **no insurable interest**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📜", title:"Consequence 3: contracts, suing, liability", body:"The company **makes contracts, sues and is sued** in its own name, and is liable for its own debts and its own **torts**."},
   {t:"card", i:"♾️", title:"Consequence 4: perpetual succession", body:"The company carries on when shareholders sell or directors resign or die. It ends only when **dissolved** (usually after liquidation)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏘️", title:"Consequence 5: groups", body:"In a **group**, parent and each subsidiary are separate persons. A parent is **not automatically liable** for a subsidiary's debts (**Adams v Cape Industries** 1990, see veil piercing)."},
   {t:"case", i:"🚲", title:"Example: Ben's Bikes Ltd", facts:"Ben is sole shareholder and director. The company buys £20,000 of stock on credit and can't pay.", rule:"The supplier's claim is against **the company, not Ben**. Ben's liability is limited to any amount unpaid on his shares."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Is a parent company automatically liable for its subsidiary's debts?", body:"**No.** Each group company is a separate legal person."}
 ]},
 {h:"Limited liability", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🎯", title:"It belongs to the members, not the company", body:"The **company** is liable **without limit** for its own debts, up to all its assets. What's limited is what **shareholders** (or LLP members) can be made to contribute."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔒", title:"Company limited by shares", body:"A shareholder's maximum liability = the amount **unpaid on their shares** (nominal value plus any agreed premium). **Fully paid** = nothing more owed, however big the debts."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎗️", title:"Other company types", body:"**Company limited by guarantee** (charities, clubs): members promise a fixed sum if it's wound up. **Unlimited companies** exist too, but are rare."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Does a company have limited liability?", body:"**No!** The company is liable in full for its debts. Its **shareholders'** liability is limited."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🧠", title:"Two different ideas", cols:["", "Separate personality", "Limited liability"], rows:[["Means","🧍 Business is its own person","🔒 Owners' contribution is capped"],["Belongs to","🏢 The business","👥 The owners"],["Usually together?","✅","✅"]]},
   {t:"case", i:"🌿", title:"Example: Asha and Fern Ltd", facts:"Asha holds 1,000 £1 shares issued at £1.50 each and has paid £1,000. Fern Ltd goes into insolvent liquidation owing £200,000.", rule:"She can be made to pay only the **£500 still unpaid** (balance of issue price, including premium), nothing towards the rest."},
   {t:"card", i:"💡", title:"Exam tip: spot the swap", body:"Wrong options say 'the **company** has limited liability' or 'shareholders are protected because the company is a separate person'. Best answer: the **company** is liable for its debts; the **shareholder's** liability is limited to any amount unpaid on their shares."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Shares fully paid, company owes millions. What does the shareholder owe?", body:"**Nothing more.** They've lost only what they paid for the shares."}
 ]},
 {h:"When owners and directors can still be personally liable", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🧥", title:"Limited liability is only a shareholder shield", body:"It protects people **as shareholders**. They can still be liable another way: under **contract**, **tort** or **statute**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚫", title:"This is NOT veil piercing", body:"In all these routes the company **stays a separate person**. The individual is liable in their own right."},
   {t:"card", i:"✍️", title:"1. Personal guarantees (contract)", body:"Banks and landlords often make small-company directors **guarantee** the company's debts. Company defaults → director pays. The **most common** way limited liability is lost in practice."},
   {t:"card", i:"🖊️", title:"2. Contracting in their own name", body:"A director who signs **personally** (not for the company), or contracts for a company not yet formed (a **pre-incorporation contract**, CA 2006 s.51), is personally liable **and can enforce it**, unless agreed otherwise. Signing 'on behalf of' the company doesn't help, and the company **can't ratify** it once formed: only a **new contract** with the company releases them."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A director signed a personal guarantee. Why must they pay?", body:"Because of the **guarantee**, a separate contract. Not veil piercing, and not because limited liability doesn't apply to small companies."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗣️", title:"3. Torts the individual commits", body:"A director isn't personally liable for the company's negligent advice unless they **personally assumed responsibility** to the claimant. They **are** liable for their own fraud or deceit."},
   {t:"case", i:"🌱", title:"Williams v Natural Life Health Foods (1998)", facts:"A director helped prepare the company's negligent business forecasts, but never dealt with the claimants personally.", rule:"**Not personally liable**: he hadn't assumed personal responsibility to them."},
   {t:"card", i:"📕", title:"4. Statute: the main examples", body:"Details are in **Clawback, directors' liability and order of priority**. Here's the headline list."},
   {t:"card", i:"🕵️", title:"Fraudulent trading (IA 1986 s.213)", body:"Anyone **knowingly** party to carrying on business with **intent to defraud creditors** can be ordered to contribute to the company's assets."},
   {t:"card", i:"📉", title:"Wrongful trading (IA 1986 s.214)", body:"Directors who kept trading after they **knew or ought to have concluded** there was no reasonable prospect of avoiding insolvent liquidation or administration, and didn't take **every step** to minimise creditors' loss. The same rule applies to **every LLP member**, designated or not."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Fraudulent vs wrongful trading: key difference?", body:"Fraudulent needs **intent to defraud** (anyone involved). Wrongful is about **directors** (and **LLP members**) who knew or **ought to have concluded** insolvency was unavoidable."},
   {t:"card", i:"🐦‍🔥", title:"Phoenix companies (IA 1986 ss.216-217)", body:"A director in the **12 months before** insolvent liquidation can't, for **5 years**, be involved in a business with the **same or similar name** without court permission. Breach = **crime** + **personal liability** for the new debts."},
   {t:"card", i:"⛔", title:"Disqualified directors (CDDA 1986 s.15)", body:"Acting as a director while **disqualified** makes you personally liable for company debts incurred while you did so."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎟️", title:"plc with no trading certificate (CA 2006 s.767)", body:"The deal is still **valid**, but if the company fails to perform within **21 days** of being asked, the **directors** are **jointly and severally liable** (each can be made to pay all) to indemnify the other party."},
   {t:"card", i:"1️⃣", title:"One-member LLP (LLPA 2000 s.4A)", body:"Trading with only one member for **more than 6 months**: that member (if they know) is personally liable, jointly and severally with the LLP, for debts incurred **after** the 6 months."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔙", title:"LLP withdrawals (IA 1986 s.214A)", body:"Member withdrawals in the **2 years** before winding up can be clawed back if the member knew or had reasonable grounds to believe the LLP was unable to pay its debts, or would become unable to as a result of the withdrawal."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Phoenix rule: how long is the ban, and who does it catch?", body:"**5 years**, for anyone who was a director in the **12 months before** the insolvent liquidation."}
 ]},
 {h:"Piercing (lifting) the corporate veil", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🎭", title:"What 'piercing the veil' means", body:"The court **ignores** the company's separate personality and treats its rights or liabilities as those of the person controlling it. It's **very rare**."},
   {t:"case", i:"🛢️", title:"Prest v Petrodel Resources Ltd (2013)", facts:"In a divorce, wealthy oil trader Mr Prest held London properties through his companies. His wife wanted them treated as his.", rule:"**No piercing**: he hadn't used the companies to **evade an existing legal obligation** (properties went in long before). But the companies held them **on trust** for him (he paid, they held for him), so they could go to his wife anyway."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏃", title:"Evasion principle (true piercing)", body:"Only where a person is under an **existing legal obligation or restriction** and **deliberately evades it** (or frustrates enforcement) by putting a company in the way. Even then, a **last resort** (Lord Sumption)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🙈", title:"Concealment principle (not true piercing)", body:"Where a company is used to **hide** the real facts or actors, the court just **looks behind it** to see what's really going on, without disregarding its personality."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"How did Mrs Prest get the properties if the veil wasn't pierced?", body:"The companies held them **on trust** for Mr Prest, so they were really his and could be transferred."},
   {t:"case", i:"🚗", title:"Gilford Motor Co v Horne (1933)", facts:"Horne promised his ex-employer not to approach its customers (a valid **restrictive covenant**), then set up a company in his wife's name to do it.", rule:"**Injunction** against him **and** the company. You can't escape your own existing obligation by acting through a company."},
   {t:"case", i:"🏡", title:"Jones v Lipman (1962)", facts:"Lipman contracted to sell his house, changed his mind, and transferred it to his company to dodge the sale.", rule:"**Specific performance** (order to complete the sale) against him and the company. Same evasion rule, for land."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🛑", title:"Courts will NOT pierce just because...", cols:["", "Pierce?", "Authority"], rows:[["⚖️ It seems fair or just","❌","Adams v Cape"],["👤 One person owns and controls it","❌","Salomon"],["💸 It's insolvent and creditors are unpaid","❌","-"],["🏘️ It's part of a group","❌","Adams v Cape"]]},
   {t:"case", i:"🏭", title:"Adams v Cape Industries (1990)", facts:"Claimants won US judgments for asbestos injuries and tried to enforce them against the English parent by treating it and its US subsidiary as one.", rule:"Refused. A group isn't a 'single economic unit', and using a subsidiary to limit **future** liability is legitimate."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"What's the one situation where true piercing is allowed?", body:"Someone **already under a legal obligation** deliberately **evades** it by interposing a company they control, and no other remedy works."},
   {t:"case", i:"⛏️", title:"Vedanta v Lungowe (2019), following Chandler v Cape (2012)", facts:"A parent was sued over harm caused by its subsidiary's operations.", rule:"A parent can be liable in **tort** if it owed a **direct duty of care** itself (e.g. by controlling or advising on the activity). That's ordinary negligence, **not** veil piercing."},
   {t:"case", i:"☕", title:"Example: Dev's café", facts:"Dev sells his café and validly promises not to open a rival within 1 mile for 2 years. Six months later his new company opens next door.", rule:"Court can **pierce the veil** and grant an injunction against Dev and the company (like Gilford v Horne). Contrast **Prest**: the properties went into the companies **before** any obligation to his wife existed, so there was nothing to evade."},
   {t:"card", i:"💡", title:"Exam tip: try the ordinary route first", body:"Check for a **trust** (Prest), **personal guarantee**, the director's **own tort**, or a statutory claim (e.g. wrongful trading). Choose veil piercing only for **an existing obligation + company used to evade it**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚨", title:"Watch out: insolvency claims aren't piercing", body:"**Wrongful and fraudulent trading are statutory claims** against the individual. 'The court will pierce the veil because the director traded while insolvent' is **wrong**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Director kept trading while insolvent. Veil piercing?", body:"**No.** That's a **wrongful trading** claim (IA 1986 s.214), a statutory claim against the director."}
 ]},
 {h:"Choosing a business structure", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🧭", title:"No single right answer", body:"The best structure depends on what the client **cares about most**. Work through 7 steps."},
   {t:"card", i:"👥", title:"Step 1: Who is involved?", body:"One person alone: **sole trader** or **company** (one shareholder/director). Two or more together are **already a partnership** unless they form an LLP or company."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚠️", title:"Step 2: How much risk is there?", body:"Big potential debts or claims (borrowing, stock, staff, risky work)? They'll want **limited liability**: **LLP** or **company**. Banks may still want **personal guarantees**; insurance may cover some risks."},
   {t:"card", i:"🤫", title:"Step 3: How important are privacy and low cost?", body:"Sole traders and partnerships: **no registration**, nothing public. Companies and LLPs: register, keep filing, public accounts, and people must **verify their identity**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Two people start working together with no paperwork. What are they?", body:"A **general partnership**, automatically."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🧾", title:"Step 4: How will the owners be taxed?", cols:["", "Sole trader / partner / LLP member", "Company"], rows:[["Who's taxed on profits","👤 Owners: income tax + NICs","🏢 Company: corporation tax"],["When","📆 All profits as made, drawn or not","💷 Owners only on what they take out"],["Leave profits in?","❌ Still taxed","✅ Possible"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"🔢", title:"Does a company save tax?", body:"Corporation tax is usually lower than **higher-rate** income tax, but it **depends on the figures**, which the exam gives you. See **Income tax** and **Corporation tax**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏦", title:"Step 5: How will the business raise money?", body:"Only a **company** can sell **shares** to investors; only a **plc** can offer them to the **public**. Companies and LLPs can give lenders a **floating charge**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Client wants to offer shares to the public. Structure?", body:"A **plc**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎛️", title:"Step 6: How do the owners want to run it and take profits?", body:"Partnerships and LLPs: **flexible**, set by agreement; profits withdrawn freely. Companies: fixed structure (directors manage, shareholders vote, CA 2006 formalities); dividends only from **distributable profits** (see **Distributions, dividends and accounting**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"♾️", title:"Step 7: Does the business need to outlast its owners?", body:"Company or LLP: continues whatever happens to owners. Sole trader: ends with them. Partnership: **dissolves** by default."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🎯", title:"Common exam answers", cols:["Client wants...", "Best fit"], rows:[["🛡️ Limited liability + partnership-style flexibility + tax transparency (often professionals)","🏛️ LLP"],["📈 To offer shares to the public","plc"],["🛡️ Limited liability, reinvest profits, or private outside investors","🏢 Ltd"],["🤫 Simple, private, cheap, low risk","🧍 Sole trader (alone) / 🤝 Partnership (with others, ideally with a written agreement)"]]},
   {t:"case", i:"📐", title:"Example: Kemi and Leon, architects", facts:"They want to protect their homes from a negligence claim beyond their insurance, keep profit-sharing flexible, and be taxed as individuals.", rule:"**LLP**: limited liability, flexible members' agreement, tax transparent. A company limits liability but taxes at company level and is less flexible; a partnership gives no limited liability."},
   {t:"card", i:"💡", title:"Exam tip: tax distractors", body:"A **company** pays **corporation tax**, never income tax, on trading profits. An **LLP** and a **partnership** pay **no tax themselves**: members/partners are taxed on their shares."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Does an LLP pay corporation tax?", body:"**No.** It's tax transparent: each member pays income tax and NICs on their share."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗺️", title:"Your 6-step route", body:"**1** Identify the structure → **2** Separate legal personality → **3** Limited liability → **4** Personal liability anyway? → **5** Piercing the veil → **6** Choosing a structure. The full route is this topic's **framework**."}
 ]}
]};

;
window.SQE_LEARN = window.SQE_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_LEARN["blp/directors"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🏢", title:"A company needs humans", body:"A company is a **separate legal person**, but it can only act through people. The **directors** manage its business and use its powers."},
   {t:"compare", i:"⚖️", title:"Shareholders vs directors", cols:["", "🧑‍💼 Directors", "👥 Shareholders"], rows:[["Run the business day to day","✅","❌"],["Own the company","❌ (unless they also hold shares)","✅"],["Also called","The board","Members"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"📜", title:"Model Articles art.3", body:"The **Model Articles** are the default rulebook (see **Forming a company**). Art.3: directors manage the business and may exercise **all the powers of the company**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Can shareholders tell the board what to do?", body:"Only by **special resolution** (75%+ vote) under Model Articles **art.4**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎯", title:"Who the duties are owed to", body:"Directors owe duties **to the company**, not to individual shareholders or creditors. The duties are in the **Companies Act 2006 (CA 2006) ss.171-177**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗺️", title:"What this topic covers", body:"Who is a director, appointment, the secretary, service contracts, removal and leaving, disqualification, the seven general duties, and what happens on breach."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔗", title:"Covered elsewhere", body:"Board decisions and deals needing shareholder approval: **Decision-making**. Derivative claims: **Shareholders' rights**. Wrongful trading: **Clawback, directors' liability and order of priority**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Who is a director?", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"📋", title:"De jure director", body:"Validly **appointed** and **registered** at Companies House. The 'official' director."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎭", title:"De facto director", body:"**Acts as a director** (e.g. joins board decisions, holds themselves out as one) without valid appointment. The law treats them as a director, **duties included**."},
   {t:"card", i:"👤", title:"Shadow director (s.251)", body:"A person whose **directions or instructions the directors are accustomed to follow**, e.g. a dominant shareholder controlling the board from behind the scenes."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Is a solicitor a shadow director if the board always follows their advice?", body:"**No.** Someone giving **professional advice** (solicitor, accountant) is not a shadow director just because the board follows it. General duties apply to real shadow directors where capable of applying (**s.170(5)**)."},
   {t:"compare", i:"⚖️", title:"Executive vs non-executive (NED)", cols:["", "💼 Executive", "🪑 Non-executive"], rows:[["Employee?","✅ full-time management role","❌"],["Example","MD, finance director","Independent board member"],["Service contract?","✅ usually","❌"],["Same legal duties?","✅","✅ (s.174 standard reflects role)"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"🧠", title:"Why have NEDs?", body:"They bring **independent judgment**. In law they owe **exactly the same duties** as executives."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔁", title:"Alternate director", body:"Someone a director appoints to **act in their place**. Not in the private Model Articles; possible under the public Model Articles or if the articles allow."}
 ]},
 {h:"Appointment", cards:[
   {t:"compare", i:"🔢", title:"Minimum directors (s.154)", cols:["", "🏠 Private", "🏛️ Public"], rows:[["Minimum directors","1","2"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"🧍", title:"At least one human (s.155)", body:"At least one director must be a **natural person** (a human). A company can still be a director alongside: the ban on corporate directors is **not yet in force**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎂", title:"Minimum age 16", body:"A director must be at least **16** (s.157)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📝", title:"First directors", body:"Named in the **IN01** statement of proposed officers when the company is formed."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Who can appoint later directors under Model Articles art.17?", body:"**Either** the shareholders by **ordinary resolution** (50%+) **or** the directors by **board resolution**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📝", title:"Tell Companies House", body:"File **AP01** (appointment) or **CH01** (change of details) **within 14 days** (s.167)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🪪", title:"Identity verification (ECCTA 2023)", body:"Since **18 November 2025**, the AP01 must confirm the director's **identity is verified**. Acting as a director without verification is an **offence**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏳", title:"Existing directors' transition", body:"Directors already in office on 18 November 2025 **had to** verify by the company's **first confirmation statement** after that date (the transition ended in November 2026)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗂️", title:"No company register of directors", body:"Abolished **18 November 2025**. The public record is now at **Companies House** only."},
   {t:"card", i:"🩹", title:"Defective appointment (s.161)", body:"A director's acts are **still valid** even if a defect in their appointment is found later."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Who cannot be a director?", body:"An **undischarged bankrupt** (without court permission) and a **disqualified** person (Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986 (CDDA) ss.11 and 13). Both are **criminal offences**. Also anyone **under 16** (s.157), and since 18 Nov 2025 nobody may act until their **identity is verified**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Company secretary", cards:[
   {t:"compare", i:"⚖️", title:"Do you need a secretary?", cols:["", "🏠 Private", "🏛️ Public"], rows:[["Must have one?","❌ only if articles require (s.270)","✅ (s.271)"],["Must be qualified?","❌","✅ (s.273)"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"🧑‍💻", title:"No secretary? No problem", body:"In a private company without one, a **director** or someone **authorised by the directors** can do anything required of a secretary."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎓", title:"Who is 'qualified' (s.273)?", body:"E.g. a solicitor, barrister, chartered accountant, chartered secretary, or someone the directors think has the **necessary knowledge and experience**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Who appoints and removes the secretary, and what's filed?", body:"The **directors**. File **AP03** (appointment) or **TM02** (termination) **within 14 days**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🖇️", title:"Secretary's apparent authority", body:"A secretary **appears** to have authority for **administrative** contracts: staff, cars, office equipment."},
   {t:"case", i:"🚗", title:"Panorama Developments v Fidelis Furnishing Fabrics (1971)", facts:"The company secretary hired cars in the company's name, claiming they were to collect customers, but used them himself.", rule:"The company **had to pay**. Hiring cars is within a secretary's apparent **administrative** authority."}
 ]},
 {h:"Directors' authority to bind the company", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"👥", title:"The board acts together", body:"The **board** acts collectively and can bind the company. An **individual** director binds it only if they have **authority**."},
   {t:"card", i:"✍️", title:"Actual authority", body:"Given by the board. **Express**: e.g. a board resolution letting a director sign a contract. **Implied** from the role: e.g. an MD can make normal management contracts."},
   {t:"card", i:"🪞", title:"Apparent (ostensible) authority", body:"The company (via the board) **represented** to an outsider that someone had authority, and the outsider **relied** on it. E.g. letting a director act as MD."},
   {t:"case", i:"📐", title:"Freeman & Lockyer v Buckhurst Park Properties (1964)", facts:"A director acted as managing director with the board's knowledge and hired architects.", rule:"The company was **bound to pay** the architects: **apparent authority**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Constitution limits a director's power. Is an outsider stuck?", body:"No. **s.40**: for a person dealing **in good faith**, the directors' power to bind the company is treated as **free of any constitutional limitation**."},
   {t:"card", i:"😇", title:"Good faith is presumed", body:"An outsider is **presumed** to act in good faith, and is **not** in bad faith just because they knew the act was beyond the directors' powers."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚨", title:"Inside the company, it's still a breach", body:"Directors who exceed their powers **breach s.171** (duty to act within powers)."},
   {t:"case", i:"🍃", title:"Example: Ffion and Leaf Ltd", facts:"Ffion, MD of Leaf Ltd, signs a **£70,000** contract without board approval. The articles require approval over **£50,000**.", rule:"The **supplier can enforce** the contract (apparent authority + s.40). Ffion has **breached s.171**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Service contracts", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"📄", title:"What's a service contract?", body:"An executive director's **employment contract** with the company, usually approved by the **board**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏰", title:"Long service contracts (s.188)", body:"If it **guarantees employment for more than 2 years** (company can't end it by notice, or only in set circumstances), the long term needs **prior ordinary resolution** of members."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔍", title:"Memorandum for inspection", body:"A memorandum of the proposed contract must be available at the **registered office** for at least **15 days** before the meeting **and** at the meeting (or sent with a written resolution)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"What if s.188 approval isn't obtained?", body:"The guaranteed-term provision is **void**, and the contract can be ended on **reasonable notice** (s.189)."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🗳️", title:"Can the director vote on their own contract?", cols:["", "👥 Members' resolution", "🪑 Board meeting"], rows:[["Can vote?","✅ s.188 doesn't stop them voting their shares","❌ Model Articles art.14"],["Counts in quorum?","n/a","❌"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"🗄️", title:"Keep copies (s.228-229)", body:"Copies of service contracts must be open to members for at least **1 year** after the contract ends. Inspection is **free** (s.229)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Leaving office", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🚪", title:"Removal by shareholders (s.168)", body:"Shareholders can remove a director before their term ends by **ordinary resolution at a general meeting**, **whatever the articles or any agreement say**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📮", title:"Special notice (s.312)", body:"The shareholder proposing it must give the company notice at least **28 days before the meeting**. The company then tells members (normally with the meeting notice, at least **14 days** before)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎤", title:"Director's right to be heard (s.169)", body:"The company sends the director a copy of the notice. They can **speak at the meeting** and have **written representations** circulated (or read out)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Can you remove a director by written resolution?", body:"**No** (s.288(2)). The director would lose their right to speak at a meeting."},
   {t:"card", i:"📣", title:"Board won't call a meeting?", body:"A shareholder with **5%** of voting capital can **require** the directors to call one (s.303). See **Decision-making**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏋️", title:"Bushell v Faith clauses", body:"Articles can give a director **weighted votes** on a resolution to remove them. This can make them **irremovable** in practice."},
   {t:"case", i:"⚖️", title:"Bushell v Faith (1970)", facts:"300 shares held 100 each by director Mr Faith and his two sisters. Articles gave a director **3 votes per share** on their removal. He won **300 to 200**.", rule:"Clause **valid**. s.168 only requires an ordinary resolution; it doesn't regulate how votes are allocated."},
   {t:"card", i:"💷", title:"Removal doesn't cancel compensation", body:"The director keeps any claim for **compensation or damages** for ending their service contract (s.168(5)). A long fixed-term contract = big claim = **practical bar** to removal."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎁", title:"Payments for loss of office (ss.215-222)", body:"Paying a director **compensation for loss of office** beyond their legal entitlement needs an **ordinary resolution** (s.217)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"When is no approval needed for a loss-of-office payment?", body:"A good faith payment for an **existing legal obligation**, e.g. contract damages (s.220), or small payments up to **£200** (s.221)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📝", title:"File TM01", body:"File **TM01** (termination of appointment) **within 14 days**."},
   {t:"card", i:"✅", title:"Exam tip: s.168 checklist", body:"1) **Ordinary resolution** 2) at a **general meeting** (never written) 3) **28 days' special notice** 4) director can **speak and circulate** 5) any **Bushell v Faith** votes 6) director's **contract damages** claim."},
   {t:"case", i:"🧮", title:"Example: Ann, Ben and Cal", facts:"Ann 40%, Ben 35%, Cal 25% (a director). Articles give a director **3 votes per share** on their removal.", rule:"Cal's 25 shares = **75 votes** v Ann and Ben's 75. Not over 50%, so **it fails**. Without the clause, their 75% would pass it."},
   {t:"card", i:"👋", title:"Resignation or rotation", body:"**Resignation** by notice to the company (Model Articles art.18(f)), or **retirement by rotation** if the articles require it (public company Model Articles)."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚡", title:"Automatic termination (art.18)", body:"They stop being a director if: **barred by law** (e.g. disqualified), become **bankrupt**, or make a **composition with creditors** generally (a deal with creditors, e.g. an IVA)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🩺", title:"More art.18 triggers", body:"A registered doctor gives a written opinion they're **physically or mentally incapable** of acting and may stay so **more than 3 months**, or the company receives their **resignation** notice."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Can the board remove a director under the Model Articles?", body:"**No.** Only shareholders remove (s.168), unless the articles give the board that power. Disqualification by the court is the other route (next section)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Disqualification (CDDA 1986)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"⛔", title:"What disqualification means", body:"The court bars someone from being a director, or being concerned in promoting, forming or managing a company, **without the court's permission**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📚", title:"Grounds", body:"Conviction of an **indictable offence** connected with a company (s.2); **persistent filing default** (s.3); **fraud in a winding up** (s.4); and most commonly **unfitness** (s.6)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📉", title:"Unfitness (s.6)", body:"For a director of an **insolvent** company whose conduct makes them **unfit**, the court **must** disqualify for **2 to 15 years**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Is court action the only route?", body:"No. The Secretary of State can accept a **disqualification undertaking** (s.1A), with the **same effect**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚔", title:"Acting while disqualified", body:"A **criminal offence** (s.13) **plus personal liability** for company debts incurred while acting, jointly and severally with the company (s.15)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧑‍🤝‍🧑", title:"Followers are liable too", body:"Anyone acting on the instructions of someone they **know** is disqualified faces the same personal liability."},
   {t:"card", i:"💸", title:"Undischarged bankrupts", body:"Acting as a director without court permission is an **offence** (s.11)."}
 ]},
 {h:"The seven general duties (ss.171-177)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🏢", title:"Owed to the company (s.170(1))", body:"So only the **company** (or a shareholder via a **derivative claim**) can enforce them."},
   {t:"card", i:"📜", title:"Old cases still count", body:"The duties replace the old common law and equitable rules but are **interpreted the same way** (s.170(4)). Pre-2006 cases still apply."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧩", title:"Duties overlap", body:"More than one duty can apply to the **same facts**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Do any duties continue after a director leaves?", body:"Yes (s.170(2)): **s.175** for property, information or opportunities learned of while a director, and **s.176** for third-party benefits for things done while a director."},
   {t:"card", i:"1️⃣", title:"s.171: act within powers", body:"Follow the **constitution** (articles and resolutions), and use powers only for the **purposes they were given** (the **proper purpose** rule)."},
   {t:"case", i:"🛢️", title:"Howard Smith v Ampol Petroleum (1974)", facts:"Directors issued new shares to a bidder to **dilute** two majority shareholders and enable the takeover they preferred.", rule:"Share allotment power is for **raising capital**, not manipulating control. Allotment **set aside**, even though directors honestly thought it was good for the company."},
   {t:"card", i:"2️⃣", title:"s.172: promote the success of the company", body:"Act in the way you consider, **in good faith**, most likely to promote the company's success **for the benefit of its members as a whole**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📋", title:"s.172 factors to 'have regard to'", body:"Among other matters: **long-term consequences**; **employees**; relationships with **suppliers, customers** and others; **community and environment**; **high standards of business conduct**; acting **fairly between members**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Is the s.172 test objective or subjective?", body:"Largely **subjective**: did they **honestly believe** it promoted success? Courts won't second-guess commercial decisions made in good faith. If they gave **no thought** to the company, ask whether an intelligent, honest director could reasonably have believed it was beneficial."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏦", title:"Creditors' interests (s.172(3))", body:"The s.172 duty is subject to any rule requiring directors to consider **creditors**."},
   {t:"case", i:"🏭", title:"BTI 2014 LLC v Sequana SA (2022)", facts:"AWA's directors paid a ~**€135m** dividend to its parent while solvent but facing a long-term contingent pollution clean-up liability. AWA later became insolvent.", rule:"The **creditor duty** triggers only when the company is **insolvent or bordering on it**, or insolvent liquidation/administration is **probable**. Not just a real risk. Not met, so **not liable**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📈", title:"Sequana: the sliding scale", body:"Once triggered, directors must **consider and give appropriate weight** to creditors. Worse finances = more weight. When insolvent liquidation is **inevitable**, creditors become **paramount**."},
   {t:"card", i:"3️⃣", title:"s.173: independent judgment", body:"Don't just do what someone else says (e.g. the shareholder who appointed you). You may **take advice** but must make **your own decision**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🟢", title:"s.173 is not breached when...", body:"Acting under an **agreement duly entered into by the company** that restricts future discretion, or in a way **authorised by the constitution**."},
   {t:"card", i:"4️⃣", title:"s.174: reasonable care, skill and diligence", body:"The standard of a **reasonably diligent person** with (a) the **general** knowledge, skill and experience **expected** of someone in that role, and (b) what the director **actually has**."},
   {t:"compare", i:"📏", title:"s.174's two-part test", cols:["", "(a) Objective", "(b) Subjective"], rows:[["Measures","What's expected for the role","What this director actually has"],["Effect","🔒 Minimum floor","⬆️ Can only raise the bar"],["Example","Inexperience is no excuse","Qualified accountant FD judged higher"]]},
   {t:"case", i:"🔥", title:"Re D'Jan of London (1994)", facts:"A director signed an insurance proposal **without reading it**. It had a wrong answer, so the insurer refused to pay after a fire.", rule:"**Negligent**: a reasonable director would have read it. But **partly excused** under s.1157."},
   {t:"card", i:"👀", title:"Delegation is OK, but supervise", body:"Directors may **delegate**, but must **supervise** delegates and keep **informed** about the company's affairs."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Recap: name s.171-174", body:"**171** within powers; **172** promote success; **173** independent judgment; **174** care, skill and diligence."},
   {t:"card", i:"5️⃣", title:"s.175: avoid conflicts of interest", body:"Avoid situations where you have, or could have, a **direct or indirect interest** that **conflicts** (or may) with the company's."},
   {t:"card", i:"💡", title:"Exploiting opportunities (s.175(2))", body:"Especially exploiting **property, information or opportunity**. It **doesn't matter whether the company could take advantage** of it."},
   {t:"case", i:"⛽", title:"Industrial Development Consultants v Cooley (1972)", facts:"IDC's MD was offered a gas board contract, which would **never** go to IDC, only to him. He faked ill health to leave, then took it.", rule:"He had to **account to IDC for all his profits**, even though IDC could never have got the contract."},
   {t:"card", i:"🟢", title:"s.175 is not breached if...", body:"The situation **can't reasonably be regarded as likely** to cause a conflict, or it's been **authorised by the directors** (s.175(4))."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🔐", title:"Board authorisation of conflicts (s.175(5))", cols:["", "🏠 Private", "🏛️ Public"], rows:[["Board can authorise?","✅ unless constitution prohibits","✅ only if articles expressly allow"],["Formed before 1 Oct 2008?","Members' ordinary resolution first","n/a"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"🚫", title:"Conflicted director sits out (s.175(6))", body:"The conflicted director (and other interested directors) **don't count in the quorum** and their **votes don't count**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Does s.175 cover a deal between the director and the company?", body:"**No.** Transactions **with the company itself** fall under **s.177** and **s.182**."},
   {t:"card", i:"6️⃣", title:"s.176: no third-party benefits", body:"Don't accept a **benefit** (money, gifts, hospitality) from a third party given because you're a director, or for anything you do or don't do as one."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🎁", title:"s.176 vs s.175", cols:["", "s.175 conflicts", "s.176 benefits"], rows:[["Board can authorise?","✅","❌"],["Members can authorise?","✅","✅ (or articles permit)"],["Safe if unlikely to cause conflict?","✅","✅ e.g. modest hospitality"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"💼", title:"Salary isn't a 'benefit'", body:"Benefits from the **company itself** or its employer (e.g. a director's **salary**) are excluded from s.176."},
   {t:"card", i:"7️⃣", title:"s.177: declare interest in a proposed deal", body:"If interested **directly or indirectly** in a **proposed deal with the company** (e.g. it's buying your land, or contracting with another company in which you hold shares), declare the **nature and extent** to the **other directors** **before** it's entered into."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗣️", title:"How to declare", body:"At a **board meeting**, by **written notice**, or by **general notice** (e.g. 'I'm a director of X Ltd: treat me as interested in any contract with it')."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"When is no s.177 declaration needed?", body:"Interest **unlikely to cause a conflict**; other directors **already know** (or ought to); it's about your **service contract** considered by the board or a committee. A **sole director** has no one to declare to."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚖️", title:"Breaching s.177", body:"**Civil** consequences only. The transaction is usually **voidable** (can be set aside)."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏱️", title:"s.182: existing deals", body:"If the company **already entered** a deal you're interested in (and you didn't declare under s.177), declare **as soon as reasonably practicable**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚔", title:"Breaching s.182", body:"A **criminal offence** (a fine), but the transaction **stays valid**."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🧭", title:"Exam tip: three conflict rules", cols:["", "s.175", "s.177", "s.182"], rows:[["Situation","🌍 Outside opportunity","🤝 Proposed deal with company","📁 Existing undeclared deal"],["Fix","Board authorises","Declare","Declare ASAP"],["Breach","Civil","Civil","❗ Crime"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"⚠️", title:"Watch out: declaring ≠ voting", body:"A s.177 declaration doesn't let the director **vote**. Under **Model Articles art.14** they can't vote or count in the quorum unless an exception applies. Big deals may also need **s.190** approval."}
 ]},
 {h:"Transactions with directors needing shareholder approval (overview)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🗳️", title:"Four deals need members' OK", body:"Each needs an **ordinary resolution**. Details and voting rules are in **Decision-making**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏰", title:"s.188: long service contracts", body:"Guaranteed term **over 2 years**. Approve **in advance**. Without it: term **void**, terminable on **reasonable notice**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏠", title:"s.190: substantial property transactions", body:"Company buys from or sells to a director (or connected person) a non-cash asset worth **over £100,000**, or **over 10% of net assets** and **over £5,000**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"s.190: when approve, and what if you don't?", body:"Ordinary resolution **in advance** (or contract **conditional** on it). Without: **voidable**; director must **account for gain** and **indemnify loss**."},
   {t:"card", i:"💳", title:"s.197: loans to directors", body:"Over **£10,000** total (or over **£50,000** for business expenditure). Without approval: **voidable**; director must **account and indemnify**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎁", title:"s.217: payment for loss of office", body:"Over **£200** and not a legal entitlement. Without approval: the payment is **held on trust** for the company."},
   {t:"compare", i:"📊", title:"Quick table", cols:["", "Threshold", "No approval"], rows:[["s.188 contract","> 2 years","Term void"],["s.190 property","> £100k, or > 10% net assets and > £5k","Voidable + account/indemnify"],["s.197 loan","> £10k (> £50k business)","Voidable + account/indemnify"],["s.217 loss of office","> £200","Held on trust"]]}
 ]},
 {h:"Consequences of breach of duty", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🧰", title:"Remedies (s.178)", body:"The same as for the old **equitable** duties. Here's the toolkit 👇"},
   {t:"card", i:"💷", title:"Damages or equitable compensation", body:"For **loss caused**. Mainly for **s.174** (negligence)."},
   {t:"card", i:"💰", title:"Account of profits", body:"The director hands over **any profit** from the breach (e.g. Cooley), **whether or not** the company lost anything."},
   {t:"card", i:"↩️", title:"Rescission, injunction, property", body:"**Rescission**: set aside a contract made in breach (e.g. an undeclared conflict), if still possible. **Injunction**: stop a threatened breach. **Restoration** of property and a **constructive trust** over property obtained in breach."},
   {t:"card", i:"👢", title:"Summary dismissal", body:"If the director is also an **employee**, they can be dismissed without notice."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Who can sue a director for breach?", body:"The **company** (decided by the board, or a **liquidator/administrator** if insolvent), or a shareholder by **derivative claim** with court permission (CA 2006 Part 11; see **Shareholders' rights**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"👍", title:"Ratification (s.239)", body:"Members can **ratify** (approve after the event) negligence, default, breach of duty or trust by **ordinary resolution**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🙅", title:"Who can't vote on ratification", body:"Votes of the **director** and **connected** members (spouse, children, a company they control) are **disregarded**. On a written resolution they're **not eligible members**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚫", title:"Some things can't be ratified", body:"E.g. **unlawful acts** or **fraud on the minority**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛟", title:"Court relief (s.1157)", body:"The court may relieve a director wholly or partly if they acted **honestly and reasonably** and ought **fairly to be excused**. Rare for negligence (it's rarely 'reasonable'), but partly granted in **Re D'Jan**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Can the articles exempt or the company indemnify a director for breach?", body:"**No**: void (ss.232-234). But the company **can** buy **D&O insurance** (directors' and officers' liability insurance) (s.233)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛡️", title:"Qualifying third-party indemnity", body:"Allowed: covers claims by **outsiders**, not by the **company**, and **not criminal fines**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏳", title:"Limitation period", body:"Generally **6 years**. **None** for fraud, or where the director still holds company property."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎯", title:"Exam tip: diverted opportunity", body:"For a s.175 diversion the usual remedy is an **account of profits**. It doesn't depend on company loss or the company being able to take the opportunity."}
 ]}
]};

;
window.SQE_LEARN = window.SQE_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_LEARN["blp/formation"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🏛️", title:"No registration, no company", body:"A **company** or **LLP** (limited liability partnership) only exists once it is **registered at Companies House**, the official registrar of companies. Before that, nothing can be done in its name."},
   {t:"card", i:"🤝", title:"Partnerships just happen", body:"A **general partnership** needs **no registration**. It arises as soon as 2+ people carry on a business in common with a view of profit (**PA 1890 s.1**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧾", title:"Partnership 'formation' is practical", body:"For a partnership, the steps are practical ones: a **partnership agreement**, **tax registration** and the **business names** rules."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Can you sign a contract in a company's name before it's registered?", body:"No. Until Companies House registers it, **there is no company**, so nothing can be done in its name."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗺️", title:"What this topic covers", body:"Filing to form a company, the **constitution** (articles + memorandum), **company names**, changing the articles, post-incorporation steps, **pre-incorporation contracts**, forming or re-registering a **plc**, forming a partnership or LLP, and **ECCTA 2023** reforms."},
   {t:"card", i:"📜", title:"ECCTA in a nutshell", body:"The **Economic Crime and Corporate Transparency Act 2023 (ECCTA)** reformed Companies House. You'll meet it throughout this topic."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔗", title:"Where the neighbours live", body:"Choosing a structure: **Business structures**. Appointing directors later: **Directors**. Filing deadlines after formation: also in **Decision-making**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Two ways to get a company", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🆕", title:"Option 1: incorporate from scratch", body:"Apply to Companies House, usually **online**, through a formation agent or a solicitor."},
   {t:"card", i:"📦", title:"Option 2: buy a shelf company", body:"A **shelf company** is already incorporated by a formation agent and kept 'on the shelf' with no business."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔄", title:"Taking over a shelf company", body:"The buyer takes a **transfer of the subscriber share(s)** (the shares taken by the first members on formation), appoints **new directors**, and usually changes the **name** and **articles**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Big plus of a shelf company?", body:"It **already exists**, so it can **contract immediately**. No pre-incorporation contract problem."},
   {t:"compare", i:"⚖️", title:"Shelf company: pros and cons", cols:["", "📦 Shelf company"], rows:[["Exists already, can contract now","✅"],["Name and articles usually need changing (special resolutions, i.e. 75% votes of members, + filings)","❌"],["First directors and shareholder must be replaced","❌"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"⚡", title:"Speed edge mostly gone", body:"Most new companies are now incorporated **online within 24 hours**, so the shelf company's speed advantage has largely gone."}
 ]},
 {h:"What is filed to register a company (CA 2006 ss.9-13)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"📝", title:"Form IN01 + fee", body:"The application is made on **form IN01** with a fee. Here's what goes in it, piece by piece."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏷️", title:"Name and type", body:"The **proposed name**, that members' liability is **limited by shares**, and whether it is **private or public**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📍", title:"Registered office", body:"Which part of the UK it is in (England and Wales, Wales, Scotland or Northern Ireland) and its address. It must be an **appropriate address** (see ECCTA)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📧", title:"Registered email address", body:"Required under **ECCTA**. It is **not shown** on the public register."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Is the registered email address made public?", body:"No. It's required (ECCTA) but **not shown** on the public register."},
   {t:"card", i:"💷", title:"Statement of capital and initial shareholdings (s.10)", body:"Number, class and **nominal value** (the fixed face value of each share) of shares taken by the **subscribers** (the first shareholders), and the amount **paid up** (paid to the company so far)."},
   {t:"card", i:"👔", title:"Statement of proposed officers (s.12)", body:"The **first directors** (and secretary, if any), with their **consent to act**. Each director must have a **verified identity**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🕵️", title:"Statement of initial significant control (s.12A)", body:"Details of each **PSC** (person with significant control): someone holding **more than 25%** of shares or votes, who can appoint/remove a **majority of the board**, or has **significant influence or control**. Each PSC must also have a **verified identity**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Holding exactly 25% of the shares: PSC?", body:"Not on that ground. The test is **more than 25%** of shares or votes."},
   {t:"card", i:"✅", title:"Two more statements", body:"A **statement of compliance** (s.13) that CA 2006 registration requirements are met, and a **statement of lawful purpose** (ECCTA) that the company is formed for a lawful purpose."},
   {t:"card", i:"✍️", title:"Memorandum of association (s.8)", body:"Short standard form: each subscriber says they **wish to form a company** and **agree to become a member**, taking at least one share. It **cannot be amended** and has no further role."},
   {t:"card", i:"📘", title:"Articles of association", body:"Filed **unless** the company adopts the **Model Articles unchanged**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎓", title:"Certificate of incorporation (ss.15-16)", body:"**Conclusive evidence** the company is validly formed (s.15(4)). It exists **from the date of incorporation stated on the certificate** (s.16). Subscribers become first members; named directors become first directors."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"When does the company legally exist?", body:"From the **date of incorporation stated on the certificate** (s.16)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Articles of association and the Model Articles", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"📘", title:"Articles = internal rulebook", body:"They cover directors' powers, board meetings, issuing and transferring shares, dividends and general meetings."},
   {t:"card", i:"📄", title:"Model Articles", body:"Standard articles in the **Companies (Model Articles) Regulations 2008**, with separate versions for private and public companies."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔁", title:"The default rule (s.20)", body:"A company limited by shares that files **no articles**, or articles that don't exclude or modify them, gets the **Model Articles by default** (to the extent not excluded)."},
   {t:"card", i:"✂️", title:"Usually: Model Articles + tweaks", body:"Most companies adopt the Model Articles **with amendments**, e.g. **pre-emption rights** on share transfers (existing members' right of first refusal), a different **quorum** (the minimum number needed for a valid meeting), removing the chair's **casting vote** (the chair's extra vote to break a tie), or a clause giving a director **weighted votes** on a resolution to remove them (a '**Bushell v Faith**' clause)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"An old 1985 Act company: do the Model Articles replace its articles?", body:"No. Older companies **keep their articles** (often **Table A**). The Model Articles don't automatically replace them."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧩", title:"The constitution (s.17)", body:"The **articles** plus any **resolutions and agreements** affecting the constitution (e.g. special resolutions, which must be filed under **s.30**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🤝", title:"Articles as a contract (s.33)", body:"The constitution binds the company and its members **as if** each had signed a contract to observe it."},
   {t:"compare", i:"⚖️", title:"Who can enforce the articles under s.33?", cols:["", "Can enforce?"], rows:[["🧑 Member, for rights **as a member** (vote, declared dividend, transfer considered)","✅"],["🧑‍💼 Member, for rights in **another capacity**","❌"],["🚪 Outsiders (directors as directors, creditors)","❌"]]},
   {t:"case", i:"⚖️", title:"Eley v Positive Government Security Life Assurance (1876)", facts:"The articles said Mr Eley would be the company's solicitor for life. The company stopped using him.", rule:"He **couldn't enforce** it: the right was given to him **as solicitor**, not as a member."},
   {t:"card", i:"📃", title:"Protect directors separately", body:"A director's rights should be in a separate **service contract**, not just the articles."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Can a CA 2006 company do anything lawful?", body:"Yes. It has **unrestricted objects** unless its articles specifically restrict them (**s.31**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗂️", title:"Old objects clauses", body:"Older companies had an **objects clause** in the memorandum. Under **s.28** it's now treated as part of the **articles**, and can be removed by special resolution."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛡️", title:"Restricted objects: act still valid", body:"An act's **validity can't be challenged** for lack of capacity (**s.39**). But directors who exceed the objects breach their duty to act within powers (**s.171**)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Company names", cards:[
   {t:"compare", i:"🏷️", title:"Required name endings", cols:["", "Must end with"], rows:[["🏢 Private company (s.59)","Limited / Ltd"],["🏦 Public company (s.58)","public limited company / plc"],["🤝 LLP","limited liability partnership / LLP"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"🏴󠁧󠁢󠁷󠁬󠁳󠁿", title:"Welsh and exemptions", body:"**Welsh equivalents** are allowed. Certain charities and similar companies can be exempt from 'Limited'."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚫", title:"Banned: same as an existing name (s.66)", body:"Minor differences such as punctuation, 'the' or 'company' are **ignored** when comparing."},
   {t:"card", i:"⛔", title:"Banned: offensive or criminal (s.53)", body:"A name can't be one whose use would be a **criminal offence** or that is **offensive** (s.53). Also banned since **ECCTA**: names suggesting a link with a **foreign government**, names intended to **facilitate fraud** (dishonesty or deception), and names made of computer code."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"'The Acme Ltd' when 'Acme Ltd' exists?", body:"Not allowed. Adding 'the' is a minor difference that's **ignored**, so it's the **same** name (s.66)."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚠️", title:"Sensitive words (s.55)", body:"Need **Secretary of State approval**, sometimes after consulting a specified body. E.g. words suggesting **national or international importance** ('British', 'International'), a **regulated activity** ('Bank', 'Insurance', 'Charity', 'University'), or a **link with government or the Crown**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏳", title:"Too like an existing name (s.67)", body:"The Secretary of State can direct a change within **12 months** of registration."},
   {t:"card", i:"👩‍⚖️", title:"Company Names Adjudicator (s.69)", body:"Anyone with **goodwill** in a name can object to a name the same as or so similar it's **likely to mislead**, e.g. registered **opportunistically** to extract money."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛡️", title:"Defence and outcome", body:"The company can defend itself, e.g. it registered **in good faith** or operates under the name. The adjudicator can **order a change**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎭", title:"Passing off", body:"A **claim in tort** if a name misrepresents a connection with another business that has goodwill."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"How does a company change its name (s.77)?", body:"**Special resolution** (or another method the articles allow), then file it with form **NM01** and the fee."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎓", title:"When the new name takes effect (s.81)", body:"From the date of the **new certificate of incorporation on change of name**. It's the **same legal person**: contracts and legal proceedings carry on unaffected."},
   {t:"card", i:"🪧", title:"Trading disclosures: the name", body:"Under the 2015 Names and Trading Disclosures Regulations, show the name at the **registered office**, any **place of business**, on the **website**, **letters, order forms, invoices** and emails."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔢", title:"Trading disclosures: extra details", body:"Letters, forms and the website must also show the **registered number**, the **part of the UK** where registered, and the **registered office address**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎯", title:"Exam tip: names", body:"Expect **procedure**: **special resolution + NM01**, effective on the **new certificate**, and the company stays the **same legal person** (existing contracts stay in force)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Changing the articles", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🗳️", title:"Special resolution (s.21)", body:"Change the articles by **special resolution**: at least **75%** of the votes. A private company can use a **written resolution**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📮", title:"Filing: 15 days x2", body:"File the **special resolution** within **15 days** (s.30), and the **amended articles** within **15 days** of the change taking effect (s.26)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔒", title:"Entrenched provisions (s.22)", body:"Articles can require conditions **stricter** than a special resolution to change a provision, e.g. unanimity or a named shareholder's consent."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"When can entrenchment be set up?", body:"Only **on formation** or by agreement of **all members**. Companies House must be notified (s.23)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔓", title:"Entrenchment isn't absolute", body:"It doesn't stop amendment by agreement of **all members** or by **court order**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛑", title:"Limit: no forced extra liability (s.25)", body:"A member isn't bound by a change requiring them to take **more shares** or increasing their liability, unless they **agree in writing**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎟️", title:"Limit: class rights (s.630)", body:"Changing rights attached to a **class of shares** needs **class consent** (see **Equity finance**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"💚", title:"Limit: bona fide for the company", body:"The change must be made **bona fide** (in good faith) **for the benefit of the company as a whole**."},
   {t:"case", i:"⚖️", title:"Allen v Gold Reefs of West Africa (1900)", facts:"Articles gave a **lien** (right to keep and sell shares to recover a debt) over partly paid shares. They were changed to cover fully paid shares, affecting only one member (who owed money and had died).", rule:"**Valid**: made in good faith for the company's benefit, even though only one member was affected."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Can a company contract out of its power to change its articles?", body:"No. But a **shareholders' agreement** promise to vote against a change is enforceable **between shareholders**."},
   {t:"case", i:"⚖️", title:"Russell v Northern Bank (1992)", facts:"A shareholders' agreement contained a promise to vote against a change to the articles.", rule:"The promise binds **the shareholders**, but the **company** can't contract out of its statutory power to change its articles (see **Shareholders' rights**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🌳", title:"Example: Oak Ltd", body:"Shareholders hold 40%, 35% and 25%. The 40% and 35% vote yes: **75% of votes cast, so it passes**. The 25% holder can't block."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"What if that holder had 26%?", body:"It could **block**: **more than 25%** blocks a special resolution."}
 ]},
 {h:"Steps after incorporation (company)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🪑", title:"First board meeting", body:"Often minuted on paper the same day. First jobs: appoint a **chair** (and a secretary if wanted), open a **bank account** and authorise signatories."},
   {t:"card", i:"📜", title:"Shares and members", body:"Allot any further shares, issue **share certificates** within **2 months** of allotment (s.769), and enter members in the **register of members**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📅", title:"Accounting reference date (ARD)", body:"The financial year end. By default: the **last day of the month** of the **anniversary of incorporation**. Change it with form **AA01**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"When are a private company's first accounts due?", body:"**21 months** after incorporation."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔍", title:"Auditors and service contracts", body:"Appoint **auditors** unless exempt as a small company (see **Distributions, dividends and accounting**). Approve directors' **service contracts**; long ones need member approval (**s.188**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"☂️", title:"Admin bits", body:"Arrange **insurance** and register for tax."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🧮", title:"Tax registration deadlines", cols:["", "When"], rows:[["🏢 Corporation tax: tell HMRC","Within **3 months** of starting to trade"],["💼 PAYE (deducting employees' income tax and NICs)","Before the **first payday**"],["🧾 VAT (value added tax)","If turnover threshold met (see **VAT**)"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"📚", title:"Registers the company still keeps", body:"At its registered office (or notified inspection place): **register of members**, **minutes** of board and general meetings, **written resolutions**, directors' **service contracts** and **indemnities**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔗", title:"Charges: copies, not a register", body:"Keep **copies of every document creating a charge** over its assets (**s.859P**). There's been **no statutory register of charges** since April 2013."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"How often is a confirmation statement filed?", body:"At least once every **12 months**, within **14 days** of the end of the review period."},
   {t:"card", i:"✔️", title:"What it confirms", body:"That Companies House's information is **up to date**, and that intended future activities are **lawful**."}
 ]},
 {h:"ECCTA 2023: Companies House reforms (law at November 2026)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🗓️", title:"Three main dates so far", body:"In force: **4 March 2024**, **18 November 2025** and **26 January 2026**. Some parts are **not yet in force** at November 2026."},
   {t:"card", i:"📍", title:"Mar 2024: appropriate registered office", body:"Documents must reach someone acting for the company, and delivery must be recordable by acknowledgment. A **PO box is not appropriate**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📧", title:"Mar 2024: email and lawful purpose", body:"A **registered email address** (not public). A **statement of lawful purpose** on incorporation, plus confirmation of lawful future activities in each **confirmation statement**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🦸", title:"Mar 2024: stronger Registrar", body:"The **Registrar** (head of Companies House) can query, reject, annotate and remove information."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Can a PO box be a registered office?", body:"No. Since **4 March 2024** it's **not an appropriate address**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🪪", title:"Nov 2025: identity verification (IDV)", body:"Compulsory for **every new director** and **every PSC** (and LLP members). Done via Companies House (GOV.UK One Login) or an **ACSP**. The person gets a **personal code**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏢", title:"What's an ACSP?", body:"An **Authorised Corporate Service Provider**: an AML-regulated agent (supervised under the anti-money laundering rules) (e.g. a law or accountancy firm) registered with Companies House."},
   {t:"card", i:"📝", title:"IDV on the forms", body:"An incorporation application or a notice of appointing a director (**AP01**) must confirm the director's identity is verified."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏱️", title:"Existing directors and PSCs", body:"Existing directors and PSCs had a **12-month transition** that ended on **17 November 2026**. Directors verified with the company's **first confirmation statement** after 18 Nov 2025, and PSCs within a set **14-day window**. A company can't file its confirmation statement unless all its directors are verified, and acting without verification is an **offence**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Director acts without verified ID: are their acts void?", body:"No, acts stay **valid**. But it's an **offence** by the director **and** the company."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗑️", title:"Nov 2025: company registers abolished", body:"No more company-held registers of **directors**, **directors' residential addresses**, **secretaries** and **PSCs**. That info lives only on the **Companies House register**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📒", title:"Jan 2026: own register of members", body:"Private companies can **no longer** keep their register of members at Companies House. Every company must keep its **own register of members**."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🚧", title:"Not yet in force (at November 2026)", cols:["", "When"], rows:[["🪪 IDV for filers + third parties file **only via ACSPs**","Not before Nov 2027"],["💻 Software-only accounts filing (abridged accounts stay)","From April 2028"],["🏢 Restrictions on corporate directors, and IDV for corporate PSCs","No timetable yet"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"🧑", title:"Corporate directors for now", body:"Still allowed, as long as **at least one director is a natural person** (**s.155**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚨", title:"Watch out!", body:"Since 18 Nov 2025 a company **doesn't keep** its own register of directors or PSCs, but **must still keep its register of members**. Director appointments/resignations still go to Companies House (**AP01, TM01**) within **14 days**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Pre-incorporation contracts (s.51)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🏗️", title:"Who's a promoter?", body:"A **promoter** takes steps to set up a company: finding premises, raising money, negotiating contracts before it exists."},
   {t:"card", i:"🫵", title:"The s.51(1) rule", body:"A contract made by or for a company **before it's incorporated** is a contract with the **person who made it**. They're **personally liable** (and can enforce it), **subject to any agreement to the contrary**."},
   {t:"case", i:"⚖️", title:"Phonogram Ltd v Lane (1982)", facts:"Mr Lane signed 'for and on behalf of' Fragile Management Ltd, which was to manage a band but was never formed.", rule:"He was **personally liable** to repay the £6,000. 'For and on behalf of' isn't an agreement to the contrary: that needs **clear words** excluding personal liability."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Can the new company ratify the contract?", body:"No. It **didn't exist** when the contract was made, so it can't ratify (adopt) it."},
   {t:"case", i:"⚖️", title:"Kelner v Baxter (1866)", facts:"A contract was made on behalf of a company that didn't yet exist.", rule:"Once formed, the company **cannot ratify** a pre-incorporation contract."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏸️", title:"Avoid liability: wait", body:"**Don't contract until the company exists**, or buy a **shelf company**. Or keep the agreement **in draft only** and sign after incorporation."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔀", title:"Avoid liability: novation", body:"After incorporation, a **novation**: a new contract between the company and the other party, same terms, releasing the promoter. The other party **must agree**."},
   {t:"card", i:"✍️", title:"Avoid liability: express terms", body:"A term **excluding** personal liability (other party unlikely to agree), or one ending the promoter's liability when the company enters an **identical contract**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Jo signs a shop lease 'for and on behalf of Jo's Deli Ltd' Monday; company formed Friday. Who's liable?", body:"**Jo, personally** (s.51). The company can't ratify. Jo should ask the landlord for a **new lease** to the company, or to **novate** it."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎯", title:"Exam tip: s.51 traps", body:"Wrong options say the company becomes liable 'on incorporation' or 'by ratifying'. Both wrong: the **signer** is liable, and only a **novation** shifts liability."}
 ]},
 {h:"Public limited companies", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🏦", title:"Plc from the start", body:"Same **IN01** process, stating it's public. Needs at least **2 directors** (s.154) and a **qualified company secretary** (ss.271, 273)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎓", title:"Qualified secretary means...", body:"E.g. a solicitor, barrister, chartered accountant or chartered secretary, or someone with relevant experience."},
   {t:"card", i:"💷", title:"Authorised minimum (s.763, s.586)", body:"Allotted share capital of at least **£50,000**, each share paid up to at least **one quarter** of nominal value plus the **whole of any premium** (any amount paid above nominal value)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"What must a new plc get before doing business or borrowing?", body:"A **trading certificate** (s.761), applied for on form **SH50** with a statement that the share capital requirement is met."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚠️", title:"Trading without one (s.767)", body:"An **offence** by the company and officers. If the company doesn't meet its obligations within **21 days** of being called on, **directors** are **jointly and severally liable** to indemnify the other party."},
   {t:"card", i:"✅", title:"But the deal stands", body:"A transaction made without a trading certificate is **still valid**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔄", title:"Re-registering private to plc (ss.90-96)", body:"**Special resolution** to re-register, and to change the **name** (to 'plc') and **articles**."},
   {t:"card", i:"💷", title:"Re-registration: share capital", body:"Allotted shares of at least **£50,000** nominal, each paid up to **one quarter** plus the **whole premium**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📊", title:"Re-registration: net assets", body:"A **balance sheet** from the previous **7 months**, an **unqualified auditor's report** on it, and an **auditor's statement** that net assets are **not less than called-up share capital plus undistributable reserves** (reserves that can't be paid out as dividends, e.g. share premium)."},
   {t:"card", i:"👥", title:"Re-registration: people", body:"At least **2 directors** and a **qualified secretary**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📨", title:"Re-registration: filing", body:"Form **RR01** with the resolution, amended articles, balance sheet and auditor's documents, and a statement of compliance. Companies House issues a **certificate of incorporation on re-registration**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Does a re-registered plc need a trading certificate?", body:"**No.** Only plcs formed as plcs from the start need one."}
 ]},
 {h:"Forming a general partnership", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🚫", title:"No paperwork required", body:"**No Companies House registration** and **no formal documents** legally required. It exists once the **s.1 test** is met (see **Business structures**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📃", title:"But get a partnership agreement", body:"A written agreement (or deed) is strongly advised to replace **PA 1890 defaults**, e.g. equal profit shares, dissolution by any partner's notice. Usual terms: see **Partnerships and LLPs**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧾", title:"HMRC", body:"Each partner registers for **self-assessment**; the firm registers to file a **partnership tax return** (one partner nominated). **PAYE** if it employs staff, **VAT** if over the threshold."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"When do the business names rules (CA 2006 Part 41) kick in?", body:"When the firm trades under a name that's **not just the surnames** of all the partners, e.g. 'Green Leaf Gardening'."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏷️", title:"Business names: name rules", body:"No name suggesting it's a company or LLP (e.g. ending 'Ltd'), and get approval for **sensitive words**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📬", title:"Business names: disclosures (ss.1202, 1204)", body:"Show **partners' names** and a **UK address for service** on letters, orders, invoices, receipts and payment demands; display them at premises; give them on request."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔧", title:"Example: Fresh Fix Plumbing", body:"Ravi and Sam must show **both names and a UK service address** on invoices and at their premises. **No Companies House filing** needed."}
 ]},
 {h:"Forming an LLP (Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"📝", title:"Form LL IN01", body:"The **incorporation document** (LLPA 2000 s.2), filed at Companies House with the fee."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏷️", title:"Name, office, email", body:"Name ending **'LLP'** or **'limited liability partnership'**; **registered office** (location and address); a **registered email address**."},
   {t:"card", i:"👥", title:"Members", body:"Name and address of each **member** (at least **2** subscribers), and which are **designated members** (at least **2**, or a statement that all are)."},
   {t:"card", i:"✅", title:"Statements", body:"A **statement of initial significant control** (PSCs), a **statement of compliance** and a **statement of lawful purpose**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Who needs verified identity in an LLP?", body:"**Members** and **PSCs**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎓", title:"Certificate of incorporation", body:"The LLP is a **body corporate** from that date."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔐", title:"Members' agreement", body:"**Not filed**, stays private. It should replace the defaults in the **LLP Regulations 2001** (e.g. equal profit shares, no expulsion; see **Partnerships and LLPs**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧾", title:"Tax", body:"Each member registers for **self-assessment**; the LLP files a **partnership return**. It is **tax transparent**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Summary table: what each structure must do to start", cards:[
   {t:"compare", i:"📋", title:"Registration", cols:["", "🤝 Partnership", "🧩 LLP", "🏢 Private co", "🏦 Plc"], rows:[["Register?","❌ None","LL IN01","IN01","IN01"]]},
   {t:"compare", i:"📘", title:"Constitutional document", cols:["", "Document"], rows:[["🤝 Partnership","Partnership agreement (optional, private)"],["🧩 LLP","Members' agreement (optional, private)"],["🏢 Private co","Memorandum + articles (Model Articles by default)"],["🏦 Plc","Memorandum + articles (public Model Articles by default)"]]},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Minimum people for a private company?", body:"**1 shareholder** and **1 director** (at least one director must be a natural person, s.155)."},
   {t:"compare", i:"👥", title:"Minimum people", cols:["", "Minimum"], rows:[["🤝 Partnership","2 partners"],["🧩 LLP","2 members, 2 designated members"],["🏢 Private co","1 shareholder, 1 director (at least one director must be a natural person, s.155)"],["🏦 Plc","2 directors, qualified secretary"]]},
   {t:"compare", i:"🪪", title:"Identity verification", cols:["", "Who"], rows:[["🤝 Partnership","❌ No"],["🧩 LLP","Members and PSCs"],["🏢 Private co","Directors and PSCs"],["🏦 Plc","Directors and PSCs"]]},
   {t:"compare", i:"🚦", title:"Before trading", cols:["", "Needs"], rows:[["🤝 Partnership","HMRC registration; business names disclosures"],["🧩 LLP","Certificate of incorporation"],["🏢 Private co","Certificate of incorporation"],["🏦 Plc","Certificate of incorporation **and trading certificate** (s.761)"]]},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Which structure needs an extra certificate before trading?", body:"The **plc** formed as a plc: a **trading certificate** (s.761)."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Source: src/content/accounts.js topic accountants-reports (Farm room 09, The Show Ring). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["accounts/accountants-reports"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A yearly independent check", body:"Once a year an independent **accountant** checks whether a firm that handles client money has complied with the Accounts Rules, and writes an **accountant's report**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What rule 12 covers", body:"Rule 12 says:\n- **who must obtain one**: any firm that held client money or operated a joint or client's own account during the year, unless exempt;\n- **when**: within **six months** of the end of the accounting period;\n- **when it must be sent to the SRA**: currently only if it is **qualified** in a way that shows client money was put **at risk**; and\n- **who can prepare it**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Changes from April 2027", body:"The SRA has approved changes from **April 2027**, but these were **not in force** at the exam cut-off."}
 ]},
 {h:"Key terms", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Accountant's report", body:"An independent accountant's report, **in the form the SRA prescribes**, on whether the firm has complied with the Accounts Rules during the accounting period."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Qualified or unqualified", body:"A **qualified** report: the accountant has found and recorded **problems** (failures to comply). An **unqualified** report is a **\"clean\"** one."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Accounting period", body:"Normally **12 months**, running from the end of the previous period (usually in line with the firm's financial year). It can be **shorter or longer** in particular cases, such as a firm's **first report** or a **change of year end**."}
 ]},
 {h:"The duty: rule 12.1", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Who must obtain a report", body:"The duty applies if you have, **at any time during an accounting period**:\n- **held or received client money**; or\n- **operated a joint account or a client's own account as signatory**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(a) Obtain it within six months", body:"**(a)** You must **obtain** an accountant's report for that accounting period **within six months of the end of the period**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(b) Send it only if money is at risk", body:"**(b)** You must **deliver it to the SRA** within six months of the end of the period **if** the report is **qualified** to show a failure to comply with the rules **such that money belonging to clients or third parties is, or has been, or is likely to be placed, at risk**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Keep or send: the table", body:"Under the current rules:\n- **Unqualified** (clean): obtain within 6 months **yes**; send to SRA **no**: keep it and produce it if asked.\n- **Qualified, minor breaches, no risk** to client money: obtain **yes**; send **no**.\n- **Qualified, client or third-party money is, has been or is likely to be at risk**: obtain **yes**; send **yes**, within 6 months of the period end."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: year end 31 March 2026", body:"A firm's accounting period ends on **31 March 2026**. It must **obtain its report by 30 September 2026**. If the accountant finds that a client's money was used to cover another client's payment, the report is **qualified showing money at risk** and must be **delivered to the SRA by 30 September 2026**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Exemptions: rule 12.2", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(a) All Legal Aid Agency money", body:"You do **not** need an accountant's report if **all** the client money held or received during the accounting period is money received from the **Legal Aid Agency (LAA)**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Watch out: all, not most", body:"The legal aid exemption needs **all** client money in the period to be LAA money. A **single private client's** money on account takes the firm outside it (though it may still fall within the low-balance exemption)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(b) The low-balance limits", body:"You do **not** need a report if, in the accounting period, the **statement or passbook balance** (a passbook is the little book some building society accounts use to record the balance) of client money held or received does **not exceed**:\n- **(i)** an **average of £10,000**; **and**\n- **(ii)** a **maximum of £250,000** (or the equivalent in foreign currency).\n\n**Both** limbs must be satisfied."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"r.12.3: which balance counts", body:"The \"statement or passbook balance\" is the total balance, **as shown by the bank statements** obtained under r.8.2 (**not** the firm's own books), of:\n- **all client accounts** held or operated by you; and\n- **any joint accounts and clients' own accounts** operated by you."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"r.12.4: the SRA can still ask", body:"The SRA can still **require** a firm to obtain or deliver a report on reasonable notice if the firm **stops operating** as an authorised body (a firm authorised by the SRA) and holding or operating a client account, or if the SRA considers it is **in the public interest**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exempt or not: five firms", body:"- Average £8,000; highest £300,000: **not exempt** (maximum exceeds £250,000).\n- Average £12,000; highest £100,000: **not exempt** (average exceeds £10,000).\n- Average £6,000; highest £90,000: **exempt** (both limits met).\n- All client money from the LAA, average £40,000: **exempt** (legal aid exemption: balances irrelevant).\n- Mostly LAA money plus £500 on account from one private client: legal aid exemption **not** available (not **all** client money is LAA money); check the balance limits instead."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: the word \"and\"", body:"Questions often give two figures and test the word **\"and\"**: a firm is exempt under r.12.2(b) only if the average is **£10,000 or less AND** the maximum is **£250,000 or less**. A **single large completion** can push a small firm over the £250,000 maximum. Also remember to **add in joint accounts and clients' own accounts** the firm operates when working out the balances."}
 ]},
 {h:"The accountant: rules 12.5 to 12.9", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"r.12.5: who can prepare it", body:"The report must be prepared and signed by an accountant who is a **member of one of the chartered accountancy bodies** and who **is, or works for, a registered auditor** (a person or firm registered to carry out company audits)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"r.12.6: disqualifying an accountant", body:"The SRA may **disqualify** an accountant from preparing reports if they have been found guilty of **professional misconduct** by their professional body, or the SRA is satisfied they **failed to exercise due care and skill** in preparing a report."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"r.12.7: terms of engagement", body:"The SRA may specify matters that must be included in the **terms of the accountant's engagement**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"r.12.8: give the accountant everything", body:"The firm must give the accountant **details of all accounts** held or operated in connection with the practice at any bank, building society or other financial institution during the period, and **all other information and documentation** the accountant needs."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"r.12.9: the prescribed form", body:"The accountant must complete and sign the report in the **prescribed form**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Changes from 2027 (decided, but not in force at 11 September 2026)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What the SRA decided", body:"After its 2025-26 consultations on protecting client money (prompted by the collapse of the firm **Axiom Ince**, where large sums of client money went missing), the SRA **decided** in 2026 that:\n- **every firm that holds client money and is not exempt** must **submit its annual accountant's report to the SRA**, whether qualified or unqualified;\n- firms must also make an **annual declaration** (including confirming any exemption they rely on); and\n- **fixed financial penalties** can be imposed for late submission or non-submission."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Approved, starting 1 April 2027", body:"The **Legal Services Board (LSB)** (the body that oversees the legal regulators) **approved** the rule changes on **31 July 2026**. The SRA has said submission of all reports starts from **1 April 2027**, and asked firms to confirm their accounting period dates in early 2027."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Separating roles: paused", body:"In the same decision the LSB approved the SRA's rules **separating management and compliance roles** in all but the smallest firms (so a manager who can make decisions alone cannot also be the **COLP** (Compliance Officer for Legal Practice) or **COFA** (Compliance Officer for Finance and Administration)). But in **October 2026** the SRA **paused** bringing these in, so there is **no confirmed start date**. See **Ethics**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Notification: only a consultation", body:"The SRA has separately **consulted** (June to August 2026) on new **notification** duties, such as telling the SRA within **28 days** of **starting to hold client money**. **No decision** had been made."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"For the exam: today's rule", body:"These changes were **not in force** at the **11 September 2026** cut-off. Answer on the **current rule 12.1(b)**: only a report **qualified to show money at risk** must be delivered to the SRA."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: \"send its unqualified report\"", body:"If an answer option says a firm must \"send its unqualified accountant's report to the SRA\", it describes the **future** (April 2027) position, not the examinable rule. Under the rules examined, an unqualified report is **obtained and kept**, not delivered."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Farm room 05 (The Orchard Packing Shed). Source: src/content/accounts.js, topic bills-vat-disbursements (notes only). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["accounts/bills-vat-disbursements"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Bills: VAT and the client's ledger", body:"When a firm sends a **bill**, it charges **VAT** on its fees and records the bill on the **client's ledger**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Disbursements", body:"Many matters also involve **disbursements**: payments to third parties for the client, such as **search fees**, **counsel's fees** (a barrister's fees) or **Land Registry fees** (fees to HM Land Registry, which registers land)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The key question: who received the supply?", body:"For each disbursement ask **who received the supply** (the goods or services): the **client** or the **firm**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What the answer decides", body:"It decides whether the **agency method** or the **principal method** applies, which in turn decides **which bank account** can pay it, whether the firm **reclaims VAT**, and whether **VAT goes on the firm's bill**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Also in this topic", body:"Reducing bills (**abatements**), writing off unpaid bills (**bad debts**) and the record of bills."}
 ]},
 {h:"VAT basics for law firms", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"VAT at 20%", body:"**VAT** (value added tax) is charged by VAT-registered businesses on their supplies. The standard rate is **20%** (the exam will give the rate)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Output tax", body:"A VAT-registered law firm charges VAT on its **profit costs** (its fees). This is **output tax**, collected for and owed to **HMRC** (HM Revenue & Customs)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Input tax", body:"VAT the firm pays on its own purchases is **input tax**. The firm can **reclaim** it from HMRC."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"When to record output VAT", body:"In the exam, record output VAT when the **bill is delivered**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No VAT at all", body:"Some payments carry **no VAT at all**, because they are not a VAT supply, e.g. **Stamp Duty Land Tax (SDLT)**, **Land Registry fees**, **court fees** and **probate application fees** (fees to apply for the court's grant that lets the people dealing with an estate act)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Entries when a bill is delivered", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Bill: £2,000 + £400 VAT", body:"**DR client ledger, business column £2,400** (the client owes the firm); **CR profit costs account £2,000** (the firm's income); **CR HMRC (VAT) account £400** (owed to HMRC)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No money moves", body:"No money moves when a bill is delivered, so there is **no cash entry**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Payment comes later", body:"Either from the client (**DR business cash; CR ledger business column**) or by transfer from money held in client account, **after the bill** (r.4.3: the firm may take its costs from client money only after giving a bill or other written notification of costs)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Central record of bills", body:"Keep a **central record** of all bills and other written notifications of costs (**r.8.4**)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Disbursements: agency method v principal method", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Agency: who received the supply?", body:"The **client**. The firm pays as the client's **agent** (go-between)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Agency: how to spot it", body:"Invoice **addressed to the client**; or **no VAT supply at all** (SDLT, Land Registry, court and probate fees)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Agency: which account can pay?", body:"**Client account** if enough is held for that client; otherwise business account."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Agency: VAT and entries", body:"The firm does **not** reclaim the input VAT. From client account: **DR ledger client column** (**gross**, i.e. including VAT); **CR client cash**. From business account: **DR ledger business column** (gross); **CR business cash**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Agency: on the firm's bill", body:"Listed as a **disbursement**; **no VAT added** by the firm."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Principal: who received the supply?", body:"The **firm** itself. It then **re-supplies** to the client as part of its own service."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Principal: how to spot it", body:"Invoice **addressed to the firm** (e.g. counsel's or an expert's **fee note**, their bill, to the firm); **property search fees**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Principal: business account only", body:"**Business account only**, even if the firm holds client money for the client. Payment from client account is **not possible**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Principal: VAT and entries", body:"The firm **reclaims** the input VAT from HMRC. Entries on payment from business account: **DR ledger business column** (**net**, i.e. before VAT); **DR HMRC** (the VAT); **CR business cash** (**gross**, i.e. including VAT)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Principal: on the firm's bill", body:"Treated as part of the **firm's own supply**: the firm charges **output VAT** on it with its fees."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Why business account only", body:"The invoice is the **firm's own debt**, and the firm is recovering the input VAT for itself. Using client money to pay the firm's own debt, **before any bill**, would breach the rules."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Search fees: principal method", body:"HMRC's view (**Revenue and Customs Brief 6 (2020)**): **property search fees** (e.g. local authority searches) that a solicitor obtains and uses in advising the client are **not** disbursements for VAT purposes. They are part of the solicitor's own supply, so **VAT must be charged** on them: treat them as **principal method**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: the surveyor's invoice", body:"The firm holds £1,500 on account for a buyer client. A surveyor's invoice for **£400 + £80 VAT is addressed to the firm**. **The firm must pay £480 from its business account, using the principal method.** Entries: DR client ledger business column £400; DR HMRC £80; CR business cash £480."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exam tip: only 41% got it right", body:"In the SRA's published sample question on exactly this point, only **41%** of candidates chose the right answer: **pay the gross amount from business account using the principal method**. The trap is thinking that because client money is held, client account should be used. **If the invoice is addressed to the firm, client account is never right.**"}
 ]},
 {h:"Worked example: principal method from payment to bill", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Paying counsel's fee", body:"Counsel's fee **£800 + £160 VAT**, fee note addressed to the firm, paid from business account: **DR ledger business column £800; DR HMRC £160; CR business cash £960.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The bill: VAT on £2,300", body:"Later the firm delivers its bill: profit costs **£1,500** plus counsel's fee **£800**. VAT at 20% is charged on **£2,300** = **£460**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Entries for the bill", body:"**DR ledger business column £1,960** (£1,500 + £460); **CR profit costs £1,500; CR HMRC £460.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Counsel's £800 is not entered again", body:"Counsel's £800 is **not** credited to profit costs and is **not entered again**: it was already debited to the ledger when paid."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What the client must pay: £2,760", body:"Ledger business balance: £800 + £1,960 = **£2,760 DR**, which is what the client must pay: £1,500 fees + £800 counsel + £460 VAT."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Watch out: no double VAT", body:"Under the principal method, do **not** add the original £160 VAT to the bill **and** charge VAT on the fee again. The firm reclaims the £160 input tax, then charges output VAT on the **net** £800 as part of its bill."}
 ]},
 {h:"Worked example: agency method", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The estate agent's invoice", body:"An estate agent's invoice for **£3,000 + £600 VAT** is **addressed to the seller client**. The firm holds the client's sale proceeds and has the client's authority to pay. Pay from client account: **DR ledger client column £3,600; CR client cash £3,600.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No reclaim, no VAT on the bill", body:"The firm does **not** reclaim the £600 (it is the **client's** VAT) and adds **no VAT** on its own bill for this item."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A Land Registry fee", body:"A **Land Registry fee** of £150 (no VAT) paid from client account: **DR ledger client column £150; CR client cash £150.**"}
 ]},
 {h:"Paying bills", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The client sends money", body:"Client pays by sending money after the bill: **DR business cash; CR ledger business column.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"From money held in client account", body:"Transfer **after the bill or written notification** (r.4.3). Client side: **DR ledger client column; CR client cash**. Business side: **DR business cash; CR ledger business column.**"}
 ]},
 {h:"Abatements (reducing a bill)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What an abatement is", body:"A reduction the firm agrees to make to a bill it has **already delivered** (e.g. after the client challenges the amount)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Abatement entries", body:"Reverse the bill entries for the amount of the reduction and issue a **VAT credit note** (a document that reduces the VAT charged on the bill): **DR profit costs** (reduction); **DR HMRC** (VAT on the reduction); **CR ledger business column** (total reduction)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: the client now owes £960", body:"A bill of £1,000 + £200 VAT is reduced by £200 + £40 VAT. **DR profit costs £200; DR HMRC £40; CR ledger business column £240.** The client now owes **£960**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Bad debts", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What a bad debt is", body:"A bill the firm accepts will **never be paid** (e.g. the client is insolvent: unable to pay its debts)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Writing it off", body:"**CR client ledger business column** with the total, clearing what the client owed, and **DR bad debts account**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"VAT bad debt relief: 6 months", body:"The firm has already accounted to HMRC for the output VAT on the bill. It can recover it through **VAT bad debt relief** once the debt is at least **6 months overdue** and **written off** in its accounts. Where relief is claimed, the debit is split: **DR bad debts** (the fees) and **DR HMRC** (the VAT)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: £1,000 + £200 written off", body:"Bad debt relief is claimed. **DR bad debts £1,000; DR HMRC £200; CR ledger business column £1,200.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exam tip: abatement v bad debt", body:"Both **credit the client ledger business column** (the client owes less). The difference is the debit: an abatement debits **profit costs** (the fee is reduced); a bad debt debits a **bad debts** expense account (the fee was earned but will not be paid)."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Farm room 02 (The Feed Store and Grain Silo). Source: src/content/accounts.js, topic client-account-withdrawals (notes only). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["accounts/client-account-withdrawals"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Once money is in client account", body:"Once client money is in a **client account**, the rules control **what that account can be used for** and **how money can leave it**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A proper, clearly named account", body:"The account must be a **proper, clearly named bank account**, and it must only be used for money linked to the firm's legal work, **never as a banking facility**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Taking money out", body:"Money may only be **withdrawn** for a **proper reason**, with **proper authorisation**, and only if **that particular client** has enough money held."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What this topic covers", body:"Rules 3 and 5 and the accounting entries for withdrawals. Paying the firm's own costs out of client money (**r.4.3**) is covered in more detail in **Keeping client and firm money separate**."}
 ]},
 {h:"What a client account is: rule 3.1 and 3.2", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Where: a bank in England and Wales (r.3.1)", body:"Client money may only be kept in a client account at a **branch (or the head office) of a bank or building society in England and Wales**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The name: firm's name + \"client\" (r.3.2)", body:"The name of the account must include **(a)** the **name of the authorised body** (the SRA-authorised firm); and **(b)** the word **\"client\"**, to distinguish it from the firm's own accounts."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"General client account", body:"Holds money for **many clients, pooled together**. A separate **client ledger** (individual account record) for each client shows how much of the pool belongs to them. Interest is paid by the bank to the **firm**, which then pays clients a **fair sum** (r.7). Used for **everyday** receipts and payments."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Separate designated client account", body:"Holds money for **one** client or matter. The account itself relates to one client, plus that client's ledger. Interest is credited **directly** to the account for that client. Used for **large sums held for a long time**, e.g. estate money awaiting distribution."},
   {t:"compare", i:"", title:"The two types of client account", cols:["","General client account","Separate designated client account"], rows:[
     ["Holds money for","Many clients, pooled together","One client or matter"],
     ["Records","A separate client ledger for each client shows how much of the pool belongs to them","The account itself relates to one client, plus that client's ledger"],
     ["Interest","Paid by the bank to the firm, which then pays clients a fair sum (r.7)","Credited directly to the account for that client"],
     ["Typical use","Everyday receipts and payments","Large sums held for a long time, e.g. estate money awaiting distribution"]
   ]},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Never overdrawn", body:"A client account can **never be overdrawn**: there must always be at least as much in the bank as the firm holds for all its clients."}
 ]},
 {h:"No banking facilities: rule 3.3", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No banking facilities (r.3.3)", body:"You must **not use a client account to provide banking facilities** to clients or third parties."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Every movement linked to regulated services", body:"**Payments into, and transfers or withdrawals from, a client account must be in respect of the delivery by you of regulated services.** Every movement of money through client account must be linked to an underlying legal service the firm is actually providing."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Why it matters: money laundering", body:"A solicitor's client account looks trustworthy, so criminals may try to use it to **launder money** (make criminal money look legitimate). The rule is a key safeguard alongside the **Proceeds of Crime Act 2002** and the **Money Laundering Regulations 2017**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The test is not suspicion", body:"The test is **not** whether anyone is suspicious. Even a **genuine client with clean money** cannot use client account for payments unconnected with the legal work."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Red flag: receive it and pay it on", body:"A client asks the firm to **receive money and pay it on** to someone else where there is **no legal transaction**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Red flag: household bills, suppliers or salaries", body:"A client asks the firm to pay their **household bills, suppliers or salaries** through client account."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Red flag: \"for safekeeping\"", body:"A client wants to **leave money** with the firm after the matter has ended, \"for safekeeping\"."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Red flag: an unexpected payer", body:"Money arrives **from an unexpected third party**, or a transaction collapses and the client asks for the money to be sent to **someone other than the payer**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Fuglers LLP v SRA (2014): the facts", body:"High Court. The firm acted for **Portsmouth City Football Club**. After **HMRC** presented a winding-up petition (a court application to close a company down because it cannot pay its debts), the club's bank withdrew its facilities. For about **four months** the club routed money (from the club itself and the Football League) through the firm's client account, and the firm paid the club's ordinary bills and creditors (people the club owed money to) from it (e.g. laundry, coffee suppliers, repairs, running costs, and tax). There was **no underlying legal transaction** for those payments."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Fuglers: the outcome", body:"The Tribunal (the Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal) fined the firm **£50,000** and its two partners **£20,000** and **£5,000**, and the High Court **upheld** the finding that the firm had **improperly used client account as a banking facility**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Fuglers: the rule", body:"Payments through client account must be **part of, or necessary to, a genuine legal service**; acting as the **client's cashier** is a breach."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: Ben's £40,000", body:"Ben's conveyancing (the legal work of selling his property) completed last month. He asks the firm to hold his **£40,000** sale proceeds **\"for a few months\"** while he decides what to buy, and to pay his **credit card bill** from it."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"What should the firm do with Ben's money?", body:"**Refuse and return the money promptly** (r.2.5), because holding it and making the payment would be a **banking facility** (r.3.3)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: written instructions, or a client well known to the firm?", body:"Options such as \"the firm may hold the money because the client has given **written instructions**\" or \"because the client is **well known** to the firm\" are **both wrong**. Instructions or trust do not cure the problem; **only a link to a regulated service** the firm is delivering does."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Signs of money laundering: the MLRO", body:"If there are also signs of money laundering, the best answer is often to **refuse** and consider a report to the firm's **MLRO** (Money Laundering Reporting Officer)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Withdrawals from client account: rule 5.1", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Only three grounds", body:"Client money may only be withdrawn from a client account on one of **three** grounds: (a), (b) and (c)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(a) The purpose it is held for", body:"For the **purpose for which it is being held**, e.g. paying the completion money on a purchase, paying a legacy (a gift in a will) to a beneficiary, paying SDLT to HMRC."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(b) Instructions", body:"Following receipt of **instructions** from the client, or from the third party for whom the money is held."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(c) SRA authorisation or prescribed circumstances", body:"On the **SRA's prior written authorisation**, or in **prescribed circumstances** set by the SRA (e.g. residual balances of **£500 or less** paid to charity: see **The Accounts Rules and client money**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Paying the firm's own costs", body:"Paying the firm's own **costs** is a withdrawal for the purpose for which the money is held, but **r.4.3** adds that a **bill or other written notification of costs** must be given first."}
 ]},
 {h:"Authorisation and supervision: rule 5.2", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Authorised and supervised (r.5.2)", body:"All withdrawals must be **appropriately authorised and supervised**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Sensible controls", body:"The rules **do not say who must sign**. The firm must have sensible controls, e.g. only named managers or senior staff can approve payments, **two-person sign-off for large sums**, checks of bank details to prevent **payment fraud** (fraudsters sending false bank details by email)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Sufficient funds: rule 5.3", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Only if this client has enough (r.5.3)", body:"You may only withdraw client money **if sufficient funds are held on behalf of that specific client or third party** to make the payment."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Paying more uses other clients' money", body:"If a payment exceeds what is held for that client, the excess is being paid with **other clients' money**. The client's ledger then shows a **debit balance on the client column** (it is **overdrawn**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Overdrawn = breach", body:"This is a **breach**, however **short** the period and however **honest** the mistake."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Not enough held: two options", body:"The firm can pay the **whole** amount from the **business account** (the firm's own money, which the client then owes the firm); or pay the **available part** from client account and the **balance** from business account."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: Rosa's court fee", body:"The firm holds **£1,000** for Rosa. It must pay a **£1,500** court fee for her today."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"How can the firm pay Rosa's £1,500 court fee?", body:"**£1,000 from client account and £500 from business account, or £1,500 from business account.** It must **not** pay £1,500 from client account."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Uncleared cheques", body:"An **uncleared cheque** (paid in but not yet honoured by the paying bank) counts as money held, so a payment against it is **not automatically a breach**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"If the cheque bounces", body:"If the cheque is later **dishonoured** (bounces), the client's ledger is overdrawn and the firm has **breached** the rules and must **replace the shortfall immediately** from its own money (r.6.1)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The safe course: wait until cleared", body:"The safe course is to **wait until funds have cleared**, especially before completion of a property transaction (completion: the day the sale is finished and the money handed over)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Transfers between clients", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"An inter-client transfer is a withdrawal", body:"Moving money held for one client so that it is held for another (an **inter-client transfer**) is a withdrawal from the first client's money and needs a **proper ground under r.5.1**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Examples", body:"Using a client's sale proceeds for **their own related purchase** on a separate ledger; transferring a **stakeholder deposit** to the seller's ledger on completion."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A private loan from one client to another", body:"A **private loan** from one client to another is a withdrawal of the lender's money, so it needs the **lending client's instructions** (r.5.1(b))."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Get clear written instructions", body:"The 2019 rules do not prescribe a form, but firms should get clear **written** instructions (the old 2011 rules expressly required written authority from both clients). Using one client's money for another **without authority** is a **serious breach**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Entries: no cash moves", body:"**DR** (debit) the transferring client's ledger, client column; **CR** (credit) the receiving client's ledger, client column. **No cash moves**, because the money stays in client account."}
 ]},
 {h:"Accounting entries for withdrawals", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Paying from client account", body:"For a client (e.g. a court fee, completion money): **DR** (debit) client ledger, **client** column; **CR** (credit) client cash account. (See **Double-entry bookkeeping: the basics**.)"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Paying from business account", body:"For a client when not enough is held, or for a principal-method disbursement (one supplied to the firm itself, e.g. an invoice addressed to the firm, so it can only be paid from business account: see **Bills, VAT and disbursements**): **DR** client ledger, **business** column; **CR** business cash account."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A split payment", body:"Each part is entered in **its own column**, as above."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: Ravi's £300 court fee", body:"The firm holds £1,000 for Ravi and pays a £300 court fee for him from client account. **DR Ravi's ledger client column £300; CR client cash £300.** Ravi's client column balance falls from **£1,000 CR** to **£700 CR**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: what three checks do you run for any withdrawal?", body:"In order: **(1)** is there a proper **ground** (purpose, instructions, SRA authority)? **(2)** is it properly **authorised and supervised**? **(3)** does **this** client have **enough** money held?"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exam tip: the trap is check 3", body:"Questions often hide the problem in **check 3**: the client account as a whole has plenty of money, but **this client's ledger** does not."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Paying it back later does not cure it", body:"Paying the shortfall back later does **not** make the original withdrawal permitted. Using one client's money for another is **always a breach** that must be **corrected and recorded** (see **Interest and correcting breaches**)."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Farm room 10 (The Cottage by the Stream). Source: src/content/accounts.js, topic conveyancing-accounts (notes only). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["accounts/conveyancing-accounts"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exam tip: FLK2 Session 2, property", body:"From **January 2027** Solicitors Accounts appears in **FLK2 Session 2 in the context of property**. Expect conveyancing scenarios (deposits, mortgage advances, completion, SDLT) where the real question is an **Accounts Rules** point."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What conveyancing is", body:"**Conveyancing** is the legal work of transferring land."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exchange, then completion", body:"On **exchange of contracts** the deal becomes binding and the buyer usually pays a **deposit**. On **completion** the rest of the price is paid and ownership passes."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Large sums, moving fast", body:"Large sums move through client account in a short time, so the property context tests the core rules hard: is the money **client money**, and **for whom** is it held (the client, the lender, or both parties as stakeholder)?"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Cleared? Authority? Returned promptly?", body:"Are funds **cleared** (actually available in the account, so the payment cannot bounce) before they are paid out? Is there **authority** for every payment, and is the balance **returned promptly**? This topic applies the general rules to a sale and a purchase."}
 ]},
 {h:"Whose money is it?", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"All transaction money is client money", body:"All money received for the transaction is **client money**: the **deposit**, the **mortgage advance** (the loan from the buyer's lender), the **balance of the price**, **SDLT** (Stamp Duty Land Tax) money, **bridging loans**, **sale proceeds**, and money to **redeem** (pay off) the seller's mortgage."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Deposit received by the buyer's solicitor", body:"A deposit received by the **buyer's** solicitor from the buyer is held for the **buyer** (the client). It is paid on to the seller's solicitor on exchange."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Deposit held by the seller's solicitor as stakeholder", body:"A deposit held by the **seller's** solicitor **as stakeholder** is held for **both** buyer and seller until completion. It is **third-party money** (r.2.1(b): money held for someone other than the client, in connection with the legal services). It **cannot be released to the seller before completion**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Deposit held by the seller's solicitor as agent", body:"A deposit held by the seller's solicitor **as agent** is held for the **seller**. The seller can use it, e.g. for a related purchase."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Mortgage advance: the lender's", body:"The **mortgage advance** is held for the **lender** until completion (where the firm also acts for the lender). It must be used only as the lender's instructions allow."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"SDLT money: for HMRC", body:"**SDLT** money is to be paid to **HMRC** (HM Revenue and Customs, the tax authority). It is client money until paid."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Bridging loan: the client's", body:"A **bridging loan** is held for the client (borrowed from the bank). It is usually repaid from the sale proceeds under an **undertaking**."}
 ]},
 {h:"The deposit", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Usually 10% on exchange", body:"The deposit is usually **10%** of the price, paid on **exchange**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Buyer's solicitor: receiving the deposit", body:"The buyer's solicitor receives the deposit from the client: **DR client cash; CR buyer's ledger client column** (DR = debit, CR = credit)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Buyer's solicitor: sending it on exchange", body:"The buyer's solicitor then sends the deposit to the seller's solicitor on exchange: **DR buyer's ledger client column; CR client cash**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Seller's solicitor: stakeholder or agent", body:"The **seller's** solicitor holds the deposit either as **stakeholder** or as **agent for the seller**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"As stakeholder: a separate stakeholder ledger", body:"**As stakeholder:** credit the deposit to a separate **stakeholder ledger** (**DR client cash; CR stakeholder ledger client column**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"On completion: an inter-client transfer", body:"On completion, move the stakeholder deposit to the seller's ledger by an **inter-client transfer** (a book entry moving money from one client ledger to another): **DR stakeholder ledger; CR seller's ledger**, client columns. There is **no cash entry**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"As agent: straight to the seller's ledger", body:"**As agent:** credit the deposit straight to the **seller's ledger**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The Standard Conditions of Sale", body:"The **Standard Conditions of Sale** (5th edition) are the standard terms for residential contracts."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"SC 2.2.5: used for the seller's purchase", body:"**SC 2.2.5**: if the seller is buying another home in England and Wales before completion, the seller may use **all or part of the deposit** as the deposit on that purchase."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"SC 2.2.6: the rest is held as stakeholder", body:"**SC 2.2.6**: any deposit not used in that way is held by the seller's conveyancer **as stakeholder**, on terms that **on completion it is paid to the seller with accrued interest**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Interest normally goes to the seller", body:"So interest on a stakeholder deposit normally goes to the **seller** on completion."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"If the deal falls through: interest follows the deposit", body:"If the contract falls through and the deposit is returned to the buyer, the **interest follows the deposit** (r.7.1: a fair sum of interest to the person entitled)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Watch out: not before completion, even if the seller asks", body:"A seller's solicitor holding a deposit **as stakeholder** must **not** release it to the seller before completion, **even on the seller's instructions**: it is held for both parties, so one party alone cannot authorise its release."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"The seller instructs you to release the stakeholder deposit before completion. Can you?", body:"**No.** A stakeholder deposit is held for **both** buyer and seller, so the seller alone cannot authorise its release before completion."}
 ]},
 {h:"Mortgage advances and acting for the lender", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Acting for the lender too", body:"In a standard residential purchase, the buyer's solicitor commonly also acts for the **lender**. The **mortgage advance** is then client money held **for the lender** until completion."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Only for the purpose the lender allows", body:"The advance may only be used for the purpose the lender allows (completing the purchase on the agreed terms)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Where it is recorded", body:"It is usually recorded on the **buyer's ledger**, clearly identified as the lender's money, or on a **separate ledger for the lender**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Completion delayed: follow the lender's instructions", body:"If completion is **delayed**, the solicitor must follow the lender's instructions, which may require the advance to be **returned** to the lender."}
 ]},
 {h:"Bridging loans and undertakings", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What a bridging loan is", body:"A **bridging loan** is a short-term loan that lets the client pay for a purchase (deposit or completion money) **before** the sale proceeds arrive."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"It is client money", body:"The loan is **client money**. Receipt: **DR client cash; CR client ledger client column**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Repaid from the sale proceeds", body:"It is used for the purchase and **repaid from the sale proceeds** on completion of the sale: **DR client ledger client column; CR client cash**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The undertaking to the bank", body:"The bank usually requires the solicitor's **undertaking** (a binding promise) to repay the loan from the sale proceeds."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Repay before any balance goes to the client", body:"Breaking an undertaking is a **serious conduct matter**, so the repayment must be made **before** any balance is sent to the client."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Interest is the client's", body:"Interest on the bridging loan is the **client's** liability, not the firm's."}
 ]},
 {h:"Completion: buyer's solicitor", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Cleared funds for the whole amount", body:"Before completion, make sure the firm holds **cleared funds** for the **whole** amount needed (mortgage advance plus the balance from the client). Paying completion money against **uncleared** funds risks an **overdrawn** client ledger if a payment fails (r.5.3: a payment for a client must be covered by money held for that client)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Completion money by CHAPS", body:"Send the completion money to the seller's solicitor, normally by **CHAPS** (a same-day electronic bank transfer): **DR buyer's ledger client column; CR client cash**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"After completion: SDLT within 14 days", body:"After completion, pay **SDLT** to **HMRC**: the land transaction return must be filed and the tax paid within **14 days** of completion (the effective date) (Finance Act 2003 **s.76** for the return, **s.86** for payment)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"After completion: Land Registry fees", body:"Also pay the **Land Registry** fees for registering the buyer as owner (HM Land Registry keeps the official register of who owns land)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Both are agency disbursements, no VAT", body:"SDLT and Land Registry fees are both **agency** disbursements (paid on the client's behalf as their agent) with **no VAT**: **DR ledger client column; CR client cash**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Costs only after a bill", body:"Transfer the firm's costs from client account only after a **bill or written notification** (r.4.3)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Worked example: buyer's ledger", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The facts", body:"Price **£300,000**; deposit **£30,000** paid on exchange; mortgage advance **£200,000**; SDLT **£5,000**; Land Registry fee **£150**; costs **£1,000 + £200 VAT** (bill delivered before completion)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Needed from the client: £76,350", body:"Balance of price £270,000 less advance £200,000 = **£70,000**, plus SDLT £5,000, Land Registry £150 and costs £1,200 = **£76,350** needed from the client before completion."},
   {t:"compare", i:"", title:"The buyer's ledger: steps 1-5", cols:["Step", "Business", "Client"], rows:[["1. Cash from client: deposit","","CR 30,000; balance 30,000 CR"],["2. Cash: deposit to seller's solicitor","","DR 30,000; balance nil"],["3. Bill: costs and VAT","DR 1,200; balance 1,200 DR","balance nil"],["4. Cash: mortgage advance from lender","","CR 200,000; balance 200,000 CR"],["5. Cash from client: balance","","CR 76,350; balance 276,350 CR"]]},
   {t:"compare", i:"", title:"The buyer's ledger: steps 6-9", cols:["Step", "Business", "Client"], rows:[["6. Cash: completion money","","DR 270,000; balance 6,350 CR"],["7. Transfer costs to business account","CR 1,200; balance nil","DR 1,200; balance 5,150 CR"],["8. Cash: SDLT to HMRC","","DR 5,000; balance 150 CR"],["9. Cash: Land Registry fee","","DR 150; balance nil"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Never overdrawn", body:"Every payment out is covered by the client column balance at that moment, so the ledger is **never overdrawn**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Completion: seller's solicitor", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Receive the completion money", body:"Receive the completion money: **DR client cash; CR seller's ledger client column.** Transfer the deposit from the stakeholder ledger (inter-client transfer)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Redeem the seller's mortgage", body:"**Redeem the seller's mortgage** (**DR ledger client column; CR client cash**), complying with any **undertaking** given to the buyer's solicitor to do so."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Pay the estate agent", body:"Pay the **estate agent** with the client's authority. The invoice is usually **addressed to the client**, so it is an **agency** disbursement and may be paid from client account (gross, including the agent's VAT, which the firm does not reclaim)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Costs after a bill", body:"Transfer the firm's costs after a bill (r.4.3)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Then pay the balance promptly", body:"Then **promptly** pay the balance to the client, or use it for the client's related purchase on their instructions (r.2.5)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Not left in client account", body:"Sale proceeds must not be left in client account after the matter ends: that would risk a **banking facility** (using client account like a bank account for the client, when it is not linked to legal services) (r.3.3)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: Mia's sale", body:"Mia's sale completes. The firm receives **£400,000**, redeems her **£250,000** mortgage, pays the agent's **£6,000** invoice addressed to her, and transfers **£1,800** billed costs."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Mia has not decided what to do with the remaining £142,200. Can the firm keep it until she decides?", body:"**No.** The remaining **£142,200** must be sent to Mia **promptly**, or applied to her purchase if she has instructed that. The firm may not keep it \"until she decides what to do with it\"."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exam tip: the three things property questions test", body:"**(1)** Are the funds **cleared** before completion? **(2)** For **whom** is the money held (client, lender, or both parties as stakeholder)? **(3)** Is there **authority** for each payment (purpose, instructions, undertaking)?"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Search fees: principal method", body:"Search fees on a purchase are a **principal-method** disbursement (the search is supplied to the firm itself, so it is paid from **business account**; see **Bills, VAT and disbursements**)."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Farm room 04 (The Ploughing Field). Source: src/content/accounts.js, topic double-entry-basics (notes only). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["accounts/double-entry-basics"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Written down twice, for the same amount", body:"**Double-entry bookkeeping** is the method law firms use to record money. Every transaction is written down **twice**, for the **same amount**: once as a **debit (DR)** in one account and once as a **credit (CR)** in another."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Debit and credit: just left and right", body:"\"Debit\" and \"credit\" are just names for the **left-hand** and **right-hand** columns. They do **not** mean good or bad."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The books always balance", body:"Because each transaction has two equal sides, the books always balance, which makes **errors and missing money easier to spot**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What SQE1 asks of you", body:"You need to know **which accounts to debit and credit** for the common transactions, and how to **read a client ledger**. Bills and VAT are in **Bills, VAT and disbursements**; interest and breach entries are in **Interest and correcting breaches**."}
 ]},
 {h:"What an \"account\" is", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"An account is a record, not a bank account", body:"In bookkeeping, an **account** is a record (a page or a computer screen), **not** a bank account. Only the **cash accounts** record what is in the firm's real bank accounts."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Three columns: DR, CR, Balance", body:"Each account has columns for **DR**, **CR** and a running **Balance**, marked DR or CR."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Two separate sets of books", body:"A law firm keeps **two separate sets of books**: the **client** books, for client money; and the **business** books, for the firm's own money."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Both entries in the same set", body:"**Both entries of a single transaction are always in the same set of books.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Client account to business account: two pairs", body:"A movement between client account and business account is **two transactions**, so it needs **two pairs** of entries (**four entries** in total)."}
 ]},
 {h:"The accounts you need", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Client cash account", body:"The firm's record of its **client bank account**. **DR** = money **in**; **CR** = money **out**. Normal balance **DR** (money held). It can **never** be CR: that would mean the client account is overdrawn."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Business cash account", body:"The firm's record of its **business bank account**. **DR** = money **in**; **CR** = money **out**. Normal balance DR, or **CR if the business account is overdrawn**: allowed, because it is the firm's own money."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Client ledger: client column", body:"Client money held for **this** client. **DR** = money **paid out** for the client; **CR** = money **received** for the client. Normal balance **CR** (the firm owes it to the client). A **DR balance** means the ledger is **overdrawn: a breach**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Client ledger: business column", body:"Dealings in the firm's own money with this client. **DR** = amounts the client **owes the firm** (bills, disbursements (payments to third parties for the client) paid from business account); **CR** = **payments** to the firm by or for the client. Normal balance **DR** (the client owes the firm)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"One ledger, two sets of columns", body:"The **client ledger** is one record for each client matter. It has **two sets of columns side by side**: the **business** section and the **client** section. Every receipt or payment for the client appears on its ledger, **in the correct section**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Profit costs account", body:"The firm's **fees** (its income). **DR** = reductions (abatements: cuts agreed to a bill already sent); **CR** = fees billed. Normal balance **CR**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"HMRC (VAT) account", body:"VAT owed to or by HM Revenue & Customs. **DR** = **input tax** the firm can reclaim (VAT it pays on its own purchases); **CR** = **output tax** the firm charges (VAT it adds to its own bills). Usually **CR** (owed to HMRC)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Expense accounts", body:"The firm's costs, e.g. **rent, wages, interest payable, bad debts**. **DR** = expense incurred; **CR** = rare corrections. Normal balance **DR**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Nominal accounts", body:"Accounts for **income and expenses** (profit costs, VAT, rent, interest) are called **nominal accounts**."}
 ]},
 {h:"The mirror rule", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Cash entries mirror the ledger", body:"Cash account entries are the **mirror image** of the ledger entries. **Money received**: **DR cash account; CR ledger**. **Money paid out**: **CR cash account; DR ledger**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Memory aid: the firm's own bank statement", body:"For the cash accounts, think of a bank statement from the **firm's own point of view**. Money **coming in** is a **DR** to cash; money **going out** is a **CR** to cash. The ledger takes the **opposite** entry."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Watch out: client cash v client column", body:"Do not confuse the **client cash account** (DR = money in the bank) with the **client ledger client column** (CR = money held for the client). They always take **opposite** entries. Reversing them makes every answer wrong."}
 ]},
 {h:"Standard entries", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Client money received", body:"E.g. **£5,000 on account** (paid in advance towards the work) from Tom: **DR client cash £5,000; CR Tom's ledger, client column £5,000.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Payment from client account", body:"For the client, e.g. a **£150 Land Registry fee** (a fee to HM Land Registry, which registers land): **DR Tom's ledger, client column £150; CR client cash £150.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Payment from business account", body:"For the client, e.g. a **£60 fee when nothing is held**: **DR Tom's ledger, business column £60; CR business cash £60.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Firm money received from the client", body:"E.g. the client paying a delivered bill: **DR business cash; CR Tom's ledger, business column.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Bill delivered", body:"Profit costs **£1,000 + £200 VAT**: **DR Tom's ledger, business column £1,200; CR profit costs £1,000; CR HMRC (VAT) £200.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Inter-client transfer", body:"Money held for A to be held for B: **DR A's ledger, client column; CR B's ledger, client column.** **No cash entry.**"}
 ]},
 {h:"Transfers between client account and business account", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A transfer needs both sets of books", body:"A transfer is **two movements** of money, so it needs entries in **both** sets of books."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Client account to business account", body:"E.g. to pay a delivered bill from money held. **Client books:** DR client ledger, client column; CR client cash. **Business books:** DR business cash; CR client ledger, business column."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Business account to client account", body:"E.g. to replace a shortfall. **Business books:** DR client ledger, business column; CR business cash. **Client books:** DR client cash; CR client ledger, client column."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Inter-client transfers: no cash entry", body:"Inter-client transfers are different: the money **stays in client account**, so there is **no cash entry** at all, just DR one ledger and CR the other."}
 ]},
 {h:"Worked example: Tom's ledger", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Tom's five dates", body:"**1 May**: receive £5,000 from Tom on account. **3 May**: pay £150 Land Registry fee from client account. **4 May**: pay a £60 Land Registry search fee (no VAT) from business account. **10 May**: deliver a bill for profit costs £1,000 + £200 VAT, which also lists the £60 fee already paid. **11 May**: transfer £1,260 from client account to business account to pay the bill and the search fee."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"1 May: cash on account", body:"Client column: **CR 5,000**. Client balance **£5,000 CR**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"3 May: Land Registry fee", body:"Client column: **DR 150**. Client balance **£4,850 CR**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"4 May: Land Registry search fee", body:"Paid from business account. Business column: **DR 60**. Business balance **£60 DR**. Client balance still £4,850 CR."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"10 May: profit costs and VAT", body:"Business column: **DR 1,200**. Business balance **£1,260 DR**. The £60 search fee is already on the ledger, so **only the £1,200 is entered now**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"11 May: transfer from client to business", body:"Business column: **CR 1,260**, balance **nil**. Client column: **DR 1,260**, balance **£3,590 CR**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The end result", body:"Tom owes the firm **nothing** (business column **nil**) and the firm holds **£3,590** for him (client column **CR**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The matching cash entries", body:"**Client cash**: DR £5,000, CR £150, CR £1,260 (balance **£3,590 DR**). **Business cash**: CR £60, DR £1,260."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"The firm pays a £300 counsel's fee (a barrister's fee) for Mia from client account. It holds £1,000 for her. What are the entries?", body:"**DR Mia's ledger client column £300; CR client cash £300.** Mia's client balance becomes **£700 CR**."}
 ]},
 {h:"The firm's own transactions", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Nothing to do with any client ledger", body:"The firm's own transactions have **nothing to do with any client ledger**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Rent", body:"Firm pays rent £2,000: **DR rent account; CR business cash.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Wages or utility bills", body:"Firm pays wages or utility bills: **DR the expense account; CR business cash.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Interest on the business account", body:"Bank pays interest on the business account: **DR business cash; CR interest received account.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Drawings", body:"Partners take money out (**drawings**): **DR drawings account; CR business cash.**"}
 ]},
 {h:"Petty cash", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What petty cash is", body:"**Petty cash** is a small float of the **firm's own cash** kept in the office for small payments, e.g. an **oath fee** (fee for witnessing a sworn statement)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Petty cash payment for a client", body:"It is firm money, so a payment for a client is: **DR client ledger, business column; CR petty cash account.**"}
 ]},
 {h:"Exam tips", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exam tip: one move", body:"Numerical SBA (single best answer) questions usually test one move: **which account and which column** is debited and which credited."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exam tip: fix two rules", body:"**Cash in = DR cash, CR ledger** (cash out is the reverse). **Client money only ever touches the client column and the client cash account.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exam tip: a mixed option is wrong", body:"If an option mixes a **client cash** entry with a **business column** entry for one movement, it is **wrong**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exam tip: working out a balance", body:"If asked for the balance on a ledger, work through **each entry** and keep the client and business columns **completely separate**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exam tip: a DR client balance", body:"A **DR balance on the client column** in an answer option **signals a breach**."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Farm room 01 (The Hen House). Source: src/content/accounts.js, topic framework-client-money (notes only). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["accounts/framework-client-money"] = {sections:[
 {h:"Exam tip: where Solicitors Accounts is tested", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"FLK2 only", body:"Solicitors Accounts sits in **FLK2 only**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Session 1: wills and probate. Session 2: property", body:"In **Session 1** it is tested in the context of **wills and probate** (dealing with the estate of someone who has died), and in **Session 2** in the context of **property**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Why might a probate or conveyancing question really be about Accounts?", body:"Its questions are **mixed in** with the other subjects, so a probate or conveyancing (buying and selling property) scenario may really be testing an **Accounts Rule**."}
 ]},
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Other people's money", body:"Law firms hold large amounts of **other people's money**: house purchase money, estate money (money of someone who has died), damages (money compensation in a claim), money paid in advance for fees."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What the SRA Accounts Rules 2019 are for", body:"The **SRA Accounts Rules 2019** exist to make sure that money is **kept safe, kept separate from the firm's own money, used only for proper purposes and accurately recorded**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The first decision: client money or firm money?", body:"Almost every question in this subject starts with one decision: **is this client money or the firm's own money?** Client money goes into a **client account** and is protected by the rules. The firm's own money goes into its **business account**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What this topic covers", body:"Who the rules apply to, the definition of client money, when it must be paid into client account (and the exceptions), and returning it, including small leftover balances. Withdrawals are in **Client account and withdrawals**; separation and mixed payments are in **Keeping client and firm money separate**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Client money v firm money", cards:[
   {t:"compare", i:"", title:"Client money v firm (business) money", cols:["","Client money","Firm (business) money"], rows:[
     ["What it is","Money held for clients or third parties, or for costs not yet billed (r.2.1)","Money that belongs to the firm"],
     ["Where it goes","Client account (r.2.3)","Business account (also called office account)"],
     ["Examples","Purchase money, sale proceeds, estate money, damages, deposit held as stakeholder, SDLT money, money on account of costs","Payment of a bill already delivered; reimbursement of a disbursement the firm has already paid"],
     ["Ledger column","Client column of the client ledger","Business column of the client ledger"]
   ]},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Business account = office account", body:"Client money goes into **client account** (r.2.3). Firm money goes into the **business account**, also called the **office account**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Two columns on the client ledger", body:"The **client ledger** (the firm's record of the money it holds and spends for each client) has two columns: client money is recorded in the **client** column, firm money in the **business** column."}
 ]},
 {h:"Who the rules apply to", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The SRA", body:"The **Solicitors Regulation Authority (SRA)** is the regulator of solicitors and law firms in England and Wales."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"SRA Accounts Rules 2019: in force 25 November 2019", body:"The SRA made the **SRA Accounts Rules 2019**, in force since **25 November 2019**. They are the version examined for the **January 2027** sitting."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Who they apply to (r.1.1)", body:"**Authorised bodies** (firms authorised by the SRA), their **managers** (partners, LLP members (members of a limited liability partnership) or directors) and their **employees** (r.1.1)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Principles-based", body:"The rules are short and **principles-based**: they say **what** must be achieved rather than prescribing detailed procedures. SRA **guidance** fills in the detail."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The COFA", body:"The firm's **COFA** (Compliance Officer for Finance and Administration) must make sure the firm complies and must report **serious breaches** to the SRA promptly (see **Interest and correcting breaches**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"\"You\" means everyone", body:"The rules use **\"you\"** to mean the firm and the individuals within it, so **everyone** involved is responsible for compliance, not just the cashier."}
 ]},
 {h:"The definition of client money: rule 2.1", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Client money: four kinds (r.2.1)", body:"Client money is money held or received by you under one of four heads, **(a)** to **(d)**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(a) Regulated services to a client", body:"Money relating to **regulated services** (the legal services the SRA regulates) delivered by you to a **client**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(a) Examples", body:"Purchase money from a buyer client, sale proceeds, a mortgage advance (the lender's loan towards a purchase), money from closing a deceased person's bank account, damages received for a claimant."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(b) On behalf of a third party", body:"Money held on behalf of a **third party** (someone who is not your client) in relation to regulated services delivered by you."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(b) Examples", body:"A **deposit held as stakeholder** (held neutrally for both buyer and seller until completion, the day the sale is finished and the buyer pays the price); **Stamp Duty Land Tax (SDLT)** money held to pay **HMRC** (HM Revenue & Customs, the UK tax authority); money held for the other side in a settlement."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(c) Trustee or specified office", body:"Money held as a **trustee** or as the holder of a **specified office or appointment**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(c) Examples", body:"A partner acting as **trustee** or **executor** (the person named in a will to deal with the estate); a solicitor acting as **donee of a power of attorney** (the person appointed under a power of attorney to manage someone else's affairs), as a **Court of Protection deputy** (someone appointed by the court to manage the affairs of a person who lacks mental capacity), or as a **trustee of an occupational pension scheme**. These are the examples the rule itself gives."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(d) Fees and unpaid disbursements before a bill", body:"Money in respect of your **fees** and any **unpaid disbursements**, if held or received **before delivery of a bill** for the same. This is money **on account of costs** (paid in advance towards future fees)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What a disbursement is", body:"A **disbursement** is a payment made to a third party on the client's behalf, e.g. a court fee, a search fee, a barrister's or expert's fee."}
 ]},
 {h:"Firm money", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Firm money: outside rule 2.1", body:"Money is the **firm's** own money once it is **no longer within r.2.1**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Paying a bill already delivered", body:"Money received to pay a **bill or other written notification of costs** that has **already been delivered** is firm money."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Reimbursing a disbursement already paid", body:"Money received to **reimburse** the firm for a disbursement it has **already paid** out of its own business account is firm money."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Money the firm owns for any other reason", body:"For example, **interest earned on its business account**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: Asha pays a delivered bill", body:"Asha's solicitor sends her a bill for £1,200 (fees plus VAT, value added tax). A week later Asha sends £1,200. **This is firm money**, because the bill was delivered before the money arrived. It goes into the **business account**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: Asha pays before any bill", body:"Before any bill is sent, Asha sends £1,000 \"towards your fees\". **This is client money** under r.2.1(d), because no bill has yet been delivered. It goes into **client account**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Costs: before the bill client, after the bill firm", body:"Money for costs is **client money before** the bill is delivered and **firm money after** it."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Billed disbursements are firm money", body:"The same is true of disbursements: once a disbursement has been **billed**, money received for it falls outside r.2.1(d) and is **firm money, even if the firm has not yet paid the third party**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A billed but unpaid barrister's fee", body:"So money received after a bill that includes an unpaid professional disbursement (such as **counsel's fee**: counsel means a barrister) may go to **business account**, but the firm must still pay that third party **promptly**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The old two-working-day rule has gone", body:"The old 2011 rule requiring such money to be paid out within **two working days** or placed in client account **no longer exists**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: in what order do you classify money?", body:"Ask in this order: **(1)** is it held for a client or a third party? **(2)** is it held as trustee or office-holder? **(3)** is it for fees or disbursements, and if so, **has a bill or written notification of costs been delivered yet?**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exam tip: look for the word \"bill\"", body:"The word **\"bill\"** in the question is usually the key fact."}
 ]},
 {h:"Duty to pay into client account promptly: rule 2.3", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Pay it in promptly (r.2.3)", body:"Client money must be paid **promptly** into a client account."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"\"Promptly\" is not defined", body:"\"Promptly\" is **not defined** in the rules and has **no fixed number of days**. It takes its natural meaning: as soon as reasonably possible in the circumstances."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The old \"same or next working day\" test", body:"The old 2011 rules' **\"same or next working day\"** test no longer applies, though paying money in that quickly will normally be prompt."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Only three exceptions", body:"There are **only three exceptions** to the duty to pay client money into client account: (a), (b) and (c)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(a) Trustee or office-holder: a conflict", body:"For money held as **trustee or office-holder** (r.2.1(c)), where paying it into client account would **conflict** with your obligations under the rules or regulations relating to that office or appointment."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(b) Legal Aid Agency payment for costs", body:"Where the client money is a payment from the **Legal Aid Agency (LAA)** (the government body that funds legal aid) **for your costs**: it may go straight into **business account**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(c) An alternative arrangement in writing", body:"Where you agree **in the individual circumstances** an **alternative arrangement in writing** with the client, or the third party, for whom the money is held."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(c) Example", body:"The client asks in writing for a sum to be held in a particular account, or for a cheque to be passed straight to a third party."}
 ]},
 {h:"Firms that need no client account: rule 2.2", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No client account needed if ALL of these apply", body:"A firm need **not** hold money in a client account if **all** of the following four conditions apply (r.2.2)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"1. Only money on account", body:"The **only** client money it holds is money **on account of fees and unpaid disbursements** before a bill is delivered (r.2.1(d))."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"2. Disbursements the firm itself is liable for", body:"Any money held for disbursements relates to costs or expenses incurred **on the client's behalf and for which the firm itself is liable** (e.g. counsel's or an expert's fee, which the firm has contracted to pay)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Not disbursements the client is liable for", body:"This does **not** cover money for disbursements the **client** is liable for, such as **SDLT, Land Registry fees or court fees**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"3. No client account for any other reason", body:"The firm does **not hold a client account for any other reason**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"4. The client is told in advance", body:"The firm has **informed the client in advance** where and how the money will be held."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Still bound by the rest of the rules", body:"Such firms often hold the money in their **business account**. They are **not exempt** from the rest of the rules (e.g. records and returning money promptly)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Available on demand and returned promptly: rules 2.4 and 2.5", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Available on demand (r.2.4)", body:"Client money must be **available on demand**, unless an alternative arrangement has been agreed **in writing** with the client or third party."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: a fixed-term deposit", body:"Money placed on a **fixed-term deposit at the client's request**, agreed in writing."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Return it promptly (r.2.5)", body:"Client money must be **returned promptly** to the client or third party **as soon as there is no longer any proper reason to hold it**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"When there is no longer a reason", body:"Examples: the **matter ends**; a **transaction falls through**; the money was **overpaid**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"\"Leave it with you for now\"", body:"Keeping funds after there is no reason to hold them, e.g. because the client \"would like to leave it with you for now\", risks turning client account into a **banking facility** (using client account like a bank account, for payments not linked to the firm's legal work), which **r.3.3** forbids (see **Client account and withdrawals**)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Residual balances", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What a residual balance is", body:"A **residual balance** is a small sum left on a client's ledger after the matter ends that cannot easily be returned, e.g. because the client has moved and cannot be traced."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Withdrawing it: r.5.1(c)", body:"Withdrawing it is a withdrawal under **r.5.1(c)**: it needs the **SRA's prior written authorisation**, unless it falls within the SRA's **prescribed circumstances** (a general authority published by the SRA)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: Mr Shah's £38", body:"After completing a sale, **£38** is left on Mr Shah's ledger. Letters to his new address are **returned**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"£500 or less: no need to apply", body:"Under the prescribed circumstances, a firm may withdraw a residual balance of **£500 or less** on any one client matter **without** applying to the SRA, if it meets all of the following conditions."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Reasonable steps to return it", body:"It has taken **reasonable steps** to return the money to the person entitled to it. More effort is expected for **larger** and **more recent** balances."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Pay it to a charity", body:"It pays the money to a **charity** of its choice."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Records kept for at least 6 years", body:"It **records** the steps taken and keeps the records, with the charity's **receipt** and any **indemnity** (a charity's promise to repay if the owner later claims), for **at least 6 years**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A central register", body:"It keeps a **central register** of these payments: name of the person entitled, amount, charity name and registered number, date of payment."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No deductions", body:"It does **not deduct** its own costs (such as tracing costs) from the balance."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Over £500: ask the SRA", body:"**Over £500**, the firm must apply to the SRA for **authorisation** before withdrawing it."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Can the firm pay Mr Shah's £38 to charity without asking the SRA?", body:"**Yes**, after reasonable steps to trace him, keeping a record and an entry in the register. If the sum were **£800**, it would need the SRA's authorisation."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: what do you check in a residual balance question?", body:"Check the figure against **£500**, then the conditions: reasonable steps to trace, paid to **charity** (not kept by the firm, not used to clear an unpaid bill without a bill being delivered), records kept, and **no deductions**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Changes on the way (not in force at 11 September 2026)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Accountant's reports to go to the SRA", body:"The SRA decided in 2026 that **every firm holding client money that is not exempt** must **submit its annual accountant's report** (the yearly report by an independent accountant on the firm's compliance) to the SRA, qualified (reporting problems) or not, together with an **annual declaration**, with **fixed financial penalties** for late or non-submission."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Approved 31 July 2026; starts 1 April 2027", body:"The **Legal Services Board (LSB)** (the body that oversees the legal regulators) **approved** these rule changes on **31 July 2026**, and submission starts from **1 April 2027**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Consultation on telling the SRA", body:"The SRA also **consulted** (19 June to 17 August 2026) on requiring firms to **notify** it, for example within **28 days** of **starting to hold client money**. **No decision** has been made."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Answer on the current rules", body:"None of this was in force at the **11 September 2026** cut-off for examinable law. Answer questions on the **current** rules. Details are in **Accountants' reports**."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Farm room 06 (The Pumpkin Patch and the Broken Fence). Source: src/content/accounts.js, topic interest-breaches (notes only). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["accounts/interest-breaches"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Two separate duties", body:"Two separate duties are covered here: **interest** and **breaches**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Interest: a fair sum (rule 7)", body:"Clients' money earns interest in the bank, and the firm must pass on a **fair sum of interest** to the person the money is held for, unless they have **agreed otherwise in writing** (rule 7)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Breaches: correct and replace (rule 6)", body:"When something goes wrong (a ledger is overdrawn, money is paid into the wrong account, costs are taken too early), the firm must **correct it promptly on discovery** and **immediately replace** any missing client money from its **own funds** (rule 6)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Serious breaches are reported", body:"Serious breaches must also be **reported** to the SRA (the Solicitors Regulation Authority, the regulator)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Interest: rule 7", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"r.7.1: a fair sum of interest", body:"You must **account to clients or third parties for a fair sum of interest** on client money held for them."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"r.7.2: a different arrangement in writing", body:"You may agree a **different arrangement** (including **no interest**) by **written agreement** with the client or third party, but you must give them **sufficient information** to give **informed consent** (agreement made with a proper understanding of what they are giving up)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No minimum: \"fair\" depends", body:"The rules set **no minimum amount, rate or period**. \"Fair\" depends on the circumstances."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The interest policy", body:"Firms should have a written **interest policy** (usually explained in the **client care letter**, the letter sent at the start setting out the terms of the work) that takes account of: the **amount** held and **how long** it is held; the **rate** the firm could reasonably obtain if the money were in a separate deposit account; the need for the money to be **instantly available**; and any **de minimis** threshold (an amount below which no interest is paid)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A threshold set too high", body:"A de minimis threshold set **so high that clients rarely receive interest** is unlikely to be fair."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Due to whoever the money is held for", body:"Interest is due to **whoever the money is held for**, including a **third party**. Example: interest on a deposit held as **stakeholder** (held neutrally for both buyer and seller until completion, when the sale is finished) goes with the deposit to whoever becomes entitled to it, normally the **seller** on completion (see **Accounts in conveyancing**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Large sums held for some time", body:"Interest is most often due on **large sums held for some time**: estate money awaiting distribution, sale proceeds held for a related purchase, damages awaiting payment out."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: £250,000 for 5 months", body:"The firm holds £250,000 of estate money for 5 months while the estate is administered, in its general client account. It has **no written agreement** with the executors (the people appointed to deal with the estate) about interest. **It must pay the estate a fair sum of interest** (r.7.1), calculated under its interest policy."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exam tip: the client care letter distractor", body:"The distractor is usually \"no interest is payable because the client care letter says so\". Contracting out needs a **written agreement** made with **informed consent** (r.7.2). A **one-line statement** that the firm keeps all interest, without explanation, is **unlikely to be enough**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exam tip: a fair sum, not all of it", body:"Equally, the rules do **not** require the firm to pay **all** the interest it earns: a **fair sum** is the test."}
 ]},
 {h:"Accounting entries for interest: general client account", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The bank pays the firm", body:"The bank pays interest on the whole pooled account to the **firm**. The firm then pays each client a fair sum out of its **own** money, so the interest paid is an **expense** of the firm."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Step 1: record what the firm owes", body:"**DR interest payable account; CR client ledger business column.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Step 2, either: pay it to the client", body:"By a business account payment: **DR client ledger business column; CR business cash.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Step 2, or: transfer it into client account", body:"To hold for the client (two pairs of entries). Business side: **DR client ledger business column; CR business cash**. Client side: **DR client cash; CR client ledger client column.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: Priya's £120", body:"The firm owes Priya £120 interest and transfers it into client account to add to her funds. **DR interest payable £120; CR Priya's ledger business column £120.** Then **DR Priya's ledger business column £120; CR business cash £120**, and **DR client cash £120; CR Priya's ledger client column £120.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Priya's ledger afterwards", body:"Her business column is back to **nil** and her client column is **£120 higher**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Accounting entries for interest: separate designated client account", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The bank credits it directly", body:"The money is in an account **just for that client**, so the bank credits the interest **directly** to it. The interest is the client's from the start."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"One pair, no business entries", body:"**DR client cash (designated deposit account); CR client ledger client column.** **No business entries** are needed."},
   {t:"compare", i:"", title:"General v separate designated client account", cols:["","General client account","Separate designated client account"], rows:[
     ["Who receives interest from the bank","The firm","The client's account directly"],
     ["Who pays the client","The firm, from business account","No payment needed"],
     ["Entries","Interest payable account + business column, then payment or transfer","Client cash + ledger client column only"]
   ]}
 ]},
 {h:"Correcting breaches: rule 6.1", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Promptly upon discovery", body:"You must **correct any breaches of the rules promptly upon discovery**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Paid in or replaced immediately", body:"Any money **improperly withheld** from a client account (e.g. client money wrongly kept in business account) or **improperly withdrawn** from it (e.g. an overpayment) must be **immediately paid into the account or replaced**, as appropriate."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The firm's own money, fault or not", body:"The firm uses **its own money** (normally from its business account). It must do so **whether or not anyone was at fault**, and **without waiting** to recover the money from the client, a bank or an insurer."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What a breach is", body:"A **breach** means any failure to comply with the Accounts Rules, **deliberate or accidental**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Common breaches", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Overdrawn client column: r.5.3", body:"Paying out more than is held for a client (**overdrawn** client column) breaks **r.5.3** (withdraw client money only if enough is held for that client). Correction: **transfer the shortfall from business account to client account immediately**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Client money in business account: r.2.3, r.4.1", body:"Client money paid into business account breaks **r.2.3** (pay client money promptly into client account) and **r.4.1** (keep client money separate from the firm's money). Correction: **transfer it to client account immediately**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Firm money left in client account: r.4.1", body:"Firm money (e.g. **billed costs**) left in client account breaks **r.4.1**. Correction: **transfer it to business account promptly**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Costs taken before a bill: r.4.3", body:"Costs transferred **before** a bill or written notification break **r.4.3**. Correction: **return the money to client account immediately** (r.6.1), **deliver the bill** before transferring again, and **record the breach**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A later bill does not cure it", body:"Sending the bill afterwards does **not** cure the breach."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A cheque later dishonoured: r.5.3", body:"A payment against a cheque that is later **dishonoured** (bounces) breaks **r.5.3**. Correction: **replace the shortfall from business account**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Bank or posting error: r.6.1", body:"A bank or posting error leaving a shortfall falls under **r.6.1**. Correction: **replace immediately; recover from the bank later**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Banking facility: r.3.3", body:"Using client account as a **banking facility** (passing money through it that is not linked to the firm's legal services) breaks **r.3.3**. Correction: **stop and return the money; consider reporting**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Entries to correct an overdrawn client ledger", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Kim: £2,000 paid out too much", body:"The firm discovers it paid **£2,000 too much** for Kim from client account. Her client column shows **£2,000 DR** (overdrawn)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Business side", body:"**DR Kim's ledger business column £2,000; CR business cash £2,000.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Client side", body:"**DR client cash £2,000; CR Kim's ledger client column £2,000** (client column back to **nil**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Kim now owes the firm", body:"Kim's business column now shows **£2,000 DR**: she owes the firm that sum, which it can try to recover from her."}
 ]},
 {h:"Dishonoured cheques", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Reverse the receipt", body:"If a client's cheque paid into client account bounces, reverse the receipt: **DR client ledger client column; CR client cash.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Already paid out against it?", body:"Then the client column is now **overdrawn**: **replace the shortfall immediately from business account**, using the entries for an overdrawn ledger."}
 ]},
 {h:"Reporting breaches", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The COFA: a prompt report", body:"The firm's **COFA** (Compliance Officer for Finance and Administration) must take all reasonable steps to ensure the firm complies with the Accounts Rules and that a **prompt report** is made to the SRA of any facts or matters they reasonably believe are capable of amounting to a **serious breach** (SRA Code of Conduct for Firms, **para 9.2**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A breach log of all breaches", body:"The firm must keep records to **demonstrate compliance** (Code for Firms, **para 2.2**), so in practice it keeps a **breach log** of **all** breaches, even minor ones that are not reported."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Is it serious? The factors", body:"The **amount**; whether client money was put **at risk** or lost; any **dishonesty**; whether it is a **pattern** of repeated breaches; and how quickly it was **corrected**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Misappropriation: always serious", body:"**Misappropriation** (taking client money for personal or firm use) is **always serious** and must be reported."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Every solicitor must report too", body:"Every individual solicitor must also report promptly to the SRA any facts or matters they reasonably believe are capable of amounting to a serious breach by themselves or anyone else regulated by the SRA (SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, RELs and RFLs (registered European and foreign lawyers), **para 7.7**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: £15 too much", body:"A cashier mistakenly pays £15 too much from one client's ledger; it is spotted at the next reconciliation (the regular check of the firm's records against the bank) and replaced the same day. **It must be corrected and recorded, but it is unlikely to be a serious breach that must be reported.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: £50,000 to cover the overdraft", body:"A partner who moves **£50,000** from client account to cover the firm's overdraft **must be reported**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exam tip: promptly and immediately", body:"Key words are **\"promptly upon discovery\"** and **\"immediately\"** replaced. Wrong options often have the firm **waiting** for the client to repay, for the bank to investigate, or for the next accountant's report."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exam tip: replacing does not cancel it", body:"Replacing the money **does not cancel the breach**: it must still be **recorded** and, if serious, **reported**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Watch out: even the bank's or a fraudster's mistake", body:"The duty to replace a shortfall falls on the firm even when the mistake was the **bank's** or a **fraudster's**. The firm pays in its own money **now** and pursues the bank, fraudster or insurer afterwards."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Source: src/content/accounts.js topic joint-own-tpma (Farm room 08, The Allotments). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["accounts/joint-own-tpma"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Not every pound sits in client account", body:"Not every pound a firm deals with sits in its own client account. Sometimes money is held in a **joint account** with someone else, sometimes a solicitor **operates the client's own bank account** (for example as an attorney), and sometimes the firm uses an independent **third party managed account (TPMA)** so that it never holds the client's money at all."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Rules 9, 10 and 11 switch most of Part 2 off", body:"The full Accounts Rules would not make sense for these, so rules **9, 10 and 11** switch off most of **Part 2** of the rules (**rules 2 to 8**, the main client money rules) and keep only the parts that still matter. The exam tests **which parts still apply** to each."}
 ]},
 {h:"Joint accounts: rule 9", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"When rule 9 applies", body:"Rule 9 applies where, **when acting in a client's matter**, you **hold or receive money jointly with the client or a third party** (for example another law firm)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: a retention", body:"In a property dispute, the buyer's and seller's solicitors put a **retention** (a sum held back from the price) into a **joint deposit account** in **both firms' names** until a problem is fixed."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What still applies", body:"**Part 2 does not apply, except**:\n- **r.8.2**: obtain **statements** at least every **five weeks**; and\n- **r.8.4**: keep a central record of **bills and notifications of costs**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No client account, no reconciliation", body:"So the money does **not** have to be in the firm's client account, and **no r.8.3 reconciliation** is required."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Still counts for the accountant's report", body:"Operating a joint account still **triggers the duty to obtain an accountant's report** (r.12.1), and its balance **counts** in deciding whether the low-balance exemption (no report is needed while the firm's client money balances stay under set limits) applies (r.12.3)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Operating a client's own account: rule 10", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"When rule 10 applies", body:"Rule 10 applies where, **in the course of practice**, you **operate a client's own account as signatory** (you can sign for or authorise payments from an account in the **client's name**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Examples: attorney, deputy, estate account", body:"- a solicitor acting as **attorney** under a **lasting power of attorney (LPA)** (a document by which a person, the **donor**, appoints someone to manage their property and finances) who runs the donor's bank account;\n- a solicitor appointed as **Court of Protection deputy** (appointed by the court to manage the affairs of someone who lacks mental capacity) operating that person's account;\n- a solicitor who is a signatory on an **estate bank account** opened in the names of the **personal representatives** (PRs: executors or administrators)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What still applies", body:"**Part 2 does not apply, except**:\n- **r.8.2**: obtain **statements** at least every five weeks;\n- **r.8.3**: **reconcile** at least every five weeks (signed off by the COFA (compliance officer for finance and administration) or a manager); and\n- **r.8.4**: central record of **bills and notifications of costs**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The money stays in the client's account", body:"The money **stays in the client's account**; it is **not** paid into the firm's client account."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Still counts for the accountant's report", body:"Operating a client's own account also **triggers the duty to obtain an accountant's report** (r.12.1), and its balance **counts towards the exemption limits** (r.12.3)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Watch out: moved into client account", body:"If the solicitor-attorney or executor **takes the money out of the client's own account and pays it into the firm's client account**, it becomes **client money** under **r.2.1(c)** (held as trustee or holder of a specified office or appointment, such as donee of a power of attorney or Court of Protection deputy) and the **full** rules apply. Rule 10 applies **only while the money remains in the client's own account**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: Jo and Mrs Patel", body:"Jo, a partner, is attorney for Mrs Patel and signs cheques on **Mrs Patel's own bank account** to pay her care home fees. **Rule 10 applies**: Jo's firm must obtain statements and reconcile at least every five weeks and record any bills, but the money need not go into client account. If Jo moved **£20,000** from Mrs Patel's account into the firm's client account, **it would be client money under r.2.1(c)** and all of Part 2 would apply."}
 ]},
 {h:"Third party managed accounts (TPMAs): rule 11", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What a TPMA is", body:"A **TPMA** is an account run by an independent **payment service provider** (a payments business regulated by the **Financial Conduct Authority**), through which payments between the client and others, including the firm's own fees, are made. **The provider, not the firm, holds the money.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Why use one", body:"Using a TPMA means the firm **does not hold client money**, which reduces risk and may mean the firm does not need a **client account** or an **accountant's report** at all."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"r.11.1(a): the firm must not hold the money", body:"You may use a TPMA for receiving payments from, or making payments to or for, the client in respect of **regulated services** (the legal services the firm is regulated for) **only if**:\n- **(a)** using the account **does not result in you receiving or holding the client's money**; and\n- **(b)** the client is properly informed (next card)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"r.11.1(b): tell the client first", body:"**(b)** You take **reasonable steps** to ensure, **before accepting instructions**, that the client is **informed of and understands**:\n- **(i)** the **terms** of the contractual arrangements for the TPMA, in particular **how any fees for its use will be paid and who will bear them**; and\n- **(ii)** the client's **right to terminate** the agreement and to **dispute payment requests** made by you."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"r.11.2: regular statements", body:"You must obtain **regular statements** from the TPMA provider and ensure they **accurately reflect all transactions** on the account."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: a small conveyancing firm", body:"A small firm doing **fixed-fee conveyancing** wants to avoid holding client money. **It can use a TPMA**, provided it explains to each client **before accepting instructions** who pays the TPMA's fees, and that the client can **end the arrangement** and **dispute the firm's payment requests**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Comparison", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Client account", body:"- **Whose name?** The firm's, with \"client\" in its name.\n- **Client money held by the firm?** Yes.\n- **Rules that apply:** **all of Part 2**.\n- **Triggers an accountant's report?** Yes (unless exempt)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Joint account (r.9)", body:"- **Whose name?** The firm's and the client's or a third party's **jointly**.\n- **Client money held by the firm?** Held **jointly**.\n- **Rules that apply:** **r.8.2** statements + **r.8.4** bills record.\n- **Triggers an accountant's report?** **Yes**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Client's own account (r.10)", body:"- **Whose name?** The **client's**.\n- **Client money held by the firm?** No (it is the client's own account).\n- **Rules that apply:** **r.8.2** statements + **r.8.3** reconciliation + **r.8.4** bills record.\n- **Triggers an accountant's report?** **Yes**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"TPMA (r.11)", body:"- **Whose name?** The **provider's**, for the client.\n- **Client money held by the firm?** **No**.\n- **Rules that apply:** the **r.11** conditions + regular statements (r.11.2).\n- **Triggers an accountant's report?** No, if the firm holds no other client money."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: rule 9 v rule 10", body:"The difference between rules 9 and 10 is a favourite question. Both keep **statements (r.8.2)** and the **bills record (r.8.4)**; only the **client's own account** (r.10) also needs a **reconciliation (r.8.3)**. One way to remember it: when the solicitor alone operates the client's account, there is **no co-holder watching it**, so it must be reconciled."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: where is the money?", body:"In probate and elderly-client scenarios ask first: **where is the money?**\n- In the firm's **client account**: full rules, r.2.1(c);\n- in an account in the **client's or PRs' name** that the solicitor signs on: r.10; or\n- in a **joint account with another firm**: r.9."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Farm room 11 (The Hay Barn). Source: src/content/accounts.js, topic probate-accounts (notes only). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["accounts/probate-accounts"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exam tip: FLK2 Session 1, wills", body:"From **January 2027** Solicitors Accounts appears in **FLK2 Session 1 in the context of wills** (and the administration of estates). Expect estate scenarios where the question is really about **client money, withdrawals, bills or interest**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What the personal representatives do", body:"When someone dies, their **personal representatives (PRs)** collect in the estate, pay debts and **inheritance tax (IHT)**, and distribute what is left under the will or the **intestacy rules** (the rules on who inherits when there is no valid will)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Large sums, often for months", body:"If the PRs instruct a firm, large sums pass through the firm's client account, often for months. The Accounts Rules apply in the usual way."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The points that come up again and again (1)", body:"The **PRs are the client** (not the beneficiaries); money held by a partner as **executor** is client money; IHT is often paid **before** the estate's money can be collected."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The points that come up again and again (2)", body:"Costs need a **bill first**; estate money earns **interest**; and the estate must be distributed **promptly** once administration is complete."}
 ]},
 {h:"Who is the client?", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The personal representatives are the client", body:"The **personal representatives** are the client."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Executors or administrators", body:"PRs are either **executors** (appointed in the will) or **administrators** (appointed by the court where there is no executor, e.g. on intestacy)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The beneficiaries are not clients", body:"The **beneficiaries** (people who inherit) are **not** the firm's clients, though the money is ultimately held for their benefit through the PRs."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Estate money is client money (r.2.1(a))", body:"Money received for the estate is **client money** (r.2.1(a)), credited to the **estate ledger**, e.g. \"Executors of Jane Smith deceased\"."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A partner or employee as executor or trustee (r.2.1(c))", body:"If a **partner or employee** of the firm is an **executor or trustee**, money they hold in that capacity is also client money (**r.2.1(c)**) and goes into client account, unless one of two exceptions applies."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exception 1: the office's rules conflict (r.2.3(a))", body:"It does not go into client account if paying it in would conflict with the rules of the office (the rules or regulations that govern that office or appointment) (**r.2.3(a)**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exception 2: an estate bank account (r.10)", body:"Or it stays in an **estate bank account** in the PRs' names that the solicitor operates as **signatory** (someone authorised to sign for the account). Then **r.10** applies: statements, reconciliation and bills record only (see **Joint accounts, clients' own accounts and TPMAs**)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Typical receipts", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Typical receipts and the entries", body:"Closing the deceased's **bank and building society accounts**, **sale of shares**, **sale of the house**, **life policy** proceeds, refunds: **DR client cash; CR estate ledger client column** (DR = debit, CR = credit)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Large sums: a separate designated client account", body:"Large sums held for a long time should be considered for a **separate designated client account** (a client account held for this one client), so the estate earns interest directly."}
 ]},
 {h:"Inheritance tax (IHT)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"IHT: due 6 months after the end of the month of death", body:"**IHT** is the tax on a person's estate when they die. IHT on most property (**non-instalment property**: property whose tax cannot be paid in instalments) is due **6 months after the end of the month of death** (Inheritance Tax Act 1984 **s.226**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Paid before the grant", body:"IHT must generally be paid **before** the **grant of representation** (the court document confirming the PRs' authority, e.g. a **grant of probate**) is issued."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The problem", body:"The PRs usually **cannot collect** the estate's money until they have the grant. So how is the IHT funded?"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Way 1: the direct payment scheme (no entries)", body:"The **direct payment scheme**: the deceased's bank or building society pays HMRC (HM Revenue and Customs) **directly** from the deceased's account. The money never reaches the firm, so there are **no entries** in the firm's books."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Way 2: a loan to the personal representatives", body:"A **loan** to the PRs from a bank. It is client money if paid to the firm: **DR client cash; CR estate ledger client column**; then paid out to HMRC."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Way 3: funds already in client account", body:"Funds already received into client account, e.g. from an asset that can be released without a grant."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Paying IHT from client account", body:"If paid **from client account**: **DR estate ledger client column; CR client cash.** There must be **sufficient funds** on **that estate's** ledger (r.5.3)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Paying IHT from business account (rare)", body:"If the firm pays IHT from its own **business account** (rare, because it is a large sum): **DR estate ledger business column; CR business cash.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Watch out: never another estate's money", body:"Never use **another estate's** money (or any other client's money) to pay an estate's IHT, **even for a day**: that is using one client's money for another, a breach of r.5.3 that must be replaced immediately."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"The estate next door has plenty in client account. Can it lend Jane's estate the IHT for a day?", body:"**No.** That is using one client's money for another, a breach of **r.5.3**, even for a day. It must be replaced immediately."}
 ]},
 {h:"Disbursements in probate", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Probate application fee: agency, no VAT", body:"The **probate application fee** (the court fee for the grant) is an **agency** disbursement: not a VAT supply, no VAT. Pay it from **client account** if funds are held for the estate; otherwise from **business account**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Statutory advertisements", body:"**Statutory advertisements** are notices under **Trustee Act 1925 s.27** in **The Gazette** (the official public record) and a local newspaper inviting creditors to come forward. They protect the PRs from unknown debts."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Advertisements: who is the invoice addressed to?", body:"The method depends on **to whom the invoice is addressed**: the client (**agency**: client or business account) or the firm (**principal**: **business account only**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Valuers', surveyors' and estate agents' fees", body:"**Valuers', surveyors' and estate agents' fees**: the same test. Who is the invoice addressed to? The account follows as above."}
 ]},
 {h:"Costs", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Bill first, then the specific sum", body:"Before taking costs from estate money, deliver a **bill or written notification of costs** to the PRs (**r.4.3**), then transfer the **specific sum**, provided it is covered by the estate's balance."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Costs without a bill: a breach", body:"Costs taken **without** a bill are a breach to be corrected immediately (r.6.1)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Distributions to beneficiaries", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Legacies and residue (r.5.1)", body:"Paying **legacies** (gifts of particular items or sums in the will) and the **residue** (what is left after debts, tax, expenses and legacies) is a withdrawal **for the purpose for which the money is held**, or on the PRs' **instructions** (r.5.1)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Within the estate ledger's balance", body:"Each payment must be within the **estate ledger's balance**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Interim distributions and a retention", body:"**Interim distributions** (part-payments to beneficiaries before the administration is finished) may be made, keeping a **retention** (a sum held back) to cover outstanding liabilities such as final income tax or costs."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Estate accounts approved: distribute promptly (r.2.5)", body:"Once the **estate accounts** (the final statement of the estate's assets, liabilities and distributions) are approved by the PRs, the balance must be distributed **promptly** (r.2.5)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Not a bank (r.3.3)", body:"Holding residue indefinitely because a beneficiary would prefer to \"leave it with the firm\" turns client account into a **banking facility** (using client account like a bank account, not linked to legal services) (r.3.3)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Worked example: estate ledger", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The facts: probate fee and IHT", body:"The firm acts for the executors of **Jane Smith**. It pays the **£526 probate application fee** (the fee for estates over £5,000 from 13 July 2026) from **business account**, because nothing is yet held for the estate. IHT is paid by the **direct payment scheme**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The facts: receipts, bill, legacy, residue", body:"After the grant it receives **£50,000** from Jane's bank and **£250,000** from the house sale. It delivers a bill for **£4,000 + £800 VAT**, which also lists the £526 probate fee already paid. It then pays a **£10,000 legacy** and the **residue** to the sole residuary beneficiary."},
   {t:"compare", i:"", title:"The estate ledger: steps 1-4", cols:["Step", "Business", "Client"], rows:[["1. Cash: probate fee (business account)","DR 526; balance 526 DR",""],["2. IHT by direct payment scheme","balance 526 DR","(no entry)"],["3. Cash: bank account closed","","CR 50,000; balance 50,000 CR"],["4. Cash: house sale proceeds","","CR 250,000; balance 300,000 CR"]]},
   {t:"compare", i:"", title:"The estate ledger: steps 5-8", cols:["Step", "Business", "Client"], rows:[["5. Bill: costs and VAT","DR 4,800; balance 5,326 DR","balance 300,000 CR"],["6. Transfer client to business","CR 5,326; balance nil","DR 5,326; balance 294,674 CR"],["7. Cash: legacy","","DR 10,000; balance 284,674 CR"],["8. Cash: residue to beneficiary","","DR 284,674; balance nil"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Why the £5,326 transfer is allowed", body:"The **£5,326** transfer is allowed because the bill (or written notification) covering both the costs and the £526 fee was delivered **before** the transfer (r.4.3)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The probate fee: no VAT", body:"The probate fee is a court fee, so it is **not a VAT supply**: it is an **agency** disbursement and the firm adds **no VAT** to it on the bill."}
 ]},
 {h:"Interest on estate money", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A fair sum of interest (r.7.1)", body:"The firm must account to the estate for a **fair sum of interest** (r.7.1) unless the PRs have agreed otherwise in writing with **informed consent** (r.7.2)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Commonly due", body:"Estates often hold large sums for months, so interest is commonly due."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"It belongs to the estate", body:"Interest belongs to the **estate** and is relevant to the estate's income tax position."}
 ]},
 {h:"Residual balances", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Small sums left over: return them", body:"Small sums left on an estate ledger after distribution (e.g. a late refund of council tax) belong to the **estate and its beneficiaries**. Take **reasonable steps** to return them."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"£500 or less: charity", body:"If they cannot be returned: **£500 or less** may be paid to **charity** under the SRA's prescribed circumstances, with records kept for **6 years** and **no deductions**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Over £500: SRA authorisation", body:"**Over £500** needs **SRA authorisation** (r.5.1(c))."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Six months after the estate is wound up, a £75 utility refund arrives. What should the firm do?", body:"**Pay it to the residuary beneficiary**, as it belongs to the estate. Only if the beneficiary cannot be traced after reasonable steps may it go to charity."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exam tip: two rules at once", body:"Probate questions often **combine two rules**. Run through the checklist on the next card."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exam tip: the checklist", body:"Is the money **client money** (including money held by a partner as executor)? Does **this estate's** ledger hold enough for the payment? Has a **bill** been delivered before costs are taken? Is **interest** due? Is the residue being paid out **promptly**?"}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Source: src/content/accounts.js topic records-reconciliation (Farm room 07, The Cow Barn). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["accounts/records-reconciliation"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Good records keep client money safe", body:"Good records are what make client money safe in practice. Rule 8 requires the firm to keep **accurate, up-to-date ledgers** for every client (a **ledger** is the running account kept for each client matter), a **list of what it owes clients**, and a **cash book** for client account."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Statements and reconciliation every five weeks", body:"The firm must get **bank statements** at least every **five weeks**, and at least every **five weeks** carry out a **three-way reconciliation** comparing the bank, the cash book and the client ledgers, **signed off by a senior person**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Keep it all for six years", body:"Rule 13 requires all accounting records to be **kept securely for at least six years**. Good records are the main way firms (and the SRA) catch **shortfalls** (less money in client account than the firm owes its clients), errors and fraud."}
 ]},
 {h:"Accounting records: rule 8.1", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Rule 8.1: accurate records", body:"You must keep and maintain **accurate**, **contemporaneous** (made at the time, not later) and **chronological** (in date order) records to:\n- **(a)** record dealings in **client ledgers**;\n- **(b)** keep a **list of all the client ledger balances**; and\n- **(c)** keep a **cash book**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(a) Client ledgers: two sides", body:"Each client ledger is identified by the **client's name** and an appropriate **description of the matter**. It records:\n- all receipts and payments of **client money**, on the **client side** of the ledger; and\n- all receipts and payments which are **not client money**, and **bills of costs**, including transactions through the **business account** (the firm's own bank account), on the **business side** of the ledger."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(b) The list of balances", body:"Maintain a **list of all the balances** shown by the client ledgers, with a **running total** of the balances. This shows the firm's total **liabilities** to clients and third parties (what it owes them)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(c) The cash book", body:"Maintain a **cash book** (the client cash account: the firm's **own record** of the client bank account), showing a **running total** of all transactions through client accounts."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Transfers, abatements, write-offs and disbursements", body:"In practice, **transfers between client ledgers**, **abatements** (reductions of a bill), **write-offs** and **disbursements** (payments the firm makes to others for the client, such as fees) are all recorded on the ledger, with the **reason and authority** for transfers. Rule 8.1 does not list these one by one (the old 2011 rules did); they follow from the duty to keep **accurate** records of all dealings with client money and bills."}
 ]},
 {h:"Bank statements: rule 8.2", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Statements at least every five weeks", body:"Obtain, **at least every five weeks**, statements from banks, building societies and other financial institutions for **all client accounts and business accounts** held or operated by you."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Business accounts too", body:"Note that rule 8.2 applies to **business accounts** too, not just client accounts."}
 ]},
 {h:"Reconciliation: rule 8.3", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Three-way reconciliation every five weeks", body:"At least **every five weeks**, for **all client accounts**, complete a reconciliation of:\n- the **bank or building society statement balance**;\n- the **cash book balance**; and\n- the **client ledger total** (the total of the list of client balances under r.8.1(b)).\n\nIt is called a **three-way reconciliation** because all three figures must agree."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Who signs it off", body:"The reconciliation must be **signed off by the COFA** (Compliance Officer for Finance and Administration) **or a manager** of the firm. In SRA terms a **manager** is a **partner**, **LLP member** (a member of a limited liability partnership) or **director**, **not** an office manager or cashier."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Investigate differences promptly", body:"You must **promptly investigate and resolve** any differences the reconciliation shows."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: how often, and who signs?", body:"Know the frequency precisely: **at least every five weeks**. **Monthly** reconciliation complies; **every six weeks** or **every two months** does not. A question saying the **cashier** prepared and \"approved\" the reconciliation is testing whether you spot that it must be **signed off by the COFA or a manager**."}
 ]},
 {h:"How a reconciliation works", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Timing differences", body:"The bank statement and the firm's cash book rarely match exactly on the day, because of **timing**. So the bank figure is first **adjusted**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Deduct unpresented, add outstanding", body:"To adjust the bank figure:\n- **deduct** **unpresented cheques or payments** (payments the firm has made and recorded, but which have not yet gone through the bank);\n- **add** **outstanding lodgements** (money the firm has received and recorded, but which the bank has not yet shown)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"All three must be the same", body:"The **adjusted bank balance**, the **cash book balance** and the **total of client ledger balances** must then be **the same**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: £48,000 three ways", body:"- Balance per bank statement: **£50,000**\n- Less: unpresented cheque to Land Registry: **(£3,000)**\n- Add: outstanding lodgement received late on the last day: **£1,000**\n- **Adjusted bank balance: £48,000**\n- **Cash book balance: £48,000**\n- **Total of client ledger balances: £48,000**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A ledger total of £49,000: a shortfall", body:"If the client ledger total were **£49,000**, the firm would owe clients **£1,000 more** than it has in the bank: a **shortfall**. It must **investigate** and, if client money is missing, **replace it immediately** from its own money (r.6.1)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What a reconciliation reveals", body:"Reconciliation reveals:\n- **shortfalls**;\n- money posted to the **wrong ledger**;\n- **overdrawn** client ledgers (which must be shown as a **breach**, not netted off against other clients' balances);\n- **bank errors**; and\n- **fraud**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Watch out: totals agree, still a breach", body:"A reconciliation that \"balances\" overall can still **hide a breach**: if one client's ledger is **overdrawn**, the firm has used **other clients' money**, even if the totals agree. The overdrawn ledger must be **identified** and the shortfall **replaced**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Central record of bills: rule 8.4", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A central record of bills", body:"Keep a **readily accessible central record** of **all bills or other written notifications of costs** given by you (a bills file or electronic record)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Storage and retention: rule 13", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Securely, for six years (r.13.1)", body:"**r.13.1**: store **all accounting records securely** and retain them for **at least six years**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What must be kept", body:"This covers:\n- ledgers;\n- cash books;\n- lists of balances;\n- reconciliations;\n- bank statements;\n- bills and notifications of costs; and\n- the records and register of **residual balance** payments (client money left over at the end of a matter, paid away because its owner cannot be traced)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: the three numbers", body:"Remember the three numbers:\n- **5 weeks**: statements and reconciliation;\n- **6 months**: obtaining the accountant's report after the end of the accounting period;\n- **6 years**: keeping records.\n\nQuestions swap them in the wrong options."}
 ]},
 {h:"Other accounts that need statements or reconciliation", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Which accounts need what", body:"- **Client account**: statements every 5 weeks (r.8.2) **yes**; reconciliation every 5 weeks (r.8.3) **yes**; central record of bills (r.8.4) **yes**.\n- **Business account**: statements **yes**; reconciliation **no**; bills: one firm-wide record covers all bills.\n- **Joint account** (r.9): statements **yes**; reconciliation **no**; bills record **yes**.\n- **Client's own account** operated as signatory (r.10): statements **yes**; reconciliation **yes** (with the client's own account balance); bills record **yes**.\n\nJoint accounts and clients' own accounts are explained in **Joint accounts, clients' own accounts and TPMAs**."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Farm room 03 (The Sheep Pens). Source: src/content/accounts.js, topic separation-mixed-payments (notes only). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["accounts/separation-mixed-payments"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Never mix the two", body:"Client money and the firm's own money must **never be mixed**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Why: hidden shortfalls and the firm's creditors", body:"If they were mixed, a **shortfall** in clients' money could be **hidden**, and if the firm failed, its **creditors** (the people it owes money to) might claim money that belongs to clients."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Two accounts, and mixed payments split", body:"Client money goes in the **client account**, firm money in the **business account**, and when a single payment contains both (a **mixed payment**) it must be **split promptly**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The turning point: the bill", body:"Money for costs stops being client money and becomes the firm's on delivery of a **bill or other written notification of costs** (r.4.3)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What this topic covers", body:"Rules **4.1 to 4.3** and the entries for mixed payments and transfers to pay bills."}
 ]},
 {h:"The separation rule: rule 4.1", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Keep them separate (r.4.1)", body:"You must **keep client money separate from money belonging to the authorised body** (the firm)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Both directions", body:"**Client money must not be held in the business account**, and **firm money must not be kept in the client account**. Both are **breaches**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The \"cushion\"", body:"Leaving firm money in client account is **not harmless**: it creates a **\"cushion\"** that can **hide a shortfall** in clients' money, and it blurs which money is protected."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Allowed: the firm's share of a mixed payment", body:"Only two kinds of firm money may properly pass through client account. The first: the firm's share of a **mixed payment**, **until it is allocated** (r.4.2)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Allowed: money paid in to replace a shortfall", body:"The second: money the firm pays in to **replace a shortfall** or **correct a breach** (r.6.1). Once paid in, it is **treated as client money**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Mixed payments: rule 4.2", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What a mixed payment is", body:"A **mixed payment** is a **single receipt** that contains **both client money and firm money**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Allocate promptly", body:"You must **allocate promptly** the funds to the **correct client account or business account**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Way 1: all into client account, then transfer", body:"Pay the **whole** sum into **client account**, then **promptly transfer** the firm's share to business account."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Way 2: split on receipt", body:"**Split** the payment on receipt, paying each part into the correct account (**if the bank allows**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Never all into business account", body:"Never pay the whole mixed payment into **business account**: that puts client money in the business account, a breach of **r.2.3** and **r.4.1**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: Sunita's £2,400", body:"The firm delivered a bill to Sunita for **£1,200** last week. She now sends **£2,400**: £1,200 to pay the bill and £1,200 on account of future work. **£1,200 is firm money and £1,200 is client money.**"},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"What does the firm do with Sunita's £2,400?", body:"It pays **£2,400 into client account** and **promptly transfers £1,200** to business account (or **splits it on receipt**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The old 14-day limit has gone", body:"The old 2011 rules gave a fixed **14-day** limit for transferring the firm's share of a mixed payment. The 2019 rules just say **\"promptly\"**, with no fixed number of days. Do not treat 14 days as automatically acceptable."}
 ]},
 {h:"Paying your costs from client money: rule 4.3", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Paying costs from client money (r.4.3)", body:"Where you hold client money and some or all of it will be used to pay your costs, three rules apply: (a), (b) and (c)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(a) Give a bill or written notification", body:"You must give a **bill of costs, or other written notification of the costs incurred**, to the client or the **paying party** (e.g. an opponent ordered to pay the client's costs)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(b) Before the transfer", body:"This must be done **before** you transfer any client money from client account to make the payment."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(c) The specific sum, covered by what is held", body:"The payment must be for the **specific sum** identified in the bill or notification and **covered by the amount held** for that client or third party."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Written notification can be less formal", body:"\"Written notification of costs\" can be less formal than a full bill, e.g. a **letter or email** setting out the costs incurred. It must be **in writing** and given **before** the transfer."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Once billed: transfer promptly", body:"Once the bill is delivered, the billed amount held in client account is **firm money** and should be transferred **promptly** to business account. Leaving it in client account **breaches r.4.1**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A central record of bills (r.8.4)", body:"A **central record** of all bills and written notifications must be kept (r.8.4)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: \"towards your bill\" before any bill is sent?", body:"A favourite trap: a client pays \"the amount of your estimate\" or \"towards your bill\" **before** the bill has been sent. With no bill or written notification delivered, it is **client money** and goes to **client account**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: costs taken first, bill sent afterwards?", body:"Another trap: the firm transfers costs from client account and sends the bill **afterwards**. That is a **breach of r.4.3(b)**, even if the amount is right."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The covering letter does not decide", body:"A payment that the client describes in their covering letter does not decide its nature: the **timing of the bill** does. A sum labelled \"for your bill\" before any bill has been delivered is still **client money**."},
   {t:"compare", i:"", title:"Six situations: client or firm money?", cols:["Situation","Client or firm money?","Where?"], rows:[
     ["Client sends money \"for your fees\" before any bill","Client money (r.2.1(d))","Client account"],
     ["Client pays a bill already delivered","Firm money","Business account"],
     ["Client sends one sum covering a delivered bill and future costs","Mixed payment","Client account then transfer the firm's share promptly, or split on receipt"],
     ["Firm holds money on account and has just delivered a bill","Billed sum becomes firm money","Transfer the specific billed sum promptly to business account"],
     ["Client reimburses a disbursement the firm already paid from business account","Firm money","Business account"],
     ["Client pays in advance for a disbursement not yet paid or billed","Client money (r.2.1(d))","Client account"]
   ]}
 ]},
 {h:"Entries: mixed payment into client account, then transfer", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Sunita's bill, entered earlier", body:"(DR = debit, CR = credit; see **Double-entry bookkeeping: the basics**.) The bill delivered earlier (£1,000 + £200 VAT) was entered as: **DR** Sunita's ledger business column £1,200; **CR** profit costs (the firm's fees) £1,000; **CR** HMRC (VAT) £200."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Receipt of £2,400 into client account", body:"**DR** client cash £2,400; **CR** Sunita's ledger **client** column £2,400."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Transfer of £1,200: two sets of entries", body:"Transferring £1,200 to business account needs **two sets of entries**, one in each set of books: the client side and the business side."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Client side", body:"**DR** Sunita's ledger client column £1,200; **CR** client cash £1,200."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Business side", body:"**DR** business cash £1,200; **CR** Sunita's ledger business column £1,200."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The result", body:"Business column balance **nil** (the bill is paid); client column **£1,200 CR** (held on account)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Entries: mixed payment split on receipt", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"£1,200 into business account", body:"**DR** business cash £1,200; **CR** ledger **business** column £1,200."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"£1,200 into client account", body:"**DR** client cash £1,200; **CR** ledger **client** column £1,200."}
 ]},
 {h:"Entries: costs paid from money held on account", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Leo: £3,000 on account, then a bill", body:"The firm holds **£3,000** for Leo on account. It delivers a bill for **£1,500 + £300 VAT**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Entering Leo's bill", body:"**DR** Leo's ledger business column £1,800; **CR** profit costs £1,500; **CR** HMRC £300."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The transfer", body:"Client side: **DR** Leo's ledger client column £1,800, **CR** client cash £1,800. Business side: **DR** business cash £1,800, **CR** Leo's ledger business column £1,800."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Leo's balances afterwards", body:"Leo's client column now **£1,200 CR**; business column **nil**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Correcting money paid into the wrong account", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Client money in business account by mistake", body:"Transfer it to client account **immediately** (a breach to correct under **r.6.1**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The correction entries", body:"The receipt was wrongly recorded as DR business cash, CR ledger business column. The correction is a **business-to-client transfer**: business side **DR** ledger business column, **CR** business cash; client side **DR** client cash, **CR** ledger client column."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Firm money left in client account", body:"E.g. a bill delivered but costs not transferred: transfer it **promptly** to business account."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Source: proposals/rooms/learn-blp-cgt.js */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["blp/cgt"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🫧", title:"CGT taxes the gain", body:"**Capital gains tax (CGT)** taxes the **gain** (increase in value) an individual makes when they **dispose of** an asset. Examples: a sole trader selling business premises, a partner selling their share of the firm, or a shareholder selling or giving away shares."},
   {t:"card", i:"📕", title:"The statute", body:"The main statute is the **Taxation of Chargeable Gains Act 1992 (TCGA 1992)**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧰", title:"The four key skills", body:"Decide whether a **chargeable disposal** has happened, **calculate** the gain, apply the **annual exempt amount** and the **rates**, and choose the best **relief**."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🍬", title:"The four reliefs at a glance", cols:["Relief", "What it does"], rows:[["**Business asset disposal relief (BADR)**","⬇️ Lower rate of tax"],["**Rollover relief**","⏸️ Defers the gain when you buy replacement business assets"],["**Gift (holdover) relief**","⏸️ Defers the gain on gifts of business assets"],["**Incorporation relief**","⏸️ Defers the gain when a business is transferred to a company"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"🏢", title:"Companies don't pay CGT", body:"A **company's** gains are charged to **corporation tax**, not CGT (see **Corporation tax**). CGT is for individuals."},
   {t:"card", i:"📅", title:"Figures used here", body:"The exam gives rates and allowances where needed. The figures in this deck are for the **2026/27** tax year (6 April 2026 to 5 April 2027)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A company sells its factory at a gain. Does it pay CGT?", body:"**No.** A company's gains are charged to **corporation tax**."}
 ]},
 {h:"When does CGT apply?", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🧩", title:"Three things needed", body:"There must be a **chargeable person** making a **chargeable disposal** of a **chargeable asset**. Miss one and there is no CGT."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧍", title:"Chargeable persons", body:"Individuals **resident in the UK** in the tax year (and trustees and personal representatives). In business law that means **sole traders**, **partners** and **shareholders**. Non-residents pay CGT on UK land."},
   {t:"card", i:"🤝", title:"Partners: a slice of every asset", body:"Each partner is treated as owning a **fractional share** of each partnership asset. When the firm disposes of an asset, each partner is taxed on **their share** of the gain. A change in profit-sharing ratios can also be a **part disposal**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔁", title:"What counts as a disposal", body:"A **sale**, a **gift**, an **exchange**, a **part disposal** (disposing of part of an asset), and the loss or destruction of an asset (for example, receiving insurance money for it)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎁", title:"A gift is a disposal", body:"Giving an asset away is a disposal at **market value**, so the donor can face CGT even though they received nothing (unless **gift relief** applies)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🕊️", title:"Death is NOT a disposal", body:"No CGT is payable on death. The person inheriting takes the asset at its **market value at the date of death** (the **probate value**). This wipes out the gain: the **tax-free uplift**. Inheritance tax may be due instead (see **VAT and IHT BPR**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"💍", title:"Spouses and civil partners", body:"A disposal between spouses or civil partners living together is on a **no gain / no loss** basis (TCGA s.58). The person giving pays no tax, and the person receiving takes over the giver's **original base cost** (what the giver paid)."},
   {t:"card", i:"💔", title:"Spouses after separation", body:"The no gain / no loss rule also applies for up to **3 tax years after separation**, and indefinitely for transfers under a **divorce agreement**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Gran leaves her shares to you in her will. CGT on her death?", body:"**No.** Death is not a disposal. You take the shares at their **probate value** (the tax-free uplift)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏠", title:"Chargeable assets", body:"**All forms of property** are chargeable assets, for example land and buildings, shares, **goodwill** (the value of a business's reputation and customers), a partnership interest and intellectual property. The only exceptions are **exempt assets**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚫", title:"Exempt assets (1)", body:"**Sterling cash**; **private cars**; **gilts** (UK government stock) and qualifying corporate bonds; investments held in an **ISA** (individual savings account)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚫", title:"Exempt assets (2)", body:"The taxpayer's **only or main residence** (**private residence relief**); **wasting chattels** (tangible movable property with a predicted life of **50 years or less**); chattels disposed of for **£6,000 or less**; betting and lottery winnings."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A sole trader sells her family car and her shop. Which is chargeable?", body:"The **shop**. A **private car** is an exempt asset."}
 ]},
 {h:"Calculating the gain", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"📆", title:"Step 1: the date of disposal", body:"Identify the disposal and its date. For a contract, the date is when the contract becomes **unconditional**, not completion."},
   {t:"card", i:"💷", title:"Step 2: start with the proceeds", body:"Start with the **disposal consideration**: the **sale price**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚖️", title:"When market value replaces the price", body:"Use **market value** instead where the disposal is a **gift**, is to a **connected person**, or is otherwise not at **arm's length** (not a normal deal between strangers) (TCGA ss.17-18)."},
   {t:"card", i:"👪", title:"Who is a connected person?", body:"For example a **relative**, a **business partner**, or a **company the taxpayer controls** (ss.18, 286)."},
   {t:"card", i:"➖", title:"Deduct: costs of disposal", body:"Take off the **incidental costs of disposal**, for example legal fees, estate agents' fees and valuation fees."},
   {t:"card", i:"➖", title:"Deduct: acquisition cost", body:"Take off the **acquisition cost** (the price paid, or **market value** if acquired by gift, or **probate value** if inherited) plus **incidental costs of acquisition** (for example legal fees and **stamp duty land tax**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧱", title:"Deduct: enhancement expenditure", body:"Take off **capital** expenditure that **improved** the asset and is reflected in its state at disposal, for example building an extension. **Repairs and maintenance are not** enhancement."},
   {t:"card", i:"📈", title:"No indexation", body:"Individuals get **no indexation allowance**: there is no relief for inflation."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🧮", title:"The gain in one line", cols:["", "Item"], rows:[["Start","💷 Proceeds (or market value)"],["Less","➖ Costs of disposal"],["Less","➖ Acquisition cost + costs of acquisition"],["Less","➖ Enhancement expenditure (not repairs)"],["Equals","🫧 **Gain**"]]},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Is the cost of repainting a shop deductible?", body:"**No.** Repairs and maintenance are not enhancement expenditure. An **extension** would be."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧺", title:"Shares: which ones did you sell?", body:"**Share matching rules**: shares sold are matched first with shares of the same class bought on the **same day**, then with shares bought in the following **30 days**, then with the **pooled** holding (all the rest, at their average cost)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛏️", title:"Why the 30-day rule exists", body:"It stops **bed and breakfasting**: selling shares and buying them back straight away just to use the annual exempt amount or to create a loss on paper."},
   {t:"card", i:"🕵️", title:"Other anti-avoidance rules (outline)", body:"**Value shifting** rules tax arrangements that move value out of an asset without a disposal. **Targeted anti-avoidance rules** stop artificially created capital losses, and the **GAAR** (general anti-abuse rule) applies too (see **Income tax**)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Ed sells shares on 1 June and buys the same shares back on 20 June. What happens?", body:"The shares sold are matched with the shares **bought back within 30 days**, so he can't use the sale to create an artificial gain or loss."}
 ]},
 {h:"Losses, the annual exempt amount, rates and payment", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"➕", title:"Step 3: reliefs, Step 4: add up", body:"Apply any reliefs to each asset's gain (rollover, gift or incorporation relief; BADR changes the **rate**). Then **aggregate** (add up) all gains for the tax year and **deduct losses**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📉", title:"Current-year losses: used in full", body:"Capital losses made in the **same year** must be set against that year's gains **in full**, even if this wastes the annual exempt amount."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏩", title:"Brought-forward losses: only down to the AEA", body:"Losses **brought forward** from earlier years are used only to bring the remaining gains **down to the annual exempt amount**. Anything unused carries on forward."},
   {t:"card", i:"♾️", title:"Losses carry forward indefinitely", body:"Unused capital losses are carried forward **indefinitely** against future gains. They can't be set against **income** (except losses on certain unquoted trading company shares)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔒", title:"Connected-person losses are ring-fenced", body:"A loss on a disposal to a **connected person** can only be set against gains on disposals to **that same person**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎈", title:"Step 5: the annual exempt amount", body:"Deduct the **annual exempt amount (AEA)**: **£3,000** for 2026/27. Losses and the AEA can be set against the gains taxed at the **highest rate first**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"This year: gains £10,000, losses £8,000. Can she keep £5,000 of the loss to save the AEA?", body:"**No.** Current-year losses are used **in full**: £10,000 − £8,000 = £2,000, all covered by the £3,000 AEA (£1,000 of the AEA is wasted)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🪜", title:"Step 6: gains sit on top of income", body:"Gains are added **on top of the taxpayer's taxable income**. The rates (from **30 October 2024**) depend on how much basic rate band is left."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🔢", title:"The CGT rates", cols:["Gains falling...", "Rate"], rows:[["Within any **unused basic rate band** (£37,700)","**18%**"],["Above the basic rate band","**24%**"],["Qualifying for **BADR** (or investors' relief), disposals from 6 April 2026","**18%**"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"🕰️", title:"BADR rate history", body:"The BADR rate was **10%** before 6 April 2025, **14%** in 2025/26, and is **18%** for disposals from **6 April 2026**. BADR gains use up the basic rate band **first**, before other gains."},
   {t:"card", i:"📮", title:"Step 7: payment", body:"CGT is reported and paid through **self-assessment** by **31 January** after the end of the tax year of disposal. Gains on **UK residential property** must be reported and paid within **60 days** of completion."},
   {t:"case", i:"📊", title:"Example: Sam's shares", facts:"In 2026/27 Sam (taxable income £30,000) sells listed shares he bought for £20,000 for £70,000, paying £1,000 of fees on sale.", rule:"**Gain** = £70,000 − £1,000 − £20,000 = £49,000. Less AEA £3,000 = **£46,000**. Unused basic rate band £37,700 − £30,000 = £7,700 at 18% = **£1,386**. The other £38,300 at 24% = **£9,192**. **Total CGT £10,578**, due by **31 January 2028**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Sam sold in 2026/27. When must he pay?", body:"By **31 January 2028** (31 January after the end of the tax year of disposal)."},
   {t:"card", i:"💡", title:"Exam tip: the gift trap", body:"A **gift** is a disposal at **market value**, so the donor can owe CGT despite receiving nothing (unless **gift relief** applies). Transfers between **spouses** are **no gain/no loss**. Assets passing on **death** are **free of CGT** with an uplift to probate value."}
 ]},
 {h:"Business asset disposal relief (BADR)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🏆", title:"What BADR does", body:"**Business asset disposal relief (BADR)** (formerly entrepreneurs' relief, TCGA ss.169H-169S) taxes qualifying gains at a **reduced rate**: **18%** for disposals on or after **6 April 2026**."},
   {t:"card", i:"💰", title:"The lifetime limit", body:"BADR covers up to a **lifetime limit of £1 million** of gains."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎯", title:"Who really benefits now", body:"The BADR rate is now the same as the basic CGT rate, so its value is mainly for **higher and additional rate** taxpayers: **18% instead of 24%**."},
   {t:"card", i:"✍️", title:"BADR must be claimed", body:"It must be **claimed** by the **first anniversary of 31 January** following the tax year of disposal."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧍", title:"Sole traders and partners: the business", body:"A **material disposal** (one that qualifies) is a disposal of the **whole or part of a business**, including a **partner's interest** in the partnership business, that the individual has owned throughout the **2 years** ending with the disposal."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔚", title:"After the business stops", body:"Also qualifying: a disposal of **assets used in the business** when it **ceased**, if the business was owned for 2 years up to cessation and the disposal is within **3 years** after cessation."},
   {t:"card", i:"🌾", title:"Not one asset from a business that carries on", body:"Selling **individual assets** while the business **carries on** does not qualify, for example a farmer selling a field but continuing to farm (**McGregor v Adcock** 1977). It must be the business, or a distinct part of it, sold as a **going concern** (a working business)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏦", title:"Business assets only", body:"Only gains on assets **used for the business** qualify, not **investment assets**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A baker sells one oven but keeps baking. BADR?", body:"**No.** It's a sale of an individual asset while the business carries on, not a disposal of the business."},
   {t:"card", i:"📈", title:"Shareholders: shares in a personal company", body:"Shares (or securities) qualify if three conditions are met **throughout the 2 years** ending with the disposal (or with the company ceasing to trade, if the disposal is within 3 years after)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏭", title:"Condition 1: trading company", body:"The company is a **trading company** (or the holding company of a trading group)."},
   {t:"card", i:"5️⃣", title:"Condition 2: personal company", body:"The individual holds at least **5% of the ordinary share capital** and **5% of the voting rights**, **and** is entitled to at least 5% of the distributable profits and of the assets on a winding up (or would get 5% of the proceeds on a sale of the whole company)."},
   {t:"card", i:"👔", title:"Condition 3: officer or employee", body:"The individual is an **officer** (for example a director) **or employee** of the company or a group company. It **need not be full time**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⛔", title:"All conditions, all 2 years", body:"**All** conditions must be met **throughout** the 2 years. A director with **4.9%** of the shares, or a **50% shareholder** who is not an officer or employee, gets **no BADR**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Nina owns 40% of a trading company for 10 years but has never worked there. BADR?", body:"**No.** She isn't an **officer or employee**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏠", title:"Associated disposals", body:"An asset owned **personally** and **used in the business** of the individual's **partnership** or **personal company** (e.g. premises owned by a director and used by the company), sold **as part of** their **withdrawal** from the business, alongside a qualifying disposal of their partnership interest or of at least **5%** of the company's shares."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗝️", title:"Associated disposals: conditions", body:"The asset must have been used in the business for the **2 years** before, and (for assets acquired on or after 13 June 2016) owned by the individual for at least **3 years**. Relief is **restricted** if the individual charged the business **rent**."},
   {t:"card", i:"💡", title:"Exam tip: find the missing condition", body:"Shares: **trading company**, **5% of shares and votes** (plus economic interest), **officer or employee**, all for **2 years**. Sole traders: is it a disposal of the **business** (not just an asset), owned **2 years**?"},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"What's the BADR lifetime limit?", body:"**£1 million** of gains."}
 ]},
 {h:"Rollover relief", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🔄", title:"Sell, reinvest, roll the gain over", body:"A trader (sole trader, partner or company) **sells** a **qualifying business asset** at a gain and **reinvests the proceeds** in **another qualifying business asset** (TCGA ss.152-159). The gain is **rolled over** into the new asset."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏸️", title:"How the deferral works", body:"The gain is **deducted from the base cost** of the new asset, so **no tax is paid now**. The gain comes back into charge when the **new asset is sold** (unless rolled over again)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏗️", title:"Qualifying assets", body:"**Land and buildings** occupied and used for the trade; **fixed plant and machinery** (not movable); **goodwill** (for individuals; a company's goodwill is under the intangible assets regime instead). Both old and new must be **used in the trade**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔀", title:"Any qualifying class will do", body:"Old and new assets must both be in a qualifying class, but **not necessarily the same class**: sell a building, buy fixed machinery, and it still works."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏱️", title:"The time window", body:"The new asset must be acquired within **1 year before** to **3 years after** the disposal of the old asset."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Sold the workshop in March 2026, bought new premises in May 2030. Rollover?", body:"**No.** That's more than **3 years after** the disposal."},
   {t:"card", i:"✂️", title:"Partial reinvestment", body:"If not all the **proceeds** are reinvested, the amount **not reinvested** (up to the whole gain) is **taxed now**. Only the rest of the gain is rolled over."},
   {t:"case", i:"🔧", title:"Example: Kate's workshop", facts:"Kate, a sole trader, sells her workshop for £400,000 (gain £150,000) and buys a new workshop for £350,000 a year later.", rule:"£50,000 of proceeds is not reinvested, so **£50,000 of the gain is taxed now**. The remaining **£100,000 is rolled over**, reducing the new workshop's base cost to **£250,000**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏳", title:"Depreciating assets: held over, not rolled", body:"A **depreciating asset** has a predictable life of **60 years or less** (e.g. fixed plant and machinery, or a lease of 60 years or less). The gain is **held over** (frozen), not deducted from the new asset's cost."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔟", title:"When a held-over gain comes back", body:"On the **earliest** of: the new asset being sold, it ceasing to be used in the trade, or **10 years** after it was acquired (unless a non-depreciating asset is bought in the meantime)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏢", title:"Assets used by your personal company", body:"Assets owned by an individual and used by their **personal company** (where they have **5% of the voting rights**) also qualify."},
   {t:"card", i:"✍️", title:"Rollover must be claimed", body:"It must be **claimed**, within **4 years** of the end of the tax year."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Kate had reinvested all £400,000. What would be taxed now?", body:"**Nothing.** The whole £150,000 gain would be rolled over into the new workshop."}
 ]},
 {h:"Gift (holdover) relief", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🎁", title:"Giving away business assets", body:"When an individual **gives away** (or sells at an **undervalue**, i.e. for less than it's worth) **business assets**, they would normally pay CGT on the market value."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏸️", title:"How gift relief works", body:"Gift relief (TCGA s.165) lets the gain be **held over**: the donor pays nothing now, and the **recipient's base cost is reduced** by the held-over gain. The recipient pays the tax when they later sell."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏪", title:"Qualifying assets (1): trading assets", body:"Assets used in a **trade** carried on by the **donor**, or by the donor's **personal company** (where the donor has at least **5% of the voting rights**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📜", title:"Qualifying assets (2): shares", body:"Shares in an **unquoted trading company** (any size of holding), or shares in a **quoted** trading company if it is the donor's **personal company** (5% of the votes)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🤝", title:"The claim: a joint election", body:"Gift relief is claimed by a **joint election** by the **donor and the recipient** (except where the recipient is a trust)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Dad gives his son 2% of an unquoted trading company. Gift relief?", body:"**Yes, if they jointly elect.** For **unquoted** trading company shares, any size of holding qualifies."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏷️", title:"Sale at an undervalue", body:"If the donor receives some payment, the **excess of the actual price over the donor's allowable costs** (the actual gain made) is **taxed now**. Only the rest of the gain is held over."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚧", title:"Restrictions", body:"If the company holds **non-business (investment) chargeable assets**, relief on its shares is **restricted proportionately**. Gift relief is not available on a transfer of shares **to a company**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏆", title:"BADR on the taxable part", body:"**BADR** can be claimed on any part of the gain that is chargeable immediately."},
   {t:"case", i:"👨‍👧", title:"Example: Raj's gift to his daughter", facts:"Raj gives his daughter his 30% shareholding in an unquoted trading company. The shares cost him £10,000 and are worth £210,000.", rule:"With a joint s.165 election, **Raj pays no CGT**. His daughter's base cost is £210,000 − £200,000 = **£10,000**. If she later sells for £250,000, her gain is **£240,000**. (Inheritance tax BPR may also be relevant to the gift.)"},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Under gift relief, who pays the held-over tax, and when?", body:"The **recipient**, when they later **sell** the asset (their base cost has been reduced by the held-over gain)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Incorporation relief", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🏗️", title:"The problem when you incorporate", body:"When a **sole trader** or **partnership** transfers its business to a **company**, it disposes of all its business assets (goodwill, premises, etc.) to the company at **market value** (the company is a connected person). That could produce large gains."},
   {t:"card", i:"✅", title:"The conditions (TCGA s.162)", body:"The business must be transferred **as a going concern**, with **all its assets** (other than **cash**, which may be kept), wholly or partly in exchange for **shares** issued by the company."},
   {t:"card", i:"✍️", title:"Incorporation relief must now be claimed", body:"For transfers on or after **6 April 2026**, incorporation relief is **no longer automatic**: it must be **claimed** in the self-assessment tax return, by the **first anniversary of 31 January** after the tax year of the transfer. A taxpayer who would rather pay tax now (for example at the BADR rate) simply does not claim. Before 6 April 2026 the relief was automatic, with an election to opt out."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏸️", title:"The effect", body:"The gain is **deducted from the base cost of the shares** received, so it is deferred until the **shares are sold**."},
   {t:"card", i:"➗", title:"Partly paid in cash or loan account", body:"If part of the consideration is **cash** or a **loan account** (the company owing the trader money), only the share part of the gain is deferred: **deferred gain = gain × (value of shares ÷ total consideration)**. The rest is taxed now (possibly with BADR)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Gain £100,000; she takes £300,000 of shares and £100,000 cash. How much is deferred?", body:"**£75,000** (£100,000 × £300,000 ÷ £400,000). The other **£25,000** is taxed now."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏚️", title:"Keeping the premises breaks s.162", body:"If the owner **keeps an asset** (e.g. keeps the business premises personally and lets the company use them), s.162 is **not available** because not all assets are transferred."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔁", title:"The alternative: gift relief", body:"The usual alternative is **gift relief (s.165)**, by selling the assets to the company at an **undervalue**."},
   {t:"card", i:"💡", title:"Exam tip: incorporation checklist", body:"Do **all assets** (except cash) go to the company **for shares**? If the client wants to **keep the premises**, the answer is **s.165 gift relief** (not s.162). If they take part **cash or loan account**, part of the gain is taxed now."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A sole trader incorporates but keeps the shop premises. s.162?", body:"**No.** Not all the assets are transferred. Use **s.165 gift relief** instead."}
 ]},
 {h:"All the reliefs side by side", cards:[
   {t:"compare", i:"🧾", title:"Who and what", cols:["Relief", "Who / what"], rows:[["🏆 **BADR**","Sole trader or partner selling the business; shareholder selling personal company shares; associated disposals"],["🔄 **Rollover** (s.152)","Trader selling and replacing business assets"],["🎁 **Gift relief** (s.165)","Individual giving away (or selling at an undervalue) business assets or unquoted trading company shares"],["🏗️ **Incorporation** (s.162)","Sole trader or partners transferring the business to a company"]]},
   {t:"compare", i:"⚙️", title:"Effect", cols:["Relief", "Effect"], rows:[["🏆 BADR","**Lower rate** (18% from 6 April 2026) on gains up to **£1m** lifetime"],["🔄 Rollover","**Deferral**: gain deducted from the new asset's cost"],["🎁 Gift relief","**Deferral**: recipient's base cost reduced"],["🏗️ Incorporation","**Deferral**: gain deducted from the shares' base cost"]]},
   {t:"compare", i:"🔑", title:"Key conditions", cols:["Relief", "Key conditions"], rows:[["🏆 BADR","Business or partnership share owned **2 years**; shares: **trading**, **5%** shares and votes, **officer or employee**, for **2 years**"],["🔄 Rollover","Land and buildings, fixed plant and machinery, goodwill; reinvest **1 year before to 3 years after**; proceeds not reinvested taxed"],["🎁 Gift relief","**Joint election**; any actual gain on an undervalue sale taxed now"],["🏗️ Incorporation","Going concern, **all assets** (cash may be kept), for **shares**; must be **claimed** (from 6 April 2026)"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"💼", title:"Investors' relief (outline)", body:"A similar **18%** rate for **external investors** in unlisted trading companies who hold **newly subscribed** shares for **3 years**, with a separate **£1 million** lifetime limit."},
   {t:"card", i:"🌱", title:"EIS and SEIS (outline)", body:"Investing in new shares of small unquoted trading companies under the **Enterprise Investment Scheme (EIS)** or **Seed Enterprise Investment Scheme (SEIS)** can give income tax relief, **CGT exemption** on the shares, and **EIS deferral relief** for gains reinvested."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗺️", title:"Your 5-step route", body:"**1** Chargeable disposal of a chargeable asset? → **2** Calculate the gain → **3** Deferral relief (rollover, gift, incorporation)? → **4** BADR? → **5** Losses, AEA and rate. Exam questions usually ask **which relief** fits the facts, often by spotting the **one missing condition**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Which relief cuts the rate rather than deferring the gain?", body:"**BADR.** Rollover, gift and incorporation relief all **defer** the gain."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Source: proposals/rooms/learn-blp-corporate-insolvency.js */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["blp/corporate-insolvency"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🍮", title:"What 'insolvent' means", body:"A company is **insolvent** when it cannot pay its debts. Because shareholders have **limited liability** (they can lose only what they put in), the company's **creditors** (people it owes money to) bear the loss."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔄", title:"The focus shifts to creditors", body:"Once a company is insolvent, the law shifts the focus to protecting the **creditors as a group**, rather than the shareholders."},
   {t:"card", i:"📕", title:"The Insolvency Act 1986", body:"The **Insolvency Act 1986 (IA 1986)** gives a menu of procedures. Some aim to **rescue** the company or its business; others **end** it."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🗂️", title:"The menu of procedures", cols:["Aim", "Procedures"], rows:[["🩹 Rescue","**Part A1 moratorium**, **company voluntary arrangement (CVA)**, **administration**"],["🏦 One secured lender enforces","**Receivership** (in practice, **fixed charge / LPA receivership**)"],["🪦 End the company","**Liquidation** (winding up): **voluntary** (members' or creditors') or **compulsory** (by court order)"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"🧑‍⚕️", title:"Insolvency practitioners", body:"Most procedures are run by a licensed **insolvency practitioner (IP)**: an accountant or solicitor licensed to act as nominee, supervisor, administrator, liquidator or trustee in bankruptcy."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Which three procedures aim to rescue the company?", body:"The **Part A1 moratorium**, a **CVA** and **administration**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗺️", title:"Covered elsewhere", body:"Reversing transactions, directors' personal liability and the order of payment are in **Clawback, directors' liability and order of priority**. Individuals and partners are in **Personal insolvency**."}
 ]},
 {h:"When is a company unable to pay its debts?", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"💸", title:"Test 1: cash flow (s.123(1)(e))", body:"The company is **unable to pay its debts as they fall due**. This is the usual test."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚖️", title:"Test 2: balance sheet (s.123(2))", body:"The **value of its assets is less than the amount of its liabilities**, **taking into account contingent and prospective liabilities** (debts that may arise, or that are due in the future)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛑", title:"Balance sheet: not a mechanical test", body:"The Supreme Court (**BNY Corporate Trustee v Eurosail**, 2013) held this is not a mechanical comparison of the balance sheet. The court decides, on the balance of probabilities and looking at all the facts, whether the company's assets are not enough to meet all its liabilities, giving less weight to debts not due until far in the future. (The Supreme Court rejected the Court of Appeal's 'point of no return' wording as a test.)"},
   {t:"card", i:"📨", title:"Deemed inability 1: statutory demand", body:"A creditor owed **more than £750** serves a **statutory demand** (a demand in the prescribed form, left at the registered office). If the company **neglects to pay, secure or compound** the debt to the creditor's reasonable satisfaction for **3 weeks**, it is deemed unable to pay its debts (s.123(1)(a))."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔨", title:"Deemed inability 2: unsatisfied execution", body:"**Execution** of a judgment against the company (enforcing a court judgment, e.g. by seizing goods) is returned **unsatisfied** in whole or part (s.123(1)(b))."},
   {t:"case", i:"🪁", title:"Example: Kite Ltd", facts:"Kite Ltd owes a supplier £2,000. The supplier serves a statutory demand on 1 May. Kite pays nothing and does not dispute the debt.", rule:"**From 22 May (after 3 weeks) Kite is deemed unable to pay its debts**, and the supplier may present a winding-up petition."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Can a company be balance sheet solvent but cash flow insolvent?", body:"**Yes.** For example, it owns valuable property but has no cash to pay bills due now (or the reverse). **Either test is enough** for s.123."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Must a creditor serve a statutory demand before petitioning?", body:"**No.** A creditor can **prove** cash-flow insolvency directly."},
   {t:"card", i:"🙅", title:"Disputed debts: no petition", body:"A winding-up petition must not be used to collect a debt that the company **genuinely disputes on substantial grounds**. The court treats such a petition as an **abuse of process**: it will **restrain** it (an injunction stopping it being presented or advertised) or **dismiss** it, often with costs against the creditor. A company cannot apply to set aside a statutory demand (only individuals can), so seeking that injunction is its remedy."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"The company says the goods were faulty and has a real defence. Can the supplier petition?", body:"**No.** The debt is **genuinely disputed on substantial grounds**, so the court would restrain or dismiss the petition. The supplier should sue for the debt in the ordinary way instead."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎯", title:"Why the tests matter", body:"They are used for: a creditor's **winding-up petition**; the **administration** and **moratorium** conditions; **clawback** claims (the company must usually have been unable to pay its debts at the time); and **wrongful trading**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Directors' first steps and the Part A1 moratorium", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"📞", title:"Directors: get advice early", body:"Directors should take advice from an **IP** promptly when trouble looms."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚠️", title:"No reasonable prospect: minimise loss", body:"Once directors know or ought to conclude there is **no reasonable prospect** of avoiding insolvent liquidation or administration, they must take **every step to minimise loss to creditors**, or risk **wrongful trading** liability (IA 1986 s.214)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧭", title:"Duty shifts toward creditors", body:"As insolvency approaches, the directors' **s.172 duty** (to promote the success of the company) shifts toward the **creditors' interests** (**BTI v Sequana**, 2022)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛤️", title:"The directors' options", body:"Informal negotiation with creditors (no statutory protection), a **moratorium**, a **CVA**, **administration**, or a **creditors' voluntary liquidation** if the business cannot be saved."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧊", title:"Part A1 moratorium: what it is", body:"A **standalone moratorium** (a freeze on creditor action) under IA 1986 Part A1. It gives a struggling but viable company **breathing space** while the **directors stay in control**, overseen by a **monitor** (an IP)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📝", title:"Moratorium: the conditions", body:"The directors file documents at court stating the company **is, or is likely to become, unable to pay its debts**. The proposed monitor states that the company is **eligible** and that it is **likely** a moratorium would result in the **rescue of the company as a going concern**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚫", title:"Moratorium: excluded companies", body:"Certain companies cannot use it, e.g. a company subject to a CVA, administration or moratorium in the previous **12 months**, or a company in liquidation."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏱️", title:"Moratorium: how long", body:"An initial **20 business days**. The directors can extend it for a further **20 business days** without creditor consent, and up to **1 year** in total with creditors' consent (or longer by court order)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛡️", title:"Moratorium: what creditors can't do", body:"Without court permission, creditors cannot enforce security, repossess goods, forfeit a lease by peaceable re-entry (the landlord retaking the premises), start or continue legal proceedings, or present a winding-up petition (except by the directors). A floating charge holder cannot **crystallise** its charge (turn it into a fixed charge) or **appoint an administrator** while the moratorium runs."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏖️", title:"Moratorium: payment holiday", body:"There is a **payment holiday** for most **pre-moratorium debts**. But the company must pay debts falling due during the moratorium (e.g. rent, wages, supplies) and certain pre-moratorium debts (e.g. wages, financial services debts)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚚", title:"Suppliers must keep supplying (s.233B)", body:"Once a company enters a moratorium, CVA, administration or liquidation, a clause letting a supplier of goods or services **end the contract or stop supplying because of that procedure** has no effect (IA 1986 **s.233B**, added by the Corporate Insolvency and Governance Act 2020). The supplier also cannot demand payment of old debts as a condition of supplying. It may end the contract only with the consent of the office-holder (or the company), or with the court's permission on grounds of **hardship**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🌉", title:"Moratorium: ending and bridging", body:"The monitor must end it if the company can no longer be rescued. It is often used as a **bridge to a CVA** or a restructuring."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Who runs the company during a Part A1 moratorium?", body:"The **directors**. The **monitor** (an IP) only oversees."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"How long can directors extend the moratorium without creditor consent?", body:"By a further **20 business days** (so 40 in total). Beyond that, up to **1 year** in total, needs creditors' consent (or a court order)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Company voluntary arrangement (CVA)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🤝", title:"CVA: what it is", body:"A **binding agreement between the company and its creditors** (IA 1986 Part I) to pay its debts in part, or over a longer period, or both, e.g. 'creditors will receive **40p in the £ over 3 years**', so the company can keep trading."},
   {t:"card", i:"🙋", title:"CVA: who proposes", body:"The **directors** (or an administrator or liquidator if the company is already in that procedure)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔎", title:"CVA step 1: the nominee", body:"An IP acts as **nominee**. They assess the proposal and report (to the court, if the directors proposed it) on whether it has a reasonable prospect of being approved and implemented, and whether creditors and members should consider it."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗳️", title:"CVA step 2: the decisions", body:"The **creditors** decide by a **qualifying decision procedure** (e.g. electronic voting or a virtual meeting). The **members** (shareholders) decide at a meeting."},
   {t:"card", i:"👷", title:"CVA step 3: the supervisor", body:"Once approved, the nominee becomes the **supervisor** and implements the CVA."},
   {t:"card", i:"📊", title:"CVA: creditor threshold", body:"At least **75% in value** of the creditors **voting** must approve. But it is **not approved** if more than **50% in value** of the **unconnected creditors** voting vote **against** it (Insolvency (England and Wales) Rules 2016 r.15.34)."},
   {t:"card", i:"👥", title:"CVA: members and disagreement", body:"**Members**: more than **50% in value** of members voting. If creditors and members disagree, the **creditors' decision prevails** (s.4A), subject to a member's right to apply to court."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔗", title:"CVA: who is bound", body:"It binds **every unsecured creditor entitled to vote**, whether or not they voted, voted against, or even received notice (s.5)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔒", title:"CVA: the limits", body:"A CVA **cannot affect the rights of a secured creditor** to enforce its security, or alter the **priority** of **preferential debts** (debts the law pays first, e.g. some employee claims), **without that creditor's consent** (s.4(3)-(4))."},
   {t:"card", i:"🌬️", title:"CVA: no automatic moratorium", body:"The company is exposed to creditor action while the CVA is being proposed, unless it obtains a **Part A1 moratorium** or is already in administration."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏳", title:"CVA: challenge within 28 days", body:"A creditor or member may apply to court within **28 days** of the report of the decision, on grounds of **unfair prejudice** or a **material irregularity** (s.6)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎛️", title:"CVA: who's in control", body:"The **directors remain in control** of management throughout. If the company fails to comply, the supervisor can petition for winding up or administration."},
   {t:"case", i:"🧮", title:"Example: counting the CVA vote", facts:"Creditors voting are owed £100,000. £80,000 vote for (including £40,000 from a director's connected company). Unconnected creditors voting are owed £60,000, and £20,000 of that votes against.", rule:"**Approved by creditors.** 80% in value voted for (at least 75%), and only a third of unconnected value voted against (not more than 50%)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Does a CVA bind the bank holding a fixed charge?", body:"**No**, not without the bank's consent. A CVA cannot affect a **secured creditor's** right to enforce, or the priority of **preferential** debts."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"An unsecured creditor voted against an approved CVA. Bound?", body:"**Yes.** It binds every unsecured creditor entitled to vote, even those who voted against or never got notice."}
 ]},
 {h:"Administration", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🧑‍⚕️", title:"Administration: what it is", body:"An **administrator** (an IP) takes control of the company (IA 1986 **Schedule B1**). They act in the interests of the **creditors as a whole** and are an **officer of the court**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🥇", title:"Purpose (a): rescue the company", body:"The administrator's first objective is **rescuing the company as a going concern** (para.3)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🥈", title:"Purpose (b): better result for creditors", body:"Only if (a) is **not reasonably practicable**: **achieving a better result for the company's creditors as a whole** than if the company were wound up (without first being in administration), e.g. selling the business as a going concern."},
   {t:"card", i:"🥉", title:"Purpose (c): pay secured/preferential creditors", body:"Only if neither (a) nor (b) is reasonably practicable: **realising property to make a distribution to one or more secured or preferential creditors**, without unnecessarily harming the creditors as a whole."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Can the administrator go straight to selling the business?", body:"Only if **rescuing the company** is **not reasonably practicable**. The purposes are a **strict order**: (a) → (b) → (c)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏛️", title:"Appointment route 1: court order", body:"Applicants: the **company**, its **directors**, one or more **creditors** (including a floating charge holder), or a CVA supervisor (paras 10-13). The court must be satisfied the company **is or is likely to become unable to pay its debts** and the order is **reasonably likely to achieve the purpose**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏦", title:"Appointment route 2: QFCH out of court", body:"A **qualifying floating charge holder (QFCH)** holds a floating charge (or charges) over the **whole or substantially the whole** of the company's property (para.14). A **floating charge** is security over a changing pool of assets, like stock."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔑", title:"QFCH route: conditions", body:"The charge must be **enforceable** (e.g. an event of default has occurred). The company **need not be shown to be insolvent**. The QFCH must give **2 business days'** notice to any prior QFCH."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏢", title:"Appointment route 3: company or directors out of court", body:"The **company** (by resolution) or its **directors** (para.22). The company must be, or be likely to become, unable to pay its debts. They must give **5 business days' notice of intention** to any **QFCH**, who may appoint its **own choice** of administrator instead."},
   {t:"card", i:"⛔", title:"Company/directors route: when it's barred", body:"Not available if a winding-up petition or administration application is pending, or an administrative receiver is in office (para.25), or within **12 months** after an earlier administration started by the company or directors ended (para.23)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📅", title:"Administrative receivership: largely abolished", body:"**Administrative receivership** (a receiver appointed by a floating charge holder over the whole business) is largely abolished for floating charges created on or after **15 September 2003** (s.72A). Such lenders now appoint an **administrator** instead."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Directors want to appoint an administrator; the bank is a QFCH. What must they do?", body:"Give the bank **5 business days' notice of intention**. The bank may appoint its **own choice** of administrator instead."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧊", title:"The administration moratorium: no winding up", body:"While the company is in administration (and under an **interim moratorium** once an application is made or a notice of intention filed), **no resolution or order** to wind up the company may be passed or made (except limited public interest cases) (paras 42-44)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛡️", title:"The administration moratorium: no enforcement", body:"No step to **enforce security**, no **repossession** of goods under hire-purchase or retention of title, no **forfeiture** of a lease by peaceable re-entry, and no **legal process** (proceedings, execution), **without the administrator's consent or the court's permission**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🕹️", title:"The administrator takes control", body:"The administrator **takes control** of the company's property and **manages** its affairs. Directors' management powers can only be used with the administrator's consent, and the administrator can remove and appoint directors (paras 59-69)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📬", title:"Proposals within 8 weeks", body:"The administrator sends **proposals** for achieving the purpose to creditors (and Companies House) as soon as reasonably practicable and within **8 weeks** (para.49). Creditors decide whether to approve them (normally within **10 weeks**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔒", title:"Proposals: secured and preferential creditors protected", body:"The administrator's proposals **cannot affect a secured creditor's right to enforce its security**, or pay **preferential creditors** out of their priority order, **without that creditor's consent** (para.73). This mirrors the CVA limit."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🔐", title:"Selling charged property", cols:["Charge", "Can the administrator sell it?", "Who gets the money?"], rows:[["🌊 Floating (para.70)","Yes, **as if it were not charged**","Charge holder keeps the **same priority** over the proceeds"],["📌 Fixed (para.71)","Only with the **court's permission** (or charge holder's consent)","**Net proceeds** to the fixed charge holder"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"👩‍🍳", title:"Employees in administration", body:"Employment contracts continue. The administrator has **14 days** to decide whether to **adopt** them. Wages under adopted contracts then have **super-priority** as expenses."},
   {t:"card", i:"⌛", title:"Administration ends after 1 year", body:"It **ends automatically after 1 year**, unless extended by the **court**, or by the **creditors** once, for up to 1 year (para.76)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚪", title:"Exit routes", body:"Return of the company to the directors (rescue), a **CVA**, a **creditors' voluntary liquidation** (if there are funds for unsecured creditors), **compulsory liquidation**, or **dissolution** (if nothing is left)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📦", title:"Pre-pack administration", body:"A **pre-pack** is where the sale of the business and assets is **negotiated before** the administrator is appointed and **completed immediately** (or shortly) after. It preserves value and jobs, but unsecured creditors have no say."},
   {t:"card", i:"👪", title:"Pre-pack to a connected person", body:"If the buyer is a **connected person** (e.g. existing directors or shareholders) and the sale is of all or a substantial part of the business within the first **8 weeks**, the administrator must first get **creditors' approval** or a **qualifying report** from an **independent evaluator** (2021 Regulations)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Can the administrator sell stock covered by a floating charge without asking the court?", body:"**Yes**, as if it were not charged (para.70). The floating charge holder keeps the same priority over the proceeds. A **fixed** charge asset needs the court's permission or the holder's consent."}
 ]},
 {h:"Receivership: fixed charge and LPA receivers", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"📌", title:"Who appoints a receiver", body:"A lender holding a **fixed charge** (security over a particular asset, or a mortgage), typically over **land**, can appoint a **receiver** over **that asset** if the borrower defaults."},
   {t:"card", i:"📜", title:"LPA receiver", body:"The power comes from the charge document or the **Law of Property Act 1925**, so the receiver is often called an **LPA receiver**."},
   {t:"card", i:"💰", title:"The receiver's job", body:"To **collect income** from the asset (e.g. rents) and/or **sell** it, and use the proceeds to pay the **appointing lender**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎭", title:"Whose side is the receiver on?", body:"Mainly the **lender's**. But the receiver is deemed the **agent of the company**, so the lender is not liable for the receiver's acts."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏷️", title:"Duty when selling", body:"A receiver must act in **good faith** and take **reasonable care to obtain the true market value**. That duty is owed to the company and later lenders."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚦", title:"No moratorium", body:"Other creditors can still take action. The company continues to exist and trade (minus the asset), and the directors stay in office for the rest of the business."},
   {t:"card", i:"➕", title:"Any surplus", body:"Any **surplus** after paying the lender goes to any later chargee (lender with a later charge) or to the company."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Does appointing an LPA receiver stop other creditors suing?", body:"**No.** There is **no moratorium** in receivership."}
 ]},
 {h:"Liquidation (winding up)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🫠", title:"Liquidation ends the company", body:"A **liquidator** (an IP) collects and **sells** (realises) the assets, investigates the directors' conduct and past transactions, **distributes** the proceeds to creditors in the **statutory order** (any surplus to shareholders), and the company is **dissolved** (removed from the register; it ceases to exist)."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🗂️", title:"Three kinds of liquidation", cols:["Type", "Company is", "Started by"], rows:[["**Members' voluntary (MVL)**","✅ Solvent","Members' special resolution + declaration of solvency"],["**Creditors' voluntary (CVL)**","❌ Insolvent","Members' special resolution"],["**Compulsory**","Usually insolvent","**Court order** on a petition"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"🏖️", title:"MVL: closing a solvent company", body:"A **members' voluntary liquidation (MVL)** closes a **solvent** company (e.g. the owners are retiring) and returns surplus assets to the shareholders."},
   {t:"card", i:"✍️", title:"MVL: declaration of solvency", body:"The **directors** (or a majority) make a **statutory declaration** (s.89) that after full inquiry they believe the company will **pay its debts in full, with interest, within a stated period not exceeding 12 months** from the start of the winding up."},
   {t:"card", i:"📆", title:"MVL: timing and offence", body:"The declaration must be made within the **5 weeks before** the resolution and include a statement of assets and liabilities. Making it without reasonable grounds is an **offence**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📣", title:"MVL: resolution and liquidator", body:"Members pass a **special resolution** (75% of votes) to wind up voluntarily (s.84); file within 15 days and advertise in the **Gazette** (the official public notices paper) within 14 days. The **members** appoint the liquidator; creditors have no role, as they expect to be paid in full."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔁", title:"MVL converts to CVL", body:"If the liquidator forms the opinion that the company **cannot pay its debts in full** within the stated period, the MVL **converts into a CVL** (ss.95-96)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Directors declared solvency 7 weeks before the resolution. Valid MVL?", body:"**No.** The declaration must be made within the **5 weeks before** the special resolution."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛑", title:"CVL: closing an insolvent company", body:"A **creditors' voluntary liquidation (CVL)** is used where an **insolvent** company's directors and shareholders decide to wind it up (e.g. after advice it can't be saved), or as an exit from administration."},
   {t:"card", i:"📋", title:"CVL: how it starts", body:"A members' **special resolution** (s.84). No declaration of solvency is possible. The winding up starts on the date of the **resolution**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗳️", title:"CVL: creditors choose the liquidator", body:"Directors prepare a **statement of affairs** (assets, liabilities, creditors). Creditors choose a liquidator by a **decision procedure** (deemed consent or virtual meeting) held within **14 days** of the resolution (s.100). Members may nominate one, but the **creditors' choice prevails**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚖️", title:"Compulsory liquidation: grounds", body:"Main grounds (s.122(1)): the company is **unable to pay its debts** (s.122(1)(f), using the s.123 tests), or it is **just and equitable** to wind it up (s.122(1)(g); see **Shareholders' rights**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🙋", title:"Who may petition (s.124)", body:"A **creditor** (the commonest), the **company**, the **directors**, a **contributory** (shareholder), a CVA supervisor, an administrator, or the Secretary of State (public interest)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📰", title:"Compulsory: procedure", body:"The petition is presented to court, served on the company and **advertised** in the Gazette (normally at least 7 business days before the hearing). The court hears it and may make a **winding-up order**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏮️", title:"Starts at the petition (s.129)", body:"A compulsory winding up is treated as starting at the **time the petition was presented**, not the date of the order. So clawback time limits run back from the **petition** date."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚫", title:"Void dispositions (s.127)", body:"Any disposition of the company's property (including bank payments) and any share transfer made **after the petition is presented** is **void** unless the court orders otherwise (a **validation order**). Banks often freeze the account once a petition is advertised."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏛️", title:"The Official Receiver", body:"On the order, the **Official Receiver** (a civil servant in the Insolvency Service and officer of the court) becomes liquidator, and may later be replaced by an IP chosen by the creditors. Proceedings against the company are **stayed** and need court permission (s.130)."},
   {t:"case", i:"🐦", title:"Example: Lark Ltd", facts:"A creditor presents a winding-up petition against Lark Ltd on 1 March; the order is made on 15 April. On 20 March Lark paid a supplier £10,000.", rule:"**The payment is void under s.127** (made after the petition) unless the court validates it, and the liquidator can recover it."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Petition 1 March, order 15 April. When did the winding up start?", body:"**1 March**, when the petition was presented (s.129)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧑‍💼", title:"Effect on directors", body:"Their powers **cease**: automatically in a compulsory liquidation; in a voluntary liquidation, except so far as the liquidator or creditors sanction. They must cooperate with the liquidator."},
   {t:"card", i:"👷", title:"Effect on employees", body:"A **compulsory winding-up order** operates as notice of **dismissal**. In a voluntary liquidation, employment usually ends when the liquidator ceases the business."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧰", title:"The liquidator's powers (Sch.4)", body:"Sell assets, bring or defend proceedings, carry on the business so far as necessary for a beneficial winding up, and bring **clawback** and **director liability** claims."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗑️", title:"Disclaiming onerous property (s.178)", body:"The liquidator can **disclaim onerous property**: unprofitable contracts or property, such as a lease with heavy rent, or unsaleable property."},
   {t:"card", i:"🕵️", title:"Investigating the directors", body:"The liquidator (or administrator) must report on the **conduct of every director** in the previous **3 years** to the Secretary of State, which can lead to **disqualification** proceedings."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏁", title:"Distribution and dissolution", body:"Proceeds are distributed in the statutory **order of priority** (see **Clawback and priority**). The company is dissolved **3 months** after the liquidator's final account (voluntary) or notice of completion (compulsory) is registered at Companies House."}
 ]},
 {h:"Comparing and choosing a procedure", cards:[
   {t:"compare", i:"🎛️", title:"Who is in control?", cols:["Procedure", "In control"], rows:[["Part A1 moratorium","**Directors** (monitor oversees)"],["CVA","**Directors** (supervisor oversees the CVA)"],["Administration","**Administrator**"],["Fixed charge / LPA receivership","Receiver over the **charged asset** only"],["MVL","Liquidator chosen by **members**"],["CVL","Liquidator chosen by **creditors**"],["Compulsory liquidation","**Official Receiver**, then liquidator"]]},
   {t:"compare", i:"🧊", title:"Is there a moratorium?", cols:["Procedure", "Moratorium?"], rows:[["Part A1 moratorium","✅ Yes"],["CVA","❌ No (unless combined with one)"],["Administration","✅ Yes"],["Receivership","❌ No"],["MVL","❌ No"],["CVL","❌ No automatic stay"],["Compulsory liquidation","Proceedings **stayed** (s.130)"]]},
   {t:"compare", i:"🏁", title:"Outcome", cols:["Procedure", "Outcome"], rows:[["Part A1 moratorium","Breathing space, then rescue"],["CVA","Company survives, debts compromised"],["Administration","Rescue, or better result than liquidation"],["Receivership","Lender repaid from the asset"],["MVL","Dissolved, surplus to members"],["CVL / compulsory","Company dissolved"]]},
   {t:"compare", i:"🎯", title:"Typical answers", cols:["Facts", "Procedure"], rows:[["Viable business, temporary cash crisis, directors want to stay in control","**Part A1 moratorium** and/or **CVA**"],["Needs protection while restructured or **sold as a going concern** (maybe a pre-pack)","**Administration**"],["Lender with a fixed charge over a property wants repaying from it","**Fixed charge (LPA) receiver**"],["QFCH wants control of the whole process","**Administrator out of court**"],["Owners of a **solvent** company want to retire","**MVL**"],["Owners of an **insolvent** company that can't be saved","**CVL**"],["**Unpaid creditor** wants to force the issue","Statutory demand (optional), then **winding-up petition**"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"‼️", title:"Watch out: CVA traps", body:"A **CVA does not bind secured or preferential creditors** without their consent, and has **no automatic moratorium**."},
   {t:"card", i:"‼️", title:"Watch out: MVL traps", body:"An **MVL** needs a **declaration of solvency** made within the **5 weeks before** the special resolution. If debts won't be paid within the stated period (maximum **12 months**), it converts to a **CVL**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A lender with a floating charge over all assets (created 2019) wants control. What does it do?", body:"Appoints an **administrator out of court** as a **QFCH**. It cannot appoint an administrative receiver (charge created after 15 September 2003)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗺️", title:"Your route through a question", body:"**1** Unable to pay debts? → **2** Rescue with directors in control (moratorium/CVA)? → **3** Administration? → **4** One secured lender wants repaying (receiver)? → **5** Insolvent and can't be saved (CVL/compulsory)? → **6** Solvent closure (MVL) → **7** Effects of liquidation."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Source: proposals/rooms/learn-blp-corporation-tax.js */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["blp/corporation-tax"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🏢", title:"What corporation tax is", body:"**Corporation tax** is the tax a **company** pays on its **profits**. That means both its **income profits** (trading profits, rent, interest received) and its **chargeable gains** (gains on selling capital assets)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚫", title:"Companies never pay income tax or CGT", body:"A company does **not** pay income tax or capital gains tax (CGT). Its income profits and its gains all go into **one** corporation tax bill."},
   {t:"card", i:"🤝", title:"Partnerships and LLPs: no corporation tax", body:"Partnerships and **limited liability partnerships (LLPs)** do not pay corporation tax. They are **tax transparent**: each partner or member pays income tax on their share of the profits (see **Income tax**)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"An LLP makes a £200,000 profit. Does the LLP pay corporation tax on it?", body:"**No.** An LLP is tax transparent. Each **member** pays income tax on their own share."},
   {t:"card", i:"📚", title:"The main statutes", body:"The **Corporation Tax Act 2009 (CTA 2009)** covers computing profits. The **Corporation Tax Act 2010 (CTA 2010)** covers rates, losses, groups and close companies. Gains use the **Taxation of Chargeable Gains Act 1992 (TCGA 1992)**, and capital allowances use the **Capital Allowances Act 2001**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗺️", title:"What SQE1 tests", body:"The **basis of charge** (income profits, chargeable gains, allowable deductions, main reliefs and exemptions), the **charge to tax** (calculation, payment and collection), the **tax treatment of distributions** to shareholders, and an **outline of anti-avoidance** rules. The exam gives you the rates where needed. The figures here are for **financial year 2026** (1 April 2026 to 31 March 2027)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Periods", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🗓️", title:"Accounting period (AP)", body:"The **accounting period (AP)** is the period for which corporation tax is charged. It normally matches the company's **period of account**, which is the period its accounts cover."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏱️", title:"An AP can never exceed 12 months", body:"If the accounts cover a longer period, it is split. The **first 12 months** form one AP and the **remainder** forms a second AP."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A company's accounts cover 18 months. How many APs?", body:"**Two**: an AP of the **first 12 months** and an AP of the **remaining 6 months**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📅", title:"Financial year (FY)", body:"The **financial year (FY)** is the year for which corporation tax **rates** are set. It runs from **1 April to 31 March** and is named after the calendar year in which it **starts**. So FY2026 is 1 April 2026 to 31 March 2027."},
   {t:"card", i:"✂️", title:"An AP that straddles two FYs", body:"If an AP falls partly in one FY and partly in the next, and the rates differ, the profits are **apportioned** (split in time proportion) between the two FYs."}
 ]},
 {h:"The calculation in four steps", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"1️⃣", title:"Step 1: Income profits", body:"Add up **trading profit**, **property income**, net income from **loan relationships** (interest received less non-trading interest paid) and any other income."},
   {t:"card", i:"2️⃣", title:"Step 2: Chargeable gains", body:"Work out the **net gains** on disposals of capital assets in the AP, after deducting capital losses."},
   {t:"card", i:"3️⃣", title:"Step 3: Total profits less reliefs", body:"Add Steps 1 and 2 to get **total profits**. Then deduct any **loss reliefs** claimed against total profits and any **qualifying charitable donations**. The result is **taxable total profits**."},
   {t:"card", i:"4️⃣", title:"Step 4: Apply the rate", body:"Apply the corporation tax rate for the FY (or FYs) the AP falls in."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Which step deducts a loss claimed against total profits?", body:"**Step 3.** Income profits (Step 1) and gains (Step 2) are added together first, then loss reliefs and charitable donations come off."}
 ]},
 {h:"Step 1: Trading profits", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🧾", title:"The trading profit formula", body:"**Trading profit** = **chargeable receipts** less **deductible expenditure** less **capital allowances** (CTA 2009 Part 3). The principles are the same as for a sole trader."},
   {t:"card", i:"💷", title:"Receipts must be revenue", body:"Only **revenue** receipts (from the day-to-day trade, such as sales) count. **Capital** receipts (from selling the business's long-term assets) are dealt with as gains instead."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎯", title:"Expenditure: revenue and wholly and exclusively", body:"An expense is deductible only if it is **revenue** (not capital) **and** incurred **wholly and exclusively** for the purposes of the trade (**CTA 2009 s.54**)."},
   {t:"compare", i:"✅", title:"Deductible or not?", cols:["Deductible", "Not deductible"], rows:[["Salaries and bonuses, including **directors' remuneration**","**Dividends** paid to shareholders (a share-out of profit, not an expense)"],["**Employer's NICs** and employer **pension contributions**","**Business entertainment** of clients"],["**Rent** and **repairs**","**Depreciation**"],["**Interest on business borrowings** (loan relationship rules)","**Capital expenditure** (but see capital allowances)"],["Insurance and professional fees for trading matters","**Fines and penalties**, and the company's own corporation tax"]]},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Can a company deduct the dividends it pays when computing its trading profit?", body:"**No.** A dividend is an **appropriation of profit** (a share-out of profit after tax), not an expense."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏭", title:"Capital allowances: the idea", body:"**Capital allowances** let a business deduct the cost of **plant and machinery** (equipment used in the business) over time or all at once, instead of depreciation, which is never deductible."},
   {t:"card", i:"💯", title:"Full expensing (companies only)", body:"A **100%** first-year deduction for **new and unused main-rate plant and machinery**. It is for **companies only** and **not cars**. New **special-rate** assets get a **50% first-year allowance** instead."},
   {t:"card", i:"4️⃣0️⃣", title:"40% first-year allowance", body:"From **1 January 2026**, new main-rate plant and machinery can get a **40% first-year allowance**. It is available to companies **and** unincorporated businesses, and covers assets bought for **leasing** (which do not qualify for full expensing). It does **not** cover **cars**, **second-hand** assets or assets leased **overseas**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎁", title:"Annual Investment Allowance (AIA)", body:"The **AIA** gives **100%** on the first **£1 million** of plant and machinery spending a year. Unlike full expensing, it covers **second-hand** assets too."},
   {t:"card", i:"📉", title:"Writing down allowances (WDA)", body:"Spending not covered by another allowance goes into a pool. The **main pool** gets **14%** a year (from **1 April 2026**; previously 18%). The **special rate pool** gets **6%** a year."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏗️", title:"Structures and buildings allowance", body:"**3%** a year on the construction costs of **commercial buildings**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A company buys a second-hand machine for £60,000. Full expensing or AIA?", body:"**AIA.** Full expensing needs **new and unused** plant and machinery. The AIA covers second-hand assets, up to £1 million a year."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎉", title:"Dividends received are exempt", body:"**Dividends received** by a UK company from other companies are generally **exempt** from corporation tax (CTA 2009 Part 9A). They are not included in taxable profits."}
 ]},
 {h:"Step 2: Chargeable gains of companies", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🧮", title:"Same method as an individual's gain", body:"A company's gain is computed under TCGA 1992 like an individual's: **proceeds** less **incidental costs**, **acquisition cost** and **enhancement expenditure** (money spent improving the asset)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📈", title:"Difference 1: indexation allowance", body:"A company deducts **indexation allowance**, which is an allowance for inflation on the cost (and enhancement expenditure). It is **frozen at December 2017**, so no indexation is given for inflation after that date."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚧", title:"Indexation cannot create a loss", body:"Indexation **cannot create or increase a loss**. At most, it reduces a gain to nil."},
   {t:"card", i:"🙅", title:"Difference 2: no annual exempt amount", body:"A company gets **no annual exempt amount** (the tax-free slice of gains that individuals get)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🥤", title:"Difference 3: taxed at the corporation tax rate", body:"Gains form part of the company's profits and are taxed at the **corporation tax rate**, not at CGT rates."},
   {t:"case", i:"🏚️", title:"Example: Oak Ltd's warehouse", facts:"Oak Ltd sells a warehouse in June 2026 for £900,000. It bought it in 2010 for £400,000. Indexation to December 2017 is £120,000.", rule:"**Chargeable gain** = £900,000 − £400,000 − £120,000 = **£380,000**. It is taxed as part of Oak's profits at its corporation tax rate. There is no annual exempt amount."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔒", title:"Capital losses are ring-fenced", body:"**Capital losses** can only be set against **chargeable gains** (of the same AP or carried forward). They cannot be set against income."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔁", title:"Rollover relief for companies", body:"**Rollover relief** (TCGA s.152) lets a company postpone a gain by reinvesting in new business assets. It covers **land and buildings** and **fixed plant and machinery**, but **not goodwill**."},
   {t:"card", i:"✨", title:"Why goodwill is left out", body:"A company's **goodwill** (the value of its reputation and customer base) falls under the **intangible fixed assets** regime in CTA 2009 Part 8, which has its own reinvestment relief."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧩", title:"Substantial shareholding exemption (SSE)", body:"Under **TCGA Sch.7AC**, a gain (or loss) when a company sells **shares** in another company is **exempt** if: it held at least **10%** of the ordinary share capital for a continuous **12 months** in the **6 years** before the sale, and the company sold is a **trading company** (or holding company of a trading group)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Company vs individual: who gets indexation and who gets the annual exempt amount?", body:"**Companies** get **indexation** (frozen at December 2017) but **no annual exempt amount**. **Individuals** get the **annual exempt amount** but **no indexation**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Step 3: Loss relief for companies", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🥛", title:"Current period first: s.37(3)(a)", body:"Under **CTA 2010 s.37(3)(a)**, a trading loss is set against the company's **total profits** (income and gains) of the **same AP**. This claim must be made **before** any carry-back."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏪", title:"Then carry back 12 months: s.37(3)(b)", body:"Under **s.37(3)(b)**, any loss left can be carried back against **total profits** of the **previous 12 months**. This is only available **after** the current period claim, and all available profits must be used."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Can a company skip the current year and carry its loss straight back?", body:"**No.** Under s.37 the loss must go against the **current period** first. Only then can it be carried back **12 months**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏩", title:"Carry forward: s.45A", body:"For losses arising from **1 April 2017**, **s.45A** lets the company carry the loss forward against **total profits** of later APs (a claim is required). It can choose how much to use."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧢", title:"The £5 million cap on carry forward", body:"Above a **£5 million** deductions allowance, only **50%** of profits can be relieved by carried-forward losses."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔂", title:"Carry forward against the same trade: s.45", body:"Losses that cannot be relieved against total profits (for example pre-April 2017 losses, or where the trade has become **small or negligible**) are carried forward under **s.45** against **profits of the same trade** only. This is **automatic**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏁", title:"Terminal loss relief: s.39", body:"A loss in the **final 12 months** of trading can be carried back against **total profits** of the **3 years** before that final 12 months, **latest year first**."},
   {t:"card", i:"👯", title:"Group relief", body:"**Group relief** (CTA 2010 ss.97-188) lets a company **surrender** its current-period losses (and carried-forward losses) to another company in a **75% group** that has profits in the corresponding period."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔗", title:"What a 75% group is", body:"One company owns (directly or indirectly) at least **75%** of the ordinary share capital of the other, with corresponding rights to profits and assets. Or both are 75% subsidiaries of a third company."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🧭", title:"Company losses at a glance", cols:["Relief", "Set against", "Timing"], rows:[["s.37(3)(a) current period","Total profits, same AP","Before any carry-back"],["s.37(3)(b) carry back","Total profits, previous 12 months","After the current period claim"],["s.45A carry forward","Total profits of later APs","Claim; 50% cap above £5m"],["s.45 carry forward","Profits of the same trade","Automatic"],["s.39 terminal loss","Total profits, 3 years before final 12 months","Latest year first"],["Group relief","Profits of a 75% group company","Corresponding period"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"⚠️", title:"Don't mix up companies and individuals", body:"The **3-year carry back** is only for **terminal** losses (the final 12 months). Individuals are different: they can use **s.64** for the current **or** previous year in either order, and **s.72** for 3 years back for early-years losses."}
 ]},
 {h:"Step 4: Rates (financial year 2026)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🪑", title:"Small profits rate: 19%", body:"Taxable profits up to **£50,000** (the **lower limit**) are taxed at **19%**, the **small profits rate**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🪜", title:"Main rate: 25%", body:"Taxable profits over **£250,000** (the **upper limit**) are taxed at **25%**, the **main rate**."},
   {t:"card", i:"↕️", title:"In between: 25% less marginal relief", body:"Profits **between £50,000 and £250,000** are taxed at **25% less marginal relief**. The effective marginal rate in this band is **26.5%**."},
   {t:"card", i:"➗", title:"The marginal relief formula", body:"**Marginal relief = 3/200 × (upper limit − profits)**. This applies where there are no exempt dividends from non-group companies; if there are, the formula uses **augmented profits** (profits plus those dividends)."},
   {t:"case", i:"🌲", title:"Example: Pine Ltd", facts:"Pine Ltd has taxable total profits of £150,000 for the year to 31 March 2027 and no associated companies.", rule:"Tax at 25% = £37,500. Marginal relief = 3/200 × (£250,000 − £150,000) = £1,500. **Corporation tax = £36,000** (an effective rate of 24%)."},
   {t:"card", i:"👥", title:"Associated companies divide the limits", body:"The £50,000 and £250,000 limits are **divided equally** between the company and its **associated companies**, meaning companies under common control (controlled by the same person or persons). With **one** associated company, each company's limits are **£25,000** and **£125,000**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏳", title:"Short APs reduce the limits", body:"For an AP **shorter than 12 months**, the limits are **reduced proportionately**. A 6-month AP has limits of £25,000 and £125,000."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A company with no associated companies has profits of £40,000 for a 12-month AP. What rate?", body:"**19%**, the small profits rate, because the profits are not over the £50,000 lower limit."}
 ]},
 {h:"Payment and collection", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"📣", title:"Notify within 3 months", body:"A company must **notify HMRC** (HM Revenue & Customs) that it is **within the charge** to corporation tax within **3 months** of starting to trade."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗓️", title:"Ordinary companies: 9 months and 1 day", body:"A company that is not \"large\" pays its corporation tax in a **single payment** due **9 months and 1 day** after the end of the AP."},
   {t:"card", i:"🐘", title:"Large companies: quarterly instalments", body:"A **large company** has taxable total profits plus exempt dividends over **£1.5 million** a year (divided equally between the company and its associated companies). It pays **quarterly instalments** based on the estimated liability for the **current** period, in months **7, 10, 13 and 16** after the start of a 12-month AP."},
   {t:"card", i:"🦕", title:"Very large companies pay earlier", body:"**Very large** companies (profits over **£20 million**) pay in months **3, 6, 9 and 12**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🆕", title:"First year as a large company", body:"A company is **not** treated as large in its first year above the threshold if its profits did not exceed **£10 million** and it was not large in the previous year."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A company with £150,000 profits has an AP ending 31 March 2027. When is its tax due?", body:"**1 January 2028**: 9 months and 1 day after the end of the AP. It is not a large company."},
   {t:"card", i:"💻", title:"The return: CT600 within 12 months", body:"The company tax return (**CT600**), with accounts and computations, must be filed **online** within **12 months** after the end of the AP. This is **self-assessment**: the company calculates its own tax."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗄️", title:"Records, interest and penalties", body:"Records must be kept for **6 years** from the end of the AP. HMRC charges **interest** on late payments and **penalties** for late or incorrect returns."}
 ]},
 {h:"Tax treatment of distributions", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🍰", title:"Dividends come out of post-tax profit", body:"**Dividends** are paid out of **post-tax** profits and are **not deductible** for corporation tax. The company does **not** deduct any tax when paying them."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧍", title:"Individual shareholders", body:"Individuals pay **income tax** on dividends at the dividend rates: **10.75% / 35.75% / 39.35%** for 2026/27, after a **£500** dividend allowance. There are **no NICs** on dividends."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏢", title:"Corporate shareholders", body:"A company that receives a dividend is normally **exempt** from corporation tax on it."},
   {t:"card", i:"✌️", title:"Dividends are effectively taxed twice", body:"Profit paid out as a dividend to an individual bears **corporation tax** on the profit, then **income tax** on the dividend. A salary is taxed only once for income tax purposes, but attracts NICs."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔙", title:"Share buybacks", body:"When a company **buys back** its own shares, the payment is treated as a **distribution** unless the **capital treatment** conditions apply (see **Equity finance**)."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🥤", title:"Five ways to extract profit from an owner-managed company", cols:["Method", "Deductible for company?", "Tax on recipient", "NICs"], rows:[["**Salary / bonus**","✅ Yes","Income tax (non-savings rates) under **PAYE**","Employee **8%/2%**, employer **15%**"],["**Dividend**","❌ No","Income tax at **dividend rates** (after £500 allowance)","**None**"],["**Interest** on a loan from the owner","✅ Yes (loan relationship)","Income tax as **savings income** (company usually deducts **20%** at source)","None"],["**Employer pension contribution**","✅ Yes (if wholly and exclusively)","Not taxed when paid in (within allowances)","None"],["**Rent** for premises the director owns","✅ Yes","Income tax as property income","None (but may restrict BADR on an associated disposal)"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"💡", title:"Profit extraction: the key points", body:"**Salary and interest are deductible** for the company; **dividends are not**. **Dividends attract no NICs**. A dividend can only be paid if the company has **distributable profits** (see **Distributions**)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Which reduces the company's corporation tax: paying the owner a £30,000 bonus or a £30,000 dividend?", body:"The **bonus**. Salary and bonuses are deductible; dividends are not. (But the bonus attracts employee and employer NICs, and the dividend does not.)"}
 ]},
 {h:"Close companies and anti-avoidance (outline)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🔐", title:"What a close company is", body:"A **close company** (CTA 2010 s.439) is a UK resident company **controlled** by **five or fewer participators**, or by **any number of participators who are directors**. Most family and owner-managed companies are close."},
   {t:"card", i:"🙋", title:"Who is a participator", body:"A **participator** is a person with a share or interest in the capital or income of the company: mainly **shareholders** and **loan creditors** (people the company owes money to)."},
   {t:"card", i:"💸", title:"Loans to participators: s.455", body:"If a close company **lends money to a participator** (or their associate), for example through an overdrawn director's loan account, and the loan is **not repaid within 9 months** of the end of the AP in which it was made, the company must pay a **temporary** tax charge."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔢", title:"The s.455 rate", body:"The charge is **35.75%** of the outstanding loan for loans made on or after **6 April 2026** (33.75% for loans made from 6 April 2022 to 5 April 2026). The rate tracks the **dividend upper rate**."},
   {t:"card", i:"↩️", title:"The s.455 charge is refunded", body:"The charge is **refunded** when the loan is **repaid**, released or written off. The refund is due 9 months after the end of the AP in which that happens."},
   {t:"case", i:"🧾", title:"Example: a £20,000 loan to the owner", facts:"A close company lends its director-shareholder £20,000 in June 2026, in its AP ending 31 March 2027. Nothing is repaid by 31 December 2027.", rule:"The company pays a s.455 charge of 35.75% × £20,000 = **£7,150**. It gets this back once the loan is repaid."},
   {t:"card", i:"🆗", title:"Exceptions to s.455", body:"Loans of up to **£15,000** in total to a director who works **full time** for the company and owns no more than **5%** of it, and loans made in the ordinary course of a **money-lending business**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛏️", title:"No 'bed and breakfasting'", body:"Anti-avoidance rules stop a participator repaying the loan just before the deadline and **re-borrowing within 30 days**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗑️", title:"If the company writes the loan off", body:"The participator is taxed on the amount as **dividend income** (ITTOIA s.415), and the company gets **no deduction**. A director may also be taxed on a **benefit in kind** for a cheap loan over £10,000."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎁", title:"Benefits to participators", body:"Expenses a close company incurs in providing benefits to a participator who is **not** an employee or director are treated as **distributions**, so they are not deductible."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A close company lends a 60% shareholder £40,000, unpaid 9 months after the AP ends. What happens?", body:"The company pays a **s.455 charge** of 35.75% of the outstanding loan. It is **refunded** when the loan is repaid, released or written off."},
   {t:"card", i:"🌍", title:"Transfer pricing", body:"Under **TIOPA 2010 Part 4**, transactions between **connected** companies (for example within a group, or with an overseas parent) must be taxed on **arm's length** (market) terms. Small and medium-sized enterprises are largely exempt."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚰", title:"Corporate interest restriction", body:"Groups with net interest expense over **£2 million** a year have their interest deductions limited (outline)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏝️", title:"Controlled foreign companies", body:"Profits diverted to **low-tax overseas subsidiaries** may be taxed in the UK under the controlled foreign companies rules (outline)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛡️", title:"General anti-avoidance rules", body:"The **GAAR** (general anti-abuse rule), **DOTAS** (disclosure of tax avoidance schemes) and the **Ramsay** principle (the courts look at a scheme as a whole) apply to companies as to individuals (see **Income tax**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🪓", title:"Associated companies rules", body:"Because the profit limits are divided between companies under common control, **splitting** a business into several companies does **not** multiply the small profits rate."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Source: proposals/rooms/learn-blp-debt-security.js */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["blp/debt-security"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🏦", title:"Debt finance means borrowing", body:"**Debt finance** means **borrowing**: the company receives money now and promises to repay it with **interest**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧾", title:"Lenders are creditors, not owners", body:"A lender is a **creditor** (someone the company owes money to), not an owner. Shareholders who buy shares are owners: that is **equity finance**, a separate topic."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔐", title:"Security: the lender's safety net", body:"Lenders reduce their risk by taking **security**: a right over the borrower's assets. If the borrower **defaults** (fails to pay) or becomes **insolvent** (cannot pay its debts), the lender can **sell those assets** and repay itself **ahead of unsecured creditors**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Why does a lender want security rather than just a promise to pay?", body:"So that if the borrower defaults or becomes insolvent, the lender can **sell the charged assets** and be repaid **ahead of unsecured creditors**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗺️", title:"What this topic covers (and what's elsewhere)", body:"This topic: **types of borrowing**, **loan agreement terms**, **types of security** (mortgage, fixed and floating charges, pledge, lien, guarantee), **crystallisation**, **registration** of charges (the **21-day** rule) and **priority** between lenders.\n\nElsewhere: s.245 avoidance, the insolvency order of priority and the prescribed part (**Clawback, directors' liability and order of priority**); administration and receivership (**Corporate insolvency procedures**)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Before borrowing: company law checks", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🎯", title:"Capacity: can the company borrow at all?", body:"A company formed under the Companies Act 2006 (CA 2006) has **unrestricted objects** unless its articles restrict them (**s.31**). So it can borrow and give security."},
   {t:"card", i:"📏", title:"Check the articles for a borrowing limit", body:"Check the **articles** (the company's internal rulebook) for any **borrowing limit** or restriction on the directors' powers. The **Model Articles** (the default articles) contain **none**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛡️", title:"s.40 protects a lender in good faith", body:"A lender acting in **good faith** is protected by **s.40** even if the directors exceed a constitutional limit. Lenders still check, to avoid any argument."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗳️", title:"The board resolution", body:"The **directors** pass a **board resolution** approving the facility and the security documents and authorising who signs them."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧭", title:"Directors' duties when borrowing", body:"The directors must consider their duties, especially **s.172**: is the borrowing likely to **promote the success of the company**? They must also **declare any interest** (**s.177**), e.g. if a director is giving a **personal guarantee**."},
   {t:"card", i:"✅", title:"A director's guarantee: still allowed to vote", body:"Under **Model Articles art.14(4)**, a director's guarantee of the company's obligations is a **permitted cause**. So the interested director **can still vote** and **count in the quorum** (the minimum number needed for a valid meeting)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A director is personally guaranteeing the company's new loan. Under the Model Articles, can she vote on the board resolution approving it?", body:"**Yes.** She must **declare her interest** (s.177), but a director's guarantee of the company's obligations is a **permitted cause** under **art.14(4)**, so she can vote and count in the quorum."},
   {t:"card", i:"🙋", title:"When shareholders must approve", body:"**Shareholder approval** may be needed where the company **guarantees or secures a loan to a director** (**s.197**), or where the deal is a **substantial property transaction** with a director (**s.190**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔎", title:"The lender's searches", body:"Before lending, the lender runs:\n- a **Companies House search** (existing charges, filings, directors);\n- an **insolvency search** (any winding-up petitions);\n- a **Land Registry search** if land is being charged."}
 ]},
 {h:"Types of borrowing", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"💳", title:"Overdraft", body:"An **overdraft** lets the company overdraw its current account up to an agreed limit. Interest is paid **only on the amount used**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚠️", title:"Overdraft: repayable on demand", body:"An overdraft is usually repayable **on demand**: the bank can ask for it back at any time. It is flexible, but **insecure for the borrower**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📅", title:"Term loan", body:"A **term loan** is a fixed sum lent for a fixed period. It is drawn down in one go or in **tranches** (instalments of the loan), and repaid in instalments (**amortising**) or all at the end (**bullet**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔒", title:"Term loan: no early demand, no redrawing", body:"The lender can only demand early repayment of a term loan on an **event of default**. Once repaid, the money **cannot be redrawn**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔄", title:"Revolving credit facility (RCF)", body:"An **RCF** lets the company **borrow, repay and re-borrow** up to a maximum amount during the term. It gives flexible working capital. The company pays a **commitment fee** on the **undrawn** amount."},
   {t:"card", i:"📜", title:"Debt securities", body:"**Debt securities** (loan notes, bonds, debentures) are instruments the company issues to investors acknowledging the debt. Investors can often **trade** them. They are used by larger companies, and holders are **creditors, not members**."},
   {t:"compare", i:"⚖️", title:"The four facilities side by side", cols:["", "Can lender demand it back any time?", "Can it be re-borrowed?"], rows:[["💳 Overdraft","✅ Yes, on demand","✅ Yes, up to the limit"],["📅 Term loan","❌ Only on an event of default","❌ No"],["🔄 RCF","❌ Only on an event of default","✅ Yes, up to the maximum"],["📜 Debt securities","❌ Repaid on the instrument's terms","❌ No (but holders can often trade them)"]]},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A company wants to borrow, repay and borrow again as its cash needs change over five years. Which facility fits?", body:"A **revolving credit facility (RCF)**: borrow, repay and **re-borrow** up to a maximum during the term, paying a **commitment fee** on undrawn amounts."},
   {t:"card", i:"📄", title:"What is a debenture?", body:"In law, a **debenture** is any document that **creates or acknowledges a debt**. In practice, \"a debenture\" usually means a document by which a company gives a lender **fixed and floating charges over all its assets**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📈", title:"Interest: fixed or floating", body:"Interest can be **fixed** or **floating** (variable, tied to a reference rate such as **SONIA** or the bank's **base rate**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧮", title:"Interest and tax", body:"Interest is **deductible** for the company's **corporation tax**. Lenders who are individuals pay **income tax** on it as **savings income**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Main terms of a loan agreement", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🎯", title:"Facility and purpose", body:"The loan agreement states the **amount**, the **type** of facility and **what it may be used for**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚦", title:"Conditions precedent (CPs)", body:"**Conditions precedent** are documents and conditions the borrower must satisfy **before it can draw down** (take the money). E.g. certified articles, board minutes approving the loan, signed security, guarantees, insurance, legal opinions."},
   {t:"card", i:"💷", title:"Interest and repayment terms", body:"The agreement sets the **rate**, the **interest periods**, the **repayment schedule**, and any **prepayment** rights (repaying early) and fees."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗣️", title:"Representations and warranties", body:"**Representations and warranties** are **statements of fact** the borrower makes about itself, e.g. it has power to borrow, there is no litigation, its accounts are accurate. If one is **untrue**, that is an **event of default**. Some are **repeated** on each interest date."},
   {t:"card", i:"🤞", title:"Undertakings (covenants)", body:"**Undertakings**, also called **covenants**, are **promises about future conduct**. They last for the **life of the loan**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📊", title:"Information covenants", body:"**Information covenants**: the borrower must supply **annual and management accounts** and **compliance certificates**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📐", title:"Financial covenants", body:"**Financial covenants** set financial tests, e.g.:\n- **interest cover**: profit must be a set multiple of interest;\n- **gearing**: the ratio of debt to equity;\n- a minimum **net worth** or **cash flow**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚫", title:"General covenants and the negative pledge", body:"**General covenants**, e.g. not to change the nature of the business and not to dispose of key assets. The key one is the **negative pledge**: the borrower will **not create any other security** over its assets (or not without the lender's consent)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🟰", title:"The pari passu clause", body:"A **pari passu** clause (often alongside the negative pledge) means the loan ranks **at least equally** with the borrower's other unsecured debt."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Is a promise not to grant security to any other lender a representation or a covenant?", body:"A **covenant** (a promise about future conduct): it is the **negative pledge**. A representation is a statement of **present fact**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚨", title:"Events of default", body:"**Events of default** include: non-payment, breach of covenant, misrepresentation, **cross-default** (default on another loan), insolvency events and **material adverse change** (a serious worsening in the borrower's position)."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏩", title:"What the lender can do on default", body:"On an event of default the lender can:\n- **accelerate**: demand immediate repayment of the **whole** loan;\n- **cancel** undrawn commitments;\n- **enforce its security**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"The borrower defaults on a different bank's loan, but is up to date on this one. Can this lender act?", body:"**Yes**, if the agreement has a **cross-default** clause: default on another loan is an event of default, so the lender can **accelerate** and enforce."}
 ]},
 {h:"Mortgage and fixed charge", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🏠", title:"Mortgage: ownership transferred", body:"A **mortgage** is a transfer of **ownership** of an asset to the lender as security, with a right to have it transferred back when the debt is repaid. That right is the **equity of redemption**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📜", title:"Legal mortgage of land", body:"Over **land**, the only legal mortgage is a **charge by deed expressed to be by way of legal mortgage** (Law of Property Act 1925 ss.85 and 87; for registered land, Land Registration Act 2002 s.23). It is registered at **HM Land Registry**. Mortgages can also be taken over **shares** and other assets."},
   {t:"card", i:"📌", title:"Fixed charge: specific assets", body:"A **fixed charge** attaches to **specific, identifiable assets**, e.g. land and buildings, heavy machinery, goodwill, shares, intellectual property, and in principle book debts if properly controlled."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔒", title:"Fixed charge: no dealing without consent", body:"The company **cannot sell or otherwise deal with** the charged asset **without the lender's consent**. That **control** is what makes the charge fixed."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔧", title:"Fixed charge: enforcement", body:"On default the lender can enforce against the asset, e.g. by appointing a **receiver** to sell it or collect its income."},
   {t:"card", i:"🥇", title:"Fixed charge: paid first on insolvency", body:"On insolvency, the fixed charge holder is paid from the proceeds of the charged asset **first**, before liquidation expenses, preferential creditors and floating charge holders."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A company gave a bank a fixed charge over its factory. Can it sell the factory without asking the bank?", body:"**No.** Under a fixed charge the company **cannot deal** with the asset **without the lender's consent**. That control is what makes it fixed."}
 ]},
 {h:"Other security: pledge, lien, guarantee, retention of title", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"✋", title:"Pledge", body:"A **pledge**: the lender takes **physical possession** of the borrower's goods (or documents of title) until the debt is repaid, with a **power of sale** on default."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚗", title:"Lien", body:"A **lien** is a right to **retain possession** of another person's goods until a debt owed by that person is paid, e.g. a repairer keeping a car until the repair bill is paid. There is usually **no power of sale** unless given by statute or contract."},
   {t:"compare", i:"⚖️", title:"Pledge vs lien", cols:["", "✋ Pledge", "🚗 Lien"], rows:[["What the creditor has","Possession given as security","A right to keep goods already held"],["Power of sale?","✅ Yes, on default","❌ Usually not (unless statute or contract)"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"🤝", title:"Guarantees: a third party's promise", body:"A **guarantee** is a promise by a **third party** (e.g. a **director personally**, or a parent or subsidiary company) to pay the borrower's debt **if the borrower does not**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧍", title:"A guarantee is not security over an asset", body:"A guarantee (often drafted as a **guarantee and indemnity**) is a **personal contract**, not a proprietary right over an asset (though a guarantor can also give security)."},
   {t:"card", i:"✍️", title:"A guarantee must be in writing", body:"A guarantee must be **in writing** and **signed by the guarantor** (**Statute of Frauds 1677 s.4**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"💥", title:"Personal guarantee: limited liability lost for that debt", body:"A director who signs a **personal guarantee** **loses the protection of limited liability** for that debt: if the company does not pay, the lender can pursue the director personally."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"The company can't repay its loan. The director gave an oral personal guarantee. Can the bank enforce it?", body:"**No.** A guarantee must be **in writing and signed** by the guarantor (Statute of Frauds 1677 s.4)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏷️", title:"Retention of title (outline)", body:"**Retention of title** is a supplier keeping ownership of goods until it is paid. It is **not a charge granted by the company** and does **not need registering** if properly drafted."}
 ]},
 {h:"Floating charges", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🌊", title:"Floating charge: a changing class of assets", body:"A **floating charge** is a charge over a **class of assets**, present and future, which in the ordinary course of business **changes from time to time**: e.g. **stock**, raw materials, **book debts** (money owed to the company by its customers), cash at bank."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛒", title:"Floating charge: free to deal", body:"The company is **free to use and dispose of** those assets **in the ordinary course of business** until the charge **crystallises** (the classic description from 1903, approved in later cases)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏪", title:"Why floating charges exist", body:"A floating charge lets a business give security over assets it **needs to keep trading with**, such as stock it must sell."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏢", title:"Who can grant a floating charge?", body:"In practice only **companies and LLPs**. Sole traders and partnerships cannot effectively give floating charges over goods: the **Bills of Sale Acts** make it impractical."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Can a sole trader give a bank an effective floating charge over her stock?", body:"**Not in practice.** Only **companies and LLPs** can effectively grant floating charges; the Bills of Sale Acts make it impractical for individuals and partnerships."},
   {t:"card", i:"👇", title:"Weakness 1: ranks behind others on insolvency", body:"On insolvency a floating charge ranks **behind** liquidation/administration **expenses**, **preferential creditors** (employees, and **HMRC** as a secondary preferential creditor) and the **prescribed part** set aside for unsecured creditors."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏳", title:"Weakness 2: s.245 can make it invalid", body:"A floating charge can be **invalid** under **Insolvency Act 1986 (IA 1986) s.245** if created within **12 months** (**2 years** for a **connected person**, such as a director) before insolvency, except to the extent of **new money**. Details are in **Clawback**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🥈", title:"Weaknesses 3 and 4: later fixed charges and vanishing assets", body:"A **later fixed charge** over the same asset can take **priority**. And the assets may have been **sold or dissipated** by the time the charge crystallises."},
   {t:"card", i:"💪", title:"The strength: a QFCH can appoint an administrator", body:"A **qualifying floating charge holder** (**QFCH**) holds a floating charge (or charges) over the **whole or substantially the whole** of the company's property. A QFCH can appoint an **administrator out of court**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔍", title:"Fixed or floating? Substance, not labels", body:"The court looks at the **rights and obligations** the parties actually agreed, especially the **degree of control** the lender has over the assets, **not the label** used in the document."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏦", title:"The book debts example", body:"A bank's debenture called its charge over book debts \"fixed\". The company had to pay what its customers paid into its account with the bank, but that was an **ordinary current account** it could **draw on freely**. The charge was **floating**, because the company could use the proceeds as it wished (Re Spectrum Plus, House of Lords, 2005)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧱", title:"Fixed charge over book debts: the blocked account", body:"For a **fixed** charge over book debts, the proceeds must be paid into a **blocked account** that the company **cannot draw on without the lender's consent**."},
   {t:"case", i:"📺", title:"Example: Beech Ltd's televisions", facts:"Beech Ltd grants Lender A a charge \"by way of fixed charge\" over its stock of televisions, but may sell them to customers without asking A.", rule:"The charge is **floating**, because Beech can deal with the stock in the ordinary course of business. It will rank **behind preferential creditors and the prescribed part** on insolvency."},
   {t:"card", i:"💡", title:"Exam tip: a \"fixed\" label can lie", body:"A charge labelled \"fixed\" over **stock** or **book debts** where the company **can freely use** the assets or their proceeds is a **floating charge**. Look for a **blocked account** or a need for the **lender's consent** before choosing \"fixed\"."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A charge is called \"fixed\" over book debts, but the company pays its customers' money into an ordinary account it uses freely. Fixed or floating?", body:"**Floating.** The company can use the proceeds as it wishes. A fixed charge would need a **blocked account**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Crystallisation", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"💎", title:"What crystallisation does", body:"**Crystallisation** turns a floating charge into a **fixed charge** over the assets in the class **at that moment**. The company can **no longer deal** with them."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚡", title:"Automatic crystallisation by law", body:"A floating charge crystallises automatically on:\n- the **winding up** (liquidation) of the company;\n- the appointment of a **receiver** (and, under most charge documents, of an **administrator**);\n- the company **ceasing to carry on business**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📨", title:"Crystallisation under the charge's own terms", body:"The charge document can also make it crystallise:\n- **express crystallisation**: on the lender giving **notice**;\n- **automatic crystallisation clause**: automatically on a specified event, e.g. the company creating another charge in breach of a **negative pledge**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏷️", title:"Once floating, always floating for insolvency", body:"Even after crystallisation, a charge **created as floating** is still treated as a **floating charge** for the insolvency rules. Preferential creditors and the prescribed part still come out of those assets (**IA 1986 s.251**)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A floating charge crystallised before liquidation. Does it now rank as a fixed charge, ahead of preferential creditors?", body:"**No.** A charge **created as floating** is still treated as floating for the insolvency rules (s.251), so preferential creditors and the prescribed part come first."}
 ]},
 {h:"Registration of company charges", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🏛️", title:"Almost every company charge must be registered", body:"**Almost every charge** created by a company (including an LLP) must be registered at **Companies House**, whether **fixed or floating**, over any assets (**CA 2006 ss.859A-859Q**). Exceptions include rent deposits in some cases and certain financial collateral arrangements."},
   {t:"card", i:"🙋", title:"Who registers?", body:"The **company** or **any person interested** in the charge. In practice the **lender** does it, because it **bears the risk** of non-registration."},
   {t:"card", i:"📮", title:"How to register", body:"Deliver a **statement of particulars** (form **MR01**) and a **certified copy of the charge instrument**, with the fee."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏱️", title:"The 21-day time limit", body:"Within **21 days** beginning with the **day after the date of creation** of the charge (**s.859A(4)**)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A charge is created on 10 May. What is the last day to deliver it for registration?", body:"**31 May.** The 21 days begin the day after creation: 11 May to 31 May."},
   {t:"card", i:"📆", title:"No extra time for weekends or bank holidays", body:"The 21 days are **calendar days**. If day 21 falls on a **weekend or bank holiday**, the deadline is **not extended** (s.859A(4) gives no extension). File early, or online, to be safe."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏚️", title:"Buying an asset that is already charged", body:"If a company **acquires property that is already subject to a charge** (e.g. it buys a building the seller had mortgaged), the company or the lender **may** register that charge (**s.859C**). There is **no 21-day limit**, and the charge is **not void** for late registration, because the company did not create it (**s.859H** applies only to charges the company **creates**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📃", title:"Certificate of registration", body:"Companies House issues a **certificate of registration** (**s.859I**). It is **conclusive evidence** that the documents were delivered within the time limit."},
   {t:"card", i:"❌", title:"Missed the deadline: the security is void", body:"If not registered in time, the charge is **void** (so far as it creates security) **against a liquidator, an administrator and any creditor** of the company (**s.859H**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"💷", title:"Missed the deadline: the loan is still owed", body:"The **debt itself remains valid** but becomes **immediately repayable** (**s.859H(4)**). So the lender becomes an **unsecured creditor** if the company goes into insolvency."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚖️", title:"Late registration: only by court order", body:"Late registration is possible only with a **court order** extending the period (**s.859F**). The court must be satisfied that the failure was **accidental** or due to inadvertence or some other sufficient cause, or is not of a nature to prejudice creditors or shareholders, or that it is **just and equitable** to grant relief."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛡️", title:"Late registration: without prejudice", body:"The court usually orders that late registration is **without prejudice** to rights acquired by others in the meantime."},
   {t:"card", i:"✔️", title:"Satisfaction: updating the register", body:"When the debt is repaid or the asset released, the company files a **statement of satisfaction** (form **MR04**) so the public register is updated (**s.859L**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗄️", title:"Company's own records", body:"The company must keep **copies of every charge instrument** available for inspection at its **registered office** (**ss.859P-859Q**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏡", title:"Land: register twice", body:"A charge over **registered land** must **also** be registered at **HM Land Registry** for the lender to have a **legal charge** and priority against later land charges."},
   {t:"case", i:"🏭", title:"Example: Cedar Ltd's factory", facts:"Cedar Ltd grants a bank a fixed charge over its factory on 1 March. The bank files the MR01 on 25 March.", rule:"The 21 days (2 to 22 March) expired on 22 March, so the charge is **void against Cedar's liquidator and creditors**. The loan becomes **immediately repayable**, but the bank is **unsecured** unless the court extends time under s.859F."},
   {t:"card", i:"💡", title:"Exam tip: security void, loan not void", body:"The most common question is the **consequence** of missing the 21-day deadline: the **security** is void against the **liquidator, administrator and creditors**, but the **loan is not void**: it becomes **immediately repayable**. The only cure is a **court order** for late registration."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A bank registers its charge on day 25. The company goes into liquidation. Is the bank's loan void?", body:"**No.** The **security** is void against the liquidator, but the **loan** stands and became **immediately repayable**. The bank claims as an **unsecured creditor**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Priority between charges", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🏁", title:"What priority means", body:"**Priority** decides which secured lender is paid first from the **same asset** if there is not enough to pay all of them."},
   {t:"card", i:"1️⃣", title:"Same type of charge: order of creation", body:"**Two fixed charges** (or **two floating charges**) over the same asset rank in **order of creation**: the first created is paid first, **provided each is registered within 21 days**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🐢", title:"Registered late loses", body:"A charge created earlier but **registered late** (with a court extension) usually **loses** to one validly registered in the meantime."},
   {t:"card", i:"🥇", title:"A later fixed charge beats an earlier floating charge", body:"A **fixed charge** takes priority over an **earlier floating charge** over the same asset. The floating charge allows the company to deal with its assets in the ordinary course of business, **including by granting fixed charges**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚫", title:"The exception: negative pledge plus actual notice", body:"If the earlier floating charge contains a **negative pledge** (a ban on creating later charges ranking ahead of or equal to it) **and** the later fixed charge holder **had actual notice** of it, the **floating charge keeps priority**."},
   {t:"card", i:"👀", title:"Registration is not notice of a negative pledge", body:"Registration at Companies House is **not by itself** treated as giving notice of a negative pledge (though lenders search and in practice usually see it)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🤝", title:"Deed of priority", body:"Lenders can agree a **different order** between themselves by contract, regardless of order of creation: a **deed of priority** (or **intercreditor agreement**). The company is usually a party."},
   {t:"case", i:"🌳", title:"Example: Elm Ltd's warehouse", facts:"On 1 April, Elm Ltd gives Bank A a floating charge over all its assets, containing a negative pledge. On 1 June it gives Bank B a fixed charge over its warehouse.", rule:"If B **knew** of A's negative pledge, **A** has priority over the warehouse. If B **did not know**, **B's fixed charge ranks first**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Two fixed charges over the same machine, both registered in time: one created in January, one in March. Who is paid first?", body:"The **January** charge: same-type charges rank in **order of creation** if each is registered within 21 days."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚠️", title:"Watch out: registered but still beaten", body:"A properly registered charge can still lose priority or value: a **later fixed charge** can beat an earlier floating charge (unless negative pledge + notice), and a **floating charge** can be **set aside under IA 1986 s.245** if granted shortly before insolvency for past debts."},
   {t:"compare", i:"⚖️", title:"Summary: fixed v floating charge", cols:["", "📌 Fixed", "🌊 Floating"], rows:[["Assets","Specific identifiable assets (land, machinery, goodwill, shares)","A changing class (stock, book debts, cash), present and future"],["Freedom to deal","None without lender's consent","Free in the ordinary course until crystallisation"],["Who can grant","Companies, LLPs, individuals","In practice only companies and LLPs"],["Registration","MR01 within 21 days","MR01 within 21 days"],["Insolvency ranking","First, from the charged asset","After expenses, preferential creditors and prescribed part"],["Vulnerability","Rarely challenged (possibly a preference, s.239)","s.245 (12 months / 2 years connected), plus s.239"],["Remedies","Receiver (e.g. LPA receiver over land)","QFCH can appoint an administrator out of court"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"🗺️", title:"Your 5-step route", body:"**1** Can the company borrow? → **2** What facility and terms? → **3** What security? → **4** Registered in time? → **5** Priority. The full route is this topic's **framework**."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Source: proposals/rooms/learn-blp-decision-making.js */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["blp/decision-making"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🏢", title:"Two levels of decision-making", body:"A company makes decisions at **two levels**: the **board of directors** and the **shareholders** (also called **members**)."},
   {t:"compare", i:"⚖️", title:"Who decides what", cols:["", "🧑‍💼 Board of directors", "👥 Shareholders"], rows:[["How they decide","**Board resolutions**, at board meetings or in writing","**Ordinary** or **special resolutions**"],["Where","Board meetings (or unanimous written decisions)","**General meetings** or, in a private company, **written resolutions**"],["What about","Managing the business","Matters the CA 2006 or the articles reserve to them"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"📜", title:"The directors run the company (art.3)", body:"The **Model Articles** are the default rulebook for a company's internal affairs. Under **art.3** the directors manage the company and exercise **all its powers**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"How can shareholders give the board directions?", body:"Only by **special resolution** (a 75% vote) under Model Articles **art.4**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔐", title:"Decisions reserved to shareholders", body:"The **Companies Act 2006 (CA 2006)** reserves many key decisions to the shareholders, for example **changing the articles**, **removing directors** and approving certain **transactions with directors**."},
   {t:"card", i:"❓", title:"The four exam questions", body:"Most questions on this topic ask:\n- **who** must make this decision;\n- **what type of resolution** is needed;\n- **how** the meeting is called and run (notice, quorum, votes);\n- **what must be filed** afterwards."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔗", title:"Covered elsewhere", body:"Directors' duties: **Directors**. Share issues and buybacks: **Equity finance**. Dividends: **Distributions**."}
 ]},
 {h:"The usual sequence for a shareholder decision", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"1️⃣", title:"Step 1: first board meeting", body:"The directors resolve to **call a general meeting** (GM, a meeting of the shareholders) or to **circulate a written resolution**, and approve the notice or the resolution wording."},
   {t:"card", i:"2️⃣", title:"Step 2: the shareholders decide", body:"The **GM** is held (after proper notice), or the written resolution is circulated and passed."},
   {t:"card", i:"3️⃣", title:"Step 3: second board meeting", body:"Often held **immediately after the GM**. The board carries out the decision, e.g. allots the shares, approves the contract, or authorises a director to sign."},
   {t:"card", i:"4️⃣", title:"Step 4: post-decision formalities", body:"**File** resolutions and forms at **Companies House** (the public registry of companies), **update** the company's registers and write up **minutes** (the written record of a meeting)."},
   {t:"case", i:"🏭", title:"Example: Teal Ltd buys Mo's warehouse", facts:"Teal Ltd wants to buy a warehouse from its director Mo for **£300,000**.", rule:"**Board meeting 1**: Mo declares his interest (s.177) and does not vote or count in the quorum (art.14); the board calls a GM. **GM**: an **ordinary resolution** approves the substantial property transaction (s.190). **Board meeting 2**: approves the purchase and authorises signing. Minutes are kept; **no Companies House filing** is needed for an ordinary resolution approving an SPT."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Why are there two board meetings?", body:"The **first** calls the GM (only the board can set the process going). The **second** carries out what the shareholders approved."}
 ]},
 {h:"Board meetings", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"📣", title:"Calling a board meeting (art.9)", body:"**Any director** can call a board meeting (or ask the secretary to) by giving **reasonable notice** to each director."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗓️", title:"What the notice says", body:"It must state the proposed **date, time and place** (and how directors will communicate if not all in one place). It **need not be in writing**, and a director can **waive** (give up the right to) notice."},
   {t:"card", i:"💻", title:"Taking part (art.10)", body:"Directors take part if they can each **communicate** their information and opinions to the others. They need not be in the same place: **video calls count**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔢", title:"Quorum (art.11)", body:"The **quorum** is the minimum number of directors who must take part for the meeting to make decisions. It is **2**, unless the directors fix a different number, which can **never be less than 2**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧍", title:"Sole director (art.7(2))", body:"A company with a **sole director** is the exception: that director can take decisions **alone**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"The number of directors falls below the quorum. What can they do?", body:"Only **appoint further directors**, or **call a general meeting** so the shareholders can appoint more (art.11(3))."},
   {t:"card", i:"🪑", title:"Chair (art.12)", body:"The directors may appoint one director to **chair** (run) their meetings."},
   {t:"card", i:"✋", title:"Voting (art.7)", body:"Decisions are taken by a **majority** of the directors **present and voting**, with **one vote each**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎯", title:"Casting vote (art.13)", body:"If votes are equal, the **chair** has a **casting vote** (a second, deciding vote). The chair loses it if they are not entitled to vote on that matter (e.g. because of art.14)."},
   {t:"card", i:"✍️", title:"Unanimous decisions without a meeting (art.8)", body:"The directors can decide without a meeting if **all eligible directors indicate they share a common view**, e.g. all sign a written resolution or agree by email."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗃️", title:"Records (art.15; s.248)", body:"The company must keep **minutes** of all board meetings and written records of directors' decisions for at least **10 years**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Directors' conflicts at board meetings (art.14)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🚫", title:"The interested director sits out", body:"A director who is **interested** in an actual or proposed transaction or arrangement with the company (e.g. selling the company something) is **not counted in the quorum** and **may not vote** on it (art.14(1))."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔓", title:"Exception 1: shareholders disapply art.14", body:"The shareholders can **disapply** (switch off) art.14 for that matter by **ordinary resolution** (art.14(3)(a))."},
   {t:"card", i:"🤷", title:"Exception 2: no likely conflict", body:"The director can vote and count if the interest **cannot reasonably be regarded as likely to give rise to a conflict** (art.14(3)(b))."},
   {t:"card", i:"📋", title:"Exception 3: permitted causes (art.14(4))", body:"- a **guarantee** given, or to be given, by or to the director for an obligation incurred by or for the company;\n- **subscribing** for (agreeing to buy new) or underwriting the company's shares or securities;\n- arrangements giving **benefits to employees and directors** that do not give the director any special benefit."},
   {t:"card", i:"👩‍⚖️", title:"Who decides a dispute about voting? (art.14(6))", body:"The **chair**, and the chair's decision is **final**, except about the chair's own interest."},
   {t:"card", i:"📢", title:"Declare AND don't vote", body:"Art.14 is separate from **declaring** the interest under **s.177** (see **Directors**). A director must **declare** the interest **and** (unless an exception applies) **not vote**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Two directors, one is interested. Can the board decide that item?", body:"**No.** Only one director counts, so the board is **inquorate** (below the quorum of 2) for that item. The usual answer: an **ordinary resolution of the shareholders** to **disapply art.14** (or to approve the matter)."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚠️", title:"Watch out: art.14 is a board rule only", body:"At a **general meeting**, a director who is also a shareholder can usually vote on matters they are interested in. The exceptions are where the Act excludes their votes, e.g. **ratification** (approving a breach of duty after the event, s.239) and **buyback** approval."}
 ]},
 {h:"Calling a general meeting", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🧑‍💼", title:"The directors call it (s.302)", body:"Normally the **directors** call a GM, by **board resolution**."},
   {t:"card", i:"✊", title:"Shareholders with 5% can require one (s.303)", body:"Shareholders holding at least **5%** of the paid-up voting share capital can **require** the directors to call a GM. The request must state the **general nature of the business** and may include the text of a resolution."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏱️", title:"The 21 / 28 day rule (s.304)", body:"The directors must call the meeting within **21 days** of the request, to be held within **28 days** of the notice of the meeting."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"The directors ignore a valid 5% request. What can the shareholders do?", body:"Call the meeting **themselves** and recover their **reasonable expenses** from the company (s.305)."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚖️", title:"The court can order a meeting (s.306)", body:"If it is **impracticable** to call a meeting otherwise, e.g. where a member deliberately stays away to make the meeting **inquorate**."},
   {t:"compare", i:"📅", title:"Annual general meeting (AGM)", cols:["", "🏠 Private company", "🏛️ Public company (plc)"], rows:[["Must hold an AGM?","❌ Only if its articles require it","✅ Within **6 months** of its financial year end (s.336)"],["AGM notice","n/a","**21 clear days**"]]}
 ]},
 {h:"Notice of a general meeting", cards:[
   {t:"compare", i:"📨", title:"How much notice? (s.307)", cols:["", "Minimum notice"], rows:[["🏠 Private company GM","**14 clear days**"],["🏛️ plc GM (not the AGM)","**14 clear days**"],["🏛️ plc AGM","**21 clear days**"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"📆", title:"Clear days (s.360)", body:"The notice period **excludes** both the **day the notice is given** (or deemed given) **and** the **day of the meeting**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📬", title:"Deemed receipt (s.1147)", body:"A notice sent by **post** or **electronically** is treated as received **48 hours** after it was sent, ignoring days that are not working days (unless the articles say otherwise)."},
   {t:"case", i:"🗓️", title:"Example: counting the days", facts:"Notice of a private company GM is **emailed on Monday 1 June**.", rule:"Deemed received **Wednesday 3 June**. Excluding 3 June and the meeting day, the 14 clear days run from **4 to 17 June**, so **the earliest meeting date is Thursday 18 June**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📝", title:"What the notice must contain (s.311, s.283, s.325)", body:"- the **date, time and place**;\n- the **general nature of the business**;\n- the **exact text** of any **special resolution**, described as a special resolution;\n- a statement of each member's right to appoint a **proxy** (someone who attends and votes for them)."},
   {t:"card", i:"👥", title:"Who gets notice (s.310)", body:"**Every member** and **every director** (and the **auditor**, s.502)."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚡", title:"Short notice (s.307(4)-(6))", body:"A GM can be held on **shorter notice** if agreed by a **majority in number** of the members entitled to attend and vote, who together hold at least:\n- **90%** of the voting shares in a **private company** (the articles may raise this to up to 95%);\n- **95%** in a **public company**.\nFor a plc **AGM**, short notice needs **all** members entitled to attend and vote (s.337(2))."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Three members; one holds 91%. Can the 91% holder agree to short notice alone?", body:"**No.** There are **two tests**: count **heads** (a majority of members by number) **and** count **shares** (90%+). One member out of three is not a majority in number."},
   {t:"card", i:"📄", title:"Members' statements (s.314)", body:"Members holding **5%** of the votes, or **100 members**, can require the company to circulate a **statement** about a proposed resolution or other business."}
 ]},
 {h:"Quorum, chair and proxies at a general meeting", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🔢", title:"GM quorum (s.318)", body:"**2 qualifying persons**: a member, a **proxy** appointed by a member, or a **corporate representative** (a person appointed by a company that is a member). If the company has only **one member**, the quorum is **1**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Do two proxies for the same member make a quorum?", body:"**No.** Two proxies of the **same** member, or two representatives of the **same** corporation, count as **one**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏳", title:"No quorum after half an hour (art.41)", body:"Under the Model Articles, if a quorum is not present within **half an hour**, the chair must **adjourn** (postpone) the meeting."},
   {t:"card", i:"🪑", title:"Chair of the GM (art.39)", body:"The **chair of the board** chairs the GM, or the directors present choose one."},
   {t:"card", i:"🙋", title:"Proxies (s.324)", body:"Every member can appoint a **proxy** to attend, **speak and vote** for them, on a show of hands and on a poll."},
   {t:"card", i:"⌛", title:"Proxy deadline (s.327)", body:"The articles may require the proxy appointment to reach the company before the meeting, but **not more than 48 hours** before (ignoring non-working days)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Voting at a general meeting", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"✋", title:"Show of hands (s.284)", body:"Each member present in person (and each proxy) has **one vote**, regardless of how many shares they hold. Under the Model Articles a resolution is decided this way **unless a poll is demanded**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗳️", title:"Poll (s.284(3))", body:"A **poll** is a vote that counts shares: each member has **one vote per share**."},
   {t:"compare", i:"📣", title:"Who can demand a poll?", cols:["", "s.321 (the minimum: articles cannot make it harder)", "Model Articles art.44"], rows:[["Members","At least **5 members** with the right to vote","**2 or more** persons with the right to vote"],["Share of votes","Members with at least **10%** of the voting rights","Person(s) with at least **10%** of the voting rights"],["Others","n/a","The **chair** or the **directors**"]]},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Hands are tied 2-2 at a private company GM. Does the chair have a casting vote?", body:"**No.** Under the private company Model Articles the **chair has no casting vote** at a general meeting, so the resolution **fails**. (Compare board meetings, art.13.)"},
   {t:"card", i:"🤝", title:"Unanimous informal consent", body:"If **all** the members entitled to vote **agree** to a matter, even informally and without a meeting, the decision is as valid as a resolution (the **Duomatic principle**, preserved by s.281(4)). It is a **fall-back** where formalities were missed."}
 ]},
 {h:"Written resolutions (private companies only)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"✉️", title:"Deciding without a meeting (ss.288-300)", body:"A **private company** can pass an ordinary or special resolution **without a meeting** by **written resolution**. **Public companies cannot.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"📤", title:"Who proposes it and who gets it", body:"The **directors** (or members with **5%** of the voting rights, s.292) propose it. It goes to **every eligible member** (those entitled to vote on the date it is first circulated) at the same time, with how to agree and the date it lapses."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧮", title:"Majority of ALL eligible votes", body:"The majority is of the **total voting rights of all eligible members**, not just those who reply:\n- **ordinary**: **more than 50%**;\n- **special**: at least **75%**.\nEach member has **one vote per share**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A member ignores a written resolution. How does that count?", body:"Effectively as a vote **against**, because the majority is of **all eligible votes**. At a **meeting**, the majority is of **votes cast**, so absent members don't count."},
   {t:"card", i:"⌛", title:"Passing and lapsing (s.296-297)", body:"It is **passed** as soon as the required majority **have signified agreement**. It **lapses** if not passed within **28 days** of circulation (unless the articles set another period). Agreement once given **cannot be revoked** (withdrawn)."},
   {t:"card", i:"⛔", title:"Two things a written resolution can't do (s.288(2))", body:"- **remove a director** under **s.168**;\n- **remove an auditor** before the end of their term under **s.510**.\nBoth need a meeting."},
   {t:"card", i:"🙅", title:"Interested members aren't eligible", body:"Where the Act disregards an interested member's votes (e.g. **buyback**, **ratification**), that member is **not an eligible member** for the written resolution."}
 ]},
 {h:"Ordinary and special resolutions", cards:[
   {t:"compare", i:"⚖️", title:"Ordinary vs special", cols:["", "Ordinary resolution", "Special resolution"], rows:[["At a meeting","**More than 50%** of votes cast (s.282)","**At least 75%** of votes cast (s.283)"],["In writing","More than 50% of **total eligible votes**","At least 75% of **total eligible votes**"],["Notice","14 clear days","14 clear days; **exact text** set out and stated to be a special resolution"],["Filed at Companies House?","Only where the Act requires (e.g. s.551 authority to allot)","**Always**, within **15 days** (s.30)"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"1️⃣", title:"Ordinary resolution examples", body:"**Removing a director** (s.168, at a meeting); **appointing** a director; **s.551 authority to allot** (issue) shares; approving **s.188, s.190, s.197 and s.217** transactions; approving a **buyback contract**; **ratification** (s.239); declaring a **final dividend**; disapplying **art.14**; removing an old authorised share capital cap."},
   {t:"card", i:"3️⃣", title:"Special resolution examples", body:"**Changing the articles** (s.21); **changing the name** (s.77); **disapplying pre-emption rights** (existing shareholders' right to be offered new shares first, ss.569-571); **buyback out of capital** (s.716); **reduction of capital**; **re-registration** (private to public or vice versa); **voluntary winding up** (IA 1986 s.84); shareholders **directing the board** (art.4)."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🥧", title:"What size of shareholding gives you power?", cols:["", "What it gives"], rows:[["**More than 50%**","Controls ordinary resolutions"],["**More than 25%**","Can **block** special resolutions"],["**75% or more**","Can pass special resolutions alone (at a meeting, depending on turnout)"]]},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Is a resolution passed by exactly 50% of the votes cast an ordinary resolution?", body:"**No.** An ordinary resolution needs **more than 50%**. Exactly 50% fails. (A special resolution passes at **exactly 75%**.)"}
 ]},
 {h:"Removing a director at a meeting (s.168)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🚪", title:"Ordinary resolution at a general meeting", body:"Shareholders can remove a director before the end of their term by **ordinary resolution at a general meeting**, whatever the articles or any agreement say (s.168(1)). Full detail is in **Directors**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📮", title:"Special notice: 28 days (s.312)", body:"The shareholder proposing the resolution must give the company **special notice** of it at least **28 days before the meeting**. The company then gives members notice of the resolution, normally with the notice of the meeting (at least **14 days** before)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗣️", title:"The director's right to be heard (s.169)", body:"The company sends the director a copy of the notice. The director can **speak at the meeting** and have **written representations** circulated to members (or read out at the meeting). That's why a **written resolution can't be used**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Transactions with directors needing shareholder approval", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"📃", title:"Long service contracts (s.188)", body:"A director's service contract with a **guaranteed term of more than 2 years** needs **prior approval by ordinary resolution**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📑", title:"The s.188 memorandum", body:"A memorandum of the contract's terms must be available for **15 days** before the meeting and at the meeting (or sent with a written resolution)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A 5-year guaranteed contract was never approved. What happens? (s.189)", body:"The **guaranteed term is void**, and the company can end the contract on **reasonable notice**."},
   {t:"card", i:"💷", title:"Loans to directors (s.197)", body:"A company may not make a **loan** to a director (of it or its holding company), or give a **guarantee or security** for a loan to them by someone else, unless approved by **ordinary resolution**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏛️", title:"Extra rules for public companies", body:"**Public companies** (and companies associated with them) also need approval for **quasi-loans**, **credit transactions** and loans to **connected persons**."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🆓", title:"Loans needing no approval", cols:["", "Limit (total)"], rows:[["Expenditure **on company business** (s.204)","**£50,000**"],["**Minor loans** (s.207)","**£10,000**"],["Money-lending company, in its ordinary course (s.209)","No set limit"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"↩️", title:"Unapproved loan (s.213)", body:"The loan is **voidable** (the company can choose to undo it). The director must **account** for (hand over) any gain and **indemnify** (make good) the company's loss."},
   {t:"card", i:"👋", title:"Payments for loss of office (ss.215-222)", body:"A payment to a director as compensation for **losing office**, beyond what they are legally entitled to, needs an **ordinary resolution** (s.217)."},
   {t:"card", i:"✅", title:"Loss of office: no approval needed", body:"- payments in **good faith** to meet an existing **legal obligation**, e.g. contractual damages (s.220);\n- small payments up to **£200** (s.221)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏠", title:"SPT: what it is (s.190)", body:"A **substantial property transaction (SPT)** is an arrangement where the company **acquires** a **substantial non-cash asset** from, or **transfers** one to, a **director** of the company or of its **holding company**, or a **person connected** with that director."},
   {t:"card", i:"📏", title:"SPT: when is an asset 'substantial'? (s.191)", body:"Its value:\n- **exceeds £100,000**; or\n- **exceeds 10% of the company's net assets** (per its latest accounts, or called-up share capital if there are none) **and is more than £5,000**.\nAssets worth **£5,000 or less** are never substantial."},
   {t:"card", i:"👨‍👩‍👧", title:"SPT: connected persons (ss.252-254)", body:"The director's **spouse or civil partner**; a person living with them in an **enduring family relationship**; their **children or stepchildren** (and under-18 children of a partner living with them); their **parents**; a **company** in which the director (with connected persons) holds at least **20%**; trustees of a trust benefiting them; their **business partners**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Is a director's brother a connected person?", body:"**No.** **Siblings** are not connected persons."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗳️", title:"SPT: approval", body:"An **ordinary resolution** of the members **before** the arrangement is entered into, **or** the arrangement must be made **conditional** on approval. If the person is a director of the **holding company**, its members must approve too."},
   {t:"card", i:"↩️", title:"SPT: without approval (s.195)", body:"The arrangement is **voidable** by the company, unless restitution is impossible, a third party has acquired rights in **good faith**, or members **affirm** it within a reasonable time. The director (and connected person) and any authorising directors must **account for any gain** and **indemnify** the company for any loss."},
   {t:"case", i:"🚐", title:"Example: Pine Ltd buys a van", facts:"Pine Ltd has net assets of **£400,000**. It agrees to buy a van worth **£45,000** from its director's **wife**.", rule:"**This is an SPT.** The wife is a connected person, and £45,000 exceeds 10% of net assets (£40,000) and £5,000. An **ordinary resolution** is needed first."},
   {t:"card", i:"✅", title:"Exam tip: the SPT four-step check", body:"**1** Is the other party a **director or connected person**? **2** Is it a **non-cash asset**? **3** Is it **substantial** (over £100,000, or over 10% of net assets and over £5,000)? **4** Was an **ordinary resolution** passed **before** (or the contract made conditional on one)?"}
 ]},
 {h:"Post-decision formalities", cards:[
   {t:"compare", i:"📮", title:"Filings at Companies House: resolutions", cols:["", "What to file", "Deadline"], rows:[["**Special resolution** (and certain ordinary ones, e.g. s.551 authority)","Copy of the resolution","**15 days** (s.30)"],["**Amended articles**","Copy of the new articles","**15 days** (s.26)"],["**Change of name**","Special resolution + **NM01**","15 days"]]},
   {t:"compare", i:"🧑‍💼", title:"Filings: officers and shares", cols:["", "Form", "Deadline"], rows:[["Director appointed / removed / details changed","**AP01 / TM01 / CH01**","**14 days** (s.167)"],["Secretary appointed / removed","**AP03 / TM02**","**14 days**"],["Allotment (issue) of shares","**SH01** (return of allotment)","**1 month** (s.555)"],["Charge (security) created","**MR01** + copy of the charge","**21 days** (s.859A)"]]},
   {t:"compare", i:"📆", title:"Filings: annual items", cols:["", "Form", "Deadline"], rows:[["Confirmation statement","**CS01**","At least every **12 months**, within **14 days** of the review date"],["Annual accounts","Accounts","**9 months** (private) / **6 months** (plc) after year end"]]},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"An ordinary resolution approves an SPT. Must it be filed?", body:"**No.** Ordinary resolutions are filed only where the Act requires (e.g. **s.551** authority). Keep it in the company's records."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗃️", title:"Records the company keeps", body:"- **minutes** of directors' meetings (s.248) and **records of resolutions and general meetings**, including written resolutions (s.355), for at least **10 years**;\n- the **register of members** (s.113), kept by the company itself;\n- copies of directors' **service contracts** (s.228), **indemnities** (s.237) and **charges** (s.859P)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📍", title:"Where records are kept", body:"At the **registered office** or a single alternative inspection location notified to Companies House. Members may inspect them (most free of charge)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🆕", title:"Registers now at Companies House only", body:"Since **18 November 2025**, the registers of **directors**, directors' residential addresses, **secretaries** and **PSCs** (people with significant control) are **no longer kept by the company**; they are on the Companies House register."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗺️", title:"Your route through any question", body:"**1** Board or shareholders? → **2** Which resolution (board, ordinary, special)? → **3** Meeting or written resolution? → **4** Notice, quorum and votes (watch art.14) → **5** Board meeting 2 to carry it out → **6** File and record."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Source: proposals/rooms/learn-blp-distributions-accounts.js */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["blp/distributions-accounts"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🧁", title:"Why shareholders invest", body:"Shareholders get a return on their investment mainly through **dividends**: payments of the company's profits to its shareholders."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏦", title:"Creditors rely on the same pot", body:"A company's capital and profits are also the fund its **creditors** (the people it owes money to) rely on to be paid. Money paid out to shareholders is money creditors can no longer reach."},
   {t:"card", i:"📏", title:"The rule: only pay out real profits", body:"So the **Companies Act 2006 (CA 2006) Part 23** only lets a company pay **distributions** out of **profits available for distribution**, measured by its **accounts**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📚", title:"The accounting side", body:"Every company must also keep proper **accounting records**, prepare **annual accounts** that give a **true and fair view**, send them to its members and **file** them at **Companies House** (the public register of companies) on time."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗺️", title:"What this topic covers", body:"What a distribution is, the **distributable profits** test, how **dividends** are declared and paid, what happens after an **unlawful dividend**, **accounting records**, **annual accounts** (size categories, audit, deadlines) and reading basic accounts."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔗", title:"Covered elsewhere", body:"How dividends are **taxed**: **Income tax** (for shareholders) and **Corporation tax** (for the company). How a **buyback** of shares is funded: **Equity finance**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Why does the law limit what a company can pay its shareholders?", body:"Because the company's capital and profits are the fund its **creditors** rely on. A company may only pay distributions out of **profits available for distribution**."}
 ]},
 {h:"What is a distribution? (s.829)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🎁", title:"Distribution: the definition", body:"A **distribution** is **every description of distribution of a company's assets to its members**, whether in cash or otherwise (s.829). **Members** means shareholders."},
   {t:"card", i:"💷", title:"The main example: a dividend", body:"The main example of a distribution is a **dividend**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚫", title:"Not distributions for these rules", body:"Four things are **not** distributions for these rules: (1) issuing fully or partly paid **bonus shares** (free new shares paid for out of the company's reserves); (2) a lawful **reduction of capital**; (3) a **redemption or buyback** of the company's own shares under **Part 18** paid for out of capital (including the proceeds of a fresh share issue) or unrealised profits, because Part 18 has its own funding rules; (4) a distribution of assets on a **winding up** (when the company is closed down)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🕵️", title:"Disguised returns still count", body:"A return to members can be a distribution even if it isn't called a dividend. Example: a company **sells an asset to a shareholder at an undervalue** (for less than it is worth)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A company issues bonus shares to all its shareholders. Is that a distribution under Part 23?", body:"**No.** Issuing fully or partly paid **bonus shares** is expressly **not** a distribution for these rules."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A company sells its van, worth £20,000, to a shareholder for £2,000. Can that be a distribution?", body:"**Yes.** Selling an asset to a shareholder **at an undervalue** is a disguised return to a member, so it can be a distribution."}
 ]},
 {h:"The distributable profits test (s.830)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"📏", title:"The basic rule (s.830(1))", body:"A company may make a distribution **only out of profits available for the purpose**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧮", title:"The formula (s.830(2))", body:"**Profits available for distribution** = **accumulated, realised profits** (so far as not already distributed or capitalised) **less accumulated, realised losses** (so far as not already written off in a reduction or reorganisation of capital)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📜", title:"'Accumulated' = the whole history", body:"**Accumulated** means you look at the **whole history** of the company, not just this year. A profit this year cannot be paid out if there are **losses brought forward** (losses from earlier years) that it has not yet cleared."},
   {t:"card", i:"✅", title:"'Realised' = actually made", body:"**Realised** means profits actually made: from trading, or from actually selling an asset. This is decided under generally accepted accounting principles."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔮", title:"Unrealised gains don't count", body:"An **unrealised** gain is not distributable. Example: an increase in the book value of land after a **revaluation** (the land is valued higher, but nothing has been sold)."},
   {t:"case", i:"🌳", title:"Example: Ash Ltd", facts:"Ash Ltd made realised trading profits of **£40,000** this year. It had accumulated realised losses of **£55,000** from the two previous years and has never reduced capital. Its land has been revalued upwards by **£100,000**.", rule:"**Ash Ltd cannot pay any dividend.** Accumulated realised profits less losses are **minus £15,000**, and the revaluation gain is unrealised, so it does not count."},
   {t:"card", i:"💡", title:"Exam tip: losses brought forward and revaluations", body:"Dividend questions usually turn on **losses brought forward** or a **revaluation**. Add up **all realised profits** and **all realised losses** since incorporation and ignore unrealised gains. If the net figure is nil or negative, no lawful dividend can be paid this year, however profitable this year is."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A private company has realised losses of £30,000 brought forward and a realised profit of £50,000 this year. What is the most it can distribute?", body:"**£20,000.** Accumulated realised profits (£50,000) less accumulated realised losses (£30,000)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏛️", title:"Plc: the extra net assets test (s.831)", body:"A **public company (plc)** must **also** pass the **net assets test**. After the distribution, its **net assets** (total assets minus total liabilities) must be **not less than** its **called-up share capital plus undistributable reserves**, and it can only distribute to the extent it doesn't go below that."},
   {t:"card", i:"📉", title:"What the net assets test adds", body:"The net assets test means a plc must also cover any **unrealised losses**. A private company only needs to pass the s.830 test."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔒", title:"Undistributable reserves", body:"These can never be paid out: the **share premium account** (money paid for shares above their nominal value), the **capital redemption reserve**, and the excess of accumulated unrealised profits over unrealised losses (e.g. a **revaluation reserve**)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Does a private limited company have to pass the net assets test?", body:"**No.** The net assets test in **s.831** applies only to **public companies**. A private company only needs enough profits available for distribution under s.830."},
   {t:"card", i:"📒", title:"Which accounts decide it? (ss.836-839)", body:"Whether a distribution is lawful is judged by reference to the company's **last annual accounts** circulated to members (the **relevant accounts**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗓️", title:"Interim and initial accounts", body:"If the last annual accounts don't show enough profits, the company may use **interim accounts** (management accounts showing the up-to-date position). If it hasn't produced annual accounts yet, it uses **initial accounts**. A plc's interim and initial accounts must be **filed at Companies House**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Last annual accounts show too little profit, but trading since has been strong. Any way to pay a dividend now?", body:"**Yes.** The company can rely on **interim accounts** showing the up-to-date position (filed at Companies House if it is a plc)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Declaring and paying dividends", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"📖", title:"The rulebook: Model Articles", body:"The procedure is in the company's articles. Under the **Model Articles** (the default articles for a private company), **art.30** sets out how dividends are declared."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎂", title:"Final dividend: who decides", body:"A **final dividend** is **recommended by the directors** and then **declared by ordinary resolution** of the shareholders (a vote passed by more than 50%) (art.30)."},
   {t:"card", i:"⛔", title:"Shareholders can't increase it", body:"The shareholders **cannot declare a dividend larger** than the amount the directors recommend (art.30). They can approve a smaller one, or none."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚡", title:"Interim dividend: directors alone", body:"An **interim dividend** may be **decided and paid by the directors alone** (art.30), without shareholder approval, but only if there are enough profits available for distribution (s.830)."},
   {t:"compare", i:"⚖️", title:"Final vs interim dividend", cols:["", "Final", "Interim"], rows:[["Who decides?","Directors recommend; shareholders declare by ordinary resolution","Directors alone"],["Can shareholders go higher?","❌ Not above the recommendation","Not involved"],["When is it a debt?","Once declared","Only once paid"],["Can it be cancelled?","❌ Not once declared","✅ By the directors before payment"]]},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"The board recommends a £10,000 final dividend. Can the shareholders declare £15,000?", body:"**No.** Under the Model Articles, shareholders can **only declare up to** the amount the directors recommend. They could declare £10,000 or less."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚖️", title:"Paid in proportion to shareholdings", body:"Unless the shareholders' resolution or the terms of issue say otherwise, dividends are **declared and paid by reference to each shareholder's holding** on the date of the resolution or decision (art.30(4)), in proportion to the shares held."},
   {t:"card", i:"💳", title:"Paid in cash, usually", body:"Dividends are paid in cash (e.g. by bank transfer) unless the articles allow non-cash dividends. Model Articles **art.34** allows a **non-cash distribution** if approved by **ordinary resolution** on the directors' recommendation."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏳", title:"No interest; 12-year forfeit", body:"**No interest** is payable on a dividend (art.32). A dividend left **unclaimed for 12 years** may be **forfeited** (taken back by the company) (art.33)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚪", title:"No right until declared", body:"Shareholders have **no right** to a dividend until one is declared."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧾", title:"Declared final dividend = a debt", body:"Once a **final dividend is declared**, it becomes a **debt** owed by the company to the shareholders, payable on the date stated."},
   {t:"card", i:"↩️", title:"Interim dividend can be cancelled", body:"An **interim dividend** decided by the directors can be **varied or cancelled** by them before it is paid. It is not a debt until paid."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"The directors decided an interim dividend last week but haven't paid it. Can they cancel it?", body:"**Yes.** An interim dividend is not a debt until paid, so the directors can **vary or cancel** it before payment."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧭", title:"Directors' duties still apply", body:"Before recommending or paying a dividend, directors must check the distributable profits **and** consider their wider duties: especially **s.172** (promoting the success of the company, e.g. keeping cash for investment)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🆘", title:"Near insolvency: think of creditors", body:"If the company is insolvent or close to it, directors must consider **creditors' interests** (BTI v Sequana, 2022). A dividend may be lawful under s.830 yet still a **breach of duty** if it leaves the company unable to pay its debts."},
   {t:"card", i:"🥇", title:"Preference shares", body:"A fixed preferential dividend on **preference shares** must still be **declared**, and paid only from distributable profits. Under the terms of issue it ranks **before** any ordinary dividend."},
   {t:"card", i:"💡", title:"Exam tip: two classic wrong options", body:"Wrong options often say the shareholders can **increase** the dividend the board recommended, or that an **interim** dividend needs an **ordinary resolution**. Both are wrong under the Model Articles."}
 ]},
 {h:"Unlawful distributions", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🙋", title:"Shareholders: when they must repay (s.847)", body:"A shareholder who receives a distribution made in breach of Part 23 must **repay it** (or its value) to the company **if, at the time, they knew or had reasonable grounds for believing** it was made in breach of the rules."},
   {t:"case", i:"🎁", title:"Example: It's a Wrap (UK) Ltd v Gula (2006)", facts:"Two shareholder-directors paid themselves 'dividends' when the company had no profits, on their accountant's advice, to save tax. The company went into liquidation. They said they hadn't known dividends could not lawfully be paid without profits.", rule:"They had to **repay**. It is enough that the shareholder **knew the facts** that made the dividend unlawful. **Ignorance of the law** is no defence."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧓", title:"The innocent shareholder keeps it", body:"A shareholder who did not know, and had no reason to believe, that the dividend was unlawful (e.g. a **passive outside investor**) **keeps it**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A director-shareholder knew there were no profits but didn't know the law banned the dividend. Must they repay?", body:"**Yes.** Knowing the **facts** that made it unlawful is enough under **s.847**. Not knowing the law is no defence."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧑‍💼", title:"Directors who authorise it: breach of duty", body:"Directors who **authorise** an unlawful distribution are in **breach of duty**: acting outside their powers (**s.171**), and possibly **s.172** and **s.174** (reasonable care, skill and diligence)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔗", title:"Directors repay the whole amount", body:"They are **jointly and severally liable** to repay the company the **full amount** of the unlawful distribution, with interest, **whether or not they received it** themselves. 'Jointly and severally' means each director can be made to pay all of it."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛟", title:"Court relief (s.1157)", body:"The court may **relieve** a director if they acted **honestly and reasonably**. Example: they relied on accounts prepared by competent professionals that turned out to be wrong."},
   {t:"card", i:"🤝", title:"Getting a contribution", body:"A director who has to repay can seek a **contribution** from shareholders who are liable under **s.847**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚰️", title:"On insolvency", body:"When the company is insolvent, a **liquidator** (the person who winds it up) commonly pursues these claims, often through **misfeasance** proceedings (a claim against officers for misusing company money) under **Insolvency Act 1986 (IA 1986) s.212**."},
   {t:"compare", i:"⚖️", title:"Who repays an unlawful dividend?", cols:["", "Must repay?"], rows:[["Shareholder who knew or had reasonable grounds to believe","✅ Yes (s.847)"],["Shareholder who knew the facts but not the law","✅ Yes"],["Innocent passive shareholder","❌ Keeps it"],["Director who authorised it","✅ Full amount, even if they got nothing (unless relieved under s.1157)"]]}
 ]},
 {h:"Accounting records (ss.386-389)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"📓", title:"Adequate accounting records (s.386)", body:"Every company must keep **adequate accounting records**: enough to **show and explain its transactions**, to disclose with **reasonable accuracy** its financial position **at any time**, and to let the directors make sure the accounts comply with the Act."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧾", title:"What the records contain", body:"**Day-to-day entries** of all money received and spent (and what for), and a record of **assets and liabilities**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📦", title:"Companies dealing in goods", body:"If the company deals in goods, the records must also include statements of **stock** held at the year end and of goods bought and sold, with buyers and sellers identified (except for **retail** sales)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔑", title:"Where kept and who can look (s.388)", body:"Kept at the **registered office** or another place the directors think fit, and **open to inspection by the officers** (directors and secretary) at all times. **Shareholders** have **no statutory right** to inspect them."},
   {t:"compare", i:"⏳", title:"How long to keep them (s.388(4))", cols:["", "Private company", "Public company"], rows:[["Keep accounting records for","3 years from when made","6 years from when made"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"🏛️", title:"HMRC's separate rule", body:"Separately, **HMRC** (HM Revenue & Customs) requires company **tax** records to be kept for **6 years** from the end of the accounting period."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚨", title:"Failing to keep records is a crime (s.387)", body:"Failure to keep adequate records is a **criminal offence** by every officer in default, unless they acted honestly and the default was excusable."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A shareholder demands to see the company's day-to-day accounting records. Must the company let them?", body:"**No.** The records are open to the company's **officers**. Shareholders have **no statutory right** to inspect them."}
 ]},
 {h:"Annual accounts, financial year and size", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"📊", title:"Individual accounts (ss.394, 396)", body:"The directors must prepare **individual accounts** for each **financial year**: a **balance sheet** as at the last day of the year, a **profit and loss account**, and **notes**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔍", title:"A true and fair view (s.393)", body:"The accounts must give a **true and fair view** of the company's assets, liabilities, financial position and profit or loss. The directors must **not approve** accounts unless satisfied of this."},
   {t:"card", i:"📝", title:"Directors' report and strategic report", body:"A **directors' report** (s.415) is required (micro-entities need not file one; small companies have a simplified version). A **strategic report** (s.414A) is also required unless the company qualifies as **small**."},
   {t:"card", i:"👪", title:"Group accounts", body:"A **parent company** must also prepare **group accounts** unless it is exempt (e.g. a small group)."},
   {t:"card", i:"✍️", title:"Approved and signed (s.414)", body:"The accounts must be **approved by the board** and **signed** on its behalf by a director, with the director's name stated on the **balance sheet**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎂", title:"Accounting reference date (s.391)", body:"The **accounting reference date (ARD)** is the date the financial year ends. By default it is the **last day of the month** in which the **anniversary of incorporation** falls."},
   {t:"case", i:"🎈", title:"Example: incorporated 10 March", facts:"A company is incorporated on **10 March**.", rule:"Its ARD is **31 March**: the last day of the month in which the anniversary of incorporation falls. Its first financial year runs from 10 March to 31 March the following year."},
   {t:"card", i:"📅", title:"The first financial year", body:"The **first financial year** begins on incorporation and ends on the ARD. So it can be **more than 12 months** (up to **18 months**), but not less than 6 months."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔁", title:"Changing the ARD (s.392, form AA01)", body:"The company can **shorten** its financial year as often as it wants. It can **extend** it only so that the period is **no more than 18 months**, and not more than **once in 5 years** (with exceptions, e.g. to line up with a group company, or if in administration)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"The company extended its financial year two years ago. Can it extend again now to 18 months?", body:"**Not usually.** An extension is allowed only **once in 5 years** (unless an exception applies, e.g. aligning with a group company or administration)."},
   {t:"compare", i:"📐", title:"Size categories (years from 6 April 2025)", cols:["", "Turnover up to", "Balance sheet total up to", "Average employees up to"], rows:[["Micro-entity","£1m","£500,000","10"],["Small","£15m","£7.5m","50"],["Medium","£54m","£27m","250"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"✌️", title:"Two out of three", body:"A company qualifies for a size category if it meets **at least 2 of the 3** conditions (in its first year; after that, generally in **two consecutive years**). **Public companies** can never be small or micro."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A private company has turnover of £12m, a balance sheet total of £9m and 40 employees. Is it small?", body:"**Yes.** It meets **2 of the 3** small conditions: turnover under £15m and employees under 50. The balance sheet total over £7.5m doesn't matter."},
   {t:"card", i:"🪶", title:"Simplified accounts for small and micro", body:"**Small and micro** companies can prepare and file **simplified** accounts. For example, a small company can choose **not to file** its profit and loss account or directors' report at Companies House (**filleted accounts**)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Audit, sending out and filing", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🔎", title:"What an audit is", body:"An **audit** is an independent examination of the accounts by a qualified **auditor**, who reports on whether they give a true and fair view."},
   {t:"card", i:"🆓", title:"Who is exempt (s.477)", body:"**Small** companies are generally **exempt** from audit, as are qualifying **dormant** companies (not trading) and certain **subsidiaries**."},
   {t:"card", i:"✋", title:"10% can demand an audit (s.476)", body:"Members holding at least **10%** of the nominal value of the issued shares (or of any class) can **require an audit**, by notice given **no later than one month before the end of the financial year**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏛️", title:"Who must be audited", body:"**Public companies**, and companies that are not small, must be audited."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗳️", title:"Appointing an auditor (s.485)", body:"A private company's auditor is appointed by the **members** by **ordinary resolution**, or by the directors in some cases."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚪", title:"Removing an auditor (s.510)", body:"An auditor can be **removed** by **ordinary resolution at a meeting** with **special notice** (28 days' notice to the company of the intention to propose it). A **written resolution cannot be used**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Shareholders want to remove the auditor by written resolution. Can they?", body:"**No.** Removing an auditor needs an **ordinary resolution at a meeting** with **special notice** (s.510). A written resolution cannot be used."},
   {t:"card", i:"📨", title:"Sending accounts to members (ss.423-424)", body:"A **private** company must send its annual accounts and reports to **every member** (and debenture holder) no later than the date it actually files them, or the filing deadline if earlier. A member can ask for another copy free (s.431)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎤", title:"Plc: laid before the AGM (s.437)", body:"A **public** company must **lay** its accounts before the members at a general meeting (the **AGM**, annual general meeting). A private company need not."},
   {t:"compare", i:"📮", title:"Filing deadlines at Companies House (s.442)", cols:["", "Private", "Public"], rows:[["Normal deadline after the end of the accounting reference period","9 months","6 months"],["First accounts covering more than 12 months","21 months from incorporation","18 months from incorporation"],["...or, if later","3 months from end of period","3 months from end of period"]]},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A private company's year ended 31 December 2025. When are its accounts due at Companies House?", body:"By **30 September 2026**: **9 months** after the end of the accounting reference period."},
   {t:"card", i:"💸", title:"Late filing penalties (s.453)", body:"**Automatic** civil penalties on the company, rising with the length of the delay and **doubled** if the accounts were also late the previous year."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚠️", title:"Further consequences", body:"Failure to file is also an **offence** by the directors (s.451). Persistent failure can lead to the company being **struck off** the register or the directors being **disqualified**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔜", title:"Not yet in force (ECCTA 2023)", body:"Under the **Economic Crime and Corporate Transparency Act 2023 (ECCTA 2023)**, **software-only** filing of accounts and some other accounts changes are planned (currently from **April 2028**) but are **not yet in force**. Companies will still be able to file **abridged** accounts and to **fillet** (leave out) the profit and loss account: the plan to make small and micro companies file it has been **paused**. Apply the current rules."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🧩", title:"LLPs, partnerships and sole traders", cols:["", "Prepare accounts?", "File publicly?"], rows:[["LLP","✅ (audited unless small)","✅ 9-month deadline"],["Partnership / sole trader","✅ for tax","❌ Nothing"]]}
 ]},
 {h:"Reading basic accounts", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🎞️", title:"Profit and loss account: the year's performance", body:"The **profit and loss account** (or **income statement**) shows how the company performed **over the year**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🪜", title:"P&L step 1: gross profit", body:"**Turnover** (sales) less **cost of sales** = **gross profit**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🪜", title:"P&L step 2: operating profit", body:"Gross profit less **overheads** (administrative and distribution expenses, including **depreciation**) = **operating profit**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🪜", title:"P&L step 3: profit after tax", body:"Operating profit less **interest** = **profit before tax**. Less **corporation tax** = **profit after tax** (profit for the year), which can be paid out as dividends or kept."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Turnover is £500k, cost of sales is £300k and overheads are £120k. What are the gross profit and the operating profit?", body:"**Gross profit £200k** (£500k − £300k). **Operating profit £80k** (£200k − £120k)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📸", title:"Balance sheet: a snapshot", body:"The **balance sheet** (statement of financial position) is a **snapshot** on the last day of the year."},
   {t:"card", i:"➕", title:"Balance sheet top half: net assets", body:"**Fixed (non-current) assets** (land, machinery, goodwill) + **current assets** (stock, debtors, cash) − **current liabilities** (creditors due within a year, e.g. trade creditors, overdraft) − **long-term liabilities** (loans due after a year) = **net assets**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚖️", title:"Balance sheet bottom half: capital and reserves", body:"**Share capital** + **share premium** + other reserves (e.g. **revaluation reserve**) + **retained earnings** = the same figure as net assets. That's why the balance sheet 'balances'."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎯", title:"Where to find distributable profits", body:"**Retained earnings** (the **profit and loss reserve**: accumulated profits not yet distributed) is the usual starting point for finding **realised** profits available for distribution. A **revaluation reserve** is **unrealised**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"The balance sheet shows retained earnings of £60k and a revaluation reserve of £90k. What is the starting point for the dividend?", body:"**£60k** (retained earnings). The **revaluation reserve** is unrealised, so it cannot be paid out."},
   {t:"card", i:"📉", title:"Depreciation", body:"**Depreciation** spreads the cost of a fixed asset over its useful life as an expense in the profit and loss account. It is **not deductible for tax**: capital allowances are given instead (see **Corporation tax**)."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Source: proposals/rooms/learn-blp-equity-finance.js */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["blp/equity-finance"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🍫", title:"Two ways to raise money", body:"A company can raise money by **equity** (selling **shares**, so investors become part-owners) or by **debt** (borrowing, so lenders become **creditors**, people the company owes money to). Debt is covered in **Debt finance and security**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗺️", title:"What this topic covers", body:"What shares are, **classes of share**, the four-question procedure for **allotting** (issuing) new shares, **pre-emption rights**, the company **buying back** its own shares (including **out of capital**), **redeeming** redeemable shares, and how the **selling shareholder is taxed** on a buyback."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚫", title:"What is NOT tested", body:"The SQE1 specification **excludes** other **reductions of share capital** and **financial assistance** (a company helping someone buy its own shares). Only **buyback** and **redemption** are tested."},
   {t:"compare", i:"⚖️", title:"Equity versus debt: who, return, repayment", cols:["", "Equity (shares)", "Debt (loans)"], rows:[["Investor is","👥 A **member** (owner)","🏦 A **creditor**"],["Return","**Dividends**, only if there are distributable profits and they are declared; capital growth","**Interest**, payable whether or not the company makes profits"],["Repayment","Not normally repaid while the company exists (except buyback, redemption)","Repayable on agreed terms or on default"]]},
   {t:"compare", i:"🔐", title:"Equity versus debt: control, security, insolvency, tax", cols:["", "Equity (shares)", "Debt (loans)"], rows:[["Control","🗳️ Usually **votes** at general meetings","❌ **No vote**, but control through **covenants** (promises) in the loan agreement"],["Security","None","Often **secured** (fixed and floating charges)"],["On insolvency","Paid **last**, after all creditors","Paid before shareholders; secured lenders first"],["Tax for the company","Dividends come from taxed profits: **not deductible**","**Interest is deductible** from profits for corporation tax"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"🪢", title:"The trade-off", body:"Equity **dilutes** (shrinks the percentage held by) the existing owners, but does not burden the company with fixed payments. Debt keeps control with the owners, but must be **serviced** (interest paid)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Which is paid first on insolvency: shareholders or lenders?", body:"**Lenders.** Creditors are paid before shareholders (secured lenders first). Shareholders are paid **last**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Can a company deduct dividends from its profits for corporation tax?", body:"**No.** Dividends are paid from taxed profits. **Interest** on a loan, by contrast, **is deductible**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Share basics", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🪙", title:"Nominal (par) value", body:"The fixed **face value** of each share, stated when it is created (e.g. £1). Every share must have one (**s.542** of the **Companies Act 2006**, CA 2006)."},
   {t:"card", i:"✨", title:"Share premium", body:"Any amount paid for a share **above** its nominal value. A £1 share issued for £3 has a **£2 premium**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔒", title:"The share premium account", body:"The premium must be credited to the **share premium account** (s.610). It is **not distributable** (cannot be used for dividends) and can only be used for limited purposes, e.g. paying up **bonus shares** or the **expenses of the issue**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A £1 share is issued for £3. Can the £2 be paid out as a dividend?", body:"**No.** The £2 premium goes to the **share premium account**, which is **not distributable**."},
   {t:"card", i:"💷", title:"Paid up", body:"How much of the issue price has been paid. Shareholders are liable for any **unpaid** amount (see **Business structures**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"⛔", title:"No discount", body:"Shares **cannot be allotted at a discount** to their nominal value (**s.580**). If they are, the **allottee** (the person receiving the shares) must pay the discount plus **interest**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎁", title:"Paying in money or money's worth", body:"Shares may be paid for in **money or money's worth**, e.g. assets, services or **goodwill** (the value of a business's reputation and customers) (**s.582**). Public companies have extra valuation rules for non-cash payment (outline only)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📏", title:"Issued share capital: no maximum", body:"**Issued share capital** is the total nominal value of shares actually issued. There is **no maximum** under CA 2006 unless the **articles** (the company's rule book) impose one."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Can a £1 share be issued for 80p?", body:"**No.** That is a **discount** to nominal value, banned by **s.580**. The allottee would have to pay the 20p plus interest."}
 ]},
 {h:"Classes of share", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🟫", title:"Ordinary shares", body:"The normal shares. Usually **one vote per share**, a right to **dividends as and when declared** (no fixed amount), and a right to the **surplus** on a solvent winding up, after everyone else."},
   {t:"card", i:"🥇", title:"Preference shares", body:"A right to a **fixed dividend** (e.g. 6% of nominal value a year) paid **before** any ordinary dividend, and usually **priority** for return of capital on a winding up. They often carry **no vote** (or votes only if the dividend is **in arrears**, meaning unpaid)."},
   {t:"card", i:"➕", title:"Cumulative preference shares", body:"Any unpaid preference dividend is **carried forward** and must be paid before ordinary shareholders get anything. Preference shares are **presumed cumulative** unless stated otherwise."},
   {t:"card", i:"🍰", title:"Participating preference shares", body:"They also **share in surplus profits** beyond the fixed dividend."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"The terms don't say whether preference shares are cumulative. Are they?", body:"**Yes.** Preference shares are **presumed cumulative**, so missed dividends carry forward."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔁", title:"Redeemable shares", body:"Issued on terms that they **will be, or may be**, **redeemed** (bought back) by the company at a future date, or at the option of the company or the shareholder."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔇", title:"Other variants", body:"**Non-voting shares**, shares with **weighted votes**, or shares carrying the **right to appoint a director** are all possible."},
   {t:"card", i:"📜", title:"Where class rights live", body:"The rights of each class are set out in the **articles** or the **terms of issue**. Creating a **new class** usually needs **amending the articles** by **special resolution** (75% of votes cast), if the articles don't already provide for it, plus **s.551 authority** to allot."},
   {t:"card", i:"✋", title:"Varying class rights (s.630)", body:"Needs the **consent of the class**: either (a) **written consent** of holders of at least **75%** in nominal value of that class's issued shares, or (b) a **special resolution** passed at a **separate meeting** of that class. The articles can provide a different procedure."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏳", title:"Objecting to a variation (s.633)", body:"Holders of at least **15%** of the class who did not consent can apply to **court** within **21 days** to have the variation cancelled."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"How can preference shareholders' rights be varied?", body:"With the **class's consent**: written consent of **75%** in nominal value of the class, or a **special resolution at a separate class meeting** (s.630). Dissenters holding **15%** can go to court within **21 days**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Allotting shares: questions 1 and 2", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🆕", title:"What allotment means", body:"**Allotment** is when a person acquires the **unconditional right to be entered in the register of members** for **new** shares. A **transfer** of existing shares is different (see **Shareholders' rights**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"4️⃣", title:"The four questions", body:"**1** Is there a cap in the articles? **2** Do the directors have authority to allot? **3** Do pre-emption rights apply? **4** Board resolution and filings."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧢", title:"Q1: no cap under CA 2006", body:"Under CA 2006 there is **no limit** on the number of shares a company can issue, and the **Model Articles** (the default articles) contain none."},
   {t:"card", i:"🕰️", title:"Q1: old companies (before 1 October 2009)", body:"They had an **authorised share capital** in their memorandum. This is now treated as a provision of the articles. If the allotment would exceed it, the company must **remove or increase** it by **ordinary resolution** (more than 50% of votes) under the transitional rules."},
   {t:"card", i:"✂️", title:"Q1: other restrictions", body:"Any other restriction on allotment in the articles (e.g. bespoke articles) must be removed by **special resolution**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A 2005 company's authorised share capital is too small. What resolution removes it?", body:"An **ordinary resolution** (transitional rules). Other restrictions in the articles need a **special resolution**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔑", title:"Q2: directors need authority (s.549)", body:"Directors may allot shares **only if authorised**. Authority comes from **s.550** or **s.551**."},
   {t:"card", i:"1️⃣", title:"Q2: s.550, the easy case", body:"In a **private company with only one class of shares**, the directors may allot more shares **of that class** **unless prohibited by the articles**. **No resolution is needed.** The Model Articles do not prohibit it."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗳️", title:"Q2: s.551, every other case", body:"A **public company**, a private company with **more than one class**, or one creating a **new class**: authority must come from the **articles** or an **ordinary resolution** of the members."},
   {t:"card", i:"📆", title:"Q2: what a s.551 authority must state", body:"The **maximum number** of shares that may be allotted and the **expiry date**, **no more than 5 years** from the resolution (or incorporation). It can be **renewed** for further periods of up to 5 years."},
   {t:"card", i:"📮", title:"Q2: file the s.551 resolution", body:"A s.551 ordinary resolution **must be filed** at **Companies House** within **15 days** (s.551(9), s.30), even though it is only an ordinary resolution."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚨", title:"Q2: allotting without authority", body:"A **criminal offence** by the directors (s.549(4)), but it does **not invalidate** the allotment."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A private company with only ordinary shares (Model Articles) wants to issue more ordinary shares. Do members need to pass a resolution for authority?", body:"**No.** **s.550** gives the directors authority (private, one class, same class, not prohibited by the articles)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Does a s.551 ordinary resolution have to be filed?", body:"**Yes**, within **15 days**, even though it is an ordinary resolution."}
 ]},
 {h:"Pre-emption rights and finishing the allotment (questions 3 and 4)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🙋", title:"Q3: pre-emption (s.561)", body:"Before allotting **equity securities** (in practice **ordinary shares**) **wholly for cash**, the company must first **offer** them to the existing **ordinary shareholders** **in proportion** to their holdings, on the same or more favourable terms. This protects them from **dilution**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📨", title:"Q3: the offer (s.562)", body:"The offer must be **in writing** and stay open for at least **14 days**. Shares not taken up can then be offered to others."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚪", title:"Q3: when pre-emption does NOT apply", body:"Shares allotted for **non-cash consideration**, e.g. for an asset or another company's shares (s.565); **bonus shares**, fully paid shares issued free to members out of reserves (s.564); shares under an **employees' share scheme** (s.566); and **preference shares** with limited rights to dividends and capital (not 'equity securities')."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Shares are issued to a seller in exchange for her land. Is a pre-emption disapplication resolution needed?", body:"**No.** Shares issued for an **asset** are **non-cash**, so outside s.561. Pre-emption only bites on **cash** issues of ordinary shares."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚫", title:"Q3: excluding pre-emption", body:"A **private company's articles** can **exclude** pre-emption entirely (**s.567**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔓", title:"Q3: disapplying pre-emption (ss.569-570)", body:"**s.569**: a private company with **one class** (allotting under s.550) can disapply by the **articles** or **special resolution**. **s.570**: where directors have a **general s.551 authority**, disapply generally by **articles** or **special resolution**, for as long as that authority lasts."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎯", title:"Q3: disapplying for one allotment (s.571)", body:"A **special resolution** for a **specific allotment**, recommended by the directors with a **written statement** of their reasons, the amount to be paid and its justification, circulated with the notice or written resolution."},
   {t:"card", i:"🤝", title:"Q3: waiver", body:"Members can simply **waive** their rights (decline the offer **in writing**). Common in small companies."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚠️", title:"Q3: breach (s.563)", body:"The company and every officer who knowingly authorised it are **jointly and severally liable to compensate** the shareholders who should have been offered shares (claims within **2 years**). The allotment **remains valid**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Shares were issued for cash without a pre-emption offer. Is the allotment void?", body:"**No.** It stays **valid**. The shareholders left out can claim **compensation** from the company and the officers who knowingly authorised it, within **2 years** (s.563)."},
   {t:"card", i:"✅", title:"Q4: board resolution to allot", body:"The **board** passes a resolution to **allot** the shares, after any shareholder resolutions."},
   {t:"card", i:"📬", title:"Q4: filings and registers", body:"Return of allotment (**form SH01**) with a **statement of capital** within **1 month** (s.555). Enter the shareholder in the **register of members** within **2 months** (s.554) and issue **share certificates** within **2 months** (s.769). File any special resolution, and the s.551 ordinary resolution, within **15 days**, plus amended articles if changed."},
   {t:"card", i:"¼", title:"Q4: plc paying up", body:"A **plc** must have the shares paid up to at least **one quarter** of nominal value plus the **whole premium** (s.586)."},
   {t:"case", i:"🍫", title:"Example: Holly Ltd", facts:"Formed 2015, Model Articles, one class of £1 ordinary shares. It wants to issue 10,000 new ordinary shares **for cash** to an outside investor.", rule:"**Q1**: no cap. **Q2**: **s.550** gives authority. **Q3**: **s.561 applies** (cash issue): offer to existing members first unless they **waive** or pass a **special resolution** to disapply (s.569). **Q4**: board resolution; **SH01** within 1 month; register and certificates within 2 months."},
   {t:"card", i:"🥇", title:"Holly Ltd twist: preference shares", body:"If the investor took **preference shares** (a **new class**), the directors would need **s.551 authority** by **ordinary resolution**, and the articles might need amending by **special resolution**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Deadline for filing form SH01?", body:"**1 month** after allotment (s.555)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Buyback of own shares: conditions, funding and approval", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"⛲", title:"What a buyback is for", body:"A **buyback** (purchase of own shares, CA 2006 Part 18) lets a company buy shares from a shareholder who wants to **leave** (retirement, a dispute, death of a member) when the other members **can't afford** to buy them, or to **return surplus cash**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📋", title:"Four conditions (ss.690-691)", body:"(1) The **articles must not prohibit** it (Model Articles don't; private companies need no express authority). (2) The shares must be **fully paid**. (3) The company must **pay in full on purchase** (no instalments, except a private company buying for an **employees' share scheme**, s.691(3)). (4) At least **one member** must remain holding **non-redeemable** shares (s.690(2))."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Can a company buy back shares and pay the seller in three yearly instalments?", body:"**No.** It must **pay in full on purchase** (s.691)."},
   {t:"card", i:"💰", title:"Funding (s.692)", body:"From **distributable profits** (see **Distributions**) and/or the **proceeds of a fresh issue** of shares made for the purpose. A **private company** may also use **capital**, but only after using any available distributable profits and fresh issue proceeds."},
   {t:"card", i:"🪙", title:"De minimis cash (s.692(1ZA))", body:"A **private company**, if **authorised by its articles**, may buy back out of **capital in cash**, without following the out-of-capital procedure, up to the **lower of £15,000 or the nominal value of 5% of its fully paid share capital** (measured at the start of the financial year) in any financial year."},
   {t:"card", i:"✨", title:"Paying a premium on buyback", body:"Any premium paid on buyback generally must come from **distributable profits** (unless the shares were issued at a premium and are bought out of fresh issue proceeds, within limits)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A private company has no distributable profits and its articles say nothing. Can it use the £15,000 de minimis cash route?", body:"**No.** The de minimis route needs **authority in the articles**. It would have to use a fresh issue or go **out of capital**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗳️", title:"Approval: ordinary resolution first (s.694)", body:"An **off-market purchase** (any buyback by a private company, or a plc buying shares not traded on a recognised market) needs the **terms of the contract** approved by **ordinary resolution** **before** the contract is entered into, or the contract must say no shares will be bought until approved."},
   {t:"card", i:"🙅", title:"The seller can't vote (s.695)", body:"On a **written resolution**, the seller is **not an eligible member** (s.695(2)). At a **meeting**, the resolution is **not effective** if it would not have passed without the seller's votes on those shares (s.695(3))."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔍", title:"Contract on show (s.696)", body:"A copy (or memorandum) of the **contract** must be available at the **registered office** for at least **15 days** before the meeting and at the meeting, or **sent with** the written resolution. The articles may require a **special** resolution instead."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A retiring 40% shareholder's buyback is put to a written resolution. Can she sign it?", body:"**No.** She is **not an eligible member** for that written resolution (s.695(2))."}
 ]},
 {h:"Buyback out of capital (private companies only)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🧮", title:"The permissible capital payment (PCP)", body:"Used where there aren't enough distributable profits. The **PCP** is the price **less** any available **distributable profits** and **fresh issue proceeds** (s.710): **profits must be used first**."},
   {t:"card", i:"✍️", title:"Step 1: directors' statement (s.714)", body:"**All the directors**, having made **full inquiry**, state that (a) **immediately after** the payment there will be **no grounds** on which the company could be found **unable to pay its debts**, and (b) for the **year immediately following**, it will carry on business as a **going concern** and **pay its debts as they fall due**. It states the PCP amount."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚔", title:"Step 1: a false statement", body:"Making the statement **without reasonable grounds** is a criminal **offence** (s.715)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔎", title:"Step 2: auditor's report", body:"Annexed to the statement (s.714(6)). It confirms the **PCP has been properly calculated** and that the auditor is not aware of anything making the directors' opinion **unreasonable**. It is needed **even if the company is normally exempt from audit**, so an audit-exempt company must appoint an auditor for this purpose."},
   {t:"card", i:"📣", title:"Step 3: special resolution within 1 week (s.716)", body:"A **special resolution** approving the payment out of capital, passed **on, or within one week after**, the date of the directors' statement. The **seller's votes are excluded** (s.717). The statement and report must be at the meeting or sent with the written resolution (s.718)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🤲", title:"Step 3: the contract resolution too", body:"The buyback **contract** still needs its **ordinary resolution** (s.694), usually passed at the same time."},
   {t:"card", i:"📰", title:"Step 4: publicity within 1 week (s.719)", body:"Within **one week** after the resolution, publish a notice in the **London Gazette** and either in a **national newspaper** or by **written notice to each creditor**, saying any creditor may apply to court within 5 weeks. File the directors' statement and auditor's report at Companies House no later than the **first notice** date."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛑", title:"Step 5: 5 weeks for objections (s.721)", body:"Any **member** who did not vote for the resolution, or any **creditor**, may apply to **court** to cancel it within **5 weeks** of the resolution."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏰", title:"Step 6: pay between 5 and 7 weeks (s.723)", body:"The capital payment must be made **no earlier than 5 weeks** and **no later than 7 weeks** after the special resolution."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Special resolution passed on 5 March. When can the capital payment be made?", body:"**Between 9 April and 23 April**: no earlier than **5 weeks** and no later than **7 weeks** after the resolution."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧯", title:"Risk if the company goes bust (IA 1986 s.76)", body:"If the company goes into **insolvent winding up** within **one year** of the payment, the **seller** and the **directors who signed** the statement (unless they had reasonable grounds) may have to **contribute** to the shortfall."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🗓️", title:"The out-of-capital timetable", cols:["", "What", "When"], rows:[["1","✍️ Directors' statement + auditor's report","Day 0"],["2","📣 Special resolution (seller excluded)","On or within **1 week** after the statement"],["3","📰 Gazette + newspaper or creditors","Within **1 week** after the resolution"],["4","🛑 Members or creditors may object","Within **5 weeks** of the resolution"],["5","💷 Pay","Between **5 and 7 weeks** after the resolution"]]},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Which has to come first: the special resolution or the directors' statement?", body:"The **directors' statement**. The special resolution is passed **on or within one week after** it."}
 ]},
 {h:"After the buyback, and redemption", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🫕", title:"Bought-back shares are cancelled (s.706)", body:"The shares are **cancelled**, reducing issued share capital, **unless** they were bought out of **distributable profits** and the company holds them **in treasury** (to sell or transfer later, e.g. to employees) (s.724)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔕", title:"Treasury shares are asleep (s.726)", body:"While the company holds shares **in treasury**, it **cannot vote** them or exercise any other right attached to them, and **no dividend** or other distribution can be paid on them. They come back to life only when sold or transferred."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏦", title:"Capital redemption reserve (s.733)", body:"Where shares are bought back wholly out of **profits**, an amount equal to the **nominal value** of the shares cancelled goes to a **capital redemption reserve** (CRR), which is **undistributable**. This is **capital maintenance**: keeping a cushion for creditors."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧱", title:"Topping up the CRR after a capital payment (s.734)", body:"Where shares are bought **out of capital** and the **PCP is less than the nominal value** of the shares bought, the **difference** is transferred to the **capital redemption reserve**. Example: shares with a nominal value of £3,000 are bought with a PCP of £1,000, so **£2,000** goes to the CRR."},
   {t:"card", i:"📮", title:"Filings within 28 days", body:"Return of purchase (**form SH03**) within **28 days** (s.707), **stamped** with stamp duty at **0.5%**, paid by the company, if the price exceeds **£1,000**. Notice of cancellation with a statement of capital (**form SH06**) within **28 days** (s.708). Update the register of members."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗄️", title:"Keep the contract 10 years (s.702)", body:"The contract (or memorandum) must be kept at the registered office for **10 years** and be open to inspection."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A buyback for £40,000 is completed. What stamp duty is due on SH03?", body:"**0.5%**, so **£200**, paid by the company (the price is over £1,000)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔁", title:"Redemption: who can issue redeemable shares (s.684)", body:"A **private company** can, unless its articles **exclude or restrict** it. A **public company** needs **authorisation in its articles**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚓", title:"Redemption: keep some non-redeemable shares (s.684(4))", body:"No redeemable shares may be issued when there are **no non-redeemable shares** in issue."},
   {t:"card", i:"📝", title:"Redemption: terms (s.685)", body:"The **terms and manner** of redemption may be fixed by the **directors**, if authorised by the articles or an ordinary resolution, **before** the shares are allotted. Otherwise they must be in the **articles**."},
   {t:"card", i:"💳", title:"Redemption: paid up and paid for (s.686)", body:"Shares may be redeemed only if **fully paid**, and must be **paid for on redemption** (unless agreed otherwise)."},
   {t:"card", i:"💰", title:"Redemption: funding (s.687)", body:"The same as buyback: **distributable profits** or a **fresh issue**, or (private company) **capital** under the same procedure (s.709)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📬", title:"Redemption: cancel and file SH02 (s.689)", body:"Redeemed shares are **cancelled**. Notify Companies House on **form SH02** with a statement of capital within **1 month**."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🆚", title:"Redemption versus buyback", cols:["", "Redemption", "Buyback"], rows:[["Terms set","When the shares were **issued**","In a **new contract** now"],["Members approve the contract?","❌ No new contract to approve","✅ Ordinary resolution (seller excluded)"],["Companies House form","**SH02**, 1 month","**SH03** + **SH06**, 28 days"]]},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Why does a redemption not need members to approve a contract?", body:"Because it follows **terms set when the shares were issued**. A buyback is a **new contract** that members must approve."}
 ]},
 {h:"Tax for the selling shareholder on a buyback", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🧾", title:"Default: income treatment", body:"The payment, so far as it exceeds the amount originally **subscribed** (nominal value plus premium paid to the company), is a **distribution** (like a **dividend**) and taxed as **dividend income** (see **Income tax**). The seller may also have a capital gain or loss on the original subscription amount."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏛️", title:"Capital treatment (CTA 2010 ss.1033-1048)", body:"The **whole payment** is a **capital disposal** taxed under **capital gains tax** (CGT), possibly with **business asset disposal relief** (BADR), if **all** the conditions are met."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏭", title:"Conditions 1-2: the company and the purpose", body:"The company is an **unquoted trading company** (or the holding company of a trading group). The buyback is **wholly or mainly for the benefit of the trade** (e.g. removing a dissident or retiring shareholder), not mainly to avoid tax."},
   {t:"card", i:"🇬🇧", title:"Conditions 3-4: residence and 5 years", body:"The seller is **UK resident**, and has **owned the shares for at least 5 years** (**3 years** if inherited, counting the deceased's period of ownership)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📉", title:"Conditions 5-6: reduction and not connected", body:"A **substantial reduction**: the seller's percentage holding after the buyback is **no more than 75%** of what it was before. And afterwards the seller is **not connected** with the company (holds **no more than 30%**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚰️", title:"Inheritance tax route (outline)", body:"Capital treatment also applies if the payment is used to pay **inheritance tax** due on a death, within **2 years**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏆", title:"Why capital treatment is usually better", body:"CGT rates (**18%/24%**, BADR **18%**) and the **annual exempt amount** can beat dividend rates (up to **39.35%**), and only the **gain**, not the whole receipt, is taxed."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Seller held 40% before and 32% after a buyback. Capital treatment?", body:"**No.** 32% is **more than 30%**, so she is still **connected**. (32 is also 80% of 40, which fails the **75%** substantial reduction test.)"},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A UK-resident founder sells all his shares back after 12 years to an unquoted trading company so he can retire. How is he taxed?", body:"**Capital treatment**: CGT on the gain (possibly with BADR), because every condition is met."},
   {t:"card", i:"💡", title:"Exam tip: three buyback questions", body:"(1) **Where does the money come from** (distributable profits, fresh issue, de minimis cash, or capital)? (2) **Who can vote** (the **seller's votes are excluded**)? (3) If out of capital, is the **timetable** right: statement → special resolution within **1 week** → notices within **1 week** → **5 weeks** for objections → pay between **5 and 7 weeks**?"},
   {t:"card", i:"🚨", title:"Watch out", body:"**Pre-emption applies only to cash issues of ordinary shares.** A **s.551 resolution** is an **ordinary** resolution but **must still be filed**. Other reductions of capital and **financial assistance** are **outside** the SQE1 syllabus."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Source: proposals/rooms/learn-blp-income-tax.js */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["blp/income-tax"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🍭", title:"Who pays income tax", body:"**Income tax** is charged on **individuals** (and on trustees and personal representatives, the people who run a dead person's estate) on their **income** for each **tax year**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📅", title:"The tax year runs 6 April to 5 April", body:"For example, **2026/27** is **6 April 2026 to 5 April 2027**. All the figures in this room are the 2026/27 figures for England and Wales."},
   {t:"card", i:"🖐️", title:"Five chargeable persons on the syllabus", body:"A **chargeable person** is someone who can be charged the tax. SQE1 tests five: **employees** (including directors), **sole traders**, **partners** (including LLP members), **shareholders** (taxed on dividends) and **lenders and debenture holders** (taxed on interest). A **debenture** is a document in which a company acknowledges a loan it owes."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏢", title:"Companies do not pay income tax", body:"A company pays **corporation tax** on its profits, never income tax (see **Corporation tax**). Gains made on selling assets are subject to **capital gains tax** (see **CGT**)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A company makes a £200,000 trading profit. Which tax does it pay on it?", body:"**Corporation tax**, not income tax. Only individuals (and trustees and personal representatives) pay income tax."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧮", title:"What the exam gives you, and what it doesn't", body:"The exam **supplies rates and allowances** where needed. You must know **how** the calculation works, **which income** falls into which category, the main **reliefs**, and how tax is **collected**."}
 ]},
 {h:"The income tax calculation", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"1️⃣", title:"Step 1: Add up total income", body:"Add up all the taxpayer's **taxable income** for the year (Income Tax Act 2007 (ITA) s.23). Split it into three types, because each type is taxed at different rates."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🌀", title:"The three types of income", cols:["", "What goes in it"], rows:[["🟣 Non-savings income","Employment income (salary, bonuses, benefits), **trading profits**, **rental (property) income**, pensions"],["🟢 Savings income","Mainly **interest**: bank and building society interest, interest on loans and debentures"],["🩷 Dividend income","**Dividends** from companies"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"🚫", title:"Leave out exempt income", body:"**Exempt income** is not taxed at all, e.g. income from an **ISA** (individual savings account), premium bond prizes, and most state benefits such as child benefit (which has a separate charge). Leave out **gains** too: they are CGT."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Rent from letting out a flat: which type of income?", body:"**Non-savings income.** Rental (property) income sits with salary and trading profits."},
   {t:"card", i:"2️⃣", title:"Step 2: Deduct reliefs to get net income", body:"Deduct any **reliefs** (ITA s.24) to get **net income**. The main reliefs are **trading loss relief** (see the loss section) and **interest on a qualifying loan**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🤝", title:"Qualifying loan: partnerships and equipment", body:"Interest is relieved on a loan to **buy an interest in a partnership** (or lend money to it) where the borrower is a partner, or to buy **plant or machinery** for use in a partnership or employment."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏢", title:"Qualifying loan: close companies", body:"Also relieved: a loan to **buy shares in, or lend to, a close trading company** (one controlled by **5 or fewer people**, or by its directors) where the borrower owns **more than 5%** of the shares, **or** owns some shares and works for the **greater part of their time** managing the company."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Sam borrows to buy 3% of a close trading company and works there full time as a manager. Is the interest relieved?", body:"**Yes.** He owns some shares and works the greater part of his time in managing the company, so the loan qualifies even though he has under 5%."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧢", title:"The cap on reliefs", body:"Trading loss relief against general income and qualifying loan interest relief **together** are capped at the **greater of £50,000 or 25% of adjusted total income** (ITA s.24A). Losses set only against profits of the **same trade** are not capped."},
   {t:"card", i:"3️⃣", title:"Step 3: Deduct the personal allowance", body:"The **personal allowance** (**£12,570**) is the amount of income you can have tax-free. Deducting it from net income gives **taxable income**. It is set first against **non-savings** income, then savings, then dividends."},
   {t:"card", i:"📉", title:"The personal allowance shrinks above £100,000", body:"It is reduced by **£1 for every £2** of adjusted net income above **£100,000**, so it is lost completely at **£125,140**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Adjusted net income is £110,000. What is the personal allowance?", body:"**£7,570.** £10,000 over the limit ÷ 2 = £5,000 lost; £12,570 − £5,000 = £7,570."},
   {t:"card", i:"4️⃣", title:"Step 4: Apply the rates in order", body:"Tax each type of income **in this order**: **non-savings first**, then **savings**, then **dividends** on top. Each type 'uses up' the tax bands in that order."},
   {t:"card", i:"5️⃣", title:"Step 5: Add up, then deduct tax already paid", body:"Add the tax on each type to get the **tax liability**. Then deduct any **tax already paid** (e.g. under **PAYE**, Pay As You Earn, or tax deducted at source from interest) to find what is still owed, or repayable."}
 ]},
 {h:"Rates, bands and allowances", cards:[
   {t:"compare", i:"📏", title:"Rates on taxable income (2026/27)", cols:["Band", "Non-savings", "Savings", "Dividends"], rows:[["**Basic rate band**: first **£37,700**","20%","20%","10.75%"],["**Higher rate**: £37,701 to £125,140","40%","40%","35.75%"],["**Additional rate**: over **£125,140**","45%","45%","39.35%"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"🌱", title:"The starting rate for savings", body:"A **£5,000** band of **savings** income taxed at **0%**. It is reduced **£1 for £1** by non-savings taxable income (after the personal allowance), so it only helps people with **little non-savings income**."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🐷", title:"Personal savings allowance (PSA)", cols:["Taxpayer", "Savings income taxed at 0%"], rows:[["Basic rate taxpayer","First **£1,000**"],["Higher rate taxpayer","First **£500**"],["Additional rate taxpayer","**£0**"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"🍬", title:"The dividend allowance", body:"The first **£500** of dividend income is taxed at **0%** for **everyone**, whatever their rate."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚠️", title:"Allowances are 0% bands, not deductions", body:"The PSA, the dividend allowance and the starting rate are **nil-rate bands**. The income they cover still **counts** towards, and **uses up**, the basic and higher rate bands."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Does income covered by the £500 dividend allowance use up the basic rate band?", body:"**Yes.** It is taxed at 0% but it still fills £500 of the £37,700 basic rate band."},
   {t:"card", i:"📈", title:"Dividend rates rose from 6 April 2026", body:"The dividend ordinary and upper rates rose by **2 percentage points**, from 8.75% and 33.75% to **10.75%** and **35.75%**. The additional rate stayed at **39.35%**. (Scotland has its own non-savings rates: not tested.)"},
   {t:"case", i:"🧺", title:"Example: Priya, part 1 (non-savings)", facts:"Priya, a sole trader, has **trading profits of £45,000**, **bank interest of £2,000** and **dividends of £6,000** in 2026/27. No reliefs.", rule:"Total income **£53,000**. The personal allowance of £12,570 comes off her trading profits, leaving **£32,430** taxable non-savings income. £32,430 × 20% = **£6,486**. Basic rate band left: £37,700 − £32,430 = **£5,270**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🟢", title:"Priya, part 2 (savings)", body:"Her total taxable income (£40,430) reaches the higher rate band, so she is a **higher rate taxpayer**: **PSA £500**. No starting rate (her non-savings taxable income is over £5,000). £500 at 0% (band left £4,770); £1,500 at 20% = **£300** (band left £3,270)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🩷", title:"Priya, part 3 (dividends)", body:"£500 at 0% (band left £2,770); £2,770 at 10.75% = **£297.78**; the remaining £2,730 at 35.75% = **£975.98**. **Total income tax: £8,059.76.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"🧾", title:"Priya also pays Class 4 NICs", body:"**National Insurance contributions (NICs)** are a separate charge on earnings and trading profits. On her trading profits: (£45,000 − £12,570) × 6% = **£1,945.80**."},
   {t:"card", i:"💡", title:"Exam tip: the band trap", body:"The most common trap is forgetting that the **£500 dividend allowance** and the **PSA** still **use up** the basic rate band. Always tax **non-savings, then savings, then dividends**, and track how much of the **£37,700** is left at each step."}
 ]},
 {h:"Employees and directors", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"💼", title:"Employment income = earnings + benefits", body:"Under the Income Tax (Earnings and Pensions) Act 2003 (**ITEPA**) s.62, **earnings** are salary, wages, **bonuses**, commission, fees and anything else of money or money's worth from the job, **plus** taxable **benefits in kind**. They are taxed in the year **received** (paid or entitled to be paid)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚗", title:"Benefits in kind are taxed on their cash equivalent", body:"**Benefits in kind** are non-cash perks. Examples: a **company car** (taxed on a percentage of its list price depending on CO2 emissions) and **fuel** for private use; **living accommodation**; private **medical insurance**; vouchers."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏦", title:"Cheap loans over £10,000", body:"A **beneficial loan** (a cheap or interest-free loan from the employer) over **£10,000** is taxed on the difference between the interest actually paid and the **official rate** of interest."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎁", title:"Exempt benefits", body:"Not taxed: employer **pension contributions**, one **mobile phone**, workplace **parking**, **trivial benefits** (up to £50, not cash and not a reward), and work-related training."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Kofi's boss gives him a £30 Christmas hamper, not as a reward for work. Taxable?", body:"**No.** It's a **trivial benefit**: under £50, not cash, not a reward."},
   {t:"card", i:"👋", title:"Termination payments: first £30,000 tax-free", body:"A genuine **non-contractual** compensation payment for losing a job (e.g. compensation for unfair dismissal, or a statutory redundancy payment) is **tax-free up to £30,000**; the excess is taxed as earnings (ITEPA ss.401-403). Any pay for notice the employee did not work (**post-employment notice pay**) is carved out first and taxed in full."},
   {t:"card", i:"📜", title:"Contractual payments are taxed in full", body:"**Contractual** payments, e.g. a **payment in lieu of notice** (money instead of working the notice period) that the contract provides for, and **post-employment notice pay**, are taxed **in full** as earnings."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"An employee receives £45,000 non-contractual compensation for loss of the job. How much is taxed?", body:"**£15,000.** The first £30,000 is tax-free; the excess is taxed as earnings."},
   {t:"card", i:"✂️", title:"Employee expenses: wholly, exclusively and necessarily", body:"An employee can deduct an expense only if it is incurred **wholly, exclusively and necessarily in the performance of the duties** (ITEPA s.336). This is **very strict**: the cost must be required by the **job itself**, not by the employee's personal circumstances."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚌", title:"Travel: business journeys yes, commuting no", body:"Business journeys are deductible, but **ordinary commuting** between home and a permanent workplace is **not** (ITEPA ss.337-338)."},
   {t:"card", i:"✅", title:"Other employee deductions", body:"Also deductible: the employee's own **pension contributions** to a registered scheme, approved **professional subscriptions**, and payroll giving."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧾", title:"PAYE: the employer collects the tax", body:"Under **PAYE**, the **employer** deducts income tax from each payment of earnings using the employee's tax code and pays it to HMRC monthly. Benefits are reported on form **P11D** (and are increasingly put through the payroll)."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🪪", title:"Class 1 NICs on earnings (2026/27)", cols:["Who", "Rate"], rows:[["👤 Employee (primary)","**8%** between **£12,570** and **£50,270** a year; **2%** above"],["🏪 Employer (secondary)","**15%** above **£5,000** a year, no upper limit; eligible employers claim the **employment allowance** (£10,500) off their bill"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"🚙", title:"Class 1A NICs on benefits", body:"The **employer** (not the employee) pays **Class 1A NICs** at **15%** on most taxable benefits."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏪", title:"For the employer, pay is a deductible cost", body:"For the employer business, salaries, benefits and employer's NICs are **deductible expenses** in computing its profits."}
 ]},
 {h:"Sole traders: trading profit", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🔍", title:"Is it a trade? The badges of trade", body:"Whether an activity is a **trade** is decided using the **badges of trade**: profit-seeking motive, number of transactions, nature of the asset, supplementary work done to it, how it was financed, and the reason for sale. A one-off sale of a personal asset is usually capital (CGT), not trading."},
   {t:"card", i:"➗", title:"The trading profit formula", body:"Under the Income Tax (Trading and Other Income) Act 2005 (**ITTOIA**): **trading profit = chargeable receipts − deductible expenditure − capital allowances**."},
   {t:"card", i:"💷", title:"Chargeable receipts are revenue receipts", body:"**Revenue** receipts from the trade (sales, fees) are chargeable. **Capital** receipts, e.g. from selling the business premises, are excluded (they may be subject to CGT)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔁", title:"Expense test 1: revenue, not capital", body:"Recurring running costs (wages, rent, repairs, stock, utilities, interest on business loans) are **revenue** and can be deducted. Buying an **enduring asset** for the business (premises, machinery, vehicles) is **capital** and cannot."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎯", title:"Expense test 2: wholly and exclusively for the trade", body:"The expense must be incurred **wholly and exclusively for the purposes of the trade** (ITTOIA s.34). Where a cost has a private and a business element (e.g. a car used partly privately), the **identifiable business part** can be deducted."},
   {t:"compare", i:"⛔", title:"Specifically not deductible", cols:["Item", "Why"], rows:[["🍽️ Business entertainment of clients; gifts to customers (except small branded gifts)","Banned by ITTOIA s.45"],["💸 The owner's **drawings** or 'salary'","Taking profit out, not a cost"],["📉 **Depreciation**","Capital allowances are given instead"],["🚨 **Fines** for breaking the law","Not deductible"],["🧾 The trader's own income tax and NICs","Not deductible"]]},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A sole trader takes a big wholesale client out to a fancy dinner. Deductible?", body:"**No.** Business entertainment of clients is specifically not deductible."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏗️", title:"Capital allowances replace depreciation", body:"**Capital allowances** (Capital Allowances Act 2001) give tax relief for capital spending on **plant and machinery** (equipment, vans, computers, machinery)."},
   {t:"card", i:"💯", title:"Annual Investment Allowance (AIA)", body:"A **100%** deduction for the first **£1 million** of qualifying expenditure each year. It does **not** cover cars."},
   {t:"card", i:"🌀", title:"Writing down allowance (WDA)", body:"Each year, a percentage of the balance in the pool: **14%** for the **main pool** (reduced from 18% from **April 2026**) and **6%** for the **special rate pool** (e.g. long-life assets, integral features, high-emission cars)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🆕", title:"The 40% first-year allowance", body:"A **40% first-year allowance** applies to **new** main-rate plant and machinery bought from **1 January 2026**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔚", title:"Selling an asset: balancing charge or allowance", body:"On **disposal**, a **balancing charge** (taxable) or a **balancing allowance** (extra relief) may arise. No capital allowances are given for buildings, except the structures and buildings allowance (3%; outline only)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A sole trader buys a new delivery van for £30,000. Which allowance gives the most relief this year?", body:"The **AIA**: 100% of the £30,000, because a van is plant and machinery and is not a car."},
   {t:"card", i:"📆", title:"The tax year basis", body:"Since **2024/25**, a sole trader is taxed on the profits **arising in the tax year itself**. If their accounting year does not end on 31 March or 5 April, the profits of two accounting periods are **apportioned** into the tax year."},
   {t:"card", i:"💵", title:"The cash basis is the default", body:"Since 2024/25, unincorporated businesses use the **cash basis** by default (income recorded when **received**, expenses when **paid**), unless they elect for normal accruals accounting."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🪪", title:"NICs for the self-employed (2026/27)", cols:["Class", "Rule"], rows:[["**Class 4**","**6%** on profits between **£12,570** and **£50,270**; **2%** above"],["**Class 2**","No longer compulsory; voluntary contributions can protect state pension entitlement where profits are below £7,105"]]}
 ]},
 {h:"Trading loss relief", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"📉", title:"What a trading loss is", body:"A **trading loss** arises when deductible expenses and capital allowances exceed receipts. The trader **chooses** which relief or reliefs to claim (all under ITA 2007)."},
   {t:"card", i:"↔️", title:"s.64: general (sideways) relief", body:"Any loss can be set against **total income** of the **tax year of the loss** and/or the **previous tax year**, either or both, in any order, subject to the **cap**. Any unused part can go against **capital gains** of the same year (s.71)."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏪", title:"s.72: early years relief", body:"A loss in the **first 4 tax years** of a new trade can be set against **total income** of the **3 tax years before** the loss year, **earliest year first**, subject to the cap. Ideal for someone who was previously employed and paid tax."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏩", title:"s.83: carry forward", body:"Any unused loss is carried forward against **future profits of the same trade only**: automatically, indefinitely, against the first available profits."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏁", title:"s.89: terminal loss relief", body:"A loss in the **final 12 months** of trading can be set against **profits of the same trade** of the final tax year and the **3 previous tax years**, **latest year first**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏢", title:"s.86: incorporation relief", body:"If the business is transferred to a company **wholly or mainly for shares**, the loss is carried forward against **income from the company** (salary, dividends) in later years, while the trader keeps the shares."},
   {t:"case", i:"🛒", title:"Example: Leo's first year", facts:"Leo left employment (salary £60,000 a year) and started trading on 6 April 2026. In 2026/27 he makes a trading loss of £20,000.", rule:"**Early years relief (s.72)** carries the loss back against his total income of **2023/24** first (then 2024/25, 2025/26), giving a **tax repayment** on his old salary: the top £9,730 of his 2023/24 taxable income was taxed at 40% and the rest of the loss saves 20%, so he gets back about **£5,946**. Alternatively, s.64 against 2025/26 or 2026/27 income."},
   {t:"card", i:"💡", title:"Exam tip: match the relief to the timing", body:"**First 4 tax years** → s.72 (back 3 years, earliest first, against total income). **Last 12 months** → s.89 (back 3 years, latest first, against trading profits only). **Incorporation for shares** → s.86. Otherwise s.64 (this year/last year) or s.83 (carry forward)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A trader makes a loss in their final 12 months. Which relief carries it back 3 years, latest year first?", body:"**Terminal loss relief (s.89)**, against profits of the same trade only."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A loss in year 2 of a new trade; the trader wants it set against their salary from 3 years ago. Which relief?", body:"**Early years relief (s.72)**: back 3 years against total income, earliest year first."}
 ]},
 {h:"Partners and LLP members", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🔍", title:"Partnerships are tax transparent", body:"**Tax transparent** means the firm itself pays no income tax. Each partner is taxed **as if they were a sole trader**, on their **share of the firm's profits** (ITTOIA s.848)."},
   {t:"card", i:"➗", title:"Compute the firm's profit, then split it", body:"First compute the **firm's trading profit** for the accounting period, as for a sole trader. Then divide it between the partners under the **profit-sharing arrangements** for that period."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚠️", title:"Partners' 'salaries' are profit shares", body:"'**Salaries**' and '**interest on capital**' paid to partners are **not deductible** expenses of the firm. They are part of each partner's **profit share**."},
   {t:"case", i:"🍭", title:"Example: Ann and Bo", facts:"Ann and Bo's firm makes £100,000. The agreement gives Ann a £20,000 'salary', then splits the rest equally.", rule:"**Ann is taxed on £60,000** (£20,000 + £40,000) and **Bo on £40,000**. The salary is not deducted from the firm's profits."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧾", title:"Each partner pays their own tax", body:"Each partner pays their own income tax and **Class 4 NICs** on their share through **self-assessment**. The firm files a **partnership return** showing how the profit was allocated."},
   {t:"card", i:"📉", title:"Losses are shared the same way", body:"Each partner then chooses their own loss relief. For example, a **new partner** may claim **s.72** for their first 4 tax years in the firm, even though the firm itself is not new."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚪", title:"Joining or leaving the firm", body:"A partner joining or leaving is treated as **starting or ceasing** their own deemed trade."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏛️", title:"LLP members", body:"**LLP members** are taxed the same way as partners, unless they are '**salaried members**', who are taxed as employees (outline only)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔗", title:"Borrowing to buy into the firm", body:"Interest on a loan to buy into the partnership is **qualifying interest**, relieved at Step 2."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Can a firm deduct a partner's 'salary' from its trading profit?", body:"**No.** It is part of that partner's profit share, taxed on the partner."}
 ]},
 {h:"Shareholders and lenders", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🍬", title:"Dividends are dividend income", body:"**Dividends** from a UK company are taxed at the **dividend rates** (10.75% / 35.75% / 39.35%) after the **£500 dividend allowance**. There is no tax credit and nothing is deducted at source."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔁", title:"Dividends come out of taxed profits", body:"Dividends are paid out of the company's **taxed profits** and are **not deductible** for corporation tax, so profits paid out as dividends are effectively **taxed twice** (see **Corporation tax**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔙", title:"Share buybacks", body:"When a company buys back its own shares, the payment is treated as a **distribution** (taxed like a dividend on the amount above the original subscription price) unless the **capital treatment** conditions are met (see **Equity finance**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏦", title:"Loans to director-shareholders", body:"A director-shareholder of a **close company** who borrows from it may trigger a **s.455** charge on the company; if the loan is written off, they are taxed on it as a **dividend** (see **Corporation tax**)."},
   {t:"compare", i:"⚖️", title:"Watch out: salary vs dividends", cols:["", "Salary", "Dividends"], rows:[["Type of income","Non-savings","Dividend income"],["Collected","Under **PAYE**","Through self-assessment"],["NICs?","✅ Class 1","❌ **No NICs**"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"💰", title:"Interest is savings income", body:"Interest an individual receives (on a loan to a company, **loan notes** or **debentures**, or bank and building society accounts) is **savings income**: taxed after non-savings income, with the **PSA** and possibly the starting rate."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏛️", title:"Banks pay interest gross", body:"**Banks and building societies** pay interest **gross**, i.e. without deducting any tax."},
   {t:"card", i:"✂️", title:"Companies deduct 20% from yearly interest", body:"A company paying **yearly interest** (on a loan expected to last more than a year, such as a debenture or loan note) **to an individual** must generally **deduct basic rate tax (20%) at source** and pay it to HMRC (ITA s.874). The individual gets the net amount and a **credit** for the tax deducted."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Stan's company debenture pays him £600 interest a year. What does he receive?", body:"**£480.** The company deducts £120 (20%) and pays it to HMRC; Stan gets a £120 credit against his own liability."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏢", title:"For the borrower, interest is deductible", body:"For the **borrower company**, interest on business borrowing is **deductible** in computing its profits."}
 ]},
 {h:"Anti-avoidance", cards:[
   {t:"compare", i:"🕵️", title:"Avoidance vs evasion", cols:["", "Tax avoidance", "Tax evasion"], rows:[["What it is","Arranging affairs to reduce tax in ways Parliament did not intend","Deliberately concealing income or lying to HMRC"],["Legal?","✅ Legal, but countered by many rules","❌ A **crime**"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"🔎", title:"The Ramsay principle: look at the whole", body:"The courts interpret tax statutes **purposively** (by their purpose) and look at a **composite transaction as a whole**, ignoring artificial steps inserted only to avoid tax."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚨", title:"The General Anti-Abuse Rule (GAAR)", body:"The **GAAR** (Finance Act 2013 Part 5) counteracts tax advantages from **abusive** arrangements: those that **cannot reasonably be regarded as a reasonable course of action** (the 'double reasonableness' test). HMRC can adjust the tax and charge a penalty of **60%** of the counteracted advantage."},
   {t:"card", i:"📣", title:"DOTAS: disclose the scheme", body:"Under **DOTAS** (Disclosure of Tax Avoidance Schemes), promoters (and sometimes users) must disclose certain schemes to HMRC, which gives each one a reference number."},
   {t:"card", i:"👨‍👧", title:"Settlements legislation", body:"Income from property a person has given away (**settled**) is still taxed on them if they keep an interest in it, or if it is paid to their **minor unmarried children** (over £100 a year) (ITTOIA ss.619-648). This stops income being shifted to family members to use their allowances."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Stan gives shares to his 8-year-old daughter Daisy. They pay her £400 of dividends a year. Whose income is it?", body:"**Stan's.** It's a parental settlement paying over £100 a year to his minor unmarried child."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧑‍💻", title:"IR35 / off-payroll working", body:"Where a worker provides services through their own **personal service company** but would be an **employee** if engaged directly, income tax and NICs are charged **as if they were employed** (ITEPA Part 2 Chapters 8 and 10)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎯", title:"Targeted anti-avoidance rules", body:"**Targeted anti-avoidance rules** (TAARs) apply in specific areas, e.g. **transactions in securities** (turning income into capital). HMRC can also issue **accelerated payment notices** and **follower notices**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Collection: self-assessment", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"📝", title:"Who uses self-assessment", body:"**Self-assessment** means filing your own tax return. It is used where tax is not fully collected at source: sole traders, partners, landlords, higher-income investors, many directors. Employees with only employment income pay under **PAYE** and do not usually file a return."},
   {t:"card", i:"📌", title:"Register by 5 October", body:"Register with HMRC by **5 October** after the end of the tax year in which the liability first arises."},
   {t:"compare", i:"📬", title:"Tax return deadlines", cols:["Return", "Due"], rows:[["📄 Paper","**31 October** after the end of the tax year"],["💻 Online","**31 January** after the end of the tax year"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"💳", title:"Payments on account", body:"Two instalments, each normally **50% of the previous year's** income tax and Class 4 NICs (less tax deducted at source), due on **31 January in the tax year** and **31 July after it**. Not needed if last year's liability was under **£1,000** or more than **80%** was collected at source."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚖️", title:"The balancing payment", body:"Any remaining tax is due on the following **31 January**, the same date as the online return and the first payment on account for the next year."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🗓️", title:"Timeline for 2026/27", cols:["Date", "What's due"], rows:[["31 January 2027","1st payment on account"],["31 July 2027","2nd payment on account"],["31 January 2028","Balancing payment + online return (+ 1st payment on account for 2027/28)"]]},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"When is the online tax return for 2026/27 due?", body:"**31 January 2028.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"⏰", title:"Late means interest and penalties", body:"**Interest** runs on late payments, and **penalties** apply for late filing and late payment."},
   {t:"card", i:"💻", title:"Making Tax Digital (MTD)", body:"From **6 April 2026**, sole traders and landlords with qualifying gross income from self-employment and property over **£50,000** must keep **digital records** and send **quarterly updates** to HMRC using compatible software. The threshold falls to £30,000 from April 2027."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗺️", title:"Your 6-step route", body:"**1** Who and what income? → **2** Compute the income → **3** Any reliefs? → **4** Personal allowance and rates → **5** NICs and collection → **6** Anti-avoidance. The full route is this topic's **framework**."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Source: proposals/rooms/learn-blp-insolvency-clawback-priority.js */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["blp/insolvency-clawback-priority"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🫙", title:"The insolvency practitioner fills the pot", body:"When a company goes into **liquidation** or **administration**, the liquidator or administrator (an **insolvency practitioner**, or **IP**: a licensed professional who runs the insolvency) tries to **maximise the pot** of assets for the **creditors** (the people the company owes money to)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📏", title:"Then pays out in a fixed order", body:"Once the pot is as big as it can be, the IP pays the creditors in a **fixed statutory order** (an order set by the Insolvency Act 1986, or **IA 1986**). Nobody can jump the queue."},
   {t:"card", i:"↩️", title:"Way 1 to grow the pot: clawback", body:"**Clawback** means asking the court to **reverse transactions** made before the insolvency that unfairly reduced the assets or favoured one creditor."},
   {t:"card", i:"🖐️", title:"The five clawback claims", body:"- **Transactions at an undervalue** (s.238)\n- **Preferences** (s.239)\n- **Invalid floating charges** (s.245)\n- **Transactions defrauding creditors** (s.423)\n- **Extortionate credit** (s.244)"},
   {t:"card", i:"🧑‍🍳", title:"Way 2 to grow the pot: personal liability", body:"Making individuals (usually directors) **contribute** money to the company's assets for **wrongful trading**, **fraudulent trading** or **misfeasance**. The **phoenix** rules and **disqualification** also punish directors of failed companies."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚶", title:"The queue in one line", body:"**Fixed charge** holders → **expenses** → **preferential** creditors → the **prescribed part** → **floating charge** holders → **unsecured** creditors → **shareholders**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗺️", title:"What's elsewhere", body:"The procedures themselves (liquidation, administration) are in **Corporate insolvency procedures**. The bankruptcy versions of clawback are in **Personal insolvency**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"What are the two ways an IP can make the pot bigger?", body:"**Clawback** (asking the court to reverse unfair transactions made before the insolvency) and **personal liability** (making directors and others contribute)."}
 ]},
 {h:"When and who: onset, insolvency and connected persons", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"⏳", title:"Time limits count backwards", body:"The time limits for **undervalues**, **preferences** and **floating charges** are counted **backwards** from the **onset of insolvency** (IA 1986 s.240(3)). Anything done in the gap between an **administration application** or **notice of intention** being filed and the administrator's **appointment** is also caught (s.240(1)(c)-(d)). If the transaction happened before the window opens, it can't be challenged under that section. (Extortionate credit counts back from the date of liquidation or administration instead, and s.423 has no time limit.)"},
   {t:"compare", i:"📅", title:"What date is the onset of insolvency?", cols:["Procedure", "Onset date"], rows:[["⚖️ Administration by court order","The date the **administration application** was filed"],["📨 Administration out of court","The date the **notice of intention** to appoint was filed, or otherwise the date of **appointment**"],["🏛️ Compulsory liquidation","The date of the **winding-up petition** (s.129)"],["🗳️ Voluntary liquidation","The date of the **winding-up resolution**"],["➡️ Liquidation straight after administration","The onset date of the **administration**"]]},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"In a compulsory liquidation, is the onset date the date of the petition or the date of the winding-up order?", body:"The date of the **winding-up petition**. Winding up is treated as starting then (s.129)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📉", title:"Was the company insolvent at the time?", body:"For a transaction at an undervalue or a preference to be challenged, the company must have been **unable to pay its debts** at the time, or have become so **as a result** of the transaction (s.240(2))."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧮", title:"'Unable to pay its debts' means...", body:"The tests in **IA 1986 s.123**: the **cash flow** test (it can't pay its debts as they fall due) or the **balance sheet** test (its liabilities are more than its assets)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔁", title:"When insolvency is presumed", body:"For a **transaction at an undervalue** with a **connected person**, insolvency at the time is **presumed**: the connected person must prove the company was solvent. There is **no** such presumption for preferences."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔗", title:"Connected persons (s.249)", body:"A person is **connected** with a company if they are a **director** or **shadow director** of it (someone whose instructions the directors usually follow), an **associate** of a director or shadow director, or an **associate of the company**."},
   {t:"card", i:"👪", title:"Who counts as an associate (s.435)?", body:"- **Spouse or civil partner**\n- **Relatives** (brothers, sisters, uncles, aunts, nephews, nieces, parents and grandparents, children and grandchildren) and their spouses\n- **Business partners**\n- **Employers and employees**\n- Trustees\n- **Companies they control** (alone or with associates, at least **one third** of the votes)"},
   {t:"card", i:"🎯", title:"Why connected people are treated more harshly", body:"Connected parties face **longer time limits** and **presumptions** against them, because they are more likely to **know** about the company's problems and to **benefit** from them."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Is a director's brother connected with the company?", body:"**Yes.** A brother is a **relative**, so an **associate** of a director, so **connected** (ss.249 and 435)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Transactions at an undervalue and transactions defrauding creditors", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🎁", title:"What is a transaction at an undervalue? (s.238)", body:"The company makes a **gift**, or receives **no consideration** (nothing in return), or receives consideration worth **significantly less** than what it gives (s.238(4))."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏱️", title:"Undervalue: time limit", body:"Within **2 years** before the onset of insolvency, **whether or not** the other party is connected (s.240(1)(a))."},
   {t:"card", i:"📉", title:"Undervalue: insolvency test", body:"The company was unable to pay its debts at the time or as a result. This is **presumed** if the other party is **connected**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛡️", title:"Undervalue: the defence (s.238(5))", body:"No order if the company acted **in good faith**, **for the purpose of carrying on its business**, and at the time there were **reasonable grounds for believing** the transaction would **benefit the company**. All three are needed."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔙", title:"Undervalue: what the court can order", body:"Whatever order it thinks fit to **restore the position** to what it would have been (ss.238(3), 241): e.g. **transfer the property back** or **pay the difference** in value. A third party who bought the property **in good faith and for value** is protected."},
   {t:"case", i:"🚐", title:"Example: Moss Ltd's van", facts:"18 months before liquidation, when already unable to pay its debts, Moss Ltd sold a van worth £30,000 to its director's brother for £5,000.", rule:"**Transaction at an undervalue** within 2 years. The brother is an associate of a director, so **connected**, and insolvency is **presumed**. The court can order him to return the van or pay **£25,000**. The good-faith business defence is unlikely to succeed."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A company gave away an asset 20 months before liquidation to an unconnected stranger. Can it be challenged as an undervalue?", body:"**Yes, potentially.** The **2-year** limit applies to everyone. But because the stranger is unconnected, the liquidator must **prove** the company was insolvent at the time or as a result."},
   {t:"card", i:"🙈", title:"Transaction defrauding creditors (s.423)", body:"A transaction at an undervalue entered into for the **purpose of putting assets beyond the reach** of someone who is making, or may make, a claim against the company, or otherwise prejudicing their interests."},
   {t:"card", i:"♾️", title:"s.423: no time limit, no insolvency needed", body:"There is **no time limit**, and the company **need not be insolvent** or in an insolvency procedure."},
   {t:"card", i:"🙋", title:"s.423: who can bring it", body:"The **liquidator** or **administrator**, or any **victim** of the transaction (a victim needs the **court's permission** if the company is in liquidation or administration; otherwise no permission is needed) (s.424)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A transaction was made 4 years ago to hide assets from a creditor. Which claim can still reach it?", body:"A **transaction defrauding creditors** (s.423): it has **no time limit**. A s.238 claim would be out of time (2 years)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Preferences", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"💛", title:"What is a preference? (s.239)", body:"The company does something (or lets something be done) that puts a **creditor**, or a **surety or guarantor** of its debts, in a **better position** in an insolvent liquidation than they would otherwise have been in (s.239(4))."},
   {t:"card", i:"📋", title:"Typical preferences", body:"- **Paying one creditor** in full before collapse\n- Giving **security** to an existing unsecured creditor for an **existing debt**\n- Repaying a loan that a **director has personally guaranteed** (this improves the **director's** position as guarantor)"},
   {t:"card", i:"💭", title:"The key: a desire to prefer (s.239(5))", body:"The court can only make an order if the company was **influenced** by a **desire** to put that person in a better position. Desire is **subjective**: the company must have **positively wished** to improve their position."},
   {t:"card", i:"➗", title:"Desire need only be one factor", body:"The desire to prefer need only be **one of the factors** influencing the decision, not the only one."},
   {t:"case", i:"🏦", title:"Re MC Bacon Ltd (1990)", facts:"A struggling company gave its bank a **debenture** (a document giving the lender security) for its existing overdraft, because the bank threatened to withdraw the overdraft otherwise.", rule:"**Not a preference.** The directors wanted to **keep trading** (commercial pressure), not to improve the bank's position."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔁", title:"Desire is presumed for connected persons (s.239(6))", body:"If the preferred person is **connected** with the company, the desire to prefer is **presumed**, and they must prove otherwise. **Exception**: no presumption if they are connected **only** because they are an **employee**."},
   {t:"compare", i:"⏱️", title:"Preference: time limit (s.240(1))", cols:["Preferred person", "Time limit"], rows:[["🙂 Not connected","**6 months** before onset"],["👪 Connected (e.g. director, director's relative)","**2 years** before onset"],["🧑‍🔧 Connected only as an employee","**6 months** (treated as unconnected)"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"📉", title:"Preference: insolvency test", body:"The company was unable to pay its debts at the time or as a result (s.240(2)). Unlike s.238, there is **no presumption** of insolvency, even for connected persons."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔙", title:"Preference: what the court can order (s.241)", body:"The court can order the creditor to **repay** the money or return property, **release** the security, or require the guarantor (e.g. the director) to **pay**."},
   {t:"case", i:"🌿", title:"Example: Fern Ltd and Gail", facts:"Two months before liquidation, while insolvent, Fern Ltd's directors repay a £20,000 overdraft that one of them, Gail, had **personally guaranteed**, leaving trade creditors unpaid.", rule:"Likely a **preference of Gail** (her liability as guarantor is gone). She is **connected**, so the **2-year** limit applies and **desire is presumed**. The court can order her to pay **£20,000** to the liquidator."},
   {t:"card", i:"💡", title:"Exam tip: look for desire", body:"**Genuine commercial pressure** (e.g. to keep the bank or a key supplier) and an **unconnected** creditor: **not** a preference. A **director**, a director's relative, or a director-guarantor who benefits: desire is **presumed**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A company pays its key supplier in full because the supplier refuses to deliver otherwise. Preference?", body:"**Probably not.** The company acted under **commercial pressure** to keep trading, not from a **desire** to improve the supplier's position, and the supplier is unconnected."}
 ]},
 {h:"Floating charges and extortionate credit", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🥛", title:"Floating charges can be invalid (s.245)", body:"A **floating charge** (security over a changing pool of assets, such as stock) created within the relevant period is **invalid**, **except** to the extent of **new consideration** given **at the same time as, or after,** its creation (s.245(2))."},
   {t:"card", i:"🆕", title:"What counts as new consideration?", body:"- **Money paid** to the company, or **goods or services** supplied to it\n- The **discharge or reduction** of a debt of the company\n- Plus **interest** on those amounts at the agreed rate"},
   {t:"compare", i:"⏱️", title:"Floating charge: relevant period", cols:["Chargee (lender)", "Period", "Insolvent at the time?"], rows:[["🙂 Unconnected","**12 months** before onset","**Required**: insolvent when created or as a result"],["👪 Connected","**2 years** before onset","**Irrelevant**: caught even if solvent"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"⏸️", title:"Floating charge: the gap before administration", body:"Also caught: any floating charge created between the filing of an **administration application** or **notice of intention** and the **appointment** of the administrator."},
   {t:"case", i:"👟", title:"Re Shoe Lace Ltd (1993)", facts:"Money was advanced in the weeks **before** the debenture was signed.", rule:"Those advances were **not** made 'at the same time' as the charge, so they didn't count. Only a truly **contemporaneous** advance (e.g. during a short break in the signing meeting) would."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚠️", title:"Securing an old debt is vulnerable", body:"A floating charge given to secure an **existing** overdraft or loan is **vulnerable**, but **fresh lending** made at or after creation stays secured."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔒", title:"s.245 is only for floating charges", body:"A **fixed charge** (security over a specific asset, like a building) given for an existing debt is attacked as a **preference** (s.239) instead."},
   {t:"case", i:"🍐", title:"Example: Pear Ltd's bank", facts:"Pear Ltd, already unable to pay its debts, owes its bank £100,000. On 1 February the bank takes a floating charge and lends a further £40,000 the same day. Pear goes into liquidation in November.", rule:"The charge is valid **only for the £40,000** new money (plus interest). For the original £100,000 the bank is **unsecured**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A director's own company takes a floating charge 18 months before onset, when the company was solvent. Valid?", body:"**Invalid** except for new value. A **connected** chargee is caught within **2 years**, and solvency at the time is **irrelevant**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🦈", title:"Extortionate credit (s.244)", body:"Credit given to the company within the **3 years** ending with the day it **entered administration or went into liquidation** (not the onset date) on terms requiring **grossly exorbitant** payments, or **grossly contravening ordinary principles of fair dealing**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚖️", title:"Extortionate credit: presumption and remedy", body:"The transaction is **presumed extortionate** unless the **lender** proves otherwise. The court can **set aside** or **vary** the terms."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🧾", title:"Clawback time limits at a glance", cols:["Claim", "Unconnected", "Connected", "Insolvent at the time?"], rows:[["🎁 Undervalue (s.238)","2 years","2 years","Required; **presumed** if connected"],["💛 Preference (s.239)","6 months","2 years","Required (no presumption)"],["🥛 Floating charge (s.245)","12 months","2 years","Required if unconnected; **irrelevant** if connected"],["🙈 Defrauding creditors (s.423)","No limit","No limit","Not required"],["🦈 Extortionate credit (s.244)","3 years","3 years","Not required"]]},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Which clawback claim has the shortest time limit, and what is it?", body:"A **preference** to an **unconnected** person: **6 months** before the onset of insolvency."}
 ]},
 {h:"Directors' personal liability", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🔥", title:"Wrongful trading (s.214; s.246ZB in administration)", body:"The court may order a **director** (including a **shadow** director and a **de facto** director, someone acting as a director without being appointed) to **contribute** to the company's assets."},
   {t:"card", i:"✅", title:"Wrongful trading: the three conditions", body:"- The company has gone into **insolvent liquidation** (or **insolvent administration**: s.246ZB)\n- At some time before that, the director **knew or ought to have concluded** there was **no reasonable prospect** of avoiding insolvent liquidation or administration\n- They were a director at that time"},
   {t:"card", i:"🧠", title:"Wrongful trading: the dual test (s.214(4))", body:"The director is judged as a **reasonably diligent person** with (a) the knowledge, skill and experience **reasonably expected** of someone doing **the same job** (**objective minimum**), and (b) the knowledge, skill and experience **that director actually has** (**subjective**, which can raise the bar). Same test as the duty of care in CA 2006 s.174."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛡️", title:"Wrongful trading: the 'every step' defence (s.214(3))", body:"After that point the director took **every step** to **minimise the potential loss to creditors** that they ought to have taken: e.g. taking advice from an **IP**, holding regular board meetings, not taking deposits or credit they can't repay, considering administration or a creditors' voluntary liquidation, ring-fencing new money. **Simply resigning** is rarely enough."},
   {t:"card", i:"😐", title:"Wrongful trading: no dishonesty needed", body:"It is a **civil** claim based on negligence-type failures. **No dishonesty** is required."},
   {t:"case", i:"🍎", title:"Re Produce Marketing Consortium Ltd (No 2) (1989)", facts:"Two directors kept trading for over a year after the company's late accounts showed it was insolvent, hoping things would improve.", rule:"Liable for **wrongful trading**: they **ought to have known** liquidation was inevitable. Ordered to contribute **£75,000**."},
   {t:"card", i:"💷", title:"Wrongful trading: how much, and to whom", body:"Compensatory: broadly the **increase in the net deficiency** to creditors caused by trading on after the critical date. The money goes into the general pot for **unsecured creditors**, not to the floating charge holder."},
   {t:"card", i:"🦹", title:"Fraudulent trading (s.213; s.246ZA in administration)", body:"If the company's business was carried on **with intent to defraud creditors** or for any **fraudulent purpose**, the court can order **anyone who was knowingly a party** to contribute: not just directors, but e.g. a parent company, a creditor, or an adviser who knowingly helped."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎭", title:"Fraudulent trading: dishonesty needed", body:"Requires **actual intent to defraud** (including 'blind-eye' knowledge), so it is much **harder to prove** than wrongful trading. Classic example: taking customer deposits or ordering goods on credit knowing there's **no prospect** of paying."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚓", title:"Fraudulent trading is also a crime", body:"It is a **criminal offence** under CA 2006 **s.993**, whether or not the company is wound up."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🆚", title:"Wrongful vs fraudulent trading", cols:["", "🔥 Wrongful (s.214)", "🦹 Fraudulent (s.213)"], rows:[["Who","**Directors** only (incl. shadow, de facto)","**Anyone** knowingly a party"],["Dishonesty?","❌ Not needed","✅ Intent to defraud"],["Trigger","Knew or **ought to have concluded** insolvent liquidation was unavoidable","Business carried on to defraud"],["Defence","**Every step** to minimise loss","-"],["Crime too?","❌","✅ CA 2006 s.993"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"🧹", title:"Misfeasance (s.212)", body:"A **summary** (quick) procedure: the liquidator (or the Official Receiver, or a creditor, or a contributory (a member) with the court's permission) asks the court to examine an **officer** (director, secretary), liquidator, administrator or promoter who has **misapplied or kept** company money or property, or **breached a fiduciary or other duty** (e.g. paying unlawful dividends, breaching CA 2006 ss.171-177)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔙", title:"Misfeasance: remedy, but no new liability", body:"The court can order them to **repay or restore** the money or property, or pay compensation. It creates **no new liability**: it is just a quick route to enforce **existing duties**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚨", title:"Watch out: this isn't piercing the veil", body:"Wrongful and fraudulent trading are **statutory claims against individuals**. The company stays a **separate person**; the individual is ordered to contribute to the company's assets."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A bank manager knowingly helped a company run a scheme to cheat its suppliers. Wrongful or fraudulent trading?", body:"**Fraudulent trading** (s.213): it catches **anyone** knowingly a party, and there was **intent to defraud**. Wrongful trading only applies to **directors**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A director honestly kept trading, hoping for a big order, after he ought to have seen liquidation was unavoidable. Which claim?", body:"**Wrongful trading** (s.214). **No dishonesty** is needed; the question is what he **knew or ought to have concluded**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Phoenix companies and disqualification", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🐦‍🔥", title:"What is a phoenix company?", body:"A new company that 'rises from the ashes' of an insolvent one, often using the **same name**, **assets** and **customers**, but leaving the **debts** behind."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚫", title:"The prohibited name rule (s.216)", body:"A person who was a **director or shadow director** in the **12 months** before the company went into **insolvent liquidation** must not, for **5 years** after the liquidation, be a director of, or be involved in forming or managing, a company or business using a **prohibited name**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏷️", title:"What is a prohibited name?", body:"The **same name** as the liquidated company, or one **so similar** that it suggests a link with it."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔓", title:"Phoenix: the exceptions", body:"- **Court permission**\n- A successor company buys the whole (or substantially the whole) business from the IP and gives **notice** in the Gazette and to creditors (Insolvency Rules 2016 r.22.4)\n- The other company has used the name for at least **12 months** before the liquidation and was not dormant"},
   {t:"card", i:"⛓️", title:"Phoenix: the consequences", body:"A **criminal offence** (s.216(4)), and the person is **personally liable**, jointly and severally with the new company, for its **debts** incurred while they were involved (s.217). So is anyone who acts on their instructions knowing they're in breach."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Two years after 'Crumbs Ltd' goes into insolvent liquidation, its director starts 'Crumbs Bakery Ltd' without permission or notice. Consequence?", body:"A **criminal offence** and **personal liability** for the new company's debts (ss.216-217). It's within **5 years** and the name is so similar it suggests a link."},
   {t:"card", i:"📝", title:"Every IP reports on the directors", body:"In every **insolvent liquidation** and **administration**, the IP **reports** on the directors' conduct to the **Secretary of State**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🟥", title:"Disqualification for unfitness (CDDA s.6)", body:"The court **must** disqualify a director whose conduct makes them **unfit**, for **2 to 15 years** (Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986, **CDDA**). The Secretary of State may accept a **disqualification undertaking** instead."},
   {t:"card", i:"📌", title:"Common grounds for unfitness", body:"**Wrongful trading**, **preferences** to directors, unpaid **Crown debts** (tax owed to HMRC), and **failure to keep records**."},
   {t:"card", i:"💰", title:"Compensation orders (CDDA s.15A)", body:"The court can also order a disqualified director whose conduct caused loss to creditors to pay **compensation**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"What is the minimum and maximum period of disqualification for an unfit director under CDDA s.6?", body:"**2 years** minimum, **15 years** maximum."}
 ]},
 {h:"Order of priority: the front of the queue", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🚶", title:"How the queue works", body:"Each class is paid **in full** before the next gets anything. Within a class, creditors share **pari passu** (rateably, in proportion to their debts) if there isn't enough."},
   {t:"card", i:"1️⃣", title:"First: fixed charge holders", body:"Paid from the proceeds of the assets **subject to their fixed charges** (after the costs of selling those assets). Any **shortfall** becomes an **unsecured** claim; any surplus goes into the general pot."},
   {t:"card", i:"2️⃣", title:"Second: expenses", body:"The **expenses** of the liquidation or administration: the IP's fees, the costs of selling assets, and in administration debts under contracts the administrator adopted. If free assets aren't enough, expenses come out of **floating charge** assets (s.176ZA)."},
   {t:"card", i:"3️⃣", title:"Third: ordinary preferential creditors", body:"Ranking equally among themselves (IA 1986 s.386 and Sch.6):\n- Employees' **unpaid wages** for the **4 months** before the insolvency, up to **£800** each\n- **Accrued holiday pay** (no cap)\n- Certain **occupational pension contributions**\n- Eligible bank deposits covered by the Financial Services Compensation Scheme"},
   {t:"card", i:"✂️", title:"Wages above £800", body:"Any wages above **£800** are an **unsecured** claim. (The government's **National Insurance Fund** often pays employees certain statutory amounts first, then takes over their preferential claim.)"},
   {t:"card", i:"4️⃣", title:"Fourth: secondary preferential creditors", body:"Since **1 December 2020**: **HMRC** for taxes the company **collected or deducted on behalf of others**: **VAT**, **PAYE income tax**, **employees' NICs**, construction industry scheme deductions and student loan deductions."},
   {t:"card", i:"❌", title:"Taxes that are NOT preferential", body:"Taxes the company owes **itself**, such as **corporation tax** and **employer's NICs**, are **not preferential**. They are **unsecured**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"An employee is owed £2,000 of wages for the last 2 months. How is it ranked?", body:"**£800** is **ordinary preferential**; the other **£1,200** is **unsecured**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"HMRC is owed VAT and corporation tax. How is each ranked?", body:"**VAT**: **secondary preferential** (collected for others). **Corporation tax**: **unsecured**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Order of priority: the back of the queue", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🍪", title:"Fifth: the prescribed part (s.176A)", body:"A slice of the **net floating charge realisations** (what's left of the floating charge assets after expenses and preferential debts) is **ring-fenced** for **unsecured creditors**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧮", title:"How to work out the prescribed part", body:"**50% of the first £10,000**, plus **20% of the rest**, capped at **£800,000** (for floating charges created on or after **6 April 2020**; **£600,000** for earlier charges). There is **no** prescribed part for floating charges created before **15 September 2003**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚧", title:"When the prescribed part doesn't apply", body:"If the net property is less than **£10,000** and the IP thinks the cost of distributing it would be **disproportionate**. And the floating charge holder **cannot share** in the prescribed part for its unsecured shortfall."},
   {t:"case", i:"➗", title:"Example: the prescribed part", facts:"After expenses and preferential creditors, **£400,000** of floating charge assets remain.", rule:"Prescribed part = 50% × £10,000 (**£5,000**) + 20% × £390,000 (**£78,000**) = **£83,000** for unsecured creditors. The floating charge holder gets the other **£317,000** (up to its debt)."},
   {t:"card", i:"6️⃣", title:"Sixth: floating charge holders", body:"Paid from the **rest** of the floating charge assets (in order of priority between them, if there are several)."},
   {t:"card", i:"7️⃣", title:"Seventh: unsecured creditors", body:"Trade suppliers, customers, landlords for unpaid rent, **HMRC for corporation tax**, and the unsecured **shortfall** of secured creditors. Paid **pari passu**, together with the prescribed part."},
   {t:"card", i:"8️⃣", title:"Eighth: interest, then deferred debts", body:"**Statutory interest** on proved debts from the start of the insolvency (only if there's a surplus), then **deferred debts**: sums owed to members **as members**, such as **declared but unpaid dividends** (s.74(2)(f))."},
   {t:"card", i:"9️⃣", title:"Last: shareholders", body:"**Shareholders** (members) get anything left, according to their rights (e.g. **preference shares** first, if they have priority for capital)."},
   {t:"compare", i:"📋", title:"The whole queue", cols:["#", "Who", "Paid from"], rows:[["1","🔒 Fixed charge holders","Their fixed charge assets"],["2","🧾 Expenses","Free assets, then floating charge assets"],["3","👩‍🍳 Ordinary preferential (wages 4 months/£800, holiday pay, pensions)","Free assets, then floating charge assets"],["4","🏛️ Secondary preferential (HMRC: VAT, PAYE, employee NICs)","Free assets, then floating charge assets"],["5","🍪 Prescribed part (for unsecured)","Floating charge assets"],["6","🥛 Floating charge holders","Floating charge assets"],["7","🧺 Unsecured, pari passu","Whatever's left"],["8","➕ Interest, then deferred debts","Surplus"],["9","👥 Shareholders","Surplus"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"💡", title:"Exam tip: fixed assets are off limits", body:"Preferential debts and the prescribed part come out of **floating charge** assets only, **never** out of **fixed charge** assets."},
   {t:"card", i:"💡", title:"Exam tip: HMRC's two places", body:"HMRC is preferential only for taxes **collected for others** (VAT, PAYE, employee NICs), ranking **after** employees. **Corporation tax** is **unsecured**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Does the prescribed part come out of fixed charge assets or floating charge assets?", body:"**Floating charge assets** only. Fixed charge assets go to the fixed charge holder."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A shareholder is owed a dividend that was declared but never paid. Where do they rank?", body:"As a **deferred debt**, after all unsecured creditors and statutory interest (s.74(2)(f)), because it is owed to them **as a member**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗺️", title:"Your 6-step route", body:"**1** Fix the onset date and whether the person is connected → **2** Undervalue? → **3** Preference? → **4** Floating charge, fraud or extortionate credit? → **5** Personal liability? → **6** Order of priority. The full route is this topic's **framework**."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Source: proposals/rooms/learn-blp-partnerships.js */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["blp/partnerships"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"📘", title:"One Act runs a general partnership", body:"A **general partnership** is governed by the **Partnership Act 1890 (PA 1890)**. The Act does two separate jobs."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏠", title:"Job 1: rules between the partners", body:"It sets **default rules** for the relationship **between the partners**: profit shares, management and leaving. They apply **unless the partners agree otherwise**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚪", title:"Job 2: rules with outsiders", body:"It sets rules on the **firm and outsiders**: when a partner's act **binds the firm**, and when partners are **personally liable**. The partners **cannot** change these rules by their agreement."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Can the partners' agreement change when an outsider can hold the firm liable?", body:"**No.** The partners can vary the rules **between themselves**, but not the rules on the **firm and outsiders**."},
   {t:"card", i:"💥", title:"No separate person, no limit", body:"A partnership has **no separate legal personality** (it is not a 'person' in law apart from the partners). So the partners are the business, and they are **personally liable for its debts without limit**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏛️", title:"An LLP is different", body:"An **LLP** (limited liability partnership) is a **separate legal person** governed by the **Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000 (LLPA 2000)** and the **LLP Regulations 2001**. But its internal default rules look very like a partnership's."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔗", title:"Where the neighbours live", body:"Setting up a partnership or LLP: **Forming a company, partnership or LLP**. Tax on partners: **Income tax** and **CGT**. A partner's bankruptcy: **Personal insolvency**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Does a partnership exist? (PA 1890 ss.1-2)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🤝", title:"The definition (s.1)", body:"Partnership is 'the relation which subsists between **persons carrying on a business in common with a view of profit**'. **All three** elements must be met."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛠️", title:"Element 1: a business", body:"'**Business**' includes every trade, occupation or profession (**s.45**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"👫", title:"Element 2: in common", body:"The business is carried on **by or on behalf of all of them**, not just by one of them."},
   {t:"card", i:"📈", title:"Element 3: with a view of profit", body:"The **aim** is to make a profit. A business that actually makes a **loss** can still be a partnership."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚡", title:"A question of fact", body:"No written agreement, no registration and not even **awareness** that they are 'partners' is needed. Whether a partnership exists is a **question of fact**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Two friends trade together for profit but never sign anything. Partners?", body:"**Yes**, if they carry on a business in common with a view of profit. No paperwork or awareness is needed."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏡", title:"s.2: co-owning property is not enough", body:"**Co-owning property** does **not by itself** create a partnership. Example: two people inherit a house and share the rent."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧾", title:"s.2: sharing gross returns is not enough", body:"Sharing **gross returns** (turnover, the takings before expenses) does **not by itself** create a partnership."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔍", title:"s.2: a profit share is evidence", body:"Receiving a **share of the profits** is **prima facie evidence** of partnership (evidence that points to it unless something else shows otherwise). It is **not conclusive**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🙅", title:"Profit share but not a partner", body:"A profit share does not by itself make someone a partner if they are, for example: a **lender** paid interest that varies with profits; an **employee** paid by a share of profits; or the **widow or child of a deceased partner** receiving an annuity."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A bank lends to a shop and is paid interest that rises with the shop's profits. Is the bank a partner?", body:"**Not by that alone.** A lender paid interest varying with profits is not made a partner just by that profit link (s.2)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Default rules between the partners (ss.19-30)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🔀", title:"Defaults give way to agreement (s.19)", body:"The default rules apply **only so far as the partners have not agreed otherwise**. The terms can be varied **by consent**, and consent can be **inferred from a course of dealing** (how the partners have actually behaved)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🍩", title:"Capital, profits and losses: equal (s.24(1))", body:"Partners share **capital and profits equally**, and bear **losses equally**, **whatever each partner contributed**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚫", title:"No interest on capital (s.24(4))", body:"**Capital** is the money a partner puts into the firm as their stake. By default it earns **no interest**."},
   {t:"card", i:"💷", title:"5% on advances (s.24(3))", body:"An **advance** is a loan by a partner to the firm **beyond their agreed capital**. It earns interest at **5% a year**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🩹", title:"Indemnity from the firm (s.24(2))", body:"The firm must **indemnify** a partner (pay them back) for payments made and liabilities incurred **properly in the ordinary conduct of the business**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧑‍🍳", title:"Everyone manages, nobody is paid (s.24(5)-(6))", body:"**Every partner may take part in management.** No partner gets a **salary** (remuneration) for acting in the business."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🗳️", title:"Who has to agree?", cols:["Decision", "Default (PA 1890)"], rows:[["Ordinary matters","✋ Majority (s.24(8))"],["Changing the nature of the business","🤝 Unanimous (s.24(8))"],["Bringing in a new partner","🤝 Unanimous (s.24(7))"],["Changing the partnership terms","🤝 Unanimous (s.19)"],["Expelling a partner","🚫 Not possible by majority unless an express power was agreed (s.25)"]]},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Two of three partners want to throw the third out. No agreement. Can they?", body:"**No.** There is **no power of expulsion** by a majority unless an express power has been agreed (**s.25**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📚", title:"The books (s.24(9))", body:"The partnership books are kept at the **principal place of business**. **Every partner** may inspect and copy them."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏪", title:"Partnership property (ss.20-21)", body:"**Partnership property** is property brought into the firm or acquired **for the firm's purposes** in the course of its business. The partners must hold and use it **only for the partnership** (s.20). Property **bought with the firm's money** is treated as bought for the firm, unless the facts show otherwise (s.21). Property a partner merely **lets the firm use** stays theirs."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A partner owns the shop building and lets the firm trade from it rent-free. The firm buys a van with firm money. Whose is each?", body:"The **van** is **partnership property** (bought with firm money, s.21). The **building** stays the **partner's own**: letting the firm use it does not make it partnership property."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔒", title:"A partner's own creditor (s.23)", body:"A creditor of one partner **personally** cannot seize partnership property. It can only get a **charging order** over that partner's **share** (and a receiver of their profits). The other partners may then **buy out** the charged share, or choose to **dissolve** the firm (s.33(2))."},
   {t:"card", i:"📝", title:"Assigning a share (s.31)", body:"A partner can **assign** (transfer) their share, for example to a lender as security. But the **assignee** (the person it is transferred to) does **not** become a partner: they cannot take part in **management** or demand to see the **accounts**, and only receive that partner's **share of the profits**. On dissolution they receive that partner's **share of the assets**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏳", title:"Duration: partnership at will", body:"If there is no fixed term, it is a **partnership at will**. **Any partner** may end it by **notice** (s.26, s.32(c))."},
   {t:"case", i:"🧮", title:"Example: Asha, Ben and Chen", facts:"No written agreement. Asha put in £60,000 capital; Ben and Chen £10,000 each. Asha also lent the firm a further £20,000.", rule:"Profits are split **equally** (one third each). **No interest on capital.** Asha gets **5% (£1,000 a year)** on her £20,000 loan, because it is an advance. Asha cannot be paid a **salary** unless all agree."},
   {t:"card", i:"💡", title:"Exam tip: 'most of the capital' or 'works full time'", body:"With no agreement, profits are still shared **equally**, with **no salary** and **no interest on capital**. Only **advances** earn **5%**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📣", title:"Duty: true accounts (s.28)", body:"Each partner must give **true accounts and full information** of all things affecting the partnership to every partner."},
   {t:"card", i:"🤫", title:"Duty: no secret profits (s.29)", body:"A partner must **account to the firm** (hand over) any benefit made **without the others' consent** from a transaction concerning the partnership, or from using partnership **property, name or business connection**. This is a **secret profit**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🥊", title:"Duty: no competing (s.30)", body:"A partner who, **without consent**, carries on a **competing business** of the same nature must account for and pay over **all the profits** made by it."},
   {t:"card", i:"💛", title:"Utmost good faith", body:"Partners owe each other a general duty of **utmost good faith**. This is a **fiduciary duty**: a duty of loyalty and honesty to the others."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A partner secretly runs a rival shop of the same kind. What must they hand over?", body:"**All the profits** of the competing business (**s.30**)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Partners' authority: binding the firm (ss.5-8)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🧑‍💼", title:"Every partner is an agent (s.5)", body:"Every partner is an **agent of the firm and of the other partners** for the purpose of the partnership business. An **agent** is someone whose acts can bind another."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔗", title:"Usual acts bind the firm (s.5)", body:"An act done by a partner **for carrying on in the usual way business of the kind carried on by the firm** **binds the firm and the partners**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚧", title:"The s.5 exception: two things together", body:"The firm is **not** bound only if: the partner in fact had **no authority** in that matter, **and** the outsider **either knew** they had no authority **or did not know or believe** them to be a partner."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A partner had no authority, but the supplier didn't know that and knew she was a partner. Is the firm bound by a usual act?", body:"**Yes.** Both parts of the exception must be met. Here the supplier neither knew of the lack of authority nor doubted she was a partner."},
   {t:"card", i:"✍️", title:"Basis 1: express actual authority", body:"The partners **agreed** this partner could do this, for example in the partnership agreement or at a partners' meeting."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧭", title:"Basis 2: implied actual authority", body:"Authority that **comes with the partner's role**, or with the partners' **past conduct**."},
   {t:"card", i:"👀", title:"Basis 3: apparent (usual) authority", body:"Under s.5, the act is of a kind **usually done** in that type of business and the outsider **did not know of any restriction**. This protects outsiders even where the partners have **internally limited** a partner's authority."},
   {t:"card", i:"🖋️", title:"Acts in the firm name (s.6)", body:"The firm is bound by acts or documents relating to the business done or signed **in the firm name**, or otherwise showing an intention to bind the firm, by **any person authorised**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎣", title:"Unconnected purposes don't bind (s.7)", body:"If a partner pledges the firm's credit for a purpose **apparently not connected** with the firm's ordinary business, the firm is **not bound** unless specially authorised."},
   {t:"card", i:"📢", title:"Internal limits bind only those with notice (s.8)", body:"If the partners agree to restrict a partner's authority, an act in breach does **not** bind the firm **with respect to persons having notice** of the restriction. Outsiders **without notice** are protected by s.5."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧾", title:"Inside the firm: indemnify", body:"A partner who **exceeds their actual authority** is in breach of the partnership agreement and must **indemnify** the other partners for their loss."},
   {t:"card", i:"✅", title:"Ratification", body:"The other partners can **ratify** an unauthorised act (approve it afterwards). It then binds the firm."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛒", title:"What counts as 'usual'?", body:"It depends on the firm. In a **trading** firm: buying stock, hiring staff, ordering ordinary supplies and selling goods; a trading partner also usually has authority to **borrow and give security**. In a **non-trading** professional firm (e.g. solicitors), borrowing is **less obviously usual**."},
   {t:"case", i:"🪵", title:"Example: Dan's timber order", facts:"A builders' firm's agreement says no partner may order materials over £5,000 without the others' consent. Dan orders £12,000 of timber from a supplier who does not know of the limit.", rule:"**The firm is bound**: ordering timber is usual for a builder, and the supplier had no notice (s.5, s.8). Dan must **indemnify** his partners for any loss."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Same facts, but the supplier had been told of the £5,000 limit. Is the firm bound?", body:"**No**, unless the others ratify. A restriction binds outsiders **with notice** of it (**s.8**). Dan is liable himself."}
 ]},
 {h:"Liability of partners to outsiders (ss.9-12)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"📄", title:"Debts: joint liability (s.9)", body:"Every partner is **jointly liable** with the others for all **debts and obligations** of the firm incurred **while they are a partner**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎯", title:"Any one partner, the whole debt", body:"Under the **Civil Liability (Contribution) Act 1978 s.3**, a judgment against one partner does not bar later action against the others. So in practice a creditor can pursue **any one partner for the whole debt**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚠️", title:"Wrongs: the firm is liable (s.10)", body:"Where a partner acting in the **ordinary course of the firm's business** (or with the others' authority) commits a **wrong** (e.g. **negligence** or **fraud**) causing loss to a non-partner, the **firm** is liable."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎯", title:"Wrongs: joint and several (s.12)", body:"For wrongs, every partner is **jointly and severally liable**: each can be sued **alone or together** with the others for the full amount."},
   {t:"card", i:"💼", title:"Misapplied client money (s.11)", body:"The firm is also liable where a partner, acting within their **apparent authority**, receives a client's money and **misapplies** it (uses it wrongly)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔗", title:"'Ordinary course' can cover wrongdoing", body:"A firm can be **vicariously liable** (liable for another's wrong) where a partner's wrongful acts are **so closely connected** with acts he was authorised to do that they can fairly be regarded as done in the ordinary course of the firm's business. Example: a law firm partner who helped draft agreements as part of a **fraud**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Debts vs wrongs: which word for each?", body:"Debts and contracts: **joint** (s.9). Wrongs such as negligence: **joint and several** (s.12). Either way a claimant can recover the **whole** amount from **one** partner."},
   {t:"card", i:"↩️", title:"Contribution between partners", body:"A partner who pays **more than their share** can claim a **contribution** (or a full **indemnity**) from the others (s.24(2) and the 1978 Act), in the ratio they agreed to **share losses**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚖️", title:"Suing the firm and enforcing", body:"The firm may be **sued in the firm name** (CPR 7.2A and PD 7A). A judgment against the firm can be enforced against **partnership property** and against the **personal assets of each partner**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A creditor wins judgment against the firm. Can it take a partner's own car?", body:"**Yes.** It can enforce against partnership property **and** each partner's **personal assets**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Holding out, new partners and retiring partners (ss.14, 17, 36)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🎭", title:"Holding out (s.14)", body:"A person who, by words or conduct, **represents themselves** as a partner, or **knowingly allows themselves to be represented** as one, is **liable as if a partner** to anyone who has **given credit to the firm** on the faith of that representation."},
   {t:"card", i:"📃", title:"Example: name left on the notepaper", body:"A **retired partner** whose name stays on the firm's notepaper **with their consent** can be liable to a **new creditor who relied on it**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🕊️", title:"Death is different (s.14(2))", body:"After a partner's **death**, continuing to use the firm name does **not** make their **estate** (what they left behind) liable."},
   {t:"card", i:"🆕", title:"Incoming partner: not liable for old debts (s.17(1))", body:"A new partner is **not liable** for debts or obligations incurred **before they became a partner**, unless they agree to take them on."},
   {t:"card", i:"👋", title:"Retiring partner: still liable for their time (s.17(2))", body:"A retiring partner **remains liable** for debts and obligations incurred **while they were a partner**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔄", title:"Escape route: novation (s.17(3))", body:"They are **discharged** only by a **novation**: an agreement (express or inferred from conduct) between the **creditor**, the retiring partner and the continuing partners that the creditor will look **only to the new firm**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛡️", title:"Indemnity: protects against partners, not creditors", body:"In practice the retiring partner takes an **indemnity** from the continuing partners in the retirement agreement. It protects them **as against their partners** but **does not bind the creditor**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A retiring partner has an indemnity from the others. Can an old creditor still sue her?", body:"**Yes**, unless there was a **novation**. The indemnity does not bind the creditor; she then claims on the indemnity."},
   {t:"card", i:"📬", title:"Debts after leaving (s.36)", body:"A person dealing with a firm after a change in its members may treat all **apparent members** of the old firm as still partners **until they have notice** of the change."},
   {t:"card", i:"✉️", title:"Protection 1: actual notice to old contacts", body:"Give **actual notice** (e.g. a letter or email) to everyone who **dealt with the firm before** the change."},
   {t:"card", i:"📰", title:"Protection 2: the London Gazette (s.36(2))", body:"An advertisement in the **London Gazette** (the official journal for public notices) counts as notice to people who had **not** dealt with the firm before."},
   {t:"card", i:"🤷", title:"Unknown partners need no notice (s.36(3))", body:"A retiring partner who was **not known to be a partner** by the person dealing with the firm is **not liable** for debts the firm runs up **after** she retires, even if that person was never given notice. This protects her even against an **old customer** who never knew she was a partner. The **estate of a dead partner** and a **bankrupt** partner are not liable for later debts either, so no notice is needed for them."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A sleeping partner retires. An old supplier never knew she was a partner and gets no notice. Is she liable for a debt run up after she left?", body:"**No.** The supplier never knew she was a partner, so s.36(3) protects her even without notice."},
   {t:"card", i:"📢", title:"Requiring the others to concur (s.37)", body:"Any partner can **publicly notify** their retirement and **require the others to concur** (join in the notice)."},
   {t:"case", i:"📦", title:"Example: Ella retires", facts:"Ella retires on 31 March. In May the firm buys goods on credit from a long-standing supplier who was never told she had left.", rule:"**Ella is liable** for the May debt (s.36(1)): the supplier had dealt with the firm before and got no actual notice. A Gazette notice alone would not protect her against existing contacts. She can claim on her **indemnity** from the continuing partners."},
   {t:"compare", i:"⏱️", title:"Three timing rules tested together", cols:["Who", "Which debts?"], rows:[["🆕 New partner","Not liable for debts from before they joined (s.17(1))"],["👋 Retiring partner: old debts","Liable for debts from their time unless a novation (s.17(2)-(3))"],["👋 Retiring partner: later debts","Liable to old customers not given actual notice (s.36)"]]}
 ]},
 {h:"Dissolution (ss.32-44)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🔚", title:"What dissolution means", body:"**Dissolution** means the partnership relationship **ends**. Under the default rules it happens in the following situations."},
   {t:"card", i:"📅", title:"Expiry or notice (s.32)", body:"On **expiry** of a fixed term, or completion of a **single venture** (s.32(a)-(b)). Or by **notice** from any partner if it is **at will**, effective from the date stated or the date of the notice (s.32(c))."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚰️", title:"Death or bankruptcy (s.33(1))", body:"On the **death or bankruptcy** of any partner, **unless the agreement provides otherwise**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔒", title:"A charged share (s.33(2))", body:"At the **option of the other partners** if a partner's share is **charged** (by a creditor's **charging order**) for that partner's own separate debts."},
   {t:"card", i:"⛔", title:"Illegality (s.34)", body:"If the business becomes **illegal**, e.g. a partner **loses a practising licence** needed for the business."},
   {t:"card", i:"👩‍⚖️", title:"Court order (s.35)", body:"Grounds include: a partner's **permanent incapacity**; **conduct prejudicial** to the business; **persistent breach** of the agreement; the business can only be carried on **at a loss**; or it is **just and equitable**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A partner dies and the agreement says nothing. What happens?", body:"The partnership is **dissolved** (**s.33(1)**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧹", title:"Winding up (ss.38-39)", body:"After dissolution, each partner keeps authority to bind the firm so far as needed to **wind up** its affairs and finish unfinished transactions (s.38). Each partner can insist that partnership property is **sold** and the proceeds used to pay debts (s.39)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🪜", title:"Who gets paid, in order (s.44)", body:"**(1)** debts to **outside creditors**; **(2)** partners' **advances** (loans); **(3)** partners' **capital**; **(4)** any **surplus**, shared in the **profit-sharing ratio**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📉", title:"Who bears losses (s.44(a))", body:"Losses, including **losses of capital**, are paid first out of **profits**, then out of **capital**, then by the **partners individually** in the proportion in which they share profits."},
   {t:"card", i:"💸", title:"Outgoing partner's share: a debt (s.43)", body:"Unless the partners agreed otherwise, the amount owed to a partner who leaves (or to a dead partner's estate) for their **share** is a **debt** owed by the continuing partners from the **date of leaving or death**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚖️", title:"Firm carries on before paying out (s.42)", body:"If the others **carry on the business** using the firm's assets **without paying out** the outgoing partner, the outgoing partner (or the estate) can choose **either** a share of the **profits made by using their share**, **or** **5% a year interest** on the value of their share. This does not apply where the agreement gave the others an **option to buy** the share and they properly used it."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Which comes first: a partner's loan to the firm or her capital?", body:"The **loan (advance)**. Order: outside creditors, advances, capital, surplus."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏚️", title:"An insolvent firm", body:"If the firm is **insolvent** (cannot pay its debts), it can be wound up as an **unregistered company** under the **Insolvent Partnerships Order 1994** (outline only)."}
 ]},
 {h:"The partnership agreement: common clauses", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"📜", title:"Why have one?", body:"A written **partnership agreement** replaces the default rules that **do not suit** the business."},
   {t:"card", i:"♾️", title:"Commencement and duration", body:"A **fixed term** or indefinite, and that the firm **continues** despite a partner's death, retirement or bankruptcy (a **continuation clause**), to avoid automatic dissolution under ss.32-33."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏷️", title:"Name, business and capital", body:"The **firm name** and the **nature of the business** (changing it needs unanimity by default). **Capital**: how much each contributes, whether **interest** is paid on it, and how it is **repaid** on leaving."},
   {t:"card", i:"💰", title:"Profits and losses", body:"The **profit-sharing ratio** (often not equal), **salaries** for working partners, and **drawings** (advance payments on account of profits)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗳️", title:"Management and decision-making", body:"Which matters need a **majority**, a **special majority** or **unanimity**; meetings; and **limits on authority** (e.g. no single partner may borrow or contract above a set amount)."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏰", title:"Duties", body:"Devote **full time and attention**; no other business; **confidentiality**; holidays and sick leave."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚪", title:"Retirement and expulsion", body:"A **notice period** for retiring; **expulsion** on specified grounds (needed because **s.25** gives no default power); **compulsory retirement** (e.g. incapacity)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧾", title:"Outgoing partners' shares", body:"How a leaving or deceased partner's share is **valued and paid out** (often by instalments), and whether the continuing partners have an **option** (not a binding obligation) to buy it. An option matters for inheritance tax **business property relief** (see **VAT and IHT BPR**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧱", title:"Restrictive covenants", body:"Limits on an outgoing partner **competing**, dealing with or **soliciting clients**, or **poaching staff** (restraint of trade). Enforceable only if they protect a **legitimate interest** and are **reasonable** in **area, duration and activity**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🕊️", title:"Disputes and accounts", body:"**Mediation** or **arbitration** instead of court proceedings. The **accounting date**, the accountants, and partners' access to the books."},
   {t:"compare", i:"💡", title:"Exam tip: match the worry to the clause", cols:["Client's worry", "Clause (default it overrides)"], rows:[["Firm will end if a partner dies or leaves","♾️ Continuation clause (ss.32-33)"],["Wants to be able to remove a partner","🚪 Express expulsion power (s.25)"],["Unequal effort or capital","💰 Profit share, salaries, interest on capital (s.24)"],["Protecting clients after a partner leaves","🧱 Reasonable restrictive covenant"]]},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A client fears the firm will collapse if her partner dies. Which clause?", body:"A **continuation clause**, overriding automatic dissolution on death (**s.33**)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Limited partnerships and LLPs", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🧑‍🤝‍🧑", title:"Limited partnership: the outline", body:"Under the **Limited Partnerships Act 1907**, a limited partnership must be **registered at Companies House**. It needs at least one **general partner** (unlimited liability, manages) and at least one **limited partner** (liability limited to their contribution)."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚠️", title:"Limited partner: stay out of management", body:"A limited partner who **takes part in management** loses limited liability. In England and Wales a limited partnership has **no separate legal personality**. Used mainly for **investment funds**. Not to be confused with an **LLP**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏛️", title:"LLP member: agent of the LLP only (s.6(1))", body:"Every member of an LLP is an **agent of the LLP** (LLPA 2000 s.6(1)), **not** of the other members."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚧", title:"When the LLP is not bound (s.6(2))", body:"The LLP is **not bound** if the member **had no authority** **and** the person dealing with them **knew** that, or **did not know or believe** them to be a member. This mirrors **PA 1890 s.5**."},
   {t:"card", i:"👻", title:"Former members (s.6(3))", body:"A former member is treated as still a member by a person who has **not had notice** that they have left **and** has not been notified via **Companies House**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔁", title:"LLP default rules (regs 7-8)", body:"If there is **no members' agreement** (or it is silent): members share **capital and profits equally** (reg.7(1)); the LLP must **indemnify** members for liabilities properly incurred; **every member may manage** (reg.7(3)); **no remuneration** (reg.7(4))."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗳️", title:"LLP decisions and duties", body:"**No new member** without **all** existing members' consent (reg.7(5)). Ordinary matters by **majority**; changing the **nature of the business** needs **unanimity** (reg.7(6)). A member must account for profits from **competing** business and benefits from using LLP property, name or connection (reg.7(9)-(10))."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚫", title:"LLP: no expulsion unless agreed (reg.8)", body:"Members **cannot expel** a member by a majority unless that power has been **expressly agreed**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚶", title:"Leaving an LLP", body:"With no agreement on leaving, a member may leave by giving **reasonable notice** to the others (LLPA 2000 s.4(3)). Changes in members go to Companies House (forms **LL AP01** and **LL TM01**) **within 14 days** (s.9)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛡️", title:"Who pays an LLP's debts?", body:"The **LLP itself** is liable for its debts and for wrongs by members in the course of its business (s.6(4)). A member is **not personally liable** for the LLP's debts."},
   {t:"card", i:"🙋", title:"A member's own negligence", body:"A member is liable in tort for their **own** negligence only if they **assumed personal responsibility** to the claimant."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏪", title:"When an LLP goes insolvent", body:"Members can be made to contribute for **wrongful trading** and **fraudulent trading** (IA 1986 ss.213-214, as applied to LLPs). Under **s.214A** they must repay **withdrawals** made in the **2 years** before winding up if they knew, or had reasonable grounds to believe, that the LLP was then unable to pay its debts or would become unable to as a result of the withdrawal."},
   {t:"card", i:"📋", title:"Designated members", body:"**Designated members** have extra statutory duties: signing and filing **accounts**, the **confirmation statement**, appointing **auditors**. They face **penalties** if these are not done."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"An LLP member dies. Does the LLP end?", body:"**No.** The LLP is a separate person and **continues**. A general partnership would be **dissolved** by default (s.33)."},
   {t:"compare", i:"⚖️", title:"General partnership vs LLP", cols:["", "General partnership", "LLP"], rows:[["Agent of","The firm and the other partners (s.5)","The LLP only (s.6)"],["Liable for debts","All partners, personally, without limit","The LLP; members only to agreed contribution (plus s.214A clawback)"],["Default profit share","Equal","Equal"],["Default salary","None","None"],["Expulsion","Only if agreed (s.25)","Only if agreed (reg.8)"],["Member dies or leaves","Dissolves by default (s.33)","LLP continues"],["Leaving","Notice dissolves a partnership at will","Reasonable notice (s.4(3))"]]}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Source: proposals/rooms/learn-blp-personal-insolvency.js */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["blp/personal-insolvency"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🧍", title:"Who personal insolvency is for", body:"**Personal insolvency** applies to **individuals**, including **sole traders** and **partners** in a general partnership. They have **unlimited liability** for business debts, so business debts are their personal debts. A company's insolvency is in **Corporate insolvency procedures**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📉", title:"When is an individual insolvent?", body:"An individual is insolvent when they **cannot pay their debts as they fall due**."},
   {t:"card", i:"✌️", title:"Two procedures on the syllabus", body:"Both are in the **Insolvency Act 1986 (IA 1986)** Parts VIII-X:\n\n- an **individual voluntary arrangement (IVA)**: a binding **deal with creditors** that avoids bankruptcy;\n- **bankruptcy**: the debtor's assets are taken and sold for the creditors by a **trustee in bankruptcy**, and the debtor is usually **discharged** (freed from most debts) after **one year**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧺", title:"Other debt options (outside the syllabus)", body:"Two other options exist but are **not** in the SQE1 specification, so they mainly appear as wrong answers:\n\n- a **debt relief order (DRO)** (IA 1986 Part 7A): for people with debts under **£50,000**, assets under **£2,000** and spare income under **£75** a month; made by the Official Receiver through a debt adviser, it lasts **12 months** and then the debts are written off;\n- **Breathing Space**: a **60-day** pause on most creditor action and interest, started through a debt adviser (longer during a mental health crisis). It is a pause, not a solution."},
   {t:"card", i:"👥", title:"The key players", body:"- The **Official Receiver (OR)**: a civil servant in the Insolvency Service and an officer of the court.\n- Licensed **insolvency practitioners (IPs)**: private professionals who act as nominee, supervisor or trustee.\n- The **Adjudicator**: an Insolvency Service official who decides debtors' **own** bankruptcy applications."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A sole trader's business can't pay its suppliers. Company or personal insolvency?", body:"**Personal insolvency.** A sole trader has no separate legal personality, so the business debts are their own debts. The options are an **IVA** or **bankruptcy**."}
 ]},
 {h:"IVA: what it is and how it starts", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🤝", title:"IVA: what it is", body:"An **IVA** (IA 1986 ss.252-263) is a **legally binding agreement** between the debtor and their creditors to pay **all or part** of the debts. Payment is usually from **income over a period** (often **5 years**) and/or from selling certain assets. The **balance is written off** at the end."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧑‍💼", title:"IVA: the supervisor", body:"An IVA is supervised by an IP called the **supervisor**. The supervisor collects the debtor's payments and shares them out among the creditors."},
   {t:"card", i:"👍", title:"IVA: advantages over bankruptcy", body:"- The debtor avoids the **stigma and restrictions** of bankruptcy: they can usually keep running their business and remain a company director.\n- They keep more **control over their assets**, often including the home (if equity is released).\n- Creditors usually get **more** than in a bankruptcy, because costs are lower."},
   {t:"card", i:"👎", title:"IVA: disadvantages", body:"- It needs **creditor approval**.\n- It **lasts longer** than bankruptcy (often 5 years; bankruptcy discharge is usually after **1 year**).\n- If it **fails**, bankruptcy usually follows anyway."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Why might creditors prefer an IVA to bankruptcy?", body:"They usually receive **more**, because an IVA costs less to run than a bankruptcy."},
   {t:"card", i:"📝", title:"IVA step 1: the proposal", body:"The debtor prepares a **proposal** (how much will be paid and how) and a **statement of affairs** (a list of their assets and debts). An IP helps and agrees to act as the **nominee**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛑", title:"IVA step 2: interim order (optional)", body:"The debtor can apply to court for an **interim order** (ss.252-253). It gives a **moratorium** (a pause on creditor action) lasting initially **14 days**, extendable."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚫", title:"What the moratorium stops", body:"While the interim order lasts, **no bankruptcy petition** can be presented or proceeded with, and no other proceedings, execution or legal process can be started or continued against the debtor or their property, **without the court's permission**. Many IVAs now go ahead **without** an interim order."},
   {t:"card", i:"📋", title:"IVA step 3: the nominee's report", body:"The nominee reports on whether the proposal has a **reasonable prospect of being approved and implemented**, and whether the creditors should be asked to consider it (s.256 or s.256A)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"How long does an interim order's moratorium initially last?", body:"**14 days**, and it can be extended. During it, no bankruptcy petition can be presented or proceeded with without the court's permission."}
 ]},
 {h:"IVA: the vote and its effect", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🗳️", title:"IVA step 4: the creditors decide", body:"The creditors decide by a **qualifying decision procedure** (usually **electronic voting**). Votes are counted by the **value** of each creditor's debt, not by heads."},
   {t:"card", i:"📏", title:"IVA step 5: the 75% test", body:"The proposal is approved if at least **75% in value** of the creditors **voting** vote in favour (Insolvency (England and Wales) Rules 2016 r.15.34)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧮", title:"IVA step 5: the unconnected creditors test", body:"**But** it is **not approved** if more than **50% in value** of the **unconnected creditors** voting vote **against** it. Unconnected creditors are those who are **not associates** of the debtor (for example, not their spouse or relatives)."},
   {t:"case", i:"🧾", title:"Example: the double test", facts:"Creditors owed £100,000 vote. £80,000 votes for (including the debtor's brother, owed £40,000). £20,000 votes against. The unconnected creditors voting are owed £60,000.", rule:"**75% test passed** (80%). But £20,000 of £60,000 unconnected is only 33% against, so **not more than 50%**: the IVA is **approved**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧑‍🏫", title:"IVA step 6: supervision", body:"Once approved, the nominee becomes the **supervisor**, collects the payments and distributes them to the creditors."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔗", title:"Effect: who is bound", body:"An approved IVA **binds every creditor who was entitled to vote**, whether they voted for, voted against, did not vote, or even had no notice of the vote (s.260)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏦", title:"Effect: secured and preferential creditors", body:"An IVA **cannot** affect a **secured creditor's** right to enforce its security, or the **priority** of **preferential debts**, **without that creditor's consent** (s.258(4)-(5)). So a mortgage lender can still repossess if mortgage payments are not kept up."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏳", title:"Challenging an IVA", body:"A creditor or the debtor can apply to court within **28 days** on the grounds of **unfair prejudice** or a **material irregularity** in the decision procedure (s.262)."},
   {t:"card", i:"💔", title:"If the debtor defaults", body:"If the debtor fails to comply with the IVA (or gave false information), the **supervisor** or any **creditor bound** by it can **petition for bankruptcy** (s.264(1)(c), s.276). An IVA also appears on the public **Individual Insolvency Register**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"An unsecured creditor voted against an IVA that got 80% approval. Is it bound?", body:"**Yes**, if the double test was met. An IVA binds every creditor entitled to vote, including those who voted against. It **never** binds secured or preferential creditors without their consent."}
 ]},
 {h:"Starting bankruptcy", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"📨", title:"Creditor's petition: the conditions", body:"One or more creditors can petition the **court** (IA 1986 ss.264-271) if, at the date of the petition (s.267):\n\n- the debt (or total debts of the petitioning creditors) is at least the **bankruptcy level**, currently **£5,000**;\n- the debt is for a **liquidated sum** (a fixed, known amount), payable now or at a definite future time;"},
   {t:"card", i:"📨", title:"Creditor's petition: more conditions", body:"- the debt is **unsecured** (a secured creditor can petition only for the unsecured part, or if it gives up its security);\n- the debtor **appears to be unable to pay** the debt (or to have no reasonable prospect of paying a future debt); and\n- there is no outstanding application to **set aside a statutory demand** for that debt."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏱️", title:"'Appears unable to pay': statutory demand", body:"Shown (s.268) by an unpaid **statutory demand**: a formal written demand in the prescribed form requiring payment. The debtor has **3 weeks (21 days)** to pay, secure or compound (settle) the debt, and the demand has not been set aside."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚖️", title:"'Appears unable to pay': unsatisfied execution", body:"The other way (s.268): **execution** of a judgment debt (enforcement action, such as enforcement agents trying to seize goods) has been returned **unsatisfied** in whole or in part."},
   {t:"card", i:"✂️", title:"Setting aside a statutory demand", body:"The debtor can apply to court to set the demand aside within **18 days** of service (Insolvency Rules 2016 r.10.4). Grounds include: the debt is **genuinely disputed** on substantial grounds, the debtor has a **cross-claim**, or the creditor holds **adequate security**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏛️", title:"The petition hearing", body:"The petition is heard by the **court**, which may make a **bankruptcy order**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧊", title:"Dispositions after the petition are void", body:"Any **disposition** of property (e.g. a sale, gift or payment) the debtor makes between the **petition being presented** (or the debtor's application being made) and the estate **vesting in the trustee** is **void** unless the **court consents** or later ratifies it (s.284). A buyer who acted in good faith, for value and **without notice** of the petition is protected."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A supplier is owed £4,800, unsecured. Can it petition for the debtor's bankruptcy alone?", body:"**No.** The debt must be at least **£5,000** (the bankruptcy level). Two creditors whose debts **together** reach £5,000 could petition jointly."},
   {t:"card", i:"💻", title:"Debtor's application", body:"The **debtor** can apply **online** to the **Adjudicator** for their own bankruptcy, with a fee (IA 1986 ss.263H-263O). There is **no minimum debt**, but the debtor must be **unable to pay their debts**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📆", title:"The Adjudicator's decision", body:"The Adjudicator must decide within **28 days**. If satisfied, they make the **bankruptcy order**; otherwise they refuse, and the debtor can appeal. **No court hearing** is involved."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Who decides a debtor's own bankruptcy application?", body:"The **Adjudicator**, online, within **28 days**, with no court hearing and no minimum debt. A creditor's petition goes to the **court**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Who takes over, and what they take", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"📅", title:"When bankruptcy starts", body:"Bankruptcy **starts on the day the bankruptcy order is made** (s.278)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔍", title:"The Official Receiver steps in", body:"The **Official Receiver** first takes control of the bankrupt's estate and investigates their affairs. The bankrupt must **cooperate**, deliver up property and records, and provide a **statement of affairs** (a list of assets and debts) if the OR asks for one (s.288)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎒", title:"The trustee in bankruptcy", body:"The **trustee in bankruptcy** **collects, realises (sells) and distributes** the estate. The **OR becomes the first trustee automatically** when the order is made (s.291A), and an IP can later be appointed in its place (s.292 onwards)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📣", title:"Publicity", body:"The bankruptcy is advertised in the **Gazette** (the official public record) and entered on the **Individual Insolvency Register**. A **bankruptcy restriction** is registered against any land the bankrupt owns."},
   {t:"card", i:"📦", title:"The estate vests automatically", body:"The bankrupt's **estate** (all property belonging to them at the start of the bankruptcy) **vests automatically** (passes in law) to the trustee when the trustee's appointment takes effect, or when the OR becomes trustee (s.306), without any transfer."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧰", title:"Excluded: tools of the trade", body:"Excluded from the estate (s.283(2)): such tools, books, vehicles and other items of equipment as are **necessary for the bankrupt personally** for use in their **employment, business or vocation**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛏️", title:"Excluded: basic domestic items", body:"Also excluded (s.283(2)): such clothing, bedding, furniture, household equipment and provisions as are necessary for satisfying the **basic domestic needs** of the bankrupt and their family."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔄", title:"Swap for a cheaper replacement", body:"If an excluded item is **worth more than a reasonable replacement**, the trustee can claim it and provide a **cheaper replacement** (s.308)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗑️", title:"Disclaiming onerous property", body:"The trustee can, by notice, **disclaim** (give up) **onerous property**: an **unprofitable contract**, or property that is **unsaleable** or likely to create a liability, such as a lease with high rent or contaminated land (s.315). Anyone who loses out, such as the landlord, can **prove as an unsecured creditor** for their loss."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗝️", title:"Also outside the estate", body:"Property held on **trust** for others, and most **pension** rights (in an approved pension scheme)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A bankrupt plumber's ordinary van, needed for work. Does it vest in the trustee?", body:"**No**, it is a **tool of the trade**. But if it is worth more than a reasonable replacement, the trustee can take it and provide a **cheaper van** (s.308)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎟️", title:"After-acquired property", body:"Property the bankrupt acquires **after** the order but **before discharge** (e.g. an **inheritance** or **lottery win**) can be **claimed** by the trustee by written notice (s.307), within **42 days** of the trustee first learning of it. The bankrupt must tell the trustee within **21 days** of acquiring it."},
   {t:"card", i:"💷", title:"Income: IPO or IPA", body:"The bankrupt can be made to pay their **surplus income** (income above what is reasonably needed for the domestic needs of the bankrupt and family) to the trustee, by an **income payments order (IPO)** made by the court (s.310) or an **income payments agreement (IPA)** agreed with the trustee or OR (s.310A)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗓️", title:"How long an IPO or IPA lasts", body:"It can last **up to 3 years** from the date of the order or agreement, so it can **continue after discharge**. But it must be made (or agreed) **before discharge**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A bankrupt inherits £20,000 six months after the order. Can the trustee take it?", body:"**Yes.** It is **after-acquired property** (acquired before discharge). The trustee claims it by written notice within **42 days** of learning of it."}
 ]},
 {h:"The family home", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🏠", title:"The home vests in the trustee", body:"The bankrupt's **beneficial interest** (their ownership share) in their home vests in the trustee. If they own it jointly with a spouse or partner, that is often a **half share**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🪧", title:"Order for sale", body:"The trustee can apply to court for an **order for sale** (Trusts of Land and Appointment of Trustees Act 1996 s.14). The court then applies **IA 1986 s.335A**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚖️", title:"In the first year", body:"In the **first year** after the estate vests, the court **balances**: the interests of the **creditors**, the conduct of the spouse or civil partner (so far as it contributed to the bankruptcy), their needs and resources, and the **needs of any children**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏰", title:"After one year", body:"**After one year**, the court must **assume** that the **interests of the creditors outweigh all other considerations**, **unless the circumstances are exceptional** (e.g. serious illness of the bankrupt's spouse or a child). Mere hardship, such as having to move or change school, is **not exceptional**."},
   {t:"case", i:"👩‍👦‍👦", title:"Example: Sara's home", facts:"Sara is made bankrupt. She owns her home jointly with her husband and two teenage children live there. Eighteen months later the trustee applies for an order for sale.", rule:"**The court will almost certainly order sale**: more than one year has passed, so the creditors' interests are assumed to outweigh everything else, and the children's schooling disruption is **not exceptional**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛋️", title:"Rights of occupation", body:"A spouse or civil partner has **rights of occupation** that bind the trustee until an order is made (s.336). A bankrupt with **children under 18** living with them also has rights of occupation (s.337)."},
   {t:"card", i:"♻️", title:"'Use it or lose it' (3 years)", body:"If the trustee has not realised or dealt with the bankrupt's interest in the home within **3 years** of the bankruptcy, it **re-vests in the bankrupt** automatically (s.283A)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🪙", title:"Low-value homes: under £1,000", body:"The court must **refuse** an order for sale (or possession) if the value of the bankrupt's interest is **below £1,000** (s.313A)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Two years after vesting, the family says the children would have to change school. Exceptional?", body:"**No.** After one year the creditors' interests are assumed to outweigh all others unless the circumstances are **exceptional**, such as serious illness. Changing school is mere hardship."}
 ]},
 {h:"Restrictions and discharge", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"💳", title:"Offence: obtaining credit", body:"It is a **criminal offence** for an **undischarged** bankrupt (one not yet discharged) to **obtain credit** of more than **£500** (alone or jointly) without disclosing that they are bankrupt (s.360)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏷️", title:"Offence: trading under another name", body:"It is also an offence to **trade** under a name other than the one in which they were made bankrupt without disclosing that name to the people they deal with (s.360)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏢", title:"Offence: acting as a director", body:"It is an offence to act as a **company director**, or take part in the promotion, formation or management of a company, **without the court's permission** (Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986 s.11)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎓", title:"Professional consequences", body:"A bankrupt cannot act as an **insolvency practitioner**, and many professional bodies suspend members: a solicitor's **practising certificate** is **automatically suspended**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🤝", title:"Partnerships and LLPs", body:"A **general partnership** is **dissolved** by the bankruptcy of a partner unless the partnership agreement says otherwise (Partnership Act 1890 s.33), and the bankrupt's share vests in the trustee. A bankrupt member of an **LLP** may have to leave under the members' agreement."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏦", title:"Secured vs unsecured creditors", body:"**Secured creditors** are not affected by the bankruptcy and can still **enforce their security** (e.g. a mortgage lender repossessing the home). **Unsecured creditors** cannot take or continue legal action for bankruptcy debts without court permission (s.285); they must **prove** (make a claim) in the bankruptcy."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"An undischarged bankrupt borrows £700 without saying they're bankrupt. Any problem?", body:"**Yes, a criminal offence** (s.360): obtaining credit of more than **£500** without disclosing the bankruptcy."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎉", title:"Automatic discharge after one year", body:"**Automatic discharge** happens **one year** after the bankruptcy order (s.279(1)). The court can **suspend** the period if the bankrupt fails to cooperate with the OR or trustee."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧹", title:"Effect of discharge", body:"The bankrupt is **released from the bankruptcy debts** (s.281), **except**:\n\n- debts incurred through **fraud**;\n- **fines**;\n- **damages for personal injury** (negligence, nuisance or breach of duty) unless the court orders otherwise;\n- obligations under **family proceedings** (e.g. child maintenance);\n- **student loans** and certain social fund loans."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎒", title:"Discharge does not give the property back", body:"Assets already vested in the trustee **stay** with the trustee, who can carry on selling them, including the **home** within the **3-year** window. An **IPO/IPA** continues for its term."},
   {t:"card", i:"↩️", title:"Annulment: cancelling the order", body:"Discharge is different from **annulment**. The court can **annul** (cancel) a bankruptcy order if it **ought not to have been made**, or if all the **bankruptcy debts and expenses have been paid** or secured (s.282). Annulment treats the bankruptcy as if it had not happened, and can happen **before or after** discharge."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚧", title:"BROs and BRUs", body:"Where the bankrupt has been **dishonest or blameworthy** (e.g. gambling, hiding assets, running up debts with no reasonable expectation of paying, preferring associates), the restrictions are extended for **2 to 15 years** (Sch.4A), by a **bankruptcy restrictions order (BRO)** made by the court or a **bankruptcy restrictions undertaking (BRU)** given to the Secretary of State. This does **not** delay the release from debts."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"After discharge, which of these is still owed: a credit card debt, a court fine, a supplier's invoice?", body:"The **court fine**. Fines survive discharge, as do fraud debts, personal injury damages, family orders and student loans. Ordinary unsecured debts are released."}
 ]},
 {h:"Clawback: undoing earlier transactions", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🎁", title:"Transaction at an undervalue (s.339)", body:"The bankrupt made a **gift**, a transaction for **no consideration** (nothing in return) or in consideration of **marriage or civil partnership**, or for consideration **significantly less** in value than what they gave."},
   {t:"card", i:"📅", title:"Undervalue: the time limit", body:"Within **5 years** before the **day the bankruptcy petition was presented** (or the debtor's application was made) (s.341)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔀", title:"Undervalue: was the bankrupt insolvent?", body:"Within **2 years** before the petition: solvency at the time is **irrelevant**. Between **2 and 5 years**: the bankrupt must have been **insolvent at the time** or become insolvent as a result. Insolvency is **presumed** if the other party is an **associate** (e.g. spouse or civil partner, relatives, business partners) (s.341(2))."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚫", title:"Undervalue: no business defence", body:"There is **no good-faith business defence** (unlike the corporate version, s.238(5)). The court can order the property to be returned, or its value paid, to the trustee (s.342)."},
   {t:"case", i:"🏡", title:"Example: Tom's gift to his wife", facts:"Three years before his bankruptcy petition, when he could not pay his debts, Tom transferred his half share of the family home (worth £150,000) to his wife for nothing.", rule:"**A transaction at an undervalue** within 5 years. His wife is an associate, so his insolvency at the time is presumed. The court can order her to transfer the share back to the trustee."},
   {t:"card", i:"🥇", title:"Preference (s.340)", body:"The bankrupt did something that put a **creditor** or **guarantor** in a **better position** than they would otherwise have had in the bankruptcy, and was **influenced by a desire** to do so. They must have been **insolvent** at the time or become so as a result."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏲️", title:"Preference: time limits and presumption", body:"Within **6 months** before the petition, or **2 years** if the person preferred is an **associate**. **Desire is presumed** where the preferred person is an associate (other than only as an employee)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🕳️", title:"Transaction defrauding creditors (s.423)", body:"A transaction at an undervalue made for the **purpose** of putting assets beyond the reach of creditors (or potential claimants). **No time limit**, and the person need not be bankrupt."},
   {t:"card", i:"👴", title:"Excessive pension contributions (s.342A)", body:"In outline: the trustee can recover pension contributions that **unfairly prejudice** creditors."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Four years before his petition, a man gives his brother £10,000. Can the trustee claw it back?", body:"**Only if he was insolvent** at the time or became insolvent as a result, because it was between 2 and 5 years before the petition. A brother is an **associate**, so insolvency is **presumed**: the gift can be clawed back unless he proves he was solvent."}
 ]},
 {h:"Who gets paid, and IVA or bankruptcy?", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"1️⃣", title:"Payment order: 1 and 2", body:"**1. Secured creditors**: paid from the proceeds of their security (outside the bankruptcy). Any shortfall ranks as an unsecured debt.\n\n**2. Expenses** of the bankruptcy: the costs of the OR and trustee, including their fees."},
   {t:"card", i:"3️⃣", title:"Payment order: 3 and 4 (preferential)", body:"**3. Ordinary preferential debts**: e.g. the bankrupt's **employees'** unpaid wages (4 months, up to **£800** each) and accrued **holiday pay**.\n\n**4. Secondary preferential debts**: **HMRC** for taxes collected on behalf of others: **VAT**, **PAYE** deductions and **employees' NICs** (where the bankrupt was a sole trader with staff)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧾", title:"Not preferential: the bankrupt's own tax", body:"The bankrupt's **own income tax and Class 4 NICs** are **not** preferential. They rank as ordinary unsecured debts."},
   {t:"card", i:"5️⃣", title:"Payment order: 5 to 8", body:"**5. Ordinary unsecured creditors**, paid **pari passu** (proportionately).\n\n**6. Interest** on proved debts from the date of the bankruptcy order (if there is a surplus).\n\n**7. Postponed debts**: credit given by the bankrupt's **spouse or civil partner** at the start of the bankruptcy (s.329).\n\n**8. The bankrupt**: any **surplus** goes back to them."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚫", title:"No prescribed part", body:"There is **no prescribed part** in bankruptcy, because individuals do not grant **floating charges**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"The bankrupt's wife lent him £5,000. Where does she rank?", body:"**Postponed** (s.329): after the ordinary unsecured creditors and after interest. Only the bankrupt (any surplus) comes after her."},
   {t:"compare", i:"⚖️", title:"IVA or bankruptcy: start and control", cols:["", "IVA", "Bankruptcy"], rows:[["Started by","Debtor's proposal; creditors vote","Creditor's petition to court (debt at least **£5,000**) or debtor's online application to the **Adjudicator**"],["Who controls assets","The debtor (subject to the IVA terms)","**Trustee in bankruptcy** (estate vests automatically)"],["Binds","Unsecured creditors entitled to vote; **not** secured or preferential without consent","All unsecured creditors; secured creditors keep their security"]]},
   {t:"compare", i:"⚖️", title:"IVA or bankruptcy: life afterwards", cols:["", "IVA", "Bankruptcy"], rows:[["Restrictions","Few (on register; terms of IVA)","Credit **> £500** disclosure, cannot be a **director**, trading-name disclosure, professional consequences"],["Typical length","Often **5 years**","**Discharge after 1 year** (IPO up to 3 years; home within 3 years)"],["Business","Can usually carry on trading","Business assets (beyond tools of trade) go to trustee; partnership dissolved by default"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"🚨", title:"Watch out", body:"**Tools of the trade** and **basic household items** are excluded from the estate, but the trustee can still take them if they are worth more than a reasonable replacement. And a **secured creditor** (e.g. the mortgage lender) is **unaffected** by both an IVA and a bankruptcy."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗺️", title:"Your 7-step route", body:"**1** IVA possible? → **2** How does bankruptcy start? → **3** What does the trustee take? → **4** The family home → **5** Restrictions and discharge → **6** Clawback → **7** Order of payment. The full route is this topic's **framework**."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Source: proposals/rooms/learn-blp-shareholders-minority.js */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["blp/shareholders-minority"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"👥", title:"Shareholders own the company", body:"**Shareholders** (also called **members**) own the company. Their main powers are to **vote** at general meetings (or on written resolutions), to receive **dividends** when declared, and to share any surplus on a **solvent winding up** (when a company that can pay its debts is closed down)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗳️", title:"Majority rule", body:"Company decisions are taken by **majority**. A shareholder who joins a company accepts that the majority will decide, and the courts will not interfere with honest decisions of the majority or of the board (the directors)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏢", title:"The proper claimant rule", body:"If a wrong is done **to the company**, the **company itself** is the proper claimant, not an individual shareholder (**Foss v Harbottle** 1843)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A director harms the company. Who is normally the right person to sue?", body:"**The company itself** (the proper claimant rule). A shareholder can only step in through a **derivative claim**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🫥", title:"The problem: the minority is exposed", body:"A **minority shareholder** (one who holds too few shares to control votes) can be outvoted on almost everything. The law protects them in three ways."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛡️", title:"Three kinds of protection", body:"- **Voting thresholds**: more than 25% blocks a special resolution\n- **Contract**: the articles and a **shareholders' agreement**\n- **Statute**: statutory rights, the **derivative claim**, the **unfair prejudice** petition and **just and equitable winding up**"},
   {t:"card", i:"🔁", title:"Also in this topic", body:"How shares are **transferred** (sold or given away) and **transmitted** (passed on automatically on death or bankruptcy)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Becoming a member", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"✍️", title:"Subscribers become members on day one", body:"The **subscribers** (the people who sign the memorandum when the company is formed) become members on **incorporation**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📖", title:"Everyone else: the register decides", body:"Anyone else becomes a member when they **agree to become one** and their name is **entered in the register of members** (CA 2006 s.112). Entry on the register is what counts, not just paying for shares or holding a share certificate."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A buyer has paid for shares but isn't on the register yet. Are they a member?", body:"**No.** They become a member only when their name is **entered in the register of members** (s.112)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗂️", title:"The register of members (s.113)", body:"The company must keep a register showing members' **names, addresses**, the **date registered**, the **date they ceased** to be a member, and the **shares held**. Since **26 January 2026** every company must keep its own register (the option to hold it at Companies House has gone)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📜", title:"Share certificates", body:"Must be issued within **2 months** of allotment (issuing new shares, s.769) or of a transfer being lodged (s.776). A certificate is **prima facie evidence** of title (s.768): it is presumed correct unless shown otherwise."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔎", title:"PSC: transparency, not rights", body:"A **person with significant control (PSC)** (e.g. holding more than 25% of shares or votes) is registered at Companies House. This is a **transparency** rule. It gives no extra shareholder rights."}
 ]},
 {h:"Transfer and transmission of shares", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🧾", title:"Transfer: the paperwork", body:"A **transfer** is a sale or gift. The seller signs a **stock transfer form** and gives it, with the **share certificate**, to the buyer, who sends them to the company for registration."},
   {t:"card", i:"💷", title:"Stamp duty", body:"**0.5%** of the price, paid by the **buyer**, rounded up to the nearest **£5**, on a transfer for **more than £1,000**. **Gifts** are not stamped. The company must not register an unstamped transfer that is liable to duty."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Shares are sold for £10,000. Who pays stamp duty, and how much?", body:"The **buyer** pays **0.5%**: **£50**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏳", title:"Before registration", body:"The buyer becomes a member only when **entered in the register** (s.112). Until then the **seller** stays the legal owner and holds the shares **on trust** for the buyer (if the buyer has paid)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📬", title:"The company's 2-month deadline (s.771)", body:"Within **2 months** of the transfer being lodged, the company must either **register** it or send the buyer **notice of refusal with reasons**, and give more information about the reasons if asked."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚪", title:"Directors may refuse (Model Articles art.26(5))", body:"The **directors may refuse** to register a transfer. It is an unrestricted discretion, but it must be used in **good faith** and for a **proper purpose**. The articles often add **pre-emption rights** on transfer (shares must first be offered to the other members)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Buyer has paid and holds a signed transfer form. Can they vote yet?", body:"**No.** They are not a member until the company **registers** the transfer. The directors can refuse under art.26(5), but must give reasons within 2 months."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚰️", title:"Transmission: by operation of law", body:"On a shareholder's **death**, their shares pass automatically to their **personal representatives** (PRs: the executors or administrators of the estate). On **bankruptcy**, they vest in the **trustee in bankruptcy**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔀", title:"The transmittee's choice (arts 27-29)", body:"A person entitled by transmission is a **transmittee**. With evidence of entitlement, they may either **become the registered holder** or **transfer** the shares to someone else (e.g. a beneficiary), subject to the directors' power to refuse registration."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔇", title:"Transmittee: dividends yes, votes no", body:"Until registered, a transmittee has the same rights as the holder (e.g. to **dividends**) but **cannot attend or vote** at general meetings or agree to written resolutions (art.27(3))."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A shareholder dies. Can the executor vote the shares at next week's meeting?", body:"**No**, not until registered as holder. Until then the executor can receive **dividends** but **cannot attend or vote** (art.27(3))."}
 ]},
 {h:"Every shareholder's rights", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🍬", title:"Even one share counts", body:"**Any shareholder**, regardless of the size of their holding, has a set of statutory rights."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎤", title:"Meetings and proxies", body:"Receive notice of, attend, speak and vote at **general meetings** (if their shares carry votes), and appoint a **proxy** (someone to attend and vote for them) (s.324)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📊", title:"Accounts", body:"Receive a copy of the **annual accounts and reports** (s.423)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔍", title:"Inspection", body:"Inspect, free of charge, the **register of members** (s.116; within 5 working days the company must comply or apply to court under **s.117**, which can stop access if the request is not for a **proper purpose**), the **minutes of general meetings** and written resolutions (s.358), and directors' **service contracts** (s.229)."},
   {t:"card", i:"💰", title:"Declared dividends", body:"Receive any **dividend** once **declared**: it then becomes a **debt** the company owes them."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚖️", title:"Court remedies and s.33", body:"Bring a **derivative claim**, an **unfair prejudice petition** (s.994) or a **just and equitable winding up** petition. Enforce the articles under **s.33** in their capacity as a member (see **Forming a company**)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Can a shareholder with just one share bring an unfair prejudice petition?", body:"**Yes.** Any member can petition under s.994, and can also bring a derivative claim or a just and equitable winding up petition."}
 ]},
 {h:"Key shareholding thresholds", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"5️⃣", title:"5% of voting rights", body:"- **Require** the directors to call a general meeting (s.303)\n- Require circulation of a **written resolution** (s.292)\n- Require circulation of a **members' statement** about a proposed resolution or business (s.314)"},
   {t:"card", i:"🔟", title:"10%", body:"- Demand a **poll** (a vote counted by shares, not by show of hands) (s.321 and Model Articles art.44; under art.44 any **two or more** voting members can also demand one)\n- Require an **audit** of a small company's accounts (s.476)"},
   {t:"card", i:"🚧", title:"More than 25%: the blocking stake", body:"Enough to **block a special resolution** (e.g. changing the articles), because the others can no longer reach 75%."},
   {t:"card", i:"👑", title:"More than 50%: control of the board", body:"Pass **ordinary resolutions**, including appointing and **removing directors** (s.168). So a majority controls the board."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏆", title:"75% or more: special resolutions", body:"Pass **special resolutions**: change the **articles** and **name**, **disapply pre-emption rights**, **wind up voluntarily**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏩", title:"90%: short notice and squeeze-out", body:"- **90%** (plus a **majority in number** of members): consent to **short notice** of a general meeting in a private company\n- **90% of a class** after a takeover offer: **squeeze out** the remaining minority (s.979; outline only)"},
   {t:"compare", i:"📏", title:"The ladder at a glance", cols:["Holding", "What it lets the shareholder do"], rows:[["Any share","Attend and vote; accounts; inspect; s.994; derivative claim; winding up petition"],["5%","Require a GM, written resolution, members' statement"],["10%","Demand a poll; small company audit"],[">25%","Block a special resolution"],[">50%","Ordinary resolutions; remove directors"],["75%+","Special resolutions"],["90%","Short notice (private company); squeeze-out after takeover"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"💡", title:"Exam tip: 25% exactly does not block", body:"SBA questions often give a shareholding (e.g. **26%** or **24%**). **More than 25%** blocks a special resolution. Exactly **25% does not** block, because the other **75%** still passes it."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A shareholder holds exactly 25%. Can they stop the articles being changed?", body:"**No.** The other 75% can still pass the special resolution. They would need **more than 25%**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A shareholder holds 6% of the votes. Can they force a general meeting?", body:"**Yes.** **5%** of voting rights can require the directors to call one (s.303)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Protection by contract: the articles", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🏋️", title:"Weighted voting rights", body:"The articles can give extra votes in certain situations, e.g. a **Bushell v Faith** clause giving a director-shareholder extra votes on a resolution to **remove them** (upheld in 1970; see **Directors**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎖️", title:"Class rights", body:"A **class** of shares can carry special rights, e.g. the right to **appoint a director** or to **veto** certain decisions."},
   {t:"card", i:"✅", title:"Varying class rights (s.630)", body:"Needs the **consent of the class**: **written consent** of holders of at least **75%** of the issued shares of that class, or a **special resolution** at a separate **class meeting**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏱️", title:"Objecting to a variation (s.633)", body:"Holders of **15%** of the class who did not consent can apply to court to **cancel** the variation within **21 days**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"How can a company change rights attached to a class of shares?", body:"With the **class's consent**: written consent of **75%** of that class, or a **special resolution** at a class meeting (s.630). **15%** who didn't consent can apply to court within **21 days** (s.633)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔐", title:"Pre-emption on transfer", body:"A member who wants to sell must **first offer the shares to the other members** (often at a fair value fixed by an accountant). This stops outsiders coming in."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧱", title:"Entrenched provisions (s.22)", body:"Articles that can be changed only on **stricter conditions** than a special resolution (e.g. **unanimous** consent)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🪑", title:"Quorum provisions", body:"The **quorum** (minimum number needed for a valid meeting) can require a **particular shareholder** to be present, so no meeting can go ahead without them."}
 ]},
 {h:"Protection by contract: the shareholders' agreement", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🤝", title:"What it is", body:"A private **contract** between some or all shareholders (and sometimes the company) about how the company will be run."},
   {t:"card", i:"📋", title:"Common terms", body:"Each party's right to **appoint a director**; **vetoes** over key decisions (issuing shares, borrowing, selling the business, changing the articles); agreement to **vote** a particular way; dividend policy; non-compete covenants; deadlock and exit provisions."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧲", title:"Drag-along and tag-along", body:"**Drag-along**: a majority selling their shares can **force the minority to sell** to the same buyer on the same terms. **Tag-along**: the minority can **join in** a sale by the majority on the same terms."},
   {t:"card", i:"👍", title:"Advantages over the articles", body:"- **Confidential**: not filed at Companies House\n- Changed only with the consent of **all parties** (articles need only 75%)\n- Gives **personal rights** enforceable as a contract"},
   {t:"card", i:"👎", title:"Disadvantages", body:"It binds only the **parties**, not future shareholders unless they sign a **deed of adherence** (a deed agreeing to be bound). And the company **cannot fetter** (restrict) its **statutory powers**."},
   {t:"case", i:"🏦", title:"Russell v Northern Bank Development Corp (1992)", facts:"The shareholders and the company signed an agreement that no further share capital would be created without every party's written consent. The others proposed to increase capital.", rule:"The promise was **void against the company** (it can't contract out of its statutory power to alter its capital), but **valid as a personal agreement between the shareholders** about how they would vote."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A shareholders' agreement says the company won't issue shares without everyone's consent. Who is bound?", body:"The **shareholders** are bound personally (how they vote). The **company** cannot be bound to give up its statutory power."},
   {t:"card", i:"🥧", title:"Statutory pre-emption on allotment (s.561)", body:"New ordinary shares issued **for cash** must first be offered to existing ordinary shareholders **in proportion** to their holdings, so their percentage isn't **diluted** (shrunk) without their agreement. Details and disapplication: **Equity finance**."}
 ]},
 {h:"The derivative claim", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🔄", title:"What it is (CA 2006 ss.260-264)", body:"A claim brought by a **member on behalf of the company**, for a wrong done **to the company**. It is an exception to the proper claimant rule, needed because the wrongdoing directors usually **control the board** and would never sue themselves."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎯", title:"What it can be for (s.260(3))", body:"Only an actual or proposed act or omission involving **negligence, default, breach of duty or breach of trust** by a **director** (including former and shadow directors). It can be against the director or **another person** (e.g. someone who knowingly received company property)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🕰️", title:"Late joiners can sue (s.260(4))", body:"It doesn't matter that the member joined **after** the wrong happened."},
   {t:"card", i:"1️⃣", title:"Permission stage 1: prima facie case", body:"The court's **permission** is needed. First the claimant must show a **prima facie case**: on their own evidence the case appears to have merit. If not, the court **dismisses** it (the company need not take part yet)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛑", title:"Stage 2: when the court MUST refuse (s.263(2))", body:"- A person acting under **s.172** (the duty to promote the success of the company) **would not continue** the claim; or\n- the act is **yet to occur** and has been **authorised** by the company; or\n- it has **already occurred** and was **authorised** beforehand or **ratified** (approved afterwards) since."},
   {t:"card", i:"🤔", title:"Stage 2: otherwise, the court weighs (s.263(3)-(4))", body:"- Is the member acting in **good faith**?\n- How **important** would a s.172 director think it is?\n- Is the act likely to be **authorised or ratified**?\n- Has the company **decided not to sue**?\n- Could the member sue **in their own right** (e.g. s.994)?\n- Views of members with **no personal interest**"},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"The breach has been validly ratified by the shareholders. What must the court do?", body:"**Refuse permission** (s.263(2)). For ratification to be valid, the wrongdoer's votes are **disregarded** (s.239)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏢", title:"Remedy: it goes to the company", body:"Any damages or property recovered go **to the company**, not to the claimant. The court can order the company to **indemnify** (pay) the claimant's **costs**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A shareholder wins a derivative claim for £100,000. Who gets the money?", body:"**The company.** A derivative claim is brought on the company's behalf."},
   {t:"card", i:"🙋", title:"Personal claims are different", body:"A shareholder can sue **personally** for a wrong done to **them** (e.g. breach of a shareholders' agreement, or of their membership rights under s.33)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🪞", title:"The reflective loss rule", body:"A shareholder **cannot** recover personally for a fall in share value or lost dividends that merely **reflects the company's loss**, where the company has its own claim against the same wrongdoer (**Prudential v Newman** 1982; confirmed and limited to shareholders in **Sevilleja v Marex** 2020). The remedy is for the company to sue, or a derivative claim."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A director's fraud halves the company's value. Can a shareholder sue him for the fall in their shares?", body:"**No.** That loss **reflects the company's loss**. The company sues (or a member brings a **derivative claim**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"💡", title:"Exam tip: the two commonest answers", body:"(1) Permission **must be refused** because the breach was **validly ratified** (wrongdoer's votes disregarded, s.239) or a s.172 director wouldn't pursue it. (2) Money recovered goes **to the company**. A wrong option often says damages go to the claimant shareholder."}
 ]},
 {h:"Unfair prejudice petition", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"📣", title:"The test (CA 2006 s.994)", body:"**Any member** may petition the court because the company's affairs **are being or have been conducted** in a way **unfairly prejudicial** to the interests of members generally or some of them (including the petitioner), or because an actual or proposed act or omission would be."},
   {t:"card", i:"💔", title:"Element 1: prejudice", body:"**Harm** to the petitioner's interests **as a member**, usually financial (e.g. reduced share value). In small companies, 'interests' is read **widely**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚖️", title:"Element 2: unfairness", body:"Judged **objectively**, against the **agreed terms** on which members joined (articles, shareholders' agreement) and, in a quasi-partnership, against their **legitimate expectations** based on **equitable considerations** (fairness and good faith)."},
   {t:"case", i:"🧱", title:"O'Neill v Phillips (1999)", facts:"Mr O'Neill, a worker, was given 25% of the shares and made a director. Increasing his stake to 50% was discussed but never agreed. When business declined, Mr Phillips (the majority) took back control and stopped the profit-sharing.", rule:"**No unfair prejudice.** Mr Phillips broke no agreement or promise, and a member has **no right to exit at will** just because trust has broken down."},
   {t:"card", i:"📌", title:"So what counts as unfair?", body:"Usually a **breach of the agreed terms** (articles, agreement), or using the rules in a way **equity would regard as contrary to good faith** (e.g. breaking an informal understanding on which a quasi-partnership was based)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Trust has broken down but no promise or term was broken. Is that unfair prejudice?", body:"**Usually no.** There's **no right to exit at will**; unfairness normally needs a breach of agreed terms or of an understanding equity will enforce."},
   {t:"card", i:"👨‍👩‍👧", title:"Quasi-partnership", body:"A small private company formed or continued on a **personal relationship of mutual trust**, an understanding that all (or some) members will **take part in management**, and **restrictions on transferring shares** (so a member can't simply sell up). **Exclusion from management** in such a company is a classic ground."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚫", title:"Typical examples (1)", body:"- **Excluding** a quasi-partner from management (e.g. removing them as director)\n- **Diverting** business or assets to a company owned by the majority\n- Paying **excessive directors' pay** to the majority while paying **no dividends**"},
   {t:"card", i:"🫗", title:"Typical examples (2)", body:"- Issuing shares to **dilute** the petitioner (e.g. a rights issue they can't afford, or ignoring pre-emption rights)\n- Serious **mismanagement**, or failing to give information or hold meetings\n- Breach of directors' duties benefiting the majority (overlaps with derivative claims; the court may allow a s.994 petition to seek relief for the company)"},
   {t:"card", i:"🔨", title:"Remedies (s.996)", body:"The court may make **any order it thinks fit**: regulate future affairs; order the company to do or stop something; authorise **civil proceedings** in the company's name; stop the articles being changed without court permission."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛒", title:"The usual remedy: a buy-out", body:"Most commonly, an order that the **other members or the company buy the petitioner's shares at a fair value**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧮", title:"Valuation: no minority discount", body:"In a quasi-partnership, shares are usually valued **pro rata** (a straight proportion of the company's value) **without a discount** for being a minority stake (**Re Bird Precision Bellows** 1986). The valuation date is up to the court (often the order date, or earlier if the majority's conduct reduced the value)."},
   {t:"case", i:"🍭", title:"Example: Kate, Liam and Mo", facts:"Each owns one third of KLM Ltd, an equal family venture in which all three work. Liam and Mo remove Kate as a director and pay themselves large bonuses but no dividends.", rule:"**Kate should petition under s.994**: exclusion from management of a quasi-partnership and diverting profits by excessive pay are classic unfair prejudice. Likely remedy: Liam and Mo **buy her shares at a fair, undiscounted value**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Kate wins her s.994 petition. Who gets the benefit?", body:"**Kate personally**, usually by a buy-out of her shares at fair value. (Contrast a derivative claim, where the company benefits.)"}
 ]},
 {h:"Just and equitable winding up", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🏚️", title:"What it is (IA 1986 s.122(1)(g))", body:"A member (here called a **contributory**) can petition the court to **wind up** (close down) the company because it is **just and equitable** to do so."},
   {t:"card", i:"💎", title:"Tangible interest: the company must be solvent", body:"The petitioner must show a **tangible interest**: in practice the company must be **solvent**, so there will be a **surplus** for members after creditors are paid."},
   {t:"card", i:"📋", title:"Recognised grounds", body:"- The company's **main object (substratum) has failed** or been achieved\n- **Deadlock** in management (e.g. two equal shareholders who can't agree, with no casting vote)\n- **Breakdown of trust** in a **quasi-partnership**, including **exclusion from management**\n- Fraud or lack of probity (honesty) by those in control"},
   {t:"case", i:"🖼️", title:"Ebrahimi v Westbourne Galleries (1973)", facts:"Mr Ebrahimi and Mr Nazar ran a business as equal partners, then incorporated, each a director with equal shares. Mr Nazar's son joined and took some shares. Nazar and son used their majority to **remove Mr Ebrahimi as a director** (validly), excluding him from management and profits (paid out as directors' pay).", rule:"**Wound up.** It was a **quasi-partnership** built on trust and the understanding he would manage, so the majority's strict legal rights were subject to **equitable considerations**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧯", title:"Last resort (IA 1986 s.125(2))", body:"If **another remedy is available** (e.g. a s.994 petition, or a **reasonable offer to buy their shares at fair value**) and the petitioner is **acting unreasonably** in seeking winding up instead, the court may **refuse** the order."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"The majority offers a fair buy-out; the petitioner refuses and asks for winding up. Likely result?", body:"The court may **refuse** the winding up order: the petitioner is **unreasonably** ignoring another remedy (s.125(2))."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏗️", title:"Why a buy-out is usually preferred", body:"Winding up is **drastic** (the business ends), so a **s.994 petition for a buy-out** is usually preferred."}
 ]},
 {h:"Choosing the right remedy", cards:[
   {t:"compare", i:"🧭", title:"Which route, and who benefits?", cols:["Situation", "Best route", "Who benefits"], rows:[["Director **wronged the company** (e.g. diverted an opportunity, negligence); board won't sue","**Derivative claim** (court permission)","🏢 The company"],["Shareholder personally treated **unfairly** (excluded, diluted, starved of dividends) and wants **out**","**s.994 petition** for a buy-out at fair value","🙋 The petitioner"],["Total breakdown or **deadlock**; solvent company; no other workable remedy","**Just and equitable winding up** (IA s.122(1)(g))","👥 All members share the surplus"],["Shareholder's **own contractual rights** broken","Personal claim under **s.33** or the **shareholders' agreement**","🙋 The shareholder"]]},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Two 50:50 shareholders can't agree on anything and there's no casting vote. Which remedy fits?", body:"**Just and equitable winding up** for **deadlock** (IA 1986 s.122(1)(g)), if the company is **solvent**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A director diverted a contract to his own firm and the board won't act. Which route?", body:"A **derivative claim** on the company's behalf (with court permission). Any recovery goes **to the company**."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Source: proposals/rooms/learn-blp-vat-iht-bpr.js */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["blp/vat-iht-bpr"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🎁", title:"Two separate taxes in one topic", body:"This topic covers two **separate** taxes that affect businesses: **value added tax (VAT)** and **inheritance tax (IHT) business property relief (BPR)**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛒", title:"VAT: a tax on spending", body:"**VAT** is a tax on **spending**. Businesses collect it at each stage of the supply chain, but the **final consumer** bears it. It is governed by the **Value Added Tax Act 1994 (VATA 1994)**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📋", title:"What the SQE1 tests on VAT", body:"The **scope** of VAT, what counts as a **supply**, **input and output tax**, **registration**, **invoices**, and **returns, payment and record keeping**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎀", title:"BPR: a relief on gifts and death", body:"**Business property relief (BPR)** **reduces the value of business assets** for inheritance tax when they are given away during life or pass on **death**. It is in the **Inheritance Tax Act 1984 (IHTA 1984) ss.103-114**, as reformed from **6 April 2026**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧭", title:"BLP tests only BPR", body:"IHT generally is in the **Wills** syllabus. Business Law and Practice tests only **BPR**, plus the IHT basics you need to apply it."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Who finally bears the cost of VAT?", body:"The **final consumer**. Businesses collect it along the chain, but a consumer cannot recover it."}
 ]},
 {h:"When is VAT charged?", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🧩", title:"The s.4 test: all elements needed", body:"VAT is charged on a **supply of goods or services** made **in the UK**, where it is a **taxable supply** made by a **taxable person** **in the course or furtherance of any business** carried on by them (**VATA 1994 s.4**). All elements must be present."},
   {t:"card", i:"📦", title:"Supply of goods or services", body:"A supply of **goods** passes the whole property in goods (e.g. selling stock). A supply of **services** is anything done for **consideration** (payment) that is not a supply of goods, e.g. advice, repairs, letting the use of equipment."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎁", title:"Free supplies and business gifts", body:"Supplies made without consideration are generally **outside the scope** of VAT. But where a business gives **gifts of goods** to the **same person** costing **more than £50 in total** (excluding VAT) in any **12-month period**, each gift is treated as a supply and **output tax** is due on them."},
   {t:"card", i:"🇬🇧", title:"In the UK", body:"Detailed **place of supply** rules decide where cross-border supplies are made. You only need the outline."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧑‍💼", title:"Taxable person", body:"A person (an individual, partnership, company or LLP) who **is registered**, or is **required to be registered**, for VAT."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚗", title:"In the course or furtherance of a business", body:"Only business supplies count. **Private sales** (e.g. an individual selling their own car) are **outside** VAT."},
   {t:"card", i:"✅", title:"Taxable supply = any supply not exempt", body:"A **taxable supply** is any supply that is **not exempt**. Taxable supplies are charged at one of three rates: **standard 20%**, **reduced 5%** or **zero 0%**."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🎚️", title:"The rates", cols:["", "Rate", "Examples", "Recover input tax?"], rows:[["Standard-rated","**20%**","Most goods and services: professional services, furniture, adult clothing, most restaurant meals, new commercial buildings under 3 years old","✅"],["Reduced-rated","**5%**","Domestic fuel and power, children's car seats, smoking cessation products","✅"],["Zero-rated","**0%**","Most **food** (not catering), **books** and newspapers, **children's clothes and shoes**, sale of **new residential** buildings, public transport, **exports** of goods","✅"],["Exempt (not taxable)","None","**Financial services** (loans, banking), **insurance**, most **land**, **education**, **health** and medical care, postal services","❌"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"🏠", title:"Exempt land", body:"Most **land** is exempt: sales and leases of **commercial** property (unless the owner has **opted to tax**) and **residential** lettings."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚫", title:"Exempt means no input tax back", body:"Input tax (VAT paid on purchases) relating to **exempt** supplies is **not recoverable**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚠️", title:"Watch out: zero-rated is not exempt", body:"A **zero-rated** supplier (e.g. a bookseller) makes **taxable** supplies at 0%. Its sales **count towards the registration threshold** and it **can recover input tax** (often getting repayments)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛡️", title:"The exempt supplier", body:"An **exempt** supplier (e.g. an insurer) makes **no taxable supplies**. Those sales **do not count** for registration, and it **cannot recover** related input tax."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A bookseller and an insurer both charge customers no VAT. Who can recover input tax?", body:"Only the **bookseller**. Books are **zero-rated** (taxable at 0%); insurance is **exempt**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏢", title:"Opting to tax (VATA Sch.10)", body:"An owner of **commercial** property can **elect** to charge VAT on its sales and rents of that property (which would otherwise be exempt), so it can **recover input tax** on costs relating to it. The option **binds the property** and can normally be **revoked** only within **6 months** of making it or after **20 years**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔄", title:"Transfer of a going concern (TOGC)", body:"The sale of a business (or part) **as a going concern** to a buyer who **is or becomes VAT-registered** and carries on the **same kind of business** is treated as **neither a supply of goods nor services**, so **no VAT** is charged (VAT (Special Provisions) Order 1995 art.5). This matters on business sales and incorporation."},
   {t:"card", i:"➗", title:"Value: the VAT fraction 1/6", body:"VAT is charged on the **consideration** (price). If a price is stated to be **VAT-inclusive**, the VAT element is **1/6** of the price at the 20% standard rate (20/120)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A VAT-inclusive price at 20% is £120. How much is VAT?", body:"**£20**: one-sixth of £120 (20/120)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Output tax and input tax", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"📤", title:"Output tax", body:"**Output tax** is the VAT a registered business **charges** on its own taxable supplies (sales)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📥", title:"Input tax", body:"**Input tax** is the VAT a registered business **pays** on goods and services it buys **for the purpose of its business** (e.g. stock, equipment, professional fees)."},
   {t:"card", i:"➖", title:"Pay output minus input", body:"For each VAT period the business pays HM Revenue & Customs (**HMRC**) **output tax minus input tax**. If input tax is higher (e.g. a zero-rated business, or one making large purchases), **HMRC repays** the difference."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔗", title:"Each business taxed on the value it adds", body:"So each business in the chain pays tax only on the **value it adds**. The **final consumer**, who cannot recover input tax, bears the whole VAT."},
   {t:"case", i:"🪑", title:"Example: the furniture maker's table", facts:"A furniture maker buys wood for £1,000 plus £200 VAT and sells a table to a shop for £2,000 plus £400 VAT. The shop sells it to a consumer for £3,000 plus £600 VAT.", rule:"The maker pays HMRC **£200** (£400 − £200). The shop pays **£200** (£600 − £400). The consumer bears the total VAT of **£600**."},
   {t:"card", i:"✔️", title:"Recovering input tax: three conditions", body:"**1** The business is **registered**. **2** The purchase was for the business and relates to its **taxable** supplies (not exempt supplies or private use). **3** It holds a valid **VAT invoice** (or other evidence)."},
   {t:"card", i:"⚖️", title:"Mixed costs and partial exemption", body:"Costs used partly privately are **apportioned**. A business making both taxable and exempt supplies is **partly exempt** and recovers only part of its input tax (outline only)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🍾", title:"Blocked input tax", body:"Some VAT can never be recovered, even if business-related: VAT on **business entertainment** (other than of employees), and on buying **cars** (unless used exclusively for business, e.g. a taxi or driving school)."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏪", title:"Pre-registration input tax", body:"On registering, a business can recover VAT on **goods** bought within **4 years** before registration (if still held) and on **services** received within **6 months** before registration."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A registered firm takes clients to a smart dinner. Can it recover the VAT?", body:"**No.** VAT on **business entertainment** (other than of employees) is **blocked**."}
 ]},
 {h:"VAT registration", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"📏", title:"Taxable turnover", body:"**Taxable turnover** is the value of **taxable supplies** (standard, reduced **and zero-rated**), excluding VAT and excluding **exempt** supplies and sales of capital assets (Sch.1 VATA 1994)."},
   {t:"card", i:"💷", title:"The threshold: £90,000", body:"The **registration threshold** is **£90,000** (since 1 April 2024)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔙", title:"Historic (look-back) test", body:"At the **end of any month**, the value of taxable supplies in the **previous 12 months** (a **rolling** period, not the tax year) **exceeds £90,000**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📨", title:"Historic test: notify and start date", body:"The business must **notify HMRC within 30 days** of the end of that month. It is registered from the **first day of the second month** after the month the threshold was exceeded (or an earlier agreed date)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🛑", title:"Historic test: the escape", body:"It need not register if HMRC is satisfied that taxable turnover in the **next 12 months** will **not exceed £88,000** (the **deregistration threshold**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔜", title:"Future (look-forward) test", body:"At **any time**, there are reasonable grounds for believing taxable supplies in the **next 30 days alone** will **exceed £90,000**. Notify HMRC **by the end of that 30-day period**. Registered from the **beginning** of the 30 days."},
   {t:"case", i:"☕", title:"Example: Jo's café", facts:"Jo's café opened on 1 January 2026. By the end of **August 2026** its taxable turnover for the previous 12 months is £92,000.", rule:"Historic test: Jo must notify HMRC by **30 September 2026** and is registered from **1 October 2026**."},
   {t:"case", i:"🍰", title:"Example: Jo's big catering contract", facts:"Instead, on 10 May Jo signs a single £95,000 catering contract to be performed in the next 30 days.", rule:"Future test: she must register from **10 May**, the start of the 30 days."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Historic test passed at the end of March. From when is the business registered?", body:"**1 May**: the first day of the **second** month after the month the threshold was exceeded (notify by 30 April)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🙋", title:"Voluntary registration", body:"A business **below** the threshold may register voluntarily (e.g. a new business with big start-up costs, or one selling mainly to VAT-registered businesses) so it can **recover input tax**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏷️", title:"Voluntary registration: the downside", body:"It must then **charge VAT** on its sales. That raises prices for customers who are **not VAT-registered** (consumers) and adds administration."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚪", title:"Deregistration", body:"**Voluntary** if taxable turnover in the next 12 months is expected to be **£88,000 or less**. **Compulsory** if the business stops making taxable supplies."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏰", title:"Late registration", body:"The business must account for the VAT it **should have charged** from when it should have been registered. This is usually treated as included in the prices it charged, so it comes out of its **own pocket**. Plus a **failure to notify penalty** and interest."},
   {t:"card", i:"💡", title:"Exam tip: the two tests run differently", body:"**Historic**: rolling **12 months**, checked at each **month end**, notify within **30 days**, registered from the **first day of the second month**. **Future**: the **next 30 days alone**, registered from the **start** of those 30 days. **Zero-rated** sales count; **exempt** sales do not."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"An insurance broker's exempt sales are £150,000 a year. Must it register?", body:"**No.** Exempt supplies are **not** taxable turnover, so they don't count towards the £90,000 threshold."}
 ]},
 {h:"Tax point, invoices and returns", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"📅", title:"Why the tax point matters", body:"The **tax point** (time of supply) decides **which VAT return** (VAT period) a supply falls into, and which rate applies if rates change."},
   {t:"card", i:"🚚", title:"Basic tax point", body:"For **goods**: when they are **removed or made available** to the customer. For **services**: when they are **performed** (completed)."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏮️", title:"Actual tax point: earlier", body:"If a **VAT invoice is issued or payment received before** the basic tax point, the tax point is the **earlier** of those dates (to the extent of the payment)."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏭️", title:"Actual tax point: the 14-day rule", body:"If a **VAT invoice is issued within 14 days after** the basic tax point (and no earlier tax point applies), the **invoice date** becomes the tax point. The business can elect otherwise, and HMRC can extend the 14 days (e.g. for monthly invoicing)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔁", title:"Continuous supplies", body:"For continuous supplies (e.g. rent, leasing), the tax point is when **payment is received** or a **VAT invoice is issued**, whichever is **earlier**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Goods delivered 3 June, invoice issued 10 June, no earlier payment. Tax point?", body:"**10 June**: the invoice was issued within **14 days after** the basic tax point."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧾", title:"When a VAT invoice is needed", body:"A registered business making a **standard- or reduced-rated** supply to **another VAT-registered person** must issue a **VAT invoice** within **30 days** of the tax point (or of receiving payment, if earlier)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📝", title:"What a full VAT invoice shows", body:"A **unique sequential number**; **date of issue**; **tax point** (if different); supplier's **name, address and VAT registration number**; customer's name and address; **description** of the goods or services; for each item the quantity, **rate of VAT** and price excluding VAT; the **total excluding VAT**, the **rate** and the **total VAT**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧻", title:"Simplified invoices", body:"A **simplified** (less detailed) invoice can be issued for retail supplies of **£250 or less** (VAT-inclusive). The customer needs a valid VAT invoice to **recover input tax**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗓️", title:"VAT periods", body:"Normally **quarterly**. **Monthly** for businesses that get regular repayments; **annual** under the annual accounting scheme."},
   {t:"card", i:"💻", title:"Making Tax Digital (MTD)", body:"Returns must be filed **digitally** with compatible software under **Making Tax Digital for VAT**, which applies to **all VAT-registered businesses** (since April 2022). They must keep **digital records**."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏳", title:"Deadline: 1 month and 7 days", body:"The return and payment are due **1 month and 7 days** after the end of the VAT period (for electronic filing and payment)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎯", title:"Penalties", body:"A **points-based** late submission penalty system, plus late payment penalties and interest (since 1 January 2023)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🗄️", title:"Records: 6 years", body:"VAT records, invoices and accounts must be kept for **6 years**."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🧰", title:"Special schemes (outline)", cols:["", "How it works", "Turnover limit"], rows:[["Cash accounting","Account for VAT when **paid**, not when invoiced","Up to **£1.35m**"],["Annual accounting","**One return a year** with interim payments","Up to **£1.35m**"],["Flat rate scheme","Pay a **fixed percentage of turnover**","Up to **£150,000**"]]},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"VAT quarter ends 31 March. When are the return and payment due?", body:"**7 May**: 1 month and 7 days after the end of the period."}
 ]},
 {h:"IHT basics and what BPR covers", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"⚰️", title:"What IHT is charged on", body:"**Inheritance tax** is charged on the value of a person's **estate on death** (all their assets less debts), plus certain **lifetime gifts**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎁", title:"Potentially exempt transfers (PETs)", body:"An outright gift to an **individual** is a **PET**. It becomes chargeable only if the donor dies within **7 years**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏦", title:"Chargeable lifetime transfers (CLTs)", body:"Mainly gifts into **most trusts**. Taxed when made at the **lifetime rate (20%)** above the nil-rate band, with more tax if the donor dies within 7 years."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧮", title:"Nil-rate band and the 40% rate", body:"The **nil-rate band (NRB)** is **£325,000**, plus a **residence nil-rate band** of up to **£175,000** for a home passing to descendants. Above that, the **death rate is 40%**. Figures are frozen until April 2031."},
   {t:"card", i:"⏳", title:"Taper relief on gifts within 7 years", body:"If the donor dies **3 to 7 years** after a PET or CLT, the **tax** on that gift (not its value) is reduced by **taper relief**: 3-4 years **20%** off, 4-5 years **40%**, 5-6 years **60%**, 6-7 years **80%**. It only helps where the gift exceeds the available nil-rate band, because otherwise there is no tax to reduce."},
   {t:"card", i:"💍", title:"Spouses and charity", body:"Gifts to a **spouse or civil partner**, and to **charity**, are **exempt**. Unused NRB can transfer to a surviving spouse or civil partner."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎚️", title:"When BPR applies", body:"BPR **reduces the value transferred** by a lifetime gift or on death, **before** the nil-rate band is applied."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Grandad gives shares outright to his grandson. What kind of transfer?", body:"A **PET**: an outright gift to an individual. Chargeable only if Grandad dies within **7 years**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧺", title:"100%: a business or an interest in one", body:"A **business**, or an **interest in a business**: e.g. a sole trader's business, a **partner's share** of a partnership, or an LLP member's interest. **100%** relief (subject to the £2.5m allowance)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🍬", title:"100%: unquoted trading company shares", body:"**Unquoted shares** in a trading company, of **any size** of holding: shares neither listed nor admitted to trading on a recognised stock exchange (e.g. most private company shares). **100%** relief (subject to the £2.5m allowance)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📉", title:"50%: AIM shares", body:"Shares **admitted to trading** on a recognised stock exchange but designated **'not listed'**, e.g. **AIM** shares: **50%** from 6 April 2026 (previously 100%)."},
   {t:"card", i:"👑", title:"50%: controlling listed shares", body:"**Quoted (listed) shares** that give the transferor **control** of the company (more than 50% of the votes): **50%**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏭", title:"50%: personally owned business assets", body:"**Land, buildings, machinery or plant** owned **personally** by the transferor and **used** wholly or mainly for the business of a **company the transferor controls**, or of a **partnership in which they are a partner**: **50%**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔐", title:"Personally owned assets: the extra condition", body:"These get 50% only if the transferor **controls** the company (or is a partner), **and** their **shares or partnership interest** also qualify as relevant business property."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧺", title:"No relief: minority listed shares", body:"A **minority holding of listed shares** gets **no BPR**."},
   {t:"compare", i:"🧺", title:"The hamper shelf: rates at a glance", cols:["", "Rate"], rows:[["Business / partnership share / LLP interest","**100%**"],["Unquoted trading company shares (any size)","**100%**"],["AIM ('not listed') shares","**50%**"],["Listed shares giving control (over 50% of votes)","**50%**"],["Premises or machinery owned personally, used by controlled company or own partnership","**50%**"],["Minority listed shares","**None**"]]},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A 5% holding in an unquoted trading company. Rate of BPR?", body:"**100%** (subject to the £2.5m allowance). For unquoted shares, the **size** of the holding doesn't matter."}
 ]},
 {h:"BPR conditions", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"⌛", title:"Two-year ownership (s.106)", body:"The transferor must have **owned** the property throughout the **2 years** immediately before the transfer."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔁", title:"Replacement and spouse property (ss.107-108)", body:"**Replacement property** can count towards the 2 years (s.107). Where property was **inherited from a spouse or civil partner**, the spouse's period of ownership counts (s.108)."},
   {t:"card", i:"🏘️", title:"Trading, not investment (s.105(3))", body:"No relief for a business (or shares in a company) that consists **wholly or mainly** of **dealing in securities, stocks or shares, land or buildings**, or **making or holding investments** (e.g. a property letting business)."},
   {t:"card", i:"📊", title:"What 'mainly' means", body:"**More than 50%**, looking at the business **in the round**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"A company whose main business is letting houses. BPR on its shares?", body:"**No.** It is **mainly** making or holding **investments** (s.105(3))."},
   {t:"card", i:"🐷", title:"Excepted assets (s.112)", body:"The value of assets **not used wholly or mainly for the business** throughout the 2 years before the transfer, and **not required for future use** in it (e.g. **surplus cash**, or an investment property held by a trading company), is **excluded** from relief."},
   {t:"card", i:"✉️", title:"Binding contract for sale (s.113)", body:"**No BPR** if, at the time of the transfer, the property is subject to a **binding contract for sale**. Example: a partnership or shareholders' agreement that **obliges** the surviving partners to buy, and the estate to sell, a deceased partner's share."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔓", title:"The fix: options, not obligations", body:"To keep BPR, agreements use **options** (cross-options, or put and call options) rather than an automatic buy-sell obligation."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"The partnership deed says survivors must buy, and the estate must sell, a dead partner's share. BPR?", body:"**No.** That is a **binding contract for sale** (s.113). Use **options** instead."},
   {t:"card", i:"🎁", title:"Lifetime gift then death within 7 years (s.113A)", body:"When the donor dies within 7 years of a gift of business property, BPR on the death calculation is available only if the **donee still owns** the property (or qualifying replacement) at the donor's death **and** it **still qualifies** as relevant business property."},
   {t:"card", i:"👶", title:"s.113A: the donee's own 2 years", body:"For the donee, the **2-year ownership** condition is **ignored**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Grandson sold the gifted shares before Grandad died 3 years later. BPR on the death calculation?", body:"**No.** Under s.113A the donee must **still own** the property (or qualifying replacement) at the donor's death."}
 ]},
 {h:"The £2.5m allowance (from 6 April 2026)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"🎀", title:"100% only on the first £2.5m", body:"For transfers on or after **6 April 2026** (Finance Act 2026), **100% relief** (BPR and agricultural property relief, **APR**, combined) applies only to the **first £2.5 million** of qualifying property per individual: the **allowance**."},
   {t:"card", i:"✂️", title:"Above £2.5m: 50%", body:"Qualifying property **above** £2.5m gets **50%** relief."},
   {t:"card", i:"🧾", title:"50% property doesn't use the allowance", body:"The allowance applies to property that would otherwise get 100% relief. Property that only gets **50%** (e.g. **AIM shares**, **personally owned premises**) is relieved at 50% and does **not** need the allowance."},
   {t:"card", i:"💑", title:"Transferable to a spouse or civil partner", body:"**Unused allowance transfers** to a surviving **spouse or civil partner** (even if the first death was before 6 April 2026). So a couple can pass up to **£5 million** of qualifying property with 100% relief."},
   {t:"card", i:"🕰️", title:"Lifetime gifts share the allowance", body:"The allowance covers **lifetime transfers in the 7 years before death** as well as the death estate, used up in **date order**."},
   {t:"card", i:"📆", title:"Start date for gifts", body:"The new rules apply to gifts made on or after **30 October 2024** if the donor dies on or after **6 April 2026**."},
   {t:"card", i:"🔟", title:"10 interest-free instalments", body:"IHT on property qualifying for BPR or APR can be paid in **10 equal annual instalments, interest-free**."},
   {t:"case", i:"🏭", title:"Example: Ana's company shares", facts:"Ana dies in October 2026 owning all the shares in her unquoted trading company, worth **£3.5 million**. She made no lifetime gifts and has no transferred allowance.", rule:"The first **£2.5m** gets **100%** relief. The remaining **£1m** gets **50%** relief (£500,000). So **£500,000** is chargeable to IHT (at 40% above any available nil-rate band), payable in 10 interest-free instalments if she wishes."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"🤔", title:"Unquoted trading shares worth £3m, no transferred allowance. How much is relieved?", body:"**£2.75m**: £2.5m at 100% plus 50% of the £500,000 above it. **£250,000** stays chargeable."},
   {t:"card", i:"💡", title:"Exam tip: the BPR route", body:"**1** What **kind** of property (100%, 50% or none)? **2** Owned **2 years**? **3** **Trading**, not mainly investment? **4** Any **excepted assets** or **binding contract for sale**? **5** Does the **£2.5m allowance** cap the 100% relief?"}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Source: src/content/dr.js topic dr-appeals (Fairground room 15, the Helter Skelter). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["dr/dr-appeals"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Asking a higher judge to look again", body:"A party who thinks a civil judge got a decision **wrong** (or the process was **seriously unfair**) can ask a higher judge to **review** it."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Three questions decide almost every appeal", body:"1. **Is permission needed, and who gives it?** Almost always yes, from the lower court or the appeal court.\n2. **Where does the appeal go?** Generally to the **next level of judge up** (the '**destination**' of the appeal).\n3. **On what grounds will it succeed?** The decision was **wrong**, or **unjust** because of a **serious procedural or other irregularity**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Plus the time limits, and where the rules are", body:"Plus the **time limits** for the appellant's notice. The rules are in **CPR Part 52** and its practice directions (**PD 52A-52E**), and the **Access to Justice Act 1999 (Destination of Appeals) Order 2016**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Examined under Dispute Resolution", body:"Since **September 2026** the SRA examines appeals in civil cases **only under Dispute Resolution**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Key terms", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Appellant and respondent", body:"**Appellant**: the party appealing. **Respondent**: the other party to the appeal."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Lower court and appeal court", body:"**Lower court**: the court whose decision is appealed. **Appeal court**: the court hearing the appeal."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"First appeal and second appeal", body:"**First appeal**: an appeal from a first-instance decision (the original decision, e.g. the trial judgment or an interim order). **Second appeal**: an appeal against a decision made on a first appeal."}
 ]},
 {h:"Permission to appeal (CPR 52.3-52.7): when permission is needed", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Almost always: you need a ticket", body:"**Permission** is required for **almost all** appeals from a decision of a judge in the **County Court** or the **High Court**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Three exceptions: no permission needed", body:"Appeals against:\n- a **committal order** (an order sending a person to prison for contempt of court);\n- a refusal to grant **habeas corpus** (a challenge to unlawful detention);\n- a **secure accommodation order** under the **Children Act 1989 s.25**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Does an appeal against a committal order need permission?", body:"**No.** It is one of the three exceptions, with habeas corpus and secure accommodation orders."}
 ]},
 {h:"Permission to appeal: who to ask", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"First, ask the lower court", body:"Ask the **lower court** (the judge who made the decision), at the **hearing at which the decision is made**, or at a later hearing adjourned for that purpose (e.g. when a reserved judgment is handed down). This is the usual and sensible course."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Then the appeal court, in the appellant's notice", body:"**If the lower court refuses** (or no application was made to it), the appellant can ask the **appeal court** for permission in the **appellant's notice**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Refused on paper? Ask for an oral hearing", body:"If the appeal court refuses permission on paper, the appellant can usually ask for the decision to be **reconsidered at an oral hearing**, unless the application is declared **totally without merit**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"The appeal court refuses permission. Can you appeal against that refusal?", body:"**No.** There is no appeal against a refusal of permission by the appeal court."}
 ]},
 {h:"Permission to appeal: the test for permission (CPR 52.6)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Test 1: a real prospect of success", body:"Permission for a **first appeal** may be given **only** where the court considers the appeal would have a **real prospect of success** (realistic, not fanciful); **or**..."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Test 2: some other compelling reason", body:"...there is **some other compelling reason** for the appeal to be heard (e.g. an issue of public importance, or the law needs clarifying)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Limits and conditions", body:"Permission may be **limited** to certain issues and made subject to **conditions** (e.g. paying money into court)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Permission to appeal: second appeals (CPR 52.7)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Second appeals: Court of Appeal only", body:"Second appeals always go to the **Court of Appeal**, and only the **Court of Appeal** can give permission."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A much stricter test", body:"Permission is given only if the appeal would have a **real prospect of success** **and** raises an **important point of principle or practice**, **or** there is **some other compelling reason** for the Court of Appeal to hear it."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A District Judge in the County Court refuses to set aside default judgment (judgment given without a trial because the defendant did not respond in time). What should the defendant do?", body:"**Ask the District Judge for permission to appeal at the end of that hearing.** If refused, it can apply to a **Circuit Judge** (the appeal court) in its appellant's notice, within **21 days**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Time limits", cards:[
   {t:"compare", i:"", title:"The three time limits", cols:["Step", "Time limit"], rows:[["**Appellant's notice** filed at the appeal court","Within **21 days after the date of the decision** being appealed, unless the **lower court** directs a different period (CPR 52.12(2))"],["**Service** of the sealed appellant's notice on each respondent (if the appellant serves it)","As soon as practicable and in any event within **14 days** after it is **sealed** by the court (CPR 52.12(3), as amended from **1 October 2025**; previously 7 days after filing)"],["**Respondent's notice** (if the respondent wants permission to appeal itself, or wants the decision **upheld for different or additional reasons**)","Within **14 days** of being served with the appellant's notice (or of being told permission has been granted, as the rules specify) (CPR 52.13)"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The 21 days run from the decision", body:"The 21 days run from the date the **decision** was made (the date of judgment or ruling), **not** from the date the sealed order is received or the reasons are given, unless the lower court directs otherwise."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"An adjournment doesn't stop the clock", body:"An adjournment of the decision hearing (e.g. to deal with consequential matters) does **not** by itself extend time. The lower court should be asked to extend time **expressly**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No extension by agreement", body:"The parties **cannot agree** to extend the time for filing an appellant's notice. An extension must be granted by the **appeal court** (or the lower court when it makes the decision)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Late? Treated like relief from sanctions", body:"A late application is treated like an application for **relief from sanctions** (asking the court to excuse a missed deadline), using the **Denton** principles: how serious the failure was, why it happened, and all the circumstances of the case."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Grounds and a skeleton argument", body:"The appellant's notice must set out the **grounds** of appeal, and be accompanied by (or followed by) a **skeleton argument** and the relevant documents (**PD 52B/52C**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No automatic stay (CPR 52.16)", body:"An appeal does **not** stop the lower court's order from taking effect (e.g. the judgment debt is still payable and can be enforced) unless the appeal court or lower court orders a **stay** (a pause on enforcing the order). An appellant who wants to stop enforcement must **apply for a stay**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"You file your appellant's notice. Can the other side still enforce the judgment?", body:"**Yes**, unless the appeal court or the lower court orders a **stay**. Filing an appeal does not suspend the judgment."}
 ]},
 {h:"Destination of appeals (who hears the appeal)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"One level up", body:"The general rule is that an appeal goes to the **next level of judge up** in the judicial hierarchy."},
   {t:"compare", i:"", title:"Where a first appeal goes", cols:["Decision made by", "Appeal (first appeal) goes to"], rows:[["**District Judge** (or Deputy) of the **County Court**","A **Circuit Judge** (County Court)"],["**Circuit Judge** or **Recorder** (a part-time judge) (County Court)","A **High Court Judge**"],["**Master** or **District Judge of the High Court** (in a District Registry: a High Court office outside London)","A **High Court Judge**"],["**High Court Judge**","The **Court of Appeal**"],["**Court of Appeal**","The **Supreme Court** (permission from the Court of Appeal or the Supreme Court; only for points of law of **general public importance**)"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Interim or final, any track", body:"This applies to **interim and final** decisions and to decisions on **all tracks**. (Before October 2016 some final multi-track decisions in the County Court went straight to the Court of Appeal; that is **no longer** the rule, apart from limited specialist exceptions.)"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Second appeals go to the Court of Appeal", body:"**Second appeals** (e.g. from a Circuit Judge hearing an appeal from a District Judge) go to the **Court of Appeal**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Transfer to the Court of Appeal", body:"The appeal court can **transfer** an appeal to the Court of Appeal if it raises an important point of principle or practice, or there is another compelling reason."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A Circuit Judge tries a £150,000 multi-track claim in the County Court. Where does the defendant's appeal go?", body:"**To a High Court Judge**, not the Court of Appeal."}
 ]},
 {h:"Grounds of appeal and the appeal hearing: review, not rehearing (CPR 52.21)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A review, not a rehearing", body:"Every appeal is limited to a **review** of the lower court's decision (checking it for error), **not** a **rehearing** (hearing the whole case again), unless a practice direction provides otherwise or the court decides a rehearing is needed in the interests of justice."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No new evidence, as a rule", body:"The appeal court will **not** hear **oral evidence** or receive **evidence that was not before the lower court** unless it orders otherwise."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Fresh evidence: three conditions (Ladd v Marshall [1954])", body:"Fresh evidence is generally admitted only if it:\n- **could not have been obtained with reasonable diligence** for the trial;\n- would probably have had an **important influence** on the result; and\n- is **apparently credible**."}
 ]},
 {h:"When an appeal will be allowed", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Ground 1: the decision was wrong", body:"The appeal court will **allow** an appeal where the decision was **wrong**: e.g. an **error of law**, an error of **fact**, or an error in the exercise of the court's **discretion** (taking into account irrelevant matters, ignoring relevant ones, or reaching a decision outside the range of reasonable decisions); or..."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Ground 2: unjust because of a serious irregularity", body:"...the decision was **unjust** because of a **serious procedural or other irregularity** in the proceedings in the lower court (e.g. the judge refused to let a party call key evidence, or showed apparent bias)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Deference to the trial judge", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Findings of fact: very reluctant", body:"Appeal courts are **very reluctant** to interfere with **findings of fact**, especially those based on the judge's assessment of **witnesses** they saw and heard. They will do so only if the finding was one no reasonable judge could have reached, or was clearly wrong."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Case management: only if plainly wrong", body:"They are also slow to interfere with **case management decisions** and other discretionary decisions, which will be overturned only if **plainly wrong** (outside the generous ambit of the judge's discretion) or based on an error of principle."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"The judge preferred the defendant's witness after hearing both. The claimant says 'the judge should have believed me'. Permission?", body:"**Unlikely.** An appeal is a review, and the appeal court will not interfere with findings of fact based on the judge's assessment of credibility unless plainly wrong."}
 ]},
 {h:"The appeal court's powers (CPR 52.20)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Affirm, set aside or vary", body:"The appeal court can **affirm** (confirm), **set aside** (cancel) or **vary** (change) any order or judgment of the lower court."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Send it back", body:"It can **refer** any claim or issue back to the lower court for determination."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A new trial", body:"It can order a **new trial** or hearing."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Interest and costs, here and below", body:"It can make orders for the payment of **interest** and **costs** (including the costs of the hearing below: '**costs here and below**')."}
 ]},
 {h:"Exam tips and watch out", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exam tip: who decided, then one level up", body:"Identify **who made the decision** first, then go **one level up**: District Judge → Circuit Judge; Circuit Judge → High Court Judge; Master or High Court District Judge → High Court Judge; High Court Judge → Court of Appeal. The track and whether the decision was interim or final do not change this."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exam tip: ask the trial judge first; 21, 14, 14", body:"The best first step is usually to ask the **trial judge** for permission **at the hearing** when the decision is given. Time limits: **21 days** from the **decision** to file; **14 days** to serve the sealed notice; respondent's notice **14 days**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Watch out", body:"Filing an appeal does **not** suspend the judgment. Unless a **stay** is obtained, the successful party can enforce. And the parties **cannot agree** between themselves to extend the 21-day time limit."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Source: src/content/dr.js topic dr-case-management (Fairground room 10, the Go-Kart Track). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["dr/dr-case-management"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The court runs the case", body:"Once a claim is defended, the **court**, not the parties, controls its progress. This is **case management**. The court is guided by the **overriding objective** (CPR 1.1): dealing with cases **justly and at proportionate cost**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Tracks, directions and budgets", body:"The court **allocates** each case to one of four **tracks** (small claims, fast, intermediate or multi-track), depending mainly on value and complexity, and gives **directions** (a timetable of steps up to trial). In multi-track cases it also controls costs through **costs budgets**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Sanctions and relief", body:"If a party breaks a rule or order, the court can impose **sanctions**, and the party must apply for **relief from sanctions**, judged by the **Denton** test. The rules are in **CPR Parts 1, 3 and 26 to 29**."}
 ]},
 {h:"The overriding objective (CPR 1.1)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Justly and at proportionate cost", body:"The CPR's **overriding objective** is to enable the court to deal with cases **justly and at proportionate cost**. This includes, so far as practicable, the points on the next cards."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Equal footing", body:"Ensuring the parties are on an **equal footing** and can **participate fully** in proceedings, and that parties and witnesses can give their **best evidence** (relevant to **vulnerable** parties and witnesses)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Saving expense, proportionately", body:"**Saving expense**; and dealing with the case in ways **proportionate** to the **amount of money** involved, the **importance** of the case, the **complexity** of the issues and the **financial position** of each party."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Fairly, a fair share, and compliance", body:"Ensuring it is dealt with **expeditiously and fairly**; allotting it an appropriate share of the **court's resources**, taking into account other cases; and **enforcing compliance** with rules, practice directions (official guidance that supplements the rules) and orders."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Promoting or using ADR", body:"**Promoting or using alternative dispute resolution (ADR)**, added from **1 October 2024**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The court applies it; the parties help", body:"The court must apply the overriding objective when it exercises any power or interprets any rule. The **parties** must help the court to further it (**CPR 1.3**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Active case management (CPR 1.4)", body:"Includes: encouraging **co-operation**, identifying the **issues early**, deciding which issues need a trial, **ordering or encouraging ADR**, fixing **timetables**, controlling **costs**, dealing with as many aspects as possible at once, and using **technology**."}
 ]},
 {h:"The court's general powers (CPR 3.1)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Time and hearings", body:"**Extend or shorten** time limits (**even after they have expired**); adjourn or bring forward hearings; require a party or its lawyer to attend court; hold hearings by **telephone or video**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Shaping the case", body:"**Stay** proceedings (pause them); **consolidate or split** claims; decide the **order of issues**; **exclude an issue**; **order the parties to engage in ADR**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Conditions on orders", body:"The court can attach **conditions** to orders, e.g. that a party **pay a sum into court**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Of its own initiative", body:"The court can act **of its own initiative**, without an application."}
 ]},
 {h:"Allocation to a track", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Notice of proposed allocation", body:"When a defence is filed, the court serves a **notice of proposed allocation** on each party (CPR 26), saying which track appears suitable and the date by which each party must file a **directions questionnaire (DQ)**: form **N180** for the small claims track; **N181** for the other tracks."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What the DQ asks", body:"Settlement and ADR (including whether a **stay** to attempt settlement is wanted, usually for **a month**), the track, witnesses, experts, the trial length, compliance with protocols, use of **Welsh**, and (in multi-track claims) costs budgets and disclosure."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No DQ: 7 days or struck out", body:"If a party fails to file its DQ, the court can order it to do so within **7 days**, failing which its claim or defence may be **struck out**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Allocation factors (CPR 26.13)", body:"The **financial value**, the **nature of the remedy**, the likely **complexity**, the **number of parties**, the value of any **counterclaim**, the amount of **oral evidence**, the **importance** of the claim to non-parties, and the parties' views and circumstances. The court also takes account of any **vulnerability** of a party or witness (overriding objective and PD 1A)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What the value ignores", body:"Value ignores **interest**, **costs** and any possible **contributory negligence**, and any amount **not in dispute**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Counterclaims are not added together", body:"Where there is a **counterclaim** (or other additional claim), the court does **not** add the claims together: it generally treats the **largest** single claim as deciding the value for allocation (PD 26 para 14(10))."}
 ]},
 {h:"The four tracks", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Value (normal limit)", body:"**Small claims**: up to **£10,000**. **Fast track**: **£10,000 to £25,000**. **Intermediate track**: **£25,000 to £100,000**. **Multi-track**: over **£100,000**, or complex lower-value claims."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Personal injury (PI)", body:"**Small claims**: total up to £10,000 **and** pain, suffering and loss of amenity up to **£1,500** (road traffic accident claims: **£5,000**). **Fast**: up to £25,000. **Intermediate**: up to £100,000. **Multi-track**: over £100,000; mesothelioma, most **clinical negligence** and abuse claims always."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Trial length", body:"**Small claims**: a short, informal hearing. **Fast**: **no more than 1 day**. **Intermediate**: **no more than 3 days**. **Multi-track**: any."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Experts", body:"**Small claims**: only with permission; rarely. **Fast**: oral evidence from **1 expert per party per field**, in **no more than 2 fields** (usually written). **Intermediate**: for allocation, no more than **2 experts per party** giving oral evidence; once allocated, normally **1 oral expert per party** unless a second is reasonably required (CPR 28.14). **Multi-track**: as directed."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Disclosure", body:"**Small claims**: none; parties send the documents they rely on. **Fast**: usually **standard disclosure** (the documents a party relies on and those that harm its case or help the other side's). **Intermediate**: the CPR 31.5 **menu** (except PI claims), proportionate to the claim. **Multi-track**: **menu** of options (see **Disclosure**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Costs", body:"**Small claims**: very limited recoverable costs. **Fast**: **fixed recoverable costs** (bands 1 to 4). **Intermediate**: **fixed recoverable costs**, **complexity bands 1 to 4**. **Multi-track**: assessed costs, controlled by **costs budgets**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Management", body:"**Small claims**: standard directions. **Fast**: standard directions; trial within about **30 weeks**. **Intermediate**: directions on paper or at a **CMC** (case management conference; discretionary); trial within about **30 weeks**. **Multi-track**: tailored directions, **CMC**, often a **pre-trial review**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Intermediate track: how many parties", body:"1 claimant v 1 or 2 defendants, or 2 claimants v 1 defendant (CPR 26.9(7)(d)). **2 v 2** does not meet the normal criteria and normally goes to the **multi-track**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Small claims track", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Up to £10,000", body:"The **normal track** for claims up to **£10,000**, plus the personal injury and housing disrepair claims on the next cards."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Personal injury on small claims", body:"Total up to £10,000 **and** damages for **pain, suffering and loss of amenity (PSLA)** no more than **£1,500**; or **£5,000** for **road traffic accident** claims within the whiplash reforms (the special regime under the **Civil Liability Act 2018**); and **£1,000** for some road traffic claims excluded from those reforms (e.g. by vulnerable road users). A personal injury claim that fails these limits but is worth up to £25,000 normally goes to the **fast track** (CPR 26.9)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Housing disrepair", body:"Claims by tenants where the estimated **cost of repairs** is no more than **£1,000** and other damages no more than **£1,000**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"An informal hearing", body:"Informal hearing; **strict rules of evidence do not apply**; usually a **District Judge** (a full-time judge, mainly in the county court). The court can decide the case **on paper** if the parties agree."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Small claims mediation", body:"Many specified money claims are first referred to free **small claims mediation** (see **Options**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Very limited costs", body:"**Costs** are limited to fixed issue costs, court fees, limited witness and expert expenses, and costs caused by a party's **unreasonable behaviour**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Fast track", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Fast track: which claims", body:"Claims between **£10,000 and £25,000** where the **trial will last no more than one day** (5 hours) and oral expert evidence is limited to **one expert per party in any field**, in **no more than two fields**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Directions on allocation (CPR 28.2, 28.7)", body:"The court normally gives **standard directions** (in the standard form published online) **on paper** when it allocates the case. It holds a **case management conference** only if it thinks one is necessary."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What the directions cover", body:"**Disclosure**, **witness statements**, **expert evidence** and **ADR**. They must either fix the **trial date** or fix a **trial period** (a window of **no more than 3 weeks**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Timetable: weeks 4, 10 and 14", body:"Typical fast track timetable (PD 28 para 9.5), counted in weeks from the **notice of allocation**: **disclosure** 4 weeks; exchange of **witness statements** 10 weeks; exchange of **experts' reports** 14 weeks."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Timetable: weeks 20, 22 and 30", body:"Court sends **pre-trial checklists** 20 weeks; parties file completed checklists 22 weeks; **trial** 30 weeks. The standard period between directions and trial is **not more than 30 weeks** (PD 28 para 3.11)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Experts on the fast track", body:"Permission is normally for **one expert per issue** (CPR 35.4(3A)), often a **single joint expert** (one expert instructed by both parties) (PD 28 para 3.9), and evidence is by **written report**. An expert attends the trial only if that is necessary in the interests of justice (CPR 35.5(2))."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Disclosure on the fast track", body:"The standard directions usually order **standard disclosure** by list, but the court can **limit** it (e.g. to the documents each party relies on)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Agreeing directions", body:"The parties may agree directions and send them to the court for approval. To be approved they must set a timetable with **calendar dates**, propose a trial date or period, and deal with **disclosure** and **factual and expert evidence** (PD 28 para 3.6)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Changing dates", body:"The parties can **agree in writing** to vary most time limits (CPR 2.11), but **not** the court-fixed dates for the **trial** (or trial period), a **case management conference**, a **pre-trial review** or the return of the **pre-trial checklist**, and not any other date if that would mean changing one of those. To change those, **apply to the court** (CPR 28.3)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"3 weeks' notice of trial", body:"The parties get at least **3 weeks' notice** of the trial date (CPR 28.5)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A £18,000 contract claim is allocated to the fast track on 1 March. What can the parties expect?", body:"Unless the court orders otherwise: **disclosure by about 29 March** (4 weeks), **witness statements by about 10 May** (10 weeks), **expert evidence by about 7 June** (14 weeks) and a **trial within about 30 weeks** of allocation. If the claimant needs more time for its witness statements, the parties can agree a short extension **in writing**, as long as it does not affect the pre-trial checklist or trial dates."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Fixed recoverable costs", body:"Costs are **fixed** (fixed recoverable costs, FRC) for claims issued on or after **1 October 2023**, based on the stage reached and a complexity band."}
 ]},
 {h:"Intermediate track (claims issued on or after 1 October 2023)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Intermediate track: which claims", body:"Claims of **£25,000 to £100,000** where the **trial** will last **no more than 3 days** and **no more than two experts per party** will give oral evidence. (A lower-value claim that does not fit the fast track, e.g. one needing a 2-day trial, also normally goes here: CPR 26.9.)"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Parties and excluded types", body:"The claim is by one claimant against one or two defendants, or two claimants against one defendant; and it is not an excluded type (e.g. **mesothelioma**, most **clinical negligence**, claims against the police for intentional torts, abuse claims, which go to the **multi-track**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Fixed costs, bands 1 to 4", body:"Costs are **fixed**, depending on the stage reached and the claim's **complexity band** (**1 to 4**), assigned at allocation."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No costs budgeting", body:"**Costs budgeting does not apply** on the intermediate track."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Directions on allocation (CPR 28.2, 28.12 to 28.14)", body:"The court gives **directions on paper** or fixes a **CMC**. A CMC and a **pre-trial review** are **discretionary**, not automatic. Directions deal with disclosure, witness statements, expert evidence, ADR, whether to hold a pre-trial review and listing, and fix the trial date or a trial period of **no more than 3 weeks**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Agreed directions (CPR 28.13)", body:"The parties must try to **agree directions**, and file agreed directions (or each side's proposals) at least **7 days before** any CMC. If the court approves agreed directions or gives its own, the CMC is **cancelled** (vacated)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Built-in limits: trial and experts (CPR 28.14)", body:"The **trial** time estimate must not exceed **3 days**. **Oral expert evidence**: **one expert per party**, unless a **second** expert is **reasonably required and proportionate**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Built-in limits: 30 and 20 pages", body:"Unless the court orders otherwise: all of a party's **witness statements** and witness summaries together must not exceed **30 pages**; each **expert report** must not exceed **20 pages** (not counting the CV, supporting materials and any necessary photographs, plans or academic articles)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Disclosure on the intermediate track", body:"Unless the court orders otherwise, the disclosure **menu** in CPR 31.5 applies as on the multi-track, **except in personal injury claims** (CPR 31.5(2)). The court picks an order proportionate to the claim."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"30 weeks; court dates need an application", body:"The standard period between directions and trial is **not more than 30 weeks** (PD 28 para 3.11). Court-fixed dates (trial, CMC, pre-trial review, pre-trial checklist) can only be changed by **applying to the court** (CPR 28.3)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A £70,000 professional negligence claim is in band 3 of the intermediate track. Each party wants two experts to give oral evidence. Allowed?", body:"**Only one each**, unless the second is **reasonably required and proportionate**. And each party's witness statements must stay within **30 pages** in total."}
 ]},
 {h:"Multi-track", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Multi-track: which claims", body:"Claims over **£100,000**, claims not suitable for the other tracks (e.g. complex lower-value claims or longer trials), and the **excluded types** listed above."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Tailored directions, CMC, pre-trial review", body:"**Tailored directions**; usually a **case management conference (CMC)** to review the issues, disclosure, experts and timetable, and a **pre-trial review** (PTR) shortly before trial in larger cases."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Costs management", body:"**Costs management** applies to most multi-track claims **under £10 million**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A £60,000 professional negligence claim against an accountant needs a 2-day trial, with one expert on each side. Which track?", body:"**The intermediate track**: under £100,000, trial no more than 3 days, no more than 2 experts per party. If it needed a **5-day trial** or **four experts each**, it would go to the **multi-track**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Directions", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Directions: the timetable to trial", body:"**Directions** are the court's orders setting the **timetable** for the steps to trial: **disclosure**, exchange of **witness statements**, **expert evidence**, **pre-trial checklists**, and the **trial** date or window."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Directions on each track", body:"**Standard** directions on the small claims and fast tracks; on the intermediate track, directions on paper or at a CMC, within the limits in CPR 28.14; **tailored** ones on the multi-track. The court must consider **ADR** when giving directions and may order a **stay** for settlement."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Agree directions", body:"Parties should try to **agree directions**, which the court will usually approve if suitable."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The case management conference (CMC)", body:"On the multi-track, the court reviews the steps taken, decides directions and costs budgets, and may fix the trial. The **legal representative** who attends must be **familiar with the case** and have authority to deal with any issues; failure can lead to a **wasted costs order** (an order that the lawyer personally pays the costs wasted)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Costs management (multi-track)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Where costs management applies", body:"Multi-track claims issued with a value of **under £10 million** (with some exceptions, e.g. claims by children). It does **not** apply on the intermediate track."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The costs budget (Precedent H)", body:"Each party files and exchanges a **costs budget** (form **Precedent H**: an estimate of its incurred and future costs for each phase of the case): **with the DQ** if the stated value is **under £50,000**; otherwise at least **21 days before the first CMC**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Budget discussion report (Precedent R)", body:"The parties then file a **budget discussion report** (**Precedent R**), setting out what is agreed and disputed, at least **7 days** before the first CMC."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The costs management order", body:"The court makes a **costs management order (CMO)**, approving or revising the budgets for future costs. At the end of the case, on a **standard basis** assessment (the usual basis: only reasonable and proportionate costs are allowed, with any doubt resolved for the paying party), the court will **not depart** from the approved budget unless there is **good reason**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Late budget: court fees only", body:"A party that fails to file a budget on time is **treated as having filed a budget comprising only the applicable court fees** (CPR 3.14), unless the court orders otherwise, so it can recover almost nothing of its future costs. It must apply for **relief from sanctions**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The CCMC", body:"A **costs and case management conference (CCMC)** deals with case management and costs management at the same hearing."}
 ]},
 {h:"Non-compliance and sanctions", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Sanctions: costs, payment in, debarring", body:"**Costs orders**, including on the **indemnity basis** (a basis more generous to the receiving party: any doubt about reasonableness is resolved in its favour), and payment of costs **immediately**; orders that a party **pay money into court**; **debarring** a party from relying on evidence (e.g. a late expert report)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Sanctions: interest and unless orders", body:"Adjusting **interest**; and **unless orders**: orders saying that **unless** a party does something by a set time, its claim or defence will be **struck out** (or another sanction will apply) automatically."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Strike out (CPR 3.4(2))", body:"The court can strike out all or part of a statement of case where it discloses **no reasonable grounds** for bringing or defending the claim; it is an **abuse of the court's process** or likely to obstruct the just disposal of the proceedings; or there has been a **failure to comply** with a rule, practice direction or court order."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Automatic sanctions", body:"These apply without any order, e.g.: a **witness statement** not served in time: the witness may not give oral evidence without permission (**CPR 32.10**); an **expert's report** not disclosed: it cannot be used without permission (**CPR 35.13**); a late **costs budget**: limited to court fees (**CPR 3.14**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Extensions by agreement: up to 28 days", body:"The parties can agree in writing to extend a time limit by **up to 28 days** without the court, provided it does not put at risk any **hearing date** (**CPR 3.8(4)**). This is separate from the 28-day defence extension in CPR 15.5."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Applying before the deadline", body:"A party that applies for an extension **before** time expires is not seeking relief from sanctions; the court simply applies the **overriding objective**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Relief from sanctions (CPR 3.9 and Denton)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What relief from sanctions is", body:"Asking the court to lift a sanction imposed for failing to comply with a rule, practice direction or order. The application must be made **promptly** and supported by **evidence**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Mitchell v News Group Newspapers Ltd [2013]", body:"Andrew Mitchell MP's solicitors filed his **costs budget late** (the day before the CMC). The Court of Appeal **refused relief**, so he was limited to recovering **court fees**. The decision was seen as **too harsh** and led to many unmeritorious refusals of extensions."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Denton v TH White Ltd [2014]", body:"In three linked appeals involving late steps (e.g. witness statements served very late), the Court of Appeal restated the approach in **three stages**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Stage 1: how serious and significant?", body:"If the breach is **neither serious nor significant** (e.g. it does not imperil hearing dates or disrupt the litigation), relief will usually be granted and the court need not spend much time on stages 2 and 3."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Stage 2: why did it happen?", body:"A good reason (e.g. **serious illness**, a **genuine accident**) helps; **pressure of work**, overlooking a deadline or a solicitor's oversight is **rarely a good reason**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Stage 3: all the circumstances", body:"All the circumstances of the case, so as to deal justly with the application, giving **particular weight** to the two factors in CPR 3.9(1): **(a)** litigation conducted **efficiently and at proportionate cost**; and **(b)** the need to **enforce compliance** with rules, practice directions and orders."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Don't pick on minor slips", body:"**Opponents** should not take advantage of minor slips: opposing relief opportunistically can lead to **costs penalties** (Denton)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Witness statements are served 2 days late after a diary error; the trial date is unaffected. Relief?", body:"**Relief is likely.** At stage 1 the breach is **not serious or significant**, so although the reason (oversight) is poor, all the circumstances favour relief. The defendant should **agree rather than oppose**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Exam tips", cards:[
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"How do you work out the track in an exam question?", body:"Track questions are mainly arithmetic plus the trial-length and expert limits. Check **value**, then **trial length** (1 day fast, 3 days intermediate) and **experts** (fast: 1 per party per field, 2 fields; intermediate: 2 per party). A claim that **fails any limit moves up a track**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"How do you answer a relief from sanctions question?", body:"Apply **Denton** stage by stage, and remember stage 3 gives particular weight to **efficiency and proportionate cost** and to **enforcing compliance**. '**Pressure of work**' is not a good reason."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Do costs budgets apply on the intermediate track?", body:"**No.** **Costs budgeting** applies on the **multi-track** (claims under £10 million), not the intermediate track (which has fixed costs). A party that fails to file a budget recovers only its **court fees** for future costs unless granted relief."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Source: proposals/rooms/stops/fairground/build/room-dr-costs.js (built only from the dr-costs notes in src/content/dr.js) */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["dr/dr-costs"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What costs are", body:"**Costs** are the legal fees and expenses (e.g. court fees, experts' fees, barristers' fees) of conducting litigation. The court decides **whether** one party must pay another's costs, **how much**, and **when**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Loser pays, but the court decides", body:"The general rule is that **the loser pays the winner's costs**, but the court has a **wide discretion** and looks at **conduct** and **settlement offers**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Fixed or assessed", body:"The amount is either **fixed** (on the fast and intermediate tracks) or **assessed** (on the **standard** or **indemnity** basis). On the multi-track it is controlled by **costs budgets**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Part 36 and security for costs", body:"**Part 36 offers** are formal settlement offers that carry **automatic** costs consequences. **Security for costs** protects a defendant against a claimant who could not pay its costs."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Where the rules are", body:"**CPR Parts 36 and 44-47**, Part 25 (security) and **s.51 Senior Courts Act 1981**."}
 ]},
 {h:"The general rules (CPR 44.2)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The court's discretion", body:"The court has **discretion** whether costs are payable by one party to another, the **amount**, and **when** they are to be paid."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The general rule: loser pays", body:"The **unsuccessful party** pays the costs of the **successful party**. The court can make a **different order**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Conduct counts", body:"The court considers the **conduct** of all the parties, before and during the proceedings: e.g. compliance with **pre-action protocols** (the steps the parties should take before issuing a claim), whether it was reasonable to raise or contest a particular issue, the manner in which a party pursued or defended its case, **exaggeration** of a claim, and any **unreasonable refusal to engage in ADR** (alternative dispute resolution, e.g. mediation or negotiation) or failure to comply with an **ADR order**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Partial success", body:"Whether a party has succeeded on **part** of its case, even if not wholly successful (e.g. the court may award a winner only a **percentage** of its costs)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Offers to settle", body:"Any admissible **offer to settle** (including **Part 36** and **Calderbank** offers: a Calderbank offer is a 'without prejudice save as to costs' offer, explained below)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Kinds of order", body:"Orders include a **proportion** of costs, costs **from or until a certain date**, or costs of a **distinct part** of the proceedings."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Pay within 14 days (CPR 44.7)", body:"Costs must be paid within **14 days** of the judgment or order (or of the assessment of the amount) unless the court orders otherwise."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"The winner unreasonably refused to engage in ADR. Must the court still give it all its costs?", body:"**No.** The court looks at **conduct**, including any **unreasonable refusal to engage in ADR**, and can make a **different order** from 'loser pays'."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Small claims track: very limited costs (CPR 27.14)", body:"Recoverable costs are fixed **issue costs**, **court fees**, limited **witness expenses** (loss of earnings up to **£95 a day**) and **expert fees** (up to **£750**). Further costs only where a party has behaved **unreasonably**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A small claims winner paid its expert £1,000. How much of that fee can it recover?", body:"Up to **£750**: expert fees on the small claims track are capped at £750."}
 ]},
 {h:"The basis of assessment (CPR 44.3)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Standard basis: the normal one", body:"The **standard basis** is the **normal** basis."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Indemnity basis: when", body:"Ordered where a party's conduct is **'out of the norm'** (e.g. unreasonable, dishonest or abusive conduct), and under **Part 36** (claimant beats its own offer)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Proportionality", body:"**Standard**: costs must be **proportionate** to the matters in issue; disproportionate costs may be disallowed **even if reasonably incurred**. **Indemnity**: proportionality is **not** applied."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Benefit of the doubt", body:"**Standard**: doubt about whether costs were reasonably incurred or reasonable in amount is resolved in favour of the **paying party**. **Indemnity**: doubt is resolved in favour of the **receiving party**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The effect", body:"**Standard**: the receiving party usually recovers **noticeably less** than it has actually spent. **Indemnity**: it recovers **more** of its actual costs."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What 'proportionate' means (CPR 44.3(5))", body:"Costs are proportionate if they bear a reasonable relationship to the **sums in issue**, the value of any non-money relief, the **complexity** of the case, any additional work caused by the paying party's conduct, and any wider factors such as **reputation** or **public importance**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Never unreasonable costs", body:"On **either** basis, the court never allows costs that were **unreasonably incurred** or are **unreasonable in amount**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The CPR 44.4 factors", body:"Conduct, amount at stake, importance, complexity, skill and specialised knowledge, time spent, the place and circumstances in which the work was done, and the receiving party's last approved or agreed **budget**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"On which basis does a doubt about the costs go in favour of the party paying?", body:"The **standard** basis. On the indemnity basis, doubt goes to the **receiving** party."}
 ]},
 {h:"How the amount is decided: fixed recoverable costs (CPR 45)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Fixed recoverable costs (FRC)", body:"**Set amounts**, not the costs actually spent. They apply to **fast track** and **intermediate track** claims **issued on or after 1 October 2023** (with some transitional exceptions, e.g. certain PI claims where the accident occurred before that date)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Other steps with fixed costs", body:"Specific steps such as **default judgment** (judgment without a trial because the defendant did not respond in time) and judgment on **admission**, and low-value **PI** (personal injury) claims under the **portal** protocols."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Stage and band", body:"The amount depends on the **stage** the case reached (e.g. pre-issue, post-issue, post-allocation, trial) and its **complexity band** (**bands 1 to 4** on both the fast track and the intermediate track), plus a fixed **trial advocacy fee**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Escaping fixed costs", body:"Only in **exceptional circumstances** (claiming **at least 20% more** than FRC)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Up or down by 50%", body:"The court can **increase or reduce** FRC by **50%** for **unreasonable behaviour**."}
 ]},
 {h:"How the amount is decided: summary assessment", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Decided at the end of the hearing", body:"In a **summary assessment** the judge decides the amount **at the end of the hearing**, usually on paper statements of costs."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"When it is used: N260, 24 hours", body:"Normally for **interim applications** and other hearings lasting **one day or less** (outside fixed costs cases). Each party files and serves a **statement of costs** (form **N260**) at least **24 hours** before the hearing."}
 ]},
 {h:"How the amount is decided: detailed assessment (CPR 47)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A separate process", body:"After the case ends, a **costs officer or costs judge** examines the receiving party's **bill of costs**. Normal after a **multi-track** trial."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Start within 3 months", body:"The receiving party starts it by serving a **notice of commencement** and its **bill** within **3 months** of the judgment or order."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Points of dispute and reply: 21 days each", body:"The paying party serves **points of dispute** within **21 days**, and the receiving party may serve a **reply** within **21 days**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"£75,000 or less: on paper", body:"Bills of **£75,000 or less** are dealt with by **provisional assessment** on paper."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Payment on account (CPR 44.2(8))", body:"The court will usually order a reasonable **payment on account** of costs pending the assessment."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Costs budgets", body:"On the multi-track, if a **costs management order** (an order in which the court approves or sets each party's costs budget) has been made, the court will not depart from the approved **budget** on a standard basis assessment unless there is **good reason** (see **Case management, tracks and sanctions**)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"The receiving party has served its bill. How long does the paying party have to serve points of dispute?", body:"**21 days**. The receiving party then has **21 days** to reply."}
 ]},
 {h:"Interim costs orders", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Who pays for an application?", body:"When the court decides an **interim application**, it decides who pays the costs of that application. The common orders are in **PD 44**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Costs in any event", body:"The party in whose favour the order is made gets the costs of the application **whatever the final outcome**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Costs in the case (or costs in the application)", body:"Whoever is ordered to pay costs at the **end of the case** also pays the costs of this application."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Claimant's (or defendant's) costs in the case", body:"That party gets its costs of the application **if it wins at the end**; if it loses, it does **not** pay the other side's costs of the application."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Costs reserved", body:"The decision is **postponed**; if no later order is made, they become **costs in the case**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Costs thrown away", body:"The costs **wasted** because of a step that has to be undone (e.g. a set-aside default judgment, an amendment), including the costs of the undone work."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Costs of and caused by", body:"Costs of preparing for and attending the application, **plus** costs of consequential steps (e.g. amending other statements of case after an amendment)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Costs here and below", body:"Costs in the **appeal court** and the **lower court**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No order as to costs", body:"**Each party bears its own** costs of the application."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: a late amendment", body:"The claimant successfully applies to amend its particulars of claim late. The usual order is that the **claimant pays the defendant's 'costs of and caused by'** the amendment, because the amendment was needed to cure the claimant's own omission."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"The defendant wins an application and the court orders 'costs in the case'. The claimant then wins at trial. Who pays the costs of that application?", body:"The **defendant**. 'Costs in the case' follow the **final result**: whoever pays costs at the end also pays for this application."}
 ]},
 {h:"Qualified one-way costs shifting (QOCS) in personal injury (CPR 44.13-44.17)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What QOCS does", body:"**QOCS** (qualified one-way costs shifting) protects **claimants in personal injury claims** from paying the defendant's costs if they lose."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Only up to what the claimant won", body:"A costs order against the claimant **can be enforced** without the court's permission only up to the **total** of any **damages**, **interest** and **costs** orders made in the claimant's favour."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Since 6 April 2023", body:"That total includes **sums under a settlement** and **costs orders** in the claimant's favour. So a claimant who **loses outright** usually **pays nothing**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Protection lost: struck out", body:"The costs order can be enforced **in full** where the claim is **struck out** (ended by the court without a trial) because it disclosed **no reasonable grounds**, was an **abuse of process** (misusing the court's procedures), or the claimant's conduct was likely to obstruct the just disposal of the proceedings."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Protection lost: fundamentally dishonest", body:"Also (with the court's **permission**) where the claim is found, on the balance of probabilities, to be **fundamentally dishonest** (e.g. grossly exaggerated injuries)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: Lena's £40,000", body:"Lena's PI claim fails at trial and she is ordered to pay the defendant's costs of **£40,000**. She recovered nothing. Under QOCS the defendant **cannot enforce** the order, unless the claim was **struck out** or found **fundamentally dishonest**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Non-party costs and wasted costs", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Non-party costs orders (s.51)", body:"The court may order a person who is **not a party** to pay costs (**Senior Courts Act 1981 s.51**), e.g. a **litigation funder** (a business that pays for a claim in return for a share of any winnings), an insurer, or a **director** or shareholder who **controlled and funded** the litigation for its own benefit (e.g. behind an insolvent company claimant). It is exceptional but common where the non-party was the **'real party'**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A fair chance to attend (CPR 46.2)", body:"The non-party must be **added as a party** for the purposes of costs only and given a **reasonable opportunity to attend** a hearing."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Wasted costs orders (s.51(6), CPR 46.8)", body:"The court may order a party's **legal representative personally** to pay costs incurred as a result of its **improper, unreasonable or negligent** act or omission."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The representative is heard first", body:"The representative must be given an opportunity to **make representations**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Part 36 offers", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What Part 36 is", body:"**Part 36** is a self-contained procedure for making formal settlement offers that carry **automatic costs and interest consequences** if they are not accepted and the **offeree** (the party the offer is made to) fails to do better at trial."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Who, when, and not small claims", body:"It does **not** apply on the **small claims track**. **Either party** can make one, **before or after** proceedings start."}
 ]},
 {h:"Part 36: formal requirements (CPR 36.5)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"In writing", body:"A Part 36 offer must be in **writing**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Says it is under Part 36", body:"It must **make clear** that it is made **pursuant to Part 36**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A relevant period of at least 21 days", body:"It must specify a period of **not less than 21 days** (the **relevant period**) within which the defendant will be liable for the claimant's costs if the offer is accepted. Not needed for an offer made **less than 21 days before trial**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Whole, part or issue", body:"It must state whether it relates to the **whole claim** or to **part** of it or an **issue**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Counterclaim", body:"It must state whether it takes into account any **counterclaim**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A defendant's money offer (CPR 36.6)", body:"A **defendant's** offer to settle a money claim must be an offer to pay a **single sum of money**, to be paid within **14 days** of acceptance."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A defendant makes a 'Part 36' offer to pay £30,000 over 28 days. Is it a valid Part 36 offer?", body:"**No.** A defendant's money offer must be a **single sum** paid within **14 days** of acceptance."}
 ]},
 {h:"Part 36: secrecy", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Kept from the trial judge (CPR 36.16)", body:"The fact or terms of a Part 36 offer must **not be communicated to the trial judge** until all questions of **liability** (whether the defendant is legally responsible) and **quantum** (how much it must pay) have been decided, unless the parties agree or the offer is relevant to an earlier issue."}
 ]},
 {h:"Part 36: withdrawing or changing an offer", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Withdrawing or worsening", body:"By **written notice**. Notice served **during** the relevant period takes effect only at the **end** of that period, and the offeree can still accept before then. After the relevant period it takes effect **on service**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A withdrawn offer", body:"A withdrawn offer **loses its Part 36 consequences**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Improving an offer", body:"An offer can be made **more** advantageous **at any time**. This counts as a **new offer** with a **new relevant period**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Part 36: acceptance", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Accept by written notice", body:"An offer can be accepted **at any time** (unless withdrawn) by **written notice**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Acceptance within the relevant period (CPR 36.13)", body:"The claim is **stayed** (paused) on the terms of the offer, and the **claimant** gets its **costs up to the date of acceptance** on the **standard basis**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Late acceptance of a defendant's offer (CPR 36.13(5))", body:"If costs are not agreed, the court will, unless it considers it **unjust**, order that the claimant gets its costs up to the **end of the relevant period** and **pays the defendant's costs** from then **until acceptance**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Not a late-acceptance consequence", body:"**Interest on costs** is a CPR 36.17 consequence after judgment, **not** a consequence of late acceptance."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: 1 March, 22 March, 30 April", body:"On **1 March** the defendant makes a Part 36 offer of **£50,000** with a 21-day relevant period (ending **22 March**). The claimant accepts on **30 April**. The claimant recovers its costs **up to 22 March**, but pays the defendant's costs **from 22 March to 30 April**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Part 36: consequences at trial (CPR 36.17)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Defendant's offer not beaten", body:"The claimant **fails to obtain a judgment more advantageous** than the defendant's offer: the claimant pays the **defendant's costs** from the **end of the relevant period**, plus **interest** on those costs. (The claimant usually still gets its own costs up to that date.)"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Claimant's offer matched or beaten: four consequences", body:"The judgment is **at least as advantageous** to the claimant as its own offer: the defendant pays four things, **unless the court considers it unjust**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(a) Interest on the damages", body:"**Interest** on the damages at up to **10% above base rate** (the Bank of England base rate) from the end of the relevant period."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(b) Indemnity costs", body:"The claimant's costs on the **indemnity basis** from that date."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(c) Interest on those costs", body:"**Interest on those costs** at up to **10% above base rate**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"(d) An additional amount", body:"An **additional amount** of **10%** of the damages awarded up to **£500,000**, plus **5%** of any amount above that, capped at **£75,000** in total."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"When it would be unjust (CPR 36.17(5))", body:"Factors include the **terms** of the offer, the **stage** at which it was made, the **information** available to the parties, the parties' **conduct** in giving information, and whether the offer was a **genuine attempt to settle**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Better by any amount (CPR 36.17(2))", body:"'**More advantageous**' means better in money terms by **any amount**, however small."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Fixed costs cases (CPR 36.24)", body:"On the fast and intermediate tracks, a claimant who beats its own offer gets, instead of indemnity costs, **35%** of the difference between the fixed costs at the stage the relevant period expired and at the stage of judgment (plus the interest and additional amount). A defendant who beats its offer gets its **fixed costs** from the end of the relevant period."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"The defendant offered £50,000. The claimant is awarded £50,001. Did the claimant beat the offer?", body:"**Yes.** Better by **any amount**, however small, counts."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Worked example: offer £80,000, won £90,000", body:"A claimant makes a Part 36 offer to accept **£80,000**; the defendant ignores it. At trial the claimant is awarded **£90,000**. The judgment is **at least as advantageous** as the claimant's offer, so the defendant pays **enhanced interest**, **indemnity costs** from the end of the relevant period, **interest on those costs**, and an **additional amount** of **£9,000** (10% of £90,000)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"In that example, why is the additional amount £9,000?", body:"It is **10%** of damages up to £500,000: 10% of **£90,000** is **£9,000**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Part 36: other (non-Part 36) offers", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Calderbank offers", body:"A '**without prejudice save as to costs**' offer (a **Calderbank** offer) is hidden from the judge until costs are decided. The court takes it into account under its general discretion (CPR 44.2(4)(c)), but it carries **no automatic** Part 36 consequences."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Offers that miss the formalities", body:"An offer that fails the Part 36 formalities (e.g. a defendant's offer **payable over 28 days**, or a relevant period of **14 days**) is **not** a Part 36 offer, but may still be relevant to costs as an **admissible offer**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Security for costs (CPR 25)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What security for costs is", body:"An order requiring a **claimant** (or a defendant bringing a **counterclaim**) to pay money **into court** or provide a **guarantee** or bond, so that, if the defendant wins, its costs will be paid."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Only a defendant can ask", body:"Only a **defendant** (or a party defending a counterclaim) can apply. It **cannot** be ordered against a defendant simply defending a claim."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The test: just, plus a condition", body:"The court may order security if it is satisfied, having regard to all the circumstances, that it is **just** to do so, **and** one or more of the **conditions** applies."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Condition: resident out of the jurisdiction", body:"The claimant lives anywhere **outside England and Wales**. The old exception for claimants in **2005 Hague Convention** states was removed when Part 25 was rewritten from **6 April 2025**. Whether an order is **just** then depends mainly on how hard it would be to **enforce** a costs order where the claimant lives (e.g. residence in a **2019 Hague Judgments Convention** state weighs against security)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Condition: a company that may not pay", body:"The claimant is a **company or other body** (anywhere) and there is **reason to believe** it will be **unable to pay** the defendant's costs if ordered to do so."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Condition: moved to evade", body:"The claimant has **changed its address** since the claim started with a view to **evading** the consequences of the litigation."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Condition: no address or a wrong one", body:"The claimant **failed to give its address**, or gave an **incorrect address**, in the claim form."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Condition: a nominal claimant", body:"The claimant is acting as a **nominal claimant** (suing for someone else's benefit), and there is reason to believe it will be **unable to pay** the defendant's costs."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Condition: hiding assets", body:"The claimant has taken steps in relation to its **assets** that would make it **difficult to enforce** a costs order against it."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Also against a funder or assignee", body:"Security can also be ordered against a **non-party** who has **funded** the claim in return for a **share of the proceeds**, or to whom the claim has been **assigned** to avoid costs liability."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What the court weighs", body:"E.g. whether the claimant's lack of funds was **caused by the defendant's conduct**, whether an order would **stifle** a genuine claim, the **strength** of the claim, and the **timing** of the application (it should be made **promptly**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"If security is not given", body:"The claim is usually **stayed**, often with an order that it will be **struck out** if security is not given by a set date."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: a new company with no assets", body:"A **newly formed company with no assets** sues a supplier for **£200,000**. The supplier **can apply** for security for costs, because there is reason to believe the company will be unable to pay its costs if the claim fails."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A poor individual in England", body:"An **individual** claimant living in England who is **simply poor cannot** be ordered to give security on that ground."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Watch out: claimants only, companies and nominal claimants", body:"Security is ordered **against a claimant** (or counterclaiming defendant), **never** against a defendant just defending. An **individual** resident in England and Wales cannot be ordered to give security merely because they are poor; the impecuniosity (lack of money) condition applies to **companies** and **nominal** claimants."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exam tip: who made it, and was it beaten?", body:"Part 36 questions turn on **who made the offer** and **whether the judgment beat it**. A **claimant's** offer **matched or beaten** by the judgment triggers the four enhanced consequences against the defendant; a **defendant's** offer that the claimant **fails to beat** makes the claimant pay the defendant's costs after the relevant period."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exam tip: interim costs orders precisely", body:"'**Costs in the case**' depends on the final result; '**costs in any event**' does not; '**claimant's costs in the case**' means the claimant gets them only if it wins and pays nothing for the application if it loses."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Fairground room 12 (The Tombola). Source: notes for topic dr-disclosure in src/content/dr.js, in notes order (the two exam tips and the watch-out sit in the sections the plan's object map ties them to). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["dr/dr-disclosure"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Disclosure", body:"**Disclosure** is the stage where each party tells the others which relevant **documents** exist or have existed, including documents that **harm its own case**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Inspection", body:"**Inspection** is the right to **see copies** of those documents."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Why disclosure matters", body:"Disclosure stops cases being won by **hiding evidence** and helps the parties assess their **prospects**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Some documents stay sealed", body:"Some documents can be **withheld from inspection**, mainly because they are **privileged**: **legal advice privilege**, **litigation privilege**, and communications made **without prejudice** in an attempt to settle."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Where the rules are", body:"**CPR Part 31**, **PD 31B** (electronic documents) and, in the Business and Property Courts, **PD 57AD**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Key definitions (CPR 31)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Disclosure (CPR 31.2)", body:"Stating that a document **exists or has existed**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Inspection", body:"The right of the other party to **see** (usually receive a copy of) a disclosed document."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Document (CPR 31.4)", body:"**Anything in which information of any description is recorded.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What counts as a document", body:"Paper documents, **emails**, **texts and instant messages**, voicemails, photographs, spreadsheets, databases, **metadata** (data about a file, e.g. who created or edited it and when), and documents stored on phones, computers, servers or the cloud, including **deleted files that can be recovered**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Control (CPR 31.8)", body:"A party must disclose documents that are or have been in its **control**: in its **physical possession**; or it has (or had) a **right to possession** of them (e.g. files held by its agent or accountant); or it has (or had) a **right to inspect or take copies** of them."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A continuing duty (CPR 31.11)", body:"The duty of disclosure is **continuing** until the proceedings end: a party who finds a further relevant document must disclose it **promptly**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Preservation", body:"Once litigation is in contemplation, parties must **preserve** relevant documents, including **suspending routine deletion** of emails and data. Solicitors must tell clients this at the outset."}
 ]},
 {h:"What disclosure is ordered: by track", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Small claims track", body:"**No standard disclosure.** Each party sends copies of documents it **intends to rely on**, usually at least **14 days** before the hearing."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Fast track", body:"Usually **standard disclosure** (often by list), as directed."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Intermediate track (except personal injury)", body:"The same **menu** as the multi-track (CPR 31.5(2), since **6 April 2024**), with the court choosing an order **proportionate** to the claim and its complexity band."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Multi-track (except personal injury)", body:"The court chooses from a **menu** (CPR 31.5)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Business and Property Courts", body:"**PD 57AD**: **Initial Disclosure**, then **Extended Disclosure** Models A to E."}
 ]},
 {h:"The disclosure menu (CPR 31.5): multi-track and intermediate track", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The disclosure report (form N263)", body:"Before the first CMC (case management conference, if there is one), each party files and serves a **disclosure report** describing what documents exist, where and how they are stored, and the estimated cost of disclosure, at least **14 days** before the first CMC."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Agree a proposal", body:"The parties must try to **agree a proposal** for disclosure at least **7 days** before the CMC."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The menu (1)", body:"The court can order **no disclosure**; disclosure of documents each party **relies on**, plus **specific requests**; or disclosure **issue by issue**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The menu (2)", body:"Or **standard disclosure**; or wide \"**train of inquiry**\" disclosure: documents that may lead to a train of inquiry which may advance the party's own case or damage the other's (like the old \"Peruvian Guano\" test); or **any other order** it considers appropriate."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Personal injury on the multi-track", body:"**Personal injury** multi-track claims usually have **standard disclosure**."}
 ]},
 {h:"PD 57AD (Business and Property Courts)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Initial Disclosure", body:"With its statement of case, each party serves the **key documents** it relies on and that are needed for the other parties to understand the case (with exceptions, e.g. where it would exceed about **1,000 pages**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Extended Disclosure", body:"Ordered **issue by issue**, using a **Disclosure Review Document**, with five **Models**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Model A", body:"Disclosure confined to **known adverse documents**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Model B", body:"**Limited disclosure**: key documents relied on and needed to understand the case, plus known adverse documents."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Model C", body:"**Request-led**, search-based disclosure of particular documents or narrow classes."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Model D", body:"**Narrow search-based** disclosure: documents likely to support or adversely affect a party's case."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Model E", body:"**Wide search-based** disclosure including **train of inquiry** documents (**exceptional**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Known adverse documents, always", body:"Under **every** Model, a party must disclose **known adverse documents** (unless privileged)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Standard disclosure (CPR 31.6)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What standard disclosure covers", body:"A party must disclose the documents on which it **relies**; documents which **adversely affect its own case**; documents which **adversely affect another party's case**; documents which **support another party's case**; and any documents required by a **practice direction**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A reasonable search (CPR 31.7)", body:"A party must make a **reasonable search** for documents in the last three categories. Reasonableness depends on the **number** of documents, the **nature and complexity** of the proceedings, the **ease and expense** of retrieval, and the **significance** of any document likely to be found."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A limited search must be declared", body:"A party that limits its search (e.g. by **date range** or **keywords**) must **say so**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The list of documents (form N265)", body:"Each party serves a list in **three parts**: documents in its control it **does not object** to being inspected; documents in its control it **objects to being inspected** (e.g. **privileged** documents), with the grounds; and documents that **were** in its control but no longer are, and what happened to them."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The disclosure statement", body:"The list includes a **disclosure statement**, signed by the **party** (not just the solicitor), setting out the extent of the search and certifying that the party **understands the duty** of disclosure and has carried it out to the best of its knowledge."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A false disclosure statement", body:"A false disclosure statement made without an honest belief in its truth is **contempt of court**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Inspection within 7 days (CPR 31.15)", body:"A party wishing to inspect gives **written notice**, and the other party must allow inspection **within 7 days**, usually by providing copies on payment of reasonable copying costs."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Documents referred to (CPR 31.14)", body:"Documents referred to in **statements of case**, **witness statements** or **expert reports** can be inspected at any time on request, subject to privilege."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Collateral use (CPR 31.22)", body:"A disclosed document may be used **only for the purpose of the proceedings** in which it was disclosed, unless it has been read to or by the court at a **public hearing**, the court **permits**, or the owner **agrees**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"In a faulty machinery claim, the supplier finds its engineer's email: \"these units have a known overheating problem\". Must it disclose it?", body:"**Yes**, under standard disclosure, because it **adversely affects its own case**. Failing to do so could lead to **sanctions** and an **adverse inference** (the court may assume the worst about what was hidden), and the solicitor **cannot continue to act** if the client refuses to disclose it."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: can a harmful document be left off the list because it is confidential or commercially sensitive?", body:"**No.** Under standard disclosure a party must disclose documents that **harm its own case**. **Confidentiality alone is not privilege.**"}
 ]},
 {h:"Specific, pre-action and non-party disclosure", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Specific disclosure (CPR 31.12)", body:"An order for a party to disclose specified documents or classes, carry out a further **search**, or disclose documents found. Used where a party believes the other's disclosure is **inadequate**. The court considers the overriding objective and **proportionality**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Pre-action disclosure (CPR 31.16)", body:"Before proceedings start. The applicant and respondent are both **likely to be parties**; the documents would fall within **standard disclosure** if proceedings had started; and early disclosure is **desirable** to dispose fairly of the anticipated proceedings, **assist the dispute to be resolved without proceedings**, or **save costs**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Non-party disclosure (CPR 31.17)", body:"After proceedings start. The documents are **likely to support the applicant's case or adversely affect another party's case**; and disclosure is **necessary** to dispose fairly of the claim or to **save costs**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Asking a non-party for documents (CPR 31.12A)", body:"From **6 April 2026**, the court may direct a **party** to ask a non-party (e.g. a former employee or an agent) to provide documents that may support or adversely affect any party's case. The order is against the **party**; the non-party is **not compelled**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Applying, and who pays", body:"Applications are made under **Part 23** with evidence. The **applicant usually pays the respondent's costs** of pre-action and non-party applications and of complying, unless the court orders otherwise."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Before suing, a patient wants her hospital records to see whether she has a negligence claim. What can she do?", body:"**Apply for pre-action disclosure** (CPR 31.16): she and the hospital are likely to be parties, the records would fall within standard disclosure, and early disclosure may avoid proceedings altogether."}
 ]},
 {h:"Electronic disclosure (PD 31B)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Where PD 31B applies", body:"**Multi-track** cases outside the Business and Property Courts (which use PD 57AD)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Discuss before the first CMC", body:"The parties must **discuss** electronic documents: the categories that exist, where they are stored, **preservation**, the scope of reasonable searches (**date ranges**, **custodians** (the people who hold the documents), **keyword searches**, technology-assisted review, where software helps sort the documents), formats for exchange and the costs."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Electronic Documents Questionnaire", body:"It can be exchanged to gather this information."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Preserve electronic documents", body:"A party must **preserve** electronic documents once litigation is contemplated, including **suspending automatic deletion** policies."}
 ]},
 {h:"Withholding inspection", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"List it, but refuse inspection", body:"A party must **list** relevant documents but can **refuse to allow inspection** on these grounds."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Privileged", body:"The most common ground. Privilege must be **claimed** in the list, describing the documents generally and stating the ground."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No longer in control", body:"The document is **no longer in the party's control**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Disproportionate (CPR 31.3(2))", body:"Inspection would be **disproportionate** to the issues, for some categories of documents."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Public interest immunity (CPR 31.19)", body:"Applies e.g. for **national security**, and needs a **court order**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Privilege", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What privilege is", body:"A right to **withhold a document from inspection** by the other side, even though it is relevant and **must be listed**. It belongs to the **client**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Legal advice privilege", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What it protects", body:"**Confidential** communications between a **client** and its **lawyer** (solicitors, barristers, in-house lawyers, foreign lawyers) made for the **dominant purpose** of giving or receiving **legal advice**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No litigation needed", body:"**Litigation need not be contemplated**: it applies to any legal advice, e.g. advice on a contract or a property purchase."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Three Rivers DC v Bank of England (No 6) [2004]", body:"\"Legal advice\" is **broad**: it includes advice about what should **prudently and sensibly be done in the relevant legal context**, not just advice on the law."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Three Rivers (No 5) [2003]: who is the client?", body:"In a company, the \"**client**\" is only the employees **authorised to seek and receive** legal advice on its behalf, not every employee. Communications from other employees to the lawyer may not be covered."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Not third parties", body:"Legal advice privilege does **not** cover communications with **third parties** (e.g. an expert or a witness)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"R (Prudential plc) v Special Commissioner of Income Tax [2013]", body:"It does not extend to **non-lawyers** giving legal advice, such as **accountants advising on tax**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Litigation privilege", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What it protects", body:"**Confidential** communications between the **lawyer or client** and a **third party** (e.g. an expert, a witness, an investigator), or between lawyer and client."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"When and why", body:"Made when **litigation is reasonably in contemplation** (not just a mere possibility), for the **dominant purpose** of that litigation (obtaining advice, evidence or information for it)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Adversarial litigation", body:"The litigation must be **adversarial** (not merely investigative)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Waugh v British Railways Board [1980]", body:"After a fatal rail crash, the railway prepared an internal accident report for two purposes: railway **safety** and legal advice for anticipated **litigation**. It was **not privileged**, because litigation was not the **dominant** purpose. Rule: a document prepared **equally** for business or safety reasons and for litigation is not privileged."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"SFO v Eurasian Natural Resources Corp [2018]", body:"Criminal prosecution could be reasonably in contemplation once an **SFO (Serious Fraud Office) investigation was likely**, and documents created by lawyers investigating to avoid or resist that litigation could attract **litigation privilege**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Comparing the two", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Communications between", body:"Legal advice privilege: client and **lawyer** only. Litigation privilege: lawyer or client and **third parties** (and lawyer and client)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Litigation contemplated?", body:"Legal advice privilege: **not needed**. Litigation privilege: must be **reasonably in contemplation** (adversarial)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Dominant purpose", body:"Legal advice privilege: giving or receiving **legal advice**. Litigation privilege: conducting the **litigation**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Examples", body:"Legal advice privilege: a solicitor's advice on whether a **lease** can be terminated. Litigation privilege: an **expert report** commissioned after a claim is threatened, to advise on liability."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"After a customer threatens to sue, a company's solicitors instruct an engineer to report on why a boiler exploded. Is the report privileged?", body:"**Yes, litigation privilege**: third-party communication, litigation contemplated, dominant purpose the claim. If the company had commissioned the same report mainly for its own **safety review** before any threat of a claim, it would **not** be privileged."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: a communication is with a third party. What must you ask?", body:"Only **litigation privilege** can protect it, so ask: was litigation **reasonably in contemplation**, and was the litigation the **dominant purpose**? A report prepared equally for safety or business reasons fails (**Waugh**)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Without prejudice communications", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Without prejudice (WP)", body:"Written or oral communications made in a **genuine attempt to settle an existing dispute**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Rush & Tompkins Ltd v Greater London Council [1989]", body:"Without prejudice communications **cannot be used as evidence** of liability at trial and the **judge must not be told** about them."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Substance, not the label", body:"A letter marked \"without prejudice\" that is **not a genuine attempt to settle** is not protected, and an **unlabelled** genuine settlement offer may be."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Belongs to both parties", body:"The protection belongs to **both parties** jointly; one party **cannot waive it alone**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Mediation", body:"**Mediation** communications are protected in the same way."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exceptions: Unilever plc v Procter & Gamble Co [2000]", body:"Without prejudice material **can be admitted** in some cases, including the six that follow."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Was a settlement reached?", body:"To decide whether a **settlement was actually reached** (and its terms)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Setting a settlement aside", body:"To show that a settlement should be set aside for **misrepresentation, fraud or undue influence** (improper pressure)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Unambiguous impropriety", body:"Where excluding it would allow **unambiguous impropriety** (e.g. **blackmail**, **threats**, **perjury**, i.e. lying on oath)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Explaining delay", body:"To explain **delay** or apparent acquiescence (seeming to accept the position) (e.g. on a strike-out for delay)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Estoppel", body:"Where a clear statement was made on which the other party was intended to act and **did act** (**estoppel**: a party cannot go back on a statement the other relied on)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"\"Without prejudice save as to costs\" (Calderbank offers)", body:"Hidden from the judge on **liability** but **can be shown on costs** once the case is decided. Named after **Calderbank v Calderbank** [1975], a family case approving this type of offer."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Calderbank offers and Part 36", body:"The court takes them into account under its general costs discretion (**CPR 44.2**), but they do **not** carry the automatic consequences of a **Part 36** offer (see **Costs**)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"In a WP meeting the defendant's director says \"we know the goods were defective, but we can only pay half\". Can the claimant use that at trial?", body:"**No**: it was made in a genuine attempt to settle. If the director had **threatened** to destroy the claimant's business unless it accepted, the **unambiguous impropriety** exception might apply."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Watch out", body:"Without prejudice material is hidden from the judge on **liability**, but a \"without prejudice **save as to costs**\" offer can be shown to the judge on **costs** after judgment. And WP protection depends on a **genuine attempt to settle an existing dispute**, not the label."}
 ]},
 {h:"Waiver and loss of privilege", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Only the client can waive", body:"Privilege belongs to the **client**, and only the client can **waive** it (give it up)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Express or deliberate waiver", body:"Deliberately **disclosing** a privileged document, or **relying on its contents** (e.g. quoting legal advice in a witness statement or pleading to justify conduct)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Collateral waiver", body:"Waiving privilege over part of a document or a series of documents may waive privilege over **related** material, so the other side is not given a misleading, **cherry-picked** picture."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Limited waiver", body:"Sharing a privileged document with a third party **in confidence** for a limited purpose (e.g. with an **insurer** or a **regulator**) does not necessarily waive privilege against the world."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Inadvertent disclosure (CPR 31.20)", body:"If a privileged document is mistakenly made available for inspection, the receiving party may use it **only with the court's permission**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Al Fayed v Commissioner of Police for the Metropolis [2002]", body:"The court will usually grant an **injunction** preventing use where the mistake would have been **obvious** to a reasonable solicitor. A solicitor who receives a document obviously disclosed by mistake should **return it unread** and tell the other side."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The iniquity exception", body:"Privilege does not protect documents that further a **crime or fraud**."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Source: src/content/dr.js topic dr-enforcement (Fairground room 16, the Coconut Shy). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["dr/dr-enforcement"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A judgment isn't money in the pocket", body:"Winning a judgment does not put money in the client's pocket. If the loser does not pay, the winner must **enforce** the judgment, and the court will **not** do this automatically: the **judgment creditor** (the party owed the money) must **choose a method and apply for it**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The method depends on the assets", body:"The right method depends on **what assets the judgment debtor has**: goods, money in a bank account, land, or a salary."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Don't know what they own? Ask first", body:"If the creditor does not know what the debtor owns, it can first get an **order to obtain information** (the SRA specification now calls this '**obtaining information from judgment debtors**'; it used to be called an '**oral examination**')."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Where the rules are", body:"**CPR Parts 70 to 73 and 83-84**, the **Tribunals, Courts and Enforcement Act 2007**, the **Charging Orders Act 1979** and the **Attachment of Earnings Act 1971**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Before enforcing", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Judgment creditor and judgment debtor", body:"**Judgment creditor**: the party entitled to the money. **Judgment debtor**: the party who must pay."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Pay within 14 days", body:"A money judgment must normally be paid within **14 days** unless the court orders otherwise, e.g. payment by **instalments** (CPR 40.11). The creditor **cannot enforce** while the debtor is keeping to an instalment order."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Interest at 8%", body:"**Interest** runs on judgment debts at **8%** a year (Judgments Act 1838): on **all High Court** judgments, and on County Court judgments of **£5,000 or more** (or under the Late Payment of Commercial Debts Act)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Is it worth it? Investigate first", body:"**Before spending money on enforcement**, the creditor should investigate the debtor's means. Searches (Land Registry, Companies House, credit reference agencies, the Register of Judgments) help, and an **order to obtain information** can compel disclosure."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Which court? £5,000 or more by taking control of goods", body:"(High Court and County Courts Jurisdiction Order 1991.) Judgments of **£5,000 or more** to be enforced by **taking control of goods** must be enforced in the **High Court**, unless the debt arises under a **regulated consumer credit agreement** (a consumer loan or credit deal covered by the Consumer Credit Act 1974)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Which court? Under £5,000", body:"Judgments from **£600 to £4,999.99**: either court. Judgments **under £600**: **County Court** only."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Transfer up; attachment stays in the County Court", body:"A County Court judgment can be **transferred** to the High Court for enforcement. **Attachment of earnings** is a **County Court** remedy only."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Mix and match methods", body:"Several methods can be used at the same time or one after another (**CPR 70.2**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The debtor can push back", body:"The debtor can apply to **suspend or stay** (pause) enforcement (e.g. to pay by instalments) or to **set aside** (cancel) the judgment."}
 ]},
 {h:"Order to obtain information (CPR 71)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Purpose: turn the lights on", body:"To find out about the debtor's **means and assets** so the creditor can choose a method of enforcement."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Applying: N316, without notice", body:"The creditor applies **without notice** (without first telling the debtor) (form **N316**, or **N316A** for an officer of a company). The application is dealt with by a **court officer** without a hearing."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Attend, answer on oath, bring documents", body:"The order requires the **debtor** (or, for a company, an **officer** such as a director) to **attend court** and:\n- answer questions **on oath** about their **means** (income, property, debts, bank accounts) and anything else needed to enforce; and\n- **produce documents** (e.g. bank statements, pay slips, accounts)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Who asks the questions", body:"Questioning is by a **court officer** (using standard questions), or by a **judge** if the creditor shows a compelling reason. The creditor may **attend and ask questions**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Serve it personally, 14 days before", body:"The order must be **served personally** on the debtor at least **14 days** before the hearing."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Travel money", body:"The debtor may, within **7 days** of service, ask the creditor for a sum reasonably sufficient for **travel expenses** to and from court, which the creditor must pay."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The creditor's affidavit, 2 days before", body:"The creditor must file an **affidavit** (a written statement sworn on oath) confirming service and payment of any travel expenses, and stating how much of the debt is still unpaid, at least **2 days** before the hearing."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Don't come? Suspended committal order", body:"**Failure to attend**, take the oath or answer questions: the judge may make a **suspended committal order** (an order sending the debtor to prison for contempt of court, suspended so long as the debtor attends a further hearing and complies)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A creditor has a £12,000 judgment against a self-employed builder but no idea what he owns. First step?", body:"**Apply for an order to obtain information**, then choose the most suitable method once it knows what he owns."}
 ]},
 {h:"Taking control of goods (writ or warrant of control)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Writ or warrant of control", body:"**Writ of control**: issued in the **High Court** and executed by a **High Court Enforcement Officer** (HCEO, an authorised private officer). **Warrant of control**: issued in the **County Court** and executed by **County Court bailiffs** (civil enforcement officers)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Notice of enforcement: 14 clear days", body:"(Tribunals, Courts and Enforcement Act 2007 Sch 12 and the **Taking Control of Goods Regulations 2013**.) The enforcement agent must give the debtor a **notice of enforcement** at least **14 clear days** before taking control. This was raised from 7 clear days for enforcement started on or after **1 May 2026**. It is extended to **28 clear days** if a **debt adviser** asks on the debtor's behalf before the notice period ends, but **not for business debts**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Entering and taking control", body:"The agent can then enter the debtor's premises (usually only **peaceably**, during **permitted hours**) and **take control** of goods, either by **removing** them or, more commonly, by a **controlled goods agreement** (the debtor keeps the goods but agrees not to dispose of them and to pay the debt by an agreed schedule)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Sale at auction", body:"If the debt is still not paid, the goods can be **sold**, usually at **public auction**, after notice. The proceeds pay the **enforcement costs**, then the **debt**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exempt: things needed for work or study, up to £1,350", body:"Items **necessary for use personally** by the debtor in their **employment, business, trade, profession, study or education** (tools, books, phones, computer equipment, vehicles), up to an aggregate value of **£1,350**, cannot be taken."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exempt: basic domestic needs", body:"Items necessary for satisfying the **basic domestic needs** of the debtor and their household (e.g. cooker, fridge, beds, bedding, clothing, a table and chairs for each member of the household) cannot be taken."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Someone else's goods can't be taken", body:"**Third party goods** cannot be taken: goods owned by someone else, such as goods on **hire purchase** (bought on instalments and owned by the finance company until fully paid) or leased, or belonging to a spouse or partner."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"When it suits", body:"**Suitable** where the debtor has valuable goods or a business with stock or equipment. **Unsuitable** where the debtor has few goods, or the goods are exempt or owned by others."}
 ]},
 {h:"Third party debt order (CPR 72)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Purpose: money owed to the debtor", body:"To capture money **owed to the debtor by a third party**, almost always a credit balance in the debtor's **bank or building society account**. The third party then pays the creditor directly."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Stage 1: interim order freezes the account", body:"An **interim third party debt order** is made by a judge on the creditor's application **without notice** (so the debtor cannot move the money). It **freezes** the money in the account up to the amount of the judgment debt and costs."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Serve the bank 21 days before", body:"The interim order is served on the **bank** at least **21 days** before the final hearing, then on the **debtor**. The bank must search for accounts and tell the court and creditor what it has frozen."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Stage 2: final hearing, 28 days on", body:"The **final hearing** is at least **28 days** after the interim order. The court decides whether to make the order **final**, after hearing any objections from the debtor or the bank."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Hardship payment order", body:"The debtor (an individual) can apply for a **hardship payment order** releasing money if the freeze prevents them meeting ordinary living expenses."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Only what's there when served", body:"It only catches money in the account **when the interim order is served**. It does **not** catch future deposits or overdrawn accounts."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Joint accounts", body:"**Joint accounts** are not caught unless the judgment is against **all** the account holders."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"When it suits", body:"**Suitable** where the debtor has a **bank account in credit**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Charging order (CPR 73 and Charging Orders Act 1979)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Purpose: secure the debt on land", body:"To **secure** the judgment debt on the debtor's **land** (e.g. their house) or securities (shares), like a mortgage. It does **not itself produce money**; it turns the creditor into a **secured** creditor."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Stage 1: interim order, registered", body:"An **interim charging order** is made **without notice** (usually by a court officer). It should be **protected** by registration: for registered land, a **notice** or **restriction** at **HM Land Registry**, so the property cannot be sold without the debt being dealt with."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Stage 2: final hearing", body:"The court decides whether to make the order **final**, taking into account all the circumstances, including any other creditors and the debtor's family."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Order for sale: Part 8, court's discretion", body:"If the debt is still unpaid, the creditor can apply (usually by a separate **Part 8** claim: a simpler claim procedure used where the facts are not really in dispute) for an **order for sale** of the property so the debt is paid from the proceeds. The court has a **discretion** and will consider the interests of the debtor's family and any co-owners."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Consumer credit: no sale under £1,000", body:"Where the debt arises under a **regulated consumer credit agreement**, no order for sale can be made if the debt is under **£1,000**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Consumer credit: not in default, no charging order", body:"A charging order **cannot be made** where the debtor is **not in default** under an instalment order made for a debt under a regulated consumer credit agreement."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Jointly owned land", body:"A charging order can be made over the debtor's **beneficial interest** (the debtor's own share of the ownership) in the property."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"When it suits", body:"**Suitable** where the debtor **owns property** (especially with equity) but has little cash. **Slow, but very secure.**"}
 ]},
 {h:"Attachment of earnings order (Attachment of Earnings Act 1971; CPR 89)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"County Court only: the employer deducts", body:"**County Court only**. An order requiring the debtor's **employer** to **deduct** regular sums from the debtor's **earnings** and pay them to the court (via the Centralised Attachment of Earnings Payment System) for the creditor."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Employed debtors only", body:"Only available against debtors who are **employed** (including those receiving an occupational pension). **Not** available against the **self-employed**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Normal deduction rate", body:"The court sets the **normal deduction rate**: the amount the employer deducts each pay day."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Protected earnings rate", body:"The court also sets the **protected earnings rate**: the minimum amount the debtor must be left with to meet basic needs. If earnings fall below it in a pay period, **nothing is deducted**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"When it suits", body:"**Suitable** where the debtor has a **stable job** with a regular salary. **Unsuitable** where the debtor changes jobs often, is unemployed or is self-employed."}
 ]},
 {h:"Other methods", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Insolvency: a collective remedy, used as leverage", body:"**Insolvency proceedings** are a collective remedy for **all creditors**, not just the judgment creditor. They are often used as **leverage** to make the debtor pay."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Statutory demand: 21 days", body:"A **statutory demand** is a formal written demand for payment. If not paid, secured or compounded (settled by an agreement with the creditor) within **21 days**, the creditor can petition the court."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Bankruptcy £5,000; winding up £750", body:"**Bankruptcy petition** against an **individual**: the debt must be at least **£5,000**. **Winding-up petition** against a **company**: the debt must be at least **£750**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Then you're just unsecured", body:"Once insolvency starts, the creditor ranks with the other **unsecured** creditors (unless it has already secured itself, e.g. by a final charging order)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Equitable execution: a receiver", body:"The court can appoint a **receiver** (a person appointed by the court to take in money for the creditor) to collect income or other assets that the ordinary methods cannot reach (e.g. rents)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Enforcing abroad: the Hague Conventions", body:"It depends on reciprocal arrangements: the **2005 Hague Convention** (judgments based on exclusive choice of court agreements) and the **2019 Hague Judgments Convention** (in force for the UK from **1 July 2025**, including with the EU)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Enforcing abroad: other routes", body:"The **Foreign Judgments (Reciprocal Enforcement) Act 1933** and **Administration of Justice Act 1920** for some Commonwealth and other countries, and a simple **registration** procedure for **Scotland and Northern Ireland**. Otherwise a **fresh claim** must be brought abroad."}
 ]},
 {h:"Choosing the method", cards:[
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A self-employed plumber owns his house (large equity) and a van he uses for work. Best method?", body:"**A charging order over the house.** Attachment of earnings is not available (self-employed), and the van is likely to be **exempt** as a tool of his trade (up to £1,350 in value) or may be on finance."},
   {t:"compare", i:"", title:"Match the asset to the method", cols:["Debtor's assets or situation", "Best method"], rows:[["**Unknown** assets","**Order to obtain information** first"],["**Bank account in credit**","**Third party debt order**"],["**Owns land** (e.g. a house with equity)","**Charging order**, then possibly an **order for sale**"],["**Employed** with a steady salary","**Attachment of earnings** (County Court)"],["**Valuable goods**, or a business with stock and equipment","**Writ of control** (High Court, £5,000+) or **warrant of control** (County Court)"],["Debtor clearly insolvent, or the creditor wants maximum pressure","**Statutory demand** and **bankruptcy** (£5,000+) or **winding-up** (£750+) petition"]]}
 ]},
 {h:"Exam tips and watch out", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exam tip: match the asset, then check the limits", body:"SBA (single best answer) questions usually describe the debtor's assets and ask for the **most suitable method**. Match the asset to the method, then check the limits: attachment of earnings needs an **employed** debtor; a third party debt order only catches money in the account **when the interim order is served**; goods may be **exempt** or belong to someone else."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exam tip: finding out comes first", body:"The 'order to obtain information' (CPR 71) is a **fact-finding tool**, not a method of getting paid. If the question says the creditor does not know what the debtor owns, this is the **first step**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Watch out", body:"**Attachment of earnings** is a **County Court** remedy for **employed** debtors only. And enforcement of a judgment of **£5,000 or more** by taking control of goods must be in the **High Court** (writ of control), unless it is a regulated consumer credit debt."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Fairground room 11 (The Hall of Mirrors). Source: notes for topic dr-evidence in src/content/dr.js, in notes order (the two exam tips and the watch-out sit in the sections the plan's object map ties them to). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["dr/dr-evidence"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Evidence proves the facts", body:"**Evidence** is the material a party uses to prove the facts it relies on. In civil cases the **court controls** what evidence it will hear."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Most evidence is in writing", body:"Most evidence is given **in writing** before trial: **witness statements** from people who saw what happened (**witnesses of fact**), and **reports** from **experts** who give opinions on technical matters."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Hearsay is allowed in", body:"**Hearsay** (a statement made out of court used to prove its truth) is **admissible** in civil cases, subject to notice rules."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The key questions", body:"Is the evidence **relevant** and **admissible**? Who has the **burden** of proving each fact, and to what **standard**? How much **weight** will the court give it?"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Where the rules are", body:"**CPR Parts 32, 33, 34 and 35**, the **Civil Evidence Act 1995** (hearsay) and the **Civil Evidence Act 1968** (convictions)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Basic principles", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Relevance", body:"Evidence is relevant if it tends to **prove or disprove a fact in issue** (a fact that must be decided). The issues are defined by the **statements of case**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Admissibility", body:"Relevant evidence is generally **admissible** unless a rule excludes it, e.g. **privileged** material (protected from being shown to the other side) or **without prejudice** communications (made in a genuine attempt to settle) (see **Disclosure, privilege and without prejudice**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The court controls evidence (CPR 32.1)", body:"The court may give directions on the **issues** on which it wants evidence, the **nature** of the evidence and the **way** it is put before the court."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Excluding evidence and limiting cross-examination", body:"Under CPR 32.1 the court may **exclude evidence that would otherwise be admissible** (e.g. to keep the case proportionate) and **limit cross-examination** (questioning of a witness by the other side)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Weight", body:"**Weight** is how persuasive the court finds the evidence. Admissible evidence may still carry **little weight** (e.g. vague, contradicted or hearsay evidence)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Burden of proof: he who asserts must prove", body:"The party who **asserts** a fact must prove it. Usually this is the **claimant**, for the elements of its claim."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The defendant's burden", body:"The **defendant** bears the burden on its **defences**, e.g. **contributory negligence**, **limitation** or **failure to mitigate** (not taking reasonable steps to reduce the loss)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Standard of proof", body:"The **balance of probabilities**: the fact is proved if it is **more likely than not**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"One civil standard: Re B (Children) [2008]", body:"There is **one** civil standard, even for serious allegations such as **fraud**. The seriousness of an allegation is just **one factor** in deciding whether it is proved."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A claimant alleges fraud in a civil claim. Must it prove fraud beyond reasonable doubt?", body:"**No.** There is one civil standard, the **balance of probabilities**, even for fraud (**Re B (Children)**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Previous convictions (Civil Evidence Act 1968 s.11)", body:"A person's criminal conviction is **admissible** to prove that they committed the offence. The person is then **taken to have committed it unless they prove otherwise**. This **shifts the burden** to the convicted party."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A driver is convicted of careless driving for a crash. Can the injured pedestrian use the conviction in a civil claim?", body:"**Yes.** The conviction can be **pleaded and proved**, and the **driver** must prove on the balance of probabilities that he was **not** careless."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Opinion evidence", body:"A witness of fact may give an opinion on matters they **perceived** (e.g. \"the car was going fast\"), but opinion on **technical** matters needs an **expert**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Admissions of facts and documents", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Notice to admit facts (CPR 32.18)", body:"A party can serve a notice asking another party to **admit specified facts** (or part of its case), so they need not be proved at trial."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"21 days, and the costs risk", body:"The notice to admit facts must be served **no later than 21 days before the trial**. If the other party refuses to admit facts that are later proved, it may be ordered to pay the **costs** of proving them."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Notice to prove documents (CPR 32.19)", body:"A party is **deemed to admit the authenticity** (that they are genuine) of documents **disclosed to it** under Part 31, unless it serves a **notice requiring them to be proved** at trial."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"When to serve a notice to prove documents", body:"By the **latest** of the date for serving witness statements or **7 days** after disclosure of the document."}
 ]},
 {h:"Witnesses of fact and witness statements (CPR 32): what a witness statement is", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A witness statement (CPR 32.4)", body:"A **written statement**, signed by the witness, containing the evidence that person would be allowed to give **orally**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Form and content (PD 32)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Own words, first person", body:"In the witness's **own words**, in the **first person**, ideally drafted from the witness's own account."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The heading and the witness's details", body:"Headed with the court and claim details. It states the witness's full **name**, **address**, **occupation**, and whether they are a **party** or **employed** by one."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Own knowledge or information and belief", body:"It says which statements are from the witness's **own knowledge** and which are **matters of information or belief**, giving the **source**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Layout", body:"**Numbered** paragraphs, **chronological**, with **exhibits** (documents attached to the statement) identified."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Statement of truth", body:"It ends with a **statement of truth** signed by the **witness** (not the solicitor), confirming they believe the facts are true and understand that **contempt of court** proceedings (which can lead to a fine or prison) may be brought for a false statement made without an honest belief in its truth."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Another first language", body:"If the witness's first language is not English, the statement must be in their **own language** with a **translation**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Business and Property Courts: PD 57AC", body:"Trial witness statements must also contain only matters within the witness's **personal knowledge** that need to be proved, **no argument** or commentary on documents, a **list of documents** the witness was referred to, and **certificates of compliance** from the witness and the solicitor."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Who signs the statement of truth on a witness statement?", body:"**The witness**, not the solicitor."}
 ]},
 {h:"Exchange and use at trial", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exchange", body:"The court orders the parties to **serve** (exchange) the statements of the witnesses they intend to call, usually **simultaneously**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Evidence in chief (CPR 32.5)", body:"At trial, the statement **stands as the witness's evidence in chief** (the main evidence for the party calling them) unless the court orders otherwise."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Amplifying, then cross-examination", body:"The witness confirms the statement on oath and may **amplify** it or deal with new matters only with the court's **permission** and for **good reason**. The witness is then **cross-examined**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Served but not called (CPR 32.5(5))", body:"If a party serves a witness statement but does **not call** the witness or put the statement in as hearsay, **any other party** may put it in as **hearsay** evidence."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Not served in time (CPR 32.10)", body:"If a witness statement is **not served** within the time ordered, the witness **may not give oral evidence** without the court's **permission**. This is an automatic sanction: relief must be sought under **Denton**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Witness summary (CPR 32.9)", body:"If a party **cannot obtain a statement** (e.g. the witness refuses to co-operate), it can apply without notice for permission to serve a **witness summary**: a summary of the evidence it expects the witness to give, or of the matters it will question the witness about. It is usually combined with a **witness summons** (a court order requiring a witness to attend) (see **Trial**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Collateral use (CPR 32.12)", body:"A witness statement may be used **only for the proceedings** in which it is served, unless the witness **consents**, the court **permits**, or it has been put in evidence at a **public hearing**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Affidavits", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What an affidavit is", body:"A written statement **sworn on oath** (or affirmed) before a person authorised to administer oaths, e.g. a solicitor or commissioner for oaths **not acting for the party**, or a court officer."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"When an affidavit is required", body:"Where a rule or court order requires it, mainly for **freezing injunctions** and **search orders** (see **Interim applications**) and **contempt** of court applications."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Otherwise, a witness statement", body:"Otherwise a **witness statement** (verified by a statement of truth) is used. A party who uses an affidavit where a witness statement would do **may not recover the extra cost**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Hearsay (Civil Evidence Act 1995 and CPR 33): what hearsay is", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Hearsay (s.1)", body:"A statement made **otherwise than by a person giving oral evidence** in the proceedings, which is used to **prove the truth of the matters stated**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Examples of hearsay", body:"A witness statement from someone who will **not attend** trial; a **letter or email** repeating what someone said; a witness repeating what a bystander told them (\"Ann told me the van went through a red light\")."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Multiple hearsay", body:"A statement passed through **more than one person** (\"Ben told me that Ann told him...\")."}
 ]},
 {h:"Hearsay is admissible", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Admissible in civil proceedings (s.1)", body:"Hearsay evidence is **admissible** in civil proceedings. It is **not excluded** just because it is hearsay."}
 ]},
 {h:"Notice (s.2 and CPR 33.2)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Give notice", body:"A party relying on hearsay must give the other parties **notice**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The maker will be called", body:"**Serving their witness statement** containing the hearsay is sufficient notice."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The maker will not be called: a hearsay notice", body:"The party must serve a **hearsay notice** (usually when serving witness statements) identifying the hearsay evidence, stating that it intends to rely on it, and giving the **reason** the maker will not be called."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No notice: still admissible (s.2(4))", body:"Failure to give notice **does not make the evidence inadmissible**. It may affect the **weight** the court gives it and the **costs**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A party forgets to serve a hearsay notice. Is the hearsay now inadmissible?", body:"**No.** It is still admissible (s.2(4)); the failure may affect **weight** and **costs**."}
 ]},
 {h:"The other side's protections", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Calling the maker for cross-examination (s.3, CPR 33.4)", body:"Where the maker is not being called, the other party can apply for **permission to call the maker** and cross-examine them on the statement. It must apply within **14 days** after service of the hearsay notice."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Attacking credibility (s.5, CPR 33.5)", body:"A party can attack the **credibility** of the maker of a hearsay statement even though the maker is not called, but must give **notice** of its intention within **14 days** after service of the hearsay notice."}
 ]},
 {h:"Weight (s.4)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"How the court weighs hearsay", body:"The court considers any circumstances from which inferences can reasonably be drawn about the statement's **reliability**, in particular six factors."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Factor 1: could the maker have been called?", body:"Whether it would have been **reasonable and practicable** for the party to **call the maker** as a witness."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Factor 2: made at the time?", body:"Whether the statement was made **contemporaneously** (at or near the time) with the events."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Factor 3: multiple hearsay?", body:"Whether it involves **multiple hearsay**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Factor 4: a motive to mislead?", body:"Whether any person involved had a **motive to conceal or misrepresent** matters."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Factor 5: edited or made for a purpose?", body:"Whether the statement was an **edited account**, or made in **collaboration** with someone else or for a **particular purpose**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Factor 6: hiding its weight?", body:"Whether the circumstances suggest an attempt to **prevent proper evaluation** of its weight."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"The claimant's witness has emigrated and will not attend trial. Can the claimant use her statement?", body:"**Yes, as hearsay.** The claimant must serve a **hearsay notice** explaining why the witness will not be called. The defendant can apply within **14 days** to call her for cross-examination, and the court weighs the statement taking into account that it cannot be tested in cross-examination."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: what do hearsay questions usually test?", body:"Rarely whether the evidence is admissible (it is). They test the **notice** requirement, the **14-day** limits to call the maker or attack credibility, or the **s.4 weight factors**. Failure to give notice affects weight and costs, **not admissibility**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Expert evidence (CPR 35 and PD 35): when expert evidence is allowed", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Only what is reasonably required (CPR 35.1)", body:"Expert evidence is restricted to what is **reasonably required** to resolve the proceedings."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No expert without permission (CPR 35.4)", body:"**No party may call an expert or put in an expert's report without the court's permission.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Asking for permission", body:"The application must identify the **field** of expertise and, where practicable, the **name** of the expert, and give an **estimate of the costs**. Permission is normally given at **allocation** or the **CMC** (case management conference)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Small claims track", body:"Expert evidence **rarely**, and only with permission."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Fast track", body:"Usually **written reports** only, **one expert per party per field**, no more than **two fields**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Intermediate track (CPR 28.14)", body:"A claim is suitable only if no more than **two experts per party** will give oral evidence. Directions normally allow **one oral expert per party** unless a second is reasonably required and proportionate. Each report is limited to **20 pages**."}
 ]},
 {h:"The expert's duty", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Overriding duty to the court (CPR 35.3)", body:"An expert's **overriding duty is to the court**. It **overrides any obligation** to the person instructing or paying them."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Independent, not an advocate", body:"Expert evidence must be **independent**, **objective** and **unbiased**, and the expert must **not act as an advocate**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Within their expertise (PD 35)", body:"Experts must stay within their **expertise**, and say if an opinion is **provisional**, or if they **change their view**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Asking the court for directions (CPR 35.14)", body:"An expert can ask the court for **directions** to help them carry out their function, **without giving notice** to the party instructing them."}
 ]},
 {h:"The report (CPR 35.10 and PD 35)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Addressed to the court", body:"The report is addressed to the **court**, not the party."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What the report must include (1)", body:"The expert's **qualifications**, the **material relied on**, the **facts and assumptions**, and any **range of opinion** and the expert's own **reasons**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What the report must include (2)", body:"A **summary of conclusions**, a statement that the expert understands and has **complied with their duty** to the court, a **statement of truth**, and the **substance of all material instructions** (written or oral) on which the report is based."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Instructions are not privileged", body:"But the court will not order disclosure of them, or allow cross-examination about them, unless there are **reasonable grounds** to think the statement of instructions is **inaccurate or incomplete**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Single joint experts (CPR 35.7-35.8)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A single joint expert (SJE)", body:"Where two or more parties want expert evidence on an issue, the court can direct that it be given by a **single joint expert**: one expert instructed by **all the parties**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Common in lower-value claims", body:"SJEs are common in the **fast track** and many **intermediate track** claims."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No agreement on who", body:"If the parties cannot agree who it should be, the court can **select** from a list or direct how the expert is chosen."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Instructing the SJE", body:"Each party can give **instructions** to the SJE, but must **send a copy** to the other parties."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The SJE's fees", body:"The parties are **jointly and severally liable** for the fees (the expert can recover the whole fee from any one of them) unless the court orders otherwise."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Unhappy with the SJE's report", body:"A party can put **written questions**, and in **exceptional** cases may get permission to instruct its **own expert**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Written questions to experts (CPR 35.6)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Who can be asked", body:"A party may put **written questions** to an expert instructed by **another party**, or to an **SJE**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Once, in 28 days, to clarify", body:"**Once only**; within **28 days** of service of the report; only to **clarify** the report; unless the court permits or the other party agrees otherwise."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The answers", body:"The answers are treated as **part of the report**. If an expert does not answer, the court may order that the party **cannot rely** on that expert's evidence or **recover the expert's fees**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: how do you put questions to the other side's expert?", body:"**In writing**, **once**, **within 28 days** of service of the report, and only to **clarify** it. Anything beyond that needs the court's permission or the other side's agreement. The answers form **part of the report**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Discussions between experts (CPR 35.12)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A discussion between experts", body:"The court may direct experts to hold a **discussion** to identify and discuss the issues, and where possible **reach agreement**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The joint statement", body:"The experts prepare a **joint statement** setting out what is **agreed**, what is **disagreed**, and **why**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Kept from the trial", body:"The content of the discussion **cannot be referred to at trial** unless the parties agree."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Not binding on the parties", body:"Any agreement between the experts does **not bind the parties** unless the parties **expressly agree** to be bound."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Watch out", body:"No expert evidence at all is allowed without the court's **permission**, and an expert's duty is to the **court**, not to the client who pays them. An agreement reached in an experts' discussion does **not bind the parties** unless they expressly agree."}
 ]},
 {h:"Other rules", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Failure to disclose (CPR 35.13)", body:"A party that fails to disclose an expert's report **cannot use it** at trial or call the expert **without permission**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Use by the other side (CPR 35.11)", body:"Once a party has disclosed a report, **any party** may use it as evidence at trial."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Changing expert", body:"A party wanting to replace an expert after permission was given (e.g. because the report is unfavourable, \"**expert shopping**\") will usually be given permission only on condition that it **discloses the first expert's report**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Edwards-Tubb v JD Wetherspoon plc [2011]", body:"A claimant who had obtained a pre-action report from one expert, then wanted to rely on another, **had to disclose the first report**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Concurrent evidence (\"hot-tubbing\")", body:"The court may hear the experts give evidence **at the same time**, questioned by the **judge** on the disputed issues."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Source: src/content/dr.js topic dr-interim (Fairground room 9, the Ghost Train). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["dr/dr-interim"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"An order before the trial", body:"An **interim application** is a request to the court for an order **before the final trial** (also called an **interlocutory** application). Some deal with procedure (e.g. an extension of time); others can decide or shape the whole case."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Station 1: summary judgment (Part 24)", body:"**Summary judgment** (CPR Part 24): ending a claim or defence early because it has **no real prospect of success**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Station 2: interim payments (Part 25)", body:"**Interim payments** (CPR Part 25): making the defendant pay **part of the likely damages** before trial."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Station 3: interim injunctions (Part 25)", body:"**Interim injunctions** (CPR Part 25): ordering a party to do or stop doing something **until trial**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Part 23 for all; Part 25 rewritten", body:"All interim applications follow the general procedure in **CPR Part 23**. Part 25 (interim remedies and security for costs) was **rewritten with effect from 6 April 2025**; the tests are unchanged in substance."}
 ]},
 {h:"How to make an interim application (Part 23)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The application notice (N244)", body:"Use an **application notice** (form **N244**). It states the **order sought** and **why** (briefly), and whether the applicant wants a **hearing** or a decision **on paper**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What goes with the N244", body:"Usually attached: a **draft order** and supporting **evidence**, normally a **witness statement** (or the facts in the N244 itself, verified by a statement of truth, a signed statement that the facts are true)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Service: at least 3 clear days", body:"Serve it as soon as practicable after issue and at least **3 clear days** before the hearing, unless a rule or practice direction sets a different period. **Clear days** exclude the day of service and the day of the hearing (and, for periods of 5 days or less, weekends and bank holidays)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Without notice: only in three cases", body:"A **without notice** application (the respondent is not told in advance) is allowed only if there is **exceptional urgency**; or giving notice would **defeat the purpose** of the order (e.g. a freezing injunction, where the respondent would move its money); or the rules permit it, or all parties consent."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Full and frank disclosure", body:"A without notice applicant owes a duty of **full and frank disclosure**: it must tell the court everything material, **including points against itself**. Breach can lead to the order being **discharged**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"After a without notice order", body:"The order must be **served** on the respondent with the evidence. The respondent can apply to **set aside or vary** it (usually within **7 days** of service)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Without a hearing, phone or video", body:"The court can deal with an application **on paper** if the parties agree or a hearing would not be appropriate. Hearings are often by **telephone or video**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Costs: statement 24 hours before", body:"For hearings lasting **one day or less**, costs are usually **summarily assessed** (the judge fixes the amount at the end of the hearing). So each party files and serves a **statement of costs** at least **24 hours** before the hearing (see **Costs**)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Summary judgment (Part 24): the test (CPR 24.3)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The two-part test", body:"The court may give **summary judgment** against a **claimant or a defendant** on the **whole of a claim or on an issue** if that party has **no real prospect of succeeding** on the claim, defence or issue, **and** there is **no other compelling reason** why the case or issue should go to **trial**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Real prospect: realistic, not fanciful", body:"'**Real prospect**' means **realistic, not fanciful**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No mini-trial", body:"The court does not conduct a '**mini-trial**'. It considers the evidence available and the evidence that can reasonably be expected at trial. It will not decide genuinely disputed facts on paper."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Other compelling reasons for a trial", body:"An '**other compelling reason**' might be that the respondent needs **more time to investigate**, that key facts are within the **applicant's knowledge only**, or that the case raises an issue of **public importance**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Who can apply, and when", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Either party can apply", body:"A **defendant** can apply against a hopeless **claim**; a **claimant** can apply against a hopeless **defence**. The court can also fix a hearing of its own initiative."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Claimant: not before an AoS or defence", body:"A **claimant** cannot apply until the defendant has filed an **acknowledgment of service (AoS)** or a **defence**, unless the court gives permission or a rule allows it (**CPR 24.4(1)**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Early application: defence can wait", body:"If a claimant applies before the defence is filed, the defendant need not file a defence until the hearing."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Where it is not available", body:"Summary judgment is available against a defendant in all proceedings **except residential possession claims against mortgagors or tenants**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Procedure and timing", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The 14, 7, 3 timetable", body:"**Notice of the hearing** to the respondent: at least **14 days** before the hearing. **Respondent's** written evidence: at least **7 days** before. **Applicant's** evidence in reply: at least **3 days** before."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What the application notice must say", body:"It must say it is for **summary judgment**, identify any point of law or document relied on, state that the applicant believes the respondent has **no real prospect** of success and knows of **no other reason** for a trial, and tell the respondent it may file evidence."}
 ]},
 {h:"Orders the court can make", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Judgment on the claim", body:"**Judgment** on the claim (for the claimant)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Striking out or dismissal", body:"**Striking out or dismissal** of the claim (for the defendant)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Application dismissed", body:"**Dismissal of the application**: the case continues to trial, with case management directions."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A conditional order", body:"Where it is **possible** that the claim or defence will succeed but **improbable**, the court can order a party to **pay a sum into court** or take a specified step, failing which its claim or defence will be **struck out**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Summary judgment v strike out", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Strike out (CPR 3.4): the pleading", body:"**Strike out** deals with defects in the **statement of case** itself: it discloses **no reasonable grounds** for bringing or defending the claim (judged on the pleaded facts, without evidence), it is an **abuse of process** (e.g. relitigating a decided case), or there has been a **failure to comply** with a rule, practice direction or order."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Summary judgment: the evidence", body:"**Summary judgment** looks at the **evidence**: even a well-pleaded case can fail if, on the evidence, it has no real prospect."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Often made together", body:"The two applications are often made together."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A buyer's defence just says 'the goods were faulty', but its own emails accept they were fine. What can the supplier do?", body:"**Apply for summary judgment.** A supplier suing for the price of goods delivered faces a defence with no details. On the evidence the defence has **no real prospect** of success and there is **no other compelling reason** for a trial."}
 ]},
 {h:"Interim payments (Part 25)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Part of the damages, in advance", body:"An **interim payment** is an order that the defendant pay **part of the damages** (or other sum) it is likely to be ordered to pay, **in advance** of the trial."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Who it helps", body:"It helps claimants who need money now, e.g. for **care after a serious injury** or to **keep a business running**."}
 ]},
 {h:"When can the claimant apply?", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"After the time for an AoS", body:"Any time **after the time for filing an AoS has expired**. The claimant can apply **more than once**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Conditions (the court must be satisfied of one)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Condition 1: liability admitted", body:"The defendant has **admitted liability** to pay damages or another sum."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Condition 2: judgment for damages", body:"The claimant has obtained **judgment** against the defendant for damages to be assessed (e.g. default judgment on liability)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Condition 3: would win a substantial sum", body:"The court is satisfied that, if the claim went to trial, the claimant **would obtain judgment for a substantial amount of money** against the defendant. A high standard: '**would**', not 'might'."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Condition 4: several defendants", body:"The court is satisfied the claimant would obtain judgment for a substantial sum against **at least one** of them (even if it cannot say which), and **every** defendant is **insured**, a **public body**, or covered by the **Motor Insurers' Bureau** (the body that compensates victims of uninsured or untraced drivers)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Amount", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A reasonable proportion", body:"**No more than a reasonable proportion of the likely amount of the final judgment**, taking into account **contributory negligence** and any **set-off or counterclaim** (a set-off is a cross-claim the defendant can deduct from what it owes)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A cautious award", body:"The court often awards a **cautious proportion** of a **conservative estimate** of the final award."}
 ]},
 {h:"Procedure (interim payments)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"14, 7, 3 again", body:"Application notice and evidence **served** at least **14 days** before the hearing. **Respondent's** evidence at least **7 days** before. **Applicant's** reply evidence at least **3 days** before."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What the evidence must cover", body:"The **sum sought** and what it is for, the **likely final judgment**, the reasons for believing a **condition** is met, and (in PI claims) details of special damages (losses that can be calculated exactly, e.g. lost earnings to trial) and past and future losses."}
 ]},
 {h:"Secrecy and adjustment", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Secret from the trial judge", body:"The **fact** that an interim payment has been made (voluntarily or by order) must **not be disclosed to the trial judge** until all questions of **liability** and **quantum** (amount) have been decided, unless the defendant agrees."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Adjustment and repayment", body:"At the end, the court **adjusts** the final judgment to take account of the interim payment, and can order **repayment** if the claimant was overpaid."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Ravi is hit by a delivery van; the insurer admits liability. His claim is worth about £400,000 and he needs home adaptations. Can he get money now?", body:"**Yes: he can apply for an interim payment** (liability is admitted). The court might award, say, **£100,000**, a reasonable proportion of the likely award after allowing for any contributory negligence."}
 ]},
 {h:"Interim injunctions (Part 25)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Do it or stop it, until trial", body:"An **interim injunction** is an order to do something (**mandatory**) or stop doing something (**prohibitory**) **until trial** or a further order. It preserves the position so the final judgment is not made pointless."}
 ]},
 {h:"The American Cyanamid test", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"American Cyanamid Co v Ethicon Ltd [1975]", body:"Cyanamid held a patent for **surgical stitches** and sought to stop Ethicon launching a rival product it said infringed the patent. The House of Lords **granted an interim injunction** and set out the approach still used today. The court asks five questions, **in order**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Step 1: a serious question to be tried?", body:"The claim must not be **frivolous or vexatious** (without real substance, or brought only to annoy). The court does **not** decide the merits at this stage."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Step 2: damages adequate for the claimant?", body:"Would damages be an adequate remedy for the claimant if the injunction is refused and the claimant wins at trial? If **yes**, and the defendant could **pay** them, the injunction should **normally be refused**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Step 3: undertaking adequate for the defendant?", body:"Would the claimant's **undertaking in damages** (a promise to the court to pay the defendant's losses) adequately protect the defendant if the injunction is granted but the defendant wins at trial? If yes, and the claimant could pay, that points towards **granting** it."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Step 4: the balance of convenience", body:"If there is doubt about the adequacy of damages, where does the **balance of convenience** lie? The court weighs the risk of injustice to each side. Where other factors are evenly balanced, it is prudent to **preserve the status quo** (the position before the conduct complained of)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Step 5: special factors", body:"E.g. where the interim decision will in practice **decide the whole case**, the court may consider the relative strength of each side's case."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Mandatory injunctions: more caution", body:"A **mandatory** interim injunction (ordering positive action) is granted **more cautiously**: the court usually needs a high degree of assurance that the claimant will win at trial."}
 ]},
 {h:"The cross-undertaking in damages", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The promise to compensate", body:"The applicant must almost always give an **undertaking in damages** (a **cross-undertaking**): a promise to the court to **compensate the respondent** (and sometimes third parties) for any loss caused by the injunction if it later turns out it should not have been granted."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Fortified", body:"The court may require it to be **fortified** (backed by security, e.g. a payment into court) if the applicant's ability to pay is doubtful."}
 ]},
 {h:"Procedure (interim injunctions)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"On notice or without notice", body:"Apply **on notice** (N244) with **evidence** (witness statement) and a draft order; or **without notice** in urgent cases or where notice would defeat the purpose."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Until the return date", body:"A **without notice** injunction usually lasts only a short time, until a **return date** at which the respondent can be heard."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Emergency: before issue, out of hours", body:"In an emergency, an injunction can be granted **before a claim is issued** (with an undertaking to issue promptly), even **by telephone out of hours**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Freezing injunctions and search orders", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Freezing injunction: what it does", body:"A **freezing injunction** stops a party **removing assets** from the jurisdiction (England and Wales) or **dissipating** them (spending, hiding or transferring them) so a future judgment would go unsatisfied. It can apply to assets in England only, or **worldwide**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Freezing injunction: what to show", body:"A **good arguable case** on the merits; that the respondent has **assets** (in or outside the jurisdiction); and a **real risk of dissipation** of those assets if the order is not made."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Living expenses and third parties", body:"It usually allows the respondent a weekly sum for **living expenses** and legal costs, and protects **third parties** such as banks."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Search order: what it does", body:"A **search order** (now including **imaging orders** for electronic material) requires the respondent to allow the applicant's representatives to **enter premises** to search for, copy and remove evidence."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Search order: what it needs", body:"An **extremely strong prima facie case** (a case that looks strong on the face of the evidence), very serious potential damage, and clear evidence that the respondent has incriminating material and a **real possibility it will be destroyed**. It must be executed under the supervision of an independent **Supervising Solicitor**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What both orders share", body:"Both are usually made **without notice**, need evidence by **affidavit** (a written statement sworn on oath), must follow the court's approved **model forms**, and require a **cross-undertaking in damages**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Exam tips", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The three stations in one look", body:"**Summary judgment**: no real prospect and no other compelling reason for a trial. **Interim payment**: one of the conditions, then a reasonable proportion. **Interim injunction**: the American Cyanamid steps, in order."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"The claimant's case looks very strong, but damages would compensate it and the defendant can pay. Injunction?", body:"**Refused.** Work through the **American Cyanamid** steps in order. Many questions are answered at **step 2**: if **damages would adequately compensate the claimant** and the defendant can pay, the injunction is refused, however strong the claimant's case looks."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"What do you look for in an interim payment question?", body:"One of the conditions (**admission**, **judgment**, or **would obtain judgment for a substantial sum**), then the cap: a **reasonable proportion** of the likely final judgment, net of contributory negligence and counterclaims. The trial judge is **not told**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Can a defendant ask for summary judgment?", body:"**Yes.** Summary judgment is available to a **defendant** against a hopeless **claim**, not only to a claimant. And the test is '**no real prospect**', not 'no prospect at all' or 'unlikely to succeed'."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Source: src/content/dr.js topic dr-issue-service (Fairground room 06, The Hoopla Stall). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["dr/dr-issue-service"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The court issues", body:"A court case starts when the court **issues** the claimant's **claim form**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The claimant serves", body:"The claimant must then **serve** it (formally deliver it) on the defendant, using a method the rules allow, within a **strict time limit**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Deemed served", body:"The rules fix the date on which each document is **deemed served** (treated in law as served). That starts the clock for the defendant's response."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Get it wrong, lose the claim", body:"Get service wrong and the claim may **fail completely**, especially if **limitation** (the legal time limit for bringing a claim) has expired in the meantime."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Where the rules are", body:"**CPR Part 7** (issuing), **CPR Part 6** (service) and **CPR Part 19** (changing parties)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Issuing the claim (CPR Part 7)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Form N1", body:"Proceedings start when the court **issues** a **claim form** (form **N1**) at the claimant's request. The date of issue is entered on the form."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Issue and limitation", body:"For limitation, the claim is brought when the court **receives** the request to issue (see **Limitation periods**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Contents: nature and remedy", body:"The claim form must (CPR 16.2) give a concise statement of the **nature of the claim** and specify the **remedy** sought (e.g. damages, an injunction)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Contents: value and interest", body:"It must contain a **statement of value** where money is claimed (see **Where to start**) and state any **interest** claimed."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Contents: statement of truth", body:"It must be verified by a **statement of truth** (a signed statement that the facts are believed to be true; see **Statements of case**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Particulars of claim", body:"The **particulars of claim** (the detailed statement of the claimant's case) can be included in the claim form, **served with** it, or **served separately** later."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Court fee", body:"The claimant pays a **court fee** based on the value."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Part 8 claims", body:"A simpler alternative procedure for claims **unlikely to involve a substantial dispute of fact**, such as interpreting a document or seeking court approval of a settlement for a child."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"How Part 8 runs", body:"There is **no defence**. The defendant files an **acknowledgment of service** (a short form confirming receipt of the claim and saying whether it will contest it) and **written evidence**, and the claim is automatically on the **multi-track** (the case-management track for higher-value or complex claims)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Time limits for serving the claim form", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"4 months (CPR 7.5)", body:"Within the jurisdiction (England and Wales), the claimant must take the **\"step required\"** for the chosen method **before 12 midnight** on the calendar day **4 months after the date of issue**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Step: post and DX", body:"**First class post** (or other next-day service): **posting** it. **Document exchange (DX)**: **leaving** it with or at the DX."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Step: address and personal service", body:"**Delivery to or leaving at** the permitted address: **delivering** it or **leaving** it there. **Personal service**: **completing** personal service (handing it to the defendant)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Step: fax and email", body:"**Fax**: **completing transmission**. **Other electronic method** (e.g. email): **sending** the email."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Outside the jurisdiction: 6 months", body:"A claim form to be served **outside the jurisdiction** must be served within **6 months** after issue."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Particulars served separately", body:"Must be served **within 14 days** after service of the claim form, and **no later than** the last day for serving the claim form (CPR 7.4)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Extending time: before expiry", body:"If the application to extend (CPR 7.6) is made **before** the time expires, the court applies the **overriding objective** (the court's duty to deal with cases justly and at proportionate cost). The claimant needs a **good reason** (e.g. the defendant is evading service)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Extending time: after expiry", body:"If made **after** the time expired, the court may extend **only if** (a) the **court** was responsible for service and failed to do it, **or** (b) the claimant **took all reasonable steps** to serve but was **unable** to do so, **and** in either case the claimant **acted promptly** in applying."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"An expired claim form", body:"If the claim form is not served in time, it **expires**. The claimant must issue a **new** claim, which is only possible if the claim is still within **limitation**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A claim form is issued on 10 March. By when must the claimant act?", body:"**The claimant must post it (or take the other required step) by midnight on 10 July.** It does not matter that the deemed date of service falls after 10 July."}
 ]},
 {h:"Methods of service (CPR 6.3)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Who serves? (CPR 6.4)", body:"The **court** serves the claim form unless the claimant tells the court that it wishes to serve it itself, a rule requires the claimant to serve, or the court orders otherwise. In practice, solicitors usually serve it themselves so that they **control the timing**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Personal service", body:"Leaving it with the individual; for a **company**, with a person holding a **senior position** (e.g. a director, treasurer, chief executive); for a **partnership**, with a partner or a person in control of the business at its principal place of business."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Post or DX", body:"**First class post**, **DX**, or another service providing **next-business-day delivery**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Leaving it", body:"**Leaving it** at a permitted address."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Fax or email: only if agreed in writing", body:"Only if the party to be served (or its solicitor) has **previously indicated in writing** that it will accept service this way and given the fax number or email address (PD 6A para 4.1)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A letterhead is not enough", body:"A solicitor's email address on its **letterhead** is **not enough** unless it says it may be used for service. The serving party should also ask about any limits (e.g. size of attachments)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Court-authorised method", body:"**Any method authorised by the court** (alternative service, below)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Companies: the registered office", body:"**Companies** can also be served by leaving the document at, or posting it to, the **registered office** (Companies Act 2006 s.1139)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Agreed in the contract (CPR 6.11)", body:"A **contractually agreed** method of service (in the parties' contract) can also be used for a contract claim."}
 ]},
 {h:"Where to serve the claim form", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Solicitor nominated (CPR 6.7)", body:"If the defendant's **solicitor** (in England and Wales) has notified the claimant **in writing** that it is **authorised to accept service** of the claim form, or the defendant has given the solicitor's business address as its address for service, the claim form **must** be served on the **solicitor**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Then not the defendant", body:"Serving the defendant personally is then **invalid**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"An address given for service", body:"Otherwise, serve at an address in the jurisdiction the defendant has **given for service**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Usual or last known address (CPR 6.9)", body:"Otherwise, at the defendant's **usual or last known address**. **Individual**: usual or last known **residence**. **Sole trader** (individual in business): usual or last known **residence**, or principal or last known **place of business**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Partnership", body:"Usual or last known residence of a **partner**, or principal or last known **place of business** of the firm."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Company", body:"A company registered in England and Wales: its **principal office**, or any **place of business** within the jurisdiction with a real connection to the claim (and the **registered office** under CA 2006 s.1139)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"LLP", body:"An **LLP** (limited liability partnership): its **principal office** or a **place of business** with a real connection to the claim."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Reason to believe they moved", body:"If the claimant has **reason to believe** the defendant no longer lives or works at the last known address, it must take **reasonable steps** to find the current address before serving there."}
 ]},
 {h:"Deemed service", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What deemed service is", body:"**Deemed service** is the date on which the law treats a document as served, whatever actually happened. It matters because time for the defendant's response runs from it."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Claim form (CPR 6.14)", body:"Deemed served on the **second business day** after the \"step required\" was completed, **whatever the method**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A business day", body:"Any day except **Saturday, Sunday**, a **bank holiday**, **Christmas Day** or **Good Friday**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A claim form is emailed (agreed in writing) at 5pm on Thursday 4 June. When is it deemed served?", body:"**Monday 8 June**: the second business day after the step (Friday is the first, Monday the second). The 4.30pm cut-off is irrelevant for a claim form."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Other documents: post or DX (CPR 6.26)", body:"For other documents (e.g. particulars of claim served separately, application notices) sent by **first class post, DX** or next-day service: the **second day** after posting or leaving with the DX, if that is a business day; otherwise the next business day after that."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Other documents: personal or leaving", body:"**Personal service**, or **delivering or leaving** at a permitted address: that day if done **before 4.30pm** on a business day; otherwise the **next business day**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Other documents: fax or email", body:"**Fax** (transmission completed) or **email or other electronic method** (sent): that day if **before 4.30pm** on a business day; otherwise the next business day."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Separate particulars of claim are posted first class on Friday 5 June. When are they deemed served?", body:"**Monday 8 June.** The second day after posting is Sunday 7 June, which is not a business day, so service is deemed on the next business day after that. (CPR 6.26: \"the second day after it was posted... if that day is a business day or, if not, the next business day after that day\".)"}
 ]},
 {h:"Certificate of service", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Form N215 within 21 days", body:"A claimant who serves the claim form itself must file a **certificate of service** (form **N215**) within **21 days** of service of the particulars, unless all defendants have filed an acknowledgment of service."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No certificate, no default judgment", body:"Without it, the claimant cannot obtain **default judgment** (judgment without a trial because the defendant has not responded in time)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Alternative service and dispensing with service", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Alternative service (CPR 6.15)", body:"The court can permit service by a method or at a place not otherwise allowed, if there is a **good reason** (e.g. the defendant is evading service, or the only contact is by social media)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"It can be backdated", body:"The court can also order **retrospectively** that steps already taken count as good service."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Abela v Baadarani [2013]", body:"The claimant could not serve a defendant in Lebanon by the usual methods, but delivered the claim form to his Lebanese lawyer. The Supreme Court upheld **retrospective validation**: the fact that the defendant learned of the claim was a **critical** factor, though **not by itself enough**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Barton v Wright Hassall LLP [2018]: facts", body:"Mr Barton, a **litigant in person** (a party acting without a lawyer), emailed his claim form to the defendant's solicitors on the **last day** for service. They had **never said they would accept service by email**. The claim form expired and the limitation period had run."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Barton: the rule", body:"The Supreme Court (by a majority) **refused** to validate service retrospectively: the defendant's **knowledge** of the claim was **not enough**, and **litigants in person** get **no special leniency**. His claim was lost."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Dispensing with service (CPR 6.16)", body:"The court can dispense with service of the claim form **only in exceptional circumstances**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Service out of the jurisdiction", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No permission: Scotland or Northern Ireland", body:"The claimant may serve out **without the court's permission** (CPR 6.32-6.33) where, for example, the defendant is in **Scotland or Northern Ireland** and the claim falls within the rules allocating UK jurisdiction."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No permission: choice of court or jurisdiction clause", body:"Also where there is an **exclusive choice of court agreement** in favour of England and Wales covered by the **2005 Hague Convention**, or a **contract** term that the courts of England and Wales have **jurisdiction** to determine the claim."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The notice of grounds", body:"When serving without permission, the claimant must file and serve a **notice** setting out the grounds, with the claim form."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Permission: a gateway", body:"With permission (CPR 6.36-6.37 and PD 6B), the claimant must show a **good arguable case** that the claim falls within a **gateway** in PD 6B (e.g. the contract was made in England, is governed by English law, or contains an English jurisdiction clause; the breach was committed in England; **damage** was sustained in England from a tort; the defendant is a necessary or proper party to a claim against another defendant served here)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Permission: merits and the proper place", body:"It must also show a **serious issue to be tried** on the merits, and that England and Wales is **the proper place** to bring the claim (**forum conveniens**, the most appropriate country to try it)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Only half the job (CPR 6.40 to 6.46)", body:"Having the right to serve out (with or without permission) is only half the job. The claimant must also use a **method of service that is valid** in the country concerned."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Treaty route (CPR 6.42(1))", body:"Through the **authority designated** under the **Hague Service Convention** 1965 (a treaty on cross-border service of court documents, to which all EU states, the USA, China and many others belong) or another civil procedure treaty, or, if local law allows, through the country's **judicial authorities** or a **British consular authority**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No treaty (CPR 6.42(2))", body:"Through the country's **government** (if willing) or a **British consular authority**, if local law allows."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Local law (CPR 6.40(3))", body:"By **any other method permitted by the law of the country** where it is served (e.g. personal service by a local process server)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The Senior Master (CPR 6.43)", body:"For service through a foreign authority, government or consul, the claimant files a request with the court, which sends it to the **Senior Master** (the Foreign Process Section at the Royal Courts of Justice), who forwards it to the foreign authority or the Foreign Office. An official **certificate of service** from abroad is evidence of the date and method."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Never against local law (CPR 6.40(4))", body:"Nothing in the CPR or a court order authorises a method that is **contrary to the law** of the country where service takes place. A method that breaks local law is not good service."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Scotland and Northern Ireland (CPR 6.40(2))", body:"There the claim form can be served by the **same methods** as in England and Wales."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Translation (CPR 6.45)", body:"When serving through a foreign authority, government or consul, the claimant must provide a **translation** into the **official language** of the country, with the translator's statement that it is correct. None is needed if **English** is an official language there, or if the person served is a **British citizen**, unless a treaty requires one."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Paying the expenses (CPR 6.46)", body:"The claimant must **undertake** to pay the expenses of the Foreign Office or foreign authority."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"More time to respond", body:"Longer than the usual 14 days. Served in **Scotland or Northern Ireland**: **21 days** after service of the particulars of claim to file an acknowledgment of service or a defence, or **35 days** for the defence if an acknowledgment of service was filed (CPR 6.35). Served **elsewhere**: the periods set by country in the **table in PD 6B** (generally longer for more distant countries)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"6 months to serve (CPR 7.5(2))", body:"A claim form to be served abroad must be **served within 6 months** of issue. Service through a foreign authority can take many months, so start **promptly** and apply for an extension before the 6 months run out if needed."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A London company sues a German company under a contract with an English jurisdiction clause. What must it do?", body:"**It can serve without permission** (the jurisdiction clause), but must serve **by a method valid in Germany**, usually through the German central authority under the Hague Service Convention, with a **German translation** of the claim form and particulars. The German company then has the period in the PD 6B table to respond."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: which two questions do you keep apart?", body:"**(1) Do I need permission to serve out?** No, if there is an English jurisdiction clause or a Hague 2005 exclusive choice of court agreement; otherwise yes, using a PD 6B gateway. **(2) How do I serve validly in that country?** A designated authority under the Hague Service Convention, the government or consul, or a method allowed by local law, plus a translation where needed."}
 ]},
 {h:"Adding, removing and substituting parties (CPR 19)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Before service: no permission", body:"**Before the claim form is served**, parties can be added, removed or substituted **without permission**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"After service: permission (CPR 19.4)", body:"**After service**, **court permission** is needed."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"When to add or remove", body:"The court may **add** a party if it is **desirable** so the court can resolve all the matters in dispute, or there is a connected issue that it is desirable to resolve. It may **remove** a party if it is **not desirable** for them to remain."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"New claimant: written consent", body:"Nobody can be added as a **claimant** without their **written consent** filed with the court."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"After limitation (CPR 19.6; Limitation Act 1980 s.35)", body:"After the limitation period has expired, a party can be added or substituted only if the relevant limitation period was **current when the proceedings were started**, **and** the addition or substitution is **necessary**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What \"necessary\" means", body:"The new party is to be **substituted** for a party named by **mistake**; or the claim **cannot properly be carried on** by or against the original party unless the new party is added; or the original party has **died** or had a **bankruptcy order** made against it and its interest has passed to the new party."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Personal injury: s.33", body:"In **personal injury** claims, the court may also add or substitute a party if it **disapplies** the limitation period under **s.33** (of the Limitation Act 1980)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A claimant sued \"Brightside Ltd\", but the contract was with \"Brightside Trading Ltd\". Limitation has now expired. Can it swap?", body:"**Yes, the court can allow substitution**, because the wrong company was named by **mistake** and the original claim was in time."}
 ]},
 {h:"Exam tips and watch out", cards:[
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: when is a claim form deemed served?", body:"Always the **second business day after the step**, whatever the method and time of day. The **4.30pm** rules and \"second day after posting\" rules apply only to **other documents**. Candidates often apply the wrong table."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: the claim form was posted on the last day of the 4 months. In time?", body:"**Yes.** The claimant beats the **4-month** deadline by completing the **step** (e.g. posting) in time, even though deemed service falls after the deadline."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Watch out: an authorised solicitor", body:"If the defendant's solicitors have confirmed in writing that they are **authorised to accept service**, the claim form **must** be served on them; serving the defendant personally is **invalid**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Watch out: email needs a written yes", body:"Email service needs **prior written** agreement to accept service by email."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Fairground room 02 (Big wheel). Source: src/content/dr.js, topic dr-limitation (notes only). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["dr/dr-limitation"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What a limitation period is", body:"A **limitation period** is the **time limit** within which a claimant must **start court proceedings**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Time-barred: a complete defence", body:"After the period expires, the claim is **time-barred**: the defendant has a **complete defence**, however good the claim is."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Limitation Act 1980", body:"Most periods are in the **Limitation Act 1980 (LA 1980)**. For every limitation question, ask **three things**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Question 1: what type of claim is it?", body:"Contract, negligence, personal injury, latent damage and so on. The type fixes the **length** of the period."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Question 2: when did time start to run?", body:"Usually when the **cause of action accrued**: the first day on which **all the elements of the claim existed**, so the claimant could first have sued."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Question 3: has anything postponed, paused or extended it?", body:"E.g. the claimant was a **child**, **fraud or concealment**, a **court discretion**, an **agreement to pause time**."}
 ]},
 {h:"How limitation works in practice", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A defence the defendant must plead", body:"Limitation is a **defence**. The defendant must **plead** it in its defence; the court will **not** raise it on its own."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Brought when the claim form is issued", body:"A claim is \"brought\" in time when the **claim form is issued** (the court enters the date on it, which formally starts the claim) before the period ends."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The date the court received it (PD 7A)", body:"If the court **receives the request to issue** before the deadline but issues it later, the claim counts as brought on the date the court **received** the request (**PD 7A**, Practice Direction 7A)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Service later does not matter", body:"Serving the claim form later (within the **4 months** allowed) does not matter for limitation: **issue is what counts**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No reviving an expired period", body:"Once the period has expired it **cannot be revived** (e.g. a later acknowledgment of a debt does not restart a period that has already run out)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"The court receives the request to issue before the deadline, but issues the claim form after it. In time?", body:"**Yes.** The claim counts as brought on the date the court **received** the request (PD 7A)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Contract", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"6 years from breach (s.5)", body:"A contract claim must be brought within **6 years** from the date of the **breach** of contract (**s.5**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Discovery does not matter", body:"Time runs from breach **even if** the claimant does not discover the breach, and even if **loss only happens later**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A deed: 12 years (s.8)", body:"**12 years** from breach if the contract is made by **deed** (**s.8**, a \"specialty\"). A deed is a formal document that makes clear it is a deed and is signed, witnessed and delivered."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No latent damage extension", body:"There is **no latent damage extension** for contract claims; **s.14A** applies **only to negligence**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A builder finishes an extension under an ordinary written contract in March 2018. Cracks appear in 2024 because of poor work. Is the contract claim in time?", body:"**No.** It **expired in March 2024** (6 years from breach, which happened when the work was done badly). The client should look at a claim in **negligence** instead."}
 ]},
 {h:"Tort (other than personal injury)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"6 years from accrual (s.2)", body:"**6 years** from the date the cause of action accrued (**s.2**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Torts needing damage: from the damage", body:"For torts that need **damage** (such as **negligence** and most **nuisance** claims), time runs from when the **damage** occurs, not from the negligent act. Negligence is not complete until there is damage."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Torts actionable per se: from the act", body:"For torts **actionable per se** (without proof of damage, e.g. **trespass**, such as going onto someone's land without permission), time runs from the **wrongful act**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Personal injury (PI)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"3 years from the later date (ss.11 and 14)", body:"**3 years** from the **later** of: the date the cause of action accrued (usually the **date of the injury**); and the claimant's **date of knowledge**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Date of knowledge (s.14): significant", body:"The date the claimant first knew **all** the facts: first, that the injury was **significant** (serious enough to justify suing a solvent defendant who did not dispute liability)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Date of knowledge: attributable", body:"That the injury was **attributable** (at least partly) to the act or omission alleged to be negligent."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Date of knowledge: identity", body:"The **identity of the defendant**; and, if someone else is responsible, that person's identity and the facts supporting the claim against the defendant."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Constructive knowledge", body:"Knowledge includes what the claimant **could reasonably have found out**, including with the help of appropriate **experts** (**constructive knowledge**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Ignorance of the law does not help", body:"Knowing that the acts were **legally negligent** is **irrelevant**: ignorance of the law does not delay time."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"However the claim is framed", body:"The 3-year PI period applies whether the claim is framed in **negligence, nuisance or breach of contract** or breach of statutory duty, as long as it claims damages for **personal injury**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Fatal accidents (s.12)", body:"Claims by dependants after a death, under the **Fatal Accidents Act 1976**: **3 years** from the **death** or from the dependant's date of knowledge, if later (**s.12**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Fatal accidents: already barred", body:"If the deceased's own claim was **already time-barred at death**, the dependants **cannot claim**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A claimant knew her injury was significant and who caused it, but not that this was legally negligence. Does time wait?", body:"**No.** Knowing the acts were legally negligent is **irrelevant**: ignorance of the law does not delay time."}
 ]},
 {h:"The court's discretion to disapply the PI period (s.33)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Out of time if equitable", body:"The court may allow a **PI or fatal accident** claim to go ahead **out of time** if it would be **equitable**, weighing the **prejudice to each side**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"s.33(3) factors (1 to 3)", body:"The **length of, and reasons for, the delay**; the effect of the delay on the **evidence** (cogency); the **defendant's conduct** after the cause of action arose (e.g. not responding to requests for information)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"s.33(3) factors (4 to 6)", body:"Any **disability** of the claimant arising **after accrual**; whether the claimant acted **promptly and reasonably** once they knew they might have a claim; steps taken to get **medical, legal or other expert advice**, and the advice received."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Only for personal injury", body:"s.33 is **only** for PI, fatal accident and similar claims. There is **no** equivalent discretion for ordinary **contract** or **property damage** claims."}
 ]},
 {h:"Latent damage in negligence (ss.14A and 14B)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What latent damage is", body:"**Hidden damage** the claimant could not discover at first, e.g. cracks caused by defective foundations, or a loss caused by negligent financial or legal advice that only comes to light years later."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The later of: 6 years from the damage", body:"For **negligence** claims that are **not** for personal injury, the claimant has the **later** of **6 years** from the date the cause of action accrued (the damage), or the 3-year period on the next card."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Or: 3 years from the starting date", body:"**3 years** from the **starting date**: the date the claimant had the knowledge needed to sue (that the damage was **significant**, that it was **attributable** to the defendant's negligence, and **who the defendant was**) and the right to sue."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Negligence only (s.14A)", body:"s.14A applies only to claims in the **tort of negligence**. It does **not** apply to **contract** claims, even where the contract imposes a duty of care."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A surveyor negligently misses subsidence in a 2012 survey. The buyers suffer loss on completion in 2012 but only discover the problem in June 2023. Until when is the negligence claim in time?", body:"**Until June 2026** (3 years from knowledge), as that date is within **15 years** of the 2012 negligent survey (the longstop expires in 2027)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The longstop: 15 years (s.14B)", body:"An **absolute bar 15 years** from the date of the **negligent act or omission** (**not the damage**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The longstop cannot be extended", body:"It applies **even if the claimant has not yet discovered** the damage, and the court **cannot extend** it."}
 ]},
 {h:"Other periods to know", cards:[
   {t:"compare", i:"", title:"Limitation periods at a glance", cols:["Claim","Period","Time runs from","Statute"], rows:[
     ["Simple contract","6 years","Breach","LA 1980 s.5"],
     ["Contract by deed","12 years","Breach","LA 1980 s.8"],
     ["Tort (not PI)","6 years","Accrual (for negligence, when damage occurs)","LA 1980 s.2"],
     ["Personal injury","3 years","Later of injury or date of knowledge; s.33 discretion","LA 1980 ss.11, 14, 33"],
     ["Latent damage (negligence, not PI)","Later of 6 years from damage or 3 years from knowledge","Subject to 15-year longstop from the negligent act","LA 1980 ss.14A, 14B"],
     ["Defamation (libel and slander)","1 year","Publication; court discretion to extend","LA 1980 ss.4A, 32A"],
     ["Defective products (Consumer Protection Act 1987)","3 years","Later of damage or knowledge; 10-year longstop from when the product was put into circulation","LA 1980 s.11A"],
     ["Contribution between people liable for the same damage","2 years","Date of the judgment or settlement fixing the claimant's liability","LA 1980 s.10"],
     ["Recovering land","12 years","When the right accrued","LA 1980 s.15"],
     ["Enforcing a judgment (by action)","6 years","Date of judgment","LA 1980 s.24"]]},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Defamation: 1 year (ss.4A, 32A)", body:"**Defamation** (libel and slander): **1 year** from **publication**, with a **court discretion to extend**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Defective products: 3 years, 10-year longstop (s.11A)", body:"Under the **Consumer Protection Act 1987**: **3 years** from the later of damage or knowledge, with a **10-year longstop** from when the product was **put into circulation**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Contribution: 2 years (s.10)", body:"**Contribution** between people liable for the same damage: **2 years** from the date of the **judgment** or **settlement** fixing the claimant's liability."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Recovering land: 12 years (s.15)", body:"**12 years** from when the right accrued."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Enforcing a judgment by action: 6 years (s.24)", body:"**6 years** from the **date of judgment**."}
 ]},
 {h:"When time is postponed, paused or extended", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Disability (s.28): time does not run", body:"Time does not run while the claimant is a **child** (under 18) or **lacks mental capacity** to conduct the proceedings (under the Mental Capacity Act 2005), **if** they were under that disability **when the cause of action accrued**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Disability: time starts when it ends", body:"A child therefore has until their **21st birthday** for a **PI** claim (3 years from turning 18) and their **24th birthday** for a **contract or ordinary tort** claim (6 years from turning 18)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Capacity lost later: time keeps running", body:"If capacity is lost **after** time has started, **time keeps running** (but this can be relevant to the **s.33** discretion)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Fraud, deliberate concealment or mistake (s.32)", body:"Where the claim is based on the defendant's **fraud**, a relevant fact has been **deliberately concealed** by the defendant, or the claim is for relief from the consequences of a **mistake**, time does not start until the claimant **discovered** it or **could with reasonable diligence** have discovered it."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A hidden breach counts as concealment", body:"**Deliberately committing a breach of duty** in circumstances where it is **unlikely to be discovered** counts as deliberate concealment."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Acknowledgment or part payment (ss.29-30)", body:"For a **debt** or other **liquidated** (fixed) sum, a **written, signed acknowledgment** of the debt, or a **part payment**, by the debtor **restarts the period** from that date."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Only before the period expires", body:"The acknowledgment or part payment must happen **before** the period expires."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Standstill agreements", body:"The parties can **agree in writing** that time will **stop running** for an agreed period while they **negotiate or try ADR**. This avoids issuing proceedings just to protect the position."}
 ]},
 {h:"Counting the period", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Day one is excluded", body:"The day the cause of action arose is **excluded** (**Marren v Dawson Bentley [1961]**: a claimant injured on **8 November 1954** who issued on **8 November 1957** was **in time** for a 3-year period)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The last day is the anniversary", body:"So the **last day to issue is the anniversary** of the accrual date: for a contract breached on **10 May 2020**, the claim form must be issued by **10 May 2026**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Court office closed? Next open day", body:"If the court office is **closed** on the last day (e.g. a weekend or bank holiday), a claim issued on the **next day it is open** is in time."}
 ]},
 {h:"Practical points", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Protocols do not stop time", body:"**Pre-action protocols do not stop time running.** If limitation is close, the claimant should **issue protectively** (issue the claim form to stop time running) and then ask the court, or agree with the defendant, to **stay** (pause) the claim while the protocol steps are completed."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Or a standstill agreement", body:"A **standstill agreement** is the alternative to issuing protectively."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"After expiry: new claims and parties", body:"Once limitation has expired, **adding new claims** by amendment (**CPR 17.4**) and **adding or substituting parties** (**CPR 19.6**, **LA 1980 s.35**) are tightly restricted. See **Statements of case** and **Issuing and serving proceedings; changing parties**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Pick the most generous cause of action", body:"Where the facts support both **contract and negligence** (e.g. a professional adviser or a builder), the negligence claim may still be in time when the contract claim is not, because tort time runs from **damage** and **s.14A** may help."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: a question gives a date of breach and a date the client \"first realised\". What do you check first?", body:"The **type of claim**: **knowledge only matters for PI and for latent damage in negligence**. For **contract**, ignore the discovery date and count **6 years from breach**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: an injured child. When does the 3 years start?", body:"From their **18th birthday**, not from the accident. A claim brought by a **litigation friend** (an adult who conducts the case for the child) before then is fine but **not compulsory**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Watch out: the longstop and s.33", body:"The **15-year longstop** runs from the **negligent act**, not from the damage or discovery, and it **cannot be disapplied**. **s.33** discretion is only available for **personal injury** (and fatal accident) claims."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Fairground room 01 (Bumper cars). Source: src/content/dr.js, topic dr-options (notes only). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["dr/dr-options"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"First job: how should it be resolved?", body:"When two people or businesses fall out, a solicitor's first job is to advise **how** the dispute should be resolved. There are **three main routes** on the SQE1 syllabus: litigation, arbitration and mediation."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Litigation: going to court", body:"A **judge** decides, applying the **Civil Procedure Rules (CPR)**, the rulebook for civil cases in the County Court, High Court and Court of Appeal."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Arbitration: a private 'court'", body:"A private \"court\" chosen by the parties. An **arbitrator** decides, and the decision (the **award**) is binding. It only happens if the parties have **agreed** to arbitrate."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Mediation: helped to settle", body:"A neutral **mediator** helps the parties negotiate their own settlement. **Nobody imposes a decision.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Two of them are ADR", body:"Arbitration and mediation are both forms of **ADR** (**alternative dispute resolution**: any way of resolving a dispute other than a court trial)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"How the exam tests it", body:"The exam usually gives a client's priorities (**privacy, cost, speed, enforcement abroad, urgency**) and asks which route fits best, or tests a specific rule such as the court's power to order ADR."}
 ]},
 {h:"Litigation", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What litigation is", body:"Proceedings issued in court under the **CPR**. The **court controls the timetable**, and a judge gives a binding **judgment**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Public", body:"Hearings are normally in **open court** and judgments are **published**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Binding and enforceable", body:"The court has its **own enforcement methods** (see **Enforcement of money judgments**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Appeals", body:"There is a **full appeal system** (see **Appeals**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Precedent", body:"Higher-court decisions **bind later cases**: useful if a client wants a **point of law settled**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Costs: the loser pays", body:"The general rule is that the **loser pays the winner's costs** (see **Costs, Part 36 offers and security for costs**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No agreement needed", body:"A claimant can **always issue a claim** unless there is a valid **arbitration agreement** (see the stay under s.9)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Does a claimant need the other side to agree before going to court?", body:"**No.** A claimant can always issue a claim, **unless** there is a valid **arbitration agreement**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Coercive powers: interim remedies", body:"The court can grant **interim remedies**: orders before trial, such as an **injunction** (an order to do or stop doing something)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Coercive powers: summary judgment and non-parties", body:"The court can give **summary judgment** (judgment without a trial where the other side has no real prospect of success), and make orders against **non-parties** (e.g. to disclose documents)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Disadvantages of litigation", body:"It can be **slow and expensive**, it is **public**, the parties **cannot choose the judge**, and it tends to **end business relationships**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Arbitration: what it is and where the law is", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What arbitration is", body:"A **private, binding** process in which one or more **arbitrators** (the **tribunal**) decide the dispute and make an **award**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Where the law is", body:"Arbitration is governed by the **Arbitration Act 1996 (AA 1996)**, as amended by the **Arbitration Act 2025 (AA 2025)**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The arbitration agreement", body:"It depends on an **arbitration agreement**: usually an **arbitration clause** in the main contract (covering **future** disputes), or a **separate agreement** made after a dispute arises."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"In writing (s.5)", body:"For the AA 1996 to apply, the agreement must be **in writing** (**s.5**). 'In writing' is widely defined, including agreements **recorded by any means**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"General principles (s.1): fair, no waste", body:"Disputes are resolved **fairly** by an **impartial** tribunal **without unnecessary delay or expense**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"General principles (s.1): the parties decide", body:"**Party autonomy** (the parties decide how it is run), and the courts should **not intervene** except as the Act allows."}
 ]},
 {h:"Key features of arbitration", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Party autonomy: the parties choose", body:"The parties choose the **arbitrator(s)**, the **seat** and the **rules** and procedure."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Choosing the arbitrator(s)", body:"Often a **specialist** (e.g. an engineer in a construction dispute or a shipping expert). If the parties do not agree the number, there is a **sole arbitrator** (**s.15(3)**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The seat", body:"The legal \"**home**\" of the arbitration. It decides **which country's courts supervise it** and its **procedural law**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The rules and procedure", body:"E.g. the rules of the **LCIA** (London Court of International Arbitration) or the **ICC** (International Chamber of Commerce), and the procedure (**documents only**, or **with a hearing**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Private and confidential", body:"Hearings are **not public** and the award is **not published**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The tribunal's general duty (s.33)", body:"Act **fairly and impartially**, giving each party a **reasonable opportunity to put its case**, and adopt procedures that **avoid unnecessary delay and expense**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Final and binding award", body:"The award is **final and binding**, with **very limited court challenges**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Costs follow the event", body:"The tribunal can award **costs**, normally on the basis that **costs follow the event** (the loser pays)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Enforcement in England (s.66)", body:"An award can be **enforced in England like a judgment** with the **court's permission** (**s.66**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Enforcement abroad: the New York Convention", body:"The **New York Convention 1958** is a treaty under which **over 170 countries** recognise and enforce each other's arbitration awards. It makes awards much easier to enforce abroad than many court judgments."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Why is arbitration attractive in a cross-border dispute?", body:"Because of the **New York Convention**: over 170 countries enforce each other's awards, so an award is much easier to enforce abroad than many court judgments. This is a **key advantage** in cross-border disputes."}
 ]},
 {h:"Stay of court proceedings (s.9)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What the stay is for", body:"If one party issues **court proceedings** about a matter covered by a valid arbitration agreement, the other can apply for a **stay** (a pause of the court case) so the arbitration goes ahead."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The stay is mandatory", body:"The court **must** grant it, unless the arbitration agreement is **null and void, inoperative or incapable of being performed**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Acknowledge, then apply before any step", body:"The applicant must first file an **acknowledgment of service** (the form a defendant files to confirm it has received the claim) and apply **before taking any step to answer the substantive claim** (e.g. filing a defence)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A defence first loses the stay", body:"**Filing a defence first loses the right to a stay.**"}
 ]},
 {h:"Challenging an award in court", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"28 days (s.70(3))", body:"Any challenge must be brought within **28 days** of the award (**s.70(3)**), after using any available **arbitral appeal or review** process."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"s.67: lack of substantive jurisdiction", body:"E.g. there was **no valid arbitration agreement**, the tribunal was **not properly constituted**, or the dispute was **outside the agreement**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"s.68: serious irregularity", body:"A **serious irregularity** causing **substantial injustice**, e.g. the tribunal **failed in its s.33 duty**, **exceeded its powers**, or the award was obtained by **fraud**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"s.69: appeal on a point of law", body:"On a point of **English law only**. It needs the **agreement of all parties** or the **court's leave** (permission)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"s.69 can be excluded", body:"An s.69 appeal can be **excluded by agreement**. Choosing institutional rules such as the **LCIA or ICC rules** usually excludes it."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No reopening the facts", body:"The court **cannot reopen** the tribunal's **findings of fact**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A party says the arbitrator believed the wrong witness. Can the court look again?", body:"**No.** The court cannot reopen the tribunal's **findings of fact**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Arbitration Act 2025: the changes", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"In force 1 August 2025", body:"For arbitral proceedings **commenced on or after 1 August 2025** (whenever the arbitration agreement was made)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Law of the arbitration agreement (s.6A)", body:"The law **expressly chosen** by the parties for the **arbitration agreement itself**; otherwise the **law of the seat**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The main contract's law does not count", body:"A governing-law clause for the **main contract** does **not** count as a choice of law for the arbitration agreement."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Arbitrator's duty of disclosure (s.23A)", body:"An arbitrator must disclose any circumstances that might reasonably give rise to **justifiable doubts about their impartiality**, including things they **ought reasonably to be aware of**, both **before appointment and throughout**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Summary disposal (s.39A)", body:"Unless the parties agree otherwise, the tribunal may, on a party's application, decide a claim, defence or issue **summarily** if that party has **no real prospect of succeeding**, after giving the parties a reasonable opportunity to make representations (similar to court summary judgment)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Emergency arbitrators", body:"**Recognised in the Act**. Their orders can be **supported by the court** (e.g. by enforcing a peremptory order: a final 'last chance' order made after a party has ignored an earlier one)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Court support against third parties (s.44)", body:"The court's powers to support an arbitration (e.g. **preserving evidence or assets**, **interim injunctions**) can be used against **third parties** (non-parties to the arbitration)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Narrower jurisdiction challenges (s.67)", body:"Where the party **took part** in the arbitration: **no new grounds or new evidence** unless they could not reasonably have been raised before the tribunal, and evidence is **not reheard**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Arbitrator immunity extended", body:"An arbitrator is **not liable for resigning** unless the resignation was **unreasonable**, and is not liable for the **costs of an application to remove them** unless they acted in **bad faith**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"English seller, French buyer: \"governed by French law, arbitrated in London\". Starts October 2025. Which law governs the arbitration agreement?", body:"**English law** (the **law of the seat**). The French-law clause covers only the **main contract**, and there is no express choice for the arbitration agreement."}
 ]},
 {h:"Disadvantages of arbitration", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Can be expensive", body:"The parties pay the **arbitrators' fees** and **hearing venue**, on top of lawyers' fees."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No precedent, little appeal", body:"**No binding precedent**, and **limited rights of appeal** (a disadvantage if the tribunal gets it wrong)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No power over third parties", body:"The tribunal has **no power over third parties** (it cannot join other parties without consent), and needs the **court's help** for coercive measures."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Needs agreement", body:"Only possible if **both parties agree**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Mediation: what it is", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What mediation is", body:"A **voluntary**, **confidential** process in which a neutral **mediator** helps the parties **negotiate a settlement**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The mediator does not decide", body:"The mediator **does not decide** the dispute and **cannot impose** an outcome."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Together, then caucuses", body:"Typically the mediator meets the parties **together** and then **separately** (\"**caucuses**\"), testing the strengths and weaknesses of each side's position."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Without prejudice and confidential", body:"Communications are **without prejudice**: what is said in a genuine attempt to settle **cannot later be used as evidence** in court (see **Disclosure, privilege and without prejudice**). Mediation agreements also usually contain **confidentiality terms**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Nothing binding until signed", body:"**Nothing is binding until** the parties sign a written **settlement agreement**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"The parties shake hands on a deal at the mediation. Is it binding?", body:"**Not yet.** Nothing is binding until they sign a **written settlement agreement**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Mediation: advantages and disadvantages", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Advantages: quick, cheap, flexible", body:"Mediation is **quick, cheap, flexible** and **private**, with **high settlement rates**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Advantage: keeps relationships", body:"It **preserves business relationships**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Advantage: outcomes a court cannot order", body:"E.g. an **apology**, a **discount on future orders**, a **new supply contract**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Disadvantage: no guaranteed result", body:"There is **no guaranteed result**, so **costs may be wasted** if it fails."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Disadvantage: fishing", body:"A party may use it to \"**fish**\" for information about the other side's case."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Disadvantage: when it is unsuitable", body:"Where a party needs an **urgent injunction** or a **binding ruling on a point of law**, or where one side **refuses to engage in good faith**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Recording a settlement", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Before proceedings: a contract", body:"A **binding contract** (the settlement agreement). If broken, the innocent party **sues on that contract**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"After proceedings: consent order", body:"The agreed terms are put into a **court order** and can be **enforced directly as a judgment**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"After proceedings: Tomlin order", body:"The claim is **stayed** (paused) on terms set out in a **confidential schedule**, with **liberty to apply** (permission to go back to court) to enforce them."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Why choose a Tomlin order?", body:"It is useful for **confidentiality**, or for **terms a court could not itself order**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Child or protected party: court approval", body:"A settlement for a **child** or **protected party** (an adult who lacks capacity) needs **court approval**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Proceedings have started. The parties settle and want the terms kept confidential. How do they record it?", body:"A **Tomlin order**: the claim is stayed on terms in a **confidential schedule**, with **liberty to apply**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Small claims mediation", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Automatic referral since 22 May 2024", body:"Since **22 May 2024**, most defended **specified money claims** (claims for a fixed sum) on the **small claims track** (generally claims up to **£10,000**) are **automatically referred** to mediation."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Free, one hour, by telephone", body:"A free, **one-hour telephone mediation** with the **HMCTS Small Claims Mediation Service** (HMCTS is HM Courts & Tribunals Service)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Attendance is compulsory", body:"A party who fails to attend **without good reason** can face **sanctions**, including its claim or defence being **struck out** (thrown out without a trial)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Other forms of ADR", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Negotiation", body:"The parties or their lawyers deal **directly**. **Cheapest and most common**; offers are usually made **without prejudice**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Early neutral evaluation (ENE)", body:"An independent person (sometimes a judge) gives a **non-binding view** of the likely outcome, to bring the parties to realistic positions."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Expert determination", body:"An **independent expert** decides a **technical issue** (e.g. a valuation or an accounting dispute). Usually **binding under the contract**, with very limited grounds of challenge."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Adjudication", body:"A fast (usually **28-day**) decision used in **construction** disputes. It is **binding until** the dispute is finally decided by litigation or arbitration (\"**pay now, argue later**\")."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Ombudsmen", body:"**Free schemes** for **consumer complaints** in sectors such as financial services and legal services."}
 ]},
 {h:"ADR and the courts: can a court force the parties to use ADR?", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Halsey v Milton Keynes General NHS Trust [2004]: facts", body:"A widow sued a hospital after her husband died. The hospital **refused her offers to mediate** and **won at trial**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Halsey: the rule", body:"The Court of Appeal said the court **could not compel** unwilling parties to mediate (**that part is now overruled**), but that a **winning party** who **unreasonably refused** ADR can be **penalised in costs**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Halsey factors (1 to 3)", body:"Was a refusal unreasonable? Look at: the **nature of the dispute** (some issues, e.g. a point of law, are unsuited to ADR); the **merits** (a party reasonably believing it has a very strong case may refuse); whether **other settlement methods** have been tried."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Halsey factors (4 to 6)", body:"Whether the **costs** of ADR would be disproportionately high; whether **delay** would be prejudicial; whether ADR had a **reasonable prospect of success**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"PGF II SA v OMFS Co 1 Ltd [2013]: facts", body:"A landlord sued its commercial tenant for dilapidations (disrepair) and **twice invited the tenant to mediate**; the tenant simply **ignored** the invitations. The landlord later accepted the tenant's Part 36 offer (a formal settlement offer with set costs consequences under CPR Part 36) after the deadline, so the tenant would normally have recovered its costs from then on."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"PGF II: the rule", body:"**Silence** in response to an invitation to ADR is **itself unreasonable**. The tenant was **deprived of those costs**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Churchill v Merthyr Tydfil County Borough Council [2023]: facts", body:"Mr Churchill sued the council in nuisance (unlawful interference with a neighbour's use of their land) because **Japanese knotweed** had spread from its land to his, **without first using the council's internal complaints procedure**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Churchill: the rule", body:"A court **can lawfully order** the parties to engage in a non-court dispute resolution process, or **stay** proceedings for that purpose, **provided** the order does not impair the **very essence of the right to a fair trial**, pursues a **legitimate aim**, and is **proportionate**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Churchill: no fixed rules", body:"The Court of Appeal **declined to lay down fixed rules**. The **Halsey factors remain relevant**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Can a court today order unwilling parties to try ADR?", body:"**Yes** (Churchill), provided the order does not impair the very essence of the right to a fair trial, pursues a legitimate aim and is proportionate. The old Halsey view that it could not compel them is **overruled**."}
 ]},
 {h:"CPR changes from 1 October 2024 (implementing Churchill)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The overriding objective (CPR 1.1)", body:"The **overriding objective** (the court's guiding aim of dealing with cases justly and at proportionate cost) now expressly includes **promoting or using ADR**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The court can order ADR (CPR 3.1)", body:"The court's case management powers include **ordering** the parties to engage in ADR (**CPR 3.1**), and the court **must consider ADR** when giving directions."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Costs (CPR 44.2)", body:"On costs, the court considers whether a party **failed to comply with an order to engage in ADR** or **unreasonably failed to engage** in ADR."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: Kim and Lee", body:"Kim wins her contract claim against Lee at trial, but before trial she **ignored two letters** from Lee proposing mediation. **Kim still wins the case**, but the court may **reduce or refuse her costs** (or even make her pay some of Lee's), because her silence was unreasonable (PGF II)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: what is the penalty for unreasonably refusing ADR?", body:"A **costs** sanction, **not** losing the case or having the claim struck out. Watch for options saying the winning party \"will be ordered to pay all the losing party's costs\" or \"loses the right to judgment\"."}
 ]},
 {h:"Comparing the three options", cards:[
   {t:"compare", i:"", title:"Litigation, arbitration and mediation", cols:["Feature","Litigation","Arbitration","Mediation"], rows:[
     ["Needs agreement?","No","Yes (arbitration agreement, in writing)","Yes (voluntary), but the court can order ADR"],
     ["Who decides?","A judge","Arbitrator(s) chosen by the parties","The parties themselves; the mediator only facilitates"],
     ["Binding?","Yes (judgment)","Yes (award), very limited challenge","Only once a settlement agreement is signed"],
     ["Privacy","Public","Private and confidential","Private, without prejudice and confidential"],
     ["Speed and cost","Often slow; costs can be high","Can be quicker, but arbitrators' fees and venue are extra","Fastest and cheapest"],
     ["Interim relief and third parties","Full powers (injunctions, summary judgment, non-party orders)","Limited; court support under s.44","None"],
     ["Appeal","Full appeal system","Only ss.67-69 AA 1996, 28 days","None (no decision to appeal)"],
     ["Enforcement","Court enforcement; abroad depends on treaties","New York Convention (170+ countries)","Sue on the settlement contract, or enforce a consent or Tomlin order"],
     ["Relationship","Usually damaged","Somewhat adversarial","Often preserved"]]},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Which route is the fastest and cheapest, and which usually preserves the relationship?", body:"**Mediation** on both counts. Litigation usually **damages** the relationship; arbitration is **somewhat adversarial**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Choosing the right option", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"When arbitration suits", body:"Clients wanting **confidentiality**, a **specialist decision-maker**, a **final** result with little room for appeal, and **international enforcement**; but only if there is (or both sides will make) an **arbitration agreement**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"When mediation suits", body:"Clients wanting **speed**, **low cost**, privacy and to **keep the commercial relationship**, where both sides are willing to compromise. It can **run alongside litigation**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"When litigation suits", body:"Clients who need **urgent interim relief**, **summary judgment** against a hopeless opponent, orders against **third parties**, a **binding precedent** on a point of law, or where the other side **will not agree** to anything else."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A UK software company and a Brazilian customer fall out over a licence. It wants privacy, a decision-maker who understands software, and an award it can enforce in Brazil. Which route?", body:"**Arbitration** (confidential, specialist arbitrator, **New York Convention** enforcement), assuming the contract has an **arbitration clause**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: the defendant has been sued in court despite an arbitration clause. What should it do?", body:"File an **acknowledgment of service** (the form confirming receipt of the claim) and apply for a **mandatory stay under s.9** **before filing a defence**. Taking a step in the action (such as serving a defence) **loses the stay**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exam tip: don't mix up the three", body:"Distinguish **mediation** (non-binding, the parties decide) from **arbitration** (binding, the arbitrator decides) and from **expert determination** (binding decision on a technical issue). Options often describe one process using the other's features."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Watch out: the main contract's law", body:"Since the Arbitration Act 2025, a choice of foreign law for the **main contract** does **not** decide the law of the **arbitration agreement**. With no express choice for the agreement itself, the **law of the seat** applies."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Fairground room 04 (Carousel). Source: src/content/dr.js topic dr-parties-law (notes only). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["dr/dr-parties-law"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Checks before issuing", body:"Before issuing a claim, a solicitor must check that the client has a **good claim** against the **right defendant**, that suing is **worthwhile**, and, if the dispute has a foreign element, **which country's courts** can hear it and **which country's law** applies."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Merits", body:"Is there a **cause of action** (a recognised legal basis for a claim) and can each element be proved?"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Parties", body:"Who should sue and be sued, and in what name? Special rules apply to children, people lacking capacity, partnerships, companies and insolvent parties."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Is it worth it?", body:"Can the defendant pay, and how will the client fund the case?"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Courts and law: two separate questions", body:"**Jurisdiction** (which country's **courts**) and **applicable law** (which country's **law**) are two separate questions."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Welsh language", body:"The right to use **Welsh** in civil proceedings in Wales."}
 ]},
 {h:"Checklist before issuing", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Cause of action and evidence", body:"Can each element be proved on the **balance of probabilities** (more likely than not)?"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Is the defendant worth suing?", body:"Is the defendant correctly identified, and **worth suing**? Check solvency, assets and **insurance** (a judgment against a person with no money is worthless). Company searches, Land Registry searches and credit checks help."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"In time, and protocol followed?", body:"**Limitation**: is the claim in time? (see **Limitation periods**). **Pre-action protocol**: complied with? (see **Pre-action conduct and protocols**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Which courts, which law, which court and track", body:"**Jurisdiction and applicable law**, if there is a foreign element. The **court** and likely **track** (small claims, fast, intermediate or multi-track) (see **Where to start** and **Case management**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Funding and the costs risk", body:"How will the case be **funded**? The **costs risk**: the loser usually pays the winner's costs."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Proportionality", body:"Will the cost of litigation be **proportionate** to what is at stake? Is **ADR** better?"}
 ]},
 {h:"Causes of action and remedies: contract", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A contract", body:"The claimant must prove a **contract**: offer, acceptance, consideration, intention to create legal relations."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Term and breach", body:"The relevant **term** (express or implied) and **breach** of that term."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Causation and remoteness", body:"**Causation**: the breach caused the loss. The loss is **not too remote**: of a type within the reasonable contemplation of the parties when the contract was made (**Hadley v Baxendale**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Mitigation", body:"The claimant took reasonable steps to **mitigate** (reduce) its loss."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Contract damages", body:"Damages aim to put the claimant in the position it would have been in **had the contract been performed**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Causes of action and remedies: negligence", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What the claimant must prove", body:"A **duty of care**, **breach** of that duty, **causation** (factual and legal), and loss that is **not too remote** (a reasonably foreseeable type of damage)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Defences", body:"**Contributory negligence** (damages reduced for the claimant's share of the blame), **consent** (volenti) and **illegality**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Negligence damages", body:"Damages aim to put the claimant in the position it would have been in **had the tort not been committed**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Which damages aim at 'as if the contract had been performed', and which at 'as if the tort had not been committed'?", body:"**Contract**: as if the contract had been performed. **Negligence**: as if the tort had not been committed."}
 ]},
 {h:"Causes of action and remedies: interest", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Interest is not automatic", body:"It must be **claimed and pleaded** (see **Statements of case**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A contractual rate", body:"If the contract sets a rate for late payment, claim **that** rate. A substantial contractual remedy for late payment means statutory interest under the 1998 Act does not apply (**s.8**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Late Payment of Commercial Debts (Interest) Act 1998", body:"For debts under contracts between **businesses** for goods or services: statutory interest at **8% above base rate** (the Bank of England base rate) plus fixed compensation (**£40, £70 or £100** depending on the size of the debt)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The court's discretion", body:"**s.35A Senior Courts Act 1981** (High Court) or **s.69 County Courts Act 1984** (County Court), usually at a commercial rate. It is the fallback where there is no contractual or statutory right."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Judgment interest", body:"Once judgment is given, **judgment interest** at **8%** a year runs on the judgment debt (High Court judgments, and County Court judgments of **£5,000 or more**)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Funding the claim", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Conditional fee agreement (CFA)", body:"'No win, no (or lower) fee'. If the client wins, the solicitor gets normal fees plus a **success fee** (a percentage uplift on base costs, up to **100%**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Who pays the CFA success fee?", body:"Since **April 2013** the success fee is **not recoverable** from the losing opponent. The client pays it out of damages (capped at **25%** of certain damages in PI claims)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Damages-based agreement (DBA)", body:"The solicitor takes an agreed **percentage of the damages** if the client wins, capped at **25%** in PI, **35%** in employment and **50%** in other cases. Nothing is payable if the client loses."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"DBA: the opponent's costs are set off", body:"Any **costs** the opponent pays are **set off** against the DBA payment, so the client pays only the balance out of damages. Disbursements (out-of-pocket expenses such as court fees and experts' fees) the opponent pays are not charged to the client again (**DBA Regulations 2013 reg 4**)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A 30% DBA on £100,000 damages. The opponent pays £12,000 costs. What does the client pay?", body:"The DBA payment is **£30,000**. Less the **£12,000** costs, the client pays **£18,000** and keeps **£82,000**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Private retainer", body:"The client pays the solicitor's fees as the case goes on (usually **hourly rates**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Before-the-event (BTE) insurance", body:"**Legal expenses insurance** bought **before** any dispute, often as an add-on to home or car insurance."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"After-the-event (ATE) insurance", body:"Bought **after** the dispute arises, mainly to cover the risk of paying the **other side's costs** and the client's own disbursements. The premium is generally **not recoverable** from the opponent."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Third-party litigation funding", body:"A **commercial funder** pays the costs in return for a **share of any proceeds**. A funder can be ordered to pay costs as a **non-party** (see **Costs**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"After PACCAR", body:"After **R (PACCAR) v Competition Appeal Tribunal** [2023] many funding agreements based on a percentage of damages were treated as **DBAs**. The government has said it will legislate to reverse this, but **no legislation was in force at September 2026**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Legal aid and union funding", body:"**Legal aid** is very rarely available for civil money claims. **Trade union** or employer funding may be available, e.g. for workplace injuries."}
 ]},
 {h:"Parties: who sues and is sued, and in what name", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Individual", body:"Sues and is sued in their own **full name**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Sole trader", body:"Sues in own name; can be sued in own name or **trading name** (e.g. 'Ann Smith trading as Smith's Bakery')."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Partnership", body:"Sues and is sued in the **firm name** (the partners can be required to disclose the names of all partners)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Company or LLP", body:"In its **registered name** (a separate legal person). An LLP is a limited liability partnership."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Child or protected party", body:"A **child** (under 18) and a **protected party** (an adult lacking capacity under the Mental Capacity Act 2005) sue and are sued through a **litigation friend**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Deceased person's estate", body:"By or against the **personal representatives** (executors or administrators)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Children and protected parties (CPR 21)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The litigation friend", body:"An **adult** who conducts the case for the child or protected party, usually a parent or relative."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Who can be one", body:"They must be able to conduct proceedings **fairly and competently**, have **no interest adverse** to the child or protected party, and (if acting for the claimant) **undertake to pay any costs** the claimant may be ordered to pay."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A child without a litigation friend", body:"A child can apply to conduct proceedings without a litigation friend with the **court's permission**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Settlements need the court's approval", body:"Any **settlement** of a claim by or against a child or protected party is **not binding unless approved by the court**. The court protects the money (e.g. it is invested by the court until the child is **18**)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A parent agrees a settlement of her child's claim. Is it binding?", body:"**Not unless the court approves it.**"}
 ]},
 {h:"Insolvent parties", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Company in administration", body:"Administration is an insolvency procedure aimed at rescuing the company. A **moratorium** (a freeze on legal action against the company) applies: no claim can be started or continued against it without the **administrator's consent** or the **court's permission**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Company in compulsory liquidation", body:"Wound up by court order: no claim without the **court's permission**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Bankrupt individual", body:"Their claims (other than purely personal ones such as PI pain and suffering) pass to the **trustee in bankruptcy** (the official who takes control of the bankrupt's property). Suing a bankrupt over a provable debt (a debt that can be claimed in the bankruptcy) needs the **court's permission**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Rarely worthwhile", body:"In practice, suing an insolvent defendant is rarely worthwhile: the claimant becomes an **unsecured creditor** (a creditor with no security over the debtor's assets, usually paid little or nothing)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Joining several parties", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Joining parties", body:"Two or more claimants or defendants can be joined in one claim where it is convenient to deal with all the issues together (**CPR 7.3, CPR 19**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Jointly and severally liable", body:"Each defendant is liable for the **whole** loss (e.g. two drivers who together caused a crash, or partners for a firm debt). The claimant can sue **any or all** of them and recover in full from any one, who then claims a **contribution** from the others."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: HaulCo's lorry", body:"A lorry driver employed by HaulCo crashes into Bea's car."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Whom should Bea sue?", body:"**HaulCo** (vicariously liable for its employee, i.e. liable for wrongs the employee commits in the course of employment, and insured), rather than, or as well as, the driver personally, because HaulCo is more likely to be able to pay."}
 ]},
 {h:"Jurisdiction: which country's courts?", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The Brussels rules have gone", body:"Since the UK left the EU, the EU jurisdiction rules (the **Brussels** regime) do not apply to proceedings started on or after **1 January 2021**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Common law: served while present", body:"The court has jurisdiction if the defendant is **served** with the claim form while present in England and Wales (even on a short visit)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Common law: submits", body:"Or if the defendant **submits** to the jurisdiction (e.g. files a defence without challenging jurisdiction; see **Responding to a claim**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Common law: served out of the jurisdiction", body:"Or if the defendant is served **out of the jurisdiction** with the court's **permission** (or, in some cases, without permission) under **CPR 6** (see **Issuing and serving proceedings**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Forum non conveniens", body:"The court may decline jurisdiction if another country's courts are clearly more appropriate (**forum non conveniens**, 'not the convenient forum')."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"2005 Hague Convention on Choice of Court Agreements", body:"The UK (in its own right from 1 January 2021) and other contracting states, including the **EU states**, must respect an **exclusive** choice of court clause in favour of a contracting state, and recognise and enforce the resulting judgment."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"2019 Hague Judgments Convention", body:"In force for the UK from **1 July 2025**. Judgments given in proceedings started after it applies between the two states (including the **EU states**) can be recognised and enforced in other contracting states, even without a choice of court clause, if a jurisdictional link in the Convention is met."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Courts and law are separate", body:"**Jurisdiction** (which **court**) and **applicable law** (which **law**) are separate. An English court can apply French law, and vice versa."}
 ]},
 {h:"Applicable law in contract: Rome I", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Rome I", body:"**Rome I** (Regulation 593/2008) decides which country's law governs a contract. With **Rome II**, it still applies in the UK as **assimilated law** (EU law kept as UK domestic law after Brexit; formerly called 'retained EU law')."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Habitual residence", body:"Where an individual acting in business has their **principal place of business**; for a company, its **place of central administration**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Freedom of choice (Art 3)", body:"The parties can choose the governing law, **expressly** or **clearly demonstrated** by the terms or circumstances (e.g. a choice of court clause can point to a choice of law)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No choice (Art 4(1)): goods, services, land", body:"**Sale of goods**: the law of the **seller's** habitual residence. **Provision of services**: the **service provider's** habitual residence. **Rights in land** (including tenancies): where the **land** is."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No choice (Art 4(1)): franchise and distribution", body:"**Franchise**: the **franchisee's** habitual residence. **Distribution**: the **distributor's** habitual residence."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Other contracts (Art 4(2))", body:"The habitual residence of the party who must perform the **characteristic performance** (the obligation that defines the contract, usually the non-money one)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Escape clause (Art 4(3))", body:"If the contract is **manifestly more closely connected** with another country, that law applies."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Consumer contracts (Art 6)", body:"If the business pursues or **directs its activities** to the consumer's country, the law of the **consumer's habitual residence** applies. A chosen law cannot deprive the consumer of the **mandatory protections** of their home law."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: German machinery", body:"A company based in Germany sells machinery to an English company; the contract has no choice of law clause."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Which law governs the machinery contract?", body:"**German law** (seller's habitual residence, Art 4(1)(a)), even if the case is heard in England."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: what do you look for first in a Rome I question?", body:"An **express choice** first. Only if there is none, identify the contract type (goods: **seller**; services: **provider**; land: **where the land is**). For a **consumer** contract, the consumer's home law protections cannot be removed."}
 ]},
 {h:"Applicable law in tort: Rome II", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Rome II", body:"**Rome II** (Regulation 864/2007) decides the law for **non-contractual** obligations such as negligence."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"General rule (Art 4(1))", body:"The law of the country where the **damage occurs**, regardless of where the event giving rise to it happened or where indirect consequences are felt."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Common habitual residence (Art 4(2))", body:"If the claimant and defendant are both **habitually resident in the same country** when the damage occurs, **that country's law** applies."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Escape clause (Art 4(3))", body:"The law of a country **manifestly more closely connected**, e.g. because of a pre-existing contract between the parties."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Choice (Art 14)", body:"The parties can agree the governing law **after** the event giving rise to the damage, or **before** it if all parties are pursuing a **commercial activity** and the agreement is **freely negotiated**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Excluded: defamation and privacy", body:"**Defamation and privacy** claims are excluded (common law rules apply)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What the applicable law covers", body:"Liability, defences, and the **existence, nature and assessment of damage** (so damages follow the applicable law). **Procedure** is always governed by the law of the court hearing the case (English procedure in an English court)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: two Leeds friends in Spain", body:"Two friends who both live in Leeds go on holiday to Spain; one injures the other in a car crash there."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Which law applies to the Spanish crash?", body:"**English law** (common habitual residence, Art 4(2)), even though the damage occurred in Spain."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: which Rome II rule do you check first?", body:"**Common habitual residence**: if both parties live in the same country, that law applies even though the accident happened elsewhere. Check this before applying the 'place of damage' rule."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Watch out: arbitration agreements", body:"Rome I governs the **main contract** only. The law of an **arbitration agreement** is set by the **Arbitration Act 1996 s.6A** (added by the Arbitration Act 2025): the law expressly chosen for it, otherwise the law of the **seat**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Welsh language in civil proceedings", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Welsh Language Act 1993 s.22", body:"Any party or witness may **speak Welsh** in legal proceedings in Wales."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The Practice Direction", body:"The **Practice Direction on the use of the Welsh language** in civil cases in or connected with Wales aims to treat English and Welsh on a basis of **equality**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Tell the court early", body:"In any case where a party or witness may use Welsh, the parties must **inform the court** so it can manage and list the case. Documents may be filed in **English or Welsh**, but the court must be told as soon as practicable if a Welsh document will be filed."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Directions questionnaire", body:"In the directions questionnaire (the form each party completes after a defence is filed, so the court can allocate the case to a track), each party must give details of any likely use of Welsh (witnesses giving oral evidence in Welsh, Welsh documents), even if the court has already been told."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Pre-trial checklist", body:"In the pre-trial checklist (the form confirming the case is ready for trial), confirm the intended use of Welsh and give any missing details."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Listing", body:"Where practicable, before a **Welsh-speaking judge**, and at a court with **simultaneous translation** facilities if translation is needed. An approved **interpreter** is arranged by the court."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The oath", body:"Every witness must be told they may take the oath or affirm (promise to tell the truth without a religious oath) in **Welsh or English**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Wholly in Welsh", body:"A hearing can be conducted **wholly in Welsh** without notice if all parties and witnesses agree."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Wasted costs", body:"A party (or its lawyer) whose failure to follow the Practice Direction causes **wasted costs** may be ordered to pay them."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: Welsh evidence in Swansea", body:"A claimant in a County Court claim in Swansea wants to give evidence in Welsh."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"What must her solicitor do, and does she need permission?", body:"**Her solicitor must tell the court** (at the latest in the directions questionnaire) so the case can be listed before a Welsh-speaking judge or with simultaneous translation. She does **not** need anyone's permission to use Welsh."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Britney's five questions", body:"1. **Good claim?** A cause of action, each element provable. 2. **Right defendant?** Correctly named for the kind of party. 3. **Worth suing?** Can they pay, and how is the case funded? 4. **Which courts?** Jurisdiction. 5. **Which law?** Applicable law, a separate question."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Fairground room 03 (Rollercoaster queue). Source: src/content/dr.js topic dr-pre-action (notes only). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["dr/dr-pre-action"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Talk before you sue", body:"Before a claimant can issue court proceedings, the parties are expected to **exchange information** about the claim and **try to settle** it."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Where the rules are", body:"The rules are in the **Practice Direction on Pre-Action Conduct and Protocols (PD-PAC)** and in a set of **pre-action protocols (PAPs)** for particular kinds of claim."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Not optional extras", body:"The court can punish a party who ignores them, usually with **costs or interest** sanctions."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Three questions to ask in the exam", body:"1. **Which set of rules applies?** A specific protocol, or the general PD-PAC?\n2. **What steps and time limits** does it require?\n3. **What happens if a party did not comply?**"}
 ]},
 {h:"Why pre-action conduct matters", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The objectives (PD-PAC para 3)", body:"Before proceedings, the parties should: **understand each other's position** and make decisions about how to proceed; try to **settle without proceedings**; consider a form of **ADR** (alternative dispute resolution, such as mediation or negotiation); support the **efficient management** of proceedings that cannot be avoided; **reduce the costs** of resolving the dispute."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Steps must be proportionate", body:"The court will not expect lengthy, expensive pre-action work in a **simple** case."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A last resort", body:"Starting proceedings should be a **last resort**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A simple case. Does the court expect lengthy, expensive pre-action work?", body:"**No.** Pre-action steps must be **proportionate**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Which rules apply?", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A specific protocol first", body:"A **specific protocol** applies where the claim falls within it."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Personal Injury", body:"Claims mainly **up to £25,000**. Low-value road traffic and employers' and public liability claims have their own **portal protocols** (online claims processes)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Clinical Disputes and Professional Negligence", body:"**Clinical Disputes**: medical negligence. **Professional Negligence**: e.g. claims against solicitors, accountants, surveyors."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Construction and Engineering, and Debt Claims", body:"**Construction and Engineering** disputes. **Debt Claims**: a business claiming a debt from an **individual**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The other protocols", body:"**Media and Communications** (defamation and privacy; formerly the Defamation protocol), **Housing Conditions** (disrepair), **Possession** claims by social landlords, **Judicial Review**, **Disease and Illness**, **Package Travel** and others."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No specific protocol: the PD-PAC", body:"Where **no specific protocol applies** (e.g. a business-to-business contract dispute), the parties follow the **PD-PAC** itself."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Litigants in person too", body:"The protocols apply to **litigants in person** (people acting without a lawyer) as well as represented parties."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Two companies argue over a supply contract. Which rules apply?", body:"**The PD-PAC.** A business-to-business contract dispute has no specific protocol."}
 ]},
 {h:"The PD-PAC steps (where no specific protocol applies)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Step 1: letter before claim", body:"The claimant sends a **letter before claim** with **concise details** of the claim: the basis of the claim and a summary of the facts."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What the letter asks for", body:"**What the claimant wants** from the defendant (e.g. payment of a sum) and, if money is claimed, **how the amount is calculated**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Key documents", body:"The letter sets out the **key documents** relied on (enclosed or listed)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Step 2: response in a reasonable time", body:"The defendant responds within a **reasonable time**: **14 days** in a straightforward case; **no more than 3 months** in a very complex one."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What the response says", body:"Whether the claim is **accepted** (in whole or part) and, if not, **why**, with the defendant's own version of events, key documents, and details of any **counterclaim** (a claim back against the claimant)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A very complex case. How long can the defendant take to respond?", body:"**No more than 3 months.** In a straightforward case, **14 days**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Step 3: disclosure of key documents", body:"**Both sides** disclose the key documents relevant to the issues."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Step 4: consider ADR", body:"The parties should consider whether negotiation or another form of ADR might resolve the dispute. If proceedings are issued, the court may require **evidence that ADR was considered**. A party who refuses ADR may face a **costs sanction** (see **Options: arbitration, mediation and litigation**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Step 5: review positions", body:"If the dispute does not settle, the parties review their positions to see if proceedings can be avoided and at least **narrow the issues** before the claimant issues."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Don't issue in the middle of the steps", body:"Parties should not issue while the steps are still being taken, **unless limitation is about to expire**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Specific protocols: key time limits", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"PD-PAC (general)", body:"Any claim with no specific protocol. Full response: **14 days** (simple) to **3 months** (complex)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Debt Claims PAP", body:"A business (including a sole trader or public body) claiming a debt from an **individual** (including a sole trader). The debtor has **30 days** to reply using the **reply form**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Personal Injury PAP", body:"PI claims (mainly fast track value: the track for most claims up to £25,000 that are too big for the small claims track). The defendant (or insurer) **acknowledges within 21 days**, then has **3 months** from acknowledgment to investigate and say whether **liability** is admitted."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Professional Negligence PAP", body:"Claims against professionals. **Acknowledge** the letter of claim within **21 days**; **letter of response** within **3 months of the acknowledgment**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Clinical Disputes PAP", body:"Medical negligence. **Acknowledge** within **14 days**; **letter of response** within **4 months of the letter of claim**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Construction and Engineering PAP", body:"Building and engineering disputes. **Letter of response** within **28 days** (any agreed extension of a step is capped at **28 days** in total), then a **pre-action meeting**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Clinical Disputes and Professional Negligence: the full response runs from what?", body:"**Clinical Disputes: 4 months from the letter of claim.** Professional Negligence: 3 months from the **acknowledgment**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Debt Claims PAP in more detail", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The creditor's letter of claim", body:"It must include the **amount** of the debt, whether **interest or charges** are being added, details of the **agreement**, and an **information sheet**, **reply form** and **financial statement** form for the debtor."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"30 days to reply", body:"The debtor has **30 days** to reply."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Debt advice or a dispute", body:"If the debtor says they are seeking **debt advice** or disputes the debt, the creditor should allow **reasonable extra time**. It should not start proceedings while the debtor is genuinely seeking advice or a reasonable repayment proposal is being considered."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Not business-to-business", body:"It does **not** apply to **business-to-business** debts (unless the debtor is a **sole trader**): those follow the **PD-PAC**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Personal Injury PAP in more detail", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The letter of claim", body:"The claimant sends a **letter of claim** with a clear summary of the **facts**, the **injuries** and the **financial losses**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"21 days, then 3 months", body:"The defendant replies within **21 days** identifying its **insurer**. The insurer then has **3 months** to investigate and give a reasoned response on **liability**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"One medical expert", body:"The parties should try to agree a **single medical expert**: the claimant proposes names and the defendant can object."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: Jo and the falling shelf", body:"Jo is injured by a falling shelf in a shop on **1 March**. Her solicitor sends a letter of claim on **1 June**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"When should the shop's insurer reply, and can Jo issue meanwhile?", body:"The insurer should **acknowledge by about 22 June** and say whether **liability** is admitted **within 3 months** of that acknowledgment. Jo should **not issue** in the meantime unless limitation is about to expire."}
 ]},
 {h:"Consequences of not complying", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Substantial, not minor slips", body:"The court looks at **substantial** non-compliance, not minor or technical slips (e.g. a response a few days late). It considers whether the breach caused real **prejudice** or extra cost."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Directions and costs orders", body:"The court can take non-compliance into account when giving **directions** (orders on how the case should proceed) and making **costs orders** (CPR 3.1(4)-(5), 44.2). Its options are in PD-PAC paras 13-16."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A stay", body:"A **stay** (pause) of the proceedings until the missed steps are taken."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Pay the other side's costs", body:"The party at fault can be ordered to pay **all or part of the other side's costs**, even on the **indemnity basis** (a more generous basis that favours the receiving party; see **Costs, Part 36 offers and security for costs**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Pay money into court", body:"The court can order a party to pay a sum of money **into court**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Striking out is very unusual", body:"**Striking out** a claim for pre-action failings alone is very unusual; it is a **last resort for abuse**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: the £40,000 claim", body:"A claimant issues a **£40,000** contract claim without sending any letter before claim. The defendant offers to pay in full within a week of service."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"What is the court likely to do about the £40,000 claimant?", body:"**Make the claimant pay the costs of the proceedings** (or deprive it of costs), because the case could have been settled with no proceedings at all."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Claimant at fault: less interest", body:"If the **claimant** is at fault, the court can deprive it of **interest** on damages for a period, or award a **lower rate** of interest."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Defendant at fault: higher interest", body:"If the **defendant** is at fault, the court can order interest on damages at a **higher rate**, up to **10% above base rate** (the Bank of England base rate), for a period."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Watch out: which party gets which interest sanction?", body:"A **claimant** at fault can **lose interest**. A **defendant** at fault can be ordered to pay **enhanced interest** (up to 10% above base rate). Do not mix them up."}
 ]},
 {h:"Other pre-action points", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Protocols don't stop time", body:"**Limitation**: protocols **do not stop time running**. If the limitation period is about to expire, the claimant should **issue protectively** (issue the claim form just to stop time running) and then apply to the court for a **stay** (or agree one) while the protocol steps are completed. A **standstill agreement** (a written agreement to pause limitation) is the alternative (see **Limitation periods**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Pre-action disclosure (CPR 31.16)", body:"A court order for documents **before proceedings start**, where both parties are likely to be parties to the claim and early disclosure is desirable (see **Disclosure, privilege and without prejudice**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Urgent injunctions", body:"**Urgent injunctions** can be sought **before a claim is issued** if the matter is urgent (see **Interim applications**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Offers before proceedings", body:"**Part 36 offers** (formal settlement offers with fixed costs consequences) and **without prejudice save as to costs** offers (offers the court cannot see until it decides costs) can be made **before proceedings start**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Pre-action investigation", body:"Before sending the letter before claim, the claimant's solicitor should check the client's **funding**, the defendant's **solvency** (whether it can pay) and **identity**, the **evidence** and **limitation** (see **Parties, causes of action, applicable law and Welsh language**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Preserving documents", body:"Once litigation is contemplated, **both parties** should preserve relevant documents, **including electronic ones**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: a business wants payment from an individual. Which rules, and how long?", body:"The **Debt Claims PAP**: the debtor (an individual, including a sole trader) has **30 days**. If **both parties are companies**, the **PD-PAC** applies, with a reasonable time (**14 days** in a simple case)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: an option says the claim 'will be struck out' for ignoring a protocol. Right?", body:"**Usually wrong.** The usual sanction is a **costs or interest** order, or a **stay** to let the steps be taken. Options saying the claim 'will be struck out' or 'cannot be issued' are usually wrong."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Britney's queue rules", body:"**Which rules?** A specific protocol, or the PD-PAC if none applies. **Which steps?** Letter before claim, response, key documents, consider ADR, review positions, within the protocol's time limits. **Which sanctions?** Usually costs, interest or a stay; striking out is very unusual."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Source: src/content/dr.js topic dr-responding (Fairground room 07, Whack-a-Mole). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["dr/dr-responding"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Respond in time", body:"Once the defendant has been served with the **particulars of claim** (the claimant's detailed statement of its case), it must respond within **strict time limits**. It can **admit** the claim, **defend** it (and **counterclaim**), or **acknowledge service** to buy more time or to **dispute the court's jurisdiction**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"If it does nothing", body:"The claimant can get **default judgment** without a trial; the defendant may then apply to **set it aside**. A claimant can also end its own claim by **discontinuing**, and the parties can **settle** at any time."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Where the rules are", body:"- **CPR Parts 9 to 15**: responding\n- **Part 11**: jurisdiction\n- **Parts 12 and 13**: default judgment\n- **Part 14**: admissions\n- **Part 38**: discontinuance\n- **Part 21**: settlements for children and protected parties"}
 ]},
 {h:"The defendant's options and time limits", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The clock starts at the particulars", body:"Time runs from **deemed service of the particulars of claim**. If they are served after the claim form, time runs from service of the **particulars**, not the claim form."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The response pack", body:"The defendant receives a **response pack** with forms to **admit**, **defend and counterclaim**, or **acknowledge service**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The response table", body:"- **Admission** (Part 14): **14 days** after service of particulars. The claimant may get judgment on the admitted part.\n- **Acknowledgment of service (AoS)** (Part 10): **14 days** after service of particulars. States whether the defendant intends to defend all or part, or contest **jurisdiction**; **extends time for the defence** to 28 days.\n- **Defence** (Part 15), no AoS filed: **14 days** after service of particulars. The claim proceeds to allocation (the court assigns it to a track).\n- **Defence**, AoS filed: **28 days** after service of particulars.\n- **Defence to counterclaim** (by the claimant): **14 days** after service of the counterclaim. **No AoS** is possible for a counterclaim."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Extending the defence by agreement (CPR 15.5)", body:"The parties may agree an extension of **up to 28 days**, so a maximum of **56 days** from service of the particulars where an AoS was filed. The defendant must **notify the court in writing**. Any longer extension needs a **court order**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No extension rule for the AoS", body:"CPR 15.5 covers only the **defence**; there is no corresponding rule for extending the time for an **AoS**. A defendant short of time should simply **file the AoS within the 14 days** (it is a short form) to gain the extra 14 days for the defence."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Six months of nothing: stayed (CPR 15.11)", body:"If the defendant does nothing and the claimant does not apply for default judgment (or take other steps) within **6 months** of the end of the defence period, the claim is **stayed** (put on hold)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Watch out: can the parties agree 2 extra months for the defence, or more time for the AoS?", body:"**No.** The agreed extension under CPR 15.5 is up to **28 days**, applies only to the **defence**, must be **notified to the court**, and cannot be stacked beyond 28 days without a **court order**. It does not apply to the **AoS**, which should simply be filed within 14 days."}
 ]},
 {h:"Admissions (Part 14)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"How to admit", body:"An admission can be made by **notice in writing** at any time, or by using the forms in the **response pack**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Withdrawing an admission", body:"An admission made in the proceedings can be withdrawn only with the **court's permission** (CPR 14.2(11)); the claimant agreeing is **not enough**. The court considers all the circumstances, including the **reasons**, **prejudice** to each side, and the **stage** of the case."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"The defendant admitted the claim and the claimant agrees it can withdraw the admission. Is that enough?", body:"**No.** Withdrawing an admission made in the proceedings needs the **court's permission** (CPR 14.2(11))."}
 ]},
 {h:"Specified sum", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Whole admission of a specified sum", body:"A **specified sum** is a fixed, calculable amount such as a debt. On a **whole admission**, the claimant can **request judgment** for the amount (plus **interest** and **fixed costs**, set amounts laid down in the rules)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Time to pay", body:"The defendant can ask for **time to pay** (by **instalments** or a **future date**), giving details of its **means** on the admission form. If the claimant **accepts**, judgment is entered on those terms. If it **rejects**, the court **decides** the rate of payment (often on paper, without a hearing)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Part admission", body:"E.g. \"I owe £3,000, not £5,000\". The claimant must tell the court whether it:\n- **accepts** the lesser amount **in full satisfaction** of the claim (and enters judgment for that amount); or\n- **rejects** it, in which case the claim continues as a **defended claim**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"The defendant admits £3,000 of a £5,000 claim. What can the claimant do?", body:"**Accept** £3,000 **in full satisfaction** and enter judgment for it, or **reject** it and the claim continues as a defended claim."}
 ]},
 {h:"Unspecified sum", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Admission of liability", body:"An **unspecified sum** means damages to be assessed by the court. On an admission of **liability**, the claimant can request **judgment on liability**, with the **amount** (quantum) decided by the court later."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Offering a sum", body:"The defendant can also **offer a sum** in satisfaction. If the claimant **accepts**, judgment is entered for that sum."}
 ]},
 {h:"Disputing the court's jurisdiction (Part 11)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"When it is used", body:"Where the defendant argues the court has **no jurisdiction** (e.g. it should be sued abroad) or **should not exercise it** (e.g. **forum non conveniens**, meaning another court is the more appropriate place, or an **arbitration clause** requiring a stay)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Two steps: AoS, then apply in 14 days", body:"The defendant must:\n- first file an **AoS** (indicating it intends to contest jurisdiction); then\n- **apply** to the court within **14 days after filing the AoS**, supported by **evidence**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No application in time", body:"If it files an AoS but **does not apply within 14 days**, it is **treated as accepting** that the court has jurisdiction."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Defence first = risk of submitting", body:"Filing a **defence** before or instead of making the application also risks being treated as **submitting** to the jurisdiction."}
 ]},
 {h:"Defence and counterclaim", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What goes in a defence", body:"The content of a defence is covered in **Statements of case**: each allegation must be **admitted**, **denied with reasons**, or **not admitted**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Counterclaim", body:"A **counterclaim** is a claim by the defendant against the claimant. **No permission** is needed if it is filed **with the defence**; otherwise the court's **permission** is needed. The defendant pays a **court fee** on the counterclaim."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Defence to counterclaim: 14 days", body:"The claimant must file a **defence to counterclaim** within **14 days**. If it does not, the defendant can obtain **default judgment on the counterclaim**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Set-off", body:"A defendant who has a money claim against the claimant can use it as a defence to **reduce or extinguish** the claim (and also counterclaim for any balance)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Default judgment (Part 12)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What default judgment is", body:"**Default judgment** is judgment **without a trial** because the defendant has **failed to respond in time**."}
 ]},
 {h:"When it is available", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No AoS and no defence: 14 days", body:"The defendant has filed **no AoS and no defence** and the **14 days** for doing so has expired."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"AoS but no defence: 28 days", body:"The defendant has filed an **AoS** but **no defence** and the **28 days** (plus any agreed extension) has expired."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"On a counterclaim too", body:"Default judgment is also available on a **counterclaim** where the claimant has filed no defence to it."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Certificate of service", body:"The claimant must have filed a **certificate of service** if it served the claim form itself."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: the defendant filed an AoS. Can the claimant request default judgment once 14 days have passed?", body:"**No.** After an AoS, default judgment cannot be requested until **28 days** after service of the particulars (plus any agreed extension). A common wrong answer applies the 14-day period."}
 ]},
 {h:"When it is NOT available", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A strike-out or summary judgment application is pending", body:"The defendant has applied to **strike out** the claim (have the court remove it) or for **summary judgment** (judgment without a trial because a side has no real prospect of success), and that application has **not been decided**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Paid, time to pay, or Part 8", body:"- The defendant has **satisfied** (paid) the whole claim, including costs.\n- The defendant has **admitted** the claim and asked for **time to pay**.\n- The claim is a **Part 8** claim."}
 ]},
 {h:"How to obtain it", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Request: specified sum", body:"A **request** is an administrative step, with **no hearing**, used for most money claims. For a **specified sum**: judgment for the amount, **interest** and **fixed costs**; the claimant can say how and when it should be paid."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Request: unspecified sum", body:"Judgment on **liability**, with the amount decided at a **disposal hearing** (a short hearing to fix the amount, usually with directions) or another hearing."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Application to a judge, with evidence", body:"Needed where, for example:\n- the defendant is a **child** or **protected party** (an adult lacking capacity to conduct the case);\n- the claim is for a remedy **other than money** (e.g. an injunction or delivery up of goods);\n- the defendant was **served out of the jurisdiction** without permission, or is the **Crown** or a **State**;\n- the claim is for **costs only**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Setting aside or varying default judgment (Part 13)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"MUST set aside (CPR 13.2)", body:"Where the judgment was **wrongly entered**, because:\n- the conditions for default judgment were **not satisfied** (e.g. it was entered before the time for the AoS or defence had expired, or the defendant had in fact filed a defence); or\n- the whole claim was **satisfied** before judgment was entered."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"MAY set aside or vary (CPR 13.3)", body:"Where:\n- the defendant has a **real prospect of successfully defending** the claim (a realistic, not fanciful, prospect, the same standard as summary judgment); **or**\n- there is **some other good reason** why the judgment should be set aside or the defendant allowed to defend (e.g. the defendant was in hospital)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Promptness and Denton", body:"The court must consider whether the defendant applied **promptly**. It applies the **Denton** three-stage approach to relief from sanctions (the court excusing a breach of a rule or order): **seriousness** of the default, **reason** for it, **all the circumstances**. In **FXF v English Karate Federation** [2023] the Court of Appeal confirmed Denton applies to applications to set aside default judgment. See **Case management, tracks and sanctions**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Evidence and a draft defence", body:"The application must be supported by **evidence**, ideally with a **draft defence** showing the real prospect of success."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Costs thrown away and conditions", body:"If set aside, the defendant usually pays the claimant's **costs thrown away** (costs wasted because of the default), and the court may impose **conditions** (e.g. paying a sum into court)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: 1 May and 20 May", body:"A defendant was served on **1 May**, filed nothing, and default judgment was entered on **20 May**. It now applies **promptly** with a draft defence showing the goods were never delivered. The judgment is **not wrongly entered** (14 days had passed), so CPR 13.2 does not apply, but the court **may** set it aside under CPR 13.3 (real prospect, prompt application), probably ordering the defendant to pay the **costs thrown away**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: judgment was entered too early, but the defence looks weak. Must it be set aside?", body:"**Yes.** Distinguish **\"must\"** (CPR 13.2: wrongly entered or claim already paid) from **\"may\"** (CPR 13.3: real prospect of defending or other good reason, promptness). A judgment entered **too early** must be set aside even if the defence is weak."}
 ]},
 {h:"Discontinuance (Part 38)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Walking away: notice of discontinuance", body:"**Discontinuance** means the claimant abandoning all or part of its claim. It can discontinue **at any time** by filing a **notice of discontinuance** and serving it on every other party."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"When permission is needed", body:"- An **interim injunction** (a temporary court order made before trial) has been granted (or an **undertaking**, a formal promise to the court, given) in the claim; or\n- the claimant has received an **interim payment** (an advance payment on account of damages) (unless the defendant **consents in writing**).\n- Where there is **more than one claimant**, the consent of the others or permission is needed."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Costs (CPR 38.6)", body:"Unless the court orders otherwise, the claimant must pay the **defendant's costs** up to the date the notice was served. A claimant who wants to avoid this must show a **good reason**, e.g. a change of circumstances caused by the defendant's conduct."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Setting aside the notice: 28 days", body:"The defendant can apply to **set aside** the notice within **28 days** of service (e.g. where the claimant is abusing the process)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Suing again (CPR 38.7)", body:"A claimant who discontinues **after a defence has been filed** needs the court's **permission** to bring another claim against the same defendant arising out of the same or substantially the same facts."}
 ]},
 {h:"Settlement", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Settle at any stage", body:"The parties can settle at any stage. If proceedings have started, the settlement should be **recorded** so the court file is closed and the terms are **enforceable**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Consent order", body:"The agreed terms are set out in a **court order**, which is **enforceable directly** like any judgment if broken (e.g. \"the defendant shall pay the claimant £20,000 by 1 July\")."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Tomlin order", body:"The claim is **stayed** on the terms set out in a **schedule**, which is **confidential** (not part of the public order), with **liberty to apply** to the court to enforce the terms without starting a new claim. Useful where the parties want **confidentiality** or terms the court **could not itself order** (e.g. a new supply agreement, an apology)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Enforcing a Tomlin order: two steps", body:"The innocent party first applies for an **order that the other party comply**, then **enforces that order**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Accepted Part 36 offer", body:"A **Part 36** offer (a formal settlement offer with set costs consequences) that is accepted **stays** the claim automatically (see **Costs, Part 36 offers and security for costs**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Child or protected party: court approval", body:"A settlement by or on behalf of a **child** or **protected party** is **not binding until approved by the court** (CPR 21.10)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Before issue: just a contract", body:"A settlement made **before issue** is simply a **binding contract**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Worked example and recap", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: 1 March, 29 March, 26 April", body:"Particulars of claim are deemed served on **1 March**. The defendant files an AoS on 10 March. Its defence is due **28 days after 1 March, i.e. by 29 March**; with an agreed 28-day extension notified to the court, by **26 April**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Recap: Britney's pop-up rules", body:"- **14 to pop**: admission, AoS, or a defence without an AoS, all within **14 days** of service of the particulars.\n- **28 to defend**: a defence after an AoS is due **28 days** after service of the particulars; an agreed extension adds up to **28 more** (**56** at most), notified to the court.\n- **Must if it's wrong**: wrongly entered or already paid, the court **must** set default judgment aside (CPR 13.2).\n- **May if it's strong**: real prospect or some other good reason, the court **may** set it aside (CPR 13.3)."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Source: src/content/dr.js topic dr-statements-of-case (Fairground room 08, Puppet Theatre). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["dr/dr-statements-of-case"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Each side's case on paper", body:"**Statements of case** (formerly called \"pleadings\") are the formal documents in which each party sets out its case: the **claim form**, **particulars of claim**, **defence**, any **Part 20 claim** (such as a counterclaim), the **reply**, and any **further information** given under Part 18."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Their job: define the issues", body:"They define the **issues** in dispute, so that each side knows the case it has to meet and the court knows what it must decide."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Facts, verified", body:"They set out the **facts** relied on concisely, not the evidence or legal argument, and they must be verified by a **statement of truth**. The rules are in **CPR Parts 16, 17, 18, 20 and 22** and **PD 16** (Practice Direction 16)."}
 ]},
 {h:"General rules", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Facts, not evidence", body:"Set out **facts** (what happened), not **evidence** (how those facts will be proved) or detailed legal argument. A party may refer to a **point of law** and attach **key documents**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Concise and numbered", body:"Be **concise**, use **numbered paragraphs**, and plead **each essential element** of the cause of action (the legal basis of the claim, e.g. breach of contract or negligence). Each statement of case must carry a **statement of truth** (Part 22)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Only what you pleaded", body:"A party can rely at trial **only on matters it has pleaded**. A party who wants to rely on something new must **amend** (Part 17)."}
 ]},
 {h:"The claim form", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The claim form (CPR 16.2)", body:"Covered in **Issuing and serving proceedings**: a concise statement of the **nature of the claim**, the **remedy** sought, a **statement of value**, any **interest** claimed, and a **statement of truth**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Particulars of claim (CPR 16.4 and PD 16)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What the particulars must contain", body:"A **concise statement of the facts** on which the claimant relies, plus details of any **interest** claimed and any claim for:\n- **aggravated** damages (extra compensation for distress caused by the manner of the wrong);\n- **exemplary** damages (to punish outrageous conduct);\n- **provisional** damages (the right to return for more damages if a specified serious deterioration happens later)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Typical structure of a contract claim", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"1. The parties", body:"Who they are, e.g. \"The Claimant is and was at all material times a company carrying on business as a supplier of office furniture\"."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"2. The contract", body:"Date, parties, and whether **written** or **oral**. For a **written** contract, **attach a copy**; for an **oral** contract, set out the **words used, by whom, to whom, when and where**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"3. The terms", body:"The relevant **terms** (express and implied)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"4. The breach", body:"What the defendant **did or failed to do**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"5. Causation and loss", body:"The **loss caused by the breach**, with **figures**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"6. Interest", body:"The **basis** and **amount** of interest."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"7. The prayer", body:"A **prayer** is the closing summary of what is claimed, e.g. \"**AND THE CLAIMANT CLAIMS**: (1) damages; (2) interest under s.69 County Courts Act 1984\"."}
 ]},
 {h:"Typical structure of a negligence claim", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Parties, duty and what happened", body:"The parties; the facts giving rise to the **duty of care** (e.g. \"The Defendant was driving a car on the A1\"); what happened."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Particulars of negligence", body:"A **numbered list** of the ways the defendant breached its duty (e.g. driving too fast, failing to keep a proper lookout)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Then causation, injury and loss", body:"**Causation**; **particulars of injury** and **particulars of loss**; **interest**; **prayer**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Extra requirements", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Personal injury claims (PD 16)", body:"- The claimant's **date of birth**\n- Brief details of the **injuries**\n- A **medical report** attached if relying on medical evidence\n- A **schedule of past and future expenses and losses**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Provisional damages", body:"If seeking **provisional damages**, say so and give the **grounds**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Interest (CPR 16.4(2))", body:"State whether it is claimed under a **contract**, a **statute** (and which) or otherwise. For a **specified sum**, also the **rate**, the **date** from which it runs, the **amount** to the date of issue and the **daily rate** after that."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Must be specifically set out (PD 16 para 8.2)", body:"If relied on, including:\n- any allegation of **fraud**;\n- the fact of any **illegality**;\n- details of any **misrepresentation**;\n- details of any **breach of trust**;\n- **notice or knowledge** of a fact;\n- details of **unsoundness of mind** or undue influence;\n- details of **wilful default**;\n- any facts relating to **mitigation** of loss (reasonable steps taken to reduce the loss)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Criminal convictions", body:"Convictions relied on (e.g. the defendant's conviction for careless driving, under **Civil Evidence Act 1968 s.11**): give the **type** of conviction, its **date**, the **court** and the **issue** to which it is relevant."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Aggravated and exemplary damages", body:"**Aggravated damages** (extra compensation for distress caused by the manner of the wrong) and **exemplary damages** (to punish outrageous conduct) must be pleaded with the **grounds**."}
 ]},
 {h:"The defence (CPR 16.5)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Answer every allegation", body:"For **each allegation** in the particulars, the defendant must say whether it is **denied**, **not admitted** or **admitted**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Denied", body:"The defendant must give its **reasons** and, if it intends to put forward a **different version of events**, set it out."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Not admitted", body:"The defendant is **unable to admit or deny** it (e.g. it has no knowledge of the claimant's losses) and **requires the claimant to prove** it."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Silence = admitted", body:"An allegation the defendant **fails to deal with** is **taken to be admitted**, unless the defendant has set out in its defence the **nature of its case** on that issue, in which case it is taken as **requiring proof**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The money must be proved", body:"The **amount of money claimed** is taken as **requiring proof** unless expressly admitted."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Disputing the statement of value", body:"If the defendant disputes the claimant's **statement of value**, it must say **why** and give its **own estimate**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Personal injury defences", body:"The defendant should state whether it **agrees**, **disputes** or **neither agrees nor disputes** the **medical report**, and serve a **counter-schedule** of losses."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Relying on limitation", body:"If the defendant relies on **limitation**, it must give **details of the expiry** of the limitation period (PD 16)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Typical defences", body:"- No contract, no such term, or the term means something else\n- No breach\n- No causation, or the loss is too **remote** (not reasonably foreseeable, or not within the parties' contemplation, so not recoverable)\n- Failure to **mitigate**\n- An **exclusion or limitation clause**\n- **Contributory negligence** (the claimant was partly to blame, so damages are reduced) or **consent**\n- **Limitation** (claim out of time)\n- **Set-off** (the defendant's own money claim against the claimant reduces what it owes)"},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"The particulars say the claimant lost £30,000 of profits. The defendant has no way of knowing. What should it plead?", body:"**Not admitted**, requiring the claimant to **prove** the loss. Silence risks the allegation being treated as **admitted** (unless the defence otherwise makes its case on loss clear)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: denied or not admitted?", body:"Use **\"not admitted\"** where the defendant genuinely **does not know** (e.g. the claimant's injuries or losses); use **\"denied\"** with reasons and its own version where it **knows** the allegation is wrong."}
 ]},
 {h:"The reply (CPR 15.8)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What a reply is", body:"A **reply** is the claimant's answer to **new matters raised in the defence** (e.g. responding to an allegation of contributory negligence or a limitation defence)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Optional", body:"A reply is **optional**. If filed, it is filed with the claimant's **directions questionnaire** (the form each party completes so the court can allocate and manage the case) and served on the other parties."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No reply is not an admission (CPR 16.7)", body:"**Not filing a reply** is **not** an admission of matters raised in the defence."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A reply silent on a point", body:"A claimant who files a reply but fails to deal with something in the defence is taken to **require proof** of it."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Watch out: a reply is optional. Is a defence to counterclaim optional too?", body:"**No.** A reply is optional and silence is not an admission, but a **defence to counterclaim** is **essential**: without one, the defendant can obtain **default judgment** on the counterclaim."}
 ]},
 {h:"Part 20 claims (additional claims)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What a Part 20 claim is", body:"A **Part 20 claim** (also called an **additional claim**) is any claim other than the claimant's main claim against the defendant."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The Part 20 table: who and permission", body:"- **Counterclaim**, against the **claimant**: no permission if filed **with the defence**; otherwise yes.\n- Counterclaim adding a **new party** (the claimant and a non-party): **always** needs a court order.\n- **Contribution or indemnity**, against a **co-defendant** (already a party): no permission if the notice is filed and served **with the defence** (or within **28 days** after the co-defendant's defence); otherwise yes.\n- **Additional claim against a non-party** (third party, a person not yet a party): no permission if issued **before or at the same time as** filing the defence; otherwise yes."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Contribution v indemnity", body:"**Contribution** means a **share** of the damages; **indemnity** means the **whole amount** the defendant has to pay (e.g. under a contractual indemnity)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Serving a non-party: 14 days", body:"A claim against a **non-party** must be **served within 14 days** after it is issued (or the court's permission given), together with copies of the statements of case already served."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The Part 20 defendant responds like any defendant", body:"The same rules on **AoS** (acknowledgment of service), **defence** and time limits generally apply (but there is **no AoS to a counterclaim**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A third party who does nothing (CPR 20.11)", body:"A **third party** (non-party) who fails to file an AoS or defence is **deemed to admit** the additional claim and is **bound by any judgment** in the main claim."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A homeowner sues her builder for a defective roof. The builder says the roofing subcontractor did the faulty work. What should the builder do?", body:"Bring a **Part 20 additional claim** against the subcontractor (a non-party) for an **indemnity** or **contribution**, ideally **issued with its defence** so no permission is needed."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: what do you check for a Part 20 claim?", body:"**Who** it is against and **when** it is made. A counterclaim or a third-party claim issued **with the defence** needs no permission; later, it does. Adding a **new party** to a counterclaim always needs a **court order**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Requests for further information (Part 18)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Clarify or add information", body:"A party can ask another party to **clarify** any matter in dispute or give **additional information** about it, even if it is not in a statement of case."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A written request first", body:"First make a **written request** (a \"Part 18 request\"), giving a **reasonable time** to respond. It must be **concise** and limited to what is **reasonably necessary and proportionate** to prepare one's own case or understand the case to be met."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No answer: apply for an order", body:"If the other side does not respond, or responds inadequately, the requesting party can **apply for a court order**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The response", body:"The response is set out in a document verified by a **statement of truth**, and becomes **part of that party's statement of case**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Amendments (Part 17)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Before service: no permission", body:"**Before service**, a party can amend its statement of case **without permission**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"After service: consent or permission", body:"**After service**, only with the **written consent of all the other parties** or the **court's permission**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What the court considers", body:"The **overriding objective** (the court's duty to deal with cases justly and at proportionate cost), whether the amendment has a **real prospect of success**, and the **stage** of the case. **Late amendments** close to trial need a very good explanation and are often refused if they would cause the trial date to be lost."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The amending party pays", body:"The amending party usually pays the **costs of and arising from** the amendment (costs other parties incur dealing with it)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"After limitation has expired (CPR 17.4)", body:"Allowed only to:\n- add or substitute a **new claim** arising out of the **same or substantially the same facts** as a claim already pleaded;\n- correct a genuine **mistake as to the name** of a party, where the mistake would not have caused reasonable doubt as to the identity of the party intended;\n- alter the **capacity** in which a party claims (e.g. as personal representative, the person who administers a dead person's estate, rather than personally), if it had that capacity when proceedings started or has since acquired it."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A new party after limitation: CPR 19.6", body:"Adding or substituting a **new party** after limitation is governed by **CPR 19.6** (see **Issuing and serving proceedings**)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Statements of truth (Part 22)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Where a statement of truth is needed", body:"- **Statements of case** (including amendments)\n- **Responses to Part 18 requests**\n- **Witness statements**\n- **Expert reports**\n- Certificates of service\n- Some applications"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The wording", body:"\"[I believe] [The claimant believes] that the facts stated in this [particulars of claim] are true. [I understand] [The claimant understands] that proceedings for **contempt of court** may be brought against anyone who makes, or causes to be made, a **false statement** in a document verified by a statement of truth **without an honest belief in its truth**.\""},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Who signs", body:"- A **statement of case**: the **party**, its **litigation friend** (someone who conducts the case for a child or an adult who lacks capacity), or its **legal representative** (solicitor) on its behalf. For a company, a person holding a **senior position**.\n- A **witness statement**: the **witness** personally.\n- An **expert report**: the **expert**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Not verified", body:"A statement of case that is **not verified** remains **effective** unless **struck out** (removed by the court), but the party **cannot rely on it as evidence** of its contents. The court may strike it out."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"False statement = contempt of court", body:"Making a false statement in a verified document without an honest belief in its truth is **contempt of court**, which can lead to a **fine** or **imprisonment**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Recap: Britney's script check", body:"- **Claim form**: nature of the claim, remedy, value, interest.\n- **Particulars**: concise facts, ending with the prayer.\n- **Defence**: denied, not admitted or admitted for each allegation.\n- **Reply**: optional; silence is not an admission.\n- **Part 20**: issued with the defence, no permission; adding a new party to a counterclaim always needs an order.\n- **Part 18**: a written request first, proportionate.\n- **Amendments**: free before service; after it, consent or permission.\n- **Statement of truth**: a false one is contempt of court."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Source: proposals/rooms/stops/fairground/build/room-dr-trial.js (built only from the dr-trial notes in src/content/dr.js) */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["dr/dr-trial"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Most cases settle; the rest go to trial", body:"Most cases settle, but those that do not end in a **trial**: a hearing where the judge hears the **evidence** and **arguments** and gives **judgment**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Before trial: three jobs", body:"The parties must confirm they are ready (**pre-trial checklists**), make sure their witnesses attend (if necessary by **witness summons**), and prepare an agreed **trial bundle**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"At the trial itself", body:"There is a **set order of events**, strict rules on how witnesses may be questioned (**no leading questions** of your own witness), and conventions on **how to address the judge**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Where the rules are", body:"Mainly in the Civil Procedure Rules, **CPR Parts 28-29, 34, 39 and 40**, and **Practice Direction (PD) 39A**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Before trial: pre-trial checklist (listing questionnaire)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Form N170", body:"On the **fast**, **intermediate** and **multi-track**, the court sends each party a **pre-trial checklist** (form **N170**). It must be returned by a fixed date, usually about **8 weeks** before the trial date or window."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What the checklist asks", body:"Whether the parties have **complied with directions** (disclosure, witness statements, experts); which **witnesses** and **experts** will attend and their availability; whether an **interpreter** is needed (and **Welsh** language arrangements); the expected **length of trial**; and, on the multi-track, an estimate of **costs**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No party files a checklist", body:"The court will usually order that **unless one is filed within 7 days**, the claim, defence and any **counterclaim** (a claim by the defendant against the claimant) will be **struck out** (removed by the court, so that part of the case ends)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Only one party fails to file", body:"The court gives the **listing directions** it thinks appropriate, possibly with a **sanction** against the defaulting party."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Neither side returns its pre-trial checklist. What will the court usually do?", body:"Order that **unless one is filed within 7 days**, the claim, defence and any counterclaim will be **struck out**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Trial date or window, and notice", body:"The court then confirms or fixes the **trial date** (or a **trial window**: a period, often a week, within which the case will be heard) and gives final directions (e.g. the trial timetable, bundles). On the **fast track** the parties get at least **3 weeks' notice** of the trial date."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Pre-trial review (PTR)", body:"On the **multi-track**, in larger cases: a hearing a few weeks before trial to check the case is ready, settle the **trial timetable** and resolve outstanding issues. It should be attended by the **advocates who will conduct the trial**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Before trial: witness summons (CPR 34)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What a witness summons does", body:"A **witness summons** is a court document **compelling** a person to **attend court to give evidence** or to **produce documents** to the court. It is used for a **reluctant witness** (e.g. a former employee who does not want to be involved) or for **documents held by a non-party**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Issued and served", body:"**Issued by the court** on the party's request. It is **served by the court** unless the party asks to serve it itself."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Binding only with 7 days", body:"A summons is **binding** only if served at least **7 days** before the date the witness is required to attend, unless the court directs a shorter period."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"When you need the court's permission", body:"Permission is needed to issue a summons: **less than 7 days before the trial**; for attendance on a date **other than the trial date** (e.g. at an interim hearing); or for attendance at a **hearing other than the trial**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Conduct money", body:"At the time of service the witness must be **offered or paid conduct money**: a sum reasonably sufficient to cover their **travel expenses** to and from court and **compensation for loss of time**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Ignoring a summons", body:"A witness who fails to comply **without lawful excuse** may be in **contempt of court** (a serious breach of a court order, punishable by a fine or prison)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: the trial on 20 May", body:"A claimant's key witness, a former colleague, refuses to come to the trial on **20 May**. The claimant should obtain a witness summons and **serve it by 12 May**, offering conduct money, so it is binding."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"7 clear days (CPR 2.8)", body:"'At least 7 days' means **7 clear days**: **neither the day of service nor the day of the trial counts**. So service on 13 May would be **one day short**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Trial on 20 May. The summons is served on 13 May, with no permission. Is it binding?", body:"**No.** It is one day short of **7 clear days**. It had to be served by **12 May**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Before trial: the trial bundle (PD 39A)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The claimant's job", body:"The **claimant** (or its solicitor) prepares and files the **trial bundle**, normally **not more than 7 and not less than 3 days** before the start of the trial."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"In the bundle (1): the case papers", body:"Unless the court orders otherwise: the **claim form** and all **statements of case** (the parties' formal documents setting out their cases, e.g. particulars of claim and defence); a **case summary** and/or **chronology** where appropriate; **requests for further information** and the responses."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"In the bundle (2): the evidence", body:"All **witness statements** to be relied on and any **witness summaries** (an outline of what a witness is expected to say, used where a full statement cannot be obtained); any **hearsay notices** (notice that a party will rely on a statement made outside court by someone not giving evidence); **expert reports** and the experts' **joint statements** and answers to questions."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"In the bundle (3): orders and the rest", body:"Any **orders** giving directions about the conduct of the trial; any other **necessary documents** (e.g. key correspondence and disclosed documents)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"How it is put together", body:"**Paginated** (page numbered) and **indexed**, in **chronological** order where appropriate, and **agreed** by the parties if possible (the other parties should be given an opportunity to agree its contents)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Identical copies and e-bundles", body:"**Identical copies** go to the court, each party and the witness box. Increasingly bundles are **electronic** (e-bundles), following court guidance."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Who prepares the trial bundle, and when is it filed?", body:"The **claimant**, **not more than 7 and not less than 3 days** before the trial starts."}
 ]},
 {h:"Before trial: skeleton arguments and authorities", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Skeleton argument", body:"In the multi-track (and often in other tracks), each party's advocate files a **skeleton argument**: a short written outline of the **issues**, the party's **submissions** and the **legal authorities** relied on, with **references to the bundle**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"List of authorities", body:"A **list of authorities** (cases and statutes) and **copies** of them are provided as directed."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Agreed documents for the judge", body:"The parties should also try to agree a **chronology**, a **list of issues**, and a **reading list** for the judge."}
 ]},
 {h:"Trial procedure: general", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Normally in public", body:"Trials are normally held **in public** (CPR 39.2). The court can sit **in private** in limited cases (e.g. to protect a **child** or **confidential information**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The judge has done the reading", body:"The judge will usually have **read the bundle** and the **skeleton arguments** before the trial starts."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Special measures", body:"**Vulnerable** parties and witnesses can be given special measures (e.g. **breaks**, **screens**, **video link**) to help them give their best evidence (PD 1A)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Trial procedure: order of events", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"1. Opening speech", body:"Usually by the **claimant's** advocate, outlining the case, the issues and the evidence. The judge may **dispense** with an opening, or ask for a short one, having read the papers."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"2. Claimant's evidence: examination in chief", body:"Each of the claimant's witnesses in turn. In **examination in chief** the witness confirms their name and address and that their witness statement is true; the statement then **stands as their evidence in chief**. Further questions (**amplification**) need the court's **permission**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Then cross-examination", body:"Each claimant witness is then **cross-examined** by the **defendant's** advocate."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Then re-examination", body:"**Re-examination** by the claimant's advocate is **limited to matters arising from the cross-examination** (clarifying or repairing damage done). **No new topics.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"3. Defendant's evidence", body:"The **defendant's witnesses**, in the same way: in chief, cross-examination, re-examination."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"4. Closing speeches", body:"Normally the **defendant goes first** and the **claimant has the last word**. The advocates summarise the evidence, comment on its credibility and make legal submissions."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"5. Judgment", body:"Last comes the **judgment**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Submission of no case to answer", body:"At the end of the claimant's case, the defendant may argue the claimant has not proved its case. In civil cases this is **rare**, and the judge will usually require the defendant to **elect not to call any evidence** before ruling on it."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Watch out: who opens, who closes first", body:"**Opening** is by the **claimant**. **Closing** speeches normally go **defendant first**, with the **claimant** having the **last word**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"In re-examination, can the claimant's advocate raise a brand new topic?", body:"**No.** Re-examination is limited to **matters arising from the cross-examination**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Trial procedure: cross-examination, the duty to 'put the case'", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Put your case", body:"The cross-examining advocate must **put its party's case** to each opposing witness on every point where its own evidence differs, so the witness has a chance to respond. Failing to challenge a witness's evidence may be treated as **accepting** it."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Credibility and reliability", body:"Cross-examination can test the witness's **credibility** (honesty) and **reliability** (accuracy, e.g. eyesight, memory, opportunity to see)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Leading questions", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What a leading question is", body:"A **leading question** **suggests the answer** or **assumes a disputed fact**. E.g. 'The car was going very fast, wasn't it?' or 'When did you see the defendant run the red light?'"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Not with your own witness", body:"**Not allowed** in **examination in chief** or **re-examination** (questioning your **own** witness), except on **introductory or undisputed** matters (e.g. 'Are you Mrs Jones of 12 High Street?')."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Fine in cross-examination", body:"Leading questions are **allowed** and usual in **cross-examination** (questioning the **other side's** witness)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Open questions", body:"A non-leading (**open**) question starts with words such as **who, what, where, when, how, why, describe, explain**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Your own witness turns against you", body:"A party cannot generally attack the credibility of its **own** witness. If its witness turns against it, the advocate may ask the judge to declare the witness **hostile**, after which leading questions and prior inconsistent statements can be put."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: your own witness and the traffic light", body:"Questioning your own witness, the proper question is '**What colour was the traffic light when you first saw it?**', not 'The light was red, wasn't it?'."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: cross-examining the defendant", body:"In cross-examination of the defendant, '**You didn't look at the light, did you?**' is perfectly proper."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Your own witness is in the box. 'The light was red, wasn't it?' or 'What colour was the light when you first saw it?'", body:"The **open** question: 'What colour was the light when you first saw it?'. 'The light was red, wasn't it?' suggests the answer, so it is **leading**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Modes of address", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"District Judge: 'Judge'", body:"A **District Judge** (County Court or High Court) and a **Deputy District Judge**: '**Judge**' (since **December 2022**; 'Sir' or 'Madam' is the old form)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Master: 'Master'", body:"A **Master** (High Court, London): '**Master**'."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Circuit Judge and Recorder: 'Your Honour'", body:"A **Circuit Judge**: '**Your Honour**'. A **Recorder** (a part-time judge, usually a practising lawyer): also '**Your Honour**'."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"High Court Judge: 'My Lord' or 'My Lady'", body:"A **High Court Judge** (and Deputy High Court Judge): '**My Lord**' or '**My Lady**' (referred to as 'your Lordship' or 'your Ladyship')."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Court of Appeal and Supreme Court", body:"**Court of Appeal** and **Supreme Court** judges: '**My Lord**' or '**My Lady**'."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The opposing advocate", body:"'**My friend**' (a solicitor) or '**my learned friend**' (a barrister)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"How do you address a Recorder in court?", body:"'**Your Honour**', the same as a Circuit Judge."}
 ]},
 {h:"Etiquette and the advocate's duties", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Court etiquette", body:"**Stand** when addressing the court and when the judge enters or leaves; do **not interrupt** the judge or the opposing advocate; **speak through the judge**, not directly to the opponent."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A witness part-way through", body:"Do not discuss the evidence with a witness who is **part-way through** giving evidence (e.g. during a lunch break in their cross-examination)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Duty: do not mislead (para 1.4)", body:"Under the **SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors**, a solicitor must not **mislead** or attempt to mislead the court, **including by omission**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Duty: cases against your client too (para 2.7)", body:"Draw the court's attention to **relevant cases and statutory provisions**, or procedural irregularities, likely to have a material effect on the outcome, **including those against the client**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Duty: properly arguable only (para 2.4)", body:"Only make assertions, statements or submissions that are **properly arguable**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Duty: hands off the evidence (para 2.2)", body:"Do not seek to **influence the substance of evidence**, e.g. by **coaching** witnesses or persuading them to change their evidence."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The court comes first", body:"The **duty to the court overrides** the duty to the client."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Your witness is halfway through cross-examination at lunch. Can you go over her answers with her?", body:"**No.** Do not discuss the evidence with a witness who is **part-way through** giving evidence."}
 ]},
 {h:"Judgment", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Ex tempore or reserved", body:"The judge gives judgment either **ex tempore** (orally at the end of the trial) or **reserved** (handed down later in writing). For a reserved judgment a **draft** is usually circulated to the parties' lawyers **in confidence** (embargoed) beforehand so typing errors can be corrected."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Consequential matters", body:"Then come **interest**, **costs**, **permission to appeal** (which should be sought from the **trial judge at this hearing**; see **Appeals**) and any **stay of execution** (a pause on enforcing the judgment, e.g. while an appeal is pursued)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Pay within 14 days (CPR 40.11)", body:"A money judgment must be paid within **14 days** of the judgment unless the court orders otherwise (e.g. **instalments**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Judgment interest: 8%", body:"**Judgment interest** runs on most judgment debts at **8%** a year (see **Enforcement of money judgments**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"When it takes effect", body:"A judgment takes effect **from the day it is given**, and is drawn up as a sealed **order**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Judgment: costs at the end of trial", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Fast and intermediate track", body:"Costs are **fixed recoverable costs** (set amounts laid down in the rules, not the actual bill), normally dealt with **at the end of the hearing**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Multi-track", body:"Costs normally go to **detailed assessment** (a separate process in which a costs judge examines the bill). The court will usually order a reasonable **payment on account** of costs (an advance part-payment before the final figure is known) in the meantime unless there is good reason not to (CPR 44.2(8))."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Small claims", body:"**Very limited** costs (see **Costs**)."}
 ]},
 {h:"If a party does not attend the trial (CPR 39.3)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The court can carry on", body:"The court may **proceed in the absence** of a party. **No party** attends: it may strike out the **whole proceedings**. The **claimant** does not attend: it may strike out the **claim**. The **defendant** does not attend: it may strike out the **defence** and give **judgment for the claimant**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Setting the judgment aside", body:"A party who did not attend can apply to **set aside** the judgment, but the court may grant it **only if** the applicant: **acted promptly** when it found out the court had exercised its power; had a **good reason** for not attending the trial; **and** has a **reasonable prospect of success** at the trial."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Watch out: all three", body:"To set aside a judgment given in a party's absence, the party must show **promptness**, **good reason** for not attending **and** a **reasonable prospect of success**. **All three** conditions must be met."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"The defendant missed the trial for a good reason and applied at once, but has no real prospect of success. Will the judgment be set aside?", body:"**No.** **All three** are needed: promptness, good reason **and** a reasonable prospect of success."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exam tip: questioning your own witness", body:"If asked how a solicitor should question their **own** witness, the correct option uses an **open** question (who, what, where, when, how). Leading questions are only for **cross-examination** or for undisputed introductory matters."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exam tip: bundle and summons", body:"The trial bundle is the **claimant's** responsibility, filed **3 to 7 days** before trial. The witness summons must be served at least **7 days** before attendance with **conduct money** to be binding."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Source: src/content/dr.js topic dr-where-to-start (Fairground room 05, The Strength Tester). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["dr/dr-where-to-start"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Two first-instance courts", body:"Civil claims in England and Wales start at **first instance** (meaning the court that first hears the case) in either the **County Court** or the **High Court**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Must, may and specialist", body:"The rules decide where a claim **must** start, where it **may** start, and which **specialist** court or list suits it."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The key test is value", body:"Money claims worth **£100,000 or less** must start in the **County Court**. For **personal injury**, the High Court threshold is **£50,000**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Above the line: a choice", body:"Above those figures the claimant **may** choose the High Court if the case justifies a **High Court judge**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Court is not track", body:"Where to start is a **separate question** from the **track** (the case-management route a defended claim is allocated to: small claims, fast, intermediate or multi-track) the case is later allocated to (see **Case management, tracks and sanctions**)."}
 ]},
 {h:"The two first-instance courts: the County Court", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"One national court", body:"The County Court is a **single national court** that sits at many **hearing centres** across England and Wales."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Most civil claims", body:"It handles the **great majority** of civil claims: debt, contract, personal injury, housing and consumer claims."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Issued centrally, often online", body:"Money claims are usually issued centrally, often **online** (e.g. **Money Claims Online** for specified sums), and then sent to a local hearing centre once defended."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"County Court judges", body:"**District Judges** hear most interim applications (applications made before trial), small claims and fast track trials. **Circuit Judges** hear intermediate and multi-track trials."}
 ]},
 {h:"The two first-instance courts: the High Court", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Three Divisions", body:"The High Court is divided into three **Divisions** (as at September 2026): the **King's Bench Division**, the **Chancery Division** and the **Family Division**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"King's Bench Division (KBD)", body:"Contract and tort claims, personal injury, defamation (the **Media and Communications List**), judicial review (a challenge to the lawfulness of a public body's decision; heard in the **Administrative Court**) and, at present, the **Commercial Court** and **Admiralty Court**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Chancery Division", body:"Business, property, trusts, probate, insolvency, companies and intellectual property."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Where it sits", body:"In London (the **Royal Courts of Justice** and the **Rolls Building**) and in **District Registries** (High Court offices at court centres outside London)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"High Court judges", body:"**High Court Judges** hear trials. **Masters** (in London) and **District Judges** (in District Registries) deal with procedural matters."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Coming change: 1 October 2026", body:"From **1 October 2026** (after the law date for this course) the Chancery Division is renamed the **Business and Property Division**. The Commercial Court, Admiralty Court, Circuit Commercial Courts and Technology and Construction Court move into it (**Civil Procedure (Amendment No. 2) Rules 2026**)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Where a claim must or may start", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Money claim: £100,000 or less", body:"A money claim (not personal injury) worth **£100,000 or less** **must** start in the **County Court**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Money claim: more than £100,000", body:"A money claim (not personal injury) worth **more than £100,000** can go in the County Court, or the **High Court** if the case justifies it."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Personal injury: less than £50,000", body:"A **personal injury** claim worth **less than £50,000** **must** start in the **County Court**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Personal injury: £50,000 or more", body:"A **personal injury** claim worth **£50,000 or more** can go in the County Court, or the **High Court** if the case justifies it."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Defamation: High Court", body:"**Defamation** (libel and slander) of any value goes to the **High Court**. The County Court has no jurisdiction unless the parties **agree in writing** (County Courts Act 1984 ss.15(2)(c) and 18; PD 7A para 2.9)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Judicial review: High Court", body:"**Judicial review** of any value goes to the **High Court** (the **Administrative Court**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Always the County Court", body:"Most **housing possession**, **consumer credit** and **small claims**, of any value, go to the **County Court**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Only if it needs a High Court judge", body:"A claim above the threshold should go to the High Court **only if** the claimant believes it ought to be dealt with by a **High Court judge** (PD 7A para 2.4)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The three reasons (PD 7A para 2.4)", body:"Its **financial value** and the amount in dispute; the **complexity** of the facts, legal issues, remedies or procedures; and/or the **importance of the outcome to the public** in general."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"High value alone is not enough", body:"A high value alone does **not oblige** the claimant to use the High Court: many claims over £100,000 are issued and tried in the **County Court**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Valuing the claim", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What the value is", body:"The **value** is the amount the claimant **reasonably expects to recover**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Disregard interest and costs", body:"When valuing for the court thresholds and for allocation, **disregard** (CPR 16.3(6)) **interest** and **costs**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Disregard these too", body:"Also disregard any possible finding of **contributory negligence** (the claimant's own share of fault reducing the damages); any **counterclaim** (a claim by the defendant against the claimant) or **set-off** (the defendant's own money claim deducted from what it owes) the defendant may raise; and, for personal injury, amounts the defendant may have to repay to the Department for Work and Pensions (**CRU benefits**, recovered through the Compensation Recovery Unit)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Statement of value: specified sum", body:"The claim form contains a **statement of value** (CPR 16.3). For a **specified** sum (a fixed, calculable amount such as a debt), it states the amount claimed."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Statement of value: unspecified sum", body:"For an **unspecified** sum (damages to be assessed), it states whether the claimant expects to recover **not more than £10,000**, **more than £10,000 but not more than £25,000**, **more than £25,000 but not more than £100,000**, **more than £100,000**, or **cannot say**. The bands match the track limits."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Statement of value: personal injury", body:"In a personal injury claim it states whether damages for **pain, suffering and loss of amenity** (PSLA) are expected to be **not more than £1,500** or **more than £1,500** (for most road traffic accident claims the figure is **£5,000**). This tells the court whether the claim may be a small claim."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Statement of value: High Court", body:"In a claim issued in the **High Court**, it states that the claimant expects to recover **more than £100,000** (or **£50,000** for personal injury), or that a statute requires the High Court."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"It fixes the court fee", body:"The statement of value also fixes the **court fee** payable on issue: generally **5%** of claims between £10,000 and £200,000, **capped at £10,000** for larger claims."}
 ]},
 {h:"Specialist courts and lists", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Business and Property Courts", body:"The **Business and Property Courts (B&PC)** are an umbrella for the specialist business courts of the High Court. They sit in London (the **Rolls Building**) and regional centres: **Birmingham, Bristol, Cardiff, Leeds, Liverpool, Manchester, Newcastle**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Commercial Court (CPR 58)", body:"Complex commercial disputes, e.g. international trade, shipping, banking, insurance and arbitration claims."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Circuit Commercial Court (CPR 59)", body:"Commercial claims of a more **local** or **less complex** nature."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Technology and Construction Court (CPR 60)", body:"The **TCC**: building, engineering, IT and other **technically complex** disputes."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Admiralty Court (CPR 61)", body:"Shipping **collisions** and **salvage**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Financial List", body:"Major **financial markets** disputes."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Chancery lists", body:"The **Business List**, **Insolvency and Companies List**, **Property, Trusts and Probate List**, **Competition List**, **Revenue List** and the **Intellectual Property List**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Intellectual Property Enterprise Court", body:"The **IPEC** is a cheaper forum for smaller IP disputes, with a **cap** on damages and on recoverable costs, and a small claims track for low-value claims."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Lists in the King's Bench Division", body:"The **Media and Communications List** (defamation, privacy, data protection), the **Administrative Court** (judicial review) and the **Planning Court**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Their own rules and guides", body:"Specialist courts have their **own rules and guides**, e.g. **CPR 58** (Commercial Court), **CPR 60** (TCC), **PD 57AD** (disclosure in the Business and Property Courts) and **PD 57AC** (trial witness statements in the B&PC)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Specialist judges in the County Court", body:"Business and property claims can also be heard by **specialist judges** in the **County Court** at regional centres where the value or complexity does not need the High Court."}
 ]},
 {h:"Transfer and penalties for starting in the wrong court", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Wrong court: not invalid", body:"A claim started in the wrong court is **not invalid**. The court can **transfer** it to the right court (**CPR 30**), or let it continue."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Costs penalty: up to 25%", body:"If a claim that should have been started in the County Court is started in the High Court, the court may **reduce the claimant's recoverable costs by up to 25%** (Senior Courts Act 1981 s.51(8)-(9))."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Other transfers", body:"Cases can also be transferred between County Court **hearing centres** (e.g. to the defendant's home court, or to a centre in **Wales** where Welsh is to be used) and into or out of **specialist lists**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Practical factors in choosing", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Value", body:"**Value** and the thresholds above."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Complexity and a specialist judge", body:"**Complexity** and the need for a **specialist judge** (e.g. the TCC for a construction dispute)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Cost", body:"High Court litigation is generally **more expensive**. Court fees depend on **value**, not on the court."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Speed and location", body:"**Speed** and **location**: convenience for the parties and witnesses, and the court's workload."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Enforcement: £5,000 or more", body:"A judgment for **£5,000 or more** must be enforced by taking control of goods (enforcement agents seizing and selling the debtor's goods) in the **High Court** (by a **writ of control** executed by a High Court Enforcement Officer), unless it arises under a regulated **consumer credit** agreement. A County Court judgment is transferred to the High Court for this purpose."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Enforcement: under £600", body:"Judgments **under £600** can only be enforced by taking control of goods in the **County Court** (see **Enforcement of money judgments**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Judgment interest", body:"County Court judgments **under £5,000** generally do **not** carry statutory judgment interest; High Court judgments do."}
 ]},
 {h:"Exam tips and watch out", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Watch out: personal injury is £50,000", body:"The personal injury threshold for the High Court is **£50,000**, not £100,000."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Don't confuse it with small claims", body:"Do not confuse it with the small claims personal injury limit: **£1,500** for pain, suffering and loss of amenity in most personal injury claims; **£5,000** for road traffic claims under the whiplash reforms."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: an £85,000 debt", body:"A company is owed **£85,000** for goods delivered. **It must issue in the County Court**, because the claim is worth £100,000 or less."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: £2 million and complex", body:"If the debt were **£2 million** with complex issues, the **High Court** (probably the Business and Property Courts) would be appropriate."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A £98,000 debt plus £6,000 interest. Which court?", body:"**The County Court.** Do the arithmetic the examiners intend: strip out **interest** and **costs** before comparing the claim with the **£100,000** (or **£50,000** personal injury) threshold."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A £60,000 contract claim. Which court, and which track?", body:"It is **issued in the County Court** and allocated to the **intermediate track**. \"Which court?\" and \"Which track?\" are **different questions**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A £150,000 claim", body:"A £150,000 claim **may** be issued in the High Court or the County Court, and goes to the **multi-track**."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Library room 04 (The Membership Desk). Source: src/content/ls.js topic aml-framework-cdd (notes only). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["ls/aml-framework-cdd"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Money laundering", body:"**Money laundering** is the process of making the **proceeds of crime look legitimate**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Why law firms attract criminals", body:"Large sums pass through their **client accounts**, and **property purchases**, **company formations** and **trusts** are good ways to hide where money came from."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"POCA 2002: the offences, for everyone", body:"The **Proceeds of Crime Act 2002 (POCA)** creates the **criminal offences** (laundering, failing to report, tipping off). It applies to **everyone**, in every area of practice. It is covered in the next topic."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"MLR 2017: duties for the regulated sector", body:"The **Money Laundering, Terrorist Financing and Transfer of Funds (Information on the Payer) Regulations 2017 (MLR 2017)** impose **preventive duties** on businesses in the **regulated sector**, which includes solicitors doing **transactional** work."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What the MLR duties are", body:"Risk assessment, policies, training, and above all **customer due diligence (CDD)**, i.e. checking who the client is. This topic covers the MLR 2017."}
 ]},
 {h:"What money laundering is", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Placement", body:"Criminal cash **enters the financial system**.\nExample: paying cash into a bank or a solicitor's **client account**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Layering", body:"The money is **moved through transactions to hide its origin**.\nExample: transfers between companies and countries; an **aborted transaction with a refund to a third party**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Integration", body:"The money **returns looking clean**.\nExample: **buying a house or business** with it."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Not all three stages", body:"A solicitor **need not be involved in all three stages** to commit an offence."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A classic red flag", body:"A client pays money into the client account and then **cancels the deal** and asks for it to be sent to a **different person** or **another country** (using the firm to layer)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A client pays into the client account, cancels, and wants the money sent abroad. What is the firm being used for?", body:"**Layering.** It is the classic red flag: the firm is being used to move the money and hide its origin."}
 ]},
 {h:"International and UK context", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"FATF", body:"The **Financial Action Task Force (FATF)** is an intergovernmental body that sets global anti-money laundering (AML) and counter-terrorist financing standards (the **FATF Recommendations**) and **assesses countries**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"FATF's black list and grey list", body:"FATF publishes lists of **high-risk jurisdictions**: the \"**call for action**\" list, often called the **black list**, and the \"**increased monitoring**\" or **grey list**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Kept after Brexit", body:"The MLR 2017 originally implemented the EU's **Fourth Money Laundering Directive**. They were **kept after Brexit** and have been amended several times."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The 2026 amendments", body:"Most recently by the **Money Laundering and Terrorist Financing (Amendment) Regulations 2026** (**SI 2026/621**, a statutory instrument), mostly in force from **30 June 2026**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Terrorist financing", body:"**Terrorist financing** offences are in the **Terrorism Act 2000**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Financial sanctions: OFSI, no exceptions", body:"**Financial sanctions** (bans on dealing with listed people, entities or countries) are a **separate regime** enforced by HM Treasury's **Office of Financial Sanctions Implementation (OFSI)**. Sanctions have **no** de minimis (small-amount) or risk-based exceptions."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Supervision: the SRA", body:"Each regulated business has an **AML supervisor** that monitors compliance. For SRA-regulated firms, the supervisor is the **SRA**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"OPBAS watches the supervisors", body:"Professional body supervisors are themselves overseen by **OPBAS** (the **Office for Professional Body AML Supervision**, part of the Financial Conduct Authority (FCA))."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Breach of the MLR is a crime", body:"Breach of the MLR 2017 is a **criminal offence** (up to **2 years**) as well as a regulatory matter, **even if no money is actually laundered**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Scope: when do the MLR 2017 apply to a law firm?", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Only when participating in transactions", body:"The MLR apply to **independent legal professionals** (solicitors and other lawyers) only when they **participate in financial or real property transactions** concerning the activities on the next cards."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Property and businesses", body:"**Buying and selling real property** (land and buildings) or **business entities** (companies, businesses)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Client money and accounts", body:"**Managing client money, securities or other assets**; **opening or managing bank, savings or securities accounts**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Companies, trusts and foundations", body:"**Organising contributions** necessary to create, operate or manage companies; **creating, operating or managing trusts, companies, foundations** or similar structures."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Tax advisers", body:"The MLR also apply to **tax advisers**: anyone giving material help or advice on tax affairs, by way of business."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Trust or company service providers (TCSPs)", body:"For example: **forming companies**, acting as or arranging for someone to act as a **director or trustee**, providing a **registered office**, and (since the 2026 amendments) **selling off-the-shelf companies** (ready-made companies)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Usually in scope (MLR apply)", body:"Residential and commercial **conveyancing**; buying or selling a **company or business**; **setting up a company or trust**, acting as **trustee**; managing **client money or investments**; **tax advice**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Usually out of scope (POCA still applies)", body:"Pure **litigation** (dispute resolution); **employment** advice; **family** work not involving property transfers; **will drafting** (but administering an estate and selling property can be in scope); **criminal** defence."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: can a litigator outside the regulated sector commit a POCA offence?", body:"**Yes.** The MLR 2017 (and the **s.330 failure to disclose** offence, which applies only in the regulated sector) do not apply to all legal work. But the POCA **principal offences** (ss.327-329) apply to **everyone**. A litigator who is not in the regulated sector can still commit **s.328** by entering into a **sham arrangement**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Firm-wide duties", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Risk assessment (reg 18)", body:"A written **firm-wide risk assessment** of the money laundering risks the firm faces, considering its **clients**, the **countries** it deals with, its **services**, its **transactions** and its **delivery channels** (e.g. clients not met face to face)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Policies, controls and procedures (reg 19)", body:"Written policies **proportionate to the firm's size**, covering **CDD**, **reporting**, **record keeping** and **internal controls**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Officers (reg 21): the MLCO", body:"A **money laundering compliance officer (MLCO)** at **board or senior management level**, responsible for the firm's compliance."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Officers (reg 21): the MLRO", body:"A **nominated officer** (usually called the **money laundering reporting officer, MLRO**) receives internal suspicion reports and decides whether to make a **suspicious activity report (SAR)** to the **National Crime Agency (NCA)**. In a small firm **one person can do both** jobs."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Screening and training (regs 21 and 24)", body:"Staff must be **screened** for their role and **trained** regularly in the law and in recognising suspicious transactions."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Record keeping (reg 40): 5 years", body:"Keep CDD records and supporting records of transactions for **5 years** after the end of the business relationship or the completion of the occasional transaction (a one-off transaction)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Beneficial owner approval", body:"Owners, officers and managers of firms must be **approved by the supervisor** (criminal records check), so a person with a relevant criminal conviction **cannot own or manage** a law firm doing in-scope work."}
 ]},
 {h:"Customer due diligence (CDD) (regs 27-28)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What CDD means", body:"**CDD** means the checks a firm must carry out to know **who its client is**, **who is behind the client**, and **what the client is doing**."}
 ]},
 {h:"When must CDD be done? (reg 27)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A new business relationship", body:"When establishing a **business relationship** (an ongoing professional relationship expected to last)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A one-off transaction of £12,000 or more", body:"When carrying out an **occasional transaction** (a one-off transaction outside a business relationship) worth **£12,000 or more**. The threshold was 15,000 euros until the 2026 amendments; a question will normally give you the figure."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Suspicion: whatever the value", body:"When the firm **suspects money laundering** or terrorist financing (**whatever the value**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Doubts about earlier ID", body:"When the firm **doubts** the truth or adequacy of identity documents or information **previously obtained**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Existing clients: risk-based", body:"For **existing clients**, at appropriate times on a **risk-sensitive** basis (e.g. when their circumstances change)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A one-off £5,000 job, but the firm suspects money laundering. Is CDD needed?", body:"**Yes.** Suspicion triggers CDD **whatever the value**."}
 ]},
 {h:"What does CDD involve? (reg 28)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Identify and verify the client", body:"**Identify the client and verify** their identity, on the basis of documents or information from a **reliable, independent source** (e.g. passport, driving licence, electronic verification, Companies House records for a company)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Someone acting for the client", body:"Where someone acts **on behalf of** the client, verify that they are **authorised** to do so and verify **their identity**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Purpose and intended nature", body:"Assess and, where appropriate, obtain information on the **purpose and intended nature** of the business relationship or transaction."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Ongoing monitoring", body:"Conduct **ongoing monitoring**: scrutinise transactions (including, where necessary, the **source of funds**, i.e. where the money for this transaction comes from) to make sure they fit what the firm knows of the client, and keep documents up to date."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Identify the beneficial owner", body:"Identify the **beneficial owner** (the individual who **ultimately owns or controls** the client), where it is not the client itself, and take reasonable measures to **verify** their identity and understand the **ownership and control structure**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Company: more than 25%", body:"For a **company**, a beneficial owner is any individual who owns or controls **more than 25%** of the shares or voting rights, or otherwise exercises control."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Trust: everyone involved", body:"For a **trust**: the **settlor** (the person who set up the trust), **trustees**, **beneficiaries** (or class) and **anyone controlling** the trust."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A register is not enough on its own", body:"Checking a register (e.g. the Companies House register of **people with significant control**) is **not enough on its own**; any **discrepancy** must be reported to Companies House."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"One person owns 30% of a client company's shares. Is she a beneficial owner?", body:"**Yes.** Any individual who owns or controls **more than 25%** of the shares or voting rights is a beneficial owner."}
 ]},
 {h:"Timing (reg 30)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Verify before", body:"Verification must normally be completed **before** the business relationship is established or the transaction is carried out."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exception: during set-up, if little risk", body:"It may be completed **during** the establishment of a business relationship if that is **necessary not to interrupt the normal conduct of business** and there is **little risk** of money laundering, provided it is completed **as soon as practicable**."}
 ]},
 {h:"If CDD cannot be completed (reg 31)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Must not act", body:"The firm **must not** carry out a transaction through a bank account with or on behalf of the client, and **must not** establish a business relationship or carry out the transaction."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Terminate and consider a SAR", body:"The firm **must terminate** any existing business relationship, and **must consider** making a SAR."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exception for lawyers", body:"This does not apply where a legal professional is in the course of **ascertaining the legal position** for the client or **defending or representing** the client in legal proceedings (including advice on starting or avoiding proceedings)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A new client wants Harlow & Co to buy a £600,000 flat through her company, but will not say who owns the company's shares. What must the firm do?", body:"**The firm cannot identify the beneficial owner, so it must not act**, must consider whether to make a SAR, and must not complete the purchase."}
 ]},
 {h:"Levels of due diligence", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Simplified due diligence (SDD) (reg 37)", body:"Where the firm assesses the relationship or transaction as **low risk** (e.g. the client is a company listed on a regulated market (an official stock market), or a **UK public authority**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"SDD is reduced, not \"no CDD\"", body:"**Reduced** checks, adjusted in extent and timing. It is **not \"no CDD\"**: identity must still be established and ongoing monitoring continues. **Not available if there is any suspicion.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Standard CDD (reg 28)", body:"**The default.** The CDD measures as above (identify and verify, beneficial owner, purpose, ongoing monitoring)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Enhanced due diligence (EDD) (regs 33-35)", body:"For **higher risk** (list on the next cards). **Extra** measures: more information on the client and beneficial owner, the **source of funds and source of wealth**, **senior management approval**, **closer ongoing monitoring**."}
 ]},
 {h:"When EDD is required (reg 33)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A high-risk third country", body:"Any business relationship or transaction with a person established in a **high-risk third country**: since the 2026 amendments, this mandatory requirement applies to **FATF \"call for action\" countries** (the FATF **black list**). **Grey-list** countries remain a **risk factor** the firm must weigh."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"PEPs, their family and close associates", body:"Where the client or a party is a **politically exposed person (PEP)** (someone entrusted with a prominent public function: see the next section), or a **family member** or **known close associate** of one."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Unusually complex or unusually large", body:"A transaction that is **unusually complex or unusually large** for its nature (since the 2026 amendments it must be *unusually* complex: ordinary complexity is not enough), or an **unusual pattern** of transactions, or one with **no apparent economic or legal purpose**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"False or stolen ID", body:"Where the client has given **false or stolen** identification documents and the firm **proposes to continue** dealing with them."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Any other high risk", body:"In **any other case** the firm's risk assessment identifies as high risk (e.g. **cash-intensive** businesses, clients **not met face to face**, **complex ownership** structures, or **unusual source of funds**)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Politically exposed persons (PEPs) (reg 35)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Who is a PEP", body:"An individual entrusted with a **prominent public function**, in the UK or abroad: e.g. heads of state and government, ministers, **MPs**, members of supreme courts and senior judges, ambassadors, senior military officers, members of central bank boards, and senior management of state-owned enterprises."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Junior officials are not PEPs", body:"**Middle-ranking and junior officials** are **not** PEPs."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Family members and close associates", body:"The rules also cover their **family members** (spouse or partner, children and their spouses or partners, parents) and **known close associates** (e.g. joint owners of a business)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"If the client is a PEP", body:"The firm must:\n- have **senior management approval** to establish or continue the relationship;\n- take adequate measures to establish the **source of wealth** (how the person built up their overall wealth) **and** the **source of funds** (where the money for this transaction comes from); and\n- conduct **enhanced ongoing monitoring**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Domestic (UK) PEPs", body:"Since **January 2024**, firms must treat a UK PEP (and their family and associates) as presenting a **lower starting level of risk** than a foreign PEP, absent other risk factors. They are **still PEPs** and **EDD still applies**, but it may be less extensive."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"At least 12 months after leaving office", body:"A person must continue to be treated as a PEP for at least **12 months** after they stop holding the public function (or longer if the risk requires). This does **not** apply to their **family members**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"The firm is asked to act on a house purchase for the spouse of a foreign government minister. What is needed?", body:"**She is a family member of a PEP, so EDD is required**: senior management approval, source of wealth and source of funds, and enhanced ongoing monitoring."}
 ]},
 {h:"Reliance (reg 39)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Relying on another firm", body:"A firm may **rely** on certain other regulated persons (e.g. another law firm, a bank, an accountant) to carry out CDD, if they **agree**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The relying firm stays liable", body:"The relying firm **remains liable** for any failure to apply the measures."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Information now, copies within 2 working days", body:"It must **immediately obtain** the CDD **information** from the other firm, and have an arrangement allowing it to obtain **copies of the identification and verification documents** within **2 working days** of a request."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Used cautiously", body:"In practice reliance is used cautiously: checking the other firm's checks is often as much work as doing your own."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Watch out: does SDD mean no checks? Does a UK PEP escape EDD?", body:"**No, and no.** **SDD is reduced CDD, not no CDD.** And a domestic PEP is **still a PEP**: the lower starting risk changes **how much** EDD is needed, not **whether** EDD applies."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: what are the three typical CDD traps?", body:"(1) Forgetting to identify and verify the **beneficial owner** of a corporate client;\n(2) assuming a firm can rely on another firm and pass on liability (it cannot: the relying firm **stays liable**); and\n(3) thinking a firm can complete a transaction and finish the checks later where CDD has failed (it must **stop and consider a SAR**)."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Library room 03 (The Everyone-Welcome Reading Room). Source: src/content/ls.js, topic equality-act (notes only). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["ls/equality-act"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"An overriding legal obligation", body:"The **Equality Act 2010 (EA 2010)** is an \"**overriding legal obligation**\": it applies to law firms **whatever the client wants**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What the Act protects", body:"It protects people who have a **protected characteristic** from **discrimination**, **harassment** and **victimisation**, and requires **reasonable adjustments** for disabled people."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Part 3: a service provider", body:"A law firm is bound in two roles. First, as a **service provider** to clients (**Part 3**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Part 5: an employer", body:"Second, as an **employer** (**Part 5**), covering **recruitment, pay, promotion, training and dismissal**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"SRA Principle 6", body:"The SRA rules overlap: **SRA Principle 6**: act in a way that encourages **equality, diversity and inclusion**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Code paras 1.1 and 1.5", body:"**Code of Conduct para 1.1**: do not **unfairly discriminate** by allowing your personal views to affect your professional relationships and the way you provide your services. **Para 1.5**: treat colleagues fairly and with respect; do not bully, harass or discriminate unfairly against them."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Also a regulatory breach", body:"A breach of the Act will **usually also be a regulatory breach**."}
 ]},
 {h:"The nine protected characteristics", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Age", body:"A particular age or range of ages. In **services**, protects only people aged **18 or over**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Gender reassignment", body:"A person proposing to undergo, undergoing or having undergone a process to change their sex. **No medical treatment is required.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Marriage and civil partnership", body:"Protected **at work only**, not in the provision of services. **Being single is not protected.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Pregnancy and maternity", body:"Protected in **work and services**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Race", body:"**Colour, nationality, and ethnic or national origins.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Religion or belief", body:"Any religion, any religious or philosophical belief, and the **lack** of religion or belief."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Sex", body:"Being a man or a woman. The Supreme Court held in **For Women Scotland v Scottish Ministers** (2025) that \"sex\" in the Act means **biological sex**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Sexual orientation", body:"Orientation towards the same sex, the opposite sex, or either sex."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Disability (s.6)", body:"A **physical or mental impairment** with a **substantial** (more than minor or trivial) and **long-term** (lasted or likely to last **12 months**) adverse effect on ability to carry out **normal day-to-day activities**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Disability from diagnosis", body:"**Cancer, HIV and multiple sclerosis** count **from diagnosis**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Direct discrimination (s.13)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Less favourably because of", body:"**Treating a person less favourably** than you treat (or would treat) others **because of** a protected characteristic."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"By association", body:"Because of someone the person is **connected with**, e.g. refusing to employ a woman because she cares for her disabled child."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"By perception", body:"Because you **wrongly think** the person has a characteristic."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Cannot be justified, except age", body:"Direct discrimination **cannot be justified**, except for **age**, where it can be justified as a **proportionate means of achieving a legitimate aim** (a genuine, lawful reason, pursued in a way that is no more than reasonably necessary)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: the practising Muslim client", body:"A partner refuses to take on a client because he is a **practising Muslim**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Is that lawful if the firm says other clients might be uncomfortable?", body:"**No.** It is **direct discrimination** on grounds of religion, and also breaches **Code para 1.1** and **Principle 6**. The firm's **motive** (e.g. \"other clients might be uncomfortable\") is **no defence**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Indirect discrimination (s.19)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A PCP applied to everyone", body:"Applying a **provision, criterion or practice (PCP)** (any rule, policy, condition or way of doing things) **to everyone**, where three things are true."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The three conditions", body:"It puts people who share a protected characteristic at a **particular disadvantage** compared with others; it puts **the individual** at that disadvantage; and the firm **cannot show** it is a **proportionate means of achieving a legitimate aim** (a genuine, lawful business reason, pursued in a way that is no more than reasonably necessary)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: full-time in the office", body:"A firm requires **all associates to work full-time in the office** with no exceptions. This puts **women** (who are more likely to have childcare responsibilities) at a particular disadvantage."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Is the full-time office rule unlawful?", body:"**It is indirect sex discrimination unless the firm can justify it** as proportionate, e.g. by showing part-time or hybrid work was genuinely unworkable."},
   {t:"compare", i:"", title:"Direct and indirect compared", cols:["", "Direct", "Indirect"], rows:[["**What happens**","Person treated worse **because of** the characteristic","Neutral rule applied to all hits one group harder"],["**Justification**","**Never**, except **age**","Yes, if **proportionate means of achieving a legitimate aim**"],["**Typical facts**","\"We don't act for people like you\"","\"All staff must...\" / \"We only communicate by...\""]]},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: how do you tell direct from indirect?", body:"Ask whether the firm's rule **mentions** or targets the characteristic (**direct**) or is **neutral on its face** but disadvantages a group (**indirect**). **Only indirect discrimination (and age) can be justified.**"}
 ]},
 {h:"Discrimination arising from disability (s.15)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Something arising in consequence", body:"Treating a disabled person **unfavourably** because of **something arising in consequence of** their disability (e.g. **time off for treatment**, **slower typing**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Unless: proportionate", body:"It is unlawful unless the treatment is a **proportionate means of achieving a legitimate aim**, or..."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Unless: did not know", body:"...the firm **did not know**, and **could not reasonably have been expected to know**, that the person was disabled."}
 ]},
 {h:"Harassment (s.26)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Unwanted conduct", body:"**Unwanted conduct related to** a relevant protected characteristic that has the **purpose or effect** of violating the person's **dignity**, or creating an **intimidating, hostile, degrading, humiliating or offensive** environment for them."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Did it have that effect?", body:"The tribunal considers the person's **perception**, the **other circumstances**, and whether it is **reasonable** for the conduct to have that effect."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Sexual harassment", body:"Also covers **sexual harassment** (unwanted conduct **of a sexual nature**) and **less favourable treatment because a person rejected or submitted to** such conduct."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Not relevant characteristics for harassment", body:"**Marriage and civil partnership** and **pregnancy and maternity** are **not** \"relevant characteristics\" for harassment (such cases are brought as **direct discrimination** instead)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Victimisation (s.27)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A detriment because of a protected act", body:"Subjecting a person to a **detriment** (any disadvantage) because they have done a **protected act**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Protected acts", body:"**Bringing proceedings** under the Act, **giving evidence** in them, **alleging** that someone has broken the Act, or doing **anything else in connection with the Act**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Not if false and in bad faith", body:"A **false** allegation made in **bad faith** is **not protected**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: the trainee and the rota", body:"A trainee complains that a partner made racist comments. She is then **left off the rota for interesting work**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Is it victimisation if her complaint is not upheld?", body:"**Yes, it is still victimisation**, whether or not her original complaint is upheld, **as long as it was made in good faith**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Pregnancy and maternity discrimination (ss.17-18)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Unfavourable treatment, no comparator", body:"**Unfavourable treatment** (**no comparator needed**: no need to show how someone else was or would be treated) because of **pregnancy, pregnancy-related illness or maternity leave**, in **work and in services**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Reasonable adjustments (ss.20-21 and Schedules 2 and 8)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The duty", body:"To take such steps as it is **reasonable** to take to avoid a **substantial disadvantage** to disabled people caused by a PCP, a physical feature, or the lack of an auxiliary aid or service."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A PCP", body:"Only taking instructions **by phone** → offer **email, video or face-to-face** meetings for a client with a hearing impairment."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A physical feature", body:"**Steps** to the office entrance → a **ramp**, or meet the client at an **accessible location**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Lack of an auxiliary aid or service", body:"Provide a **British Sign Language interpreter**, documents in **large print** or **Braille** (raised dots read by touch), a **hearing loop** (which sends sound straight to hearing aids), or **extra time**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Service providers: anticipatory", body:"For **service providers** the duty is **anticipatory**: a firm must think **in advance** about what disabled clients in general may need, rather than waiting until a particular client asks."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Employers: a known person", body:"For **employers**, the duty is owed to a **particular disabled applicant or employee** the employer **knows (or should know)** about."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No passing on the cost", body:"The firm **cannot pass the cost** of a reasonable adjustment on to the disabled person."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What is reasonable", body:"It depends on how **effective** the step would be, how **practicable** it is, its **cost**, and the firm's **resources** and size."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Failure is discrimination (s.21)", body:"**Failure to make a reasonable adjustment is itself discrimination** (s.21)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: which answer wins in a reasonable adjustments question?", body:"The one that removes the disadvantage **at no cost to the client**. Options that **charge the client for an interpreter**, or ask the client to **bring a family member to interpret**, are wrong (a family member may also create **confidentiality** problems)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Liability, prevention and positive action", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Vicarious liability (s.109)", body:"The firm is liable for discriminatory acts of its **employees** done **in the course of employment**, and of its **agents** acting with its authority, **whether or not it knew or approved**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The only defence: all reasonable steps", body:"Its only defence for employees is that it took **all reasonable steps** to prevent them (e.g. clear **policies**, effective **training**, **acting on complaints**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Individual liability (s.110)", body:"The employee who discriminated can **also be personally liable** (s.110)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Duty to prevent sexual harassment (s.40A)", body:"In force **26 October 2024**: employers must take **reasonable steps** to prevent **sexual harassment** of their employees in the course of employment."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Up to 25% more compensation", body:"If an employee wins a sexual harassment claim and the employer **breached this duty**, the **employment tribunal** may **increase compensation by up to 25%**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Later changes: Employment Rights Act 2025", body:"The **Employment Rights Act 2025** strengthens this to \"**all** reasonable steps\" and adds liability for harassment by **third parties** such as clients, but those changes take effect **after September 2026**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Positive action (ss.158-159)", body:"An employer **may** take **proportionate** steps to help people from an **under-represented** or disadvantaged group, e.g. **targeted encouragement** to apply and **mentoring**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The tie-breaker", body:"In recruitment or promotion, preferring a candidate from an under-represented group where candidates are **equally qualified** (the \"**tie-breaker**\")."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Positive discrimination is unlawful", body:"**Positive discrimination** (choosing someone because of their characteristic **regardless of merit**, or **quotas**) is generally **unlawful**."}
 ]},
 {h:"The SRA angle", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Refusing a client: legitimate reasons", body:"Refusing a client is allowed for a **legitimate reason** (e.g. lack of **expertise**, lack of **capacity** (not enough time or resources to take the work on), a **conflict of interest**, or the client wanting you to act **unlawfully**), but **never** because of a protected characteristic."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Workforce diversity data", body:"SRA firms must also **collect and report workforce diversity data** to the SRA and **publish a summary**, as part of encouraging equality, diversity and inclusion."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Watch out: the real reason", body:"A firm can turn away a client for a good **business reason**, but if the **real reason** is a protected characteristic, it is **direct discrimination**, which cannot be justified (except for age)."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Library room 06 (The Newspaper Room). Source: src/content/ls.js topic financial-services (notes only). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["ls/financial-services"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Solicitors meet investments", body:"Solicitors often come across investments in their work: **selling shares** in a company, **administering an estate** holding unit trusts, or **arranging insurance** for a client."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"FSMA: authorised or exempt", body:"The **Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 (FSMA)** makes it a **crime** to carry on a **regulated activity** without being **authorised** by the financial regulators or **exempt**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Most law firms are not authorised", body:"So they must make sure that what they do either:\n- is **not a regulated activity** at all (no specified investment, no specified activity, or an **exclusion** applies), or\n- falls within the **Part 20 exemption** for professional firms regulated by the SRA."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Financial promotions, and a four-question route", body:"Separately, they must not make **financial promotions** (invitations to invest) unless an exemption applies. This topic gives you a **four-question route** through the rules."}
 ]},
 {h:"The framework", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"FSMA 2000", body:"**FSMA 2000** is the main Act."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The RAO", body:"The **Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 (Regulated Activities) Order 2001 (RAO)** lists the **specified investments**, **specified activities** and **exclusions**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The FCA and the PRA", body:"The **Financial Conduct Authority (FCA)** regulates conduct and **authorises most firms**. The **Prudential Regulation Authority (PRA)** (part of the **Bank of England**) authorises **banks, insurers and major investment firms**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The SRA's two rule books", body:"The **SRA Financial Services (Scope) Rules** and **SRA Financial Services (Conduct of Business) Rules** govern what SRA firms may do under the **Part 20 exemption**."}
 ]},
 {h:"The general prohibition (s.19)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"s.19: authorised or exempt only", body:"No person may carry on a **regulated activity** in the UK, or **purport** to do so (claim to), unless they are **authorised** or **exempt**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"s.23: a crime, up to 2 years", body:"Breach is a **criminal offence** (s.23: up to **2 years'** imprisonment and/or a fine). It is a defence to show you took **all reasonable precautions** and exercised **all due diligence**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"s.26: agreements unenforceable", body:"Agreements made in breach are **unenforceable** against the other party, who can **recover** any money or property they paid and **compensation** for loss."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Also a regulatory matter", body:"For a solicitor, a breach would also be a serious **regulatory** matter."}
 ]},
 {h:"Is it a regulated activity? Four questions (s.22 and the RAO)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"1. A specified investment?", body:"Is there a **specified investment**?"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"2. A specified activity?", body:"Is there a **specified activity** in relation to that investment?"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"3. By way of business?", body:"Is it carried on **by way of business**? (For solicitors, who are paid for their work, this is **normally yes**.)"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"4. An exclusion? Then Part 20?", body:"Does an **exclusion** apply? If not, can the firm use the **Part 20 exemption**? If not, it must be **authorised** (or **refer** the client to someone who is)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Step 1: Specified investments", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Shares", body:"**Shares** in companies (UK or overseas, listed or private)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Debentures, bonds and gilts", body:"**Debentures** (a company's written acknowledgment of a debt), **bonds**, loan stock, **gilts** (UK government bonds)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Units in collective investment schemes", body:"**Units in collective investment schemes** (pooled funds: **unit trusts**, OEICs (open-ended investment companies))."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Options, futures and contracts for differences", body:"**Options, futures** and **contracts for differences** (contracts whose value depends on the price of something else)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Contracts of insurance", body:"**Long-term** (life policies, pensions) and **general** (including after-the-event (**ATE**) legal expenses insurance, bought after a dispute arises to cover legal costs)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Deposits, mortgages, funeral plans, credit", body:"**Deposits** (e.g. bank accounts); **regulated mortgage contracts**, **funeral plans**, rights under **credit agreements**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Not specified investments", body:"**Land and buildings** themselves; **cash**, cheques and bank notes; physical commodities and goods (e.g. **gold bars, art**); **ordinary contracts** for goods and services."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A client asks you to sell his house. Is that regulated under FSMA?", body:"**No.** The house is **land, not a specified investment**, so selling it is not regulated under FSMA (although arranging the client's **mortgage** could be)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Step 2: Specified activities", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Dealing as principal", body:"Buying, selling, subscribing for or underwriting (agreeing to take up any not bought by others) investments **for yourself**.\nExample: a firm buying shares for its own account."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Dealing as agent", body:"Buying or selling investments **on behalf of a client**.\nExample: signing a share transfer as attorney for a client."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Arranging deals in investments", body:"Making **arrangements** for another person to buy, sell, subscribe for or underwrite, or arrangements with a view to someone participating.\nExample: introducing buyer and seller of shares and sorting out the paperwork."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Managing investments", body:"Managing investments belonging to another **with discretion**.\nExample: running a client's share portfolio."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Safeguarding and administering", body:"**Holding and administering** investments for others.\nExample: holding share certificates and dealing with dividends."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Advising on investments", body:"Advice to a person as an investor (or potential investor) on the **merits** of buying, selling, subscribing for or underwriting a **particular** investment.\nExample: \"You should sell your shares in X plc\"."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Insurance, consumer credit and mortgages", body:"**Insurance distribution** (arranging or advising on insurance); **consumer credit** (lending or credit broking); **regulated mortgage** activities (mortgage advice and arranging).\nExample: arranging ATE insurance for a litigation client."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Generic advice is not advising on investments", body:"For example: \"**shares are riskier than gilts**\", advice on the **legal effect of a document**, or **tax consequences in general terms**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Watch out: \"buy shares in X plc\", or \"go and see an IFA\"?", body:"Advising on a **particular** investment (e.g. \"buy shares in X plc\") is **regulated**; recommending a client **see an IFA** (independent financial adviser) is an **excluded introduction**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Step 3: Exclusions (RAO)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exclusions: not regulated at all", body:"If an exclusion applies, the activity is **not regulated** at all, so **no authorisation or exemption** is needed."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Introducing (art 33)", body:"Introducing a client to an **authorised person** (e.g. an **independent financial adviser, IFA**) for independent advice or discretionary management."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Dealing or arranging through an authorised person (arts 22, 29)", body:"Dealing as agent or arranging where the transaction is entered into **with or through an authorised person**, and the client has **not sought or received advice** from you on the merits (or you receive no commission you do not pass on)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Necessary part of other professional services (art 67)", body:"Arranging or advising that is a **necessary** part of other services (legal advice, tax advice) and is **not remunerated separately**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Trustees and personal representatives (art 66)", body:"Dealing, arranging, managing or safeguarding by a **trustee or PR** (personal representative: the person administering a deceased person's estate) acting as such, provided they are **not separately paid** for it (and advice is to fellow trustees or PRs or beneficiaries)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Sale of a body corporate (art 70)", body:"Dealing, arranging or advising on the sale of shares carrying **50% or more** of the voting rights in a company (or with a view to that), where the parties are individuals or bodies corporate (companies)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Execution only", body:"Acting on a client's instructions **without giving advice** (often combined with art 22/29)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"An executor client asks the firm to sell the deceased's shareholding in a listed company through a stockbroker; the firm gives no advice. Which exclusion?", body:"**The art 22/29 exclusion applies** (dealing through an authorised person, no advice). If a partner acts as **executor** herself, the **art 66** exclusion can apply."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: what should you check before going near Part 20?", body:"Many questions are answered at **Step 1** or **Step 3**. Check whether the asset is a specified investment at all (**land is not**), and whether an exclusion such as **introducing to an IFA** or the **sale of a body corporate** (50%+ of the voting shares) applies."}
 ]},
 {h:"Step 4: The Part 20 exemption for professional firms (FSMA ss.325-327)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Part 20: no FCA authorisation needed, if all conditions are met", body:"A firm regulated by a **designated professional body** (the **Law Society**, which regulates through the **SRA**) may carry on certain regulated activities **without FCA authorisation** if **all** these conditions are met (s.327)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No unaccounted-for reward", body:"The firm must not receive any **pecuniary reward** (money) or other advantage for the activity from anyone other than its client, unless it **accounts to the client** for it (e.g. **commission** on a policy must be passed on to the client, unless the client agrees otherwise after being told)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Incidental", body:"The activity must be **incidental** to the provision of professional (legal) services: it **arises out of, or is complementary to**, a particular legal service to that client, and is **not a major part** of the firm's practice."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Permitted", body:"The firm may only carry on activities **permitted by the SRA Scope Rules**, and not activities the **FCA** has prohibited for exempt firms."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Compliance", body:"The firm must **comply** with the SRA **Scope Rules** and **Conduct of Business Rules**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Incidental (Part 20) v necessary (art 67)", body:"**Incidental** (Part 20): the investment work arises from a legal service, e.g. **selling shares as part of administering an estate**.\n**Necessary** (art 67 exclusion): the investment activity is a **necessary part** of the legal service and **not separately paid for**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: what trap do questions set with \"incidental\" and \"necessary\"?", body:"Questions sometimes offer the **wrong one** as the reason the firm does not need authorisation. Incidental = **Part 20**; necessary = the **art 67 exclusion**."}
 ]},
 {h:"SRA Financial Services (Scope) Rules: what is prohibited", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Market making and dealing as principal", body:"**Market making** (holding yourself out as willing to buy and sell an investment at prices you set) and **dealing as principal** with a view to profit."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Recommending retail investments or pensions", body:"**Recommending** a client to buy or invest in a **retail investment product** (e.g. units in a unit trust, life policies, investment bonds), or a **personal pension**, or advising on **pension transfers or opt-outs**, except by passing the client to an **authorised person** and acting only on their advice."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Discretionary management", body:"Managing investments **with discretion** for private clients, unless the decisions are taken by (or on the advice of) an **authorised person**, with limited exceptions for trustees and PRs."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Lending", body:"Acting as a **lender** under regulated credit or mortgage agreements, and certain other high-risk activities."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Insurance distribution: on the FCA Register", body:"Firms carrying on **insurance distribution** (e.g. arranging ATE or title insurance (insurance against defects in the legal title to land)) under Part 20 must be on the **FCA Register** as an exempt professional firm."}
 ]},
 {h:"SRA Financial Services (Conduct of Business) Rules", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Status disclosure", body:"Tell clients **in writing** that the firm is **not authorised by the FCA**, is **regulated by the SRA**, and **how to complain** (including to the **Legal Ombudsman**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Best execution", body:"Get the **best terms reasonably available** for the client when carrying out a transaction."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Records", body:"Keep records of **transactions** executed for clients and of **commissions** received and how they were accounted for."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Execution-only: confirm in writing", body:"**Execution-only** transactions must be **confirmed in writing** to the client."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Insurance distribution extras", body:"Extra rules for **insurance distribution** (e.g. a **demands and needs statement**: a written statement of what the client needs from the insurance)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"In a divorce, a solicitor advises the wife to cash in a particular life policy and reinvest in a named unit trust. Can Part 20 apply?", body:"**No.** This is **advising on particular specified investments**, not an excluded activity, and **recommending a retail investment product is prohibited** by the Scope Rules, so Part 20 cannot apply. The solicitor should instead **introduce her to an IFA** (excluded under art 33)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Financial promotion (s.21)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"s.21: no invitation to invest", body:"A person must not, **in the course of business**, **communicate an invitation or inducement** to engage in **investment activity** (e.g. \"buy these shares\"), unless one of the next two cards applies."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Unless authorised or approved", body:"They are an **authorised person**; or the content has been **approved** by an authorised person with **FCA permission to approve** promotions."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Or an exemption applies", body:"An **exemption** in the Financial Promotion Order applies, e.g. **one-off** non-real-time communications (such as a letter, not a live conversation) **tailored** to the recipient, communications about the **sale of a body corporate**, and communications by exempt professional firms that are **incidental** to their professional services."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Breach: a crime, up to 2 years", body:"Breach is a **criminal offence** (up to **2 years**), and agreements resulting from it may be **unenforceable**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Consumer credit", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Fees by instalments may be credit", body:"Letting clients pay **fees by instalments** may be providing **credit**, a **regulated activity**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exempt: 12 or fewer instalments, within 12 months, no charges", body:"It is **exempt** if the credit is repayable in **12 or fewer instalments** within **12 months**, with **no interest or other charges**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A firm lets a client pay a £3,000 bill in six monthly instalments, interest-free. Does it need consumer credit authorisation?", body:"**No. This is an exempt agreement.** Charging **interest** would take it **outside** the exemption."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Library room 08 (The Community Room). Source: src/content/ls.js, topic funding-legal-aid-tpf-lei (notes only). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["ls/funding-legal-aid-tpf-lei"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Who else can help pay?", body:"If a client cannot or does not want to pay privately, or on a CFA or DBA, there are **three further funding sources** on the SQE1 specification."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Civil legal aid", body:"**State funding** for people of **limited means**, but only for the **types of case listed** in the legislation, and only if the case passes a **means test** and a **merits test**. (Criminal legal aid is tested in Criminal Practice, not here.)"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Third-party (litigation) funding", body:"A **commercial funder** pays the costs of a claim in return for a **share of the proceeds** if it wins."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Legal expenses insurance", body:"Insurance that pays legal costs, bought either **before** a dispute arises (**BTE**, before-the-event) or **after** it (**ATE**, after-the-event)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The other four are next door", body:"Private retainers, fixed fees, CFAs and DBAs are in the previous topic."}
 ]},
 {h:"Civil legal aid: the framework", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Where civil legal aid comes from", body:"**Part 1** of the **Legal Aid, Sentencing and Punishment of Offenders Act 2012 (LASPO)** and regulations under it."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The Legal Aid Agency", body:"Administered by the **Legal Aid Agency (LAA)**, an executive agency of the Ministry of Justice. Only providers with an **LAA contract** can do legal aid work."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Three hurdles", body:"The case must be **in scope**, the client must pass the **means test**, and the case must pass the **merits test**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Hurdle 1: scope (LASPO Schedule 1)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"In scope: family with abuse evidence, and care proceedings", body:"**Family** cases where there is evidence of **domestic abuse** or **child abuse** (for the victim), and **care proceedings** (public law children cases)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"In scope: housing", body:"**Housing**: possession of the home, homelessness, serious **disrepair** risking health."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"In scope: debt where the home is at risk", body:"**Debt** where the client's **home is at risk** (e.g. mortgage repossession)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"In scope: discrimination", body:"**Discrimination** claims under the Equality Act 2010."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"In scope: asylum, detention, trafficking", body:"**Asylum**, immigration detention, trafficking."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"In scope: public law and mental health", body:"**Judicial review**, **mental health**, community care, and claims against public authorities for serious abuse of power."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"In scope: baby brain injury only", body:"**Clinical negligence** only for infants with **birth-related brain injury** (in the womb or within **8 weeks** of birth)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Out of scope: private family without abuse evidence", body:"Private family disputes (divorce, finances, child arrangements) **without** abuse evidence."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Out of scope: most personal injury", body:"Most **personal injury** claims."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Out of scope: most contract and consumer", body:"Most **contract** and consumer disputes."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Out of scope: most employment", body:"Most **employment** claims (**except discrimination**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Out of scope: most non-asylum immigration", body:"Most non-asylum **immigration**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Out of scope: welfare benefits advice", body:"**Welfare benefits** advice (except appeals on a **point of law**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Out of scope: other clinical negligence and more", body:"Other clinical negligence, defamation, business disputes, wills and trusts."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exceptional case funding (s.10)", body:"Under LASPO **s.10**, an out-of-scope case can be funded if refusing legal aid would **breach the client's rights** under the **European Convention on Human Rights** (e.g. the right to a fair trial, **Article 6**) or would **risk** such a breach."}
 ]},
 {h:"Hurdle 2: the means test", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Income and capital", body:"The applicant must be financially eligible on **income** and **capital**. The LAA looks at three things."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Gross income", body:"**Gross income** (total income before deductions) must be below an upper limit."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Disposable income", body:"**Disposable income** (gross income minus allowable deductions such as tax, national insurance contributions (NICs), housing costs, childcare and allowances for dependants) must be below a limit."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Disposable capital", body:"**Disposable capital** (savings, investments, equity in property above certain allowances) must be below a limit."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Passporting", body:"People receiving certain means-tested benefits (e.g. **Universal Credit**, income support, income-based jobseeker's allowance, income-related employment and support allowance (ESA), guarantee credit) **automatically satisfy the income test**. Their **capital is still assessed**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Free or a contribution", body:"Depending on income and capital, the client may receive legal aid **free** or have to pay a **contribution** from income and/or capital."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Not means tested", body:"**Some cases are not means tested**, e.g. parents and children in **care proceedings**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The exam gives you the figures", body:"The thresholds change from time to time (and are being revised under the government's means test review), so the exam will **give you any figures** needed."}
 ]},
 {h:"Hurdle 3: the merits test", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Where the merits criteria are", body:"Set out in the **Civil Legal Aid (Merits Criteria) Regulations 2013**, and depends on the level of service. For **full representation** in court, the main criteria follow."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Prospects: at least moderate (50% or more)", body:"Generally the case must have at least **moderate** prospects of success (**50% or more**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Marginal sometimes enough; poor fails", body:"In some categories (e.g. where the case has significant **wider public interest**, or is of **overwhelming importance to the client**) **marginal** prospects may be enough. \"**Poor**\" prospects (**below 45%**) fail."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Cost-benefit", body:"The likely benefit (e.g. damages) must **justify the likely costs**; e.g. for a money claim with moderate prospects, the likely damages must exceed the likely costs by a set ratio."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Reasonable private paying client", body:"Would someone paying privately, with enough means, **bring the case**?"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Levels of service", body:"**Legal Help** (initial advice and assistance, no court representation), **Family Help**, and **Legal Representation** (investigative representation to look into the strength of a case, and full representation for proceedings)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Consequences of having legal aid", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The statutory charge (s.25)", body:"Under LASPO **s.25**, if the client **recovers or preserves** money or property in the proceedings (e.g. damages, or keeping the family home in a divorce), the LAA has a **first charge** on it to recover the legal aid costs it paid that were **not recovered from the opponent**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Warn about the charge at the outset", body:"The client must be **warned at the outset**, because it can take much of what they win. Some items are **exempt** and the charge on a home can sometimes be **postponed**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Costs protection (s.26)", body:"Under LASPO **s.26**, if a legally aided client **loses**, the court can order them to pay the opponent's costs only up to an amount that is **reasonable** having regard to all the circumstances, including the **financial resources** of both parties and their **conduct**. In practice this is **often nil**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Reporting duties", body:"Legal aid clients must **report changes in their means**, and the solicitor must **report to the LAA** if the client is acting **unreasonably** or the case **loses merit**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: Maya", body:"Maya, on Universal Credit, faces possession proceedings by her landlord. **Housing possession is in scope, she is passported through the income test (her capital is still checked), and her defence must have sufficient merit.** If she keeps her home, the statutory charge could attach to anything recovered or preserved, so she must be warned."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: a very poor client has a straightforward road traffic accident claim. Legal aid?", body:"**No.** Legal aid questions often hinge on **scope**. A straightforward road traffic accident claim, a contract dispute or a private child arrangements case without abuse evidence is **out of scope**, so legal aid is not available however poor the client is. **Check scope before means or merits.**"}
 ]},
 {h:"Third-party (litigation) funding", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A commercial funder pays for a return", body:"A **commercial litigation funder** with no other interest in the case pays some or all of the client's legal costs (and sometimes provides cover for **adverse costs**, the other side's costs if the claim fails) in return for a **return** out of the proceeds if the claim succeeds: often a **multiple** of the money invested, or a **percentage** of damages."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Nothing if the claim fails", body:"If the claim fails, the funder gets **nothing** (it is \"**non-recourse**\")."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What it is used for", body:"Used mainly for **high-value commercial** claims, **class actions** (group claims) and **insolvency** claims, where damages are large enough to give the funder a good return."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No statutory regulator", body:"There is **no statutory regulator**. Funders are **self-regulated** through the voluntary **Association of Litigation Funders** Code of Conduct (e.g. on **capital adequacy** and **not controlling the litigation**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The client still instructs", body:"The funded client must still give the solicitor **instructions**: the solicitor's **duties are owed to the client, not the funder** (watch for **conflicts of interest**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Adverse costs: a non-party costs order", body:"A funder can be ordered to **pay the opponent's costs** if the claim fails (a **non-party costs order**)."}
 ]},
 {h:"PACCAR and its aftermath", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A percentage-of-damages LFA is a DBA (PACCAR, 2023)", body:"In **R (PACCAR Inc) v Competition Appeal Tribunal** ([2023] UKSC 28), funders of competition claims against members of a trucks cartel were entitled to a **percentage of any damages**. The Supreme Court held that a litigation funding agreement (**LFA**) under which the funder's return is calculated as a **percentage of damages** is a **damages-based agreement (DBA)** under s.58AA of the Courts and Legal Services Act 1990."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Unenforceable unless compliant; never in opt-out CAT claims", body:"So such an LFA is **unenforceable** unless it complies with the **DBA Regulations 2013**, and it **cannot be used at all** in **opt-out collective proceedings** (one claim brought on behalf of a whole class of people, who are included unless they choose to opt out) in the **Competition Appeal Tribunal (CAT)**, where DBAs are banned."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Funders switched to multiples", body:"Most LFAs were rewritten so the funder's return is a **multiple of its investment**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Multiples are not DBAs (Sony v Neill, 2025)", body:"In **Sony Interactive Entertainment Europe Ltd v Neill** ([2025] EWCA Civ 841), funders' returns in several competition class actions (including one against Sony over PlayStation pricing) were set as **multiples** of their investment, with a cap linked to the damages. The Court of Appeal held these LFAs were **not DBAs**, even though the return was capped by reference to damages, because the return was **not calculated as a share of damages**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Legislation: May 2024 and June 2025", body:"A bill to reverse PACCAR **fell** when Parliament was dissolved in **May 2024**. The **Civil Justice Council** recommended reversal and light-touch regulation (**June 2025**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Legislation: still the law (September 2026)", body:"The government announced in **December 2025** that it would legislate to reverse PACCAR when parliamentary time allows. No bill was included in the **May 2026 King's Speech**. **As at September 2026, PACCAR remains the law.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Watch out: percentage or multiple?", body:"A funding agreement giving the funder a **percentage of damages** is a **DBA** (PACCAR) and is unenforceable unless DBA-compliant. One based on a **multiple of the investment** is **not** (Sony v Neill)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Legal expenses insurance (LEI): before-the-event (BTE) insurance", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Bought before any dispute", body:"Cover bought **before** any dispute arises, usually as a cheap **add-on** to home, motor, travel or credit card insurance, or provided through **trade union** membership or an employer."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Always check for BTE", body:"Solicitors should **always check** whether the client has BTE cover: it may make other funding unnecessary, and failing to consider it could be a **breach of the duty to give best possible costs information**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Typical terms", body:"A **limit** on the amount covered (e.g. **£50,000 to £100,000**), a requirement for **reasonable prospects** of success (often **more than 50%**), and the **insurer's consent** before steps are taken."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Choice of lawyer", body:"Insurers often insist on their own **panel solicitors** at first. But under the **Insurance Companies (Legal Expenses Insurance) Regulations 1990**, the insured is **free to choose their own lawyer** once **proceedings or an inquiry** are started, or where a **conflict of interest** arises."}
 ]},
 {h:"After-the-event (ATE) insurance", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Bought after the dispute arises", body:"Cover bought **after** the dispute has arisen, usually alongside a **CFA** or **DBA**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What ATE covers", body:"The client's liability for the **opponent's costs** if they lose, and often their **own disbursements** (e.g. expert fees, court fees)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The premium", body:"The **premium** can be large; it is often **deferred** and payable only if the case wins, sometimes **self-insured** (payable only out of damages)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Premium not recoverable since April 2013", body:"Since **April 2013** (LASPO), the **ATE premium is not recoverable** from the losing opponent, so it comes out of the client's damages. **Exception**: in **clinical negligence**, the part of the premium covering the cost of **expert reports on liability and causation** is still recoverable."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Personal injury: QOCS", body:"In **personal injury** claims, **qualified one-way costs shifting (QOCS)** (CPR 44.13-44.17) generally protects claimants from paying the defendant's costs if they lose, so ATE is mainly used there to cover the claimant's **own disbursements**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Arranging ATE is insurance distribution", body:"Arranging ATE (or any insurance) is **insurance distribution**, a regulated activity under the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 (**FSMA**): a law firm must do it under the **Part 20** exempt regime (and be on the **FCA Register**, the Financial Conduct Authority's list) or through an **FCA-authorised intermediary**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"BTE v ATE: bought", body:"**BTE**: before any dispute. **ATE**: after the dispute arises."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"BTE v ATE: covers", body:"**BTE**: own costs (and usually the opponent's), up to a limit. **ATE**: opponent's costs and own disbursements."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"BTE v ATE: typical source", body:"**BTE**: add-on to a home or motor policy, trade union. **ATE**: arranged by the solicitor with a CFA or DBA."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"BTE v ATE: choice of solicitor", body:"**BTE**: panel at first; free choice once proceedings start or there is a conflict. **ATE**: the client's own solicitor."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"BTE v ATE: premium recoverable from the opponent?", body:"**BTE**: not applicable. **ATE**: **no** (since April 2013), except clinical negligence expert reports."}
 ]},
 {h:"Other sources of funding", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Trade union", body:"**Trade union** or membership organisation funding (common in employment and personal injury)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Employer and directors' and officers' insurance", body:"**Employer** funding (e.g. for directors sued in their capacity as directors), and **directors' and officers' insurance**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Crowdfunding", body:"**Crowdfunding** (raising money from many people, often online), especially in **public law** cases."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Pro bono and law centres", body:"**Pro bono** (free) work by solicitors or advice agencies, and **law centres**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Litigation loans", body:"**Litigation loans** from banks, sometimes linked to ATE."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Watch out: the statutory charge", body:"Legal aid's **statutory charge** can take a large part of what a client recovers or preserves. **Warn the client at the outset.**"},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: in what order do you give funding advice?", body:"(1) Check for **BTE** cover or **union** funding; (2) consider **legal aid** if the case is **in scope** and the client is eligible; (3) then a **CFA or DBA**, usually with **ATE** insurance; (4) **third-party funding** only for high-value claims; otherwise **private** funding."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Library room 07 (The Library Cafe). Source: src/content/ls.js, topic funding-private-cfa-dba (notes only). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["ls/funding-private-cfa-dba"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"First: how will the client pay?", body:"Before taking on any matter, a solicitor must discuss **how the client will pay** and give the client the best possible information about **cost**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The seven funding options", body:"The SQE1 specification lists: **private retainer**, **fixed fees**, **conditional fee agreements (CFAs)**, **damages-based agreements (DBAs)**, **civil legal aid**, **third-party funding** and **legal expenses insurance**. This topic covers the first four."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Private retainer", body:"The client pays the firm's charges as the work goes on (usually **by the hour**), **whatever the outcome**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Fixed fee", body:"An **agreed price** for a **defined piece of work**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"CFA: \"no win, no fee\"", body:"The lawyer is paid normal charges plus a **success fee** (a percentage uplift on **costs**) **only if the client wins**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"DBA: a contingency fee", body:"The lawyer takes a **percentage of the damages** the client wins, and **nothing if they lose**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The other three are next door", body:"Legal aid, third-party funding and insurance are in the next topic."}
 ]},
 {h:"The duty to give costs information", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"SRA Code para 8.7: best possible information", body:"You must ensure clients receive the **best possible information**, both **at the time of engagement** and when appropriate **as the matter progresses**, about **how their matter will be priced** and, as appropriate, the **likely overall cost**, including any **costs incurred**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"At the outset: basis and estimate", body:"In practice, discuss at the outset the **basis** of charging (hourly rate, fixed fee, CFA, DBA) and an **estimate** of the total."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"At the outset: disbursements and VAT", body:"Discuss **disbursements** (payments to third parties for the client, e.g. **court fees**, barristers' (**counsel's**) **fees**, **experts' fees**, **search fees**) and **VAT**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Does the client already have funding?", body:"Check whether the client may already have funding: **before-the-event (BTE) insurance** (e.g. legal cover attached to home or car insurance), **trade union** or employer support, or eligibility for **legal aid**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Costs risk: the loser pays", body:"In litigation the general rule is that the **loser pays the winner's costs** (Civil Procedure Rules (CPR) Part 44), but the court has a **discretion**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Costs risk: you still pay your own solicitor", body:"The winner **rarely recovers all** its costs, and the client remains **primarily liable to pay their own solicitor**, whatever is recovered from the other side."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Is it worth it?", body:"Discuss whether the likely outcome **justifies the expense** (cost-benefit)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Link: the Transparency Rules", body:"The **Transparency Rules** (see **Transparency Rules, client care and costs information**) also require price information to be **published** for some services."}
 ]},
 {h:"Private retainer", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Win or lose, the client pays", body:"The client pays the firm directly under the **retainer** (the contract between solicitor and client), **regardless of whether the matter succeeds**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Usually an hourly rate", body:"Usually charged at an **hourly rate** (which may vary with the **seniority** of the fee earner), plus **disbursements** and **VAT**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Estimate: not binding", body:"An **estimate** is **not binding**, but it must be **realistic** and **updated** if it is going to be exceeded. The client should be **told promptly** if costs will exceed it."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Quotation or fixed fee: binding", body:"A **quotation** (or fixed fee) **is binding**: the firm cannot charge more for the work described. Define the **scope** carefully; extra work outside the scope can be charged separately **if agreed**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Cap: binding", body:"A **cap** **is binding**: costs cannot go above the agreed limit. The **firm bears the risk** above the cap."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Money on account is client money", body:"A firm can ask for a **payment on account** of costs and disbursements before starting. Until a bill is delivered (or the money is spent on a disbursement), it is **client money** and must be held in the **client account** under the **SRA Accounts Rules**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Interest only if agreed", body:"**Interest** on unpaid bills can be charged **only if the client has agreed it** (usually in the client care letter or terms of business)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Challenging a bill: assessment", body:"The client can challenge a solicitor's bill by asking the court to **assess** it (check the amount) under the **Solicitors Act 1974**. The client must normally apply within **one month** of delivery of the bill for an assessment **as of right**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Complaining about fees", body:"The client can also complain to the **firm** and then the **Legal Ombudsman** about excessive fees."}
 ]},
 {h:"Fixed fees", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Whatever the time spent", body:"A **fixed fee** is an agreed amount for a defined piece of work, payable **whatever time is spent**. It is common in **residential conveyancing**, **probate**, **wills**, **immigration**, **motoring offences** and simple employment matters."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Certainty for the client, risk for the firm", body:"Advantage for the client: **certainty**. Risk for the firm: if the matter takes longer than expected, the **firm bears the extra cost**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Say what is included and excluded", body:"The firm must define clearly what is **included and excluded** (e.g. **disbursements**, **VAT**, extra work if the matter becomes **contested**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Not the same: fixed recoverable costs", body:"Do not confuse a fixed fee with **fixed recoverable costs (FRC)**: in many lower-value civil claims (the **fast track** and **intermediate track**, the court's tracks for lower-value claims, which together cover most claims up to **£100,000**; FRC apply to them since October 2023), the **winner can recover only fixed amounts** of costs from the loser, set by the CPR."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"FRC limit recovery from the opponent only", body:"FRC limit what is recovered from the **opponent**; they do **not** limit what the client and solicitor agree between themselves, so the client **may have to pay the difference**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Conditional fee agreements (CFAs)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Where CFAs come from", body:"**Courts and Legal Services Act 1990 (CLSA) ss.58-58A**; **Conditional Fee Agreements Order 2013**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No win, no fee: base costs plus a success fee", body:"The solicitor's fees (or part of them) are payable **only if the client wins**. If the client wins, the solicitor is paid **base costs** (normal hourly charges) plus, if agreed, a **success fee**: a **percentage uplift on the base costs**, to reward the lawyer for the risk of being paid nothing."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Discounted CFA: no win, lower fee", body:"A **discounted CFA** is also allowed: the solicitor charges a **reduced rate if the client loses** and full rates (perhaps plus a success fee) if they win."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Formalities (s.58)", body:"The CFA must be **in writing**; it must **not** relate to **criminal** or **family** proceedings; and if it provides for a success fee, it must **state the percentage** of the uplift."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Success fee cap: 100% of base costs", body:"The general cap on the success fee is **100%** of base costs."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Personal injury cap: 25% of PSLA and past loss", body:"In **personal injury** (claimant, **first-instance** proceedings, i.e. the first court to hear the case, not an appeal), the success fee cannot exceed **25%** of the damages for **pain, suffering and loss of amenity (PSLA)** and **past pecuniary (financial) loss**, after deducting any benefits recoverable by the **Compensation Recovery Unit (CRU)**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Future loss is not counted", body:"Damages for **future loss** are **excluded** from the 25% calculation, to protect the money the claimant needs for **future care**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The client pays the success fee", body:"Since **1 April 2013** (**Legal Aid, Sentencing and Punishment of Offenders Act 2012 (LASPO)**, s.44), a success fee is **not recoverable from the losing opponent** (with limited exceptions for older agreements and some specialist claims). The **winning client pays it**, usually out of their **damages**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The opponent still pays base costs", body:"The opponent still pays the client's **base costs** (subject to assessment)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"If the client loses", body:"No base costs or success fee to their own solicitor (under a pure CFA), but the client may still have to pay **disbursements** and the **opponent's costs** (hence **after-the-event (ATE) insurance**, bought after the dispute arises, or **QOCS** protection in personal injury cases: qualified one-way costs shifting, which generally stops a losing personal injury claimant having to pay the defendant's costs)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: Asha's CFA", body:"Asha's personal injury claim succeeds. Damages: PSLA **£30,000**, past loss of earnings **£10,000**, future care **£50,000**. Base costs **£20,000** with a **100%** success fee (£20,000). **The defendant pays the base costs; Asha pays the success fee, capped at 25% of £40,000 (PSLA plus past loss) = £10,000.** The future care damages are not counted, and she keeps the rest."}
 ]},
 {h:"Damages-based agreements (DBAs)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Where DBAs come from", body:"**CLSA 1990 s.58AA**; **Damages-Based Agreements Regulations 2013**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A percentage of what is won", body:"The lawyer is paid a **percentage of the sum recovered** by the client (a contingency fee), and **only if the client wins**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What the caps include", body:"The caps on the DBA payment **include VAT** and, in civil litigation, **counsel's fees**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Cap: personal injury 25%", body:"**Personal injury** (first instance): maximum **25%** of damages for **PSLA** and **past pecuniary loss** (net of CRU)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Cap: employment tribunal 35%", body:"**Employment tribunal** matters: maximum **35%** of sums recovered."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Cap: all other civil claims 50%", body:"**All other civil claims** (commercial, contract, etc.): maximum **50%** of sums recovered."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Formalities, or it is unenforceable", body:"The DBA must be **in writing**, specify the **claim**, the circumstances in which the **payment is due** and the **reason for the percentage**, and comply with the Regulations, or it is **unenforceable** (the lawyer gets nothing under it)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Where DBAs are not allowed", body:"Not in **criminal** or **family** proceedings, or in **opt-out collective proceedings** (one claim brought on behalf of a whole class of people, who are included unless they choose to opt out) in the **Competition Appeal Tribunal (CAT)**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Recovered costs are set off", body:"If the client wins in court, the opponent is ordered to pay the client's costs, assessed in the usual way. Those costs are **set off** against the DBA payment: the client pays only the **balance** out of damages."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The indemnity principle", body:"The client cannot recover more costs from the opponent than the **total DBA payment** (the **indemnity principle**: costs recovered cannot exceed what the client is liable to pay their own lawyer)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Hybrid DBAs: generally not permitted", body:"**Hybrid DBAs** (a reduced fee if the client loses, plus a percentage if they win) are generally regarded as **not permitted** under the current Regulations: the lawyer's only payment on a win is the agreed percentage (plus expenses)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: Ben's DBA", body:"Ben brings a commercial contract claim under a DBA at **40%**. He wins **£100,000**, and the court orders the defendant to pay **£25,000** of his costs. **His lawyer is entitled to £40,000 in total: £25,000 comes from the recovered costs and Ben pays the remaining £15,000 out of his damages**, keeping **£85,000**. A **55%** DBA would be **unenforceable** (over the 50% cap)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Funders taking a percentage: also DBAs (PACCAR, 2023)", body:"In **R (PACCAR Inc) v Competition Appeal Tribunal** (2023), funders financing competition claims against truck manufacturers were to receive a **percentage of any damages**. The Supreme Court held that such **litigation funding agreements** are **damages-based agreements** within s.58AA, so they are **unenforceable unless they comply with the DBA Regulations**. (Effect on litigation funding: see the next topic.)"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"CFA v DBA: paid if the client wins", body:"**CFA**: base costs + **success fee**. **DBA**: **% of damages**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"CFA v DBA: calculated on", body:"**CFA**: **costs** (uplift up to 100%). **DBA**: **damages**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"CFA v DBA: the personal injury cap", body:"**CFA**: success fee ≤ **25%** of PSLA + past loss. **DBA**: payment ≤ **25%** of PSLA + past loss."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"CFA v DBA: other caps", body:"**CFA**: 100% of base costs. **DBA**: **35%** employment; **50%** other."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"CFA v DBA: recoverable from the opponent?", body:"**CFA**: base costs yes; **success fee no** (since April 2013). **DBA**: opponent's costs set off; client pays the balance."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"CFA v DBA: not allowed", body:"**CFA**: criminal, family. **DBA**: criminal, family, opt-out CAT class actions."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Watch out: costs or damages?", body:"A **CFA** success fee is a percentage of **costs**; a **DBA** payment is a percentage of **damages**. Many questions turn on this difference."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Watch out: the client bears the success fee and ATE premium", body:"Since April 2013 a winning claimant generally **cannot recover** the CFA success fee or the ATE insurance premium (the price of after-the-event insurance) from the defendant. The client bears them."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: how do you do the short calculation?", body:"For a **CFA**, work out base costs × success fee %, then apply the **25% of PSLA + past loss** cap in personal injury. For a **DBA**, apply the percentage to the damages, check it is within the cap for that **type of claim**, then deduct any costs recovered from the opponent to find what the client pays."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Library room 02 (The Book Repair Bindery). Source: src/content/ls.js, topic pii-other-providers (notes only). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["ls/pii-other-providers"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Two financial safety nets", body:"When a client deals with an **SRA-authorised firm**, they get **two financial safety nets** that clients of unregulated providers do not."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Net 1: professional indemnity insurance (PII)", body:"**Compulsory insurance** that pays a client's claim if the firm is **negligent** (makes a mistake that causes loss). The rules are designed so the **insurer pays the client even if the firm has broken the policy terms**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Net 2: the SRA Compensation Fund", body:"A fund of **last resort**, **paid for by the profession**, that may compensate clients who lose money through a solicitor's **dishonesty** or a firm's **failure to account** for client money, when no other remedy is available."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Other providers", body:"This topic also covers the other people who can provide legal services: **alternative business structures (ABSs)**, other regulated lawyers (barristers, chartered legal executives, licensed conveyancers and others), and **unregulated** providers. Reserved activities and the SRA's general role are in the previous topic."}
 ]},
 {h:"Professional indemnity insurance (PII): who must have it", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Every SRA-authorised firm", body:"Every **SRA-authorised firm** (sole practice, recognised body or ABS) must take out and maintain PII complying with the **SRA Indemnity Insurance Rules**, with cover that meets the **Minimum Terms and Conditions (MTC)**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"A participating insurer", body:"The policy must be written by a **participating insurer**: an insurer that has signed an agreement with the SRA to provide cover on the MTC."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Freelance solicitors", body:"**Freelance solicitors** doing reserved work must have **adequate and appropriate** insurance, but do **not** have to buy MTC cover."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"In-house and unregulated businesses", body:"Solicitors working **in-house** or in **unregulated businesses** are not covered by the firm rules; they must make sure **clients understand what protections apply** (Code paras **8.10-8.11**)."}
 ]},
 {h:"The key Minimum Terms", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Minimum cover: £3 million for LLPs and companies", body:"**£3 million for each and every claim** for firms that are **LLPs** (limited liability partnerships) **or companies** (with limited liability)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Minimum cover: £2 million for all other firms", body:"**£2 million** for all other firms (**sole practitioners and partnerships**). **Defence costs** (the cost of defending the claim) are **paid on top**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Top-up cover", body:"The minimum may not be enough. A firm should consider buying cover **above the minimum** where its work could give rise to bigger claims (e.g. high-value commercial property or corporate deals)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: Lee & Co LLP", body:"Lee & Co LLP negligently fails to register a client's charge (record a mortgage or other security on the official register, e.g. at HM Land Registry, so that it is protected), causing a loss of **£3.5 million**. The firm has the minimum cover."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Lee & Co: who pays the £3.5 million?", body:"**The insurer must pay up to £3 million** (the LLP minimum) **plus defence costs**. The client must look to the **LLP itself for the other £500,000**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Claims-made basis", body:"The policy in force **when the claim is first made** against the firm pays, **not** the policy in force when the mistake happened."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Who is covered", body:"The **firm**, its **principals** (partners, members, directors) and its **employees**, for **civil liability** arising from **private legal practice**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Excess (deductible)", body:"The firm can agree an excess with the insurer, but the excess **cannot be deducted from the client's payment**. The insurer **pays the claimant in full** and **recovers the excess from the firm**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: a £20,000 excess", body:"If Lee & Co had an excess of **£20,000**, the insurer still pays the client the covered amount **in full** and **recovers the £20,000 from the firm**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No avoidance", body:"The insurer **cannot avoid, repudiate** (refuse to honour) **or cancel** the policy, or **reduce the payment** to the claimant, because of the firm's **non-disclosure** or **misrepresentation** when buying cover, or any **breach of policy terms**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The insurer can sue the firm afterwards", body:"It can **sue the firm afterwards** to get its money back."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Watch out: the firm lied on its proposal form", body:"An insurer that discovers the firm lied on its **proposal form** (the application for cover) **still has to pay the client**. It can only **recover the money from the firm afterwards**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Failure to renew: 30 days, then 60 days", body:"If a firm has no new policy at renewal, the old policy continues for an **extended policy period** of **30 days**, followed by a **cessation period** of a further **60 days** (**90 days in total**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The cessation period", body:"During the cessation period the firm **must not take on new work**; if it has **no cover by the end, it must close**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Run-off cover: 6 years", body:"If a firm closes **without a successor practice** (another firm taking over its business), the policy provides **6 years' run-off cover** for claims made after closure about work done before."}
 ]},
 {h:"SRA Compensation Fund", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Discretionary", body:"**No client has a legal right to a payment.** The SRA decides whether to pay and how much."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Last resort", body:"It pays **only if the applicant cannot get redress elsewhere** (e.g. from PII, a bank, or the solicitor personally)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Paid for by the profession", body:"Paid for by annual **contributions** from SRA-regulated firms and solicitors."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What it covers: dishonesty", body:"Loss caused by the **dishonesty** of a solicitor, a firm or their employees (e.g. **theft of client money**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What it covers: failure to account", body:"**Failure to account** for money received by the firm (e.g. the firm becomes **insolvent** (cannot pay its debts) or **vanishes** and the client money is missing)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Grants where there was no PII", body:"It can also make **grants** where a firm **failed to have the PII it was required to have**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Who can apply", body:"**Individuals**, and **smaller businesses, charities and trusts** (broadly, those with annual turnover, income or assets **below £2 million**). **Large businesses are expected to protect themselves.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Limits: £2 million, 12 months", body:"The maximum grant is normally **£2 million per claim**, and the claim should normally be made within **12 months** of the applicant becoming aware of the loss."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Not for unregulated businesses", body:"It is **not available** to clients of **unregulated** businesses, **even if a solicitor works there**."},
   {t:"compare", i:"", title:"PII and the Compensation Fund compared", cols:["", "PII", "Compensation Fund"], rows:[["**Covers**","**Negligence** (civil liability for mistakes)","**Dishonesty** or **failure to account** for money"],["**Right to payment?**","Yes: compulsory insurance, insurer must pay","**No**: discretionary"],["**Order**","First port of call","**Last resort**"],["**Limit**","£2 million / £3 million per claim minimum","Normally £2 million per claim"],["**Paid for by**","Each firm's own premiums","Contributions from the whole profession"]]},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: missed a limitation date, or stole the completion money?", body:"Match the loss to the protection. A solicitor who **missed a limitation date** (the deadline for starting a claim) has been negligent: the answer is **PII**. A solicitor who **stole the completion money** (the purchase money paid when a property sale completes) has been dishonest: the answer is the **Compensation Fund**, as a **last resort**, if PII does not respond."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Watch out: do not confuse them", body:"Do not confuse the **SRA Compensation Fund** (dishonesty or failure to account, last resort, discretionary) with **PII** (negligence, compulsory, insurer must pay)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Alternative business structures (ABSs)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Part 5 of the LSA 2007", body:"ABSs were introduced by **Part 5 of the LSA 2007**. An ABS (formally a **licensed body**) is a firm providing reserved legal activities in which **non-lawyers** have an **ownership or management** role."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Who the non-lawyers can be", body:"E.g. **accountants**, **insurers**, **private equity investors**, or a **company listed on a stock exchange**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Licensed by a licensing authority", body:"Licensed by a **licensing authority**: the **SRA**, the **CLC**, or certain other approved regulators."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Same rules as any SRA firm", body:"An ABS licensed by the SRA must follow the **same Codes, Accounts Rules and PII requirements** as any other SRA firm."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"COLP and COFA", body:"Must have a **COLP** (compliance officer for legal practice: responsible for compliance with the rules generally) and a **COFA** (compliance officer for finance and administration: responsible for the Accounts Rules), both **approved** by the regulator. **All SRA firms need these, not only ABSs.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Ownership control: material interest", body:"Anyone who holds a **material interest** (generally **10% or more** of the shares or voting rights, or significant influence) must be **approved** by the licensing authority as **fit and proper**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Higher fines", body:"The SRA can fine an ABS up to **£250 million** and its managers and employees up to **£50 million**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: the accountancy practice", body:"An accountancy practice wants to offer conveyancing (the legal work of transferring property) to its clients, with two non-lawyer accountants as partners and owners."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"What must the accountancy practice do?", body:"**It must be licensed as an ABS** (by the SRA or CLC), appoint an **approved COLP and COFA**, and have the accountants **approved as owners of a material interest**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Other providers of legal services", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Barristers: Bar Standards Board (BSB)", body:"**Rights of audience in all courts**; advice and drafting. **Public access** barristers can take instructions directly from the public without a solicitor. Some barristers are authorised to **conduct litigation**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Chartered legal executives: CILEx Regulation (CRL)", body:"CILEX members are **lawyers in their own right**. They can gain **practice rights** in particular areas (e.g. litigation, advocacy, conveyancing, probate) and run their **own CILEX-regulated firms**, or become **partners in SRA firms**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Licensed conveyancers: Council for Licensed Conveyancers (CLC)", body:"Specialist property lawyers: **reserved instrument activities** (conveyancing) and **probate**. The **CLC also licenses ABSs**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Costs lawyers: Costs Lawyer Standards Board", body:"**Costs work**, including some litigation and advocacy rights in costs matters."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Patent and trade mark attorneys: IPReg", body:"Regulated by the **Intellectual Property Regulation Board (IPReg)**: **IP rights** (intellectual property, such as patents and trade marks) and related litigation."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Notaries: the Faculty Office", body:"Regulated by the **Faculty Office of the Archbishop of Canterbury**: **notarial activities**, e.g. authenticating documents for use abroad."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Unregulated providers", body:"E.g. **will writers**, unregulated **employment and family advisers**, and **online legal businesses**. They can do any **non-reserved** work."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What their clients do not get", body:"**No compulsory PII**, **no Compensation Fund**, and usually **no access to the Legal Ombudsman** (LeO deals with complaints about **authorised persons**, i.e. lawyers authorised by an approved regulator, not unregulated businesses)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Immigration advisers and claims management companies", body:"**Immigration advisers** are separately regulated by the **Immigration Advice Authority**, and **claims management companies** by the **Financial Conduct Authority (FCA)** (since **April 2019**; complaints about them go to the **Financial Ombudsman Service**, not LeO). They are **regulated, but not as lawyers**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: a solicitor in an unregulated firm gives negligent advice. What protection does the client have?", body:"**No compulsory PII and no Compensation Fund**, but the solicitor is **still personally bound by the SRA Principles and Code** (and must have told the client about this **at the outset**)."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Library room 05 (The Quiet Study Room). Source: src/content/ls.js topic poca-offences-reporting (notes only). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["ls/poca-offences-reporting"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Part 7 POCA: two groups of offences", body:"**Part 7 of the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002 (POCA)** contains the money laundering offences. They fall into two groups: direct involvement and non-direct involvement."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Direct involvement: the principal offences", body:"The **principal offences** (**ss.327-329**): doing something with criminal property yourself, or helping someone else to do so. These apply to **everyone**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Non-direct involvement", body:"**ss.330-333A and s.342**: **failing to report** suspicions, **tipping off** the suspect, and **prejudicing an investigation**. Most of these apply only to people in the **regulated sector** (businesses covered by the Money Laundering Regulations, such as solicitors doing transactional work)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The main defence: report, then wait for consent", body:"Make an **authorised disclosure** (report to the firm's **MLRO**, the money laundering reporting officer, who reports to the **National Crime Agency (NCA)**) and wait for **consent** before going ahead. Client checks under the Money Laundering Regulations are in the previous topic."}
 ]},
 {h:"Key definitions (s.340)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Criminal conduct", body:"Conduct that is an offence **anywhere in the UK**, or conduct **abroad** that would be an offence if it happened here."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Any crime, no minimum value", body:"**Any** crime counts, however small, including **tax evasion**, benefit fraud and breaches of regulations that are criminal. There is **no minimum value**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Criminal property", body:"Property that **constitutes or represents** a person's **benefit** from criminal conduct (in whole or part, directly or indirectly), where the alleged offender **knows or suspects** that it does."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Property, and saved costs", body:"Property includes money, land, goods and intangibles (things you cannot touch, such as debts or shares); **saved costs** (e.g. **tax not paid**) can be a benefit."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A client is buying a flat with money \"saved from cash jobs I never told the taxman about\". Is it criminal property?", body:"**Yes.** That money represents the benefit of **tax evasion**, so it is criminal property, even though the work itself was lawful."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Suspicion: a low threshold", body:"**Suspicion** is a **low threshold**: thinking there is a **possibility, which is more than fanciful**, that the relevant facts exist."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"R v Da Silva (2006)", body:"Mrs Da Silva's husband, a coffee bar manager, claimed wages for non-existent \"ghost\" workers, which were paid into bank accounts she ran. She was convicted of helping him retain the proceeds of his crime because she **suspected** it. The Court of Appeal held that \"suspecting\" means thinking there is a **possibility, which is more than fanciful**, that the relevant facts exist."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Not vague unease, and not firmly grounded", body:"A **vague feeling of unease** is **not enough**, but the suspicion need **not** be \"clear\" or \"firmly grounded\"."}
 ]},
 {h:"Situations in practice where you should be suspicious (red flags)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Secretive or evasive", body:"The client is **secretive or evasive** about identity, the source of funds or the purpose of a transaction, or will not meet in person without good reason."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Large cash, unknown third parties", body:"**Large cash** payments, or funds from an **unknown third party** or from a **high-risk country**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Unusual instructions", body:"Paying money into the client account with **no underlying transaction**, or **cancelling** a transaction and asking for the money to go to a **third party** or **abroad**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No economic sense", body:"A transaction that makes **no economic sense**, e.g. buying property well above market value, or a quick resale at a big profit or loss."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Complex structures", body:"**Complex structures** (overseas companies, trusts) with no obvious legitimate reason."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"High fees, frequent changes", body:"The client is happy to pay **unusually high fees**, or **changes instructions or lawyers frequently**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Sudden wealth", body:"**Sudden changes in a client's wealth** that do not fit their known circumstances."}
 ]},
 {h:"The principal offences (direct involvement) (ss.327-329)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Anyone can commit them: up to 14 years", body:"These apply to **anyone** (not just the regulated sector). Maximum sentence: **14 years** and/or an **unlimited fine**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"s.327 Concealing", body:"**Concealing, disguising, converting or transferring** criminal property, or **removing it** from England and Wales, Scotland or Northern Ireland."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"s.327: the solicitor's risk", body:"Transferring dirty money **through the client account**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"s.328 Arrangements", body:"Entering into or becoming concerned in an **arrangement** which you know or suspect **facilitates** (makes easier) the acquisition, retention, use or control of criminal property **by or for another person**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"s.328: the main risk for transactional lawyers", body:"Acting on a **purchase funded with criminal money**, or **setting up a trust or company** to hold it."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A buyer tells his conveyancer that £50,000 of the purchase money comes from his brother's drug dealing. What if she proceeds without consent?", body:"**She commits s.328**: she is concerned in an arrangement facilitating the brother's retention or use of criminal property."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"s.329 Acquisition, use and possession", body:"**Acquiring, using or having possession** of criminal property. The typical solicitor risk: **receiving criminal money for fees**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"s.329 defence: adequate consideration", body:"**Adequate consideration** was given (e.g. **normal fees for genuine legal work**), but **not** if you know or suspect the services help the criminal."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Bowman v Fels (2005): the facts", body:"In a dispute between former partners over a share of a house, the claimant's solicitors suspected the defendant had paid for work on the house through his business to avoid VAT (value added tax). They made a report and asked the court to adjourn (postpone the case)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Genuine litigation and settlement are not s.328", body:"The Court of Appeal held that s.328 was **not intended to cover the ordinary conduct of legal proceedings**, or a consensual **settlement** of them. So litigators do not commit s.328 by taking steps in **genuine litigation**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Sham litigation is caught", body:"**Sham litigation**, created to launder money, is **still caught**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Defences to the principal offences", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Authorised disclosure (s.338)", body:"You disclose your suspicion to your **nominated officer (MLRO)** or to the NCA **before** doing the prohibited act."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Appropriate consent (s.335): the DAML", body:"After the disclosure you have **consent** to go ahead. The NCA's consent is called a **DAML** (\"defence against money laundering\")."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Disclosure after the act", body:"A disclosure made **after** the act can be a defence if there was **good reason** for not disclosing earlier and you disclosed **as soon as practicable** on your **own initiative**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Reasonable excuse", body:"A **reasonable excuse** for not making a disclosure (interpreted **narrowly**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Overseas conduct", body:"The conduct was **lawful where it happened** and is not an offence that would carry more than **12 months'** imprisonment in the UK."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Adequate consideration (s.329 only)", body:"**Adequate consideration** is a defence to **s.329 only**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Consent timings", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Notice period: 7 working days", body:"**7 working days**, starting the **first working day after the disclosure**. If the NCA does not refuse consent within it, consent is **deemed** given and you can proceed."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Moratorium period: 31 days", body:"**31 days** from the day the NCA **refuses** consent. You **must not** proceed. If nothing more happens by the end, you may go ahead."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Extensions: up to 186 days", body:"The court can extend the moratorium by up to **31 days at a time**, to a maximum of **186 days** in total (beyond the initial 31). This gives law enforcement more time to investigate."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"The NCA refuses consent. How long must the firm wait before it may go ahead?", body:"**31 days** (the moratorium), if nothing more happens. The court can extend it, up to 186 days in total beyond the initial 31."}
 ]},
 {h:"Failure to disclose (non-direct involvement): s.330 in the regulated sector (maximum 5 years)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"s.330: all four must apply", body:"**Failure to disclose in the regulated sector**, maximum **5 years**. You commit the offence if **all** of the next four apply."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"1. Know, suspect, or reasonable grounds", body:"You **know or suspect**, or have **reasonable grounds** for knowing or suspecting, that another person is engaged in money laundering. This includes an **objective** test: you can be guilty if a reasonable person in your position would have suspected, even if you did not (**negligence is enough**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"2. In the course of business in the regulated sector", body:"The information came to you **in the course of business in the regulated sector**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"3. Can identify, or it will assist", body:"You can **identify** the launderer or the **whereabouts** of the property, or believe (or it is reasonable to expect) the information will **assist** in identifying them."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"4. Not reported as soon as practicable", body:"You do not make the required disclosure to your **MLRO** or the NCA **as soon as practicable**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Defence: reasonable excuse", body:"A **reasonable excuse** for not disclosing."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Defence: privileged circumstances", body:"A **professional legal adviser** (or relevant professional adviser) is not required to disclose information that came to them in **privileged circumstances**, i.e. from a client in connection with giving **legal advice**, or from anyone in connection with **legal proceedings** (actual or contemplated)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Not if it furthers a criminal purpose", body:"The privileged circumstances defence does **not** apply if the information was given with the intention of **furthering a criminal purpose**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Defence: lack of training", body:"An **employee** who did not actually know or suspect, and whose employer had **not provided the required AML (anti-money laundering) training**, has a defence (to the objective limb)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Asking the MLRO for advice is fine", body:"A professional legal adviser who passes information to the MLRO **for the purpose of obtaining advice** on whether to disclose is **not** making an unauthorised disclosure."}
 ]},
 {h:"Other failure to disclose offences", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"s.331: the MLRO in the regulated sector", body:"A **nominated officer (MLRO)** in the regulated sector who receives an internal report and **fails to pass it to the NCA** as soon as practicable, when they know or suspect (or have **reasonable grounds** to know or suspect) money laundering."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"s.332: a nominated officer outside the regulated sector", body:"A nominated officer **outside** the regulated sector who fails to pass on a report (**subjective test only**: actual knowledge or suspicion)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Tipping off and prejudicing an investigation", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"s.333A Tipping off", body:"**Regulated sector** only. Disclosing that a **disclosure (SAR, suspicious activity report) has been made**, or that an **investigation** is being contemplated or carried out, where the disclosure is **likely to prejudice** (harm) any investigation. Maximum **2 years**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Tipping off: main defences", body:"Disclosure within the **same firm or group**, or between professionals in the same type of business about a shared client or transaction, to prevent an offence; disclosure to the **supervisor**; a legal adviser disclosing to a client to **dissuade** them from committing an offence (**s.333D**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"s.342 Prejudicing an investigation", body:"**Anyone**. Making a disclosure likely to prejudice a money laundering (or confiscation) investigation, or **falsifying, concealing or destroying** relevant documents. Maximum **5 years**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"s.342: main defences", body:"Did **not know or suspect** the disclosure would prejudice the investigation; a professional legal adviser disclosing to a client in connection with **legal advice** or proceedings (**not to further a crime**)."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Watch out: is telling a client \"there will be a short delay\" tipping off?", body:"**No.** Tipping off means revealing that a **SAR** has been made or an **investigation** is under way. Simply telling a client that you **cannot act**, or that there is a **delay**, without explaining why, is not tipping off. But you must **not lie** to the client either."}
 ]},
 {h:"How to report: the correct procedure", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"1. Stop", body:"Do not take any further step in the transaction that could be a principal offence."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"2. Report internally, promptly", body:"The fee earner tells the firm's **MLRO** about the suspicion. This internal report satisfies the fee earner's **s.330** duty and is an **authorised disclosure** for the principal offences."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"3. The MLRO decides", body:"Whether there is knowledge or suspicion; if so, the MLRO submits a **suspicious activity report (SAR)** to the **NCA**, asking for a **DAML** if the firm wants to proceed with the transaction."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"4. Wait", body:"Do not proceed until consent is **given or deemed** (end of the **7 working day** notice period, or end of the **31 day** moratorium if consent is refused and not extended)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"5. Do not tip off", body:"Delay the client **without saying why**. Do not tell them a SAR has been made."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"6. Record", body:"**Record** the reasons for decisions, and the MLRO keeps the report records."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Confidentiality: a SAR is permitted by law", body:"A SAR made in accordance with POCA is a disclosure **permitted by law**, so it does not breach the duty of confidentiality (SRA Code para **6.3**), and POCA protects the reporter from **civil claims** for breach of confidence."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Legal professional privilege (LPP)", body:"Privileged communications **do not have to be disclosed**. But privilege never protects communications made to **further a crime or fraud** (the **crime-fraud exception**), and the s.330 privileged circumstances defence is lost in the same situation."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Watch out: when do the privileged circumstances defence and LPP disappear?", body:"**Both** disappear if the client is using the lawyer to **further a crime**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"A client wants to pay a house deposit with £80,000 in cash from his restaurant. The solicitor suspects the business evades VAT. What should she do?", body:"**Tax evasion is criminal conduct; proceeding would be a s.328 arrangement.** Report to the **MLRO** straight away, take **no further steps** until consent is received or deemed, and tell the client only that there will be a **short delay**, without saying why."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: in a reporting question, who do you report to, and when may you proceed?", body:"Almost always \"report to the **MLRO**\" (not directly to the NCA, the police or the SRA) and \"**do not proceed until consent**\". Watch for options that let the solicitor \"proceed but report afterwards\" or \"tell the client a report has been made\": **both are wrong**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: how is s.330 wider, and how is it narrower, than the principal offences?", body:"**Wider**: it is committed by **negligence** (reasonable grounds to suspect). **Narrower**: it applies only to information received in the **regulated sector**, and the **privileged circumstances** defence protects genuine legal advice."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Library room 01 (The Reserves Room). Source: src/content/ls.js, topic sra-regulatory-role (notes only). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["ls/sra-regulatory-role"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The Legal Services Act 2007", body:"Legal services in England and Wales are regulated under the **Legal Services Act 2007 (LSA)**. The Act does three things you need to know."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"1. A structure of regulators", body:"It sets up a **structure of regulators**: an oversight body at the top, professional regulators underneath, and a separate complaints ombudsman."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"2. Objectives and principles", body:"It sets out **regulatory objectives** and **professional principles** that every regulator and lawyer must work towards."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"3. Six reserved legal activities", body:"It lists six **reserved legal activities** that only authorised (or exempt) people may carry out. **Everything else** (for example writing wills or giving general legal advice) **can be done by anyone**, regulated or not."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"How the SRA regulates", body:"This topic also covers how the **Solicitors Regulation Authority (SRA)** regulates: through high-level **Principles**, **Codes of Conduct**, a **risk-based** approach and its enforcement powers. Professional indemnity insurance, the Compensation Fund, ABSs and other providers are in the next topic."}
 ]},
 {h:"Who regulates whom", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Legal Services Board (LSB)", body:"The **oversight regulator**. It supervises the **approved regulators** (the bodies approved under the LSA to regulate each kind of lawyer); it does **not** regulate individual lawyers."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Law Society", body:"The **approved regulator** for solicitors (also the solicitors' **representative body**). It has **delegated** all its regulatory functions to the SRA."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Solicitors Regulation Authority (SRA)", body:"The **independent regulator** of solicitors and SRA-authorised firms. It **sets the rules, authorises, supervises and disciplines**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Other approved regulators", body:"The **Bar Council** (regulating through the **Bar Standards Board**), **CILEX** (the Chartered Institute of Legal Executives, through **CILEx Regulation**), the **Council for Licensed Conveyancers (CLC)**, and regulators for costs lawyers, patent and trade mark attorneys and notaries."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal (SDT)", body:"An **independent tribunal** hearing the **most serious cases**, usually brought by the SRA. **Only the SDT can strike off** (remove a solicitor from the roll of solicitors) **or suspend** a solicitor."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Legal Ombudsman (LeO)", body:"Deals with consumers' complaints about **poor service** (delay, poor communication, mistakes). Set up by the LSA, overseen by the **Office for Legal Complaints**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Service vs conduct", body:"A complaint about **poor service** (e.g. the solicitor never returned calls) goes to the **firm first and then to LeO**. A **breach of the rules** (e.g. dishonesty, conflict of interest, misuse of client money) is a **conduct** matter for the **SRA**. **One set of facts can raise both.**"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"LeO time limits: firm first, then 8 weeks", body:"The client must **first use the firm's own complaints procedure**. LeO normally accepts a complaint once **8 weeks** have passed without resolution, or the firm has given its **final response**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"LeO time limits: 6 months and 1 year", body:"The client must go to LeO within **6 months** of the firm's final response, and within **1 year** of the problem (or of when they should reasonably have known about it)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"What LeO can order", body:"LeO can order an **apology**, a **fee reduction** or **compensation** (up to **£50,000**)."}
 ]},
 {h:"The regulatory objectives and professional principles (LSA s.1)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Regulatory objectives: goals for the regulators", body:"The **regulatory objectives** are the goals the **LSB and every approved regulator** must pursue. There are nine."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Objectives 1 to 3", body:"Protecting and promoting the **public interest**. Supporting the constitutional principle of the **rule of law**. Improving **access to justice**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Objectives 4 to 6", body:"Protecting and promoting the **interests of consumers**. Promoting **competition** in the provision of legal services. Encouraging an **independent, strong, diverse and effective** legal profession."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Objectives 7 and 8", body:"Increasing **public understanding** of citizens' legal rights and duties. Promoting and maintaining adherence to the **professional principles**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Objective 9: economic offences", body:"Promoting the **prevention and detection of economic offences**, added by the **Economic Crime and Corporate Transparency Act 2023 (ECCTA)**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Professional principles: duties on lawyers (s.1(3))", body:"The **professional principles** (s.1(3)) bind every **authorised person** (a person authorised to carry out a reserved activity). There are five."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Principles 1 to 3", body:"Act with **independence and integrity**. Maintain **proper standards of work**. Act in the **best interests of clients**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Principles 4 and 5", body:"Comply with the **duty to the court** to act with independence in the interests of justice (when exercising rights of audience or conducting litigation). Keep clients' affairs **confidential**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: \"promoting competition\" and \"confidentiality\": which list is each on?", body:"Do not mix up the lists. \"Promoting competition\" and \"access to justice\" are **regulatory objectives** (aims for the regulators); \"confidentiality\" and \"duty to the court\" are **professional principles** (duties on lawyers). The SRA's own **seven Principles** are a **third, separate list**."}
 ]},
 {h:"How the SRA regulates: the rulebook (SRA Standards and Regulations)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"In force since 25 November 2019", body:"The **SRA Standards and Regulations** have been in force since **25 November 2019**. The parts you need: the SRA Principles, two Codes of Conduct and the other rules."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Code of Conduct for Solicitors, RELs, RFLs and RSLs", body:"Standards for **individuals**. RELs, RFLs and RSLs are **registered European lawyers, registered foreign lawyers and registered Swiss lawyers**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Code of Conduct for Firms", body:"Standards for **authorised firms** and their **managers** and **compliance officers** (the **COLP**, compliance officer for legal practice, and the **COFA**, compliance officer for finance and administration)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The other rules", body:"**Accounts Rules** (client money), **Authorisation** rules (firms and individuals), **Indemnity Insurance Rules**, **Compensation Fund Rules**, **Transparency Rules**, **Financial Services** rules, and the **Statement of Solicitor Competence** (what a solicitor must be able to do)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The SRA Principles: seven fundamental duties", body:"You act: (1) in a way that upholds the constitutional principle of the **rule of law** and the proper administration of justice; (2) in a way that upholds **public trust and confidence** in the profession; (3) with **independence**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Principles 4 to 7", body:"(4) with **honesty**; (5) with **integrity**; (6) in a way that encourages **equality, diversity and inclusion**; (7) in the **best interests of each client**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"When Principles conflict", body:"Where Principles conflict, those protecting the **public interest** (including the rule of law and public confidence) **take precedence over the client's interests**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Principles-based and outcomes-focused", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"High-level Principles, not a long list of rules", body:"Rather than a long list of **detailed prescriptive rules**, the SRA sets **high-level Principles** and **short Codes** describing the standards expected."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Professional judgement", body:"It leaves solicitors to use **professional judgement** in applying them. **Guidance and case studies** explain what the standards mean in practice."}
 ]},
 {h:"Risk-based", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Resources at the greatest risks", body:"The SRA targets its resources at the greatest **risks to the public and consumers**, rather than inspecting every firm in the same way."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The Risk Outlook", body:"It identifies and publishes current risks in its **Risk Outlook**: for example **money laundering**, protecting **client money**, **cybercrime and conveyancing fraud**, and the needs of **vulnerable clients**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"SLAPPs", body:"The Risk Outlook also names firms bringing **SLAPPs** (strategic lawsuits against public participation: **abusive legal threats or claims designed to silence critics or journalists**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Closer supervision where risk is greater", body:"Firms and individuals are supervised **more closely where the risk is greater**: e.g. **thematic reviews** (a look at one risk across many firms), **inspections**, and **requirements to report**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Enforcement powers", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"SRA: finding and warning, rebuke", body:"The SRA can make a **finding and warning** (a finding that the rules were broken, with a warning about future conduct) or a **rebuke** (a formal reprimand)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"SRA fines: £25,000", body:"The SRA can fine up to **£25,000** for solicitors and **traditional (non-ABS) firms**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"SRA fines: economic crime unlimited", body:"An **unlimited fine** for conduct relating to **economic crime** (e.g. money laundering, sanctions breaches), since **2024** under **ECCTA**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"SRA fines: ABSs", body:"Fines up to **£250 million** for an **ABS** (an alternative business structure: a licensed firm with non-lawyer owners or managers) and **£50 million** for its managers and employees."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"SRA: conditions and s.43 orders", body:"**Conditions** on a **practising certificate** (a solicitor's annual licence to practise) or a firm's authorisation; controls on employing non-solicitors (**s.43 orders**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"SRA: intervention", body:"**Intervention** into a firm: **closing it and taking control of client money and files**, e.g. for suspected dishonesty."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"SDT sanctions", body:"The SDT can **strike off** the roll, **suspend**, impose an **unlimited fine in any case**, and impose **restrictions**. It hears cases the SRA brings."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: the missing costs update", body:"A solicitor at a traditional firm forgets to send a costs update and the client is annoyed. The client's remedy is a **complaint to the firm and then LeO** (service)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: money from the client account", body:"If instead the solicitor **took money from the client account**, that is a **conduct** matter: the SRA investigates and, because dishonesty usually leads to striking off, **refers it to the SDT**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: can the SRA strike off a solicitor?", body:"**No.** A common distractor gives the SRA the power to strike off. **Only the SDT (or the High Court)** can strike off or suspend a solicitor. The SRA's own fines are capped at **£25,000** for traditional firms and individuals, except in **economic crime** cases."}
 ]},
 {h:"Reserved legal activities (LSA s.12 and Schedule 2)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Who may carry on a reserved legal activity", body:"Only an **authorised person** (authorised by an approved regulator for that activity, e.g. a **solicitor with a practising certificate**) or an **exempt person**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Exempt persons", body:"For example a **litigant in person** acting for themselves (someone running their own case without a lawyer), or someone the **court gives permission** to address it."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"There are six", body:"Exercise of a right of audience; conduct of litigation; reserved instrument activities; probate activities; notarial activities; administration of oaths."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"1. Exercise of a right of audience", body:"**Appearing as an advocate before a court.** Solicitors have rights in **all lower courts**; they need a **higher rights qualification** for the higher courts."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"2. Conduct of litigation", body:"**Issuing proceedings**, commencing, prosecuting and defending them, and **ancillary formal steps** (e.g. **serving documents**). See **Mazur** below."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"3. Reserved instrument activities", body:"Preparing instruments **transferring or creating interests in land** (transfers, leases, mortgages) and **lodging documents at HM Land Registry** (the government body that registers land)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Reserved instruments: the fee exemption", body:"Reserved **whether or not paid**, but an **individual acting without any fee, gain or reward is exempt**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"4. Probate activities", body:"Preparing the papers on which a **grant of probate or letters of administration** (the court document that lets someone deal with a dead person's estate) is applied for. Reserved **only if done for a fee**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"5. Notarial activities", body:"The work of a **notary** (a lawyer who authenticates documents), e.g. **certifying documents for use abroad**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"6. Administration of oaths", body:"Taking sworn statements: **affidavits** (written statements sworn on oath) and **statutory declarations** (written statements solemnly declared to be true)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Not reserved", body:"**Will writing**, general legal **advice**, drafting most **contracts**, **employment** tribunal representation, most **immigration** advice (regulated separately by the **Immigration Advice Authority**), **tax** advice, **mediation** and **estate administration after the grant**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Offences (ss.14, 16, 17)", body:"Carrying on a reserved activity when not entitled is a **criminal offence** (**s.14**, up to **2 years'** imprisonment), and so is **pretending** to be entitled (**s.17**). An **employer** whose employee does so also commits an offence (**s.16**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Watch out: will writing is not reserved", body:"An unregulated will-writing company can lawfully **draft a will for a fee**, but it **cannot, for a fee, prepare the papers for a grant of probate**, which is a reserved **probate activity**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: the online business", body:"An unregulated online business offers (a) drafting wills, (b) advice on employment disputes, (c) preparing a **TR1** transfer (the HM Land Registry form to transfer a registered property) of a client's house for a fee, and (d) taking a client's statutory declaration."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Which of the online business's four services can it not lawfully offer?", body:"**(c) and (d).** Preparing a land transfer for a fee is a **reserved instrument activity**, and taking a statutory declaration is **administration of oaths**. (a) and (b) are **not reserved**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: a friend prepares a transfer for free. Reserved activity?", body:"**No.** Questions usually list four or five services offered by a non-lawyer business and ask which one breaches the LSA: run through the six reserved activities. Remember the **\"for a fee\"** limit on reserved instrument and probate activities: a friend preparing a transfer for free is not carrying on a reserved activity."}
 ]},
 {h:"Mazur: who may do litigation tasks?", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The complaint", body:"In **Mazur v Charles Russell Speechlys LLP**, clients complained that a fee claim against them had been conducted by a member of a firm's staff who was **not an authorised person**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"High Court, September 2025", body:"In **September 2025** the High Court (Sheldon J, [2025] EWHC 2341 (KB)) held that an unauthorised employee **could not conduct litigation** even under a solicitor's supervision; they could only **support** an authorised person. This caused great concern, especially for **CILEX members** (legal executives and other members of the Chartered Institute of Legal Executives) and **paralegals**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Court of Appeal reversed, 31 March 2026", body:"The **Court of Appeal reversed** this on **31 March 2026**, on an appeal brought by CILEX (**Mazur v Chartered Institute of Legal Executives [2026] EWCA Civ 369**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"For and on behalf of an authorised individual", body:"An unauthorised person may lawfully carry out tasks that amount to the conduct of litigation **for and on behalf of** an authorised individual, provided that individual **retains responsibility** for the litigation and has appropriate **supervision, management and control** arrangements in place."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Not every step", body:"The authorised person **need not approve every step personally**; the level of supervision depends on the circumstances."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Rule to take away", body:"The right to conduct litigation belongs to the **authorised person**, who can **delegate tasks** to properly supervised staff, but **cannot hand over responsibility** for the litigation to someone unauthorised."}
 ]},
 {h:"Who can provide legal services as a solicitor", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"SRA-authorised firms: three kinds", body:"A **recognised sole practice** (a sole practitioner's firm), a **recognised body** (owned and managed **only by lawyers**) or a **licensed body (ABS)**, with **non-lawyer** owners or managers."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"SRA-authorised firms: what clients get", body:"Solicitors there can do **reserved and non-reserved** work, and clients get compulsory **PII** (professional indemnity insurance) and **Compensation Fund** protection."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Freelance solicitors", body:"Self-employed solicitors practising in their own name. They may do **reserved** work without being an authorised firm **only if** they: **employ no one**, hold **no client money other than money for fees or disbursements on account** (expenses paid out for the client, e.g. court fees), and have **adequate and appropriate insurance**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Solicitors in unregulated businesses", body:"E.g. an unregulated will-writing or employment advice company. They may provide **non-reserved** services **only**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Still bound, and must tell clients", body:"They remain **personally bound** by the SRA Principles and Code, but must make sure clients understand that they do **not** get the protection of compulsory **PII** or the **Compensation Fund** (Code paras **8.10-8.11**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"In-house solicitors", body:"Employed by a non-law business or public body: they **act for their employer**, and may act for others **only in limited circumstances**."}
 ]}
]};

;
/* Rooms preview deck (?rooms=1 only): registers into window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN, which only the rooms engine in scene.js reads. Library room 09 (The Information Desk). Source: src/content/ls.js, topic transparency-client-care (notes only). */
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN = window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN || {};
window.SQE_ROOM_LEARN["ls/transparency-client-care"] = {sections:[
 {h:"The big idea in one paragraph", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Price, service and protection", body:"Clients cannot judge whether a legal service is good value unless they have clear information about **price**, **service** and **regulatory protection**. Three sets of rules make sure they get it."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"1. The SRA Transparency Rules", body:"What a firm must **publish** (usually on its website) **before** anyone becomes a client: prices for certain services, complaints procedures and regulatory status. (SRA: the Solicitors Regulation Authority.)"},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"2. SRA Code of Conduct, section 8", body:"\"Client identification, complaints handling and client information\": what each client must be **told** during the matter."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"3. Client care letter and consumer law", body:"The **client care letter** and terms of business put this information **in writing at the start**, plus consumer law (the **Consumer Contracts Regulations 2013**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Funding is elsewhere", body:"How the work is funded (CFAs, legal aid etc.) is covered in the two funding topics."}
 ]},
 {h:"SRA Transparency Rules: who and where", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Who the rules apply to", body:"**SRA-authorised firms**, and **freelance solicitors** (with some adaptations)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Not for legal aid work", body:"The **price** rules do not apply to **publicly funded** (legal aid) work."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Where: prominent on the website", body:"The information must be published on the firm's **website** in a **prominent** place. A firm **without a website** must make it **available on request**."}
 ]},
 {h:"Price (costs) information: only for listed services", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Only the listed services", body:"A firm that offers any of the **listed services** must publish costs information for them. Price information is required **only** for these services."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Individuals: residential conveyancing", body:"**Residential conveyancing**: purchase, sale, freehold or leasehold, and remortgage."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Individuals: uncontested probate", body:"**Uncontested probate** (all assets in the UK)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Individuals: motoring offences", body:"**Motoring offences**: **summary-only** road traffic offences (offences that can be tried only in the magistrates' court) dealt with in the magistrates' court at a **single hearing**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Individuals: bringing unfair or wrongful dismissal claims", body:"**Employment tribunals**: **bringing** claims for **unfair or wrongful dismissal**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Individuals: immigration (not asylum)", body:"**Immigration** applications and First-tier Tribunal immigration appeals (**excluding asylum**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Businesses: debt recovery up to £100,000", body:"**Debt recovery** up to **£100,000**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Businesses: defending unfair or wrongful dismissal claims", body:"**Employment tribunals**: **defending** claims for **unfair or wrongful dismissal**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Businesses: licensing applications", body:"**Licensing applications** for business premises."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Must include: total cost", body:"The **total cost** of the service or, where not practicable, the **average cost** or **range** of costs."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Must include: basis of charges", body:"The **basis** for the charges, including any **hourly rates** or **fixed fees**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Must include: experience and qualifications", body:"The **experience and qualifications** of anyone carrying out the work, and of their **supervisors**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Must include: disbursements and VAT", body:"A description of any likely **disbursements** and their cost (or average or range), and whether **VAT** is payable on fees and disbursements."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Must include: CFA or DBA fees", body:"Whether any fees may be payable **under a CFA or DBA** (where relevant, e.g. employment claims), and the circumstances in which the client may have to pay."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Must include: services included", body:"A description of the **services included** (and anything likely to be excluded) in the displayed price."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Must include: stages and timescales", body:"The **key stages** of the matter and the likely **timescales** for each stage."}
 ]},
 {h:"Information every firm must publish", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Complaints procedure", body:"The firm's **complaints procedure**, including how and when a complaint can be made to the **Legal Ombudsman (LeO)** and to the **SRA**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Regulatory information", body:"A statement that the firm is **authorised and regulated by the SRA**, with its **SRA number**, on its **website and letterhead**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"The SRA digital badge", body:"Every SRA-authorised firm, **including an ABS** (alternative business structure: a firm licensed to have non-lawyers among its owners or managers), must display the **SRA clickable logo** or \"**digital badge**\" prominently on its website; it links to the SRA's register entry and explains the protections clients have."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Watch out: listed services v every firm", body:"The duty to publish **price** information applies only to the **listed services**, but the duty to publish **complaints** and regulatory information applies to **every** firm."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: £950 plus VAT", body:"A firm's website advertises residential conveyancing at \"£950 plus VAT\" and lists the stages and typical timescales, but says nothing about **search fees or Land Registry fees**, or about who will do the work. **It breaches the Transparency Rules**: it must describe likely **disbursements** and the **experience and qualifications** of the staff and supervisors."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: a commercial-only firm", body:"If the firm only did **commercial property** work, it would **not** need to publish prices at all (commercial property is not a listed service), but it would **still** need to publish its **complaints procedure**."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: a question describes a firm's website. Which omission breaches the Transparency Rules?", body:"First check whether the service is **on the list** (e.g. commercial property, family law, personal injury and contested probate are **not**). Then check whether each required element is there, especially **disbursements**, **timescales** and **staff experience**, which candidates forget."}
 ]},
 {h:"Client information duties: SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, section 8", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"8.1 Who are you acting for?", body:"Identify **who you are acting for** in each matter."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"8.2 A complaints procedure", body:"Have (or take part in) a **complaints procedure** in which clients' complaints are handled."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"8.3 Tell clients about complaints at the start", body:"Tell clients **in writing at the time of engagement** about their right to **complain** to you about your services and charges, how and to whom to complain, and any right to complain to the **Legal Ombudsman** and **when** they can do so."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"8.4 Not resolved within 8 weeks", body:"If a complaint has not been resolved to the client's satisfaction **within 8 weeks**, tell the client **in writing** of any right to complain to LeO, the **time frame** and LeO's **full contact details**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"8.4 Procedure exhausted: ADR", body:"If your complaints procedure has been **exhausted**, also tell the client in writing that you **cannot settle** the complaint, the name and website of an approved **alternative dispute resolution (ADR)** body, and **whether you agree to use it**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"8.5 Promptly, fairly, free", body:"Deal with complaints **promptly, fairly and free of charge**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"8.6 Information clients can understand", body:"Give clients information in a way they can **understand**, and make sure they are in a position to make **informed decisions** about the services they need, how their matter will be handled and the options available."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"8.7 Best possible costs information", body:"Give the **best possible information** about how the matter will be **priced** and the **likely overall cost**, at the time of engagement and as the matter progresses, including any costs incurred."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"8.8 Publicity not misleading", body:"Make sure **publicity** is **accurate and not misleading**, including about **charges** and the circumstances in which **interest** is payable by or to clients."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"8.9 No unsolicited approaches", body:"Do not make **unsolicited approaches** to members of the public (e.g. **cold calling**, approaching people **in the street** or **at hospitals after accidents**) to advertise legal services, **except to current or former clients**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"8.10 Regulated or not?", body:"Make sure clients understand **whether and how the services are regulated**: which activities are carried out by you as an **authorised person**, which services are regulated by an **approved regulator**, and **not suggesting** that an unregulated business is regulated by the SRA."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"8.11 The protections", body:"Make sure clients understand the **regulatory protections** available to them (e.g. **professional indemnity insurance (PII)** and the SRA **Compensation Fund**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: the unregulated will-writing company", body:"A solicitor working in an **unregulated** will-writing company drafts a will for a client. **Under paras 8.10-8.11 she must make sure the client understands** that the company is **not regulated by the SRA** and that the client will **not** have the protection of compulsory PII or the Compensation Fund."}
 ]},
 {h:"Complaints and the Legal Ombudsman", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Firm first: up to 8 weeks", body:"The client must normally complain to the **firm first**. The firm has up to **8 weeks** to resolve it."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"When the client can go to LeO", body:"If the complaint is **not resolved within 8 weeks**, or once the firm has given its **final response**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Time limit: 6 months from the final response", body:"Complain to LeO within **6 months** of the firm's **final response** (provided the firm's response told the client about LeO and this time limit)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Time limit: and 1 year from the problem", body:"**And** within **1 year** of the act or omission complained of, or of when the client should reasonably have known there was cause for complaint."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"LeO: service; SRA: conduct", body:"LeO deals with **service**; the **SRA** deals with **conduct** (misconduct can be reported to the SRA **at any time**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"2026: proposals, not rules", body:"In 2026 the SRA consulted on more detailed rules for how firms handle first-tier complaints; as at September 2026 these are **proposals**, not rules."},
   {t:"reveal", i:"", title:"Exam tip: \"We only need to tell clients about LeO if they complain.\" Right?", body:"**Wrong.** The complaints information must be given **in writing at the start** of the matter (para 8.3) **and again** at the end of the firm's complaints procedure or after 8 weeks (para 8.4)."}
 ]},
 {h:"The client care letter (best practice)", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"No prescribed form", body:"There is **no prescribed form**, but a client care letter (with terms of business) is the usual way to satisfy section 8 at the outset."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Who", body:"The **name and status** of the person doing the work, and the person who **supervises** them."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Scope", body:"What the firm has been instructed to do (and not do) under the **retainer** (the contract between solicitor and client), and the client's **objectives**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Costs", body:"The **basis** of charging, an **estimate** or fixed fee, **disbursements**, **VAT**, payments on account, billing intervals, **interest** on unpaid bills, the **costs risk** (e.g. paying the other side's costs) and **funding options**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Complaints", body:"The firm's procedure and the right to complain to **LeO** (with time limits) (paras 8.3-8.4)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Regulation", body:"That the firm is **regulated by the SRA**, and the **protections** available (para 8.11)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Other terms", body:"**Anti-money laundering** identity checks, **data protection**, **file storage and destruction**, how either side can **end the retainer**, and **limitation of liability** (if any)."}
 ]},
 {h:"Consumer Contracts Regulations 2013", cards:[
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Off-premises or at a distance: 14 days to cancel", body:"If a contract for legal services is made with a **consumer** **off-premises** (e.g. at the client's home or hospital bedside) or **at a distance** (by phone, email or online, without meeting face to face), the firm must give the consumer specified **pre-contract information** and tell them of their right to **cancel within 14 days**."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Not told? Up to 12 months more", body:"If the firm does not give the cancellation information, the cancellation period is **extended** (by up to **12 months**)."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Starting within the 14 days", body:"If the client wants work to start **within the 14 days**, they must **expressly request** it; if they then cancel, they must **pay for work done** up to cancellation."},
   {t:"card", i:"", title:"Example: the lease extension by phone", body:"A solicitor takes instructions **over the phone** from a consumer client on a lease extension and starts work the next day. **She needs the client's express request to start within the cancellation period**, and must give written information about the 14-day right to cancel; otherwise the client may be able to cancel and **pay nothing** for work done."}
 ]}
]};

