window.SQE_FRAMEWORKS["contract/offer-acceptance"] = {
 "intro": "Use this for any question asking whether, when or on whose terms an agreement was made.",
 "steps": [
  {
   "h": "Find the offer",
   "ask": "Did someone make a definite statement showing they would be bound as soon as the other side accepted?",
   "body": "- Judged **objectively**: what would a reasonable person understand from the words and conduct?\n- **Gibson v Manchester CC**: council said it \"may be prepared to sell\" the house: not an offer\n- **Invitations to treat**, not offers: shop displays and websites, most adverts, auction requests for bids, invitations to tender, a price given in reply to a question\n- Exceptions: a clear advert promising payment for an act is a **unilateral offer** (**Carlill**: £100 smoke ball promise backed by a £1,000 deposit); an auction **without reserve** (promise to sell to the highest bidder); a promise to accept the highest **fixed** tender\n> If the first statement is only an invitation to treat, the reply (the bid, the tender, the customer at the till) is the offer",
   "yes": "identify offeror and offeree, then go to step 2",
   "no": "treat the reply as the offer and start again from it"
  },
  {
   "h": "Is the offer still open?",
   "ask": "Had the offer ended before the acceptance took effect?",
   "body": "- **Revocation**: allowed any time before acceptance, even if the offeror promised to keep it open, unless the offeree **paid** for that promise (an **option**)\n  - Effective only when **received** (**Byrne v Van Tienhoven**: posted revocation arrived after the telegram acceptance), including via a reliable **third party** (**Dickinson v Dodds**: heard the house was sold elsewhere)\n  - Unilateral offer: no revocation once the offeree has **started performing**; offer to the world revoked by **reasonable steps** such as the same publicity\n- **Rejection** or **counter-offer** ends it; a mere **request for information** does not\n- **Lapse** of the stated time, or a **reasonable time** (five months too long for a share offer)\n- **Death** of the offeree; death of the offeror if the offeree knows or the contract is personal; failure of a **condition**",
   "yes": "no contract on that offer (check whether the reply was itself a new offer)",
   "no": "go to step 3"
  },
  {
   "h": "Is the reply an acceptance?",
   "ask": "Did the offeree agree unconditionally to all the terms, knowing of the offer?",
   "body": "- **Mirror image rule**: any new or changed term is a **counter-offer** (**Hyde v Wrench**: £950 reply destroyed the £1,000 offer)\n- Asking a question (e.g. \"can I take delivery over two months?\") is only a **request for information**\n- Acceptance can be by **conduct** (acting on the terms), but **silence** is never acceptance (**Felthouse v Bindley**: uncle's \"I consider the horse mine\")\n- Must act **in response to** and **knowing of** the offer; identical cross-offers are not enough\n- **Prescribed method**: another method that is **no less effective** still counts unless the offer makes its method mandatory",
   "yes": "go to step 4",
   "no": "no contract yet; treat the reply as a counter-offer or an enquiry"
  },
  {
   "h": "Battle of the forms",
   "ask": "Did both businesses send their own standard terms?",
   "body": "- List every document in **date order**; each new form is a counter-offer\n- The **last shot** (last form sent before the other side performs) is accepted by conduct\n- **Butler v Ex-Cell-O**: the seller signed and returned the buyer's tear-off slip, so the **buyer's terms** applied and the seller's price variation clause was lost\n- If they never agreed whose terms applied but performed anyway, there may be a contract on the **essential terms** only",
   "yes": "the last counter-offer before performance usually governs; then go to step 5",
   "no": "go to step 5"
  },
  {
   "h": "When did acceptance count?",
   "ask": "Was the acceptance communicated, and when did it take effect?",
   "body": "- General rule: when and where **received** (**Entores**: telex acceptance from Amsterdam, contract made in London); out-of-hours messages usually when they would be read\n- Email, phone, fax: on receipt, **no postal rule**\n- **Postal rule**: a letter of acceptance counts when **properly posted** (correctly addressed and stamped), even if delayed or lost\n  - Only where post is a **reasonable** method, and not if the offer requires **actual receipt** (**Holwell v Hughes**: option to be exercised by \"notice in writing to\" the seller)\n- Unilateral contract: **performing the act** is acceptance; no notice needed",
   "yes": "go to step 6",
   "no": "no contract"
  },
  {
   "h": "Draw the timeline",
   "ask": "Which took effect first: the acceptance, or the event that ended the offer?",
   "body": "- Write the date each message was **sent** and **received**\n- Postal acceptance counts when **posted**; revocations, rejections, counter-offers, emails and calls count when **received**\n!! The postal rule never helps a **revocation**\n- Acceptance first: contract on the offer's terms (then check consideration, intention, certainty and capacity)",
   "yes": "contract formed at that moment",
   "no": "no contract"
  }
 ],
 "outro": "**Exam tip:** most questions give dated letters, emails and calls, or a shop, auction or tender scenario: always draw the sent and received timeline."
};
window.SQE_FRAMEWORKS["contract/consideration"] = {
 "intro": "Use this when a party wants to enforce a promise and you must ask what they gave for it, or when a creditor or promisor tries to go back on a promise.",
 "steps": [
  {
   "h": "Identify the promise",
   "ask": "Which promise does the claimant want to enforce, and what did they give in return?",
   "body": "- Consideration is the **price paid for the promise**: a promise, an act or a forbearance\n- It must **move from the promisee**: the person suing must have given it\n- It must be **sufficient** (some legal value) but need not be **adequate** (**Thomas v Thomas**: £1 a year rent was enough)\n- Vague promises and moral feelings are not enough; forbearing to sue on a **reasonable, good faith** claim is enough\n> Promises in a **deed** bind without consideration",
   "yes": "go to step 2",
   "no": "no contract on that promise; consider promissory estoppel at step 6"
  },
  {
   "h": "Past consideration?",
   "ask": "Was the act done before the promise was made?",
   "body": "- Past consideration is **not** good consideration (**Re McArdle**: £488 promised after the house work was already done)\n- Exception (**Lampleigh v Brathwait**: asked to seek a royal pardon, then promised £100; restated in Pao On v Lau Yiu Long): all three needed\n  - act done at the **promisor's request**\n  - both **understood** it would be **paid for**\n  - payment, if promised in advance, would have been **legally enforceable**\n> Where one business asks another to do work, payment is readily assumed",
   "yes": "promise unenforceable unless the exception applies",
   "no": "go to step 3"
  },
  {
   "h": "An existing duty?",
   "ask": "Was the promisee already bound to do what it did?",
   "body": "- **Public duty**: not consideration unless the promisee does **more** (**Collins v Godefroy**: subpoenaed witness; **Glasbrook**: police sent more officers than needed to a mine during a strike)\n- Duty owed to a **third party**: good consideration (**Shadwell v Shadwell**: nephew already contractually bound to marry)\n- Duty owed to the **promisor**: is the promise to **pay more** (step 4) or to **accept less** (step 5)?",
   "yes": "go to step 4 or 5",
   "no": "consideration present; the promise is binding"
  },
  {
   "h": "Promise to pay more",
   "ask": "Did the promisor get a practical benefit from promising extra for the same work?",
   "body": "- Traditional rule: no consideration (**Stilk v Myrick**: two sailors deserted; the rest were promised their wages), unless the promisee does **more** (**Hartley v Ponsonby**: so many deserted that sailing on was dangerous)\n- **Williams v Roffey**: main contractor promised the struggling carpenter £575 more per flat; it avoided a late-completion penalty: binding\n- Test: the promisor had reason to **doubt** completion; it **promised extra** to complete on time; it got a **practical benefit** (or avoided a disbenefit); **no economic duress or fraud**\n!! Threatening to stop work unless paid more points to **economic duress**: the promise can be set aside",
   "yes": "the promise to pay more is binding",
   "no": "no consideration; the promise is not binding"
  },
  {
   "h": "Promise to accept less",
   "ask": "Did the creditor agree to take part payment of a fixed, undisputed debt in full settlement?",
   "body": "- Rule in Pinnel's Case, confirmed by **Foakes v Beer** (judgment debt paid by agreed instalments; creditor still recovered the interest): part payment is **not** consideration; creditor can sue for the balance\n- **Practical benefit does not apply** (**Re Selectmove**: tax paid by instalments); the Supreme Court left this open in **Rock Advertising** (2018: deal to reschedule licence fee arrears), so Foakes v Beer stands\n- Balance **cannot** be claimed if, **at the creditor's request**, the debtor paid **early**, at a **different place**, or with **something different** (a cheque is not different)\n- Also binding: part payment by a **third party** in full settlement; a **composition** with all creditors; settlement of a **disputed** or unliquidated claim",
   "yes": "creditor can sue for the balance unless an exception or estoppel applies",
   "no": "settlement binding"
  },
  {
   "h": "Promissory estoppel",
   "ask": "Can the promisee use estoppel as a defence to the promisor going back on its word?",
   "body": "- **Existing legal relationship** (e.g. lease, loan, contract)\n- **Clear and unequivocal promise** not to enforce strict rights (**High Trees**: landlord halved the ground rent during the war)\n- **Reliance**: the promisee acted on it\n- **Inequitable** to go back: not if the promise was extracted by pressure (**D and C Builders v Rees**: \"£300 or nothing\" to builders near insolvency)\n- **Shield, not sword**: cannot found a claim (**Combe v Combe**: wife could not sue on her ex-husband's £100 a year promise)\n- Usually **suspends** rights: promisor can restore them for the **future** on **reasonable notice**; past amounts given up generally cannot be claimed",
   "yes": "promisor cannot enforce strict rights for the period covered",
   "no": "promisor can enforce its strict rights"
  }
 ],
 "outro": "**Exam tip:** contrast **pay more** (practical benefit can make it binding) with **accept less** (Foakes v Beer, exceptions or estoppel only)."
};
window.SQE_FRAMEWORKS["contract/intention-certainty-capacity"] = {
 "intro": "Use this once offer, acceptance and consideration are present, to check intention to create legal relations, certainty and the parties' capacity.",
 "steps": [
  {
   "h": "Set the presumption",
   "ask": "Is the agreement domestic or social, or commercial?",
   "body": "- **Domestic or social** (family, friends): presumed **not** intended to be binding (**Balfour v Balfour**: husband's £30 a month to his wife while living together)\n- **Commercial**: presumed intended to be binding; the party denying it carries a **heavy burden**\n> Family businesses and friends going into business are often commercial",
   "yes": "domestic: go to step 2",
   "no": "commercial: go to step 3"
  },
  {
   "h": "Rebut: domestic",
   "ask": "Do the facts show the parties did mean to be legally bound?",
   "body": "- Spouses **separating** or separated (**Merritt v Merritt**: husband who had left signed a note promising the house once she paid off the mortgage)\n- **Serious reliance** (**Parker v Clark**: younger couple sold their home to move in with the older couple)\n- Money **pooled** in a joint venture (**Simpkins v Pays**: lodger shared a weekly competition entry and the winnings)\n- Agreement put in **writing**",
   "yes": "intention present: go to step 4",
   "no": "no contract"
  },
  {
   "h": "Rebut: commercial",
   "ask": "Is there clear evidence the parties did not mean to be bound?",
   "body": "- **Honour clause**: \"binding in honour only\" (**Rose and Frank v Crompton**: distribution agreement not binding)\n- **\"Subject to contract\"**: no contract until formal exchange or signing, unless **waived by conduct** (**RTS v Müller**: work done and all key terms agreed and acted on)\n- **Letter of comfort** stating **current policy**, not a promise about the future (**Kleinwort Benson v Malaysia Mining**: parent's stated \"policy\" to keep its subsidiary able to pay was not a promise)\n!! \"Ex gratia\" is **not** enough (**Edwards v Skyways**: redundancy payment to pilots was binding)",
   "yes": "no contract",
   "no": "intention present: go to step 4"
  },
  {
   "h": "Certain and complete?",
   "ask": "Are the essential terms clear and agreed, or is there a way to fill the gap?",
   "body": "- Vague **essential** term: no contract (**Scammell v Ouston**: \"on hire-purchase terms\" had no settled meaning)\n- **Meaningless** extra phrase: sever it (**Nicolene v Simmonds**: non-existent \"usual conditions\")\n- Price \"to be agreed\" is an **agreement to agree**: no contract (**May and Butcher**: tents), unless a gap filler applies\n- Gap fillers: **previous dealings** or trade custom; **machinery** such as arbitration (**Foley v Classique Coaches**: petrol bought for three years); **reasonable price** under the Sale of Goods Act 1979 s.8, Supply of Goods and Services Act 1982 s.15 and Consumer Rights Act 2015 s.51; an **implied term** (**Wells v Devani**: commission payable on completion)\n- Agreement to **negotiate in good faith** is unenforceable; a **lock-out** binds only for a **fixed period** (**Walford v Miles**: sellers of a photographic business promised to negotiate only with the buyers, then sold elsewhere: no time limit, unenforceable)",
   "yes": "go to step 5",
   "no": "no contract"
  },
  {
   "h": "Is a party a minor?",
   "ask": "Was either party under 18 when the contract was made?",
   "body": "- Rule: binding on the adult, **not enforceable against the minor**\n- **Necessaries** (suitable to the minor's condition in life and **actual needs**): minor pays a **reasonable price** only, Sale of Goods Act 1979 s.3 (**Nash v Inman**: student already had enough clothes, so 11 waistcoats were not necessaries)\n- **Beneficial service contracts** (job, apprenticeship, training) bind unless oppressive overall; **trading contracts** never bind\n- **Continuing contracts** (lease, shares, partnership) bind unless repudiated before or within a **reasonable time after 18**\n- **Minors' Contracts Act 1987**: guarantor is liable (**s.2**); court may order the minor to return property if just and equitable (**s.3**)",
   "yes": "apply these categories",
   "no": "go to step 6"
  },
  {
   "h": "Mental incapacity or drink",
   "ask": "Could the party not understand the contract, and did the other side know or ought to have known?",
   "body": "- Both shown: contract **voidable** at the incapacitated party's option (**Imperial Loan v Stone**: lender did not know, so the guarantee bound)\n- **Affirmation** once recovered or sober means bound (**Matthews v Baxter**: drunk buyer at auction affirmed when sober)\n- **Reasonable price** still payable for necessaries (Mental Capacity Act 2005 s.7)\n- Companies: capacity is not limited by the constitution (Companies Act 2006 s.39)",
   "yes": "contract voidable",
   "no": "contract binding"
  }
 ],
 "outro": "**Exam tip:** set the presumption first (domestic or commercial), then hunt for the facts that rebut it."
};
window.SQE_FRAMEWORKS["contract/privity-third-parties"] = {
 "intro": "Use this when someone who is not a party to a contract wants to enforce it, or the parties want to change a contract that benefits a third party.",
 "steps": [
  {
   "h": "Spot the privity problem",
   "ask": "Is the person suing (or being sued) a party to the contract?",
   "body": "- Common law: only parties can enforce a contract or be bound by it (**Dunlop v Selfridge**: tyre maker could not sue a retailer on its promise to the distributor)\n- Contracts can **never** impose a burden on a third party\n- Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999 applies to contracts made on or after **11 May 2000**",
   "yes": "ordinary contract claim",
   "no": "go to step 2"
  },
  {
   "h": "Is the third party identified?",
   "ask": "Does the contract itself identify the third party by name, class or description?",
   "body": "- **s.1(3)**: name, member of a **class** or answering a **description**; need **not exist** when the contract is made\n- \"Future owners\", \"my grandchildren\", \"the subcontractors\" are enough\n- **Avraamides v Colwill**: new owners promised to pay the company's \"liabilities\"; customers were not identified, so could not sue",
   "yes": "go to step 3",
   "no": "no right under the Act: go to step 6"
  },
  {
   "h": "Express right or benefit?",
   "ask": "Does the contract expressly give the right, or does the term purport to benefit the third party?",
   "body": "- **s.1(1)(a)**: contract **expressly** says the third party may enforce: right exists\n- **s.1(1)(b)**: the term **purports to confer a benefit**: **presumed** enforceable\n- **s.1(2)**: presumption rebutted only if, properly construed, the parties **did not intend** the third party to enforce; **silence is not enough** (**Nisshin Shipping v Cleaves**: brokers could enforce their commission term)\n- Check **s.6** exceptions (negotiable instruments, company constitution, employment terms against an employee, carriage of goods) and any clause **excluding the Act**\n- Third party enforces **subject to** the contract's other terms (**s.1(4)**), with the **same remedies** as a party (**s.1(5)**), including relying on an **exclusion clause** (**s.1(6)**)",
   "yes": "go to step 4",
   "no": "no right under the Act: go to step 6"
  },
  {
   "h": "Was the contract varied?",
   "ask": "Did the parties vary or cancel the contract, and could they do so without the third party's consent?",
   "body": "- Consent needed (**s.2(1)**) once: the third party **communicated assent** to the promisor; or the promisor **knew** of reliance; or reliance was **reasonably foreseeable and happened**\n- Assent by words or conduct counts only when **received**: **no postal rule** (**s.2(2)**)\n- The contract can **expressly** allow the parties to vary freely (**s.2(3)**); the court can dispense with consent (**s.2(4)-(6)**)",
   "yes": "variation binds the third party only if consent was not needed",
   "no": "go to step 5"
  },
  {
   "h": "Defences and promisee",
   "ask": "What defences can the promisor raise, and what about the promisee?",
   "body": "- **s.3**: any defence or set-off arising from the contract that the promisor would have had against the **promisee**, plus personal defences against the third party\n- **s.4**: the promisee can still enforce; **s.5**: no **double recovery**\n- **s.7(2)**: an exclusion of negligence liability (other than death or personal injury) need not pass the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 reasonableness test against the third party"
  },
  {
   "h": "Other ways around privity",
   "ask": "If the Act does not help, is there another route?",
   "body": "- **Promisee sues**: specific performance where damages would be nominal (**Beswick v Beswick**: widow as administratrix enforced the nephew's promise of weekly payments); exceptionally damages for the third party's loss (family holiday; later owner of a building with no direct right)\n- **Agency**: the principal is the real party\n- **Collateral contract** (**Shanklin Pier v Detel**: paint maker promised seven years' life to the pier owners, who specified its paint to their painters)\n- **Trust of a promise** (rare); **tort** (negligence)\n- **Assignment** of the benefit: legal under **LPA 1925 (Law of Property Act 1925) s.136** if absolute, in writing signed by the assignor, with written notice to the debtor; otherwise equitable. Burdens need a **novation**",
   "yes": "third party (or promisee) may recover",
   "no": "no claim by the third party"
  }
 ],
 "outro": "**Exam tip:** in the Act, check identification (s.1(3)) before benefit (s.1(1)(b)), and assent counts on **receipt** under s.2."
};
window.SQE_FRAMEWORKS["contract/express-terms"] = {
 "intro": "Use this when a question asks whether a statement or written clause is part of the contract, what it means, whether its breach allows termination, or whether it was validly changed.",
 "steps": [
  {
   "h": "Term or representation?",
   "ask": "Would a reasonable person think the maker was promising the statement was true?",
   "body": "- **Importance** made clear to the maker points to a term (**Bannerman v White**: buyer said he would not even ask the price if sulphur was used on the hops)\n- **Special knowledge**: dealer's statement is a term (**Dick Bentley**: Bentley's mileage); private seller's is not (**Oscar Chess v Williams**: relied on forged logbook)\n- **Verification**: \"no need to look\" points to a term; \"have it surveyed\" points to a representation\n- Long **gap** before contracting, or **left out** of a later written contract, points to a representation\n> Term broken: breach of contract. Representation false: go to the misrepresentation framework",
   "yes": "go to step 2",
   "no": "consider misrepresentation"
  },
  {
   "h": "Written contract issues",
   "ask": "Does the parol evidence rule or an entire agreement clause stop the statement counting?",
   "body": "- **Parol evidence rule**: outside evidence cannot normally add to, vary or contradict a written contract, but many exceptions apply\n- An oral promise may be a **collateral contract** (**City and Westminster v Mudd**: tenant assured he could sleep in the shop)\n- **Entire agreement clause** stops a statement becoming a term or collateral warranty, but does **not** by itself exclude **misrepresentation**",
   "yes": "statement is not a term",
   "no": "go to step 3"
  },
  {
   "h": "Incorporated?",
   "ask": "Was the written term incorporated by signature, reasonable notice or course of dealing?",
   "body": "- **Signature**: bound whether read or not (**L'Estrange v Graucob**), unless its effect was **misrepresented** (**Curtis**: told it covered only beads and sequins), non est factum or fraud\n- **Reasonable notice**: before or at the time of contracting (**Olley**: bedroom notice too late after booking at reception; **Thornton**: car park ticket after the barrier); in a **contractual document**, not a receipt (**Chapelton**: deckchair ticket); **onerous terms** need special highlighting (**Interfoto**: £5 per transparency per day)\n- **Course of dealing**: **regular and consistent** (about 100 dealings over 3 years between businesses: yes; 3 or 4 garage visits in 5 years by a consumer: no), or common trade understanding between businesses\n> Pinpoint the **moment the contract was made**",
   "yes": "go to step 4",
   "no": "term not part of the contract"
  },
  {
   "h": "What does it mean?",
   "ask": "What would a reasonable person with the parties' background knowledge understand the words to mean?",
   "body": "- **Clear words** prevail even if the result is harsh (**Arnold v Britton**: service charge rising 10% compound each year)\n- Only if **genuinely ambiguous**, prefer the **commercially sensible** meaning (**Rainy Sky**: refund guarantees covered the builder's insolvency)\n- Not admissible: **pre-contract negotiations**, **subjective intentions**, **later conduct**\n- Ambiguity: **contra proferentem** (against the party relying on it); consumer contract: meaning **most favourable to the consumer** (Consumer Rights Act 2015 s.69)\n- Document fails to record a **common intention**: **rectification** changes the words",
   "yes": "go to step 5"
  },
  {
   "h": "Classify the term",
   "ask": "Is the broken term a condition, a warranty or an innominate term?",
   "body": "- **Statute**: Sale of Goods Act 1979 implied terms on the right to sell (s.12(1)), description, quality, fitness and sample (ss.13-15) are **conditions** (business and private sales); the s.12(2) terms (freedom from undisclosed charges, quiet possession) are only **warranties** (s.12(5A))\n- **Case law**: time clauses in commercial contracts are usually **conditions** (**Bunge v Tradax**: 15 days' notice of the vessel given late); time of payment is not, unless agreed\n- **Wording**: \"condition\" is strong but not conclusive evidence (**Schuler v Wickman**: weekly visits to six car makers; a few missed visits did not justify termination)\n- Otherwise **innominate**: look at the **consequences** of the breach",
   "yes": "go to step 6"
  },
  {
   "h": "Can the innocent party terminate?",
   "ask": "What remedy does the breach of that type of term give?",
   "body": "- **Condition**: terminate and/or damages, however minor the effect (**Poussard v Spiers**: singer missed the opening night)\n- **Warranty**: damages only (**Bettini v Gye**: singer missed some rehearsals)\n- **Innominate**: terminate only if the breach deprives the innocent party of **substantially the whole benefit** (**Hongkong Fir**: ship off hire about 20 weeks of a 24 month charter: no)\n!! Business buyer (Sale of Goods Act 1979 s.15A): cannot reject for a **slight** breach of ss.13-15; damages only"
  },
  {
   "h": "Was the term varied?",
   "ask": "Was there a valid variation of the contract?",
   "body": "- Variations need **consideration** from both sides (or a deed); a promise to pay more may be supported by practical benefit\n- **No oral modification clauses** are effective (**Rock Advertising**: phone agreement to reschedule licence fees was not binding)\n- Estoppel needs **unequivocal** words or conduct that the informal variation was valid, and something more\n- **Waiver**: a party who gives up a right may need to give reasonable notice before relying on it again",
   "yes": "contract applies as varied",
   "no": "original terms apply"
  }
 ],
 "outro": "**Exam tip:** do not confuse the seriousness of the **term** (condition) with the seriousness of the **breach** (innominate term)."
};
