/* DFPH-style comic pages (one tall page = title + 3 numbered panels). Made from content/dfph-script-*.json; images proof-checked (see img/dfph/<topic>/CHECK.md). */
window.SQE_DFPH = window.SQE_DFPH || {};
window.SQE_DFPH["blp/business-structures"] = [
{
"img": "img/dfph/blp-business-structures/p01.webp",
"title": "Business structures: two big questions",
"alt": "Business structures: two big questions. 1. Every business is run through a structure: sole trader, general partnership, LLP, private limited company or public limited company. 2. Separate legal personality means the business is a person in law. Companies and LLPs are; sole traders and partnerships are not. 3. Sole traders and partners have unlimited liability; shareholders and LLP members have limited liability, losing only what they agreed to put in."
},
{
"img": "img/dfph/blp-business-structures/p02.webp",
"title": "The sole trader",
"alt": "The sole trader. 1. A sole trader owns and runs a business alone, though they can employ staff. Legally, the business is just the person. 2. Sole traders have unlimited personal liability for every business debt; if the business fails, the owner can be made bankrupt. 3. No formalities: just start trading and register with HMRC for self-assessment. Nothing is filed at Companies House, so accounts stay private."
},
{
"img": "img/dfph/blp-business-structures/p03.webp",
"title": "General partnership: it just happens",
"alt": "General partnership: it just happens. 1. Partnership Act 1890 s.1: a partnership is persons carrying on a business in common with a view of profit. 2. It arises automatically from the facts: no written agreement, registration or even intention to be partners is needed. 3. s.2: sharing profits is evidence of partnership but not conclusive; merely co-owning property or sharing gross returns is not enough."
},
{
"img": "img/dfph/blp-business-structures/p04.webp",
"title": "Partnership: no separate person, unlimited liability",
"alt": "Partnership: no separate person, unlimited liability. 1. 'The firm' is not a legal person, just the partners' collective name, though it can sue and be sued in that name. 2. Every partner has unlimited liability for firm debts while a partner; a creditor can recover the whole debt from any one partner. 3. By default the partnership is dissolved when a partner dies or becomes bankrupt, or, if at will, any partner gives notice."
},
{
"img": "img/dfph/blp-business-structures/p05.webp",
"title": "The limited liability partnership (LLP)",
"alt": "The limited liability partnership (LLP). 1. An LLP exists only by registering at Companies House under the LLPA 2000. It is a separate legal person. 2. Members' liability is limited to what they agreed to contribute. It needs at least 2 members and 2 designated members. 3. Inside, members organise management and profits freely by agreement; outside, it files accounts and a confirmation statement publicly."
},
{
"img": "img/dfph/blp-business-structures/p06.webp",
"title": "The private limited company (Ltd)",
"alt": "The private limited company (Ltd). 1. A private company is a separate legal person, owned by shareholders, run by directors; one person can be sole shareholder and director. 2. At least 1 shareholder and 1 human director; secretary optional. No minimum share capital: a single 1p share is enough. 3. Shareholders' liability is limited to any amount unpaid on their shares. A private company cannot offer its shares to the public."
},
{
"img": "img/dfph/blp-business-structures/p07.webp",
"title": "The public limited company (plc)",
"alt": "The public limited company (plc). 1. A plc may offer its shares to the public, and its name must end 'public limited company' or 'plc'. 2. A plc needs at least 2 directors, a qualified secretary and £50,000 allotted share capital, each share at least quarter-paid plus all of any premium. 3. A company formed as a plc needs a trading certificate (s.761) before doing business or borrowing; a re-registered private company does not."
},
{
"img": "img/dfph/blp-business-structures/p08.webp",
"title": "Who pays tax?",
"alt": "Who pays tax?. 1. Sole traders and partners pay income tax and Class 4 NICs on profits (partners on their own share), drawn or not. 2. A partnership and an LLP pay no tax themselves: each partner or member is taxed on their own share. 3. The company pays corporation tax. Shareholders pay income tax on dividends; directors and employees pay income tax and NICs on salaries."
},
{
"img": "img/dfph/blp-business-structures/p09.webp",
"title": "Key differences and a trap",
"alt": "Key differences and a trap. 1. Companies and LLPs file a yearly confirmation statement and accounts at Companies House; accounts within 9 months, or 6 for a plc. 2. Companies and LLPs continue when owners change; a sole trader's business ends with them; a partnership dissolves by default. 3. An LLP is not a limited partnership (LP): an LP needs a general partner with unlimited liability and has no separate personality."
},
{
"img": "img/dfph/blp-business-structures/p10.webp",
"title": "Salomon: the company is a separate person",
"alt": "Salomon: the company is a separate person. 1. Salomon (1897): Mr Salomon sold his boot business to his own company, holding nearly all the shares and a secured loan. 2. When it went insolvent, the House of Lords held the company was a different person: he was not liable for its debts. 3. Rule: a validly formed company is separate from its shareholders and directors, even a one-person company. The same applies to an LLP."
},
{
"img": "img/dfph/blp-business-structures/p11.webp",
"title": "What separate personality means",
"alt": "What separate personality means. 1. The company owns its own property. Macaura (1925): an owner insured his company's timber in his own name and could not claim. 2. The company can contract with its own directors. Lee (1961): a sole director-pilot was the company's employee, so his widow could claim. 3. Each company in a group is a separate person; a parent is not automatically liable for its subsidiary's debts."
},
{
"img": "img/dfph/blp-business-structures/p12.webp",
"title": "Limited liability belongs to the shareholders",
"alt": "Limited liability belongs to the shareholders. 1. Limited liability is a feature of the members, not the company: the company is liable without limit for its own debts. 2. A shareholder's maximum liability is the amount unpaid on their shares, including any premium. Fully paid shares mean nothing more to pay. 3. Separate personality and limited liability are different ideas: personality makes the business a person; limited liability caps the owners' contribution."
},
{
"img": "img/dfph/blp-business-structures/p13.webp",
"title": "Still personally liable: not veil piercing",
"alt": "Still personally liable: not veil piercing. 1. Directors often give banks and landlords personal guarantees; if the company defaults, the director pays under that separate contract. 2. Directors are personally liable on contracts signed in their own name or for unformed companies, and for their own fraud or deceit. 3. Statute can make individuals liable, such as for fraudulent or wrongful trading. These are statutory claims, not piercing the veil."
},
{
"img": "img/dfph/blp-business-structures/p14.webp",
"title": "Piercing the corporate veil",
"alt": "Piercing the corporate veil. 1. Piercing the veil means the court ignores the company's separate personality and treats its rights or liabilities as the controller's. Very rare. 2. Prest (2013): no piercing, as Mr Prest evaded no existing obligation; but his companies held the properties on trust for him. 3. Evasion principle: pierce only, as a last resort, where someone deliberately evades an existing obligation using a company. Concealment: look behind it."
},
{
"img": "img/dfph/blp-business-structures/p15.webp",
"title": "Evasion cases, and when courts will not pierce",
"alt": "Evasion cases, and when courts will not pierce. 1. Gilford v Horne (1933): Horne used a company to approach customers he promised not to; injunction against him and the company. 2. Jones v Lipman (1962): Lipman transferred his house to his company to avoid the sale; specific performance against him and the company. 3. Courts will not pierce merely because it seems fair, one person controls it, it is insolvent, or it is in a group."
},
{
"img": "img/dfph/blp-business-structures/p16.webp",
"title": "Choosing a structure: seven questions",
"alt": "Choosing a structure: seven questions. 1. Ask who is involved and the risk: one person can be a sole trader or company; risky businesses want limited liability. 2. Privacy, cost, tax: sole traders and partnerships file nothing public; companies pay corporation tax, owners taxed only on what they take. 3. Only companies can sell shares, only a plc to the public; partnerships and LLPs are flexible; companies and LLPs outlast their owners."
},
{
"img": "img/dfph/blp-business-structures/p17.webp",
"title": "Choosing a structure: common answers",
"alt": "Choosing a structure: common answers. 1. Want limited liability with partnership-style flexibility and tax transparency, often as professionals? Choose an LLP. 2. Want to offer shares to the public? Choose a plc. Want limited liability, reinvested profits or private investors? Choose a private company. 3. Want simplicity, privacy and low cost with low risk? Sole trader alone, or partnership with others, ideally with a written agreement."
}
];

