window.SQE_TIPS_MD="# Examiner tips for SQE1 (January 2027)\n\nThese tips come from what the SRA and Kaplan (the company that runs the SQE) have published. Every claim has a link. Research done 19 November 2026.\n\n**What the official reports contain:** The SRA does **not** publish a written \"examiner's report\" that says \"candidates kept getting X wrong\". It publishes two kinds of report:\n\n- a **statistical report** after each sitting, with pass rates and charts but no written comments on subjects, and\n- an **annual report** each year. This has a table of average scores for each subject and a few sentences on which subjects were hardest and easiest.\n\nSo the subject tips below tell you **which subjects candidates score lowest and highest in**. They cannot tell you which topics inside a subject went wrong, because the official reports don't break it down that far. No full past papers are published. The nearest thing is the SRA's sample questions, which include 130 questions that \"have all been used in SQE1 assessments\" ([SQE1 sample questions page](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-sample-questions)).\n\n---\n\n## Part 1: How the exam works\n\n### Format and timing (new from January 2027)\n\n- **From January 2027 there are 170 questions in each paper (FLK1 and FLK2), down from 180.** That means 85 questions in each session and 340 questions across SQE1 in total. ([SRA news, 10 Sept 2026: \"Important changes to SQE1 for January 2027 onwards\"](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/news-item/2026/09/10/important-changes-to-sqe1-for-january-2027-onwards))\n- **Each session is still 2 hours 33 minutes, with a one-hour break between the two sessions on each day.** ([SRA news, 10 Sept 2026](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/news-item/2026/09/10/important-changes-to-sqe1-for-january-2027-onwards); [SRA blog \"Understanding SQE1\"](https://www.sra.org.uk/news/blogs/understanding-sqe1/))\n- **You get about 1.8 minutes (about 108 seconds) per question.** That is 153 minutes ÷ 85 questions. The old format gave about 102 seconds. ([Legal Futures, \"SRA gives SQE1 candidates extra six seconds per question\" – news report, secondary](https://www.legalfutures.co.uk/latest-news/sra-gives-sqe1-candidates-extra-six-seconds-per-question))\n- **The SRA made this change because candidates said SQE1 \"can feel rushed and pressurised\".** Practise working at a steady pace. ([SRA news, 10 Sept 2026](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/news-item/2026/09/10/important-changes-to-sqe1-for-january-2027-onwards))\n- **From January 2027 you know in advance which subjects each session covers:**\n  - FLK1 session 1: Business Law & Practice, Dispute Resolution, Legal Services\n  - FLK1 session 2: Tort, Contract, Legal System\n  - FLK2 session 1: Wills & Administration of Estates, Solicitors Accounts (wills), Trusts, Land Law\n  - FLK2 session 2: Property Practice, Solicitors Accounts (property), Criminal Liability, Criminal Practice\n\n  ([SRA news, 10 Sept 2026](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/news-item/2026/09/10/important-changes-to-sqe1-for-january-2027-onwards))\n- **Within a session, questions still come in a random order, so subjects are mixed together.** Practise switching between topics. ([SRA news, 10 Sept 2026](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/news-item/2026/09/10/important-changes-to-sqe1-for-january-2027-onwards))\n- **Each subject's share of the questions has not changed.** ([SRA news, 10 Sept 2026](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/news-item/2026/09/10/important-changes-to-sqe1-for-january-2027-onwards))\n\n### Marking\n\n- **Each correct answer scores 1 mark and wrong answers lose nothing, so never leave a question blank.** ([SRA \"Preparing for the SQE\"](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/preparing-for-sqe))\n- **Scores are reported on a 0–500 scale where 300 is always the pass mark.** 0 equals 0% and 500 equals 100%. The SRA has used this scale since January 2025. ([SQE Annual Report 2024/25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n- **Different candidates may get different versions of the paper (\"forms\"), and each version has its own pass mark.** This makes things fair if one version is slightly harder. ([SQE Annual Report 2024/25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n- **You must pass FLK1 and FLK2 separately to pass SQE1.** Both reports below give separate FLK1, FLK2 and SQE1 pass rates. ([SQE1 statistical reports](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/reports))\n\n### How the questions are written (\"single best answer\")\n\n- **Every question has three parts:** a story (\"stem\"), the actual question (\"lead-in\") and five answer options. ([SRA \"Single best answer questions\"](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-single-best-answer))\n- **All five options are designed to look plausible, but only one is correct.** Expect more than one answer to look right, and pick the one that best answers the exact lead-in. ([SRA \"Single best answer questions\"](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-single-best-answer))\n- **The questions \"do not assess simple recall\".** They test whether you can apply the law to a client's situation. This is what \"functioning legal knowledge\" means. ([SRA \"Single best answer questions\"](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-single-best-answer); [SRA blog \"Understanding SQE1\"](https://www.sra.org.uk/news/blogs/understanding-sqe1/))\n- **The questions are written by qualified solicitors and set at the level of a newly qualified solicitor.** ([SRA blog \"Understanding SQE1\"](https://www.sra.org.uk/news/blogs/understanding-sqe1/); [SQE1 Assessment Specification](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/exam-arrangements/assessment-information/sqe1-assessment-specification))\n\n### What you do and don't need to memorise\n\n- **In general you do not need to remember case names or section numbers.** The specification says \"candidates are not required to recall specific case names, or cite statutory or regulatory authorities\". ([SQE1 Assessment Specification](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/exam-arrangements/assessment-information/sqe1-assessment-specification))\n- **The exception is a name that is the usual label for a rule.** The specification's examples are *Rylands v Fletcher*, CPR Part 36 and a \"Section 25 notice\". Learn those kinds of names. ([SQE1 Assessment Specification](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/exam-arrangements/assessment-information/sqe1-assessment-specification))\n- **Tax only comes up inside Business Law & Practice, Property Practice and Wills.** Where you need to use tax rates or thresholds, \"these will be provided\", but you must know which reliefs and exemptions exist. ([SQE1 Assessment Specification](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/exam-arrangements/assessment-information/sqe1-assessment-specification))\n- **Solicitors Accounts only comes up inside Property Practice and Wills questions, never on its own.** ([SQE1 Assessment Specification](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/exam-arrangements/assessment-information/sqe1-assessment-specification))\n- **Ethics can come up in any question in either paper, so look for a professional conduct issue in every scenario.** ([SQE1 Assessment Specification](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/exam-arrangements/assessment-information/sqe1-assessment-specification); [SRA news, 10 Sept 2026](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/news-item/2026/09/10/important-changes-to-sqe1-for-january-2027-onwards))\n\n### Preparation and resits\n\n- **Use the official sample questions.** The original set has 90 questions. A further 130 \"pre-tested\" questions have all been used in real SQE1 exams. The SRA warns the samples are not a full mock and don't cover everything. ([SQE1 sample questions](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-sample-questions))\n- **Try the official exam software demo before the day.** The SRA says \"You can get an understanding of the SQE1 assessment platform before sitting the assessment.\" ([SRA \"Preparing for the SQE\"](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/preparing-for-sqe))\n- **Resits pass at much lower rates, so don't rush back in.** The second-attempt SQE1 pass rate was 25% in January 2025 and 20% in July 2025. The SRA says candidates \"should consider taking more time (and/or putting in more work or training) between sittings\". ([SQE Annual Report 2024/25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n- **If you fail, the SRA suggests reviewing the subject-by-subject scores in its reports.** The subjects with the biggest gap between passing and failing candidates \"may require more focus/preparation for future attempts at SQE1\". ([SQE Annual Report 2022/23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023))\n- **The SQE's independent reviewer said feedback can show \"a lack of understanding of key assessment processes\".** He added that understanding why the exam is designed as it is \"can help with preparation\", and pointed candidates to the official pre-tested sample questions. ([SQE Independent Reviewer annual report 2025, published April 2026](https://www.sra.org.uk/sra/research-publications/sqe-independent-25/))\n\n---\n\n## Part 2: Pass rates over time\n\nAll figures are copied exactly from the official SRA/Kaplan reports. \"SQE1\" means candidates who sat both papers and passed both.\n\n| Sitting | FLK1 pass rate | FLK2 pass rate | SQE1 overall | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Nov 2021 | 67% | 54% | 53% | [SQE1 Nov 2021 report](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe1-november-2021) |\n| Jul 2022 | 64% | 55% | 53% | [SQE1 Jul 2022 report](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe1-july-2022) |\n| Jan 2023 | 59% | 56% | 51% | [Annual Report 2022/23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023) |\n| Jul 2023 | 66% | 58% | 53% | [Annual Report 2022/23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023) |\n| Jan 2024 | 63% | 61% | 56% | [Annual Report 2024](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2024) |\n| Jul 2024 | 55% | 50% | 44% | [Annual Report 2024](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2024) |\n| Jan 2025 | 64% | 61% | 56% | [Annual Report 2024/25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025) |\n| Jul 2025 | 51% | 48% | 41% | [Annual Report 2024/25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025) |\n| Jan 2026 | 62% | 57% | 53% | [SQE1 Jan 2026 report](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe1-january-2026) |\n| Jul 2026 | 50% | 50% | 42% | [SQE1 Jul 2026 report](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe1-july-2026) |\n\nWhat the table shows:\n\n- **First-time candidates do better than the overall figures.** For example, the January 2026 SQE1 pass rate for first attempts was 58%, against 53% for everyone. ([SQE1 Jan 2026 report](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe1-january-2026))\n- **FLK2 has had a lower or equal pass rate than FLK1 in every sitting.**\n- **Since 2024, January sittings have had higher pass rates than July sittings.** This is our own reading of the numbers. The SRA has not given a reason for it.\n\n---\n\n## Part 3: Subject by subject\n\n**How to read this section.** The SRA's annual reports give the **average score** for each subject. The 2021–2023 reports use percentages, and the pass marks were 57% (FLK1) and 56% (FLK2) in Nov 2021, and 56% and 55% in Jul 2022. The 2024 and 2025 reports use the **0–500 scale, where 300 is the pass mark**, so a subject average below 300 means the average candidate scored below pass level in that subject.\n\nSources used below:\n\n- **AR22** = [SQE Annual Report 2021/22](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2022) (Nov 2021 and Jul 2022 sittings)\n- **AR23** = [SQE Annual Report 2022/23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023) (2023 sittings)\n- **AR24** = [SQE Annual Report 2024](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2024) (2024 sittings)\n- **AR25** = [SQE Annual Report 2024/25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025) (2025 sittings)\n\nThe statistical reports for each sitting show subjects only as charts with no written comments. The reports never say which **topics** inside a subject went wrong, so none is claimed here.\n\n### FLK1\n\n**Business Law & Practice (BLP)**\n- Average scores: 58.5% (Nov 2021), 49.6% (Jul 2022), 55.8% (2023), 279 (2024), 292 (2025). ([AR22](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2022), [AR23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023), [AR24](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2024), [AR25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n- The 2025 report says BLP and Dispute Resolution \"continue to be the more challenging practice areas for candidates\". ([AR25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n- BLP is where passing and failing candidates differ most. It had the biggest pass/fail gap in FLK1 in 2023 (25.5 percentage points) and was named again for this in 2024 and 2025, so strong BLP knowledge goes a long way. ([AR23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023), [AR25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n- BLP includes tax, but the rates and thresholds you need are given in the question. ([SQE1 Assessment Specification](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/exam-arrangements/assessment-information/sqe1-assessment-specification))\n\n**Dispute Resolution (DR)**\n- Average scores: 55.8% (Nov 2021), 52.6% (Jul 2022), 52.3% (2023), 278 (2024 – the lowest in FLK1), 291 (2025 – joint lowest in FLK1). ([AR22](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2022), [AR23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023), [AR24](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2024), [AR25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n- Dispute Resolution has been one of the two lowest-scoring FLK1 subjects in every annual report, so give it extra revision time. ([AR22](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2022), [AR23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023), [AR25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n- Procedural labels such as \"CPR Part 36\" are named by the SRA as things you are expected to know by name. ([SQE1 Assessment Specification](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/exam-arrangements/assessment-information/sqe1-assessment-specification))\n\n**Contract Law**\n- Average scores: 70.0% (Nov 2021), 65.5% (Jul 2022), 63.2% (2023), 335 (2024), 315 (2025). ([AR22](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2022), [AR23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023), [AR24](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2024), [AR25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n- Contract is consistently one of the best-scoring subjects, so candidates generally do well here. The 2025 report says candidates \"have consistently performed better in Ethics, Contract Law and Tort (FLK1) and Ethics, Criminal Liability and Land Law (FLK2) than in the other practice areas\". ([AR25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n\n**Tort**\n- Average scores: 57.7% (Nov 2021), 61.9% (Jul 2022), 57.5% (2023), 299 (2024), 300 (2025). ([AR22](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2022), [AR23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023), [AR24](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2024), [AR25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n- In both 2024 and 2025, Tort was one of the two FLK1 subjects with the widest gap between passing and failing candidates, so it separates strong and weak candidates. ([AR24](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2024), [AR25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n- *Rylands v Fletcher* is the SRA's own example of a case name you are expected to know. ([SQE1 Assessment Specification](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/exam-arrangements/assessment-information/sqe1-assessment-specification))\n\n**Legal System (English legal system, constitutional and administrative law)**\n- Average scores: 60.5% (Nov 2021), 60.9% (Jul 2022), 57.3% (2023), 294 (2024), 292 (2025). ([AR22](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2022), [AR23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023), [AR24](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2024), [AR25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n- Legal System sits in the middle of FLK1 but has been below the 300 pass level in both 2024 and 2025. ([AR24](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2024), [AR25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n- No topic-level commentary in the official reports.\n\n**Legal Services**\n- Average scores: 59.9% (Nov 2021), 61.4% (Jul 2022), 51.6% (2023 – the lowest in FLK1), 302 (2024), 291 (2025 – joint lowest in FLK1). ([AR22](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2022), [AR23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023), [AR24](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2024), [AR25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n- Scores in Legal Services swing a lot between years, and it was the lowest or joint-lowest FLK1 subject in 2023 and 2025, so don't treat it as an \"easy\" subject. ([AR23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023), [AR25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n- Legal Services has one of the smallest gaps between passing and failing candidates (for example 17.0 percentage points in 2023), meaning even strong candidates find it hard. ([AR23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023))\n\n### Ethics (both papers)\n\n**Ethics & Professional Conduct**\n- Ethics has the highest average score of every subject in every annual report. In FLK1 it scored 73.4% (Nov 2021), 69.0% (Jul 2022), 70.7% (2023), 362 (2024) and 352 (2025). ([AR22](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2022), [AR23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023), [AR24](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2024), [AR25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n- In FLK2, Ethics scored 65.1% (Nov 2021), 74.8% (Jul 2022), 63.5% (2023), 354 (2024) and 348 (2025). ([AR22](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2022), [AR23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023), [AR24](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2024), [AR25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n- The 2023 report found candidates scored \"consistently higher for the ethics items compared to the non-ethics items\". Ethics is a reliable source of marks, so don't lose them by missing an ethics point hidden in a question about another subject. ([AR23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023))\n- Ethics can be tested inside any subject on either day. ([SQE1 Assessment Specification](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/exam-arrangements/assessment-information/sqe1-assessment-specification))\n\n### FLK2\n\n**Wills & Administration of Estates (the reports call this \"Wills and Intestacy\")**\n- Average scores: 55.6% (Nov 2021), 50.7% (Jul 2022), 49.9% (2023), 265 (2024), 282 (2025). ([AR22](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2022), [AR23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023), [AR24](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2024), [AR25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n- Wills has been the **lowest-scoring subject in FLK2** in 2023, 2024 and 2025. In 2024 its average (265) was the lowest of any SQE1 subject that year, so this is the biggest weak spot to fix. ([AR23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023), [AR24](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2024), [AR25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n- In 2023, Wills was also one of the two FLK2 subjects with the widest gap between passing and failing candidates. ([AR23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023))\n- Wills questions can include inheritance tax (with rates provided) and Solicitors Accounts. ([SQE1 Assessment Specification](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/exam-arrangements/assessment-information/sqe1-assessment-specification))\n\n**Trusts**\n- Average scores: 56.8% (Nov 2021), 56.0% (Jul 2022), 55.1% (2023), 300 (2024), 294 (2025). ([AR22](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2022), [AR23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023), [AR24](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2024), [AR25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n- Trusts sits around the pass level. The 2024 report listed it among the FLK2 subjects with a wider gap between passing and failing candidates. ([AR24](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2024))\n- No topic-level commentary in the official reports.\n\n**Land Law**\n- Average scores: 59.1% (Nov 2021), 56.9% (Jul 2022), 55.0% (2023), 303 (2024), 299 (2025). ([AR22](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2022), [AR23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023), [AR24](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2024), [AR25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n- Land Law usually has the **biggest gap between passing and failing candidates** of any subject. It was the biggest in Nov 2021 (28.0 percentage points), 2024 (125 points) and 2025 (130 points), and close behind Business Law & Practice in 2022 and 2023, so being strong in Land Law is closely linked to passing FLK2. ([AR22](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2022), [AR23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023), [AR24](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2024), [AR25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n\n**Property Practice (conveyancing)**\n- Average scores: 48.3% (Nov 2021), 46.4% (Jul 2022), 50.7% (2023), 278 (2024), 285 (2025). ([AR22](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2022), [AR23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023), [AR24](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2024), [AR25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n- Property Practice has been one of the two lowest-scoring FLK2 subjects in every annual report since 2022. The 2025 report calls it one of \"the more challenging practice areas for candidates\". ([AR22](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2022), [AR23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023), [AR25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n- Property Practice questions can include tax such as SDLT (with rates provided) and Solicitors Accounts. A \"Section 25 notice\" (a business lease term) is one of the SRA's examples of a name you must know. ([SQE1 Assessment Specification](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/exam-arrangements/assessment-information/sqe1-assessment-specification))\n\n**Criminal Law (the reports call this \"Criminal Liability\")**\n- Average scores: 71.8% (Nov 2021), 58.8% (Jul 2022), 60.3% (2023), 317 (2024), 318 (2025). ([AR22](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2022), [AR23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023), [AR24](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2024), [AR25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n- Criminal Law was the best-scoring FLK2 subject after Ethics in 2023, 2024 and 2025, and in Nov 2021 it beat even Ethics, so candidates generally do well here. ([AR22](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2022), [AR23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023), [AR24](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2024), [AR25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n\n**Criminal Practice (the reports call this \"Criminal Law and Practice\")**\n- Average scores: 48.0% (Nov 2021), 61.4% (Jul 2022), 53.0% (2023), 290 (2024), 287 (2025). ([AR22](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2022), [AR23](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2023), [AR24](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2024), [AR25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n- Criminal Practice has usually scored lower than Criminal Law (July 2022 was the exception), and in 2025 it was one of the two FLK2 subjects with the widest gap between passing and failing candidates. ([AR25](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports/sqe-annual-report-2025))\n\n**Solicitors Accounts**\n- No subject-specific commentary in the official reports. Solicitors Accounts is not scored separately; its questions sit inside Wills and Property Practice. ([SQE1 Assessment Specification](https://sqe.sra.org.uk/exam-arrangements/assessment-information/sqe1-assessment-specification))\n- Because Wills and Property Practice are the two lowest-scoring FLK2 subjects, Solicitors Accounts questions sit inside the weakest areas. This is our own observation, not a statement in the reports.\n\n---\n\n## The short version\n\n1. **About 1.8 minutes per question** (85 questions in 2 h 33 min). Don't get stuck, and answer everything because wrong answers cost nothing.\n2. **More than one answer will look right.** Read the lead-in carefully and pick the *best* answer.\n3. **Wills, Property Practice, Dispute Resolution and BLP** are the subjects candidates score lowest in, year after year.\n4. **Land Law, Tort and BLP** have the biggest gaps between passing and failing candidates, so getting good at them pays off most.\n5. **Ethics is the highest-scoring subject.** Look for an ethics angle in every question so you don't drop easy marks.\n";

;
