window.SQE_FULLMOCKS={"accounts-p1":[{"t":"framework-client-money","p":"FLK2","q":"Marlow & Finch LLP acts for the executors of the late Mr Dariusz Kowalczyk. Last week it delivered a bill to the executors covering its profit costs plus VAT and a fee of £600 plus VAT for counsel's advice on the meaning of a clause in the will. The firm has not yet paid counsel. The executors now send a single payment for the full amount of the bill from the estate bank account.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the firm should treat the payment?","o":["It is firm money and may go into business account, but counsel must be paid promptly.","It is client money and must be paid into client account until counsel has been paid.","It is client money, and counsel must be paid within two working days of receipt.","It is firm money, but it must be held in client account until counsel has been paid.","It is a mixed payment and must be split between client and business account on receipt."],"a":0,"e":"Money for fees and unpaid disbursements is client money only if received **before a bill is delivered** (r.2.1(d)); once billed, money received for an unpaid disbursement such as counsel's fee is **firm money**, though the firm must pay counsel promptly. The 'two working days' requirement belonged to the old 2011 rules and no longer exists. It is not a mixed payment, because no part of it is held for the executors any longer."},{"t":"framework-client-money","p":"FLK2","q":"Halden Solicitors finished administering the estate of the late Mrs Bernadette O'Shea eight months ago. A late refund of £620 from the deceased's energy supplier has since arrived and been credited to the estate ledger. The sole residuary beneficiary has moved abroad, and the firm's letters, emails and a tracing agent have all failed to find her.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the firm may do with the £620?","o":["Pay it to a charity of its choice after reasonable tracing steps, keeping records for six years.","Transfer it to business account to cover the cost of instructing the tracing agent.","Pay £500 of it to charity without authorisation and transfer £120 to business account.","Apply to the SRA for authorisation before withdrawing it from client account.","Keep it in client account indefinitely until the beneficiary makes contact with the firm."],"a":3,"e":"Under the SRA's prescribed circumstances a firm may pay a residual balance to charity without permission only if it is **£500 or less**; a balance **over £500 needs the SRA's prior authorisation** (r.5.1(c)). The firm may never deduct its own tracing costs, and it cannot split the sum to fit under the limit. Holding the money indefinitely is not required and risks client account being used as a store for money there is no longer any reason to hold."},{"t":"framework-client-money","p":"FLK2","q":"Brightwater Legal is a small conveyancing practice that has no client account. It relies on the exemption for firms whose only client money is money on account of fees and disbursements for which the firm is liable, and it tells clients in advance that such money is held in its business account. A buyer client, Ms Tamsin Adeyemi, offers to send £9,750 now so that the firm can pay her Stamp Duty Land Tax (SDLT) after completion.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's position?","o":["It may hold the money in business account, because it is money for an unpaid disbursement.","It may not hold the money under the exemption, because the client is liable for the SDLT.","It may hold the money in business account, provided it pays HMRC within 14 days.","It may hold the money in business account, because it has told the client where it is held.","It may not hold the money under the exemption unless the SDLT is less than £10,000."],"a":1,"e":"The r.2.2 exemption covers only money on account of fees and disbursements **for which the firm itself is liable** (such as counsel's fees); **SDLT is the client's own liability**, so this money falls outside it and would need a client account. Telling the client in advance is only one of the conditions, and the 14-day SDLT filing deadline has nothing to do with where the money is held. There is no £10,000 threshold in the exemption."},{"t":"client-account-withdrawals","p":"FLK2","q":"Oakfield Solicitors acts for Mr Kwame Osei on the sale of his flat and holds £85,000 of his sale proceeds in client account. It also acts for Mr Osei's business partner, Ms Imogen Fairweather, on her house purchase. Ms Fairweather telephones the firm to say that Mr Osei has agreed to lend her £15,000 for her deposit and asks the firm to move that sum from Mr Osei's ledger to hers today.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm may make the transfer?","o":["It may make the transfer on Ms Fairweather's instructions, as she has agreed to repay the loan.","It may make the transfer because both are clients and the money stays in client account.","It may not make the transfer, because loans between clients are prohibited by the rules.","It may make the transfer only with the SRA's prior written authorisation to do so.","It may make the transfer only on instructions from Mr Osei, which should be in writing."],"a":4,"e":"An inter-client transfer is a **withdrawal of the lending client's money**, so it needs a ground under r.5.1, here **instructions from the person for whom the money is held** (r.5.1(b)), and good practice is to obtain them in writing. The borrower's say-so is not enough, and the money staying in client account does not change the need for authority. Private loans between clients are not banned, and SRA authorisation is needed only where no other ground applies."},{"t":"client-account-withdrawals","p":"FLK2","q":"Greaves & Lomax acts for the executors of the late Mr Rajinder Bhatt. Part of the estate is a buy-to-let flat, which the executors intend to sell once probate is granted. Until then they ask the firm to receive the tenant's monthly rent into client account and to pay the flat's buildings insurance and service charge from the estate money it holds.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's position?","o":["It must refuse, because rent may never be received into a client account.","It must refuse unless the executors give written instructions for each payment.","It may agree, because the receipts and payments form part of administering the estate.","It may agree, but only if the rent is held in a separate designated client account.","It must refuse, because only the executors personally may collect income of the estate."],"a":2,"e":"Rule 3.3 forbids using client account as a **banking facility**: receipts and payments must be **in respect of regulated services the firm is delivering**. Collecting income from an estate asset and paying that asset's outgoings while the estate is administered is part of the legal service, so it is permitted. The test is the link to the service, not the form of the instructions or the type of client account used."},{"t":"separation-mixed-payments","p":"FLK2","q":"Penrose Hall LLP holds £4,000 on account of costs for the executors of the late Mrs Yetunde Bakare. A fee earner finishes a stage of the work and, before any bill or other written notification of costs has been sent, asks the cashier to transfer £1,500 plus VAT of £300 to business account. The transfer is made that morning. The COFA spots it in the afternoon, before anything has been sent to the executors.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["No breach would occur if a bill for the same amount is sent to the executors tomorrow.","No breach has occurred, because the money was held on account of costs on this matter.","A breach has occurred; the £1,800 must go back to client account at once and be recorded.","A breach has occurred, but it will be cured if the executors later approve the bill.","A breach has occurred, and it must be reported to the SRA as a serious breach."],"a":2,"e":"Rule 4.3 requires a **bill or other written notification of costs to be given before** client money is transferred to pay costs, so the transfer was a breach. Under r.6.1 it must be **corrected promptly on discovery** by returning the money to client account, and recorded; the bill can then be sent and the transfer made properly. Sending or approving the bill later does not cure the breach, and a one-off mistake corrected the same day is unlikely to be a serious breach that must be reported."},{"t":"separation-mixed-payments","p":"FLK2","q":"Castell Solicitors acted for Mr Owain Pritchard on his remortgage. Two weeks ago it delivered a bill for £900 plus VAT of £180. Mr Pritchard now sends a single bank transfer of £1,230: £1,080 to pay the bill and £150 for a Land Registry fee that the firm has not yet paid or billed.\n\nWhich of the following is the correct way for the firm to deal with the £1,230?","o":["Pay it into client account and promptly transfer £1,080 to business account.","Pay it into business account and promptly transfer £150 to client account.","Pay it into client account and leave it there until the Land Registry fee is paid.","Pay £1,080 into business account and return the £150 to Mr Pritchard.","Pay it into business account, because most of the payment is the firm's money."],"a":0,"e":"This is a **mixed payment**: £1,080 pays a bill already delivered (firm money) and £150 is for an unbilled, unpaid disbursement (client money under r.2.1(d)). Under r.4.2 the firm must **allocate it promptly**, either by paying it all into client account and promptly transferring the firm's share, or by splitting it on receipt. Paying it into business account puts client money there (a breach of r.4.1), and leaving the firm's £1,080 in client account also breaches the separation rule."},{"t":"double-entry-basics","p":"FLK2","q":"Linton Ward Solicitors drafted a will for Mr Haruto Sato and delivered a bill for profit costs of £600 plus VAT of £120, which was entered in the firm's books when it was sent. The firm held no money for Mr Sato. Mr Sato now pays £720 by bank transfer into the firm's business account.\n\nWhich of the following entries should be made to record the receipt?","o":["DR client cash account; CR Mr Sato's ledger, client column.","DR Mr Sato's ledger, business column; CR business cash account.","DR business cash account; CR profit costs account.","DR client cash account; CR Mr Sato's ledger, business column.","DR business cash account; CR Mr Sato's ledger, business column."],"a":4,"e":"Money paying a bill already delivered is **firm money**, and money received is recorded as **DR cash, CR ledger** in the same set of books: **DR business cash; CR client ledger business column**. Profit costs were already credited when the bill was delivered, so crediting them again would double count. Any entry using the client cash account or client column is wrong, because no client money is involved."},{"t":"double-entry-basics","p":"FLK2","q":"Redbank Solicitors acts for Ms Adaeze Nwankwo on her house purchase. On 2 March it receives £3,000 from her on account of costs and disbursements. On 5 March it pays a £150 Land Registry search fee (no VAT) from client account. On 9 March it pays a surveyor's invoice of £400 plus VAT of £80, addressed to the firm, from business account using the principal method. On 20 March it delivers a bill for profit costs of £1,000 plus the surveyor's fee, with VAT at 20%. No other entries have been made.\n\nWhat are the balances on Ms Nwankwo's client ledger after the bill has been entered?","o":["Business column £1,760 DR; client column £2,850 CR.","Business column £1,680 DR; client column £2,850 CR.","Business column £1,600 DR; client column £2,850 CR.","Business column £1,680 DR; client column £1,170 CR.","Business column £1,680 DR; client column £3,000 CR."],"a":1,"e":"Under the **principal method** the ledger business column is debited with the **net** fee (£400) and the £80 input VAT goes to HMRC; on the bill the firm charges **output VAT on fees plus the surveyor's fee** (20% of £1,400 = £280), so the bill debit is £1,280 and the business balance is £1,680 DR. The client column is £3,000 less £150 = £2,850 CR, and the two columns are never netted off against each other. £1,760 wrongly debits the gross surveyor's fee, and £1,600 charges VAT on the fees alone."},{"t":"bills-vat-disbursements","p":"FLK2","q":"Tennant Rowe LLP acts for the executors of the late Mr Florian Weiss. It delivered a bill for profit costs of £2,000 plus VAT of £400. The executors challenged the time spent, and the firm agrees to reduce its profit costs by £500, issuing a VAT credit note.\n\nWhich of the following entries records the reduction?","o":["DR Mr Weiss's estate ledger, business column £600; CR profit costs £500; CR HMRC £100.","DR bad debts £500; DR HMRC £100; CR Mr Weiss's estate ledger, business column £600.","DR profit costs £600; CR Mr Weiss's estate ledger, client column £600.","DR profit costs £500; DR HMRC £100; CR Mr Weiss's estate ledger, business column £600.","DR profit costs £500; CR Mr Weiss's estate ledger, business column £500."],"a":3,"e":"An **abatement** reverses the bill entries for the amount of the reduction: **DR profit costs** with the fee reduction, **DR HMRC** with the VAT on it, and **CR the ledger business column** with the total, so the estate owes £600 less. Debiting bad debts is for writing off an unpaid bill, not reducing one. The VAT must be adjusted too, and the client column is not involved because no client money moves."},{"t":"bills-vat-disbursements","p":"FLK2","q":"Ashworth Solicitors acts for the executors of the late Mrs Lucia Romano. It placed statutory advertisements for creditors and paid the invoice of £250 plus VAT of £50, which was addressed to the firm, from its business account using the principal method. It now delivers a bill to the executors for profit costs of £3,000 and the advertisement fee, with VAT at 20%.\n\nWhich of the following entries should be made when the bill is delivered?","o":["DR estate ledger, business column £3,900; CR profit costs £3,250; CR HMRC £650.","DR estate ledger, business column £3,600; CR profit costs £3,000; CR HMRC £600.","DR estate ledger, business column £3,700; CR profit costs £3,000; CR HMRC £700.","DR estate ledger, business column £3,650; CR profit costs £3,000; CR HMRC £650.","DR estate ledger, client column £3,650; CR profit costs £3,000; CR HMRC £650."],"a":3,"e":"Under the **principal method** the disbursement becomes part of the firm's own supply, so **output VAT is charged on fees plus the disbursement** (20% of £3,250 = £650), but only the £3,000 fees are credited to profit costs because the £250 was already debited to the ledger when paid. The firm reclaimed the original £50 input VAT, so adding it again (£700) double counts. Charging VAT on the fees alone (£600) ignores the principal method, and bills are always entered in the business column."},{"t":"interest-breaches","p":"FLK2","q":"Kingsley Moor LLP held £35,000 for the executors of the late Mr Eamon Gallagher, due as a legacy to his niece. Shortly before payment, a fraudster intercepted the firm's emails and sent false bank details, and the firm transferred the £35,000 to the fraudster's account. The niece has received nothing. The firm has notified its bank and its insurer.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the firm must do about the missing money?","o":["Replace the £35,000 in client account immediately from its own money.","Replace nothing until its insurer has decided whether to meet the claim.","Replace the money only if a member of staff is found to have been at fault.","Ask the executors to authorise a second payment of the legacy from the estate.","Wait for the outcome of the bank's investigation before taking any further step."],"a":0,"e":"Under r.6.1 money **improperly withdrawn** from client account must be **replaced immediately** from the firm's own funds, **whether or not anyone was at fault**, and the firm then pursues the bank, the fraudster or its insurer. Waiting for the insurer or the bank leaves the estate short in the meantime. Asking the executors to pay again would make the estate bear a loss caused by the firm's withdrawal."},{"t":"interest-breaches","p":"FLK2","q":"Fenwick Solicitors acts for Mr Gareth Llewellyn on the sale of his house. The buyer paid a deposit of £45,000 on exchange, which the firm has held as stakeholder in its general client account for three months. The contract is then rescinded by agreement on terms that the whole deposit is returned to the buyer. The firm has no written agreement with anyone about interest.\n\nTo whom must the firm account for a fair sum of interest on the deposit?","o":["The seller, because the deposit was paid under the seller's contract of sale.","The firm itself, because the deposit was held as stakeholder and not for its own client.","The buyer, because the interest follows the deposit to the person entitled to it.","Nobody, because interest is never payable on money that is held as stakeholder.","The seller and the buyer equally, because the deposit was held for both of them."],"a":2,"e":"Rule 7.1 requires a **fair sum of interest** to be paid to the **client or third party for whom the money is held**, and interest on a stakeholder deposit **goes with the deposit** to whoever becomes entitled to it. Normally that is the seller on completion, but here the deposit is being returned, so the buyer is entitled. A stakeholder deposit is third-party client money (r.2.1(b)), so interest is payable on it and the firm cannot keep it without a written agreement."},{"t":"records-reconciliation","p":"FLK2","q":"The cashier at Linden Grove Solicitors prepares the client account reconciliation at the end of a five-week period. The client bank statement shows £126,300. A cheque for £8,450 sent to a beneficiary has not yet been presented, and £2,150 received on the last afternoon has not yet appeared on the statement. The client cash book shows £120,000, and the total of the client ledger balances is £121,250.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the result of the reconciliation?","o":["The three figures agree, so no further action is needed.","There is a surplus of £1,250 to be transferred to business account.","There is a shortfall of £6,300 to be replaced from business account.","There is a shortfall of £1,250 that may wait until the next reconciliation.","There is a £1,250 shortfall to investigate and, if confirmed, replace at once."],"a":4,"e":"The adjusted bank balance is £126,300 less the **unpresented cheque** (£8,450) plus the **outstanding lodgement** (£2,150) = £120,000, matching the cash book, but the firm owes clients £121,250, so there is a **£1,250 shortfall**. Rule 8.3 requires differences to be **promptly investigated and resolved**, and any missing client money must be **replaced immediately** under r.6.1. The £6,300 figure compares the unadjusted bank balance with the cash book, and a shortfall is never left until the next reconciliation."},{"t":"records-reconciliation","p":"FLK2","q":"Kestrel Law is moving to paperless working. To save costs, the managing partner proposes two changes: first, to obtain statements for the firm's business account only once a quarter (client account statements will still be obtained monthly); second, to shred all accounting records more than five years old.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the proposals?","o":["Both comply, because the rule on statements applies only to client accounts.","Neither complies: statements are due every five weeks and records kept six years.","The first complies, but accounting records must be kept for at least six years.","The second complies, but business account statements are needed every five weeks.","Both comply, provided the client account is reconciled at least every five weeks."],"a":1,"e":"Rule 8.2 requires statements **at least every five weeks** for **all client and business accounts**, so quarterly business statements are not enough. Rule 13.1 requires all accounting records to be **stored securely for at least six years**, so shredding records after five years also breaches the rules. Only the reconciliation duty (r.8.3) is limited to client accounts, which is the trap in the other options."},{"t":"joint-own-tpma","p":"FLK2","q":"The Court of Protection has appointed Priya Raman, a partner at Elmstead Solicitors, as property and affairs deputy for Mrs Odile Achterberg, aged 91. Through the firm, Priya now has online banking authority over the current account held in Mrs Achterberg's sole name, and uses it each month to settle utility bills and pay a live-in carer's wages. Mrs Achterberg's savings are never transferred to Elmstead.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's obligations under the SRA Accounts Rules in relation to that current account?","o":["The money must be moved into the firm's client account, as a partner holds it as deputy.","The firm must obtain statements and reconcile the account at least every five weeks.","The firm need only obtain statements every five weeks; no reconciliation is required.","None of the rules apply, because the account is in Mrs Achterberg's sole name.","All of the rules apply, exactly as if the account were one of the firm's client accounts."],"a":1,"e":"Where a solicitor, **in the course of practice, operates a client's own account as signatory** (the classic case is a deputy or attorney), r.10.1 disapplies Part 2 except **statements every five weeks (r.8.2), reconciliation every five weeks (r.8.3)** and the record of bills (r.8.4). There is no need to move the money into client account, so the full rules do not apply, but nor are the rules switched off entirely. Statements without reconciliation is the regime for **joint accounts** (r.9.1), not for a client's own account."},{"t":"accountants-reports","p":"FLK2","q":"Pemberton Ashe is a small firm doing mainly wills work. Its bank statements show that, over the year, its client account had an average balance of £6,200 and peaked at £232,000 on the day a single estate's house sale proceeds arrived. For three weeks of the year, including the day of that peak, it also held a £20,000 retention, in a joint deposit account in the names of Pemberton Ashe and the other side's solicitors, which brought that account's average for the year to about £1,200. None of its client money came from the Legal Aid Agency.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm must obtain an accountant's report for the year?","o":["It is exempt, because its client account balances are within both limits.","It is exempt, because the joint account was held for only part of the year.","It is not exempt, because the combined highest balance exceeds £250,000.","It is not exempt, because the combined average balance exceeds £10,000.","It is not exempt, because operating any joint account requires a report."],"a":2,"e":"The low-balance exemption applies only if the statement balance does not exceed **an average of £10,000 and a maximum of £250,000** (r.12.2(b)), and that balance **includes any joint accounts and clients' own accounts operated by the firm** (r.12.3). Adding the £20,000 joint account to the £232,000 peak gives £252,000, so the firm fails the maximum limit even though its combined average (about £7,400) is well within £10,000. Operating a joint account brings the firm within r.12.1, but the exemption could still have applied had both limits been met."},{"t":"accountants-reports","p":"FLK2","q":"Hollins Carter LLP's accounting period ended on 30 April 2026. Its accountant's report is qualified because one client account reconciliation was signed off a few days late. The accountant states that no money belonging to clients or third parties is, has been or is likely to be placed at risk.\n\nUnder the SRA Accounts Rules as in force on 11 September 2026, which of the following best describes the firm's obligations?","o":["It must obtain the report by 31 October 2026; it need not send it to the SRA.","It must obtain the report by 31 October 2026 and send it to the SRA by that date.","It must obtain the report by 31 July 2026; it need not send it to the SRA.","It must obtain the report by 30 April 2027 and send it to the SRA by that date.","It must obtain the report by 31 October 2026 and send it to the SRA within 28 days."],"a":0,"e":"A firm must **obtain** its accountant's report **within six months of the end of the accounting period** (r.12.1(a)), here by 31 October 2026. It must **deliver** it to the SRA only if it is **qualified to show that client or third-party money is, has been or is likely to be at risk** (r.12.1(b)), which is not the case here. The requirement for every report to be submitted was approved for the future (from April 2027) and was not in force at the assessment date."},{"t":"conveyancing-accounts","p":"FLK2","q":"Sefton Barr Solicitors acts for Mr Callum Reid and his lender on his purchase, which completes tomorrow. The firm holds the £240,000 mortgage advance as cleared funds. Today Mr Reid hands in a personal cheque for the £60,000 balance needed. The firm sends the full completion money tomorrow before the cheque has cleared, and the cheque is later dishonoured.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["No breach has occurred, because the firm acted in good faith when it paid.","No breach has occurred, because the client account as a whole held enough money.","A breach has occurred, but the firm may wait until Mr Reid repays before correcting it.","A breach has occurred, and the lender must make good the shortfall from its advance.","A breach has occurred, and the firm must replace the shortfall at once from its own money."],"a":4,"e":"Once the cheque is dishonoured, more was paid out than was held for Mr Reid, so his client ledger is overdrawn and other clients' money has been used, a breach of **r.5.3 (sufficient funds held for that client)**. Under r.6.1 the firm must **replace the shortfall immediately from its own money** and then recover it from Mr Reid. Good faith, and the overall balance of the client account, are irrelevant; the safe course is to wait for **cleared funds** before completion."},{"t":"conveyancing-accounts","p":"FLK2","q":"Ingram & Sole acts for Mr Desmond Achebe on the sale of his house for £420,000, which has just completed. Before completion it delivered a bill for £1,750 plus VAT of £350, and it gave an undertaking to Mr Achebe's bank to repay his bridging loan and accrued interest, now £60,900 in total, from the net sale proceeds. It has redeemed his £150,000 mortgage. Mr Achebe has no related purchase and asks the firm to send him everything, saying he will repay the bank himself.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the firm should do with the remaining money?","o":["Send £267,900 to Mr Achebe, who has agreed to repay the bank himself.","Pay £60,000 to the bank and send £207,900 to Mr Achebe promptly.","Pay £60,900 to the bank and send £207,000 to Mr Achebe promptly.","Pay £60,900 to the bank and hold £207,000 until Mr Achebe next instructs it.","Send £207,000 to Mr Achebe and hold £60,900 until the bank asks for it."],"a":2,"e":"After redeeming the mortgage, £270,000 remains; the firm must honour its **undertaking** by repaying the bridging loan **with interest** (£60,900), may transfer its billed costs of £2,100 (r.4.3), and must then **return the balance of £207,000 promptly** (r.2.5). Following the client's instruction to send everything would break the undertaking, and the undertaking covers the accrued interest as well as the capital, so paying £60,000 would not discharge it. Holding any money with no reason to do so risks using client account as a banking facility."},{"t":"probate-accounts","p":"FLK2","q":"Thornbury Solicitors acts for the executors of the late Mrs Gwendolyn Price and holds £230,000 for the estate. The will leaves the residue to Mrs Price's daughter, Mrs Halima Price-Rahman, who is not an executor. Mrs Price-Rahman telephones and asks the firm to pay her £20,000 now as an interim distribution, sending it to her son's bank account. The executors have given no instructions about distributions.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm may make the payment?","o":["It may not make the payment without the executors' instructions, as they are the clients.","It may make the payment, because the beneficiary is entitled to the residue of the estate.","It may make the payment, provided it is within the beneficiary's share of the residue.","It may make the payment if the beneficiary confirms her request to the firm in writing.","It may not make the payment, because interim distributions are not allowed by the rules."],"a":0,"e":"The **personal representatives are the client**, not the beneficiaries, so estate money may be withdrawn only for the **purpose for which it is held or on the executors' instructions** (r.5.1). A beneficiary's entitlement to residue, even confirmed in writing, does not authorise the firm to pay her before the executors decide to distribute. Interim distributions are allowed, keeping a retention for outstanding liabilities, but only when the executors instruct them."},{"t":"probate-accounts","p":"FLK2","q":"Belmont Lane LLP acts for the executors of the late Mr Aurelio Santos. It receives £95,000 from the deceased's bank and £210,000 from the sale of his house. It pays a valuer's invoice of £400 plus VAT of £80, addressed to the executors, from client account. It delivers a bill for profit costs of £3,500 plus VAT of £700 and transfers that sum from client account. It pays two pecuniary legacies of £15,000 and £5,000. It then credits the estate with £900 interest under its interest policy, transferred into client account. The residue is left equally to Mr Santos's two children.\n\nHow much should each child receive?","o":["£140,160.","£140,850.","£142,710.","£140,610.","£140,510."],"a":3,"e":"Receipts of £305,000, less the valuer's invoice paid **gross** under the **agency method** (£480), the billed costs and VAT transferred after the bill (£4,200) and the legacies (£20,000), plus the **fair sum of interest** credited to the estate (£900), leave £281,220, so each child receives £140,610. Leaving out the interest gives £140,160, and forgetting the VAT on costs while ignoring the interest gives £140,510. An agency disbursement is paid gross because the supply was to the executors, so its VAT is not reclaimed by the firm."}],"accounts-p2":[{"t":"framework-client-money","p":"FLK2","q":"Ms Folasade Adebayo, a partner at Wexcombe Solicitors, is the sole executor of the late Mr Desmond Clarke. No estate bank account has been opened. Ms Adebayo receives a cheque for £48,000 from a life insurer, payable to her as executor. She suggests paying it into the firm's business account, since the firm will be billing the estate for its work in due course.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the £48,000 must be dealt with?","o":["It is client money held as executor and must be paid promptly into client account.","It is the firm's money and may be paid into business account, as costs will be billed.","It is not client money, because the cheque is payable to the partner and not to the firm.","It is client money, but may stay in business account until the bill is delivered.","It is the executor's personal money and falls outside the SRA Accounts Rules altogether."],"a":0,"e":"Money held by a solicitor as **trustee or holder of an office such as executor is client money (r.2.1(c))** and must be paid **promptly into client account (r.2.3)**. It cannot go into business account because no bill has been delivered, and even then only the billed sum would become the firm's money. The fact that the cheque names the partner rather than the firm does not take it outside the rules; the rules would apply differently only if it stayed in an estate account in the executor's name that she operated (r.10)."},{"t":"framework-client-money","p":"FLK2","q":"Hartfield Bray LLP completed the sale of Mr Jonah Whitlock's flat two years ago. £210 remains on his ledger. Letters and emails to his last known addresses have gone unanswered, and the firm paid a tracing agent £45 from its business account, without success. The cashier proposes to transfer £45 to business account to cover the tracing fee and pay the remaining £165 to a registered charity.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the firm may deal with the balance?","o":["It may carry out the proposal, as the tracing fee was a proper cost of returning the money.","It must apply to the SRA for authorisation before paying any of the balance to charity.","It may keep the whole £210, as the client cannot be found after reasonable steps.","It may pay the whole £210 to charity, without deducting the tracing fee, keeping records.","It must keep the £210 in client account until six years have passed since completion."],"a":3,"e":"Under the SRA's **prescribed circumstances**, a residual balance of **£500 or less** may be withdrawn and **paid to charity without SRA authorisation**, provided reasonable steps have been taken to return it, records are kept for at least six years, and **no deduction is made for the firm's own costs**, such as tracing fees. So the cashier's plan to take £45 first is not allowed. SRA authorisation (r.5.1(c)) is needed only above £500, and the firm may never simply keep the money."},{"t":"framework-client-money","p":"FLK2","q":"Pellow & Grant acts for the executors of the late Mrs Ruth Abernethy and holds £180,000 of estate money. The estate cannot be distributed for at least six months because of a dispute about a legacy. The executors ask the firm to place the money in a separate designated client deposit account that requires 95 days' notice of withdrawal, because it pays a much better rate of interest.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm may do so?","o":["It may not, because client money must always be available to the client on demand.","It may, provided the arrangement is agreed with the executors in writing.","It may, but only after obtaining the SRA's prior written authorisation.","It may not, because estate money must be kept in the firm's general client account.","It may, provided the executors ask for it orally and the firm notes the request."],"a":1,"e":"Client money must be **available on demand unless an alternative arrangement has been agreed in writing (r.2.4)** with the client, here the executors. A notice account is therefore allowed once the executors agree in writing; an oral request noted on the file is not enough. No SRA authorisation is needed, and a **separate designated client account** is a perfectly proper home for a large estate balance held for a long time."},{"t":"client-account-withdrawals","p":"FLK2","q":"The partners of Okafor Bell LLP, a firm in Manchester that does mainly conveyancing, want to move their general client account to a bank branch in Jersey that offers a higher interest rate. The account would be named \"Okafor Bell LLP Client Account\" and the bank is well regarded.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the proposed account may be used as a client account?","o":["It may, because its name includes both the firm's name and the word \"client\".","It may, provided every client agrees in writing to their money being held there.","It may, but only for money held in separate designated client accounts.","It may, provided the bank is authorised by the Financial Conduct Authority.","It may not, because a client account must be held at a branch in England and Wales."],"a":4,"e":"**Client money may only be held in a client account at a branch (or head office) of a bank or building society in England and Wales (r.3.1).** Jersey is outside England and Wales, so the account cannot be a client account, however it is named. The naming rule (firm's name plus \"client\", r.3.2) is a separate requirement that the account meets but does not cure the location problem, and client consent cannot override r.3.1."},{"t":"client-account-withdrawals","p":"FLK2","q":"Ravensworth Solicitors acts for the administrators of the late Mr Idris Bowen. So far it holds only £300 for the estate, from a small refund. It must pay the probate application fee of £526 today. The estate's bank has confirmed it will release £30,000 next week, and the firm's general client account holds over £2 million for other clients.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the fee may properly be paid?","o":["£526 from client account, as the estate will be in funds within a week.","£526 from client account, if the administrators authorise it in writing.","£300 from client account and £226 from the firm's business account.","£526 from another estate's ledger, to be repaid when the bank releases funds.","£300 from client account, and the remaining £226 next week from estate funds."],"a":2,"e":"Client money may only be withdrawn **if sufficient funds are held for that specific client (r.5.3)**. Paying £526 from client account when only £300 is held for this estate would use £226 of other clients' money and overdraw the ledger, a breach whatever the administrators say and however soon funds will arrive. The firm can split the payment, taking the shortfall from its **business account** (or pay it all from business account); paying only part of a fee due today is not an option."},{"t":"separation-mixed-payments","p":"FLK2","q":"Lowther Keane acts for Ms Anjali Mistry on her house purchase. At the start it gave her a written estimate of its fees of £1,200 plus VAT. A week before completion, and before any bill or other notification of costs has been sent, Ms Mistry transfers £1,440, with a note saying \"to settle your fees as per your estimate\".\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the £1,440 should be treated?","o":["It is client money and must be paid into client account.","It is firm money, because the client describes it as payment of fees.","It is firm money, because the written estimate counts as a notification of costs.","It is a mixed payment, to be split between client and business accounts.","It is firm money, provided the bill is sent to the client within 14 days."],"a":0,"e":"Money received for fees **before a bill or other written notification of costs has been delivered is client money (r.2.1(d))** and goes into client account. The client's label does not change its nature, and an estimate of future fees is not a **notification of costs incurred** for r.4.3 purposes. Once the bill is delivered, the firm may transfer the specific billed sum to business account."},{"t":"separation-mixed-payments","p":"FLK2","q":"Dunmore Fairley holds £6,000 on account for the executors of the late Mr Lionel Shaw. On 3 March it delivered a bill for £2,000 plus VAT of £400. On reviewing the ledger on 12 May, the cashier notices that nothing has been transferred to business account. The partner says it does no harm, as the money is safe in client account.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["There is no breach, as firm money may stay in client account until it is needed.","There is no breach, provided the costs are transferred before the next reconciliation.","There is a breach only if the executors object to the money remaining there.","There is a breach, as the £2,400 became firm money and should have been moved promptly.","There is a breach, as the firm should have waited for the executors' approval of the bill."],"a":3,"e":"Once the bill was delivered, the **£2,400 became the firm's money and had to be transferred promptly to business account**; keeping firm money in client account breaches the **separation rule (r.4.1)**. Leaving it there is not harmless because it creates a cushion that could hide a shortfall in clients' money. Nothing in the rules requires the client to approve a bill before the transfer: r.4.3 only requires the bill to be given first."},{"t":"double-entry-basics","p":"FLK2","q":"Ashbrook Solicitors acts for Mr Tobias Lindqvist on the sale of his house. A statutory declaration is needed, and the firm pays a commissioner's fee of £7 in cash from its petty cash float. It holds £400 of client money for Mr Lindqvist.\n\nWhich of the following entries records the payment?","o":["DR Mr Lindqvist's ledger, client column £7; CR client cash £7.","DR Mr Lindqvist's ledger, business column £7; CR petty cash £7.","DR petty cash £7; CR Mr Lindqvist's ledger, business column £7.","DR Mr Lindqvist's ledger, client column £7; CR petty cash £7.","DR Mr Lindqvist's ledger, business column £7; CR business cash £7."],"a":1,"e":"**Petty cash is the firm's own money**, so a payment from it for a client is recorded as **DR the client ledger business column; CR petty cash account**. Client money is not used, so neither the client column nor client cash is touched, even though the firm holds money for the client. Crediting business cash would be wrong because the money came from the petty cash float, not the business bank account."},{"t":"double-entry-basics","p":"FLK2","q":"Brennan Toft acts for Mrs Carys Morgan on the sale of her house and the purchase of a flat, on two separate ledgers. The sale has completed and the firm holds £92,000 on the sale ledger. On Mrs Morgan's instructions, it transfers £60,000 to the purchase ledger towards the purchase price.\n\nWhich of the following entries records the transfer?","o":["DR client cash £60,000; CR purchase ledger, client column £60,000.","DR sale ledger, client column £60,000; CR client cash £60,000.","DR purchase ledger, client column £60,000; CR sale ledger, client column £60,000.","DR sale ledger, business column £60,000; CR purchase ledger, business column £60,000.","DR sale ledger, client column £60,000; CR purchase ledger, client column £60,000."],"a":4,"e":"An **inter-client transfer** moves money from one ledger to another while it stays in client account, so there is **no cash entry**: **DR the transferring ledger (client column); CR the receiving ledger (client column)**. The sale ledger is debited because money is going out of it, and the purchase ledger credited because it now holds more. Option C reverses the entries, and business columns are wrong because this is client money."},{"t":"bills-vat-disbursements","p":"FLK2","q":"Merriott Lane acts for Mr Sanjay Patel on his house purchase and holds £1,000 from him on account of costs and disbursements. It orders property searches, and the search provider's invoice for £240 plus VAT of £48 is addressed to the firm. The firm treats the searches as part of its own supply to Mr Patel.\n\nWhich of the following entries records payment of the invoice?","o":["DR Mr Patel's ledger, client column £288; CR client cash £288.","DR Mr Patel's ledger, business column £288; CR business cash £288.","DR Mr Patel's ledger, business column £240; DR HMRC £48; CR business cash £288.","DR Mr Patel's ledger, client column £240; DR HMRC £48; CR client cash £288.","DR HMRC £48; CR Mr Patel's ledger, business column £240; CR business cash £288."],"a":2,"e":"An invoice addressed to the firm for a supply the firm uses in its own service is a **principal-method disbursement**: it must be paid from **business account**, the firm **reclaims the input VAT**, and the entries are **DR ledger business column (net); DR HMRC (VAT); CR business cash (gross)**. Property search fees are treated this way. Paying from client account is wrong even though money is held for Mr Patel, because the debt is the firm's own; posting the gross sum to the ledger is the agency treatment."},{"t":"bills-vat-disbursements","p":"FLK2","q":"Kirkby Hale delivered a bill to Thornwell Properties Ltd, a client for whom it acted on the purchase of a commercial unit, for profit costs of £1,500 plus VAT of £300. Nine months later the bill is still unpaid and the company has gone into insolvent liquidation. The firm writes the debt off and claims VAT bad debt relief.\n\nWhich of the following entries records the write-off?","o":["DR bad debts £1,500; DR HMRC £300; CR Thornwell Properties Ltd ledger, business column £1,800.","DR profit costs £1,500; DR HMRC £300; CR Thornwell Properties Ltd ledger, business column £1,800.","DR bad debts £1,800; CR Thornwell Properties Ltd ledger, business column £1,800.","DR Thornwell Properties Ltd ledger, business column £1,800; CR bad debts £1,500; CR HMRC £300.","DR bad debts £1,500; DR HMRC £300; CR Thornwell Properties Ltd ledger, client column £1,800."],"a":0,"e":"Writing off a **bad debt** clears what the client owes, so the **client ledger business column is credited** with the full £1,800; the fees are **debited to a bad debts expense account**, and because **VAT bad debt relief** is claimed (the debt is over six months overdue and written off), the £300 VAT is debited to HMRC. Debiting profit costs is the entry for an **abatement** (an agreed reduction of the bill), not a write-off, and debiting the whole £1,800 to bad debts would be right only if no relief were claimed."},{"t":"interest-breaches","p":"FLK2","q":"Calloway Reece holds £400,000 in its general client account for the executors of the late Mr Arthur Penhaligon for seven months. Its client care letter, which the executors signed and returned, contains one sentence among the standard terms: \"The firm will retain any interest earned on money it holds for you.\" Nothing further was said or explained to the executors about interest.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's obligation regarding interest?","o":["None, because the executors signed the client care letter containing the term.","It must pay the estate every penny of interest that the bank paid on the money.","None, because interest is payable only on separate designated client accounts.","It is likely to owe a fair sum, as the term was not agreed with informed consent.","It must pay a fair sum of interest directly to the beneficiaries of the estate."],"a":3,"e":"A firm must **account for a fair sum of interest (r.7.1)** unless it has made a **written agreement to a different arrangement after giving enough information for informed consent (r.7.2)**. A single unexplained sentence buried in standard terms is unlikely to meet that test for a large sum held for months. The rule requires a fair sum, not all the interest the bank paid, and it is owed to the client, here the executors, not to the beneficiaries directly."},{"t":"interest-breaches","p":"FLK2","q":"Garside Moran acts for Ms Imelda Rourke on her house purchase. After completion it received her cheque for £8,000 for Stamp Duty Land Tax (SDLT), and the next day paid £8,000 to HMRC from client account. Her cheque has now been dishonoured, and her ledger shows a debit balance of £8,000 on the client column. Ms Rourke says she will send new funds within a fortnight.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the firm must do?","o":["Wait for Ms Rourke's new funds, which will correct the ledger within a fortnight.","Transfer £8,000 from its business account to client account immediately.","Ask HMRC to return the £8,000 until Ms Rourke's new funds have cleared.","Note the debit balance and correct it at the next client account reconciliation.","Report the matter to the SRA and await its instructions before taking any step."],"a":1,"e":"The dishonoured cheque means £8,000 of **other clients' money** paid the SDLT, a breach of the sufficient funds rule (r.5.3). The firm must **correct breaches promptly on discovery and immediately replace money improperly withdrawn (r.6.1)**, using its own money from business account; it then recovers the sum from Ms Rourke. Waiting for the client, HMRC or the next reconciliation leaves other clients' money unprotected, and the duty to replace does not depend on any SRA instruction."},{"t":"records-reconciliation","p":"FLK2","q":"At Holloway Pike Solicitors the cashier reconciles the client bank account every four weeks, comparing the bank statement, the cash book and the total of client ledger balances. Each reconciliation is then checked and signed off by the practice manager, an experienced bookkeeper who is employed by the firm but is neither a partner nor its COFA (Compliance Officer for Finance and Administration).\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm complies with the reconciliation rule?","o":["It complies, because the reconciliation is done at least every five weeks.","It complies, provided the practice manager is suitably qualified in bookkeeping.","It does not comply, because reconciliations must be carried out every calendar month.","It does not comply, because the reconciliation must also include the business account.","It does not comply, because the reconciliation must be signed off by the COFA or a manager."],"a":4,"e":"A **three-way reconciliation of all client accounts is needed at least every five weeks and must be signed off by the COFA or a manager (r.8.3)**. A **manager** in SRA terms means a partner, LLP member or director, not a practice or office manager, however experienced. The four-week frequency is fine, and the reconciliation duty does not extend to business accounts (only statements are needed for those)."},{"t":"records-reconciliation","p":"FLK2","q":"While preparing the five-weekly client account reconciliation, the cashier at Ferrars Quinn finds that the adjusted bank balance is £850 less than both the cash book and the total of client ledger balances. Investigation shows that the bank wrongly took a £850 repayment on the firm's own business loan from the general client account instead of the business account. The bank says it will look into the error within 30 days.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the firm should do?","o":["Wait for the bank to reverse the debit, since the error was the bank's and not the firm's.","Record the difference as an unexplained item and carry it forward to the next reconciliation.","Pay £850 into client account from its own money now, and pursue the bank for the sum.","Deduct the £850 proportionately from the client ledgers until the bank corrects its error.","Ask the partners to approve the reconciliation as it stands, noting the bank's error."],"a":2,"e":"The firm must **promptly investigate and resolve any differences** shown by a reconciliation (r.8.3) and **immediately replace any money improperly withdrawn from client account (r.6.1)**, whether or not the firm was at fault. Here client money has paid the firm's own debt, so the firm must pay £850 into client account from its own funds now and recover it from the bank afterwards. Carrying the difference forward or waiting 30 days leaves clients' money short, and the client ledgers must never be reduced to hide a shortfall."},{"t":"joint-own-tpma","p":"FLK2","q":"Stannard Weir acts for Mr Gideon Asante on the sale of his house. At completion, £15,000 of the price is placed in a joint deposit account in the names of Stannard Weir and the buyer's solicitors, as a retention until Mr Asante finishes some roofing works. The money will not pass through Stannard Weir's client account.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Stannard Weir's obligations under the SRA Accounts Rules in relation to the joint account?","o":["It must apply all the client money rules, as if the money were in its client account.","It must obtain statements at least every five weeks and keep a record of its bills.","It must obtain statements and carry out a reconciliation at least every five weeks.","It has no obligations, because the money is held jointly with another firm.","It must transfer the money to its own client account until the works are finished."],"a":1,"e":"Where a firm **holds money jointly with a client or third party (r.9)**, Part 2 of the rules is switched off **except obtaining statements at least every five weeks (r.8.2) and keeping the central record of bills (r.8.4)**. No reconciliation under r.8.3 is required, unlike where a solicitor operates a client's own account (r.10). The money need not be moved into client account, but the rules still apply, and the joint account also counts towards the duty to obtain an accountant's report."},{"t":"joint-own-tpma","p":"FLK2","q":"Larkspur Conveyancing is a new firm that wants to avoid holding client money. It plans to route all purchase money, disbursements and its own fees through a third party managed account (TPMA) run by an FCA-regulated payment service provider, so that the firm itself never receives or holds clients' money.\n\nWhich of the following must the firm do before accepting instructions from each client?","o":["Obtain the SRA's written approval of the TPMA arrangement it intends to use.","Obtain the client's written consent to every payment the provider will make.","Arrange for the TPMA to be reconciled every five weeks and signed off by a manager.","Obtain an accountant's report confirming the TPMA meets the SRA's requirements.","Ensure the client understands who pays the TPMA's fees and their rights under it."],"a":4,"e":"A firm may use a **TPMA only if it does not result in the firm holding client money and, before accepting instructions, it takes reasonable steps to ensure the client is informed of and understands the terms, especially how the TPMA's fees are paid and who bears them, and the client's right to terminate and to dispute the firm's payment requests (r.11.1)**. The firm must also obtain regular statements from the provider (r.11.2). No SRA approval or accountant's report is required, and r.8.3 reconciliation does not apply to a TPMA."},{"t":"accountants-reports","p":"FLK2","q":"Elmore Brook is a small wills and probate firm. During its last accounting period its client account statements showed an average balance of £7,500 and a highest balance of £140,000. One of its partners is a Court of Protection deputy and, as signatory, operates the bank account of the person she acts for, which held an average of £4,000 and never more than £20,000 in the same period. The firm holds no other money.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm needs an accountant's report?","o":["No, because its client account balances are within both of the exemption limits.","No, because money in the deputy's account is not client money held by the firm.","Yes, because the combined highest balance takes it over the £250,000 limit.","Yes, because once the deputy account is included the average exceeds £10,000.","No, provided the deputy account is reconciled at least every five weeks."],"a":3,"e":"The low-balance exemption applies only if the statement balance **does not exceed an average of £10,000 and a maximum of £250,000 (r.12.2(b))**, and that balance **includes any clients' own accounts and joint accounts the firm operates (r.12.3)**. Adding the deputy account gives an average of about £11,500, so the firm is not exempt and must obtain a report within six months of the period end. The combined maximum (at most £160,000) is within the £250,000 limit, so it is the average that defeats the exemption."},{"t":"conveyancing-accounts","p":"FLK2","q":"Cardew Hollis acts for Mrs Eleanor Fitch on the sale of her house for £420,000. Contracts were exchanged under the Standard Conditions of Sale and the buyer paid a deposit of £42,000, which the firm holds as stakeholder; Mrs Fitch is not buying another home. Completion is in four weeks. Mrs Fitch gives the firm written instructions to pay £10,000 of the deposit to her removal company now.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the firm should do?","o":["Refuse, because the deposit is held for both parties and cannot be released yet.","Make the payment, because Mrs Fitch has given her instructions in writing.","Make the payment, because the deposit will belong to Mrs Fitch on completion.","Make the payment, provided it keeps at least half of the deposit until completion.","Refuse, because a deposit held as stakeholder may only be paid to the buyer."],"a":0,"e":"A deposit held **as stakeholder is held for both buyer and seller until completion** (third-party money under r.2.1(b)), so the seller **alone cannot authorise its release**, even in writing. Only if it were held as agent for the seller, or used for a related purchase under the Standard Conditions, could Mrs Fitch's instructions suffice. On completion it will normally be paid to the seller with interest, so it is not true that it can only ever go to the buyer."},{"t":"conveyancing-accounts","p":"FLK2","q":"Tremayne Gould acts for Mr Kofi Boateng on the purchase of a new home and the sale of his current one. His sale will not complete until after exchange on the purchase, so his bank lends him £35,000 as a bridging loan to fund the deposit. The bank sends the £35,000 to the firm, which will pay it to the seller's solicitors on exchange next week.\n\nWhich of the following entries records receipt of the bridging loan?","o":["DR Mr Boateng's ledger, client column £35,000; CR client cash £35,000.","DR business cash £35,000; CR Mr Boateng's ledger, business column £35,000.","DR client cash £35,000; CR Mr Boateng's ledger, client column £35,000.","DR client cash £35,000; CR bridging loan account £35,000.","No entries, because the loan is a liability of the client and not of the firm."],"a":2,"e":"A **bridging loan paid to the firm for the client is client money**, so its receipt is recorded as **DR client cash; CR client ledger, client column**, and it will later be paid out as the deposit. Option A is the entry for a payment out, not a receipt, and business account entries are wrong because this is not the firm's money. The loan is the client's liability, but money the firm actually receives must still be recorded on the client's ledger; there is no separate loan account in the firm's books."},{"t":"probate-accounts","p":"FLK2","q":"Ainsley Rhodes acts for the executors of the late Mrs Philippa Stowe. To pay inheritance tax before the grant, the executors borrow £64,000 from a bank, which sends the loan to the firm; the firm pays it straight on to HMRC. After the grant, the firm receives £310,000 from the sale of Mrs Stowe's house and, on the executors' instructions, repays the loan of £64,000.\n\nWhich of the following entries records the repayment of the loan?","o":["DR client cash £64,000; CR estate ledger, client column £64,000.","DR estate ledger, business column £64,000; CR business cash £64,000.","DR HMRC £64,000; CR estate ledger, client column £64,000.","DR estate ledger, client column £64,000; CR business cash £64,000.","DR estate ledger, client column £64,000; CR client cash £64,000."],"a":4,"e":"The loan was paid to the firm for the estate, so it was **client money**, and its repayment from the house proceeds is a **payment out of client account for the estate**: **DR estate ledger, client column; CR client cash**. The estate holds ample funds, so there is no reason to use business account. Option A is the entry for receiving money, not paying it out, and a client column entry must always be paired with client cash, not business cash."},{"t":"probate-accounts","p":"FLK2","q":"Vance Okoro LLP acts for the executors of the late Mr Clement Hardy. The estate accounts have been approved and the firm holds £120,000, the whole residue due to Mr Hardy's niece, Ms Rosalind Hardy. She asks the firm to keep the money in client account for about a year while she decides how to invest it, and in the meantime to pay her council tax and car insurance from it. The executors are happy with this.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the firm should do?","o":["Agree, provided the executors confirm the arrangement in writing.","Decline and pay the residue out promptly, as there is no longer a reason to hold it.","Agree, provided it accounts to Ms Hardy for a fair sum of interest on the money.","Agree to hold the money, but refuse to make the bill payments from it.","Agree, provided the money is moved to a separate designated client account."],"a":1,"e":"Once the estate accounts are approved there is no longer any proper reason to hold the residue, so it must be **returned promptly (r.2.5)**; holding it for a year and paying the beneficiary's household bills would use client account as a **banking facility (r.3.3)**, because those payments are not part of any legal service the firm is delivering. Neither the executors' consent nor paying interest cures this, and moving the money to a designated account does not change its purpose. Holding the money without making the payments is still a breach of r.2.5."}],"accounts-p3":[{"t":"framework-client-money","p":"FLK2","q":"Hadley Crane Solicitors acts for the executors of the late Mr Tomasz Nowicki. Nothing was yet held for the estate, so the firm paid the £526 probate application fee from its business account. Before any bill or other notification of costs has been sent, one of the executors, Mrs Halina Nowicka, transfers £526 to the firm, saying it is to reimburse the probate fee.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the firm should deal with the £526?","o":["Pay it into client account, because no bill has yet been delivered to the executors.","Pay it into business account, because it reimburses a payment the firm has already made.","Pay it into client account, then transfer it to business account once a bill is sent.","Pay it into business account, but only if the executors agree to this in writing.","Pay it into client account, because money received for an estate is always client money."],"a":1,"e":"Money received to **reimburse the firm for a disbursement it has already paid out of its business account** is firm money, so it goes into business account even though no bill has been sent. Rule 2.1(d) treats money for fees and **unpaid** disbursements as client money until a bill is delivered, so the options that send the £526 to client account to await a bill misapply it: this disbursement has already been paid. No written agreement with the executors is needed, and money is not client money simply because it relates to an estate when it is the firm that is out of pocket."},{"t":"framework-client-money","p":"FLK2","q":"Kearsley Doyle LLP, a probate practice, wants to cut its bank charges. Its office manager proposes that all cheques received for clients be kept in the office safe and paid into client account in one batch every second Friday, saying that the Accounts Rules allow up to 14 days. On a Monday the firm receives a cheque for £18,400 from a building society, closing an account of the late Mr Benedict Oyelaran, for whose executors the firm acts.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the proposed practice complies with the SRA Accounts Rules?","o":["It complies, because the rules allow client money to be banked within 14 days of receipt.","It complies, provided each cheque is entered on the client's ledger on the day it arrives.","It does not comply, because client money must be banked on the same day it is received.","It is unlikely to comply, because client money must be paid into client account promptly.","It does not comply, because client money must be banked by the next working day after receipt."],"a":3,"e":"Rule 2.3 requires client money to be paid into a client account **promptly**, which is not defined but means as soon as reasonably possible; routinely holding cheques for up to two weeks to save bank charges is unlikely to meet that. There is no 14-day allowance (that figure comes from the old rule on mixed payments), and the old 2011 \"same or next working day\" test no longer applies, nor is there a same-day rule. Recording the cheque on the ledger does not satisfy the separate duty to bank it."},{"t":"framework-client-money","p":"FLK2","q":"Ashgrove Solicitors acts for Ms Ngozi Eze on the sale of her house for £310,000 under the Standard Conditions of Sale. Ms Eze is not buying another home. On exchange of contracts, the buyer's solicitors send the £31,000 deposit, to be held by Ashgrove Solicitors as stakeholder. A newly recruited cashier suggests that, because the buyer is not the firm's client, the deposit falls outside the Accounts Rules and can be kept in the business account until completion.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the status of the £31,000?","o":["It is client money held for a third party and must be paid into client account.","It is the seller's money and must be credited straight away to Ms Eze's ledger.","It falls outside the rules until completion, when it becomes the seller's money.","It is client money, but may be paid to Ms Eze now because she is the firm's client.","It becomes client money only once Ms Eze authorises the firm to hold it for her."],"a":0,"e":"A deposit held **as stakeholder** is held for both buyer and seller until completion, so it is client money under **r.2.1(b)** (money held on behalf of a third party in connection with the firm's regulated services) and must be paid promptly into client account, normally credited to a stakeholder ledger. The fact that the buyer is not the firm's client does not take the money outside the rules. It is not yet the seller's money, so it can be neither credited to her ledger as hers nor released to her before completion, and its status does not depend on her authority."},{"t":"client-account-withdrawals","p":"FLK2","q":"Penhallow & Reed LLP acts for the executors of several large estates and wants a separate instant-access account for estate money. It opens an account at the London branch of a high street bank, to be named \"Penhallow & Reed LLP Estates Deposit Account\". Each estate will have its own ledger in the firm's books.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the account may be used to hold the estate money?","o":["It may, because it is held at a bank branch in England and Wales.","It may, provided each estate ledger shows how much belongs to that estate.","It may not, because estate money must be held in a general client account.","It may, provided the executors of each estate agree to its name in writing.","It may not, because the account name must include the word \"client\"."],"a":4,"e":"Under **r.3.2** the name of a client account must include **the name of the firm and the word \"client\"**, so this account, as named, cannot be used for client money. Being at an England and Wales branch satisfies r.3.1 only, and neither accurate ledgers nor the executors' agreement cures a non-compliant name. Estate money can properly be held in a separate designated client account, so there is no requirement to use a general client account."},{"t":"client-account-withdrawals","p":"FLK2","q":"Linfield Morrow acts for Mr Arjun Malhotra, a client of many years, on the purchase of a flat. He asks the firm to receive £20,000 from his brother's UK bank account and pay it straight on to a builder who is renovating Mr Malhotra's holiday cottage in Wales. The renovation has nothing to do with the flat purchase and the firm is not acting on any matter concerning the cottage. Mr Malhotra offers to confirm his instructions in writing.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the firm should respond?","o":["Agree, because Mr Malhotra will confirm his instructions for the payment in writing.","Agree, provided the firm first checks the source of the brother's funds.","Refuse, because the payment is not linked to any legal service the firm is providing.","Refuse, unless the brother also agrees to become a client of the firm.","Agree, because the firm is already acting for Mr Malhotra on a purchase."],"a":2,"e":"Under **r.3.3** a firm must not use its client account to provide **banking facilities**: every payment in and out must relate to regulated services the firm is delivering. Receiving money and passing it to a builder for unconnected work is a pure banking service, so written instructions, a long relationship, the existing retainer on an unrelated purchase and clean funds make no difference. Taking the brother on as a client would not create any underlying legal work to which the payment relates."},{"t":"separation-mixed-payments","p":"FLK2","q":"Holt & Varma acts for the executors of the late Mrs Eunice Okonjo. It receives a cheque for £3,200 from HMRC, a repayment of income tax the deceased had overpaid. No bill or notification of costs has been sent to the executors. By mistake the cashier pays the cheque into the firm's business account. A partner spots the error the next day.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the firm should do?","o":["Transfer the £3,200 to client account and report the matter to the SRA at once.","Keep the £3,200 in business account and set it against the firm's next bill.","Transfer the £3,200 to client account at the time of the next reconciliation.","Keep the £3,200 in business account until the executors give instructions.","Transfer £3,200 to client account immediately and record the breach."],"a":4,"e":"The refund is client money, so holding it in business account breaches r.2.3 and the **separation rule (r.4.1)**; under **r.6.1** money improperly withheld from client account must be **paid in immediately** and the breach recorded. Keeping it against a future bill would use client money for costs before any bill is delivered (r.4.3), and waiting for the next reconciliation or for instructions delays a correction that must be immediate. A single honest error put right at once is unlikely to be a serious breach that must be reported to the SRA, though the firm must still record it."},{"t":"separation-mixed-payments","p":"FLK2","q":"Tamworth Bray holds £9,000 on account of costs for the executors of the late Mr Ifeanyi Chukwu. At the end of a stage of the work, the fee earner emails both executors: \"Our costs incurred to date are £2,000 plus VAT of £400.\" That afternoon the cashier transfers £2,400 from client account to business account. A formal bill covering the whole matter will be delivered when the administration is complete.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the transfer?","o":["It is a breach, because a formal bill must be delivered before any costs are transferred.","It complies, because a written notification of the costs was given before the transfer.","It is a breach, because the executors had not first agreed the costs in writing.","It complies only if a formal bill for £2,400 follows within 14 days of the transfer.","It is a breach, because costs may be taken from client account only at the end of the matter."],"a":1,"e":"Under **r.4.3** a firm may take its costs from client money once it has given **a bill or other written notification of costs**, before the transfer, for the **specific sum** and covered by the money held. An email setting out the costs incurred is a written notification, it was sent before the transfer, and the £2,400 matches it and is covered by the £9,000. The rules do not require a formal bill first, the client's agreement to the costs, a follow-up bill within a set time, or the end of the matter."},{"t":"double-entry-basics","p":"FLK2","q":"Grantham Lowe holds £5,000 in client account for Mrs Rukhsana Begum, who is buying a house. The firm has delivered a bill for profit costs of £750 plus VAT of £150, and the bill has already been entered in its books. It now transfers £900 from client account to business account to pay the bill.\n\nWhich of the following sets of entries records the transfer?","o":["DR Mrs Begum's ledger client column £900; CR client cash £900; DR business cash £900; CR Mrs Begum's ledger business column £900.","DR client cash £900; CR Mrs Begum's ledger client column £900; DR Mrs Begum's ledger business column £900; CR business cash £900.","DR Mrs Begum's ledger client column £900; CR Mrs Begum's ledger business column £900; no entry in either cash account.","DR Mrs Begum's ledger client column £750; CR client cash £750; DR business cash £750; CR Mrs Begum's ledger business column £750.","DR client cash £900; CR business cash £900; DR Mrs Begum's ledger business column £900; CR Mrs Begum's ledger client column £900."],"a":0,"e":"A transfer from client to business account is **two movements of money**, so it needs a pair of entries in **each set of books**: in the client books, DR the ledger client column and CR client cash (money out); in the business books, DR business cash and CR the ledger business column (the bill is paid). The reversed set treats the transfer as money coming into client account, and the set with no cash entries describes an inter-client transfer, which this is not. The whole £900 including VAT is transferred, and each pair of entries must stay within one set of books rather than mixing client and business accounts."},{"t":"double-entry-basics","p":"FLK2","q":"Ellery Shah acts for the executors of the late Mrs Hyacinth Barrow. In order, it: (1) pays the £526 probate application fee from business account; (2) receives £42,000 from the deceased's bank; (3) pays the funeral director's invoice of £3,800, addressed to the executors, from client account; (4) delivers a bill for profit costs of £2,500 plus VAT of £500, which also lists the probate fee already paid; and (5) transfers £3,526 from client account to business account.\n\nWhich of the following shows the balances on the estate ledger after these entries?","o":["Business column £526 DR; client column £35,200 CR.","Business column nil; client column £35,200 CR.","Business column £3,526 DR; client column £38,200 CR.","Business column nil; client column £34,674 CR.","Business column £526 DR; client column £34,674 CR."],"a":3,"e":"The business column is debited £526 for the fee and £3,000 for the bill (the fee is already on the ledger, so it is not entered again), making £3,526 DR, which the **transfer clears to nil**. The client column receives £42,000 and is debited £3,800 and the £3,526 transfer, leaving **£34,674 CR**. The £35,200 figures come from transferring only the £3,000 bill and leaving the probate fee unpaid, and £38,200 ignores the transfer altogether; the £3,526 transfer reduces both columns, so nil and £34,674 must go together."},{"t":"bills-vat-disbursements","p":"FLK2","q":"Brannigan Osei acts for the executors of the late Mrs Gwendoline Tasker. Before any money has been received for the estate, a valuer inspects the deceased's jewellery for inheritance tax purposes. The valuer's invoice for £600 plus VAT of £120 is addressed to the executors, and the firm pays it from its business account so that the valuer is not kept waiting.\n\nWhich of the following sets of entries records the payment?","o":["DR estate ledger business column £600; DR HMRC £120; CR business cash £720.","DR estate ledger client column £720; CR client cash £720.","DR estate ledger business column £720; CR business cash £720.","DR estate ledger business column £600; CR business cash £600.","DR business cash £720; CR estate ledger business column £720."],"a":2,"e":"An invoice **addressed to the client** (here the executors) is an **agency** disbursement: the firm pays as the client's agent, does not reclaim the VAT and records the **gross** amount. As nothing is held for the estate it must come from business account, so the entries are DR the estate ledger **business** column and CR business cash, £720 each. Splitting out £120 to HMRC is the principal method, which applies only to invoices addressed to the firm, client account entries are impossible with nothing held, and recording only £600 or reversing the entries misstates what the estate owes."},{"t":"bills-vat-disbursements","p":"FLK2","q":"Marsden Akhtar acts for the administrators of the late Mr Kenji Watanabe. With nothing yet held for the estate, it paid the £526 probate application fee from business account, and that payment was entered in its books. It now delivers a bill for profit costs of £3,000, which also lists the probate fee. VAT is charged at 20%.\n\nWhich of the following sets of entries records the delivery of the bill?","o":["DR estate ledger business column £3,600; CR profit costs £3,000; CR HMRC £600.","DR estate ledger business column £4,231.20; CR profit costs £3,526; CR HMRC £705.20.","DR estate ledger business column £4,126; CR profit costs £3,526; CR HMRC £600.","DR estate ledger business column £3,705.20; CR profit costs £3,000; CR HMRC £705.20.","DR estate ledger client column £3,600; CR profit costs £3,000; CR HMRC £600."],"a":0,"e":"The probate application fee is a court fee and **not a VAT supply**, so it is an agency disbursement and the firm **adds no VAT** to it; VAT is due only on the £3,000 profit costs, giving £600. The fee was debited to the business column when it was paid, so it is not entered again or credited to profit costs. The options charging £705.20 wrongly add VAT to the fee, and a bill is always recorded in the **business** column, never the client column."},{"t":"interest-breaches","p":"FLK2","q":"Wetherby Quayle held £180,000 for the executors of the late Mr Desmond Achterberg in its general client account for six months. There is no agreement about interest, and under the firm's interest policy a fair sum of £1,140 is due to the estate. The firm intends to pay the interest into client account, to be held with the rest of the estate money.\n\nWhich of the following entries records the interest the firm owes the estate, before any payment or transfer is made?","o":["DR client cash £1,140; CR estate ledger client column £1,140.","DR estate ledger business column £1,140; CR interest payable £1,140.","DR business cash £1,140; CR interest received £1,140.","DR interest payable £1,140; CR estate ledger client column £1,140.","DR interest payable £1,140; CR estate ledger business column £1,140."],"a":4,"e":"On a **general client account** the bank pays interest to the firm, which then pays the client a fair sum out of its own money, so the first step is **DR interest payable (an expense); CR the client ledger business column**. Only then does the firm transfer the sum to client account (business side, then client side). Direct client cash and client column entries are used for a **separate designated client account**, where the bank credits interest straight to the client; crediting the client column against an interest payable debit wrongly mixes the two sets of books, and the reversed or interest received entries treat the interest as the firm's income."},{"t":"interest-breaches","p":"FLK2","q":"The cashier at Brockley Imani holds £150 for Ms Delphine Mbeki on her purchase and pays the £150 Land Registry fee from client account. Through a software fault the payment is sent twice, leaving Ms Mbeki's client column £150 overdrawn. The duplicate is spotted the next morning, the firm transfers £150 from business account to client account the same day, and the error is entered in the firm's breach register. The Land Registry later refunds the duplicate fee. The COFA asks whether a report to the SRA is needed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["A report is needed, because one client's money was used for another client.","A report is unlikely to be needed, as it is probably not a serious breach.","Neither a record nor a report is needed, because it was corrected the same day.","A report is needed, but only once the Land Registry has refunded the duplicate.","A report is needed only if Ms Mbeki complains to the firm about the error."],"a":1,"e":"The COFA must report promptly to the SRA facts capable of amounting to a **serious breach** of the Accounts Rules; seriousness depends on the amount, risk to client money, any dishonesty, any pattern and how quickly it was put right. A small, honest, one-off error replaced the same day is **unlikely to be serious**, although it must still be corrected under r.6.1 and **recorded**, which the firm has done. Not every use of one client's money for another must be reported, prompt correction does not remove the duty to keep a record, and neither the refund nor a complaint decides whether a report is required."},{"t":"records-reconciliation","p":"FLK2","q":"Ingleby Rashid is a growing probate firm. Its bills are produced by each fee earner and a copy is kept only on the relevant client's matter file, with no other list or file of them. Client account statements are obtained monthly and the client account is reconciled every four weeks, with each reconciliation signed off by the COFA. All accounting records are kept for seven years.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm complies with the SRA Accounts Rules?","o":["It complies, because a copy of each bill is kept on the client's matter file.","It complies, because its reconciliations are done within the five-week limit.","It does not comply, because bills must be approved by the COFA before delivery.","It does not comply, because it must keep a central record of all bills it gives.","It complies, because its records are kept for longer than the six-year minimum."],"a":3,"e":"**Rule 8.4** requires a firm to keep a **readily accessible central record of all bills or other written notifications of costs** it gives, so copies scattered across individual matter files are not enough. Its monthly statements, four-weekly reconciliations signed off by the COFA and seven-year retention meet r.8.2, r.8.3 and r.13, but they are separate duties and do not satisfy r.8.4. The rules do not require the COFA to approve bills before they are sent."},{"t":"records-reconciliation","p":"FLK2","q":"Fothergill Jain is a small conveyancing firm. To save time, its part-time cashier enters all receipts and payments of client money on the client ledgers every second Friday, working from the bank statements. In between, fee earners keep their own spreadsheets of what they think they hold for each client. The client account is reconciled every four weeks and each reconciliation is signed off by the COFA, who is a partner.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm's record-keeping complies with the SRA Accounts Rules?","o":["It complies, because the client account is reconciled at least every five weeks.","It complies, because each reconciliation is signed off by a partner who is the COFA.","It is unlikely to comply, because client ledgers must be kept contemporaneously.","It complies, provided all the ledgers and spreadsheets are kept for at least six years.","It does not comply, because client ledgers must be written up by a manager of the firm."],"a":2,"e":"Rule 8.1 requires **accurate, contemporaneous and chronological** records of all dealings with client money, so ledgers written up only once a fortnight, with fee earners relying on unofficial spreadsheets in between, are unlikely to comply and risk payments being made without enough held. A compliant reconciliation every four weeks, signed off by the COFA, and keeping records for six years are separate duties that do not cure out-of-date ledgers. The rules do not require a manager to write up the ledgers."},{"t":"joint-own-tpma","p":"FLK2","q":"Mr Rhys Vaughan, a partner at Caldey Price, is the attorney under a lasting power of attorney for Mrs Marjorie Hendry, aged 91. He signs on her own current account to pay her care home fees, and the firm obtains statements and reconciles that account every five weeks. To make several large payments easier, Mr Vaughan now transfers £25,000 from Mrs Hendry's current account into the firm's general client account.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the £25,000 now in client account?","o":["It is client money, and all of the main client money rules now apply to it.","Rule 10 still applies, because the money continues to belong to Mrs Hendry.","Only statements, reconciliation and a record of bills are needed, as before.","It may not be held in client account while Mr Vaughan acts as her attorney.","It is outside the rules, because Mr Vaughan holds it as attorney, not as a solicitor."],"a":0,"e":"Rule 10 (operating a client's own account) applies only **while the money stays in the client's own account**. Once Mr Vaughan moves it into the firm's client account, it is held by him as **donee of a power of attorney** (the person appointed to act under it), so it is **client money under r.2.1(c)** and the whole of Part 2 applies, including sufficient funds, interest and full records. The lighter statements-and-reconciliation regime therefore no longer covers it, and nothing prevents money held as attorney being placed in client account."},{"t":"accountants-reports","p":"FLK2","q":"Okonkwo Lister is a small firm doing mainly legally aided family work. During its last accounting period most of the client money it received came from the Legal Aid Agency, but it also received £850 on account of costs from one private client for whom it drafted a will. Its client account bank statements for the period show an average balance of £7,400 and a highest balance of £96,000. It held no joint accounts and operated no clients' own accounts.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm must obtain an accountant's report for the period?","o":["No, because nearly all of its client money came from the Legal Aid Agency.","Yes, because not all of its client money came from the Legal Aid Agency.","Yes, because it held client money at some point during the accounting period.","No, provided its COFA certifies to the SRA that it has complied with the rules.","No, because its balances fall within both limits of the low-balance exemption."],"a":4,"e":"The **Legal Aid Agency exemption** in r.12.2(a) applies only if **all** the client money in the period came from the Legal Aid Agency, so the private client's £850 takes the firm outside it. But under **r.12.2(b)** a firm is also exempt if its statement balances do not exceed an **average of £10,000 and a maximum of £250,000**; at £7,400 and £96,000 both limits are met, so no report is needed. Failing one exemption does not mean a report is required, holding client money triggers the duty only subject to the exemptions, and a COFA certificate is not a substitute."},{"t":"accountants-reports","p":"FLK2","q":"Strand Mabey LLP does wills and probate work. Its accounting period ended on 31 December 2025. Its accountant's report for that period is qualified: the accountant found that on several occasions money held for one estate was used to pay inheritance tax and disbursements for another estate before that estate's funds arrived.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's obligations in respect of the report?","o":["It must obtain the report by 30 June 2026 and keep it to produce if asked.","It must obtain the report and deliver it to the SRA by 30 June 2026.","It must obtain the report and deliver it to the SRA by 31 December 2026.","It must obtain the report by 30 June 2026 and deliver it by 31 December 2026.","It must obtain the report and deliver it to the SRA within 28 days of signing."],"a":1,"e":"Under **r.12.1** a firm must **obtain** its report within **six months of the end of the accounting period**, and must **deliver it to the SRA within the same six months** if it is qualified to show that client or third-party money **is, has been or is likely to be placed at risk**. Using one estate's money for another estate puts client money at risk, so this report must be both obtained and sent by 30 June 2026. Simply keeping the report applies to unqualified reports and those qualified without any risk, and the rules set no twelve-month or 28-day period."},{"t":"conveyancing-accounts","p":"FLK2","q":"Pryce Ottaway acts for Ms Thandiwe Moyo and her lender on her purchase of a house. It holds the lender's mortgage advance of £275,000 in client account, ready for completion. The day before completion, the seller's own onward purchase collapses and completion is postponed with no new date in sight. The lender's instructions to the firm require the advance to be returned if completion does not take place within a stated period. Ms Moyo asks the firm to keep the money in client account until a new date is agreed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the firm should deal with the advance?","o":["Keep the advance, because Ms Moyo is the firm's client and has instructed it.","Keep the advance, provided the firm pays Ms Moyo a fair sum of interest on it.","Move the advance to a separate designated client account in Ms Moyo's name.","Follow the lender's instructions, returning the advance if they require it.","Pay the advance to Ms Moyo, because the loan was made to her personally."],"a":3,"e":"Where the firm also acts for the lender, the **mortgage advance is client money held for the lender** until completion and may be used or kept only as the **lender's instructions** allow, so if completion is delayed beyond the permitted period the advance must be returned to the lender. Ms Moyo's instructions cannot override the lender's, and paying interest or moving the money to a designated account does not change whose money it is. Paying it to Ms Moyo would be a withdrawal without authority from the person for whom it is held."},{"t":"conveyancing-accounts","p":"FLK2","q":"Delaney Achebe acts for Mrs Corinne Lafleur on the sale of her house for £290,000 under the Standard Conditions of Sale. She is not buying another home. On exchange the firm received a deposit of £29,000, which it holds as stakeholder and credited to its stakeholder ledger. Completion has now taken place and the firm has received the balance of the price.\n\nWhich of the following sets of entries records the release of the deposit to Mrs Lafleur on completion?","o":["DR Mrs Lafleur's ledger client column £29,000; CR stakeholder ledger client column £29,000.","DR client cash £29,000; CR Mrs Lafleur's ledger client column £29,000.","DR stakeholder ledger client column £29,000; CR Mrs Lafleur's ledger client column £29,000.","DR stakeholder ledger client column £29,000; CR client cash £29,000.","DR stakeholder ledger business column £29,000; CR Mrs Lafleur's ledger business column £29,000."],"a":2,"e":"On completion the deposit stops being held for both parties and becomes the seller's, so it is moved by an **inter-client transfer**: **DR the stakeholder ledger and CR the seller's ledger, client columns**. The money never leaves client account, so there is **no cash entry**, which rules out the options debiting or crediting client cash. Reversing the entries would reduce what is held for the seller, and the business columns are never used for client money."},{"t":"probate-accounts","p":"FLK2","q":"Haverford Mensah acts for the executors of the late Ms Philomena Agyei. Because the estate's money will be held for many months while a property dispute is resolved, the firm, with the executors' agreement, places £300,000 of estate money in a separate designated client account for the estate. At the end of the quarter the bank credits interest of £2,150 to that account.\n\nWhich of the following entries records the interest?","o":["DR client cash £2,150; CR estate ledger client column £2,150.","DR interest payable £2,150; CR estate ledger business column £2,150.","DR business cash £2,150; CR interest received £2,150.","DR estate ledger client column £2,150; CR client cash £2,150.","DR interest payable £2,150; CR estate ledger client column £2,150."],"a":0,"e":"Interest on a **separate designated client account** is credited by the bank **directly to that account**, so it belongs to the estate from the start and is recorded as money received for it: **DR client cash; CR the estate ledger client column**, with no business entries. The interest payable entries are used for a **general client account**, where the bank pays the firm and the firm then pays the client a fair sum from its own money. Crediting interest received treats the interest as the firm's income, and debiting the estate's client column records money going out rather than coming in."},{"t":"probate-accounts","p":"FLK2","q":"Lockhart Ebi acts for the executors of the late Mr Barnaby Quist and, separately, for the executors of the late Mrs Leonora Fitzgerald; the two estates are unconnected. The Quist executors must pay inheritance tax of £22,000 before the grant can issue, but the firm holds nothing for that estate, and the deceased's bank will release funds only in ten days. The firm holds £150,000 for the Fitzgerald estate. The cashier suggests paying the Quist tax from client account and putting it back when the Quist funds arrive.\n\nWhich of the following may the firm properly do?","o":["Pay the £22,000 from client account, as the account as a whole holds enough.","Pay the £22,000 from its own business account, if it is willing to fund it.","Pay the £22,000 from client account, provided it is repaid within ten days.","Pay the £22,000 from client account and tell the Fitzgerald executors afterwards.","Pay the £22,000 from client account, because both estates are clients of the firm."],"a":1,"e":"Under **r.5.3** client money may be withdrawn only if **sufficient funds are held for that specific client**; the Quist estate has nothing, so any payment from client account would use the Fitzgerald estate's money, a breach however briefly it lasts and whatever the account's total balance. The firm may instead fund the tax from its **own business account** (DR estate ledger business column; CR business cash) and recover it from the estate later. Repaying within ten days, telling the other executors afterwards or the fact that both estates are clients does not make the withdrawal permitted."}],"blp-p1":[{"t":"business-structures","p":"FLK1","q":"Ifeoma runs a catering business through Spice Route Ltd, of which she is the sole director and shareholder. Two years ago she personally guaranteed the company's £60,000 bank overdraft. The company has now ceased trading owing £60,000 to the bank, £25,000 to food suppliers and £8,000 to its landlord. Its assets are worth nothing, and there is no suggestion of any wrongdoing by Ifeoma.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ifeoma's personal liability for the company's debts?","o":["She is personally liable for all £93,000 of the debts, because she is the only shareholder and director.","She is not personally liable for any of the debts, because the company has separate legal personality.","She is personally liable to the bank under her guarantee, but not to the food suppliers or the landlord.","She is personally liable to the bank and the landlord, but not to the suppliers of food to the business.","She is liable to all the creditors up to the nominal value of her shares, in addition to the guarantee."],"a":2,"e":"The debts are the company's, because a company has **separate legal personality** and its shareholders have **limited liability** (here her shares are presumably fully paid). Ifeoma is liable to the bank only because she gave a **personal guarantee**, a separate contract by which she promised to pay if the company did not. Being the sole shareholder and director does not by itself make her liable to the suppliers or landlord, and the nominal value of fully paid shares is not an extra fund for creditors."},{"t":"business-structures","p":"FLK1","q":"Mark sold his plumbing business to a buyer and agreed in the sale contract not to solicit the business's customers for two years. A month later he formed Flowright Ltd, owning all its shares and appointing his partner as its sole director. Flowright immediately began contacting the customers of the business he had sold. Mark says the restriction binds only him personally, not the company.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the buyer's position?","o":["The buyer has no remedy against Flowright, because a company is not bound by its shareholder's contracts.","The buyer can sue the director of Flowright personally for breach of the non-solicitation covenant.","The buyer can only claim damages from Mark, because a court will never disregard a company's personality.","The buyer can petition to have Flowright wound up on the ground that it was formed for an improper purpose.","The buyer can obtain an injunction against Mark and Flowright, as the company is a device to evade his obligation."],"a":4,"e":"The court may **pierce the corporate veil** where a company is interposed to **evade an existing legal obligation** of its controller (the evasion principle), and grant relief against both the individual and the company (Gilford Motor Co v Horne; Prest v Petrodel). Mark already owed the non-solicitation obligation and set up Flowright to get round it, so an injunction can restrain both. Separate personality is the normal rule, but it is not absolute; the director is not a party to the covenant, and winding up is not the remedy for this wrong."},{"t":"formation","p":"FLK1","q":"On 3 March, Tamsin signed a two-year hire agreement for office equipment 'for and on behalf of Brightlane Ltd'. She intended to be its director, but the company was not incorporated until 20 March. After incorporation the board resolved to adopt the agreement, and the company paid three monthly instalments before running into financial difficulty. The supplier now seeks the remaining instalments. Nothing in the agreement excludes personal liability.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Tamsin is personally liable on the agreement, because the company did not exist when it was signed and cannot ratify it.","Only Brightlane Ltd is liable, because the board's resolution validly ratified the agreement made on its behalf.","Tamsin and Brightlane Ltd are jointly liable, because the company adopted the agreement by paying instalments.","Neither Tamsin nor Brightlane Ltd is liable, because a contract made for a company not yet formed is void.","Tamsin is liable only for instalments due before 20 March, after which liability passed to Brightlane Ltd."],"a":0,"e":"Under **CA 2006 s.51**, a contract made on behalf of a company **before it is incorporated** takes effect as a contract with the **person who made it**, who is **personally liable**, subject to any agreement to the contrary. A company cannot **ratify** (approve after the event) a contract made before it existed, so the board resolution and payments do not transfer liability; the company would only be bound by a **novation** (a new contract with the supplier replacing the old one). The contract is not void, and Tamsin's liability does not stop on incorporation."},{"t":"formation","p":"FLK1","q":"Kwame was appointed a director of Harbourline Ltd at a board meeting on 2 November 2026. He has not verified his identity with Companies House. Since his appointment, and with the board's authority, he has signed a supply contract on the company's behalf. The company secretary is now preparing to notify Companies House of his appointment.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Kwame may act without verification until the company files its next confirmation statement, so no offence is committed.","Kwame's appointment is void until he verifies his identity, so the supply contract does not bind the company.","Kwame may act unverified for 12 months from appointment, as the transitional period applies to all new directors.","Kwame and the company commit an offence by his acting unverified, but the supply contract still binds the company.","Only the company commits an offence, because verifying a new director's identity is the company's duty alone."],"a":3,"e":"Since **18 November 2025** (ECCTA 2023), every **new director** must have **verified identity** before acting, and the notice of appointment (form AP01) must confirm it. **Acting as a director without verified identity is an offence by both the director and the company**, but it **does not invalidate the director's acts**, so the contract binds the company. The transition period linked to the first confirmation statement applied only to **existing** directors and has now ended; it never allowed new appointees to act unverified."},{"t":"directors","p":"FLK1","q":"Hannah is one of three directors of Coldstream Ltd, a joinery company with unamended Model Articles. A hotel group approaches Hannah personally and offers her a large refurbishment contract. The board has previously decided that the company will not take on hotel work. Without telling the other directors, Hannah takes the contract through her own separate business and makes a profit of £40,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Hannah's position?","o":["She has breached no duty, because the company had decided against hotel work and could not take the contract.","She has breached her duty to avoid conflicts and is liable to account for her £40,000 profit to the company.","She has breached her duty to declare an interest in a proposed transaction, so the contract is voidable.","She has breached only her duty of care, skill and diligence, so the company can recover only its own loss.","She has breached no duty, provided she declares her interest at the next board meeting after the work ends."],"a":1,"e":"Under **CA 2006 s.175** a director must avoid situations in which they have an interest that conflicts with the company's, and this applies to exploiting an opportunity **whether or not the company could take advantage of it** (s.175(2)). The breach could have been avoided only by **authorisation from the other directors** (permitted in a private company unless the articles forbid it), which Hannah did not seek, so she must **account for the profit**. The s.177 duty concerns transactions with the company itself, not this outside contract, and a later declaration does not cure a s.175 breach."},{"t":"directors","p":"FLK1","q":"Gareth holds 60% of the shares in Westmoor Ltd. The remaining 40% are held by Leona, who is also a director. Gareth wants to remove Leona as a director as quickly as possible and suggests circulating a written resolution. The company has unamended Model Articles, and Leona's service contract has three years left to run.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Leona can be removed?","o":["By written resolution signed by Gareth alone, as he holds more than 50% of the total voting rights.","By special resolution at a general meeting, as removing a director requires 75% of the votes cast.","By board resolution, as the Model Articles let the directors remove one of their number from office.","By ordinary resolution at a meeting after special notice, leaving Leona free to claim damages under her contract.","By ordinary resolution at a meeting, which also ends her service contract without any claim for compensation."],"a":3,"e":"Under **CA 2006 s.168** a director can be removed by **ordinary resolution** at a **general meeting**, of which **special notice** (28 days) must be given; a **written resolution cannot be used** (s.288(2)) because the director has the right to be heard at the meeting. Gareth's 60% is enough to pass the resolution, but removal **does not deprive Leona of compensation** for breach of her service contract (s.168(5)). A special resolution is not required, and the Model Articles give the board no power of removal."},{"t":"decision-making","p":"FLK1","q":"Orchard Bakeries Ltd has net assets of £200,000 according to its latest accounts. The board proposes that the company sell a used delivery van, valued at £28,000, to Callum, who is the brother of one of the company's directors. Callum holds no shares in the company and is not in business with the director.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the approval required for the sale?","o":["An ordinary resolution of the members before the sale, because the van exceeds 10% of the company's net assets.","A special resolution of the members, because the sale of a company asset is to a close relative of a director.","An ordinary resolution of the members, which may be passed after the sale has completed in order to affirm it.","A board resolution only, but the van must be sold at full market value to avoid any breach of directors' duties.","A board resolution only, because a director's brother is not a person connected with the director for this purpose."],"a":4,"e":"A **substantial property transaction** under **CA 2006 s.190** needs members' approval only if the other party is a **director or a person connected with a director**. The van's value would be substantial (it exceeds 10% of net assets and £5,000), but **siblings are not connected persons** under ss.252-254 (which cover spouses, partners, children, parents and certain companies and trusts), so the sale is a matter for the board. The value test alone is the tempting trap; it matters only once a connected party is involved."},{"t":"decision-making","p":"FLK1","q":"Fernhill Ltd, a private company with unamended Model Articles, has four shareholders: Asha (55%), Ben (30%), Chidi (8%) and Dora (7%). The directors want to hold a general meeting in three days' time to pass a special resolution changing the articles. Asha, Ben and Chidi have agreed in writing to the meeting being held on short notice. Dora has not replied.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the meeting can be held on short notice?","o":["Yes, because a majority in number of the members, together holding at least 90% of the voting shares, have agreed.","No, because short notice of a meeting to pass a special resolution requires the agreement of every member.","No, because the members agreeing must together hold at least 95% of the voting shares in the company.","Yes, because Asha alone holds a majority of the shares and can consent to short notice on the company's behalf.","No, because short notice is never available for a general meeting called to amend the company's articles."],"a":0,"e":"A private company's general meeting normally needs **14 clear days' notice**, but under **CA 2006 s.307(4)-(6)** it can be held on short notice if agreed by a **majority in number** of the members who together hold at least **90%** of the voting shares. Three of the four members (a majority by number) holding 93% have agreed, so both tests are met. The 95% figure applies to public companies, unanimity is not needed, and there is no bar on using short notice for a special resolution."},{"t":"shareholders-minority","p":"FLK1","q":"Three friends, Priya, Owen and Lucas, set up Kestrel Design Ltd on the understanding that all three would be directors and share in running the business. Each holds one third of the shares. The company has never paid dividends, distributing its profits as directors' fees instead. After a disagreement, Priya and Owen removed Lucas as a director and stopped his fees. The company is profitable and solvent. Lucas wants to recover the value of his investment.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate remedy for Lucas to seek?","o":["A derivative claim on the company's behalf for breach of the directors' duty to promote its success.","An unfair prejudice petition, asking the court to order Priya and Owen to buy his shares at a fair value.","A petition to wind up the company on the just and equitable ground, as his first and preferred remedy.","A personal claim for damages for breach of the articles, which entitle every shareholder to be a director.","A claim to be restored as a director, as removing a director requires the consent of every member."],"a":1,"e":"Excluding a member from management of a **quasi-partnership** company (one formed on mutual trust with an understanding of shared management) is classic **unfair prejudice** under **CA 2006 s.994**, and the usual remedy under s.996 is an order that the others **buy the petitioner's shares at a fair value**. A just and equitable winding up is a **last resort** and may be refused where another remedy is available, which matters for a profitable company. A derivative claim is for wrongs to the company, not to Lucas personally, and the Model Articles give no right to be a director."},{"t":"shareholders-minority","p":"FLK1","q":"Neha owns 15% of the shares in Larchfield Ltd. She discovers that the managing director, who holds 60% of the shares, sold company land to his own company at half its market value without disclosing his interest. As a result, the value of Neha's shares has fallen. She wants to sue the managing director in her own name for the fall in value of her shares.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Neha's position?","o":["She can recover the fall in value of her shares, because she has suffered a direct and personal loss.","She can sue in her own name, but only once the board has refused to sue on the company's behalf.","She cannot bring any claim, because only the board can decide whether the company should sue a director.","Her loss merely reflects the company's, so her route is a derivative claim, which needs court permission.","She can bring a derivative claim without court permission, because the wrongdoer controls the company."],"a":3,"e":"The wrong (a breach of the director's duties) was done to the **company**, and a fall in share value that simply mirrors the company's loss is **reflective loss**, which a shareholder **cannot recover personally**. The proper route is a **derivative claim** under **CA 2006 ss.260-264**, brought by a member on the company's behalf, which always needs the **court's permission** to continue. Control by the wrongdoer explains why the board will not sue, but it does not remove the permission stage."},{"t":"partnerships","p":"FLK1","q":"Rosa, Imran and Gwen are partners in a garden landscaping firm. Their partnership agreement provides that no partner may buy equipment costing more than £10,000 without the others' consent. Without consulting anyone, Imran orders a mini-digger costing £18,000 from a plant supplier, signing the order in the firm's name. The supplier has never dealt with the firm before and knows nothing of the agreement. Rosa and Gwen refuse to pay.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's liability to the supplier?","o":["The firm is not bound, because Imran acted in breach of an express restriction in the partnership agreement.","The firm is bound only up to £10,000, because that was the limit of Imran's actual authority.","The firm is bound, because the purchase is usual for such a firm and the supplier did not know of the limit.","Only Imran is liable, because the supplier had not dealt with the firm before and cannot rely on holding out.","The firm is bound only if Rosa and Gwen later ratify the order at a meeting of all the partners."],"a":2,"e":"Under **PA 1890 s.5** every partner is an agent of the firm, and an act done for carrying on the firm's business **in the usual way** binds the firm unless the partner lacked authority **and** the third party **knew** this or did not believe them to be a partner. Buying equipment is usual for a landscaping firm, and under **s.8** an internal restriction binds only outsiders **with notice** of it. The firm is therefore bound for the full price, although Imran may have to **indemnify** his partners for breaching the agreement."},{"t":"partnerships","p":"FLK1","q":"Sophie retired from a firm of interior designers on 31 May. The firm placed a notice of her retirement in the London Gazette but did not write to any of its customers or suppliers. In July, a fabric wholesaler that had regularly supplied the firm on credit for several years, and which did not know of Sophie's retirement, delivered goods worth £15,000 to the firm. The firm has not paid.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Sophie's liability for the July debt?","o":["She is liable, because the wholesaler had dealt with the firm before and had no actual notice of her retirement.","She is not liable, because the Gazette notice counts as notice of her retirement to everyone.","She is not liable, because a retired partner is never responsible for debts the firm incurs after leaving.","She is liable only if her name still appeared on the firm's notepaper used for the July order.","She is liable only for a share of the debt matching her former share of the firm's profits."],"a":0,"e":"Under **PA 1890 s.36**, a person who dealt with the firm **before** a change in its membership may treat the former partner as still a partner until they receive **actual notice** of the change. A **Gazette notice** is enough only for people who **had not dealt** with the firm before, so it does not protect Sophie against an existing supplier. Partners' liability for firm debts is joint, not limited to their profit share, though Sophie may seek an indemnity from the continuing partners."},{"t":"equity-finance","p":"FLK1","q":"Quayside Ltd has unamended Model Articles and two classes of shares: ordinary shares and non-voting preference shares. Morwenna, a property developer, has agreed to transfer a warehouse to the company. In return, the directors want to issue her 20,000 new ordinary shares instead of paying cash. No shareholder resolution about allotting shares has ever been passed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the shareholder approval needed before the shares are issued?","o":["A special resolution disapplying pre-emption rights, as the directors already have authority to allot.","An ordinary resolution authorising allotment and a special resolution disapplying pre-emption rights.","No shareholder approval, because shares issued for a non-cash asset are outside the allotment rules.","A special resolution only, because the warehouse must be valued before the shares can be issued.","An ordinary resolution authorising the directors to allot the shares, with no further resolution needed."],"a":4,"e":"Because Quayside has **more than one class of shares**, the simple s.550 authority is unavailable and the directors need authority under **CA 2006 s.551**, given by the articles or an **ordinary resolution** (which must be filed within 15 days). **Statutory pre-emption rights do not apply** to shares allotted wholly or partly for **non-cash consideration** (s.565), so no special resolution to disapply them is needed. Valuation of non-cash consideration is a public company requirement, not a private company one."},{"t":"equity-finance","p":"FLK1","q":"Thornbury Ltd is buying back shares from a retiring director, partly out of capital. The directors made the required statement, with the auditor's report annexed, on 1 September 2026. The special resolution approving the payment out of capital was passed on 4 September 2026, and the required notices were published on 8 September 2026. No member or creditor has applied to the court.\n\nWhich of the following best describes when the company may make the capital payment?","o":["At any time after 4 September 2026, as soon as the special resolution has been passed.","Not earlier than 5 weeks and not later than 7 weeks after 4 September 2026.","Not earlier than 5 weeks and not later than 7 weeks after 8 September 2026.","At any time within 12 months of the directors' statement made on 1 September 2026.","Not earlier than one week after 8 September 2026, once the notices have been published."],"a":1,"e":"Under **CA 2006 s.723**, a payment out of capital must be made **no earlier than 5 weeks and no later than 7 weeks after the date of the special resolution**. The 5-week gap matches the period in which a non-assenting member or a creditor may apply to court to cancel the resolution (s.721). The timetable runs from the **resolution**, not from the publication of the notices or the directors' statement."},{"t":"debt-security","p":"FLK1","q":"On 1 March 2026 Linwood Ltd borrowed £200,000 from a lender and granted it a fixed charge over its factory. The lender's solicitor forgot to deliver the charge for registration at Companies House. On 15 May 2026 Linwood went into insolvent liquidation.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the failure to register?","o":["The charge is valid against the liquidator, because registration only gives notice to later lenders.","The charge remains valid, because the lender may register it late without a court order before distribution.","The charge is void and the loan is also unenforceable, so the lender cannot prove in the liquidation.","The charge is void against the liquidator, so the lender is an unsecured creditor for the £200,000.","The charge is valid, but the lender ranks after the preferential creditors because it was not registered."],"a":3,"e":"A company charge must be delivered for registration within **21 days** of its creation; if not, it is **void against a liquidator, an administrator and the company's creditors** (**CA 2006 s.859H**). The **debt itself remains valid** (and becomes immediately repayable), so the lender can prove as an **unsecured creditor**. Late registration needs a **court order** under s.859F, which is very unlikely once liquidation has begun."},{"t":"debt-security","p":"FLK1","q":"Greenway Furniture Ltd granted a lender a properly registered floating charge over all its assets, including its stock of sofas. No event has occurred that would cause the charge to crystallise. Last month Greenway sold forty sofas from its stock to a retail chain in the ordinary course of its business, and the retailer paid in full. The lender has now discovered the sale and wants to recover the sofas.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the retail chain's position?","o":["It must return the sofas, because the charge was registered and so gave it notice of the lender's security.","It keeps the sofas free of the charge, as Greenway could sell stock in the ordinary course of business.","It keeps the sofas, but must pay the lender their value because they were subject to the charge when sold.","It must return the sofas unless the lender consented in writing to the sale before it took place.","It keeps the sofas only if it did not know of the floating charge at the time it bought them."],"a":1,"e":"The defining feature of a **floating charge** is that, until it **crystallises** (for example on liquidation, administration or the appointment of a receiver), the company remains **free to deal with the charged assets in the ordinary course of business** without the lender's consent. A buyer of stock sold in that way therefore takes it **free of the charge**, whether or not it knew of the charge or of its registration. The lender's security simply continues over whatever assets of the relevant class the company holds when crystallisation occurs, such as the sale proceeds or new stock."},{"t":"distributions-accounts","p":"FLK1","q":"Moorgate Print Ltd's last annual accounts show accumulated realised losses of £50,000. In the current year the company made a realised profit of £30,000. The directors, who are also its only shareholders, declared and paid themselves a dividend of £20,000, believing that any profit made in the current year could be distributed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The dividend is lawful, because it does not exceed the profit made by the company in the current year.","The dividend is unlawful, but the directors need not repay it, as they honestly thought it lawful.","The dividend is unlawful and the directors, as shareholders who knew the facts, must repay it.","The dividend is lawful, provided the directors are satisfied the company can pay its debts as they fall due.","The dividend is unlawful only if the company goes into insolvent liquidation within the next two years."],"a":2,"e":"A company may distribute only its **distributable profits**: **accumulated realised profits less accumulated realised losses** (**CA 2006 s.830**). After setting this year's £30,000 against the £50,000 brought-forward losses, the company still has a £20,000 deficit, so the dividend is unlawful. A shareholder must repay under **s.847** if they **knew or had reasonable grounds to believe** the distribution was unlawful, and knowing the facts is enough: **ignorance of the law is no defence** (It's a Wrap v Gula). Solvency alone does not make a dividend lawful."},{"t":"corporate-insolvency","p":"FLK1","q":"The directors of Pemberton Joinery Ltd believe the company is unable to pay its debts and want to put it into administration out of court as quickly as possible. Its bank holds a floating charge over the whole of the company's property, which is enforceable. The directors have identified an insolvency practitioner they would like to act.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the directors must do?","o":["Apply to court for an administration order, because directors can never appoint an administrator out of court.","Appoint their chosen administrator immediately, because the bank's consent is needed only for a court application.","Pass a special resolution of the members before the directors can file a notice of appointment at court.","Give the bank 5 business days' notice of intention to appoint, during which the bank may appoint its own choice.","Give the bank 2 business days' notice, after which the bank must accept the directors' chosen administrator."],"a":3,"e":"Directors may appoint an administrator **out of court** under **IA 1986 Sch.B1 para.22** where the company is or is likely to become unable to pay its debts, but they must first give **5 business days' notice of intention** to any **qualifying floating charge holder** (QFCH: the holder of a floating charge over the whole or substantially the whole of the company's property). During that period the bank may **appoint its own choice** of administrator instead. Directors do not need a members' resolution, and a court application is only one of the available routes."},{"t":"corporate-insolvency","p":"FLK1","q":"A supplier presented a winding-up petition against Elmbridge Ltd on 2 February 2026. The company continued trading and on 20 February paid £12,000 from its bank account to its electricity supplier. A winding-up order was made on 30 March 2026 and a liquidator was appointed. No application has been made to the court about the payment.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the status of the £12,000 payment?","o":["It is valid, because the winding up begins only when the winding-up order is made on 30 March.","It is valid unless the liquidator proves it was a preference made with a desire to prefer the supplier.","It is void, because it was made after the petition was presented and the court has not validated it.","It is void only if the electricity supplier knew that the winding-up petition had been presented.","It is voidable at the liquidator's option as a transaction at an undervalue made within two years."],"a":2,"e":"In a compulsory liquidation, **any disposition of the company's property made after the presentation of the winding-up petition is void** unless the court orders otherwise (**IA 1986 s.127**), so payments from the company's account are caught. The company can apply for a **validation order**, but none has been sought, so the liquidator can recover the money. The payee's knowledge is not a condition (option D). Because s.127 makes the payment void automatically, the liquidator need not prove a preference with a desire to prefer (option B), and paying for supplies received is not a transaction at an undervalue (option E)."},{"t":"insolvency-clawback-priority","p":"FLK1","q":"Fourteen months before Brookside Ltd went into insolvent liquidation, and at a time when it was unable to pay its debts, it repaid in full a £40,000 unsecured loan made to it by Daniel, one of its directors. Its other unsecured creditors are likely to receive nothing. The board says the repayment was made because Daniel had been pressing hard for payment.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the liquidator can challenge the repayment?","o":["No, because the repayment was made more than six months before the onset of insolvency.","No, because a payment made in response to pressure from a creditor can never be a preference.","Yes, as a transaction at an undervalue, because the company received nothing in return for the payment.","Yes, as a preference, because Daniel is connected, so a two-year period and a presumed desire apply.","Yes, but only if the liquidator proves the board was positively influenced by a desire to prefer Daniel."],"a":3,"e":"A **preference (IA 1986 s.239)** puts a creditor in a better position on insolvency than they would otherwise have been, and was influenced by a **desire to prefer**. For a **connected person** such as a director, the relevant period is **2 years** (not 6 months) before the onset of insolvency and the desire to prefer is **presumed**, so the burden is on Daniel to rebut it; mere pressure claimed by the board will not necessarily do so. Repaying a genuine debt is not an undervalue, because the company's liability is reduced pound for pound."},{"t":"insolvency-clawback-priority","p":"FLK1","q":"Holloway Engineering Ltd is in insolvent liquidation. A bank holding a floating charge over all its assets is owed £500,000. Tom, an employee, is owed £1,500 of unpaid wages for the two months before the liquidation. The company also owes HMRC £20,000 of corporation tax.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Tom's claim and HMRC's claim rank?","o":["Tom's claim is preferential in full, and HMRC's corporation tax claim is a secondary preferential debt.","£800 of Tom's claim is preferential and £700 is unsecured, and HMRC's corporation tax claim is unsecured.","£800 of Tom's claim is preferential and £700 is unsecured, and HMRC's claim is a secondary preferential debt.","Tom's claim is unsecured in full, and HMRC's corporation tax claim ranks ahead of the floating charge holder.","Tom's claim is preferential in full, and HMRC's corporation tax claim is unsecured and paid pari passu."],"a":1,"e":"Employees' unpaid wages for the **4 months** before insolvency are **ordinary preferential debts** up to **£800** each, with any excess ranking as an **unsecured** claim. HMRC is a **secondary preferential creditor** only for taxes the company **collected on behalf of others** (VAT, PAYE income tax and employees' NICs); **corporation tax is an unsecured debt**. Preferential debts are paid out of floating charge assets ahead of the floating charge holder, so the distinction matters to how much each recovers."},{"t":"personal-insolvency","p":"FLK1","q":"Victor was made bankrupt in October 2022. He owns his home jointly with his wife. His trustee in bankruptcy has taken no steps to sell or otherwise deal with Victor's interest in the home. Victor was discharged from bankruptcy in October 2023. It is now November 2026, and the trustee has written saying she intends to apply for an order for sale.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The trustee can apply for sale, because the home stays in the bankrupt's estate until all creditors are paid.","The trustee cannot apply, because Victor's interest in the home re-vested in him on his discharge in October 2023.","The trustee can apply, but the court must postpone any sale for one year to protect the interests of Victor's wife.","The trustee can apply, because the three-year period runs from Victor's discharge rather than the bankruptcy.","The trustee cannot apply, because Victor's interest re-vested in him three years after the bankruptcy began."],"a":4,"e":"Under the **'use it or lose it' rule (IA 1986 s.283A)**, if the trustee does not realise or deal with the bankrupt's interest in the family home within **3 years of the bankruptcy**, it **re-vests in the bankrupt** automatically. That happened in October 2025, so the trustee can no longer seek a sale. Discharge after one year does **not** itself return the home, which is why the trustee could have acted after October 2023 but before the three years ran out."},{"t":"personal-insolvency","p":"FLK1","q":"Sana, a self-employed photographer, has unsecured debts of £120,000 and proposes an individual voluntary arrangement (IVA). At the creditors' decision, creditors owed £100,000 vote: £80,000 in favour (including £10,000 owed to her brother) and £20,000 against. A credit card company owed £20,000 was entitled to vote but did not do so.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the outcome?","o":["The IVA is approved and binds the credit card company, even though it did not vote.","The IVA is not approved, because creditors owed 75% of all her debts did not vote in favour.","The IVA is approved but does not bind the credit card company, because it did not vote.","The IVA is not approved, because the brother's vote is ignored, leaving less than 75% in favour.","The IVA is approved, but the creditors who voted against it may still petition for her bankruptcy."],"a":0,"e":"An IVA is approved if at least **75% in value of the creditors voting** vote in favour, unless more than **50% in value of the unconnected creditors** voting are against it. Here 80% of those voting approved, and only £20,000 of the £90,000 of unconnected votes (about 22%) opposed it; the brother's vote counts towards the 75%. An approved IVA **binds every creditor entitled to vote**, whether or not they voted (s.260), so neither the card company nor the dissenting creditors can pursue her for those debts while it continues."},{"t":"income-tax","p":"FLK1","q":"Joseph was dismissed as sales director of Avonmere Ltd in July 2026. His service contract contains a clause entitling the company to pay him in lieu of notice, and the company paid him £10,000 under that clause, covering his full notice period. It also paid him £45,000 as non-contractual compensation for loss of office, to settle a potential unfair dismissal claim.\n\nHow much of these payments is taxable as employment income?","o":["£10,000","£15,000","£25,000","£45,000","£55,000"],"a":2,"e":"A payment made under a **contractual** pay in lieu of notice clause is **earnings** and is **taxed in full** (£10,000). A genuine **non-contractual** compensation payment for loss of employment is **tax-free up to £30,000** under ITEPA ss.401-403, with only the **excess** taxed, so £15,000 of the £45,000 is taxable. As the notice period has already been paid for, there is no further post-employment notice pay to carve out, giving **£25,000** taxable in total."},{"t":"income-tax","p":"FLK1","q":"Freya was employed as a marketing manager on a salary of £55,000 a year for several years until March 2026. On 6 April 2026 she started trading as a sole trader running an online design business. She made a trading loss of £18,000 in 2026/27 and had no other income in that year. She expects the business to be profitable from 2027/28.\n\nWhich loss relief is likely to give Freya the most valuable tax saving soonest?","o":["Carrying the loss forward against her future profits from the same trade.","Setting the loss against her total income of 2026/27 under general (sideways) relief.","Carrying the loss back against her total income of the three previous tax years, earliest year first.","Carrying the loss back against her trading profits of the three previous tax years, latest year first.","Setting the loss against income she receives from a company to which she transfers the business."],"a":2,"e":"A loss in the **first four tax years** of a new trade qualifies for **early years relief (ITA 2007 s.72)**, which sets it against **total income** of the **three tax years before** the loss year, **earliest first**, so Freya can reclaim tax paid on her salary now. Setting the loss against 2026/27 income achieves nothing because she has no other income, and carrying forward delays relief. Carry-back against trading profits, latest year first, describes **terminal loss relief**, which applies only when a trade ceases."},{"t":"cgt","p":"FLK1","q":"Eleni has owned 8% of the ordinary shares (carrying 8% of the votes) in Corbel Ltd, an unquoted trading company, since 2018. She worked elsewhere until she was appointed a non-executive director of Corbel in May 2025. In November 2026 she sells all her shares, realising a gain of £300,000. She is a higher rate taxpayer and has never claimed business asset disposal relief (BADR).\n\nWhich of the following best describes the capital gains tax position on her gain?","o":["BADR applies, so her gain after the annual exempt amount is taxed at 18%.","BADR is unavailable as she has not been an officer for two years, so 24% applies.","BADR is unavailable because a non-executive director is not an officer of the company.","BADR applies because she has held at least 5% of the shares and votes for two years.","BADR is unavailable because she needed to hold at least 10% of the shares in Corbel."],"a":1,"e":"For **BADR** on shares, **all** the conditions must be met **throughout the 2 years** ending with the disposal: a trading company, at least **5%** of the shares and votes (with the economic interest), and the seller being an **officer or employee**. Eleni has held enough shares since 2018, but she has been an officer (a non-executive director counts) for only about 18 months, so BADR fails. As a higher rate taxpayer, her gain above the £3,000 annual exempt amount is taxed at **24%** rather than the BADR rate of 18%."},{"t":"corporation-tax","p":"FLK1","q":"Saltmarsh Ltd prepares accounts to 31 March each year. For the year ended 31 March 2027 it made a trading loss of £90,000 and a chargeable gain of £20,000. In the year ended 31 March 2026 its total profits were £150,000. The company is continuing to trade and wants relief for the loss as soon as possible.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the company can relieve the loss?","o":["It can carry back the whole £90,000 against the previous year's profits, leaving the current gain taxable.","It can set the loss only against future trading profits, because a trading loss cannot be set against gains.","It can carry the loss back against the profits of the three previous years, taking the latest year first.","It can set the loss against its current gain or carry it back, choosing whichever year gives more relief.","It must first set £20,000 against the current year's gain, then may carry the remaining £70,000 back 12 months."],"a":4,"e":"Under **CTA 2010 s.37** a company's trading loss can be set against its **total profits** (income and gains) of the **same accounting period**, and only **after** that claim can any balance be **carried back 12 months**. So £20,000 relieves the current gain and the remaining £70,000 can be set against the previous year's profits. Unlike an individual under s.64, the company cannot skip the current period, and the **3-year carry-back** applies only to **terminal losses** in the final 12 months of trading."},{"t":"vat-iht-bpr","p":"FLK1","q":"Ravi opened a clothes shop on 1 September 2025. He is not registered for VAT. At the end of July 2026 his sales for the previous 12 months total £95,000, made up of £68,000 of children's clothing (zero-rated) and £27,000 of adult clothing (standard-rated). His rolling 12-month sales first went above £90,000 during July 2026, and he does not expect sales in any single 30-day period to exceed £90,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ravi's VAT position?","o":["He must notify HMRC by 30 August 2026 and will be registered from 1 September 2026.","He need not register, because his standard-rated sales are well below the £90,000 threshold.","He must notify HMRC by 30 August 2026 and will be registered from 1 August 2026.","He need not register until his turnover for a full tax year from 6 April exceeds £90,000.","He must register from 1 September 2025, because his first year's sales exceeded the threshold."],"a":0,"e":"Under the **historic test** (VATA 1994 Sch.1), a business must register if at the end of any month its **taxable supplies** in the previous **rolling 12 months** exceed **£90,000**. **Zero-rated supplies are taxable supplies** and count towards the threshold, so Ravi's £95,000 triggers registration at the end of July. He must **notify HMRC within 30 days** and is registered from the **first day of the second month** after the month the threshold was exceeded, which is 1 September 2026."}],"blp-p2":[{"t":"business-structures","p":"FLK1","q":"Amara and Tobias, both architects, are setting up a practice together. They want to be protected from personal liability for the practice's debts beyond what they put into it. They want each of them to be taxed as a self-employed person on their own share of the profits, rather than the business itself paying tax. They want to agree their own internal rules without having to hold board or shareholder meetings, and they accept that the practice's accounts will be filed publicly.\n\nWhich business structure best meets their requirements?","o":["A general partnership, with a written partnership agreement setting out how decisions are made.","A limited liability partnership, with a written members' agreement setting out how decisions are made.","A private company limited by shares, with each of them holding half the shares and acting as a director.","A limited partnership, with each of them registered as a limited partner in the firm.","Two separate sole trader businesses that share an office and split the running costs between them."],"a":1,"e":"**An LLP has separate legal personality and its members have limited liability, but it is tax transparent: each member is taxed on their share of the profits as if they were a partner**, and its internal management is governed by a members' agreement rather than company procedures. A company (C) also gives limited liability, but it pays corporation tax on its own profits and must follow board and shareholder decision-making rules. A general partnership (A) and sole traders (E) have unlimited liability, and a limited partnership (D) must have at least one general partner with unlimited liability, so both of them cannot be limited partners."},{"t":"formation","p":"FLK1","q":"Halden Ltd was formed three years ago. Its articles, adopted on formation with the agreement of all the members, contain a clause giving Rhona, a founder who holds 10% of the shares, the right to appoint one director. The articles state that this clause may be amended or removed only with Rhona's written consent, and Companies House was notified of this. The other shareholders, who together hold 90% of the shares, have now passed a special resolution at a general meeting deleting the clause. Rhona voted against and has not consented.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the resolution?","o":["It does not remove the clause, because the clause is an entrenched provision that can be changed only with Rhona's consent or the agreement of all the members.","It removes the clause, because a special resolution passed by at least 75% of the votes can amend any provision of a company's articles.","It removes the clause, because a private company cannot make a provision of its articles harder to change than by special resolution.","It does not remove the clause, because a shareholder holding more than 5% of the shares can veto any amendment to the articles affecting their rights.","It removes the clause, but Rhona can claim damages from the company for breach of the statutory contract formed by the articles."],"a":0,"e":"**An entrenched provision (s.22 CA 2006) is a provision of the articles that can be amended only if conditions more restrictive than a special resolution are met, such as a named member's consent.** Entrenchment can be set up on formation or with the agreement of all members, as here, so a special resolution alone cannot remove it (making B wrong), though it could still be changed by agreement of all the members or court order. Private companies can entrench provisions (C is wrong), there is no general 5% veto (D), and E is wrong because the clause has not been removed at all."},{"t":"formation","p":"FLK1","q":"Corvane plc was incorporated as a public company on 1 October 2026 and received its certificate of incorporation. It has allotted 50,000 ordinary shares of £1 each at par, and each shareholder has paid 25p per share. The directors want the company to sign a loan facility with a bank and start trading next week. No other documents have been filed with Companies House since incorporation.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The company may sign the facility and start trading at once, because its certificate of incorporation is conclusive evidence that it may do business.","The company must have its shares paid up in full before it is permitted to borrow money or start trading as a public company.","The company must re-register as a private company before it can borrow, because a new plc cannot borrow until it has filed its first accounts.","The company may start trading without restriction, but it must obtain a trading certificate before it is permitted to borrow money from the bank.","The company must obtain a trading certificate before doing business or borrowing, and its allotted share capital meets the requirement for one."],"a":4,"e":"**A company formed as a plc must not do business or exercise any borrowing powers until Companies House issues a trading certificate (s.761 CA 2006), which requires allotted share capital of at least £50,000 nominal, each share paid up to at least one quarter of its nominal value plus any premium.** Corvane has £50,000 nominal allotted and 25p paid on each £1 share, so it can apply (form SH50) and then trade and borrow. Full payment (B) is not required, the certificate of incorporation alone is not enough for a new plc (A), and the restriction covers doing business as well as borrowing (D)."},{"t":"directors","p":"FLK1","q":"Elif is the purchasing director of Northgate Ltd. Last month she recommended to the board that a new packaging contract be awarded to Brightpak Ltd, and the board agreed. Brightpak has now invited Elif and her husband on a week's all-expenses-paid luxury holiday in Barbados, saying it is a thank-you for her support. At a board meeting the other directors resolved that Elif may accept the holiday. Northgate's articles say nothing about benefits from third parties, and the shareholders have not been asked.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Elif's position if she accepts?","o":["She will not breach her duties, because the board has authorised her to accept the holiday at a properly held board meeting.","She will not breach her duties, because the contract had already been awarded to Brightpak before the holiday was offered to her.","She is likely to breach her duty not to accept benefits from third parties, because the board's resolution cannot authorise this benefit.","She will only need to declare the nature and extent of her interest in the Brightpak contract to the other directors.","She will breach her duties only if Northgate can show that it suffered a financial loss as a result of the Brightpak contract."],"a":2,"e":"**Under s.176 CA 2006 a director must not accept a benefit from a third party conferred because they are a director or because of anything they do as a director, unless it cannot reasonably be regarded as likely to give rise to a conflict of interest; unlike a s.175 conflict, the board cannot authorise it (only the members, or the articles, can).** A luxury holiday given as thanks for recommending a supplier is likely to give rise to a conflict, so the board resolution in A does not help. The fact that the contract has already been awarded (B) is no answer, because the duty covers benefits given for things already done, and no loss to the company (E) is needed for a breach. Declaring an interest (D) addresses conflicts in transactions with the company, not the receipt of a third-party benefit."},{"t":"directors","p":"FLK1","q":"Oscar was disqualified from acting as a company director for five years in 2025. In 2026 he began running the business of Brightwater Ltd, a catering company owned by his sister. He was never formally appointed, but he negotiated with suppliers, ordered stock and made all the major decisions, while his sister, the only registered director, followed his instructions. Brightwater has now gone into insolvent liquidation owing a food supplier £30,000 for stock Oscar ordered during this period.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Oscar's position in relation to the supplier's debt?","o":["He is not liable for the debt, because he was never formally appointed as a director of the company.","He is liable for the debt only if the liquidator proves that he is guilty of wrongful trading.","He is liable for the debt only if he signed the supply contracts in his own name rather than for the company.","He is jointly and severally liable with the company, because he was managing it in breach of his disqualification.","He is not liable for the debt, but the supply contracts are void because they were made by a disqualified person."],"a":3,"e":"**A person who is involved in the management of a company in breach of a disqualification order or undertaking commits an offence and is personally liable, jointly and severally with the company, for the company's debts incurred while they were so involved (CDDA 1986 ss.13 and 15).** Formal appointment is irrelevant (A): Oscar was managing the company and acting as a de facto director. Liability under s.15 is automatic, so there is no need to prove wrongful trading (B) or that he contracted in his own name (C), and the contracts remain binding on the company (E)."},{"t":"decision-making","p":"FLK1","q":"Carys is a director of Pinewood Ltd, which has unamended Model Articles and is not a money-lending company. Six months ago the company lent her £4,000 to help with a house move, and all of it is still outstanding. She has now asked the company to lend her a further £7,000 to pay for a family holiday. The board is willing to make the loan.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what approval, if any, is required for the new loan?","o":["No approval beyond a board resolution is needed, because each of the two loans is below the £10,000 limit for minor loans.","Approval by ordinary resolution of the members is needed, because the total of her loans would exceed the £10,000 limit for minor loans.","Approval by special resolution of the members is needed, because the loan is for a personal purpose rather than for company business.","No approval is needed, because loans to directors of up to £50,000 in total are exempt from the requirement for shareholder approval.","Approval by ordinary resolution of the members is needed, because every loan by a company to one of its directors needs approval, whatever the amount."],"a":1,"e":"**A company may not make a loan to a director unless it is approved by ordinary resolution of the members (s.197 CA 2006), subject to exceptions including minor loans where the total outstanding does not exceed £10,000 (s.207).** The limit is applied to the aggregate, so £4,000 plus £7,000 (£11,000) takes Carys over it, making A wrong. The £50,000 exception (D) applies only to loans to fund expenditure on company business, not a holiday, and the approval required is an ordinary resolution, not a special resolution (C); E is wrong because small loans within the exceptions need no approval."},{"t":"decision-making","p":"FLK1","q":"Ivybridge Ltd, a private company, has four shareholders, each with one vote per share: Salma (40%), George (30%), Ruth (20%) and Kenji (10%). The directors circulate a written resolution to all four shareholders proposing a special resolution to change the company's name. Within the time allowed, Salma and George return signed indications of agreement. Ruth and Kenji do not respond at all.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the outcome?","o":["The resolution is not passed, because a written special resolution needs the agreement of members holding at least 75% of the total voting rights of eligible members.","The resolution is passed, because every shareholder who responded agreed and members who do not respond are treated as abstaining.","The resolution is passed, because a written special resolution needs only the agreement of members holding more than half of the total voting rights.","The resolution is not passed, because a written special resolution needs the unanimous agreement of all the members of a private company.","The resolution is passed, because shareholders who do not respond within the time allowed are treated as having agreed to the resolution."],"a":0,"e":"**A written special resolution is passed only when members representing at least 75% of the total voting rights of all eligible members have signified agreement (s.296 CA 2006); non-responses count in effect as votes against.** Salma and George hold only 70%, so the resolution fails; unlike at a meeting, it is not measured against votes actually cast (B). More than half (C) is the threshold for a written ordinary resolution, unanimity (D) is not required, and silence is never treated as agreement (E)."},{"t":"shareholders-minority","p":"FLK1","q":"Leah holds 6% of the paid-up voting shares in Marlow Ltd. She is concerned about the conduct of one of the directors and wants the shareholders to vote on removing him. Last week she delivered a written request to the company asking the directors to call a general meeting, stating the general nature of the business and attaching the text of an ordinary resolution. The directors have told her they do not think a meeting is necessary.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the directors' obligation?","o":["They need not call a meeting, because only shareholders holding at least 10% of the paid-up voting shares can require one to be called.","They may decide whether to call the meeting, because calling a general meeting is a matter for the board's discretion under the Model Articles.","They need not act on her request, because Leah must first apply to the court for an order that a general meeting be held.","They need not act on her request, because Leah may instead call the general meeting herself at any time she chooses.","They must call the meeting within 21 days of her request, to be held on a date not more than 28 days after the notice convening it."],"a":4,"e":"**Members holding at least 5% of the paid-up voting share capital can require the directors to call a general meeting (s.303 CA 2006); the directors must call it within 21 days of the request, for a date not more than 28 days after the notice (s.304).** Leah's 6% is enough, so the board has no discretion (B), and 10% (A) is not the threshold for a private company. Only if the directors fail to comply can she call the meeting herself at the company's expense (s.305), so D is wrong, and a court order under s.306 (C) is a fallback for when a meeting is impracticable to hold."},{"t":"partnerships","p":"FLK1","q":"Ade, Bea and Cyril run a removals business in partnership. They have no written or oral agreement about money. Ade contributed £90,000 of capital when the firm started and Bea contributed £10,000; Cyril contributed no capital but works full time in the business. Later, when the firm needed a new lorry, Ade lent it a further £20,000 beyond his capital contribution. This year the firm made a profit of £120,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ade's entitlement?","o":["Three quarters of the profit, reflecting the proportion of the total capital that he contributed to the firm.","A one third share of the profit, plus interest at 5% a year on both his capital and his £20,000 loan to the firm.","A one third share of the profit, plus interest at 5% a year on his £20,000 loan to the firm, but no interest on his capital.","A one third share of the profit, with no interest on either his capital or his £20,000 loan to the firm.","A one third share of the profit, plus a reasonable salary for managing the firm's finances and its vehicle fleet."],"a":2,"e":"**Under the Partnership Act 1890 default rules, partners share capital and profits equally whatever they contributed (s.24(1)), receive no interest on capital (s.24(4)) and no salary (s.24(6)), but a partner who advances money beyond their agreed capital is entitled to interest at 5% a year on the advance (s.24(3)).** So Ade receives one third of the profit plus 5% interest on the £20,000 loan. Sharing in proportion to capital (A) and interest on capital (B) would need an agreement, and D wrongly ignores the statutory interest on advances."},{"t":"partnerships","p":"FLK1","q":"Kemi joined a firm of accountants as a partner on 1 February 2026. In December 2025 the firm ordered specialist accounting software from a supplier for £15,000, payable within 90 days. The invoice was not paid, and the firm now cannot pay its debts. The supplier has started proceedings against all the current partners, including Kemi. Kemi did not agree with anyone to take on the firm's existing debts when she joined.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Kemi's liability for the software debt?","o":["She is jointly liable with all the current partners, because a partner is liable for every debt of the firm that is unpaid while she is a partner.","She is liable for the debt, because payment of the invoice did not fall due until after she had become a partner in the firm.","She is liable for the debt, but only up to the amount of the capital that she contributed to the firm when she joined it.","She is not liable for the debt, because an incoming partner is not liable for obligations incurred before she became a partner.","She is not liable for the debt, provided she can show that the supplier did not know that she had joined the firm."],"a":3,"e":"**A person admitted as a partner does not thereby become liable to creditors for anything done before they became a partner (Partnership Act 1890 s.17(1)), unless they agree to assume those debts.** The debt was incurred when the order was placed in December 2025, before Kemi joined, so the later due date (B) does not matter and A is wrong. Partners in a general partnership do not have liability limited to capital (C), and the supplier's knowledge (E) is irrelevant to an incoming partner's liability for earlier debts."},{"t":"equity-finance","p":"FLK1","q":"Riverton Ltd was incorporated in 2015 with unamended Model Articles. Its issued share capital consists of ordinary shares and non-voting preference shares. The directors want to allot 10,000 new ordinary shares to an outside investor, who will pay £5 per share in cash, and they want to do so without first offering the shares to the existing shareholders. The existing ordinary shareholders do not wish to buy any of the new shares, but they have not given written waivers of any rights.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the shareholder resolutions needed before the allotment?","o":["None, because the directors of a private company have authority to allot shares under the Act unless the articles prohibit it.","An ordinary resolution authorising the directors to allot, and a special resolution disapplying the statutory pre-emption rights.","A special resolution amending the articles to raise the maximum number of shares the company is permitted to issue.","Only a special resolution disapplying the statutory pre-emption rights, because the directors already have authority to allot.","Only an ordinary resolution authorising the directors to allot, because pre-emption rights do not apply in private companies."],"a":1,"e":"**Directors of a private company may allot without shareholder authority under s.550 CA 2006 only where it has one class of shares; with more than one class, authority must be given by the articles or an ordinary resolution under s.551.** Because the shares are ordinary shares issued wholly for cash, the s.561 pre-emption rights apply and, absent waivers, must be disapplied by special resolution (s.570 or s.571), so A, D and E are wrong. A company incorporated under the 2006 Act has no authorised share capital limit unless its articles impose one, so C is unnecessary."},{"t":"equity-finance","p":"FLK1","q":"Ashgrove Ltd has agreed to buy back the 20% shareholding of Pavel, a retiring shareholder, for £100,000. The company has ample distributable profits to fund the whole price. To help its cash flow, the directors propose that the contract provide for £50,000 to be paid on completion of the buyback and the remaining £50,000 twelve months later. The company has unamended Model Articles and the shares are not held under an employees' share scheme.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the proposed buyback?","o":["It is lawful if the contract is approved by an ordinary resolution of the members, on which Pavel may vote his shares.","It is lawful if the contract is approved by a special resolution of the members, because payment is being deferred.","It is lawful, because a private company may agree any payment terms it chooses when buying back shares out of profits.","It is lawful only if the directors make a solvency statement and an auditor's report is annexed to it before payment.","It is unlawful as proposed, because shares bought back by a company must be paid for in full at the time of purchase."],"a":4,"e":"**A company buying back its own shares must pay for them in full on purchase (s.691 CA 2006); payment by instalments is not allowed, except for a private company's purchase for an employees' share scheme.** So the deferred payment structure is unlawful whatever resolution is passed, making A, B and C wrong; in any event, Pavel's votes on the shares being bought would not count on the ordinary resolution approving the contract (s.695). A directors' statement and auditor's report (D) are needed only for a payment out of capital, and Ashgrove is using distributable profits."},{"t":"debt-security","p":"FLK1","q":"In January 2026 Mayfield Ltd granted Bank A a floating charge over all its assets and undertaking. The charge contains no restriction on the company granting later charges. In June 2026 Mayfield granted Bank B a fixed charge over its warehouse to secure a new loan. Both charges were delivered to Companies House for registration within 21 days of creation. Mayfield has now gone into liquidation and the warehouse has been sold, but the proceeds are not enough to repay both banks in full.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the warehouse proceeds will be applied?","o":["Bank B will be paid first, because a later fixed charge ranks ahead of an earlier floating charge with no negative pledge.","Bank A will be paid first, because both charges were properly registered and charges rank in the order in which they were created.","The two banks will share the proceeds in proportion to the amounts that the company owes each of them under their loans.","Bank A will be paid first, because its floating charge crystallised over the warehouse when the company went into liquidation.","Bank B will be paid first, but only if Bank A gave its written consent to the creation of the later fixed charge."],"a":0,"e":"**A later fixed charge has priority over an earlier floating charge over the same asset, because the floating charge leaves the company free to deal with its assets in the ordinary course of business, including by creating fixed charges; the exception is where the floating charge contains a negative pledge of which the later chargee had actual notice.** Bank A's charge has no negative pledge, so Bank B ranks first. The order-of-creation rule (B) applies between two charges of the same type, and crystallisation on liquidation (D) does not reverse priority already lost to the fixed charge; no consent was needed (E)."},{"t":"debt-security","p":"FLK1","q":"Tanfield Storage Ltd has just bought a freehold warehouse from Bexley Holdings Ltd. Under the sale agreement, Tanfield took the warehouse subject to an existing fixed charge that Bexley had granted to a bank three years ago, which was registered against Bexley at Companies House at the time. Tanfield has not granted any security of its own over the warehouse. Tanfield's finance director asks whether Tanfield must now register the bank's charge at Companies House within 21 days.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Tanfield must register the charge within 21 days of completion, or the charge will be void against its liquidator, administrator and creditors.","Tanfield must register the charge within 21 days of completion, or the debt secured by the charge will become immediately repayable.","Tanfield may register the charge but is not obliged to, and the charge will not be void against anyone if it is not registered.","Tanfield need not register the charge at Companies House, but it must apply to the court for an order confirming the bank's priority.","Tanfield must register the charge, but because the bank created it, the 21 days run from the date the charge was originally made."],"a":2,"e":"**Where a company acquires property that is already subject to a charge, it may register that charge at Companies House, but registration is optional, there is no time limit and the charge is not void if it is not registered (CA 2006 s.859C); the voidness rule in s.859H applies only to charges the company itself creates.** A and B describe the consequences of a company failing to register its own charge within 21 days, which do not apply here. No court order is needed (D), and E wrongly applies a deadline that does not exist for acquired charges."},{"t":"distributions-accounts","p":"FLK1","q":"Wenlock Ltd has unamended Model Articles and ample profits available for distribution. At a board meeting the directors recommended a final dividend of 10p per ordinary share for the last financial year. At the general meeting called to consider it, Farah, who holds 60% of the shares, proposes an ordinary resolution declaring a dividend of 15p per share instead, arguing that the company has more than enough profits to pay it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the dividend the shareholders can declare?","o":["A dividend of 15p per share, because an ordinary resolution of the shareholders can declare any dividend that is covered by distributable profits.","A dividend of 15p per share, but only if it is approved by a special resolution rather than by an ordinary resolution of the shareholders.","No dividend at all, because once the directors have recommended a dividend it is payable without any resolution of the shareholders.","A dividend of 10p per share or less, because the shareholders cannot declare a final dividend larger than the amount recommended by the directors.","A dividend of 15p per share, because a shareholder holding more than half the votes can override the directors' recommendation."],"a":3,"e":"**Under Model Articles art.30, a final dividend is declared by ordinary resolution but must not exceed the amount recommended by the directors.** The shareholders can therefore declare 10p per share or a smaller amount, but not 15p, whether by ordinary or special resolution (A and B are wrong). A recommendation alone does not create an entitlement: the dividend must still be declared (C). Farah's majority holding (E) does not change this: the cap on the amount applies however large the majority voting for the resolution."},{"t":"distributions-accounts","p":"FLK1","q":"Fernbrook Ltd qualifies as a small company and has never had its accounts audited. Its financial year ends on 31 December 2026. On 15 November 2026 Hamid, who holds 12% of the nominal value of the company's issued shares, deposited a notice at the registered office requiring the company to obtain an audit of its accounts for the year ending 31 December 2026. The directors say an audit would be an unnecessary expense.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The company need not have its accounts audited, because small companies are always exempt from audit whatever their members want.","The company must have its accounts audited, because members holding at least 10% of the shares gave notice at least one month before the year end.","The company need not have its accounts audited, because only members holding at least 25% of the shares can require an audit of a small company.","The company must have its accounts audited, but only if the members also pass an ordinary resolution appointing an auditor for that year.","The company need not have its accounts audited, because a notice requiring an audit must be given before the start of the financial year concerned."],"a":1,"e":"**A small company is exempt from audit (s.477 CA 2006), but members holding at least 10% of the nominal value of its issued shares can require an audit by notice deposited not later than one month before the end of the financial year (s.476).** Hamid holds 12% and gave notice on 15 November, more than a month before 31 December, so the exemption is lost for that year (A and E are wrong). The threshold is 10%, not 25% (C), and the members' notice itself removes the exemption without any further resolution (D); the directors can then appoint an auditor."},{"t":"corporate-insolvency","p":"FLK1","q":"The directors of Kingsley Ltd want to close the company down. It is solvent and has a large cash balance to return to its shareholders. On 1 March all three directors made a statutory declaration of solvency, with a statement of assets and liabilities, stating that the company will pay its debts in full, with interest, within 10 months. Because of delays in arranging the general meeting, the special resolution to wind the company up will not be proposed until 20 April, seven weeks later.\n\nWhat advice should be given to the directors?","o":["They should make a fresh declaration of solvency, because it must be made within the five weeks before the special resolution for a members' voluntary liquidation.","They may rely on the declaration already made, because it remains valid for up to 12 months after the date on which the directors made it.","They need not worry about the timing, because the shareholders, not the directors, decide whether the liquidation is a members' voluntary liquidation.","They should withdraw the declaration, because a members' voluntary liquidation must be started by an ordinary resolution within 14 days of it.","They should apply to the court for a winding-up order, because a solvent company cannot be wound up by its members without one."],"a":0,"e":"**For a members' voluntary liquidation, the directors (or a majority of them) must make a statutory declaration of solvency within the five weeks immediately before the special resolution to wind up (IA 1986 s.89).** A declaration made on 1 March will be outside that window on 20 April, so the directors should make a fresh one; without a valid declaration the winding up would be a creditors' voluntary liquidation. The 12-month figure (B) is the maximum period within which the debts must be paid, an MVL starts with a special resolution, not an ordinary resolution (D), and no court order is needed (E)."},{"t":"corporate-insolvency","p":"FLK1","q":"Delmont Retail Ltd went into administration last week. It owes its landlord three months' rent for its main shop, and the lease allows the landlord to forfeit the lease by peaceable re-entry if rent is unpaid for 21 days. The landlord wants to change the locks this weekend. A finance company that supplied the shop's tills on hire purchase also wants to repossess them because instalments are overdue.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the landlord and the finance company can take these steps?","o":["They may take these steps at once, because rights of forfeiture and repossession of goods are proprietary rights unaffected by administration.","They may take these steps once they have given the administrator 14 days' written notice of their intention to do so.","They must wait until the creditors have decided whether to approve the administrator's proposals, after which they may act freely.","Only the finance company may act, because the moratorium protects the company's own property and the tills belong to the finance company.","They may take these steps only with the administrator's consent or the court's permission, because of the moratorium in administration."],"a":4,"e":"**While a company is in administration, the moratorium (IA 1986 Sch.B1 paras 42-43) prevents any forfeiture of a lease by peaceable re-entry, any repossession of goods held under a hire-purchase agreement, any enforcement of security and any legal process without the administrator's consent or the court's permission.** So neither creditor can act on its own initiative (A is wrong). The moratorium expressly covers hire-purchase goods even though they belong to the finance company (D), and there is no 14-day notice route (B) or automatic release on approval of the proposals (C)."},{"t":"insolvency-clawback-priority","p":"FLK1","q":"Sunil is a director of Copperleaf Ltd and holds 30% of its shares. In 2024 he lent the company £50,000, unsecured. Eighteen months before Copperleaf went into insolvent liquidation, and at a time when it was able to pay its debts, the company granted Sunil a floating charge over all its assets to secure the existing £50,000 loan. Sunil provided no further money, goods or services to the company after the charge was granted.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the validity of Sunil's floating charge?","o":["It is valid, because the company was able to pay its debts at the time the charge was granted to Sunil.","It is valid, because it was granted more than 12 months before the onset of the company's insolvency.","It is invalid, because it was granted to a connected person within two years and no new consideration was given.","It is invalid only if the liquidator proves that the company was influenced by a desire to put Sunil in a better position.","It is valid as to the £50,000, because a charge securing money actually lent to the company counts as given for value."],"a":2,"e":"**A floating charge granted to a connected person within two years before the onset of insolvency is invalid except to the extent of new consideration (money, goods or services, or discharge of a debt) given at the same time as or after its creation (IA 1986 s.245); for connected persons, the company's solvency at the time is irrelevant.** As a director, Sunil is connected, so A and B (which describe the rules for unconnected chargees) are wrong. The existing loan was advanced before the charge, so it is not new consideration (E), and a desire to prefer (D) is an element of a preference claim under s.239, not s.245."},{"t":"insolvency-clawback-priority","p":"FLK1","q":"Ten months before Hartwell Fabrications Ltd went into insolvent liquidation, and when it was already unable to pay its debts, it sold a surplus cutting machine worth £80,000 to an unconnected buyer for £50,000. The directors had tried to sell it at full value for several months without success. They accepted the lower price because the company urgently needed cash to pay wages and keep trading through a seasonal downturn, and their accountant had advised that this would give the business a realistic chance of recovery. The liquidator is considering a claim against the buyer.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome of a claim that the sale was a transaction at an undervalue?","o":["It will fail, because a transaction at an undervalue can be challenged only where the other party is connected with the company.","It will succeed, because the sale was at a significant undervalue within two years and the company was unable to pay its debts.","It will fail, because the sale took place more than six months before the onset of the company's insolvency.","It will fail, because the company sold in good faith for its business with reasonable grounds for believing the sale would benefit it.","It will fail, because the liquidator's only remedy is to bring a wrongful trading claim against the directors personally."],"a":3,"e":"**The court will not make an order under IA 1986 s.238 if satisfied that the company entered into the transaction in good faith, for the purpose of carrying on its business, and that there were reasonable grounds for believing it would benefit the company (s.238(5)).** The facts (a genuine attempt to sell at full value, urgent need for working capital, professional advice) point strongly to that defence, so B overlooks it. The relevant time for an undervalue is two years for any recipient, connected or not, so A and C are wrong (six months is the period for a preference to an unconnected person), and the s.238 claim exists alongside any wrongful trading claim (E)."},{"t":"personal-insolvency","p":"FLK1","q":"Nadia was made bankrupt on a creditor's petition on 5 February 2026, and the Official Receiver became her trustee. She has not yet been discharged. In September 2026 her aunt died and left Nadia £30,000 in her will. Nadia told the trustee about the inheritance within a week of receiving it. She would like to use the money to pay a deposit on a flat.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position regarding the inheritance?","o":["It vested automatically in the trustee on the date of the bankruptcy order, because it forms part of her estate as an expectancy.","The trustee can claim it for the estate by written notice, because it was acquired after the bankruptcy order but before discharge.","Nadia can keep it, because only property she owned when the bankruptcy order was made forms part of her bankruptcy estate.","The trustee can obtain it only by applying to the court for an income payments order covering the next three years.","Nadia can keep it, provided she discloses the inheritance to anyone from whom she obtains credit of more than £500."],"a":1,"e":"**Property acquired by a bankrupt after the bankruptcy order but before discharge (after-acquired property) does not vest automatically, but the trustee can claim it for the estate by written notice, normally within 42 days of first learning of it (IA 1986 s.307).** An inheritance received in September 2026, before Nadia's discharge, can therefore be claimed, so C and E are wrong. It did not exist at the date of the order and so did not vest automatically (A), and an income payments order (D) deals with surplus income, not capital such as an inheritance."},{"t":"income-tax","p":"FLK1","q":"Gwen has been a self-employed graphic designer for several years. None of her income is taxed at source. Her income tax and Class 4 NICs liability for 2025/26 was £8,000, and she made no payments on account for that year because her liability for 2024/25 was below £1,000. She expects her total liability for 2026/27 to be £10,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes when Gwen must pay her income tax and Class 4 NICs for 2026/27?","o":["£4,000 on 31 January 2027 and £4,000 on 31 July 2027, with a balancing payment of £2,000 on 31 January 2028.","£5,000 on 31 January 2027 and £5,000 on 31 July 2027, with no balancing payment due on 31 January 2028.","The whole £10,000 on 31 January 2028, because she made no payments on account for the previous tax year.","£4,000 on 31 July 2027 and £4,000 on 31 January 2028, with a balancing payment of £2,000 on 31 July 2028.","£2,500 at the end of each quarter of the 2026/27 tax year, based on her estimated liability for that year."],"a":0,"e":"**Under self-assessment, a taxpayer whose tax is not mainly collected at source makes two payments on account, each normally 50% of the previous year's income tax and Class 4 NICs liability, on 31 January in the tax year and 31 July after it, with any balancing payment due on the following 31 January.** For 2026/27 each payment on account is 50% of £8,000 (£4,000), due on 31 January 2027 and 31 July 2027, and the remaining £2,000 is due on 31 January 2028. B wrongly bases the instalments on the current year's estimate, C is wrong because payments on account are required whenever the previous year's liability was £1,000 or more, D puts each payment six months too late, and quarterly instalments (E) apply to large companies paying corporation tax, not individuals."},{"t":"cgt","p":"FLK1","q":"Jonah, a sole trader who manufactures garden furniture, sold the freehold workshop he had used wholly in his business throughout his ownership in June 2026 for £500,000, realising a chargeable gain of £180,000. In September 2027 he bought a larger freehold factory for £420,000, which he uses wholly in the same business. He wants to claim the relief available for replacing business assets.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of a claim?","o":["The whole gain of £180,000 is rolled over, so no tax is payable now and the factory's base cost is reduced to £240,000.","£80,000 of the gain is rolled over and £100,000 is chargeable now, and the factory's base cost is reduced to £340,000.","£80,000 of the gain is chargeable now and £100,000 is rolled over, and the factory's base cost is reduced to £320,000.","No relief is available, because the factory was bought more than one year after the sale of the original workshop.","£80,000 of the gain is chargeable now and the remaining £100,000 is held over for up to ten years without reducing the factory's base cost."],"a":2,"e":"**Rollover relief (TCGA s.152) applies where qualifying business assets are replaced within one year before to three years after the disposal; if only part of the proceeds is reinvested, the amount not reinvested (up to the whole gain) is taxed now and the rest of the gain is deducted from the new asset's base cost.** Jonah reinvested £420,000 of £500,000, so £80,000 is chargeable now and £100,000 is rolled over, giving a base cost of £420,000 − £100,000 = £320,000. The time limit is three years after, not one (D), B reverses the figures, and the ten-year hold-over (E) applies only where the new asset is a depreciating asset, which a freehold factory is not."},{"t":"cgt","p":"FLK1","q":"Mei has run a bakery business as a sole trader since 2015. On 1 September 2026 she transferred the whole business as a going concern, with all its assets except cash, to a new company, Mei's Bakes Ltd. The business was worth £400,000 and the transfer produced a total chargeable gain of £200,000. In return, the company issued Mei shares worth £300,000 and credited £100,000 to a loan account in her name. Mei claims incorporation relief in her tax return.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the relief?","o":["The whole £200,000 gain is deferred, and the base cost of her shares is reduced to £100,000.","The relief is not available, because part of the consideration was credited to a loan account instead of being satisfied in shares.","£100,000 of the gain is deferred and £100,000 is chargeable now, and the base cost of her shares is reduced to £200,000.","The whole £200,000 gain is deferred automatically, without any claim, and the base cost of her shares is reduced to £100,000.","£150,000 of the gain is deferred and £50,000 is chargeable now, and the base cost of her shares is reduced to £150,000."],"a":4,"e":"**Incorporation relief (TCGA s.162) defers the gain on transferring a business as a going concern with all its assets (other than cash) to a company for shares; where part of the consideration is not shares, only the proportion gain × (value of shares ÷ total consideration) is deferred, by deducting it from the base cost of the shares.** Here £200,000 × £300,000/£400,000 = £150,000 is deferred, £50,000 is chargeable now, and the shares' base cost is £300,000 − £150,000 = £150,000. A and D ignore the loan account, C deducts the loan account from the gain pound for pound instead of apportioning, and partial non-share consideration restricts the relief rather than ruling it out (B); for transfers from 6 April 2026 the relief must be claimed, so D is wrong on that point too."},{"t":"corporation-tax","p":"FLK1","q":"In September 2026 Redcliffe Printing Ltd sold a plot of land that it had held as an investment. It bought the land in 2012 for £300,000, paying £4,000 in legal fees and stamp duty land tax, and sold it for £500,000, paying £6,000 in legal and agent's fees. The indexation allowance on the allowable acquisition expenditure (including the acquisition costs), calculated to December 2017, is £45,000. The company has no capital losses.\n\nWhat is the chargeable gain that will be included in Redcliffe Printing Ltd's taxable total profits?","o":["£190,000","£155,000","£142,000","£145,000","£200,000"],"a":3,"e":"**A company's chargeable gain is the disposal proceeds less incidental costs of disposal, acquisition cost and incidental costs of acquisition, less an indexation allowance frozen at December 2017; unlike individuals, companies get no annual exempt amount, and the gain is taxed at the corporation tax rate as part of total profits.** The gain is £500,000 − £6,000 − £300,000 − £4,000 − £45,000 = £145,000. A (£190,000) omits indexation, C (£142,000) wrongly deducts an individual's £3,000 annual exempt amount, B (£155,000) ignores the incidental costs of acquisition and disposal, and E (£200,000) deducts only the purchase price."},{"t":"corporation-tax","p":"FLK1","q":"Thornhill Design Ltd is a close company with an accounting period ending on 31 December. On 1 July 2026 it lent £40,000 interest-free to Isla, a director who works full time for the company and owns 40% of its shares. Isla intends to repay the loan when she sells her flat, which she expects to happen in 2028. The company does not carry on a money-lending business.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the corporation tax consequences for the company if none of the loan has been repaid by 30 September 2027?","o":["There are none, because Isla works full time for the company and the loan is for less than £50,000.","The company must pay a temporary tax charge of £14,300, which will be repaid to it after Isla repays the loan.","The company must pay a temporary tax charge of £13,500, which will be repaid to it after Isla repays the loan.","The company must pay a permanent tax charge of £14,300, which will not be repaid even if Isla later repays the loan.","There are none for the company, but Isla must pay income tax on the £40,000 as if she had received a dividend."],"a":1,"e":"**If a close company lends money to a participator and the loan is not repaid within nine months after the end of the accounting period in which it was made, the company must pay a temporary tax charge under CTA 2010 s.455 at the dividend upper rate (35.75% for loans made on or after 6 April 2026), which is refunded after the loan is repaid, released or written off.** The charge is £40,000 × 35.75% = £14,300; C uses the 33.75% rate that applied only to loans made before 6 April 2026, and the charge is not permanent (D). The exception (A) covers only loans totalling up to £15,000 to a full-time director owning no more than 5%, and Isla is taxed on the loan as a dividend (E) only if it is written off."},{"t":"vat-iht-bpr","p":"FLK1","q":"Penhallow Builders Ltd is registered for VAT. In its latest quarterly VAT period it made standard-rated supplies of building services of £60,000 (excluding VAT). During the same period it paid, all at the standard rate and each supported by a valid VAT invoice: £20,000 plus £4,000 VAT for building materials; £15,000 plus £3,000 VAT for a new van used only for the business; £30,000 plus £6,000 VAT for a car for its sales manager, who also uses it privately; and £1,000 plus £200 VAT for a dinner to entertain prospective clients.\n\nAssume a standard rate of VAT of 20%.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the company's VAT position for the period?","o":["It must pay HMRC £5,000.","It must pay HMRC £8,000.","It must pay HMRC £4,800.","It is entitled to a repayment from HMRC of £1,000.","It is entitled to a repayment from HMRC of £1,200."],"a":0,"e":"**A registered business pays HMRC its output tax less recoverable input tax, but input tax on business entertainment of non-employees and on cars not used exclusively for business is blocked.** Output tax is £60,000 × 20% = £12,000; recoverable input tax is £4,000 (materials) + £3,000 (van) = £7,000, so the company pays £5,000. C (£4,800) wrongly recovers the VAT on client entertainment, D and E wrongly recover the VAT on the car (with or without the entertainment), and B ignores the input tax on the van, which is a commercial vehicle used only for the business."},{"t":"vat-iht-bpr","p":"FLK1","q":"Harriet died in October 2026. Since 2010 she had owned 40% of the shares in Ashdown Engineering Ltd, an unquoted trading company; the shares are worth £800,000. She also personally owned the freehold factory from which the company has traded since 2012, worth £600,000; the company paid her no rent. Harriet made no lifetime gifts and no allowance was transferred to her from a spouse. No member of her family holds shares in Ashdown, and there is no binding contract for the sale of either asset.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the business property relief available on her death?","o":["100% relief on both the shares and the factory, because the total value of the two assets is below the £2.5 million allowance.","100% relief on the shares, and 50% relief on the factory because it was used by a company in which she held shares.","100% relief on the shares, and no relief on the factory because she did not control the company that used it.","50% relief on the shares because she held a minority stake, and no relief on the factory because it was owned outside the company.","No relief on the shares or the factory, because a minority shareholding in a company is not relevant business property."],"a":2,"e":"**Unquoted shares in a trading company qualify for 100% BPR whatever the size of the holding (within the £2.5 million allowance), but land or buildings owned personally and used by a company qualify for 50% relief only if the transferor controlled the company (more than 50% of the votes).** Harriet's 40% holding qualifies for 100% relief, so D and E are wrong, but she did not control Ashdown, so the factory gets no relief at all, which rules out B. The £2.5 million allowance (A) limits how much property gets 100% relief; it does not make non-qualifying property qualify."}],"blp-p3":[{"t":"business-structures","p":"FLK1","q":"Grace and Daniel set up a limited partnership, registered under the Limited Partnerships Act 1907, to run a boutique hotel. Grace is the general partner and runs the hotel. Daniel is the limited partner: he contributed £50,000, and the partnership agreement says he is not to be involved in running the business. During Grace's six-month illness last year, Daniel managed the hotel day to day, hiring staff and ordering supplies. The partnership now cannot pay a laundry company £70,000 for services ordered while Daniel was managing the hotel.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Daniel's liability to the laundry company?","o":["He is liable only up to his £50,000 contribution, because he is registered as a limited partner.","He is not liable at all, because the limited partnership is a separate legal person that owes the debt.","He is liable for the debt as if he were a general partner, because he took part in management when it was incurred.","He is liable only if the laundry company knew that he was managing the hotel when it agreed to supply.","He is liable only if Grace's personal assets are not enough to pay the debt in full."],"a":2,"e":"A **limited partner who takes part in managing the business loses limited liability** and is liable, as if a general partner, for the firm's debts incurred while doing so (Limited Partnerships Act 1907). So the cap at his £50,000 contribution no longer protects Daniel for this debt, and nothing in the rule depends on what the laundry company knew. A limited partnership in England and Wales has **no separate legal personality** (unlike an LLP), so the answer saying the firm alone owes the debt is wrong."},{"t":"business-structures","p":"FLK1","q":"Leah owns a canal boat. Kofi, a qualified skipper, agrees to run sightseeing trips on it. They agree to split the gross ticket takings equally, and each pays their own costs: Leah pays the mooring fees and maintenance, and Kofi pays for fuel and advertising. They have no other arrangement. Kofi orders £8,000 of leaflets from a printer under the name 'Canal Cruises' and does not pay for them. The printer wants to sue Leah.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Leah is liable to the printer?","o":["She is liable, because people who share the takings of a business they carry on together are partners.","She is liable, because the boat used to generate the takings belongs to her.","She is liable for half the debt, because she receives half of the takings.","She is not liable, because a partnership can exist only where the parties have signed a written agreement.","She is not liable, because sharing gross returns does not of itself make the two of them partners."],"a":4,"e":"A partnership is persons carrying on a business **in common with a view of profit** (Partnership Act 1890 s.1), but **sharing gross returns** (takings before expenses) **does not of itself create a partnership** (s.2(2)); here each bears their own costs, so there are no shared profits. Owning the asset that earns the takings is not enough either, and if Leah were a partner she would be liable for the whole debt, not half. The written-agreement answer is wrong because a partnership can arise from conduct alone; it is the absence of profit-sharing that saves Leah."},{"t":"formation","p":"FLK1","q":"The articles of Rowan Digital Ltd, adopted on incorporation, provide that Bilal, who designed the company's original website, 'shall be retained as the company's web developer for so long as he holds shares in the company'. Bilal holds 15% of the shares. The board has now appointed a different web developer. Bilal wants to rely on the articles to require the company to reinstate him.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Bilal's position?","o":["He can enforce the clause, because the articles form a contract between the company and each of its members.","He cannot enforce the clause, because the right is given to him as web developer and not in his capacity as a member.","He can enforce the clause, because the right is tied to his continuing to hold shares in the company.","He cannot enforce the clause, because only the company, and never a member, can enforce the articles.","He can enforce the clause only if he holds more than 25% of the shares in the company."],"a":1,"e":"Under **CA 2006 s.33** the articles bind the company and its members as a contract, but only as to rights held **in their capacity as members**; a right given in some **other capacity** (an 'outsider right') cannot be enforced (Eley v Positive Government Security Life Assurance). Linking the right to his shareholding does not change the capacity in which it is given: it is still a right to be employed as web developer. Members can enforce genuine membership rights under s.33, so the answer saying only the company can enforce the articles is wrong, and no shareholding threshold applies."},{"t":"directors","p":"FLK1","q":"Westfield Bakery Ltd has three directors, Aisha, Brendan and Callum, and unamended Model Articles. The board is to consider a one-off contract worth £3,000 to buy flour from Millstone Ltd. Brendan's wife owns all the shares in Millstone; Brendan himself holds none. No resolution has been passed disapplying any of the articles.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the board should deal with the contract?","o":["Brendan need not declare anything, because the interest belongs to his wife and not to him.","Brendan must declare his interest, and the meeting must be adjourned because the board cannot then be quorate.","Brendan must declare his interest, and the contract then needs approval by ordinary resolution of the shareholders.","Brendan must declare his interest, after which he may vote on the contract because it has been disclosed.","Brendan must declare his interest, and Aisha and Callum can then approve the contract without him."],"a":4,"e":"A director must **declare the nature and extent of any direct or indirect interest** in a proposed transaction before the company enters into it (**CA 2006 s.177**); his wife's ownership of the supplier is an indirect interest. Under **Model Articles art.14** an interested director **cannot vote or count in the quorum**, but the default quorum is two, so Aisha and Callum can still decide; declaring the interest does not give Brendan a vote. No shareholder approval is needed: at £3,000 the contract is far below the thresholds for a substantial property transaction (s.190)."},{"t":"directors","p":"FLK1","q":"Marcus, a chartered accountant, was appointed a non-executive director of Hollins Haulage Ltd. He attended board meetings but never read the management accounts sent to directors beforehand, relying entirely on the finance director. Had he read them, he would have seen obvious signs that the finance director was making unauthorised payments, which have cost the company £120,000. The company is considering a claim against Marcus.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the standard against which Marcus's conduct will be judged?","o":["That of a reasonably diligent non-executive director, ignoring his accountancy qualification entirely.","His own actual knowledge, skill and experience only, because he is a non-executive director.","That expected of a reasonable person in his role, raised to reflect his own accountancy expertise.","No standard of care applies, because a non-executive director may rely entirely on the executive directors.","That of a reasonably diligent finance director, because the losses arose from financial matters."],"a":2,"e":"The **duty of care, skill and diligence (CA 2006 s.174)** sets a dual test: the general knowledge, skill and experience **reasonably expected of a person carrying out the director's functions** (an objective minimum), **plus** the knowledge, skill and experience the director **actually has**, which can raise the standard. So Marcus's accountancy expertise is taken into account, and it cannot lower the objective minimum. Non-executive directors owe the same duty and cannot simply rely on others without reading the board papers, and he is judged by his own functions, not those of the finance director."},{"t":"decision-making","p":"FLK1","q":"Larkspur Ltd has unamended Model Articles. In March 2026 the board, with the interested director not voting, approved a service contract for its sales director, Dominika, giving her a fixed term of four years during which the company cannot end it except for gross misconduct. No resolution of the shareholders was passed. The company now wants to end Dominika's employment, although she has committed no misconduct.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The fixed-term provision is void, and the contract is treated as terminable by the company on reasonable notice.","The whole service contract is void, so Dominika has been working without any contract.","The contract is valid as agreed, because the board approved it with the interested director not voting.","The contract is valid, but any payment to end it needs shareholder approval as compensation for loss of office.","The fixed-term provision is valid, because shareholder approval is needed only for contracts of over five years."],"a":0,"e":"A director's service contract that **guarantees employment for more than 2 years** needs **prior approval by ordinary resolution** of the members (**CA 2006 s.188**). If it is not approved, **the guaranteed-term provision is void** and the contract is deemed to contain a term allowing the company to end it **on reasonable notice**; the rest of the contract survives, so it is not wholly void. Board approval is not enough, and the threshold is two years, not five."},{"t":"decision-making","p":"FLK1","q":"Ferncliffe Ltd has four directors and unamended Model Articles. All four attend a properly called board meeting chaired by Olu, the chair of the board, to decide whether the company should lease a second warehouse. No director has any personal interest in the decision. Olu and Priyanka vote in favour, and the other two directors vote against.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the outcome?","o":["The resolution fails, because a board resolution needs a majority of the directors present.","The resolution fails, because a chair has a casting vote only at general meetings.","The matter must be referred to the shareholders, because the board is deadlocked.","The resolution is passed, because the chair has a casting vote when the votes are equal.","The resolution is passed only if the directors in favour hold a majority of the shares."],"a":3,"e":"Under **Model Articles art.13**, if the numbers of votes for and against a proposal at a board meeting are equal, **the chair has a casting vote**, unless the chair is not entitled to vote on the matter (for example, because of a conflict under art.14). Olu has no interest, so his casting vote carries the resolution. The general meeting position is the reverse: under the private company Model Articles the chair has **no** casting vote at a general meeting, and directors vote one each at board meetings regardless of their shareholdings."},{"t":"shareholders-minority","p":"FLK1","q":"Brookvale Ltd has 1,000 ordinary shares and 1,000 'B' shares. Its articles give the holders of the B shares the right to appoint one director and say nothing about how class rights may be varied. The company wants to remove that right. Holders of 820 of the B shares have given their written consent, and the company has passed the necessary special resolution to amend its articles. Tamara, who holds 180 B shares, did not consent and is strongly opposed. The variation was made eight days ago.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Tamara's position?","o":["She can do nothing, because holders of more than 75% of the class have consented in writing to the variation.","She can apply to court within 21 days of the variation to have it cancelled, because she holds at least 15% of the class.","She can prevent the variation taking effect, because varying class rights needs the consent of every holder of the class.","She can apply to court to cancel the variation only if she can show that the special resolution was invalidly passed.","She can require the company to buy her B shares at a fair value, because her class rights have been varied."],"a":1,"e":"Class rights can be varied with the **written consent of holders of at least 75% of the class** (or a special resolution at a class meeting) where the articles are silent (**CA 2006 s.630**), and 82% have consented here, so the variation is properly made and unanimity is not required. However, **holders of at least 15% of the class who did not consent may apply to court within 21 days** to have the variation **cancelled** (s.633); Tamara holds 18% and is within time, and the court can cancel the variation if it would unfairly prejudice the class. There is no automatic right to be bought out simply because class rights are varied."},{"t":"shareholders-minority","p":"FLK1","q":"On 2 November 2026 Ngozi agreed to buy 4,000 shares in Kelso Ltd from Harry for £61,200. Harry signed a stock transfer form and handed it to Ngozi with his share certificate, and Ngozi paid the price in full. Ngozi has sent the documents to Kelso, which has unamended Model Articles, but its directors have not yet considered registering the transfer. A general meeting of Kelso is due to be held next week.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ngozi's position?","o":["She is not yet a member and cannot vote at the meeting, and she must pay stamp duty of £310.","She became a member when she paid the price, so she can vote, and she must pay stamp duty of £310.","She is not yet a member and cannot vote at the meeting, and Harry must pay stamp duty of £306.","She became a member when she received the signed form and certificate, and no stamp duty is payable.","She is not yet a member, but she is entitled to vote as Harry's proxy, and she must pay stamp duty of £306."],"a":0,"e":"A buyer becomes a member only when **entered in the register of members** (**CA 2006 s.112**), so until the directors register the transfer (which they may refuse under art.26(5), giving reasons within 2 months), Ngozi cannot vote; she has no automatic right to be Harry's proxy. **Stamp duty** is **0.5% of the price, payable by the buyer, rounded up to the nearest £5**: 0.5% of £61,200 is £306, rounded up to **£310**. Paying the price or holding the form and certificate does not by itself make her a member."},{"t":"partnerships","p":"FLK1","q":"Jas, Kieran and Lola have run a recruitment agency in partnership for six years. They have never agreed how long the partnership will last, or anything about removing a partner. Jas and Kieran have lost confidence in Lola, who regularly misses client meetings, and want her out of the business so that the two of them can carry it on together.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the options available to Jas and Kieran?","o":["They can expel Lola by majority decision, because ordinary matters are decided by a majority of the partners.","They can expel Lola only by applying to court, which must make the order if the majority wish it.","They cannot expel Lola or end the partnership without her consent, because there is no fixed term.","They cannot expel Lola, but either of them can end the partnership by notice and they can then continue without her.","They can expel Lola by giving her three months' written notice, which is the statutory default period."],"a":3,"e":"**No majority of partners can expel a partner unless an express power to do so has been agreed** (**Partnership Act 1890 s.25**), and there is no default notice period for expulsion. However, a partnership with no fixed term is a **partnership at will**, which **any partner can dissolve by giving notice** (ss.26 and 32), after which Jas and Kieran can form a new firm (subject to winding up the old firm's affairs and Lola's share of its assets). The majority rule covers ordinary matters only, and the lack of a fixed term makes ending the partnership easier, not harder."},{"t":"equity-finance","p":"FLK1","q":"Harbour Crafts Ltd has a single class of ordinary shares of £1 each and unamended Model Articles. Needing money urgently, the directors used their authority to allot 20,000 new ordinary shares to Gemma for 80p each in cash, which she paid in full. The statutory pre-emption rights had been validly disapplied by special resolution for this allotment.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Gemma's position?","o":["The allotment is valid as it stands, because pre-emption rights were validly disapplied for it.","The allotment is void, so Gemma is not a shareholder and must be repaid what she paid.","She must pay the 20p a share shortfall with interest, because shares cannot be issued at a discount.","She owes nothing further, because the price of shares is a matter for the directors' commercial judgment.","She owes nothing further, but the directors must transfer £4,000 to the share premium account."],"a":2,"e":"**Shares must not be allotted at a discount to their nominal value** (**CA 2006 s.580**); if they are, the **allottee must pay the company the amount of the discount plus interest**, so Gemma owes £4,000 (20,000 × 20p) with interest. The allotment is not void; Gemma is a shareholder but must make up the shortfall. Disapplying pre-emption deals only with who may be offered the shares, not the minimum price, and a share premium arises only where shares are issued **above** nominal value."},{"t":"equity-finance","p":"FLK1","q":"Selina has owned 2,000 of the 10,000 issued ordinary shares in Dunmore Engineering Ltd, an unquoted trading company, since subscribing for them at £1 each in 2015. She is UK resident. To resolve a disagreement with her about the company's direction, which the board considers is harming the business, Dunmore has agreed to buy back 400 of her shares at £30 each out of distributable profits. She will keep her other 1,600 shares, and no other shares will be issued or cancelled.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Selina will be taxed on the buyback payment?","o":["Under capital gains tax on her gain, because she has owned the shares for more than five years.","Under capital gains tax on her gain, because the buyback is made for the benefit of the company's trade.","As a distribution taxed as dividend income on the whole £12,000, because a buyback is always treated as income.","Under capital gains tax on her gain, because she will hold no more than 30% of the company afterwards.","As dividend income on the amount above £1 a share, because her interest is not substantially reduced."],"a":4,"e":"A buyback payment is taxed as a **distribution** (dividend income on the excess over the amount subscribed) unless **all** the **capital treatment** conditions are met, one of which is a **substantial reduction**: the seller's percentage after the buyback must be **no more than 75%** of what it was before (CTA 2010). Selina goes from 20% (2,000 of 10,000) to 16.7% (1,600 of 9,600), which is about 83% of her former interest, so the test fails even though the 5-year ownership, trade-benefit and 30% connection conditions are satisfied. Income treatment applies only to the excess over her £1 subscription price, not the whole £12,000."},{"t":"debt-security","p":"FLK1","q":"Calder Print Ltd owns a specialist printing press. On 3 June 2026 it granted Bank A a fixed charge over the press, and the charge was delivered to and registered at Companies House on 20 June 2026. On 8 June 2026 it granted Lender B a fixed charge over the same press, which was registered on 12 June 2026. Neither charge refers to the other, and there is no agreement between the lenders about priority. The press is now to be sold, and the proceeds will not pay both lenders in full.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the priority of the two charges?","o":["Bank A ranks first, because its charge was created first and registered within 21 days.","Lender B ranks first, because its charge was registered at Companies House first.","The two charges rank equally, because both are fixed charges over the same asset.","Lender B ranks first, because Bank A's charge was not yet registered when Lender B's charge was created.","Bank A ranks first only if Lender B had actual notice of Bank A's charge when it lent."],"a":0,"e":"Two fixed charges over the same asset **rank in order of creation, provided each is registered within 21 days** of creation (CA 2006 s.859A). Bank A's charge was created on 3 June and registered on 20 June, inside the 21-day period, so it ranks ahead of Lender B's later charge even though B registered first and A's charge was unregistered when B lent. Actual notice matters only for a negative pledge in an earlier **floating** charge, which is not the situation here."},{"t":"debt-security","p":"FLK1","q":"In January 2026 Arden Textiles Ltd granted its bank a properly registered floating charge over all its stock and book debts. The charge document contains no automatic or express crystallisation provisions. In April 2026 the company breached a financial covenant in the loan agreement, but the bank took no action. In June 2026 the company granted a fixed charge over its factory to another lender. In August 2026 its managing director resigned. On 15 September 2026 its members passed a resolution for a creditors' voluntary winding up.\n\nWhen did the bank's floating charge crystallise?","o":["In April 2026, when the company breached the financial covenant in its loan agreement.","In June 2026, when the company granted another lender a fixed charge over its factory.","In August 2026, when the managing director of the company resigned from the board.","On 15 September 2026, when the resolution to wind the company up was passed.","It has not crystallised, because the bank has not yet given notice to the company."],"a":3,"e":"**Crystallisation** turns a floating charge into a fixed charge over the assets then in the class, and it happens **automatically by law on winding up**, on the appointment of a receiver, or when the company **ceases to carry on business**; otherwise it occurs only as the charge document provides. This charge has no crystallisation clauses, so a covenant breach the bank did not act on, the grant of a later fixed charge and a director's resignation have no effect. Notice from the lender is needed only where the document provides for express crystallisation, and the winding-up resolution crystallised the charge regardless."},{"t":"distributions-accounts","p":"FLK1","q":"Bramley Ltd has unamended Model Articles and profits available for distribution of £60,000. On 1 September 2026 the directors resolved to pay an interim dividend totalling £40,000 on 1 October 2026. On 20 September, after losing a major customer, the directors resolved to cancel the dividend; none of it had been paid. Zainab, one of the shareholders, says the company owes her share of the dividend.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The company owes Zainab her share, because a dividend becomes a debt as soon as it is decided on.","The directors could cancel it, because an interim dividend does not become a debt until it is paid.","The directors could cancel it only with the shareholders' approval by ordinary resolution.","The cancellation is invalid, because the dividend was within the company's distributable profits.","The company owes Zainab her share, because only the shareholders could declare a dividend."],"a":1,"e":"Under the Model Articles (art.30) the **directors alone may decide to pay an interim dividend**, and because it is not a debt **until it is paid**, they **may vary or cancel it before payment**. It is a **final dividend declared by the shareholders** that becomes a debt as soon as it is declared, which is why the answer saying a dividend is a debt once decided is tempting but wrong here. Having enough distributable profits allows a dividend to be paid but does not oblige the company to pay it."},{"t":"corporate-insolvency","p":"FLK1","q":"Lumen Supplies Ltd is owed £4,200 by Penrose Kitchens Ltd for goods delivered in July 2026. The debt is not disputed and is not secured. On 1 October 2026 Lumen left a statutory demand in the prescribed form at Penrose's registered office. Penrose has not paid, secured or offered any arrangement for the debt. It is now 26 October 2026.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Lumen can rely on the statutory demand to petition for Penrose to be wound up?","o":["It can, because the debt exceeds £750 and Penrose has neglected to pay it for three weeks after the demand.","It cannot, because the debt is below the £5,000 minimum for a petition based on a statutory demand.","It cannot, because Penrose has 28 days from service to respond and that period has not yet ended.","It can, but only once it has obtained a court judgment for the debt and tried to enforce it.","It cannot, because only the company, its directors or its members can petition for a winding-up order."],"a":0,"e":"A company is deemed **unable to pay its debts** if a creditor owed **more than £750** serves a statutory demand at its registered office and the company **neglects to pay, secure or compound the debt for 3 weeks** (IA 1986 s.123(1)(a)), which is a ground for a creditor's winding-up petition (s.122(1)(f)). Three weeks from 1 October expired on 22 October, so Lumen can petition. The £5,000 figure is the **bankruptcy** level for individuals, no judgment is needed first, and creditors are among those who can petition."},{"t":"corporate-insolvency","p":"FLK1","q":"Moreland Leisure Ltd went into administration last month. The administrator has concluded that the company itself cannot realistically be rescued as a going concern, because its debts far exceed any likely future profits. However, a buyer will pay £2 million for the business and assets as a going concern, which would produce a better return for the creditors as a whole than if the company were wound up. The company's bank, which holds a qualifying floating charge and is owed £1.5 million, wants the assets sold off piecemeal as quickly as possible to repay it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the objective the administrator must pursue?","o":["Rescuing the company as a going concern, because that objective must be pursued in every administration.","Realising property to repay the bank, because a qualifying floating charge holder can direct the administrator.","Achieving a better result for the creditors as a whole than a winding up, by selling the business as a going concern.","Realising property to repay the bank, because a secured creditor's interests come before those of other creditors.","Whatever objective the unsecured creditors choose, because the administrator must act on their instructions."],"a":2,"e":"An administrator must pursue the statutory objectives **in strict order** (IA 1986 Sch B1 para.3): **(a) rescue the company** as a going concern; if that is not reasonably practicable, **(b) achieve a better result for the creditors as a whole** than a winding up; and only if neither is practicable, (c) realise property for secured or preferential creditors. Rescue is not reasonably practicable here, but the going-concern sale achieves objective (b), so the administrator cannot drop down to (c) to suit the bank. The administrator acts **in the interests of the creditors as a whole**, not on the instructions of any one creditor or group."},{"t":"insolvency-clawback-priority","p":"FLK1","q":"Rhys was the finance director of Talbot Print Ltd. The company's management accounts for March 2025, which Rhys prepared, showed that it was insolvent, that its losses were growing each month and that it had no realistic prospect of new work. The board carried on trading and taking credit from suppliers until the company went into insolvent liquidation in May 2026, by which time its deficit had grown by £300,000. Rhys took no advice and proposed no change of course, honestly believing that the market would eventually recover.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Rhys's potential liability?","o":["He has no liability, because he honestly and genuinely believed that the market would recover.","He has no liability, because the company is a separate legal person and its debts are its own alone.","He is liable for fraudulent trading, because the company kept taking credit from suppliers while insolvent.","He may be ordered to contribute only if the liquidator proves that he intended to defraud the creditors.","He may be ordered to contribute for wrongful trading, as he ought to have seen that liquidation was unavoidable."],"a":4,"e":"**Wrongful trading (IA 1986 s.214)** applies where a director **knew or ought to have concluded** that there was **no reasonable prospect of avoiding insolvent liquidation** and did not take **every step to minimise loss to creditors**; the standard is that of a reasonably diligent person with the knowledge expected of a finance director (and Rhys's own knowledge). His honest optimism is no defence, because the test does not require dishonesty, and separate legal personality does not prevent this statutory claim. **Fraudulent trading** (s.213) needs **intent to defraud**, which the facts do not show."},{"t":"insolvency-clawback-priority","p":"FLK1","q":"Three months before Linden Joinery Ltd went into insolvent liquidation, and at a time when it was unable to pay its debts, it paid in full the £35,000 it owed to its main timber supplier, Northwood Timber Ltd. Northwood is not connected with Linden or its directors. Northwood had told Linden that it would stop all further deliveries unless the debt was paid, and the directors paid it only because they believed the company could not keep trading without Northwood's timber. Linden's other unsecured creditors are likely to receive about 10p in the pound.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the liquidator can recover the £35,000 as a preference?","o":["Yes, because the payment was made within six months before the liquidation while the company was insolvent.","No, because the company was not influenced by a desire to improve Northwood's position but by commercial pressure.","Yes, because Northwood has been put in a better position than the company's other unsecured creditors.","No, because a preference can be challenged only where the creditor is connected with the company.","No, because the company received full value in timber for the debt that it paid to Northwood."],"a":1,"e":"A payment is set aside as a **preference (IA 1986 s.239)** only if, besides being made within the relevant time (6 months for an unconnected creditor) while the company was insolvent, the company was **influenced by a desire** to put the creditor in a better position. Paying a key supplier under **genuine commercial pressure** in order to keep trading is not such a desire (Re MC Bacon), and because Northwood is unconnected there is no presumption of desire. Timing and an improved position alone are not enough; connected creditors simply face a longer period and a presumption, and the full-value point is relevant to a transaction at an undervalue, not a preference."},{"t":"personal-insolvency","p":"FLK1","q":"Imogen, a self-employed florist, owes £3,200 to a flower wholesaler and £2,100 to a van hire company. Neither debt is secured or disputed. Each creditor served a statutory demand on her on 1 September 2026. She has not paid, secured or compounded either debt, and has not applied to set either demand aside. It is now 15 October 2026.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether a bankruptcy petition can be presented against Imogen?","o":["Neither creditor can petition, because each petitioning creditor must itself be owed at least £5,000.","Either creditor can petition alone, because there is no minimum debt for a creditor's petition.","Neither creditor can petition, because a statutory demand must be followed by a court judgment.","The two creditors can petition jointly, because together they are owed more than £5,000.","Only Imogen can start bankruptcy proceedings, by applying to the Adjudicator online."],"a":3,"e":"A creditor's bankruptcy petition requires **the debt, or the aggregate of the debts owed to the petitioning creditors**, to be at least the **bankruptcy level of £5,000** (IA 1986 s.267). Creditors can therefore **join together**: £3,200 + £2,100 = £5,300, and Imogen appears unable to pay because each statutory demand has gone unpaid for more than 3 weeks. Neither creditor can petition alone, since each debt is below £5,000; the no-minimum rule applies only to a **debtor's** own application to the Adjudicator, and no judgment is needed after an unpaid statutory demand."},{"t":"personal-insolvency","p":"FLK1","q":"Kwabena, a self-employed plumber, was made bankrupt on 1 October 2026. He uses a new, top-of-the-range van worth £38,000, owned outright, to carry his tools to jobs, and he cannot work without a van. A reliable second-hand van suitable for his work would cost £9,000. The Official Receiver, as trustee, wants to realise the van for the creditors.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the trustee's position?","o":["The trustee cannot claim the van, because vehicles needed by the bankrupt for his business are excluded from the estate.","The trustee can claim the van and sell it, and Kwabena must then find his own replacement from his income.","The trustee can claim the van only after Kwabena's discharge, when exclusions from the estate cease to apply.","The trustee can claim the van if the cost of a reasonable replacement is provided from the estate, so Kwabena can keep working.","The trustee cannot claim the van unless Kwabena agrees to sell it, because it is needed for his work."],"a":3,"e":"Tools, vehicles and equipment **necessary for the bankrupt personally for use in his business** are **excluded from the estate** (IA 1986 s.283(2)), so the van does not automatically vest in the trustee. But if the item's value **exceeds the cost of a reasonable replacement**, the trustee can claim it by notice, and must **provide a reasonable replacement** (or funds for one) **out of the estate** (ss.308-309). That lets the creditors benefit from the £29,000 difference while Kwabena keeps working, so the answer saying the van can never be claimed misses this rule, and Kwabena's consent is not required."},{"t":"income-tax","p":"FLK1","q":"Nadia runs a small pottery business as a sole trader. In 2026/27 her trading profit is £14,570, and she receives £6,000 of interest on loan notes issued by an unquoted company. She has no other income and is entitled to the full personal allowance. Assume a personal allowance of £12,570, a starting rate for savings band of £5,000 at 0%, a personal savings allowance of £1,000 for a basic rate taxpayer, and a basic rate for savings of 20%.\n\nWhat is Nadia's income tax liability on the loan note interest, before taking account of any tax deducted at source?","o":["£0","£400","£600","£1,000","£1,200"],"a":1,"e":"The personal allowance is set against **non-savings income first**, leaving £2,000 of taxable trading profit. The **£5,000 starting rate band is reduced £1 for £1 by taxable non-savings income**, so £3,000 of the interest is taxed at 0%; as a basic rate taxpayer she then has a **£1,000 personal savings allowance** at 0%, leaving £2,000 at 20% = **£400**. £600 ignores the personal savings allowance, £1,000 ignores the starting rate, £1,200 ignores both, and £0 wrongly assumes the full £5,000 starting rate band is available."},{"t":"income-tax","p":"FLK1","q":"In May 2025 Owen borrowed £60,000 from his bank to buy 8% of the ordinary shares in Castleford Brewing Ltd, a close company that carries on a brewing trade. He is not an employee or director of the company and has no other involvement in it. In 2026/27 he pays £3,000 of interest on the loan. His other income for 2026/27 is a salary of £70,000 from an unrelated employer, and he has no losses or other reliefs.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the income tax treatment of the £3,000 of interest?","o":["It is not deductible, because relief is given only where the borrower works for the greater part of their time for the company.","It is deductible only from the dividends he receives from the company, because the loan was used to buy its shares.","It is deductible from his total income only if he owns more than 25% of the shares in the company.","It is not deductible, because interest on money borrowed personally by an individual never attracts relief.","It is deducted from his total income as qualifying loan interest, because he owns more than 5% of a close trading company."],"a":4,"e":"Interest on a loan to **buy shares in a close trading company** is **qualifying loan interest**, deducted from **total income** at Step 2 of the calculation, if the borrower holds **more than 5%** of the shares, **or** holds some shares and works for the greater part of their time in managing the company. Owen's 8% holding satisfies the first condition, so he need not work for the company, and the relief is given against total income (including his salary), not just dividends. The amount is well within the cap of the greater of £50,000 or 25% of adjusted total income."},{"t":"cgt","p":"FLK1","q":"In October 2026 Graham sold his 25% shareholding in Ashby Tools Ltd, an unquoted trading company with no investment assets, to his daughter, Holly, for £60,000. He had bought the shares in 2012 for £20,000. Their market value at the date of the sale was £200,000. Graham and Holly have made a valid joint election for gift relief.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the election?","o":["Graham is taxed now on nothing, and the whole £180,000 gain is held over, giving Holly a base cost of £20,000.","Graham is taxed now on a gain of £40,000, and £140,000 is held over, giving Holly a base cost of £60,000.","Graham is taxed now on a gain of £40,000, and £140,000 is held over, giving Holly a base cost of £20,000.","Graham is taxed now on the whole £180,000 gain, because gift relief is unavailable on a sale to a connected person.","Graham is taxed now on a gain of £60,000, and £120,000 is held over, giving Holly a base cost of £80,000."],"a":1,"e":"A sale to a connected person is treated as made at **market value**, so Graham's gain is £200,000 − £20,000 = £180,000; **gift relief (TCGA s.165)** can apply to a **sale at an undervalue** of unquoted trading company shares. **The excess of the actual price over the donor's allowable cost** (£60,000 − £20,000 = £40,000) is **taxed now**, and the remaining £140,000 is held over, reducing Holly's base cost from £200,000 to **£60,000**. The answer holding over the whole gain ignores the price Graham actually received, and being connected is what triggers market value, not a bar to the relief."},{"t":"cgt","p":"FLK1","q":"In 2026/27 Fatima sold her 30% shareholding in Corran Ltd, an unquoted trading company of which she had been a director since buying the shares in 2016. The sale produced a gain of £100,000, which qualifies for business asset disposal relief (BADR); she has never claimed BADR before. In the same tax year she sold shares in a listed company at a gain of £30,000. She made no other disposals, has no capital losses, and her taxable income for 2026/27 is £20,000. Assume an annual exempt amount of £3,000, a basic rate band of £37,700, a BADR rate of 18%, and CGT rates of 18% and 24%.\n\nWhat is Fatima's total CGT liability for 2026/27?","o":["£22,860","£23,418","£24,480","£24,660","£25,200"],"a":2,"e":"**BADR gains are taxed at 18%** and are treated as **using up any unused basic rate band first**, so the BADR gain (£100,000 × 18% = £18,000) absorbs Fatima's remaining £17,700 of band. The annual exempt amount is best set against the gain taxed at the higher rate, leaving £27,000 of the listed-share gain taxed at **24%** (£6,480): total **£24,480**. £23,418 wrongly lets the listed-share gain use the basic rate band, £24,660 sets the exempt amount against the BADR gain, £25,200 ignores the exempt amount, and £22,860 taxes everything at 18%."},{"t":"corporation-tax","p":"FLK1","q":"Elstow Ltd prepares accounts for the year ended 31 March 2027 and has taxable total profits of £100,000 for that year. It receives no dividends. Victor, its sole shareholder, also controls one other trading company, which is an associated company of Elstow; there are no other associated companies. Assume a small profits rate of 19%, a main rate of 25%, lower and upper limits of £50,000 and £250,000, and a marginal relief fraction of 3/200.\n\nWhat is Elstow's corporation tax liability for the year?","o":["£19,000","£22,750","£24,625","£25,000","£26,500"],"a":2,"e":"The lower and upper limits are **divided equally between the company and its associated companies**, so with one associated company Elstow's limits are **£25,000 and £125,000**. Its profits of £100,000 fall between them, so tax is 25% (£25,000) less **marginal relief of 3/200 × (£125,000 − £100,000) = £375**, giving **£24,625**. £22,750 forgets to divide the limits, £25,000 ignores marginal relief, £19,000 wrongly applies the small profits rate, and £26,500 applies the effective marginal rate to the whole profit."},{"t":"vat-iht-bpr","p":"FLK1","q":"Oakridge Furniture Ltd is registered for VAT, does not use the cash accounting scheme, and makes quarterly returns for periods ending 31 March, 30 June, 30 September and 31 December. A customer ordered a bespoke dining table for £6,000 plus VAT and paid a deposit of £1,200 plus VAT on 20 March 2027. The table was delivered to the customer on 4 April 2027, and Oakridge issued a VAT invoice for the balance on 12 April 2027. No other invoice was issued.\n\nWhich of the following best describes when Oakridge must account for the output tax on the table?","o":["VAT on the deposit in the quarter to 31 March 2027, and VAT on the balance in the quarter to 30 June 2027.","All the VAT in the quarter to 30 June 2027, because the table was delivered on 4 April 2027.","All the VAT in the quarter to 31 March 2027, because the first payment fixes the tax point for the whole supply.","VAT on the deposit in the quarter to 31 March 2027, and VAT on the balance only when the customer pays it.","All the VAT in the quarter to 30 June 2027, because a deposit is not payment until the goods are delivered."],"a":0,"e":"The **basic tax point** for goods is when they are **made available** (4 April), but a **payment received before** the basic tax point creates an **earlier actual tax point to the extent of that payment**, so VAT on the £1,200 deposit falls into the quarter to 31 March. For the balance, an **invoice issued within 14 days after** the basic tax point makes the invoice date (12 April) the tax point, which is in the quarter to 30 June. The deposit does not pull the whole supply back into March, and without the cash accounting scheme the balance does not wait for payment."},{"t":"vat-iht-bpr","p":"FLK1","q":"In March 2024 Frank gave his son, Ellis, all his shares in Fenwick Signs Ltd, an unquoted trading company with no investment assets, which Frank had owned since 2010. The shares were then worth £400,000. In June 2025 Ellis sold all the shares to an unconnected buyer and put the proceeds in a savings account. Frank died in August 2026. He made no other lifetime gifts, and Ellis is not his spouse or civil partner.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the business property relief position on Frank's gift?","o":["100% relief is available, because the shares qualified as relevant business property when Frank gave them.","100% relief is available, because Frank had owned the shares for more than two years before the gift.","50% relief is available, because Ellis sold the shares to a buyer who was not connected with him.","No question of relief arises, because a gift made more than two years before death is exempt.","No relief is available on Frank's death, because Ellis no longer owned the shares when Frank died."],"a":4,"e":"The gift was a **potentially exempt transfer** that became chargeable when Frank died within 7 years. When IHT is calculated on such a gift because of the donor's death, **BPR is available only if the donee still owns the property (or qualifying replacement property) at the donor's death** and it still qualifies (**IHTA 1984 s.113A**). Ellis sold the shares and kept the cash, so no relief is available, even though the shares qualified at the date of the gift and Frank had owned them for 2 years. Only gifts made more than **7 years** before death escape IHT altogether."}],"contract-p1":[{"t":"offer-acceptance","p":"FLK1","q":"On 2 March, Priya, who runs a printing business, writes to Callum offering to sell him a used industrial printer for £8,000 and saying that the offer will stay open for 14 days. On 5 March Priya receives a better offer from someone else and posts a letter to Callum withdrawing her offer. On 6 March Callum posts a properly addressed and stamped letter accepting her offer. Priya's letter of withdrawal reaches Callum on 7 March, and his acceptance reaches Priya on 8 March.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the legal position?","o":["No contract was made, because Priya posted her withdrawal before Callum posted his letter of acceptance.","A contract was made, because Priya's promise to keep the offer open for 14 days made her offer irrevocable.","A contract was made on 6 March, because acceptance took effect on posting, before the withdrawal was received.","A contract was made on 8 March, because an acceptance has no effect until it is received by the offeror.","No contract was made, because the withdrawal reached Callum before his acceptance reached Priya."],"a":2,"e":"Where acceptance by post is reasonable, **an acceptance takes effect when the letter is posted (the postal rule), but a revocation (withdrawal of an offer) takes effect only when it is received by the offeree** (Byrne v Van Tienhoven). On 6 March the offer was still open, so the contract was made then; the dates on which the withdrawal was posted or the acceptance arrived are irrelevant. Priya's promise to keep the offer open did not bind her, because Callum gave no consideration for it (it was not an option), but she withdrew too late."},{"t":"offer-acceptance","p":"FLK1","q":"Tomasz emails Hannah, a furniture maker, offering to buy 20 oak tables at £450 each for delivery by 30 June. Hannah replies: \"Thanks. Would you consider taking delivery in mid-July instead? I am short of seasoned timber.\" Tomasz replies that he needs the tables by 30 June. The next day, before Tomasz has said anything else, Hannah emails: \"Fine, I accept your order on your terms.\" Tomasz has since found a cheaper supplier and says there is no contract.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether there is a contract?","o":["There is a contract, because Hannah's first reply was a request for information that did not reject the offer.","There is no contract, because Hannah's first reply was a counter-offer that destroyed Tomasz's original offer.","There is no contract, because Tomasz's reply that he needed the tables by 30 June revoked his offer.","There is a contract, but delivery is due in mid-July, the date that Hannah first proposed to Tomasz.","There is no contract, because an offer lapses as soon as the offeree raises any query about its terms."],"a":0,"e":"**A counter-offer (a reply proposing new terms) rejects and destroys the original offer, but a mere request for information, such as asking whether the offeror would consider different terms, leaves the offer open** (Stevenson v McLean; contrast Hyde v Wrench). Hannah only asked a question; she did not say she would deliver only in July. Tomasz's answer simply restated his terms and did not withdraw the offer, so Hannah's later unqualified acceptance created a contract on Tomasz's terms, including the 30 June delivery date."},{"t":"consideration","p":"FLK1","q":"Kestrel Homes Ltd engages Dev, a plastering subcontractor, to plaster 12 new flats for a fixed price of £36,000. Halfway through, Dev tells Kestrel he underpriced the job and may not be able to finish. Kestrel faces a heavy financial penalty under its own contract with the developer if the flats are late, so it promises Dev an extra £500 for each remaining flat he completes. Dev did not threaten Kestrel and finishes the remaining flats on time. Kestrel now refuses to pay the extra money, arguing that Dev only did what he was already bound to do.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Dev can recover the extra money?","o":["No, because performing an existing contractual duty owed to the promisor can never be good consideration.","No, because Dev's promise to finish the work was past consideration for Kestrel's later promise to pay.","Yes, because Kestrel's promise was a variation of the contract, and a variation never needs consideration.","No, because the extra money was out of proportion to the value of the plastering work that remained.","Yes, because Kestrel obtained a practical benefit from Dev's continued work and there was no economic duress."],"a":4,"e":"**Where a party promises to pay more for the performance of an existing contractual duty owed to it, that performance can be consideration if the promisor obtains a practical benefit and the promise was not obtained by economic duress or fraud** (Williams v Roffey). Avoiding the penalty and the trouble of finding a replacement plasterer is a practical benefit, and Dev applied no illegitimate pressure. The older rule that an existing duty is never consideration has been qualified, a variation does normally need consideration, and consideration need only be sufficient, not adequate in value."},{"t":"consideration","p":"FLK1","q":"Olumide leases a shop unit to Grace at a rent of £2,000 a month. In January, roadworks outside the shop cut the number of passing customers, and Olumide writes to Grace: \"Pay £1,200 a month until the roadworks are finished.\" Relying on this, Grace pays £1,200 a month and uses the savings to keep her business going. The roadworks finish at the end of June and trade returns to normal. In September, Olumide demands the £800 monthly shortfall for January to June and the full rent from July onwards. Grace gave nothing in return for Olumide's promise, and there was no deed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Olumide's position?","o":["He can recover the shortfall for January to June, because Grace gave no consideration for his promise.","He cannot recover the shortfall for January to June, but he is entitled to the full rent from July onwards.","He cannot recover the shortfall and cannot return to the full rent at all unless Grace agrees to it.","He can recover the shortfall, because promissory estoppel only ever suspends rights to future payments.","He cannot recover the shortfall, and he is entitled to full rent only from the date he gives written notice."],"a":1,"e":"**Promissory estoppel prevents a promisor going back on a clear promise not to enforce strict legal rights where the promisee has relied on it and it would be inequitable to go back on it; for periodic payments it extinguishes the right to the shortfall for the period covered, but full rights revive when the promise ends** (Central London Property Trust v High Trees House). Grace relied on the promise, so Olumide cannot claim the January to June shortfall even though there was no consideration. His promise was expressly limited to the period of the roadworks, so it ended on its own terms in June and no further notice was needed before full rent became payable again."},{"t":"intention-certainty-capacity","p":"FLK1","q":"Marcus and Leah, a married couple, separate and Marcus moves out of their jointly owned home. At a meeting in a café they sign a note agreeing that Marcus will pay Leah £900 a month and that, once Leah has paid off the mortgage, Marcus will transfer his share of the home to her. Leah pays off the mortgage, using the monthly payments and her own savings. Marcus now refuses to transfer his share, saying that agreements between husband and wife are not legally binding.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Leah can enforce Marcus's promise?","o":["Marcus is right, because agreements between spouses are conclusively presumed not to create legal relations.","Leah can enforce it only if it was made by deed, because it is a domestic arrangement between spouses.","Leah can enforce it only if she proves that Marcus privately intended to be bound when he signed it.","Leah can enforce it, because the presumption against legal intention does not apply to separated spouses.","Marcus is right, because Leah gave no consideration for his promise to transfer his share to her."],"a":3,"e":"**In domestic agreements there is a presumption that the parties do not intend to create legal relations, but it does not apply where spouses are separating or have separated, and in any event it can be rebutted** (Merritt v Merritt; contrast Balfour v Balfour, where the couple were living together). Intention is judged objectively, so Marcus's private state of mind is not the test, and no deed is needed. Leah gave consideration by paying off the mortgage, so the promise is enforceable."},{"t":"intention-certainty-capacity","p":"FLK1","q":"Ellie, aged 17, is a student with a part-time café job. She buys a £3,500 designer handbag on credit from a boutique that knows her age, agreeing to pay in monthly instalments. She already owns several perfectly good bags. After paying one instalment, she stops paying. The boutique wants to sue her for the balance.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the boutique's position?","o":["It can sue Ellie for a reasonable price, because a handbag is a necessary for a student with a job.","It cannot sue Ellie for the balance, but the court may order her to return the handbag if just and equitable.","It can sue Ellie for the full balance, because she affirmed the contract by paying the first instalment.","It cannot sue Ellie and cannot recover the handbag, because a minor's contract for luxuries is void.","It can sue Ellie for the balance once she turns 18, unless she repudiates within a reasonable time."],"a":1,"e":"A minor (a person under 18) must pay a reasonable price for **necessaries, meaning goods suitable to the minor's condition in life and actual requirements at the time** (Sale of Goods Act 1979 s.3(3)); an expensive luxury bought when Ellie already has plenty of bags is not a necessary (compare Nash v Inman). **Other contracts are not void but are unenforceable against the minor, and under the Minors' Contracts Act 1987 s.3 the court may order the minor to return property acquired under the contract if just and equitable.** The rule about repudiating within a reasonable time after turning 18 applies only to continuing contracts such as leases or shares, not a one-off purchase."},{"t":"privity-third-parties","p":"FLK1","q":"Hartwell Ltd contracts with Brightside Cleaning Ltd for the cleaning of Hartwell's offices. One term states that Brightside will also clean, free of charge, the flat above the offices, which is occupied by Hartwell's retired founder, Mr Okafor, \"who shall be entitled to enforce this term\". On 1 May Mr Okafor posts a letter to Brightside saying he accepts the benefit of the term. On 2 May Hartwell and Brightside agree to delete the term. Mr Okafor's letter arrives on 3 May. He has done nothing in reliance on the term, and the contract says nothing about variation.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Mr Okafor can enforce the term?","o":["Yes, because his assent to the term took effect when he posted his letter on 1 May.","Yes, because a third party's right under the Act can never be removed without his consent.","No, because a third party cannot enforce a term under which he is to pay nothing.","No, because only a party to the contract can enforce any of the terms of the contract.","No, because his assent had not been received by Brightside when the term was deleted."],"a":4,"e":"Mr Okafor had a right under the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999 s.1(1)(a), because the contract expressly said he could enforce the term. **Under s.2, the parties cannot vary or cancel the contract to remove a third party's right without consent once the third party has communicated assent to the promisor or relied on the term, and an assent sent by post counts only when it is received (s.2(2)): the postal rule does not apply.** His letter arrived after the term was deleted and he had not relied on it, so the deletion was effective. The third party need not provide consideration, and the old privity rule has been modified by the Act."},{"t":"privity-third-parties","p":"FLK1","q":"Fairview Events Ltd hires a marquee from Canopy Hire Ltd for a festival. The hire contract, negotiated between the two companies, says Canopy will erect the marquee using its \"approved riggers\" and that \"no approved rigger shall be liable for any loss of or damage to the hirer's property, however caused\". Canopy uses Rigwise Ltd, one of its approved riggers. Rigwise's staff carelessly damage Fairview's sound equipment, and Fairview sues Rigwise in negligence. The contract says nothing else about third parties.\n\nIgnoring any statutory controls on exclusion clauses, which of the following best describes whether Rigwise can rely on the clause?","o":["Yes, because the term purports to benefit an identified class and a third party may rely on an exclusion.","No, because Rigwise is not a party to the hire contract and so cannot take any benefit from it.","No, because a third party can rely on a term only if the contract identifies it by its own name.","Yes, but only if Rigwise gave Fairview some consideration in return for the protection of the clause.","No, because the Act lets third parties enforce positive promises but not rely on exclusion clauses."],"a":0,"e":"**Under the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999 s.1(1)(b), a third party may enforce a term that purports to confer a benefit on it, unless the contract shows the parties did not intend this (s.1(2)); the third party may be identified by name, class or description (s.1(3)), and enforcing includes relying on an exclusion or limitation clause (s.1(6)).** Rigwise falls within the class of \"approved riggers\", and nothing in the contract rebuts the presumption. The third party does not have to provide consideration, and naming it individually is not required."},{"t":"express-terms","p":"FLK1","q":"Nadia takes her laptop to a computer repair shop. At the counter she agrees the repair price and pays a £60 deposit; there is no sign displaying any terms. The assistant then hands her a receipt. On the back, in small print, is a clause excluding the shop's liability for any loss of data. Nadia puts the receipt in her bag without reading it. When she collects the laptop, she finds the shop has wiped all her files.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the clause on the receipt is part of the contract?","o":["It is, because Nadia accepted the receipt and is bound by it whether or not she read it.","It is, because a receipt is a contractual document that customers are expected to read.","It is not, because the contract was made at the counter before Nadia was given the receipt.","It is not, because terms in small print on the back of a document are never incorporated.","It is, because customers generally know that repair shops exclude liability for lost data."],"a":2,"e":"**An unsigned term is incorporated only if reasonable notice of it was given before or at the time the contract was made; terms first shown after the contract is concluded come too late** (Olley v Marlborough Court). The contract was made at the counter when the price was agreed and the deposit paid, so the later receipt could not add terms; in any case a receipt is usually seen as a mere acknowledgement of payment, not a contractual document (Chapelton v Barry UDC). The rule that a person is bound by a document whether or not they read it applies to signed documents, which this was not."},{"t":"express-terms","p":"FLK1","q":"Seaview Logistics Ltd charters a refrigerated ship from Norland Shipping Ltd for 18 months. The charter requires Norland to keep the ship \"in a thoroughly efficient state\". In the second month, the ship's cooling system fails because of poor maintenance, and the ship is out of service for three weeks while it is repaired. Seaview loses some cargo and immediately tells Norland it is terminating the charter. The term is not described as a condition anywhere, and no statute classifies it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Seaview was entitled to terminate?","o":["Yes, because any breach of an obligation about the state of the ship is a breach of a condition.","Yes, because Seaview lost some of its cargo, and any breach causing loss is a repudiatory breach.","No, because the term is a warranty, so a breach of it can only ever give rise to a claim for damages.","No, because the term is innominate and the breach did not deprive Seaview of substantially the whole benefit.","Yes, because the breakdown was caused by Norland's own poor maintenance rather than by bad luck."],"a":3,"e":"**An innominate (intermediate) term is one that can be broken in trivial or serious ways; the innocent party may terminate only if the breach deprives it of substantially the whole benefit of the contract, otherwise it is limited to damages** (Hongkong Fir Shipping). A three-week stoppage early in an 18-month charter falls well short of that, so Seaview's termination was itself wrongful. The term is not a condition, as neither the parties nor statute made it one, and it is not a mere warranty either, because a serious enough breach could justify termination; loss of cargo or fault alone does not make a breach repudiatory."},{"t":"express-terms","p":"FLK1","q":"Avalon Interiors Ltd agrees to fit out a new restaurant for Saffron Kitchen Ltd. The written contract sets a completion date of 1 September and states: \"No variation of this contract shall be effective unless it is in writing and signed on behalf of both parties.\" In July, during a phone call, Saffron's operations manager agrees with Avalon's director that completion can move to 15 September. Nothing is put in writing and nothing else is said or done about it. Avalon completes on 15 September, and Saffron claims damages for late completion.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Saffron's claim?","o":["Saffron can claim, because the oral agreement did not comply with the clause and so did not vary the contract.","Saffron cannot claim, because parties are always free to vary a written contract orally despite such a clause.","Saffron cannot claim, because the oral agreement to extend the completion date was a binding variation.","Saffron can claim, but only if Avalon gave no consideration for the oral agreement to extend the date.","Saffron cannot claim, because a clause restricting oral variations is void as being against public policy."],"a":0,"e":"**A \"no oral modification\" clause, requiring variations to be in writing and signed, is valid and effective, so an oral variation that does not comply with it has no legal effect** (Rock Advertising v MWB Business Exchange Centres). The completion date therefore remained 1 September and Saffron can claim for the delay. Consideration is irrelevant once the clause defeats the variation. An estoppel might prevent Saffron relying on the clause, but that would need words or conduct unequivocally representing that the informal variation was valid, and something more than the oral agreement itself, which the facts do not show."},{"t":"implied-terms","p":"FLK1","q":"On 3 February, Rahul buys a coffee machine for £400 from an online retailer for use at home. It is delivered on 5 February. On 20 April it stops working because of an internal fault; Rahul has not misused it. He emails the retailer demanding his money back. The retailer has not yet attempted any repair or replacement.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Rahul's rights under the Consumer Rights Act 2015?","o":["He can reject the machine for a full refund, because it failed within six months of delivery.","He can reject the machine for a refund, less a deduction for his use of it since February.","He can require a repair or replacement, and the fault is presumed to have existed at delivery.","He must prove the fault existed at delivery before he can claim any remedy from the retailer.","He can claim a price reduction at once, because the machine is not of satisfactory quality."],"a":2,"e":"The **short-term right to reject lasts only 30 days** from delivery, so it expired in early March. **After that, a consumer must first seek repair or replacement (s.23); a price reduction or final right to reject is available only after one failed repair or replacement, or if repair or replacement is impossible or not done within a reasonable time or without significant inconvenience (s.24).** Because the fault appeared within six months of delivery, it is presumed to have existed at delivery (s.19(14)), so Rahul does not have to prove it. The six-month period is about this presumption and about deductions on final rejection; it does not extend the right to reject."},{"t":"implied-terms","p":"FLK1","q":"Cobalt Print Ltd orders 5,000 sheets of 120gsm paper from a wholesaler. The contract has no relevant express terms. The wholesaler delivers 5,000 sheets of 115gsm paper, which works perfectly in Cobalt's printers and cannot be told apart from 120gsm paper in use. Paper prices have since fallen, and Cobalt wants to reject the delivery and buy more cheaply elsewhere.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Cobalt's position under the Sale of Goods Act 1979?","o":["It can reject the paper, because a breach of the implied term as to description is always a breach of condition.","It cannot reject the paper, because the paper is of satisfactory quality and fit for the purpose of printing.","It can reject the paper, but only after first giving the wholesaler a chance to repair or replace the paper.","It can reject the paper, because a buyer's motive for rejecting goods that do not conform is irrelevant.","It cannot reject the paper, because the breach is too slight for rejection to be reasonable, but it can claim damages."],"a":4,"e":"Supplying 115gsm paper breaches the **implied condition that goods correspond with their description (s.13)**. However, **under s.15A, which since 2015 governs only non-consumer sales (consumer sales fall under the Consumer Rights Act 2015), a buyer cannot reject for breach of ss.13 to 15 where the breach is so slight that rejection would be unreasonable; the breach is treated as a breach of warranty, giving damages only, and it is for the seller to show the breach is slight (s.15A(3)).** Cobalt is a business buyer and the difference has no effect in use, so it is limited to (probably nominal) damages. Satisfactory quality is beside the point because the breach is of description, and the repair or replacement tiers belong to the Consumer Rights Act, not business sales."},{"t":"exemption-clauses","p":"FLK1","q":"Marlow Bakery Ltd, a small family bakery, buys a commercial oven for £18,000 from Thermex Ltd, a large national supplier, on Thermex's written standard terms of business. One clause limits Thermex's liability for any breach of contract to £250. Thermex is insured against such claims, and the bakery could not have bought a similar oven elsewhere without similar terms. The oven is not of satisfactory quality and the bakery loses £12,000 of business. Thermex relies on the clause.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court will decide whether Thermex can rely on the clause?","o":["The clause is automatically void, because it restricts liability for breach of the implied term as to quality.","The clause is effective only if Thermex proves it was reasonable, judged by the circumstances when the contract was made.","The clause is effective unless the bakery proves it was unreasonable, judged by the circumstances when the oven failed.","The clause is effective, because the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 does not control contracts between businesses.","The clause is effective only if it was individually negotiated, because it appears in written standard terms."],"a":1,"e":"Between businesses, **a clause restricting liability for breach of the implied terms as to quality (UCTA 1977 s.6(1A)), or for breach of contract on one party's written standard terms (s.3), is valid only if it satisfies the reasonableness test**. **The test is judged by what was known or contemplated when the contract was made (s.11(1)), and the burden of proving reasonableness is on the party relying on the clause (s.11(5)).** Only exclusion of the implied term as to title is automatically void (s.6(1)), and automatic invalidity for quality terms applies to consumer contracts under the Consumer Rights Act 2015. Inequality of bargaining power, Thermex's insurance and the tiny cap suggest Thermex will struggle to prove reasonableness."},{"t":"exemption-clauses","p":"FLK1","q":"Imogen books a horse-riding lesson at a riding school. The booking form she signs states: \"The school accepts no liability for any injury to riders, however caused.\" During the lesson, the instructor carelessly lets Imogen's horse bolt out of an open gate onto a road, and Imogen breaks her wrist. When she claims compensation, the school relies on the clause and argues that by signing the form Imogen accepted the risks of riding.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the school can rely on the clause?","o":["It can, because Imogen signed the booking form and is bound by its terms whether or not she read them.","It can, but only if it proves that the term was fair, taking account of the nature of horse riding.","It cannot, but only because the clause was not specifically drawn to Imogen's attention before she signed.","It cannot, because a trader cannot exclude liability to a consumer for personal injury caused by negligence.","It can, because by signing the form Imogen voluntarily accepted the risk of being injured while riding."],"a":3,"e":"**Under the Consumer Rights Act 2015 s.65(1), a trader cannot by a term or notice exclude or restrict liability for death or personal injury resulting from negligence; and under s.65(2), a consumer's agreement to or awareness of such a term does not by itself show voluntary acceptance of the risk.** Signing the form incorporates the clause, but statute makes it ineffective, so no fairness test is needed. The school's careless supervision is negligence, so Imogen can claim despite the clause."},{"t":"misrepresentation","p":"FLK1","q":"Gwen is selling the freehold of a small industrial unit to Ravi, who wants to run a joinery workshop there. Before contracts are exchanged, Ravi asks whether there have been any problems with the neighbours. Gwen replies: \"I am not aware of any court proceedings.\" This is literally true, but Gwen knows that the owner of the adjoining unit has sent her a solicitor's letter threatening to sue over noise and dust. Ravi buys the unit relying on her answer, and the neighbour then sues him.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Gwen made a misrepresentation?","o":["She did, because a literally true statement that misleads by what it leaves out can be a misrepresentation.","She did not, because everything she said in reply to Ravi's question was literally true at the time she said it.","She did not, because a seller of property is under no duty to tell the buyer about any dispute with neighbours.","She did not, because Ravi could have made his own enquiries of the neighbour before he agreed to buy the unit.","She did, but only if her reply about the neighbours was included in the contract of sale as an express term."],"a":0,"e":"Silence is generally not a misrepresentation, but **a half-truth, meaning a statement that is literally true but misleading because of what it omits, can amount to a misrepresentation** (Dimmock v Hallett; Nottingham Patent Brick and Tile v Butler). By mentioning only court proceedings when she knew of a threatened claim, Gwen gave the false impression that there was no dispute. It does not matter that Ravi could have checked for himself (Redgrave v Hurd), and misrepresentation concerns pre-contract statements, so her answer did not need to be a term."},{"t":"misrepresentation","p":"FLK1","q":"Hollis Ltd buys a fleet of second-hand delivery vans from Dunmore Motors Ltd after Dunmore's sales manager says that every van has \"a full service history\". He honestly believed this, but he had not looked at the records, several of which were in fact missing. Because of the vans' poor condition, Hollis suffers losses, some of which could not reasonably have been foreseen when the contract was made. Hollis claims damages under the Misrepresentation Act 1967 s.2(1).\n\nWhich of the following best describes Hollis's claim?","o":["Hollis must prove the manager was careless; if it does, it can recover only the losses that were reasonably foreseeable.","Dunmore must prove it had reasonable grounds for its belief; if not, it is liable for all direct loss, even unforeseeable loss.","Dunmore is not liable to pay any damages, because its sales manager honestly believed that his statement was true.","Dunmore must prove it had reasonable grounds for its belief; if not, it is liable only for losses that were foreseeable.","Hollis must prove the manager was dishonest, because damages under s.2(1) are assessed as if the statement were fraudulent."],"a":1,"e":"**Under s.2(1), once the claimant proves a misrepresentation that induced the contract, the representor is liable unless it proves it had reasonable grounds to believe, and did believe, the statement was true up to the time of the contract; damages are then assessed as for fraud, covering all loss directly flowing from the misrepresentation, whether or not foreseeable** (Royscot Trust v Rogerson). Honest belief is not enough without reasonable grounds, and Dunmore had none because its manager did not check. Hollis does not need to prove carelessness or dishonesty: the \"fiction of fraud\" affects only the measure of damages."},{"t":"mistake-duress-ui-illegality","p":"FLK1","q":"A man calling himself \"Dr Simon Clarke\", the name of a well-known local surgeon, visits Beatrix at home to buy a vintage watch she has advertised for £6,000. Beatrix checks online that a surgeon of that name exists and then accepts the man's cheque. He is in fact a fraudster called Lee, and the cheque bounces. Before Beatrix discovers the fraud, Lee sells the watch to Jun, who pays £5,500 for it in good faith. Beatrix seeks to recover the watch from Jun.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Beatrix can recover the watch?","o":["She can, because her mistake about the buyer's identity made her contract with Lee void from the start.","She can, because a fraudster can never pass to a buyer any better title to goods than he himself has.","She cannot, because she was careless in accepting a cheque from a buyer she had never met before.","She can, because her online check shows that the buyer's identity was vital to her decision to sell.","She cannot, because her contract with Lee was voidable and she had not avoided it before Jun bought the watch."],"a":4,"e":"**Where the parties deal face to face, the seller is presumed to intend to contract with the person physically present, whoever he is; a fraud about identity makes the contract voidable for misrepresentation, not void for mistake** (Lewis v Averay; Shogun Finance v Hudson). A voidable contract passes title to the rogue until it is avoided (rescinded), so Jun, who bought in good faith before Beatrix rescinded, gets good title (Sale of Goods Act 1979 s.23). Checking that the named person exists does not displace the face-to-face presumption, and Beatrix's carelessness is not the reason she loses."},{"t":"mistake-duress-ui-illegality","p":"FLK1","q":"Quayside Events Ltd has a fixed-price contract with Lumen AV Ltd for the lighting at a large corporate conference. Three days before the conference, Lumen says it will not turn up unless Quayside pays an extra £15,000. Lumen has no legitimate reason for this, and no other supplier is available at such short notice. Quayside agrees, protesting in writing, and pays after the conference. It then does nothing about the payment for ten months, during which it books Lumen for two more events on normal terms, before seeking to recover the £15,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Quayside can rescind the agreement to pay the extra £15,000?","o":["It can, because Quayside protested in writing at the time it agreed to pay the extra money.","It cannot, because a threat to break a contract is always legitimate commercial pressure.","It cannot, because although there was economic duress, Quayside has since affirmed the agreement.","It can, because economic duress makes the agreement void rather than merely voidable.","It cannot, because Quayside could have refused to pay and sued Lumen for breach instead."],"a":2,"e":"**Economic duress (illegitimate pressure, such as an unjustified threat to break a contract, that leaves the victim no practical alternative) makes the resulting agreement voidable, but the right to rescind is lost if the victim affirms the agreement, for example by long delay without complaint** (The Atlantic Baron). Lumen's threat was illegitimate and suing later for breach was no realistic alternative three days before the conference, so there was duress. But waiting ten months and continuing to deal normally with Lumen amounts to affirmation, so protesting at the time is not enough to save the claim."},{"t":"mistake-duress-ui-illegality","p":"FLK1","q":"Rosa agrees to charge the home she owns jointly with her husband, Gareth, as security for a £150,000 overdraft for Gareth's building company, in which Rosa has no shares or role. The bank sends all the documents to Gareth, who brings them back signed by Rosa. The bank never contacts Rosa directly or asks for confirmation that she has had independent legal advice. Rosa later proves that Gareth obtained her signature by undue influence. The company defaults and the bank seeks to enforce the charge.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Rosa can have the charge set aside against the bank?","o":["She cannot, because the bank itself did not put any pressure on Rosa to sign the charge over the home.","She cannot, because the bank had no actual knowledge that Gareth had used undue influence over Rosa.","She can, because any charge a wife gives to secure her husband's debts is presumed to be invalid.","She can, because the bank was put on inquiry and failed to take reasonable steps to protect her interests.","She cannot, because she owns the home jointly and so benefited from the company's overdraft facility."],"a":3,"e":"**A lender is put on inquiry whenever a person in a non-commercial relationship with the debtor, such as a spouse, offers to stand surety for the debts of the debtor or the debtor's company; it must then take reasonable steps, such as dealing with the surety directly and obtaining a solicitor's written confirmation that she has been advised, or it is fixed with notice of any undue influence** (Royal Bank of Scotland v Etridge (No 2)). The bank dealt only through Gareth, so it is affected by his undue influence even without actual knowledge. There is no automatic presumption against wives' guarantees, and Rosa did not benefit from a loan to Gareth's company merely by co-owning the house."},{"t":"discharge-frustration","p":"FLK1","q":"On 1 March, Bellamy Ltd hires a conference hall from Northgate Council for a trade fair on 1 July. On 1 May, Northgate tells Bellamy that it has decided to use the hall for another event and will not honour the booking. Bellamy replies that it insists on the contract being performed and will hold Northgate to it. On 1 June, the hall is destroyed by a fire that is nobody's fault. Bellamy now claims damages from Northgate for its lost profit on the fair.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Bellamy's claim?","o":["It will succeed, because Northgate's anticipatory breach on 1 May gave Bellamy an accrued right to damages.","It will fail, because Bellamy affirmed the contract and the contract was then frustrated before performance was due.","It will succeed, because a party that has committed an anticipatory breach cannot rely on later frustration.","It will fail, because an anticipatory breach never gives a right to damages until the date for performance.","It will succeed, but the damages will be reduced for Bellamy's failure to mitigate its loss after 1 May."],"a":1,"e":"**On an anticipatory breach (a party saying in advance that it will not perform), the innocent party may accept it, terminate and sue at once, or affirm and keep the contract alive; if it affirms, it takes the risk that a later event frustrates the contract and ends both parties' obligations before any actual breach** (Avery v Bowden). Bellamy affirmed, and the fire frustrated the contract before 1 July, so there was never an accepted breach to sue for. Had Bellamy accepted the repudiation in May, it could have sued immediately (and would then have had to mitigate)."},{"t":"discharge-frustration","p":"FLK1","q":"Kofi books a historic barn as the venue for his daughter's wedding on 10 June, for a total fee of £15,000. He pays £5,000 on booking. A further £4,000 was due on 1 May, but Kofi has not paid it. The balance of £6,000 is due on 1 July. On 20 May the barn is destroyed by lightning, frustrating the contract. Before the fire, the barn's owner had spent £2,500 on bespoke preparations for the wedding. The contract says nothing about frustration.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position under the Law Reform (Frustrated Contracts) Act 1943?","o":["Kofi can recover the £5,000 and need not pay the £4,000, but the court may allow the owner up to £2,500 for its expenses.","Kofi can recover the £5,000 but must still pay the £4,000, and the court may allow the owner up to £2,500 for its expenses.","Kofi cannot recover the £5,000, because money paid before a frustrating event stays with the party who received it.","Kofi can recover the £5,000 and need not pay the £4,000, and the owner cannot claim anything for its expenses.","Kofi can recover the £5,000 and need not pay the £4,000, but the court may allow the owner up to £9,000 for its expenses."],"a":0,"e":"**Under s.1(2), money paid before the frustrating event is recoverable and money payable before it ceases to be payable, but the court may, if just, allow the payee to keep or recover a sum for expenses incurred in performing the contract, up to the total paid or payable before the event.** Here the cap is £9,000 (£5,000 paid plus £4,000 payable), but the allowance cannot exceed the expenses actually incurred, so the most the owner can be allowed is £2,500, and only at the court's discretion. The £6,000 due after the fire is irrelevant, and the old common law rule that losses lie where they fall was reversed by the Act."},{"t":"discharge-frustration","p":"FLK1","q":"Pembury Construction Ltd agrees to build a sports pavilion for Ashdown Rugby Club for a fixed price of £600,000, to be completed in eight months. Because of a national shortage of skilled labour and a sharp rise in the price of steel, neither of which was Pembury's fault, the work takes 14 months and costs Pembury £850,000. Pembury argues that the contract was frustrated and that it should be paid a reasonable sum for the work instead of the fixed price.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Pembury's argument?","o":["It succeeds, because the delays were caused by events that were outside Pembury's control and not its fault.","It succeeds, because completing the pavilion cost Pembury over 40% more than the price it had agreed to.","It fails, because a contract can be frustrated only where its subject matter is physically destroyed.","It fails, because performance has become more costly and onerous but not radically different from what was agreed.","It succeeds, because the work took almost twice as long as the period the parties originally agreed."],"a":3,"e":"**A contract is frustrated only where, without the fault of either party, a supervening event makes performance impossible, illegal or radically different from what was undertaken; delay, hardship or a rise in cost, even a large one, is not enough** (Davis Contractors v Fareham UDC). The pavilion could still be built; it simply took longer and cost more, a risk Pembury accepted by agreeing a fixed price. Frustration is not limited to destruction of the subject matter, but events beyond a party's control do not frustrate a contract unless they change the nature of performance."},{"t":"damages","p":"FLK1","q":"Elmfield Dyeworks Ltd orders a new industrial boiler from Vantage Engineering Ltd for delivery on 1 March. Vantage knows that Elmfield is a dyeing business that needs the boiler to expand its production. Vantage delivers ten weeks late. Elmfield claims (1) the ordinary profits it would have made from the extra production during those ten weeks, and (2) the profit on an exceptionally lucrative government dyeing contract that it lost because of the delay. Vantage knew nothing about the government contract.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Elmfield can recover?","o":["It can recover both, because both losses were in fact caused by Vantage's late delivery.","It can recover neither, because lost profits are too remote unless expressly agreed.","It can recover only the government contract profit, as it is a specific identifiable loss.","It can recover both, because a party in breach must take the claimant as it finds it.","It can recover the ordinary lost profits, but not the profit on the government contract."],"a":4,"e":"**Under the remoteness rule, a claimant can recover loss that arises naturally in the usual course of things, or that was in the reasonable contemplation of both parties when the contract was made because of special knowledge the defendant had** (Hadley v Baxendale). Vantage knew the boiler was needed for production, so ordinary lost profits were within contemplation, but it had no knowledge of the exceptionally lucrative contract, so that loss is too remote (Victoria Laundry v Newman Industries). Causation alone is not enough, and the \"take the claimant as you find them\" principle belongs to personal injury in tort, not contract remoteness."},{"t":"damages","p":"FLK1","q":"Torridon Software Ltd licenses its stock-control system to Pike Retail Ltd for £2,000 a month under a three-year contract negotiated by both companies' lawyers. One clause states that if Pike is more than seven days late with any monthly payment, Pike must immediately pay Torridon £250,000. Pike pays one month's fee nine days late. Torridon's only loss is a small amount of interest, and it has no interest in the performance of the contract beyond being paid on time. Torridon sues Pike for £250,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the clause is enforceable?","o":["It is enforceable, because it was negotiated by commercial parties who each had legal advice.","It is enforceable, because a court will never interfere with a sum agreed as payable on breach.","It is a penalty, because it is out of all proportion to Torridon's legitimate interest in payment.","It is a penalty, because it was not a genuine pre-estimate of the loss Torridon would suffer.","It is a penalty, because any sum payable on breach that exceeds the actual loss is a penalty."],"a":2,"e":"**A clause is a penalty, and unenforceable, if it is a secondary obligation that imposes a detriment on the contract-breaker out of all proportion to any legitimate interest of the innocent party in the performance of the primary obligation** (Cavendish Square Holding v Makdessi; ParkingEye v Beavis). £250,000 for a nine-day delay in a £2,000 payment is out of all proportion to Torridon's interest in being paid on time, so Torridon can recover only its actual loss. The old \"genuine pre-estimate of loss\" test has been replaced, a sum exceeding actual loss is not automatically penal, and legal advice and equal bargaining power make a clause more likely, but not certain, to be upheld."},{"t":"damages","p":"FLK1","q":"Larchmont Garden Centre Ltd has a contract with Fernhill Nurseries Ltd for weekly deliveries of bedding plants at £1,000 a week for the season, with 30 days' credit. Fernhill, in breach of contract, announces that it will continue deliveries at the same price only if Larchmont pays on delivery. Larchmont, which could easily have paid on delivery, refuses, terminates the contract and buys the plants from another nursery at the market price of £1,400 a week.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Larchmont's damages?","o":["They are limited to what Larchmont would have lost by paying on delivery, because it unreasonably refused Fernhill's offer.","They include the full extra £400 a week, because Larchmont was entitled to terminate the contract and buy elsewhere.","They are nil, because a party that chooses to terminate a contract cannot also claim damages for the breach.","They include the full extra £400 a week, because an innocent party never has to accept an offer from the party in breach.","They are reduced by half, because both parties' conduct contributed to the increase in the cost of the plants."],"a":0,"e":"**A claimant cannot recover loss it could have avoided by taking reasonable steps to mitigate, and this can include accepting a reasonable offer from the party in breach** (Payzu v Saunders). Larchmont could easily have paid on delivery at the contract price, so its damages are limited to the cost of losing the credit period, not the extra £400 a week. Termination does not prevent a damages claim, and there is no general apportionment of contract damages for \"contribution\" of this kind; the burden of proving a failure to mitigate is on the defendant."},{"t":"equitable-remedies-guarantees","p":"FLK1","q":"Granary Wharf Ltd owns a small shopping parade and leases the largest unit to Harvest Foods Ltd for 20 years. The lease includes a covenant by Harvest to keep the unit open as a supermarket during normal trading hours. With 15 years of the lease left, Harvest closes the supermarket because it is losing money, though it keeps paying the rent. The other shops in the parade lose trade as a result. Granary Wharf asks the court to order Harvest to reopen and keep trading.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court is likely to respond?","o":["It will probably make the order, because damages are always treated as inadequate where land is concerned.","It will probably refuse the order, because it would require supervising the running of a business for many years.","It will probably make the order, because Harvest broke its covenant deliberately for its own financial gain.","It must make the order, because specific performance is available as of right for breach of a covenant in a lease.","It will probably refuse the order, because Harvest is still paying the rent and so has not breached the lease."],"a":1,"e":"**Specific performance is a discretionary remedy, and the court will not normally order a party to carry on a business or other continuing activity over a long period, because it would require constant supervision and repeated applications to court** (Co-operative Insurance Society v Argyll Stores). Harvest has breached the keep-open covenant even though it pays the rent, but Granary Wharf will be left to claim damages. Damages are often inadequate for contracts to buy land, but that does not make specific performance automatic, and a deliberate breach does not remove the supervision bar."},{"t":"equitable-remedies-guarantees","p":"FLK1","q":"Daniel runs a small catering business and wants to buy kitchen equipment on credit from Steelcraft Ltd. Steelcraft will not give him credit until Daniel's mother, Ruth, telephones Steelcraft's manager and says: \"Give Daniel the credit. If he doesn't pay you, I will.\" Nothing is put in writing. Relying on Ruth's promise, Steelcraft supplies the equipment on credit. Daniel fails to pay, and Steelcraft sues Ruth.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Ruth is liable?","o":["She is, because her promise was an indemnity, which is enforceable even though it was made only orally.","She is, because Steelcraft relied on her oral promise when it agreed to supply the equipment on credit.","She is not, because she received no benefit herself in return for promising to pay Steelcraft for Daniel.","She is not, because her promise was a guarantee, which is unenforceable unless evidenced in signed writing.","She is, because her promise made her primarily responsible for paying the debt alongside Daniel."],"a":3,"e":"**A guarantee is a promise to answer for another person's debt if that person defaults, and under the Statute of Frauds 1677 s.4 it cannot be enforced unless it, or a note of it, is in writing and signed by the guarantor.** \"If he doesn't pay you, I will\" makes Ruth's liability secondary, depending on Daniel's default, so it is a guarantee, not an indemnity (a primary, independent promise to cover a loss, which needs no writing). Steelcraft's reliance on the oral promise cannot be used to get round the statute (Actionstrength v International Glass Engineering), and consideration is not the problem, because Steelcraft's giving of credit to Daniel at Ruth's request is consideration."}],"contract-p2":[{"t":"offer-acceptance","p":"FLK1","q":"Harrow Fabrications Ltd sends Delta Steel Ltd a purchase order for 40 tonnes of steel sheet on Harrow's standard terms, which limit Delta's liability for late delivery to £5,000 rather than excluding it. Delta replies with an 'order acknowledgement' stating that the sale is on Delta's own standard terms, which exclude all liability for late delivery and are stated to prevail over any other terms. Harrow does not respond, but when the steel arrives two weeks late it accepts it and uses it. Harrow now wants to claim for losses caused by the delay.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whose terms govern the contract?","o":["Harrow's terms, because the purchase order was the offer and Delta's acknowledgement simply confirmed the order.","Neither set of terms, because the parties never agreed on delay and so no contract was ever concluded.","Delta's terms, because its acknowledgement was a counter-offer which Harrow accepted by taking the steel.","Harrow's terms, because Delta's acknowledgement came after the order and could not alter the terms offered.","A mixture of both, because the court will pick whichever terms on each form are the most reasonable."],"a":2,"e":"**A reply that introduces different terms is a counter-offer, which kills the original offer, and a counter-offer can be accepted by conduct.** Delta's acknowledgement was the 'last shot', and Harrow accepted it by taking delivery and using the steel (Butler Machine Tool v Ex-Cell-O). A and D are wrong because the acknowledgement did not mirror Harrow's order, so it was not an acceptance; B is wrong because the parties' conduct shows a contract was made, and E is wrong because the court does not blend the two sets of terms. Delta's exclusion clause would still have to pass the reasonableness test under the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977, but the question asks only whose terms apply."},{"t":"offer-acceptance","p":"FLK1","q":"Siobhan, who owns a classic car, tells her neighbour Arjun: 'If you drive my car to the vintage show in York on Saturday and bring it back, I'll pay you £300.' Arjun says nothing in reply. On Saturday he sets off, and he has driven 60 miles when Siobhan phones to say she has changed her mind and he must bring the car straight back. Arjun ignores her, drives to York and back as planned and asks for the £300.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Arjun's position?","o":["He can claim the £300, because once he had begun performance Siobhan was not entitled to revoke her offer.","He cannot claim the £300, because he never told Siobhan that he accepted her offer before setting off.","He cannot claim the £300, because an offer may be revoked at any time before it has been accepted.","He can claim only a reasonable sum for the 60 miles he had driven before Siobhan revoked her offer.","He can claim the £300, because his silence in reply to the offer amounted to an acceptance of it."],"a":0,"e":"This is a **unilateral contract** (a promise in return for an act): it is accepted by completing the act, and **once the offeree has started to perform, the offeror impliedly promises not to revoke** (Errington v Errington). B is wrong because acceptance of a unilateral offer need not be communicated in advance. C states the general rule, but it is displaced once performance of a unilateral offer has begun. E reaches the right result for the wrong reason: silence is not acceptance, and here it was Arjun's performance that accepted the offer."},{"t":"offer-acceptance","p":"FLK1","q":"At 9.30 am on Monday, Corbin Joinery Ltd emails Westway Builders Ltd offering to supply 200 fire doors at £180 each and stating that the offer is open until 5 pm on Friday. Westway's buyer emails an acceptance to Corbin's sales address at 11 am on Wednesday, during Corbin's normal business hours. At 2 pm on Wednesday, Corbin's sales director, who has not yet looked at the inbox, emails Westway withdrawing the offer. He reads Westway's acceptance at 3 pm.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["There is no contract, because an email acceptance takes effect only when the offeror actually reads it.","There is no contract, because Corbin sent its withdrawal before its director had read the acceptance.","There is a contract, because an acceptance sent by email takes effect at the moment the offeree sends it.","There is no contract, because an offer stated to be open for a fixed period may be withdrawn at any time.","There is a contract, because the acceptance took effect when it reached Corbin's inbox in business hours."],"a":4,"e":"**An acceptance by instantaneous communication such as email takes effect when it is received, which means when it arrives during business hours and could reasonably be expected to be read**, not when it is actually read (Entores; Brinkibon). The acceptance therefore took effect at 11 am, before the 2 pm withdrawal. A and B wrongly make actual reading the test. C reaches the right result for the wrong reason: the postal rule (acceptance on sending) does not apply to email. D is true as far as it goes, since a promise to keep an offer open is not binding without consideration, but a withdrawal is too late once acceptance has taken effect."},{"t":"consideration","p":"FLK1","q":"Faisal, a freelance web developer, is asked by Moorland Cycles Ltd to rebuild its online shop urgently before a sales launch. Nothing is said about payment, but Faisal always charges for his work. Two weeks after the new site goes live, Moorland's managing director emails Faisal: 'Thank you for all your hard work. We will pay you £4,000 for it.' Moorland later refuses to pay, arguing that Faisal's work was past consideration.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Faisal can enforce the promise to pay £4,000?","o":["He cannot, because his work was finished before the promise was made and so is past consideration.","He can, because he did the work at Moorland's request and both sides understood it would be paid for.","He cannot, because no price was agreed in advance and so there was never any contract for the work.","He can, because a promise made in writing by a company director does not need any consideration.","He can, because the promise to pay was made within a reasonable time of the work being completed."],"a":1,"e":"Past consideration is generally no consideration, but **an earlier act counts as consideration if it was done at the promisor's request, both parties understood it would be paid for, and a promise of payment made in advance would have been enforceable** (Pao On v Lau Yiu Long). All three conditions are met here, so the later promise simply fixes the price. A states the general rule but ignores this exception. D is wrong because only a deed removes the need for consideration, and E is wrong because the timing of the promise is not the test."},{"t":"consideration","p":"FLK1","q":"Brennan Plant Hire Ltd is owed £20,000 by Oakridge Groundworks Ltd under a hire contract, payable on 30 September. In July, Brennan's finance director, who needs cash quickly to meet a tax bill, writes to Oakridge: 'If you pay us £16,000 by 31 July, we will accept it in full settlement.' Oakridge pays £16,000 on 25 July. In October, Brennan sues Oakridge for the remaining £4,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Brennan's claim will succeed?","o":["It will succeed, because paying a smaller sum can never discharge a debt for a larger amount.","It will fail, because Brennan obtained a practical benefit by having part of the money paid to it.","It will succeed, because Brennan's letter was not a deed and so could not release the balance.","It will fail, because payment before the due date at Brennan's request was good consideration.","It will succeed, because promissory estoppel only suspends rights and Brennan may now claim."],"a":3,"e":"**Part payment of a debt does not discharge the balance, unless the creditor receives something extra at its request, such as payment earlier than the due date** (Pinnel's Case; Foakes v Beer). Oakridge paid in July, two months early, at Brennan's request, so it gave fresh consideration and the debt is discharged. A overstates the rule by ignoring this exception. B is wrong because the practical benefit idea has not been extended to part payment of debts (Re Selectmove). C is wrong because a deed is needed only where there is no consideration, and E is irrelevant because Oakridge does not need estoppel when it has given consideration."},{"t":"consideration","p":"FLK1","q":"Haverley Removals Ltd has a contract with Joseph to move his furniture out of his rented flat on 14 May. Joseph's landlord, Iris, is anxious that the flat is cleared that day so that new tenants can move in, and she promises Haverley £500 if it completes the move on time. Haverley does so, but Iris refuses to pay, saying that Haverley was already bound to do the work.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Haverley can enforce Iris's promise?","o":["It can, because performing a contractual duty already owed to a third party is good consideration.","It cannot, because performing a duty already owed under an existing contract is never consideration.","It cannot, because only Joseph may enforce the obligation to carry out the removal on 14 May.","It can, but only if it shows it gave Iris a practical benefit going beyond the move itself.","It cannot, because Iris received no benefit, as she was not a party to the removal contract."],"a":0,"e":"**Performing, or promising to perform, a duty already owed to a third party under a contract is good consideration for a fresh promise by someone else** (Scotson v Pegg; Pao On v Lau Yiu Long). Haverley's duty was owed to Joseph, so completing the move was consideration for Iris's separate promise. B is wrong because the 'existing duty' problem arises only where the duty is owed to the promisor. D wrongly applies the practical benefit test, which concerns two parties varying their own contract, and E is wrong because Iris did benefit: her flat was cleared in time for the new tenants."},{"t":"intention-certainty-capacity","p":"FLK1","q":"Calloway Fragrances Ltd agrees with Rossiter Retail Ltd that Rossiter will stock Calloway's perfumes in its 30 stores for two years. The signed agreement sets out prices and quantities in detail but includes a clause stating: 'This arrangement is not entered into as a formal or legal agreement, shall not be subject to the jurisdiction of the courts and is binding in honour only.' After eight months Calloway stops supplying without notice, and Rossiter wants to sue for the profits it will lose over the rest of the two years.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Rossiter's position?","o":["It can sue, because in a commercial agreement the presumption of legal intention cannot be displaced.","It can sue, because the detailed prices and quantities make the agreement certain enough to enforce.","It can sue, because a clause ousting the jurisdiction of the courts is void as against public policy.","It cannot sue, because the express wording rebuts the presumption that legal relations were intended.","It cannot sue, because a supply agreement lasting longer than a year must be made by deed to bind."],"a":3,"e":"In commercial agreements there is a **presumption that the parties intend legal relations, but it can be rebutted by clear express words such as 'binding in honour only'** (Rose and Frank v Crompton). A is wrong because the presumption is rebuttable. B confuses certainty with intention: a detailed agreement can still lack the intention to be legally bound. C is wrong because the courts treat an honour clause as showing there was no intention to create legal relations, not as a void ouster clause; individual orders already placed and accepted may still be enforceable, but the two-year arrangement is not."},{"t":"intention-certainty-capacity","p":"FLK1","q":"Wilfred, who has advanced dementia, signs an agreement with Sunrise Conservatories Ltd for a new conservatory costing £28,000. The sales representative who visits him notices that he repeatedly forgets who she is and cannot say what year it is, but she completes the sale anyway. Wilfred's daughter discovers the agreement before any work has begun and wants to know whether Wilfred is bound by it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the status of the contract?","o":["It is void, because a person who lacks mental capacity can never enter into a binding contract.","It is voidable, because Wilfred lacked capacity and the representative knew or ought to have known.","It is valid, because a conservatory is a necessary and Wilfred must pay a reasonable price for it.","It is valid, because Wilfred signed the agreement and is bound whether or not he understood it.","It is voidable only if Wilfred can show the price was unfair compared with the market rate."],"a":1,"e":"**A contract made by a person who lacks mental capacity is voidable at that person's option if the other party knew, or ought to have known, of the incapacity** (Imperial Loan Co v Stone; Hart v O'Connor). The representative saw clear signs of confusion, so Wilfred (acting through someone with authority) can avoid the contract. A overstates the rule: such contracts are voidable, not void. C is wrong because only necessaries, goods and services suitable to the person's condition in life and actual needs, attract a duty to pay a reasonable price, and an expensive conservatory is not one. E is wrong because, where the other party has knowledge, the contract can be avoided without proving the price was unfair."},{"t":"privity-third-parties","p":"FLK1","q":"Pinehurst Developments Ltd contracts with Kerr Roofing Ltd to re-roof a block of flats owned by its subsidiary, Pinehurst Lettings Ltd. The contract expressly states that Pinehurst Lettings may enforce Kerr's obligations under it, and also allows Kerr to set off against any claim the amount of any unpaid invoice under the contract. Kerr does the work defectively, and Pinehurst Developments has not paid Kerr's final invoice of £15,000. Pinehurst Lettings sues Kerr for £40,000, the cost of repairs.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Kerr's position?","o":["It has no defence, because Pinehurst Lettings is not responsible for the debts of its parent company.","It can defeat the claim entirely, because a third party can enforce a term only if it gave consideration.","It has no defence, because a third party enforcing a term takes it free of any set-off between the parties.","It can defeat the claim entirely, because a parent and its subsidiary count as one party under the Act.","It can reduce the claim by £15,000, because it may rely on a set-off it would have had against the promisee."],"a":4,"e":"Pinehurst Lettings can enforce the term because the contract expressly says so (Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999, s1(1)(a)), but **under s3 the promisor (the party who made the promise, here Kerr) may raise against the third party any defence or set-off arising from the contract that it could have raised against the promisee**. Kerr can therefore set off the unpaid £15,000 and the claim is reduced to £25,000. A and C ignore s3, and B is wrong because the Act does not require the third party to have given consideration. D is wrong because the Act treats the two companies as separate persons."},{"t":"privity-third-parties","p":"FLK1","q":"Avonmouth Marina Ltd wants new pontoons and asks Brookes Paints Ltd whether its marine coating is suitable. Brookes's technical manager assures Avonmouth in writing that the coating will protect the pontoons from corrosion for at least ten years. Avonmouth then instructs its contractor, Delaney Marine Ltd, to buy that coating from Brookes and use it. Delaney does so, but the coating fails within a year and the pontoons corrode. The contract for the coating was between Delaney and Brookes only.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Avonmouth can sue Brookes in contract?","o":["It cannot, because Avonmouth was not a party to the contract under which the coating was bought.","It cannot, because a written assurance about goods is a mere representation and not a promise.","It can, because the assurance was given in return for Avonmouth arranging for the coating to be bought.","It cannot, because Delaney is the only party that has suffered loss from the failure of the coating.","It can, because the 1999 Act gives contractual rights to anyone who ends up using the goods supplied."],"a":2,"e":"**A collateral contract arises where one party gives a promise in return for the other entering into, or arranging for a third party to enter into, a main contract** (Shanklin Pier v Detel Products). Avonmouth's consideration was causing Delaney to buy Brookes's coating, so it can sue Brookes on the ten-year assurance. A ignores the collateral contract, which gets round privity. B is wrong because a specific written assurance like this can be a contractual promise, and D is wrong because Avonmouth owns the corroded pontoons. E is wrong because the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999 applies only where a term of the contract confers a benefit on, or allows enforcement by, the third party, not simply because the third party uses the goods."},{"t":"express-terms","p":"FLK1","q":"Leon, who knows little about cars, visits Prestige Motors Ltd, a specialist dealer in used sports cars, and asks about a coupé on its forecourt. After checking the car's records, the salesman tells him: 'It has done just 22,000 miles since a full engine rebuild.' Leon buys the car for £31,000. The short written contract does not mention the engine. In fact the engine has never been rebuilt and the car is worth £24,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court is likely to treat the salesman's statement?","o":["As a mere representation, because it was not included in the written contract that Leon signed.","As a term, because a dealer with special knowledge made it to a buyer who relied on that expertise.","As a mere puff, because sales talk about used cars is not meant to be taken seriously by buyers.","As a mere representation, because Leon could have checked the car's history himself before buying.","As a term, because every statement about a car made before it is sold becomes a contract term."],"a":1,"e":"Whether a statement is a term depends on what the parties intended, judged objectively, and **a statement by a party with special knowledge, relied on by a party without it, is likely to be a term** (Dick Bentley v Harold Smith; contrast Oscar Chess v Williams, where a private seller's statement was not). A is wrong because a contract can be partly written and partly oral, so leaving the statement out of the document is not decisive. C is wrong because a specific statement of fact is not a mere puff (vague sales talk), and D is wrong because the buyer's ability to check does not stop the statement being a term. E overstates the position: many pre-contract statements remain mere representations, which is why the maker's special knowledge and the buyer's reliance matter here (Leon could also claim for misrepresentation)."},{"t":"express-terms","p":"FLK1","q":"Imran, a private individual, has taken his car to Halstead Garage Ltd for servicing three times in the past four years. On one of those occasions he signed a form containing a clause excluding the garage's liability for damage by fire to customers' cars. This time he books the service by phone and nothing is said or signed about terms. While the car is at the garage it is damaged by a fire caused by an employee's carelessness, and the garage relies on its fire clause.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the fire clause is part of this contract?","o":["It is, because Imran had signed the clause before and is therefore taken to know about it.","It is, because the garage's standard terms apply automatically to every service it carries out.","It is not, because exclusion clauses about cars can be incorporated only by a written notice.","It is, because three visits over four years amount to a regular course of dealing between them.","It is not, because three visits over four years are not a regular and consistent course of dealing."],"a":4,"e":"**A term can be incorporated by a previous course of dealing only if the dealings were regular and consistent** (McCutcheon v MacBrayne; Hollier v Rambler Motors). Three visits in four years, with the clause signed on only one of them, is not enough, especially for a consumer, so the clause was never part of this phone contract. A is wrong because signing the clause once on an earlier contract does not carry it into a later contract made without it. B is wrong because standard terms apply only if incorporated, and C invents a rule: signature, reasonable notice and course of dealing are all possible routes."},{"t":"implied-terms","p":"FLK1","q":"Grafton Hall Ltd hires out its country house to Mayfield Weddings Ltd for 20 weddings over the summer. The detailed written contract, negotiated by lawyers on both sides, says nothing about the house's car park. Two weeks before the first wedding, Grafton says the car park will be closed to wedding guests. The house is in a remote area with no public transport and no other parking nearby. Mayfield argues that a term giving guests access to the car park should be implied.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the court is likely to imply such a term?","o":["It is likely to, because without guest parking the contract would lack commercial or practical coherence.","It is unlikely to, because the court will never imply a term into a contract that lawyers have drafted.","It is likely to, because implying the term would make the contract fairer and more reasonable overall.","It is unlikely to, because a term can only be implied into a contract where a statute provides for it.","It is likely to, because terms are implied whenever one party would have liked the contract to include them."],"a":0,"e":"**A term is implied in fact only if it is necessary to give the contract business efficacy, or is so obvious that it goes without saying** (Marks and Spencer v BNP Paribas). Weddings at a remote venue cannot practically take place without guest parking, so the term is necessary. C is the tempting wrong answer: a term is not implied just because it would be reasonable or fair. B overstates the position, since careful drafting makes implication harder but not impossible, and D confuses terms implied in fact with terms implied by statute."},{"t":"implied-terms","p":"FLK1","q":"Esme pays Tilecraft Ltd £800 to tile her kitchen walls using tiles she has bought herself. The tiler works carelessly: several rows are crooked and the gaps between tiles are uneven. The contract is silent on the standard of work. Esme asks what remedy she should pursue first under the Consumer Rights Act 2015.\n\nWhich of the following best describes her position?","o":["She can reject the service and recover the full £800, because the work was done without reasonable care.","She has no statutory remedy, because the Act implies terms only into contracts for the supply of goods.","She can claim a price reduction straight away, without giving the trader any chance to put the work right.","She can require the trader to redo the work properly, within a reasonable time and at the trader's cost.","She can claim only damages in negligence, because no term to take care is implied into such contracts."],"a":3,"e":"**In a consumer service contract the trader must perform the service with reasonable care and skill (Consumer Rights Act 2015, s49), and the consumer's first remedy is repeat performance (s55)**, which must be done within a reasonable time, without significant inconvenience and at the trader's cost. A price reduction under s56 is available only if repeat performance is impossible or is not done within a reasonable time or without significant inconvenience, so C is wrong. A is wrong because there is no short-term right to reject a service. B and E are wrong because the Act implies the reasonable care and skill term into service contracts."},{"t":"exemption-clauses","p":"FLK1","q":"Kingsley Bakeries Ltd hires a refrigerated van for a month from Rapid Van Hire Ltd. Kingsley's manager signs Rapid's standard hire agreement without reading it. Clause 14, in small print on the back, states that Rapid accepts no liability for loss of or damage to goods carried in the van, 'whether caused by negligence or otherwise'. The refrigeration unit, which Rapid negligently serviced, fails and £6,000 of Kingsley's stock is ruined.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Rapid can rely on clause 14?","o":["It cannot, because a clause in small print on the back of a document is not part of the contract.","It can, because Kingsley signed the agreement and so the clause binds it whatever its effect.","It can only if Rapid proves that the clause is reasonable under the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977.","It cannot, because liability for negligence can never be excluded in a contract between businesses.","It can only if Kingsley's manager actually read clause 14 and understood its effect before signing."],"a":2,"e":"**A signed document binds the signer whether or not they read it** (L'Estrange v Graucob), and the clause expressly covers negligence, so it is incorporated and covers this loss; however, **under the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977, s2(2), liability for negligence causing loss other than death or personal injury can be excluded only so far as the term is reasonable, and the party relying on it must prove this** (s11(5)). A and E are wrong because signature incorporates the clause. B ignores the statutory control, and D is wrong because the absolute ban in s2(1) applies only to death and personal injury."},{"t":"exemption-clauses","p":"FLK1","q":"Nkechi buys a £900 sofa in a furniture shop's sale. Her receipt states: 'Sale items: no refunds or returns under any circumstances.' Ten days after delivery she discovers that one of the sofa's legs is cracked and the frame is unstable, a fault that was present on delivery and was not pointed out to her. The shop refuses to deal with her, relying on the wording on the receipt.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the receipt wording?","o":["It is not binding, because a trader cannot exclude liability for goods being of satisfactory quality.","It is binding on Nkechi, because she bought the sofa in a sale and so accepted the risk of any defects.","It is binding only if the shop shows that the wording is fair and was brought to Nkechi's attention.","It is not binding on Nkechi unless the shop proves that the wording was reasonable in the circumstances.","It is binding on Nkechi, because the wording appeared on the receipt she received when she paid."],"a":0,"e":"**Under the Consumer Rights Act 2015, s31, a term that excludes or restricts the trader's liability for the goods being of satisfactory quality (s9) is not binding on the consumer.** Nkechi can therefore use her short-term right to reject, since she is within 30 days. C is tempting, but the general fairness test in s62 is irrelevant where s31 makes the term automatically non-binding. D applies the reasonableness test from the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977, which does not govern contracts between a trader and a consumer. B is wrong because only defects specifically drawn to the buyer's attention are excluded, and E is wrong because wording on a receipt cannot override s31 (and usually comes too late to be incorporated anyway)."},{"t":"exemption-clauses","p":"FLK1","q":"Felling Engineering Ltd buys a second-hand lathe for £12,000 from Marsh Machinery Ltd, a dealer, on Marsh's standard terms. One clause states: 'All conditions and warranties implied by statute, including as to title, quality and fitness for purpose, are excluded.' Four months later the police remove the lathe from Felling's workshop because it had been stolen from its true owner before Marsh acquired it. Felling claims the return of its £12,000 from Marsh.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Marsh can rely on the clause?","o":["It can, because Felling is a business and the clause is reasonable between commercial parties.","It can only if Marsh proves that the clause is reasonable under the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977.","It cannot, because a clause in a seller's standard terms is never effective against a business buyer.","It cannot, because liability for breach of the implied term as to the right to sell cannot be excluded.","It can, because Marsh did not know that the lathe had been stolen when it sold it on to Felling."],"a":3,"e":"**Under the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977, s6(1), liability for breach of the implied term that the seller has the right to sell the goods (Sale of Goods Act 1979, s12) cannot be excluded or restricted by any contract term**, even between businesses. Marsh had no right to sell a stolen lathe, so Felling can recover the full price because it got nothing of what it paid for (Rowland v Divall). B is the tempting wrong answer: the reasonableness test applies to clauses excluding the quality and description terms in a business sale (s6(1A)), not to title. A and C misstate the law, and E is wrong because liability under s12 does not depend on the seller's knowledge."},{"t":"misrepresentation","p":"FLK1","q":"Ottoline is selling a small office building that is let to Garnet Ltd. In the sale particulars she describes Garnet as 'a most reliable tenant'. In fact Ottoline knows that Garnet has paid the last three quarters' rent late, and only after being threatened with legal action. Hamza buys the building, relying on the description, and Garnet goes into insolvency shortly afterwards.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ottoline's description of the tenant?","o":["It is not actionable, because describing a tenant as reliable is merely a statement of opinion.","It is not actionable, because Hamza could have checked the tenant's payment record before buying.","It is actionable, because the opinion implied facts within Ottoline's knowledge that were untrue.","It is not actionable, because Ottoline was under no duty to disclose the tenant's late payments.","It is actionable, but only as a breach of contract, because it appeared in the sale particulars."],"a":2,"e":"**A statement of opinion by someone who knows the facts better than the other party impliedly represents that they know facts which justify the opinion**; if they do not, it is a misrepresentation (Smith v Land and House Property Corp). Ottoline knew of the late payments, so her description was false. A ignores this implied statement of fact. B is wrong because a misrepresentee does not lose the claim by failing to check (Redgrave v Hurd), and D misses the point: this is a positive statement, not mere silence. E is wrong because statements in sale particulars are normally representations, giving the remedies for misrepresentation."},{"t":"misrepresentation","p":"FLK1","q":"In January, Bartholomew begins negotiating to sell his dental practice to Anjali. He tells her, truthfully, that the practice earns £180,000 a year in fees. In March, before contracts are signed, two of the practice's three dentists resign and patient numbers fall sharply, roughly halving the likely fees. Bartholomew says nothing about this, and Anjali signs the contract in May, still relying on his January statement.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Anjali's position?","o":["She has no claim, because the statement was true when made and silence is not a misrepresentation.","She has a claim for misrepresentation, because he failed to correct a statement that became untrue.","She has no claim, because she should have asked for updated figures before she signed the contract.","She has a claim only if the January statement was included in the written contract as a term.","She has a claim for misrepresentation, because a seller of a business must disclose all material facts."],"a":1,"e":"Silence is generally not a misrepresentation, but **a statement that was true when made becomes a misrepresentation if circumstances change before the contract is made and the maker does not correct it** (With v O'Flanagan). Bartholomew's January statement was still operating on Anjali's mind in May. A states the general rule but misses this exception. E overstates the law, because there is no general duty to disclose in a sale of a business. C is wrong because a misrepresentee need not check for themselves, and D confuses misrepresentation with breach of contract."},{"t":"misrepresentation","p":"FLK1","q":"Sunil buys a narrowboat from Waterside Boats Ltd for £45,000 after its salesman says, without checking, that the hull was surveyed last year. A month after buying, Sunil learns that no survey was ever done. He nevertheless keeps using the boat for a two-month holiday cruise, and then writes to Waterside saying he wants to rescind the contract and get his money back. The salesman had no reasonable grounds for his statement.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Sunil's remedies?","o":["He can rescind and recover the price, because negligent misrepresentation always allows rescission.","He cannot rescind, because he affirmed the contract, but he can claim damages under the 1967 Act.","He has no remedy, because by using the boat he gave up all his rights arising from the misrepresentation.","He cannot rescind, and he can claim damages only if he proves that the salesman acted fraudulently.","He can rescind, but the court must award damages instead because the misrepresentation was negligent."],"a":1,"e":"**Rescission (setting the contract aside) is lost if the misrepresentee affirms the contract, for example by continuing to use the goods after discovering the truth.** Sunil's damages claim survives: **under the Misrepresentation Act 1967, s2(1), the representor is liable in damages unless it proves it had reasonable grounds to believe the statement was true**, which Waterside cannot do. C is wrong because affirmation bars only rescission, not damages, and D is wrong because s2(1) does not require proof of fraud. E is wrong twice over: Sunil can no longer rescind, and s2(2) only gives the court a discretion to award damages instead of rescission; it never obliges the court to do so."},{"t":"mistake-duress-ui-illegality","p":"FLK1","q":"On 3 June, Gideon agrees to sell Pemberton Antiques Ltd a specific Georgian bookcase, which is in storage at a warehouse, for £9,000. Unknown to both parties, the bookcase was destroyed in a fire at the warehouse on 1 June. Pemberton has paid a £2,000 deposit, and Gideon argues that Pemberton must now pay the balance.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The contract is frustrated, so Pemberton recovers its deposit but must pay Gideon's expenses.","The contract is valid, and the risk of loss passed to Pemberton as soon as the contract was made.","The contract is voidable at Pemberton's option, because both parties made the same mistake.","The contract is void, because the specific goods had perished before the contract was made.","The contract is valid, but Gideon is in breach and Pemberton can claim damages for non-delivery."],"a":3,"e":"**Under the Sale of Goods Act 1979, s6, a contract for the sale of specific goods is void if, without the seller's knowledge, the goods have perished before the contract is made**; this reflects the common law rule on common mistake about the existence of the subject matter (Couturier v Hastie). Pemberton owes nothing and can recover its deposit. A is wrong because frustration concerns events after the contract is made, and B is wrong because no valid contract ever existed under which risk could pass. C is wrong because a common mistake that is operative at common law makes the contract void, not voidable (Great Peace Shipping)."},{"t":"mistake-duress-ui-illegality","p":"FLK1","q":"Beaumont Textiles Ltd negotiates with Kaur Fashions Ltd for the sale of 10,000 metres of silk. Throughout the negotiations the price discussed is about £12 per metre, the usual market price. Beaumont then sends a written offer which, through a typing error, quotes £12 per roll; each roll holds 50 metres. Kaur's buyer realises at once that this must be a mistake but accepts immediately.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Kaur can enforce the contract at the price quoted?","o":["It can, because the offer was clear on its face and an objective reading binds Beaumont to it.","It cannot, because Kaur knew that the offer did not reflect Beaumont's true intention on price.","It can, because a mistake caused by the offeror's own carelessness is a risk the offeror bears.","It cannot, because any mistake about the price makes a contract void whether or not it was known.","It can, because the offer was in writing and evidence of the earlier negotiations is inadmissible."],"a":1,"e":"**A unilateral mistake about the terms prevents a contract arising where the other party knew, or ought to have known, of the mistake** (Hartog v Colin and Shields). Kaur's buyer realised the price was wrong, so Kaur cannot snap up the offer. A is tempting because agreement is normally judged objectively, but the objective approach does not protect a party who knows the other's real intention. D overstates the rule, since an unknown mistake about price usually leaves the contract binding. E is wrong because the earlier negotiations are admissible to show what Kaur knew."},{"t":"discharge-frustration","p":"FLK1","q":"Ferrous Haulage Ltd owns two specialist heavy-lift trucks. It contracts to move a transformer for Northpower plc on 12 March, and separately to move a turbine for Glenmore Energy Ltd on the same day. One truck is then destroyed in a fire which is not Ferrous's fault. Ferrous chooses to use its remaining truck for the Glenmore job, which is more profitable, and tells Northpower that its contract is frustrated.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the Northpower contract is frustrated?","o":["It is frustrated, because the fire was not Ferrous's fault and made it impossible to perform both contracts.","It is frustrated, because Ferrous was entitled to choose which of its contracts to perform after the fire.","It is not frustrated, because the failure to perform resulted from Ferrous's own choice of how to use its truck.","It is frustrated, but only if Ferrous pays Northpower a fair share of the profit on the Glenmore job.","It is not frustrated, because the destruction of a vehicle is a commercial risk that can never frustrate."],"a":2,"e":"**A contract is not frustrated where the supervening event results from a party's own election; self-induced frustration is no frustration** (The Super Servant Two). Ferrous could have performed the Northpower contract with its remaining truck and chose not to, so it is in breach. A and B are tempting because the fire was not Ferrous's fault, but it was Ferrous's choice that caused this particular contract to fail. E overstates the position: destruction of something essential to performance can frustrate a contract (Taylor v Caldwell), but not where the party could still perform and chose not to."},{"t":"discharge-frustration","p":"FLK1","q":"Penrose agrees to pay Aldridge Builders Ltd £60,000 on completion for building a garden studio. Aldridge finishes the studio on time, but several windows have faulty seals and the guttering leaks. The defects will cost £3,000 to put right. Penrose refuses to pay anything, arguing that payment depended on complete performance.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Aldridge's entitlement?","o":["It is entitled to nothing, because payment depended on complete performance and the work is defective.","It is entitled to a reasonable sum for the value of the work it has done, but not to the contract price.","It is entitled to the full £60,000, because the defects are minor and do not amount to any breach.","It is entitled to nothing until it has fixed the defects, after which the full price becomes payable.","It is entitled to the contract price less a deduction of £3,000 for the cost of remedying the defects."],"a":4,"e":"**Under the doctrine of substantial performance, a party who has substantially performed an entire obligation can claim the price, less a deduction for the cost of putting right the defects** (Hoenig v Isaacs; contrast Bolton v Mahadeva, where the defects were too serious). Defects costing £3,000 on a £60,000 job are minor, so Aldridge can claim £57,000. A applies the strict entire obligations rule, which substantial performance softens. C is wrong because minor defects are still a breach, for which Penrose is compensated by the deduction. B is wrong because Aldridge claims under the contract rather than a reasonable sum."},{"t":"damages","p":"FLK1","q":"Kendra pays Stonecraft Landscapes Ltd £40,000 to lay a patio of natural York stone at her home. Stonecraft instead lays a high-quality reconstituted stone that looks similar and is just as durable. A surveyor confirms that the patio adds the same value to the house as York stone would have done. Taking up and relaying the patio in York stone would cost £18,000, and Kendra has no real intention of doing that.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the damages Kendra is likely to recover?","o":["The full £18,000 cost of relaying, because she is entitled to receive exactly what she contracted for.","Nothing, because the value of the property is the same as it would have been with a York stone patio.","The £18,000 cost of relaying, but only if she undertakes to the court that she will relay the patio.","A modest sum for loss of amenity, because relaying would be out of all proportion to the benefit gained.","The difference between the contract price and what an ordinary reconstituted stone patio would cost."],"a":3,"e":"**The cost of cure is not awarded where it would be wholly disproportionate to the benefit obtained, but the court may award a modest sum for loss of amenity (the loss of the particular pleasure or satisfaction the claimant contracted for)** (Ruxley Electronics v Forsyth). Relaying at £18,000 for no gain in value is unreasonable, especially as Kendra does not intend to do it. A ignores the reasonableness limit on the cost of cure, and C invents a rule: intention is relevant evidence but there is no requirement for an undertaking. B is wrong because Kendra did lose something she bargained for, even though the value is unaffected."},{"t":"damages","p":"FLK1","q":"Rowena buys a country cottage as a peaceful weekend retreat. She instructs Hendry Surveys Ltd to inspect it, and specifically asks it to check whether the property is affected by aircraft noise. Hendry reports that it is not. In fact the cottage lies under a flight path used by light aircraft every weekend. The cottage's market value is unaffected, but Rowena's enjoyment of it is seriously spoiled.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Rowena can recover damages for her disappointment and discomfort?","o":["She can, because an important object of the contract was to give her peace of mind about noise.","She cannot, because damages for disappointment are never recoverable for a breach of contract.","She cannot, because she has suffered no financial loss, as the value of the cottage is unaffected.","She can, but only if her distress amounts to a recognised psychiatric illness caused by the breach.","She can, because every claimant may recover damages for distress caused by a breach of contract."],"a":0,"e":"The general rule is that damages for distress and disappointment are not recoverable in contract, but **there is an exception where a major or important object of the contract was to provide pleasure, relaxation or peace of mind** (Farley v Skinner). Rowena expressly asked Hendry to check for aircraft noise, so the exception applies. B and E state the law too absolutely in opposite directions. C is wrong because non-financial loss can be recovered under this exception, and D wrongly imports the psychiatric injury requirement from the tort of negligence."},{"t":"equitable-remedies-guarantees","p":"FLK1","q":"Ignatius, a collector, agrees to buy a 1930s racing motorcycle from Lorcan for £85,000. It is one of only two surviving examples of that model, and the other is in a museum. Before the delivery date, Lorcan receives a higher offer from another buyer and tells Ignatius that he will not go ahead, offering to pay him damages instead.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the court is likely to order specific performance?","o":["It is likely to, because the motorcycle is unique and damages would not adequately compensate Ignatius.","It is unlikely to, because specific performance is available only for contracts for the sale of land.","It is unlikely to, because Lorcan has offered to pay damages, which is all Ignatius can insist upon.","It is likely to, because specific performance is available as of right whenever a contract is broken.","It is unlikely to, because an order to deliver goods would require constant supervision by the court."],"a":0,"e":"**Specific performance is a discretionary remedy granted where damages would be inadequate, as with unique goods that cannot be bought elsewhere** (and the Sale of Goods Act 1979, s52, allows it for specific goods). Ignatius could not buy another example with damages, so an order is likely. B is wrong because, although land is the usual example, unique goods also qualify, and C is wrong because the defendant cannot avoid specific performance by offering damages where they are inadequate. D is wrong because the remedy is never available as of right, and E is wrong because delivery is a single act that needs no ongoing supervision."},{"t":"equitable-remedies-guarantees","p":"FLK1","q":"Marisol guarantees in writing the repayment of a £30,000 business loan made by Fenwick Finance Ltd to her son's company, Coastline Cafés Ltd, repayable over five years at 6% interest. The guarantee contains no clause permitting changes to the loan. Two years later, without telling Marisol, Fenwick and Coastline agree to raise the interest rate to 11% and extend the loan by three years. Coastline then defaults, and Fenwick demands payment from Marisol.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Marisol's liability?","o":["She is liable for the full debt, because her guarantee was made in writing and was signed by her.","She is liable only for the debt as it would have been at 6% interest over the original five years.","She is liable for the full debt, because a guarantor is liable for whatever the principal debtor owes.","She is discharged, because a guarantee of a company's debts is unenforceable against a relative.","She is discharged, because the lender and borrower made a material variation without her consent."],"a":4,"e":"**A guarantor is discharged entirely if the creditor and the principal debtor make a material variation to the principal contract without the guarantor's consent**, unless the change is plainly insubstantial or cannot prejudice the guarantor (Holme v Brunskill). Raising the interest rate and extending the term clearly increase Marisol's risk, and the guarantee contains no clause allowing variations. B is tempting, but the rule does not simply limit her to the original terms; it releases her completely. A is wrong because writing makes the guarantee enforceable but does not prevent discharge, and C ignores the variation rule."}],"contract-p3":[{"t":"offer-acceptance","p":"FLK1","q":"On Monday, Lucia writes to Hamish offering to sell him her vintage tractor for £14,000 and stating that the offer is open until noon on Friday. On Wednesday morning, Hamish's cousin, an auctioneer who regularly handles Lucia's farm sales, tells Hamish that he has just watched Lucia sign an agreement selling the tractor to another farmer. Hamish believes him. On Thursday, hoping to hold Lucia to her deadline, Hamish emails her accepting the offer.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether a contract was made between Lucia and Hamish?","o":["Yes, because an offer can only be revoked by a communication that comes from the offeror herself.","Yes, because Lucia had promised to keep the offer open until noon on Friday and Hamish accepted before then.","No, because Hamish learned from a reliable source that the offer had been withdrawn before he accepted.","Yes, because Hamish's email acceptance took effect when sent, before any revocation reached him.","No, because the offer ended automatically when Lucia sold the tractor, whether or not Hamish knew."],"a":2,"e":"**Revocation must be communicated before acceptance, but it can be communicated by a reliable third party**, so an offeree who knows the offeror has sold elsewhere can no longer accept (Dickinson v Dodds). Hamish heard of the sale from a reliable source and believed it, so the offer was revoked before his email. A is wrong because the communication need not come from the offeror, and B is wrong because a promise to keep an offer open binds only if the offeree paid for it (an option). E is tempting but wrong: the sale itself did not end the offer; it was Hamish's knowledge of it that did. D is wrong because the postal rule does not extend to email, and in any event the offer had already been revoked when Hamish learned of the sale on Wednesday."},{"t":"offer-acceptance","p":"FLK1","q":"Tavistock Wines Ltd writes to Ines, who owns a restaurant, offering to supply her with 12 cases of its new rosé every month for a year at £90 a case. The letter ends: \"Unless we hear from you by 31 May, we will treat you as having accepted.\" Ines decides she wants the wine and tells her head chef to clear space in the cellar, but she does not contact Tavistock. In June, demand for the rosé soars and Tavistock refuses to supply Ines.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ines's position?","o":["There is no contract, because Ines never communicated acceptance and silence cannot amount to acceptance.","There is a contract, because Tavistock waived the need for communication by stating that silence would do.","There is a contract, because clearing the cellar was conduct showing that Ines had accepted the offer.","There is no contract, because a letter offering goods at a stated price is only an invitation to treat.","There is a contract, because Ines had made her decision to accept before the deadline of 31 May."],"a":0,"e":"**Silence is not acceptance, even where the offeror says it will be**: an offeror cannot impose a contract on someone merely because they fail to reply (Felthouse v Bindley). Ines's private decision and her instruction to her chef were never communicated to Tavistock, so there was no acceptance. B is tempting, but the rule protects the offeree and an offeror cannot turn silence into acceptance. C and E fail because an internal decision or a step taken within Ines's own business is not acceptance communicated to the offeror, and D is wrong because a definite proposal addressed to one person on stated terms is an offer."},{"t":"consideration","p":"FLK1","q":"Marek owes Pinewood Timber Ltd £12,000 for timber delivered to his joinery business. The debt is due and Marek does not dispute it. Marek's father, Zbigniew, contacts Pinewood and offers to pay it £8,000 \"in full and final settlement of my son's account\". Pinewood agrees and banks Zbigniew's payment. Two months later, Pinewood sues Marek for the remaining £4,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome of Pinewood's claim?","o":["It will succeed, because paying part of a debt that is due is no consideration for a promise to forgive the rest.","It will succeed, because Marek gave no consideration himself for Pinewood's promise to accept less.","It will fail, but only if Marek can show that he relied on the settlement to his detriment.","It will succeed, because a cash payment by a relative is not something different from what was owed.","It will fail, because suing after accepting the father's payment in settlement would be a fraud on him."],"a":4,"e":"**Where a third party pays part of a debt and the creditor accepts it in full settlement, the creditor cannot sue the debtor for the balance**, because that would be a fraud on the third party (Hirachand Punamchand v Temple). A states the general rule that part payment is no consideration (Pinnel's Case, Foakes v Beer), but this is a recognised exception to it. B misses the point that the exception does not depend on consideration from the debtor, and C wrongly makes the outcome depend on promissory estoppel, which is a separate route requiring reliance. D is wrong because the exception turns on the fraud on the third party, not on whether the payment was something different from what was owed."},{"t":"consideration","p":"FLK1","q":"Ashgrove Estates Ltd holds a large private fireworks display in the grounds of its country house. The local police consider that four officers on the public road outside would be enough to keep order. Ashgrove insists on having 20 officers stationed inside its grounds all evening and promises to pay £6,000 for them. The police provide the 20 officers. Ashgrove now refuses to pay, arguing that the police were already bound to keep the peace.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the police can recover the £6,000?","o":["They cannot, because a promise to pay someone for doing what they are already legally obliged to do is unenforceable.","They can, because by stationing officers inside the grounds they provided more than their public duty required.","They cannot, because the officers had already been deployed before any payment was actually made.","They can recover only a reasonable sum, because the price was fixed before the work was done.","They can recover only for the 16 officers beyond the four that the police considered necessary."],"a":1,"e":"**Performing a public duty is not good consideration, but doing more than the duty requires is** (Glasbrook Bros v Glamorgan CC, contrasted with Collins v Godefroy). The police judged that four officers outside would be enough; providing 20 inside the grounds at Ashgrove's request went beyond their duty, so Ashgrove's promise is enforceable. A states the basic rule but ignores the extra service, and E is tempting but the promise was to pay £6,000 for the whole service requested, and consideration need not be adequate. C confuses the timing of payment with past consideration: the promise came before the service was provided."},{"t":"intention-certainty-capacity","p":"FLK1","q":"Three friends who share a flat, Abena, Kristof and Moira, agree to enter an online lottery every week. Each pays £2 a week into Abena's bank account, Abena buys the ticket in her own name, and they agree that any winnings will be split equally. Nothing is put in writing. After eight months the ticket wins £90,000. Abena refuses to share, saying the arrangement was only a friendly understanding.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Kristof and Moira can each claim £30,000?","o":["They cannot, because agreements between friends are presumed not to be legally binding and nothing rebuts that.","They cannot, because the agreement was not in writing and the ticket was bought in Abena's own name.","They cannot, because sharing the cost of a ticket is not sufficient consideration for a share of a prize.","They can, because pooling their money in a joint venture on agreed terms shows an intention to be bound.","They can recover only the stakes they paid in, because a social arrangement creates no right to a prize."],"a":3,"e":"**Social and domestic agreements are presumed not to create legal relations, but the presumption is rebutted where people pool money in a joint venture on agreed terms** (Simpkins v Pays). Each friend contributed regularly on the understanding that winnings would be shared, which shows they intended to be bound. A states the starting presumption but ignores the facts that rebut it. B is wrong because no writing is needed and whose name is on the ticket does not matter, and C is wrong because each person's contribution is good consideration; it need not be adequate."},{"t":"intention-certainty-capacity","p":"FLK1","q":"Halden Coaches Ltd signs a five-year agreement to buy all its diesel from Ferris Fuels Ltd \"at a price to be agreed each quarter, and failing agreement at a price to be fixed by an independent fuel-industry valuer appointed jointly by the parties\". Both companies perform the agreement for 18 months, agreeing a price every quarter. Halden then finds cheaper diesel elsewhere and stops buying from Ferris, claiming there was never a binding contract.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the agreement?","o":["It is binding, because it contains machinery for fixing the price and the parties have acted on it.","It is not binding, because leaving the price to be agreed in future makes it an agreement to agree.","It is binding only for the quarters in which a price was actually agreed between the parties.","It is binding, but the court will fix a market price itself and disregard the valuer provision.","It is not binding, because an agreement to negotiate the price in good faith is too uncertain."],"a":0,"e":"**An agreement to agree an essential term such as price is not a contract, unless the agreement supplies machinery for fixing it**, such as a valuer or arbitration, and the court is especially willing to uphold an agreement the parties have acted on (Foley v Classique Coaches, contrasted with May and Butcher v R). Here the price falls to be fixed by the valuer if the parties fail to agree, and both sides performed for 18 months. B is tempting but ignores the fallback mechanism, and E confuses this with a bare agreement to negotiate (Walford v Miles), which has no such mechanism."},{"t":"privity-third-parties","p":"FLK1","q":"Corinne, a property developer, contracts with Brightwell Architects Ltd for the design of a block of flats. Clause 8 sets out Brightwell's duties of care, and clause 9 states: \"The obligations in clause 8 are owed to, and may be enforced by, the management company to be formed to manage the block.\" Six months later Elmcourt Management Ltd is incorporated for that purpose. A design defect later causes serious water damage to the common parts.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Elmcourt can sue Brightwell?","o":["No, because Elmcourt did not exist when the contract was made and so cannot acquire rights under it.","No, because a third party can only enforce a contract term if it is identified in the contract by name.","Yes, because the contract expressly allows it to enforce the term and it is identified by description.","No, because only Corinne, as a party to the contract, can sue Brightwell for breach of clause 8.","Yes, but only if Elmcourt provided consideration to Brightwell after it was incorporated."],"a":2,"e":"**Under the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999 s.1(1)(a) a third party can enforce a term where the contract expressly says so, and s.1(3) allows it to be identified by name, class or description, and it need not exist when the contract is made.** Clause 9 expressly confers the right, and \"the management company to be formed\" is a description that fits Elmcourt. A is the most tempting wrong answer, but s.1(3) expressly covers future third parties. B is wrong because a description is enough, D states the old common law privity rule that the Act modifies, and E is wrong because the third party does not need to provide consideration."},{"t":"privity-third-parties","p":"FLK1","q":"Arthur sells his bakery business to his niece, Delyth. In return, Delyth promises in the written sale agreement to pay Arthur's long-term partner, Maureen Hale, £300 a week for the rest of her life after Arthur dies. The agreement also states: \"No term of this agreement is enforceable by any person who is not a party to it.\" Arthur dies, and Maureen is appointed executor of his estate. Delyth makes two payments to Maureen and then stops.\n\nWhich of the following is the best way for Maureen to compel Delyth to keep paying?","o":["To sue in her own name under the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999, as the term benefits her by name.","To seek specific performance as executor of Arthur's estate, because damages to the estate would be nominal.","To claim substantial damages as executor of the estate, measured by the payments she will personally lose.","To claim that a collateral contract arose between her and Delyth when Delyth made the first payment.","None, because the doctrine of privity prevents anyone enforcing a promise made for a third party's benefit."],"a":1,"e":"**The promisee (or the promisee's personal representative) can sue the promisor, and where damages would be nominal the court may order specific performance so that the promisor must perform for the third party** (Beswick v Beswick). The clause excluding third-party rights shows the parties did not intend Maureen to enforce the term personally, so the 1999 Act does not help her (s.1(2)), which rules out A. C is wrong because the estate itself suffers no real loss from the non-payment, so its damages would be nominal. E is wrong because the promisee's estate can enforce the promise even though the third party cannot."},{"t":"express-terms","p":"FLK1","q":"Lark Productions Ltd, which has never dealt with Velvet Wardrobe Ltd before, telephones it to ask for 30 period costumes for a television drama. Velvet sends the costumes with a delivery note. Printed among 15 conditions on the back is a clause imposing a late-return fee of £40 per costume per day; the usual rate in the trade is about £3 a day. It is agreed that the contract was made when Lark accepted the delivery. Lark returns the costumes ten days late, and Velvet bills it £12,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Velvet's entitlement?","o":["It can recover £12,000, because the delivery note was handed over at the time the contract was made.","It can recover nothing for the late return, because a delivery note is a mere receipt and not a contractual document.","It can recover £12,000, because Lark is a business and can be expected to read the terms it receives.","It can recover £12,000 only if it proves that the fee is reasonable under the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977.","It can recover only a reasonable charge, because so onerous a term was not fairly brought to Lark's attention."],"a":4,"e":"**The more onerous or unusual a term, the more clearly it must be drawn to the other party's attention before it is incorporated** (Interfoto Picture Library v Stiletto; the red hand rule in J Spurling v Bradshaw). A fee more than ten times the normal rate, buried among printed conditions, was not fairly brought to Lark's attention, so it is not part of the contract and Velvet recovers a reasonable charge for the late return instead. A is tempting because the timing was right, but timing is not the only requirement. B goes too far, since a delivery note setting out conditions can be contractual, and D is wrong because this is a charge, not an exemption clause controlled by the 1977 Act."},{"t":"express-terms","p":"FLK1","q":"Before buying a second-hand laser cutter from Harlow Machine Sales Ltd, the managing director of Kirkby Signs Ltd is told by Harlow's salesman that the machine \"was fully reconditioned at the factory last year\". The written contract, which Kirkby signs, contains a clause stating: \"This document contains the entire agreement between the parties and supersedes all prior statements, representations and understandings.\" The machine had never been reconditioned. Kirkby wants to sue Harlow.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the clause?","o":["It has no effect, because the salesman's assurance is enforceable as a collateral contract despite the clause.","It prevents Kirkby bringing any claim at all, whether for breach of contract or for misrepresentation.","It is void unless Harlow proves it is reasonable under the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977.","It prevents the assurance being treated as a term, but by itself it does not exclude a misrepresentation claim.","It means that, under the parol evidence rule, the assurance cannot be relied on for any purpose."],"a":3,"e":"**An entire agreement clause stops a party claiming that a pre-contract statement was a term or a collateral warranty, but by itself it does not exclude liability for misrepresentation**, which needs clear words and is then subject to the reasonableness test in Misrepresentation Act 1967 s.3 (Inntrepreneur Pub Co v East Crown). Kirkby therefore cannot sue for breach of contract on the assurance, but may still claim misrepresentation. A is wrong because the clause is designed precisely to defeat collateral warranties, B overstates its effect, and E is wrong because the parol evidence rule does not prevent evidence of misrepresentation. C is wrong because an entire agreement clause of this kind is not itself an exclusion of liability needing to be shown reasonable; the 1967 Act s.3 test applies only to a term that excludes or restricts liability for misrepresentation."},{"t":"implied-terms","p":"FLK1","q":"Glenroy Brewery Ltd tells the sales engineer of Hartley Pumps Ltd that it needs a pump to transfer liquid at about 95°C between vessels in its brewhouse. The engineer recommends a particular model, and Glenroy buys it. The pump works well with cold liquids and is of a standard widely used in industry, but its seals fail within days when used at high temperatures. The written contract has no express terms on quality or suitability.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Glenroy's position?","o":["Hartley is in breach of the implied condition of fitness for purpose, as Glenroy relied on its advice.","Hartley is not in breach, because the pump is of satisfactory quality for the purposes for which pumps are usually bought.","Hartley is not in breach, because Glenroy should have stated the required temperature in the written contract.","Hartley is in breach of the implied condition that the goods correspond with the description given by the seller.","Hartley is in breach of an implied warranty, so Glenroy can claim damages but can never reject the pump."],"a":0,"e":"**Under Sale of Goods Act 1979 s.14(3), where the buyer makes known a particular purpose, goods sold in the course of a business must be reasonably fit for it, unless the buyer did not rely, or it was unreasonable to rely, on the seller's skill or judgement.** Glenroy told Hartley's engineer about the high temperature and bought the model he recommended, so the pump is not fit for that purpose. B is tempting, but satisfactory quality under s.14(2) is a separate term and being fine for ordinary uses is no answer to s.14(3). C is wrong because the purpose can be made known orally, and E is wrong because s.14(3) is a condition, so rejection is available unless Glenroy has accepted the goods."},{"t":"implied-terms","p":"FLK1","q":"Ostrava Design Ltd buys ten second-hand desks from a dealer in used office furniture. Before the contract, the dealer points out that one desk has a cracked drawer runner. Ostrava's office manager then inspects the desks and notices deep scratches on two of the desktops. After delivery, Ostrava discovers that two other desks have an active woodworm infestation inside their frames, which could not have been seen on an ordinary inspection.\n\nIn respect of which defects can Ostrava claim that the desks were not of satisfactory quality?","o":["The cracked runner, the scratches and the woodworm, because a business seller must supply goods free from defects.","Only the woodworm, because the other defects were pointed out or revealed by Ostrava's own examination.","None of the defects, because Ostrava chose to examine the goods before entering into the contract.","The cracked runner and the woodworm, but not the scratches that the office manager actually saw.","The scratches and the woodworm, but not the cracked runner that the dealer pointed out beforehand."],"a":1,"e":"**The implied term of satisfactory quality (SGA 1979 s.14(2)) does not cover defects specifically drawn to the buyer's attention before the contract or, where the buyer examines the goods, defects that examination ought to reveal (s.14(2C)).** The runner was pointed out and the scratches were revealed by the manager's inspection, but the hidden woodworm was neither, so only that defect can found a claim. C is wrong because examining the goods excludes only defects the examination ought to have revealed, not all defects. D and E each treat one of the excluded defects as claimable, and A ignores the s.14(2C) exceptions altogether."},{"t":"implied-terms","p":"FLK1","q":"On 1 February, Callum buys a used car for £9,000 from a car dealer for his personal use, and it is delivered that day. On 20 March a gearbox fault appears. At Callum's request, the dealer repairs it. On 10 May the same fault returns and the car cannot be driven. Callum tells the dealer he wants his money back.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Callum's rights under the Consumer Rights Act 2015?","o":["He can exercise the short-term right to reject and must receive a full refund with no deduction.","He can reject the car and must receive a full refund, because he is rejecting within six months of delivery.","He can exercise the final right to reject, but the refund may be reduced to reflect his use of the car.","He must allow the dealer a second attempt at repair before he can ask for any refund at all.","He can reject the car, but only if he first proves that the fault was present when it was delivered."],"a":2,"e":"**The final right to reject arises after one failed repair or replacement, and the refund may be reduced for the consumer's use, except that no deduction is allowed within six months of delivery, an exception that does not apply to motor vehicles** (CRA 2015 ss.24(5) and 24(8)-(10)). One repair has failed, so Callum may reject finally, but as the goods are a car the dealer may make a deduction for use. B is the tempting trap, since the six-month protection does not cover cars, and A is wrong because the 30-day short-term right had expired. E is wrong because a fault appearing within six months of delivery is presumed to have existed at delivery. D is wrong because the final right to reject arises after just one failed repair or replacement (s.24(5)(a))."},{"t":"exemption-clauses","p":"FLK1","q":"Westbay Seafoods Ltd stores frozen fish at a cold store run by Northgate Storage Ltd under Northgate's standard terms. One clause states that Northgate \"guarantees that goods will be kept at or below minus 18°C at all times\". Another states: \"Northgate shall not be liable for any spoilage of goods.\" A Northgate employee carelessly leaves a freezer door open overnight, and Westbay's stock of fish is spoiled.\n\nAs a matter of construction, which of the following best describes whether the clause covers Northgate's liability for the spoilage?","o":["It does, because its words are wide enough in their ordinary meaning to cover every cause of spoilage.","It does, because ambiguous clauses are never read against the party relying on them between businesses.","It does not, because a business can never exclude its negligence liability to another business.","It does not, because liability for negligence can be excluded only by clauses that limit liability rather than exclude it.","It probably does not, because it never mentions negligence and could cover Northgate's strict liability."],"a":4,"e":"**A clause that does not expressly refer to negligence will not usually be construed to cover it if it could realistically apply to some other liability, such as a strict contractual obligation** (Canada Steamship Lines v The King, applied as guidelines rather than rigid rules). Northgate's temperature guarantee makes it strictly liable even without fault, so the clause can sensibly be confined to that liability. A is tempting, but wide words are not enough where another realistic head of liability exists. C is wrong because between businesses negligence liability for property damage can be excluded if the clause passes the reasonableness test (UCTA s.2(2)), and B misstates the law, as contra proferentem can still apply to genuinely ambiguous commercial clauses."},{"t":"exemption-clauses","p":"FLK1","q":"Tobias books a country house hotel for his wedding reception, signing the hotel's standard contract. A clause, set out clearly on the front page, states that if the client cancels for any reason \"the full hire fee of £18,000 remains payable, regardless of when notice is given\". Fourteen months before the wedding, Tobias cancels. The hotel quickly rebooks the date for another wedding and demands £18,000 from him, arguing that the clause concerns the price.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the clause?","o":["It cannot be assessed for fairness, because it concerns the price and is transparent and prominent.","It is automatically not binding, because it appears on the blacklist in the Consumer Rights Act 2015.","It is binding, because Tobias signed the contract and the clause was clearly set out on the front page.","It can be assessed for fairness and is likely to be unfair, as it demands a disproportionate sum.","It is valid only if the hotel proves it is reasonable under the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977."],"a":3,"e":"**Under CRA 2015 s.62 a term is unfair if, contrary to good faith, it causes a significant imbalance to the consumer's detriment, and a term requiring a consumer who cancels to pay a disproportionately high sum is on the grey list, which can never benefit from the core terms exemption** (s.64(6)). The clause is about a cancellation charge rather than whether the price is too high for the service, and demanding the full fee 14 months ahead when the date was rebooked is likely to be unfair, so it would not bind Tobias. A is tempting but s.64 protects only the price itself, and B is wrong because grey list terms are not automatically unfair. E applies the wrong statute to a consumer contract."},{"t":"misrepresentation","p":"FLK1","q":"Fenella is buying a seaside guesthouse from Gordon, who tells her that its rooms have averaged 85% occupancy over the past three years. Fenella says she will not take his word for it and instructs her own accountant to examine the booking records. The accountant carelessly confirms the 85% figure, and Fenella buys the guesthouse relying entirely on the accountant's report. The true average was 55%.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Fenella can rescind the contract for misrepresentation?","o":["She cannot, because she relied on her own adviser's investigation rather than on Gordon's statement.","She can, because a representee is not required to check whether the statement made to her is true.","She can, because Gordon's statement was a false statement of existing fact made to her before the contract.","She cannot, because an occupancy figure for a business is a statement of opinion rather than fact.","She can, but only if she proves that Gordon knew that the occupancy figure he gave was untrue."],"a":0,"e":"**A misrepresentation is actionable only if it induced the contract, and there is no inducement where the representee relied on her own judgement or investigations instead** (Attwood v Small). Fenella refused to rely on Gordon's figure and bought relying entirely on her accountant's report, so Gordon's statement did not induce the contract; her remedy, if any, is against the accountant. B is the tempting trap: it is true that a representee need not check (Redgrave v Hurd), but that rule helps only a representee who did rely on the statement. C ignores inducement, and D is wrong because a past occupancy figure is a statement of fact. E is wrong because rescission is available for an innocent or negligent misrepresentation as well as a fraudulent one."},{"t":"misrepresentation","p":"FLK1","q":"Bertrand buys a small commercial unit for £650,000. Before contracts, the seller told him that the roof had been replaced in 2021. The seller honestly believed this, relying on a surveyor's report that appeared reliable, and can prove he had reasonable grounds for his belief. In fact the roof was replaced in 2017. It is sound, and the difference will mean about £8,000 of extra maintenance over the roof's life. Since completion, commercial property values in the area have fallen by 15%, and Bertrand wants to rescind.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome?","o":["Bertrand is entitled to rescind as of right, because he has proved that the misrepresentation induced the contract.","The court may declare the contract subsisting and award damages in lieu of rescission, not covering the fall in value.","Bertrand can recover damages for all his losses, including the fall in value, because the seller has not proved honesty.","Bertrand has no remedy at all, because the seller had reasonable grounds to believe that the statement was true.","Bertrand cannot rescind or claim damages, because the misrepresentation was about a minor matter."],"a":1,"e":"**For a non-fraudulent misrepresentation, Misrepresentation Act 1967 s.2(2) allows the court to declare the contract subsisting and award damages in lieu of rescission where that is equitable, and those damages cover loss caused by the misrepresentation, not a later market fall** (William Sindall v Cambridgeshire CC). Rescinding a £650,000 purchase over an £8,000 problem would be drastic and would really shift the market fall onto the seller. D is tempting because the seller proved reasonable grounds and so has a defence to damages under s.2(1), but rescission or s.2(2) damages remain available. C is wrong because the facts state the seller proved reasonable grounds, and A ignores the court's discretion under s.2(2)."},{"t":"mistake-duress-ui-illegality","p":"FLK1","q":"Ottilie sells a landscape painting to Ramon, a collector, for £40,000. Both of them believe it is by a well-known twentieth-century artist, and the price reflects that belief, but neither makes any statement about who painted it. The written contract states that the painting is \"sold as seen, with no warranty as to attribution\". An expert later shows that it is by one of the artist's followers and worth about £3,000. Ramon claims the contract is void.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ramon's claim?","o":["It succeeds, because both parties shared a fundamental mistake about the value of the painting.","It succeeds in part, because the court can set the contract aside in equity for a shared mistake.","It fails, because a shared mistake about quality does not make the painting essentially different.","It succeeds, because the specific goods described in the contract did not exist when it was made.","It fails, because Ramon's only remedy is rescission for misrepresentation against Ottilie."],"a":2,"e":"**A common mistake as to quality makes a contract void only if it makes the subject matter essentially and radically different from what the parties believed**, which is almost never satisfied (Bell v Lever Brothers; Great Peace Shipping). Ramon received the very painting he agreed to buy, and the contract allocated the risk of attribution to him by excluding any warranty. A is tempting but a mistake about value, however large, is not enough. B is wrong because there is no separate equitable jurisdiction to rescind for common mistake (Great Peace), D is wrong because the painting existed, and E is wrong because Ottilie made no statement that could be a misrepresentation."},{"t":"mistake-duress-ui-illegality","p":"FLK1","q":"Aisha works as a junior bookkeeper for Hallam Accounts, a small firm with a single office in Leeds that serves local sole traders. Her employment contract states that for three years after leaving she will not \"work in any capacity for any business anywhere in the United Kingdom that competes with Hallam Accounts\". Aisha resigns and takes a job as a bookkeeper with a large national accountancy firm in Leeds. She dealt with only a handful of Hallam's clients and had no access to confidential information.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the restriction?","o":["It is enforceable, because Aisha agreed to it freely when she accepted the employment.","It is enforceable, but the court will rewrite it so that it lasts six months and covers only Leeds.","It is enforceable, because an employer always has a legitimate interest in its customer connections.","It is void only as to its area, so it remains enforceable within Leeds for the full three years.","It is likely to be void, because it goes further than needed to protect any legitimate interest."],"a":4,"e":"**A covenant restraining a person's freedom to work after a contract ends is void unless it protects a legitimate interest, such as trade secrets or customer connections, and is reasonable in area, time and activity**; mere protection from competition is not enough. A three-year, UK-wide ban on any work for any competitor goes far beyond anything needed to protect a small Leeds firm from a junior employee with little client contact and no confidential information. B is wrong because the court may sever offending words but will not rewrite or add to a covenant (Tillman v Egon Zehnder), and D wrongly assumes the remaining words would be reasonable. C overstates the position: the interest must be real and the restraint reasonable."},{"t":"discharge-frustration","p":"FLK1","q":"Gethin, a builder, agrees to build a double garage at Priya's house for £24,000, payable on completion. When the walls are half-built he runs out of money and abandons the job, leaving a pallet of unused bricks and a stack of roof timbers on Priya's drive. Priya hires another builder, who completes the garage on the existing walls using Gethin's bricks and timbers. Gethin now claims payment for his work and materials.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Gethin can recover?","o":["The contract price less the cost of completing the garage, because he substantially performed.","A reasonable sum for the bricks and timbers Priya chose to use, but nothing for the half-built walls.","Nothing at all, because his obligation was entire and he did not complete the garage.","A reasonable sum for all his work and materials, because Priya accepted the benefit by finishing the garage.","A proportion of the contract price that reflects how much of the garage he had built."],"a":1,"e":"**A party who has not completed an entire obligation can recover a reasonable sum (quantum meruit) only if the other party freely chose to accept the partial performance** (Sumpter v Hedges). Priya had a real choice whether to use the loose materials, so Gethin can be paid for those, but she had no real choice about half-built walls on her own land. D is tempting but treats finishing the garage as a free acceptance of the walls, which it is not. A is wrong because abandoning the job halfway is not substantial performance, and C ignores the materials Priya chose to use."},{"t":"discharge-frustration","p":"FLK1","q":"Marina Tours Ltd hires a passenger boat and crew from Solent Boats Ltd for one day for £8,000. The contract describes the purpose as \"to carry Marina's customers to watch the Tall Ships Parade and for a cruise around the tall ships at anchor in the harbour\". The day before, the parade is cancelled because of forecast high winds, but the ships remain at anchor in the harbour and the weather on the day is calm. Many of Marina's customers ask for refunds, and Marina refuses to pay Solent the hire charge.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the contract is frustrated?","o":["It is frustrated, because the parade was the foundation of the contract and both parties knew it.","It is frustrated, because the cancellation was a supervening event beyond either party's control.","It is frustrated, but Marina must pay Solent's expenses under the Law Reform (Frustrated Contracts) Act 1943.","It is not frustrated, because the cruise around the ships could still go ahead, so the purpose had not failed.","It is not frustrated, because frustration applies only where the subject matter of the contract is destroyed."],"a":3,"e":"**A contract is frustrated by the failure of an event only where that event was the sole foundation of the contract, so that performance would be radically different** (Krell v Henry, contrasted with Herne Bay Steam Boat v Hutton). Here the contract had two purposes, and the cruise around the anchored ships could still go ahead; Marina will simply make less money, which is not frustration. A is tempting but ignores the second purpose that remained possible. B is wrong because an event beyond the parties' control frustrates only if it makes performance radically different, and E is wrong because frustration is not limited to destruction of the subject matter."},{"t":"discharge-frustration","p":"FLK1","q":"Saffron, a mural artist, agrees to paint a large mural on the wall of Theo's café for £10,000, payable when the mural is finished. Nothing has been paid. When she has completed about three-quarters of it, she suffers a serious injury and will never be able to paint at that scale again. The contract is frustrated. Theo keeps the unfinished mural on display, and it has become a popular local attraction that brings customers into the café.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Saffron can recover?","o":["A just sum fixed by the court, not exceeding the value of the benefit Theo has obtained from her work.","Nothing, because payment was due only on completion and the mural was never finished.","£7,500 as of right, because she completed three-quarters of the work before the frustrating event.","The full £10,000, because the frustrating event was not her fault and Theo has kept the mural.","Nothing under the 1943 Act, because it allows recovery only of money paid before the frustrating event."],"a":0,"e":"**Under the Law Reform (Frustrated Contracts) Act 1943 s.1(3), where one party has obtained a valuable benefit (other than money) from the other's performance before the frustrating event, the court may order a just sum not exceeding the value of that benefit** (BP Exploration v Hunt). Theo has received and is using three-quarters of a mural, so Saffron can claim a just sum. B states the common law position, which the Act alters for frustrated contracts, and E wrongly limits the Act to money paid under s.1(2). C is wrong because the award is a discretionary just sum capped by the value of the benefit, not a pro rata share of the price."},{"t":"damages","p":"FLK1","q":"Hollowbrook Productions Ltd plans a touring stage show. Before signing anyone, it spends £18,000 on set design and rehearsal space. It then contracts with a well-known singer to star in the show, and spends a further £12,000 on publicity and costumes. A week before opening night the singer pulls out, in breach of contract. Hollowbrook cannot find a replacement and abandons the show. It cannot prove what profit, if any, the show would have made.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the damages Hollowbrook can recover from the singer?","o":["£12,000, because only expenditure incurred after the contract was made can be recovered.","Nothing beyond nominal damages, because Hollowbrook cannot prove that it would have made a profit.","£30,000, because the singer must have known that the earlier expenditure would be wasted if she pulled out.","£18,000, because expenditure after the contract was within Hollowbrook's control and should have been mitigated.","A sum for the profit the show would probably have made, estimated by the court as best it can."],"a":2,"e":"**Where lost profits are too speculative to prove, a claimant may claim reliance damages for wasted expenditure, including pre-contract expenditure that the defendant must have contemplated would be wasted by a breach** (Anglia Television v Reed). The singer must have known that money already spent on sets and rehearsals would be wasted if she withdrew, so all £30,000 is recoverable, unless she proves the show would have lost money anyway. A is tempting but wrongly excludes pre-contract spending. B is wrong because a claimant who cannot prove profit can still choose the reliance measure, and E is wrong because the profit cannot be proved."},{"t":"damages","p":"FLK1","q":"Calder Mills Ltd, a flour miller, contracts to buy 200 tonnes of milling wheat from Fenton Grain Ltd at £210 a tonne, for delivery on 1 September. Fenton fails to deliver. On 1 September wheat of that grade is readily available in the market at £250 a tonne. Calder decides to wait in the hope that prices will fall, but they rise, and on 1 November it buys 200 tonnes at £290 a tonne.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the damages Calder can recover from Fenton?","o":["£16,000, because Calder is entitled to the actual extra cost it incurred in buying replacement wheat.","£42,000, because that is the contract price of the wheat that Fenton failed to deliver.","Nothing, because Calder failed to mitigate its loss by buying replacement wheat immediately.","£16,000, because the date of the actual replacement purchase is the date on which damages are assessed.","£8,000, because damages are the difference between the contract and market prices when delivery was due."],"a":4,"e":"**Where a seller fails to deliver and there is an available market, the buyer's damages are generally the difference between the contract price and the market price at the time the goods should have been delivered** (Sale of Goods Act 1979 s.51(3)). That is £40 a tonne on 200 tonnes, giving £8,000; Calder bore the risk of waiting, so it cannot charge Fenton for the later rise. A and D are tempting because they reflect Calder's actual outlay, but the extra loss from waiting was caused by Calder's own decision. C is wrong because failure to mitigate reduces damages to what reasonable steps would have avoided; it does not wipe out the claim. B is wrong because the buyer saves the contract price it would have paid, so its loss is only the extra cost of buying in the market."},{"t":"damages","p":"FLK1","q":"Okonjo Architects Ltd pays a courier firm to deliver its tender for a council design contract to the council's offices by noon on 30 June, explaining that late tenders will be excluded. The courier carelessly delivers it at 2 pm and the tender is rejected. Okonjo had been shortlisted as one of four firms, and the evidence shows it had a real and substantial chance of winning. Winning would have produced a profit of £200,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Okonjo's claim for damages against the courier?","o":["It can recover nothing beyond the courier fee, because it cannot prove on the balance of probabilities that it would have won.","It can recover damages for its lost chance of winning, discounting the profit for the probability of success.","It can recover the full £200,000, because the courier knew the consequences of delivering the tender late.","It can recover only nominal damages, because the council's choice of winner was outside the courier's control.","It can recover a quarter of the £200,000 as of right, because it was one of four shortlisted firms."],"a":1,"e":"**Damages can be awarded for the loss of a real and substantial chance of a benefit, assessed by discounting the value of the benefit for the probability that it would have been obtained** (Chaplin v Hicks). The courier was told late tenders would be excluded, so the loss is not too remote, and Okonjo recovers a proportion of the £200,000 reflecting its chance of winning. A is tempting but applies an all-or-nothing approach that does not apply to loss of a chance. C overcompensates because winning was never certain, and E is wrong because the court assesses the real probability on the evidence rather than dividing mechanically."},{"t":"equitable-remedies-guarantees","p":"FLK1","q":"Ravi, a well-known chef, signs a two-year contract to be head chef at Copperpot Restaurants Ltd. He also promises that during those two years he will not cook professionally for any other restaurant within ten miles of the city centre. After six months he leaves to become head chef at a rival restaurant two miles away. He could easily find well-paid chef's work elsewhere in the country, or as a television presenter or consultant. Copperpot goes to court.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the remedy the court is most likely to grant?","o":["Specific performance ordering Ravi to return to work as Copperpot's head chef for the rest of the two years.","Damages only, because any order enforcing a contract for personal services is always refused.","An injunction preventing Ravi from working as a chef anywhere in the country for the rest of the two years.","An injunction stopping Ravi cooking for the rival restaurant, but no order that he work for Copperpot.","Damages only, because an injunction would in practice force Ravi to work for Copperpot."],"a":3,"e":"**The court will not order specific performance of a contract for personal services, but it will usually enforce an express negative promise by injunction, unless the injunction would in practice compel the defendant to work for the claimant** (Lumley v Wagner; Warner Bros v Nelson, contrasted with Page One Records v Britton). Ravi can still earn a good living outside the restricted area, so enforcing his promise does not force him back to Copperpot. A is wrong because personal service contracts are not specifically enforced, and E is tempting but the facts show Ravi has realistic alternatives. C goes beyond the negative promise he actually made."},{"t":"equitable-remedies-guarantees","p":"FLK1","q":"Tyrese, aged 17, wants to buy a £6,000 motorbike on credit from Rapid Motors Ltd to ride for fun at weekends. Rapid refuses to deal with him unless his father, Darnell, backs the deal. Darnell telephones Rapid's manager and says: \"Let him have the bike on credit, and I will make good any loss you suffer on the deal, whatever happens.\" Nothing is put in writing. Tyrese pays two instalments and then stops. The credit agreement cannot be enforced against Tyrese because of his age.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Rapid can recover its loss from Darnell?","o":["It can, because Darnell's promise is an indemnity, which needs no writing and is a primary obligation.","It cannot, because Darnell's promise is a guarantee and it was not evidenced in writing signed by him.","It cannot, because a guarantee falls with the principal debt when that debt cannot be enforced.","It can, because statute preserves a guarantee of a minor's contract even where the debt is unenforceable.","It cannot, because Rapid knew that Tyrese was a minor when it agreed to let him have the bike."],"a":0,"e":"**An indemnity is a primary promise to make good another's loss: unlike a guarantee, it needs no signed writing and is not discharged because the third party's obligation is unenforceable** (Yeoman Credit v Latter; Statute of Frauds s.4 applies only to guarantees). Darnell's promise to make good any loss \"whatever happens\" is an independent undertaking, not a promise to pay only if Tyrese defaults. B and C would be right if this were a guarantee, but the wording points to an indemnity. D is tempting, but the statutory protection for guarantees of a minor's contract would not cure the lack of signed writing for a guarantee."},{"t":"equitable-remedies-guarantees","p":"FLK1","q":"In 2022 Esther contracts to buy a ten-acre field from Nikolai for £120,000. On the completion date Nikolai refuses to complete. Esther does nothing until 2025, when she learns that the field has been allocated for housing in the local plan and is now worth £900,000. She immediately starts proceedings seeking specific performance. Nikolai has spent the intervening years farming the field and planning his retirement on the basis that no sale would take place.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the court's likely approach?","o":["It will order specific performance, because land is unique and damages are never an adequate remedy for a buyer.","It will order specific performance, because her claim was brought within the six-year limitation period.","It may refuse specific performance because of her unreasonable delay, and award her damages instead.","It must refuse any remedy, because Nikolai's refusal to complete ended the contract automatically.","It will refuse specific performance, because it is never available where the buyer is acting as an investor."],"a":2,"e":"**Specific performance is discretionary even where damages are inadequate, and it may be refused because of the claimant's unreasonable delay (laches)**, especially where the defendant has acted on the assumption that the claim was abandoned; the court may then award damages in lieu (Senior Courts Act 1981 s.50). Esther waited three years and moved only when the value soared. A is tempting because land is normally specifically enforceable, but that only satisfies the first step, and B is wrong because a claim within the limitation period can still fail for delay in equity. D is wrong because a refusal to perform does not end a contract unless the innocent party accepts it as a repudiation, and E invents a rule that does not exist."}],"crimlaw-p1":[{"t":"actus-reus","p":"FLK2","q":"Mr Okafor, a frail 84-year-old widower with no family, moves into the spare room of his neighbour, Priya, after a fall. Priya tells the hospital discharge team that she will make sure he eats and takes his medication. Over the following weeks she becomes busy at work, stops taking meals to his room and does not call a doctor when he becomes confused and bedbound. He dies of dehydration.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Priya can be criminally liable for failing to act?","o":["She cannot be liable, because English law does not impose liability for a failure to act in any circumstances.","She cannot be liable, because she had no family relationship with the man and so owed him no duty.","She may be liable, because she voluntarily took on the care of a man unable to look after himself.","She may be liable, but only if she had been paid or otherwise rewarded for caring for the man.","She may be liable, but only if the man had expressly asked her for help in the days before he died."],"a":2,"e":"There is no general duty to act, but **a duty arises where the defendant voluntarily assumes the care of a person who is unable to look after themselves** (Stone and Dobinson). By taking the man in and promising to feed him and manage his medication, Priya assumed that duty, so her failure to act can be the actus reus (the physical element) of a homicide offence. A blood relationship and payment are other possible sources of a duty, but neither is required."},{"t":"actus-reus","p":"FLK2","q":"Tomasz forces his way into his ex-partner's first-floor flat holding a kitchen knife and shouts that he is going to 'finish' her new boyfriend, Emeka. Emeka panics, runs onto the balcony and jumps to the garden below, breaking both ankles. Tomasz is charged with an offence requiring proof that he caused Emeka's injuries.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Tomasz caused the injuries?","o":["He caused them if Emeka's reaction was within the range of reasonably foreseeable responses.","He caused them only if he intended that Emeka would jump from the balcony to escape.","He did not cause them, because Emeka's decision to jump was his own free and voluntary act.","He caused them only if he personally foresaw that Emeka might jump from the balcony.","He caused them only if jumping was the most sensible way for Emeka to escape from the flat."],"a":0,"e":"Where the victim is injured escaping a threat, **the chain of causation is broken only if the victim's reaction was so 'daft' or unexpected that no reasonable person could have foreseen it** (Roberts, Williams). Panicking and jumping from a first-floor balcony to escape a man with a knife is within the range of foreseeable responses. Causation is an objective question, so Tomasz's own intention or foresight is irrelevant, and the victim's reaction need not be the wisest available."},{"t":"mens-rea","p":"FLK2","q":"Dimitri owns a struggling furniture shop. To claim on his insurance, he pours petrol through the letterbox at 2am and sets it alight. He knows that the night caretaker, Agnes, sleeps in the flat above the shop, which has only one staircase leading down through the shop. He says he hoped she would wake up and escape. Agnes dies in the fire and Dimitri is charged with murder.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the jury should be directed on intention?","o":["They must find intention if death or serious injury was a natural and probable consequence of his act.","They must find intention because he foresaw that death or serious injury was a possible consequence.","They may find intention only if causing death or serious injury was his aim in starting the fire.","They may find intention if a reasonable person would have foreseen death or serious injury as highly likely.","They may find intention only if death or serious injury was a virtual certainty and he appreciated that it was."],"a":4,"e":"Where the result was not the defendant's aim, **the jury may find intention only if death or serious injury was a virtual certainty (barring some unforeseen intervention) and the defendant appreciated that** (Woollin). This is oblique intention, and the jury is entitled, not required, to find intention on that basis. Foresight of a mere possibility or probability is not enough, and the test is subjective, so what a reasonable person would have foreseen is not the question; direct intention (aim) is not the only form of intention."},{"t":"mens-rea","p":"FLK2","q":"During a heated argument outside a café, Callum picks up a heavy stone and throws it at his rival, Rashid, intending to hit him. Rashid ducks and the stone smashes the café's plate-glass window behind him. Callum says he never thought about the window at all. He is charged with criminal damage to the window.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Callum's liability for criminal damage?","o":["He is guilty, because his intention to hit Rashid transfers to the damage to the window.","He is guilty only if he was himself aware of a risk that the window might be damaged.","He is not guilty, because he did not intend to damage any property belonging to another.","He is guilty, because a reasonable person would have seen an obvious risk to the window.","He is not guilty, because the damage was caused by accident and not by any deliberate act."],"a":1,"e":"**Transferred malice operates only within the same kind of offence**: an intention to injure a person cannot be transferred to make the defendant liable for damaging property (Pembliton). Callum is therefore guilty of criminal damage only if he had the mens rea for that offence in his own right, which would include recklessness: subjective awareness of a risk to the window that it was unreasonable to take (R v G). An objective 'reasonable person' test no longer applies, and intention is not the only route to liability."},{"t":"non-fatal-offences","p":"FLK2","q":"In a supermarket car park, Kwame accuses Lewis of scratching his car. Lewis steps towards Kwame, raises a clenched fist level with Kwame's face and says: 'If your kids weren't sitting in that car watching, I'd knock you out right now.' Lewis then walks away. Kwame was frightened.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Lewis has committed an assault?","o":["Yes, because raising a clenched fist is enough on its own, whatever is said.","Yes, because Kwame was in fact frightened by what Lewis did and said.","No, because words alone can never be enough to amount to an assault.","No, because his words made clear that no force would be used at that moment.","No, because there was no physical contact between Lewis and Kwame."],"a":3,"e":"An assault requires the victim to **apprehend immediate unlawful force**, and **words can negate a threatening gesture** where they show that no force will be used (Tuberville v Savage). Lewis's words made clear he would not strike while the children were watching, so Kwame could not apprehend immediate force, even though he was frightened. Words can also create an assault, and no contact is needed because contact would be a battery."},{"t":"non-fatal-offences","p":"FLK2","q":"Outside a nightclub, Jordan punches Oliver once in the face, intending only to give him a bruise to 'teach him a lesson'. Oliver falls backwards, strikes his head on the kerb and suffers a fractured skull.\n\nWhich of the following is the most serious offence Jordan is likely to be convicted of?","o":["Causing grievous bodily harm with intent under section 18 of the Offences Against the Person Act 1861.","Maliciously inflicting grievous bodily harm under section 20 of the Offences Against the Person Act 1861.","Assault occasioning actual bodily harm under section 47 of the Offences Against the Person Act 1861.","Battery, because Jordan intended to cause no more than minor harm to Oliver when he struck him.","Attempting to cause grievous bodily harm with intent, because Jordan did not foresee the fracture."],"a":1,"e":"A fractured skull is grievous bodily harm (really serious harm). For section 20, **'maliciously' requires only that the defendant intended or foresaw some harm, not serious harm** (Mowatt), which Jordan did. Section 18 needs an intention to cause grievous bodily harm, which he lacked, and an attempt also requires that intent. Section 47 and battery fit the facts but are less serious than the section 20 offence that is made out."},{"t":"theft","p":"FLK2","q":"Bilal lent his friend Josh £250, which Josh promised to repay within a month. Months later Josh refuses to pay. Bilal goes to Josh's house, takes his mountain bike, worth about £250, and tells him he can have it back when he repays. Bilal says he genuinely believed the law allowed him to take property of equal value to settle a debt. That belief is wrong.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Bilal is dishonest for the purposes of theft?","o":["He is dishonest, because ordinary decent people would regard taking another person's property in this way as dishonest.","He is not dishonest only if a reasonable person in his position would also have believed that he had a legal right.","He is dishonest, because he had no legal right to take the bike and ignorance of the law is no defence.","He is not dishonest if he genuinely believed he had a legal right to take the bike, even if that belief was unreasonable.","He is not dishonest, because he intended to return the bike once the debt had been repaid to him."],"a":3,"e":"Under **s.2(1)(a) Theft Act 1968 an appropriation is not dishonest if the defendant believes he has the right in law to deprive the owner**, and the belief need only be genuine, not reasonable. Where s.2 applies, the general test of what ordinary decent people would think (Ivey) is not reached. Returning the bike only on payment of the debt is an intention to treat it as his own to dispose of under s.6, so that answer is wrong."},{"t":"theft","p":"FLK2","q":"Hannah's employer mistakenly pays £1,800 into her bank account instead of her usual £1,200 monthly salary. She notices the extra £600 the next day, decides to say nothing and spends it on a holiday.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the extra £600 belongs to another for the purposes of theft?","o":["It does, because she got it by another's mistake and is under an obligation to make restoration.","It does not, because a credit balance in her own bank account is property belonging only to her.","It does not, because she did nothing to cause the mistake and simply received the payment.","It does not, because a bank balance is a debt owed by the bank and so is not property at all.","It does, but only if she had told her employer something false to obtain the overpayment."],"a":0,"e":"**Under s.5(4) Theft Act 1968, property got by another's mistake, where the recipient is under a legal obligation to restore it, is treated as belonging to the person entitled to restoration.** So the £600 credit is treated as belonging to the employer, and spending it can be appropriation of property belonging to another. A bank balance is a thing in action, which is property under s.4, and it does not matter that Hannah did not cause the mistake."},{"t":"robbery-burglary","p":"FLK2","q":"Ruth is walking home with her handbag on her shoulder. Marcus runs up behind her and grabs the bag. Ruth holds onto the strap for a moment, but Marcus wrenches it from her grip and runs off with it. Ruth is not hurt.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the offence Marcus has committed?","o":["Theft only, because the force was applied to the bag and not to Ruth's body.","Theft only, because the force used was too slight to amount to robbery.","Robbery, but only because Ruth suffered some fear of being injured.","Theft only, because robbery requires the victim to suffer some injury.","Robbery, because he used force on Ruth in order to steal the bag."],"a":4,"e":"**Robbery is theft where the defendant uses force on any person immediately before or at the time of stealing, in order to steal** (s.8 Theft Act 1968). Wrenching a bag from the victim's grip is force on the person, even though it is applied through the property (Clouden). Whether force was used is an ordinary question for the jury and little is needed; no injury or fear is required where force is actually used."},{"t":"robbery-burglary","p":"FLK2","q":"Nadia enters a department store during opening hours as an ordinary shopper. She then walks through a door marked 'Staff only', intending to steal from the staff lockers. The lockers are all empty, and a security guard stops her as she leaves the staff area. Nothing has been taken.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Nadia's liability for burglary?","o":["She is not guilty of burglary, because she entered the store lawfully as a customer during its normal opening hours.","She is guilty of burglary only if she actually stole something after entering.","She is guilty of burglary, because she entered part of a building as a trespasser intending to steal.","She is not guilty of burglary, because a staff area is not a separate building.","She is guilty only of attempted theft, because there was nothing in the lockers to steal."],"a":2,"e":"**Under s.9(1)(a) Theft Act 1968, burglary is committed by entering a building or part of a building as a trespasser with intent to steal**; the offence is complete on entry. By going beyond the area customers are permitted to enter, Nadia entered part of the building as a trespasser, so her lawful entry to the shop floor does not help her. Actually stealing is needed only for s.9(1)(b), and the empty lockers are irrelevant to an intention to steal whatever she found."},{"t":"criminal-damage","p":"FLK2","q":"Ellis, aged 22, has a learning disability. He lights a bonfire of garden waste right against the wooden wall of his neighbour's shed. The fire spreads and destroys the shed. Expert evidence shows that Ellis genuinely did not appreciate that the fire might spread to the shed, although the risk would have been obvious to most adults.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ellis's liability for arson?","o":["He is guilty, because the risk would have been obvious to a reasonable person.","He is guilty, because arson is an offence of strict liability as to the damage caused.","He is not guilty unless he intended that the fire would spread and destroy the shed.","He is not guilty unless he was himself aware of a risk that the shed would catch fire.","He is guilty, because a reasonable person with his learning disability would have seen the risk."],"a":3,"e":"**Recklessness in criminal damage and arson is subjective: the defendant must be aware of a risk and it must be unreasonable to take it** (R v G). As Ellis genuinely did not appreciate the risk, he was not reckless, however obvious it would have been to others. Intention is not required because recklessness suffices, and the courts do not apply a 'reasonable person with his characteristics' test here."},{"t":"criminal-damage","p":"FLK2","q":"After a night out, Freya is very drunk. Her brother has told her to break in through his back door whenever she is locked out. She goes to a house in the same terrace, honestly but wrongly believing it is her brother's, and kicks the back door in. The house belongs to a stranger. She is charged with criminal damage under section 1(1) of the Criminal Damage Act 1971.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Freya has a lawful excuse?","o":["She has a lawful excuse if she honestly believed the owner would have consented, even though she was drunk.","She has no lawful excuse, because her belief that the house was her brother's was not a reasonable one.","She has no lawful excuse, because a belief caused by voluntary intoxication cannot support any defence.","She has a lawful excuse only if the person who actually owned the house had consented to the damage.","She has no lawful excuse, because her brother had no authority to consent to damage to another's house."],"a":0,"e":"**Under s.5(2)(a) Criminal Damage Act 1971 a defendant has a lawful excuse if she believed the person entitled to consent would have consented, and s.5(3) makes it immaterial whether the belief is justified, provided it is honestly held.** A drunken but honest belief is enough (Jaggard v Dickinson), unlike in self-defence, where a drunken mistake cannot be relied on. The real owner's actual consent is not needed."},{"t":"murder","p":"FLK2","q":"Gareth and Sam fall out over a drug debt. Gareth attacks Sam with a baseball bat, intending to break both his legs so that he cannot work for months, but not intending to kill him. Sam is taken to hospital, where he receives proper treatment, but he dies a week later from a blood clot caused by his leg injuries.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Gareth's liability?","o":["He is guilty only of manslaughter, because he did not intend to kill Sam when he attacked him.","He is guilty of murder, because an intention to cause really serious harm is sufficient mens rea.","He is guilty of murder only if he foresaw Sam's death as a virtually certain result of the attack.","He is guilty of unlawful act manslaughter, because Sam's death from a blood clot was unforeseen.","He is guilty only of causing grievous bodily harm with intent, because Sam died from a complication."],"a":1,"e":"**The mens rea of murder is an intention to kill or an intention to cause grievous bodily harm (really serious harm)** (Vickers, Cunningham). Breaking both legs is really serious harm, so Gareth has the mens rea of murder even though he did not intend death. His attack remained the operating cause of death, as a medical complication arising from the injuries under proper treatment does not break the chain of causation, so manslaughter or a section 18 offence alone would understate his liability."},{"t":"voluntary-manslaughter","p":"FLK2","q":"Late one evening Kieran learns that his teenage daughter was assaulted by Victor, a local man. Kieran spends the night pacing and thinking about what to do. The next morning he takes a hammer from his shed, waits outside Victor's workplace and kills him with several blows as he arrives. Kieran is charged with murder.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the partial defence of loss of control is available?","o":["It is not available, because his loss of self-control was not sudden.","It is available, because the assault on his daughter was extremely grave.","It is not available, because he acted in a considered desire for revenge.","It is available, if a man of his age might have reacted in the same way.","It is not available, because the wrong was done to his daughter, not him."],"a":2,"e":"**Loss of control is excluded where the defendant acted in a considered desire for revenge** (s.54(4) Coroners and Justice Act 2009). Brooding overnight, arming himself and lying in wait show considered, planned action rather than a loss of self-control. The loss of control need not be sudden (s.54(2)), and a qualifying trigger or the objective test does not help where revenge excludes the defence; a wrong done to a family member can found the trigger."},{"t":"voluntary-manslaughter","p":"FLK2","q":"Fiona has a long-standing severe depressive illness. One evening, after drinking a bottle of wine, she stabs her partner to death during an argument. Psychiatrists agree that her depression substantially impaired her ability to exercise self-control, and that she would not have killed but for both the depression and the drink. She is charged with murder and pleads diminished responsibility.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the jury should approach the defence?","o":["It must fail, because she had voluntarily consumed alcohol shortly before the killing took place.","It can succeed only if her depression, rather than the drink, was the sole cause of the killing.","It must fail, because depression is not a recognised medical condition for this defence.","It can succeed because voluntary intoxication is in itself an abnormality of mental functioning.","It can succeed if her depression, despite the drink, substantially impaired her and explains the killing."],"a":4,"e":"**Where the defendant has a recognised medical condition and was also drunk, the jury asks whether the condition, despite the drink, substantially impaired her ability and provides an explanation for the killing** (Dietschmann). Under s.2 Homicide Act 1957 the abnormality need only be a significant contributory factor, not the sole cause. Depression can be a recognised medical condition, but voluntary intoxication on its own cannot found the defence (Dowds)."},{"t":"involuntary-manslaughter","p":"FLK2","q":"Darren breaks into a bungalow at night to steal. In the hallway he meets the occupier, Mr Bell, who is visibly elderly and frail and leans on a walking frame. Darren shouts at him to stay where he is while he searches the rooms. Mr Bell suffers a heart attack from the shock and dies.\n\nWhich of the following best describes when Darren is guilty of unlawful act manslaughter?","o":["Only if Darren himself foresaw that his conduct risked causing some physical harm to Mr Bell.","Never, because burglary is an offence against property and cannot found a manslaughter charge.","Only if a sober and reasonable person would have recognised the risk of serious harm to Mr Bell.","If a sober and reasonable person present would have recognised the risk of some physical harm to Mr Bell.","Never, because death from a heart attack is not a reasonably foreseeable result of shouting."],"a":3,"e":"Unlawful act manslaughter requires an unlawful act that is **dangerous, meaning one that a sober and reasonable person would recognise as carrying a risk of some physical harm, though not necessarily serious harm**. That person has the knowledge the defendant gained during the act, so once Mr Bell's frailty was obvious the burglary could become dangerous (Watson). Death itself need not be foreseeable, only the risk of some physical harm, so the heart attack does not rule out liability provided the act caused the death. The test is objective, so Darren's own foresight is irrelevant, and any criminal offence, including a property offence, can be the unlawful act if it is dangerous."},{"t":"involuntary-manslaughter","p":"FLK2","q":"Sunil, a qualified electrician, rewires a rented flat in a hurry. He connects the earth wire incorrectly and does not carry out the standard safety tests. Two days later the tenant, Grace, touches a live metal light switch and is electrocuted. Sunil is charged with gross negligence manslaughter.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the prosecution must prove about risk and fault?","o":["A reasonably prudent electrician would have foreseen a serious and obvious risk of death, and his breach was so bad as to be criminal.","A reasonably prudent electrician would have foreseen a serious and obvious risk of injury, and his breach was more than trivial.","Sunil himself was aware that his work created a risk of death to the tenant, and he went on to take that risk unreasonably.","Sunil committed an unlawful act that a sober and reasonable person would recognise as carrying a risk of some harm.","Sunil fell below the standard of a reasonably competent electrician, and that breach was a cause of the tenant's death."],"a":0,"e":"Gross negligence manslaughter requires a duty, breach causing death, **a serious and obvious risk of death that a reasonably prudent person in the defendant's position would have foreseen, and a breach so bad that it should be judged criminal** (Adomako; Misra). A risk of injury, even serious injury, is not enough, and the test is objective, so Sunil's own awareness is not required. Mere civil negligence is not sufficient, and the 'unlawful act' test belongs to unlawful act manslaughter."},{"t":"fraud","p":"FLK2","q":"Chidi advertises his car for sale online. When a buyer, Leah, comes to view it, he tells her it has had one previous owner, knowing that it has had four. Leah checks the vehicle history on her phone, discovers the truth and leaves without buying.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Chidi's liability?","o":["He is guilty only of attempted fraud, because Leah was not deceived by what he said and did not buy the car from him.","He is not guilty of any offence, because he did not obtain any money or other property from Leah as a result.","He is guilty of fraud by false representation, because the offence is complete when he makes the dishonest lie.","He is not guilty of any offence, because Leah suffered no financial loss and was never exposed to any risk of loss.","He is guilty of fraud by failing to disclose information, because he did not reveal the car's true history to Leah."],"a":2,"e":"**Fraud by false representation (s.2 Fraud Act 2006) is a conduct crime: it is complete when the defendant dishonestly makes a representation he knows is or might be untrue, intending to make a gain or cause a loss.** Nobody need be deceived and no gain or loss need actually result, so it is the full offence, not an attempt. Fraud by failing to disclose (s.3) requires a legal duty to disclose and is not the right charge for a positive lie."},{"t":"general-defences","p":"FLK2","q":"After drinking heavily at a party, Ryan smashes a glass into Toby's face, causing a deep wound. Ryan says he was so drunk that he has no memory of the incident and had no intention of harming anyone. He is charged with causing grievous bodily harm with intent under section 18 of the Offences Against the Person Act 1861.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely effect of his voluntary intoxication?","o":["He will be convicted under section 18, because voluntary intoxication is never relevant to criminal liability.","He will be acquitted of all offences, because he lacked the mens rea for any offence against the person.","He may be acquitted under section 18 and section 20 if he lacked intent, but convicted under section 47 only.","He will be convicted under section 18, because a drunken intent is still an intent for the purposes of the offence.","He may be acquitted under section 18 if he lacked intent, but convicted under section 20, a basic intent offence."],"a":4,"e":"**Voluntary intoxication can be relied on to show that the defendant did not form the intent for a specific intent offence such as section 18, but it is no defence to a basic intent offence such as section 20 or section 47** (Majewski). A wound is covered by section 20, so a jury that accepts Ryan lacked intent would convict under section 20 rather than acquit altogether. It is true that a drunken intent is still an intent, but that only matters if he actually formed the intent."},{"t":"general-defences","p":"FLK2","q":"At 3am, Imran is woken by noises downstairs in his home. He finds a man he believes to be a burglar in his kitchen. Fearing for his family, he hits the man several times with a cricket bat, causing serious injuries. The jury may well find that the force used was disproportionate, but not grossly disproportionate, in the circumstances as Imran believed them to be.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Imran can rely on self-defence?","o":["It must fail, because the force he used was disproportionate in the circumstances he believed.","It may succeed, because disproportionate force can still be reasonable in a householder case.","It must succeed, because a householder may use any degree of force against a trespasser.","It must fail, because he could have retreated upstairs or called the police instead.","It may succeed, but only if the intruder had attacked Imran or his family first."],"a":1,"e":"In a **householder case, force is not reasonable if it is grossly disproportionate, but force that is disproportionate without being grossly so may still be reasonable**, depending on all the circumstances as the defendant believed them (s.76(5A) Criminal Justice and Immigration Act 2008). In an ordinary case disproportionate force is never reasonable, which is why option A tempts. There is no duty to retreat, a pre-emptive strike can be lawful, and householders cannot use unlimited force."},{"t":"parties","p":"FLK2","q":"Liam drives his friend Asif to the home of Paul, a man Asif blames for his brother's arrest. Liam knows Asif is carrying a large knife and that he wants to 'sort Paul out'. Liam waits in the car while Asif goes inside and stabs Paul to death, intending to kill him.\n\nWhich of the following best describes when Liam is guilty of murder as an accessory?","o":["If he foresaw that Asif might kill or cause serious harm, even if he did not intend to assist him.","Never, because he did not enter the house and took no part in the stabbing that killed Paul.","If he intended to help Asif to attack Paul with intent to kill or cause serious harm.","Only if he and Asif had expressly agreed in advance that Paul should be killed during the visit.","Only if he had himself intended that Paul should die when he agreed to drive Asif to the house."],"a":2,"e":"**An accessory must intend to assist or encourage the principal to commit the crime, with knowledge of any facts needed to make it criminal, including the principal's intent** (Jogee). Liam need not intend Paul's death himself, but mere foresight that Asif might kill is only evidence from which intention may be inferred, not a test of liability. Presence at the scene and an express agreement are not required for secondary liability."},{"t":"attempts","p":"FLK2","q":"Zara plans to rob a sub-post office. She puts on a balaclava, takes an imitation pistol and a holdall, and walks towards the post office. Police, who have been watching her, arrest her on the pavement about 30 metres from the door. She has not touched the door or spoken to anyone.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Zara's liability for attempted robbery?","o":["She is guilty, because she intended to rob and had equipped herself to carry out the robbery.","She is guilty, because she had done everything she could do before reaching the post office.","She is not guilty, because an attempt requires the last act before the full offence is committed.","She is not guilty, because her acts had not gone beyond mere preparation for the robbery.","She is not guilty, because robbery is not an offence that can be attempted under the 1981 Act."],"a":3,"e":"**Under s.1(1) Criminal Attempts Act 1981, the defendant must do an act that is more than merely preparatory, which means embarking on the crime proper.** Approaching the target in disguise with an imitation weapon, without entering or confronting anyone, is still preparation (Campbell). Intention and equipment alone are not enough, but the old 'last act' test is too strict, and robbery, being indictable, can be attempted."}],"crimlaw-p2":[{"t":"actus-reus","p":"FLK2","q":"Aaron offers his colleague, Bethany, a lift home after a works party. On a quiet road, travelling at about 25 miles per hour, he locks the doors, puts his hand on her thigh and tells her that she will not be getting out until she has 'been nice to him'. Terrified, Bethany unlocks her door and jumps out, fracturing her wrist. Aaron is charged with assault occasioning actual bodily harm and argues that Bethany caused her own injury.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Aaron caused Bethany's injury?","o":["He caused it, because her escape was a foreseeable reaction to his threats and not a daft one.","He did not cause it, because her free and deliberate decision to jump was a new intervening act.","He caused it only if he personally foresaw that she might jump from the moving car.","He did not cause it, because jumping from a moving car was an unreasonable overreaction to his words.","He caused it only if a reasonable person in her position would have reacted in exactly the same way."],"a":0,"e":"Where the victim is injured escaping from the defendant, **the defendant remains a legal cause unless the victim's reaction was so 'daft' or unexpected that no reasonable person could have foreseen it** (R v Roberts; R v Williams). The test is objective, so Aaron need not personally have foreseen the jump, and a frightened victim is not expected to weigh her options as calmly as a reasonable person would. An escape made in fear is not a free, deliberate and informed act, so it does not break the chain of causation (the link between the defendant's conduct and the result)."},{"t":"actus-reus","p":"FLK2","q":"Kofi is staying overnight at his friend Ben's flat. While Ben is out, Kofi lights a scented candle in the spare room and puts it on a bookshelf. He dozes off and wakes to find that the candle has fallen over and the curtains are smouldering. Realising the flat could catch fire and worried that he will be blamed, Kofi does nothing to put out the flames or call for help; he packs his bag and leaves. The fire spreads and badly damages the flat.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Kofi's liability for criminal damage?","o":["He is not liable, because a person is never criminally liable for simply failing to act.","He is not liable, because he was asleep and acting involuntarily when the fire started.","He is not liable, because he owed Ben no duty arising from a contract or family relationship.","He is liable, because having created a dangerous situation he became aware of it and failed to act.","He is liable, because any guest who sees a fire in a host's home is under a duty to put it out."],"a":3,"e":"**A person who inadvertently creates a dangerous situation comes under a duty to take reasonable steps to deal with it once aware of it**; failing to do so, with the required mens rea, is an omission that can found liability (R v Miller). Although there is generally no liability for omissions, the creation of danger is a recognised exception, so the absence of a contractual or family duty does not help Kofi. It does not matter that he was asleep when the fire started: the relevant conduct is his failure to act after he woke and realised the risk, which also supplies recklessness."},{"t":"mens-rea","p":"FLK2","q":"During a violent argument, Grant strangles his flatmate, Lucas, intending to kill him, until Lucas goes limp. Believing Lucas is dead, Grant drives to a reservoir in the early hours and throws the body into the water to get rid of it. A post-mortem shows that Lucas was still alive when he entered the water and died from drowning. Grant argues that when Lucas actually died, he had no intention to harm him because he thought he was disposing of a corpse.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Grant's liability for murder?","o":["He is not guilty of murder, because his act and his intention did not coincide at the moment of death.","He can be guilty of murder, because the strangling and the disposal can be treated as one series of acts.","He is guilty only of gross negligence manslaughter, because he failed to check whether Lucas was alive.","He can be guilty only of attempted murder, because the strangling itself did not cause the death.","He is not guilty of murder, because throwing the body into the water was a new intervening act."],"a":1,"e":"The actus reus and mens rea must coincide, but **where the defendant's acts form one series or transaction, it is enough that the mens rea existed at some point during it** (Thabo Meli v R; R v Church). Grant's intention to kill during the strangling therefore covers the later act of throwing Lucas into the reservoir, which actually caused death. His own later act cannot be a new intervening act breaking the chain, and there is no need to fall back on attempted murder or manslaughter."},{"t":"non-fatal-offences","p":"FLK2","q":"PC Shah lawfully arrests Morgan for shoplifting and takes hold of his arm. Desperate to get away, Morgan pulls a key from his pocket and slashes at the officer's hand, breaking the skin and causing a cut that needs three stitches. Morgan says he had no wish to seriously hurt PC Shah and only wanted to escape, although he accepts he realised the officer might be cut.\n\nWhich of the following is the most serious offence Morgan has committed against PC Shah?","o":["Assault occasioning actual bodily harm (s.47 OAPA 1861)","Unlawful and malicious wounding (s.20 OAPA 1861)","Battery, charged under s.39 Criminal Justice Act 1988","Causing grievous bodily harm with intent to do GBH (s.18 OAPA 1861)","Wounding with intent to resist lawful apprehension (s.18 OAPA 1861)"],"a":4,"e":"**Section 18 can be committed by wounding with intent to resist or prevent lawful apprehension (arrest), provided the defendant also has malice, meaning foresight of some harm.** A 'wound' is a break in both layers of the skin, which a cut needing stitches clearly is. Morgan cannot be convicted of the GBH-intent form of s.18, because a stitched cut is not really serious harm and he did not intend such harm, but the arrest-resisting form makes s.18 available, so s.20 and s.47 are not the most serious offences."},{"t":"non-fatal-offences","p":"FLK2","q":"On the last day of term at their sixth-form college, a group of friends start a boisterous game of throwing each other into the air and catching them on the grass. Rhys has joined in for several rounds and laughed throughout. On his next turn, Ade and Samir throw him high but fail to catch him, and he breaks his collarbone. Neither intended to hurt anyone, and both genuinely believed Rhys was happy to take part. They are charged under s.20 Offences Against the Person Act 1861.\n\nWhich of the following best describes their liability?","o":["They are guilty, because consent can never be a defence where grievous bodily harm results.","They are guilty, because consent is a defence only to common assault and battery.","They are not guilty, because a genuine belief in consent to rough horseplay is a defence.","They are not guilty only if Rhys expressly agreed in advance to the risk of a broken bone.","They are guilty, because a belief in consent is relevant only if it was also reasonably held."],"a":2,"e":"Consent is generally no defence to ABH or worse, but **rough horseplay without intent to injure is a recognised exception, and a genuine belief that the victim consented is enough even if serious harm results** (R v Jones; R v Aitken). The belief need not be reasonable, and consent to horseplay can be implied from the victim joining in, so no express advance agreement to the specific risk is needed. Options stating that consent covers only assault and battery ignore the recognised exceptions such as sport, surgery, tattooing and horseplay."},{"t":"theft","p":"FLK2","q":"Mrs Aldridge, aged 88, has early dementia and is easily influenced. Over several weeks, her paid carer, Tanya, persuades her to make 'gifts' to Tanya totalling £18,000 from her savings. Under civil law, Mrs Aldridge had just enough mental capacity for the gifts to be valid, and Tanya made no false statements to her. Ordinary decent people would regard Tanya's conduct as dishonest.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Tanya can be guilty of theft?","o":["She cannot be guilty, because Mrs Aldridge consented to the transfer of the money.","She cannot be guilty, because a valid gift passes full ownership to the person receiving it.","She can be guilty only of fraud, because theft requires property to be taken without consent.","She can be guilty, because receiving a valid gift can be an appropriation if she is dishonest.","She can be guilty only if the prosecution proves that Mrs Aldridge lacked capacity to make the gifts."],"a":3,"e":"**An appropriation (any assumption of the rights of an owner) can occur even where the owner consents, and even where the defendant receives a gift that is valid in civil law** (R v Hinks; R v Gomez). The decisive question is therefore dishonesty under the Ivey test: Tanya's knowledge of Mrs Aldridge's vulnerability, judged by the standards of ordinary decent people, makes her conduct dishonest. Consent and the validity of the gift do not prevent theft, and the prosecution need not prove a lack of capacity."},{"t":"theft","p":"FLK2","q":"Marisol works in a large accounts team. When a long-serving colleague announces her retirement, Marisol offers to organise a leaving present and collects £340 in cash from twenty colleagues, each of whom hands it over expressly 'for the retirement gift'. A week later, short of money, Marisol spends the whole sum on a weekend break for herself and tells the team that the collection was stolen from her bag.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the £340 was 'property belonging to another' when Marisol spent it?","o":["Yes, because she received it under an obligation to the contributors to use it in a particular way.","No, because ownership of the cash passed to her outright when her colleagues handed it over.","No, because the retiring colleague had not yet received the money and so had no interest in it.","Yes, but only if the contributors made a written agreement setting out how it was to be spent.","No, because money mixed with her own cash can no longer be identified as theirs."],"a":0,"e":"**Under s.5(3) Theft Act 1968, where a person receives property from or on account of another and is under an obligation to retain and deal with it (or its proceeds) in a particular way, it is treated as belonging to the other person.** Each contributor handed over the cash for the specific purpose of buying the gift, so it is regarded as theirs even though Marisol had possession and legal title. No written agreement is required, and the fact that the retiring colleague had no interest yet is irrelevant because the property belongs to the contributors; her false story is also strong evidence of dishonesty."},{"t":"robbery-burglary","p":"FLK2","q":"Shaun breaks into a house at night through a rear window, intending to steal. He is unarmed when he climbs in. In the kitchen he takes a large carving knife from a knife block in case he is disturbed. The homeowner, Mr Patel, comes downstairs. Shaun holds up the knife, makes Mr Patel hand over his wallet and leaves with it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Shaun's liability for aggravated burglary?","o":["He is not guilty, because he did not have a weapon with him when he entered the house.","He is not guilty, because a kitchen knife is not an article made or adapted for causing injury.","He is guilty, because he had the knife with him when he stole from Mr Patel inside the house.","He is guilty only if he intended to use a knife when he first decided to break in.","He is not guilty, because the knife belonged to the householder and was not brought in."],"a":2,"e":"**For a s.9(1)(b) burglary, the relevant time for aggravated burglary is when the defendant commits the theft (or GBH) inside the building, so a weapon picked up inside counts** (R v O'Leary). A kitchen knife is a weapon of offence because Shaun had it with him intending to use it to cause injury to, or incapacitate, anyone who disturbed him (s.10(1)(b) Theft Act 1968): it need not be made or adapted for that purpose. Lack of a weapon on entry matters only where the charge is based on the s.9(1)(a) burglary committed at the moment of entry."},{"t":"criminal-damage","p":"FLK2","q":"After falling out with his former business partner, Duncan goes to the partner's house at night and fires an air rifle at the lit living-room window, behind which the partner and his wife are watching television. The pellet shatters the glass and passes close to the wife's head. Duncan accepts that he intended to break the window and realised the pellet might hit someone. The jury is satisfied that life was endangered by the pellet, but that the broken glass created no danger to life.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Duncan's liability under the Criminal Damage Act 1971?","o":["He is guilty of aggravated criminal damage, because he was reckless as to whether life would be endangered.","He is guilty of aggravated criminal damage, because life was in fact endangered by his conduct.","He is not guilty of any offence under the Act, because nobody was actually injured.","He is guilty of aggravated criminal damage, because he intended to damage another's property.","He is guilty of simple criminal damage only, because the danger to life did not come from the damage."],"a":4,"e":"For aggravated criminal damage under s.1(2), **the defendant must intend or be reckless that life will be endangered by the damage to property itself, not by the means used to cause it** (R v Steer). Here the danger came from the pellet, while the shattered glass created none, so the s.1(2) offence is not made out however reckless Duncan was about the pellet. He intentionally damaged property belonging to another, so he is guilty of simple criminal damage under s.1(1); no injury is needed for either offence."},{"t":"murder","p":"FLK2","q":"Ewan is furious with his brother-in-law, Kevin, who cannot swim. On a ferry crossing at night in January, far from land, Ewan pushes Kevin over the rail into the freezing sea. He later says he only wanted to 'give him a fright' and expected the crew to fish him out. Kevin's body is recovered the next day. The prosecution cannot prove that killing or seriously harming Kevin was Ewan's aim or purpose.\n\nHow should the judge direct the jury on whether Ewan intended to kill or cause grievous bodily harm?","o":["They must find intention if death or serious harm was a highly probable result of what Ewan did.","They may find intention if death or serious harm was a virtual certainty and Ewan appreciated that.","They must find intention if a reasonable person would have foreseen death as a virtual certainty.","They may find intention if Ewan foresaw a real risk that Kevin might die or be seriously hurt.","They cannot find intention, because Ewan's aim was only to frighten Kevin and not to harm him."],"a":1,"e":"Where direct intention (aim or purpose) cannot be shown, **the jury may find oblique intention only if death or serious harm was a virtual certainty as a result of the defendant's act and the defendant appreciated that this was so** (R v Woollin). Foresight is evidence from which intention may be found, not intention itself, so the jury is never obliged to find it. Foresight of a high probability or a real risk is not enough for murder, and the test turns on what Ewan himself appreciated, not on what a reasonable person would have foreseen."},{"t":"murder","p":"FLK2","q":"Jade, who is seven months pregnant, is stabbed in the abdomen by her former partner, Marlon, during an argument. Marlon intends to cause Jade really serious harm. Jade recovers, but the knife also injures the foetus, and the baby, Ella, is born prematurely two weeks later. Ella lives for three months and then dies as a direct result of the injuries caused by the stabbing.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Marlon's liability for Ella's death?","o":["He is guilty of murder, because his intention to cause GBH to Jade transfers to Ella.","He is not liable for her death, because Ella was not a person in being when he stabbed Jade.","He is guilty of murder, because Ella was born alive and died from injuries he intended to inflict.","He is guilty of murder only if he knew that Jade was pregnant when he stabbed her.","He is not guilty of murder, but he can be guilty of unlawful act manslaughter of Ella."],"a":4,"e":"A foetus is not a 'reasonable creature in being', and **malice aimed at the mother cannot be transferred first to the foetus and then to the child once born, so the killing cannot be murder; but where the child is born alive and later dies from the injury, unlawful act manslaughter is available** (Attorney-General's Reference (No 3 of 1994)). Ella was born alive and so became a person, which is why Marlon is not free of all liability. The stabbing was an unlawful and dangerous act that caused her death, which is all unlawful act manslaughter requires."},{"t":"voluntary-manslaughter","p":"FLK2","q":"For more than a year, Liam's wife, Grace, repeatedly mocks him about losing his job and his recent treatment for depression. One evening she tells him she has been having an affair with his best friend, laughs that he is 'not man enough' to keep either her or a job, and dares him to kill himself. Liam loses his self-control and strangles her. Charged with murder, he relies on loss of control.\n\nHow should the jury treat Grace's disclosure of the affair when deciding whether there was a qualifying trigger?","o":["They must ignore everything Grace said that evening, because it was linked to sexual infidelity.","They may treat the affair on its own as a qualifying trigger, as it was said in extremely grave circumstances.","They must disregard the affair as a trigger in itself, but may consider it as context for the other things said.","They may consider the affair only if Liam proves on the balance of probabilities that it caused his loss of control.","They must treat the affair as a trigger, because it gave Liam a justifiable sense of being seriously wronged."],"a":2,"e":"Under **s.55(6)(c) Coroners and Justice Act 2009, sexual infidelity must be disregarded as a qualifying trigger on its own, but it may be taken into account as context where other potential triggers are present** (R v Clinton). The prolonged taunts about Liam's job loss and mental health are capable of being things said of an extremely grave character that gave him a justifiable sense of being seriously wronged, so the jury need not ignore everything said. Once there is sufficient evidence of the defence, the prosecution must disprove it beyond reasonable doubt; Liam does not bear a legal burden."},{"t":"voluntary-manslaughter","p":"FLK2","q":"Nathan kills his neighbour with a hammer during a dispute about noise. Charged with murder, he pleads diminished responsibility. Two psychiatrists agree that he has a recognised personality disorder, that it impaired his ability to exercise self-control and that it explains why he attacked. However, both describe the impairment as 'more than trivial, but not significant or weighty'.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the defence is likely to succeed?","o":["It is likely to fail, because the impairment must be substantial and not merely more than trivial.","It is likely to succeed, because any impairment that is more than trivial is enough for the defence.","It is likely to succeed, because the prosecution cannot disprove the defence beyond reasonable doubt.","It is likely to fail, because a personality disorder cannot be a recognised medical condition.","It is likely to succeed, because the disorder explains his conduct and was a cause of the killing."],"a":0,"e":"Diminished responsibility under s.2 Homicide Act 1957 requires an abnormality of mental functioning from a recognised medical condition that **substantially impairs** the defendant's ability to understand his conduct, form a rational judgment or exercise self-control, and that explains the killing. **'Substantially' means important or weighty, not merely more than trivial** (R v Golds), so the agreed evidence points against the defence. The burden is on the defendant to prove it on the balance of probabilities, and a personality disorder can be a recognised medical condition."},{"t":"involuntary-manslaughter","p":"FLK2","q":"Craig, a heroin dealer, visits his customer, Owen, at Owen's flat. At Owen's request, Craig prepares a syringe of heroin and hands it to him. Owen, an experienced adult user who knows exactly what the syringe contains, injects himself and dies of an overdose shortly afterwards. Craig is charged with unlawful act manslaughter.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Craig's liability?","o":["He is guilty, because supplying heroin is an unlawful act that is objectively dangerous.","He is guilty, because preparing the syringe made him a substantial cause of Owen's death.","He is guilty, because as the supplier he owed Owen a duty of care that he breached.","He is not guilty, because Owen's free and informed choice to inject broke the chain of causation.","He is not guilty, because supplying a drug is not an unlawful act for the purposes of manslaughter."],"a":3,"e":"Unlawful act manslaughter requires the unlawful and dangerous act to cause death, and **the free, deliberate and informed act of a fully informed adult in injecting himself breaks the chain of causation** (R v Kennedy (No 2)). Supply of heroin is an unlawful act, but it is Owen's self-injection, not the supply or preparation, that caused death. A duty of care is relevant to gross negligence manslaughter, not to the unlawful act charge Craig faces."},{"t":"fraud","p":"FLK2","q":"Rohan holds a lasting power of attorney for property and financial affairs for his elderly father, who lives in a care home. Under pressure from his own creditors, Rohan transfers £9,000 from his father's savings into his own account to clear his credit card, intending to put the money back when his bonus arrives in six months. He tells nobody. A jury would find his conduct dishonest.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Rohan's liability under the Fraud Act 2006?","o":["He has committed no offence under the Act, because he intended to repay the money in full.","He has committed fraud by abuse of position, even though he intended the gain to be temporary.","He has committed fraud by false representation, because the transfer implied it was for his father.","He has committed fraud by failing to disclose information, because he did not tell his father.","He has committed no offence under the Act, because his father suffered no loss that he knew of."],"a":1,"e":"**Fraud by abuse of position (s.4) is committed by a person in a position where he is expected to safeguard another's financial interests who dishonestly abuses it, intending a gain or loss; 'gain' and 'loss' include temporary ones (s.5).** An attorney is a clear example of such a position, so Rohan's plan to repay does not prevent the offence, though it could be relevant to dishonesty, which the jury has found. There was no representation made to anyone, and s.3 requires a legal duty to disclose, so s.4 is the best fit."},{"t":"fraud","p":"FLK2","q":"Abigail runs a small cleaning company. She sends an invoice for £3,400 to a local primary school for 'deep-cleaning services' that her company never carried out, hoping that it will be paid without checking. The school's business manager immediately notices that no such work was booked and refuses to pay. Abigail is arrested.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Abigail's liability?","o":["She is guilty only of attempted fraud, because the school was not deceived and paid nothing.","She is not guilty of any offence, because no money or property was obtained from the school.","She is guilty of fraud by false representation, because the offence was complete when she sent the invoice.","She is guilty of fraud by abuse of position, because she was in a position of trust with the school.","She is not guilty of fraud, because the invoice was sent to an organisation rather than to an individual."],"a":2,"e":"**Fraud under s.2 Fraud Act 2006 is a conduct crime: it is complete when the defendant dishonestly makes a false representation, knowing it is or might be untrue, intending to make a gain or cause a loss.** Nobody needs to be deceived and nothing needs to be obtained, so Abigail is guilty of the full offence, not merely an attempt. Sending an invoice impliedly represents that the work was done, and an ordinary supplier is not in a position of trust for the purposes of s.4."},{"t":"general-defences","p":"FLK2","q":"Tom's partner has just ended their relationship. Upset, he takes four sleeping tablets that had been prescribed to her, hoping they will calm him down. He has never taken them before, and they are not normally known to cause aggressive or unpredictable behaviour. Instead of making him drowsy, they leave him confused, and he smashes her glass coffee table. He is charged with criminal damage, an offence of basic intent.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Tom can rely on his intoxicated state?","o":["He cannot rely on it, because voluntary intoxication is never a defence to a basic intent offence.","He cannot rely on it, because taking medication prescribed to someone else is always voluntary intoxication.","He can rely on it only if he proves on the balance of probabilities that he lacked mens rea.","He can rely on it, because intoxication is a defence to every offence of criminal damage.","He can rely on it to show he lacked mens rea, unless he was reckless in taking the tablets."],"a":4,"e":"**Taking a drug normally regarded as non-dangerous, such as a sedative, which has an unexpected effect is treated like involuntary intoxication, so it can negate mens rea even for a basic intent offence unless the defendant was reckless in taking it** (R v Hardie). The rule that voluntary intoxication is no defence to basic intent offences applies to alcohol and drugs known to cause aggression or unpredictability. The prosecution must still prove mens rea, so Tom bears no burden of proof."},{"t":"general-defences","p":"FLK2","q":"Walking through a shopping centre, Seb sees a man in jeans and a hoodie pin a teenager face-down on the floor. Seb genuinely believes the teenager is being attacked and punches the man once to pull him off. In fact, the man is a plain-clothes store detective lawfully detaining the teenager for theft. Most people in Seb's position would have realised what was happening. Seb is charged with battery.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Seb can rely on defence of another?","o":["He cannot rely on it, because his belief that the teenager was being attacked was not reasonable.","He can rely on it if, on the facts as he honestly believed them, a single punch was reasonable force.","He cannot rely on it, because the store detective was using lawful force against the teenager.","He can rely on it only if he proves that his mistake was one a reasonable person might have made.","He cannot rely on it, because defence of another protects only members of the defendant's own family."],"a":1,"e":"**The defendant is judged on the facts as he honestly believed them to be, whether or not his mistake was reasonable; the jury then asks whether the force used was reasonable in those believed circumstances** (s.76(3)-(4) Criminal Justice and Immigration Act 2008; R v Gladstone Williams). The unreasonableness of Seb's belief matters only as evidence of whether he genuinely held it. The defence protects any person, and once raised the prosecution must disprove it beyond reasonable doubt."},{"t":"parties","p":"FLK2","q":"Femi, Callum and Jamal agree to break into a jeweller's shop on Saturday night. Femi is to act as lookout, and on the Wednesday he hands Callum a set of bolt cutters to cut the shop's shutter lock. By Saturday Femi has changed his mind. He stays at home and switches off his phone without telling the others. Callum and Jamal use the bolt cutters to break in and steal jewellery.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Femi's liability for the burglary?","o":["He is liable as an accessory, because he did not clearly tell the others he was withdrawing.","He is not liable, because he was not present at the scene when the burglary was committed.","He is not liable, because he genuinely changed his mind before the offence was committed.","He is liable only for attempted burglary, because he took no part in the break-in itself.","He is not liable, because his agreed role as lookout was never performed and gave no help."],"a":0,"e":"**To withdraw from a planned offence, an accessory must at least give timely and unequivocal communication of withdrawal to the principals; simply failing to turn up is not enough** (R v Rook). Femi had already aided the burglary by supplying the bolt cutters, so his absence and private change of heart do not end his liability, and presence at the scene is not required. An accessory is guilty of the full offence the principals commit, not of an attempt (s.8 Accessories and Abettors Act 1861)."},{"t":"parties","p":"FLK2","q":"Gordon runs a tool hire and sales business. Kelvin, a regular customer, asks to buy a heavy-duty angle grinder and mentions that he and 'the lads' are going to 'do a warehouse' next month and take whatever stock they can sell. Gordon does not know which warehouse or when, but sells him the grinder at a premium price, hoping to keep his custom. Kelvin later uses it to break into a distribution warehouse and steal electrical goods.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Gordon's liability for the burglary?","o":["He is not liable, because he did not know which premises would be targeted or on what date.","He is not liable, because he was motivated by profit and did not want the burglary to happen.","He is not liable, because selling goods in the ordinary course of business cannot amount to aiding.","He is liable as an accessory, because he intentionally assisted knowing the type of crime intended.","He is liable only if he foresaw that Kelvin might use violence against people at the warehouse."],"a":3,"e":"**An accessory must intend to assist or encourage the principal with knowledge of the essential matters of the offence, but need not know the particular premises, victim or date; knowing the type of crime intended is enough** (R v Bainbridge; R v Jogee). Supplying equipment knowing it is to be used for a burglary is intentional assistance even if the seller's motive is profit or he is indifferent to the result. Foresight of violence is irrelevant because the offence Kelvin committed was burglary by stealing."},{"t":"attempts","p":"FLK2","q":"In a pub car park, a man offers Lena a nearly new laptop for £60, telling her it 'fell off the back of a lorry'. Lena believes it is stolen and buys it anyway. In fact, the laptop was the seller's own and had never been stolen; he lied to make a quick sale. Lena is charged with attempting to handle stolen goods.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Lena's liability?","o":["She is not guilty, because it is legally impossible to handle goods that were never stolen.","She is guilty, because a person can be guilty of an attempt even where the full offence is impossible.","She is not guilty, because she did not do an act that was more than merely preparatory.","She is guilty of handling stolen goods itself, because she believed the laptop was stolen.","She is not guilty, because a mistaken belief about the facts cannot form the mens rea of an attempt."],"a":1,"e":"Under **s.1(2) and (3) Criminal Attempts Act 1981, a person may be guilty of attempt even though the facts make the full offence impossible, and her intention is judged on the facts as she believed them to be** (R v Shivpuri). Lena intended to handle goods she believed were stolen and did far more than a preparatory act by buying the laptop. She cannot be guilty of the full offence of handling, because the goods were not in fact stolen."},{"t":"attempts","p":"FLK2","q":"Dominic waits outside a gym for a rival, Tyrone. When Tyrone comes out, Dominic fires a handgun at his legs, intending to cripple him but not to kill him. The bullet misses and hits a wall, and Tyrone is unhurt. Dominic is charged with attempted murder.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Dominic's liability for attempted murder?","o":["He is guilty, because an intention to cause grievous bodily harm is enough for murder.","He is guilty, because firing a gun at a person is an act more than merely preparatory to murder.","He is not guilty, because attempted murder requires proof of an intention to kill.","He is not guilty, because a gun aimed only at the legs is not capable of causing death.","He is guilty, because he was reckless as to whether Tyrone might be killed by the shot."],"a":2,"e":"**Attempted murder requires an intention to kill; an intention to cause grievous bodily harm, although sufficient for murder itself, is not enough for the attempt** (R v Whybrow). Firing at Tyrone was clearly more than merely preparatory, but the actus reus alone does not make Dominic guilty without the necessary intent, and recklessness is never sufficient. He could instead be convicted of attempting to cause GBH with intent under s.18 Offences Against the Person Act 1861."}],"crimlaw-p3":[{"t":"actus-reus","p":"FLK2","q":"Gareth is employed by a hotel as its pool attendant. His contract requires him to stay at the poolside and supervise swimmers whenever the pool is open. One afternoon he leaves the pool unattended for forty minutes to watch a football match in the bar. During that time a nine-year-old guest, Amira, gets into difficulty in the deep end and drowns. Gareth could easily have rescued her had he been there.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Gareth's failure to act can form the actus reus of a homicide offence?","o":["It can, because his contract of employment gave him a duty to act for the guests.","It cannot, because English law imposes no general duty to rescue a stranger in danger.","It cannot, because his contractual duty was owed only to the hotel, not to Amira.","It can, but only if Amira's parents had asked him personally to watch over her.","It cannot, because a failure to act can never cause a death in criminal law."],"a":0,"e":"There is **no general duty to act** in English law, but a duty can arise from a **contract**, and it extends to **people the contract is meant to protect**, not just the other contracting party (Pittwood: a level-crossing keeper who left the gate open). Gareth's job was to supervise swimmers, so his failure to do so can form the actus reus, typically of gross negligence manslaughter. No personal request from the parents is needed, and an omission in breach of duty can be a legal cause of death."},{"t":"mens-rea","p":"FLK2","q":"At a crowded bus stop, Declan aims a punch at Owen, who has just insulted him. Owen steps aside and the punch lands on the face of Mrs Achebe, who was standing just behind Owen, breaking her nose. Declan says he never saw her and had no wish to hurt anyone but Owen.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Declan's liability for the injury to Mrs Achebe?","o":["He is not liable, because he neither intended nor foresaw any harm to Mrs Achebe.","He is liable only if a reasonable person would have foreseen that someone behind Owen might be hit.","He is liable for battery only, because a transferred intention cannot support any offence causing injury.","He is liable, because his intention to strike Owen transfers to Mrs Achebe as the actual victim.","He is not liable, because the mens rea must relate to the particular person who is injured."],"a":3,"e":"Under the doctrine of **transferred malice**, where the defendant has the mens rea (guilty mind) for an offence against one person and commits the actus reus (guilty act) of the **same offence** against another, the mens rea transfers to the actual victim (Latimer). So the fact that Declan did not see or intend to hit Mrs Achebe is irrelevant, and no reasonable-person test applies. The offence is not limited to battery: an assault occasioning actual bodily harm needs only the mens rea for the battery, which transfers with it."},{"t":"mens-rea","p":"FLK2","q":"Ffion, a delivery driver, reverses her van and, without realising, stops with a rear wheel resting on the foot of a pedestrian, Mr Haddad. He shouts at her to move. Ffion hears him and sees what has happened but, annoyed at being shouted at, switches off the engine and sits in the cab for a minute before driving forward.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Ffion is guilty of battery?","o":["She is not guilty, because the force was first applied by accident, before she had any mens rea.","She is guilty, because keeping the wheel on his foot once she realised was part of one continuing act.","She is not guilty, because a battery cannot be committed by failing to move a vehicle.","She is guilty, because a driver is strictly liable for any force applied by her vehicle.","She is not guilty, because the actus reus was complete before she formed any intention."],"a":1,"e":"The actus reus and mens rea must **coincide**, but where the actus reus is a **continuing act**, it is enough that the mens rea is formed at some point while the act continues (Fagan). The wheel resting on the foot was a continuing application of force, which became a battery when Ffion knowingly chose to leave it there. The options saying the force was accidental or complete before she formed intent overlook the continuing act, and battery is not a strict liability offence."},{"t":"non-fatal-offences","p":"FLK2","q":"In a bar, Shona decides to throw the contents of her wine glass over Kezia, who has been flirting with Shona's boyfriend. As she swings her arm, the glass slips from her hand and hits Kezia's cheek, causing a cut that needs stitches. Shona says she only meant to soak Kezia and never thought that anyone might be hurt.\n\nWhich of the following is the most serious offence under the Offences Against the Person Act 1861 that Shona is likely to be guilty of?","o":["Unlawful wounding under section 20 of the 1861 Act, as the skin broke.","Wounding with intent under section 18 of the 1861 Act, as she meant to throw.","Battery only, because she foresaw no injury at all to Kezia.","No offence at all, because the glass slipped from her hand by accident.","Assault occasioning actual bodily harm under section 47 of the 1861 Act."],"a":4,"e":"Throwing wine over someone intentionally is a battery, and for **section 47** the prosecution need only prove the mens rea for the assault or battery: **the defendant need not intend or foresee any harm** (Savage). Section 20 requires the defendant to **foresee some physical harm**, which Shona did not, even though the cut is a wound (a break in both layers of the skin), and section 18 needs intent to cause serious harm. Battery alone understates her liability because actual bodily harm in fact resulted."},{"t":"non-fatal-offences","p":"FLK2","q":"After a row over a parking space, Tariq and Ollie, both adults, agree to 'settle it with fists' in a quiet side street. Both throw punches willingly. Tariq knocks Ollie down, leaving him with a black eye and a split lip. Tariq is charged with assault occasioning actual bodily harm and says that Ollie consented.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether consent is a defence?","o":["It is, because both men were adults who freely agreed to fight on equal terms.","It is, because the injuries were minor and did not amount to really serious harm.","It is not, because consent cannot be given to actual bodily harm caused in a fight of this kind.","It is not, because consent is relevant only to a charge of assault and never to battery.","It is, unless Tariq was the one who first suggested that they should fight."],"a":2,"e":"**Consent is no defence where actual bodily harm or worse is intended or caused**, unless the activity falls within a recognised exception such as properly conducted sport, surgery, tattooing or rough horseplay (Attorney-General's Reference (No 6 of 1980)). An agreed fist fight in the street is not an exception, so free agreement between adults does not help Tariq. A black eye and a split lip are actual bodily harm (hurt or injury more than transient or trifling), so it does not matter that they fall short of serious harm."},{"t":"theft","p":"FLK2","q":"Tobias buys a nearly new road bike for £900 from a man who advertises it online. The price is fair and Tobias has no reason to suspect anything. Three months later he sees a police appeal and realises that the bike was stolen from its owner, Mrs Davies, the week before he bought it. He decides to keep riding it anyway.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Tobias is guilty of theft of the bike?","o":["He is guilty, because keeping the bike after learning it was stolen is a later assumption of the owner's rights.","He is guilty, because stolen property always continues to belong to the original owner.","He is not guilty, because he became the legal owner of the bike when he paid a fair price.","He is not guilty, unless Mrs Davies has asked him to return the bike to her and he refused.","He is not guilty, because a good-faith buyer for value does not steal by later keeping it."],"a":4,"e":"A **later assumption** of the owner's rights by someone who first came by property innocently is normally an appropriation (s.3(1) Theft Act 1968), which is why keeping the bike looks like theft. But **section 3(2)** provides that a person who acquires property **in good faith and for value** does not steal it by later keeping or dealing with it, even after discovering the seller had no right to sell. Tobias does not become the legal owner, since Mrs Davies still owns the bike in civil law, and no demand for its return is needed."},{"t":"theft","p":"FLK2","q":"Anwen picks several large bags of wild garlic and wild mushrooms growing in a privately owned woodland and sells them to a local restaurant. Her friend Rhiannon picks a small bag of wild blackberries in the same woodland to eat at home. Neither has the landowner's permission to be there or to pick anything.\n\nWhich of the following best describes their liability for theft?","o":["Only Anwen may be guilty, because she picked the wild plants for sale.","Both may be guilty, because the plants belong to the landowner and were taken without consent.","Neither is guilty, because things growing wild can never be stolen in any circumstances.","Only Rhiannon may be guilty, because she took fruit rather than foliage or fungi.","Both may be guilty, because neither had the landowner's permission to enter the woodland."],"a":0,"e":"Under **section 4(3) Theft Act 1968**, a person who picks **mushrooms, flowers, fruit or foliage from a plant growing wild** does not steal them **unless** it is done **for reward, sale or other commercial purpose**. Anwen picked for sale, so the exception does not protect her, while Rhiannon picked for her own use. The lack of permission to pick or to enter the land does not override section 4(3), and the rule treats fruit, fungi and foliage alike."},{"t":"robbery-burglary","p":"FLK2","q":"Bradley and Jess break into Mrs Okoro's home at night. Jess goes upstairs, takes a jewellery box and puts it in a holdall. A few minutes later, as they are about to leave with the holdall, Mrs Okoro comes into the hallway. Bradley pushes her into a cupboard and locks the door so that they can get away with the jewellery.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether they are guilty of robbery?","o":["They are not, because the theft was complete when Jess put the jewellery box in the holdall.","They are not, because force used after the property has been picked up can only be an assault.","They may be, because the appropriation can be a continuing act and force was used in order to steal.","They are not, because force used to escape from a building turns the offence into burglary instead.","They may be, but only Bradley, because Jess did not use or threaten any force herself."],"a":2,"e":"Robbery requires force **immediately before or at the time of** stealing and **in order to** steal (s.8 Theft Act 1968). **Appropriation can be a continuing act**, and the jury may decide that the theft was still going on while the defendants were in the house with the goods (Hale), so the theft was not necessarily complete when the box went into the holdall. Jess can be liable as a joint principal or accessory to the robbery if it was part of their joint plan, so the force need not be her own."},{"t":"robbery-burglary","p":"FLK2","q":"Ryan, who is homeless, climbs through an open window of an empty holiday cottage looking for somewhere to sleep. He has no intention of taking or damaging anything. Once inside, he becomes angry when he cannot get the heating to work and smashes the television and a mirror. He then leaves, taking nothing.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ryan's liability for burglary?","o":["He is guilty under section 9(1)(a), because he entered as a trespasser and then caused damage.","He is guilty under section 9(1)(b), because he committed an offence inside after entering as a trespasser.","He is not guilty, because an empty holiday cottage is not a building for the purposes of burglary.","He is not guilty, because criminal damage committed after entry is not an offence covered by section 9(1)(b).","He is guilty under section 9(1)(b), because he was reckless as to damaging property once inside."],"a":3,"e":"**Section 9(1)(a)** requires an **intent at the time of entry** to steal, inflict grievous bodily harm or do unlawful damage, which Ryan did not have. **Section 9(1)(b)** covers only **stealing or inflicting (or attempting) grievous bodily harm** after a trespassory entry, and **criminal damage is not on that list**. So Ryan commits criminal damage but not burglary, and the cottage plainly is a building, empty or not."},{"t":"criminal-damage","p":"FLK2","q":"During a protest, Ines sprays slogans in water-based paint on the paving outside the town hall. The paint would eventually wear off in the rain, but the council pays a contractor £400 to jet-wash it off the next morning. On the same day, Bryn chalks a hopscotch grid on the same paving. It disappears in the next shower and costs nothing to remove.\n\nWhich of the following best describes their liability for criminal damage?","o":["Both are guilty, because each deliberately altered the appearance of property belonging to another.","Ines may be guilty, because the cost and effort needed to remove temporary marks can make them damage.","Neither is guilty, because neither caused any permanent harm to the paving stones.","Ines is guilty only if the paint caused lasting harm to the surface of the paving.","Neither is guilty, because paving forms part of the land and cannot itself be damaged."],"a":1,"e":"**Damage** is a matter of fact and degree: it includes **temporary** impairment of value or usefulness, and marks can be damage where **expense or effort is needed to remove them** (Hardman). The council had to pay £400 to clean off Ines's paint, so she may be guilty even though the harm was not permanent. Bryn's chalk vanished at no cost, so it is unlikely to count, and land is property that can be damaged under the Criminal Damage Act 1971."},{"t":"criminal-damage","p":"FLK2","q":"After drinking eight pints at a wedding reception, Nathan goes outside and starts kicking a football hard against the wall of the hotel car park. One kick sends the ball into the window of a guest's car, which shatters. Nathan is charged with criminal damage on the basis that he was reckless. The jury accept that, because of the drink, he did not realise there was any risk of damaging the car, but that he would have realised it if sober.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Nathan is guilty?","o":["He is not guilty, because recklessness requires that he was himself aware of the risk of damage.","He is not guilty, because he honestly believed that the car would not be damaged by his kicking.","He is guilty only if the prosecution prove that he intended the ball to strike the car.","He is not guilty, because intoxication negates mens rea for any offence of criminal damage.","He is guilty, because voluntary intoxication is no defence where recklessness suffices."],"a":4,"e":"Recklessness is normally **subjective**: the defendant must have been aware of the risk (R v G). But criminal damage charged as **reckless** is a **basic intent** offence, and **voluntary intoxication is no defence** to it (Majewski), so Nathan is treated as if he had foreseen what he would have foreseen when sober. There is no need to prove intent, and the special rule allowing a drunken belief in consent under section 5(2)(a) Criminal Damage Act 1971 does not cover a mere failure to see a risk."},{"t":"murder","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2021 Callum attacked Luke with a hammer, intending to kill him. Luke survived with severe brain injuries and was cared for in a nursing home. In 2026 Luke died, and medical evidence shows that the head injuries were still a substantial cause of his death. Callum has never been prosecuted for the attack.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Callum can now be prosecuted for murder?","o":["He can, but proceedings require the consent of the Attorney General.","He cannot, because Luke died more than a year and a day after the attack.","He can, but only for attempted murder, because Luke survived the attack itself.","He cannot, because more than three years passed between the injury and the death.","He can, without any further consent, because there is no time limit for murder."],"a":0,"e":"The old rule that death must occur within **a year and a day** was **abolished** by the Law Reform (Year and a Day Rule) Act 1996, so Callum can be prosecuted for murder as the injuries still caused Luke's death. However, the **Attorney General's consent** is needed where death occurs **more than three years** after the injury. The three-year period is therefore a consent requirement, not a bar, and murder is available because the victim has now died."},{"t":"voluntary-manslaughter","p":"FLK2","q":"Saira's husband, Gavin, has beaten her badly several times. One night during an argument he picks up a heavy glass ashtray, steps towards her and says he is going to 'cave her head in'. Terrified, Saira loses her self-control, grabs a kitchen knife and stabs him repeatedly, killing him. The jury are likely to find the force she used unreasonable, so self-defence will fail.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the partial defence of loss of control is available to Saira?","o":["It is not, because fear of violence is relevant only to the defence of self-defence.","It is not, because a loss of control must be caused by things said or done that are gravely wrong.","It may be, but only if Saira proves each element on the balance of probabilities.","It may be, because a fear of serious violence from the victim is a qualifying trigger.","It is not, because a defendant who uses excessive force in self-defence is guilty of murder."],"a":3,"e":"Under **section 55(3) Coroners and Justice Act 2009**, a **fear of serious violence from the victim** against the defendant or another identified person is a **qualifying trigger**, and it is designed for cases like this where self-defence fails because the force was excessive. A gravely wrongful act is an alternative trigger, not a requirement. Once there is sufficient evidence of loss of control, **the prosecution must disprove it** beyond reasonable doubt, so Saira does not bear the burden of proof."},{"t":"voluntary-manslaughter","p":"FLK2","q":"Ethan, aged 17, has been bullied for months by an older youth, Dale, who has posted humiliating videos of him online. Ethan has always had an unusually short temper. When Dale mocks Ethan's recently dead mother in front of a crowd, Ethan loses his self-control and fatally stabs him with a knife he happened to be carrying. Charged with murder, he relies on loss of control. Assume a qualifying trigger is found.\n\nWhich of the following questions should the jury ask on the third element of the defence?","o":["Whether a reasonable adult with ordinary self-control, in Ethan's circumstances, would have reacted the same way.","Whether a 17-year-old with normal tolerance and self-restraint, in Ethan's circumstances, might have reacted similarly.","Whether a 17-year-old sharing Ethan's short temper, in Ethan's circumstances, might have reacted similarly.","Whether a 17-year-old with normal self-restraint might have reacted similarly to the final insult alone.","Whether Ethan himself genuinely lost his self-control, whatever a normal person might have done."],"a":1,"e":"Under **section 54(1)(c) Coroners and Justice Act 2009** the jury ask whether **a person of the defendant's sex and age, with a normal degree of tolerance and self-restraint and in the circumstances of the defendant**, might have reacted in the same or a similar way. The **circumstances** include the history of bullying, but not characteristics whose only relevance is to reduce the defendant's **general capacity for tolerance or self-restraint**, such as a short temper. The test uses a person of Ethan's age, not an adult, and the defendant's genuine loss of control is the first element, not the third."},{"t":"involuntary-manslaughter","p":"FLK2","q":"Kayleigh's two-year-old son develops a high fever and becomes listless. Worried that social services will become involved if she takes him to hospital, she does nothing for three days despite his obvious deterioration. He dies of meningitis, which prompt treatment would almost certainly have cured.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the form of manslaughter that Kayleigh may be convicted of?","o":["Unlawful act manslaughter, because wilful neglect of a child is a criminal offence in itself.","Unlawful act manslaughter, because a reasonable person would have recognised the risk of harm.","Gross negligence manslaughter, because she grossly breached her duty of care to him.","No form of manslaughter, because she did no positive act that caused her son's death.","Gross negligence manslaughter, but only if she herself foresaw that her son might die."],"a":2,"e":"Unlawful act manslaughter needs an **act**, and **cannot be based on an omission**, even an omission that is itself a crime such as wilful neglect (Lowe). **Gross negligence manslaughter** can be committed by omission where there is a **duty of care**, as a parent has towards a young child, breached in a way that the jury find **gross**, causing death. The test of foreseeability is **objective** (a reasonably prudent person would have foreseen a serious and obvious risk of death), so Kayleigh need not have foreseen death herself."},{"t":"involuntary-manslaughter","p":"FLK2","q":"Keeley wants revenge on her former landlord. At 3am she sets fire to the wheelie bins stored in the ground-floor entrance hall of his block of flats, intending only to damage his property. Smoke spreads up the stairwell and a tenant, Mr Nowak, dies of smoke inhalation. Keeley says she never thought the fire would spread beyond the bins.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Keeley's liability for unlawful act manslaughter?","o":["She is not liable, because her unlawful act was aimed at property rather than at any person.","She is not liable, because she did not herself foresee any risk of harm to anyone.","She is liable only if she foresaw that the fire might cause serious harm to a tenant.","She is not liable, because criminal damage is not a serious enough unlawful act.","She is liable, because arson was a dangerous unlawful act that caused Mr Nowak's death."],"a":4,"e":"Unlawful act manslaughter requires an **unlawful (criminal) act** that is **dangerous**, meaning that **a sober and reasonable person** would inevitably recognise the risk of **some physical harm**, and that causes death. Arson is a criminal act, and setting fire to the entrance of an occupied block of flats at night is plainly dangerous on that **objective** test. The act need **not be aimed at a person** (Goodfellow), and Keeley's own failure to foresee harm is irrelevant."},{"t":"fraud","p":"FLK2","q":"Kieran climbs over a perimeter fence into a music festival without buying the £180 ticket and watches the headline acts all weekend. He knows that tickets must be bought in advance and never intends to pay. Nobody speaks to him or asks to see his wristband.\n\nWhich of the following offences has Kieran most clearly committed?","o":["Obtaining services dishonestly, because he obtained the services by a dishonest act, intending not to pay.","Fraud by false representation, because his presence impliedly represented that he held a ticket.","Theft, because he appropriated the value of the performances, which belonged to the organisers.","Fraud by failing to disclose information, because he did not tell the organisers he had not paid.","No offence, because nobody was deceived and no property was taken from anyone."],"a":0,"e":"**Obtaining services dishonestly (s.11 Fraud Act 2006)** is committed where the defendant **obtains services by a dishonest act**, knowing they are to be paid for, without paying, intending not to pay; it needs no deception, only that the services are actually obtained. Climbing the fence was the dishonest act. Kieran made no representation to anyone, **services are not property** that can be stolen, and he owed no **legal duty** to disclose information, so the other offences do not fit."},{"t":"fraud","p":"FLK2","q":"Lewis buys a watch at a car boot sale for £40. He suspects it might be a fake, but without checking he lists it online as 'a genuine Rolex Submariner' for £6,000. Mrs Kaur pays the asking price. An expert later confirms the watch is a fake. Lewis says that he honestly was not sure either way.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Lewis is guilty of fraud by false representation?","o":["He is not guilty, because he did not know for certain that the watch was a fake.","He is not guilty, because a description in an online listing is mere sales talk, not a representation.","He is guilty, because the offence is one of strict liability once a false statement is made.","He is guilty if dishonest, because it is enough that he knew the description might be untrue.","He is not guilty, because Mrs Kaur could have had the watch checked before paying."],"a":3,"e":"Under **section 2(2) Fraud Act 2006** a representation is false if it is untrue or misleading and the defendant **knows that it is, or might be**, untrue or misleading, so **awareness of the possibility** is enough and certainty is not needed. Describing the watch as genuine is a clear statement of fact. The offence is not strict liability, since dishonesty and an intention to gain must still be proved, and the victim's failure to check is irrelevant because no one even needs to be deceived."},{"t":"general-defences","p":"FLK2","q":"After a heavy evening's drinking, Jamal is walking home when a man, Pete, jogs up behind him and taps him on the shoulder to return a wallet Jamal has dropped. Because of the drink, Jamal wrongly believes he is about to be mugged and punches Pete, breaking his jaw. A sober person would have realised that Pete posed no threat.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Jamal can rely on self-defence?","o":["He can, because self-defence is judged on the facts as he honestly believed them to be.","He can, but only to reduce a specific intent offence to the basic intent equivalent.","He cannot, because a mistaken belief caused by voluntary intoxication cannot be relied on.","He cannot, because self-defence is never available to a defendant who has made a mistake.","He can, if the punch was a proportionate response to a mugging as he believed it to be."],"a":2,"e":"Normally self-defence is judged on the facts **as the defendant honestly believed them**, even if the belief was mistaken or unreasonable, so the defence is not lost merely because of a mistake. But under **section 76(5) Criminal Justice and Immigration Act 2008**, the defendant **cannot rely on a mistaken belief attributable to voluntary intoxication** (O'Grady). Jamal's belief that he was being mugged arose from the drink, so whether his response was proportionate to the imagined threat does not matter."},{"t":"general-defences","p":"FLK2","q":"Oscar decides to kill his business partner, Ross, who he believes has cheated him. He buys a knife, then drinks most of a bottle of vodka to steady his nerves before going to Ross's flat, where he stabs him to death. Medical evidence suggests that, by the time of the stabbing, Oscar may have been too drunk to form any intention.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of Oscar's intoxication on the charge of murder?","o":["It reduces murder to manslaughter, because murder is a specific intent offence.","It is no defence, because he formed the intention to kill while sober and drank to carry it out.","It is a complete defence, because he lacked the intent at the moment of the killing.","It reduces murder to manslaughter only if the drink caused an abnormality of mental functioning.","It is no defence, because intoxication can never be relevant to a charge of murder."],"a":1,"e":"A defendant who **forms the intent while sober and then drinks for 'Dutch courage'** to carry it out **cannot rely on intoxication**, even for a specific intent offence such as murder (Gallagher). Without that feature, voluntary intoxication negating the intent for murder would reduce the offence to manslaughter, so it is wrong to say that intoxication is never relevant to murder. Voluntary intoxication is not itself an abnormality of mental functioning for diminished responsibility."},{"t":"parties","p":"FLK2","q":"Hamza and his friend Josh are walking through a park when Hamza spots Craig, a man who owes him money. Hamza walks over and punches Craig repeatedly, breaking his cheekbone. Josh stays where he is, about ten metres away, says nothing, does nothing to intervene and leaves with Hamza afterwards. There is no evidence of any plan between them.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Josh's liability as an accessory?","o":["He is not liable unless his presence actually encouraged Hamza and he intended it to do so.","He is liable, because he was present and failed to take any steps to stop the attack.","He is liable, because leaving with Hamza afterwards showed approval of what was done.","He is liable, because anyone present at the scene of a crime is treated as a joint principal.","He is not liable, because an accessory must always have agreed to the crime in advance."],"a":0,"e":"**Mere presence** at the scene, even with a failure to intervene, is **not enough** for secondary liability unless the presence **actually encourages** the principal and the defendant **intends** it to (Clarkson). There is no general duty to stop a crime, and leaving together afterwards does not in itself assist or encourage an offence that has already been committed. Prior agreement is not essential, since spontaneous assistance or encouragement can be enough."},{"t":"attempts","p":"FLK2","q":"Gemma decides to set fire to her ex-partner's car. At 2am she stuffs a petrol-soaked rag under its rear wheel arch and takes out a lighter. Hearing a dog bark nearby, she has second thoughts, puts the lighter back in her pocket, pulls out the rag and goes home. CCTV records everything.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Gemma's liability for attempted arson?","o":["She is not guilty, because she voluntarily abandoned her plan before any damage was caused.","She is not guilty, because her acts were mere preparation until she actually lit the lighter.","She is guilty only of having an article with intent to damage property.","She is guilty, because she went beyond mere preparation and withdrawal is no defence.","She is not guilty, because no part of the car ever actually caught fire."],"a":3,"e":"An attempt is committed when, **with intent**, the defendant does an act **more than merely preparatory** to the offence (s.1(1) Criminal Attempts Act 1981), meaning she has embarked on the crime proper. Placing a petrol-soaked rag under the car and taking out a lighter is likely to be beyond preparation, even though the final act of lighting it was not done. Once the attempt is complete, **there is no defence of withdrawal**, so her change of heart and the absence of any fire do not help her."}],"crimprac-p1":[{"t":"police-station-detention","p":"FLK2","q":"Callum Reid is arrested at 13:40 on Wednesday on suspicion of burglary of a warehouse, an either-way offence. He arrives at the police station at 14:15 and the custody officer authorises his detention at 14:45. At 11:00 on Thursday, after the second review, a superintendent authorises his continued detention, being satisfied that the investigation is being conducted diligently and expeditiously and that further detention is necessary. The police do not apply to the magistrates' court.\n\nBy what time must Callum be charged or released?","o":["14:15 on Thursday","01:40 on Friday","02:15 on Friday","02:45 on Friday","11:00 on Friday"],"a":2,"e":"The detention clock runs from the **relevant time**, which here is the time of **arrival at the first police station** (14:15), not the time of arrest or the time detention was authorised. A superintendent may extend detention for an indictable offence (which includes an either-way offence such as burglary) **up to 36 hours from the relevant time**, so the limit is 02:15 on Friday. 14:15 on Thursday is the basic 24-hour limit that the extension has displaced, and the 14:45 authorisation time is used only to time reviews; any longer detention would need a **warrant of further detention** from the magistrates."},{"t":"police-station-detention","p":"FLK2","q":"Priya Desai is arrested on suspicion of robbery and taken to the police station, where she asks to speak to a solicitor and for her brother to be told of her arrest. An inspector authorises a delay on both requests, recording that he has reasonable grounds to believe that contact with either person would lead to a second suspect, who has not yet been arrested, being alerted. Priya has not been charged.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the lawfulness of the inspector's decisions?","o":["Informing her brother may lawfully be delayed, but only a superintendent can delay her access to a solicitor.","Both delays are lawful, because robbery is an indictable offence and the inspector has recorded his grounds.","Neither delay is lawful, because a suspect's rights cannot be delayed before an interview has taken place.","Her access to a solicitor may lawfully be delayed, but only a superintendent can delay informing her brother.","Both delays are lawful, but each must end within 24 hours of her arrival at the police station."],"a":0,"e":"**An inspector** may delay the right to have someone informed, but delaying **access to legal advice** needs the authority of **a superintendent**. Both require an indictable offence, no charge yet, reasonable grounds to fear a listed harm (such as alerting other suspects) and a maximum delay of **36 hours** from the relevant time. Option B wrongly treats the inspector as able to authorise both, option D reverses the ranks, and option E gives the wrong time limit; a wrongful refusal of legal advice risks the exclusion of a later interview under s.78 PACE."},{"t":"police-interview","p":"FLK2","q":"Owen Mbeki is interviewed under caution about an assault outside a bar. The police disclose only that a witness saw him strike the victim. His solicitor advises him to make no comment because of the limited disclosure, and Owen does so, saying on the recording that he is following legal advice. At his trial he gives evidence that he punched the victim only after the victim came at him with a bottle.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether an adverse inference may be drawn from Owen's silence in interview?","o":["No inference may be drawn, because Owen stated on the recording that he was acting on legal advice.","No inference may be drawn, because the police gave only limited disclosure before the interview.","An inference must be drawn, because self-defence is a fact Owen could reasonably have mentioned.","The jury may draw an inference unless they accept he genuinely and reasonably relied on the advice.","An inference may be drawn only if Owen waives privilege by explaining the reasons for the advice."],"a":3,"e":"Under **s.34 CJPOA 1994**, an inference may be drawn where the defendant relies at trial on a fact (here, self-defence) that he could reasonably have been expected to mention when questioned. Silence on legal advice **does not automatically prevent** an inference: the jury must decide whether the defendant **genuinely and reasonably relied** on the advice or used it as a shield because he had no answer that would stand up to questioning (R v Hoare; R v Beckles). Limited disclosure is relevant to that question but is not a bar, an inference is never compulsory, and waiving privilege is a choice Owen may make to explain his silence, not a precondition of an inference."},{"t":"police-interview","p":"FLK2","q":"Freya Lindqvist, aged 15, is arrested on suspicion of criminal damage to a bus shelter. Her father attends the police station as her appropriate adult, and Freya's solicitor arrives shortly afterwards. Before the interview, Freya tells the solicitor that she does not want her father in the room while she discusses the allegation with the solicitor. Her father insists that he is entitled to be present at all times.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Her father must be present for the consultation, because an appropriate adult must attend all dealings with a child.","Freya may consult her solicitor in private without her father, but he must be present for the interview.","Freya may consult privately only if the custody officer agrees that her father's presence would be unhelpful.","Her father may be excluded from both the consultation and the interview, because Freya objects to him.","Freya's solicitor should take over as her appropriate adult so that her father can be released from the role."],"a":1,"e":"A child may consult a solicitor **in private without the appropriate adult** present (Code C Note 1E), and conversations with an appropriate adult are not protected by legal professional privilege, which is a good reason for Freya to speak to the solicitor alone. The appropriate adult **must**, however, be present for the **interview**, so option D goes too far; a child's objection matters only where the parent is estranged. No custody officer approval is needed for a private consultation, and a solicitor present in that capacity can never act as the appropriate adult."},{"t":"identification","p":"FLK2","q":"Tomasz Nowak is arrested on suspicion of a street robbery. The victim told the police that the robber had a noticeable scar across his left eyebrow, and Tomasz has such a scar. Tomasz denies being involved, and the identification officer arranges a video identification. Tomasz's solicitor is concerned that Tomasz will be the only person in the images with a scar.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the identification officer should deal with the scar?","o":["Hold an identification parade instead, because video identification is unsuitable for a suspect with a scar.","Arrange a confrontation instead, because no set of images can fairly reflect such a distinctive feature.","Proceed with the images as they are, and tell the victim that the robber may or may not be in the set.","Proceed only if at least twelve other people are shown, so that the scar is less likely to stand out.","Conceal the scar on Tomasz's image or replicate it on all the other images, so that he does not stand out."],"a":4,"e":"Under **Code D Annex A**, where the suspect has a **distinctive feature** such as a scar or tattoo, it may be **concealed or replicated** on the images so that the suspect does not stand out, and the solicitor must be given the chance to see the complete set and object. Video identification remains the normal first choice, and a confrontation is a last resort used only where no other procedure is practicable. Twelve others are needed only where two suspects of similar appearance are shown together; the usual minimum is eight, and numbers alone would not stop the scar singling him out."},{"t":"bail","p":"FLK2","q":"Mohammed Rahman is on conditional bail awaiting trial in the Crown Court for burglary of a dwelling. One condition is a curfew between 21:00 and 06:00 at his home address. Police officers carrying out a curfew check at 23:30 find that he is not at home, and they arrest him the following morning. He says he was visiting his sick grandmother.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the consequences of the breach?","o":["He must be brought before a magistrate within 24 hours, who may remand him in custody or grant bail again.","He commits the offence of failing to surrender, which is punishable by up to 3 months' imprisonment.","He must be brought before the Crown Court within 48 hours, which must then decide whether to forfeit his bail.","He commits an offence of breaching bail, but may be bailed again on the same conditions if he pleads guilty.","He must be remanded in custody until trial, because a defendant who breaks a curfew loses the right to bail."],"a":0,"e":"Breaching a bail condition is **not an offence** in itself: under **s.7(3) Bail Act 1976** the police may arrest without warrant a defendant reasonably believed to have broken a condition, and he must be brought before a **magistrate within 24 hours**. The magistrate decides whether a condition has been broken and, if so, may **remand him in custody** or grant bail again on the same or stricter conditions; nothing makes custody automatic. The **s.6** offence of failing to surrender covers failing to attend court, which he has not done, though the breach will count against him in later bail decisions."},{"t":"bail","p":"FLK2","q":"Hannah Brooks is charged with assault occasioning actual bodily harm and is granted bail by the magistrates with conditions of residence at her home address and reporting to her local police station every day. Hannah has just started a job that involves shift work, and she says that daily reporting will make it impossible to keep the job. She wants to challenge the reporting condition.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate course of action?","o":["Appeal to the Crown Court against the condition, relying on a certificate of full argument.","Appeal to the High Court against the condition by way of case stated from the magistrates.","Apply to the magistrates' court to vary the condition, explaining her new shift pattern.","Appeal to the Crown Court against the condition under the Criminal Justice Act 2003.","Apply to the High Court for judicial review of the decision to impose the condition."],"a":2,"e":"Either party may apply to the magistrates to **vary** bail conditions. A defence appeal to the Crown Court under **s.16 Criminal Justice Act 2003** is available only for certain conditions (residence away from an area, a surety or security, curfew, electronic monitoring and non-contact), so a **reporting** condition must be challenged by applying to the magistrates, as option D overlooks. A certificate of full argument is needed for an appeal against a **refusal** of bail, and neither case stated nor judicial review is the appropriate route for a condition like this."},{"t":"first-hearings","p":"FLK2","q":"Leah Fitzgerald, aged 38, receives universal credit and is charged with common assault after a fight at a house party. She has no previous convictions and the offence is unlikely to lead to custody. She says two other guests saw the victim attack her first, but she knows them only by their first names and has no contact details for them. She speaks fluent English and works part time as a receptionist.\n\nWhich of the following is the strongest ground for arguing that the interests of justice require Leah to have a representation order?","o":["She is likely to lose her liberty if she is convicted of the offence.","She is likely to lose her livelihood if she is convicted of the offence.","She may be unable to understand the proceedings or state her own case.","Her case involves the tracing and interviewing of defence witnesses.","It is in the interests of another person that she is legally represented."],"a":3,"e":"The **interests of justice** test (LASPO 2012 s.17(2)) is met if one or more of the listed factors applies, and the one that fits these facts is the need to **trace and interview witnesses for the defence**, which she cannot realistically do herself. Custody is unlikely, nothing suggests a conviction would cost her a receptionist job, and she speaks fluent English, so options A to C do not fit; option E is aimed at cases where the defendant would otherwise cross-examine a child or sexual offence complainant. She passes the **means test** automatically because she receives universal credit (she is 'passported')."},{"t":"allocation-sending","p":"FLK2","q":"Kieran Walsh, aged 29, appears before the magistrates charged with two offences of theft from the same supermarket on different days. The goods taken in the first theft were worth £120 and in the second £110. He has not yet indicated a plea to either offence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the magistrates' court should deal with the offences?","o":["Treat both thefts as summary offences, but ask him whether he wishes to elect trial in the Crown Court.","Treat both thefts as either way, taking his plea before venue and, if needed, deciding allocation.","Treat both thefts as summary offences and try them summarily, as he has no right to elect jury trial.","Send both thefts to the Crown Court forthwith, because their combined value is more than £200.","Treat each theft as a separate summary offence, because each is worth less than £200 on its own."],"a":1,"e":"Low-value shoplifting (**s.22A Magistrates' Courts Act 1980**) is treated as a summary offence only where the value is **£200 or less**, using the **aggregate** value where several offences are charged together. Here the aggregate is £230, so the thefts are ordinary **either-way** offences and the court follows the usual order of **plea before venue** and then **allocation**. Option A describes low-value shoplifting, where the defendant may still elect, option E ignores aggregation, and option D is wrong because only indictable-only offences are sent forthwith."},{"t":"case-management-disclosure","p":"FLK2","q":"Ruth Okafor is charged with assault by beating and will be tried in the magistrates' court. The prosecution has made initial disclosure, which does not include CCTV footage from a shop opposite the scene that Ruth believes shows the complainant striking the first blow. She has not served a defence statement. Her solicitor wants the court to order the prosecution to disclose the footage.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the advice the solicitor should give?","o":["Apply now for a disclosure order, because the prosecution must disclose anything that might assist the defence.","Apply now for a disclosure order, because a defence statement is only voluntary in the magistrates' court.","Do not apply, because the prosecution's duty of disclosure ended once it made initial disclosure.","Do not apply, because orders for specific disclosure can be made only in the Crown Court.","Serve a defence statement first, because an application for specific disclosure can only follow one."],"a":4,"e":"An application under **s.8 CPIA 1996** for specific disclosure can be made **only after a defence statement has been served**. In the magistrates' court the statement is voluntary (within **14 days** of initial disclosure), but serving one is necessary for a s.8 application and also prompts the prosecution's review under its **continuing duty** of disclosure (s.7A), so option C is wrong. The trade-off is that any later departure from the statement can attract comment and adverse inferences under s.11."},{"t":"evidence-identification-silence","p":"FLK2","q":"Sasha Petrova is on trial in the Crown Court for robbery. The only evidence against her is that of the victim, who picked her out at a properly conducted video identification. In cross-examination the victim accepts that the robbery happened at night in an unlit alley, that she saw the robber's face for about two seconds as the robber ran past, and that the robber wore a hood. There is no other evidence linking Sasha to the robbery.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the judge should do if the defence submits at the close of the prosecution case that there is no case to answer?","o":["Withdraw the case from the jury, because the identification is poor and there is no supporting evidence.","Leave the case to the jury with a full warning, because the weight of identification is a jury matter.","Leave the case to the jury, because the identification was made at a properly conducted procedure.","Withdraw the case only if the defence shows that the video identification procedure breached Code D.","Leave the case to the jury, but direct them that they may not convict on the victim's evidence alone."],"a":0,"e":"Under the **Turnbull guidelines**, where the case depends wholly or substantially on a disputed identification of **poor quality** (for example a fleeting glimpse in difficult conditions) and there is **no other evidence supporting** it, the judge should **withdraw the case** from the jury and direct an acquittal. A Turnbull warning is enough only where the identification is of good quality or is supported, so option B is tempting but wrong. A properly run Code D procedure shows the witness picked out the defendant, but it cannot cure the poor conditions of the original sighting."},{"t":"evidence-identification-silence","p":"FLK2","q":"Imran Siddiqui is arrested near a burgled house. He has a fresh cut on his hand and fragments of glass on his jacket. In interview, with his solicitor present and after a proper caution but without any special warning, he is asked to explain the cut and the glass and declines to answer. At trial he gives evidence that he cut himself at work earlier that day.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the adverse inferences available to the jury?","o":["An inference may be drawn under s.36 for his failure to account for the cut and the glass, as he was arrested.","No inference of any kind may be drawn, because he was not given a special warning in the interview.","No inference may be drawn under s.36, but one may be drawn under s.34 for not mentioning his explanation.","Inferences may be drawn under both s.34 and s.36, because the special warning is only good practice.","No inference may be drawn under s.34, because s.36 is the only provision dealing with marks on a suspect."],"a":2,"e":"A **s.36 CJPOA 1994** inference for failing to account for an object, substance or **mark** requires a **special warning** in ordinary language, so none is available here. **s.34** has no special warning requirement: Imran relied at trial on a fact (cutting himself at work) that he could reasonably have been expected to mention when questioned, so the jury may draw an inference under s.34. Option B overlooks s.34, and option D wrongly treats the special warning as optional."},{"t":"hearsay-confessions","p":"FLK2","q":"Grace Mensah witnessed a stabbing and gave a signed statement to the police identifying the defendant. Two weeks before the trial she tells the officer in the case that she will not attend court because she is frightened of reprisals from the defendant's friends; there is no evidence that the defendant himself has threatened her. The prosecution wishes to read her statement to the jury.\n\nWhich of the following best describes when her statement may be admitted?","o":["Automatically, because a witness in fear is one of the statutory reasons for being unavailable.","Only if the prosecution proves that the defendant himself was responsible for causing her fear.","Not at all, because a witness who is in the UK and fit to attend must give oral evidence.","With the court's leave, if admission is in the interests of justice, considering special measures.","Only if the defence consents, because fear is not a recognised ground for admitting hearsay."],"a":3,"e":"Under **s.116(2)(e) CJA 2003**, the statement of a witness who does not give oral evidence **through fear** can be admitted, but **only with the court's leave**, given if it is in the **interests of justice**; the court must consider, among other things, whether **special measures** (such as screens or a live link) would allow her to testify. Fear is widely construed and need not be caused by the defendant; option B confuses this with s.116(5), which stops a party who caused the unavailability relying on it. Option A ignores the leave requirement, which applies to fear alone among the five reasons."},{"t":"hearsay-confessions","p":"FLK2","q":"Lewis Grant, aged 19, has a mild learning disability that was obvious to the custody officer. He is interviewed about a burglary without an appropriate adult or a solicitor. Near the end of the interview an officer tells him that if he admits the burglary he will be released on bail that evening to see his girlfriend, and Lewis then confesses. The defence accepts that the officers did not shout at or threaten Lewis.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the court's approach if the defence represents that the confession was obtained improperly?","o":["It has a discretion under s.78 PACE to exclude the confession, but only because no appropriate adult was present.","It must exclude it unless the prosecution proves beyond reasonable doubt that s.76(2)(b) does not apply.","It must exclude the confession only if the defence proves on the balance of probabilities that it is untrue.","It must admit the confession, because the absence of shouting or threats means there was no oppression.","It may admit the confession if other evidence shows that it is true, as reliability goes only to its weight."],"a":1,"e":"Under **s.76(2)(b) PACE**, once the defence represents that a confession may have been obtained in consequence of something said or done likely to render it **unreliable**, the court **must exclude** it unless the **prosecution proves beyond reasonable doubt** that it was not so obtained. A promise of bail made to a vulnerable suspect interviewed without an appropriate adult is a classic example, and it **does not matter whether the confession is true**, so options C and E are wrong. The lack of threats only defeats an oppression argument under s.76(2)(a), and s.78 is a fallback discretion, not the primary route."},{"t":"bad-character","p":"FLK2","q":"Nadia Haddad and Ben Carter are jointly tried for robbery. Each gives evidence blaming the other: Ben says Nadia planned the robbery and used the knife, and Nadia says Ben forced her to take part. Nadia's counsel applies to adduce Ben's two previous convictions for perjury and fraud to show he is likely to be lying. Ben's counsel asks the judge to exclude them on the ground that admitting them would be unfair.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the judge's position?","o":["The judge must exclude the convictions if admitting them would have an adverse effect on the fairness of the trial.","The judge may admit the convictions only if the prosecution applies to adduce them through the propensity gateway.","The judge must exclude the convictions, because a co-defendant cannot rely on convictions for dishonesty.","The judge may admit the convictions only if Ben has attacked the character of a prosecution witness.","The judge may admit the convictions, as Ben's defence undermines Nadia's, and cannot exclude them as unfair."],"a":4,"e":"Under **gateway (e) (s.101(1)(e) and s.104 CJA 2003)**, a co-defendant may adduce evidence of the defendant's **propensity to be untruthful** where the nature or conduct of the defendant's defence **undermines the co-defendant's defence**, as in this cut-throat case. The court has **no discretion** to exclude gateway (e) evidence under s.101(3), which applies only to gateways (d) and (g) on the defendant's application, so option A is wrong. Gateway (e) belongs to co-defendants alone, so the prosecution's position and any attack on a witness are irrelevant."},{"t":"bad-character","p":"FLK2","q":"Jordan Hughes is charged with possession of cocaine with intent to supply after drugs were found in his car. His defence is that a named police officer planted the drugs because of a grudge from an earlier arrest, and his counsel puts this to the officer in cross-examination. Jordan has three previous convictions for theft, the most recent five years ago. The prosecution applies to adduce the convictions.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the admissibility of Jordan's convictions?","o":["They may be admitted because he has attacked another person's character, unless admitting them would be unfair.","They are inadmissible, because convictions for theft do not show a propensity to commit drug offences.","They may be admitted only if Jordan gives evidence himself and repeats his allegation against the officer.","They are inadmissible, because an allegation against a police officer is a denial, not an attack on character.","They may be admitted automatically, and the court has no power to exclude them once the gateway is opened."],"a":0,"e":"Alleging that a police officer **planted evidence** is an attack on another person's character, opening **gateway (g)** (s.101(1)(g) and s.106 CJA 2003), and putting the allegation in cross-examination is enough without Jordan giving evidence. The convictions go mainly to his credibility, so it does not matter that thefts show no propensity to supply drugs. On Jordan's application the court **must exclude** gateway (g) evidence if admitting it would have **such an adverse effect on the fairness** of the proceedings that it ought not to be admitted (s.101(3)), having regard to the time that has passed, so option E is wrong."},{"t":"trial-procedure","p":"FLK2","q":"Aaron Clarke is charged with assault occasioning actual bodily harm on a 15-year-old boy who lives next door. Aaron's civil partner, Simon, saw the incident from their front window. Simon has told the police that he does not want to give evidence against Aaron. Nobody else is charged in connection with the incident.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Simon's position as a prosecution witness?","o":["He is competent but not compellable, because he is the civil partner of the defendant.","He is neither competent nor compellable, because he is the civil partner of the defendant.","He is competent and compellable, because the assault was on a person under 16.","He is competent and compellable, but only if he is no longer living with the defendant.","He is competent and compellable only if the victim's parents consent to his being called."],"a":2,"e":"A defendant's spouse or civil partner is **competent** for the prosecution (s.53 YJCEA 1999) but, under **s.80(2A) and (3) PACE**, **compellable** for it only for **specified offences**, which include an offence involving an assault on, or injury to, a person who was **under 16** at the time. The victim is 15, so Simon can be required to give evidence for the prosecution. Option A would be right if the victim were an adult, and the rule depends on the civil partnership still existing, not on whether the couple live together (a former civil partner is compellable like any ordinary witness)."},{"t":"trial-procedure","p":"FLK2","q":"Gethin Pritchard is to be tried in the Crown Court sitting in Caernarfon for causing grievous bodily harm with intent. Welsh is his first language and he wants to give his evidence in Welsh, although he also speaks English fluently. His solicitor raises this at the plea and trial preparation hearing.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court should deal with Gethin's wish?","o":["Refuse it, because a defendant who speaks English fluently must give evidence in English before a jury.","Allow it, and arrange for the jury to be selected from people able to understand Welsh.","Allow it only if the prosecution agrees, since the whole trial would then be conducted in Welsh.","Allow it, and arrange simultaneous translation into English for any jurors who do not speak Welsh.","Allow it only if the judge is satisfied that Gethin cannot express himself adequately in English."],"a":3,"e":"Under **s.22 Welsh Language Act 1993**, any party or witness in legal proceedings **in Wales** may **speak Welsh**, whether or not they also speak English, and the need should be raised early, as here at the PTPH. There is **no power to select jurors** for their ability to speak Welsh, so the court arranges **simultaneous translation** for jurors who do not understand it. The right is to speak Welsh, not to have the whole trial conducted in Welsh, and it depends neither on the prosecution's agreement nor on any weakness in his English."},{"t":"sentencing","p":"FLK2","q":"Ellie Morgan, aged 34, is convicted in July 2026 of two unrelated offences of fraud committed against different victims. The judge decides that each offence crosses the custody threshold and that the appropriate sentence for each is 8 months' imprisonment, to run consecutively. There are no exceptional circumstances.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the sentences must be suspended?","o":["They must be suspended, because each individual sentence is for 12 months or less.","They need not be suspended, because together the sentences exceed 12 months.","They must be suspended, because Ellie was convicted on or after 22 March 2026.","They cannot be suspended, because the total exceeds the longest term that can be suspended.","They need not be suspended, because fraud offences are excluded from the presumption."],"a":1,"e":"The **presumption of suspension** (Sentencing Act 2026 s.1, for adults convicted on or after **22 March 2026**) requires a custodial sentence of **12 months or less** to be suspended unless there are exceptional circumstances, but it does **not apply** where the custodial sentences imposed on the same occasion **together exceed 12 months**. Here the total is 16 months, so the court may choose whether to suspend. Option D is wrong because a term of up to **3 years** can now be suspended for convictions on or after 22 March 2026, and fraud is not an excluded offence."},{"t":"sentencing","p":"FLK2","q":"Femi Adebayo is charged with wounding with intent, an indictable-only offence. At his first appearance in the magistrates' court he indicates that he will plead guilty, and he formally pleads guilty at the plea and trial preparation hearing. His written basis of plea says he struck the victim once in a panic; the prosecution says he attacked the victim repeatedly. The judge holds a Newton hearing and rejects Femi's account.\n\nWhat is the maximum reduction that Femi would normally receive for his guilty plea?","o":["One sixth","One quarter","One third","One tenth","One eighth"],"a":0,"e":"Femi **indicated** his plea at the **first stage** of proceedings (the first hearing in the magistrates' court for an indictable-only offence), which would normally earn a reduction of **one third**, even though he formally pleaded at the PTPH. Where the defendant's version is **rejected after a Newton hearing** (a hearing where the judge decides a disputed factual basis of plea), the reduction is normally **halved**, giving one sixth. One third ignores the Newton hearing, and one quarter (or half of it, one eighth) would apply only if the plea had first been indicated after the first stage."},{"t":"appeals","p":"FLK2","q":"Zara Ahmed pleaded not guilty and was convicted of assault by beating by the magistrates on 2 March. Sentence was adjourned for a pre-sentence report, and on 30 March she received a community order. She wants to appeal against her conviction and asks about the time limit and the risks.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the advice her solicitor should give?","o":["She must serve notice within 15 business days of 2 March, and the Crown Court cannot increase her sentence.","She must serve notice within 21 days of 30 March, and the Crown Court cannot increase her sentence.","She must obtain leave within 28 days of 30 March, and the Crown Court may increase her sentence.","She must serve notice within 15 business days of 2 March, and the Crown Court may increase her sentence.","She must serve notice within 15 business days of 30 March, and the Crown Court may increase her sentence."],"a":4,"e":"An appeal from the magistrates' court to the Crown Court (**s.108 MCA 1980**) is a full rehearing and needs **no leave** if notice is served within **15 business days** after **sentence**, even where the appeal is against conviction and sentence was adjourned, so time runs from 30 March. The Crown Court may impose **any sentence the magistrates could have imposed**, including a **heavier** one, which is a real risk to explain. Twenty-one days is the limit for case stated, and the 28-day leave requirement applies to appeals from the Crown Court to the Court of Appeal."},{"t":"appeals","p":"FLK2","q":"Rhys Thomas is serving a sentence of four years' imprisonment imposed in the Crown Court. His application for leave to appeal against sentence was refused by the single judge, who described the grounds as wholly without merit. Rhys wants to renew his application to the full Court of Appeal.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the main risk of renewing the application?","o":["The full court may increase his sentence if it concludes that the original sentence was too lenient.","The full court may order a fresh sentencing hearing before a different and more senior Crown Court judge.","The full court may direct that time in custody pending the appeal will not count towards his sentence.","The full court may refer his case to the Criminal Cases Review Commission for a full investigation.","The full court may refuse to consider the renewal unless he makes it within 28 days of the sentence."],"a":2,"e":"Where the court considers an application for leave **without merit**, particularly a **renewed** application after refusal by the single judge, it may make a **loss of time direction** under **s.29 Criminal Appeal Act 1968**, so that time in custody pending the appeal does not count towards the sentence. The Court of Appeal **cannot increase** a sentence on the defendant's own appeal; an increase is possible only on an Attorney General's reference of an **unduly lenient** sentence. A renewal must usually be made within **14 days** of the single judge's refusal, not 28 days of sentence."},{"t":"youth-court","p":"FLK2","q":"Kayden Brown, aged 15, has no previous convictions. He is charged in the youth court with theft of a bicycle and a connected offence of criminal damage to the lock securing it. He pleads guilty to the theft but not guilty to the criminal damage, and is convicted of it after a trial. The court is considering a community sentence and is not considering custody, a hospital order or a discharge.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the court's powers?","o":["It must make a referral order, because he is a first-time offender who pleaded guilty to an imprisonable offence.","It cannot make a referral order, because he was convicted of one of the offences after a trial.","It must make a youth rehabilitation order, because a referral order requires guilty pleas to all offences.","It may make a referral order but is not obliged to, because he did not plead guilty to every connected offence.","It may make a referral order only if the youth offending team recommends one in a pre-sentence report."],"a":3,"e":"A **referral order** (an order referring the child to a youth offender panel to agree a contract of reparation and other activities) is **compulsory** only where the offence is imprisonable, the child has no previous convictions and has **pleaded guilty to the offence and every connected offence** (Sentencing Code s.85). Because Kayden was convicted of the criminal damage after trial, the compulsory conditions are not met, but the **discretionary** conditions are, since he pleaded guilty to at least one offence. Option B confuses this with a child who pleads not guilty to everything, for whom a referral order is not available at all."},{"t":"youth-court","p":"FLK2","q":"Tyler Okonjo, aged 15, is convicted after a trial in the youth court of theft of designer clothing from a department store in the town centre, together with an older friend. He has one previous conviction, for which he received a referral order that he completed. The court decides to impose a youth rehabilitation order, and the youth offending team proposes a package of requirements aimed at keeping him busy and away from the town centre.\n\nWhich of the following requirements could NOT lawfully be included in Tyler's youth rehabilitation order?","o":["A curfew requirement with electronic monitoring","An unpaid work requirement of 60 hours","A supervision requirement with the youth offending team","An exclusion requirement covering the town centre","An activity requirement involving a sports programme"],"a":1,"e":"A **youth rehabilitation order** (the main community sentence for children, lasting up to 3 years) can include many requirements, such as **supervision**, **activity**, **curfew** with electronic monitoring and **exclusion** from a place. An **unpaid work** requirement, however, is available only for children aged **16 or 17** (40 to 240 hours), so it cannot be imposed on Tyler at 15. The other proposed requirements are all available at any age from 10 to 17, provided they are suitable and proportionate."}],"crimprac-p2":[{"t":"police-station-detention","p":"FLK2","q":"Nkechi Obi is arrested at 08:10 on Thursday on suspicion of fraud by false representation. She arrives at the police station at 09:00 and the custody officer authorises her detention at 09:40. An inspector who is not involved in the investigation carries out the first review of her detention at 15:40 on Thursday and authorises her continued detention.\n\nBy what time at the latest must the second review of Nkechi's detention be carried out?","o":["18:40 on Thursday","21:40 on Thursday","00:40 on Friday","00:00 on Friday","09:00 on Friday"],"a":2,"e":"Reviews are timed from when the custody officer first **authorised** detention (09:40), not from the relevant time. The **first review must be within 6 hours and later reviews at intervals of no more than 9 hours** (PACE s.40), so after a first review at 15:40 the second is due by 00:40 on Friday. Option D wrongly counts from her arrival at 09:00, and option A adds the 9-hour interval to the authorisation time instead of to the first review."},{"t":"police-station-detention","p":"FLK2","q":"Darius Farahani is arrested on suspicion of being concerned in the supply of cannabis, an either-way offence, and asks to speak to a named local solicitor. Before he is charged, a superintendent authorises a delay in his access to legal advice. The custody record notes that this solicitor routinely advises clients to make no comment in interview and that the investigation team need Darius to answer questions about his phone contacts.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the superintendent's decision?","o":["It is lawful, because a superintendent authorised it and the offence is indictable.","It is lawful, provided that Darius is told the reason and it is noted on the record.","It is lawful, but only until 24 hours have passed since his arrival at the station.","It is unlawful, because legal advice can never be delayed once it has been requested.","It is unlawful, because a risk that the solicitor will advise silence is not a ground."],"a":4,"e":"For an indictable offence a superintendent may delay legal advice, but only on **reasonable grounds for believing that the solicitor would cause one of the specified harms** (interference with evidence or people, alerting other suspects, or hindering recovery of property). **Code C Annex B states that delay cannot be authorised because the solicitor might advise the suspect not to answer questions**, so options A and B are wrong even though the rank and offence are right. Option D overstates the rule, because delay on proper grounds is possible for up to 36 hours from the relevant time, which also makes C wrong."},{"t":"police-interview","p":"FLK2","q":"Chloe Barker is interviewed under caution about a burglary. Her solicitor, Mr Osei, repeatedly answers the officers' questions on her behalf and, when an officer asks Chloe where she was on the night in question, he writes an answer on a notepad and passes it to her to read out. The interviewing sergeant stops the interview and tells Mr Osei that he must leave the station.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The sergeant may remove him straight away, because the interviewing officer controls the interview.","A superintendent, or an inspector if none is readily available, may remove him after speaking to him.","He cannot be removed, because his conduct in the interview is protected by legal professional privilege.","He may be removed, and the interview may then resume at once without Chloe having any solicitor.","Only the custody officer may remove him, and must report him to the regulator before resuming."],"a":1,"e":"**A solicitor may be removed from an interview only if their conduct prevents or unreasonably obstructs proper questions being put**, which answering for the client and writing out answers does. **The decision is for a superintendent or above (or an inspector if no superintendent is readily available)**, after speaking to the solicitor, so the sergeant cannot decide (A) and the custody officer has no such role (E). Chloe must be given the chance to consult another solicitor before the interview continues (D); a report to the Solicitors Regulation Authority should be considered but is not a precondition."},{"t":"identification","p":"FLK2","q":"Marcus Bell is arrested after a fight outside a takeaway in which the victim suffered a broken nose. In interview he says he was there and pushed someone away, but that the man who punched the victim was his friend in a blue jacket. A passer-by who does not know Marcus told the police that the man in the grey tracksuit, which matches Marcus's clothing, threw the punch, and she says she would recognise him again. The officer in the case says no identification procedure is needed because Marcus admits being present.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position under PACE Code D?","o":["No procedure is needed, because Marcus admits that he was present at the scene of the fight.","No procedure is needed, because the passer-by can be asked to identify him in court at trial.","A procedure is needed only if Marcus's solicitor makes a written request for one to be held.","A procedure must be held, because the witness's account contradicts Marcus's version of events.","A confrontation should be held, because Marcus's presence at the scene is already admitted."],"a":3,"e":"Under Code D para 3.12 a procedure **must be held** where a witness may be able to identify a suspect who disputes being the person seen. **An admission of presence makes a procedure pointless only where the eyewitness sees nothing that contradicts the suspect's account**; here the witness says Marcus, not his friend, threw the punch, so the identity of the attacker is disputed and the officer's view (A) is wrong. An identification in the dock is no substitute (B), the duty does not depend on a request (C), and a confrontation is a last resort used only where no other procedure is practicable (E)."},{"t":"identification","p":"FLK2","q":"Daniel Kowalczyk is arrested on suspicion of stealing a handbag from a passenger on a train. He denies being the thief, and the victim has said she could identify the man who took it. The identification officer explains the video identification procedure to Daniel and gives him the required written notice. Daniel says he will not take part in any procedure.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what may now happen?","o":["The officer may arrange a covert video identification, and the refusal may be given in evidence.","No identification procedure may take place, because the suspect's consent is a requirement.","The officer must arrange an identification parade instead, as Daniel has refused a video.","The victim may be shown a single photograph of Daniel to confirm that he is the right man.","The officer must hold a confrontation straight away, as the refusal rules out other methods."],"a":0,"e":"The notice under Code D para 3.17 tells the suspect that **he need not consent, but that a refusal may be given in evidence and the police may proceed covertly**, for example a video identification using images obtained without consent or a covert group identification. Consent is therefore not a requirement (B), and a parade would need his co-operation in any event (C). Showing a single photograph of a known suspect is a breach of Code D (D), and a confrontation is the last resort rather than the automatic next step (E)."},{"t":"bail","p":"FLK2","q":"Rory Adeyemi, aged 24, is charged with theft of a mobile phone worth £350 from a colleague's desk. When the theft is alleged to have happened he was on bail for a separate offence of criminal damage. He has one previous caution and a stable home and job. At his first appearance the prosecution opposes bail solely on the ground that he was on bail when the theft was committed. His solicitor submits that the theft could not realistically lead to a prison sentence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the court's approach to bail?","o":["Bail must be refused, because the theft was allegedly committed while he was already on bail.","Bail may be refused on that ground, whatever sentence the theft is realistically likely to attract.","That ground cannot be used if there is no real prospect of custody other than a suspended sentence.","That ground can be relied on only if the earlier proceedings were for an indictable-only offence.","That ground can be relied on only if he has previously failed to surrender to bail in some case."],"a":2,"e":"Being on bail when the offence was allegedly committed is an exception to the right to bail for imprisonable indictable offences (Bail Act 1976 Sch 1 Part I para 2A). However, **the main exceptions, including the offence-on-bail ground, do not apply where the defendant is 18 or over and there is no real prospect of a custodial sentence other than a suspended sentence**. Option A is wrong because the exceptions allow, but never require, refusal, and B ignores the no-real-prospect rule. Neither the type of earlier proceedings nor a past failure to surrender is a condition of the exception (D and E)."},{"t":"bail","p":"FLK2","q":"Yusuf Demir is charged with burglary of a dwelling and appears before the magistrates for the first time. His solicitor makes a full bail application, relying on his settled address and family ties and offering a curfew and daily reporting. The magistrates refuse bail on the ground that there are substantial grounds for believing he would fail to surrender, and remand him in custody for 7 days. Nothing in his circumstances is expected to change before the next hearing.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Yusuf's solicitor may do at the next hearing?","o":["Apply for bail only by putting forward a new argument or a change in Yusuf's circumstances.","Make a further full application, using any argument, including ones already put forward.","Make no application to the magistrates, since the only route now is an appeal to the Crown Court.","Make a full application at that hearing and at every later remand hearing, as of right.","Apply for bail again only if the prosecution consents to the arguments being reheard."],"a":1,"e":"If bail is refused at the first hearing, **at the next hearing the defendant may support a bail application with any argument of fact or law, whether or not it has been advanced before** (Bail Act 1976 Sch 1 Part IIA), so A is wrong at this stage. **After that, the court need not hear arguments it has heard before**, so repeated full applications at every hearing (D) are not available; only new arguments or a change in circumstances will then be heard. Appeal to the Crown Court, using the certificate of full argument, is an additional option rather than the only route (C), and no prosecution consent is needed (E)."},{"t":"first-hearings","p":"FLK2","q":"Stefan Ionescu, aged 41, is unemployed and receives universal credit. He is charged with assault by beating of his 12-year-old nephew during a family argument and intends to plead not guilty. The nephew is the main prosecution witness. Stefan has two previous convictions for shoplifting, and the offence is unlikely to lead to a custodial sentence. No legal issues about the evidence are expected.\n\nWhich interests of justice factor most strongly supports Stefan's application for a representation order?","o":["He is likely to lose his liberty if he is convicted of the offence.","A conviction would cause serious damage to his good reputation.","The case involves a substantial question of law about the evidence.","It is in the nephew's interests that Stefan does not question him himself.","He is likely to lose his livelihood if he is convicted of the offence."],"a":3,"e":"**It is in the interests of justice to grant legal aid where it is in another person's interests that the defendant is represented**, typically so that a defendant does not personally **cross-examine** a child witness (LASPO 2012 s.17(2)). Custody is unlikely, so loss of liberty (A) does not fit, and his previous convictions make serious damage to reputation (B) unlikely. No legal issue is expected (C) and he is not in work (E); he passes the means test automatically because he receives universal credit."},{"t":"first-hearings","p":"FLK2","q":"A solicitor represents Bethany Clarke at her first hearing in the magistrates' court, where Bethany will plead guilty to theft. The record of previous convictions served by the prosecution shows none. In private, Bethany tells the solicitor that she was convicted of theft three years ago under a different surname, and asks the solicitor to tell the magistrates in mitigation that she has never been in trouble before.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the solicitor's position?","o":["She may continue to act, but must not say Bethany has no convictions and need not reveal the conviction.","She must tell the magistrates about the conviction, because she must not allow the court to be misled.","She may tell the magistrates that Bethany is of good character, because the court relies on the record.","She must stop acting at once, because she now knows that the prosecution's record is not accurate.","She may continue to act, but must first tell the prosecutor about the conviction so it can be added."],"a":0,"e":"A solicitor must **not mislead the court** (SRA Code of Conduct para 1.4), so she cannot tell the magistrates that Bethany has never been in trouble (C). But **there is no duty to volunteer the client's previous convictions**, and what Bethany has said is confidential (para 6.3), so the solicitor must not reveal it to the court or the prosecutor without consent (B and E). She can continue to act and mitigate on other matters; she would have to stop acting only if Bethany insisted on her misleading the court, so D is wrong."},{"t":"allocation-sending","p":"FLK2","q":"Ross Hamilton, aged 26, is charged with robbery of a cyclist. A passer-by who tried to intervene during the same incident was punched by Ross and suffered a black eye, and Ross is also charged with assault occasioning actual bodily harm on the passer-by. Both charges are listed together at his first appearance before the magistrates.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court must deal with the assault charge?","o":["It must take Ross's plea before venue on the assault and then, if needed, decide allocation.","It must keep the assault for summary trial while sending the robbery to the Crown Court.","It must send the assault with the robbery only if Ross elects trial by jury on the assault.","It must send the assault with the robbery only if it decides the assault is unsuitable for it.","It must send the assault with the robbery, without plea before venue or any allocation decision."],"a":4,"e":"An adult charged with an **indictable-only** offence such as robbery must be sent to the Crown Court straight away (Crime and Disorder Act 1998 s.51). **A related either-way offence is sent with it, without plea before venue or allocation** (ss.50A and 51), so the procedure in A, and the election and allocation routes in C and D, do not arise. Keeping the assault in the magistrates' court (B) would split related charges that the Act requires to be sent together."},{"t":"allocation-sending","p":"FLK2","q":"Harvey Whitlock, aged 27, is charged with criminal damage after kicking dents into the panels of his neighbour's car during an argument. A garage has estimated the cost of repair at £3,800, and Harvey does not dispute that figure. He denies the offence and tells his solicitor that he wants to be tried by a jury.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Harvey's position?","o":["He cannot elect Crown Court trial, and the maximum sentence is 3 months' custody and/or a level 4 fine.","He may elect Crown Court trial, because criminal damage is an offence that is triable either way.","He may be tried in the Crown Court, but only if the magistrates decide that the case is unsuitable for them.","He cannot elect Crown Court trial, and the maximum sentence is 12 months' custody and/or a fine.","He cannot elect Crown Court trial, and the maximum sentence is 6 months' custody and/or a fine."],"a":0,"e":"**Criminal damage (other than by fire) where the value involved is £5,000 or less must be tried as if it were summary only** (Magistrates' Courts Act 1980 s.22). Harvey therefore has **no right to elect** Crown Court trial, and the magistrates cannot decline jurisdiction, so B and C are wrong (contrast low-value shoplifting, where an adult may elect). **The maximum in the magistrates' court is 3 months' imprisonment and/or a level 4 fine**, not the 12-month either-way limit (D) or the general 6-month summary limit (E)."},{"t":"case-management-disclosure","p":"FLK2","q":"Tariq Hussain is on trial in the Crown Court for burglary of a shop. His defence statement said that he was at home all evening with his partner, whose details were given. At trial, Tariq instead calls his cousin, who says that Tariq spent the evening at the cousin's flat watching football. The cousin was not named in the defence statement or in any defence witness notice.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the consequences?","o":["The cousin cannot give evidence, because he was not named in the defence statement.","The cousin may give evidence, but the failure may attract comment and adverse inferences.","The cousin may give evidence only if Tariq shows a good reason for not naming him earlier.","The jury must convict if they reject Tariq's explanation for the change in his alibi.","No comment may be made, because a defence statement in the Crown Court is voluntary."],"a":1,"e":"Relying at trial on an **alibi or alibi witness not mentioned in the defence statement**, or calling a witness not named in a defence witness notice, is a **fault under CPIA s.11**. The **evidence is not excluded** (A and C), but the court or the prosecution (with leave where required) may comment, and the jury may draw such **adverse inferences** as appear proper. Tariq **cannot be convicted solely on such an inference**, so D is wrong, and a defence statement is compulsory in the Crown Court (E)."},{"t":"case-management-disclosure","p":"FLK2","q":"Lucy Fairbanks is charged with supplying heroin to an undercover purchaser and says the police have the wrong woman. The prosecution holds material showing that a registered police informant was present at the deal and told his handler that the seller was taller than Lucy. Revealing the material would identify the informant. The prosecution does not intend to rely on it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the prosecution must deal with the material?","o":["Withhold it, because material listed on the sensitive schedule is never given to the defence.","Withhold it unless the defence makes an application for specific disclosure that names him.","Ask the court to rule on withholding it, and disclose it or drop the case if refused.","Disclose it to the defence in full, because it meets the test for prosecution disclosure.","Withhold it if the prosecutor decides that the public interest outweighs Lucy's interests."],"a":2,"e":"The material meets the **disclosure test** because it might reasonably be considered capable of assisting Lucy's defence, but it is **sensitive**. **Only the court can authorise withholding it, on an application for public interest immunity (PII)**, balancing the public interest against Lucy's right to a fair trial; the prosecutor cannot decide alone (E), and listing it on the sensitive schedule does not settle the matter (A). If the court rules that fairness requires disclosure, the prosecution must **disclose it or drop** the case, so automatic disclosure (D) is wrong."},{"t":"evidence-identification-silence","p":"FLK2","q":"Nathan Asante is on trial in the Crown Court for robbery. The only evidence against him comes from the victim, who went to school with Nathan and has known him for years. She says she saw his face clearly for about ten seconds under a streetlight. Nathan says he was not there and that she is honestly mistaken.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the judge should deal with this evidence?","o":["Leave it to the jury without a special warning, because this is a case of recognition.","Withdraw the case from the jury, because there is no evidence supporting the identification.","Direct the jury that recognition evidence must be supported before they can convict on it.","Give a special warning only if the defence alleges that the victim is deliberately lying.","Leave it to the jury with a warning that mistakes occur even with people who are well known."],"a":4,"e":"Where the case depends wholly or substantially on disputed identification, the judge must give a **Turnbull warning**, and **this applies to recognition too, because mistakes are made even in recognising close friends and relatives**. Good-quality identification, such as a well-known person seen for ten seconds under a streetlight, may be left to the jury without supporting evidence, so B and C are wrong. Option A wrongly treats recognition as removing the need for a warning, and D reverses the position, since the warning is aimed at honest but mistaken witnesses."},{"t":"evidence-identification-silence","p":"FLK2","q":"Olivia Grant is arrested for assault occasioning actual bodily harm after a man was injured in a pub. At the start of her interview, her solicitor reads out a prepared statement in which Olivia says the man came at her with a glass and she pushed him away in self-defence. She then answers no comment to all questions. At trial she gives evidence to the same effect and relies on no fact that is not in the statement. The prosecution asks the judge to direct the jury that they may draw an inference from her silence in interview.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["No inference may be drawn, because she relies on no fact at trial that was missing from her statement.","An inference may be drawn, because she refused to answer questions after giving her prepared statement.","An inference may be drawn, unless she explains the reasons her solicitor gave for advising no comment.","No inference may be drawn, because she was entitled to remain silent on her solicitor's legal advice.","An inference may be drawn, but only if the police gave her a special warning before the interview."],"a":0,"e":"A s.34 inference (Criminal Justice and Public Order Act 1994) arises from **failing to mention a fact later relied on**, not from silence as such. **Where a prepared statement contained all the facts the defendant relies on at trial, no inference should be drawn even though she then answered no comment** (R v Knight), so B and C are wrong. D gives the wrong reason, because silence on legal advice does not by itself prevent an inference, and a special warning (E) is relevant to ss.36-37 (failure to account for objects, marks or presence), not s.34."},{"t":"hearsay-confessions","p":"FLK2","q":"Ryan Cole is charged with fraud for falsely claiming a refund from an online retailer on the basis that a parcel was never delivered. The prosecution wants to rely on the courier company's electronic delivery log, which records the time of delivery and a photograph taken by the driver on his handheld device as he delivered the parcel. The driver still works for the company, but the prosecution does not plan to call him.\n\nOn what basis, if any, is the delivery log admissible?","o":["It is admissible under s.116, but only if the driver cannot be found despite reasonable steps.","It is admissible only with the defence's agreement, because the driver cannot be cross-examined.","It is admissible under s.117, but only if the driver is called to confirm what the entries show.","It is admissible under s.117 as a business record, with no need to show why the driver is absent.","It is inadmissible hearsay, because the driver who supplied the information is not being called."],"a":3,"e":"The log is hearsay, but it is admissible under **CJA 2003 s.117** because it was **created in the course of a business** from information supplied by someone (the driver) **with personal knowledge** of the delivery. **The extra requirement that the supplier is unavailable or cannot be expected to remember applies only to documents prepared for criminal proceedings or an investigation**, which a routine delivery log is not, so A and C are wrong. Agreement is only one route to admitting hearsay (B), and the court can still exclude the entry if its reliability is doubtful."},{"t":"hearsay-confessions","p":"FLK2","q":"Femi Okoro and Jack Langley are jointly charged with burglary of a house. In his police interview, Jack said that he committed the burglary on his own and that Femi was not involved. Femi's defence is that he was not there. The prosecution does not intend to rely on Jack's interview, and Jack's counsel argues that the admission was made when Jack was exhausted and is unreliable. Femi wants the jury to hear Jack's admission.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Femi can rely on it?","o":["He can, but only if the prosecution proves beyond reasonable doubt that it was properly obtained.","He can, if he proves on the balance of probabilities that it was not obtained unreliably.","He cannot, because a confession is admissible only against the person who made it and not for others.","He can, but only if Jack chooses not to give evidence at trial and so cannot be questioned about it.","He can, automatically, because the conditions on admitting confessions apply only to the prosecution."],"a":1,"e":"Under **PACE s.76A a co-defendant may adduce another defendant's confession**. If it is challenged, **the co-defendant must prove on the balance of probabilities that it was not obtained by oppression or in circumstances likely to make it unreliable**, a lower standard than the beyond reasonable doubt test the prosecution faces under s.76 (A), and not an automatic right (E). The rule in C, that a confession is not evidence against a co-defendant it implicates, limits the prosecution and does not stop a co-defendant relying on a confession that helps him."},{"t":"bad-character","p":"FLK2","q":"Joseph Mwangi is on trial for assaulting a doorman outside a nightclub. In his evidence in chief he tells the jury: \"I am a peaceful man and I have never been in a fight in my life.\" He has a conviction for affray two years ago and two convictions for shoplifting from five years ago. The prosecution applies to put his previous convictions before the jury.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the court may admit?","o":["All three convictions, because Joseph has presented himself to the jury as a man of good character.","None of the convictions, because the claim was made in his own evidence rather than by a witness.","The affray conviction only, because evidence correcting a false impression must go no further than needed.","The affray conviction only, and only if Joseph fails to show that admitting it would be unfair under s.101(3).","All three convictions, because claiming good character is treated as an attack on another's character."],"a":2,"e":"By claiming that he has never been in a fight, Joseph has given a **false or misleading impression** about himself, opening **gateway (f)** (CJA 2003 ss.101(1)(f) and 105). **Evidence admitted under gateway (f) must go no further than is necessary to correct the false impression**, so the affray conviction may go in but the shoplifting convictions, which say nothing about violence, may not (A). An assertion in his own evidence clearly counts (B); s.101(3) applies only to gateways (d) and (g), not (f) (D); and claiming good character is not an attack on anyone else (E)."},{"t":"trial-procedure","p":"FLK2","q":"Hannah Lowe, aged 45, is on trial in the Crown Court for theft from her employer. She has no previous convictions or cautions, and no other misconduct is alleged against her. In her police interview she gave a full account denying the theft, and the interview is part of the evidence. On legal advice she does not give evidence at trial.\n\nWhich good character direction, if any, should the judge give the jury?","o":["The propensity limb only, because Hannah did not give evidence at her trial.","No direction, because a good character direction is always at the judge's discretion.","The credibility limb only, because the propensity limb requires her to give evidence.","Both limbs, but only if the prosecution formally agrees that she is of good character.","Both the credibility and propensity limbs, because her interview is relied on."],"a":4,"e":"A defendant of **absolute good character** (no convictions, cautions or other reprehensible conduct) is **entitled** to a direction. **The propensity limb (she is less likely to have committed the offence) is given in every case, and the credibility limb (she is more likely to be telling the truth) is given if she gave evidence or made pre-trial statements, such as an interview, that are relied on** (R v Vye). Option A overlooks that the interview attracts the credibility limb even though she did not testify, and C reverses the two limbs. The judge's discretion (B) arises only for effective good character, such as old or minor convictions."},{"t":"sentencing","p":"FLK2","q":"Gareth Price, aged 36, is subject to a restraining order that forbids him from going to his former partner's address. In February 2026 he goes there and slaps her new partner, and in June 2026 he is convicted of assault by beating in the magistrates' court. The magistrates decide that the custody threshold is crossed and that the right term is 14 weeks. No exceptional circumstances are identified.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the sentence must be suspended?","o":["It must be suspended, because the term is 12 months or less and Gareth is an adult.","It must be suspended, unless there are exceptional circumstances in Gareth's case.","The presumption of suspension does not apply, because the offence breached a court order.","The presumption of suspension does not apply, because it covers only Crown Court sentences.","The presumption of suspension does not apply, because the offence predates 22 March 2026."],"a":2,"e":"For adults convicted on or after 22 March 2026, a court imposing a custodial sentence of **12 months or less must suspend it** unless there are exceptional circumstances (Sentencing Act 2026 s.1). **The presumption does not apply where the offence was committed in breach of a court order such as a restraining order**, so the magistrates may impose immediate custody, though they could still choose to suspend. Options A and B overlook this exception, E is wrong because the 2026 rules run from the date of **conviction**, not the offence, and D is wrong because the presumption applies in both courts."},{"t":"sentencing","p":"FLK2","q":"Amir Shah indicates a guilty plea to assault occasioning actual bodily harm at his first hearing in the magistrates' court and is committed to the Crown Court for sentence. His written basis of plea says that he punched the victim once. The prosecution says that he also kicked the victim repeatedly while the victim was on the ground, which would place the offence in a higher culpability category. After hearing evidence without a jury, the judge is sure that the prosecution version is correct and rejects Amir's account.\n\nWhat is the maximum reduction Amir should normally receive for his guilty plea?","o":["One third","One quarter","One tenth","One sixth","No reduction"],"a":3,"e":"A guilty plea indicated at the **first stage** of proceedings normally attracts a **one third** reduction, even if the defendant is then committed for sentence. However, **where the defendant's version of the facts is rejected after a Newton hearing, the reduction is normally halved**, giving one sixth here. One third (A) ignores the Newton hearing, one quarter (B) is the maximum for a plea first indicated after the first stage, and losing the reduction entirely (E) is not the normal consequence."},{"t":"appeals","p":"FLK2","q":"Lena Fischer is tried in the magistrates' court for driving with excess alcohol. The magistrates rule that the prosecution's evidence of the breath test procedure is inadmissible and, as there is no other evidence, acquit her. The Crown Prosecution Service believes the magistrates misinterpreted the relevant statutory provision.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the prosecution can challenge the acquittal?","o":["Apply to the magistrates to state a case for the opinion of the High Court within 21 days.","Appeal to the Crown Court for a full rehearing of the case within 15 business days.","Apply to the Court of Appeal for leave to appeal against the acquittal within 28 days.","Apply to the Crown Court to state a case for the High Court within 21 days of acquittal.","It cannot challenge the acquittal, because an acquittal by magistrates is always final."],"a":0,"e":"**Appeal by way of case stated (Magistrates' Courts Act 1980 s.111) is open to either party, including the prosecution against an acquittal, where the decision was wrong in law or in excess of jurisdiction**; the application is made to the magistrates **within 21 days** and the case is decided by the High Court. Only a defendant can appeal to the Crown Court for a rehearing (B), and the Court of Appeal hears appeals from the Crown Court, not the magistrates (C). The Crown Court states a case only after it has itself heard an appeal (D), and E is wrong because case stated is the main way the prosecution can challenge a magistrates' acquittal."},{"t":"youth-court","p":"FLK2","q":"Oliver Brennan, aged 16, and Craig Dunmore, aged 31, are jointly charged with burglary of a commercial warehouse. Craig indicates not guilty and elects Crown Court trial. Oliver also intends to plead not guilty; the prosecution says he acted only as a lookout. The witnesses are two adult security guards who are not regarded as vulnerable.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court should deal with Oliver's case?","o":["He must be sent to the Crown Court with Craig, because a child jointly charged follows the adult.","He must be asked whether he too wishes to elect trial by jury in the Crown Court with Craig.","He must be sent to the Crown Court, because burglary is a grave crime for a child of his age.","He should be sent only if a joint trial is necessary in the interests of justice; otherwise not.","He should be tried in the adult magistrates' court, because he is jointly charged with an adult."],"a":3,"e":"When an adult is sent to the Crown Court, **a child jointly charged with a related indictable offence is sent too only if that is necessary in the interests of justice** (Crime and Disorder Act 1998 s.51(7)); otherwise the child is tried in the youth court, which seems likely here given Oliver's lesser role and the lack of vulnerable witnesses. **A child never has a right to elect jury trial** (B). Burglary of non-residential premises carries a maximum of 10 years for an adult, so it is not a grave crime, which needs a maximum of 14 years or more (C)."},{"t":"youth-court","p":"FLK2","q":"Kai Morrison, aged 13, has no previous convictions or reprimands. He is convicted in the youth court of assault occasioning actual bodily harm after punching another boy and breaking his tooth. The court considers that the offence is so serious that it crosses the custody threshold.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the court's sentencing powers?","o":["It may impose a detention and training order, as these are available for any child aged 12 to 17.","It may not impose a detention and training order, because Kai is under 15 and not a persistent offender.","It may impose a detention and training order of 3 months, as the shortest term the court may set.","It may impose a youth rehabilitation order with intensive supervision and surveillance instead.","It may send Kai to the Crown Court for long-term detention, because the offence is so serious."],"a":1,"e":"A **detention and training order** is available for children aged 12 to 17, but **for 12 to 14 year olds only if the court considers them a persistent offender**, which Kai, with no previous convictions, is not (A). A **youth rehabilitation order with intensive supervision and surveillance** is likewise limited to persistent offenders where the child is under 15 (D). Detention and training orders start at 4 months (C), and assault occasioning actual bodily harm (maximum 5 years for an adult) is not a grave crime, so long-term detention in the Crown Court (E) is not available."}],"crimprac-p3":[{"t":"police-station-detention","p":"FLK2","q":"Aisha Kamara attends Eastfield police station voluntarily at 10:00 on Monday to be interviewed about a suspected fraud by abuse of position, an either-way offence. The interview begins at 10:20. At 11:30, after she is shown documents bearing what appears to be her signature, the officer arrests her, and the custody officer authorises her detention at 11:50. No extension of detention is sought.\n\nWhich of the following best describes when the basic period of detention without charge expires?","o":["10:00 on Tuesday, 24 hours after she arrived at the station.","10:20 on Tuesday, 24 hours after the interview began.","11:30 on Tuesday, 24 hours after she was arrested.","11:50 on Tuesday, 24 hours after detention was authorised.","17:50 on Monday, 6 hours after detention was authorised."],"a":2,"e":"The 24-hour clock runs from the **relevant time** (PACE s.41), and for a person who **attends the station voluntarily and is then arrested there, the relevant time is the time of arrest**, so C is right. Arrival time (A) is the relevant time only for someone brought to the station after arrest, and she was not under arrest at 10:00 or at 10:20 (B). The time detention was authorised (D) starts the clock for **reviews**, and E is the deadline for the first review, not the end of detention."},{"t":"police-station-detention","p":"FLK2","q":"Kwame Asante is arrested on suspicion of aggravated burglary and arrives at the police station at 09:00. A superintendent authorises a delay in his access to a solicitor, on reasonable grounds that the particular solicitor he has named would inadvertently alert his brother, a suspected accomplice who has not yet been arrested. His brother is arrested at 13:00. The police interview Kwame at 15:00 without a solicitor, telling him that the delay can last for up to 36 hours.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the lawfulness of the interview?","o":["It was unlawful, because access should have been allowed once his brother was arrested at 13:00.","It was lawful, because a superintendent's authorisation lasts for 36 hours from the relevant time.","It was lawful, because a delay of legal advice may continue until the next review of detention.","It was unlawful, because only a magistrates' court can delay legal advice for an indictable offence.","It was unlawful, because legal advice can never be delayed once a suspect names his own solicitor."],"a":0,"e":"Thirty-six hours is only the **maximum** delay: **once the reason for the delay stops applying, the suspect must be allowed to consult a solicitor at once** (PACE s.58 and Code C Annex B), so A is right. With the brother arrested, there was no one left to alert, which is why B and C are wrong, and an interview obtained by continuing the delay risks exclusion under **s.78 PACE** (R v Samuel). A superintendent is the correct rank (D), and a delay can apply to a named solicitor if the grounds relate to that particular solicitor (E)."},{"t":"police-interview","p":"FLK2","q":"Samuel Achebe is arrested on suspicion of theft from his employer and asks for the duty solicitor. Two hours later the solicitor has still not arrived, and Samuel tells the custody officer that he no longer wants to wait and is happy to be interviewed without a lawyer. The interviewing officer wants to start the interview straight away.\n\nWhich of the following best describes when the interview may begin?","o":["At once, because a suspect may withdraw a request for legal advice at any time without formality.","Only after a superintendent authorises an urgent interview because of the delay in the solicitor arriving.","Only once the solicitor arrives, because a suspect who has asked for advice cannot be interviewed without it.","Once he confirms this in writing and an inspector, having asked his reasons, authorises the interview.","Only after his detention has been reviewed and the review officer has agreed to the interview."],"a":3,"e":"Once a suspect has asked for legal advice he must not be interviewed until he has received it, unless an exception in **Code C para 6.6** applies. One exception is that the suspect **changes his mind** and **confirms in writing** (by signing the custody record) that he wants to be interviewed without advice, and an officer of **inspector** rank or above, having asked his reasons, authorises the interview in writing (the change of mind is then also confirmed on the interview recording), so D is right; A ignores these safeguards, and C is wrong because the right can be given up. An urgent interview (B) needs a risk of harm or of unreasonable delay to the investigation, and the review officer (E) has no role in this decision."},{"t":"police-interview","p":"FLK2","q":"Ruby Thornton is interviewed under caution about a burglary of a neighbour's flat. The officers put the fingerprint and CCTV evidence to her and she answers every question, giving an account that they do not believe. Every relevant question has now been put. The officer in the case reasonably believes that, taking account of all the other evidence, there is enough to give a realistic prospect of conviction, but wants to carry on questioning her for another hour in the hope of obtaining a confession.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the officer should do?","o":["Continue the interview, as long as Ruby is given a short refreshment break about every 2 hours.","End the interview and take Ruby to the custody officer so that a decision on charge can be made.","Continue the interview, because a suspect may be questioned until the basic 24-hour limit expires.","End the interview and release Ruby under investigation while the file is sent to the CPS.","Suspend the interview until a superintendent authorises further questioning of the suspect."],"a":1,"e":"Under **Code C para 11.6**, once all relevant questions have been put and the officer reasonably believes there is **sufficient evidence to provide a realistic prospect of conviction**, the interview **must stop** and the suspect must be taken to the custody officer for a decision on charge, so B is right. Carrying on to try to obtain a confession (A and C) is not allowed whatever breaks are given, and no superintendent can authorise it (E). D is wrong because the decision on charge, or on release for a CPS charging decision, is for the custody officer once the interview has ended, not for the officer in the case to make instead of taking her to custody."},{"t":"identification","p":"FLK2","q":"Patrick Doherty is arrested on suspicion of snatching a handbag at a railway station. He denies being the thief, and the victim says she could identify the man. Patrick is willing to take part in a video identification, which the identification officer says can be compiled within two days. To save time, the officer in the case proposes to bring the victim to the custody suite and show her Patrick on his own.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the officer's proposal?","o":["It is permitted, because a confrontation may be used wherever it is quicker than a video identification.","It is permitted, provided Patrick's solicitor is present when the victim is shown Patrick.","It is permitted, because Patrick has not objected to a confrontation taking place.","It is not permitted, because a confrontation can never be used once a suspect has been arrested.","It is not permitted, because a confrontation is a last resort and a video identification is practicable."],"a":4,"e":"A **video identification** is the normal procedure, and a **confrontation** (showing the witness the suspect alone) may be used only as a **last resort** where none of the other procedures is practicable (Code D), so E is right. Speed alone does not make a video identification impracticable when it can be ready in two days, so A is wrong, and the presence of a solicitor (B) or the suspect's lack of objection (C) does not cure this. D overstates the rule, because a confrontation can be used after arrest where nothing else is practicable."},{"t":"bail","p":"FLK2","q":"Stuart Reeves, aged 30, is charged with theft of tools worth £1,200 from a building site, an either-way offence. At his first appearance the magistrates consider that there is no real prospect of an immediate custodial sentence, but a real prospect of a suspended sentence. Electronic monitoring is available in the area. The prosecution invites the court to grant bail with an electronically monitored curfew \"as an extra safeguard\", while accepting that bail would be granted without it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the court may impose the electronically monitored curfew?","o":["It may, because electronic monitoring is available locally and a curfew is a proportionate condition.","It may not, unless without it there are substantial grounds to fear absconding, offending or interference.","It may, because the restrictions on monitoring apply only where there is no real prospect of any custody.","It may not, because only the police and not the court can arrange the electronic monitoring of a defendant.","It may not, because a court can never impose a curfew where the likely sentence is a suspended one."],"a":1,"e":"Since **22 March 2026 (Bail Act 1976 s.3AAB)**, where an adult has no real prospect of custody other than a suspended sentence but a real prospect of a suspended one, an electronic monitoring condition may be imposed only if monitoring is available **and** either bail would not be granted without it, or there are **substantial grounds for believing that without it the defendant would fail to surrender, offend or interfere with witnesses**. The prosecution accepts bail would be granted anyway, so B is right and A and C ignore the new restriction. Only a court, not the police, can impose electronic monitoring (D), and an ordinary curfew without monitoring is not ruled out (E)."},{"t":"bail","p":"FLK2","q":"Natalia Nowicka, aged 29, is convicted after a trial in the magistrates' court in June 2026 of theft from her employer. The magistrates adjourn sentence for three weeks for a pre-sentence report, indicating that they think the custody threshold is crossed but that any short sentence would very probably be suspended. She has two previous convictions for failing to surrender. The prosecution opposes bail on the ground that there are substantial grounds for believing she would fail to surrender.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court should approach bail?","o":["There is no right to bail after conviction, so the court may remand her in custody on that ground.","The ground is available, because the no real prospect rule applies only before conviction.","The ground is available, because a suspended sentence is still a custodial sentence.","The ground is not available, as there is no real prospect of custody other than a suspended one.","The ground is not available, and the court has no power to attach any conditions to her bail."],"a":3,"e":"A convicted defendant whose case is adjourned for reports keeps the **right to bail** (Bail Act 1976 s.4), so A is wrong. Since **22 March 2026** (Sentencing Act 2026 s.44), the **no real prospect rule in Sch 1 Part I para 1A** stops the fail-to-surrender, offending and interference grounds being used for an adult where there is **no real prospect of a custodial sentence other than a suspended one**, and it now applies **after conviction** too, so D is right and B and C state the old law. The court can still impose **conditions** to secure her surrender, so E is wrong."},{"t":"first-hearings","p":"FLK2","q":"Mei Lin Chen, aged 26, works part time and passes the magistrates' court means test. She has no previous convictions and is charged with assault by beating of a neighbour, an offence unlikely to lead to custody. Her job does not depend on her having a clean record. The only eyewitness has moved abroad, and the prosecution has served notice that it will apply to read his statement as hearsay, which her solicitor intends to oppose.\n\nWhich interests of justice factor most strongly supports her application for a representation order?","o":["The case may involve a substantial question of law.","She is likely to lose her liberty if she is convicted.","She is likely to lose her livelihood if she is convicted.","She may be unable to understand the proceedings.","It is in another person's interests that she is represented."],"a":0,"e":"Of the **LASPO 2012 s.17(2)** interests of justice factors, a contested application to admit **hearsay** raises a **substantial question of law**, so A is right. Custody is unlikely (B), her job is not at risk (C), and nothing suggests she cannot follow the proceedings (D). The interests of another person (E) usually arise where the defendant would otherwise personally cross-examine a child or a sexual offence complainant."},{"t":"allocation-sending","p":"FLK2","q":"Dean Fowler, aged 38, is charged with assault occasioning actual bodily harm and indicates a not guilty plea. At the allocation stage the prosecutor begins to tell the magistrates about Dean's six previous convictions for violence. Dean's solicitor objects that magistrates who may later try Dean should not hear about his record.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court should deal with the objection?","o":["Uphold it, because previous convictions may be revealed only after a defendant is convicted.","Uphold it, unless the prosecution has already served a bad character notice in the case.","Reject it, because the court must consider any previous convictions when deciding allocation.","Reject it, but only if Dean has already said that he intends to elect Crown Court trial.","Adjourn so that a different bench can decide allocation without hearing about the record."],"a":2,"e":"In deciding whether an either-way offence is more suitable for summary trial, the court **must consider the defendant's previous convictions** as given by the prosecution, together with representations, the adequacy of its sentencing powers and the Allocation guideline (**MCA 1980 s.19**), so C is right. A and B confuse allocation with the trial itself, where bad character needs a gateway. The record matters because it affects the likely sentence, whatever Dean intends about election (D), and allocation does not have to be decided by a different bench (E)."},{"t":"case-management-disclosure","p":"FLK2","q":"Elliot Barnes is charged with fraud and his case has been sent to the Crown Court. Within the time limit his solicitor served a defence statement which says only: \"The defendant denies the offence and puts the prosecution to proof.\" At a pre-trial hearing the judge reviews the statement.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the judge should do?","o":["Accept it, because a defendant is entitled to put the prosecution to proof without giving reasons.","Strike out the defence and direct that Elliot may not call any evidence at his trial.","Order the solicitor to disclose Elliot's privileged instructions so the issues can be identified.","Accept it, because the contents of a defence statement are a matter for the defence alone.","Warn Elliot that, if he does not comply fully, comment may be made and adverse inferences drawn."],"a":4,"e":"A defence statement must set out the **nature of the defence**, the **matters of fact in issue and why**, and any points of law (**CPIA s.6A**), so a bare denial does not comply, and A and D are wrong. Where at a pre-trial hearing the judge thinks the defendant has not complied fully, the judge **must warn** him that **comment and adverse inferences** may follow at trial (**s.6E(2)**), so E is right. The court cannot exclude the defence evidence (B), and the duty of defence disclosure does not override legal professional privilege (C)."},{"t":"evidence-identification-silence","p":"FLK2","q":"Simone Baptiste is on trial in the Crown Court for theft. At the close of the prosecution case, her counsel tells the judge that she will not give evidence and relies on an unchallenged psychiatric report stating that, because of a severe psychotic illness, giving evidence would be likely to cause her serious harm and that her evidence would be confused and unreliable. The prosecution asks the judge to direct the jury that they may draw an inference from her failure to give evidence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the judge should deal with the prosecution's request?","o":["Refuse it, if satisfied that her mental condition makes it undesirable for her to give evidence.","Grant it, because a defendant's mental condition is relevant only to the weight of the inference.","Grant it, because the s.35 inference is available whenever a defendant chooses not to testify.","Refuse it, because no inference can be drawn unless the defendant is cautioned again at trial.","Grant it, but direct the jury that they cannot convict her solely because she did not give evidence."],"a":0,"e":"**CJPOA 1994 s.35** does not apply where it appears to the court that the defendant's **physical or mental condition makes it undesirable for her to give evidence**, so if the judge accepts the unchallenged report, no inference may be drawn and A is right. B and C ignore this exception, and E describes a safeguard that applies only where an inference is properly available. There is no requirement to caution a defendant again at trial (D); where s.35 applies, she is instead told of the consequences of not giving evidence."},{"t":"evidence-identification-silence","p":"FLK2","q":"Jordan Pike is on trial for assault occasioning actual bodily harm after a fight at a bus stop. A witness who did not know him picked him out at a video identification. In interview and at trial, Jordan accepts that he was at the bus stop and that he punched the victim, but says he did so in self-defence after the victim came at him with a bottle.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the judge must give the jury a Turnbull warning?","o":["Yes, because the witness did not know Jordan before the incident.","Yes, because the case relies on a video identification by a stranger.","Yes, but only if the defence asks for one at the end of the evidence.","No, because Jordan does not dispute that he was the person involved.","No, because a warning is needed only where the witness knew the defendant."],"a":3,"e":"A **Turnbull warning** is needed where the case depends wholly or substantially on an identification that the defence says is **mistaken**. Where the defendant **admits being present** and doing the act, and the dispute is about **what happened** (here self-defence), identity is not in issue, so D is right. The witness being a stranger (A) or the use of a video identification (B) matters only if identity is disputed, the warning does not depend on a defence request (C), and it applies to recognition and stranger cases alike (E)."},{"t":"hearsay-confessions","p":"FLK2","q":"Kelechi Okeke is charged with theft from a shop. Two days after the theft, a police officer took a written statement from the shop assistant, Lauren, describing what she saw. Lauren still works at the shop, is willing to attend court and says she remembers the incident well. To save her time, the prosecution applies to admit her statement under s.117 of the Criminal Justice Act 2003 as a business document.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the application?","o":["It should be granted, because the statement was received by the officer in the course of his job.","It should be granted, because Lauren had personal knowledge of the matters in her statement.","It should be refused, because a statement prepared for an investigation needs an extra condition.","It should be refused, because a police witness statement can never be admitted as hearsay.","It should be granted under s.117, provided that the defence is given notice of it before trial."],"a":2,"e":"A document **prepared for criminal proceedings or a criminal investigation** is admissible under **CJA 2003 s.117** only if, in addition, **one of the s.116 reasons for unavailability applies** or the supplier **cannot reasonably be expected to remember** the matters. Lauren is available and remembers the incident, so the application should be refused and C is right, even though the basic business conditions in A and B are met. A witness statement can be admitted through other routes in some cases, so D is wrong, and giving notice (E) does not replace the extra condition."},{"t":"hearsay-confessions","p":"FLK2","q":"Ahmed Rashid is charged with robbery. The main prosecution witness, Carla, made a statement identifying him but died before the trial, and the judge admits her statement under s.116 of the Criminal Justice Act 2003. The defence has learned that Carla had two recent convictions for perverting the course of justice by giving false information to the police, and that she told a friend soon after the robbery that she never saw the robber's face.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the defence may deal with Carla's evidence?","o":["It cannot challenge it, because Carla cannot be cross-examined and her statement has been admitted.","It may rely only on her convictions, because her account to the friend is itself hearsay.","It may rely only on her inconsistent account, because her convictions are irrelevant to the issue.","It may challenge it only by asking the judge to stop the case as unsafe at the close of the evidence.","It may attack her credibility with evidence that would have been admissible had she given evidence."],"a":4,"e":"Under **CJA 2003 s.124**, where hearsay is admitted and its maker does not give oral evidence, evidence relevant to the maker's **credibility** is admissible **as if she had given evidence**, including evidence that she made an **inconsistent statement**, so E is right and A, B and C are too narrow. Her convictions are non-defendant bad character, so the defence will need leave under s.100, but they are plainly relevant to whether she is telling the truth. The judge's power to stop a case based on unconvincing hearsay (s.125) is an extra safeguard, not the only one (D)."},{"t":"bad-character","p":"FLK2","q":"Declan Thorne is on trial for burglary of a dwelling. While on bail, he sent text messages to the main prosecution witness, a neighbour, warning her that she \"would regret it\" if she gave evidence. The prosecution wants to adduce the messages. Declan's counsel objects that this is bad character evidence and that the prosecution has not given notice relying on any gateway.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the admissibility of the messages?","o":["They are bad character evidence, admissible only through gateway (d) as relevant to an important matter in issue.","They are not bad character evidence, because they are misconduct in connection with the prosecution of this offence.","They are bad character evidence, admissible only if Declan attacks the neighbour's character at trial.","They are inadmissible, because intimidating a witness is a separate offence that must be charged first.","They are bad character evidence, but are admissible as important explanatory evidence without notice."],"a":1,"e":"**CJA 2003 s.98** excludes from the definition of bad character evidence that **has to do with the alleged facts** of the offence, or of **misconduct in connection with the investigation or prosecution** of that offence, so the threatening messages are admissible in the ordinary way if relevant, and B is right. No gateway or bad character notice is therefore needed, which is why A, C and E are wrong. The prosecution does not have to charge the intimidation before relying on it (D)."},{"t":"bad-character","p":"FLK2","q":"Craig Holloway is charged with assault occasioning actual bodily harm after a fight in a pub. He says he acted in self-defence and will give evidence. He has three previous convictions for shoplifting, to each of which he pleaded guilty, and no convictions for violence. The prosecution applies to adduce the shoplifting convictions under gateway (d) to show that Craig has a propensity to be untruthful.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome of the application?","o":["They will be admitted, because any offence of dishonesty shows a propensity to be untruthful.","They will be admitted, because Craig's account of self-defence is said to be untrue.","They will be admitted, but only to show a propensity to commit offences of the kind charged.","They are unlikely to be admitted, as shoplifting on guilty pleas does not show untruthfulness.","They are unlikely to be admitted, because gateway (d) is available only to a co-defendant."],"a":3,"e":"**Propensity to be untruthful** under gateway (d) (CJA 2003 s.103) needs a history of dishonesty that is relevant to **lying**, such as offences involving false statements or an account given in evidence that a court rejected, and simple shoplifting admitted on **guilty pleas** does not normally show it, so D is right and A is wrong. The prosecution disputing his account (B) is not enough unless the convictions actually show the propensity. Shoplifting shows no propensity to violence (C), and gateway (d) is for the **prosecution**, while gateway (e) is the co-defendant's route (E)."},{"t":"trial-procedure","p":"FLK2","q":"Ryan Matthews is on trial for assault occasioning actual bodily harm on an adult neighbour. His former wife, Kerry, saw the incident. They divorced three months before the trial. The prosecution wants to call Kerry as a witness, but she does not want to give evidence against Ryan.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Kerry's position as a prosecution witness?","o":["She is competent and compellable, because she is no longer married to Ryan.","She is competent but not compellable, because the victim was an adult.","She is competent and compellable only because the offence involved violence.","She is not competent, because she was married to Ryan at the time of the offence.","She is competent, but compellable only with the consent of the defence."],"a":0,"e":"A spouse is compellable for the prosecution only for **specified offences**, such as an assault on the spouse or on a person under 16 (PACE s.80(3)), which this is not; but a **former spouse is compellable as if she had never been married** to the defendant (s.80(5)), so A is right. B would be correct only if they were still married. Violence alone does not make a spouse compellable (C), marriage at the time of the offence is irrelevant (D), and the defence has no veto (E)."},{"t":"trial-procedure","p":"FLK2","q":"Peter Lindgren, aged 26, is on trial in the Crown Court for affray. He has a diagnosed mental disorder that causes severe anxiety in crowded rooms, and a psychiatrist reports that this would prevent him from participating effectively if he gave evidence from the witness box. His counsel applies for him to give evidence by video link from another room, as the main prosecution witness is doing.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the court's powers?","o":["It may direct a live link as a special measure, because he is a vulnerable witness.","It cannot help him, because a defendant who testifies must give evidence from the witness box.","It may allow a live link under a separate power, as special measures do not apply to a defendant.","It may allow a live link only if the prosecution consents, because he is the defendant.","It must adjourn the trial until he is well enough to give evidence from the witness box."],"a":2,"e":"**Special measures** under YJCEA 1999 ss.16-30 are available only to **witnesses other than the defendant**, so A is wrong. Under **s.33A**, however, the court may allow an adult defendant whose **mental disorder** prevents him participating effectively as a witness to give evidence by **live link**, where that is in the interests of justice, so C is right. The power does not depend on prosecution consent (D), and the court can also appoint an intermediary under its inherent powers, so B and E are wrong."},{"t":"sentencing","p":"FLK2","q":"Kamal Siddiqui was remanded in custody for 20 days after being charged with burglary of a shop. He was then released on bail with an electronically monitored curfew from 19:00 to 07:00 each night, which lasted for 60 days. He did not breach the curfew. He is now convicted and sentenced to an immediate term of imprisonment, and the remand and bail relate only to this offence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the credit that counts towards his sentence?","o":["20 days, because only time spent in custody counts towards a sentence.","80 days, because each day of remand and of curfew counts in full.","40 days, because half of both the custody and the curfew days count.","30 days, because only half of the qualifying curfew days counts.","50 days, being every day in custody and half of the curfew days."],"a":4,"e":"Every day spent **in custody on remand** for the offence counts towards the sentence, and **half of the days on bail with an electronically monitored curfew of at least 9 hours a day** are credited (Criminal Justice Act 2003 s.240ZA for remand time, and Sentencing Code s.325 for qualifying curfew time). Kamal's curfew was 12 hours a night, so he gets 20 + 30 = 50 days and E is right. A ignores the curfew credit, B counts the curfew days in full, C halves the custody days as well, and D leaves the custody days out."},{"t":"sentencing","p":"FLK2","q":"Liam Carter, aged 31, is convicted in the magistrates' court in August 2026 of assault occasioning actual bodily harm on his former partner, Jade. He is not subject to any court order. The magistrates decide that the custody threshold is crossed and that the right term is 20 weeks' imprisonment. The pre-sentence report concludes that, if he remains in the community, he poses a significant risk of physical harm to Jade.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the sentence must be suspended?","o":["It must be suspended, because the term is 12 months or less and he is not in breach of an order.","It need not be suspended, because suspension would put a particular person at significant risk.","It must be suspended, because the risk to a named person can be managed by requirements.","It need not be suspended, because the presumption does not apply to offences of violence.","It cannot be suspended, because the magistrates have no power to suspend a custodial term."],"a":1,"e":"From **22 March 2026** a custodial sentence of **12 months or less** on an adult must be suspended unless there are exceptional circumstances, but the **presumption does not apply** where the court considers that suspension would put a **particular individual at significant risk of physical or psychological harm** (Sentencing Act 2026 s.1), so B is right. A overlooks this exception, and C is wrong because the exception removes the duty to suspend whatever requirements could be attached, although the court may still choose to suspend. There is no general exclusion for violent offences (D), and magistrates can suspend sentences within their powers (E)."},{"t":"appeals","p":"FLK2","q":"Laura Bennett pleads guilty in the magistrates' court to criminal damage to a neighbour's fence, causing £600 of damage. The magistrates mistakenly treat it as an ordinary either-way offence and impose 20 weeks' imprisonment, suspended for 12 months. The next day her solicitor realises that the maximum sentence for the offence in the magistrates' court is 3 months.\n\nWhich of the following is the quickest appropriate way to correct the sentence?","o":["Appeal to the Crown Court against sentence within 15 business days.","Apply to the Court of Appeal for leave to appeal against the sentence.","Apply for judicial review of the sentence in the Administrative Court.","Ask the magistrates to vary the sentence under s.142 of the 1980 Act.","Apply to the magistrates to state a case for the High Court's opinion."],"a":3,"e":"**MCA 1980 s.142** allows the magistrates to **vary or rescind a sentence** they imposed where it is in the interests of justice, and it is the quick remedy for an obvious mistake such as exceeding the **3-month maximum for criminal damage of £5,000 or less**, so D is right. An appeal to the Crown Court (A), judicial review (C) or case stated (E) would also be possible, but each is slower and more costly for a plain error. The Court of Appeal (B) does not hear appeals from the magistrates' court."},{"t":"appeals","p":"FLK2","q":"Victor Hale is acquitted of fraud by a jury in the Crown Court after the judge directs the jury on the meaning of \"dishonestly\" in a way that the prosecution believes is wrong in law. There is no suggestion of jury interference and no new evidence has emerged, but the Crown Prosecution Service wants the point of law clarified for future cases.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what can be done?","o":["The Attorney General may refer the point of law to the Court of Appeal, but the acquittal will stand.","The prosecution may appeal against the acquittal to the Court of Appeal within 28 days.","The Attorney General may refer the case as unduly lenient, and the Court of Appeal may convict.","The prosecution may ask the Court of Appeal to quash the acquittal and order a retrial.","The prosecution may apply to the Crown Court to state a case for the High Court's opinion."],"a":0,"e":"After an acquittal on indictment, the **Attorney General may refer a point of law to the Court of Appeal** for its opinion (Criminal Justice Act 1972 s.36), but **the acquittal is unaffected** whatever the answer, so A is right. The prosecution has no general right to appeal against a jury's acquittal (B), and an unduly lenient sentence reference (C) concerns sentences, not acquittals. Quashing an acquittal and ordering a retrial (D) needs **new and compelling evidence** of a qualifying offence, and the Crown Court cannot state a case on a trial on indictment (E)."},{"t":"youth-court","p":"FLK2","q":"Ella Morris, aged 13, has no previous convictions. She pleads guilty in the youth court to theft of a lipstick worth £8, an imprisonable offence. The court considers the offence trivial and that the most suitable sentence is a conditional discharge.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the court's powers?","o":["It must make a referral order, because she is a first-time offender who pleaded guilty to an imprisonable offence.","It must make a referral order, although it may set the length at the minimum of 3 months.","It may impose the conditional discharge, because a referral order is unavailable if a discharge is proposed.","It may impose the conditional discharge only if her parents agree to a referral order being dispensed with.","It must impose a youth rehabilitation order, because a conditional discharge is not available for a child."],"a":2,"e":"A referral order is **compulsory** for a child who pleads guilty to an imprisonable offence and has no previous convictions, but only where it is **available**, and it is not available where the court proposes **custody, a hospital order, or an absolute or conditional discharge** (Sentencing Code ss.84-85), so C is right. A and B overlook that limit. Parental agreement has no role (D), and a conditional discharge is available for children as it is for adults (E)."},{"t":"youth-court","p":"FLK2","q":"Zain Hussain, aged 16, and two friends surround a 15-year-old boy in a park. Zain produces a kitchen knife and holds it towards the boy while one of the friends takes his phone. The boy is not physically injured but is badly shaken. Zain has no previous findings of guilt and is found guilty after a trial in the youth court.\n\nApplying the robbery section of the Sentencing Children and Young People guideline, which of the following best describes the sentence that may be justified?","o":["A referral order, because Zain has no previous findings of guilt and is only 16.","A fine payable by his parents, because the boy suffered no physical injury.","A conditional discharge, because one of his friends rather than Zain took the phone.","A youth rehabilitation order with a standard level of supervision and no other requirements.","Custody, or a youth rehabilitation order with intensive supervision and surveillance or fostering."],"a":4,"e":"Under the robbery guideline for children, the **production of a bladed article** is an example of when a **custodial sentence, or a youth rehabilitation order with intensive supervision and surveillance (ISS) or fostering, may be justified**, so E is right. A referral order (A) is not available after a not guilty plea, and a fine or discharge (B and C) does not reflect the use of a knife. That a friend took the phone does not lessen Zain's role in producing the knife (C), and a standard-level youth rehabilitation order (D) is for less serious cases."}],"dr-p1":[{"t":"dr-options","p":"FLK1","q":"Harlow Data Ltd licensed warehouse software to Penrose Logistics Ltd. The licence says that any dispute arising out of it shall be referred to arbitration seated in London. After the software failed, Penrose issued a claim in the County Court for damages. Harlow has just been served with the claim form and particulars of claim and wants the dispute decided by an arbitrator instead.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Harlow should do?","o":["File a defence and counterclaim that pleads the arbitration clause, and ask the court to transfer the case to an arbitrator.","Take no step in the court proceedings, because the court has no jurisdiction over a dispute covered by an arbitration clause.","Apply to strike out the claim as an abuse of process, because the claimant has ignored the agreed dispute resolution route.","File an acknowledgment of service and apply for a stay before taking any step to answer the claim, which the court must grant.","File an acknowledgment of service and apply for a stay, which the court may grant if arbitration would be more convenient."],"a":3,"e":"Under **s.9 Arbitration Act 1996** the court **must** stay (pause) court proceedings covered by a valid arbitration agreement unless that agreement is null and void, inoperative or incapable of being performed, so the stay is not discretionary. The applicant must file an acknowledgment of service and apply **before taking any step to answer the substantive claim**: filing a defence first loses the right to a stay. Doing nothing risks default judgment, because the court does have jurisdiction until a stay is granted."},{"t":"dr-options","p":"FLK1","q":"Mirela, a building contractor, arbitrated a payment dispute with a developer under an arbitration agreement that adopted the LCIA Rules. Three days before the hearing the tribunal refused, without giving reasons or hearing from Mirela, to allow her quantity surveyor to give evidence, although the surveyor's evidence was central to her claim. The award, made 10 days ago, rejected her claim for lack of evidence on quantum.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Mirela's position?","o":["She may challenge the award for serious irregularity causing substantial injustice, within 28 days of the award.","She may appeal to the court on a point of law, because the tribunal misapplied the rules of evidence.","She may challenge the award for lack of substantive jurisdiction, because the tribunal exceeded its role.","She may ask the court to rehear the quantum evidence, because the tribunal's findings of fact were wrong.","She may appeal directly to the Court of Appeal with permission, within 21 days of the award."],"a":0,"e":"Refusing a party a fair opportunity to present central evidence is a breach of the tribunal's general duty to act fairly, which can found a challenge for **serious irregularity causing substantial injustice under s.68**, brought **within 28 days of the award** (s.70(3) and (3A), as amended by the Arbitration Act 2025). An appeal on a point of law under s.69 is not available on these facts and is usually excluded by choosing institutional rules such as the LCIA Rules. A s.67 challenge is about whether the tribunal had jurisdiction at all, and the court never reopens the tribunal's findings of fact."},{"t":"dr-limitation","p":"FLK1","q":"It is September 2026. Tasker Joinery Ltd invoiced Alder Homes Ltd £18,000 for work done under a contract, with payment due on 15 June 2019. Alder paid nothing at first. On 2 September 2024 Alder paid £1,500 towards the invoice, but has paid nothing since. Tasker now wants to sue for the balance of £16,500.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the limitation position?","o":["The claim is time-barred, because the six-year period ran from 15 June 2019 and expired in June 2025.","The claim is time-barred unless the court exercises its discretion to allow it to proceed out of time.","The claim is in time, because the part payment started a fresh six-year period running from 2 September 2024.","The claim is in time, but only until 2 September 2027, because the part payment started a fresh three-year period.","The claim is in time, because the period runs from the last date on which the debt could have been paid."],"a":2,"e":"For a debt (a **liquidated**, fixed sum), a **part payment** made by the debtor **before** the limitation period expires restarts the period from the date of payment (**Limitation Act 1980 ss.29-30**). The original contract period of six years would have expired on 15 June 2025, but the payment on 2 September 2024 came first, so a new six-year period runs to 2 September 2030. The court's discretion to disapply a time limit (s.33) applies only to personal injury claims, and the fresh period is six years, not three."},{"t":"dr-pre-action","p":"FLK1","q":"Brightline Electrical Wholesale Ltd supplied cable and fittings to Tomasz Nowak, who trades on his own as an electrician. Tomasz owes £6,800 on three unpaid invoices and has ignored two reminders. Brightline's credit manager asks what it must do before issuing a claim.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Brightline should do?","o":["Send a letter before claim under the general practice direction, giving Tomasz 14 days to reply because the debt is simple.","Issue the claim straight away, because no pre-action steps are required where the sum claimed is fixed and undisputed.","Send a letter of claim asking Tomasz to acknowledge within 21 days and to give a full response within three months.","Send a letter before claim under the general practice direction only, because the protocol for debts covers consumers alone.","Send a letter of claim with an information sheet, reply form and financial statement, giving Tomasz 30 days to reply."],"a":4,"e":"The **Debt Claims Pre-Action Protocol** applies where a business claims a debt from an **individual, including a sole trader** such as Tomasz. The letter of claim must enclose the information sheet, reply form and financial statement, and the debtor has **30 days** to reply. The general practice direction (with its 14 days to 3 months) applies to business-to-business debts only where the debtor is not a sole trader, and the 21-day acknowledgment with a three-month response is the personal injury and professional negligence pattern."},{"t":"dr-parties-law","p":"FLK1","q":"Ayesha, aged 16, was knocked off her bicycle by a car. Before any claim has been issued, the driver's insurer offers £9,000 in full settlement of her personal injury claim. Ayesha and her mother, Farida, both want to accept the offer.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Ayesha can accept the offer herself, because she is over 16 and the sum offered is below £10,000.","Farida can accept on Ayesha's behalf, but the settlement will not be binding until the court approves it.","Farida can accept on Ayesha's behalf, and the settlement will bind Ayesha as soon as it is accepted.","The offer cannot be accepted until Ayesha turns 18, because a child cannot settle a claim at all.","Ayesha can accept the offer herself, provided that Farida gives her written consent to the settlement."],"a":1,"e":"A **child** (anyone under 18) conducts a claim through a **litigation friend**, usually a parent, and under **CPR 21.10** any settlement by or on behalf of a child is **not binding unless approved by the court**, even if it is reached before proceedings are issued. The court checks that the figure is fair and protects the money until the child is 18. There is no exception for 16- and 17-year-olds or for small sums, and a parent's consent does not replace court approval."},{"t":"dr-parties-law","p":"FLK1","q":"Graham, who lives in Bristol, booked a holiday directly with a hotel company based in Spain. He was injured in Spain when a balcony rail at the hotel gave way. He has issued a negligence claim against the hotel company in England. The parties have made no agreement about which law applies to the claim.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the law that will apply to liability and the assessment of damages?","o":["Spanish law, because that is where the damage occurred, although the court will apply English procedure.","English law, because Graham is habitually resident in England and suffers the consequences of his injury there.","English law, because the claim has been brought in the English courts and the court applies its own law.","Spanish law for liability, but English law for damages, because damages are a matter for the court hearing the case.","English law, because the hotel directed its business at English customers and Graham booked as a consumer."],"a":0,"e":"Under **Rome II Art 4(1)** a tort claim is governed by the law of the country where the **damage occurs**, regardless of where indirect consequences are felt, so Spanish law applies; the common habitual residence rule does not help because the parties live in different countries. The applicable law covers liability, defences and the **assessment of damages**, while **procedure** follows the law of the court hearing the case. The consumer rule directing activities at a consumer's country is a Rome I rule for contracts, not for a negligence claim."},{"t":"dr-where-to-start","p":"FLK1","q":"Oluwaseun was injured at work when a scaffold collapsed. His solicitor values his claim for damages at about £46,000. Interest on the damages up to the date of issue is likely to add a further £6,000. Oluwaseun would like the case heard in the High Court because he believes it is important.\n\nWhere should the claim be issued?","o":["The High Court, because the total of damages and interest exceeds the personal injury threshold of £50,000.","Either court at the claimant's choice, because the claim is worth more than the small claims limit.","The County Court, because a personal injury claim worth less than £50,000 must be started there.","The High Court, because personal injury claims arising at work must be started in the King's Bench Division.","The County Court, because any claim worth £100,000 or less must be started there, whatever its type."],"a":2,"e":"A **personal injury** claim worth **less than £50,000** must be started in the **County Court**, and in valuing the claim for this purpose **interest and costs are disregarded** (PD 7A para 2.2 and art 9 of the High Court and County Courts Jurisdiction Order 1991), so the relevant figure is £46,000. Adding interest to reach £52,000 is the tempting error. The £100,000 threshold applies to non-personal injury money claims, so it gives the right court for the wrong reason, and a claimant's view of the case's importance cannot override the value rule."},{"t":"dr-issue-service","p":"FLK1","q":"A claim form was issued on Wednesday 14 January 2026. The claimant's solicitor, who had not received any nomination of solicitors, posted it by first class post to the defendant's home address at 3pm on Thursday 14 May 2026. It arrived on Monday 18 May 2026. The defendant says the claim form was served too late.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["It was served out of time, because it was deemed served on 18 May, after the four-month period had expired.","It was served in time only if it actually reached the defendant's address on or before 14 May.","It was served out of time, because it was deemed served on 16 May, after the four-month period had expired.","It was served in time, but only because the court will always extend time where the step is taken on the last day.","It was served in time, because it was posted before midnight on 14 May, and it is deemed served on 18 May."],"a":4,"e":"Under **CPR 7.5** the claimant must take the **step required** for the chosen method (for first class post, **posting**) before midnight on the calendar day **4 months after issue**, here 14 May 2026, so posting on that day is in time. The deemed date of service of a claim form is the **second business day after the step** (CPR 6.14), which is Monday 18 May because the weekend does not count, but it does not matter that this falls after the four-month period. No extension is needed, and actual arrival is irrelevant."},{"t":"dr-issue-service","p":"FLK1","q":"Kestrel Interiors Ltd intends to sue Fenwick Properties Ltd for unpaid fees. Before issue, Fenwick's solicitors wrote to Kestrel's solicitors confirming that they were instructed and authorised to accept service of proceedings on Fenwick's behalf. Kestrel's solicitors then issued the claim form and had a process server hand it to Fenwick's managing director at its registered office.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the validity of service?","o":["Service is valid, because personal service on a director of a company is always a permitted method.","Service is invalid, because the claim form had to be served on the solicitors who had been nominated.","Service is valid, because the registered office is an address at which a company may always be served.","Service is invalid, because a claim form against a company may only be served by post or at its office.","Service is valid, but the claimant must also send a copy of the claim form to the nominated solicitors."],"a":1,"e":"Under **CPR 6.7**, where the defendant's solicitor has notified **in writing** that it is **authorised to accept service** of the claim form, the claim form **must** be served on that solicitor. Personal service on a director, or service at the registered office, would normally be valid, but they are not permitted once a solicitor has been nominated, so service here is invalid. Sending a copy to the solicitors afterwards does not cure the defect."},{"t":"dr-responding","p":"FLK1","q":"Rashida's firm acts for the defendant in a professional negligence claim. The particulars of claim were deemed served on 6 July and the firm filed an acknowledgment of service on 14 July, stating that the whole claim is defended. The defendant's expert evidence will not be ready for some time, and Rashida estimates that she needs six weeks beyond the normal deadline to prepare the defence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Rashida can obtain the extra time?","o":["The parties can agree the full six weeks in writing, and the defendant must then notify the court of the agreement.","The parties can agree the full six weeks in writing, and no notification to the court is required for the extension.","No extension of any kind can be granted, because the acknowledgment of service has already extended time for the defence.","The parties can agree up to 28 more days in writing, but any longer extension requires an application to the court.","Any extension requires an application to the court, because the parties cannot agree to extend time for a defence."],"a":3,"e":"Where an acknowledgment of service is filed, the defence is due **28 days after service of the particulars of claim**, and under **CPR 15.5** the parties may agree in writing to extend that by **up to 28 days**, provided the defendant **notifies the court**. A six-week extension goes beyond what the parties can agree, so the extra time over 28 days needs a **court order**. The acknowledgment does not prevent a further extension, and the parties do have a limited power to agree one."},{"t":"dr-responding","p":"FLK1","q":"Particulars of claim for an unpaid invoice of £4,200 were deemed served on Pemberton Ltd on 2 March 2026. Pemberton filed nothing at court. On 16 March, after realising it owed the money, Pemberton transferred to the claimant the full £4,200 together with the interest and fixed costs claimed. The claimant's solicitors, unaware of the payment, requested default judgment, which was entered on 18 March. Pemberton applies to have it set aside.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the court will do?","o":["It must set the judgment aside, because the whole claim had been paid before the judgment was entered.","It may set the judgment aside only if the defendant shows a real prospect of successfully defending the claim.","It will refuse to set the judgment aside, because the time for an acknowledgment of service had expired.","It may set the judgment aside, applying the three-stage test for relief from sanctions to the defendant's delay.","It must set the judgment aside, but only on condition that the defendant pays the claimant's costs thrown away."],"a":0,"e":"Under **CPR 13.2** the court **must** set aside a default judgment that was **wrongly entered**, which includes a judgment entered after the **whole claim** (including interest and costs) had been **paid**. Although the 14 days had expired, so the conditions for default judgment were otherwise met, the payment means the judgment should never have been entered. The real prospect test and the relief from sanctions approach apply only to the discretionary power in CPR 13.3, and there is no merits test or condition attached to the mandatory power."},{"t":"dr-statements-of-case","p":"FLK1","q":"Cormac's company sued a supplier for the cost of replacing defective machinery. The particulars of claim were served two months ago and a defence has been filed. Cormac now realises that his company also lost profits while the machinery was out of action and wants to add a claim for them. The limitation period has not expired. The supplier refuses to consent to any amendment.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the claimant can add the new claim?","o":["It can amend without permission, because a statement of case may be amended at any time before trial.","It cannot amend, because a claim may not be changed once the defendant has filed a defence.","It must apply to the court for permission and will usually pay the costs of and arising from the amendment.","It must issue a separate claim for the lost profits and then apply for the two claims to be heard together.","It must apply to the court for permission, which will be granted only if the supplier would suffer no prejudice."],"a":2,"e":"Once a statement of case has been **served**, it can be amended only with the **written consent of all other parties** or the **court's permission** (CPR 17.1). The court applies the overriding objective, considering whether the amendment has a **real prospect of success** and the stage of the case, and the amending party usually pays the **costs of and arising from** the amendment. Free amendment applies only **before service**, and there is no absolute bar after a defence or a rule that any prejudice prevents an amendment, because costs can often compensate for it."},{"t":"dr-statements-of-case","p":"FLK1","q":"Priya sued her neighbour, Daniel, in negligence after his tree fell onto her car. Daniel's defence denies negligence and also alleges that Priya was contributorily negligent in parking under a tree she knew to be unsafe. Priya's solicitor has not filed a reply. Daniel's solicitor now argues that, by failing to reply, Priya has admitted the allegation of contributory negligence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of Priya not filing a reply?","o":["She is taken to admit the allegation, because a claimant must respond to every new allegation in the defence.","She is taken to admit the allegation unless she obtains the court's permission to file a reply out of time.","Daniel may obtain judgment on the issue of contributory negligence, because no reply was filed in time.","She cannot dispute the allegation at trial, because she has not pleaded any response to it.","She is not taken to admit the allegation, and Daniel must still prove the contributory negligence he alleges."],"a":4,"e":"A **reply** (the claimant's answer to new matters in the defence) is optional, and under **CPR 16.7(1)**, if a claimant **does not file a reply**, the **defendant must prove** the matters raised in the defence, so Priya is **not taken to admit** them. Daniel, who alleges contributory negligence, therefore still has to prove it. The tempting confusion is with a **defence to counterclaim**, which is essential, because without one the claimant risks default judgment on the counterclaim."},{"t":"dr-interim","p":"FLK1","q":"Eastgate Coffee Ltd claims that a large, financially sound competitor is selling a blend under a name that breaches a contractual restriction in a settlement agreement between them. Eastgate has issued proceedings and applies for an interim injunction until trial. The evidence shows there is a serious question to be tried. Eastgate's only loss would be lost sales, which its accountants can calculate precisely from its monthly figures.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court is likely to deal with the application?","o":["It will grant the injunction, because Eastgate has shown that there is a serious question to be tried.","It will refuse the injunction, because damages would adequately compensate Eastgate and the defendant can pay them.","It will refuse the injunction, because Eastgate has not shown that it is more likely than not to win at trial.","It will grant the injunction, because the balance of convenience always favours keeping matters as they are.","It will grant the injunction, provided that Eastgate pays a sum equal to the defendant's lost profits into court."],"a":1,"e":"Under the **American Cyanamid** approach, once a **serious question to be tried** is shown, the court asks whether **damages would be an adequate remedy** for the claimant and whether the defendant could pay them; if so, an interim injunction is normally refused. Here Eastgate's loss is precisely calculable and the defendant is solvent, so the court does not reach the balance of convenience. A serious question is necessary but not sufficient, and the claimant does not have to show it will probably win."},{"t":"dr-interim","p":"FLK1","q":"In a personal injury claim, the defendant's insurer voluntarily paid the claimant £20,000 before trial to help with his rehabilitation costs, while continuing to dispute the amount of his losses. At the start of the trial, the claimant's counsel wants to tell the judge about the £20,000 payment, arguing that it shows the defendant accepts that the claim is worth a substantial sum. The defendant does not agree to this.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The payment may be mentioned, because it was made voluntarily and not under a court order.","The payment may be mentioned, but only when the claimant's counsel opens the case on quantum.","The payment may be mentioned, because the fact of payment is relevant to the value of the claim.","The payment must not be mentioned until all questions of liability and quantum have been decided.","The payment must not be mentioned at any stage, and the judge will not deduct it from the damages."],"a":3,"e":"The **fact that an interim payment has been made**, whether **voluntarily** or under a court order, must **not be disclosed to the trial judge** until all questions of **liability and quantum** have been decided, unless the defendant agrees (CPR 25). This prevents the judge being influenced by the payment. Once the amount is decided, the court **adjusts the final judgment** to take account of the £20,000, and can order repayment if the claimant was overpaid."},{"t":"dr-case-management","p":"FLK1","q":"Bethan sues a firm of surveyors for £68,000, alleging that they negligently failed to spot subsidence in a house she bought. There is one claimant and one defendant. Each party wishes to call one expert surveyor to give oral evidence. Both parties' directions questionnaires estimate that the trial will last four days.\n\nTo which track is the claim most likely to be allocated?","o":["The small claims track","The fast track","The intermediate track, in complexity band 2","The intermediate track, in complexity band 4","The multi-track"],"a":4,"e":"A claim worth £25,000 to £100,000 normally goes to the **intermediate track** only if, among other things, the **trial will last no more than 3 days** and no more than two experts per party give oral evidence. Although the value, the number of parties and the expert evidence fit the intermediate track, the **four-day trial estimate** exceeds the limit, so the claim is likely to go to the **multi-track**. The fast track is for claims up to £25,000 with a one-day trial."},{"t":"dr-case-management","p":"FLK1","q":"In a multi-track contract claim, the court ordered the claimant to serve its witness statements by 2 February, failing which it could not call any witness of fact. The claimant's solicitor, who was busy with other cases, served them on 23 March. Because the defendant then needed time to respond, the trial fixed for April had to be adjourned. The claimant has now applied for relief from the sanction.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome?","o":["Relief is likely to be refused, because the breach was serious and significant and there was no good reason for it.","Relief is likely to be granted, because any prejudice to the defendant can be compensated by an order for costs.","Relief is likely to be granted, because a client should not be penalised for the default of their solicitor.","Relief cannot be granted, because the court has no power to relieve a party from a sanction it has already imposed.","Relief is likely to be granted, because the claimant applied before the defendant had asked for judgment."],"a":0,"e":"Applications for **relief from sanctions** under **CPR 3.9** follow the three **Denton** stages: the **seriousness and significance** of the breach, **why** it happened, and **all the circumstances**, giving particular weight to efficiency and proportionate cost and to enforcing compliance with orders. A delay of about seven weeks that caused the **trial date to be lost** is serious and significant, and **pressure of work** is not a good reason, so relief is likely to be refused. The court does have power to grant relief, and the fact that costs could compensate the defendant does not outweigh these factors."},{"t":"dr-evidence","p":"FLK1","q":"Sanjay is suing a garage over a faulty car repair. A former mechanic at the garage, Mr Achterberg, gave Sanjay's solicitors a signed statement describing how the repair was rushed. Mr Achterberg has since moved permanently to Canada and has refused to attend the trial or give evidence by video link. Sanjay still wants to rely on the statement.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Sanjay cannot rely on the statement, because hearsay is admissible only with the court's permission.","Sanjay can rely on the statement only if the garage agrees, because Mr Achterberg cannot be cross-examined.","Sanjay can rely on the statement, but should serve a hearsay notice explaining why Mr Achterberg will not be called.","Sanjay cannot rely on the statement unless Mr Achterberg confirms it in an affidavit sworn before a notary.","Sanjay can rely on the statement, but only if he serves a hearsay notice, as otherwise it is inadmissible."],"a":2,"e":"Hearsay is **admissible** in civil proceedings (**Civil Evidence Act 1995**), but a party relying on a statement from someone who **will not be called** must serve a **hearsay notice** identifying the evidence and giving the **reason** the maker will not attend (s.2 and CPR 33.2). **Failure to give notice does not make the evidence inadmissible**; it may affect its **weight** and the **costs**. The garage's protection is to apply to cross-examine the maker or attack his credibility, not to veto the evidence."},{"t":"dr-evidence","p":"FLK1","q":"In a fast track claim over water damage to a flat, the court directed a single joint expert, a chartered building surveyor, to report on the cause of a leak. The report was served on both parties on 3 August. The defendant's solicitor finds one paragraph on the timing of the leak ambiguous and wants to clarify what the expert meant.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the defendant may do?","o":["Instruct its own surveyor to give oral evidence at trial, because it is entitled to challenge a joint report.","Require the expert to attend trial for cross-examination, because the report deals with a disputed issue.","Write to the expert privately to ask what was meant, sending a copy of the reply to the claimant afterwards.","Put written questions to the expert to clarify the report, once only and within 28 days of service of the report.","Apply to the court to exclude the report, because an ambiguous report does not comply with the expert's duty."],"a":3,"e":"Under **CPR 35.6** a party may put **written questions** to another party's expert or a **single joint expert** (one expert instructed for both parties), **once only**, **within 28 days of service** of the report and only to **clarify** it, unless the court permits or the other party agrees otherwise; the answers form part of the report. A party who wants its own expert needs the court's permission, and on the fast track an expert attends trial only if necessary in the interests of justice. Private contact with a joint expert by one party is improper."},{"t":"dr-disclosure","p":"FLK1","q":"A customer slipped on a wet floor in a supermarket. The next morning the store manager completed the company's standard accident report form, as company policy requires after every incident, so that safety procedures could be reviewed. No claim had been threatened at that time. Months later the customer sued, and the report shows that the floor had not been checked for two hours.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the status of the report in standard disclosure?","o":["It is protected by litigation privilege, because accidents of this kind commonly lead to claims.","It must be disclosed and may be inspected, because it was not made for the dominant purpose of litigation.","It is protected by legal advice privilege, because it was later sent to the supermarket's solicitors.","It need not be disclosed, because standard disclosure does not include documents that harm a party's own case.","It must be disclosed, but inspection may be withheld because the report is an internal confidential document."],"a":1,"e":"**Litigation privilege** protects documents made for the **dominant purpose** of litigation that is reasonably in contemplation; a routine report prepared under company policy to review safety procedures, before any claim was threatened, does not meet that test. Legal advice privilege covers communications between lawyer and client for advice, and sending an existing document to solicitors later does not make it privileged. **Standard disclosure** expressly includes documents that **adversely affect** a party's own case, and confidentiality alone is not a ground for withholding inspection."},{"t":"dr-disclosure","p":"FLK1","q":"During inspection of documents in a breach of contract claim, the claimant's solicitors provided an electronic folder to the defendant's solicitor, Ruth. The folder included a memo headed \"Privileged and confidential: counsel's advice on merits\", in which counsel advised the claimant that its claim was weak. It appears to have been included by mistake. Ruth's client wants to use the memo at trial.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The defendant may use the memo freely, because privilege is lost once a document has been made available for inspection.","The defendant may use the memo, but only to challenge the credibility of the claimant's witnesses at trial.","The defendant may use the memo if Ruth gives the claimant notice of her intention to do so within 14 days.","The defendant may not use the memo in any circumstances, and Ruth must report the claimant's solicitors to the SRA.","The defendant may use the memo only with the court's permission, which is unlikely as the mistake was obvious."],"a":4,"e":"Under **CPR 31.20**, where a party **inadvertently** allows a **privileged** document to be inspected, the receiving party may use it **only with the court's permission**. The court will usually refuse permission, and may grant an injunction, where the mistake would have been **obvious to a reasonable solicitor**, as it is with a memo headed as counsel's advice. Ruth should not read further and should return it and tell the other side; privilege is not automatically lost by a mistaken disclosure."},{"t":"dr-trial","p":"FLK1","q":"Lucia is the claimant in a fast track claim listed for trial on Thursday 18 June 2026. Her key witness, a former colleague, has said he will not come to court voluntarily. Lucia's solicitor has obtained a witness summons requiring him to attend the trial and intends to serve it on him personally.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the latest date by which the summons must be served if it is to be binding without a further court order?","o":["Thursday 4 June 2026, with no need to offer the witness any money","Thursday 11 June 2026, together with an offer of conduct money","Wednesday 10 June 2026, together with an offer of conduct money","Monday 15 June 2026, together with an offer of conduct money","Wednesday 17 June 2026, with no need to offer the witness any money"],"a":2,"e":"A **witness summons** (a court document compelling a person to attend) is binding only if served at least **7 days before** the date the witness must attend, and the witness must be offered **conduct money** (a sum for travel and loss of time) when it is served (CPR 34). Under **CPR 2.8** this means **7 clear days**, so neither the day of service nor the trial day counts: service on Wednesday 10 June leaves the seven clear days from 11 to 17 June. Service on 11 June would be one day short."},{"t":"dr-trial","p":"FLK1","q":"Gareth, a defendant in a County Court contract claim, did not attend his trial because he had confused the date. The judge proceeded in his absence and gave judgment for the claimant. Gareth applied to set the judgment aside four days later. His evidence shows his mistake was genuine, but his draft case relies on a defence that the judge had already ruled at a case management hearing could not succeed in law.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court is likely to deal with the application?","o":["It will refuse the application, because the defendant has no reasonable prospect of success at a retrial.","It will set the judgment aside, because the defendant acted promptly and his mistake was a genuine one.","It must set the judgment aside, because a judgment given in a party's absence is not a decision on the merits.","It will refuse the application, because the only way to challenge a judgment given at trial is by appeal.","It will set the judgment aside, on condition that the defendant pays the amount of the judgment into court."],"a":0,"e":"Under **CPR 39.3(5)** the court may set aside a judgment given in a party's absence **only if** the applicant **acted promptly**, had a **good reason for not attending** and has a **reasonable prospect of success** at a retrial. All three conditions must be met, so even if Gareth's promptness and genuine mistake were enough for the first two, the lack of any arguable defence is fatal. There is no automatic right to have such a judgment set aside, and an application under CPR 39.3(3) is an alternative to an appeal."},{"t":"dr-costs","p":"FLK1","q":"Ravensworth Holdings Ltd sued a contractor for defective building work on the multi-track. Early in the case it made a valid Part 36 offer to accept £600,000 in settlement, which the contractor did not accept. At trial the claimant was awarded damages of £800,000. The court does not consider it unjust to apply the usual Part 36 consequences.\n\nWhat additional amount must the contractor pay under the Part 36 rules?","o":["£40,000","£80,000","£65,000","£75,000","£20,000"],"a":2,"e":"Where a judgment is **at least as advantageous** to the claimant as its own Part 36 offer, **CPR 36.17(4)(d)** adds an **additional amount** of **10% of the damages up to £500,000** and **5% of any amount above £500,000**, capped at **£75,000** in total (alongside enhanced interest and indemnity costs). Here that is 10% of £500,000 (£50,000) plus 5% of £300,000 (£15,000), giving £65,000, which is below the cap. £80,000 wrongly applies 10% to the whole award, and £75,000 is the cap, not the figure."},{"t":"dr-costs","p":"FLK1","q":"Northbank Ventures Ltd, a company incorporated in England eight months ago, has sued Ellison Engineering Ltd for £300,000, alleging breach of a consultancy agreement. Northbank's only filed accounts show no assets and no trading income. Ellison has a strong defence and expects its costs to be substantial, and is worried that it will never recover them even if it wins.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the most appropriate step for Ellison?","o":["Apply for security for costs, because there is reason to believe the claimant will be unable to pay costs if ordered.","Apply for summary judgment, because a claimant that cannot meet an adverse costs order has no real prospect of success.","Apply for security for costs, but only if it can show that the claimant is resident outside England and Wales.","Apply for an order requiring the claimant's directors to guarantee personally any costs that are awarded at trial.","Apply for an interim payment from the claimant, equal to the costs it expects to incur in defending the claim."],"a":0,"e":"**Security for costs** (an order that a claimant pay money into court or provide a guarantee to protect the defendant's costs) may be ordered under **CPR 25** if it is **just**, and one of the conditions is that the claimant is a **company** and there is **reason to believe it will be unable to pay** the defendant's costs if ordered to do so. Residence abroad is a separate condition, not a requirement. A claimant's lack of funds says nothing about the merits, so it is no basis for summary judgment, and interim payments are made by defendants towards damages."},{"t":"dr-appeals","p":"FLK1","q":"On Thursday 4 June 2026 a District Judge sitting in the County Court gave judgment for the claimant at the end of a fast track trial. The defendant believes the judge misinterpreted a key clause in the contract. The defendant did not ask the District Judge for permission to appeal at the hearing, and the District Judge gave no direction about time.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the defendant may appeal?","o":["It may appeal to a High Court Judge without permission, filing its notice within 14 days of the decision.","It must ask the District Judge for permission, and an appeal can no longer be brought because it did not do so.","It may appeal to the Court of Appeal with permission, filing its notice within 21 days of the sealed order.","It may seek permission from a Circuit Judge, filing its appellant's notice within 21 days of the decision.","It may appeal to a Circuit Judge without permission, provided both parties agree to extend the time limit."],"a":3,"e":"An appeal from a **District Judge** of the County Court goes to a **Circuit Judge**, and **permission** is required (CPR 52.3); it may be sought from the lower court at the hearing or, if not sought or refused there, from the **appeal court** in the appellant's notice. The notice must be filed within **21 days after the date of the decision** (CPR 52.12), not from receipt of the sealed order, and the parties **cannot agree** to extend that time. The High Court Judge hears appeals from Circuit Judges, and the Court of Appeal from High Court Judges."},{"t":"dr-enforcement","p":"FLK1","q":"Daria obtained a County Court judgment for £38,000 against Kwame, a self-employed builder. Kwame has not paid. An order to obtain information shows that he owns his house in his sole name, with substantial equity, but keeps very little money in his bank account and has no employer. Daria wants the most effective way to make sure she is paid.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the most suitable method of enforcement?","o":["An attachment of earnings order, requiring deductions to be made from Kwame's earnings from his building work.","A charging order over Kwame's house, followed if necessary by an application for an order for sale.","A third party debt order against Kwame's bank account, which will also catch any later deposits he makes.","A writ of control, allowing enforcement agents to take control of and sell Kwame's house to pay the debt.","A bankruptcy petition, because a judgment creditor of an individual must use insolvency to reach his home."],"a":1,"e":"A **charging order** secures the judgment debt on the debtor's **land**, making the creditor a secured creditor, and if the debt remains unpaid the creditor can apply for an **order for sale** of the property; it suits a debtor with **equity in property** but little cash. An **attachment of earnings** order is not available against the **self-employed**, and a **third party debt order** catches only the credit balance at the time the interim order is served, not later deposits. A writ of control is used against goods, not land, and bankruptcy is optional, not required."},{"t":"dr-enforcement","p":"FLK1","q":"Hamid obtained a County Court judgment for £9,500 against Yvonne, who has not paid. Yvonne has worked as a hospital pharmacist for the same NHS trust for twelve years and is paid a regular monthly salary. She rents her flat, owns no valuable goods, and her only bank account is usually overdrawn.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the most suitable method of enforcement?","o":["A charging order, which would secure the judgment debt against the flat in which Yvonne lives.","An attachment of earnings order, requiring Yvonne's employer to deduct regular sums from her salary.","A third party debt order, which would freeze the money in Yvonne's bank account until the final hearing.","A writ of control, which would allow enforcement agents to take control of and sell Yvonne's goods.","A winding-up petition, which would put pressure on Yvonne to pay the judgment debt in full."],"a":1,"e":"An **attachment of earnings order** (County Court only) requires the debtor's **employer** to deduct regular sums from the debtor's **earnings**, leaving a **protected earnings rate**, and suits a debtor in stable employment such as Yvonne. A **charging order** needs an interest in land, and Yvonne only rents; a **third party debt order** is useless against an overdrawn account; and taking control of goods achieves nothing where there are no valuable goods. Winding-up applies to companies, not individuals."}],"dr-p2":[{"t":"dr-options","p":"FLK1","q":"Hollins Fabrications Ltd sues Ardent Retail Ltd in the County Court for £140,000 over a failed shopfitting contract. At the first case management conference, the District Judge proposes to order both parties to attend a mediation within the next eight weeks and to stay the claim for that period. Ardent's director tells the judge that his company will not take part, because \"nobody can be forced to mediate\" and he wants a trial.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the court's power in this situation?","o":["The court can only encourage mediation, because ordering an unwilling party to mediate would breach its right of access to the court.","The court can order mediation only if both parties consent, so Ardent's refusal means the claim must be listed for trial.","The court can order the parties to engage in mediation if the order is proportionate and does not impair the essence of their right to a trial.","The court can order mediation, and any settlement terms the mediator recommends will then be binding on both parties.","The court can order mediation, and if Ardent fails to attend its defence will automatically be struck out."],"a":2,"e":"Since 1 October 2024 the CPR expressly allow the court to **order the parties to engage in ADR** (and to stay proceedings for that purpose), provided the order is **proportionate** and does not impair the very essence of the right to a fair trial (Churchill). Option A states the old view, now overruled, that unwilling parties cannot be compelled. Option D is wrong because a mediator does not impose terms: any settlement is the parties' own agreement, and option E is wrong because the usual consequence of failing to engage is a **costs** sanction, not automatic striking out."},{"t":"dr-options","p":"FLK1","q":"Two family-run companies, Penhallow Dairies Ltd and Marchetti Foods Ltd, have traded with each other for 20 years. They now disagree about whether a price review clause in their supply agreement allows Penhallow to increase its prices by 12%. Both want to keep trading together, to keep the disagreement private, and to resolve it quickly and cheaply. Neither is willing to have a third party impose a decision on them, and the agreement contains no dispute resolution clause.\n\nWhich of the following methods of dispute resolution best meets their aims?","o":["Mediation, because a neutral third party helps them negotiate their own confidential settlement without imposing an outcome.","Arbitration, because the arbitrator's award would be private and could be enforced in the same way as a court judgment.","Litigation in the County Court, because a judge can give an authoritative ruling on the meaning of the price review clause.","Arbitration, because the parties could agree that the award will not be binding unless both of them accept it.","Litigation on the small claims track, because it is quick and the hearing would be held in private."],"a":0,"e":"**Mediation** is a confidential, without prejudice process in which a neutral mediator helps the parties reach their **own** settlement; nobody imposes a decision, which suits parties who want to preserve a long-term relationship. Options B and D are wrong because **arbitration** produces a **binding** award imposed by the arbitrator, which these parties do not want. Options C and E are wrong because litigation is public, adversarial and ends in an imposed judgment."},{"t":"dr-limitation","p":"FLK1","q":"Zara was born on 3 May 2006. On 10 August 2021, aged 15, she suffered a serious knee injury when a poorly maintained climbing wall at a leisure centre collapsed. She knew at once that the injury was serious and that the leisure centre operator was responsible. No claim was made while she was a child. It is now September 2026 and Zara, aged 20, consults a solicitor for the first time.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the limitation position on Zara's personal injury claim?","o":["It became time-barred on 10 August 2024, three years after the accident, and can proceed only if the court exercises its discretion.","It became time-barred on 10 August 2026, five years after the accident, because the period for a child is extended by two years.","It became time-barred on 3 May 2024, when Zara turned 18, because she knew all the relevant facts from the date of the accident.","It remains in time until 3 May 2030, six years after Zara's 18th birthday, because the claim arises from an occupier's breach of duty.","It remains in time until 3 May 2027, three years after Zara's 18th birthday, because time did not run while she was a child."],"a":4,"e":"**Time does not run while a claimant is under 18** if they were a child when the cause of action accrued (LA 1980 s.28), so the 3-year personal injury period starts on her 18th birthday and ends on her **21st birthday**, 3 May 2027. Options A and C wrongly start the clock at the accident or treat her knowledge as overriding the disability rule, and her knowledge does not matter while she is a child. Option D applies the 6-year period, but any claim for damages for personal injury attracts the **3-year** period, however it is framed."},{"t":"dr-limitation","p":"FLK1","q":"Ifeoma and Paul bought a house in April 2011, relying on a survey report prepared by a chartered surveyor and sent to them on 14 March 2011. The survey negligently failed to mention signs of subsidence. In February 2026 cracks appeared, and an engineer told them for the first time that the subsidence had been visible in 2011 and should have been reported. It is now September 2026 and no claim has been issued.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether a negligence claim against the surveyor is in time?","o":["It is in time until February 2029, because the claim may be brought within three years of the date they first had the relevant knowledge.","It is time-barred, because more than 15 years have passed since the negligent survey, whatever the date of their knowledge.","It is in time until February 2032, because the six-year period for negligence runs from when the damage was discovered.","It is time-barred, unless the court decides that it would be equitable to disapply the limitation period in the circumstances.","It is in time until February 2029, because the longstop runs from the date of knowledge rather than the date of the survey."],"a":1,"e":"For latent damage in negligence the claimant has the later of 6 years from the damage or 3 years from the date of knowledge (LA 1980 s.14A), but this is subject to an **absolute 15-year longstop running from the negligent act or omission** (s.14B). The negligent survey was on 14 March 2011, so the longstop expired on 14 March 2026, which defeats the knowledge-based period in option A. Option D is wrong because the **s.33 discretion** applies only to personal injury and fatal accident claims, and option E wrongly runs the longstop from the date of knowledge rather than the negligent act."},{"t":"dr-pre-action","p":"FLK1","q":"Meera's company believes its former accountants negligently failed to claim valuable tax reliefs. Her solicitor sent the accountants a letter of claim complying with the Pre-Action Protocol for Professional Negligence on Monday 2 February 2026. The accountants acknowledged the letter on Monday 16 February 2026 but have said nothing further. Limitation is not an issue for several years. Meera wants to issue proceedings now.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position under the protocol?","o":["The accountants should have sent a full response within 14 days of the letter of claim, so Meera's company may issue proceedings now.","The accountants should have sent a full response within 21 days of the letter of claim, so Meera's company may issue proceedings now.","The accountants have until 2 June 2026, four months after the letter of claim, to send a letter of response, and issue should wait until then.","The accountants have until 16 May 2026, three months after their acknowledgment, to send a letter of response, and issue should wait until then.","The accountants have until 2 May 2026, three months after the letter of claim, to send a letter of response, and issue should wait until then."],"a":3,"e":"Under the **Professional Negligence Pre-Action Protocol** the professional acknowledges within 21 days and then sends a **letter of response within 3 months of the acknowledgment**, here by 16 May 2026. Option B confuses the 21-day acknowledgment with the full response, and option A uses the general 14-day period for simple cases under the Practice Direction on Pre-Action Conduct. Option C uses the 4-month period from the Clinical Disputes protocol, and option E counts from the wrong starting date; since limitation is not pressing, issuing early risks a costs sanction."},{"t":"dr-pre-action","p":"FLK1","q":"Corvan Plastics Ltd sent Delahaye Packaging Ltd a detailed letter before claim for £70,000 under a supply contract, enclosing key documents and proposing mediation. No pre-action protocol applies. Delahaye did not respond to the letter or to two reminders. Corvan issued proceedings, Delahaye defended, and at trial Corvan obtained judgment for the full amount. The judge finds that Delahaye's failure to engage before proceedings was a substantial breach of the Practice Direction on Pre-Action Conduct.\n\nWhich of the following best describes an order the court may make because of Delahaye's conduct?","o":["An order that Delahaye pay interest on the damages at a higher rate, up to 10% above base rate, for all or part of the period.","An order that Delahaye's defence be struck out and judgment entered again on the indemnity basis for the full claim.","An order that Corvan be deprived of interest on its damages for the period during which Delahaye failed to respond.","An order that Delahaye pay Corvan an additional sum equal to 10% of the damages as a penalty for its non-compliance.","An order that Delahaye cannot appeal against the judgment because of its failure to comply with the practice direction."],"a":0,"e":"Where a **defendant** has not complied with pre-action conduct, the court may order it to pay **interest on damages at a higher rate, up to 10% above base rate**, as well as costs sanctions such as costs on the indemnity basis. Option C describes the sanction for a **claimant** at fault, so it is the wrong way round. Option B is pointless after judgment, and options D and E describe sanctions that the practice direction does not provide."},{"t":"dr-parties-law","p":"FLK1","q":"Rosalind instructed Okafor Lindqvist Architects, a firm of three architects practising together as an ordinary partnership (not an LLP) from an office in Leeds, to design an extension to her house. She alleges that the design was negligent. She does not know the full names of all three partners and asks how the claim should be brought against the firm.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Rosalind may bring the claim?","o":["She must name each of the three partners individually as defendants, because a partnership has no separate legal personality.","She must sue the senior partner alone as a representative defendant, and judgment will then bind the other two partners.","She may sue the partners in the name of the firm, and can require the firm to state the names of the partners at the relevant time.","She may sue only the partner who prepared the design, because the other partners have no personal liability for his negligence.","She must first apply to court for permission to sue the partnership, because the firm is not registered at Companies House."],"a":2,"e":"A claim against partners who carry on business under a firm name is brought **against the name of the firm** (PD 7A para 7.3), and the claimant can require a **partnership membership statement** giving the **names of the partners** when the cause of action accrued (PD 7A para 8). Option A is the most tempting, but although a partnership has no legal personality the rules allow the firm name to be used, so naming each partner is not compulsory. Option D is wrong because partners are jointly liable for the firm's wrongful acts, and no permission is needed to sue a partnership."},{"t":"dr-parties-law","p":"FLK1","q":"Clearwater Biotech Ltd, a company with its central administration in Cambridge, contracted with Van Dijk Vertalingen BV, a translation company whose central administration is in Rotterdam, to translate clinical trial documents. All the translation work was done in the Netherlands and invoices were issued in euros. The written contract contains no choice of law clause and no other indication of which law the parties intended. Clearwater now sues Van Dijk in England for breach of contract, alleging serious translation errors.\n\nWhich law is most likely to govern the contract?","o":["English law, because the claim is being heard by an English court and the court applies its own law to the dispute.","English law, because the claimant is habitually resident in England and suffered its financial loss there.","English law, because the law of the country where the damage occurs governs claims arising from defective services.","Dutch law, because the contract was concluded electronically and the last acceptance was sent from the Netherlands.","Dutch law, because a services contract with no choice of law is governed by the law of the provider's habitual residence."],"a":4,"e":"Under **Rome I** (assimilated law), where there is no choice of law a **contract for the provision of services** is governed by the law of the country where the **service provider** is habitually resident (Art 4(1)(b)); for a company this is its place of central administration, the Netherlands. Option A confuses **procedure**, which follows the court hearing the case, with the law governing the contract. Option B wrongly makes the claimant's residence or place of loss decisive, and option D relies on where the contract was concluded, which Art 4 does not use. Option C applies the **Rome II** rule for non-contractual claims, and nothing suggests the contract is manifestly more closely connected with England."},{"t":"dr-where-to-start","p":"FLK1","q":"Dr Funmi Adeyemi runs a dental practice in Swindon. A former patient has published a post on a local community website falsely stating that she performs unnecessary treatment to inflate her fees. Her solicitor estimates that damages for libel are likely to be about £12,000. There has been no agreement with the former patient about where any claim should be heard.\n\nIn which court should the claim be issued?","o":["The County Court, because the claim is worth less than £100,000 and must therefore start there.","The High Court, because the County Court has no jurisdiction to hear a defamation claim unless the parties agree in writing.","The County Court, because the claim is likely to be allocated to the fast track and fast track claims cannot be heard in the High Court.","Either court at the claimant's choice, because the £100,000 threshold does not apply to claims that are not for a debt.","The County Court, because the claim is a professional reputation claim worth less than the £50,000 threshold for injury claims."],"a":1,"e":"**Defamation claims must be issued in the High Court** (in practice the Media and Communications List of the King's Bench Division), because the County Court has **no jurisdiction** over libel and slander unless the parties agree in writing (County Courts Act 1984 s.15(2)(c); PD 7A para 2.9). Option A applies the general £100,000 rule for money claims, which does not override the specific defamation rule. Option E wrongly borrows the £50,000 threshold, which applies only to personal injury claims."},{"t":"dr-where-to-start","p":"FLK1","q":"Brennan Tool Hire Ltd is owed £72,000 by a customer under a straightforward equipment hire agreement. The customer has given no reason for not paying. Brennan's solicitor issued the claim in the King's Bench Division of the High Court because she thought it would be dealt with more quickly there. The customer's solicitors say the claim was issued in the wrong court.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the consequences of issuing in the High Court?","o":["The claim is a nullity, so Brennan must issue a fresh claim in the County Court and pay the costs of the first claim.","The claim will automatically be struck out, but Brennan may apply to reinstate it in the County Court within 14 days.","There are no consequences, because a claimant may issue any money claim worth more than £50,000 in the High Court.","The claim remains valid, but it may be transferred to the County Court and Brennan's recoverable costs may be reduced by up to 25%.","The claim remains valid, but Brennan's recoverable costs will be reduced by 50% because the claim did not justify a High Court judge."],"a":3,"e":"A money claim (other than personal injury) worth **£100,000 or less must start in the County Court**, but a claim started in the wrong court is **not invalid**: the court can **transfer** it (CPR 30), and may **reduce the claimant's recoverable costs by up to 25%** (Senior Courts Act 1981 s.51(8)). Options A and B are wrong because there is no nullity or automatic striking out. Option C applies the £50,000 threshold, which is for personal injury only, and option E states the wrong percentage."},{"t":"dr-issue-service","p":"FLK1","q":"A claim form was issued on behalf of Nadia on Monday 12 January 2026. Her solicitor intended to serve it on the defendant, who has lived at the same address throughout, but the file was mislaid and nothing was done. The solicitor discovered the error on 20 May 2026 and applied the next day for an order extending the time for service of the claim form. The defendant opposes the application.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court is likely to deal with the application?","o":["It is likely to refuse it, because a late application succeeds only if the claimant took all reasonable steps to serve.","It is likely to grant it, because Nadia's solicitor applied promptly once the error was discovered and the delay was only a few days.","It is likely to grant it, because the defendant will suffer no real prejudice and the court applies the overriding objective.","It is likely to grant it if Nadia shows a good reason for the failure, such as the pressure of work in her solicitor's office.","It need not decide the application, because a claim form remains valid for service for six months after the date of issue."],"a":0,"e":"The claim form had to be served by 12 May 2026 (CPR 7.5). Where the application is made **after the four-month period has expired**, the court may extend time **only if** the court was responsible for service and failed to do it, or the claimant **took all reasonable steps** to serve but was unable to, **and** in either case applied promptly (CPR 7.6(3)). Here the solicitor simply did nothing, so promptness alone (option B) and lack of prejudice (option C) are not enough. Option D describes the more lenient test for an application made **before** the deadline, and option E uses the six-month period for service **outside** the jurisdiction."},{"t":"dr-issue-service","p":"FLK1","q":"In a defended contract claim, the defendant's solicitors stated in writing on their headed paper that they would accept service of documents by email at a named email address. The claimant's solicitors emailed their reply to the defence to that address at 4.45pm on Friday 6 March 2026. No bank holiday affects the dates.\n\nOn what date is the reply deemed to be served?","o":["Friday 6 March 2026, because an email is deemed served on the day it is sent if it arrives during office hours.","Sunday 8 March 2026, because a document sent electronically is deemed served on the second day after it is sent.","Tuesday 10 March 2026, because a document is deemed served on the second business day after the step required is taken.","Wednesday 11 March 2026, because a document sent after 4.30pm is deemed served three business days later.","Monday 9 March 2026, because an email sent after 4.30pm on a business day is deemed served on the next business day."],"a":4,"e":"For documents **other than the claim form**, an email sent **before 4.30pm on a business day** is deemed served that day; otherwise it is deemed served on the **next business day** (CPR 6.26), so an email sent at 4.45pm on a Friday is deemed served on Monday 9 March. Option C applies the special rule for the **claim form** (the second business day after the step taken), which does not apply to a reply. Options A, B and D misstate the time of day and the number of days."},{"t":"dr-responding","p":"FLK1","q":"Wexford Signs Ltd sues Hollybush Garden Centre Ltd for £14,500, the unpaid price of signage it supplied. Hollybush files an admission of the whole claim within the time allowed, together with a statement of its finances, and offers to pay by instalments of £300 a month. Wexford rejects the offer and wants payment in full within 14 days.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what will happen next?","o":["The claim will proceed as a defended claim, because the parties have not agreed the terms of payment.","Wexford can enter judgment for immediate payment in full, because it has rejected the offer of instalments.","The court will decide the rate of payment, taking account of Hollybush's finances, and enter judgment on those terms.","The admission will be treated as withdrawn, and Hollybush will be given 14 days to file a defence instead.","The court must enter judgment for payment at £300 a month, because a defendant's offer of instalments binds the claimant."],"a":2,"e":"Where a defendant **admits the whole of a specified sum** and asks for **time to pay**, the claimant may accept or reject the proposal; if it **rejects** it, the **court decides the rate of payment** (often on paper) and judgment is entered on those terms (CPR Part 14). Option A is wrong because liability is admitted, so there is nothing left to defend. Option B ignores the court's role in deciding the rate, and option E wrongly treats the defendant's offer as binding."},{"t":"dr-responding","p":"FLK1","q":"Thornbury Marine Ltd was served with a claim by a customer. Its contract with the customer contains a clause giving the courts of Ireland exclusive jurisdiction over any dispute. Thornbury filed an acknowledgment of service on Wednesday 3 June 2026, stating that it intended to contest the court's jurisdiction. Its solicitor then overlooked the file. On 25 June 2026 Thornbury instructs the solicitor to apply for an order declaring that the English court should not hear the claim.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Thornbury's position?","o":["It may still apply, because an application disputing jurisdiction can be made at any time before a defence is filed.","It is treated as having accepted the court's jurisdiction, because it did not apply within 14 days after filing its acknowledgment.","It may still apply, because it has 28 days after service of the particulars of claim in which to dispute jurisdiction.","It need not apply, because the court must decline jurisdiction on its own initiative where there is an exclusive jurisdiction clause.","It need not apply now, because it can raise the jurisdiction clause as a defence at trial and ask for the claim to be dismissed."],"a":1,"e":"A defendant who wishes to dispute jurisdiction must file an acknowledgment of service and then **apply within 14 days after filing it** (CPR Part 11). If it does not, it is **treated as having accepted** that the court has jurisdiction; here the 14 days ended on 17 June. Options A and C invent longer periods, and options D and E are wrong because the court does not take the point itself and a party who misses the deadline cannot revive it at trial."},{"t":"dr-statements-of-case","p":"FLK1","q":"Kestrel Bakeries Ltd sues the supplier of an industrial oven for breach of contract. Paragraph 6 of the particulars of claim alleges that the supplier received Kestrel's written notice of the oven's defects on 3 March 2026. The supplier's defence denies that the oven was defective and denies that any defect caused Kestrel's losses, giving reasons, but it says nothing at all about paragraph 6 and does not set out any case about the notice.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the defence on the allegation in paragraph 6?","o":["Kestrel must prove the allegation at trial, because a defendant is taken to require proof of anything it does not admit.","The allegation is taken to be denied, because the defence denies the claim as a whole and gives reasons for the denial.","The defence will be struck out, because a defence that fails to deal with every allegation does not comply with the rules.","The supplier is taken to admit the allegation, because it failed to deal with it and set out no case of its own on the issue.","The supplier has 14 days to amend its defence, because the court must give a defendant the chance to correct an omission."],"a":3,"e":"A defendant must state, for each allegation, whether it is admitted, denied (with reasons) or not admitted; an allegation it **fails to deal with is taken to be admitted**, unless the defence sets out the **nature of its case** on that issue, in which case it is taken to require proof (CPR 16.5). Option A is wrong because the supplier set out no case on the notice, so the default rule is admission, not proof. Option B wrongly treats a general denial as covering an allegation that is not addressed, and options C and E describe consequences the rule does not impose."},{"t":"dr-interim","p":"FLK1","q":"Halden Components Ltd sues Ravel Engineering Ltd for £55,000, the price of precision parts. Ravel's defence says that the parts failed to meet the agreed tolerances and that it rejected them. It is supported by a witness statement from Ravel's quality manager and photographs of the parts. Halden's evidence is a witness statement from its own engineer saying the parts were tested before dispatch and were within tolerance. Halden applies for summary judgment.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court is likely to deal with the application?","o":["It is likely to grant it, because Halden's test evidence is more detailed and more persuasive than Ravel's photographs.","It is likely to grant it, unless Ravel can show that it will probably succeed in its defence at trial.","It is likely to grant it, because Ravel has not yet obtained any independent expert evidence on the tolerances.","It is likely to dismiss it, but only on condition that Ravel pays the full price into court within 14 days.","It is likely to dismiss it, because there is a genuine dispute of fact which the court will not resolve on the application."],"a":4,"e":"Summary judgment is given only if the respondent has **no real prospect** (a realistic, not fanciful, prospect) of success **and** there is no other compelling reason for a trial; the court **does not conduct a mini-trial** to resolve genuinely conflicting evidence. Option A invites exactly that weighing of evidence, and option B reverses the test by requiring Ravel to show it will probably win. Option C is wrong because the court considers the evidence that can reasonably be expected at trial, and nothing suggests Ravel's defence is so weak that a condition such as a payment into court (option D) is needed."},{"t":"dr-interim","p":"FLK1","q":"Saltmarsh Capital Ltd obtained a freezing injunction against a former director, Mr Kovacs, at a without notice hearing. Its evidence said that Mr Kovacs was about to move money abroad. It did not tell the judge that, two weeks earlier, Mr Kovacs's solicitors had written offering to keep £500,000 in their client account until the claim was resolved. Saltmarsh's solicitor says the letter was overlooked by mistake. Mr Kovacs applies to discharge the injunction.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the court's approach to the application?","o":["The court may discharge the injunction, because Saltmarsh breached its duty of full and frank disclosure, even if the omission was innocent.","The court cannot discharge the injunction before trial, because a freezing injunction can be challenged only at the trial itself.","The court may discharge the injunction only if Mr Kovacs proves that Saltmarsh deliberately misled the judge about the letter.","The court cannot discharge the injunction, because Saltmarsh's cross-undertaking in damages already protects Mr Kovacs.","The court may discharge the injunction only on an appeal, because a judge cannot revisit an order made by another judge."],"a":0,"e":"An applicant at a without notice hearing owes a duty of **full and frank disclosure**: it must tell the court everything material, **including points against itself**, and a breach can lead to the order being **discharged**, whether or not the failure was deliberate. The offer to hold £500,000 was plainly relevant to the alleged **risk of dissipation**, so option C sets the bar too high. Option B is wrong because a respondent can apply promptly to set aside or vary a without notice order, and option D is wrong because the **cross-undertaking in damages** compensates for loss caused by a wrongly granted order but does not cure a failure to disclose."},{"t":"dr-case-management","p":"FLK1","q":"Grace sued a kitchen company for £7,200 because the units it fitted were defective. The claim was allocated to the small claims track. Grace won at the hearing. She had paid a solicitor £3,800 to help her prepare, paid a surveyor £600 for a report that the court allowed, and lost a day's wages of £140 to attend the hearing. The kitchen company behaved reasonably throughout.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the costs Grace is likely to recover from the kitchen company?","o":["All of her costs and expenses, because the general rule is that the losing party pays the winning party's costs.","None of her costs or expenses, because each party bears its own costs on the small claims track whatever the outcome.","Her fixed issue costs, court fee, surveyor's fee and lost wages up to £95, but not her solicitor's fees.","Her solicitor's fees up to a cap of £1,500, together with the court fees, but not the surveyor's fee or loss of earnings.","Her court fees and loss of earnings in full, but no part of the surveyor's fee, because experts are not used on this track."],"a":2,"e":"On the **small claims track** recoverable costs are very limited: **fixed issue costs**, **court fees**, loss of earnings up to **£95 a day** and **expert fees up to £750**, with further costs only where a party has behaved **unreasonably**. Option A applies the general rule, which is displaced on this track, while option B overstates the restriction because some limited sums are recoverable. Options D and E misstate what can be recovered: there is no £1,500 cap on solicitor's fees, and a permitted expert's fee is recoverable within the limit."},{"t":"dr-case-management","p":"FLK1","q":"Lindqvist Shipping Ltd's claim for £800,000 against a ship repairer has been allocated to the multi-track. The court's notice required each party to file and exchange a costs budget at least 21 days before the first case management conference. Lindqvist's solicitors forgot and filed its budget only two days before the conference. The repairer filed its budget on time.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the consequence for Lindqvist, unless the court orders otherwise?","o":["Its claim will be struck out, because filing a costs budget is a condition of continuing a multi-track claim.","It will be treated as having filed a budget of only the applicable court fees, limiting the future costs it can recover.","Its recoverable costs will be reduced by 25%, because it failed to comply with a direction given by the court.","There is no consequence, because the court can simply consider its budget at the case management conference.","Its recoverable costs will be limited to the fixed recoverable costs that would apply on the intermediate track."],"a":1,"e":"A party that **fails to file a costs budget on time** is **treated as having filed a budget comprising only the applicable court fees** (CPR 3.14), unless the court orders otherwise, so Lindqvist must apply for **relief from sanctions** (judged by the three-stage Denton approach) to recover more of its future costs. Option D ignores this automatic sanction (Mitchell, where a budget filed the day before the conference was limited to court fees). Options A, C and E describe sanctions the rule does not impose."},{"t":"dr-evidence","p":"FLK1","q":"Dariusz, a delivery driver, struck a cyclist, Ama, at a junction. He was convicted of careless driving by a magistrates' court after a trial at which he pleaded not guilty, and he did not appeal. Ama now sues Dariusz for damages for her injuries. His insurer's defence still denies that he was negligent. Ama's solicitor wants to rely on the conviction.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the conviction in the civil claim?","o":["It is inadmissible, because the magistrates' finding is merely the opinion of another court on the same facts.","It is conclusive proof of negligence, so Dariusz cannot call any evidence to dispute that he drove carelessly.","It is admissible only because Dariusz pleaded not guilty and the facts were therefore tested at a criminal trial.","It is admissible, and Dariusz is taken to have committed the offence unless he proves that he did not.","It is admissible, but Ama must still prove beyond reasonable doubt that Dariusz drove without due care and attention."],"a":3,"e":"Under the **Civil Evidence Act 1968 s.11**, a criminal conviction is **admissible** to prove that the person committed the offence, and they are **taken to have committed it unless they prove otherwise** on the balance of probabilities, so the burden shifts to Dariusz. Option B overstates the effect, because the conviction is not conclusive and he may try to disprove it. Option C is wrong because s.11 applies whether the conviction followed a guilty plea or a trial, and option E applies the criminal standard of proof, which is not used in civil claims."},{"t":"dr-disclosure","p":"FLK1","q":"Fernhill Developments Ltd sues a building contractor for delay in completing a block of flats. Standard disclosure has been ordered. While searching its records, Fernhill finds an internal email from its own project manager to a director, written during the works, saying: \"Most of the delay is down to our late design changes, not the contractor.\" The email was not written for the purpose of any litigation or legal advice. Fernhill's director asks whether it must be disclosed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Fernhill's obligation?","o":["It need not disclose the email, because standard disclosure covers only documents on which a party intends to rely at trial.","It need not disclose the email, because internal communications between a company's employees are confidential and privileged.","It must list the email and allow inspection, because standard disclosure includes documents that adversely affect a party's own case.","It must list the email but may refuse inspection, because a party can withhold any document that damages its case.","It must disclose the email only if the contractor makes a specific application to the court for documents about the delay."],"a":2,"e":"**Standard disclosure** requires a party to disclose the documents it relies on **and** documents that **adversely affect its own case** or another party's case, or support another party's case (CPR 31.6). The email is not privileged: it is neither a lawyer-client communication for legal advice nor a document created for the **dominant purpose of litigation**, so option B is wrong. Option D is wrong because inspection may be withheld only on recognised grounds such as privilege, not because a document is harmful."},{"t":"dr-disclosure","p":"FLK1","q":"Tamsin sued a furniture retailer for the late delivery of goods for her café. Before trial, the retailer's solicitor wrote to Tamsin's solicitor in a letter headed \"without prejudice\", offering £20,000 in settlement and saying: \"Our client accepts that the delivery was late, but disputes your losses.\" The offer was rejected. At trial the retailer now denies that the delivery was late. Tamsin's counsel wants to show the letter to the judge as evidence on liability.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the letter can be used?","o":["It cannot be used as evidence on liability, because it was written in a genuine attempt to settle an existing dispute.","It can be used, because a clear admission of fact is outside the protection given to settlement negotiations.","It can be used, because the protection for settlement correspondence ends once the offer has been rejected.","It can be used, because a party who changes its position at trial is taken to have waived the protection.","It can be used only if the retailer's solicitor is called to give evidence about why the letter was written."],"a":0,"e":"**Without prejudice** communications made in a **genuine attempt to settle an existing dispute** cannot be used as evidence of liability at trial, and the protection covers admissions made in them. Options B and C are the tempting errors: an admission inside a genuine settlement letter is protected, and protection does not end because the offer was rejected. The protection can be waived only with the consent of both parties, and if the letter had been marked \"without prejudice save as to costs\" it could be shown to the judge only on **costs** after judgment."},{"t":"dr-trial","p":"FLK1","q":"A fast track claim between Owusu Landscapes Ltd (the claimant) and a property management company (the defendant) is listed for trial on Monday 15 June 2026. The court has given no specific direction about the trial bundle. The defendant's solicitor asks Owusu's solicitor which party is responsible for the trial bundle and when it must be filed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The defendant must prepare and file the bundle, because the claimant bears the burden of proof and should not control the documents.","The court office prepares the bundle from the documents on the court file, and the parties need only send in their witness statements.","Each party must file its own bundle of the documents it relies on, at least 14 days before the start of the trial.","The parties must agree a bundle, and the claimant must file it at least 21 days before the start of the trial.","The claimant must prepare and file the bundle, not more than 7 days and not less than 3 days before the start of the trial."],"a":4,"e":"The **claimant** is responsible for preparing and filing the **trial bundle**, normally **not more than 7 and not less than 3 days** before the start of the trial (PD 39A), so here between 8 and 12 June 2026. The parties should try to agree its contents, but that does not shift the responsibility or change the timing, so option D is wrong. Options A, B and C misstate who prepares the bundle and invent different time limits."},{"t":"dr-costs","p":"FLK1","q":"Isabel sues a builder for £60,000 for defective work. On 1 February 2026 the builder makes a valid Part 36 offer to pay £45,000, with a relevant period of 21 days. Isabel does not accept it. At trial in October 2026 she is awarded £44,200. The judge finds nothing that would make the usual consequences unjust.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely costs order?","o":["Isabel recovers all her costs of the claim, because she won at trial and the general rule is that the loser pays.","Isabel recovers her costs up to the end of the relevant period, and pays the builder's costs from then onwards, with interest.","Isabel pays all the builder's costs of the claim from the date it started, because she failed to beat the offer.","The offer has no effect on costs, because the difference between the offer and the judgment is less than 5%.","Isabel recovers her costs but must also pay the builder interest at up to 10% above base rate on the damages awarded."],"a":1,"e":"Where a claimant **fails to obtain a judgment more advantageous than a defendant's Part 36 offer**, the court will, unless unjust, order the claimant to pay the **defendant's costs from the end of the relevant period**, with **interest** on those costs, while the claimant usually still recovers its own costs up to that date (CPR 36.17). Option D is wrong because \"more advantageous\" means better by **any amount**, however small. Option C overstates the consequence, and option E muddles in the enhanced interest that applies when a **claimant's** own offer is beaten."},{"t":"dr-costs","p":"FLK1","q":"Callum sued his former employer for damages for a back injury he says he suffered lifting heavy boxes at work. The claim was not struck out and the judge found that Callum was an honest witness, but it failed at trial because the judge was not satisfied that the work caused the injury. Callum recovered nothing and was ordered to pay the employer's costs of £35,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the employer can enforce the costs order?","o":["It can enforce the order in full, because the general rule is that the unsuccessful party pays the successful party's costs.","It can enforce the order up to 50% of the costs, because Callum's claim failed on causation rather than on liability.","It can enforce the order only after a detailed assessment, which will reduce the costs to a proportionate amount.","It cannot enforce the order, because enforcement is limited to the total of damages, interest and costs awarded to Callum, which is nil.","It cannot enforce the order against Callum, but it can recover the costs from his solicitors as wasted costs instead."],"a":3,"e":"Under **qualified one-way costs shifting (QOCS)** in personal injury claims, a costs order against the claimant can be enforced only up to the **total of any damages, interest and costs** ordered in the claimant's favour, so a claimant who recovers nothing usually pays nothing. The protection is lost only if the claim is struck out on specified grounds or found **fundamentally dishonest**, neither of which applies, so option A is wrong. Options B and C invent limits, and option E is wrong because wasted costs require improper, unreasonable or negligent conduct by the lawyers, not merely a failed claim."},{"t":"dr-appeals","p":"FLK1","q":"After a trial in the County Court, a Circuit Judge gave judgment for Ngozi on her contract claim, holding that the defendant had breached an express term. The judge rejected Ngozi's alternative argument that an implied term had also been breached. The judge granted the defendant permission to appeal, and Ngozi has just been served with the defendant's appellant's notice. Ngozi wants to argue on the appeal that, even if the judge was wrong about the express term, the judgment should be upheld because of the implied term.\n\nWhat should Ngozi do?","o":["File a respondent's notice within 14 days of being served with the appellant's notice, setting out the additional ground for upholding the decision.","Do nothing until the appeal hearing, because a respondent may raise any argument that supports the judgment without notice.","File her own appellant's notice within 21 days of the decision, because she is challenging part of the judge's reasoning.","Apply to the Court of Appeal for permission to raise the implied term argument, because the appeal will go to that court.","File a respondent's notice within 7 days of being served with the appellant's notice, setting out the additional ground for upholding the decision."],"a":0,"e":"A respondent who wants the appeal court to **uphold the decision for reasons different from or additional to** those given by the lower court must file a **respondent's notice**, within **14 days** of being served with the appellant's notice where the lower court gave permission (CPR 52.13). Option B is wrong because such arguments need a respondent's notice, and option C is wrong because Ngozi is not challenging the order itself, only adding a ground to support it. Option D misidentifies the destination: an appeal from a Circuit Judge goes to a **High Court Judge**, and option E states the wrong period."},{"t":"dr-appeals","p":"FLK1","q":"On 2 July 2026 a High Court Judge gave judgment for Ashgrove Holdings Ltd for £240,000 against Penlow Estates Ltd. The judge granted Penlow permission to appeal, and Penlow filed its appellant's notice at the Court of Appeal within time. No other order was made. Ashgrove's solicitors have now applied for a charging order over Penlow's land. Penlow's director says Ashgrove cannot enforce the judgment while the appeal is pending.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Penlow's director is right, because filing an appellant's notice automatically suspends enforcement of the judgment until the appeal is decided.","Penlow's director is right, because enforcement is suspended once the lower court has granted permission to appeal against the judgment.","Penlow's director is partly right, because Ashgrove may enforce only with the permission of the Court of Appeal while the appeal is pending.","Penlow's director is wrong, but Ashgrove must wait until 21 days after the decision before taking any steps to enforce the judgment.","Penlow's director is wrong, because an appeal does not operate as a stay unless the appeal court or the lower court orders one."],"a":4,"e":"**An appeal does not operate as a stay** of the lower court's order unless the appeal court or the lower court orders otherwise (CPR 52.16), so the judgment remains enforceable. If Penlow wants to stop enforcement it must **apply for a stay**. Options A and B are the common misconception that filing an appeal or obtaining permission suspends the judgment, and options C and D invent conditions that the rules do not impose (a money judgment is generally payable within 14 days, CPR 40.11, not 21)."},{"t":"dr-enforcement","p":"FLK1","q":"Hollis obtained a County Court judgment for £16,000 against Lorcan, a self-employed graphic designer, who has not paid. Lorcan rents his flat and owns few goods apart from the laptop and software he uses for his work. Information obtained from Lorcan shows that he has £21,000 in a current account in his sole name at a high street bank. Hollis is worried that Lorcan will move the money if he learns of any enforcement step.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the most suitable method of enforcement?","o":["An attachment of earnings order, because Lorcan receives a regular income from his design work.","A charging order over Lorcan's flat, followed if necessary by an application for an order for sale.","An interim third party debt order, made without notice to freeze the account, followed by a final hearing.","A writ of control to take Lorcan's laptop and software, because the judgment exceeds £5,000.","A third party debt order applied for on notice, so that Lorcan can object before the bank is served."],"a":2,"e":"A **third party debt order** captures money owed to the debtor by a third party, usually a **bank account in credit**; the **interim order is made without notice** and freezes the account, then a **final hearing** decides whether to make it final (CPR 72). Option A fails because attachment of earnings needs an **employer**, and Lorcan is self-employed, while option B fails because he rents rather than owns his flat. Option D is unsuitable because tools and equipment necessary for the debtor's work are **exempt** goods up to an aggregate value of £1,350 (Taking Control of Goods Regulations 2013 reg 4), and option E would defeat the purpose by warning Lorcan before the money is frozen."}],"dr-p3":[{"t":"dr-options","p":"FLK1","q":"Ashbourne Lettings Ltd successfully defended a £140,000 claim brought by a former contractor. Before trial, the contractor's solicitors wrote to Ashbourne three times proposing mediation. Ashbourne did not reply to any of the letters. The trial judge found that the claim had little merit and that mediation would have had a reasonable chance of producing a settlement.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely costs position?","o":["Ashbourne will be ordered to pay all the contractor's costs, because refusing ADR reverses the general rule.","Ashbourne will recover all its costs, because a party that wins at trial is always entitled to its costs.","Ashbourne may have its recoverable costs reduced or refused, because ignoring an ADR proposal is unreasonable.","Ashbourne's judgment will be set aside, because ignoring ADR proposals breaches the overriding objective.","Ashbourne will recover all its costs, because ADR can be penalised only where the court has ordered it."],"a":2,"e":"**Silence in response to an invitation to ADR (alternative dispute resolution, such as mediation) is itself unreasonable, and a winning party who unreasonably fails to engage in ADR can be penalised in costs** (PGF II; CPR 44.2 now expressly refers to unreasonable failure to engage). The court has a discretion, so Ashbourne may lose some or all of its costs, but it keeps the judgment. A penalty does not need a prior court order to mediate, and the sanction is a costs one, not setting aside the result or an automatic order to pay all the loser's costs."},{"t":"dr-options","p":"FLK1","q":"A supply contract between Pennine Valves Ltd, based in Leeds, and a buyer based in Switzerland states: \"This agreement is governed by Swiss law. Any dispute shall be finally resolved by arbitration seated in London.\" There is no other term about governing law. The buyer started arbitration in March 2026. The parties now disagree about whether the arbitration clause covers a claim the buyer has made in tort.\n\nWhich law governs the arbitration agreement?","o":["English law, because there is no express choice of law for the arbitration agreement itself, so the law of the seat applies.","Swiss law, because the parties' choice of law for the main contract extends to the arbitration clause within it.","Swiss law, because the arbitration agreement is governed by the law with which the contract is most closely connected.","English law, because a tribunal seated in England must apply English law to every issue in the arbitration.","Whichever law the tribunal considers appropriate, because the parties have not expressly addressed the question."],"a":0,"e":"For arbitrations commenced on or after 1 August 2025, **the arbitration agreement is governed by the law the parties expressly chose for that agreement, otherwise by the law of the seat** (Arbitration Act 1996 s.6A, added by the Arbitration Act 2025). The **seat** is the legal home of the arbitration, here London. A governing-law clause for the main contract does **not** count as a choice for the arbitration agreement, so the Swiss law clause does not apply to it. The tribunal will still apply Swiss law to the substantive contract dispute, so it is wrong to say English law governs every issue."},{"t":"dr-limitation","p":"FLK1","q":"In August 2016 Osagie executed a deed of guarantee, properly signed, witnessed and delivered, under which he guaranteed the rent payable by his company under a commercial lease. The company failed to pay the rent due in September 2016. The landlord made a written demand on Osagie under the guarantee on 3 October 2016, which he ignored, and the landlord's cause of action against him accrued then. It is now September 2026 and no proceedings have been issued.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether a claim against Osagie on the guarantee is in time?","o":["It is time-barred, because the six-year period ran from the company's failure to pay the rent in 2016.","It is time-barred, because a claim for breach of contract must be brought within six years of the breach.","It is in time only if the court exercises its discretion to allow the claim to proceed out of time.","It is in time, because a claim on a contract made by deed may be brought within 12 years of the breach.","It is in time, because time does not start to run on a guarantee until the landlord issues proceedings."],"a":3,"e":"**An action on a specialty (a contract made by deed) has a limitation period of 12 years from the breach** (Limitation Act 1980 s.8), not the 6 years for a simple contract under s.5. A **deed** is a formal document that makes clear it is a deed and is signed, witnessed and delivered, as here. Even measured from 2016, the claim is well within 12 years. The court's discretion to allow a claim out of time (s.33) applies only to personal injury and fatal accident claims, and time runs from accrual, not from the issue of proceedings."},{"t":"dr-pre-action","p":"FLK1","q":"Wren Office Supplies Ltd instructs a solicitor on 1 September 2026 about a business-to-business claim for breach of a supply contract. No specific pre-action protocol applies. The breach occurred on 15 September 2020. The solicitor sends a detailed letter before claim on 2 September 2026, but the defendant cannot reasonably be expected to respond before the limitation period expires.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the solicitor should do?","o":["Wait for the defendant's response, because issuing before the pre-action steps are complete will lead to a strike out.","Issue the claim before the period expires and seek a stay of the proceedings while the pre-action steps are completed.","Wait for the defendant's response, because time stops running once a compliant letter before claim has been sent.","Issue the claim and press on to trial, because pre-action conduct is irrelevant once limitation is close to expiring.","Apply to the court for an order extending the limitation period until the pre-action steps have been completed."],"a":1,"e":"**Pre-action protocols and the Practice Direction on Pre-Action Conduct do not stop time running, so where limitation is about to expire the claimant should issue protectively and then apply for (or agree) a stay while the pre-action steps are completed.** A **stay** is a pause of the proceedings. Waiting would risk a time-barred claim, and sending a letter before claim does not pause limitation (only a written standstill agreement could). The court has no general power to extend a contract limitation period, and the pre-action steps still matter after issue."},{"t":"dr-parties-law","p":"FLK1","q":"Calloway Catering Equipment Ltd is owed £38,000 by Brookes Hospitality Ltd for kitchen equipment supplied and installed. Before Calloway issues proceedings, it learns that administrators were appointed over Brookes last week. Calloway's director wants to sue at once, because Brookes still owns several restaurants.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Calloway can issue a claim against Brookes?","o":["It may issue only after serving a statutory demand on the administrators and waiting for 21 days.","It may issue the claim in the usual way, because an administration restricts only claims by secured creditors.","It may issue the claim, but it cannot enforce any judgment until the administration has come to an end.","It cannot sue at all, because a company's unsecured debts are extinguished when it enters administration.","It may issue only with the administrators' consent or the court's permission, because a moratorium applies."],"a":4,"e":"**When a company enters administration a moratorium applies: no claim can be started or continued against it without the administrator's consent or the court's permission.** **Administration** is an insolvency procedure aimed at rescuing the company, and a **moratorium** is a freeze on creditor action. The restriction applies to unsecured creditors such as Calloway, not only secured ones, and it bites at the start of proceedings, not only at enforcement. The debt is not extinguished, and a statutory demand is a step towards a winding-up petition, not a way round the moratorium."},{"t":"dr-parties-law","p":"FLK1","q":"Siobhan and Marcus are colleagues who both live and work in Manchester. While on a cycling holiday together in France, Marcus rode carelessly into Siobhan, who was badly injured. She has issued a negligence claim against him in England. The parties have made no agreement about which law should apply.\n\nWhich law will the English court apply to decide liability and the assessment of Siobhan's damages?","o":["French law for liability, but English law for the assessment of damages as a matter of procedure.","French law, because the law of the country where the damage occurs governs every tort claim.","English law, because both parties were habitually resident in England when the damage occurred.","English law, because an English court always applies its own law to a claim issued in England.","French law, because the event giving rise to the damage took place in France."],"a":2,"e":"Under Rome II, the general rule is the law of the country where the damage occurs, but **where the claimant and defendant are both habitually resident in the same country when the damage occurs, that country's law applies** (Art 4(2)). **Habitual residence** is, broadly, where a person has their settled home. The applicable law covers liability and the **assessment of damages**, so splitting French liability from English damages is wrong; only procedure follows the law of the court. An English court does not automatically apply English law to a foreign tort."},{"t":"dr-where-to-start","p":"FLK1","q":"Hartwell Steel Ltd is owed £118,000 by a construction company for steel supplied. The customer has written saying that it will counterclaim £45,000 for late delivery, so that the \"true value\" of the dispute is only £73,000. The claim raises complex issues about the meaning of the contract's price-variation clause. It is not a personal injury claim.\n\nWhich of the following best describes where Hartwell may issue its claim?","o":["It may issue in the High Court, but only if the customer agrees in writing to the claim being heard there.","It must issue in the County Court, because the value of the dispute after the counterclaim is below £100,000.","It must issue in the High Court, because the claim is worth more than £100,000 and involves complex issues.","It must issue in the County Court, because any claim that will face a counterclaim must start there.","It may issue in the High Court if the case justifies it, because the counterclaim is ignored when valuing the claim."],"a":4,"e":"**A money claim may be started in the High Court only if it is worth more than £100,000, and in valuing it the court disregards interest, costs, contributory negligence and any counterclaim or set-off** (CPR 16.3(6)). Hartwell's claim is therefore valued at £118,000, so it may issue in the High Court if the case justifies a High Court judge because of its value, complexity or public importance (PD 7A). It is never obliged to use the High Court: it could also issue in the County Court. Written agreement is relevant to defamation claims in the County Court, not here."},{"t":"dr-issue-service","p":"FLK1","q":"A claim form was issued on Monday 2 February 2026. The claimant's solicitor posted it by first class post to the defendant's home address on Tuesday 26 May 2026, the day after a bank holiday. The claim form stated that the particulars of claim would follow. The defendant has not nominated solicitors to accept service.\n\nWhich of the following is the latest date by which the particulars of claim must be served?","o":["Tuesday 9 June 2026, 14 days after the claim form was posted","Tuesday 2 June 2026, the last day for serving the claim form","Thursday 11 June 2026, 14 days after the claim form was deemed served","Thursday 28 May 2026, the date on which the claim form was deemed served","Sunday 2 August 2026, six months after the claim form was issued"],"a":1,"e":"**Particulars of claim served separately must be served within 14 days after service of the claim form, and in any event no later than the latest time for serving the claim form** (CPR 7.4), which is midnight on the calendar day four months after issue (CPR 7.5), here 2 June 2026. The claim form is deemed served on the second business day after posting, Thursday 28 May, so the 14-day period would otherwise run to 11 June, but the four-month cut-off comes first. Six months applies only to service of a claim form outside the jurisdiction."},{"t":"dr-issue-service","p":"FLK1","q":"In a defended claim, the defendant's solicitors sent their list of documents to the claimant's solicitors by first class post on Thursday 2 April 2026. Friday 3 April 2026 was Good Friday and Monday 6 April 2026 was Easter Monday, a bank holiday.\n\nOn which date is the list of documents deemed to have been served?","o":["Monday 6 April 2026, the first weekday after the second day after posting","Wednesday 8 April 2026, the second business day after the day of posting","Saturday 4 April 2026, the second day after the day of posting","Tuesday 7 April 2026, the next business day after the second day after posting","Friday 3 April 2026, the day after the day of posting"],"a":3,"e":"**A document other than a claim form sent by first class post is deemed served on the second day after posting if that is a business day, or otherwise on the next business day after that day** (CPR 6.26). The second day after posting is Saturday 4 April, which is not a business day; Monday 6 April is a bank holiday, so the first business day after 4 April is Tuesday 7 April. A **business day** excludes weekends, bank holidays, Good Friday and Christmas Day. The \"second business day after the step\" rule, which would give 8 April, applies only to the claim form (CPR 6.14)."},{"t":"dr-responding","p":"FLK1","q":"Imogen sues a decorating company, Pell & Sons Ltd, for damages for negligently damaging her oak floors, which she says will cost about £14,000 to replace. The claim form describes the claim as one for damages to be assessed. Pell & Sons was deemed served with the particulars of claim on 1 June and has filed nothing at court. On 20 June Imogen's solicitor wants to obtain judgment.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["She can request default judgment on liability, with the amount of damages decided by the court later.","She can request default judgment for £14,000 plus interest and fixed costs, as that is the sum claimed.","She must apply for summary judgment, because default judgment is not available in a claim for damages.","She must wait until 29 June, because the defendant has 28 days after service to file its defence.","She can obtain judgment only by applying to a judge, with evidence proving the amount of her loss."],"a":0,"e":"Where the defendant has filed no acknowledgment of service or defence within 14 days of service of the particulars, the claimant may obtain default judgment, and **in a claim for an unspecified sum (damages to be assessed) a request gives judgment on liability only, with the amount decided at a later disposal hearing**. A **disposal hearing** is a short hearing to fix the amount. Judgment for a fixed figure with fixed costs is available only for a specified sum. The 28-day period applies only where an acknowledgment of service was filed, and an application to a judge is needed only in special cases (for example, against a child or for a non-money remedy)."},{"t":"dr-responding","p":"FLK1","q":"Hadley Removals Ltd is sued by a customer whose antique piano was damaged in transit. The claim is for damages to be assessed. Hadley's director, without taking advice, filed a form admitting liability for the whole claim. Two weeks later, Hadley's new solicitors find CCTV footage suggesting the piano was already damaged when it was collected. The customer refuses to agree to the admission being withdrawn, and judgment has not yet been entered.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Hadley can withdraw its admission?","o":["It cannot withdraw it at all, because an admission made after proceedings have started is binding and final.","It may withdraw it simply by giving written notice at any time before judgment has been entered on liability.","It may withdraw it only with the court's permission, which the court will decide in light of all the circumstances.","It may withdraw it automatically, because the admission was made by a director acting without legal advice.","It may withdraw it only by appealing to a Circuit Judge against the admission within 21 days of making it."],"a":2,"e":"**Once proceedings have started, a party may withdraw an admission only with the consent of the other party or the court's permission** (CPR 14). As the customer refuses consent, Hadley must apply, and the court will weigh all the circumstances, including the reasons for withdrawal, any new evidence such as the CCTV footage, the prejudice to each party and the stage the case has reached. An admission is not final, but nor can it be withdrawn by notice or automatically because advice was not taken. An admission is not a judicial decision, so an appeal is not the route."},{"t":"dr-statements-of-case","p":"FLK1","q":"Noor sues a builder, Castlerigg Construction Ltd, for defective electrical work in her new extension. Castlerigg filed its defence three weeks ago, denying liability. It has now discovered that the faulty wiring was installed by its electrical subcontractor, Voltrex Ltd, and wants Voltrex to indemnify it against any damages it has to pay Noor. Voltrex is not a party to the proceedings.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Castlerigg's position?","o":["It can issue an additional claim against Voltrex without permission at any time before the trial date.","It needs the court's permission to bring an additional claim, as its defence has already been filed.","It must amend its own defence so as to add Voltrex as a co-defendant to Noor's claim.","It cannot claim against Voltrex in these proceedings and must start a separate claim after judgment.","It can make an additional claim against Voltrex without permission within 28 days of filing its defence."],"a":1,"e":"**A defendant may make an additional (Part 20) claim against a person who is not already a party without the court's permission only if it is issued before or at the same time as the defence is filed; after that, permission is needed** (CPR 20.7). An **indemnity** is a claim for the whole amount the defendant has to pay. The only 28-day window in Part 20 is for a contribution or indemnity notice against a co-defendant added later, running from that co-defendant's defence (CPR 20.6), so it does not help against a non-party. A defendant cannot make someone a defendant to the claimant's claim by amending its own defence, and there is no need for separate proceedings."},{"t":"dr-statements-of-case","p":"FLK1","q":"Teodora's company issued a claim against an IT supplier for breach of contract shortly before the six-year limitation period expired, alleging that the supplier failed to deliver software by the agreed date. The defence has been filed and the limitation period has now expired. Teodora's solicitor wants to amend the particulars of claim to add a claim in negligence based on the same facts about the late delivery and claiming the same loss. The supplier will not consent.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the amendment can be made?","o":["It must be allowed, because amendments to particulars of claim are always permitted before the trial.","It cannot be allowed, because no new claim may be added to a statement of case once limitation has expired.","It can be made without permission, because the claim form itself was issued within the limitation period.","It can be allowed only if the company proves that the supplier deliberately concealed the facts relied on.","The court may allow it, because the new claim arises out of substantially the same facts as the existing claim."],"a":4,"e":"After service, an amendment needs the other parties' written consent or the court's permission, and **after a limitation period has expired the court may allow an amendment adding a new claim only if it arises out of the same or substantially the same facts as a claim already pleaded** (CPR 17.4). The negligence claim relies on the same facts about late delivery, so the court may permit it. Issuing in time does not make later amendments free of permission, and concealment is relevant to postponing limitation, not to this test. Amendments are discretionary, never automatic."},{"t":"dr-interim","p":"FLK1","q":"Kofi was injured when scaffolding fell on him outside a shop. The scaffolding company has admitted liability but alleges that Kofi was 25% to blame because he walked under a cordon. His solicitor values the full claim at £240,000 before any reduction, and Kofi needs money now to adapt his home. He applies for an interim payment.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court will approach the amount?","o":["It may order a reasonable proportion of the likely final judgment, taking account of the alleged contributory negligence.","It may order up to the full £240,000, because liability has been admitted by the defendant.","It may order a reasonable proportion of £240,000, disregarding the alleged contributory negligence until trial.","It cannot order any payment until the contributory negligence allegation has been decided at trial.","It must order £180,000, because that is the least Kofi will recover even if the allegation succeeds."],"a":0,"e":"An admission of liability satisfies one of the conditions for an interim payment, but **the court must not order more than a reasonable proportion of the likely amount of the final judgment, taking into account contributory negligence and any set-off or counterclaim** (CPR 25). An **interim payment** is a payment on account of damages before trial, and **contributory negligence** reduces damages for the claimant's own share of blame. The court does not ignore the allegation, need not wait for trial, and is not obliged to order any particular figure."},{"t":"dr-interim","p":"FLK1","q":"Ruskin Print Ltd sues a customer for £36,000 for printing work. The customer's defence alleges that Ruskin orally agreed to a 40% discount. Ruskin applies for summary judgment. The judge considers that the discount story is possible but improbable, because the customer's own emails after the alleged agreement chased the work without mentioning any discount.\n\nWhich order is the judge most likely to make?","o":["An order striking out the defence, because it discloses no reasonable grounds for defending the claim.","Summary judgment for Ruskin for the full sum, because the customer's defence is improbable on the evidence.","Dismissal of the application with no conditions, because the court never weighs evidence on a summary judgment application.","A conditional order requiring the customer to pay a sum into court, failing which its defence will be struck out.","An order that the customer give security for Ruskin's costs before it may continue to defend."],"a":3,"e":"Summary judgment requires that the respondent has **no real prospect** of success, meaning a realistic rather than fanciful one, so an improbable defence is not enough. **Where it appears possible that a claim or defence may succeed but improbable that it will, the court may make a conditional order, for example requiring a payment into court, failing which the claim or defence is struck out** (CPR Part 24). Strike out for no reasonable grounds looks at the pleaded case itself, not the evidence. Security for costs is ordered only against a party who is bringing a claim or appeal (a claimant, counterclaiming defendant or appellant), not against a defendant simply defending."},{"t":"dr-case-management","p":"FLK1","q":"Ffion Rees and Amir Haddad own adjoining houses. Together they sue a developer and its architect for £80,000 in total for subsidence damage to both houses. The trial is expected to last two days, and each party wants one expert to give oral evidence. None of the excluded types of claim is involved.\n\nTo which track is the claim most likely to be allocated?","o":["The fast track, because each party will call only one expert to give oral evidence at the trial.","The intermediate track, because the claim is under £100,000 and the trial will last no more than three days.","The multi-track, because two claimants suing two defendants falls outside the intermediate track criteria.","The intermediate track, because every claim valued between £25,000 and £100,000 must be allocated there.","The multi-track, because any claim alleging professional negligence by an architect must be allocated there."],"a":2,"e":"The **intermediate track** is the normal track for claims of £25,000 to £100,000 where the trial will last no more than three days and no more than two experts per party give oral evidence, but **only where the claim is by one claimant against one or two defendants, or two claimants against one defendant** (CPR 26.9). Two claimants against two defendants does not meet the normal criteria, so the claim normally goes to the **multi-track**. The fast track is limited to claims up to £25,000 with a one-day trial. Professional negligence claims are not automatically multi-track."},{"t":"dr-case-management","p":"FLK1","q":"In a fast track claim, the court's directions required witness statements to be exchanged by 4 September, pre-trial checklists to be filed by 16 October, and fixed the trial for 30 November. The defendant's solicitor asks the claimant's solicitor to agree to extend the date for exchanging witness statements to 18 September. The extension will not affect any later date in the timetable.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The parties may agree any extension in writing, including changes to the checklist and trial dates.","An application to the court is needed, because any date in the court's directions can be changed only by the court.","The parties may agree the extension, but only if they also agree to move the trial date to make room for it.","The court's approval is needed, because the parties may agree only extensions of up to seven days.","The parties may agree the extension in writing without applying, because it does not affect the checklist or trial dates."],"a":4,"e":"**The parties may vary most time limits by written agreement (CPR 2.11), but on the fast track they cannot vary the dates fixed for the trial or trial period, a case management conference, a pre-trial review or the return of the pre-trial checklist, or any other date if that would mean changing one of those** (CPR 28.3). Exchange of witness statements is not one of the protected dates and the extension affects nothing later, so no application is needed. Moving the checklist or trial date would require an application to the court, and there is no seven-day limit."},{"t":"dr-evidence","p":"FLK1","q":"In a professional negligence claim, the court gave the claimant permission to rely on expert evidence from a named chartered surveyor, Ms Delacroix. Her report was unfavourable to the claimant and has not been served. The claimant now applies for permission to rely on a different surveyor instead.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the court's likely approach?","o":["It will refuse permission, because a party can never replace an expert once permission has been given.","It is likely to grant permission only on condition that the claimant discloses Ms Delacroix's report.","It will grant permission without conditions, because Ms Delacroix's report is privileged and need not be seen.","It will grant permission, but will order Ms Delacroix to give oral evidence for the defendant at trial.","It will grant permission only if the claimant proves that Ms Delacroix breached her duty to the court."],"a":1,"e":"No party may rely on expert evidence without the court's permission (CPR 35.4), and **where a party seeks to replace an expert after permission has been given, the court will usually grant permission only on condition that the first expert's report is disclosed**, to discourage \"expert shopping\" (Edwards-Tubb). The first report would otherwise be protected by litigation privilege, which is why the condition is imposed rather than unconditional permission. Replacement is not barred outright, and the claimant need not prove misconduct by the first expert."},{"t":"dr-evidence","p":"FLK1","q":"During disclosure in a claim for repayment of a loan, the claimant disclosed a signed letter, apparently from the defendant, acknowledging the debt. The defendant says that the signature is not his. His solicitor has taken no step in relation to the letter. Witness statements are due to be exchanged in three weeks.\n\nWhat should the defendant's solicitor do to challenge the authenticity of the letter?","o":["Serve a notice requiring the letter to be proved at trial, or he is taken to admit it is genuine.","Do nothing yet, because the claimant must prove the authenticity of every document it relies on at trial.","Serve a notice to admit facts on the claimant, asking it to admit that the signature on the letter is forged.","Apply for specific disclosure of the original letter, which automatically requires the claimant to prove it.","Raise the point in cross-examination at trial, which is the only way to challenge a disclosed document."],"a":0,"e":"**A party is deemed to admit the authenticity of a document disclosed to it unless it serves a notice requiring the document to be proved at trial** (CPR 32.19). The notice must be served by the latest of the date for serving witness statements or seven days after disclosure of the document, so there is still time here. Doing nothing, or waiting until cross-examination, would leave the defendant taken to admit that the letter is genuine. A notice to admit facts asks the other side to admit facts, so it is no use for putting the claimant to proof."},{"t":"dr-disclosure","p":"FLK1","q":"Lowther Haulage Ltd sues the manufacturer of one of its lorries, alleging that a brake defect caused a crash. A repair garage, which is not a party, serviced the lorry's brakes two weeks before the crash and holds its inspection records. The garage refuses to provide them voluntarily. Lowther believes the records will show that the brakes were in good order when serviced and that the fault lay in the design.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Lowther can obtain the records?","o":["Serve a notice to prove documents on the garage, requiring it to produce the records at the trial.","Apply for pre-action disclosure against the garage, because the garage is likely to become a party.","Rely on the manufacturer's standard disclosure, which must include documents held by any repairer of the lorry.","Apply for disclosure against the garage, showing the records are likely to support its case and disclosure is necessary.","Nothing can be done, because a non-party cannot be ordered to disclose documents in civil proceedings."],"a":3,"e":"**After proceedings have started, the court may order a non-party to disclose documents if they are likely to support the applicant's case or adversely affect another party's case, and disclosure is necessary to dispose fairly of the claim or to save costs** (CPR 31.17). Pre-action disclosure applies only before proceedings start, between likely parties. The manufacturer must disclose only documents in its own **control** (possession, or a right to possess or inspect), which the garage's records are not. A notice to prove documents concerns the authenticity of documents already disclosed."},{"t":"dr-disclosure","p":"FLK1","q":"Bellingham Estates Ltd is sued by a former joint venture partner over the structure of a property deal. Standard disclosure has been ordered. Bellingham holds two relevant documents, both written in 2023, before any dispute arose: a confidential memo from its external accountants advising on the tax and legal consequences of structuring the deal, and a confidential letter from its solicitors advising on the legal effect of the deal documents.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Bellingham can withhold inspection?","o":["Both can be withheld, because both are confidential advice that Bellingham obtained before the dispute.","Only the solicitors' letter can be withheld, as legal advice privilege does not cover accountants.","Neither can be withheld, because litigation was not in contemplation when either document was written.","Only the accountants' memo can be withheld, because it is advice from an independent third party.","Both can be withheld if Bellingham shows that allowing inspection would be disproportionate."],"a":1,"e":"**Legal advice privilege protects confidential communications between a client and its lawyer for the dominant purpose of giving or receiving legal advice, whether or not litigation is contemplated, but it does not extend to non-lawyers such as accountants, even when they advise on law** (Prudential). The solicitors' letter is privileged and can be withheld, though it must still be listed. The accountants' memo is not, because litigation privilege also fails: no litigation was reasonably in contemplation in 2023. Disproportionality is not a ground for withholding key relevant documents like these."},{"t":"dr-trial","p":"FLK1","q":"At a County Court trial, the claimant's witness, a site foreman, was cross-examined about whether he had been wearing his glasses when he saw a forklift strike the claimant. He agreed that he had taken them off earlier that morning. The claimant's advocate now wishes to re-examine him.\n\nWhich of the following questions is proper in re-examination?","o":["\"Can you tell the court about the defendant's earlier safety breaches?\"","\"You had put your glasses back on by then, hadn't you?\"","\"Where were your glasses at the moment you saw the accident?\"","\"You would agree that the forklift was going far too fast?\"","\"Is there anything further you would like to add to your statement?\""],"a":2,"e":"**Re-examination is limited to matters arising out of the cross-examination, and leading questions are not allowed when questioning one's own witness.** A **leading question** suggests the answer or assumes a disputed fact, as the questions about putting the glasses back on and the forklift going too fast both do. The open question about where the glasses were arises directly from the cross-examination and lets the witness repair any damage in his own words. Earlier safety breaches are a new topic, and an invitation to add to the statement goes beyond re-examination (amplification needs the court's permission)."},{"t":"dr-trial","p":"FLK1","q":"Tobias, the defendant in a fast track claim, did not attend the trial on 12 March 2026 because he was admitted to hospital that morning with appendicitis. The judge gave judgment against him in his absence. Tobias received a copy of the judgment on 20 March 2026 but did nothing until 30 June 2026, when he applied to set it aside. His evidence shows a reasonable prospect of success at a retrial.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome of the application?","o":["It will be granted, because there is no time limit on such an application if there was a good reason for absence.","It will be granted, because he had a good reason for not attending and a reasonable prospect of success.","It will be granted, because a judgment given in a party's absence must be set aside if the absence was involuntary.","It is likely to be refused, because his only route is an appeal to a Circuit Judge within 21 days.","It is likely to be refused, because he did not act promptly after finding out about the judgment."],"a":4,"e":"**The court may set aside a judgment given in a party's absence only if the applicant acted promptly when it found out about the judgment, had a good reason for not attending, and has a reasonable prospect of success at the trial; all three conditions must be met** (CPR 39.3(5)). Tobias had a good reason and a reasonable prospect, but waiting over three months after receiving the judgment is not prompt. Setting aside is not automatic for involuntary absence, and a set-aside application under CPR 39.3, not an appeal, is the proper route for a party who did not attend."},{"t":"dr-costs","p":"FLK1","q":"Mateus, a building contractor who lives and works in Brazil, sues an English company, Ormsby Developments Ltd, in the High Court for £180,000 he says is due for work on a project in London. He has no assets in England and Wales. Ormsby has a reasonable defence and is worried that, if it wins, it will be unable to recover its costs from him.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Ormsby can obtain security for costs?","o":["It can apply, and the court may order security if just, as Mateus is resident out of the jurisdiction.","It cannot obtain security, because security is available only against claimants that are companies.","The court must order security, because a claimant resident abroad is always required to provide it.","It cannot obtain security, because an individual cannot be ordered to give security merely for lack of funds.","It can obtain security only if it shows that Mateus changed his address in order to evade the litigation."],"a":0,"e":"**The court may order a claimant to give security for costs if it is just to do so and a condition applies, one of which is that the claimant is resident out of the jurisdiction** (CPR 25.27). **Security for costs** means money paid into court or a guarantee to protect the defendant's costs. Whether it is just depends mainly on how hard it would be to enforce a costs order where the claimant lives, so the order is discretionary, not automatic. The impecuniosity condition is limited to companies and nominal claimants, but residence abroad is a separate condition that applies to individuals too."},{"t":"dr-costs","p":"FLK1","q":"In a contract claim, the claimant applied for an order requiring the defendant to give specific disclosure. The District Judge granted part of the application and ordered \"claimant's costs in the case\". At trial, the defendant wins and the claimant's claim is dismissed with costs.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who bears the costs of the specific disclosure application?","o":["The claimant pays the defendant's costs of the application, because the claimant lost at trial.","The claimant recovers its costs of the application, because it succeeded in obtaining the order.","Neither party recovers its costs of the application from the other, because the claimant did not win at trial.","The defendant pays the claimant's costs of the application, set off against the costs of the trial.","The trial judge decides the costs of the application afresh, because the order was only provisional."],"a":2,"e":"**Under an order for \"claimant's costs in the case\", the claimant recovers its costs of the application only if it is awarded costs at the end of the case; if it loses, it does not pay the other party's costs of the application.** As the claimant lost, each side bears its own costs of the application. Recovery whatever the outcome would require \"costs in any event\", and the claimant paying the defendant's application costs would follow from plain \"costs in the case\". A decision postponed to the trial judge describes \"costs reserved\"."},{"t":"dr-enforcement","p":"FLK1","q":"Sabine obtained a County Court judgment for £12,500 against Harcourt Plant Hire Ltd, which has not paid. The company owns several unencumbered (debt-free) diggers and other plant at its yard, but its bank account is overdrawn and it owns no land. The debt does not arise under a regulated consumer credit agreement.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate way for Sabine to enforce the judgment?","o":["Apply for a third party debt order directed to the company's bank.","Apply to the County Court for a warrant of control, because the judgment was given there.","Apply for an attachment of earnings order against the company's managing director.","Transfer the judgment to the High Court and enforce it by a writ of control.","Apply for a charging order over the company's diggers and other plant."],"a":3,"e":"**A judgment of £5,000 or more to be enforced by taking control of goods must be enforced in the High Court (unless it arises under a regulated consumer credit agreement), so the County Court judgment should be transferred and enforced by a writ of control** executed by a High Court Enforcement Officer. A **writ of control** authorises the officer to take and sell the debtor's goods, which suits a company with valuable, debt-free plant. A third party debt order catches only money in an account in credit, attachment of earnings is unavailable against a company's director for the company's debt, and a charging order secures land or securities, not machinery."},{"t":"dr-enforcement","p":"FLK1","q":"Dunmore Fabrics Ltd has a judgment for £22,000 against Talbot Interiors Ltd, which has not paid. Dunmore has no idea what assets Talbot has, so it obtained an order requiring Talbot's managing director, Mr Finch, to attend court to provide information about the company's means. The order was served personally on Mr Finch 21 days before the hearing, and he did not ask for travel expenses. He failed to attend the hearing and gave no explanation.\n\nWhat is the court most likely to do?","o":["Enter judgment against Mr Finch personally for the company's debt, because he failed to co-operate.","Make a suspended committal order, which takes effect only if he fails to attend a further hearing.","Make an immediate order sending Mr Finch to prison for contempt of court.","Set aside the order, because only the judgment debtor itself can be ordered to give information.","Order Mr Finch to pay the company's debt by instalments out of his own earnings."],"a":1,"e":"An **order to obtain information** (CPR 71) requires the judgment debtor or, for a company, an **officer** such as a director, to attend court and answer questions on oath about its means. **If the person fails to attend, the judge may make a committal order, which is suspended provided he attends a further hearing and complies.** **Committal** means being sent to prison for contempt of court, but the first step is a suspended order, not immediate imprisonment. A director does not become personally liable for the company's judgment debt by failing to attend."},{"t":"dr-appeals","p":"FLK1","q":"A District Judge in the County Court dismissed Rhiannon's claim after a fast track trial. She appealed, with permission, to a Circuit Judge, who dismissed her appeal. Rhiannon wants to appeal further. Her grounds have a real prospect of success but concern only how the Circuit Judge applied settled law to the particular facts of her case.\n\nWhich of the following best describes her position?","o":["She may appeal without permission, because permission was already granted for the first appeal.","She may appeal to a High Court Judge, with the Circuit Judge's permission, because her appeal has a real prospect of success.","She may appeal to the Court of Appeal, which must give permission because her appeal has a real prospect of success.","She may appeal straight to the Supreme Court, because every second appeal in a civil case goes there.","She needs the Court of Appeal's permission, which is unlikely without an important point of principle or practice."],"a":4,"e":"**A second appeal goes to the Court of Appeal, only the Court of Appeal can give permission, and it will do so only if the appeal has a real prospect of success and raises an important point of principle or practice, or there is some other compelling reason** (CPR 52.7). A real prospect of success alone, which is enough for a first appeal, is not sufficient. A case about applying settled law to its own facts is unlikely to qualify. Permission for the first appeal does not carry over, and second appeals do not go to a High Court Judge or the Supreme Court."}],"ethics-p1":[{"t":"principles-competence","p":"FLK1","q":"Bethany Okafor, a solicitor two years qualified, works in the commercial litigation team of a firm. Her supervising partner, who is also the firm's compliance officer for legal practice (COLP), failed to respond to a letter of claim on behalf of a client company by the agreed date. He asks Bethany to change the date on the firm's response letter so that it appears to have been sent a week earlier, saying the client will never know and no harm will be done.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Bethany should do?","o":["Change the date as instructed, because the partner is accountable for the file and for work done through her.","Refuse to change the date and tell the COLP, which will fully satisfy her own duty to report the matter.","Refuse to change the date and report the matter promptly and directly to the Solicitors Regulation Authority.","Refuse to change the date but take no further action, because no letter has yet been falsely dated.","Change the date but make a private file note recording that she acted on the partner's instruction."],"a":2,"e":"Backdating the letter would be **dishonest** (Principle 4) and misleading (para 1.4), and a senior colleague's instruction is no defence because each solicitor is **personally accountable** for their own compliance. A request to act dishonestly is capable of amounting to a serious breach, so it must be reported promptly (para 7.7) even though the letter has not yet been altered. Telling the COLP satisfies the duty only if she can be confident the COLP will pass it on (para 7.12); here the COLP made the request, so she should **report directly to the SRA**."},{"t":"principles-competence","p":"FLK2","q":"Edward Lindqvist, a private client solicitor, cannot find the original of a will he prepared for Mrs Adeyemi, which she signed three years ago. She is in good health and her wishes have not changed. Without telling anyone, Edward prints a fresh copy, has Mrs Adeyemi and two members of staff sign it, and inserts the date of the original will. He genuinely believes this is harmless because the contents are identical and the client is content.\n\nIf the Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal considers whether Edward acted dishonestly, which of the following best describes the approach it will take?","o":["It will decide what Edward actually knew or believed, then judge his conduct by the standards of ordinary decent people.","It will ask whether Edward himself realised that ordinary decent people would regard his conduct as dishonest.","It will find that he was not dishonest, because he genuinely believed his conduct caused no harm to anyone.","It will ask only whether his conduct fell below the standard of a reasonably competent private client solicitor.","It will find dishonesty only if it is proved beyond reasonable doubt, applying the criminal standard of proof."],"a":0,"e":"The test for dishonesty has two stages: first, what the person **actually knew or believed** about the facts (subjective), and second, whether the conduct was dishonest by the **standards of ordinary decent people** (objective) (Ivey v Genting Casinos). There is no requirement that Edward realised ordinary people would see it as dishonest, so his belief that it was harmless does not save him: knowingly presenting a newly signed will as made three years earlier is likely to be dishonest. The SDT applies the **civil standard** (balance of probabilities), and competence is a separate question from honesty."},{"t":"trust-fairness-undertakings","p":"FLK1","q":"Priya Nair acts for Harlow Fabrications Ltd, the defendant in a breach of contract claim. The claim settles and, in an email to the claimant's solicitor, Priya writes: 'We confirm that we will pay your client's agreed costs of £6,400 from the funds we hold within 14 days of the consent order being sealed.' Relying on this, the claimant's solicitor agrees to lodge the consent order, which is then sealed. A week later, the company's managing director instructs Priya not to make the payment because the company needs the money for cash flow.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Priya's position?","o":["She need not pay, because her email did not use the word 'undertake' and so it was not an undertaking.","She need not pay, because an undertaking given on behalf of a client ends if the client withdraws authority.","She should apply to the court for directions before paying, because the client has withdrawn its instructions.","She must pay the costs within the 14 days, because she is bound by her undertaking whatever the client now says.","She must tell the claimant's solicitor the payment is delayed, and pay once the client's cash flow improves."],"a":3,"e":"An undertaking is any statement, **whether or not it uses the word 'undertake'**, that the solicitor will do something, made to someone who reasonably relies on it; the claimant's solicitor plainly relied on Priya's promise. It binds her **personally**, and the **client cannot release it**: only the recipient can agree to vary or release it. She must perform it within the agreed 14 days (para 1.3), and delaying until it suits the client would be a breach."},{"t":"trust-fairness-undertakings","p":"FLK2","q":"Marcus Delaney acts for the executors of the late Mr Fenwick, whose will leaves nothing to his adult son, Callum. The grant of probate was issued two months ago. Callum, who has no solicitor, telephones Marcus to ask whether it is 'too late' for him to bring a claim against the estate. The executors have told Marcus they would like him to discourage any claim.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Marcus should respond to Callum?","o":["Tell Callum that any claim is now out of time, as the executors are entitled to distribute the estate without delay.","Explain that he acts only for the executors and cannot advise Callum, and suggest Callum takes independent legal advice.","Advise Callum fully on the merits and time limits of a claim, because Callum is unrepresented and at a disadvantage.","Refuse to speak to Callum at all, because any contact with an unrepresented potential claimant breaches confidentiality.","Tell Callum that a claim would be bound to fail, because the executors have instructed him to protect the estate."],"a":1,"e":"Marcus acts for the executors, but he must not **take unfair advantage** of an unrepresented person (para 1.2) or **mislead** him (para 1.4), whatever his clients want. A claim under the Inheritance (Provision for Family and Dependants) Act 1975 can normally be brought within six months of the grant, so telling Callum it is out of time would be untrue. Marcus is not obliged to advise Callum, who is not his client, and doing so would conflict with his duty to the executors; the proper course is to say he cannot advise and suggest **independent advice**."},{"t":"duty-to-court","p":"FLK2","q":"Joel Mensah is charged with burglary. In his police interview he said he was at home alone on the night in question. At a later conference with his solicitor he says he was in fact at a friend's flat across town, and asks the solicitor to take a statement from the friend. He has never told his solicitor that he committed the burglary.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the solicitor's position?","o":["The solicitor must withdraw, because the change of account shows that Joel intends to mislead the court.","The solicitor must tell the prosecution about the change of account, because it affects the evidence in the case.","The solicitor may continue, but only by testing the prosecution case and without calling any defence evidence.","The solicitor may continue only if Joel first signs a written admission that his earlier account was false.","The solicitor may continue and present the new account, having first questioned Joel about why it has changed."],"a":4,"e":"**Inconsistent instructions are not an admission of guilt.** The solicitor may question Joel about the change, but would only be knowingly misleading the court if Joel had actually said that the defence is false; deciding where the truth lies is for the court, not the solicitor. The limits on running a positive case apply only where the client has admitted the offence, and the duty of **confidentiality** (para 6.3) prevents the solicitor telling the prosecution what Joel has said."},{"t":"duty-to-court","p":"FLK1","q":"Sian Llewellyn acts for a software company in a county court claim against a former customer for unpaid licence fees. The customer, a sole trader, is representing himself and relies on a clause in the contract that he says caps his liability. While preparing for the hearing, Sian finds a recent Court of Appeal decision on an identically worded clause which strongly supports the customer's reading. The customer has not mentioned it and appears unaware of it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Sian's duty in relation to the decision?","o":["She need not mention it, because it is for the customer to research the law that supports his own case.","She should mention it only if the judge asks whether there is any authority on the meaning of the clause.","She must draw it to the court's attention, although she may still argue that it can be distinguished.","She must tell the customer about it privately before the hearing, but need not raise it with the court.","She must withdraw from the case, because she cannot cite it and also act in her client's best interests."],"a":2,"e":"Under para 2.7 a solicitor must draw the court's attention to **relevant cases and statutory provisions** of which they are aware and which are **likely to have a material effect on the outcome**, even where they harm the client and the opponent has missed them. The duty concerns **law**, not facts, and is owed to the court, so quietly telling the opponent does not discharge it. Sian can still argue that the decision is distinguishable, so there is no need to withdraw."},{"t":"duty-to-court","p":"FLK1","q":"Natasha Brookes acts for the claimant in a professional negligence claim against a firm of surveyors. She needs an expert surveyor's report. To keep costs down, the client suggests that the expert should be paid nothing unless the claim succeeds and should then receive 10% of any damages recovered. A self-employed factual witness has also asked to be compensated for the day's earnings she will lose by attending the trial.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Natasha may agree to?","o":["She may pay the witness reasonable compensation for lost earnings, but must not agree the proposed expert fee.","She may agree the proposed expert fee, but must not pay the witness anything beyond her travel expenses.","She may agree both arrangements, provided they are disclosed to the court and to the defendant before trial.","She may agree neither arrangement, because any payment to a witness other than a fixed expert fee is banned.","She may agree the expert fee only if the expert confirms in writing that it will not influence the report."],"a":0,"e":"Para 2.3 prohibits any benefit to a witness that is **dependent on the nature of their evidence or the outcome** of the case, so an expert fee payable only on success, as a share of damages, is not allowed. A witness may, however, be paid **reasonable expenses** and reasonable **compensation for loss of time**, such as lost earnings, and an expert may be paid a proper fee. Disclosure or the expert's written assurance cannot cure an outcome-related payment."},{"t":"instructions-capacity-service","p":"FLK2","q":"Lucas Ferreira asks a firm to prepare a lifetime trust under which his widowed mother, aged 86, will transfer £250,000 of her savings into a discretionary trust. Lucas will be one of the trustees and one of the beneficiaries. He says his mother is 'happy with everything', that he will pay the firm's fees, and that she finds meetings tiring, so he will pass on her instructions.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the firm should proceed?","o":["Act on Lucas's instructions, because he is paying the fees and his mother has delegated the matter to him.","Decline to act, because an elderly client cannot validly make a large lifetime gift to a family member.","Obtain a medical report on the mother's capacity before doing anything else, because she is over 85.","Act on Lucas's instructions, provided his mother signs a letter confirming that she agrees with the trust.","Meet the mother without Lucas present to confirm her instructions, her understanding and that she acts freely."],"a":4,"e":"Under para 3.1 a solicitor acts only on instructions from the client or someone properly authorised, and must not act where there is **reason to suspect** the instructions do not reflect the client's wishes. Lucas benefits from the trust, so there is a real risk of **undue influence** (improper pressure overriding the client's free will): the firm should **see the mother alone** and get her consent before discussing her affairs with Lucas. Paying the fees does not make Lucas the client, and capacity is **presumed**, so a medical report is needed only where there is real doubt."},{"t":"instructions-capacity-service","p":"FLK1","q":"A firm acts for Kestrel Logistics Ltd, the claimant in a contract claim. The firm's terms of business, agreed at the outset, require the company to pay £5,000 on account of costs before the firm prepares witness statements. Despite three written reminders, the company has not paid. Witness statements are due to be exchanged in seven weeks.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's position?","o":["It must continue to act until the claim ends, because a retainer cannot be ended once proceedings have started.","It may end the retainer, as non-payment is a good reason, but must first give the company reasonable notice.","It may stop work immediately without any notice, because the company is in breach of the terms of business.","It may end the retainer only if the company agrees, because only a client may decide to end a retainer.","It may end the retainer only after reporting the company's failure to pay to the court and the defendant."],"a":1,"e":"A solicitor may end a retainer only for **good reason** and on **reasonable notice**, and a client's failure to pay an agreed sum **on account** (an advance towards costs) is a good reason (in contentious business this is expressly deemed good cause by the Solicitors Act 1974 s.65(2)). Stopping work immediately could seriously prejudice the client with a deadline approaching, so the company must be given time to pay or instruct another firm. The client may end the retainer at any time, but that does not mean the solicitor needs the client's agreement to stop acting."},{"t":"instructions-capacity-service","p":"FLK1","q":"Ravi Chandran suffered a head injury in a road traffic accident. His solicitor advises him that the defendant's offer of £40,000 is well below the likely value of his claim. Ravi says he understands the advice and the risks, and repeats them back accurately, but wants to accept because he wants the matter over before his daughter's wedding. There is no medical evidence that the injury affects his decision-making.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the solicitor should proceed?","o":["Refuse to accept the offer, because accepting it would not be in Ravi's best interests as his solicitor sees them.","Apply to the Court of Protection, because Ravi's choice to accept a low offer suggests that he lacks capacity.","Take instructions from Ravi's family instead, because his head injury makes his capacity too uncertain.","Accept the offer on Ravi's instructions, because an unwise decision does not in itself show a lack of capacity.","Accept the offer only once a doctor has certified that Ravi has the capacity to settle his claim."],"a":3,"e":"Under the Mental Capacity Act 2005 an adult is **presumed to have capacity**, and is not to be treated as lacking it merely because they make an **unwise decision** (s.1(4)). Ravi can understand, retain, use and weigh the information and communicate his choice, so he has capacity to decide whether to settle. The solicitor should give clear advice and record it, but the decision is Ravi's; a medical opinion is needed only where there is real doubt."},{"t":"client-money","p":"FLK1","q":"Ayesha Rahman acts for a client buying a private company. On her recommendation the client takes out warranty and indemnity insurance through a broker, and the broker then pays Ayesha's firm a commission of £1,800. The client has not been told about the commission.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the firm must deal with the commission?","o":["The firm may keep it, because it was paid by the broker and does not come from the client's own money.","The firm may keep it, provided the amount is shown on the firm's final bill to the client for the purchase.","The firm must account for it to the client, unless the client agrees with full information that it may keep it.","The firm must return it to the broker, because solicitors are prohibited from receiving commission of any kind.","The firm must pay it to the Solicitors Regulation Authority, because it was received without client consent."],"a":2,"e":"Under para 4.1 a solicitor must **properly account to the client for any financial benefit** received as a result of the client's instructions, **unless the client has agreed otherwise**. The commission arose from Ayesha's work for the client, so it belongs to the client unless the client, properly informed, agrees the firm may keep it; showing it on a bill afterwards is not agreement. Commission is not banned outright, and the SRA does not collect it."},{"t":"client-money","p":"FLK2","q":"A conveyancing firm's cashier, intending to send £12,000 to a seller's solicitor, mistypes the account number, and the money leaves the firm's client account to an unknown recipient. The money belonged to Mrs Okonjo, whose sale proceeds the firm is holding until her own purchase completes next month. The bank says recovering the payment may take several weeks.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the firm must do?","o":["Wait for the bank to recover the money, because the shortfall was caused by an innocent mistake, not dishonesty.","Replace the £12,000 in client account immediately from the firm's own money, whether or not it is later recovered.","Tell Mrs Okonjo about the shortfall and replace it only if the money is not recovered by her completion date.","Require the cashier to repay the £12,000 personally, because the error was the cashier's rather than the firm's.","Use money held for other clients to cover the shortfall on Mrs Okonjo's ledger until the bank recovers the payment."],"a":1,"e":"Under the Accounts Rules (rule 6.1) breaches must be **corrected promptly**, and money improperly withdrawn from client account must be **replaced immediately from the firm's own money**, whether or not anyone was at fault. Waiting for the bank or the cashier leaves a **shortfall** on Mrs Okonjo's ledger, and using other clients' money to fill it would be a further breach, because one client's money must never be used for another. The firm can still pursue recovery and should be honest with the client about what happened."},{"t":"business-referrals-publicity","p":"FLK2","q":"Hollins & Co is a criminal defence firm. A company that runs a telephone helpline for people who have been arrested offers to pass callers to Hollins & Co. In return the firm would pay the company a monthly 'marketing fee' which rises with the number of callers who go on to instruct the firm on their criminal cases. The company says the arrangement will be fully explained to every caller.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm may enter into the arrangement?","o":["It may, because a monthly marketing fee is a payment for services and so cannot be treated as a referral fee.","It may, provided the fee arrangement is recorded in writing and each client is told about the payment.","It may, provided the firm keeps its independence and does not let the company influence its advice.","It may not, because a solicitor can never pay anyone for introducing clients, whatever the type of matter.","It may not, because payments to an introducer for clients subject to criminal proceedings are prohibited."],"a":4,"e":"Para 5.1(d) prohibits making payments to an introducer **in respect of clients who are the subject of criminal proceedings**, and disclosure to clients or a written agreement cannot cure this. A fee that rises with the number of clients instructing the firm will be **treated as a referral fee unless the firm shows otherwise** (para 5.2), so the label 'marketing fee' does not help. Referral fees are not banned in every type of matter: outside criminal and personal injury work they are allowed if disclosed and independence is kept."},{"t":"business-referrals-publicity","p":"FLK1","q":"Grace Thompson is a partner in an SRA-authorised firm. She also owns an HR consultancy which is not authorised by the SRA or any other legal regulator. An employer client of the firm that is restructuring would benefit from the consultancy's help in running staff consultation meetings, and Grace would like to recommend it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Grace must do before referring the client to the consultancy?","o":["Get the client's informed consent, including that SRA protections will not apply to the consultancy's work.","Get the SRA's approval, because a solicitor may not refer clients to a business that the solicitor owns.","Nothing further, because the client is a business and the rules on separate businesses protect only individuals.","Tell the client the consultancy is regulated by the SRA, because a partner of an authorised firm owns it.","Arrange for the consultancy to do the work in the firm's name, so that the client keeps the SRA protections."],"a":0,"e":"Under para 5.3 a solicitor may refer a client to a **separate business** (one not authorised by a legal regulator) only with the client's **informed consent**, which includes understanding that it is **not regulated by the SRA**, so protections such as the Compensation Fund, compulsory insurance and the Legal Ombudsman do not apply. Para 8.10 forbids presenting it as SRA-regulated, and passing its work off under the firm's name would be misleading. No SRA approval is needed, and the rule applies whatever kind of client is involved."},{"t":"conflicts-of-interest","p":"FLK1","q":"A firm acts for Norton Engineering Ltd and Pelham Tooling Ltd, both long-standing clients. The administrators of an insolvent competitor have invited sealed bids for its business and assets, and each company asks the firm to advise on and submit its bid. Both are large, commercially experienced companies with their own in-house lawyers.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm may act for both companies?","o":["It may act for both, because the two companies have a substantially common interest in buying the business.","It may act for both once each company consents, because both are sophisticated clients with in-house lawyers.","It may not act for both, because the rules never allow a firm to act for clients whose interests conflict.","It may act for both with each company's written informed consent, effective safeguards and if it is reasonable.","It may act for only one of them, because the conflict has arisen within a formal insolvency process."],"a":3,"e":"The companies are **competing for the same objective**: assets sought through an insolvency process and sealed bids, which if won by one will be unattainable to the other (para 6.2(b)). The firm may act for both only if **all three conditions** are met: **informed consent given or evidenced in writing**, effective safeguards (such as information barriers) to protect each client's confidential information, and the firm being satisfied it is **reasonable** to act. Consent alone is not enough, and their interests are opposed rather than common."},{"t":"conflicts-of-interest","p":"FLK2","q":"Daniel Osei, a residential conveyancing solicitor, is asked by a long-standing client, Ms Harrington, to act for her in buying a flat. It turns out that the seller is Daniel's brother, who has instructed another firm. Ms Harrington says she does not mind, is happy to sign a written consent, and would prefer Daniel to act because she trusts him.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Daniel may act for Ms Harrington?","o":["He may act, provided Ms Harrington gives informed consent in writing and he is satisfied that it is reasonable.","He may act, provided the price has already been agreed and his brother is advised by a different firm.","He may not act, because he has an own interest conflict which Ms Harrington's consent cannot overcome.","He may not act unless another partner supervises the matter and his brother also consents in writing.","He may act, because there is no conflict when the seller is a relative of the solicitor rather than a client."],"a":2,"e":"Daniel's duty to get the best deal for his client conflicts with the interests of his close relative, which count as his **own interests** (para 6.1). An **own interest conflict has no exceptions**, so the client's consent, an agreed price or supervision cannot cure it. Written consent and reasonableness are conditions that apply only to **client conflicts** under para 6.2."},{"t":"conflicts-of-interest","p":"FLK1","q":"A firm acts for Brightwater Media Ltd in negotiating a £500,000 loan facility from a bank. The bank requires the company's managing director, Olu Adebayo, to give a personal guarantee of the loan. Olu asks the firm to advise him personally on the guarantee as part of the same matter, saying this would be quicker and cheaper.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the firm should respond?","o":["Advise Olu, because as managing director he is the person who gives the company's instructions to the firm.","Suggest that Olu takes separate advice, because his interests on the guarantee may conflict with the company's.","Advise Olu, provided the company's board first passes a resolution consenting to the firm acting for him.","Advise Olu, because a company and its managing director always share a substantially common interest.","Stop acting for the company, because Olu's request has created an own interest conflict for the firm."],"a":1,"e":"The client is the **company**, not the director who instructs on its behalf (para 8.1). On a personal guarantee the company wants the best terms for itself while Olu wants to limit his own liability, so there is a **significant risk of conflict** between two clients in the same matter (para 6.2), and he should take **separate advice**. A company and its director do not automatically share a common interest, and this is a client conflict, not an own interest conflict, so the firm can continue to act for the company."},{"t":"confidentiality-disclosure","p":"FLK1","q":"In a commercial contract dispute, Imran Shah receives an email from the defendant's solicitors attaching a document which is plainly counsel's confidential advice to the defendant on the weaknesses of its case. The covering email makes it obvious that it was meant for the defendant, not for Imran. His client would find the advice very useful in settlement negotiations.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Imran should do?","o":["Read the advice in full and tell his client what it says, because he must disclose all material information.","Use the advice in negotiations but not refer to it in court, because privilege protects it only at a hearing.","Keep the advice on his file without telling anyone, in case it becomes relevant later in the proceedings.","Send the advice to his client, telling the client it must not be used without the court's permission.","Stop reading once he realises the mistake, tell the defendant's solicitors and return it without using it."],"a":4,"e":"The duty to tell a client all material information (para 6.4) does not apply where the information is in a **privileged document** the solicitor knows about only because it was **mistakenly disclosed** (para 6.4(d)). Good practice is to **stop reading, tell the sender and return it**, and not use it; exploiting an obvious mistake would also call his integrity into question, and the court is likely to restrain its use. Privilege is not limited to court hearings, and quietly keeping or forwarding the document is not appropriate."},{"t":"confidentiality-disclosure","p":"FLK2","q":"Helen Ward prepared a will for Mr Pritchard, who has now died. The will appoints his friend, Mrs Clarke, as sole executor. Mr Pritchard's daughter, who receives less under the will than she expected, asks Helen for a copy of Helen's attendance notes recording why her father made the will in that form. Mrs Clarke has not been asked about the request.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Helen's position?","o":["She must not disclose the notes without Mrs Clarke's consent, because confidentiality continues after death.","She may disclose the notes, because the duty of confidentiality owed to Mr Pritchard ended when he died.","She must disclose the notes, because a child of the deceased is entitled to know the reasons behind the will.","She may disclose the notes once probate is granted, because the will and the whole file then become public.","She may disclose the notes if the daughter first agrees in writing to keep their contents confidential."],"a":0,"e":"The duty of confidentiality (para 6.3) covers **current and former clients** and **continues after the client's death**; the right to waive it passes to the **personal representatives**, here Mrs Clarke as executor. A will becomes a public document once a grant of probate is issued, but the solicitor's file and attendance notes do not. Without the executor's consent, or a legal requirement such as a court order, Helen must not disclose them, and the daughter's promise of confidentiality does not change that."},{"t":"cooperation-reporting","p":"FLK2","q":"Owen Barker acted for Ms Kaur in buying a house with a mortgage. He has just discovered that, through his oversight, the application to register the transfer and the lender's charge was not made before the priority period protecting the purchase expired. An entry in favour of the seller's creditor has since been made on the register. Ms Kaur does not yet know.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Owen must do?","o":["Try to resolve the problem with the seller's creditor first, and tell Ms Kaur only if it cannot be put right.","Tell Ms Kaur what has happened and advise her himself on whether she has a claim against the firm.","Ask Ms Kaur to sign a waiver of any claim against the firm in return for a reduction in the firm's fees.","Tell Ms Kaur promptly, advise her to take independent legal advice and notify the firm's insurers.","Report the matter to the Legal Ombudsman, which will then decide whether Ms Kaur should be compensated."],"a":3,"e":"Para 7.11 requires a solicitor to be **honest and open with clients when things go wrong** and to explain fully and promptly what has happened and its likely impact, so Owen cannot delay telling her while he tries to fix it. Because she may have a claim against the firm there is an **own interest conflict** (para 6.1), so he must not advise her on the claim but should recommend **independent legal advice**, and the firm's insurers must be notified. Asking her to waive her rights without advice would take unfair advantage, and the Legal Ombudsman acts only on a complaint by the client."},{"t":"cooperation-reporting","p":"FLK1","q":"Thomas Reid is a solicitor in a firm's commercial litigation department. After an argument outside a bar on a Saturday night, he is charged with common assault. He denies the charge and his trial is listed in three months' time. The firm's partners tell him there is no need to tell anyone unless he is convicted.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Thomas's obligation to the Solicitors Regulation Authority?","o":["He need not tell the SRA, because the incident happened outside practice and has no link to his clients.","He must tell the SRA only if he is convicted, because a charge that he denies is not yet misconduct.","He must notify the SRA promptly that he has been charged, whatever the eventual outcome of the trial.","He must notify the SRA only if the court imposes a prison sentence or a fine above a set amount.","He must tell the firm's clients about the charge, but need not tell the SRA until the trial is over."],"a":2,"e":"Under para 7.6 a solicitor must **notify the SRA promptly** if they are subject to any **criminal charge**, conviction or caution, subject to the Rehabilitation of Offenders Act 1974. The duty is triggered by the **charge itself**, so Thomas must not wait for the trial, and it applies even though the incident was outside practice. The partners' view does not displace his personal obligation."},{"t":"complaints-legal-ombudsman","p":"FLK2","q":"Mr Adeola is the executor of his late mother's estate and instructed a firm to administer it. The firm sent him its final estate accounts on 10 January 2025; they contained an error which led to an overpayment to a beneficiary. Mr Adeola did not discover the error, and could not reasonably have known of it, until 2 March 2026. He complained to the firm on 9 March 2026, and the firm sent its final response on 20 April 2026, telling him about the Legal Ombudsman and the six-month time limit.\n\nIgnoring any extension for exceptional circumstances, what is the latest date by which Mr Adeola can refer his complaint to the Legal Ombudsman?","o":["10 January 2026","9 March 2027","2 March 2027","20 April 2027","20 October 2026"],"a":4,"e":"A complaint must reach the Legal Ombudsman within **six months of the firm's final response** (where that response explained LeO and the time limit), here 20 October 2026, **and** within **one year of the act or omission or of when the complainant should reasonably have known** of the cause for complaint. Mr Adeola is more than a year from the error but within a year of when he should have known (until 2 March 2027), so that gate is met; the earlier six-month deadline therefore governs. Personal representatives are eligible to complain about services provided to the estate."},{"t":"complaints-legal-ombudsman","p":"FLK1","q":"A firm has handled commercial contract disputes for several organisations. Each is unhappy about delay and poor communication, complained to the firm, and has received the firm's final response explaining the right to go to the Legal Ombudsman.\n\nWhich of the following organisations is eligible to complain to the Legal Ombudsman?","o":["A bakery company with 6 employees and an annual turnover of €1.5 million.","A haulage company with 45 employees and an annual turnover of €3 million.","A local authority that instructed the firm to recover unpaid rent from a tenant.","A company that was the opposing party in a dispute in which the firm acted.","A manufacturing company with 12 employees and an annual turnover of €1.8 million."],"a":0,"e":"The Legal Ombudsman hears complaints from individuals and **micro-enterprises**: businesses with **fewer than 10 employees** and an annual turnover or balance sheet of **no more than €2 million**. The bakery company meets both tests; the manufacturing company fails because it has 12 employees despite its low turnover, and the haulage company fails both. Public bodies are not eligible, and an opponent cannot complain about the firm because it was not that firm's client."},{"t":"firms-compliance-protections","p":"FLK2","q":"Mrs Achebe sold her house through a sole practitioner, who dishonestly transferred her £210,000 sale proceeds to his own account instead of paying them to her. She discovered the theft last month. The practice has closed, the solicitor has been made bankrupt with no assets, and Mrs Achebe has been unable to recover the money from any other source.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate way for Mrs Achebe to seek to recover her loss?","o":["A complaint to the Legal Ombudsman, which can direct that she is repaid the full £210,000 she lost.","An application to the SRA Compensation Fund, which may make a discretionary payment to her.","A claim against the SRA itself, on the basis that it is liable for failing to supervise the practice.","A claim to the Solicitors Indemnity Fund, which pays all claims made against firms that have closed.","A complaint to the SRA, which will order the solicitor to repay her as part of its disciplinary action."],"a":1,"e":"The SRA **Compensation Fund** is a **discretionary fund of last resort** for losses caused by a solicitor's **dishonesty** or failure to account for money, where the loss cannot be recovered elsewhere; the usual maximum is £2 million per claim, and she is applying well within 12 months of discovering the loss. The Legal Ombudsman deals with poor service and its remedies are capped at **£50,000**, and the SRA does not itself award compensation. The Solicitors Indemnity Fund covers negligence claims made after a closed firm's six-year run-off cover has ended, not recent theft."},{"t":"firms-compliance-protections","p":"FLK2","q":"Marsh & Penn is a two-partner firm whose work is almost entirely residential conveyancing. Two major lenders have removed it from their panels of approved firms, its monthly income has halved, and the partners are unsure whether they can pay next quarter's rent and salaries. Client account is fully reconciled and no client has suffered any loss.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's obligation to the Solicitors Regulation Authority?","o":["It need not tell the SRA anything unless and until the firm enters a formal insolvency process.","It must tell the SRA only if client money is put at risk or a client suffers loss as a result.","It must tell the SRA only once the partners have decided to close the firm and begin a wind-down.","It must notify the SRA promptly, because there are indicators of serious financial difficulty.","It need only mention the position in its next periodic information report to the SRA."],"a":3,"e":"Under Code for Firms para 3.6(a) a firm must **promptly notify the SRA of any indicators of serious financial difficulty**; it need not wait for insolvency, a decision to close or any risk to client money. An insolvency event and an intention to cease are separate notification triggers (para 3.6(b) and (c)), and if the firm does close it must carry out an **orderly wind-down** (para 2.4). Waiting for the next information report would not be prompt."},{"t":"ndas-slapps","p":"FLK1","q":"Ellie Morgan, a trainee in a firm's commercial litigation team, is asked to prepare a chronology from 400 pages of documents relating to a dispute between two companies, including the client's internal emails and legal advice it has received. To save time, she proposes to upload them to a free, publicly available AI chatbot and ask it to produce the chronology. The firm has no agreement with the chatbot's provider.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Ellie should do this?","o":["She may, because using AI tools is permitted and her supervisor will check the output before it is used.","She may, provided she deletes the names of the parties from the documents before she uploads them.","She should not, because without proper safeguards this risks breaching confidentiality and waiving privilege.","She should not, because the SRA prohibits solicitors and trainees from using AI tools on client matters.","She may, because a free chatbot's provider has no commercial interest in the documents she uploads."],"a":2,"e":"The SRA's **Misuse of AI** warning notice (17 August 2026) permits AI use, but client information should be entered into an AI system only with appropriate **contractual, technical and organisational safeguards**. Putting it into a **public AI tool** risks breaching **confidentiality** (para 6.3) and may permanently waive **legal professional privilege** (the client's right to withhold certain communications from disclosure), and free tools pose these risks as much as paid ones. Removing names leaves the rest of the content exposed, and later supervision of the output cannot undo a breach that has already happened."}],"ethics-p2":[{"t":"principles-competence","p":"FLK1","q":"Hannah Mbeki acts for a manufacturing company in settlement negotiations over a disputed supply contract. During a call, the company's finance manager mentions in passing that he 'thinks' a variation increasing the price was agreed. Without checking the file or asking further, Hannah tells the buyer's solicitor that her client 'holds a signed variation increasing the price'. No signed variation exists. Hannah genuinely believed one did.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Hannah's position?","o":["She has not breached the SRA Principles, because she genuinely believed that what she said was true.","She has acted dishonestly, because any statement to an opponent that turns out to be false is dishonest.","She may be found to have lacked integrity, because she recklessly made a misleading statement to the opponent.","She has committed only a service failure, which is a matter for the Legal Ombudsman rather than the SRA.","She will be in breach only if the buyer has relied on the statement and suffered a financial loss as a result."],"a":2,"e":"**Integrity** (Principle 5) is wider than honesty: it means meeting the ethical standards of the profession, and **recklessly making misleading statements** is a classic example of a lack of integrity even where the solicitor is not dishonest (Wingate v SRA). A is wrong because an honest belief answers only the dishonesty charge, and B is wrong because under the Ivey test Hannah's genuine belief about the facts matters, so a false statement is not automatically dishonest. She must also correct the statement promptly (para 1.4), and the breach does not depend on loss to the opponent (E) or fall only to the Legal Ombudsman (D)."},{"t":"principles-competence","p":"FLK2","q":"Femi Adebayo is a private client solicitor who drafts wills and administers estates. He has attended no formal training courses in the past year, but he regularly reviews new probate case law, discusses difficult files with colleagues and has noted that he needs to learn more about the tax treatment of trusts created by will. He is about to renew his practising certificate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the SRA requires of Femi in relation to his continuing competence?","o":["He must reflect on his practice, identify and address his learning needs, and confirm this in an annual declaration.","He must complete at least 16 hours of accredited training each year before his practising certificate is renewed.","He must pass an SRA assessment in each area of law he practises before his practising certificate is renewed.","He must submit a log of the courses he has attended, signed by the firm's compliance officer, to the SRA each year.","He need do nothing further, because the Statement of Solicitor Competence applies only at the point of qualification."],"a":0,"e":"There is **no minimum number of CPD hours**: solicitors must **reflect on their practice, identify and address their learning needs**, and confirm this in an **annual declaration** when renewing their practising certificate, which is how para 3.3 (keep knowledge and skills up to date) is met. B describes the old hours-based scheme, and C and D invent requirements the SRA does not impose. E is wrong because the Statement of Solicitor Competence applies **throughout a solicitor's career**, not just on qualification."},{"t":"trust-fairness-undertakings","p":"FLK1","q":"Tomasz Nowak, a solicitor, acts for a building supplies company which is owed £7,200 for goods sold to Mr Ellis, a sole trader with no solicitor. The company asks Tomasz to write to Mr Ellis saying that unless he pays within seven days he will be 'arrested and prosecuted for fraud', and adding a £1,500 'collection fee'. There is no evidence of fraud, and the collection fee is not provided for by the contract or by law.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Tomasz may do?","o":["Send the letter as instructed, because a solicitor must follow the client's lawful instructions on how a debt is pursued.","Send the letter as instructed, provided he also tells Mr Ellis that he should take independent legal advice on it.","Send the letter with the threat of prosecution removed, but keep the collection fee because the client has asked for it.","Refuse to write to Mr Ellis at all, because a solicitor may not correspond directly with a person who is unrepresented.","Send a letter demanding the £7,200 and warning of court proceedings, but without the threat or the collection fee."],"a":4,"e":"Under para 1.2 a solicitor must not **take unfair advantage** of others, which includes **demanding sums not legally due** and **threatening criminal proceedings to extract a civil payment**, and para 1.4 forbids misleading anyone. Tomasz can firmly assert the genuine debt and the genuine remedy of a court claim, so E is right. A and B are wrong because the client's wishes never override these duties and a suggestion to take advice does not cure a misleading letter; C still demands a sum not due; and D is wrong because writing to an unrepresented person is permitted if done fairly."},{"t":"trust-fairness-undertakings","p":"FLK2","q":"Chloe Park, a trainee solicitor, has been authorised by her supervising partner to handle the completion of a client's sale of a leasehold flat. On the day of completion the buyer's solicitor telephones and asks about the landlord's consent to assignment. Chloe replies: 'We'll send you the original landlord's licence by Friday.' Relying on this, the buyer's solicitor completes. The partner later says the firm is not bound because Chloe is not a solicitor and never used the word 'undertake'.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's position?","o":["It is not bound, because only a solicitor can give an undertaking that is binding on the firm.","It is bound, because Chloe's statement was a promise relied on and she gave it with authority.","It is not bound, because a statement is an undertaking only if it uses the word 'undertake'.","It is bound only if the client confirms in writing that it agrees to the licence being sent.","It is not bound, because an oral statement made by telephone cannot amount to an undertaking."],"a":1,"e":"An **undertaking** is a statement, **oral or written**, **whether or not it uses the word 'undertake'**, made to someone who **reasonably relies on it**, that the giver will do something. Chloe's promise was relied on when the buyer's solicitor completed, and an undertaking given by a **trainee or other staff member with authority binds the firm**, so A, C and E are wrong. D is wrong because performance does not depend on the client's later approval: the client cannot release an undertaking, only the recipient can."},{"t":"duty-to-court","p":"FLK2","q":"Darren Hughes has pleaded guilty in the magistrates' court to criminal damage. When outlining the facts, the prosecutor tells the court that Darren has no previous convictions. Darren's solicitor knows from Darren that he has two previous convictions for theft. Before mitigation, Darren asks his solicitor to stress to the magistrates his 'clean record and good character'.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the solicitor's position?","o":["The solicitor must tell the court about the previous convictions before mitigation begins.","The solicitor may rely on good character, because it was the prosecutor who misled the court.","The solicitor must withdraw from the case immediately, telling the court only that it is for professional reasons.","The solicitor need not correct the prosecutor, but must not put Darren forward as a person of good character.","The solicitor may refer to Darren's good character, provided Darren himself does not make that assertion."],"a":3,"e":"A solicitor has **no duty to tell the court about a client's previous convictions**, so the solicitor need not correct the prosecutor's error (A is wrong), but must **not take advantage of the mistake** by asserting good character, which would mislead the court (para 1.4). B and E are wrong because relying on the error would make the solicitor complicit in misleading the court, whoever made it. Withdrawal (C) is needed only if Darren insists on the false assertion; at this stage the solicitor can continue and mitigate on other grounds."},{"t":"duty-to-court","p":"FLK1","q":"Kemi Oduya acts for a retailer in a breach of contract claim against a logistics company. Under the court's disclosure order, the retailer must disclose documents that adversely affect its case. While preparing the list of documents, Kemi finds an internal email in which the retailer's operations director admits the late deliveries were caused by its own warehouse error. The director tells Kemi to leave the email out because 'nobody else knows it exists'.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Kemi should do?","o":["Advise that the email must be disclosed and, if the client refuses, stop acting without telling the court the reason.","Leave the email out, because the duty of confidentiality to the client prevents its disclosure to the other side.","Leave the email out but tell the court privately that a damaging document exists which the client has withheld.","Disclose the email to the logistics company immediately, whether or not the client agrees that it should be disclosed.","Leave the email out, because a solicitor is not obliged to volunteer facts that are damaging to the client's case."],"a":0,"e":"Disclosure obligations override the client's wishes: withholding a document that must be disclosed would **misuse evidence** (para 2.1) and **mislead** the court and the opponent (para 1.4), so B and E are wrong. Kemi must advise the client that the email must be disclosed and, if it refuses, **cease to act**, telling the court only that she is withdrawing for professional reasons, because **confidentiality still applies** to what she knows, which rules out C and D. The principle that a solicitor need not volunteer damaging facts does not apply where a court order requires disclosure."},{"t":"instructions-capacity-service","p":"FLK1","q":"Nadia Hussain is a sole practitioner whose work is mainly commercial leases and debt recovery. A long-standing client, a growing software company, asks her to advise on a complex competition law investigation into its pricing agreements with distributors. Nadia has never handled competition law. The company says it trusts her, does not mind that she is inexperienced in the area, and prefers her because her rates are lower.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Nadia should respond?","o":["Accept the instructions, because the company has given informed consent to her lack of experience.","Accept the instructions, provided she agrees a reduced fee to reflect her lack of relevant experience.","Accept the instructions, provided her retainer letter excludes liability for any competition law advice.","Accept the instructions, because a solicitor must not refuse work from an existing client without good reason.","Act only if she can obtain the expertise needed to advise competently, and otherwise refer the company elsewhere."],"a":4,"e":"Para 3.2 requires a **competent and timely service**, and a solicitor must not take on work they lack the competence to do: the answer is to obtain help or expert support, or **refer or decline** the work. The client's consent (A) or a lower fee (B) does not make an incompetent service acceptable, and trying to exclude liability (C) does not meet the duty either. D is wrong because a solicitor is generally free to decline instructions and, where they lack competence, should do so."},{"t":"instructions-capacity-service","p":"FLK2","q":"Mr Fraser, aged 81, instructed a firm to sell his house to fund his care. Before contracts were exchanged he suffered a severe stroke, and his doctors confirm that he now lacks capacity to make decisions about his property. His daughter, Isla, holds a lasting power of attorney for health and welfare, registered with the Office of the Public Guardian. There is no other power of attorney. Isla asks the firm to proceed to exchange on her instructions.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's position?","o":["It may proceed on Isla's instructions, because her lasting power of attorney has been registered.","It may proceed on Isla's instructions, because she is Mr Fraser's closest relative and acts in his interests.","It may not proceed on Isla's instructions, and someone will need to apply to the Court of Protection for a deputy.","It may proceed on its original instructions, because Mr Fraser gave them while he still had capacity.","It may not proceed until Mr Fraser has been helped to sign a property and financial affairs power of attorney."],"a":2,"e":"A health and welfare LPA gives no authority over **property and financial affairs**, and being a close relative gives none either, so A and B are wrong. When a client **loses capacity** and no one has authority to instruct for them, the firm's **authority to act ends**, so D is wrong; the usual route is an application to the **Court of Protection** for a **deputy**. E is wrong because a person who lacks capacity cannot make a valid LPA."},{"t":"instructions-capacity-service","p":"FLK1","q":"A firm acted for Mr Asante, the claimant in a professional negligence claim against his former accountants. Mr Asante has ended the retainer after a disagreement over strategy, leaving an unpaid bill of £8,000. The trial is listed in three weeks. His new solicitors ask for the file, which they need to prepare for trial. The firm's partner wants to keep all the papers until the bill is paid.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's position?","o":["It may keep all the papers until the bill is paid, because its lien is an absolute right that the court cannot override.","It should not use its lien in a way that seriously prejudices the trial, and the court may order the papers handed over.","It must hand over the papers and write off the bill, because a client who ends a retainer owes nothing for the work done.","It must hand over the papers immediately, because a firm can never keep a client's papers after the retainer has ended.","It may keep the papers, but only if it first obtains the SRA's permission to rely on its lien over the client's file."],"a":1,"e":"A firm may generally keep a client's papers until its bill is paid under its **lien** (a right to retain property until a debt is paid), so D is wrong. But it should not use the lien where this would **seriously prejudice the client** or **obstruct court proceedings**, and the court can order the papers to be handed over on terms, so A is wrong. C is wrong because a client who ends the retainer may still have to pay for work done, and E invents a requirement for SRA permission."},{"t":"client-money","p":"FLK2","q":"A firm has finished administering the estate of the late Mrs Begum. The final estate accounts are approved, and her son, Rashid, the sole residuary beneficiary, is entitled to £60,000 held in client account. Rashid asks the firm to keep the money in client account for the next year and to pay his monthly rent and car finance instalments from it, as he finds it easier than managing a bank account.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the firm should do?","o":["Agree to hold the money, provided Rashid confirms his instructions in writing.","Agree to hold the money, provided it pays Rashid a fair sum of interest on it.","Agree to hold the money, because it arose from legal work the firm has done.","Refuse, and pay the £60,000 to Rashid promptly now that the estate is finished.","Refuse, and pay the £60,000 to the SRA until Rashid opens his own bank account."],"a":3,"e":"A firm must **not use client account to provide banking facilities** (Accounts Rules rule 3.3): payments in and out must relate to the regulated services the firm is delivering, and paying Rashid's rent and car finance is unrelated to the completed estate administration. Client money must be **returned promptly** once there is no longer a proper reason to hold it, so the firm should pay Rashid now. Written instructions (A), paying interest (B) or the money's origin in legal work (C) do not change this, and E invents a procedure."},{"t":"client-money","p":"FLK1","q":"Olivia Grant instructs a firm to defend a county court claim brought against her business by a former supplier. Before any bill has been sent, she transfers £5,000 'on account of your costs'. The firm's cashier pays it into the firm's business account. When the error is noticed, the partner says the money can stay there because the firm's costs will certainly exceed £5,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position under the SRA Accounts Rules?","o":["The money is firm money, because it was paid to meet the firm's costs.","The money may stay in business account if Olivia consents in writing.","The money may stay in business account until the firm sends its bill.","The money is firm money once the firm has done £5,000 of work on it.","The money is client money and must be moved to client account promptly."],"a":4,"e":"Money received **on account of costs before a bill or other written notification of costs** has been given is **client money** (rule 2.1(d)), so A and D are wrong. Client money must be kept **separate** from firm money in a client account, and a breach must be **corrected promptly**, so it cannot stay in business account with consent (B) or until a bill is sent (C). The firm may transfer its costs from client account only after giving Olivia a bill or other written notification of them."},{"t":"client-money","p":"FLK2","q":"Rachel Doyle is a freelance solicitor who practises on her own account, without being part of an SRA-authorised firm, preparing wills and lasting powers of attorney. An executor client asks her to receive £150,000 from the sale of the deceased's house and hold it while she distributes it to the beneficiaries. Rachel does not have a client account.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Rachel may hold the £150,000?","o":["She may not, because a freelance solicitor may hold only limited sums, such as fees paid in advance.","She may, provided she opens a client account in her own name at a bank in England and Wales before receiving it.","She may, provided the executor agrees in writing and she pays the money out to the beneficiaries within 14 days.","She may, because money received in the administration of an estate is not client money under the Accounts Rules.","She may, provided she obtains an accountant's report confirming that the money was properly held and paid out."],"a":0,"e":"Under para 4.3 a solicitor must **not personally hold client money** except as permitted, and a **freelance solicitor** may receive only limited payments, such as **fees and disbursements paid in advance**, and **cannot operate a client account**, so B, C and E are wrong. D is wrong because money received in an estate is plainly client money. The executor should use an authorised firm, which can hold the money in its client account."},{"t":"business-referrals-publicity","p":"FLK1","q":"A firm handles road traffic accident claims. A claims management company offers to refer injured motorists to the firm. The firm would pay it £300 for each motorist who instructs the firm, described in the agreement as a 'marketing services fee'. The agreement would be in writing and every client would be told about the payment.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm may enter into the arrangement?","o":["It may, because the payment is disclosed to clients and is recorded in a written agreement.","It may, because the payment is described as a fee for marketing services and not for referrals.","It may not, because referral fees in personal injury claims are banned by statute whatever is disclosed.","It may not, because a firm may never pay any introducer for referring clients in any type of matter.","It may, provided each client gives informed consent in writing to the payment before instructing it."],"a":2,"e":"**Referral fees in personal injury and death claims are banned by statute** (Legal Aid, Sentencing and Punishment of Offenders Act 2012 s.56), so disclosure, a written agreement or client consent cannot make the payment lawful, which rules out A and E. B is wrong because, under para 5.2, a payment that appears to be a referral fee is **treated as one unless the firm shows otherwise**, and a fee paid per client who instructs the firm is plainly for referrals. D is too wide: in most other matters referral fees are allowed if properly disclosed and independence is preserved."},{"t":"business-referrals-publicity","p":"FLK2","q":"A firm that specialises in probate is planning a marketing campaign. Its first idea is for staff to visit the bereavement suites of local hospitals and hand cards to recently bereaved relatives, offering to help with the estate. Its second idea is to deliver a leaflet advertising its fixed-fee probate service to every household in the town.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm may carry out these ideas?","o":["It may carry out both, because each advertises the firm's services accurately and without misleading anyone.","It may deliver the leaflets, but approaching relatives at the hospitals would be an unsolicited approach.","It may carry out neither, because a firm may not advertise its services to people who are not already its clients.","It may approach relatives at the hospitals, but the leaflets would be unsolicited approaches to the public.","It may carry out both, provided the cards and leaflets state clearly that the firm is regulated by the SRA."],"a":1,"e":"Para 8.9 bans **unsolicited approaches to members of the public**, other than current or former clients, to advertise legal services; approaching bereaved relatives in person at a hospital is a targeted personal approach and is caught, so A and E are wrong. **General advertising**, such as a leaflet delivered to every household, is not a targeted approach and is allowed if it is accurate and not misleading (para 8.8), so C and D are wrong."},{"t":"conflicts-of-interest","p":"FLK1","q":"A firm is acting for Ama and Callum, who are setting up a company together in which each will hold half the shares. At the outset they agreed all the main terms and each gave written informed consent to the firm acting for both. While the shareholders' agreement is being drafted, they fall out over who should have the casting vote at board meetings, and each asks the firm to argue for their position. Each has given the firm confidential information about their personal finances.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's position?","o":["It may continue to act for both, because each gave written informed consent at the start of the matter.","It may continue to act for both, provided it puts information barriers in place between two separate teams.","It may continue to act for Ama alone, because she was the first of the two to contact the firm for advice.","It will usually have to stop acting for both, because a real conflict has arisen between its two clients.","It may continue to act for both, because founders of a company always have a substantially common interest."],"a":3,"e":"The firm could act for both only while they had a **substantially common interest** (a clear common purpose and a strong consensus on how to achieve it). Once they negotiate against each other over control, the conflict is **real** and the firm must usually **stop acting for both**, so the original consent (A), barriers (B) or a general assumption about founders (E) do not help. It could continue for one only if it held no **material confidential information** of the other or the other consented; being first to make contact (C) is irrelevant, and here it holds both clients' confidential information."},{"t":"conflicts-of-interest","p":"FLK2","q":"Mrs Okeke wishes to sell her flat to her son, Emeka, for £180,000, a price they agreed between themselves before contacting a solicitor. There will be no negotiation of the price or other terms, and Emeka is buying with savings. They ask the firm which has acted for the family for many years to act for both of them.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm may act for both Mrs Okeke and Emeka?","o":["It may not act for both, because a firm can never act for the buyer and the seller of the same property.","It may not act for both, because acting for two members of the same family creates an own interest conflict.","It may act for both, provided each of them gives oral consent, as the price was agreed without the firm.","It may act for both, because there is no conflict at all where the price is agreed before the firm is instructed.","It may act for both if they have a substantially common interest, give written informed consent and it is reasonable."],"a":4,"e":"A buyer and seller of the same property have a **client conflict**, but the firm may act for both under the **substantially common interest** exception (para 6.2(a)), as in a family transfer on terms already agreed, if **all** the conditions are met: **written** informed consent, effective safeguards where appropriate, and it is **reasonable** to act. A is too absolute, and D is wrong because the interests of buyer and seller are still opposed, so the exception and its conditions are needed. B is wrong because the clients' relationship to each other is not the firm's own interest, and C fails because consent must be given or evidenced in writing."},{"t":"conflicts-of-interest","p":"FLK2","q":"A firm acts for Mr Lindgren, who is buying a house for £400,000 with a mortgage, and also for his lender, on the lender's standard terms. Shortly before exchange, Mr Lindgren mentions that the seller has privately agreed to give him a £30,000 'cashback' after completion, so the true price is £370,000. The lender's instructions require the firm to report any such arrangement. Mr Lindgren tells the firm not to mention it to the lender.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the firm should do?","o":["Advise Mr Lindgren that the lender must be told and, if he still refuses, stop acting for both clients.","Continue to act for both clients, because the cashback is a private matter between buyer and seller.","Tell the lender about the cashback at once, because the duty of disclosure to the lender overrides confidentiality.","Stop acting for the lender but continue to act for Mr Lindgren in the purchase without telling the lender.","Continue to act for both clients, provided Mr Lindgren agrees in writing to indemnify the firm against any loss."],"a":0,"e":"Acting for a borrower and lender on standard terms is normally allowed, but a **conflict arises** once the firm learns that the borrower is **misleading the lender** about the real price. The information is **confidential** to Mr Lindgren (para 6.3), and **confidentiality prevails over the duty of disclosure** to the lender, so the firm cannot simply tell the lender (C) and must stop acting if he will not agree. It cannot continue for him alone (D) or for both (B, E), because completing the purchase on that basis would help deceive the lender; the firm should also consider its money laundering reporting duties."},{"t":"confidentiality-disclosure","p":"FLK1","q":"Three years ago, the corporate team in the London office of a large national firm advised Halden plc on a merger and learned confidential details of its pricing and finances. A new client now wants the firm's Manchester litigation team to sue Halden plc over an unrelated supply contract, and the Halden information would be material. The firm has a permanent information barrier, set up years ago, separating the two teams' files, IT systems and supervisors. Halden plc refuses to consent.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm may act for the new client?","o":["It may not act, because a firm may never act against a former client without that client's written consent.","It may act, because the duty of confidentiality to a former client ends when the earlier retainer comes to an end.","It may act without Halden plc's consent if its measures mean there is no real risk of the information being disclosed.","It may act, but only if the new client gives written informed consent to the firm holding Halden plc's information.","It may not act, because the firm's two clients do not have a substantially common interest in relation to the matter."],"a":2,"e":"Under para 6.5 a firm holding a former client's **material confidential information** must not act against it **unless** either **effective measures** mean there is **no real risk of disclosure**, or the former client gives written informed consent. These are alternatives, so A is wrong: a well-established, permanent barrier in a large firm with separate teams and supervisors is the kind most likely to satisfy the test (Prince Jefri Bolkiah v KPMG). B is wrong because confidentiality continues after the retainer ends; D asks for consent from the wrong client; and E applies the para 6.2 exceptions, which do not apply to para 6.5."},{"t":"confidentiality-disclosure","p":"FLK2","q":"Liam Carter is being interviewed at a police station about a burglary. In a private consultation he tells his solicitor that, once released, he will 'make sure' the neighbour who reported him 'ends up in hospital', describing how he plans to attack her with a knife that evening. The solicitor reasonably believes he means it. Liam refuses to allow the solicitor to tell anyone.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the solicitor's position?","o":["The solicitor must not disclose anything, because the conversation is protected by legal professional privilege.","The solicitor may disclose to the police the information needed to prevent the attack, despite Liam's refusal.","The solicitor must give the police a full account of everything Liam has said, including about the burglary.","The solicitor must stop acting for Liam but may not disclose his threat to anyone without his express consent.","The solicitor may warn the neighbour personally, but may not pass any information to the police about Liam."],"a":1,"e":"Confidentiality (para 6.3) gives way where disclosure is **permitted by law**, including in exceptional cases to prevent a **criminal act likely to result in serious bodily harm**; the solicitor should disclose **only what is necessary**, to the **appropriate authority**. C is wrong because disclosing what Liam said about the burglary goes far beyond what is necessary, and E points to the wrong recipient. A is wrong because there is **no privilege for communications made to further a crime**, and D ignores the exception that permits disclosure."},{"t":"cooperation-reporting","p":"FLK1","q":"Samira Patel acts for the claimant in a commercial contract claim. In a telephone call, the defendant's solicitor, who works at another firm, tells her that he 'tidied up' the date on a letter exhibited to his client's witness statement so that it would appear to have been written before the contract was signed. He says he did it to help his client and that it will not affect the result.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Samira should do about the defendant's solicitor's conduct?","o":["Raise it with the defendant's solicitor and report it only if he refuses to correct the exhibit.","Leave it to the defendant's firm, because she has no duty to report a solicitor at another firm.","Wait until the claim has concluded and then report it, so that her client's case is not disrupted.","Report it promptly to the SRA, because it is capable of amounting to a serious breach by him.","Report it only if the court later finds that the letter was altered and that he acted dishonestly."],"a":3,"e":"Para 7.7 requires a solicitor to **report promptly** to the SRA facts they **reasonably believe are capable of amounting to a serious breach** by **any person the SRA regulates**, including solicitors at other firms, so B is wrong. Altering the date of an exhibited document is very likely **dishonest**, which is always serious. Samira need not be certain or wait for a court finding (E), and must not delay to suit the litigation (C) or make reporting depend on whether the solicitor puts things right (A)."},{"t":"cooperation-reporting","p":"FLK2","q":"Grace Nwosu is a solicitor in a firm's wills and probate department. Following serious personal debts, she has entered into an individual voluntary arrangement (IVA) with her creditors. She has not been made bankrupt, no client money is involved, and her practising certificate is not due for renewal for another seven months. She would prefer nobody at work or at the SRA to know.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Grace's obligation to the SRA?","o":["She need not notify the SRA, because only a bankruptcy order counts as an insolvency event.","She need not notify the SRA, because her debts are private and do not affect any client money.","She must notify the SRA, but only when she next renews her practising certificate in seven months.","She must notify the SRA only if her creditors fail to approve the arrangement she has proposed.","She must notify the SRA promptly, because an IVA is a relevant insolvency event for a solicitor."],"a":4,"e":"Under para 7.6 a solicitor must **notify the SRA promptly** if a **relevant insolvency event** occurs in relation to them, and this includes an **individual voluntary arrangement** as well as bankruptcy, so A is wrong. The duty does not depend on client money being affected (B), and it must be done **promptly**, not left until renewal of the practising certificate (C). D is back to front: the IVA has been entered into, which is what triggers the duty."},{"t":"complaints-legal-ombudsman","p":"FLK1","q":"Mr Haddad, a sole trader who runs a small joinery business, complained to a firm about its delay in pursuing a contract claim for him. After the firm's final response, he took the complaint to the Legal Ombudsman. The ombudsman's final decision directed the firm to reduce its fees by £3,000 and pay him £500 for inconvenience, and Mr Haddad accepted the decision. He now wants to sue the firm in court for £20,000 for the same delay.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Mr Haddad's position?","o":["He cannot sue over the same matter, because the decision became binding when he accepted it.","He can sue, because a Legal Ombudsman decision is only a recommendation that binds neither side.","He can sue, because the Legal Ombudsman cannot award more than £500 for any one complaint.","He cannot sue, because a sole trader is not entitled to bring a negligence claim against solicitors.","He can sue, provided he first returns the £3,500 that the firm paid under the ombudsman's decision."],"a":0,"e":"If a complainant **accepts** the ombudsman's final decision, it becomes **binding on both parties** and enforceable through the courts, and the complainant **cannot then sue over the same matter**, so B and E are wrong. Had Mr Haddad rejected the decision, it would not bind him and he could go to court. C misstates the limit, since remedies are **capped at £50,000** per complaint, and D is wrong because a sole trader can sue in negligence like anyone else."},{"t":"firms-compliance-protections","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2023 Ms Ivanova bought a house through a two-partner firm, which closed in 2024 when the partners retired; no other firm took over its practice. In 2026 she discovers that the firm negligently failed to notice a restrictive covenant which prevents her planned extension, reducing the value of her house. Neither partner acted dishonestly.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate route for Ms Ivanova to seek compensation?","o":["An application to the SRA Compensation Fund, because the firm has closed and cannot pay.","A claim against the Solicitors Indemnity Fund, which handles all claims against closed firms.","A negligence claim, which will be met by the firm's indemnity insurance run-off cover.","A complaint to the SRA, which can order the former partners to pay her compensation.","A complaint to the Legal Ombudsman, which is the only route once a firm has closed."],"a":2,"e":"Loss caused by **negligence** is met by the firm's compulsory **professional indemnity insurance**, and when a firm closes with **no successor practice** the policy provides **six years' run-off cover**, so a claim made two years after closure falls within it. The **Compensation Fund** (A) is a discretionary last resort mainly for **dishonesty** or failure to account, not for negligence where insurance is in place. The **Solicitors Indemnity Fund** (B) deals with claims arising **after** the six-year run-off period, the SRA does not award compensation (D), and the Legal Ombudsman (E) handles service complaints, with a £50,000 cap, and is not the only route."},{"t":"firms-compliance-protections","p":"FLK1","q":"Three solicitors are setting up a new commercial law firm as a limited liability partnership, to be authorised by the SRA as a recognised body. They plan to appoint Marta Silva, an experienced chartered accountant who will be the firm's finance director but is not a lawyer, as both its compliance officer for legal practice (COLP) and its compliance officer for finance and administration (COFA).\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Marta may hold these roles?","o":["She may hold both roles, because one person is allowed to be both COLP and COFA in a small firm.","She may be the COFA, but not the COLP, because the COLP must be a lawyer working in the firm.","She may be the COLP, but not the COFA, because the COFA must be a solicitor holding a practising certificate.","She may hold neither role, because both compliance officers must be lawyers who are managers of the firm.","She may hold both roles without SRA approval, because compliance officers are chosen solely by the firm."],"a":1,"e":"The **COLP must be a lawyer** (such as a solicitor) working in the firm, whereas the **COFA need not be a lawyer** and is often a finance director or practice manager, so C and D are wrong. One person can hold **both** roles in a small firm, but only if they meet the requirements for each, so A fails because Marta is not a lawyer. E is wrong because both compliance officers must be **approved by the SRA**."},{"t":"ndas-slapps","p":"FLK1","q":"A marketing agency is settling a contract dispute with Priya Raman, a freelance consultant who also alleges that the agency's managing director assaulted her at a client event. The agency asks its solicitor to draft a settlement agreement under which Priya must never disclose 'the dispute or the events at the client event to anyone, including the police or any regulator', and must repay the settlement sum if she does. The solicitor knows that a clause preventing a victim of crime from reporting to the police would be void.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the solicitor should draft the agreement?","o":["Include the clause as instructed, because it will cause no harm if a court later treats it as void.","Include the clause as instructed, provided that Priya first takes independent legal advice on it.","Refuse to draft the agreement, because confidentiality clauses in settlement agreements are not allowed.","Protect the settlement terms, but make clear that reports to the police and regulators are not prevented.","Include the clause, but remove the repayment term so that Priya is not penalised for any disclosure."],"a":3,"e":"Under the SRA's **NDA warning notice**, a confidentiality clause may protect legitimate interests such as the settlement terms, but must **not prevent or deter** reporting to the **police** or a **regulator** (para 7.5), and the agreement should say clearly what can be disclosed. A is wrong because solicitors should **not include clauses they know are unenforceable** (since 1 October 2025 the Victims and Prisoners Act 2024 s.17 makes this one void), as it would mislead Priya. Independent advice (B) does not cure the problem, removing the clawback (E) still leaves a clause that purports to stop reports, and C is wrong because NDAs are lawful in themselves."},{"t":"ndas-slapps","p":"FLK1","q":"A wealthy property developer learns that a freelance journalist plans to publish an online article about alleged bribery of planning officials linked to his companies. The journalist has given him five days to respond. He instructs a solicitor to write to the journalist personally, not to the publisher, threatening a £2 million defamation claim and demanding an undertaking within 24 hours to drop the story, with the letter headed 'Without prejudice and not for publication'. He tells the solicitor he has no intention of actually suing.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the solicitor should do?","o":["Send the letter as instructed, because a client is entitled to protect its reputation through legal threats.","Send the letter as instructed, provided it is addressed to the publisher rather than to the journalist.","Refuse to act for the developer in any way, because defamation claims against journalists are prohibited.","Send the letter as instructed but remove the heading, as the threats and deadline are a matter for the client.","Refuse to send it in that form, though a measured letter raising any properly arguable claim may be sent."],"a":4,"e":"The SRA's **SLAPPs warning notice** treats threats made for an **improper purpose** (with no intention of suing), **exaggerated** claims, **unreasonably short deadlines**, targeting an individual journalist, and **misusing labels** such as 'without prejudice' as improper, breaching paras 1.2, 1.4 and 2.4. A and D are wrong because the client's instructions do not override these duties, and changing the addressee (B) leaves the oppressive content. C goes too far: a client may pursue a genuine, **properly arguable** complaint about inaccuracies in a measured way."}],"ethics-p3":[{"t":"principles-competence","p":"FLK1","q":"Marcus Webb, a solicitor in a firm's construction disputes team, sells his own car privately through an online listing. He tells the buyer, Mrs Forde, that the car has never been in an accident, knowing that it was written off and repaired two years ago. Mrs Forde later discovers the truth and reports Marcus to the SRA. Marcus says the sale had nothing to do with his work as a solicitor.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the SRA's position?","o":["It cannot act, because the SRA Principles apply only to work done for clients of the firm.","It cannot act unless a court first finds that Marcus misrepresented the car under contract law.","It may act, because the Principles of honesty and integrity can apply to conduct outside practice.","It may act only if Marcus used the firm's name or email account when advertising the car.","It may act, but only by passing the matter to the Legal Ombudsman as a service complaint."],"a":2,"e":"The Principles apply **outside practice** so far as the conduct affects the trust the public places in solicitors, which in practice means mainly **honesty, integrity and public trust** (Principles 2, 4 and 5), so C is right. A knowing lie to a buyer about a car's history is dishonest whether or not it happens at work, which is why A and D are wrong. The SRA does not need a prior court finding (B), and the Legal Ombudsman deals only with poor service to clients (E), not misconduct."},{"t":"principles-competence","p":"FLK2","q":"The SRA brings proceedings before the Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal (SDT) against Gwen Pryce, a probate solicitor. It alleges that she transferred £4,500 from an estate's funds to pay her own tax bill, intending to repay it, and recorded the payment as an estate expense. She admits the transfer but denies being dishonest, saying she believed she was entitled to 'borrow' the money for a short time.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the SDT will approach the allegation?","o":["It will decide the facts on the balance of probabilities and, if dishonesty is proved, strike-off is the usual outcome.","It will decide the facts beyond reasonable doubt and, if dishonesty is proved, a rebuke is the usual outcome.","It will decide the facts on the balance of probabilities, but cannot find dishonesty if her belief was genuine.","It will decide the facts beyond reasonable doubt, and dishonesty requires her to know others would see it as dishonest.","It will refer the case back to the SRA, because only the SRA can decide allegations of dishonesty by a solicitor."],"a":0,"e":"The SDT decides allegations on the **civil standard, the balance of probabilities** (Solicitors (Disciplinary Proceedings) Rules 2019), and a finding of **dishonesty almost always leads to strike-off**, so A is right. B and D wrongly apply the old criminal standard, and D also misstates the test, as there is no need for her to realise that ordinary decent people would regard her conduct as dishonest. C is wrong because a genuine belief is only the first, subjective stage: the SDT then asks whether conduct based on that belief is dishonest by the standards of ordinary decent people (Ivey). E is wrong because the SDT, not the SRA, hears serious cases and can strike a solicitor off."},{"t":"trust-fairness-undertakings","p":"FLK2","q":"Shona Mackie, a solicitor, acts for Mr and Mrs Bello, who are buying a house. The seller is represented by Oakfield Conveyancing, a firm of licensed conveyancers regulated by the Council for Licensed Conveyancers, not by the SRA. Before completion, Oakfield's conveyancer promises in writing to redeem the seller's mortgage from the sale proceeds and to send evidence of discharge.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position if Oakfield fails to redeem the mortgage?","o":["The SRA can discipline Oakfield's conveyancer, because the promise was given to a solicitor.","The High Court can order performance under its supervisory jurisdiction over solicitors.","The promise has no effect at all, because only a solicitor is able to give an undertaking.","Shona becomes personally liable to redeem the mortgage, because she accepted the promise.","Shona must look to the conveyancer's own regulator or the general law, not to the SRA."],"a":4,"e":"An undertaking from someone who is not a solicitor **cannot be enforced through the SRA or the High Court's supervisory jurisdiction over solicitors**, which rests on solicitors being officers of the court (Solicitors Act 1974 s.50); another regulated lawyer answers to their own regulator, here the Council for Licensed Conveyancers, so E is right. A and B are wrong because the SRA's powers and that jurisdiction reach only those the SRA regulates and solicitors respectively. C overstates the point, and D is wrong because accepting a promise does not bind Shona to perform it, although she should take care before relying on it."},{"t":"trust-fairness-undertakings","p":"FLK1","q":"In a dispute over a former employee's misuse of trade secrets, the defendant's solicitor, Callum Reeve, writes to the claimant's solicitors: 'We undertake to deliver up to you by 5 pm on 3 March the two laptops supplied by your client.' Relying on this, the claimant does not apply for an injunction. The laptops are not delivered for six weeks, by which time data on them has been passed to a competitor and the claimant has lost a contract.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the claimant's position against Callum?","o":["Its only remedy is a complaint to the SRA, which may order Callum to pay it compensation.","It may ask the High Court to order Callum to compensate it, without proving a contract.","It has no remedy against Callum, because he gave the undertaking on behalf of his client.","It must complain to the Legal Ombudsman, which can award up to £50,000 for the loss.","It may sue Callum only if it proves that he gave consideration for the claimant's promise."],"a":1,"e":"Solicitors are officers of the court, so the High Court can use its **supervisory jurisdiction** to order a solicitor to perform an undertaking or **compensate the recipient** without the recipient having to prove a contract, which makes B right and E wrong. A is wrong because the SRA can discipline Callum for breaching para 1.3 but **cannot award compensation**. C is wrong because an undertaking given on a client's behalf binds the solicitor personally unless personal liability is clearly excluded, and D is wrong because the claimant was not Callum's client and so cannot use the Legal Ombudsman."},{"t":"trust-fairness-undertakings","p":"FLK1","q":"Fatima Chowdhury is a partner in a firm's banking team. She overhears a senior associate in her team, Jonathan, repeatedly mocking a trainee's regional accent in front of others and telling her she is 'too slow for this job'. The trainee has not complained and, when Fatima asks, says she does not want to 'make a fuss'. Jonathan is the team's highest biller.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Fatima's professional obligation?","o":["She need do nothing, because the trainee has chosen not to make a complaint.","She should leave the matter to the firm's human resources team as an employment issue.","She should wait until the trainee's next appraisal and review whether it is continuing.","She must challenge Jonathan's behaviour, because as a manager she must not tolerate it.","She must report Jonathan to the SRA at once, before raising it with anyone in the firm."],"a":3,"e":"Para 1.5 requires solicitors to treat colleagues **fairly and with respect** and not to bully or harass them, and a **manager must challenge** behaviour that falls below this standard, so D is right. The trainee's reluctance to complain (A) and Jonathan's billing do not remove that duty, and passing it to human resources (B) or waiting (C) is not challenging the behaviour. E is not the best first step: Fatima must act herself, although a report to the SRA may also be needed if the conduct is capable of amounting to a serious breach."},{"t":"duty-to-court","p":"FLK2","q":"Aaron Whitfield is charged with stealing a bicycle from outside a gym. The only evidence against him is the victim's identification of him from a distance, at night. In a private conference Aaron tells his solicitor: 'I did take it, but she can't have seen my face properly.' He wants to plead not guilty.\n\nWhich of the following may the solicitor properly do at the trial?","o":["Cross-examine the victim about the lighting and distance to challenge her identification.","Call Aaron to give evidence that he was somewhere else when the bicycle was taken.","Suggest in cross-examination that another member of the gym took the bicycle.","Tell the court that Aaron has admitted the offence and then withdraw from the case.","Refuse to act at all, because entering a not guilty plea would mislead the court."],"a":0,"e":"A defendant who admits guilt to their solicitor may still plead not guilty and **put the prosecution to proof**, so the solicitor may test the identification evidence in cross-examination, as in A. The solicitor must **not advance a positive case known to be false**, such as an alibi (B) or a suggestion that someone else did it (C). D breaches the duty of confidentiality, and E is wrong because a not guilty plea is simply a requirement that the prosecution proves its case."},{"t":"duty-to-court","p":"FLK1","q":"Isobel Grant acts for a haulage company in a contract claim. Three of the company's drivers will give evidence about disputed deliveries. Isobel plans a meeting at which the three drivers will sit together, read each other's witness statements and practise answering the questions she expects the defendant's barrister to ask, so that their accounts 'line up'.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Isobel may properly do?","o":["She may hold the meeting as planned, because the drivers are her client's own employees.","She may hold the meeting if each driver first confirms that his own statement is true.","She may explain court procedure and cross-examination generally, but not rehearse their evidence.","She must not meet the drivers at all before the trial, because they are witnesses of fact.","She may hold the meeting only if the defendant's solicitor is invited to attend it."],"a":2,"e":"A solicitor may **prepare** a witness by explaining procedure and what giving evidence and being cross-examined involve, but must **not coach** them, for example by rehearsing their evidence or sharing other witnesses' evidence, because that seeks to influence the substance of evidence (para 2.2); so C is right. The drivers being employees (A) or confirming their statements (B) does not make rehearsal proper, and inviting the other side (E) does not cure it. D is too strict, as meeting witnesses and familiarising them with the process is permitted."},{"t":"instructions-capacity-service","p":"FLK1","q":"A firm acts for Larkfield Interiors Ltd in a claim against a supplier. Its instructions have always come from the company's managing director. The company's office manager, Ben Sato, who is not a director, emails the firm asking it to accept the supplier's settlement offer of £18,000 immediately, saying the managing director is abroad and 'would want this'. The offer remains open for another ten days.\n\nWhat should the firm do?","o":["Accept the offer, because Ben is an employee and so can instruct the firm for the company.","Accept the offer, because the managing director is unavailable and the offer may lapse.","Reject the offer, because only a meeting of the full board can instruct the firm.","Ask Ben to confirm his instructions in a signed letter and then accept the offer.","Not act on the email until it has confirmed instructions with someone authorised by the company."],"a":4,"e":"Under para 3.1 a solicitor acts only on instructions from the client or someone **properly authorised** to give them and, where there is **reason to suspect** that instructions do not represent the client's wishes, must not act until satisfied that they do; the client is the company, so E is right. Ben's employment (A) or a signed letter from him (D) does not show he has authority, and with ten days left there is time to contact the managing director (B). C is wrong because a company can authorise a director or employee to instruct its solicitors; a full board meeting is not needed every time."},{"t":"instructions-capacity-service","p":"FLK2","q":"Mrs Nkemelu, aged 72, wants to make a new will. Since a stroke last year she has great difficulty speaking, although her daughter says she 'understands everything'. At the first meeting, with the daughter waiting outside, Mrs Nkemelu answers the solicitor's questions by writing short notes and pointing to a letter board, and indicates clearly who her children are and what assets she owns.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the solicitor should proceed?","o":["Decline to act, because a person who cannot speak cannot communicate a decision.","Continue, using the methods she can use to communicate and keeping a careful note.","Take instructions from the daughter, who knows her mother's wishes and can speak for her.","Refuse to act until a Court of Protection deputy has been appointed for Mrs Nkemelu.","Treat her as lacking capacity, because the stroke is an impairment of the brain."],"a":1,"e":"Capacity is **presumed**, and a person is not treated as unable to make a decision unless **all practicable steps** to help them have been taken without success (Mental Capacity Act 2005 s.1); a decision may be communicated **by any means**, such as writing or a letter board (s.3), and para 3.4 requires the solicitor to take account of the client's attributes, needs and circumstances, so B is right. A and E are wrong because the stroke does not of itself mean she lacks capacity and she can communicate. C is wrong because instructions must come from the client, and D is unnecessary where she has capacity."},{"t":"client-money","p":"FLK1","q":"A firm acted for Delphine Moreau in a breach of contract claim, which has settled. The defendant has paid the £30,000 settlement into the firm's client account. The firm's costs for the final stage of the work are £4,000 plus VAT, and Delphine agreed in the client care letter that costs could be deducted from any damages. No bill has yet been sent. The supervising partner asks the cashier to move £4,800 to business account today.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position under the SRA Accounts Rules?","o":["The transfer may be made, because Delphine agreed at the outset that costs may be deducted.","The transfer may be made, because the work is finished and the costs are therefore due.","The transfer may be made, provided Delphine is told about it in writing soon afterwards.","The transfer may be made only after Delphine has been given a bill or written notification.","The transfer may not be made, because a firm's costs can never be paid out of damages."],"a":3,"e":"Costs may be taken from client money only after the client has been given a **bill or other written notification of costs**, so D is right. Neither finishing the work (B) nor the general agreement in the client care letter (A) replaces that requirement, and telling Delphine afterwards (C) is too late. E is wrong because, once she has been billed, costs can properly be paid from money held for her where she has agreed."},{"t":"client-money","p":"FLK2","q":"Hywel Rhys, a solicitor in an SRA-authorised firm, is the attorney for Mrs Durrant under a registered lasting power of attorney for property and financial affairs. He arranges for £20,000 from the sale of her shares to be paid to him in that capacity, to fund her care home fees over the coming year.\n\nHow must the £20,000 be dealt with?","o":["It is the firm's money, so it may be paid into the firm's business account.","It may be held in Hywel's personal account, because an attorney acts personally.","It is not client money, because Mrs Durrant is not otherwise a client of the firm.","It must be paid to Mrs Durrant's care home immediately as a single lump sum.","It is client money, so it must be held in the firm's client account."],"a":4,"e":"Money held or received **as attorney** under a power of attorney is **client money** (Accounts Rules rule 2.1(c)), even if the donor is not otherwise a client, so it must be paid into client account as E says, and C is wrong. A wrongly treats it as the firm's own money, and B breaches para 4.3, under which a solicitor working in an authorised firm does not hold client money personally. D is wrong because the money is properly held for the stated purpose of paying the fees as they fall due."},{"t":"business-referrals-publicity","p":"FLK2","q":"Holbrook Estates, a local estate agent, offers to recommend Penrose & Co to buyers of homes it sells. In return Penrose & Co would pay the agent £200 for each buyer who instructs the firm on a purchase. The agent asks that the payment be 'kept between ourselves', so that buyers do not think the recommendation is biased.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Penrose & Co may enter into the arrangement?","o":["It may, but each buyer must be told of the payment and the firm's advice must stay independent.","It may, on the agent's terms, because referral fees are lawful in residential conveyancing.","It may not, because all payments to introducers are prohibited by the SRA Code of Conduct.","It may not, because referral fees are banned by statute in all residential property work.","It may, provided the payment is described in the agreement as a marketing services fee."],"a":0,"e":"Referral fees are allowed outside **personal injury** and **criminal** matters, but para 5.1 requires clients to be **informed of any financial interest** an introducer has in referring them, and the firm must keep its **independence**, so A is right and the request for secrecy (B) cannot be accepted. C and D overstate the bans, which apply only to clients in criminal proceedings (the Code) and to personal injury and death claims (LASPO 2012 s.56). E is wrong because relabelling the payment changes nothing: it is treated as a referral fee unless the firm shows otherwise (para 5.2), and it must still be disclosed."},{"t":"business-referrals-publicity","p":"FLK1","q":"Thorne Legal LLP, an SRA-authorised firm, runs online adverts aimed at small businesses: 'Debt recovery: fixed fee £295 per claim, no hidden extras.' In fact clients are also charged VAT on the fee and must pay the court issue fee, which the firm passes on to them. The partners say these charges are standard and that any business would expect them.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's position?","o":["The adverts are acceptable, because business clients are expected to know about VAT and court fees.","The adverts are acceptable, because the rules on publicity protect only individual members of the public.","The adverts are misleading, because they do not make clear that VAT and court fees are also payable.","The adverts are misleading only if a client complains to the firm and the complaint is upheld.","The adverts are prohibited, because firms may not advertise fixed fees for debt recovery claims."],"a":2,"e":"Para 8.8 requires publicity, including publicity about **charges**, to be **accurate and not misleading**, which means making clear whether prices include **VAT and disbursements**; 'no hidden extras' is plainly misleading here, so C is right. A and B are wrong because the duty is not limited to individual consumers or excused by what clients might assume (it is para 8.9, on unsolicited approaches, that is limited to members of the public). D is wrong because the breach does not depend on a complaint, and E is wrong because accurate fixed-fee advertising is lawful; indeed debt recovery is a service for which firms must publish price information under the Transparency Rules."},{"t":"conflicts-of-interest","p":"FLK2","q":"Kyle Ramsay and Dean Okonkwo are arrested together on suspicion of burglary of a warehouse and taken to the same police station. Both ask for the duty solicitor, Paula Grieve. In a private consultation Kyle tells Paula that the burglary was Dean's idea and that he only went along because Dean threatened him. Paula has not yet spoken to Dean, who is waiting to be seen.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Paula's position?","o":["She may act for both, because they are accused of the same offence and share a defence.","She may act for both if each of them gives his written informed consent to her doing so.","She must act for Dean only, because he asked for the duty solicitor at the same time.","She cannot act for both, because Kyle's account conflicts with Dean's interests.","She may act for both until the police decide whether to charge either of them."],"a":3,"e":"Kyle's account blames Dean, so their interests in the same matter conflict and Paula **cannot act for both**: a client conflict under para 6.2 can be overcome only where there is a substantially common interest or the clients are competing for the same objective, and neither applies to co-accused blaming each other, so D is right. A wrongly assumes they share a defence, and B is wrong because **consent alone never cures** a conflict where no exception applies. C is wrong because Paula holds Kyle's confidential information that is material to Dean, and E is wrong because the conflict exists now, not only after charge."},{"t":"conflicts-of-interest","p":"FLK1","q":"A firm has acted for many years for both Rohan Mehta and Ellen Shaw. Rohan has agreed to buy all the shares in Ellen's company, Shawcraft Ltd, for £1.2 million, and they have agreed the price between themselves. The share purchase agreement will still need to deal with warranties about the company, indemnities and the timing of payment. Both ask the firm to act for them to save costs, and both are willing to sign written consent.\n\nMay the firm act for both Rohan and Ellen?","o":["Yes, because they have agreed the price and so have a substantially common interest.","No, because their interests in the terms of the sale conflict and no exception applies.","Yes, because both are long-standing clients who will give written informed consent.","Yes, provided that a different partner in the firm acts for each of them throughout.","No, but only because the price is above the limit for acting for both parties."],"a":1,"e":"A buyer and seller in the same transaction have **opposing interests**, and here the warranties, indemnities and payment terms still have to be negotiated, so there is a **client conflict** and neither exception in para 6.2 (substantially common interest or competing for the same objective) applies; B is right. A is wrong because agreeing the price alone does not create a substantially common interest when key terms remain open. C and D are wrong because **consent and separate fee-earners cannot cure** a conflict where no exception applies, and E is wrong because the rule contains no monetary limit."},{"t":"conflicts-of-interest","p":"FLK1","q":"Gareth Bowen, an experienced property developer, and his aunt, Mrs Edna Bowen, aged 84, ask a firm to set up a company to buy and refurbish flats. Edna will put in £300,000, most of her savings, and Gareth will contribute his expertise. Gareth will be the sole director and hold 60% of the shares. They say the terms are agreed and both will give written informed consent to the firm acting for them both. Edna has no business experience and says she will 'leave the details to Gareth'.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm may act for both?","o":["It may act for both, because they share a common purpose and both will give written consent.","It may act for both, provided it puts an information barrier in place between the two of them.","It should not act for both, because that is unlikely to be reasonable given Edna's weaker position.","It should not act for Edna at all, because her age means she lacks capacity to make the investment.","It may act for both, provided that Gareth agrees to pay all of the firm's fees for the work."],"a":2,"e":"Even where clients have a substantially common interest, para 6.2 also requires the firm to be satisfied that it is **reasonable** to act for all of them, which is unlikely where one client is **vulnerable or much less sophisticated** and in a far weaker bargaining position; Edna is risking most of her savings on terms that favour Gareth, so C is right. A and B are wrong because written consent and safeguards are only two of the conditions, and all must be met. D is wrong because age alone does not mean a person lacks capacity, and E is irrelevant: who pays does not change whose interests the firm must protect."},{"t":"confidentiality-disclosure","p":"FLK1","q":"After a judgment for £250,000 is entered against Brannock Ltd, its director, Mr Hale, emails the company's solicitor asking how the company's machinery and contracts could be moved into a new company he controls 'so the judgment creditor cannot get at them'. The solicitor refuses to act. The assets are later transferred anyway, and the judgment creditor brings a claim to set the transfer aside, applying for disclosure of Mr Hale's emails to the solicitor.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the status of those emails?","o":["They are protected by legal advice privilege, because they were sent to a lawyer for advice.","They are protected by litigation privilege, because they were sent after judgment was given.","They are protected, because the solicitor refused to give the advice that was requested.","They can be disclosed only if the company agrees to waive its privilege in them.","They are not privileged, because communications made to further a fraud are not protected."],"a":4,"e":"Legal professional privilege does not attach to communications made **to further a crime or fraud** (the crime/fraud exception), and seeking advice on moving assets so that a creditor cannot reach them is such a purpose, so E is right. A and B are wrong for that reason, although legal advice privilege would normally cover lawyer-client communications for the purpose of legal advice. C is wrong because it is the client's purpose that matters, not whether the solicitor helped, and D is wrong because there is no privilege for the company to waive."},{"t":"confidentiality-disclosure","p":"FLK2","q":"A woman telephones a firm's private client department. She says she is the daughter of Mr Gerald Ostrowski, that he has early dementia and that she fears a neighbour has 'persuaded him to change his will'. She asks the solicitor to tell her whether her father has made a new will with the firm in the past year.\n\nHow should the solicitor respond?","o":["Decline to confirm whether Mr Ostrowski is a client or has made a will, without his consent.","Confirm whether a will was made, as long as the contents of the will are not revealed.","Tell her the contents of any will, as a concern about undue influence overrides confidentiality.","Confirm whether a will was made, because she is his next of kin and may be a beneficiary.","Confirm the position, but only after checking her identity against the firm's records."],"a":0,"e":"Under para 6.3 the duty of confidentiality covers **all information** about a client's affairs, **including the fact that someone is a client**, so the solicitor cannot confirm whether Mr Ostrowski has instructed the firm or made a will without his consent, as A says. B, D and E all disclose confidential information: being next of kin or a possible beneficiary gives the daughter no right to it, and checking her identity does not supply consent. C is wrong because the permitted exceptions, such as preventing serious harm, are narrow, and a general worry about undue influence does not justify revealing a will."},{"t":"cooperation-reporting","p":"FLK1","q":"Nikhil Bose, an associate in a commercial litigation team, notices that a trainee posted £350 received from a client for counsel's fees to the wrong client's ledger. The error was spotted and corrected the same day, before any payment was made from either ledger, and no client suffered any loss or delay. The trainee is mortified and asks Nikhil not to 'make it into a big deal'.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Nikhil's reporting obligations require?","o":["He must report it to the SRA promptly, because every breach of the Accounts Rules is serious.","He must report it directly to the SRA, because the trainee has asked him to keep it quiet.","He must ask the trainee to pay £350 into client account out of her own money.","He need not report it to the SRA, but it should be recorded under the firm's systems.","He must report it to the Legal Ombudsman, as the body that oversees client money."],"a":3,"e":"Only facts **capable of amounting to a serious breach** must be reported to the SRA (para 7.7); a one-off, inadvertent error **corrected promptly** with no loss is usually not serious, but the firm must keep a **record** of breaches, so D is right. A and B are wrong because not every Accounts Rules breach is serious, and the trainee's request does not turn a minor error into a serious one. C is wrong because no money left client account and nothing needs replacing, and E is wrong because the Legal Ombudsman deals with service complaints, not reports of breaches."},{"t":"cooperation-reporting","p":"FLK2","q":"Leah Fairbanks prepared a will for Mr Gopal Iyer six months ago. Reviewing the file, she realises that she left out the clause leaving the residue of his estate to his two children, so that on his death the residue would pass under the intestacy rules, partly to the wife from whom he is separated but not divorced. Mr Iyer is alive and well, and the error has had no effect yet.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Leah should do?","o":["Say nothing unless Mr Iyer asks, as no loss has yet been suffered and none may ever be.","Tell Mr Iyer promptly what has happened and arrange a corrected will without charge.","Stop acting and tell Mr Iyer that he must instruct another firm to prepare a new will.","Prepare a codicil and ask Mr Iyer to sign it at his next appointment without explanation.","Tell Mr Iyer only once she has worked out how much his wife would inherit on intestacy."],"a":1,"e":"Para 7.11 requires a solicitor to be **honest and open** with a client when things go wrong and to **put matters right** if possible, explaining fully and promptly what has happened; here the error can be fully corrected while Mr Iyer is alive, so B is right. Because the mistake can be remedied and no conflict remains, Leah need not stop acting (C), although she would have to recommend independent advice if a claim against the firm were likely. A, D and E all conceal or delay telling him about the mistake, which breaches the duty of openness."},{"t":"complaints-legal-ombudsman","p":"FLK1","q":"Mr Teodor Lisowski, a self-employed plumber, complained to a firm that it had handled his claim against a former customer slowly and missed chances to settle. He says this cost him £70,000 in lost settlement value and caused him great stress. The firm's final response rejected the complaint and told him about the Legal Ombudsman and the six-month time limit. He went to the Ombudsman two months later.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the Legal Ombudsman can do?","o":["Direct remedies, such as compensation for loss and for distress, up to £50,000 in total.","Order the firm to pay the full £70,000, together with compensation for his distress.","Fine the firm and refer it to the Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal for poor service.","Award compensation for distress only, because financial loss must be claimed in court.","Direct a refund of fees only, because it cannot award compensation for financial loss."],"a":0,"e":"The Legal Ombudsman can direct an apology, a refund or reduction of fees, **compensation for financial loss and for distress and inconvenience**, and remedial action, but the total value of remedies is **capped at £50,000** per complaint, so A is right and B is wrong. If Mr Lisowski wants more, he can reject the decision and sue the firm in negligence. C is wrong because the Ombudsman deals with poor service and is not a disciplinary body, and D and E wrongly restrict the types of award it can make."},{"t":"complaints-legal-ombudsman","p":"FLK2","q":"Ms Precious Okoro is the residuary beneficiary of her late uncle's estate. The executors instructed a firm to administer it 14 months ago and the estate is still not finalised, with long periods in which nobody answered her emails. On 2 June she complained in writing to the firm about the delay. The firm acknowledged the complaint the next day but has sent nothing since. It is now 15 August.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Ms Okoro can now take her complaint to the Legal Ombudsman?","o":["She cannot, because the firm's clients are the executors and not Ms Okoro.","She cannot, until the firm has sent its final response to her complaint.","She cannot, because complaints about an estate must be brought by the executors.","She cannot, because more than one year has passed since the firm was instructed.","She can, as a beneficiary, because eight weeks have passed since her complaint."],"a":4,"e":"**Beneficiaries of an estate** can complain to the Legal Ombudsman about services provided to the estate, even though the executors are the firm's clients, so A and C are wrong. The Ombudsman will normally accept a complaint once the firm has sent its final response **or eight weeks have passed** since the complaint was made; 2 June to 15 August is more than eight weeks, so B is wrong and E is right. D is wrong because the one-year limit runs from the act or omission complained of (or from when she should reasonably have known of it), and the delay is continuing."},{"t":"firms-compliance-protections","p":"FLK1","q":"Calder Brook LLP, an SRA-authorised firm, contracts with an external document review company to review 20,000 documents for disclosure in a client's commercial claim. The review company is not regulated by the SRA. It fails to identify several privileged documents, which the firm then discloses to the other side without checking. The client is unhappy, and the firm's managers say any fault lies with the review company.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's regulatory position?","o":["It has no responsibility, because the work was done by a separate company under contract.","It remains accountable for compliance, even though the work was done through a contractor.","It is responsible only if the client gave written consent to the use of the review company.","It is responsible only if the review company itself is also regulated by the SRA.","It has no responsibility, because only the review company's staff handled the documents."],"a":1,"e":"Under para 2.3 of the Code for Firms the firm **remains accountable** for compliance with the SRA's regulatory arrangements where its work is carried out **through others**, including those it contracts with: **outsourcing a task does not outsource responsibility**, so B is right. A and E are wrong for that reason, and D is wrong because accountability does not depend on the contractor being regulated. C is wrong because the client's consent to outsourcing would not remove the firm's duty to supervise and check the work for competence and confidentiality."},{"t":"firms-compliance-protections","p":"FLK2","q":"Imogen Clarke, a solicitor with a practising certificate, leaves her firm to work as an employee of a will-writing company that is not authorised by the SRA or by any other legal regulator. The company would like her to draft wills for its customers and also to prepare and submit probate applications for the families of customers who have died.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Imogen may do in this role?","o":["She may do both, because a solicitor with a practising certificate may do any legal work.","She may do neither, because solicitors may work only for businesses authorised by the SRA.","She may draft wills but not prepare probate applications for the company's customers.","She may prepare probate applications but not draft wills, because will-writing is reserved.","She may do both, provided the company tells its customers that it is regulated by the SRA."],"a":2,"e":"A solicitor may work in a business that is not authorised by a legal regulator but may **not carry out reserved legal activities** there; **probate activities** (preparing papers for a grant) are reserved, but will-writing is not, so C is right and D reverses the position. A is wrong because the restriction applies whatever her practising status, and B is wrong because solicitors may work in unregulated businesses. E is wrong because para 8.10 forbids presenting an unauthorised business as SRA-regulated, and Imogen must ensure customers understand that the business is not regulated and which protections do not apply (para 8.11)."},{"t":"ndas-slapps","p":"FLK1","q":"After losing an interim application in a commercial dispute, Daniel Pryor, a solicitor, shares on his personal social media account, which only his 'friends' can see, a cartoon ridiculing the opposing company's director's stammer, adding: 'This is who we're up against. Embarrassing.' The director hears about it from a mutual contact. Daniel says it was private banter and nothing to do with his work.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Daniel's position?","o":["No breach, because the post was on a personal account that only his friends could see.","No breach, because a solicitor's freedom of expression outside work is not regulated.","A breach only if the director brings a successful defamation claim against Daniel.","A likely breach of the Principles, as a private setting does not excuse the post.","A breach only of the firm's social media policy, which the firm alone can enforce."],"a":3,"e":"The SRA's warning notice on **offensive communications** makes clear that **intending a message to be private is no excuse** and that 'banter' at someone's expense can cross the line; mocking the disability of an opponent in a case Daniel is handling is likely to breach Principles 2 (public trust), 5 (integrity) and 6 (equality, diversity and inclusion), so D is right. A and B are wrong because the Principles apply outside practice where conduct touches on professional standing, as it plainly does here. C and E are wrong because a breach does not depend on a civil claim succeeding or on any firm policy."},{"t":"ndas-slapps","p":"FLK2","q":"Ama Boateng, a solicitor, is preparing written submissions for a client's appeal against sentence in the Crown Court. A paralegal she supervises drafted them using an AI research tool. The submissions cite four cases on sentencing for the offence, two of which Ama does not recognise. The hearing is tomorrow morning and Ama is short of time.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Ama should do?","o":["File the submissions as drafted, because the paralegal is responsible for the research.","File the submissions with a note that they were prepared with the help of an AI tool.","File the submissions, but ask the court to disregard any case that turns out to be wrong.","Remove all four cases and file the submissions without citing any authority at all.","Check each case in a reliable source before filing, and remove any that cannot be verified."],"a":4,"e":"Under the SRA's **Misuse of AI** warning notice, every authority put before the court must be **genuine, relevant and verifiable**, and the solicitor who files a document remains responsible for it, so Ama must check each case herself, as E says. A is wrong because a supervisor remains **accountable** for work done through those she supervises (para 3.5), and B and C are wrong because labelling the work or leaving the court to find errors does not stop the court being misled (para 1.4). D is not the best answer because genuine authorities may help the client and relevant ones must be drawn to the court's attention (para 2.7)."}],"land-p1":[{"t":"nature-of-land","p":"FLK2","q":"Priyanka sold her registered freehold house to Tobias. Neither the contract nor the fittings and contents form mentions the following items. Before completion she removed: solar panels bolted to the roof frame and wired into the house's electrics; a heavy stone sculpture resting by its own weight on a plinth, placed as the centrepiece of a garden she had professionally landscaped around it; and a plumbed-in washing machine.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Tobias's position?","o":["Only the solar panels were fixtures that passed to him, because only they were physically attached.","The solar panels and the sculpture were fixtures that passed to him, but the washing machine was a chattel.","All three items were fixtures that passed to him, because each was connected to or placed on the land for its use.","None of the items passed to him, because anything not listed in the contract or the form remains the seller's.","The sculpture was a fixture that passed to him, but the solar panels and washing machine were chattels."],"a":1,"e":"Where the contract and fittings form are silent, the court applies the **degree and purpose of annexation tests**, giving most weight to **purpose**. The bolted and wired solar panels are a permanent improvement to the house, and a sculpture that forms an integral part of a designed garden layout is a fixture even though it rests by its own weight. A plumbed-in washing machine can be removed easily and is a chattel (Botham v TSB Bank), so the option relying only on physical attachment is wrong, as is the idea that unlisted items stay with the seller: fixtures pass automatically unless excluded."},{"t":"nature-of-land","p":"FLK2","q":"Olumide owns a registered freehold house. By a deed that is validly signed, witnessed and delivered, he grants his neighbour Bea, for the benefit of her adjoining cottage, a right to use a footpath across his garden 'for the rest of Bea's life'. Nothing is entered on Olumide's register. Bea uses the path every day. Olumide then sells his house to Callum for full value, and Callum is registered as proprietor.\n\nIs Callum bound by Bea's right to use the footpath?","o":["Yes, because the right was granted by a valid deed and so takes effect as a legal easement.","Yes, because Bea's daily use of the path would have been obvious on a reasonably careful inspection.","No, because a right lasting for Bea's life can only be equitable and it was not protected by a notice.","No, because a right of way cannot be an easement if it is granted to a named individual.","Yes, because Bea's daily use of the path means she is in actual occupation of it."],"a":2,"e":"An easement can be legal only if it is for a period **equivalent to a fee simple or a term of years** (LPA 1925 s.1(2)(a)); a right for someone's **life** is neither, so it is **equitable** however it was made. An equitable easement binds a buyer for value of registered land only if protected by a **notice** (LRA 2002 s.29); the 'obvious on inspection' rule in Sch 3 para 3 applies only to **legal** easements. Using a path is not actual occupation of it, and the easement here benefits Bea's cottage, so it is not a personal right for that reason."},{"t":"formalities","p":"FLK2","q":"Gareth is the registered proprietor of a flat. He signs a transfer of the flat to his niece, which states that it is executed as a deed. He signs it alone in his study. The next morning he shows it to his neighbour Olu, who is not a party, tells her that he signed it the night before, and asks her to sign as his witness, which she does. The document is then handed to the niece.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the transfer is a valid deed?","o":["It is a valid deed, because it states on its face that it is intended to be a deed.","It is a valid deed, because the witness who attested it is not a party to the transfer.","It is not a valid deed, because an individual's deed must also be sealed to be effective.","It is a valid deed, because handing it to the niece amounted to delivery as a deed.","It is not a valid deed, because Gareth did not sign it in the presence of the witness."],"a":4,"e":"Under **LP(MP)A 1989 s.1**, a deed must be clear on its face, **signed by the individual in the presence of a witness who attests the signature**, and delivered. A witness who signs later, on being told about the signature, has not attested it, so the document is not validly executed however clear its wording and even though it was handed over. Seals are no longer required for individuals, and the witness not being a party is necessary but not sufficient."},{"t":"formalities","p":"FLK2","q":"Tomasz and his partner Wen are the registered proprietors of a house, which they hold on trust for themselves as tenants in common in equal shares. During a family lunch, in front of several relatives, Tomasz tells his sister Agata: 'I am giving you my half share of the house, outright, from today.' Nothing is put in writing. A month later Tomasz dies. His will leaves his whole estate to his friend Rhys.\n\nWho is entitled to Tomasz's half share in the house?","o":["Agata, because a gift of a beneficial share is effective if it is made in front of witnesses.","Agata, because the oral gift took effect as a trust that is merely unenforceable.","Wen, because Tomasz's share passed to her by survivorship on his death.","Rhys, because the oral attempt to dispose of Tomasz's equitable interest was void.","Agata, because Wen as the other trustee was present and did not object."],"a":3,"e":"Tomasz held an existing **equitable interest** (a beneficial share under a trust), and **LPA 1925 s.53(1)(c)** requires a disposition of a subsisting equitable interest to be **in writing signed** by the person disposing of it; otherwise it is **void**, not just unenforceable. The share therefore stayed with Tomasz and passes under his will to Rhys. Survivorship does not apply because they were **tenants in common**, and witnesses or a trustee's acquiescence cannot replace the signed writing."},{"t":"registered-land","p":"FLK2","q":"Farah, the registered proprietor of a house, granted her neighbour Kenji a valid written option to buy a strip at the end of her garden. Kenji has not protected the option on the register and does not use or occupy the strip. Farah later transfers the whole house to her nephew Lewis for £1, 'to help him get started', and Lewis is registered as proprietor. Kenji now wishes to exercise the option.\n\nIs Lewis bound by the option?","o":["Yes, because a transfer for nominal consideration does not take free of earlier unprotected interests.","No, because the option was not protected by a notice when Lewis was registered.","No, because a transferee who gives any consideration and registers takes free of unprotected interests.","Yes, because an option to purchase overrides a registered disposition whether or not it is protected.","No, because an option can only be protected by a restriction and none was entered."],"a":0,"e":"The **special priority rule in LRA 2002 s.29** protects only a registrable disposition **for valuable consideration**, and **nominal consideration** such as £1 does not count. Lewis is therefore in the same position as a donee and is bound under the **basic rule (s.28)**: interests rank in order of creation, protected or not. The options based on lack of a notice would be right only for a genuine buyer; an option is not overriding unless its holder is in actual occupation, and it is protected by a notice, not a restriction."},{"t":"registered-land","p":"FLK2","q":"Callum is the registered proprietor of a house that he lets out between tenancies; it is currently empty while he works abroad. A fraudster forges Callum's signature on a transfer of the house to himself, is registered as proprietor, and then sells the house for full value to Ingrid, who carries out all proper searches and enquiries. Ingrid is registered as proprietor and moves in with her family. Callum discovers the fraud on his return.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome?","o":["The register will be altered in Callum's favour as of right, because the forged transfer was void.","The register is unlikely to be altered against Ingrid, and Callum can instead claim an indemnity.","The register will be altered in Callum's favour, and Ingrid has no indemnity claim against the registry.","Neither party has any remedy, because registration conclusively vests the legal estate in Ingrid.","The register will be altered against Ingrid only if Callum proves she knew of the forgery."],"a":1,"e":"Correcting a forged registration is **rectification**, but a **registered proprietor in possession** is protected (LRA 2002 Sch 4): the register cannot be rectified against her without her consent unless she **caused or substantially contributed to the mistake by fraud or lack of proper care**, or it would be **unjust** not to rectify. Ingrid is innocent, careful and living there, so rectification is unlikely, and Callum, who suffers loss because rectification is refused, can claim an **indemnity** from HM Land Registry (Sch 8). Registration under s.58 is not the end of the matter, and knowledge is not the only route to rectification, so those options are wrong."},{"t":"unregistered-land","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2004 Harriet, the owner of an unregistered farm, granted her neighbour Joel a right to drive across one of her fields to reach his barn, by a document signed by both of them but not executed as a deed. Joel has used the track ever since but never registered anything. In 2026, after a dispute with Joel, Harriet sells the field to her brother Isaac for £5,000, although it is worth £60,000. Isaac knows all about Joel's use of the track.\n\nIs Isaac bound by Joel's right of way?","o":["Yes, because Isaac had actual knowledge of the right of way when he bought the field.","Yes, because a sale at a large undervalue to defeat the right is not made in good faith.","No, because it is an equitable easement that was not registered as a land charge.","Yes, because legal rights of way bind every later owner of unregistered land.","No, because a right of way created without a deed can only be a personal licence."],"a":2,"e":"Without a deed the right is not legal, but as a written grant signed by Harriet it is an **equitable easement**, which, if created after 1925, must be registered as a **Class D(iii) land charge**. If not, it is **void against a purchaser of a legal estate for money or money's worth** (LCA 1972 s.4(6)), and **actual knowledge, motive and the adequacy of the price are irrelevant** (Midland Bank Trust Co v Green). The doctrine of notice does not apply to registrable land charges, and the right is proprietary rather than a licence."},{"t":"unregistered-land","p":"FLK2","q":"Amara is buying an unregistered freehold house in 2026. The seller's title deeds include: a 1994 conveyance on sale of the whole house; a 2008 voluntary conveyance by which the then owner gave the house to his son; and a 2015 conveyance on sale of the house by the son to the seller. Each document deals with the whole legal and equitable interest and describes the property adequately.\n\nWhich document would provide the best root of title for Amara's solicitor to accept?","o":["The 1994 conveyance on sale, because it is over 15 years old and was a sale for value.","The 2015 conveyance on sale, because it is the most recent transaction in which title was checked.","The 2008 voluntary conveyance, because it is the most recent document over 15 years old.","Any of the three documents, because each deals with the whole interest and describes the land.","None of the documents, because a good root of title must be at least 30 years old."],"a":0,"e":"A buyer is entitled to title going back at least **15 years** from a **good root** (LPA 1925 s.44): a document dealing with the whole legal and equitable interest, describing the land, casting no doubt on title, and at least 15 years old. The **conveyance on sale** is the best root because a buyer paying value would have investigated title then; a **voluntary conveyance** (a gift) is weaker because nobody checked title, so the 2008 document is acceptable but not the best. The 2015 conveyance is too recent, and the 30-year period was reduced to 15 years in 1969."},{"t":"co-ownership","p":"FLK2","q":"Asha, Bilal, Chidi and Dara bought a house together and are registered as proprietors holding as beneficial joint tenants. Asha sends written notice of her wish to sever immediately by recorded delivery to Bilal and Chidi only. Bilal then secretly mortgages his beneficial interest to a lender to raise money. Chidi later dies, leaving everything by will to his wife Esme.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the beneficial ownership of the house now?","o":["Asha and Bilal each hold a quarter as tenants in common, and Dara holds a half by survivorship.","Asha, Bilal, Dara and Esme each hold a quarter of the house as tenants in common.","Bilal holds a quarter as a tenant in common, and Asha, Dara and Esme share three quarters.","Asha, Bilal and Dara hold the whole as joint tenants, so each has an equal interest.","Bilal holds a quarter as a tenant in common, and Asha and Dara hold three quarters as joint tenants."],"a":4,"e":"A **written notice of severance under LPA 1925 s.36(2)** must be given to **all** the other joint tenants, so Asha's notice, not sent to Dara, did not sever. Bilal's mortgage of his own interest is an **act operating on his share**, which severs it, giving him a quarter as a **tenant in common** while the other three remain joint tenants of three quarters. On Chidi's death **survivorship** passes his interest to Asha and Dara, and his will cannot operate on a joint tenancy, so Esme takes nothing."},{"t":"co-ownership","p":"FLK2","q":"Maryam and her husband Idris own their home as beneficial joint tenants. Two years ago Idris was made bankrupt, and his trustee in bankruptcy now applies to court for an order for sale of the house. Maryam has recently been diagnosed with a terminal illness and has been told she has a few months to live; her doctors say a move would seriously harm her care.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court is likely to deal with the application?","o":["It must order an immediate sale, because more than one year has passed since the bankruptcy.","It will weigh the purposes of the trust and Maryam's wishes equally with the creditors' interests.","It cannot order a sale, because Maryam's beneficial interest is not part of the bankrupt's estate.","It may postpone a sale, because a co-owner's terminal illness can amount to exceptional circumstances.","It will order a sale unless Maryam can buy the trustee's share within a reasonable period."],"a":3,"e":"Where a **trustee in bankruptcy** applies for sale, the court applies **Insolvency Act 1986 s.335A**, not the TLATA s.15 factors. **After one year** the court must assume the **creditors' interests outweigh all other considerations unless the circumstances are exceptional**, and a co-owner's serious or terminal illness is the classic example of exceptional circumstances that can justify postponing sale (ordinary hardship is not: Re Citro). The one-year rule does not make sale automatic, and although Maryam's own share is protected, the trustee can still apply for sale of the house to realise Idris's share."},{"t":"easements","p":"FLK2","q":"Ruth owned a cottage fronting a road and a field behind it. She always reached the field by a gravelled track running beside the cottage. The field also has its own gate onto a farm lane on the far side, although that lane is often muddy. Ruth sold the cottage and the land on which the track runs to Sanjay, keeping the field. The transfer deed says nothing about any rights. Ruth wants to continue using the track to reach her field.\n\nIs Ruth entitled to use the track?","o":["No, because easements implied in a seller's favour are limited to necessity and common intention.","Yes, because the track was visible, in use at the sale and necessary for reasonable enjoyment of the field.","Yes, because the general words implied into every conveyance turned her use of the track into an easement.","Yes, because a way of necessity arises where the other access to the land is much less convenient.","No, because an easement can never be implied into a transfer that is silent about rights."],"a":0,"e":"Ruth is the **seller** claiming a right over land she has sold, so she needs an **implied reservation**, and only **necessity** and **common intention** can give one; the quasi-easement rule and the general words in LPA 1925 s.62 operate **only in favour of a buyer**. There is no necessity because the field has another access, and mere inconvenience is not enough. Easements can be implied into silent transfers, but not on these facts for the seller."},{"t":"easements","p":"FLK2","q":"For the last 30 years Kemi's registered freehold house has drained foul water through an underground pipe running beneath her neighbour's garden. No right was ever granted and the neighbour never gave permission, although the neighbour has always known about the pipe. Nothing is entered on either register, and there is no visible sign of the pipe on the surface. The neighbour sells the garden land to Lucas for full value. Lucas did not know about the pipe, which Kemi's house uses every day.\n\nIs Lucas bound by a right for Kemi to drain through the pipe?","o":["No, because the pipe was neither known to Lucas nor obvious on a reasonably careful inspection.","No, because an easement arising from long use must be entered on the register to bind a buyer.","Yes, because a legal easement that was exercised within the year before the sale overrides.","Yes, because Kemi is in actual occupation of the land through which her drainage pipe runs.","No, because a right to drain water through a neighbour's land cannot be an easement."],"a":2,"e":"Thirty years' use as of right (without force, secrecy or permission; it was not secret because the neighbour always knew of the pipe) gives Kemi a **legal easement by prescription**. Under **LRA 2002 Sch 3 para 3**, a legal easement overrides a registered disposition if the buyer **knew** of it, **or** it was **obvious on a reasonably careful inspection**, **or** it was **exercised within one year** before the disposition. Daily use satisfies the last limb, so Lucas is bound even though the pipe was hidden and unknown; using a pipe is not actual occupation, and drainage rights are well-recognised easements."},{"t":"covenants","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2008 Gwen sold part of her garden to Hamid, who covenanted for himself and his successors to keep the boundary wall between the two plots in good repair. In 2015 Hamid sold his plot to Isla, who gave Hamid a covenant to observe the obligation and to indemnify him. In 2022 Isla sold to Jonah without taking any covenant from him. The wall is now collapsing and Gwen wants it repaired.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Gwen's position?","o":["She can sue Jonah, because the covenant expressly binds Hamid's successors in title.","She can sue only Hamid, who may then seek to recover his loss from Isla under her indemnity.","She can sue Isla directly, because Isla gave a covenant when she bought the plot.","She can sue Jonah, because the covenant touches and concerns her retained land.","She cannot sue anyone, because positive covenants are unenforceable between freeholders."],"a":1,"e":"The **burden of a positive covenant** (here, to repair) **never runs** with freehold land, at law or in equity, however the deed is worded (Rhone v Stephens), so Jonah is not bound. The **original covenantor remains liable** on his contract, so Gwen can sue Hamid, who can claim under the **indemnity covenant** (a promise to reimburse) given by Isla. Isla promised Hamid, not Gwen, so Gwen cannot sue her directly, and the chain breaks at Jonah, who gave no covenant."},{"t":"covenants","p":"FLK2","q":"In 1999 Kofi sold a plot to Leanne, who covenanted not to use it for any business purpose. The conveyance identified Kofi's retained land, known as Elmfield, and did not exclude any statutory provisions. In 2010 Kofi sold Elmfield to Mei. In 2018 Leanne sold the plot to Nils; the covenant is protected by a notice on the register of the plot. Nils has now opened a dog grooming business there.\n\nCan Mei enforce the covenant against Nils?","o":["No, because the benefit was not expressly assigned to Mei when she bought Elmfield.","No, because neither Mei nor Nils was a party to the original covenant.","Yes, because both the benefit and the burden pass at common law with a legal estate.","No, because the covenant ended when Kofi first sold the land that it benefited.","Yes, because the burden passed in equity and statute annexed the benefit to Elmfield."],"a":4,"e":"Against a successor of the covenantor, the **burden must run in equity** (restrictive, benefiting the covenantee's land, intended to run under s.79, and protected by notice), and the **benefit must also pass in equity**. **LPA 1925 s.78 automatically annexes** the benefit of a post-1925 covenant to **identifiable** benefited land unless excluded (Federated Homes v Mill Lodge), so it passed to Mei with Elmfield without any express assignment. The burden never runs at common law, and a covenant is not ended by a sale of the benefited land."},{"t":"mortgages","p":"FLK2","q":"Sunil's house is subject to a first legal charge to Cobalt Bank, which is owed £220,000, and a second legal charge to Delta Loans, which is owed £90,000. Sunil defaults on both. Cobalt Bank validly exercises its power of sale and sells the house for £300,000. The costs of the sale are £10,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Delta Loans' position?","o":["It receives £70,000 from the proceeds and can sue Sunil personally for the remaining £20,000.","It receives £80,000 from the proceeds, because the costs of sale are paid after both lenders.","It receives £90,000 in full, because both charges rank equally against the proceeds.","It receives nothing from the proceeds, because a sale by the first lender extinguishes later charges.","It receives £70,000 from the proceeds, and its claim for the remaining £20,000 is extinguished."],"a":0,"e":"Under **LPA 1925 s.105** the selling lender holds the proceeds on trust and applies them to any prior mortgages, then the **costs of sale**, then **its own debt**, then **later lenders** in order, then the borrower. That leaves £300,000 minus £10,000 minus £220,000, so **£70,000** for Delta Loans. The sale **overreaches** Delta's charge, which is paid from the proceeds rather than lost, and the unpaid £20,000 remains recoverable from Sunil on his **personal covenant** to repay."},{"t":"mortgages","p":"FLK2","q":"Nadia and her husband Owen jointly own their home. They jointly apply to Fir Building Society for a loan secured by a charge over the home, stating on the application form that the money is to buy a holiday flat for the family. Fir has no reason to doubt this and deals with them together, without asking for any solicitor's confirmation. In fact Owen pressured Nadia into signing and uses the money to pay his personal gambling debts. The loan is now in arrears.\n\nCan Nadia have the charge set aside against Fir Building Society?","o":["Yes, because a lender is put on inquiry whenever a wife charges her home with her husband.","Yes, because the lender failed to obtain written confirmation that Nadia had been advised.","No, because undue influence cannot be proved between spouses who own their home jointly.","No, because a joint loan that appears to be for joint purposes does not put the lender on inquiry.","Yes, because Owen's undue influence makes any charge she signed void against all lenders."],"a":3,"e":"A lender is **put on inquiry** where a surety in a non-commercial relationship guarantees **another person's** debts, but **not** where a couple take a **joint loan that appears to be for their joint purposes**, because on its face the loan benefits both (CIBC Mortgages v Pitt). Fir had no reason to know the money was really for Owen alone, so it did not need to take the Etridge steps and is **not affected** by Owen's undue influence, even though Nadia may be able to prove it. Undue influence makes a transaction voidable against the wrongdoer and those with notice, not void against everyone."},{"t":"leases-nature","p":"FLK2","q":"Pavel lets a room in his house to Quinn for £650 a month under a written document headed 'Tenancy Agreement'. Under the agreement Pavel keeps a key, cleans the room every day and changes the bed linen each week, and Quinn has no right to refuse him entry for those purposes. Eight months later Pavel tells Quinn he wants the room back.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Quinn's status?","o":["Tenant, because the document is headed tenancy agreement and rent is paid monthly.","Tenant, because a landlord's reserved right to enter is consistent with exclusive possession.","Licensee, because Pavel's services need unrestricted access, so Quinn lacks exclusive possession.","Licensee, because a monthly arrangement with no fixed end date fails for uncertainty of term.","Tenant, because any occupier paying a market rent for a room has a lease of it."],"a":2,"e":"A lease requires **exclusive possession** for a term (Street v Mountford), and an occupier who receives **attendance or services** requiring the owner to have **unrestricted access**, such as daily cleaning and changing linen, is a **lodger** and so a **licensee**. The label on the document does not decide the question either way. A limited right to enter to inspect or repair would be consistent with exclusive possession, but this is unrestricted access, and a monthly periodic arrangement is certain because each period is certain."},{"t":"leases-nature","p":"FLK2","q":"Yasmin is the registered proprietor of a freehold workshop. She and Zeke sign a written agreement containing all the agreed terms under which Yasmin will grant Zeke a five-year lease of the workshop at a market rent. No deed is ever executed. Zeke moves in and trades from the workshop daily. Yasmin then sells the freehold to Alba for full value without mentioning Zeke, and Alba is registered as proprietor. Nothing about Zeke is on the register, and Alba never asked him about his rights.\n\nIs Alba bound by Zeke's right to occupy the workshop?","o":["Yes, because a lease granted for seven years or less overrides a registered disposition.","Yes, because Zeke holds an equitable lease and was in actual occupation when Alba bought.","No, because a lease of more than three years not made by deed gives only a licence.","No, because an equitable lease binds a buyer only if it is protected by a notice.","Yes, but only if Alba actually knew of the written agreement before completion."],"a":1,"e":"A lease of more than three years needs a **deed** to be legal, so Zeke has no legal lease and cannot rely on the short legal lease rule in LRA 2002 Sch 3 para 1. But a written contract complying with **s.2 LP(MP)A 1989** creates an **equitable lease** (Walsh v Lonsdale), which is a proprietary interest. It **overrides under Sch 3 para 2** because Zeke was in **actual occupation** at completion, was not asked about his rights, and his occupation was obvious, so a notice was not needed and Alba's knowledge is irrelevant."},{"t":"leases-covenants","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2014 Ash Properties granted Birch Ltd a 15-year lease of an office, with a qualified covenant against assignment. In 2020 Ash consented to an assignment to Cedar Ltd on the lawful condition that Birch entered an authorised guarantee agreement, which it did. Cedar has failed to pay the quarter's rent due on 25 March 2026 and the quarter's rent due on 24 June 2026. On 1 November 2026 Ash serves a notice on Birch in the prescribed form claiming both quarters.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Birch's liability?","o":["It is liable for both quarters, because it guaranteed its immediate assignee under the guarantee agreement.","It is liable for neither quarter, because a tenant is released from all liability on a lawful assignment.","It is liable only for the March quarter, because its guarantee covers only the assignee's first default.","It is liable for both quarters, because the six-month time limit applies only to leases granted before 1996.","It is liable only for the June quarter, because the notice was served within six months of that sum falling due."],"a":4,"e":"Although Birch was released on the lawful assignment (LTCA 1995 s.5), its **authorised guarantee agreement** makes it liable for its immediate assignee's defaults. But to recover a **fixed charge** such as rent from a former tenant or its guarantor, the landlord must serve a **s.17 notice within six months of the sum falling due**. The notice was served more than six months after 25 March but within six months of 24 June, so only the June quarter is recoverable; s.17 applies to both old and new leases."},{"t":"leases-covenants","p":"FLK2","q":"Fennel Ltd holds a commercial lease granted in 2019 containing a covenant not to assign without the landlord's consent. It applies in writing for consent to assign to Orchid Ltd, which has strong accounts and good references and intends to use the premises for the permitted use. Within a week the landlord refuses in writing, giving one reason: Orchid currently leases another unit from the same landlord in the same parade, and the landlord fears that unit would be left empty.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the landlord's refusal?","o":["It is unreasonable, because it pursues an aim unconnected with the letting of these premises.","It is reasonable, because a landlord may protect the value of its other property nearby.","It is reasonable, because the landlord replied promptly in writing and stated its reason.","It is unreasonable, because a landlord may refuse consent only under an absolute covenant.","It is reasonable, because the tenant has the burden of proving that a refusal is unreasonable."],"a":0,"e":"A qualified covenant implies that consent is **not to be unreasonably withheld** (LTA 1927 s.19(1)(a)), and a landlord cannot refuse for a **collateral purpose** unconnected with the landlord and tenant relationship in respect of the premises, such as protecting the occupancy of its other units (Houlder Bros v Gibbs). Replying promptly with reasons satisfies the procedural duty in LTA 1988 s.1 but does not make a bad reason good, and under s.1(6) the **burden is on the landlord** to show its refusal was reasonable. Fennel can claim **damages** under the 1988 Act."},{"t":"leases-remedies","p":"FLK2","q":"Garnet Ltd holds a written lease of a shop with a flat above. The lease permits the flat to be used as living accommodation for Garnet's staff, and Garnet's manager lives there. Garnet owes £15,000 in rent for the premises. The landlord wants to instruct an enforcement agent to take control of and sell the stock in the shop to recover the arrears.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the landlord can use Commercial Rent Arrears Recovery (CRAR)?","o":["It can, because the premises are let under a written lease to a company.","It can, but only for arrears that amount to at least one quarter's rent.","It cannot, because CRAR may be used only after forfeiture proceedings have begun.","It cannot, because part of the premises is lawfully let or used as a dwelling.","It can, provided that it first gives Garnet fourteen clear days' notice of enforcement."],"a":3,"e":"**CRAR** (Tribunals, Courts and Enforcement Act 2007 Part 3) lets a landlord use an enforcement agent to seize and sell the tenant's goods, but only for premises let wholly for **commercial** use: it is **not available if any part is let or used as a dwelling**, unless that use breaches the lease. Here residential use of the flat is permitted, so CRAR is unavailable and the landlord must sue for the debt or forfeit. Where CRAR applies, the minimum arrears are **seven days' rent** and the notice period is **seven clear days**, and CRAR is used to keep the lease alive, not after forfeiture."},{"t":"leases-remedies","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2018 Hazel Estates granted Juno Ltd a 15-year lease of a warehouse containing a tenant's repairing covenant and a right of re-entry. The warehouse is now in disrepair. In 2026 Hazel serves a valid notice under s.146 of the Law of Property Act 1925, which correctly tells Juno of its right to serve a counter-notice. Two weeks later Juno serves a counter-notice claiming the benefit of the Leasehold Property (Repairs) Act 1938.\n\nWhat is the effect of Juno's counter-notice?","o":["Hazel may forfeit once a reasonable time to repair has passed, as the counter-notice has no effect.","Hazel may claim damages for the disrepair, but the counter-notice prevents it from forfeiting.","Hazel needs the court's leave before it can forfeit the lease or claim damages for the disrepair.","Hazel can never forfeit the lease for disrepair, because the 1938 Act prohibits it entirely.","Hazel needs the court's leave only if more than seven years of the term remain unexpired."],"a":2,"e":"The **Leasehold Property (Repairs) Act 1938** applies to leases granted for **7 years or more** with **3 years or more** left to run, which this lease satisfies. If the tenant serves a **counter-notice within 28 days**, the landlord cannot **forfeit or sue for damages** for the disrepair **without the court's leave**, which it gets only by proving a statutory ground (for example, that immediate repair is needed to prevent substantial loss in the value of its reversion). The Act restricts both remedies rather than barring forfeiture altogether, and the threshold is 3 years remaining, not 7."}],"land-p2":[{"t":"nature-of-land","p":"FLK2","q":"Halima ran a bakery from a shop let to her under a ten-year lease which expired on 31 March 2026. During the lease she installed two commercial ovens bolted to the floor and wired into a dedicated electricity supply, and glass display counters screwed to the walls. The lease says nothing about removal. She moved out on 31 March, leaving everything behind, and handed back the keys. In May she asks the landlord to let her in to collect the ovens and counters, promising to make good any damage.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Halima's position?","o":["She can remove the ovens and counters, because trade fixtures always remain the tenant's own chattels.","She can remove the counters but not the ovens, because only items not wired into the building are removable.","She cannot remove any of them, because her right to take away trade fixtures ended when the lease did.","She can remove all of them within a reasonable time after the lease ends, provided she makes good any damage.","She cannot remove any of them, because items bolted or screwed to a building are always landlord's fixtures."],"a":2,"e":"The ovens and counters were attached to the building, so they were fixtures, but as **trade fixtures** a tenant may remove them **before the lease ends** (or while it stays on in possession), making good any damage. Halima left at the end of the term without removing them, so they now belong to the landlord with the building (C). D is tempting, but there is no general period of grace after the tenant gives up possession; A wrongly treats trade fixtures as chattels, and E ignores the tenant's right of removal during the term."},{"t":"nature-of-land","p":"FLK2","q":"Felix owns a registered freehold house with a large back garden. A developer is building flats on the neighbouring plot. Its tower crane is positioned so that, whenever it rotates, the jib swings about 20 metres above Felix's garden. The jib carries no loads over the garden and has caused no damage. The developer has not asked Felix's permission. Felix wants the jib to stop entering the space above his land.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Felix's position?","o":["The jib is a trespass into his airspace, and he can seek an injunction to stop it.","There is no trespass, because the jib carries no loads and has caused no physical damage.","There is no trespass, because a landowner owns no airspace above the height of his buildings.","The jib is not a trespass, but he can claim damages in nuisance for the loss of his view.","There is no trespass, because a developer has an automatic right to oversail during building works."],"a":0,"e":"A landowner owns the airspace above the land **so far as is reasonably necessary for the ordinary use and enjoyment** of the land. A low-level intrusion such as a crane jib or an overhanging sign interferes with space the owner could use, so it is a **trespass**, actionable without proof of damage, and an injunction is the usual remedy (A). Contrast an aircraft flying high overhead, which does not trespass (Bernstein v Skyviews). B wrongly requires damage, C states the limit far too narrowly, the developer has no automatic right to oversail (E), and there is no right to a view (D)."},{"t":"formalities","p":"FLK2","q":"Rohan agreed with Bethan to buy her house together with a separate lock-up garage two streets away for a total price of £410,000. Their solicitors drew up a single contract, which both of them signed and which set out the parties, the price and the house, but by an oversight it did not mention the garage. Contracts were then exchanged. Before completion Bethan receives a higher offer for the house and tells Rohan she will not proceed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether there is a binding contract for the house?","o":["There is a binding contract, because the document identifies the parties, the property and the price.","There is a binding contract for the house only, and the garage can be dealt with by a separate oral agreement.","There is a binding contract, because exchange of contracts cures any omission from the written terms.","There is no binding contract, because the signed document omits a term the parties expressly agreed.","There is no binding contract, because each party had to sign a separate copy before exchange could happen."],"a":3,"e":"Under **s.2 Law of Property (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1989** a contract for the sale of land must be in writing, **contain all the terms the parties have expressly agreed** (in one document or by reference to another) and be signed by or for each party. The agreed inclusion of the garage is missing, so as it stands the document is void as a contract (D), unless the court later rectifies it to record the agreed terms. A and C overlook the all-terms rule, B fails because the garage was part of the same land deal, and E is wrong because one document signed by both parties is enough."},{"t":"formalities","p":"FLK2","q":"For 25 years Declan worked long hours on his aunt Moira's dairy farm for far less than a farm worker's usual wage. Moira repeatedly told him, in front of neighbours, that 'all this will be yours when I'm gone', and relying on this Declan turned down a well-paid job elsewhere. Nothing was ever put in writing. Moira later fell out with Declan, and when she died last month her will left the farm to an animal charity.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Declan's position?","o":["He has no claim, because a promise to leave land by will is unenforceable unless made in signed writing.","He has no claim, because Moira was free to change her will at any time up to her death.","He can claim only a reasonable wage for his work, because the court cannot order a transfer of land.","He is entitled to the whole farm automatically, because the court must always fulfil the promise in full.","He is likely to establish proprietary estoppel, and the court will decide what remedy is needed to do justice."],"a":4,"e":"Declan has a clear **assurance**, **reliance** and **detriment** (years of low pay and a job given up), which make it **unconscionable** for Moira's estate to go back on the promise, so **proprietary estoppel** is likely to succeed (E). The starting point is to fulfil the expectation, but the court has a discretion over the remedy and may award the land, part of it or money (Guest v Guest), so D overstates it. Lack of writing (A) does not defeat an estoppel, the revocability of a will (B) is no answer once the claimant has relied on the assurance to his detriment (Thorner v Major), and C is wrong because the court can order a transfer of land."},{"t":"registered-land","p":"FLK2","q":"Gloria is the registered proprietor of a terraced house. Last year she granted Ade a six-year lease of the house by deed, at a market rent. Ade works abroad and has not yet moved in, so the house was empty when Gloria sold the freehold to Marcus for full value. Nothing about Ade's lease appears on the register, and Marcus, who has now been registered as proprietor, knew nothing about it.\n\nIs Marcus bound by Ade's lease?","o":["No, because Ade was not in actual occupation of the house when Marcus bought it.","Yes, because a legal lease granted for seven years or less overrides a registered disposition.","No, because a lease for more than three years must be protected by a notice on the register.","Yes, because Marcus was a purchaser who ought to have inspected the house before completion.","No, because a lease granted by deed becomes legal only once it is registered at HM Land Registry."],"a":1,"e":"A legal lease for **seven years or less** cannot be registered with its own title, and it **overrides** a registered disposition under **Land Registration Act 2002 Sch 3 para 1**, whether or not the tenant is in occupation and whatever the buyer knew. Ade's six-year lease was granted by deed, so it is legal and binds Marcus (B). A is wrong because actual occupation matters only for para 2, the registration threshold for leases is more than seven years, not three (C, E), and D relies on constructive notice, which plays no part in registered land."},{"t":"registered-land","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2012 Sian fenced off a strip of overgrown land belonging to her neighbour Tariq, the registered proprietor, and began using it as part of her garden without his permission. She knew the strip was Tariq's. In 2023 she applied to be registered as proprietor of it. Tariq served a counter-notice objecting, none of the statutory exceptions applied, and her application was rejected. Tariq has since taken no steps to recover the strip, and Sian has stayed in possession throughout. It is now December 2026.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Sian's position now?","o":["She can never acquire the strip, because Tariq objected in time and her application failed.","She must wait until she has completed 12 years of possession from the date of the rejection.","She can apply again, and she is entitled to be registered as proprietor of the strip.","She can apply again, but Tariq can defeat the application by serving a further counter-notice.","She can be registered only if she proves she reasonably believed the strip belonged to her."],"a":2,"e":"Under **Land Registration Act 2002 Sch 6**, a squatter with 10 years' adverse possession may apply to be registered; if the owner objects and none of the three conditions applies, the application is rejected. But if the squatter stays in adverse possession for a further **two years** after the rejection and the owner has not started possession proceedings, the squatter can **re-apply and is entitled to be registered** (C). The owner cannot block the second application with another counter-notice (D), so A is wrong; B applies the 12-year rule for unregistered land, and the boundary condition in E is relevant only to the first application."},{"t":"unregistered-land","p":"FLK2","q":"Priya is buying an unregistered freehold field in 2026. A 1921 conveyance in the title deeds, which her solicitor has read, contains a covenant by the then buyer not to build on the field, made for the benefit of adjoining land that is still owned by the covenantee's successor. No land charge has ever been registered in respect of the covenant. The seller tells Priya that the covenant is 'ancient and unenforceable'.\n\nIf Priya completes the purchase, will she be bound by the covenant?","o":["No, because every restrictive covenant must be registered as a land charge to bind a buyer.","No, because a covenant made more than a century ago can no longer be enforced by anyone.","No, because Priya will be a purchaser of the legal estate who has paid full value for it.","Yes, because legal interests bind every buyer of unregistered land, whatever the buyer knows.","Yes, because a pre-1926 covenant depends on the doctrine of notice, and Priya has notice of it."],"a":4,"e":"Only restrictive covenants made **after 1925** are registrable as **Class D(ii) land charges**; a **pre-1926** covenant binds a buyer unless it is a **bona fide purchaser of a legal estate for value without notice**. Priya's solicitor has read the 1921 conveyance, so she has notice (imputed through her agent) and will be bound (E). A applies the land charges rule to the wrong period, C ignores that the purchaser defence also requires the absence of notice, D is wrong because a restrictive covenant is an equitable interest, not a legal one, and age alone does not end a covenant (B)."},{"t":"unregistered-land","p":"FLK2","q":"Gordon is the sole owner of an unregistered house. His partner Leah paid a third of the purchase price when they bought it in 2010 and has lived there with him ever since. Gordon agrees to sell the house to Yusuf, telling him that he lives alone. Yusuf's surveyor inspects the house and reports a second wardrobe full of women's clothing and post addressed to Leah on the doormat, but Yusuf makes no further enquiries. Gordon conveys the house to Yusuf for full value and receives the whole price himself.\n\nIs Yusuf bound by Leah's interest?","o":["Yes, because Yusuf is fixed with notice of what his surveyor found and failed to follow up.","No, because Leah's interest had to be registered as a land charge in order to bind a buyer.","No, because Yusuf had no actual knowledge that anyone else had a share in the house.","No, because Leah's interest was overreached when Yusuf paid the price to the legal owner.","Yes, because every beneficial interest under a trust binds a buyer of unregistered land."],"a":0,"e":"A beneficial interest under a trust is not a land charge (B), and it is **overreached** only if the money is paid to **at least two trustees**, so payment to Gordon alone did not overreach it (D). It therefore depends on the **doctrine of notice**: a buyer has **constructive notice** of what reasonable inspection and inquiry would reveal, and **imputed notice** of what its agent learns, so the surveyor's findings bind Yusuf (A); Leah's actual occupation also protects her on the first registration that follows. C ignores constructive and imputed notice, and E overstates the rule because a buyer without notice, or one who pays two trustees, takes free."},{"t":"co-ownership","p":"FLK2","q":"Three friends, Kwame, Lorna and Ibrahim, bought a house together as their home. The transfer was registered in all three names and stated that they held the property 'in equal shares'. Kwame has now died. His will leaves everything to his sister Esi.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the ownership of the house now?","o":["Lorna and Ibrahim hold the legal title and the whole beneficial interest as joint tenants.","Lorna, Ibrahim and Esi hold the legal title on trust for themselves in equal shares.","Lorna and Ibrahim hold the legal title, and each of them owns half of the beneficial interest.","Lorna and Ibrahim hold the legal title on trust for themselves and Esi in equal shares.","The house must now be sold, and the proceeds divided equally between Lorna, Ibrahim and Esi."],"a":3,"e":"The legal title can only be held as a **joint tenancy**, so on Kwame's death it passes by survivorship to Lorna and Ibrahim. In equity, 'in equal shares' are **words of severance** creating a **tenancy in common**, so Kwame's one-third share does not pass by survivorship but goes under his will to Esi (D). A and C wrongly apply survivorship to the beneficial interest, B is wrong because Esi takes only an equitable share and does not become a legal owner, and nothing requires a sale (E)."},{"t":"co-ownership","p":"FLK2","q":"Bronwen and Gethin own their home as beneficial tenants in common in equal shares. Their children grew up and left home several years ago. Gethin, who is not bankrupt, owes a business creditor, Maple Finance, £140,000 and has no means of paying. Maple has obtained a final charging order over Gethin's beneficial share, and the debt grows each month as interest accrues. Maple now applies to court for an order for sale of the house. Bronwen, who has no means of buying out Gethin's share, opposes a sale.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court is likely to deal with the application?","o":["It will refuse a sale, because only a trustee in bankruptcy can apply to force a sale of a home.","It will apply the TLATA factors, and the creditor's growing unpaid debt makes a sale likely to be ordered.","It will refuse a sale unless Maple shows exceptional circumstances, because the house is a family home.","It must order a sale at once, because a secured creditor's interests always override those of a co-owner.","It will refuse a sale, because Bronwen's wish to stay outweighs Gethin's share by value under the statute."],"a":1,"e":"A person with an interest in the property, including a **secured creditor** with a charging order over a co-owner's share, can apply under **TLATA 1996 s.14**, and the court weighs the **s.15 factors**, one of which is the **interests of any secured creditor**. No factor automatically wins (Mortgage Corporation v Shaire), but where a large and growing debt has no prospect of being repaid and the family purpose has ended, a sale is usually ordered (Bank of Ireland v Bell), so B is best. A and C misapply the bankruptcy regime in s.335A Insolvency Act 1986, which is not engaged because Gethin is not bankrupt; D overstates the creditor's position; and E is wrong because Bronwen holds only half by value, so she is not a majority."},{"t":"easements","p":"FLK2","q":"Benedict owns a block of flats with a 40-space car park. When he sold the freehold of an adjoining house to Chen, the transfer deed granted Chen and his successors, for the benefit of the house, a right to park one private car in any available space in the car park. The grant was completed by registration and noted on both titles. Benedict has now sold the flats to Delphine, who has put up a barrier and refuses to let Chen park.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Chen's right?","o":["It is a licence only, because a right to park a car can never exist as an easement.","It is not an easement, because Chen does not need the right to reach or use his house.","It is a personal right only, because a parking space does not benefit Chen's land.","It is not an easement, because parking excludes the owner from using the car park.","It is a valid legal easement, because Delphine keeps possession and control of the car park."],"a":4,"e":"A right to park can be an easement provided it does not amount to **ouster**, meaning it must not deprive the servient owner of possession or reasonable use of its land. A right to park one car in any available space in a 40-space car park takes up only a small part of the land, so Delphine keeps possession and control; as it was granted by deed and completed by registration, it is legal and binds her (E). D would be right only if the right took up the whole of a small area for most of the time (Batchelor v Marlow); B confuses this with an easement of necessity, and parking can accommodate a house, so A and C are wrong."},{"t":"easements","p":"FLK2","q":"Since 2015 Nisha has rented a flat from Owain under a written monthly tenancy. Owain, who lives in the house next door, allowed Nisha as a favour to walk through his side passage to reach the street from her back door. In 2026 Owain sold Nisha the freehold of the flat by a transfer deed which did not mention the passage and did not exclude any statutory provisions. Owain now says Nisha can no longer use the passage.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Nisha's position?","o":["Nisha has an easement, because the transfer converted Owain's permission into a legal right of way.","Nisha has no easement, because her use of the passage was only ever with Owain's permission.","Nisha has no easement, because rights implied on a sale are limited to necessity and common intention.","Nisha has an easement only if she has used the passage for at least 20 years without permission.","Nisha has no easement, because a right not mentioned in the transfer deed cannot pass with the land."],"a":0,"e":"**LPA 1925 s.62** implies general words into a conveyance by deed, passing all privileges enjoyed with the land, and can **upgrade a mere permission into a legal easement** where, before the conveyance, the two parts were occupied by different people (**prior diversity of occupation**), as with Owain as landlord and Nisha as tenant (A). B is the very point s.62 overcomes, and E ignores implied grant. C states the rule for an easement implied in the seller's favour, not a grant to the buyer, and D describes prescription, which is unnecessary here."},{"t":"covenants","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2010 Ingrid sold part of her garden to Barnaby, who covenanted for himself and his successors 'not to allow the boundary hedge to become overgrown or to exceed two metres in height'. The covenant was protected by a notice on the register of Barnaby's plot. In 2024 Barnaby sold the plot to Celeste, who has let the hedge grow to four metres. Ingrid still owns the rest of her garden.\n\nCan Ingrid enforce the covenant against Celeste?","o":["Yes, because the covenant is worded negatively and has been protected by a notice.","Yes, because a covenant made for the covenantor's successors runs with the land.","No, because the covenant is positive in substance, so its burden does not pass to Celeste.","No, because a covenant can only ever be enforced against the person who gave it.","Yes, but the court can award only damages in lieu of an injunction against her."],"a":2,"e":"Whether a covenant is restrictive or positive depends on **substance, not wording**: if compliance requires the covenantor to take action or spend money, it is **positive**. Keeping the hedge trimmed requires work, so despite its negative wording the covenant is positive and its **burden cannot run** to a successor at law or in equity (C). A is the wording trap, since a notice cannot make a positive covenant run, and s.79 only presumes an intention for the burden to run (B); D is wrong because restrictive covenants can bind successors. Ingrid's remedy is a contract claim against Barnaby, who as original covenantor remains liable, including (by s.79) for breaches by his successors."},{"t":"covenants","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2005 Westbridge Developments Ltd, which owned no other land in the area, sold a plot to Harvey. In the transfer Harvey covenanted, for himself and his successors, not to use the plot for any purpose other than as a single private dwelling. The covenant was entered by notice on the register of the plot. In 2025 Harvey sold the plot to Juniper, who now plans to open a children's nursery there. Westbridge objects.\n\nCan Westbridge enforce the covenant against Juniper?","o":["Yes, because the covenant is restrictive and was protected by a notice on the register.","Yes, because Westbridge is the original covenantee and can always enforce against later owners.","No, because a covenant restricting business use is positive in substance and cannot bind successors.","No, because Westbridge owned no land capable of benefiting from the covenant when it was made.","No, because the covenant was made over 20 years ago and has therefore become obsolete."],"a":3,"e":"For the burden of a restrictive covenant to run in equity, it must **benefit land owned by the covenantee when the covenant was made**; a covenantee with no benefited land cannot enforce against a successor (London County Council v Allen). Westbridge kept no land, so the burden never passed to Juniper (D). A and B overlook this requirement, C is wrong because Harvey can comply by doing nothing, so the covenant is restrictive, and age alone does not end a covenant: discharge needs an application to the Upper Tribunal (E)."},{"t":"mortgages","p":"FLK2","q":"Thornbury Bank holds a legal charge over a disused warehouse owned by Mosaic Ltd, which is in serious arrears. The bank's power of sale has arisen and become exercisable. Two months earlier the council granted planning permission to convert the warehouse into 30 flats. The bank instructs auctioneers, who advertise it simply as 'a vacant warehouse' without mentioning the planning permission, and it sells to an unconnected buyer for £600,000. Valuers agree it would have fetched £1.1 million if the permission had been advertised.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Mosaic's position?","o":["It has no claim, because a lender may choose when to sell and owes no duty about the price it obtains.","It can claim the shortfall, because the bank failed to take reasonable care to obtain the true market value.","It can have the sale set aside as of right, because the buyer paid far less than the market value.","It has no claim, because a sale by public auction shows that the price was the best one available.","It can claim the shortfall, but only if it proves that the bank acted dishonestly in arranging the sale."],"a":1,"e":"A selling lender may choose **when** to sell, but it must act in good faith and take **reasonable care to obtain the true market value** at the time of sale (Cuckmere Brick v Mutual Finance). Failing to advertise valuable planning permission breaches that duty, so the bank is liable to Mosaic for the difference (B). A confuses the freedom over timing with a duty about price, an auction does not excuse a badly advertised sale (D), and E describes only the separate duty of good faith; a sale to an independent buyer in good faith will not normally be set aside, so C is wrong."},{"t":"mortgages","p":"FLK2","q":"Cerys has a 25-year repayment mortgage of her home with Larch Bank, of which 15 years remain. After losing her job she fell £9,000 into arrears, and the mortgage contains a clause making the whole loan payable on any default. She has now started a permanent new job, and her budget shows she can pay the normal monthly instalments plus about £50 a month towards the arrears. Larch Bank brings a possession claim, arguing that the whole loan of £180,000 is now due.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the court's powers?","o":["It must make a possession order, because the clause has made the whole £180,000 loan immediately due.","It can adjourn only for a short period, to give Cerys time to find a buyer for the house.","It must make a possession order, because the arrears cannot be cleared within two years.","It can postpone possession only if Cerys pays off all of the arrears within a few months.","It can suspend possession, taking the remaining 15-year term as the starting point for repayment."],"a":4,"e":"Under **s.36 Administration of Justice Act 1970**, the court may adjourn, suspend or postpone possession of a dwelling if the borrower is likely to pay the **sums due within a reasonable period**, and under **s.8 AJA 1973** the sums due are only the **arrears**, not the whole loan accelerated by the default clause (so A is wrong). The **starting point** for a reasonable period is the **remaining term** of the mortgage (Cheltenham & Gloucester BS v Norgan), and £50 a month clears £9,000 in 15 years (E). C and D impose short periods with no basis in the rule, and B describes the different case of a borrower relying on a planned sale."},{"t":"leases-nature","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2018 Harbour District Council let a strip of land beside its depot to Kestrel Coaches Ltd 'until the council requires the land for an extension of the depot', at a market rent of £3,000 a year payable yearly. No deed was used. Kestrel took possession straight away and has paid the rent each year since. The council's solicitor is now reviewing the arrangement.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the legal effect of the arrangement?","o":["It is a yearly periodic tenancy, because the stated term is uncertain and yearly rent has been paid.","It is a valid lease that continues until the council actually requires the land for the extension.","It is a licence only, because a lease must always be made by deed to create an estate in land.","It is an equitable lease, because the council and Kestrel did not use a deed for the arrangement.","It is void and of no effect, so Kestrel occupies the land as a trespasser and owes no rent."],"a":0,"e":"A lease must have a **maximum duration that is certain at the start**, so a term 'until the council requires the land' cannot take effect as a fixed term (Prudential Assurance v London Residuary Body). However, because Kestrel took possession and pays a **yearly rent**, a **yearly periodic tenancy** is implied, and each period is certain (A). B ignores the certainty rule and E ignores the implied periodic tenancy; C and D are wrong because a periodic tenancy at a market rent taking effect in possession is a legal lease without any deed (s.54(2) LPA 1925)."},{"t":"leases-nature","p":"FLK2","q":"Amelia agreed orally with Bruno to rent his lock-up garage, where she keeps her vintage car as a hobby, for four years at the full market rent of £150 a month, with no premium. No document was signed. Amelia took possession the same day and has paid £150 on the first of each month for the past year. Bruno now wants to end the arrangement and argues that Amelia never had a valid four-year lease.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Amelia's position?","o":["She holds a legal four-year lease, because she took possession and pays the full market rent.","She holds an equitable four-year lease, because equity treats an agreement for a lease as a lease.","She is only a licensee, because a lease of more than three years has no effect unless made by deed.","She holds a monthly periodic tenancy, implied from her possession and her monthly payments of rent.","She holds a yearly periodic tenancy, because she has now occupied the garage for a full year."],"a":3,"e":"A lease for more than three years must be made by **deed** to be legal, because the **s.54(2) LPA 1925** exception covers only terms of three years or less, so A is wrong. Nor is there an equitable lease, because a contract to grant one must be in signed writing under s.2 LP(MP)A 1989 (B). However, by taking possession and paying rent calculated by the month, Amelia is likely to hold an **implied legal periodic tenancy** measured by the rent period, which is **monthly** (D). C overlooks the implied tenancy, and E measures the period by the length of occupation rather than by how the rent is calculated."},{"t":"leases-covenants","p":"FLK2","q":"In 1992 Pembroke Estates granted Quill Ltd a 40-year lease of offices. In 2006 Quill assigned the lease to Rowan Ltd, which did not enter into any covenant with Pembroke. In 2016 Rowan assigned the lease to Saffron Ltd. Saffron has failed to pay the rent due on 29 September 2026. On 10 October 2026 Pembroke serves notices in the prescribed form on both Quill and Rowan claiming the arrears.\n\nWho is liable to Pembroke for the arrears?","o":["Saffron only, because a tenant is released from its covenants when it assigns the lease.","Saffron and Rowan, because both have held the lease in privity of estate with Pembroke.","Saffron and Quill, because Quill remains liable on its original covenant in privity of contract.","Saffron, Rowan and Quill, because every tenant in the chain is jointly liable for the rent.","Quill only, because Pembroke's contract was made with Quill and not with any assignee."],"a":2,"e":"The lease was granted **before 1 January 1996**, so the release on assignment under the Landlord and Tenant (Covenants) Act 1995 does not apply (A). The **original tenant** remains liable for the whole term through **privity of contract**, subject to a s.17 notice within six months of the sum falling due, which Pembroke has served, and the current assignee is liable through **privity of estate** (C). Rowan's privity of estate ended when it assigned to Saffron and it gave no direct covenant, so it is not liable (B, D), and E ignores the current tenant's liability."},{"t":"leases-covenants","p":"FLK2","q":"Tobias rents a two-bedroom flat from Ursula under a written tenancy which began in 2025. A clause in the agreement states that 'the tenant shall be responsible for all repairs to the flat and its installations'. In November the gas boiler, which provides the flat's heating and hot water, breaks down through ordinary wear, and Tobias tells Ursula straight away. Ursula refuses to repair it, relying on the clause.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Tobias must repair the boiler himself, because the parties freely agreed that he would do all repairs.","Ursula must repair the boiler, because her statutory repairing duty cannot be excluded by agreement.","Ursula need not repair the boiler, because the landlord's statutory duty covers only the structure and exterior.","Ursula must repair the boiler only if it broke down because of her own negligence or other fault.","Tobias must repair the boiler, because small jobs are part of using the flat in a tenant-like manner."],"a":1,"e":"Where a dwelling is let for **less than seven years**, **s.11 Landlord and Tenant Act 1985** obliges the landlord to keep in repair and proper working order the installations for **space heating and hot water**, as well as the structure and exterior, and a term putting that burden on the tenant is void (B). Ursula has notice of the breakdown, so her duty is engaged. C omits the installations limb, repairing a boiler goes far beyond the small jobs expected of a tenant-like tenant (E), and the duty does not depend on fault (D)."},{"t":"leases-remedies","p":"FLK2","q":"Vale Properties lets a shop to Willow Ltd under a lease containing an absolute covenant against subletting and a right of re-entry. In March 2026 Willow sublets the upper floor to Xander without asking Vale. Vale's managing agent inspects in April, learns of the subletting and reports it to Vale. In June Vale's accounts team sends Willow its usual demand for the quarter's rent due on 24 June, marked 'without prejudice'. In August Vale issues forfeiture proceedings for the subletting.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Vale's position?","o":["It can forfeit, because a rent demand marked 'without prejudice' does not waive the right to forfeit.","It can forfeit, because unlawful subletting is a continuing breach that recurs every day.","It can forfeit, but only after serving a s.146 notice and allowing time to remedy the breach.","It cannot forfeit for the subletting until it has first claimed damages for the breach.","It cannot forfeit for the subletting, because it waived that right by demanding rent knowing of the breach."],"a":4,"e":"A landlord **waives** the right to forfeit if, **knowing of the breach**, it does an unequivocal act treating the lease as continuing, such as **demanding rent** that fell due after the breach; its managing agent's knowledge counts as Vale's, and marking the demand 'without prejudice' makes no difference (E, so A is wrong). Unlawful subletting is a **once-and-for-all breach**, so the waiver is permanent (B), although Vale can still claim damages. C describes procedure that would matter only if the right had not been waived, and D invents a precondition."},{"t":"leases-remedies","p":"FLK2","q":"Yarrow Ltd holds a ten-year lease of a warehouse which allows it to end the lease on 30 June 2026 by giving six months' written notice, 'provided that on the break date the tenant has paid all rent due under this lease'. Yarrow gave valid notice in December 2025 and moved out before the break date. By then it had paid all the basic rent, but it had overlooked £85 of interest on a late payment in 2024, which the lease defines as rent. The landlord says the lease continues.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of Yarrow's notice?","o":["It did not end the lease, because the conditions of a break clause must be strictly complied with.","It ended the lease, because the unpaid £85 is too small to matter under the de minimis principle.","It ended the lease, and the landlord's only remedy is to recover the £85 from Yarrow as a debt.","It did not end the lease, because Yarrow also had to pay the rent for the quarter after the break date.","It ended the lease, because the condition refers to rent and interest is not part of the basic rent."],"a":0,"e":"Conditions attached to a **break clause** must be **strictly complied with**, and even a small shortfall in the sums the condition requires defeats the break, so the lease continues (A). There is no de minimis exception (B, C), and E ignores the lease's own definition of rent as including interest. D is wrong because the condition requires payment only of rent due by the break date, not rent for a later period."}],"land-p3":[{"t":"nature-of-land","p":"FLK2","q":"Morwenna is the registered proprietor of a hill farm in Cornwall. The register of her title contains no exception or reservation of mines and minerals. A geological survey she commissioned shows three things beneath her fields: a large deposit of sand and gravel suitable for construction, a bed of good-quality building stone, and a narrow vein of gold about 50 metres down. She would like to quarry and sell all three.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who owns these materials?","o":["She owns the sand and gravel, the stone and the gold, because land includes everything beneath the surface.","She owns the sand and gravel and the stone, but the gold belongs to the Crown rather than to her.","She owns none of them, because her rights below ground extend only as far as ordinary use requires.","She owns the gold and the sand and gravel, but the building stone belongs to the Crown.","She owns only the sand and gravel, because the stone and the gold are both treated as minerals."],"a":1,"e":"**Land includes the soil beneath the surface and the mines and minerals in it (LPA 1925 s.205(1)(ix)), but gold and silver belong to the Crown**, not the landowner (as do coal, oil and gas, which are vested in the state). So option B is right: sand, gravel and building stone are part of Morwenna's land. Option A ignores the Crown's rights over gold, and option C wrongly applies the airspace limit (rights only so far as reasonably necessary for ordinary use) to ordinary minerals in her own soil."},{"t":"nature-of-land","p":"FLK2","q":"Graham died in 2025. His will, which has been proved, leaves his registered freehold house to his executors 'to hold for my wife Ruth for the rest of her life, and after her death for my son Elliot absolutely'. Ruth has continued to live in the house since Graham's death, the administration of the estate is complete, and the executors have not yet transferred the house to anyone.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ruth's interest in the house?","o":["A legal life estate, because she is entitled to occupy the house immediately.","A legal fee simple, because she is in possession of the house as its owner.","An equitable life interest, with the legal estate held on trust for her and Elliot.","A licence to occupy, because Elliot is already the outright owner of the house.","A legal lease for her life, which takes effect as a lease for 90 years."],"a":2,"e":"**Only two estates can be legal: the fee simple absolute in possession and the term of years absolute (LPA 1925 s.1(1)); a life interest can exist only in equity behind a trust.** So the executors (and later trustees) hold the legal estate on a trust of land for Ruth for life, then Elliot, whose remainder is also equitable: option C. Option A is wrong because a life estate cannot be legal, and option E is wrong because the conversion of leases for life into 90-year terms applies only to leases granted at a rent or for a premium, not to a gift by will."},{"t":"formalities","p":"FLK2","q":"In September 2026 Saoirse and Hamza exchanged contracts, each in identical form and signed, under which Hamza agreed to buy Saoirse's house for £520,000, with completion fixed for 30 October. After exchange, a damp specialist reported serious rising damp. Hamza emailed Saoirse: 'Will you accept £505,000 because of the damp?' She replied by email: 'Yes, agreed. Saoirse.' A week before completion Saoirse tells Hamza she now insists on the full contract price.\n\nCan Hamza insist on completing at £505,000?","o":["No, because a variation of a material term must itself satisfy the statutory formalities, and this one does not.","Yes, because Saoirse's typed name at the end of her email counts as her signature on the variation.","Yes, because the original contract was valid and the emails changed only one of its terms.","No, because the terms of a contract for the sale of land can never be varied after exchange.","Yes, because Hamza can rely on proprietary estoppel to hold Saoirse to the reduced price."],"a":0,"e":"**A variation of a material term (such as the price) of a land contract must itself comply with LP(MP)A 1989 s.2: in writing, incorporating all the terms, and signed by or for each party**, so option A is right. A one-line exchange of emails does not set out all the terms in a single document signed by both, so even if Saoirse's typed name were a signature (option B), Hamza has not signed and the terms are not incorporated. Option C is wrong because the parties then remain bound by the original price, and option D overstates the law: a variation that complies with s.2 is perfectly valid."},{"t":"formalities","p":"FLK2","q":"Kirsty and Nathan, who are not married, bought a house in 2016 for £300,000. The transfer was into their joint names, as registered proprietors, but contained no declaration of how they held the beneficial interest. Kirsty paid the whole £60,000 deposit from an inheritance. The balance came from a joint mortgage, repaid from a joint account into which both paid their salaries and from which all household bills were paid. They have now separated and cannot agree their shares.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how they are likely to hold the beneficial interest?","o":["Kirsty is entitled to the larger share, because a resulting trust arises in proportion to the money each provided.","Kirsty is entitled to the larger share, because the house was bought as an investment between unmarried partners.","Kirsty owns the whole beneficial interest, because Nathan contributed nothing to the deposit.","They hold in equal shares as tenants in common, because unmarried couples are always treated in that way.","They hold in equal shares, because joint legal owners are presumed to own the beneficial interest equally."],"a":4,"e":"**Where a home is bought in joint names for a couple with no express declaration, equity follows the law: they are presumed to be beneficial joint tenants in equal shares, and a party seeking a different share must show a different common intention from the whole course of dealing** (Stack v Dowden as a memory hook), so option E is right. Here they pooled their finances and shared the mortgage and bills, so Kirsty's larger deposit is unlikely to displace equality. Option A applies the resulting trust approach, which is not used for the family home held in joint names, and option D is wrong because there is no rule that couples are always tenants in common."},{"t":"registered-land","p":"FLK2","q":"Dev and Kiran, two brothers, are the registered proprietors of a house. In 2015 their mother Usha contributed £80,000 towards its purchase on the understanding that she would have a share, and she has lived there ever since. No entry relating to her appears on the register. In 2026 the brothers sell the house to Leon for £450,000, which Leon pays to both brothers on completion. Leon did not inspect the house before completion.\n\nIs Leon bound by Usha's interest in the house?","o":["Yes, because Usha was in actual occupation of the house when Leon completed his purchase.","Yes, because Leon failed to inspect the house and so cannot claim to be a buyer without notice.","No, because Usha's interest was not protected by a notice entered on the register of title.","No, because Usha's interest was overreached and now takes effect in the money from the sale.","No, because a parent's contribution to a child's home is presumed to be a gift to the child."],"a":3,"e":"**Overreaching: where a buyer pays the purchase money to at least two trustees (LPA 1925 ss.2 and 27), the beneficial interests under the trust are detached from the land and attach to the proceeds** (City of London BS v Flegg as a memory hook), so option D is right. Once overreached, Usha has nothing left in the land that could override, so her actual occupation (option A) does not help. Option C is wrong because an interest under a trust of land cannot be protected by a notice at all (it would be a restriction), and the doctrine of notice in option B has no role in registered land."},{"t":"registered-land","p":"FLK2","q":"In January 2026 Desmond gave his unregistered freehold cottage to his daughter Freya by a deed of gift that was properly executed and delivered. Freya was unaware that she needed to do anything further, and no application to HM Land Registry has been made. In November 2026 Freya consults a solicitor because she wants to sell the cottage.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the current position of the legal estate?","o":["The deed of gift is void in every respect, and Desmond once again owns the cottage outright.","Freya holds the legal estate, because a gift does not trigger compulsory first registration.","Desmond holds the legal estate on a bare trust for Freya, who must arrange a fresh transfer.","Freya holds the legal estate but cannot sell it until she applies to register her title.","Freya holds only an estate contract, which she must protect by registering a land charge."],"a":2,"e":"**A transfer of an unregistered freehold, including a gift, triggers compulsory first registration (LRA 2002 s.4), and the application must be made within two months (s.6); otherwise the transfer becomes void as regards the legal estate, which reverts to the transferor on a bare trust for the transferee (s.7).** So option C is right: Freya owns the beneficial interest, and a new transfer (at her cost) followed by a prompt application is needed. Option B wrongly limits the triggers to sales, and option A is wrong because Freya is still entitled in equity under the bare trust (a trust under which the trustee simply holds for the beneficiary)."},{"t":"unregistered-land","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2019 Malcolm, the owner of an unregistered farm, granted his nephew Rory a written option, signed by both, to buy the farm for £600,000 at any time within ten years. Rory never registered a land charge. In 2026, after a family quarrel and with the aim of defeating the option, Malcolm conveys the legal estate in the farm, worth £900,000, to his partner Anita for £20,000. Anita knew all about Rory's option. Rory now tries to exercise it.\n\nCan Rory enforce the option against Anita?","o":["Yes, because Anita had actual knowledge of the option when she bought the farm.","Yes, because Anita paid far less than full value and so is not a purchaser at all.","Yes, because Anita did not buy the farm in good faith but to defeat the option.","No, because his unregistered option is void against her as a purchaser for money.","No, because an option over unregistered land binds only the person who granted it."],"a":3,"e":"**An option is an estate contract (Class C(iv)); if not registered, it is void against a purchaser of a legal estate for money or money's worth (LCA 1972 s.4(6)), and the purchaser's knowledge is irrelevant (LPA 1925 s.199).** So option D is right. The Act requires neither good faith nor adequate value, so a deliberately low price paid to defeat the option still makes Anita a purchaser for money (Midland Bank v Green as a memory hook), ruling out options B and C. Option E is wrong because a registered option would have bound Anita."},{"t":"unregistered-land","p":"FLK2","q":"Rhiannon is buying an unregistered house from Owen, who is married to Bethan; Bethan is not named in the title deeds. Rhiannon's solicitor obtains an official search certificate of the Land Charges Register against the correct full names of all the estate owners, including Owen. It is dated 2 November 2026 and reveals no entries. On 6 November Bethan registers a Class F land charge to protect her home rights against Owen. Rhiannon completes her purchase on 13 November 2026.\n\nIs Rhiannon bound by Bethan's home rights?","o":["No, because she completed within the priority period given by her clear official search.","Yes, because the registration of a land charge is actual notice to everyone for all purposes.","Yes, because Bethan's home rights were registered before Rhiannon's purchase was completed.","No, because a spouse's home rights are personal and can never bind a buyer of the house.","Yes, because Bethan was living in the house and so her rights override the sale."],"a":0,"e":"**An official land charges search protects the buyer against entries made after the search if it completes within 15 working days of the certificate (LCA 1972 s.11(5)).** Completion on 13 November is well within that period, so Rhiannon takes free of the Class F charge registered on 6 November: option A. Options B and C state the general effect of registration (LPA 1925 s.198) but ignore the priority period, option D is wrong because a registered Class F charge does bind a buyer, and option E imports the registered land rule on actual occupation, which does not apply to land charges."},{"t":"co-ownership","p":"FLK2","q":"Aled, Bryony and Cai bought a house together in 2019 and are its registered proprietors, holding the beneficial interest as joint tenants. At a meeting in 2025, Cai told the other two that he wanted 'his third' to be his own from now on; they said nothing in reply, and nothing was put in writing. Later that year Cai made a valid will leaving 'my one-third share of the house' to his nephew Gruffydd. Cai died in 2026.\n\nWho is now entitled to the beneficial interest in the house?","o":["Aled, Bryony and Gruffydd, as tenants in common in equal thirds.","Aled and Bryony, as tenants in common each holding a half share.","Gruffydd as to one third, and Aled and Bryony jointly as to two thirds.","Aled and Bryony jointly, but Gruffydd may claim a third of any proceeds.","Aled and Bryony, as beneficial joint tenants of the whole house."],"a":4,"e":"**A beneficial joint tenancy is severed only by written notice (LPA 1925 s.36(2)), an act operating on one's own share, mutual agreement or a mutual course of dealing; an oral unilateral statement and a gift by will do not sever.** Cai's words were oral and the others did not agree, and a will takes effect only at death, too late to sever, so survivorship gives the whole to Aled and Bryony as joint tenants: option E. Option C is what would have happened had Cai validly severed, and option B wrongly treats his death as severing the survivors' joint tenancy."},{"t":"co-ownership","p":"FLK2","q":"Femi, aged 61, and his wife Grace, aged 57, owned their registered freehold home as beneficial joint tenants. Both died in a house fire in 2026, and the evidence cannot show which of them died first. Femi's will leaves his whole estate to his brother Tunde. Grace's will leaves her whole estate to her sister Abena. Both wills are valid.\n\nWho is entitled to the beneficial interest in the home?","o":["Tunde and Abena equally, because the deaths are treated as having occurred together.","Abena, because Grace is presumed to have survived Femi as the younger of the two.","Tunde, because Femi is presumed to have survived Grace as the elder of the two.","Tunde and Abena equally, because the fire severed the joint tenancy before death.","Their nearest relatives under the intestacy rules, because neither will can apply."],"a":1,"e":"**Where it is uncertain which of two people died first, the younger is presumed to have survived the elder (LPA 1925 s.184).** Grace, the younger, is therefore treated as surviving Femi, so survivorship vests the whole beneficial interest in her, and it passes under her will to Abena: option B. Option C reverses the presumption, and options A and D are wrong because the law does not treat the deaths as simultaneous and a joint death does not sever a joint tenancy."},{"t":"easements","p":"FLK2","q":"Tamsin owned a house and an adjoining paddock. For many years she drove from the garage of the house along a clearly surfaced concrete driveway across the paddock to the main road. The house also has a narrow footpath to a back street, too narrow for cars. In 2025 she sold the house to Wei and kept the paddock. The transfer did not mention the driveway, and it expressly excluded the operation of s.62 of the Law of Property Act 1925. Tamsin has now blocked the driveway.\n\nDoes Wei have a right to use the driveway?","o":["Yes, because a visible right used by the seller for the house before a sale of part passes to the buyer.","No, because the house has another access by the footpath, so no easement of necessity can arise.","No, because before the sale Tamsin owned both the house and the paddock, so no easement existed.","Yes, because the use of the driveway was converted into an easement by the general words in the transfer.","No, because an easement over land can be created only by a deed that expressly grants it."],"a":0,"e":"**On a sale of part, the buyer gets by implied grant any quasi-easement that was continuous and apparent, necessary for the reasonable enjoyment of the land sold and used by the seller at the time of the sale (the rule in Wheeldon v Burrows).** A surfaced driveway in use is visible and needed for reasonable vehicle access, so option A is right. Option B confuses this with necessity, which requires that the land be landlocked, and option D is wrong because s.62 (the statutory general words) was expressly excluded; option C describes the position before the sale, which is exactly why the right is called a quasi-easement."},{"t":"easements","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2012 a deed granted the owner of Ivy Cottage and his successors in title a right of way on foot and with vehicles over a track across Larkfield Farm 'to and from Ivy Cottage'. The grant was completed by registration. In 2025 Joseph, the current owner of Ivy Cottage, bought a field adjoining the cottage, which the track does not reach. He has planning permission for a holiday let in the field and intends that guests will drive along the track and then through his cottage garden to reach it. The farm's owner objects.\n\nCan the farm's owner object to this use of the track?","o":["It cannot, because an express right of way may be used for any purpose whatever.","It cannot, because Joseph now owns both Ivy Cottage and the adjoining field.","It cannot, because the right of way is a legal easement which binds the farm.","It can, because the right cannot be used to give access to land beyond the cottage.","It can, but only if the extra traffic substantially interferes with the farm."],"a":3,"e":"**An easement can be used only for the benefit of the dominant tenement (the land it serves); using it as a means of access to additional land is excessive use, even if that land is reached through the dominant land** (Harris v Flower as a memory hook). The way was granted for Ivy Cottage only, so option D is right. Options A and C are wrong because, although the legal easement binds the farm, it binds only within its scope, and option E applies a test of substantial interference that is not the issue here."},{"t":"covenants","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2001 Oakfield Homes laid out an estate of 40 plots on a plan and sold each plot subject to identical covenants not to build more than one dwelling on the plot. Each transfer stated that the covenants were for the benefit of every plot on the estate shown on the plan, and each buyer was told this. Priti bought plot 3 in 2001 and still owns it. Plot 28 was sold to Lewis in 2004 and bought from him by Zara in 2024; the covenants are noted on the register of plot 28. Zara now plans a second house on plot 28.\n\nCan Priti enforce the covenant against Zara?","o":["No, because Priti's plot was sold before the covenant on plot 28 was given.","No, because Priti was not an original party to the covenant given by Lewis.","Yes, because a building scheme allows each plot owner to enforce against every other.","Yes, but she can sue only Lewis, because the burden cannot pass to Zara.","No, because the benefit of the covenant was never expressly assigned to Priti."],"a":2,"e":"**Under a building scheme (a common seller lays out an estate in plots subject to restrictions intended to benefit every plot, and buyers know this), every plot owner and successor can enforce the covenants against every other, whatever the order of purchase.** So option C is right. Option A states the normal problem with annexation (benefit cannot be annexed to land sold before the covenant was made), which a scheme overcomes, and option D is wrong because the burden of a restrictive covenant protected by a notice passes in equity to Zara."},{"t":"covenants","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2010 Holly sold part of her registered garden to Imran, who covenanted for himself and his successors not to build anything in front of the existing building line, for the benefit of Holly's retained house. The benefit was annexed to the retained house, but no notice of the covenant was ever entered on the register of Imran's plot. In 2020 Imran transferred the plot to his daughter Jasmin as a gift. Holly still owns the house, and Jasmin now plans to build a garage in front of the building line.\n\nCan Holly enforce the covenant against Jasmin?","o":["No, because the covenant was never protected by a notice on the register of the plot.","No, because the burden of a covenant cannot pass to a successor at common law.","No, because only Imran, as the original covenantor, can be liable on the covenant.","Yes, but only if Jasmin actually knew about the covenant when she received the plot.","Yes, because Jasmin received the plot as a gift and so is not protected against it."],"a":4,"e":"**Only a registrable disposition made for valuable consideration takes free of an unprotected interest (LRA 2002 s.29); a disponee who gives nothing takes subject to existing interests under the basic priority rule (s.28).** The restrictive covenant's burden runs in equity (Tulk v Moxhay as a memory hook), and because Jasmin received a gift she is bound despite the lack of a notice: option E. Option A would be right for a buyer for value, and option D is wrong because knowledge is irrelevant in registered land."},{"t":"mortgages","p":"FLK2","q":"Quarry Ltd borrowed £2 million from Redwood Capital Ltd, secured by a legal charge over its freehold depot. Both companies took legal advice. A clause in the charge, agreed and signed at the same time as the rest of it, gives Redwood an option to buy the depot for £3 million, its current market value, at any time within ten years. Quarry now has the money to repay the loan early and redeem the charge, as the charge permits, but Redwood says it intends to exercise the option.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the option?","o":["It is valid, because both companies were commercial parties acting with legal advice.","It is void, because an option for the lender in the mortgage clogs the right to redeem.","It is valid, because the option price reflects the full market value of the depot.","It is valid, but it ceases to have effect once the loan has been repaid in full.","It is valid, unless the option price is shown to be oppressive and unconscionable."],"a":1,"e":"**An option for the lender to buy the mortgaged property, granted as part of the mortgage transaction, is void as a clog on the equity of redemption (the borrower's right to get the property back on repayment)**, so option B is right. The rule applies even between commercial parties and at a fair price (options A and C), because the option could stop the borrower ever recovering its property. An option granted later in a separate, independent transaction would be valid, and the unconscionability test in option E applies to collateral advantages and interest rates, not to this type of clause."},{"t":"mortgages","p":"FLK2","q":"Ellis is the registered proprietor of a house. Northgate Bank lent him money secured by a legal charge: it obtained an official search with priority on 2 March 2026, completed the charge on 9 March, but through an oversight did not apply to register the charge until 1 June 2026. Meanwhile Southmead Finance lent Ellis money secured by a second legal charge, completed on 20 March and registered on 27 March 2026. Neither lender knew of the other's charge.\n\nWhich charge has priority?","o":["Southmead's, because charges rank in the order they are entered on the register.","Northgate's, because its charge was created before Southmead's charge was created.","Northgate's, because its official search protects it until it applies to register.","Neither, because the charges rank equally as neither lender knew of the other.","Southmead's, because a later charge always ranks ahead of an earlier charge."],"a":0,"e":"**Registered charges rank according to the order in which they are entered on the register, not the order of creation (LRA 2002 s.48).** Northgate's official search gave it a priority period of 30 working days from 2 March, which had expired long before it applied on 1 June, so Southmead, registered first, has priority: option A. Option C overstates the search, which protects only for the priority period, and option B states the wrong test."},{"t":"leases-nature","p":"FLK2","q":"Lakeside Academy employs Ade as its live-in site manager. His employment contract requires him to live in a bungalow in the school grounds so that he can unlock the gates at 6 am, respond to alarms during the night and deal with emergencies at weekends. He has exclusive possession of the bungalow, and £200 a month is deducted from his salary for it. The academy has dismissed Ade and wants the bungalow back.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ade's right to occupy the bungalow?","o":["He is a tenant, because he has exclusive possession for a term in return for rent.","He is a tenant, because the deduction from his salary is rent paid for the bungalow.","He is a tenant at will, because no fixed period of occupation was ever agreed.","He is a licensee, because he was required to live there to perform his job better.","He is a licensee, because an academy has no power to grant leases of school land."],"a":3,"e":"**Exclusive possession for a term at a rent normally creates a lease, but a service occupier (an employee required to live in the property for the better performance of their duties) is a licensee.** Ade's contract requires him to live on site to respond to alarms and emergencies, so option D is right. Options A and B apply the usual test without the service occupancy exception, and option E wrongly suggests a general lack of power to grant leases."},{"t":"leases-nature","p":"FLK2","q":"On 1 August 2026 Marguerite let a flat she owns in Leeds to Tolu under a written agreement described as an 'assured shorthold tenancy for a fixed term of 12 months' at a market rent. Marguerite does not live in the building, and this is an ordinary private residential letting in England. In November 2026 Marguerite tells Tolu she wants the flat back, although Tolu has not broken any term of the agreement.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Tolu's tenancy?","o":["A 12-month fixed-term tenancy, which neither party can end before 31 July 2027.","An assured shorthold tenancy, which Marguerite can end by a no-fault possession notice.","A licence, because the agreement is for less than three years and was not made by deed.","A tenancy at will, which either party can end at any time without giving notice.","An assured periodic tenancy, which Marguerite can end only on a statutory ground."],"a":4,"e":"**Since 1 May 2026 the Renters' Rights Act 2025 has made most private residential lettings in England assured periodic tenancies: fixed terms can no longer be created, section 21 'no fault' evictions are abolished, and the landlord can recover possession only on a statutory ground through the court.** So despite its label, Tolu's agreement is an assured periodic tenancy (option E), which Tolu can end by two months' notice. Option B describes the law before May 2026, option A wrongly gives effect to the fixed term, and option C is wrong because a lease of up to three years needs no deed."},{"t":"leases-covenants","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2019 Juniper Estates Ltd granted Kale Ltd a 20-year lease of offices, in which Juniper covenanted to keep the roof and structure in repair. In March 2026 Juniper sold the freehold reversion to Larch Investments Ltd. Juniper did not serve any notice on Kale about the sale or seek a release from its covenants. In September 2026 the roof began to leak badly and Larch refuses to repair it.\n\nCan Kale sue Juniper for breach of the repairing covenant?","o":["No, because the benefit and burden of the landlord's covenants passed to Larch on the sale.","Yes, because Juniper has not been released from its covenants, having never applied for release.","No, because a landlord is automatically released from its covenants when it sells the reversion.","Yes, but only if Kale entered into an authorised guarantee agreement with Juniper.","No, because the lease was granted after 1995 and so privity of contract does not apply."],"a":1,"e":"**Under the Landlord and Tenant (Covenants) Act 1995, a landlord who sells the reversion is not released automatically; it must serve notice on the tenant before or within four weeks of the sale, and is released only if the tenant does not object or the court declares release reasonable (ss.6 to 8).** Juniper never applied, so it remains bound alongside Larch and can be sued: option B. Option A is true as to Larch but does not release Juniper, option C states the wrong rule, and option D confuses the landlord's position with an authorised guarantee agreement, which concerns an outgoing tenant."},{"t":"leases-covenants","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2017 Maple Holdings Ltd granted Nutmeg Ltd a 25-year lease of a shop. The tenant covenanted not to use the premises other than as a bookshop and to keep the interior in good repair. In 2024, with Maple's consent, Nutmeg sublet the whole shop to Osprey Ltd; Osprey gave no covenant directly to Maple. Osprey now runs a hot food takeaway from the shop and has let the interior fall into disrepair.\n\nWhat action can Maple take directly against Osprey?","o":["It can sue Osprey for both breaches, because all covenants pass automatically under the 1995 Act.","It can take no action against Osprey, because there is no privity of contract or estate between them.","It can obtain an injunction to stop the takeaway use, but cannot sue Osprey for the disrepair.","It can sue Osprey for damages for the disrepair, but cannot stop the takeaway use.","It can sue Osprey for both breaches, because Osprey is in possession of the whole shop."],"a":2,"e":"**There is no privity of contract or estate between a head landlord and a subtenant, so the head landlord cannot sue on positive covenants, but it can enforce restrictive covenants directly against the subtenant (in equity and under LTCA 1995 s.3(5)).** The user covenant is restrictive, so Maple can seek an injunction; the repairing covenant is positive, so it cannot sue Osprey on it: option C. Option B overlooks the enforcement of restrictive covenants, and options A and E wrongly treat a subtenant as an assignee; Maple could instead pursue Nutmeg or forfeit the head lease."},{"t":"leases-remedies","p":"FLK2","q":"Pippin Ltd holds a ten-year lease of a shop at a rent of £24,000 a year payable quarterly, with a right of re-entry for non-payment. It falls £12,000 into arrears, and the landlord issues a claim for possession by forfeiture in the County Court. The hearing is fixed for 10 December 2026. On 1 December 2026 Pippin pays into court the whole of the arrears together with the landlord's costs.\n\nWhat is the effect of Pippin's payment?","o":["The landlord can still forfeit, because Pippin was in breach when the claim was issued.","The court must decide whether to grant relief, having regard to Pippin's past conduct.","Pippin must also seek a new lease, because issuing the claim ended the old lease.","The landlord can still forfeit unless Pippin also pays the next quarter's rent in advance.","The claim comes to an end and the lease continues, because payment was made in time."],"a":4,"e":"**In the County Court, if the tenant pays into court all the rent arrears and the landlord's costs at least five clear days before the hearing, the claim ceases and the tenant holds under the lease as before (County Courts Act 1984 s.138(2)).** Payment on 1 December leaves more than five clear days before 10 December, so option E is right. Option B describes the court's discretion where payment is not made in time, and option C is wrong because the lease continues automatically without any new grant."},{"t":"leases-remedies","p":"FLK2","q":"Quince Ltd's 15-year lease of a warehouse expired on 30 June 2026. The lease contained a full repairing covenant by the tenant but no clause allowing the landlord to enter, carry out repairs and recover the cost from the tenant. At the end of the lease the warehouse was in disrepair and the repairs would cost £120,000. A valuer reports that the disrepair has reduced the value of the landlord's reversion by £45,000. The landlord does not intend to demolish or alter the building and plans to relet it.\n\nWhat is the most the landlord can recover as damages for the disrepair?","o":["£45,000, because damages cannot exceed the reduction in the value of the reversion.","£120,000, because the landlord is entitled to the full cost of carrying out the repairs.","Nothing, because the landlord can still relet the warehouse in its present condition.","£82,500, because the court will divide the difference between the two figures.","£120,000, because the lease has ended and so the statutory cap no longer applies."],"a":0,"e":"**Damages for breach of a tenant's repairing covenant cannot exceed the amount by which the disrepair reduces the value of the landlord's reversion (LTA 1927 s.18(1)).** So the landlord recovers at most £45,000: option A. The cap applies at the end of the lease as well as during it, so options B and E are wrong; nothing would be recoverable only if the landlord intended to demolish or alter the building so that the repairs were valueless, which is not the case here (option C)."}],"legalsys-p1":[{"t":"courts-judiciary","p":"FLK1","q":"Grace Okafor is a solicitor who does not hold a higher rights of audience qualification. Her firm represented Daniel Brennan at his summary trial in the magistrates' court, where he was convicted of common assault. Daniel has appealed against his conviction to the Crown Court and wants Grace to represent him at the appeal hearing. The following week another client, Mehmet Arslan, asks Grace to act as his advocate at his jury trial for robbery in the Crown Court.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Grace's position?","o":["She may appear for Daniel at his appeal, but she may not act as Mehmet's advocate at his jury trial.","She may act as advocate for both clients, because solicitors are authorised to appear in all courts on admission.","She may act as Mehmet's advocate at his jury trial, but she may not appear for Daniel at his appeal.","She may appear for neither client, because the Crown Court is a higher court for every type of hearing.","She may appear for both clients, but only if the Crown Court judge gives her permission to address the court."],"a":0,"e":"Although a solicitor has rights of audience in all courts on admission, **a solicitor may act as advocate at trials and substantive hearings in the higher courts (including the Crown Court) only with the higher rights qualification**. There is an exception for an **appeal from a magistrates' court (or a committal for sentence) where the solicitor or their firm acted in the court below**, so Grace may represent Daniel but Mehmet needs a barrister or solicitor-advocate with higher rights. Option B ignores the higher rights requirement, and option E confuses the court's discretion to let a lay person speak with the rules for authorised advocates."},{"t":"courts-judiciary","p":"FLK1","q":"Priya Sharma is the managing director of a building company. A former customer owes the company £85,000 for completed building works under a written contract and refuses to pay. The claim is a straightforward debt claim with no personal injury element and no unusual complexity. Priya asks her solicitor to issue proceedings in the High Court because she believes it will be quicker.\n\nWhich of the following best describes where the claim should be started?","o":["In the High Court, because the value of the claim is more than £50,000.","In the High Court, because a claimant may choose either court for any debt claim.","In the Business and Property Courts, because the claim arises from a commercial contract.","In the County Court, because the value of the claim is not more than £100,000.","In the County Court, because the High Court hears only judicial review claims and appeals."],"a":3,"e":"**A claim may be started in the High Court only if it is worth more than £100,000 (or £50,000 or more for a personal injury claim)**, or a statute requires it (CPR Practice Direction 7A), so this £85,000 debt claim must start in the **County Court**. Option A wrongly applies the personal injury threshold to a non-injury claim. Option C fails because the Business and Property Courts are part of the High Court, so the same threshold applies, and option E is wrong because the High Court also hears high-value first-instance claims."},{"t":"precedent","p":"FLK1","q":"A High Court judge sitting alone is hearing a negligence claim brought by Chloe Adeyemi. A 2019 Court of Appeal decision on materially identical facts held, as the basis of its decision, that no duty of care is owed in this situation. In a 2024 Supreme Court appeal on a different issue, three of the five Justices remarked in passing that they considered the 2019 Court of Appeal decision to be wrong. The Supreme Court decided the 2024 appeal on other grounds.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the High Court judge should treat the 2019 decision?","o":["The judge must not follow it, because the Supreme Court has said that it was wrongly decided.","The judge is bound to follow it, because the Supreme Court's remarks were obiter and did not overrule it.","The judge may choose between the two decisions, because they conflict and both of them are binding.","The judge may depart from it, because a single High Court judge is not bound by the Court of Appeal.","The judge must follow the Supreme Court's remarks, because a majority of the Justices agreed on them."],"a":1,"e":"**Only the ratio decidendi (the legal reason necessary for the decision) of a higher court binds a lower court; obiter dicta (remarks made in passing) are persuasive only.** The Supreme Court's criticism was obiter and did not overrule the 2019 decision, so the High Court remains bound by the Court of Appeal's ratio. Options A and E wrongly treat obiter remarks as binding, even when made by a majority, and option C misapplies the conflicting-decisions exception, which concerns the Court of Appeal choosing between its own decisions."},{"t":"precedent","p":"FLK1","q":"The Court of Appeal (Civil Division) is hearing an appeal about the meaning of a clause in a standard-form commercial lease. Counsel for the landlord, Mr Novak, relies on a 2012 Court of Appeal decision that supports his client. Counsel for the tenant relies on a 2018 Court of Appeal decision which reached the opposite conclusion on the same point without mentioning the 2012 decision. Neither decision has been considered by the Supreme Court.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the Court of Appeal's position?","o":["It must follow the 2018 decision, because the later of two Court of Appeal decisions always prevails.","It must refer the conflict to the Supreme Court, because it cannot resolve conflicts in its own case law.","It may follow neither decision, because it is free to decide the point afresh as a matter of principle.","It must follow whichever of the two decisions was given by the larger number of appeal judges.","It may choose which decision to follow, and the decision it does not follow is treated as overruled."],"a":4,"e":"Under the first exception in **Young v Bristol Aeroplane**, where two of its own previous decisions **conflict**, the Court of Appeal **must choose which one to follow**, and the other can no longer stand. There is no rule that the later decision automatically prevails (option A), and there is no procedure for referring a conflict to the Supreme Court (option B): the point can reach the Supreme Court only on an ordinary appeal. Outside the Young exceptions the Court of Appeal is bound by its own decisions, so it is not free to ignore both (option C)."},{"t":"legislation-structure","p":"FLK1","q":"The (fictional) Holiday Lets (Wales) Act 2026 was passed by Senedd Cymru. It received Royal Assent on Tuesday 14 July 2026, when the Letters Patent were notified to the Clerk of the Senedd. The Act contains no provision about when it comes into force. Section 2 makes it an offence to advertise a property in Wales for holiday letting without first registering it. Rhys Morgan placed online adverts for his unregistered cottage in Pembrokeshire on 14 July 2026 and again on 15 July 2026.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Rhys's potential liability under section 2?","o":["Both adverts are caught, because the Act came into force at the start of the day of Royal Assent.","Neither advert is caught, because the Act cannot come into force until commencement regulations are made.","Only the 15 July advert is caught, as the Act came into force at the start of the day after Royal Assent.","Both adverts are caught, because an Act of the Senedd takes effect from the day it is passed at Stage 4.","Neither advert is caught, because Acts of the Senedd come into force two months after Royal Assent."],"a":2,"e":"**An Act of Senedd Cymru that says nothing about commencement comes into force at the beginning of the day after the day on which it receives Royal Assent** (s.30 Legislation (Wales) Act 2019), so section 2 was in force from the start of 15 July and the earlier advert was not an offence. Option A states the Westminster default rule for Acts of Parliament (s.4 Interpretation Act 1978), which does not apply to Senedd Acts. Commencement regulations are needed only where the Act provides for them, and there is no general two-month rule."},{"t":"statutory-interpretation","p":"FLK1","q":"Section 5 of the (fictional) Rural Transport Act 2025 provides grants for 'community vehicles'. The phrase is not defined, and it is genuinely unclear whether it includes electric minibuses owned by parish councils. During the Commons second reading debate, the minister in charge of the Bill said clearly that parish council minibuses 'will be covered'. At committee stage a backbench MP, Ms Lindqvist, said she understood the opposite. Hollins Parish Council has been refused a grant and wishes to rely on Hansard in its challenge.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the use the court may make of Hansard?","o":["It may not refer to Hansard at all, because doing so would question proceedings in Parliament.","It may refer to both statements, but should give more weight to the statement made by the minister.","It may refer to the minister's statement, because the words are ambiguous and the statement is clear.","It may refer to the minister's statement, but only if the Explanatory Notes also leave the point unclear.","It may refer to Ms Lindqvist's statement, because committee stage provides the most detailed scrutiny."],"a":2,"e":"A court may refer to Hansard only where **the legislation is ambiguous or obscure (or leads to absurdity), the material consists of statements by a minister or other promoter of the Bill, and those statements are clear** (Pepper v Hart). All three conditions are met for the minister's statement, but statements by backbench MPs such as Ms Lindqvist cannot be used, so options B and E are wrong. Using Hansard to interpret an Act does not 'question' proceedings under Article 9 of the Bill of Rights 1689, and there is no rule that the Explanatory Notes must be considered first."},{"t":"statutory-interpretation","p":"FLK1","q":"The (fictional) Exotic Animals Act 2024 makes it an offence to keep 'lions, tigers, bears or other dangerous animals' at a residential property without a licence. Tomasz Kowalski keeps a large and aggressive dog of a breed that is not regulated by any other legislation at his home, without a licence. He is prosecuted on the basis that the dog is an 'other dangerous animal'.\n\nWhich of the following is most likely to help Tomasz, and why?","o":["Ejusdem generis, because the general words are limited to wild animals of the same kind as those listed.","Noscitur a sociis, because the word 'dangerous' takes its meaning from the word 'residential'.","Expressio unius est exclusio alterius, because naming three species excludes every other animal.","The literal rule, because a dog is plainly not a lion, a tiger or a bear in ordinary language.","Ejusdem generis, because general words that follow a list of specific words must be disregarded."],"a":0,"e":"Under the **ejusdem generis rule (\"of the same kind\"), general words that follow a list of specific words are limited to things of the same kind as the list**. Lions, tigers and bears form a class of wild, exotic animals, so 'other dangerous animals' probably does not extend to a domestic dog. Option E misstates the rule, since the general words are narrowed rather than ignored, and options C and D wrongly treat the general words 'other dangerous animals' as if they did not exist."},{"t":"sovereignty-rule-of-law","p":"FLK1","q":"The (fictional) Coastal Fisheries Act 2015 provides in section 20 that 'no later Act shall have effect so far as it is inconsistent with this Act unless it expressly refers to this section'. In 2025 Parliament passes the Marine Licensing Act 2025, which sets a licence fee for trawlers that is inconsistent with the fee set by the 2015 Act. The 2025 Act does not mention section 20. Marta Silva, a trawler owner, is charged the 2025 fee and argues that the 2015 fee still applies. The 2015 Act is not regarded as a constitutional statute.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome?","o":["The 2015 fee applies, because section 20 validly requires later Acts to refer to it expressly.","The 2015 fee applies, because courts must give effect to the earlier of two inconsistent Acts.","The 2025 Act is void, because Parliament did not follow the manner and form set by section 20.","The court will declare the two Acts inconsistent and refer the question back to Parliament.","The 2025 fee applies, because the later Act impliedly repeals the earlier inconsistent provision."],"a":4,"e":"Because **no Parliament can bind its successors, a later Act prevails over an earlier inconsistent Act by implied repeal**, even where the earlier Act tries to prevent this (Ellen Street Estates). Section 20 cannot dictate the content of future legislation, so options A and B fail, and whether Parliament can bind itself as to 'manner and form' remains unresolved and would not make an Act void here. The answer could differ only for a **constitutional statute**, which can be repealed only expressly or by unmistakably clear words."},{"t":"sovereignty-rule-of-law","p":"FLK1","q":"A (fictional) statute allows a Secretary of State to issue a certificate overriding a tribunal's order to disclose government information if the Secretary of State has 'reasonable grounds' for doing so. After a full hearing, the Upper Tribunal orders the Department for Transport to disclose correspondence with a rail operator to a journalist, Ms Kaur. The next day the Secretary of State issues a certificate overriding the order, stating only that she has considered the same evidence and has reached a different view from the tribunal.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the lawfulness of the certificate?","o":["It is lawful, because the statute gives the Secretary of State an unrestricted power to override the tribunal.","It is likely to be unlawful, because a minister cannot override a judicial decision merely by disagreeing with it.","It is lawful, because the separation of powers prevents the courts from reviewing ministerial certificates.","It is likely to be unlawful, because no statute can ever allow a minister to override an order of a tribunal.","It is lawful, but the Secretary of State must resign under the convention of individual ministerial responsibility."],"a":1,"e":"**The rule of law means that a member of the executive cannot override a judicial decision simply because they take a different view of the same evidence**, and general statutory words are read narrowly so as not to permit this (R (Evans) v Attorney General). The certificate is therefore open to challenge by judicial review. Option D overstates the position, since Parliament could authorise an override in sufficiently clear words, and option C is wrong because the courts do review the lawfulness of ministerial decisions."},{"t":"crown-executive-prerogative","p":"FLK1","q":"The (fictional) Emergency Shipping Act 2024 allows the Secretary of State to requisition privately owned vessels during a national emergency and requires compensation at market rates, assessed by an independent panel. During a fuel crisis, the Secretary of State does not use the Act but instead relies on the royal prerogative to take control of three tankers owned by Fenwick Marine Ltd, offering a much lower ex gratia payment (a payment made voluntarily, without admitting any legal obligation).\n\nWhich of the following best describes the legal position?","o":["The requisition is lawful, because emergencies and defence are non-justiciable prerogative matters.","The requisition is lawful, because the prerogative and the statute coexist and the minister may choose between them.","The requisition is unlawful, because prerogative powers can never be used to take private property.","The requisition is unlawful, because the statute covers the same ground and puts the prerogative into abeyance.","The requisition is lawful, provided the House of Commons approves it by resolution after the event."],"a":3,"e":"**Where a statute covers the same ground as a prerogative power, the prerogative is put into abeyance (suspended) and the minister must act under the statute**, including its compensation provisions (De Keyser's Royal Hotel). Option B is therefore wrong, as the minister has no choice. Option A confuses the question whether a decision is justiciable with the prior question whether the power exists at all, which is always for the courts, and a Commons resolution cannot make lawful what the law does not permit."},{"t":"crown-executive-prerogative","p":"FLK1","q":"Farah Iqbal applied to a central government agency for a licence. Her application was delayed for 14 months because staff repeatedly lost her documents and failed to reply to her letters. The licence has now been granted, but she lost business in the meantime. Farah wishes to complain to the Parliamentary and Health Service Ombudsman and asks how a complaint would work and what the Ombudsman can do.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Farah's complaint to the Ombudsman?","o":["She may complain directly, and the Ombudsman can order the agency to pay her compensation.","She must first bring judicial review proceedings, and may complain only if those proceedings fail.","Her MP may refer the complaint, and the Ombudsman can then quash the agency's original decision.","Her MP must refer the complaint, and any recommendation the Ombudsman makes is not legally binding.","She may complain directly, but the Ombudsman can investigate only bias or rudeness, not delay."],"a":3,"e":"**Complaints of maladministration (poor administration, such as delay or losing documents) by government bodies must be referred to the Parliamentary Ombudsman by an MP (the 'MP filter'), and the Ombudsman's recommendations are not legally binding.** The Ombudsman cannot order compensation or quash decisions, so options A and C are wrong, although recommendations are usually followed. Delay is a classic example of maladministration (option E), and there is no requirement to bring judicial review first."},{"t":"parliament-legislation-privilege","p":"FLK1","q":"The government's (fictional) Water Ownership Bill was introduced in the House of Commons. Its Commons second reading took place on 3 March 2025. It passed the Commons and was sent to the House of Lords in June 2025, and the Lords rejected it. In the next session, the Commons passed the identical Bill again on 9 February 2026 and sent it to the Lords, which rejected it again in March 2026. The Bill is not a money Bill and would not extend the life of Parliament.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the Bill can receive Royal Assent without the Lords' consent?","o":["Yes, because the Commons has passed the same Bill in two successive sessions of Parliament.","No, because a year has not passed between the first Commons second reading and the second Commons passing.","Yes, because the Salisbury convention prevents the Lords from rejecting a government Bill a second time.","No, because the Parliament Acts may be used only for Bills certified by the Speaker as money Bills.","Yes, because the House of Lords may delay an ordinary public Bill for no more than one month."],"a":1,"e":"Under the **Parliament Acts 1911 and 1949**, a public Bill (other than a money Bill) can receive Royal Assent without the Lords' consent only if the Commons passes it in two successive sessions **and one year has elapsed between its Commons second reading in the first session and its passing by the Commons in the second session**. Here only about eleven months passed (3 March 2025 to 9 February 2026), so the requirement is not met even though option A's condition is satisfied. The one-month rule in option E applies only to money Bills, and the Salisbury convention is a political convention about manifesto Bills, not a legal rule."},{"t":"parliament-legislation-privilege","p":"FLK1","q":"During a debate in the House of Commons, Paul Whitaker MP alleges that a named care home operator, Ms Danso, has falsified fire safety records. The following week he repeats the allegation while giving evidence to a Commons select committee. He then publishes a constituency newsletter reproducing the allegation in full under the headline 'What I told Parliament'. Ms Danso wishes to sue him for defamation.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ms Danso's prospects of bringing a defamation claim?","o":["She may sue over the newsletter only, because the debate and the committee evidence are proceedings in Parliament.","She may sue over the committee evidence and the newsletter, because only debates in the chamber are protected.","She cannot sue over any of the statements, because an MP's communications with constituents are proceedings in Parliament.","She may sue over all three statements, because privilege does not cover allegations of dishonesty against named people.","She may sue over the debate statement only if she proves that the MP knew that the allegation was false."],"a":0,"e":"**Article 9 of the Bill of Rights 1689 gives absolute immunity for anything said in 'proceedings in Parliament', which include debates and evidence given to committees.** A constituency newsletter repeating the allegation is not a proceeding in Parliament, so Ms Danso may sue over it. Option B wrongly limits privilege to debates in the chamber, and option E is wrong because the immunity is absolute, so even a knowingly false statement in a debate cannot found a claim."},{"t":"devolution-wales","p":"FLK1","q":"Senedd Cymru passed the (fictional) Rail Services (Wales) Bill at Stage 4 three weeks ago. The Bill has not yet been submitted for Royal Assent. The UK Government believes that one of its provisions relates to a reserved matter and would also have an adverse effect on the operation of the law as it applies in England.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the steps now open to the UK Government?","o":["The Secretary of State for Wales may refer the Bill to the Supreme Court, and the Attorney General may order that it is not submitted for Royal Assent.","Only the Counsel General for Wales may refer the Bill to the Supreme Court, because it is a Bill of the Senedd rather than of Parliament.","The Attorney General may refer the Bill to the Supreme Court, and the Secretary of State for Wales may order that it is not submitted for Royal Assent.","Nothing can be done before Royal Assent, but after Royal Assent the question can be raised in the courts as a devolution issue.","Nothing can be done, because the Presiding Officer's statement that the Bill is within competence is conclusive."],"a":2,"e":"Within **four weeks** of a Senedd Bill being passed, the **Counsel General or the UK Attorney General may refer the question of competence to the Supreme Court (s.112 GoWA 2006)**, and the **Secretary of State for Wales may make an order prohibiting its submission for Royal Assent** on grounds including an adverse effect on a reserved matter or on the law as it applies in England (s.114). Option A swaps the two roles. The Presiding Officer's statement on competence is not binding (option E), and option D ignores the pre-Assent procedures that are still available because the four-week window has not closed."},{"t":"devolution-wales","p":"FLK1","q":"An Act of Senedd Cymru allows the Welsh Ministers to make regulations about the licensing of residential landlords in Wales, subject to the Senedd annulment procedure. The Welsh Ministers make regulations allowing council officers to enter and inspect any rented home at any time, without notice or a warrant. The Senedd does not annul them within 40 days. Bronwen Hughes, a tenant in Swansea whose home was inspected at 6am without notice, wishes to challenge the regulations as a breach of Article 8 (right to respect for private and family life and home).\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The regulations are immune from challenge, because the Senedd did not resolve to annul them within 40 days.","The regulations can be challenged only by a reference to the Supreme Court made by the Counsel General.","The court may only make a declaration of incompatibility, because the regulations are Welsh primary legislation.","The regulations are valid, because Article 8 does not apply to legislation made by devolved governments.","If they breach Article 8, the regulations are outside the Welsh Ministers' powers and may be quashed."],"a":4,"e":"**The Welsh Ministers have no power to make subordinate legislation that is incompatible with Convention rights (s.81 GoWA 2006)**, so incompatible regulations can be **quashed by the High Court on judicial review** like any other secondary legislation. Surviving the 40-day annulment period (option A) does not protect regulations from legal challenge. The regulations are subordinate, not primary, legislation, so the court is not limited to a declaration of incompatibility (option C), and the Supreme Court reference procedure applies to Senedd Bills, not regulations."},{"t":"public-order","p":"FLK1","q":"A campaign group plans a march through the centre of Leicester on a Saturday afternoon and has given the required written notice to the police. The Chief Constable of Leicestershire Police reasonably believes that the march will cause serious disruption to the life of the community, because it will block the main shopping streets for several hours, but he has no reason to think that it will lead to any disorder. He wants to stop the march taking place at all.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the Chief Constable's powers?","o":["He may ban the march himself, because he reasonably believes it will cause serious disruption.","He may apply to the council for a banning order, which will also need the Home Secretary's consent.","He may ban the march, but only if the organisers have failed to give six clear days' written notice.","He may arrest the organisers to prevent a breach of the peace, because serious disruption is expected.","He cannot have the march banned, but may impose conditions, such as on its route, to prevent serious disruption."],"a":4,"e":"**A procession can be banned under s.13 Public Order Act 1986 only where the chief officer reasonably believes that conditions will not be enough to prevent serious public disorder**; serious disruption is not a ground for a ban. Option B describes the correct procedure outside London (an application to the council, with the Home Secretary's consent), but the trigger is missing, so it is not available. The Chief Constable may instead impose **conditions under s.12** (for example on the route) where he reasonably believes the march may cause serious disruption to the life of the community, and a breach of the peace requires actual or threatened violence, not mere disruption."},{"t":"public-order","p":"FLK1","q":"Ayesha Rahman stands on a box in a town square giving a peaceful talk criticising the owners of a well-known football club. A group of supporters gathers, shouting abuse and threatening to 'shut her up', and two of them start pushing towards her. Several officers are nearby. PC Hartley arrests Ayesha to prevent a breach of the peace, without first warning the supporters or asking them to move back.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the lawfulness of Ayesha's arrest?","o":["It is lawful, because the officer reasonably believed that a breach of the peace was imminent.","It is lawful, because Ayesha committed the offence of breach of the peace by provoking the crowd.","It is likely to be unlawful, because the threat of violence came from the supporters, not from Ayesha.","It is likely to be unlawful, because only a magistrate may authorise action to prevent a breach of the peace.","It is lawful, because a public assembly of two or more people may be dispersed by any police officer."],"a":2,"e":"**Action to prevent a breach of the peace should normally be taken against the people threatening violence; action against a person behaving lawfully is justified only in extreme cases where it is necessary and there is no other way to prevent an imminent breach** (Redmond-Bate v DPP). A breach may well have been imminent (option A), but the officer acted against the lawful speaker without trying to deal with the supporters, even though other officers were available. Breach of the peace is not itself a criminal offence (option B), and any person, including a police officer, may act to prevent one without a magistrate's authority."},{"t":"judicial-review-procedure","p":"FLK1","q":"On 2 February 2026 a county council decided to close a day centre for older people. On 20 February, solicitors for Mr Osei, who uses the centre, sent the council a letter before claim. The council asked for time to review the matter, and both sides agreed in writing that no claim would be issued until the review ended. On 22 May 2026 the council wrote only to say that its review was over and the decision of 2 February stood. Mr Osei now wishes to issue a claim for judicial review.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the time limit for Mr Osei's claim?","o":["Time ran from 22 May, when the council told Mr Osei that its review had ended.","Time was suspended by the parties' written agreement until the council's review ended.","Time ran from the letter before claim, and the claim had to be filed within six weeks.","The claim was due promptly and by 2 May; only the court, not the parties, can extend time.","The claim may be filed within one year, because the closure decision affects Mr Osei's rights."],"a":3,"e":"**A judicial review claim must be filed promptly and in any event within three months of the date the grounds first arose (CPR 54.5(1)), and the parties cannot extend this by agreement (CPR 54.5(2)); only the court can extend time.** Time ran from the decision of 2 February, so the claim was due by 2 May, and neither the letter before claim nor the agreed review stopped the clock (option B). A letter merely confirming the original decision does not start time again (option A), the six-week limit applies to planning decisions, and the one-year period is the Human Rights Act limit, which does not displace the shorter judicial review limit."},{"t":"judicial-review-procedure","p":"FLK1","q":"The Online Gambling Standards Panel is a non-statutory body set up by the gambling industry. It has no powers under any statute, prerogative or contract over firms that are not its members. The government has said publicly that it will not legislate to regulate gambling adverts because the Panel does the job, and the Panel's rulings are routinely enforced by the industry against non-members. The Panel rules that adverts by Northgate Bets Ltd, which is not a member, are misleading, and they are withdrawn. Northgate Bets wishes to challenge the ruling.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the Panel's ruling is open to judicial review?","o":["It is likely to be reviewable, because the Panel performs a public regulatory function woven into government policy.","It is not reviewable, because the Panel derives none of its powers from statute or from the royal prerogative.","It is not reviewable, because a body set up by an industry is a private body whose rulings are matters of contract.","It is likely to be reviewable, but only if Northgate Bets first becomes a member and accepts the Panel's rules.","It is not reviewable, because only bodies listed as public authorities under the Human Rights Act are amenable."],"a":0,"e":"**Amenability to judicial review depends on the nature of the function, not only the source of the power: a non-statutory body performing an important public regulatory function, backed by government, is reviewable** (R v Panel on Take-overs and Mergers ex p Datafin). The Panel regulates non-members in place of legislation, so options B and C, which look only at the source of its power, are wrong. A body whose power over the claimant comes from **contract** (such as a sports body over its members) is usually not reviewable, which is why joining the Panel (option D) would weaken rather than create a public law claim."},{"t":"judicial-review-grounds","p":"FLK1","q":"A district council has a statutory discretion to award hardship grants to local businesses 'where the council considers it appropriate'. It adopts a policy that grants will be paid only to businesses that have traded for at least five years. Lucia Ferreira's bakery has traded for three years and was badly flooded last month. She writes to the council explaining her exceptional circumstances. A council officer replies: 'Your letter has not been considered. The policy admits no exceptions.'\n\nWhich of the following best describes Lucia's strongest ground of challenge?","o":["Irrationality, because a five-year trading requirement is so unreasonable that no council could adopt it.","Illegality, because the council has fettered its discretion by refusing to consider making an exception.","Procedural impropriety, because the council was required to hold an oral hearing before refusing her.","Illegality, because a council with a statutory discretion is not permitted to adopt any policy at all.","Legitimate expectation, because every local business can reasonably expect to receive a hardship grant."],"a":1,"e":"**A body with a discretion may adopt a policy, but it unlawfully fetters that discretion if it applies the policy so rigidly that it refuses to consider whether an exception should be made** (British Oxygen v Board of Trade); this is a form of illegality. Option D overstates the rule, since policies are lawful if the body remains willing to listen. A five-year requirement is unlikely to meet the very high Wednesbury irrationality threshold (option A), and nothing suggests a clear promise or settled practice that could found a legitimate expectation."},{"t":"judicial-review-grounds","p":"FLK1","q":"The (fictional) Heritage Grants Act 2024 sets up a statutory panel to decide applications for restoration grants and provides that 'any determination of the panel shall not be called in question in any court of law'. The panel rejects an application from the Lakeside Mill Trust after misreading the statutory definition of a 'listed structure', which is an error of law. The Trust wishes to apply for judicial review within a few weeks of the decision.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the ouster clause?","o":["It prevents any challenge, because Parliament is sovereign and the words of the clause are clear.","It does not prevent review, because a decision based on an error of law is not a true determination.","It prevents review of the panel's decision, but the Trust may bring a claim for damages instead.","It does not prevent review, because every ouster clause is void as contrary to the rule of law.","It prevents review unless the Trust can show that the panel members acted in bad faith."],"a":1,"e":"**Ouster clauses are read very narrowly: a decision vitiated by an error of law is only a 'purported' determination (a nullity), so a clause protecting 'determinations' does not stop the court reviewing it** (Anisminic v Foreign Compensation Commission). Option D goes too far, because time-limited ouster clauses and some very clearly drafted modern ousters are given effect. Option A ignores the courts' approach to interpreting such clauses, and there is no general right to damages for unlawful administrative action (option C)."},{"t":"human-rights-act","p":"FLK1","q":"Joseph Mensah is defending a possession claim brought by his private landlord in the County Court. He argues that a section of the (fictional) Housing Act 2023, on which the landlord relies, is incompatible with Article 8 (right to respect for private and family life and home). The district judge concludes that the section cannot possibly be read in a way that is compatible with Article 8.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the district judge should do?","o":["Declare the section incompatible, which means that it ceases to have effect in Joseph's case.","Refuse to apply the section, because Convention rights take priority over inconsistent legislation.","Make a declaration of incompatibility, which binds the landlord but does not affect the Act's validity.","Apply the section as it stands, because the County Court cannot make a declaration of incompatibility.","Adjourn the case and refer the question of compatibility to the European Court of Human Rights."],"a":3,"e":"**Only the higher courts (the High Court and above) can make a declaration of incompatibility under s.4 Human Rights Act 1998, and even a declaration does not affect the validity or operation of the Act or bind the parties.** The County Court must therefore apply the incompatible primary legislation, leaving Joseph to pursue the point on appeal. Option B is wrong because courts cannot disapply Acts of Parliament for incompatibility, options A and C misstate both who can make a declaration and its effect, and there is no reference procedure to the Strasbourg court."},{"t":"human-rights-act","p":"FLK1","q":"Fourteen months ago, police officers searched the home of Liam Doyle and his partner under a warrant that was later found to have been obtained using inaccurate information. The couple now wish to bring a claim against the police under s.7 Human Rights Act 1998 for breach of Article 8. A civil liberties charity, which has campaigned against the use of such warrants but was not involved in the search, also wishes to bring a claim in its own name.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The couple are out of time and cannot claim, but the charity may sue as a body with a sufficient interest.","Both the couple and the charity may sue, because the one-year period runs from when they took advice.","The couple may sue only if the court considers a longer period equitable, and the charity is not a victim.","The couple may sue as of right at any time within two years, and the charity may sue if it has local members.","Neither may sue, because claims against the police must be brought by judicial review within three months."],"a":2,"e":"**A claim under s.7 Human Rights Act 1998 must be brought within one year of the act complained of, or such longer period as the court considers equitable, and only by a 'victim', meaning someone directly affected by the act.** The couple are outside the year, so they need the court to extend time, and the charity is not a victim merely because it campaigns on the issue. Option A confuses the victim test with the wider 'sufficient interest' test for ordinary judicial review, and time runs from the act itself, not from when advice was taken."},{"t":"eu-law-brexit","p":"FLK1","q":"In 2026 a High Court judge is hearing a dispute between Valera Foods Ltd and one of its suppliers about the meaning of a provision in an assimilated regulation (an EU regulation converted into domestic law at the end of 2020). A 2018 decision of the Court of Justice of the European Union (CJEU) interpreted the provision in the supplier's favour. A 2023 CJEU decision interpreted the identical provision of the EU regulation in Valera Foods' favour. There is no relevant decision of a UK appeal court.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the High Court should treat the two CJEU decisions?","o":["It is bound by the 2018 decision, and may have regard to the 2023 decision without being bound by it.","It is bound by the 2023 decision, because it is the most recent statement of the law by the CJEU.","It may depart from the 2018 decision if it considers it right to do so under the Practice Statement test.","It should refer the question to the CJEU, because the provision is derived from an EU regulation.","It is bound by neither decision, because EU case law ceased to have any effect on 1 January 2024."],"a":0,"e":"**CJEU decisions made before the end of 2020 are assimilated EU case law and bind the High Court and lower courts; decisions made after 31 December 2020 bind no UK court, though a court may have regard to them** (s.6 European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018). Only the Court of Appeal and the Supreme Court may depart from pre-2021 CJEU decisions using the Practice Statement test, so option C is wrong for the High Court. UK courts can no longer refer questions to the CJEU outside limited Withdrawal Agreement routes, and the 2024 reforms renamed this case law rather than abolishing it."}],"legalsys-p2":[{"t":"courts-judiciary","p":"FLK1","q":"A High Court judge, Mrs Justice Adebayo, rules in a judicial review claim that a government minister acted unlawfully when cutting funding for legal advice centres. The minister tells a newspaper that the judge is 'out of touch' and that he will ask for her to be removed from office if she makes another ruling of that kind.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how a High Court judge can be removed from office?","o":["By the Lord Chancellor, with the agreement of the Lord Chief Justice, for misbehaviour.","By the King, acting on the advice of the Prime Minister after a Cabinet decision.","By the King, on an address presented to him by both Houses of Parliament.","By the Judicial Appointments Commission, after a hearing at which she may respond.","By a resolution of the House of Commons alone, passed by a simple majority."],"a":2,"e":"Senior judges hold office **during good behaviour** and can be removed only by the monarch on an **address presented by both Houses of Parliament** (s.11(3) Senior Courts Act 1981), a key safeguard of **judicial independence**. Option A describes the disciplinary route for some lower-tier judges, not High Court judges. Options B and E wrongly give the power to the executive or to the Commons alone, and the Judicial Appointments Commission (option D) selects judges but does not remove them."},{"t":"precedent","p":"FLK1","q":"The Court of Appeal (Civil Division) is hearing an appeal by Halima Yusuf about whether a landlord's notice was validly served. In 2017 the Court of Appeal decided that notices of this kind are valid even if sent to an old address. Counsel for Halima shows that a 2011 Supreme Court decision, which held the opposite on the same point, was not cited to the court in 2017 or mentioned in its judgments.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the Court of Appeal should approach the issue?","o":["It should follow the 2011 Supreme Court decision, because its own 2017 decision was made per incuriam.","It should follow its own 2017 decision, because the Court of Appeal is bound by its earlier decisions.","It may choose freely between the two decisions, because they are conflicting decisions of equal weight.","It should refer the conflict to the Supreme Court, because only that court may resolve the inconsistency.","It may depart from the 2017 decision only by applying the test in the Practice Statement of 1966."],"a":0,"e":"The Court of Appeal is normally bound by its own decisions, but one exception is a decision made **per incuriam** (through lack of care), in ignorance of a **binding authority** that would have changed the result; a Supreme Court decision binds the Court of Appeal in any event. Option B ignores that exception, and option C describes the different exception for two conflicting Court of Appeal decisions, not a clash with a higher court. The Practice Statement (option E) is used by the Supreme Court, and there is no procedure for 'referring' a conflict upwards (option D)."},{"t":"precedent","p":"FLK1","q":"Callum Reid was convicted in the Crown Court of an offence requiring proof that he 'knowingly' handled prohibited goods. The trial judge directed the jury, following a 2019 decision of the Court of Appeal (Criminal Division), that it was enough that a reasonable person would have known the goods were prohibited. On Callum's appeal, the Court of Appeal (Criminal Division) is satisfied that the 2019 court misunderstood the law and that, applied correctly, the jury might well have acquitted him.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position of the Court of Appeal (Criminal Division)?","o":["It must follow the 2019 decision, because it is bound by its own previous decisions without exception.","It must follow the 2019 decision, and only the Supreme Court can correct the error on a further appeal.","It may depart from the 2019 decision only if that decision overlooked a binding statute or authority.","It may depart from the 2019 decision, because the law was misunderstood and Callum's liberty is at stake.","It may depart from the 2019 decision only after certifying a point of law of general public importance."],"a":3,"e":"The Court of Appeal (Criminal Division) is generally bound by its own decisions, but it has **extra flexibility where the defendant's liberty is at stake**: it may depart where the law was **misapplied or misunderstood** and following the earlier decision would cause injustice (R v Taylor). Options A and B ignore this flexibility, and option C confines the court to the per incuriam exception, which is narrower than the court's actual freedom in criminal cases. Certifying a point of law (option E) is a step towards an appeal to the Supreme Court, not a precondition for departing from its own decision."},{"t":"legislation-structure","p":"FLK1","q":"The (fictional) Drone Safety Act 2026 is an Act of Parliament. It received Royal Assent at 4pm on Thursday 9 July 2026. It contains no provision about when it comes into force. Section 3 makes it an offence to fly a drone over school premises during the school day. At 10am on 9 July 2026 Kwame Asante flew a drone over a primary school during lessons.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether section 3 was in force when Kwame flew the drone?","o":["It was not in force, because the Act took effect only at the moment Royal Assent was given at 4pm.","It was not in force, because an Act silent on commencement takes effect at the start of the next day.","It was not in force, because a minister must first make regulations appointing a commencement day.","It was not in force, because an Act silent on commencement takes effect two months after Royal Assent.","It was in force, because the Act took effect from the beginning of the day on which it received Royal Assent."],"a":4,"e":"Where an **Act of Parliament** contains no commencement provision, it comes into force at the **beginning of the day on which it receives Royal Assent** (s.4 Interpretation Act 1978), so section 3 covered conduct from midnight on 9 July. Option B states the different default rule for **Acts of Senedd Cymru** (start of the day after Royal Assent, s.30 Legislation (Wales) Act 2019). Commencement regulations (option C) and a two-month delay (option D) apply only where the Act itself provides for them, and the time of day of assent (option A) is irrelevant."},{"t":"legislation-structure","p":"FLK1","q":"Section 70 of the (fictional) Private Rented Homes Act 2026, an Act of Parliament, provides: 'This Act extends to England and Wales.' Section 4 provides: 'A landlord of a dwelling in England must register with the national landlord database.' The Explanatory Notes published with the Act describe it as 'applying across England and Wales'. Owain Pritchard owns a single rented house in Newport, Wales, and asks whether he must register under section 4.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Owain's position?","o":["He must register, because the Act extends to England and Wales and so governs dwellings in both.","He need not register, because section 4 applies only to dwellings in England despite the Act's extent.","He must register, because the Explanatory Notes confirm that the Act applies across England and Wales.","He need not register, because an Act of Parliament cannot make provision about housing in Wales.","He must register, unless Senedd Cymru has passed an Act disapplying section 4 in relation to Wales."],"a":1,"e":"**Extent** (which legal system a law forms part of) is different from **application** (who or what it governs): section 4 is part of the law of England and Wales, but its wording limits it to **dwellings in England**. Option A confuses extent with application, and option C fails because **Explanatory Notes are not part of the Act** and cannot override its words. Option D is wrong because Parliament is sovereign and can legislate for Wales on any matter (the Sewel convention is political only), and option E wrongly assumes section 4 reaches Wales in the first place."},{"t":"statutory-interpretation","p":"FLK1","q":"Section 2 of the (fictional) Household Waste Act 2025 gives district councils power to charge for collecting 'grass cuttings, hedge trimmings and fallen leaves' from homes. The section contains no general words such as 'or other garden waste'. A district council begins charging residents for collecting old tree stumps. Ifeoma Nwosu, a resident, argues that the council has no power to charge for this.\n\nWhich rule of language most directly supports Ifeoma's argument?","o":["Ejusdem generis, because general words following a list are limited to things of the same kind.","Noscitur a sociis, because the word 'leaves' takes its meaning from the other words in the section.","Expressio unius est exclusio alterius, because listing particular items excludes items not mentioned.","The golden rule, because charging for tree stumps would produce an absurd or repugnant result.","The literal rule, because tree stumps are not ordinarily described as a type of household waste."],"a":2,"e":"Under **expressio unius est exclusio alterius** (expressing one thing excludes others), a closed list of specific items is read as **excluding things not mentioned**, so tree stumps fall outside the power. Ejusdem generis (option A) needs **general words** after the list, and the section has none. Noscitur a sociis (option B) helps decide the meaning of an unclear word in the list, which is not the issue here, and there is nothing absurd (option D) about the council's reading that would engage the golden rule. The literal rule (option E) is a general approach to meaning rather than a rule of language about lists, and its reasoning turns on the words 'household waste', which are not the words of the power."},{"t":"statutory-interpretation","p":"FLK1","q":"Section 9 of the (fictional) Gambling Premises Act 2025 makes it an offence for any person to 'allow premises to be used for unlicensed betting'. The section says nothing about knowledge or intention. Lucy Achterberg let a shop to a tenant, who ran an illegal betting operation in a back room. Lucy visited the shop twice a year, saw nothing suspicious and had no idea what the tenant was doing. She is charged under section 9.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how a court is likely to interpret section 9?","o":["As requiring proof of a guilty mind, because Parliament is presumed to intend mens rea unless it clearly excludes it.","As imposing strict liability, because the section is silent about knowledge and must be given its literal meaning.","As imposing strict liability, because offences regulating licensed activities are always offences of strict liability.","As requiring proof of a guilty mind, because Parliament is presumed not to give legislation retrospective effect.","As requiring proof of a guilty mind, because the Explanatory Notes are part of the Act and must say so expressly."],"a":0,"e":"There is a **presumption that mens rea (a guilty mind) is required** for a criminal offence, which applies unless Parliament **clearly excludes it** by express words or necessary implication (Sweet v Parsley). Silence (option B) is not enough to rebut the presumption, and there is no rule that regulatory offences are always strict liability (option C), though the presumption may be rebutted in some regulatory contexts. The presumption against retrospectivity (option D) concerns past events and is irrelevant, and Explanatory Notes are not part of the Act (option E)."},{"t":"sovereignty-rule-of-law","p":"FLK1","q":"The (fictional) Harbour Development Act 2025 is a private Act of Parliament that transferred land at Fairhaven harbour to a development company. The Fairhaven Boatyard Trust, which used part of the land, has evidence that the company made false statements to the parliamentary committee that considered the Bill and that it did not give the notice to affected landowners required by Parliament's standing orders. The Trust asks the High Court to declare the Act invalid.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the High Court is likely to respond?","o":["It will declare the Act invalid if the Trust proves that Parliament was deliberately misled by the company.","It will refuse to investigate how the Act was passed, because an Act on the roll cannot be questioned.","It will disapply the Act, because the standing orders were breached and the Act is only a private Act.","It will make a declaration of incompatibility, leaving it to Parliament to decide whether to repeal it.","It will refer the validity of the Act to the Supreme Court, because only that court may rule on Acts."],"a":1,"e":"Under the **enrolled Act rule**, once an Act is on the Parliament roll the courts will **not investigate the procedure by which it was passed**, even if Parliament was misled (British Railways Board v Pickin), reflecting **parliamentary sovereignty** and Article 9 Bill of Rights. Options A and C are wrong because neither fraud nor breach of standing orders allows a court to invalidate or disapply an Act, private or public. A declaration of incompatibility (option D) is available only for breach of Convention rights under the Human Rights Act, and no court, including the Supreme Court (option E), may rule an Act invalid."},{"t":"sovereignty-rule-of-law","p":"FLK1","q":"Officers employed by a district council enter Samir Haddad's locked warehouse without his consent and remove 300 pairs of trainers, believing them to be counterfeit. No statute or other legal rule gives the officers power to enter or seize goods in these circumstances. The council says the officers acted in good faith and in the public interest, and tests later show that the trainers were indeed counterfeit.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the lawfulness of the officers' actions?","o":["They were lawful, because the council acted in good faith to protect the public from counterfeit goods.","They were lawful, because the trainers turned out to be counterfeit and so the seizure was justified.","They were lawful, because public bodies may rely on the royal prerogative where no statute applies.","They were unlawful only if Samir can show that the officers acted out of malice or for an improper motive.","They were unlawful, because public officials need positive legal authority to interfere with property."],"a":4,"e":"A core principle of the **rule of law** is that public officials may interfere with a person's property or liberty only if they can point to **positive legal authority** for doing so (Entick v Carrington). Good faith and public interest (option A), and the fact the goods proved counterfeit (option B), cannot supply a power that does not exist. The **royal prerogative** belongs to the Crown and does not provide a general power for council officers to search and seize (option C), and showing malice (option D) is unnecessary because the officers lacked any power at all."},{"t":"crown-executive-prerogative","p":"FLK1","q":"In September 2026 the Prime Minister asks the King to dissolve Parliament, and the King does so. A select committee was due to publish a highly critical report on the Prime Minister's conduct the following week, and the dissolution means it will not be published before the general election. A campaign group wishes to bring a judicial review claim, arguing that the dissolution was requested for an improper purpose, relying on the Supreme Court's ruling that a prorogation of Parliament was unlawful.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome of the claim?","o":["It will be heard, because the limits of prerogative powers affecting Parliament are justiciable, as with prorogation.","It will be heard, because the exercise of any prerogative power is reviewable if the subject matter is justiciable.","It will be heard only if the group can show that the Prime Minister gave the King misleading advice.","It will fail, because statute prevents the courts from questioning the exercise of the power to dissolve Parliament.","It will fail, because the courts may never review the exercise of a prerogative power by the Crown."],"a":3,"e":"The **Dissolution and Calling of Parliament Act 2022** revived the prerogative power to dissolve Parliament, and **s.3** is an **ouster clause** providing that a court may not question the exercise of that power, decisions about it, or its limits or extent. The prorogation ruling (option A) concerned a different prerogative power that is not covered by any ouster, and the general GCHQ approach (option B) is displaced by the statute. Option E is wrong because many prerogative powers are reviewable, and option C invents an exception the Act does not contain."},{"t":"parliament-legislation-privilege","p":"FLK1","q":"The government's (fictional) Fuel Duty (Rates) Bill deals only with the rates of a national tax and has been certified by the Speaker as a Money Bill. It passed the House of Commons and was received by the House of Lords on Monday 2 March 2026, several months before the end of the session. On 20 March 2026 the House of Lords voted to reject the Bill.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the Bill can become law without the consent of the House of Lords?","o":["Yes, but only if the Commons passes it again in the next session at least a year after its Commons second reading.","No, because the House of Lords has rejected the Bill and a Money Bill cannot be presented without its consent.","Yes, because once a month has passed since the Lords received it without passing it, it may receive Royal Assent.","Yes, and it may be presented for Royal Assent immediately, because the Lords have no power to vote on Money Bills.","No, because the House of Lords may delay any Bill, including a Money Bill, for a period of up to one year."],"a":2,"e":"Under the **Parliament Acts**, a Bill certified by the Speaker as a **Money Bill** may receive Royal Assent without the Lords' consent if the Lords do not pass it **within one month** of receiving it (provided it was sent up at least a month before the end of the session). Option A states the one-year and next-session procedure for **other** public Bills, and option E wrongly applies the one-year delay to Money Bills. The Lords may debate and vote on a Money Bill, so the Commons must still wait the month (option D), and a Lords rejection does not prevent it becoming law (option B)."},{"t":"parliament-legislation-privilege","p":"FLK1","q":"Jonathan Pryce MP is charged with fraud after allegedly submitting false claims to the parliamentary expenses authority for rent on a constituency office that did not exist. His lawyers argue that the prosecution must be stopped because the claims were part of his work as an MP, so that they are protected by Article 9 of the Bill of Rights 1689 and fall within the exclusive right of the House of Commons to regulate its own affairs.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Jonathan can be prosecuted?","o":["He can be prosecuted, because submitting expenses claims is not a proceeding in Parliament.","He cannot be prosecuted, because Article 9 protects all acts done by MPs in their official role.","He cannot be prosecuted, because only the House of Commons may deal with how its members claim expenses.","He can be prosecuted, but only if the House of Commons first votes to waive his parliamentary privilege.","He can be prosecuted, but only with the consent of the Speaker, because the claims relate to Parliament."],"a":0,"e":"Article 9 protects **proceedings in Parliament** (debates, questions and committee work), and submitting **expenses claims** is an administrative act, not a proceeding in Parliament; nor do such ordinary crimes fall within **exclusive cognisance** (the Houses' right to regulate their own internal affairs) (R v Chaytor). Option B overstates Article 9, which does not cover everything an MP does in an official capacity, and option C overstates exclusive cognisance. No vote of waiver (option D) or Speaker's consent (option E) is needed for a prosecution."},{"t":"devolution-wales","p":"FLK1","q":"Section 10 of the (fictional) Social Housing Allocation (Wales) Act 2026, an Act of Senedd Cymru which has received Royal Assent, provides that section 6 of the Human Rights Act 1998 (the duty of public authorities to act compatibly with Convention rights) 'shall not apply to decisions of local authorities in Wales about the allocation of social housing'. In a claim in the County Court, Bethan Lloyd argues that her council's allocation decision breached Article 8.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of section 10?","o":["It is valid, because housing is a devolved matter on which the Senedd may legislate as it sees fit.","It is valid, because once a Senedd Bill has received Royal Assent its competence can no longer be challenged.","It is valid, but the County Court may declare it incompatible, leaving the Senedd to decide whether to amend it.","It is valid unless the Counsel General or Attorney General referred it to the Supreme Court within four weeks.","It is not law, because modifying the Human Rights Act is outside the Senedd's legislative competence."],"a":4,"e":"A provision of an Act of the Senedd is **not law** so far as it is outside **legislative competence** (s.108A GoWA 2006), and **Schedule 7B** prevents the Senedd modifying **protected enactments**, including the **Human Rights Act 1998** (s.108A(2)(e) also bars provisions incompatible with Convention rights). Competence can be challenged after Royal Assent as a **devolution issue** in ordinary litigation, so options B and D are wrong. Option A ignores the Schedule 7B restriction even on devolved subjects, and option C treats the Senedd Act like an Act of Parliament, when a provision outside competence simply has no effect."},{"t":"devolution-wales","p":"FLK1","q":"The UK Parliament passes the (fictional) Farm Support Act 2026, which sets the rules for agricultural grants to farmers in Wales, a devolved matter. Before the Act was passed, Senedd Cymru voted against a legislative consent motion. The Welsh Government asks whether it can obtain a declaration from the courts that the Act has no legal effect in Wales.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome?","o":["The declaration will be granted, because the Sewel convention is now set out in the Government of Wales Act 2006.","The declaration will be refused, because the Sewel convention is a political convention that the courts will not enforce.","The declaration will be granted, because the UK Parliament no longer has power to legislate on devolved Welsh matters.","The declaration will be refused, because the Senedd's consent is needed only for Bills that change the Senedd's powers.","The declaration will be granted only if the Counsel General referred the Bill to the Supreme Court before Royal Assent."],"a":1,"e":"The **Sewel convention** (Parliament will not normally legislate on devolved matters without the Senedd's consent) is a **political convention**, and its recognition in **s.107(6) GoWA 2006** did not make it legally enforceable (Miller 1), so the Act is valid law in Wales. Option A wrongly assumes statutory recognition made it a legal rule, and option C ignores that devolution did not remove Parliament's **sovereign** power to legislate for Wales. Option D misstates the convention's scope, and the Counsel General's reference power (option E) applies to Senedd Bills, not Acts of Parliament."},{"t":"public-order","p":"FLK1","q":"Rhys Morgan organises a march through Swansea city centre to oppose the closure of a local hospital. He and his group fixed the date, Saturday 18 July 2026, at a meeting on 1 July. Rhys delivers written notice with the date, start time, route and his name and address to a police station in Swansea on Monday 13 July 2026. The march goes ahead peacefully on the notified route on 18 July.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Rhys's position under the Public Order Act 1986?","o":["He commits no offence, because he gave written notice to the police before the march took place.","He commits no offence, because notice is required only if the police intend to impose conditions.","He commits no offence, because a march to oppose a hospital closure is an assembly and needs no notice.","He commits an offence, because notice was not given six clear days before the march when this was practicable.","He commits an offence, because notice of a march must be given at least 28 days before the march takes place."],"a":3,"e":"Under **s.11 Public Order Act 1986**, written notice of a procession held to oppose the actions of a body must be given **at least six clear days** before it (not counting the day of delivery or of the march), unless that is not reasonably practicable; each **organiser** commits an offence if this is not done. Here there were only four clear days (14 to 17 July), and as the date was fixed on 1 July, earlier notice was practicable, so option A is wrong. Notice does not depend on police conditions (option B), a moving march is a **procession**, not an assembly (option C), and there is no 28-day rule (option E)."},{"t":"public-order","p":"FLK1","q":"Climate campaigners plan a demonstration outside a gas-fired power station. Police receive intelligence that a small number of those attending may try to damage the station's fences. Officers stop four minibuses of campaigners at a motorway service station 60 miles from the power station, about an hour's drive away. Although nothing suggests that violence is about to break out at the service station, they escort all the minibuses back to their starting point.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the lawfulness of the police action?","o":["It is lawful, because the police reasonably believed that a breach of the peace would occur later that day.","It is lawful, because breach of the peace is a criminal offence which the police have a duty to prevent.","It is likely to be unlawful, because no breach of the peace was imminent and the action was indiscriminate.","It is likely to be unlawful, because preventive action against a breach of the peace requires a warrant.","It is lawful, because the police may ban any assembly that they reasonably believe may cause damage."],"a":2,"e":"Police may take preventive action against a breach of the peace only if it is **imminent**, and the action must be **proportionate**; turning back everyone an hour away, without any sign of imminent violence, fails both requirements (R (Laporte) v Chief Constable of Gloucestershire). A belief that trouble may happen later (option A) is not enough. **Breach of the peace is not a criminal offence** (option B), no warrant is needed for action against a genuinely imminent breach (option D), and the police have no general power to ban static assemblies (option E)."},{"t":"judicial-review-procedure","p":"FLK1","q":"The Environment Agency grants a water company a permit to discharge treated sewage into a river in Cumbria. No individual resident has challenged the permit. Riverwatch UK, a national charity with 3,000 members living near the river and long experience of water-quality campaigns, wishes to bring a judicial review claim, arguing that the Agency ignored a relevant consideration. It also wants to argue that the permit breaches Article 8 of the European Convention on Human Rights.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Riverwatch UK's ability to bring these claims?","o":["It is likely to have standing for the ordinary judicial review claim, but it is not a victim for an Article 8 claim.","It is likely to have standing for both claims, because it has sufficient interest in the issues raised by the permit.","It is unlikely to have standing for either claim, because it is not itself directly affected by the discharge permit.","It is likely to have standing for the Article 8 claim only, because Convention rights may be enforced by any person.","It is unlikely to have standing for either claim, unless one of its members joins the claim as a named claimant."],"a":0,"e":"For ordinary judicial review the claimant needs a **sufficient interest** (s.31(3) Senior Courts Act 1981), and a responsible pressure group with local members and expertise will usually have standing where no one else is likely to challenge (R v Inspectorate of Pollution ex p Greenpeace (No 2)). A Human Rights Act claim is narrower: the claimant must be a **victim**, directly affected by the act (s.7 HRA 1998), which the charity is not, so options B and D are wrong. Options C and E apply the narrow victim test to ordinary judicial review, which the modern approach does not require."},{"t":"judicial-review-procedure","p":"FLK1","q":"A borough council granted planning permission for a block of flats. Its procedures required it to notify neighbours, but it failed to notify Mr Dimitriou, who lives next door. His only objection is about parking. The committee report shows that parking was raised by several other neighbours and considered in detail before permission was granted. Mr Dimitriou files a judicial review claim in time. At the permission stage, the council shows that it is highly likely the decision would have been the same if he had been notified.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the court must do?","o":["Grant permission, because a failure to follow a required procedure always makes the decision unlawful.","Grant permission, and consider at the full hearing whether to refuse a remedy because of the council's evidence.","Refuse permission, but only if satisfied that Mr Dimitriou would certainly have lost on the merits of his objection.","Grant permission, because the court cannot consider the likely outcome until the council files detailed grounds.","Refuse permission, unless it certifies that there is an exceptional public interest in allowing the claim to proceed."],"a":4,"e":"Under **s.31(3C)-(3F) Senior Courts Act 1981**, the court **must refuse permission** if it appears **highly likely** that the outcome for the claimant would **not have been substantially different** had the conduct complained of not occurred, unless it certifies an **exceptional public interest**. The rule applies at the permission stage itself, so options B and D are wrong, and the test is 'highly likely', not certainty (option C). Procedural failures do not automatically lead to relief (option A), because all judicial review remedies are discretionary and subject to this statutory rule."},{"t":"judicial-review-grounds","p":"FLK1","q":"A district council's planning committee considers an application by Northgate Stores Ltd for planning permission for a new supermarket. The six other members are split three to three, and the chair, Councillor Hughes, uses his casting vote to grant permission. It later emerges that he owns 10% of the shares in Northgate Stores Ltd, which he did not declare. There is no evidence that the shares influenced his vote. A local residents' association wishes to challenge the decision.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome of a judicial review claim?","o":["The decision will stand, because there is no evidence that Councillor Hughes was actually influenced by the shares.","The decision is likely to be quashed, because his financial interest in the applicant automatically disqualified him.","The decision will stand, because a fair-minded observer would see no real possibility of bias from a minority holding.","The decision is likely to be quashed, but only if the residents' association proves that he was actually biased.","The decision will stand, because the duty to act fairly does not apply to planning decisions by a local council."],"a":1,"e":"Where a decision-maker has a **direct financial or proprietary interest** in the outcome, such as shares in a party, he is **automatically disqualified** without any inquiry into whether bias was likely (Dimes v Grand Junction Canal), and his casting vote decided the matter. The **fair-minded and informed observer** test for apparent bias (option C) applies to other kinds of interest, not to a direct financial one. Actual bias need not be proved or disproved (options A and D), and planning committees are bound by the rules of **natural justice** (option E)."},{"t":"judicial-review-grounds","p":"FLK1","q":"A county council is under a statutory duty to consult the public before closing any library. In January 2026 it signs a binding contract to sell the building of Brookfield Library to a developer, with completion in September. In March it launches a six-week public consultation on 'the future of Brookfield Library', receives over 2,000 responses and considers them all at a committee meeting before formally resolving to close the library.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the lawfulness of the closure decision?","o":["It is lawful, because the council consulted for six weeks and conscientiously considered every response received.","It is lawful, because a duty to consult does not require the council to follow the views of those who respond.","It is unlawful, because the council must follow the view of the majority of those who responded to a consultation.","It is unlawful, because the consultation took place after the council had already effectively made its decision.","It is unlawful, because a decision to close a public library is so unreasonable that no council could make it."],"a":3,"e":"Where there is a duty to consult, the consultation must meet the **Gunning principles**, approved in R (Moseley) v Haringey LBC, the first of which is that it must take place when proposals are still at a **formative stage**; signing a binding sale contract beforehand meant the outcome was already fixed. Adequate time and conscientious consideration (option A) cannot cure that defect. Option B states a true rule but misses the flaw, there is no duty to follow the majority (option C), and closing a library is not in itself irrational (option E)."},{"t":"human-rights-act","p":"FLK1","q":"To reduce litter, a district council uses a statutory power to make a byelaw banning all distribution of free leaflets in its town centre at any time. Zainab Hussain is fined for handing out leaflets about a campaign against a local incinerator. Evidence shows that a licensing scheme requiring distributors to collect discarded leaflets nearby has reduced litter effectively in neighbouring towns. Zainab argues that the ban breaches her right to freedom of expression under Article 10.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court is likely to assess the ban?","o":["It is likely to be lawful, because preventing litter is a legitimate aim and that is enough to justify the ban.","It is likely to be unlawful, because Article 10 is an absolute right that cannot be restricted for any reason.","It is likely to be unlawful, because a less intrusive measure could have achieved the aim of reducing litter.","It is likely to be lawful, unless the ban is so unreasonable that no reasonable council could have adopted it.","It is likely to be unlawful, because a byelaw is secondary legislation and so is not 'prescribed by law'."],"a":2,"e":"Article 10 is a **qualified right**, so an interference must be prescribed by law, pursue a legitimate aim and be **proportionate**, which includes asking whether a **less intrusive measure** could have been used without compromising the objective (Bank Mellat v HM Treasury (No 2)). A legitimate aim alone (option A) is not enough, and Article 10 is not absolute (option B). The court applies proportionality rather than the lower Wednesbury standard (option D), and a byelaw made under statutory powers can satisfy the 'prescribed by law' requirement (option E)."},{"t":"human-rights-act","p":"FLK1","q":"An NHS trust disclosed details of Graham Osei's mental health treatment to his employer without his consent, breaching his right to respect for private life under Article 8. In his claim under the Human Rights Act 1998, the High Court finds the breach proved and makes a declaration to that effect. Graham asks for damages of £50,000, the sum he says he would receive in a tort claim for similar distress.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the court's approach to damages?","o":["It may award damages only if necessary to afford just satisfaction, guided by Strasbourg principles.","It must award damages, because a declaration alone is never a sufficient remedy for a breach of Article 8.","It must assess damages in the same way as in a tort claim, so £50,000 should be awarded if proved.","It may not award damages, because the only remedy under the Act is a declaration of incompatibility.","It may not award damages, because only the European Court of Human Rights can award compensation."],"a":0,"e":"Under **s.8 Human Rights Act 1998** the court may grant any just and appropriate remedy, but it may award damages only if, taking account of other relief granted, an award is **necessary to afford just satisfaction**, applying **Strasbourg principles**, which usually produce modest awards (R (Greenfield) v Secretary of State for the Home Department). Damages are therefore not automatic (option B) and are not assessed on tort scales (option C). A declaration of incompatibility (option D) concerns legislation, not remedies against public authorities, and domestic courts with civil damages powers can award compensation (option E)."},{"t":"eu-law-brexit","p":"FLK1","q":"In 2026 Brightwell Foods Ltd is prosecuted for breaching a food labelling requirement in a section of a (fictional) Act of Parliament passed in 2015. Brightwell argues that the section conflicts with an EU regulation on food labelling made in 2019, which became assimilated direct legislation (a converted EU regulation) at the end of 2020, and that the regulation must therefore prevail.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court should resolve the conflict?","o":["The regulation prevails, because it was made later than the 2015 Act and so impliedly repeals the conflicting section.","The 2015 Act prevails, because assimilated direct legislation now takes effect subject to domestic enactments.","The regulation prevails, because EU law supremacy still applies to law made before the end of 2020.","The court must refer the question of priority to the Court of Justice of the European Union for a ruling.","The 2015 Act prevails only if the court first makes an incompatibility order declaring the regulation invalid."],"a":1,"e":"From 1 January 2024 the **Retained EU Law (Revocation and Reform) Act 2023** abolished EU **supremacy** and reversed the old priority rule: **assimilated direct legislation** must be read and given effect **subject to domestic enactments, whenever made**, so the 2015 Act wins. Option C describes the position before 2024, and option A wrongly treats a converted EU regulation as able to repeal an Act of Parliament. UK courts can no longer refer such questions to the CJEU (option D), and an incompatibility order (option E) is a tool to manage the effect of assimilated law giving way, not a precondition for the Act prevailing."},{"t":"eu-law-brexit","p":"FLK1","q":"Ana Ferreira, a Portuguese national, has lived and worked in the UK since 2015 and was granted settled status under the EU Settlement Scheme. In 2026 the government makes regulations that restrict her access to a social benefit in a way that is inconsistent with the citizens' rights provisions of the Withdrawal Agreement. Ana appeals to a tribunal against the refusal of the benefit.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the tribunal's approach?","o":["It must apply the regulations, because EU law supremacy was abolished from 1 January 2024.","It must apply the regulations, because the Withdrawal Agreement is a treaty with no effect in domestic law.","It must disapply the regulations, because they breach the general principle of equal treatment in EU law.","It must give effect to her Withdrawal Agreement rights, because these prevail over inconsistent domestic law.","It must give effect to her Withdrawal Agreement rights, but only after the regulations are revoked by Parliament."],"a":3,"e":"Rights under the **Withdrawal Agreement**, including citizens' rights, are given effect by **s.7A European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018** and **prevail** over inconsistent domestic law, so the tribunal must apply them. The abolition of supremacy (option A) does not affect this exception, and option B describes the position of an unincorporated treaty such as the Trade and Cooperation Agreement, not the Withdrawal Agreement. The **general principles** of EU law (option C) were abolished from 2024, and no revocation by Parliament (option E) is needed for her rights to prevail."}],"legalsys-p3":[{"t":"courts-judiciary","p":"FLK1","q":"Marcus Bell appeared before a Recorder in the Crown Court for sentence. Misunderstanding the extent of her powers, the Recorder ordered that he be held in custody for three days while a further report was prepared. It is accepted that she acted honestly and believed she had the power to make the order. On appeal it was held that the order should not have been made. Marcus now wants to bring a claim for false imprisonment against the Recorder personally.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Marcus's position?","o":["He can sue her, because she acted beyond her powers and the order caused him a loss of liberty.","He cannot sue her, because judges are immune for acts done in good faith in their judicial capacity.","He can sue the Lord Chancellor instead, who is liable for the errors judges make in court.","He cannot sue her now, but may do so if the Judicial Appointments Commission finds misconduct.","He can sue her, but only after obtaining the permission of the Lord Chief Justice to do so."],"a":1,"e":"**Judicial immunity**: a judge cannot be sued for anything done or said in a **judicial capacity** in good faith, even if mistaken, so that judges can decide without fear (Sirros v Moore). The proper way to correct the error was the appeal Marcus has already pursued, so A is wrong. The Lord Chancellor is a minister, not liable for judges' decisions (C), and neither the Judicial Appointments Commission (which selects judges) nor the Lord Chief Justice can lift the immunity (D and E)."},{"t":"courts-judiciary","p":"FLK1","q":"Tendai Moyo is bringing a claim in the County Court against a builder for defective work on her kitchen. She has no lawyer. Her brother-in-law, Felix, a retired engineer with no legal qualification, will attend the trial with her. Felix would like to sit beside Tendai, take notes and quietly advise her during the hearing. He would also like to cross-examine the builder's witness and make the closing speech on her behalf.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Felix may do?","o":["He may do all of these things, because a litigant in person may choose anyone to speak for them.","He may do none of these things, because only an authorised person may assist a party at a hearing.","He may address the court, but only if Tendai signs a written authority appointing him as her agent.","He may cross-examine the witness, but the closing speech is reserved to an authorised advocate.","He may sit with her, take notes and advise quietly, but needs the court's permission to address it."],"a":4,"e":"Felix would act as a **McKenzie friend** (a lay helper), who may sit with a litigant in person, take notes and quietly advise, but has **no right of audience**. A right of audience (appearing as an advocate, including examining witnesses) is a **reserved legal activity** under the Legal Services Act 2007, so he may address the court only if the court exceptionally gives permission. A and C are wrong because Tendai's own right to speak for herself does not pass to anyone she chooses, B is too strict because quiet assistance is not a reserved activity, and D is wrong because cross-examining a witness is as much an exercise of a right of audience as the closing speech."},{"t":"crown-executive-prerogative","p":"FLK1","q":"The (fictional) Seed Imports Act 2025 gives 'the Secretary of State' power to revoke an import licence if satisfied that the holder has breached its conditions. Nothing in the Act requires the Secretary of State to take the decision personally. A senior civil servant in the relevant department reviewed the evidence and revoked the licence of Greenfield Agritrade Ltd, signing the decision in the Secretary of State's name. The Secretary of State never saw the file. The company argues that the revocation is invalid for this reason alone.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the company's argument?","o":["It is likely to fail, because officials may exercise a minister's powers in the minister's name.","It is likely to succeed, because a power given to a named minister must be used by that minister.","It is likely to succeed, because the power was delegated without an order approved by Parliament.","It is likely to fail, but only if the Secretary of State later ratifies the decision in writing.","It is likely to fail, because a decision by a civil servant cannot be challenged by judicial review."],"a":0,"e":"Under the **Carltona principle**, officials may exercise a minister's statutory powers **in the minister's name**, because the minister cannot take every decision personally and remains **accountable to Parliament** for them. This is not unlawful delegation, so B and C are wrong; the position would differ only if the Act required the Secretary of State to decide personally. No later ratification is needed (D), and decisions taken by officials under Carltona are as open to judicial review as the minister's own (E)."},{"t":"crown-executive-prerogative","p":"FLK1","q":"The government has signed a (fictional) treaty with another state about cooperation in marine research. The treaty requires no change to domestic law. A copy was laid before Parliament with an explanatory memorandum. Within 21 sitting days the House of Lords resolved that the treaty should not be ratified. The House of Commons made no resolution. The minister still wishes to ratify it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the treaty may be ratified?","o":["It may not be ratified unless the Lords withdraw their resolution or an Act authorises ratification.","It may not be ratified until a further 21 sitting days have passed without a Lords resolution.","It may be ratified at once, because treaty-making is a prerogative power outside Parliament's control.","It may be ratified if the minister lays a statement before Parliament explaining why it should be.","It may be ratified only after both Houses have approved it by an affirmative resolution."],"a":3,"e":"Under **Part 2 Constitutional Reform and Governance Act 2010**, a treaty laid for 21 sitting days may be ratified unless the **Commons** resolves against it; where only the **Lords** has resolved against ratification, the minister may still ratify after **laying a statement explaining why** it should nevertheless be ratified. A and B overstate the Lords' role: only a Commons resolution can delay ratification repeatedly. C ignores the statutory control over this prerogative power, and E is wrong because no positive approval by either House is required."},{"t":"precedent","p":"FLK1","q":"The Court of Appeal (Civil Division) is hearing an appeal by Bayo Adeleke in a claim for malicious prosecution. A 2005 decision of the Court of Appeal supports the respondent. In 2023 the Judicial Committee of the Privy Council, hearing an appeal from a Caribbean state with a panel of seven Supreme Court Justices, held that the approach in the 2005 decision was wrong. The panel expressly directed that courts in England and Wales should treat its decision as representing English law.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the Court of Appeal's position?","o":["It must follow its 2005 decision, because Privy Council decisions are never binding in England and Wales.","It must follow the Privy Council decision, because all Privy Council decisions bind the Court of Appeal.","It must follow the Privy Council decision, because the panel expressly directed that it represents English law.","It may follow the Privy Council decision only if one of the Young exceptions applies to the 2005 decision.","It must refer the conflict to the Supreme Court, which alone may choose between the two decisions."],"a":2,"e":"Privy Council decisions are normally only **persuasive**, but where a Privy Council panel **expressly directs** that domestic courts should treat its decision as representing English law, it has the same effect as a Supreme Court decision, so the Court of Appeal **must follow it** instead of an earlier conflicting domestic decision (Willers v Joyce). A is therefore too absolute, and B reaches the right result for the wrong reason, since without such a direction a Privy Council decision is not binding. The Young exceptions (D) are a separate route concerning the Court of Appeal's own decisions, and there is no procedure for referring a conflict to the Supreme Court (E)."},{"t":"precedent","p":"FLK1","q":"Harbourside Logistics Ltd lost a claim in the High Court about the meaning of a provision in an assimilated regulation (an EU regulation converted into domestic law at the end of 2020). The judge followed a 2016 judgment of the Court of Justice of the European Union. The company has appealed to the Court of Appeal, arguing that the 2016 judgment should no longer be followed because circumstances have changed since the UK left the EU.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the Court of Appeal's position on the 2016 judgment?","o":["It is bound by the judgment, because only the Supreme Court may depart from pre-2021 CJEU decisions.","It may depart from it only if the High Court first referred the point to it under the REUL Act.","It may depart from it by applying the REUL Act's statutory factors, such as changes of circumstances.","It must ask the CJEU for a ruling on whether the 2016 judgment still applies to the regulation.","It may depart from it, applying the test the Supreme Court uses to depart from its own decisions."],"a":4,"e":"Pre-2021 CJEU decisions are **assimilated EU case law**: they bind the High Court and below, but the **Court of Appeal** (by 2020 regulations) and the Supreme Court may depart from them, applying the **Practice Statement** test of whether it 'appears right to do so' (s.6(5) EUWA 2018). The reference procedure and statutory factors such as changes of circumstances in B and C come from **s.6 REUL Act 2023, which was never brought into force**. A forgets the 2020 regulations, and D is wrong because UK courts can no longer refer questions to the CJEU."},{"t":"legislation-structure","p":"FLK1","q":"Owen Pritchard runs a livestock market. Section 4 of the (fictional) Livestock Markets Act 2026, which is in force, provides that 'Schedule 2 has effect'. Paragraph 3 of Schedule 2 requires market operators to keep sale records for three years. The Explanatory Notes published with the Act state that records must be kept for two years. Relying on the Notes, Owen destroyed his records after two years.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Owen's legal obligation?","o":["He had to keep them for three years, as the Schedule is part of the Act and the Notes are not law.","He had to keep them for two years only, because the Notes state the government's intended meaning.","He was not bound by the requirement, because only sections in the body of an Act impose legal duties.","He had to keep them for two years, because any doubt about a regulatory duty is resolved in his favour.","He had to keep them for three years, but only once a minister makes regulations bringing Schedules into force."],"a":0,"e":"**Schedules are as much part of an Act as its sections**, so paragraph 3 of Schedule 2 imposes a binding three-year duty, which rules out C. **Explanatory Notes are not part of the Act** and have no legal force: a court may use them as an external aid to context, but they cannot override clear enacted words, so B and D are wrong. E is wrong because the Act is in force and Schedules do not need separate commencement."},{"t":"statutory-interpretation","p":"FLK1","q":"Section 2 of the (fictional) Defence Sites Act 2025 makes it an offence to 'obstruct a member of the armed forces on duty in the vicinity of a protected site'. Kai Lindqvist climbed the perimeter fence of an army barracks, which is a protected site, and obstructed a soldier on duty inside the barracks. Kai argues that 'in the vicinity of' means near the site, not inside it. The court rejects this, holding that it would be absurd for the Act to punish obstruction near the site but not within it, and reads the phrase as including 'in'.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the approach the court has taken?","o":["The literal rule, because the court gave 'in the vicinity of' its plain dictionary meaning.","The mischief rule, because the court identified a defect in the common law before the Act.","Noscitur a sociis, because the phrase was read in light of the words immediately around it.","The golden rule, because the court chose a meaning of the words that avoids an absurd result.","The presumption of mens rea, because the offence was read as requiring proof of intention."],"a":3,"e":"The **golden rule** (narrow approach) lets a court depart from the literal meaning, or choose between possible meanings, to avoid an **absurd** result (Adler v George). A is wrong because the plain meaning of 'in the vicinity of' is 'near', which is what Kai argued for. The court did not ask about the defect in the earlier common law (B) or derive meaning from neighbouring words (C), and the question of a guilty mind (E) was not in issue."},{"t":"statutory-interpretation","p":"FLK1","q":"The (fictional) Community Woodlands (Wales) Act 2026, an Act of Senedd Cymru, gives local community groups a right to buy certain 'woodland'. In a dispute in the High Court in Cardiff, it emerges that the word used in the Welsh text of the Act is wide enough to include open scrubland, while the English word 'woodland' probably is not. The landowner, Gareth Hughes, argues that the English text should prevail because the proceedings are being conducted in English.\n\nHow should the court approach the difference between the two texts?","o":["It should apply the English text, because the proceedings are being conducted in English.","It should treat both texts as equally authoritative and seek the meaning that reconciles them.","It should apply the Welsh text, because Acts of Senedd Cymru are drafted first in Welsh.","It should apply the narrower meaning, because doubts about property rights favour the owner.","It should refer the difference to the Counsel General for an authoritative translation."],"a":1,"e":"The English and Welsh texts of an Act of Senedd Cymru have **equal standing** (**s.156 Government of Wales Act 2006**): neither is a translation of the other and neither prevails. The court reads both together and looks for the meaning they share or that best **reconciles** them, in light of the Act's purpose. A and C wrongly give priority to one language, D invents a rule that decides the point automatically, and E is wrong because interpretation is for the court, not the Counsel General."},{"t":"sovereignty-rule-of-law","p":"FLK1","q":"The (fictional) Coastal Defences Act 2026 is an ordinary Act of Parliament. One of its sections, read literally, is inconsistent with a provision of the Government of Wales Act 2006 about the functions of the Welsh Ministers. The 2026 Act does not mention the 2006 Act, and nothing in it shows that Parliament considered the effect on the Welsh devolution settlement. The Welsh Ministers ask whether the 2006 provision still has effect.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the 2026 Act?","o":["The 2026 Act prevails, because a later Act always impliedly repeals an earlier inconsistent one.","The 2026 Act is invalid, because Parliament cannot legislate inconsistently with devolution.","The 2006 provision is not impliedly repealed, because constitutional statutes need clear words.","The 2006 provision prevails only if the Senedd refused consent to the 2026 Act under Sewel.","The 2006 provision can never be repealed, because the Government of Wales Act is entrenched."],"a":2,"e":"Ordinary Acts can be impliedly repealed by a later inconsistent Act, but **constitutional statutes**, such as the devolution Acts, can be repealed only by **express or unmistakably clear words** (Thoburn v Sunderland City Council). A therefore overstates implied repeal. B and E go too far: Parliament remains sovereign and could repeal the 2006 provision expressly. D is wrong because the Sewel convention is political and does not decide which statute has legal effect."},{"t":"sovereignty-rule-of-law","p":"FLK1","q":"A (fictional) Act gives the Lord Chancellor a general power to 'prescribe fees' for proceedings in a tribunal. Using it, the Lord Chancellor makes a statutory instrument setting an issue fee of £2,000 for tenants' disrepair claims. Evidence shows that claims fell by 75% afterwards and that many low-income tenants with valid claims can no longer afford to bring them. A tenants' association brings a claim for judicial review of the fees order.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome?","o":["It is likely to be unlawful, because general words do not permit fees that block access to justice.","It is likely to be lawful, because the Lord Chancellor acted within the literal words of the power.","It is likely to be lawful, but the court may declare it incompatible with Convention rights.","It is likely to be unlawful, but only if the association proves that the Lord Chancellor acted in bad faith.","It cannot be challenged, because a statutory instrument laid before Parliament is immune from review."],"a":0,"e":"**Access to the courts** is inherent in the rule of law, and under the **principle of legality** a general fee-setting power will not be read as authorising fees that effectively prevent people bringing claims (R (UNISON) v Lord Chancellor), so the order is ultra vires and can be quashed. B ignores this principle, and C confuses the position with primary legislation, which alone is limited to a declaration of incompatibility. Bad faith is not required (D), and secondary legislation is open to judicial review even if laid before Parliament (E)."},{"t":"parliament-legislation-privilege","p":"FLK1","q":"Following a report by the House of Commons Committee on Standards, the House resolves to suspend Rebecca Thornton MP from its sittings for ten sitting days for breaching the House's code of conduct. Rebecca says the committee's process was unfair because she was not allowed to call a witness. She asks whether she can bring a claim for judicial review in the Administrative Court to have the suspension quashed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes her position?","o":["She can bring the claim, because every public decision affecting a person's rights is open to review.","She can bring the claim, but only on the ground of procedural unfairness, not on the merits.","She cannot bring the claim yet, because she must first appeal to the House of Lords.","She cannot bring the claim, unless the suspension also breaches her Convention rights.","She cannot bring the claim, because each House has exclusive control over its internal affairs."],"a":4,"e":"Under the privilege of **exclusive cognisance**, each House has the **exclusive right to regulate its own internal affairs**, including the discipline of its members, free from interference by the courts (Bradlaugh v Gossett). A and B are wrong because proceedings in Parliament are not amenable to judicial review at all, even on procedural grounds. The House of Lords plays no appellate role over the Commons (C), and Parliament is excluded from the definition of public authority in s.6 HRA, so D does not provide a route either."},{"t":"parliament-legislation-privilege","p":"FLK1","q":"A whistleblower gives evidence to a House of Commons select committee alleging that Anya Kowalczyk, the director of a care company, falsified staff training records. The committee's report, which repeats the allegation, is published by order of the House. A regional newspaper publishes a fair and accurate report of the committee hearing in good faith. Anya says the allegation is untrue and wants to sue for defamation.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Anya's position?","o":["She can sue over both publications, because a whistleblower's evidence is not a proceeding in Parliament.","She cannot sue over the committee's report, and can sue the newspaper only if she proves malice.","She cannot sue over either publication, because any repetition of proceedings is absolutely privileged.","She can sue over the committee's report, but the newspaper is protected because it relied on it.","She can sue the newspaper only, because anything published to the general public loses privilege."],"a":1,"e":"Papers published **by order of either House**, such as committee reports, have **absolute privilege** under the **Parliamentary Papers Act 1840**, and evidence to a committee is itself a proceeding in Parliament, so A and D are wrong. A **fair and accurate report** of proceedings in the press has only **qualified privilege**, which is lost if the publisher acts with **malice** (an improper motive or knowledge of falsity). C overstates the newspaper's protection and E understates it."},{"t":"devolution-wales","p":"FLK1","q":"Senedd Cymru passes the (fictional) Allotments (Wales) Act 2026, which has received Royal Assent. It amends a section of the (fictional) Allotments Act 1985, an Act of the UK Parliament, so far as that section applies in relation to Wales, increasing the notice that landlords must give to end an allotment tenancy. Allotments are not a reserved matter and no restriction in the Government of Wales Act 2006 applies. A landlord in Newport argues that the amendment is not law because only the UK Parliament can amend an Act of Parliament.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the amendment?","o":["It is not law, because the Senedd cannot amend or repeal any provision of an Act of Parliament.","It is not law unless the UK Parliament passes a legislative consent motion approving the change.","It is law, and it also changes the notice for allotments in England because the Act extends there.","It is law, because within competence the Senedd may amend an Act of Parliament as it applies to Wales.","It is law only once the Secretary of State for Wales confirms by order that it is within competence."],"a":3,"e":"Within its **legislative competence**, an Act of Senedd Cymru can **amend or repeal provisions of Acts of Parliament so far as they apply in relation to Wales**, so the amendment is valid law and A is wrong. It cannot change the law as it applies in England (C), because a Senedd Act must apply only in relation to Wales (s.108A GoWA 2006). Legislative consent motions are passed by the Senedd for Westminster Bills, not the other way round (B), and no confirming order is needed once Royal Assent is given (E)."},{"t":"devolution-wales","p":"FLK1","q":"Section 3 of the (fictional) Visitor Accommodation (Wales) Act 2026, an Act of Senedd Cymru, provides: 'Every operator of short-stay accommodation must register with the Welsh Revenue Authority.' Sophie Whitfield lives in Chester and lets two holiday cottages, both in Cheshire in England. She has received a letter saying she must register under section 3.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Sophie's position?","o":["She must register, because 'every operator' is clear and must be given its plain meaning.","Section 3 is not law at all, because its wording is wide enough to reach operators in England.","Section 3 is read as applying only to accommodation in Wales, so she need not register.","She must register, because an Act of Senedd Cymru extends to the whole of England and Wales.","Section 3 is valid, but she may refuse to register until a court rules that it is within competence."],"a":2,"e":"A provision of a Senedd Act that could be read as outside competence must be **read as narrowly as is required for it to be within competence**, if possible (**s.154 Government of Wales Act 2006**). Read widely, section 3 would apply otherwise than in relation to Wales, so it is read as covering only accommodation in Wales and Sophie need not register. A ignores the duty to read down, B is wrong because reading down saves the provision, E is wrong because the narrow reading applies now without any ruling, and D confuses **extent** (England and Wales) with **application** (Wales only)."},{"t":"public-order","p":"FLK1","q":"About 40 residents plan to stand together for two hours one evening in an open town square outside the council offices, to oppose the closure of a swimming pool. The organiser, Bronwen Jones, gives no notice to the police. When the vigil begins, the inspector at the scene reasonably believes that the noise from amplified speeches may result in serious disruption to an outpatient clinic next to the square.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the legal position?","o":["Bronwen commits no offence by giving no notice, and the inspector may impose conditions on noise.","Bronwen commits an offence by not giving six clear days' notice, and the inspector may ban the vigil.","Bronwen commits no offence, but the inspector may impose conditions only on place, numbers and duration.","Bronwen commits an offence by not giving notice, but the inspector cannot impose any conditions.","Bronwen commits no offence, and the inspector may disperse the vigil because of the disruption."],"a":0,"e":"The vigil is a **public assembly** (two or more people in a public place open to the air), not a procession, so **no advance notice** is required, which rules out B and D. Under **s.14 Public Order Act 1986** the senior officer present may impose **any conditions** that appear necessary where noise may cause serious disruption to nearby activities. C states the position before the 2022 reforms, and E is wrong because the power is to impose conditions, not to disperse a lawful assembly."},{"t":"public-order","p":"FLK1","q":"The Chief Constable of Wiltshire Police receives reliable intelligence that about 200 people intend to gather, without the owner's permission, at a privately owned Iron Age hillfort over a ten-day solstice period. The public has no right of access to the hillfort, which is a scheduled ancient monument. The Chief Constable reasonably believes that the gathering may result in significant damage to the monument.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the Chief Constable's powers?","o":["He may make an order himself prohibiting all gatherings at the hillfort for the full ten days.","He may apply to the council for an order prohibiting the gathering for up to three months.","He cannot act in advance, because an assembly may be prohibited only once damage occurs.","He may impose conditions but cannot seek a prohibition, because assemblies can never be banned.","He may apply to the council for an order, needing Home Secretary consent, of up to four days."],"a":4,"e":"This is a **trespassory assembly** (20 or more people on land with no public right of access, without the occupier's permission, which may cause significant damage to a monument of historical importance). Under **ss.14A-14C Public Order Act 1986** the chief officer outside London may **apply to the council**, which may make an order with the **Home Secretary's consent** for up to **four days** within a radius of up to **five miles**. A wrongly lets the police act alone, C is wrong because the order is made in advance on a reasonable belief about what may happen, B uses the three-month period for banning processions, and D is wrong because trespassory assemblies are the exception to the rule that assemblies cannot be banned."},{"t":"judicial-review-procedure","p":"FLK1","q":"Under the (fictional) Animal Boarding Act 2024, a district council refused to renew a licence for kennels run by Leon Sutherland, finding that the premises were unsafe. The Act gives a licence holder a right of appeal to the magistrates' court within 28 days, and the court may consider the facts afresh and substitute its own decision. Leon disputes the council's findings about the premises. Instead of appealing, he files a claim for judicial review in the Administrative Court within the time limit.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the court's likely approach to permission?","o":["It will grant permission, because judicial review is always available against a council's licensing decision.","It will probably refuse permission, because Leon has an adequate alternative remedy he has not used.","It will grant permission, because judicial review allows the court to reconsider the facts afresh.","It will probably refuse permission, because licensing decisions are private law matters.","It will grant permission, but only if Leon first gives the council 14 days to reconsider its decision."],"a":1,"e":"Judicial review is a **remedy of last resort**: where an **adequate alternative remedy** exists, such as a statutory appeal, the claimant should normally use it, and the court may refuse permission if he has not. The statutory appeal here suits Leon's case well, because the magistrates' court can rehear the facts, whereas judicial review is **supervisory** and does not reconsider the merits, which is why C is wrong. A council's licensing decision is a public law matter (D), but that does not make judicial review 'always' available (A), and the 14-day period in E is the time for replying to a letter before claim."},{"t":"judicial-review-procedure","p":"FLK1","q":"Mr Abdullahi brought a claim for judicial review of a Home Office decision. A High Court judge considered the papers and refused permission. The order was served on Mr Abdullahi's solicitors two days later. The judge did not record the claim as totally without merit. Mr Abdullahi wishes to continue the challenge.\n\nWhich of the following best describes his next step?","o":["He may appeal directly to the Court of Appeal, which must hear the appeal within 7 days.","He has no further remedy, because a refusal of permission on the papers is final.","He may issue a fresh claim, as long as he stays within three months of the original decision.","He may ask for the refusal to be reconsidered at an oral hearing, applying within 7 days of service.","He may ask for the refusal to be reconsidered at an oral hearing, applying within 21 days of the order."],"a":3,"e":"Where permission is refused **on the papers**, the claimant may request that the decision be **reconsidered at an oral hearing**, filing the request within **7 days of service** of the refusal (**CPR 54.12**). That route is closed only where the judge records the claim as **totally without merit**, in which case the claimant may seek permission to appeal from the Court of Appeal, so A and B are wrong here. C would be an abuse of process, and 21 days (E) is the time for filing an acknowledgment of service."},{"t":"judicial-review-grounds","p":"FLK1","q":"Five years ago, a county council moved eight adults with severe learning disabilities into a small residential home, Maple House. To persuade them and their families, the council wrote to each resident promising that Maple House would be their home for as long as they needed it. The council has now decided to close Maple House to save money, without considering the promise or identifying any reason beyond general budget pressure. The residents' families challenge the decision.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome?","o":["The closure is lawful, because a public body cannot bind itself by promises about its future discretion.","The closure is lawful, because the promise gave the residents only a right to be consulted first.","The closure is likely to be unlawful, unless an overriding public interest justifies breaking the promise.","The closure is likely to be unlawful, because any promise by a public body is enforceable as a contract.","The closure is lawful, because breaking the promise gives the residents a right only to compensation."],"a":2,"e":"A clear, specific promise of a **particular benefit** to a small group can create a **substantive legitimate expectation**; frustrating it is unlawful as an **abuse of power** unless an **overriding public interest** justifies the change (R v North and East Devon Health Authority ex p Coughlan). A overstates the rule against fettering, which does not prevent a body being held to a specific promise of this kind. B describes a procedural expectation, but this promise was of an outcome, and D and E wrongly treat the promise as a matter of contract or damages rather than public law."},{"t":"judicial-review-grounds","p":"FLK1","q":"A district council has a statutory power to revoke street trading licences 'where necessary to secure the proper management of street trading in the area'. The council increased its annual licence fee from 1 April. A group of traders, including Bilal Chaudhry, renewed their licences in March at the old fee. None has broken any licence condition. The council writes to them saying their licences will be revoked unless they pay the difference between the old and new fees.\n\nOn which ground is a judicial review challenge to the threatened revocations most likely to succeed?","o":["Irrationality, because charging the higher fee to some traders but not to others is illogical.","Procedural impropriety, because the traders were not consulted before the fee was increased.","Fettering of discretion, because the council applied its new fee policy without any exceptions.","Legitimate expectation, because renewing a licence is a promise that it will never be revoked.","Illegality, because the revocation power is being used for a purpose other than its intended one."],"a":4,"e":"A power given for one purpose must not be used for another: using a power to revoke licences, given to secure proper management of street trading, as a way of **raising money** is an **improper purpose**, a form of **illegality** (Congreve v Home Office). Irrationality (A) sets a very high threshold and is not the natural ground on these facts. The traders' complaint is not about consultation on the fee (B), there is no rigid policy refusing exceptions (C), and a renewal is not a promise never to revoke (D)."},{"t":"human-rights-act","p":"FLK1","q":"Oakfield Lodge is a care home run by a private company. Bernadette Clarke lives there in a place arranged and paid for by the county council under its duties to meet her care needs. Henryk Nowak lives there in a place he arranged and pays for himself. After complaints from staff, the company serves notices requiring both residents to leave within 28 days without giving either a chance to respond. Both want to argue that the company has breached their right to respect for their home under Article 8.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether they can rely on the Human Rights Act 1998 against the company?","o":["Bernadette can, because her care was arranged by the council, but Henryk cannot.","Both can, because a care home performs public functions for all of its residents.","Neither can, because a private company can never be a public authority under the Act.","Henryk can, but Bernadette must bring her claim against the council instead.","Both can, but only if the home receives most of its income from public funds."],"a":0,"e":"A private body is a **hybrid public authority** under **s.6(3)(b) HRA** only when exercising **functions of a public nature**. **s.73 Care Act 2014** provides that a registered care provider is exercising a public function when providing care **arranged or paid for by a local authority**, so the company is bound by s.6 towards Bernadette (reversing YL v Birmingham City Council). Henryk's privately arranged and funded care is not covered, so B and E are wrong and D reverses the position; C ignores the hybrid category altogether."},{"t":"human-rights-act","p":"FLK1","q":"Section 6 of the (fictional) Tied Cottages Act 1990 provides that when a farmworker who occupies a tied cottage dies, 'his or her surviving husband or wife' may remain in the cottage for life. Dafydd Price lived in a tied cottage with his civil partner, a farmworker, who has died. The farm owner brings a possession claim in the County Court, arguing that Dafydd is not a 'husband or wife'. Dafydd argues that excluding civil partners would breach Article 14 (discrimination) read with Article 8, and the court accepts that it would.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the County Court should approach section 6?","o":["It must give the words their ordinary meaning and make a declaration of incompatibility.","It must disapply section 6, because Convention rights prevail over Acts passed before 1998.","It may read the words compatibly only if they are ambiguous on their ordinary meaning.","It must read the words compatibly so far as possible, even beyond their ordinary meaning.","It must apply section 6 as written, because s.3 does not apply to Acts passed before 1998."],"a":3,"e":"Under **s.3(1) HRA**, legislation **whenever passed** must be read and given effect compatibly with Convention rights **so far as it is possible to do so**, which can mean reading words in or down even where the words are not ambiguous, provided this does not contradict a fundamental feature of the Act (Ghaidan v Godin-Mendoza). C and E therefore understate s.3. A is wrong because a declaration is a last resort, and only higher courts (not the County Court) can make one; B is wrong because courts cannot disapply an Act of Parliament."},{"t":"eu-law-brexit","p":"FLK1","q":"Joanna Mensah has worked for a private logistics company since March 2024. In 2026 she discovers that a male colleague doing equal work is paid more than she is. Her representative proposes to bring her claim in the employment tribunal by relying only on Article 157 of the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union (the right to equal pay for equal work), arguing that it continues to have direct effect in the UK.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the proposed argument?","o":["It will succeed, because directly effective Treaty rights were saved as retained EU law at the end of 2020.","It will fail, because such rights ended after 2023, so she should rely on the Equality Act 2010.","It will succeed only if the tribunal refers the meaning of Article 157 to the CJEU for a ruling.","It will succeed if she shows that the Equality Act 2010 gives her less protection than Article 157.","It will fail, because all EU Treaty rights ceased to have any effect in the UK on exit day in January 2020."],"a":1,"e":"Directly effective Treaty rights such as Article 157 were saved by **s.4 EUWA 2018** at the end of 2020, but **s.2 REUL Act 2023** ended their recognition from **1 January 2024**, so Joanna's claim, which concerns pay from 2024 onwards, must rely on **domestic law**, here the **Equality Act 2010**. A describes the position only up to the end of 2023, and D wrongly assumes EU law can still top up domestic law. E is wrong on the date, because EU law continued to apply during the transition period until 31 December 2020, and references to the CJEU (C) are no longer possible."}],"ls-p1":[{"t":"sra-regulatory-role","p":"FLK1","q":"Priya runs 'Final Wishes Direct', an online business that is not authorised by any legal regulator and employs no lawyers. For a fee, it offers to: draft wills; advise families on how to divide a deceased relative's personal belongings; prepare and submit the application papers for a grant of probate; help executors close bank accounts after the grant has been issued; and write letters to beneficiaries explaining an estate's progress.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the service that the business cannot lawfully offer?","o":["Drafting wills for customers in return for a fee.","Advising families on dividing personal belongings.","Preparing the probate application papers for a fee.","Closing bank accounts after the grant has been issued.","Writing letters to beneficiaries about estate progress."],"a":2,"e":"Preparing the papers on which a grant of probate is applied for is a **reserved probate activity** under the Legal Services Act 2007 when done **for a fee**, so only an authorised or exempt person may do it. **Will writing is not a reserved activity**, so drafting wills for a fee is lawful even for an unregulated business. General advice and estate administration after the grant are also not reserved, so the remaining services can be offered by anyone."},{"t":"sra-regulatory-role","p":"FLK1","q":"Following an investigation, the Solicitors Regulation Authority (SRA) concludes that Gareth, a solicitor at a traditional partnership (not an alternative business structure), deliberately backdated a document in a client's matter and lied to a colleague about it. The matter does not involve economic crime. The SRA considers that Gareth's dishonesty is so serious that he should no longer be allowed to practise.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the SRA should proceed?","o":["Bring proceedings before the Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal seeking strike-off.","Strike Gareth off the roll itself, as the regulator of individual solicitors.","Impose an unlimited fine on Gareth, as the misconduct involves dishonesty.","Refer the case to the Legal Ombudsman, which deals with serious misconduct.","Ask the Legal Services Board to strike Gareth off as the oversight regulator."],"a":0,"e":"**Only the Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal (SDT) (or the High Court) can strike off or suspend a solicitor**, so the SRA must prosecute the case before the SDT. The SRA's own fining power over individuals at traditional firms is capped at **£25,000**; the unlimited SRA fine applies only to **economic crime** conduct, which is not involved here. The Legal Ombudsman deals with poor service complaints, not misconduct, and the Legal Services Board oversees regulators rather than disciplining individual solicitors."},{"t":"pii-other-providers","p":"FLK1","q":"Okafor & Reid is a general partnership of solicitors with professional indemnity insurance at the minimum level required by the SRA. A partner negligently misses a limitation date and the firm's client, Ms Lindqvist, suffers a loss of £2.4 million. When the claim is notified, the insurer discovers that the firm failed to disclose a previous claim on its proposal form and wants to refuse to pay anything.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The insurer may avoid the policy entirely because of the firm's failure to disclose.","The insurer must pay Ms Lindqvist £3 million, as this is the minimum level of cover.","The insurer must pay the full £2.4 million and cannot seek repayment from the firm.","Ms Lindqvist must apply to the SRA Compensation Fund, as the policy has been avoided.","The insurer must pay up to £2 million and may then seek to recover it from the firm."],"a":4,"e":"Under the **Minimum Terms and Conditions**, an insurer **cannot avoid or refuse to pay the claimant because of non-disclosure or misrepresentation**; it must pay and may then seek to recover the money from the firm. The minimum cover is **£2 million per claim for a partnership** (£3 million applies only to firms with limited liability, such as LLPs and companies), so Ms Lindqvist must pursue the partners for the remaining £400,000. The Compensation Fund is a discretionary fund of last resort mainly for dishonesty or failure to account, not a substitute for negligence cover."},{"t":"pii-other-providers","p":"FLK1","q":"Hollins Solicitors' professional indemnity insurance policy expired on 1 October. The firm has been unable to find a participating insurer willing to renew its cover. It is now 15 November and the firm still has no new policy. A prospective client, Mr Adeyemi, asks the firm to take on a new boundary dispute.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's position?","o":["The firm is in the extended policy period and may accept Mr Adeyemi's instructions.","The firm is in the cessation period and must not take on Mr Adeyemi's new matter.","The firm's run-off cover has started and it may accept new work for six years.","The firm has no cover at all and must close immediately and stop all existing work.","The firm may accept new work if it warns Mr Adeyemi that it is currently uninsured."],"a":1,"e":"If a firm fails to renew, the old policy continues for an **extended policy period of 30 days**, followed by a **cessation period of a further 60 days**, during which the firm **must not take on new work**. By 15 November, 45 days have passed, so the firm is in the cessation period and cannot accept the new matter, but it is not yet required to close. Run-off cover applies when a firm closes without a successor practice; it does not allow new work, and warning the client does not override the rules."},{"t":"equality-act","p":"FLK1","q":"Brightwater Law LLP introduces a new rule that all new private clients must give instructions and upload their documents through an online portal; the firm will no longer accept instructions by telephone, post or in person. Mr Kowalczyk, aged 81, has no internet access and cannot use the portal. Evidence shows that clients over 75 are far less likely than younger clients to be able to use online services. The firm says the rule cuts its administrative costs.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's position under the Equality Act 2010?","o":["The rule is direct age discrimination, which can never be justified in the provision of services.","The rule is lawful because it applies equally to every client, whatever their age.","The rule is lawful because age is not a protected characteristic in the provision of services.","The rule is indirect age discrimination unless the firm can objectively justify it.","The rule is lawful provided the firm offers Mr Kowalczyk a reasonable adjustment at his own cost."],"a":3,"e":"A neutral rule applied to everyone that puts people sharing a protected characteristic at a particular disadvantage is a **provision, criterion or practice** and amounts to **indirect discrimination unless it is objectively justified**, meaning a proportionate means of achieving a legitimate aim. Age is protected in services for people **aged 18 or over**, and cost saving alone is unlikely to make a rule with no alternatives proportionate. It is not direct discrimination because the rule does not treat anyone less favourably because of age, and in any event direct age discrimination can be justified; reasonable adjustments relate to disability and can never be charged to the client."},{"t":"equality-act","p":"FLK1","q":"Tomasz, a paralegal at a law firm, gave honest evidence supporting a colleague's race discrimination claim against the firm in the employment tribunal. The colleague's claim failed. Two months later, Tomasz's supervising partner, who was annoyed about his evidence, removed him from a high-profile matter and refused his request to attend a training course that other paralegals were allowed to attend.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the partner's treatment of Tomasz?","o":["It is victimisation, because Tomasz was subjected to a detriment for doing a protected act.","It is not unlawful, because the colleague's discrimination claim was ultimately dismissed.","It is direct race discrimination, because the treatment arose from a race discrimination claim.","It is harassment, because the treatment created a hostile working environment for Tomasz.","It is not unlawful, because Tomasz does not himself share the colleague's protected characteristic."],"a":0,"e":"**Victimisation** (s.27) is subjecting a person to a **detriment because they have done a protected act**, which includes **giving evidence in proceedings under the Act**. Protection does not depend on the original claim succeeding; only a false allegation made in bad faith loses protection, and Tomasz gave honest evidence. Victimisation does not require Tomasz to share any protected characteristic, and the treatment was not because of his race, nor was it unwanted conduct related to a protected characteristic, so direct discrimination and harassment do not fit."},{"t":"aml-framework-cdd","p":"FLK1","q":"A firm is instructed to act for Marlowe Developments Ltd, a private company, in buying a commercial site for £1.8 million. The company's shares are held as follows: Hana owns 40%, Dev owns 30%, Lucia owns 20% and Femi owns 10%. Each share carries one vote and no shareholder has any other right to control the company.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whose identity the firm must identify and take reasonable measures to verify as beneficial owners under the Money Laundering Regulations 2017?","o":["Hana only, as she is the single largest shareholder in the company.","Hana, Dev, Lucia and Femi, as every shareholder must be verified.","Hana and Dev, as each owns more than 25% of the company's shares.","Hana, Dev and Lucia, as each owns at least 20% of the company's shares.","None of them, as verifying the company itself at Companies House is enough."],"a":2,"e":"For a company, a **beneficial owner is any individual who owns or controls more than 25% of the shares or voting rights**, or otherwise exercises control, so Hana (40%) and Dev (30%) must be identified and verified. Lucia and Femi fall below the threshold and have no other control rights. Verifying the company alone is not enough, because the firm must also **identify the beneficial owner and understand the ownership and control structure**, and a register check cannot be relied on by itself."},{"t":"aml-framework-cdd","p":"FLK1","q":"Rhian stood down as a Member of the UK Parliament six months ago. She now asks a firm to act on her purchase of a house in Cardiff for £950,000, funded partly by the sale of shares and partly by savings. The firm has identified no other risk factors and has verified her identity.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the due diligence the firm must carry out?","o":["Simplified due diligence, as a domestic politically exposed person is treated as low risk.","Standard due diligence only, as Rhian is no longer a Member of Parliament.","Standard due diligence only, as only foreign public officials are politically exposed persons.","No further checks, as her identity has already been verified using reliable documents.","Enhanced due diligence, though it may be less extensive than for a foreign official."],"a":4,"e":"A person must continue to be treated as a **politically exposed person (PEP) for at least 12 months after leaving the public function**, so Rhian is still a PEP six months after leaving Parliament. **Enhanced due diligence** therefore applies: senior management approval, establishing **source of wealth and source of funds**, and enhanced ongoing monitoring. Since 2024 a **domestic PEP has a lower starting level of risk** than a foreign PEP, which affects how extensive the measures are, not whether they apply, so simplified or standard due diligence is wrong."},{"t":"aml-framework-cdd","p":"FLK1","q":"Castell & Moore is acting for a new client on the purchase of a buy-to-let flat. The client's bank, a regulated institution, has already verified her identity and agrees that the firm may rely on its customer due diligence. The trainee on the file suggests the firm need not hold anything on its own file and that any failure in the bank's checks would be the bank's responsibility.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's position if it relies on the bank?","o":["The firm may rely on the bank, and liability for any failure in the checks passes to the bank.","The firm remains liable and must obtain the due diligence information from the bank immediately.","The firm may not rely on the bank, as reliance is only permitted between two law firms.","The firm may rely on the bank only if it repeats the identity checks itself within two working days.","The firm remains liable but need not obtain any information unless the bank later becomes insolvent."],"a":1,"e":"Under **reg 39**, a firm may rely on another regulated person who agrees to carry out customer due diligence, but **the relying firm remains liable for any failure**. It must **immediately obtain the due diligence information** and have arrangements to obtain copies of the identification and verification documents **within 2 working days of a request**. Reliance is not limited to law firms, and it does not require the firm to repeat the checks itself."},{"t":"poca-offences-reporting","p":"FLK1","q":"Ewan, a conveyancing solicitor, suspects that part of his client's purchase money comes from tax evasion. He reports his suspicion to his firm's money laundering reporting officer, who submits a suspicious activity report to the National Crime Agency (NCA) on Monday 2 March, asking for a defence against money laundering. No bank holidays fall in the following weeks, and the NCA does not respond.\n\nWhich of the following best describes when the firm may proceed with the purchase?","o":["Immediately, as an authorised disclosure has been made before the transaction is completed.","From Tuesday 10 March, at the end of seven calendar days from the disclosure.","From Friday 3 April, once a 31-day moratorium period has expired after the disclosure.","From Thursday 12 March, as consent is deemed once the seven working day notice period ends.","Only when the NCA gives express written consent, however long the NCA takes to respond."],"a":3,"e":"The **notice period is 7 working days, starting on the first working day after the disclosure**; if the NCA does not refuse consent within it, **consent is deemed** and the firm can proceed. Counting from Tuesday 3 March, the seventh working day is Wednesday 11 March, so the firm may proceed from Thursday 12 March. The **31-day moratorium** only runs if the NCA **refuses** consent, and express consent is not required once the notice period expires."},{"t":"poca-offences-reporting","p":"FLK1","q":"Ngozi, a solicitor in the regulated sector, is acting on a client's purchase of a restaurant business. The firm's money laundering reporting officer has made a suspicious activity report about the source of the client's funds and is waiting for a response from the National Crime Agency. The client telephones Ngozi, angry that exchange has not happened, and demands to know what is causing the hold-up.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Ngozi should respond?","o":["Explain that a routine report has been made to the authorities, as all firms do this.","Tell the client that the seller's solicitors are causing the delay with their enquiries.","Explain that a report has been made but reassure the client that nothing will come of it.","Tell the client that the firm can no longer act because it suspects money laundering.","Tell the client that there is a delay on the firm's side, without giving the reason for it."],"a":4,"e":"**Tipping off** (s.333A) is disclosing that a **suspicious activity report has been made** (or that an investigation is under way) where this is likely to prejudice an investigation, so any answer revealing the report or the suspicion is wrong, however reassuringly it is put. Simply telling the client there is a **delay, without explaining why**, is not tipping off. Ngozi must not lie to the client either, so blaming the seller's solicitors is wrong."},{"t":"poca-offences-reporting","p":"FLK1","q":"Isabel, a litigation solicitor, acts for a claimant in a genuine dispute with her former business partner over the proceeds of sale of a jointly owned warehouse. During disclosure, Isabel learns that the former partner probably paid for repairs to the warehouse in cash to evade VAT. Isabel wishes to negotiate a settlement under which her client receives an agreed share of the proceeds.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Isabel would commit the arrangements offence under section 328 of the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002?","o":["No, because taking ordinary steps in genuine litigation, including settling, is not an arrangement.","Yes, because any settlement involving property tainted by tax evasion is an arrangement under section 328.","No, because section 328 applies only to people working in the regulated sector, which excludes litigation.","Yes, unless she obtains a defence against money laundering from the National Crime Agency before settling.","No, because VAT evasion is a civil tax matter that cannot amount to criminal conduct under the Act."],"a":0,"e":"Section 328 was **not intended to cover the ordinary conduct of legal proceedings or a consensual settlement of them** (Bowman v Fels), so Isabel does not commit the offence by negotiating a genuine settlement; only **sham litigation** set up to launder money is caught. The principal offences in ss.327-329 apply to **everyone**, not just the regulated sector. Tax evasion is **criminal conduct**, so the property may well be criminal property, but that does not make genuine litigation an arrangement or require a defence from the NCA."},{"t":"financial-services","p":"FLK1","q":"Siobhan and her brother Callum, both individuals, own 65% and 35% respectively of the shares in Tarn Engineering Ltd. A buyer company wants to acquire the whole company. Their solicitor advises them on whether the price offered for their shares is acceptable, negotiates with the buyer and prepares the share purchase agreement. The firm is not authorised by the Financial Conduct Authority.\n\nWhich of the following best describes why the firm does not need to be authorised for this work?","o":["Shares in a private company are not a specified investment under the Regulated Activities Order.","The work is a necessary part of legal services and is not separately remunerated.","The firm is introducing the sellers to an authorised person for independent advice.","The sale of a body corporate exclusion applies, as 50% or more of the voting shares are sold.","The firm is acting as an execution-only agent and is giving no advice on the investment."],"a":3,"e":"Shares in any company, including a private company, **are specified investments**, and advising on the merits of selling them and arranging the sale are specified activities. However, the **sale of a body corporate exclusion (art 70)** applies to dealing, arranging and advising on the sale of shares carrying **50% or more of the voting rights** where the parties are individuals or bodies corporate, so the activity is not regulated at all. The **art 67** exclusion is narrower: it covers only investment activity that is a **necessary part** of other professional services, and advising on whether the price is acceptable goes beyond that, whereas art 70 applies however the work is charged. The firm is advising on the price, so it is not execution-only, and it is not merely introducing the clients to an authorised person."},{"t":"financial-services","p":"FLK1","q":"Akosua instructs a firm in a contested inheritance dispute. She cannot pay the firm's final bill of £9,000 in one go. The firm agrees in writing that she may pay it in 18 equal monthly instalments, with no interest or other charges. The firm has no authorisation from the Financial Conduct Authority for consumer credit.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the instalment arrangement?","o":["It is an exempt agreement, because the firm charges no interest or other fees.","It is an exempt agreement, because a solicitor's fees are not a form of credit.","It is not exempt, because the credit is repayable in more than 12 instalments.","It is not exempt, because any deferred payment of fees requires authorisation.","It is an exempt agreement, because the total sum owed is less than £10,000."],"a":2,"e":"Allowing a client to pay fees by instalments can amount to providing **credit**, a regulated activity. It is **exempt only if repayable in 12 or fewer instalments within 12 months, with no interest or other charges**, so 18 monthly instalments fall outside the exemption even though no interest is charged. Being interest-free is necessary but not sufficient, and there is no exemption based on the amount owed or for solicitors' fees as such."},{"t":"funding-private-cfa-dba","p":"FLK1","q":"Malik succeeds in a personal injury claim brought under a conditional fee agreement with a 50% success fee. He is awarded £18,000 for pain, suffering and loss of amenity, £6,000 for past loss of earnings and £40,000 for future care costs. No state benefits are recoverable. His solicitor's base costs are £16,000, which the defendant is ordered to pay in full.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the amount Malik must pay his solicitor out of his damages?","o":["£8,000, being the full success fee of 50% of the base costs.","£6,000, being the success fee as capped by reference to his damages.","£16,000, being the base costs, as the defendant pays the success fee.","£16,000, being 25% of all the damages awarded to him in the claim.","Nothing, as the success fee is recoverable from the defendant in full."],"a":1,"e":"The success fee is 50% of £16,000, which is £8,000, but in personal injury it is **capped at 25% of damages for pain, suffering and loss of amenity and past pecuniary loss**: 25% of £24,000 is £6,000. **Future loss is excluded** from the calculation, so the £40,000 for future care is ignored. Since April 2013 the **success fee is not recoverable from the losing opponent**, so Malik pays it, while the defendant pays the base costs."},{"t":"funding-private-cfa-dba","p":"FLK1","q":"Esi wants to bring an unfair dismissal claim in the employment tribunal but cannot afford to pay a solicitor by the hour. Her solicitor proposes a written damages-based agreement under which the firm will receive 45% of any compensation Esi recovers, including VAT, and nothing if she loses.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the proposed agreement?","o":["It is enforceable, as the maximum payment under a damages-based agreement is 50% of sums recovered.","It is unenforceable, as the payment exceeds the 35% cap for employment tribunal matters.","It is enforceable, as caps on damages-based agreements apply only to personal injury claims.","It is unenforceable, as damages-based agreements cannot be used in employment tribunal matters.","It is enforceable, provided the firm's success fee does not exceed 100% of its base costs."],"a":1,"e":"The **maximum payment under a damages-based agreement in employment tribunal matters is 35%** of sums recovered (including VAT), so a 45% agreement does not comply with the Regulations and is **unenforceable**. The **50%** cap applies to other civil claims, and the 25% cap applies to personal injury. DBAs are permitted in employment matters, and the 100% cap on a success fee relates to conditional fee agreements, which are calculated on costs rather than damages."},{"t":"funding-legal-aid-tpf-lei","p":"FLK1","q":"Ruth, who has evidence that her former partner was violent towards her, receives civil legal aid for financial remedy proceedings. At the end of the case the court orders that she keeps the former family home, worth £280,000, and the other side pays none of her costs. The Legal Aid Agency has paid £22,000 for her legal representation.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of legal aid on Ruth's position?","o":["Ruth keeps the home free of any liability, as legal aid is a grant that never has to be repaid.","Ruth must repay the £22,000 immediately in cash, as she is no longer eligible for legal aid.","Ruth has no liability, as the costs protection rules prevent any recovery from a legally aided party.","The Legal Aid Agency can recover the £22,000 only by suing Ruth's former partner for the costs.","The Legal Aid Agency has a charge over the home for the £22,000, as she preserved it in the proceedings."],"a":4,"e":"Under the **statutory charge** (LASPO s.25), where a legally aided client **recovers or preserves money or property** in the proceedings, the Legal Aid Agency has a **first charge** on it for the legal aid costs not recovered from the opponent. Ruth preserved the home, so the charge attaches to it for £22,000, although the charge on a home can sometimes be postponed rather than paid immediately. **Costs protection** (s.26) protects a legally aided party against paying the **opponent's** costs, not against the statutory charge."},{"t":"funding-legal-aid-tpf-lei","p":"FLK1","q":"Dariusz was injured when his car was hit by a delivery van. He receives Universal Credit, has no savings and no legal expenses insurance, and his claim for about £15,000 has good prospects of success. He asks his solicitor whether he can get civil legal aid.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the solicitor's advice?","o":["He is eligible, as receiving Universal Credit passports him through the whole means test.","He is eligible, as his claim has more than moderate prospects and he has no savings at all.","He is eligible for Legal Help only, which covers advice but not representation in court.","He is not eligible, as road traffic injury claims are generally outside legal aid scope.","He is not eligible, as people receiving Universal Credit cannot also receive civil legal aid."],"a":3,"e":"**Scope is the first hurdle**: most **personal injury claims, including road traffic accident claims, are outside the scope** of civil legal aid under LASPO Schedule 1, so legal aid is unavailable however strong the case or limited his means. Universal Credit **passports a client through the income test only** (capital is still assessed), but that is irrelevant if the case is out of scope, and Legal Help is equally unavailable for out-of-scope matters. A conditional fee agreement with after-the-event insurance is the realistic alternative."},{"t":"transparency-client-care","p":"FLK1","q":"Pemberton Solicitors, an SRA-authorised firm, does only two types of work: claimant personal injury claims and commercial property transactions. Its website explains its services but publishes no prices. It also does not publish its complaints procedure, which it sends to clients only when they complain.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's position under the SRA Transparency Rules?","o":["It need not publish prices for these services but must publish its complaints procedure.","It must publish prices for both services and must publish its complaints procedure.","It must publish prices for personal injury work but need not publish its complaints procedure.","It need not publish prices or its complaints procedure, as neither service is a listed service.","It must publish prices for commercial property work but need not publish its complaints procedure."],"a":0,"e":"The duty to publish **price information applies only to listed services**, such as residential conveyancing, uncontested probate and certain employment and immigration work; **personal injury and commercial property are not listed**, so no prices need be published. However, **every SRA-authorised firm must publish its complaints procedure**, including how and when a complaint can be made to the Legal Ombudsman and the SRA. Sending it only to clients who complain is also inconsistent with the duty to tell clients about complaints in writing at the start of the matter."},{"t":"transparency-client-care","p":"FLK1","q":"A solicitor visits Mrs Bello, a consumer, at her home and agrees to act for her in a dispute with a builder. The solicitor gives her written pre-contract information, including notice of her right to cancel within 14 days, but does not ask whether she wants work to start immediately. The solicitor begins work the next day anyway. Ten days later, Mrs Bello cancels the contract.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Mrs Bello's liability for the work done before she cancelled?","o":["She must pay for the work done, because she received written notice of her cancellation right.","She cannot cancel, because the solicitor had already started work on her matter.","She need not pay for the work done, because she did not expressly ask for it to begin early.","She must pay half the work done, because she cancelled after the midpoint of the period.","She cannot cancel, because the right to cancel applies only to contracts made at a distance."],"a":2,"e":"Under the **Consumer Contracts Regulations 2013**, a contract made **off-premises** (such as at the client's home) gives a consumer a **right to cancel within 14 days**. A consumer must pay for work done before cancellation **only if they expressly requested** that it begin within the cancellation period; Mrs Bello made no such request, so she can cancel and pay nothing. Starting work does not remove the right to cancel, and the right applies to off-premises as well as distance contracts."}],"ls-p2":[{"t":"sra-regulatory-role","p":"FLK1","q":"Farah instructed Pennine Law LLP on her house sale. In November 2025 she discovered that the firm had repeatedly failed to return her calls, which delayed completion. She complained to the firm, which sent its final response on 10 February 2026. The response explained her right to complain to the Legal Ombudsman and the time limits for doing so. Farah does nothing further until 21 September 2026, when she contacts the Legal Ombudsman.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the Legal Ombudsman is likely to accept Farah's complaint?","o":["It will accept, as she has complained within one year of discovering the problem.","It will normally decline, as she has missed the six-month limit after the final response.","It will accept, as no time limit applies to complaints about delay and poor communication.","It cannot consider it, as complaints about poor service must go to the SRA instead.","It will decline, as she had to contact it within eight weeks of the final response."],"a":1,"e":"A complainant must normally go to the Legal Ombudsman (LeO) **within 6 months of the firm's final response** (where that response explained the time limit) **and** within 1 year of the problem or of when they should have known of it. Both limits must be met, so being inside the one-year limit does not save Farah. Eight weeks is the time the firm has to resolve the complaint before LeO will usually step in, not a deadline for the client, and poor service is for LeO, not the SRA (which deals with conduct)."},{"t":"sra-regulatory-role","p":"FLK1","q":"Ashworth & Daley, a traditional partnership authorised by the SRA, is found to have failed for several years to carry out customer due diligence on its conveyancing clients, in breach of the Money Laundering Regulations 2017. There is no evidence of dishonesty. The SRA decides that a financial penalty is appropriate and does not intend to refer the firm to the Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the SRA's power to fine the firm?","o":["It may fine the firm up to £25,000, the maximum for any traditional firm.","It cannot fine the firm, as only the Tribunal may impose financial penalties.","It may impose an unlimited fine, as the breach relates to economic crime.","It may fine the firm up to £250 million, as it may for any authorised firm.","It cannot fine the firm, as breaches of the Regulations are for the police."],"a":2,"e":"The SRA's own fining power for solicitors and traditional (non-ABS) firms is normally capped at **£25,000**, but since 2024 the Economic Crime and Corporate Transparency Act 2023 has allowed it to impose an **unlimited fine for conduct relating to economic crime**, which includes breaches of the money laundering rules. The £250 million limit applies to alternative business structures (ABSs), not traditional firms. The SRA can fine without going to the Tribunal: it is striking off and suspension that only the Tribunal can order."},{"t":"pii-other-providers","p":"FLK1","q":"Alder Grove LLP negligently drafted a commercial lease for a client in 2023, when its professional indemnity insurance was with Northgate Insurance. From October 2025 the firm moved its cover to Westbury Insurance, also a participating insurer, on the minimum terms. In February 2026 the client first makes a claim against the firm for £2.8 million.\n\nWhich of the following best describes which insurer must deal with the claim?","o":["Westbury, up to £3 million, as its policy was in force when the claim was first made.","Northgate, up to £3 million, as it insured the firm when the negligent drafting was done.","Westbury, but only up to £2 million, as that is the minimum level of cover required.","Northgate and Westbury, each paying half, as both insured the firm at relevant times.","Neither insurer, as the client must apply to the SRA Compensation Fund instead."],"a":0,"e":"Cover under the Minimum Terms and Conditions operates on a **claims-made basis**: the policy in force **when the claim is first made** responds, not the one in force when the mistake happened. As the firm is an LLP, the minimum cover is **£3 million** per claim (£2 million applies to sole practitioners and partnerships), so the whole £2.8 million is within cover. The Compensation Fund is a last resort for dishonesty and failure to account, not for negligence."},{"t":"pii-other-providers","p":"FLK1","q":"Brightwater Logistics Ltd, a company with an annual turnover of about £40 million, paid £350,000 to its solicitors to fund a property purchase. A partner at the firm stole the money and has since been made bankrupt. Brightwater's finance director asks whether the company can obtain a grant from the SRA Compensation Fund.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Brightwater's position in relation to the Compensation Fund?","o":["It is entitled to a grant, as the fund pays out as of right where client money is stolen.","It is eligible, as the fund covers any loss caused by a solicitor's dishonesty.","It is not eligible, as the fund covers only losses caused by a solicitor's negligence.","It is not normally eligible, as the fund is meant for individuals and smaller bodies.","It is eligible, but only after it obtains judgment against the partner personally."],"a":3,"e":"The SRA Compensation Fund is a **discretionary fund of last resort** for loss caused by dishonesty or failure to account, but it is aimed at **individuals and smaller businesses, charities and trusts** (broadly those with turnover, income or assets below £2 million). A company turning over £40 million is expected to protect itself, so it would not normally qualify and must pursue other remedies, such as a claim against the firm. No applicant has a right to a grant, and the fund covers dishonesty, not negligence."},{"t":"equality-act","p":"FLK1","q":"Hollins & Shah LLP has very few trainees from Black and minority ethnic backgrounds compared with the local population. The two final candidates for its last training contract, Grace and Joseph, performed equally well at every stage of a fair selection process. Joseph is from an ethnic group that is under-represented in the firm, and the firm offers him the contract for that reason. The firm has no policy of always preferring candidates from that group.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's decision under the Equality Act 2010?","o":["It is unlawful direct race discrimination against Grace, which cannot be justified.","It is lawful, as firms may reserve a quota of places for under-represented groups.","It is unlawful unless the firm first obtains the SRA's approval for its approach.","It is lawful only if Grace consents in writing to the reason for the decision.","It is lawful positive action, as the two candidates were equally qualified."],"a":4,"e":"Under the **tie-breaker** provision (s.159), an employer may prefer a candidate from an **under-represented group** in recruitment where the candidates are **as qualified as each other**, provided this is proportionate and not an automatic policy. This is **positive action**, which the Act permits; **positive discrimination**, such as quotas or choosing someone regardless of merit, is unlawful. No SRA approval or consent from the unsuccessful candidate is required."},{"t":"equality-act","p":"FLK1","q":"Priya, who uses a wheelchair, books an appointment online with a small high-street firm to discuss a dispute with her landlord. When she arrives, she finds that the firm's only meeting rooms are on the first floor and there is no lift. She did not mention her disability when booking. The receptionist tells her the firm cannot help because she gave no warning.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's obligation?","o":["It owes her no duty, as she did not tell it about her needs when booking.","It should arrange to meet her at an accessible place at no extra cost to her.","It may arrange an accessible venue but ask her to pay for hiring the room.","It may decline to act, as its premises were lawfully built and are unaltered.","It must install a lift before it can lawfully take instructions from her."],"a":1,"e":"For service providers the duty to make **reasonable adjustments is anticipatory**: the firm must think in advance about the needs of disabled people generally, so Priya's lack of warning is no answer. Where a **physical feature** puts disabled clients at a substantial disadvantage, a reasonable step such as meeting at an accessible location removes it, and the firm **cannot pass the cost** of the adjustment on to her. Installing a lift is unlikely to be a reasonable step for a small firm, given its cost, when a cheaper and effective alternative exists."},{"t":"aml-framework-cdd","p":"FLK1","q":"Marta, a solicitor at an SRA-authorised firm, is instructed by Leon to bring an unfair dismissal claim in the employment tribunal against his former employer. The firm does no other work for Leon. A trainee asks Marta whether the firm must carry out customer due diligence on Leon under the Money Laundering Regulations 2017.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The Regulations do not apply, but the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002 offences still do.","The Regulations apply to all work done by an SRA-authorised firm, so full checks are needed.","The Regulations apply only if Leon's expected compensation is £12,000 or more.","The Regulations apply, but the firm may use simplified due diligence for tribunal work.","The Regulations do not apply, so no money laundering offence could ever arise."],"a":0,"e":"The MLR 2017 apply to lawyers only when they **participate in financial or real property transactions** (such as buying or selling land or businesses, managing client money, or setting up companies or trusts) or give tax advice. **Pure litigation**, such as a tribunal claim, is outside their scope, so their customer due diligence duties do not apply and the £12,000 occasional transaction threshold is irrelevant. However, the POCA **principal offences apply to everyone**, so the firm could still commit an offence if it became concerned in an arrangement involving criminal property."},{"t":"aml-framework-cdd","p":"FLK1","q":"Fenwick Sutton LLP is instructed by Calder plc, whose shares are listed on a regulated market, to act on its purchase of a warehouse. The firm's risk assessment rates the matter as low risk and nothing about it is suspicious. A partner suggests that, because the client is a listed company, the firm can skip customer due diligence altogether.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the due diligence the firm must carry out?","o":["No due diligence at all, as listed companies fall outside the Regulations entirely.","Enhanced due diligence, as any purchase of commercial property is treated as high risk.","Standard due diligence, including verifying the identity of every one of its shareholders.","Simplified due diligence, still identifying the client and monitoring the matter.","Simplified due diligence, with no need for any ongoing monitoring of the transaction."],"a":3,"e":"Where the firm assesses a relationship as **low risk**, for example because the client is listed on a regulated market, it may apply **simplified due diligence** (reg 37). SDD is **reduced CDD, not no CDD**: the firm must still establish the client's identity and continue **ongoing monitoring**, and SDD is unavailable if there is any suspicion. Nothing here calls for enhanced due diligence, and even standard CDD looks only at beneficial owners (those with more than 25%), not every shareholder."},{"t":"aml-framework-cdd","p":"FLK1","q":"Mr Okafor served as a government minister in another country until he left office eight months ago. He now instructs a London firm to act on his purchase of a £2.4 million house. The firm's risk assessment identifies no other risk factors. Mr Okafor's personal assistant tells the firm that, as he no longer holds office, ordinary checks will be enough.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the due diligence the firm must apply?","o":["Standard due diligence, as he stopped being a politically exposed person on leaving office.","Simplified due diligence, as the firm has identified no other risk factors in the matter.","Enhanced due diligence, as he must still be treated as a politically exposed person.","Enhanced due diligence, but only because the house costs more than £1 million.","Standard due diligence, as only UK officeholders count as politically exposed persons."],"a":2,"e":"A person must continue to be treated as a **politically exposed person (PEP) for at least 12 months** after leaving the prominent public function, and a foreign government minister is a PEP. The firm must therefore apply **enhanced due diligence**: senior management approval, measures to establish **source of wealth and source of funds**, and enhanced ongoing monitoring. PEP status covers officeholders abroad as well as in the UK, and it is his status, not the price of the house, that triggers EDD here."},{"t":"poca-offences-reporting","p":"FLK1","q":"Rhys, a criminal defence solicitor, represents Mr Vance, who is charged with a large fraud. Mr Vance pays Rhys's firm its normal fees for preparing and conducting his defence at trial. Rhys suspects that the money used to pay the fees may come from the alleged fraud.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm commits an offence under section 329 of the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002 by receiving the fees?","o":["Yes, because any receipt of money suspected to be criminal property is an offence.","No, because the principal offences apply only to work in the regulated sector.","Yes, unless the firm first obtains a defence against money laundering from the NCA.","No, because the money cannot be criminal property until Mr Vance is convicted.","No, because it receives the money as adequate consideration for genuine legal services."],"a":4,"e":"Section 329 (acquisition, use and possession) has a specific defence where the property is acquired **for adequate consideration**, such as normal fees for genuine legal work; it is lost only where the services are known or suspected to help the client carry out criminal conduct, which ordinary defence work does not. The principal offences apply to **everyone**, not just the regulated sector, and property can be criminal property on **suspicion** alone, without any conviction. A defence against money laundering (DAML) from the NCA is one route to a defence, but it is not needed where adequate consideration applies."},{"t":"poca-offences-reporting","p":"FLK1","q":"Josh is a conveyancing assistant at an SRA-authorised firm, which has never given him any anti-money laundering training. He handles a purchase in which the client is paying well above market value using funds sent from an unknown company overseas. Josh genuinely does not suspect anything and makes no report, although a reasonable person in his position would have suspected money laundering.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Josh commits the failure to disclose offence under section 330 of the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002?","o":["Yes, because the test is objective and a reasonable person would have suspected.","No, because only the firm's nominated officer can commit the section 330 offence.","Yes, because a lack of training is never relevant to criminal liability under POCA.","No, because he did not suspect and his employer had not given him the required training.","No, because the offence is committed only where a person actually knows of laundering."],"a":3,"e":"Section 330 has an **objective limb**: a person in the regulated sector can be guilty if they had **reasonable grounds** to suspect money laundering, even if they did not actually suspect it. But an employee who did not actually know or suspect has a defence if the employer **had not provided the training required** by the Money Laundering Regulations. The offence is not limited to nominated officers (their separate offence is s.331), and it does not require actual knowledge."},{"t":"poca-offences-reporting","p":"FLK1","q":"Daniel, a solicitor in the tax department of an SRA-authorised firm, meets a new client, Ms Hartley, who wants advice on putting her tax affairs right. She tells him that for several years she did not declare rental income to HM Revenue and Customs. She wants advice on making a voluntary disclosure and has no intention of continuing to evade tax.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Daniel's position under section 330 of the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002?","o":["He need not report, as the information came to him in privileged circumstances.","He must report to the MLRO, as tax advice is work in the regulated sector.","He must report directly to the NCA, as she has admitted a criminal offence.","He need not report, as tax evasion is not criminal conduct for POCA purposes.","He must report, as the privilege defence applies only to litigation work."],"a":0,"e":"Tax advice is regulated sector work and undeclared income is criminal property, but a professional legal adviser is **not required to disclose information received in privileged circumstances**, that is, from a client in connection with **legal advice**. The defence is lost only where the information is given with the intention of **furthering a criminal purpose**, which is not the case where Ms Hartley wants to regularise her affairs. Privileged circumstances cover legal advice as well as litigation, and tax evasion is criminal conduct like any other offence."},{"t":"financial-services","p":"FLK1","q":"Henry, a solicitor in an SRA-authorised firm that is not authorised by the Financial Conduct Authority, is drafting a will for Mrs Lindqvist. She asks him whether, in general, shares tend to be riskier than government bonds, as she is thinking about investing an inheritance. Henry explains the general difference in risk but does not mention any particular shares, bonds or funds, and suggests she speak to a financial adviser before deciding.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Henry's explanation under the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000?","o":["It is advising on investments, so the firm must rely on the Part 20 exemption.","It is not a regulated activity, as it is generic advice about types of investment.","It is a regulated activity, so Henry has breached the general prohibition.","It is not regulated, as shares and government bonds are not specified investments.","It is excluded, as it is a necessary part of the will work and not paid separately."],"a":1,"e":"**Advising on investments** means advice on the merits of buying or selling a **particular** investment. **Generic advice**, such as explaining that shares are generally riskier than gilts, is not a specified activity, so no regulated activity arises and neither an exclusion nor the Part 20 exemption is needed. Shares and government bonds are specified investments, so that reason is wrong, and the 'necessary part of other services' exclusion is aimed at regulated advising or arranging that the legal work cannot do without, which this is not."},{"t":"financial-services","p":"FLK1","q":"Waverley Solicitors, an SRA-authorised firm not authorised by the Financial Conduct Authority, acts for the executors of the late Mr Patel. On the executors' instructions, and without advising on whether to sell, the firm arranges through a stockbroker for the sale of the estate's shareholding in a listed company. The stockbroker pays the firm a commission of £400, which the firm keeps without telling the executors.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's position under the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000?","o":["It is exempt under Part 20, as the sale is incidental to administering the estate.","It is outside the Act, as shares held by an estate are not specified investments.","It may keep the commission, as the stockbroker is an FCA-authorised person.","It is exempt under Part 20, as the commission is below the SRA's permitted limit.","It cannot rely on Part 20, as it kept a commission without accounting for it."],"a":4,"e":"A condition of the **Part 20 exemption** is that the firm must not receive any reward from a third party for the activity unless it **accounts to the client** for it, for example by passing it on (unless the client agrees otherwise after being told). The sale is incidental to the estate work, but keeping an undisclosed commission defeats the exemption, and it also takes the firm outside the exclusion for arranging through an authorised person. Shares are specified investments whoever holds them, and there is no permitted level of retained commission."},{"t":"funding-private-cfa-dba","p":"FLK1","q":"Marcus is the respondent in financial remedy proceedings following his divorce, in which his wife is seeking a share of the family home and his pension. He cannot afford to pay privately and asks his solicitor to act on a 'no win, no fee' conditional fee agreement, with a 50% success fee if he keeps the home.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the solicitor can act on this basis?","o":["Yes, provided the agreement is in writing and states the success fee percentage.","Yes, provided the success fee is no more than 25% of the value of what he keeps.","No, because a conditional fee agreement cannot be used in family proceedings.","No, but the solicitor could act under a damages-based agreement instead.","Yes, because a 50% success fee is within the general 100% cap on uplifts."],"a":2,"e":"Under s.58A(1) of the Courts and Legal Services Act 1990, a conditional fee agreement (CFA) is unenforceable if it relates to **criminal or family proceedings**, so financial remedy proceedings cannot be funded this way, whatever the formalities or the level of success fee. The writing requirement and the 100% cap matter only where CFAs are allowed, and the 25% cap is specific to personal injury damages. **Damages-based agreements are also banned in family proceedings**, so they are no alternative."},{"t":"funding-private-cfa-dba","p":"FLK1","q":"Ewan instructs a firm on a dispute with his builder. The client care letter estimates total costs of £4,000 plus VAT and explains that this is an estimate, not a fixed fee. The matter becomes more complicated, but the firm does not tell Ewan. At the end it sends him a bill for £7,500 plus VAT, which is a fair charge for the work actually done.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's position?","o":["It can charge only £4,000 plus VAT, as an estimate binds the firm like a quotation.","It acted properly, as an estimate never needs to be updated once it has been given.","It acted properly, provided Ewan keeps the right to have the bill assessed by the court.","It should have warned him of the overrun, though the estimate was not a binding cap.","It breached its duty, so Ewan need not pay any part of the bill for the work done."],"a":3,"e":"An **estimate is not binding** in the way a quotation, fixed fee or cap is, but under **Code para 8.7** the client must receive the best possible information about the likely overall cost **as the matter progresses**, so the firm should have told Ewan promptly that the estimate would be exceeded. The failure is a breach of the firm's duties, and Ewan may complain or apply for the bill to be assessed, but it does not automatically cancel the whole bill. Equally, the availability of assessment does not excuse the failure to update him."},{"t":"funding-legal-aid-tpf-lei","p":"FLK1","q":"Ngozi has before-the-event legal expenses insurance attached to her home insurance policy. She wants to sue her neighbour in nuisance and asks to instruct Clarke & Mensah, a firm she trusts. The insurer insists that she use one of its panel solicitors. The panel firm has advised that proceedings should be started, and the claim form is about to be issued.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ngozi's right to choose her own solicitor?","o":["She may instruct Clarke & Mensah once proceedings are issued, as she then has free choice.","She can never choose, as the insurer's panel terms bind her for the whole of the claim.","She may instruct Clarke & Mensah at any time, as insurers may not operate panels.","She may choose only if the panel firm agrees to transfer the file to Clarke & Mensah.","She may choose only after trial, if she is unhappy with how the claim was handled."],"a":0,"e":"Insurers may require a before-the-event (BTE) policyholder to use **panel solicitors** in the early stages, but under the **Insurance Companies (Legal Expenses Insurance) Regulations 1990** the insured is **free to choose their own lawyer once proceedings or an inquiry are started**, or if a conflict of interest arises. So the panel requirement is lawful before issue but cannot stop Ngozi instructing Clarke & Mensah once the claim is issued. The panel firm's agreement is irrelevant."},{"t":"funding-legal-aid-tpf-lei","p":"FLK1","q":"Abena, who has civil legal aid, brings a housing disrepair claim against her landlord, which fails at trial. The landlord asks the court to order her to pay its costs of £18,000. Abena lives on a low income, has no savings, and conducted the claim reasonably throughout.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the costs order the court is likely to make against Abena?","o":["The full £18,000, as the loser pays the winner's costs whatever their means.","The full £18,000, as costs protection applies only in family proceedings.","No order of any kind, as legally aided parties can never be ordered to pay costs.","Half of the landlord's costs, as fixed by the statutory charge for aided parties.","An amount that is reasonable given both parties' resources and conduct, possibly nil."],"a":4,"e":"**Costs protection** under s.26 of the Legal Aid, Sentencing and Punishment of Offenders Act 2012 means a legally aided party who loses can be ordered to pay the opponent's costs only up to an amount that is **reasonable having regard to all the circumstances**, including the **financial resources** of both parties and their conduct; for someone in Abena's position this is often nil. The 'loser pays' rule is limited, not abolished, so an absolute bar is wrong, and the protection is not confined to family cases. The statutory charge is different: it is the Legal Aid Agency's charge over property the client **recovers or preserves**."},{"t":"transparency-client-care","p":"FLK1","q":"Dominic, a personal injury solicitor, is looking for new ways to attract work. His firm's marketing manager suggests several ideas.\n\nWhich of the following would Dominic be permitted to do under the SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors?","o":["Hand business cards to injured patients waiting in a hospital emergency department.","Telephone people from a purchased list to offer them free accident claim reviews.","Write to a former client whose claim he settled last year about a new service.","Approach people in the street near a busy junction to offer accident claim advice.","Knock on doors in a street where residents were injured in a gas explosion."],"a":2,"e":"**Code para 8.9** prohibits **unsolicited approaches to members of the public** to advertise legal services, which covers cold calling, approaching people in the street or at hospitals, and door-to-door canvassing. The only exception is for approaches to **current or former clients**, so writing to a former client is allowed, provided the publicity is accurate and not misleading (para 8.8). Each of the other ideas targets members of the public who have not asked to be contacted."},{"t":"transparency-client-care","p":"FLK1","q":"On 3 March, Lucia complained to her solicitors about delay in the administration of her late father's estate. The firm acknowledged the complaint, but by early May, more than eight weeks later, the complaint remains unresolved and the firm is still investigating it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the firm must now do?","o":["Nothing yet, as it need mention the Legal Ombudsman only once it gives her its final response.","Write to her about her right to go to the Legal Ombudsman, its time limits and contact details.","Tell her orally that she may now ask the SRA to investigate her complaint.","Pass her complaint to the Legal Ombudsman itself and close its own file on the complaint.","Tell her that continuing its investigation will now incur a separate charge."],"a":1,"e":"Under **Code para 8.4**, if a complaint has not been resolved to the client's satisfaction **within 8 weeks**, the firm must tell the client **in writing** of any right to complain to the **Legal Ombudsman (LeO)**, the time frame for doing so and LeO's full contact details. It cannot wait for its final response, and it is for the client, not the firm, to decide whether to go to LeO. Complaints must be handled **free of charge** (para 8.5), and poor service is a matter for LeO rather than the SRA."}],"ls-p3":[{"t":"sra-regulatory-role","p":"FLK1","q":"Kwame, a paralegal at an SRA-authorised firm, is not an authorised person under the Legal Services Act 2007. In a debt claim for a business client, he drafts and issues the claim form and serves it on the debtor. Throughout, Anjali, a solicitor with a practising certificate, retains responsibility for the claim, and the firm has documented arrangements for her to supervise and control Kwame's work, although she does not approve every step personally. The debtor argues that the claim has been conducted unlawfully.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position as at September 2026?","o":["The claim has been conducted unlawfully, because an unauthorised person may only support an authorised person and never conduct litigation tasks.","The claim has been conducted lawfully, because issuing and serving a claim form is not part of the reserved activity of conducting litigation.","The claim has been conducted lawfully, because Kwame acted for Anjali, who kept responsibility for it under proper supervision arrangements.","The claim has been conducted unlawfully, because Anjali did not personally approve each step that Kwame took in the litigation.","The claim has been conducted lawfully only if Kwame is a member of the Chartered Institute of Legal Executives."],"a":2,"e":"Conducting litigation (issuing, prosecuting and defending proceedings and ancillary formal steps such as service) is a reserved legal activity, so option B is wrong. On 31 March 2026 the Court of Appeal, allowing an appeal brought by CILEX, reversed the September 2025 High Court decision in Mazur and held that **an unauthorised person may carry out litigation tasks for and on behalf of an authorised individual who retains responsibility for the litigation and has appropriate supervision, management and control arrangements**. Option A states the High Court view that was reversed, and option D is wrong because the authorised person need not approve every step personally: the level of supervision depends on the circumstances. Option E is wrong because the delegation route does not depend on Kwame holding any qualification of his own."},{"t":"sra-regulatory-role","p":"FLK1","q":"Bronwen, a solicitor with a current practising certificate, is leaving her firm to work for herself as a freelance solicitor, practising in her own name and not through an authorised firm. She plans to prepare probate applications for a fee, to employ a part-time assistant, and to hold money received on her clients' behalf from the sale of estate assets until it is distributed. She has arranged adequate and appropriate insurance.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Bronwen can carry on this practice as a freelance solicitor?","o":["She can, because her practising certificate alone entitles her to carry on any reserved legal activity wherever she practises.","She can, because preparing probate applications is reserved only for unregulated providers and not for solicitors.","She cannot, because a freelance solicitor may only carry on non-reserved activities such as will drafting and general advice.","She cannot, because a freelance solicitor must buy cover on the full Minimum Terms and Conditions before doing reserved work.","She cannot, because a freelance solicitor doing reserved work must employ no one and hold no client money beyond fees and disbursements."],"a":4,"e":"Preparing the papers for a grant of probate for a fee is a reserved probate activity. **A freelance solicitor may carry on reserved activities without being an authorised firm only if they employ no one, hold no client money other than money on account of fees or disbursements, and have adequate and appropriate insurance**, so Bronwen's plans to employ an assistant and hold estate sale proceeds take her outside those conditions and she would need an authorised firm. Option C is wrong because freelances can do reserved work if the conditions are met, and option D is wrong because freelances need adequate and appropriate insurance, not full Minimum Terms cover."},{"t":"pii-other-providers","p":"FLK1","q":"Fairley & Osei, a partnership of solicitors, has professional indemnity insurance on the Minimum Terms and Conditions with a £10,000 excess (the amount of each claim the firm itself must bear). When applying for the policy, the managing partner deliberately failed to mention two earlier negligence claims. The firm then negligently fails to register a client's charge over a property, and the client, Mr Haddad, suffers a loss of £400,000. On learning of the earlier claims, the insurer says it intends to refuse to deal with Mr Haddad's claim.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the insurer's position?","o":["It must pay Mr Haddad £400,000 and may then seek to recover money, including the excess, from the firm.","It may avoid the policy because of the firm's non-disclosure, leaving Mr Haddad to claim against the partners personally.","It must pay Mr Haddad £390,000, as the excess is deducted from the amount paid to the claimant.","It need not pay, because Mr Haddad should instead apply to the SRA Compensation Fund for his loss.","It must pay Mr Haddad only after the firm has first reimbursed the insurer for the amount of the excess."],"a":0,"e":"**Under the Minimum Terms and Conditions the insurer cannot avoid, repudiate or cancel the policy, or reduce what it pays the claimant, because of the firm's non-disclosure or misrepresentation**; its remedy is to recover its outlay from the firm afterwards, so option B is wrong. **The excess cannot be deducted from the claimant's payment**: the insurer pays in full and recovers the excess from the firm, which rules out options C and E. Option D is wrong because the Compensation Fund is a discretionary fund of last resort for dishonesty or failure to account, not a substitute for insurance covering negligence."},{"t":"pii-other-providers","p":"FLK1","q":"Calloway Law Ltd is an SRA-authorised recognised body whose shareholders and directors are all solicitors. Ravi, a chartered accountant who is not a lawyer, wants to buy 15% of the company's shares and join its board. The firm will continue to carry on reserved legal activities, including conveyancing.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what is required if Ravi becomes a shareholder and director?","o":["Nothing further, because a non-lawyer may hold up to 25% of the shares of a recognised body without approval.","Nothing further, because only an owner of 50% or more of the shares needs to be approved by the regulator.","The firm must give up reserved work, because a firm with non-lawyer owners can carry on only non-reserved activities.","The firm must be licensed as an alternative business structure, and Ravi must be approved as he will hold a material interest.","The firm must transfer its regulation to the Council for Licensed Conveyancers, as the SRA cannot license non-lawyer ownership."],"a":3,"e":"**A firm carrying on reserved activities with non-lawyer owners or managers must be licensed as an alternative business structure (a licensed body)** under Part 5 of the Legal Services Act 2007, and **anyone holding a material interest, generally 10% or more of the shares or voting rights, must be approved as fit and proper** by the licensing authority. Ravi's 15% exceeds that threshold, so options A and B, which use the wrong thresholds, are wrong. The SRA is itself a licensing authority, so option E is wrong, and ABSs may carry on reserved work, so option C is wrong."},{"t":"equality-act","p":"FLK1","q":"Ines, an associate at a law firm, has Crohn's disease, which she has had for several years and which substantially affects her day-to-day activities. The firm knows about her condition. Over the past year she took 15 days of sick leave because of flare-ups of the disease. Under the firm's bonus policy, which applies to all staff, anyone with more than 10 days' sickness absence in a year receives no bonus, so Ines is refused a bonus.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's refusal of the bonus under the Equality Act 2010?","o":["It is direct disability discrimination, which the firm cannot justify in any circumstances.","It is discrimination arising from disability, unless the firm can objectively justify the refusal.","It is not discrimination, because the same absence rule is applied to every member of staff in the firm.","It is harassment, because the refusal has the effect of creating a hostile environment for Ines at work.","It is not discrimination, because she was refused the bonus for her absences rather than for her disability."],"a":1,"e":"**Discrimination arising from disability (section 15) is treating a disabled person unfavourably because of something arising in consequence of their disability**, here sickness absence caused by flare-ups, unless the treatment is a **proportionate means of achieving a legitimate aim** or the employer did not know and could not reasonably have known of the disability. The firm knew, so only justification is available. Option E is wrong because the absences arise from the disability, and option A is wrong because she was not treated less favourably because of disability itself (a non-disabled colleague with the same absences would have been treated the same way)."},{"t":"equality-act","p":"FLK1","q":"Harjit, a trainee solicitor who is a practising Sikh, wears a turban. Over several months an associate in his team, Liam, repeatedly makes jokes about Harjit's turban in open-plan meetings, despite Harjit asking him to stop. Harjit finds the comments humiliating. The firm has no equality policy and has never given its staff any equality or diversity training. Harjit brings a claim in the employment tribunal against the firm and Liam.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely liability?","o":["Only Liam is liable, because the firm did not know about the jokes and did not approve of them.","Neither is liable, because Liam intended the jokes as humour and did not mean to offend Harjit.","Only the firm is liable, because an employee cannot be personally liable for acts done at work.","Only Liam is liable, because harassment is not something for which an employer can be held responsible.","Both are liable for harassment, as the firm cannot show it took all reasonable steps to prevent it."],"a":4,"e":"**Harassment (section 26) is unwanted conduct related to a protected characteristic (here religion or race) that has the purpose or effect of violating dignity or creating a humiliating or offensive environment**, so Liam's lack of intention, relied on in option B, does not matter where it is reasonable for the conduct to have that effect. **An employer is vicariously liable for its employees' acts in the course of employment whether or not it knew or approved, unless it took all reasonable steps to prevent them**; a firm with no policy or training cannot show this, so options A and D are wrong. Liam can also be personally liable (section 110), so option C is wrong."},{"t":"aml-framework-cdd","p":"FLK1","q":"Thornbury Legal LLP is asked to act for Halcyon Holdings Inc, a company registered abroad, in buying a flat in London for £750,000. Instructions come from a representative who provides the company's certificate of incorporation but refuses to say who owns or controls its shares, saying the owners value their privacy. The buyer wants to exchange contracts within two weeks.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the firm must do?","o":["It must not act on the purchase, and it must consider whether to make a suspicious activity report.","It may act on the purchase, provided it verifies the representative's identity before exchange.","It may act on the purchase, but must complete the ownership checks as soon as practicable after completion.","It may act on the purchase, because a company's certificate of incorporation verifies all that is needed.","It must report the company directly to the police and continue acting so as not to alert the client."],"a":0,"e":"Customer due diligence requires the firm to **identify the beneficial owner** (any individual owning or controlling more than 25% of the shares or voting rights, or otherwise controlling the client) and take reasonable measures to verify their identity. **Where CDD cannot be completed, the firm must not establish the business relationship or carry out the transaction, must terminate any existing relationship and must consider making a suspicious activity report.** Option C is wrong because verification cannot be put off until after completion where it has failed, and option D is wrong because incorporation documents do not reveal who ultimately owns or controls the company."},{"t":"aml-framework-cdd","p":"FLK1","q":"A solicitor at Penhale & Co is instructed by Corvane Trading Ltd, a company incorporated and established in a country that is currently on the Financial Action Task Force's 'call for action' list. The company wants the firm to act on its purchase of a small warehouse in Leeds. The company's ownership is simple, its director attends in person, and the firm's own matter risk assessment rates the transaction as otherwise low risk.\n\nWhich level of due diligence must the firm apply?","o":["Simplified due diligence, as the firm has assessed the transaction as low risk.","Standard customer due diligence, as the company's ownership structure is straightforward.","Enhanced due diligence, as the client is established in a high-risk third country.","Enhanced due diligence, but only if the firm's senior management chooses to require it.","No due diligence, as the firm must refuse to act for a company from such a country."],"a":2,"e":"Under regulation 33, **enhanced due diligence is mandatory for any business relationship or transaction with a person established in a high-risk third country**, which since the 2026 amendments means a country on the FATF 'call for action' list (the black list). The firm's own low-risk assessment cannot override this, so options A and B are wrong, and EDD is not a matter of choice, so option D is wrong. The Regulations do not prohibit acting for such a client, so option E is wrong; countries on the FATF grey list are a risk factor to weigh rather than a mandatory trigger."},{"t":"aml-framework-cdd","p":"FLK1","q":"Mr and Mrs Adebayo, long-standing local residents, instruct a firm to act on their purchase of a family home. They want initial advice on the draft contract this week; exchange of contracts is several weeks away. They have provided copies of their passports, but the firm's electronic verification check has not yet come back. The firm has assessed the risk of money laundering as low, and nothing about the matter is suspicious.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm may begin work?","o":["It must not do any work at all until verification of both clients is fully complete.","It may begin work and need not verify the clients, because the matter is low risk.","It may begin work and complete verification within 30 days after completion of the purchase.","It may begin work and complete verification as soon as practicable, as the risk is low.","It may begin work only after its money laundering reporting officer has reported to the National Crime Agency."],"a":3,"e":"Verification must normally be completed before a business relationship is established, but under regulation 30 **it may be completed during the establishment of the business relationship if that is necessary not to interrupt the normal conduct of business and there is little risk of money laundering, provided it is completed as soon as practicable**. Option A overstates the rule, and option B is wrong because even low-risk clients must be verified. Option C is wrong because verification cannot be left until after the transaction, and option E is wrong because there is no suspicion to report."},{"t":"poca-offences-reporting","p":"FLK1","q":"A firm's money laundering reporting officer submitted a suspicious activity report to the National Crime Agency (NCA), asking for consent to complete a client's share purchase. On Monday 1 June 2026, within the notice period, the NCA gave notice that consent was refused. No application has been made to the court to extend any period, and the NCA has taken no further action.\n\nWhen, if at all, may the firm complete the purchase without committing a principal money laundering offence?","o":["Immediately, because the seven working day notice period has now ended.","Once the 31-day moratorium period starting on 1 June 2026 has ended.","Only after a total of 186 days has passed since consent was refused.","Only after the NCA later writes to the firm to give positive consent.","Never, because consent has been refused and the refusal cannot lapse."],"a":1,"e":"**If the NCA refuses consent within the seven working day notice period, a 31-day moratorium period begins on the day of the refusal, during which the firm must not proceed; if nothing more happens by the end of it, consent is treated as given.** Option A is wrong because the refusal replaces the deemed consent that would otherwise follow the notice period, and options D and E are wrong because the refusal lapses at the end of the moratorium. The court can extend the moratorium by up to 31 days at a time, to a maximum of 186 days beyond the initial period, but option C is wrong because no extension has been sought."},{"t":"poca-offences-reporting","p":"FLK1","q":"Marisol is an employment solicitor at a firm; she does no transactional, tax or trust work and is not in the regulated sector. She acts for Mr Grant in an unfair dismissal claim. Mr Grant tells her that the police are investigating him for money laundering, and asks her to delete some emails on her file that refer to payments he received from an overseas company. Marisol is considering doing as he asks.\n\nWhich offence would Marisol most likely commit if she deleted the emails?","o":["Tipping off, because she would be disclosing information about a money laundering investigation.","Failure to disclose, because she has reasonable grounds to suspect money laundering by Mr Grant.","Concealing criminal property, because the emails would themselves be the proceeds of Mr Grant's crime.","Entering into an arrangement, because deleting the emails is not part of the ordinary conduct of litigation.","Prejudicing an investigation, because she would be destroying documents relevant to a known investigation."],"a":4,"e":"**Prejudicing an investigation (section 342) can be committed by anyone, and includes falsifying, concealing or destroying documents relevant to a money laundering investigation that the person knows or suspects is being or is about to be conducted**; it carries up to 5 years' imprisonment. Option A is wrong because tipping off applies only in the regulated sector and involves disclosing information, and option B is wrong because the section 330 failure to disclose offence applies only to information received in the regulated sector. Option C is wrong because emails are not property representing a benefit from crime."},{"t":"poca-offences-reporting","p":"FLK1","q":"Dmitri instructs a firm on his purchase of a flat for £320,000. While discussing the deposit, he mentions that £4,000 of it comes from weekend cash decorating jobs that he never declared to HM Revenue and Customs. The rest of the money comes from his salary and a mortgage. A colleague tells the conveyancing solicitor that the amount is too small to matter.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the solicitor should do?","o":["Report the suspicion to the firm's money laundering reporting officer and take no further step until consent is given or deemed.","Continue with the purchase, because the decorating work itself was lawful and so the funds are not criminal property.","Continue with the purchase, because the amount is below the minimum value that must be reported as suspicious.","Continue with the purchase but make a report to the money laundering reporting officer after completion.","Advise Dmitri to declare the income to HM Revenue and Customs and then continue without any report."],"a":0,"e":"**Criminal conduct includes any offence, including tax evasion, and there is no minimum value**: money that represents the benefit of evading tax on lawful work is criminal property, so options B and C are wrong. Proceeding would risk the section 328 arrangements offence, so the solicitor should make an **authorised disclosure to the money laundering reporting officer before acting and wait for consent**. Option D is wrong because a disclosure after the act is a defence only where there was good reason for the delay, and option E is wrong because advice to the client does not replace the disclosure."},{"t":"financial-services","p":"FLK1","q":"Zainab has just received a lump sum of £150,000 in her financial settlement on divorce. She asks her solicitor, at an SRA-authorised firm that is not authorised by the Financial Conduct Authority, to tell her which particular investment bond she should buy with the money. The firm would receive no commission.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the solicitor should respond?","o":["Recommend a bond under the Part 20 exemption, as the advice is incidental to the divorce.","Recommend a bond under the exclusion for activities that are a necessary part of legal services.","Recommend a bond, provided the firm first tells her in writing that it is not authorised by the Financial Conduct Authority.","Introduce her to an independent financial adviser, as the firm cannot recommend a retail investment product.","Recommend a bond, as advice on investments is not a regulated activity where no commission is received."],"a":3,"e":"Advising on the merits of buying a particular investment bond is advising on investments, a regulated activity, and receiving no commission does not change this, so option E is wrong. **The SRA Financial Services (Scope) Rules prohibit firms relying on the Part 20 exemption from recommending a client to buy a retail investment product** such as an investment bond, so option A fails even though the work arises from the divorce, and a status disclosure (option C) cannot cure it. **Introducing the client to an authorised person such as an independent financial adviser is an excluded activity** (article 33), so that is the right course."},{"t":"financial-services","p":"FLK1","q":"Mr Okonjo, a retired engineer, instructs a firm that is not authorised by the Financial Conduct Authority on several matters arising from his move abroad. He asks the firm to do the following, and to charge him its normal fees for each.\n\nWhich of the following activities is NOT a regulated activity under the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000?","o":["Signing transfers to sell his shares in a listed company on his behalf as his attorney.","Advising him whether he should sell his holding of particular UK government bonds.","Acting for him in the sale of his holiday cottage in Cornwall to a private buyer.","Arranging after-the-event legal expenses insurance for his dispute with a builder.","Managing his portfolio of unit trusts with discretion while he is living overseas."],"a":2,"e":"An activity is regulated only if it involves a **specified investment**, and **land and buildings are not specified investments**, so selling the cottage falls outside the Act altogether. Shares, government bonds (gilts), units in collective investment schemes and contracts of insurance (including after-the-event insurance) are all specified investments, and dealing as agent, advising on the merits of a particular investment, arranging insurance and managing investments with discretion are specified activities. Those other options would therefore need an exclusion, the Part 20 exemption or a referral to an authorised person."},{"t":"funding-private-cfa-dba","p":"FLK1","q":"Rania, a business owner, brings a breach of contract claim against a supplier under a written conditional fee agreement. The agreement provides for a success fee of 80% of her solicitor's base costs. She wins and is awarded damages of £200,000. Her solicitor's base costs are £30,000, and the court orders the supplier to pay those costs in full.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who bears the success fee?","o":["The supplier pays the £24,000 success fee, because it lost the claim and must pay the winner's costs.","Rania pays the £24,000 success fee herself, usually out of the damages she has recovered.","Rania pays a success fee capped at 25% of her damages, as the cap applies to every claim.","The solicitor cannot charge a success fee, because the supplier has already paid the base costs.","The supplier and Rania each pay half of the £24,000 success fee under the costs rules."],"a":1,"e":"The success fee is a percentage uplift on base costs: 80% of £30,000 is £24,000, which is within the general cap of 100% of base costs. **Since 1 April 2013 a success fee is not recoverable from the losing opponent**, so option A is wrong: the opponent pays the base costs and the client pays the success fee, usually from damages. Option C is wrong because the 25% cap (of damages for pain, suffering and loss of amenity and past financial loss) applies only to personal injury claims, not to a commercial contract claim."},{"t":"funding-private-cfa-dba","p":"FLK1","q":"Lena brings a claim for £60,000 against a former business associate. The claim is allocated to the intermediate track. She instructs her solicitors under a private retainer at hourly rates, and her solicitors' charges for the whole claim come to £28,000. She wins, but the costs the court allows her to recover from the defendant are limited to the fixed recoverable costs set by the Civil Procedure Rules, which are considerably less than £28,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who bears the difference between her solicitors' charges and the costs recovered?","o":["Her solicitors must write it off, because fixed recoverable costs cap what a solicitor may charge the client.","The defendant must pay it, because the loser must pay all of the winner's costs in a fixed costs case.","The court must increase the recoverable costs to match her solicitors' charges, as she won the claim.","Nobody pays it, because her solicitors' entitlement ends once the costs have been recovered from the defendant.","Lena must pay it to her solicitors herself, as fixed recoverable costs limit only what the defendant pays."],"a":4,"e":"In most claims on the fast track and intermediate track (which covers most claims up to £100,000), **fixed recoverable costs limit what the winner can recover from the loser; they do not limit what the client and solicitor have agreed between themselves**. Under her private retainer Lena remains primarily liable to pay her own solicitors, so she bears the shortfall, and options A and D are wrong. Option B is wrong because the loser-pays rule operates subject to the fixed costs regime, and option C is wrong because a win does not entitle the court to replace the fixed amounts with the actual charges."},{"t":"funding-legal-aid-tpf-lei","p":"FLK1","q":"Brightline Ltd wants to bring a £12 million claim for breach of contract in the High Court against a former joint venture partner. A commercial litigation funder agrees to pay its legal costs. The written funding agreement provides that, if the claim succeeds, the funder will receive 30% of the damages recovered, and nothing if the claim fails. The agreement does not comply with the Damages-Based Agreements Regulations 2013.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the funding agreement as at September 2026?","o":["It is a damages-based agreement and is unenforceable because it does not comply with the regulations.","It is enforceable, because the funder is not acting as Brightline's legal representative in the claim.","It is enforceable, provided the funder is a member of the Association of Litigation Funders.","It is unenforceable, because third-party funding of commercial litigation is not permitted in England and Wales.","It is enforceable, because legislation reversing the Supreme Court's decision on funding agreements is now in force."],"a":0,"e":"**A litigation funding agreement under which the funder's return is calculated as a percentage of damages is a damages-based agreement, and so is unenforceable unless it complies with the DBA Regulations** (PACCAR, 2023). Option B is wrong because that decision applied the statutory definition to funders who were not the claimant's lawyers, and option E is wrong because, although the government has announced it will legislate, no reversing legislation was in force at September 2026. Third-party funding itself is lawful and self-regulated, so option D is wrong; a return set as a multiple of the funder's investment would not be a damages-based agreement."},{"t":"funding-legal-aid-tpf-lei","p":"FLK1","q":"Mr Petrov, who receives Universal Credit and has no savings, is the respondent to an application by his former partner about the arrangements for their child. There is no evidence of domestic abuse or child abuse, so the case is outside the scope of civil legal aid. Mr Petrov has a significant learning disability, the issues are legally and factually complex, and his former partner is represented by a barrister. His solicitor believes he could not present his case effectively on his own.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Mr Petrov's position on legal aid?","o":["He is automatically entitled to legal aid, because receiving Universal Credit passports him through the means test.","He cannot obtain legal aid in any circumstances, because private child arrangements cases are outside scope.","He will obtain legal aid if his case has strong prospects of success, as merits override the scope rules.","He may apply for exceptional case funding, as refusing legal aid may breach his human rights.","He may apply for legal aid only once his former partner has first been granted legal aid in the case."],"a":3,"e":"Scope is the first hurdle, and private family cases without evidence of abuse are generally out of scope, so passporting on means (option A) or strong merits (option C) cannot by themselves bring the case within legal aid. However, **exceptional case funding under section 10 of the Legal Aid, Sentencing and Punishment of Offenders Act 2012 allows an out-of-scope case to be funded where refusal would breach, or risk breaching, the client's rights under the European Convention on Human Rights**, such as the right to a fair trial in Article 6. That is why option B is too absolute; if funding is granted he must still satisfy the means and merits tests."},{"t":"transparency-client-care","p":"FLK1","q":"Marsh & Kaur, an SRA-authorised firm, does residential conveyancing. Its website shows its fixed fee for purchases and sales, the likely search fees and HM Land Registry fees, whether VAT is payable, the key stages of a transaction with typical timescales, and what the fee includes and excludes. It also publishes its complaints procedure and displays the SRA clickable logo. It says nothing about who will carry out the work.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the website complies with the SRA Transparency Rules?","o":["It complies, because a firm offering a fixed fee does not need to publish any further costs information.","It does not comply, because it must also publish a binding quotation for each type of property transaction.","It does not comply, because it must state the experience and qualifications of those doing the work and their supervisors.","It complies, because information about staff need only be given in the client care letter once instructed.","It does not comply, because it must also publish the hourly rates of every fee earner in the firm."],"a":2,"e":"Residential conveyancing is a listed service, so the firm must publish costs information, which includes the total cost or range, the basis of charging, likely disbursements and VAT, the services included, key stages and timescales, and **the experience and qualifications of anyone carrying out the work and of their supervisors**. The website omits only that last element, so options A and D are wrong. Option E is wrong because hourly rates need be shown only where they are the basis of charging, and here the firm charges a fixed fee; nor do the Rules require a binding quotation as option B suggests."},{"t":"transparency-client-care","p":"FLK1","q":"Gwen runs Gwen Pryce Solicitors, an SRA-authorised sole practice in a market town. The firm does uncontested probate for individuals and debt recovery for local businesses, with claims usually of £5,000 to £40,000. The firm has no website and does not intend to set one up; it attracts clients through local recommendations.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's obligations under the SRA Transparency Rules?","o":["It has no obligations under the Rules, because they apply only to firms that have a website.","It must make its costs, complaints and regulatory information available to anyone on request.","It must publish costs information for its probate work only, because debt recovery is not a listed service.","It must set up a website, because the Rules require every authorised firm to publish information online.","It need only give costs information to each client in the client care letter once they instruct the firm."],"a":1,"e":"Uncontested probate for individuals and **debt recovery for businesses up to £100,000** are both listed services, so costs information is required for each and option C is wrong; complaints and regulatory information must be published by every firm. **The information must be published prominently on the firm's website, and a firm without a website must make it available on request**, so options A and D are wrong. Option E is wrong because the Rules require the information to be available before anyone becomes a client, not just in the client care letter."}],"property-p1":[{"t":"transaction-overview","p":"FLK2","q":"A seller is inviting sealed bids for a two-bedroom flat in Bristol. Two existing clients of the same solicitor, Priya Shah and Marcus Webb, each separately ask the solicitor to act for them in submitting a bid and, if successful, in buying the flat. Neither client knows the other has approached the firm.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the solicitor may act for both clients?","o":["The solicitor must refuse to act for both clients, because acting for two would-be buyers of the same property is always prohibited.","The solicitor may act only for whichever client gave instructions first, and must decline the other client without giving any reason.","The solicitor may act for both if each gives informed written consent, confidences are protected and it is reasonable to act.","The solicitor may act for both without telling them, because no conflict arises until one of the bids has been accepted by the seller.","The solicitor may act for both clients only if the two of them agree to submit a joint bid and buy the flat together as co-owners."],"a":2,"e":"The clients are **competing for the same objective**, which is one of the two exceptions in **SRA Code para 6.2** allowing a solicitor to act despite a conflict, provided **all clients give informed consent (in writing), effective safeguards protect confidential information, and the solicitor is satisfied it is reasonable to act**. There is a conflict from the outset, so acting without telling them is wrong, and there is no blanket ban on acting for two would-be buyers. A first-come rule and a forced joint bid have no basis in the Code."},{"t":"transaction-overview","p":"FLK2","q":"Chloe Adeyemi instructs a solicitor on her purchase of a house for £320,000 with a mortgage. She says £60,000 of the deposit will be transferred to the firm's client account by her uncle, who lives in Manchester, as a gift. The solicitor has no other information about the uncle.\n\nWhat should the solicitor do about the money from the uncle?","o":["Check the uncle's identity and source of funds, and get his signed letter that the money is a gift with no interest in the house.","Accept the money without further checks, because due diligence is needed only on the solicitor's own client and not on third parties.","Refuse to accept any money from a third party and insist that Chloe pays the whole deposit from her own bank account.","Accept the money and make a suspicious activity report at once, because any gifted deposit is treated as proceeds of crime.","Accept the money only after the uncle has signed the contract as a joint buyer, so that the gift is recorded on the register."],"a":0,"e":"Under the **Money Laundering Regulations** the solicitor must check the **source of funds** (where the money for the deal comes from), which for a gifted deposit means checking the **donor's identity and where the donor's money came from**. A signed **gift letter** confirming the donor takes **no interest** in the property is also needed, because the lender must be told if a third party will claim an interest. Checks are not limited to the client, there is no rule against third-party money, and a gift is not in itself suspicious enough to require a report."},{"t":"title","p":"FLK2","q":"Owen Pritchard is the sole registered proprietor of a house in Swansea, which he is selling with vacant possession to Hannah Reid. The register contains no restriction. On a visit, Hannah sees that Owen's adult daughter, Ffion, lives at the house. Owen's replies to enquiries reveal that Ffion paid £40,000 towards the original purchase price.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate step for Hannah's solicitor to take to protect Hannah?","o":["Rely on Owen's full title guarantee, because the covenants for title will give Hannah a remedy if Ffion later claims an interest.","Take no action, because only interests protected by an entry on the register can bind a buyer of registered land.","Apply to enter a notice on the register protecting Ffion's interest, so that it is clear on the title before completion.","Require Ffion to sign the contract, or a separate release, agreeing to give up any rights and leave the house by completion.","Ask Owen to declare in the contract that no other person has any interest in the house, and accept that as sufficient."],"a":3,"e":"Ffion probably has a beneficial interest under a trust, and as a **person in actual occupation** whose occupation is obvious on inspection, her interest can **override** a registered disposition (**LRA 2002 Sch 3 para 2**), binding Hannah even though nothing is on the register. The safe course is for Ffion to **sign the contract or a release** agreeing to the sale and to vacate. Covenants for title only give a damages claim against Owen, a beneficial interest under a trust cannot be protected by a notice, and Owen's own declaration does not bind Ffion."},{"t":"title","p":"FLK2","q":"Kwame Mensah is buying a registered freehold bungalow. The charges register refers to a 1984 transfer in which the then buyer covenanted to maintain the boundary wall, and gave an indemnity covenant to the seller. The draft transfer prepared by the current seller's solicitor contains a covenant by Kwame to observe and perform the 1984 covenants and to indemnify the seller against any breach.\n\nWhich of the following best explains why the seller wants this indemnity covenant?","o":["The burden of a positive covenant passes to every buyer automatically, so HM Land Registry will not register the transfer without one.","The burden of a positive covenant does not pass automatically, so the seller, who stays liable on its own promise, wants protection.","The indemnity covenant makes Kwame directly liable to the original covenantee's successors and is implied into every transfer anyway.","The indemnity covenant is needed only because the seller is giving full title guarantee and wants to limit that guarantee.","The indemnity covenant protects Kwame against any breach of the wall covenant that the seller committed before completion."],"a":1,"e":"**The burden of a positive covenant does not run with freehold land**, so the person entitled to enforce it must rely on a **chain of indemnity covenants**: each owner stays liable on its own promise and so requires the next buyer to indemnify it. That is why the seller wants Kwame's indemnity. HM Land Registry does not require one, the covenant is not implied in a transfer of freehold land, and it protects the seller, not Kwame."},{"t":"searches","p":"FLK2","q":"Ruth Okafor is buying a semi-detached house in Leicester and plans to build a single-storey rear extension soon after completion. The drainage and water search (CON29DW) shows that a public sewer runs across the rear garden, beneath the area where she intends to build.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate advice for Ruth's solicitor to give her?","o":["The sewer will be moved by the water company at its own cost once the extension has received planning permission.","The sewer is irrelevant to the extension, because building over drains is a matter for building regulations only.","Ruth may build over the sewer freely because the sewer belongs to the seller and passes to Ruth on completion.","Ruth should rely on the local authority search, which will confirm whether building over the sewer is permitted.","Ruth will need a build-over agreement from the water company, which may refuse it or impose conditions."],"a":4,"e":"A **public sewer** belongs to the water company, and building over or near it needs the company's consent, usually by a **build-over agreement**, which may be refused or require the design to change. The solicitor should report this before exchange so Ruth can decide whether to proceed. The sewer does not belong to the seller, the company will not move it at its own cost, and the local search does not deal with sewers (that is the CON29DW's job)."},{"t":"searches","p":"FLK2","q":"Daniel Brooks is buying a cottage in a Suffolk village. The plan attached to the official copies shows that the front garden adjoins the village green, and the seller's replies mention that local residents walk across part of the front garden to reach the green.\n\nWhich additional search or enquiry is most relevant on these facts?","o":["A coal mining search, to reveal whether past mine workings affect the garden and the boundary with the green.","A search of the index map, to reveal whether the cottage is registered and has a caution against first registration.","The optional enquiry on commons registration, to reveal whether any of the land is registered as common land or a green.","A chancel repair search, to reveal whether the cottage is liable to contribute to repairs of the parish church.","A land charges search against the seller, to reveal any rights of way registered as land charges over the garden."],"a":2,"e":"Land next to a **village green** may itself be registered as common land or a town or village green, which would stop Daniel fencing or building on it, so the **CON29O optional enquiry on commons registration** should be raised. The other searches fit different clues: a mining area (CON29M), unregistered land (index map), church land (chancel) and unregistered land with names to search (land charges). The cottage is already registered, since official copies exist."},{"t":"finance-lender","p":"FLK2","q":"A solicitor is acting for Sophie Lambert on her purchase of a flat for £285,000 and for her lender, which instructs on the terms of the UK Finance Mortgage Lenders' Handbook. The official copies show that the seller was registered as proprietor only three months ago, having paid £215,000. Sophie says the lender's valuer has already approved the price.\n\nWhat should the solicitor do?","o":["Report to the lender that the seller has owned the flat for less than six months, before the mortgage advance is released.","Take no action, because the lender's valuer has approved the price and valuation is not the solicitor's concern.","Report the matter to the lender only if Sophie consents, and otherwise complete the purchase and mortgage as instructed.","Stop acting for Sophie at once, because a quick resale at a higher price must be reported to the SRA as possible fraud.","Ask the seller's solicitor to reduce the price to £215,000, because the lender will lend only on the seller's purchase price."],"a":0,"e":"The **Lenders' Handbook** requires the solicitor to report to the lender where the **seller has owned the property for less than six months**, because a quick resale at a higher price may indicate fraud or an inflated valuation. The duty to the lender is a separate client duty, so the valuer's view does not remove it. If Sophie objected to disclosure, the solicitor could not simply carry on for the lender: it would have to stop acting for the lender, and there is no rule requiring a report to the SRA or a price reduction."},{"t":"contract","p":"FLK2","q":"Grace Mitchell is selling her house in Leeds for £450,000, and the contract incorporates the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition, 2018 revision) without amendment. On exchange the buyer will pay a deposit of £45,000 to Grace's solicitor. Grace is buying a flat in Edinburgh on the same day and wants to use the deposit towards the deposit on that purchase.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Grace can use the deposit in this way?","o":["Yes, because the seller may use the deposit for any related purchase, wherever the property is, if the buyer is told.","Yes, because the seller's solicitor holds the deposit as agent for the seller and must pay it to her on request.","No, because a deposit under the standard conditions must always be held as stakeholder until completion.","No, because the deposit may be used only towards a related residential purchase in England and Wales.","No, because a deposit may be passed up a chain only where the buyer's mortgage lender has consented."],"a":3,"e":"The deposit is normally held **as stakeholder**, but the standard conditions allow the seller to use it towards the deposit on a **related residential purchase in England and Wales**, held on similar terms up the chain (**SC 2.2.5**). Grace's purchase is in Scotland, so the exception does not apply and the deposit stays with her solicitor as stakeholder. The deposit is not held as agent, the chain exception is not unlimited, and the buyer's lender has no say."},{"t":"contract","p":"FLK2","q":"Halverton Estates Ltd has opted to tax a vacant freehold office building in Reading, which it is selling for £800,000. The contract incorporates the Standard Commercial Property Conditions (3rd edition, 2018 revision) without amendment and says nothing about VAT. The buyer's solicitor has assumed the price includes any VAT.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the buyer's position on VAT?","o":["The buyer pays no VAT, because the price stated in the contract is treated as inclusive of any VAT that is due.","The buyer must pay VAT on top of the price on completion, because the price is exclusive of VAT.","The buyer pays no VAT, because the sale of a freehold commercial building is always an exempt supply.","The seller must bear the VAT out of the price, because the contract does not expressly mention VAT.","The buyer must pay VAT only if the buyer also opts to tax the building before completion takes place."],"a":1,"e":"Under the **SCPC the price is exclusive of VAT**, so if the sale is a taxable supply the buyer must pay VAT **on top** of the price, against a VAT invoice. The seller's **option to tax** makes the sale standard-rated. The 'inclusive' rule is the residential **SCS** position, a freehold sale is exempt only where there is no option and the building is not new, and the buyer's own option is irrelevant to the seller's supply."},{"t":"exchange","p":"FLK2","q":"Ibrahim Hassan's solicitor holds Ibrahim's signed part of the contract for his purchase of a house. The seller's solicitor holds the seller's signed part. The deposit is ready to be sent. Both solicitors agree on the telephone that contracts should now be exchanged, with completion in four weeks.\n\nWhich Law Society formula should they use, and what does it require?","o":["Formula A, under which the seller's solicitor confirms it holds both parts and undertakes to send the seller's part that day.","Formula C, under which each solicitor releases its part only once every other link in the chain has exchanged.","Formula A, under which the buyer's solicitor sends its client's part to the seller's solicitor and exchange takes place on receipt.","Formula C, under which each solicitor holds its part to the other's order until the agreed completion date.","Formula B, under which each confirms it holds its client's signed part and undertakes to send it to the other that day."],"a":4,"e":"Where **each solicitor holds its own client's signed part**, **Formula B** is used: each confirms it holds its part, the completion date is agreed, and each **undertakes** to send its part (and the buyer's solicitor the deposit) that day. **Formula A** is for where one solicitor already holds both parts, which is not the case here. **Formula C** is a two-stage formula for chains, and there is no chain on these facts."},{"t":"pre-completion","p":"FLK2","q":"Natalia Kowalski has exchanged contracts to buy part of the garden of a registered freehold house, as a building plot. She is borrowing from a bank, and her solicitor also acts for the bank. Completion is in two weeks.\n\nWhich pre-completion searches should the solicitor make?","o":["An OS1 search against the seller's title and a K16 bankruptcy search against Natalia.","A K15 search against the seller and a K16 bankruptcy search against Natalia.","An OS2 search against the seller's title and a K16 bankruptcy search against Natalia.","An OS2 search against the seller's title only, since the register shows any bankruptcy.","An OS1 search against the seller's title and a K15 search against the seller only."],"a":2,"e":"For a purchase of **part** of a registered title the buyer makes an **OS2** search, which gives a **30-working-day priority period** (OS1 is for the whole of a title). The bank also needs a **K16 bankruptcy search against the borrower**, because Natalia's bankruptcy would not appear on the seller's register. The K15 search is for unregistered land, and the seller's bankruptcy, not the buyer's, is what the OS2 result would reveal."},{"t":"completion","p":"FLK2","q":"Fenwick Logistics Ltd completed its purchase of a freehold warehouse on 3 November 2026, funded partly by a legal mortgage granted to a bank on that day. The solicitor acted for both the company and the bank.\n\nBy when must the charge be delivered for registration at Companies House, and what is the consequence of failing to do so?","o":["By 24 November 2026, failing which the charge is void against a liquidator, administrator and creditors.","By 17 November 2026, failing which the company becomes liable to a penalty but the charge remains valid.","By 3 December 2026, failing which the charge is void against everyone, including the company itself.","By 15 December 2026, failing which HM Land Registry will refuse to register the bank as chargee.","By 3 January 2027, failing which the bank loses priority to any later charge registered at Companies House."],"a":0,"e":"A charge created by a company must be delivered to **Companies House within 21 days beginning with the day after its creation** (Companies Act 2006 s.859A), so here by **24 November 2026**. If not, the charge is **void against a liquidator, administrator and creditors** (s.859H), though the debt remains owed. The 14-day limit is for SDLT, 30 working days is the Land Registry priority period, and 2 months is the first registration deadline."},{"t":"delayed-completion","p":"FLK2","q":"Under a contract incorporating the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition, 2018 revision), Amira Haddad agreed to buy a house and paid a 10% deposit to the seller's solicitor as stakeholder. The seller failed to complete on the agreed date because of a problem redeeming his mortgage. Amira, who was ready, able and willing to complete, served a valid notice to complete. The notice period has now expired and the seller still cannot complete.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Amira's position?","o":["Amira may only claim compensation at the contract rate for each day of delay, and must wait until the seller is able to complete.","Amira may rescind the contract and recover the deposit, but she loses any right to damages for her wasted costs and other losses.","Amira must serve a second notice to complete giving a further 10 working days before she is entitled to end the contract.","Amira may rescind the contract, recover the deposit with accrued interest, and claim damages for her losses from the seller.","Amira may rescind the contract, but the deposit is forfeited to the seller because it was paid to the seller's solicitor."],"a":3,"e":"A **notice to complete** makes time **of the essence**, so if the **seller** fails to complete by the end of the notice period the buyer may **rescind** the contract, have the **deposit repaid with accrued interest**, and **claim damages** for its losses (SC 7.5.2). She is not confined to contractual compensation, and one valid notice is enough. Forfeiture of the deposit is the **seller's** remedy against a defaulting buyer, not a consequence of where the deposit is held."},{"t":"lease-contents","p":"FLK2","q":"A 15-year lease of retail premises in Norwich provides for an upwards-only rent review at the end of year 5. The landlord may start the review by serving a notice 'not later than six months before the review date'. The lease does not say that time is of the essence and contains no break clause. The landlord served the notice two months late.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the late notice?","o":["The notice is invalid, because any deadline in a lease must be met exactly or the right is lost.","The notice is valid, because rent review time limits are presumed not to be of the essence.","The notice is valid only if the tenant agrees in writing to accept it despite the delay.","The notice is invalid, and the rent can now be reviewed only at the next review date.","The notice is valid, but the reviewed rent can take effect only from six months after it was served."],"a":1,"e":"**Time limits in a rent review procedure are presumed not to be of the essence** (United Scientific Holdings v Burnley BC), so a late notice is still valid unless the lease **expressly** makes time of the essence or the timetable is **linked to a break clause**. Neither applies here, so the review proceeds and the new rent runs from the review date. The tenant's agreement is not needed and the right is not postponed to the next review."},{"t":"lease-contents","p":"FLK2","q":"Fairbanks Design Ltd holds a 10-year lease of offices with a tenant's break at year 5, conditional on the tenant having paid 'all rent, interest and other sums due under this lease' by the break date and giving vacant possession. The tenant served a valid break notice and left the offices empty. After the break date, the landlord points out that £85 of interest on a late quarter's rent was never paid.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the break notice?","o":["The break is effective, because a sum as small as £85 is ignored under the rule that the law does not concern itself with trifles.","The break is effective, because the tenant substantially complied with the conditions and gave vacant possession.","The break is effective, but the landlord may sue the tenant for the £85 of interest as a debt.","The break is effective only if the landlord, having been told of the unpaid interest, chooses to waive it.","The break fails and the lease continues, because the conditions of a break clause must be strictly complied with."],"a":4,"e":"**Break conditions are applied strictly**: the tenant must comply exactly with any condition by the break date, and **substantial compliance is not enough**. Because the condition expressly covered interest and £85 remained unpaid, the break fails and the lease, with its rent, continues. There is no general trifles exception for an express payment condition, and the landlord is under no duty to waive the shortfall."},{"t":"lease-dealings","p":"FLK2","q":"Seren Bakeries Ltd holds a lease of a shop containing a covenant not to assign 'without the landlord's consent, such consent not to be unreasonably withheld'. Ten weeks ago it sent the landlord a written application for consent to assign to a well-established buyer, with full references and accounts. The landlord raised one question, which was answered the same day, and has said nothing since.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Seren Bakeries Ltd's position?","o":["The landlord is treated as having consented, because it did not reply within 28 days of receiving the application.","The landlord is under no time limit, so the tenant must wait until it decides or withdraw the application.","The landlord has probably breached its statutory duty to decide in a reasonable time, so damages may be claimed.","The tenant must prove that the landlord's delay is unreasonable before it can bring any claim against the landlord.","The landlord can delay for as long as it wishes, because the covenant does not mention any period for a decision."],"a":2,"e":"Under the **Landlord and Tenant Act 1988 s.1**, a landlord receiving a written application under a qualified covenant must, **within a reasonable time**, give consent unless it is reasonable not to, and give written notice of its decision with reasons; a reasonable time is measured in **weeks, not months**. A landlord in breach is liable in **damages for breach of statutory duty**, and the **burden of proof is on the landlord** to show it acted reasonably. There is no deemed consent after 28 days and the covenant need not specify a period."},{"t":"lease-dealings","p":"FLK2","q":"In 1992 a landlord granted a 35-year lease of a factory to Lowther Engineering Ltd. In 2004 Lowther assigned the lease, with consent, to Pennine Plastics Ltd, which assigned it with consent in 2019 to Arkwright Metals Ltd. Pennine gave no direct covenant to the landlord. Arkwright failed to pay the rent due on 24 June 2026, and on 1 August 2026 the landlord served a notice on Lowther claiming that rent.\n\nCan the landlord recover the unpaid rent from Lowther?","o":["Yes, because Lowther remains liable for the whole term under an old lease and the notice was served within six months.","No, because Lowther was released from the tenant covenants automatically when it assigned the lease in 2004.","No, because Lowther would be liable for a later tenant's breach only if it had given an authorised guarantee agreement.","No, because the landlord must first sue Arkwright to judgment before it can bring a claim against any former tenant.","No, because liability passed to Pennine on the 2004 assignment and Pennine is liable for Arkwright's breaches instead."],"a":0,"e":"The lease was granted **before 1 January 1996**, so it is an **old lease**: the **original tenant remains liable for the whole term** through privity of contract, even after assigning. To claim a fixed charge such as rent from a former tenant the landlord must serve a **s.17 notice within 6 months** of the sum falling due (LTCA 1995 s.17), which it did. Automatic release and AGAs apply only to leases granted on or after 1 January 1996, and Pennine was liable only while it held the lease."},{"t":"lease-remedies","p":"FLK2","q":"Harbourside Storage Ltd holds a lease of a warehouse used only for business. The quarterly rent of £15,000 and a service charge of £4,000, which the lease reserves as rent, were both due three weeks ago and remain unpaid. The landlord wants to instruct an enforcement agent to take control of goods at the warehouse to recover £19,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the landlord's use of Commercial Rent Arrears Recovery (CRAR)?","o":["CRAR is available for the full £19,000, because the lease reserves the service charge as rent.","CRAR is not available, because the landlord must first obtain a court judgment for the arrears.","CRAR is not available, because the arrears must have been outstanding for at least three months.","CRAR is available only for the £15,000 rent, plus interest and VAT, not the service charge.","CRAR is available for the full £19,000, but only after the landlord has forfeited the lease."],"a":3,"e":"**CRAR** lets a landlord of purely commercial premises recover arrears of **rent for possession and use of the premises** (with any interest and VAT) by taking control of goods without going to court, provided at least **7 days' rent** is owed (Tribunals, Courts and Enforcement Act 2007 Part 3). **Service charges and insurance are excluded even if the lease reserves them as rent**, so only the £15,000 can be recovered this way. No judgment is needed, three weeks' arrears is enough, and using CRAR affirms the lease, so it cannot follow forfeiture."},{"t":"lta1954","p":"FLK2","q":"A landlord and Olumide Foods Ltd agreed to grant a five-year lease of a unit, contracted out of the security of tenure provisions of the Landlord and Tenant Act 1954. The landlord served the prescribed warning notice on the tenant 9 days before the lease was completed. Before completion, the tenant's director signed a simple declaration in the prescribed form. The lease contains the agreed contracting-out wording.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the tenant has security of tenure?","o":["The tenant has no security of tenure, because the warning notice was served and the declaration was signed before completion.","The tenant has security of tenure, because notice was served under 14 days before, so a statutory declaration was needed.","The tenant has no security of tenure, because the lease contains the agreed wording, which overrides any procedural defect.","The tenant has security of tenure, because a contracting-out agreement is effective only if it is approved by the court.","The tenant has no security of tenure, because a lease for a fixed term of five years is outside the 1954 Act altogether."],"a":1,"e":"To contract out, the landlord serves a **warning notice** and the tenant makes a declaration: a **simple declaration if the notice was served at least 14 days** before the tenant entered into the lease, but a **statutory declaration** (sworn before an independent solicitor or other person authorised to take oaths) if served **less than 14 days** before. Notice was given 9 days before, so a simple declaration was not enough and the lease is **protected**. Court approval was abolished in 2004, and only tenancies of six months or less are outside the Act."},{"t":"lta1954","p":"FLK2","q":"A landlord has successfully opposed the renewal of a business tenancy of a restaurant under ground (f) (intention to demolish or reconstruct). The current tenant, Bella Cucina Ltd, has occupied the premises for 9 years. Before that, the same restaurant business was run there for 6 years by Marco Rossi, who sold the business to Bella Cucina Ltd and assigned the lease to it. The rateable value is £30,000. The lease contains a clause excluding compensation. The appropriate multiplier is 1.\n\nHow much compensation, if any, is the tenant entitled to?","o":["Nothing, because the lease contains a valid clause excluding the tenant's right to compensation.","Nothing, because compensation is payable only where the landlord relies on the tenant's breaches.","£30,000, because the tenant itself has occupied the premises for less than 14 years.","£90,000, because the business has been carried on at the premises for more than 10 years.","£60,000, because the tenant and its predecessor in the same business occupied for over 14 years."],"a":4,"e":"A tenant refused a new lease on a **no-fault ground such as (f)** is entitled to compensation of the **rateable value x the multiplier**, **doubled** where the tenant **or its predecessors in the same business** occupied for business purposes for the whole of the **14 years** before termination (LTA 1954 s.37). Bella Cucina's 9 years plus Marco's 6 years in the same business exceed 14, giving £60,000. The exclusion clause is **void** because there has been **5 years or more** of business occupation (s.38(2)), and there is no tripling."},{"t":"planning","p":"FLK2","q":"Kestrel Homes Ltd is negotiating to buy a field in Worcestershire, England, which has the benefit of a full planning permission for six houses, granted on 14 February 2023. The permission contains the standard condition on commencement and no other time condition. No work of any kind has been carried out on the site. It is now October 2026.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the status of the planning permission?","o":["The permission remains valid, because a full planning permission lasts for 5 years from the date on which it is granted.","The permission remains valid, because it runs with the land and does not expire while the land is still undeveloped.","The permission has lapsed, because development had to begin within 3 years of the date the permission was granted.","The permission remains valid until 2 years after approval of reserved matters, which have not yet been applied for.","The permission has lapsed, because development had to be completed within 3 years of the date the permission was granted."],"a":2,"e":"A **full planning permission** is subject to a condition that development must **begin within 3 years** of the grant (TCPA 1990 s.91), so a permission granted in February 2023 with no work started lapsed in February 2026 and Kestrel would need a fresh application. Permission does run with the land, but it still expires if not implemented in time, and there is no 5-year period. **Reserved matters** apply only to **outline** permission, and the condition requires development to start, not to be finished, within the period."},{"t":"planning","p":"FLK2","q":"Mehmet Yilmaz is taking a lease of a vacant unit in a parade in Coventry that was last used as a clothes shop. He plans to run a hot food takeaway from it. No external building works are proposed, and there is no relevant condition or Article 4 direction.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether planning permission is needed?","o":["Permission is needed, because a hot food takeaway is a sui generis use, so the change is a material change of use.","Permission is not needed, because shops, restaurants and takeaways all fall within Class E of the Use Classes Order.","Permission is not needed, because no external works are planned and the building's appearance will be unchanged.","Permission is not needed, because any change of use from a shop is permitted development under the GPDO.","Permission is needed only if the takeaway will open after 11pm, otherwise building regulations approval is enough."],"a":0,"e":"A **change between use classes, or to a sui generis use** (a use in no class), is generally a **material change of use** needing planning permission. A **hot food takeaway is sui generis**, so moving from a Class E shop to a takeaway needs permission, even with no external works. A restaurant is in Class E but a takeaway is not, which is the common trap, and there is no general permitted development right covering every change from a shop."},{"t":"tax","p":"FLK2","q":"Lena Fischer is buying the freehold of a building in Derby with a shop on the ground floor and a flat above, for £400,000. Use the following SDLT rates. Residential: up to £125,000, 0%; £125,001 to £250,000, 2%; £250,001 to £925,000, 5%. Non-residential and mixed use: up to £150,000, 0%; £150,001 to £250,000, 2%; over £250,000, 5%.\n\nHow much SDLT is payable?","o":["£10,000, because the flat makes the building a dwelling, so the residential rates apply to the price.","£20,000, because mixed-use property is charged at the highest rate on the whole of the price.","£7,500, because only the part of the price above £250,000 is charged to SDLT on mixed-use land.","£9,500, because mixed-use property is charged at the non-residential rates on a slice basis.","£12,500, because the flat is charged at the residential rates and the shop at the non-residential rates."],"a":3,"e":"**Mixed-use property** (part residential, part commercial) is charged at the **non-residential rates**, using the **slice system** in which each rate applies only to the part of the price within its band. So: 0% on £150,000, 2% on £100,000 (£2,000) and 5% on £150,000 (£7,500) = **£9,500**. Applying the residential rates gives £10,000, and the tax is never charged at one rate on the whole price."},{"t":"tax","p":"FLK2","q":"Nadia Rahman bought a flat in London in 2014 and lived in it as her only home for 2 years. From 2016 to 2023 she worked in Dubai under a contract of employment with a UK company, performing all her duties there, and the flat stayed empty. She returned in 2023, lived in the flat again as her only home until she sold it in 2026, making a large gain.\n\nHow much of the gain is exempt under Private Residence Relief?","o":["Only the gain for her years of actual occupation and the final 9 months, because the absence was not covered by any relief.","All of the gain, because any period of employment abroad counts as occupation if she lived there before and after.","All of the gain except that for the 4 years by which her absence exceeded the 3-year limit for absences for any reason.","All of the gain except that for the 3 years by which her absence exceeded the 4-year limit for absences required by work.","None of the gain, because relief is lost permanently once an owner lives abroad for more than 3 years in total."],"a":1,"e":"**Private Residence Relief** exempts the gain on an individual's only or main residence for periods of occupation, including **deemed occupation**: **any period** working in an **employment abroad** counts, provided the owner **actually lived there before and after** the absence (TCGA 1992 s.223(3)). Nadia lived there before 2016 and after 2023, so the whole period counts and the whole gain is exempt. The 3-year (any reason) and 4-year (UK work) caps apply to other kinds of absence, not employment abroad."}],"property-p2":[{"t":"transaction-overview","p":"FLK2","q":"Fatima Begum is selling her house for £240,000. Redemption statements show £170,000 owing on a first legal charge to a building society and £74,000 owing on a second charge to a finance company. Fatima has no other savings in the firm's client account. In the completion information form, the buyer's solicitor asks Fatima's solicitor to undertake to redeem both charges on completion.\n\nWhat should Fatima's solicitor do about the requested undertaking?","o":["Give the undertaking for both charges now, as the completion money will arrive before redemption is due.","Give the undertaking for the first charge only, and leave the buyer's solicitor to deal with the second.","Give the undertaking for both charges only once enough money to redeem them both is held for Fatima.","Give the undertaking for both charges, but make it conditional on the estate agent deferring its fee.","Give no undertaking, because the buyer's solicitor should obtain one directly from each of the lenders."],"a":2,"e":"The two charges need £244,000 but the price is only £240,000, and **an undertaking binds the solicitor personally, so a solicitor should only promise what is within its control**: here, once Fatima has paid in the £4,000 shortfall (or the finance company agrees to accept less and release its charge), as in C. Giving it now (A) would leave the solicitor personally liable for the shortfall. Undertaking only for the first charge (B) leaves a charge binding the buyer, which no buyer will accept, and deferring the agent's fee (D) does not cure the shortfall on the charges themselves. Undertakings to redeem are routinely given by the seller's solicitor, not the lenders (E)."},{"t":"title","p":"FLK2","q":"Harold Jenkins, the sole registered proprietor of a house in Newport, has died. Probate of his will was granted to both of his executors, his sons Gareth and Dylan. Gareth now lives in Canada. Dylan has signed the transfer to the buyer and says that Gareth agreed to the sale by email and does not wish to be involved further.\n\nWhat should the buyer's solicitor require before completion?","o":["Dylan's signature alone, provided that a copy of Gareth's email agreeing to the sale is attached to the transfer.","Dylan's signature alone, because any one personal representative has power to sell the deceased's land.","Dylan's appointment of a second trustee to join with him in signing the transfer and receiving the price.","A fresh grant of probate naming Dylan as the only executor, before the transfer is signed by him.","Gareth's signature on the transfer as well as Dylan's, because both executors took out the grant."],"a":4,"e":"**All the personal representatives (PRs) who have proved the will must join in a transfer of land** (Administration of Estates Act 1925 s.2(2)), so Gareth must also execute the transfer, as in E; he can sign it in Canada before a witness. A single PR may deal with personal property, but not land, so B is wrong, and an informal email (A) is not execution of a deed. Appointing a second trustee (C) is the answer for a surviving co-owner under a Form A restriction, not for a sale by two proving executors."},{"t":"title","p":"FLK2","q":"Lucy Fernandes is buying a repossessed house from Northgate Bank plc, which is selling as mortgagee under its statutory power of sale. The register shows Paul Morris as proprietor, the bank's legal charge, and a later charge in favour of a finance company. Lucy's solicitor has been given no information about Paul's arrears or how the bank came to take possession.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Lucy's position?","o":["She need not check that the power of sale has become exercisable, and will take free of Paul's interest and the later charge.","She must see evidence of Paul's arrears, and will take free of the later charge only if it is separately redeemed.","She need not check that the power of sale is exercisable, but will take subject to the later charge unless it is released.","She must obtain Paul's signature on the transfer as well, because he remains the registered proprietor.","She must see the court order for possession, as without one the bank cannot give her good title."],"a":0,"e":"When a mortgagee sells under its power of sale, **a buyer need not enquire whether the power has become exercisable** (Law of Property Act 1925 s.104), and the sale passes the property **free of the borrower's equity of redemption** (the borrower's right to the property once the debt is paid) **and of later charges**, whose holders look instead to any surplus proceeds, so A is right. The later charge does not bind Lucy (C), and she need not see evidence of arrears (B) or a possession order (E). Paul's signature (D) is not needed because the bank transfers in exercise of its own statutory power."},{"t":"searches","p":"FLK2","q":"Callum Reid is buying a newly built house on a small estate in Kent from the developer. The local authority search (CON29) states that the estate road giving access to the house is not a highway maintainable at public expense. The developer's sales brochure says that the road 'will be adopted by the council in due course'.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate step for Callum's solicitor to take?","o":["Proceed, because the council is required to adopt every road serving a new housing estate.","Proceed, because the developer's full title guarantee will cover any future road charges.","Ask the developer to grant Callum a private right of way over the estate road in the transfer.","Ask whether there is a road agreement under the Highways Act 1980 s.38 supported by a bond.","Ask the council for a highways search to confirm the extent of the public highway nearby."],"a":3,"e":"An unadopted road may leave the frontagers liable to pay for making it up to adoptable standard, so the buyer's solicitor should check for **a road agreement under Highways Act 1980 s.38 backed by a bond**, which pays for the works if the developer fails, as in D, and report the risk to Callum and his lender. A brochure promise means nothing, and there is no duty on a council to adopt every estate road (A). Title guarantee covers title, not road charges (B); a right of way (C) secures access but does nothing about making-up costs, and a highways search (E) shows the extent of the highway, which is not the issue here."},{"t":"finance-lender","p":"FLK2","q":"A solicitor acts for Tomasz Nowak on his purchase of a new-build flat for £310,000 and for his lender, which instructs on the terms of the UK Finance Mortgage Lenders' Handbook. Tomasz tells the solicitor that the developer has privately agreed to pay him £8,000 towards his legal costs and furniture after completion. He asks the solicitor not to mention this to the lender in case it reduces its valuation.\n\nWhat should the solicitor do?","o":["Keep the payment confidential, since a contribution towards costs and furniture does not change the price.","Explain that it must be reported, and if Tomasz refuses consent, stop acting for the lender without giving reasons.","Report the payment to the lender without Tomasz's consent, because the duty to the lender overrides confidentiality.","Continue to act for both clients, because checking for any incentives is the job of the lender's valuer.","Suggest that the developer instead reduces the price by £8,000, so that nothing needs to be reported."],"a":1,"e":"An incentive from the seller is a **matter the lender's instructions require to be reported**, because it affects the true value of its security, so the solicitor's duty of disclosure to the lender conflicts with the duty of **confidentiality** to Tomasz. The answer is to seek his consent and, if he refuses, **cease acting for the lender** without breaching confidentiality, as in B. Disclosing without consent (C) breaches confidentiality, while keeping quiet and acting on (A, D) risks assisting mortgage fraud; a price reduction (E) would itself have to be reported to the lender, so it does not avoid disclosure."},{"t":"finance-lender","p":"FLK2","q":"Ayesha Siddiqui is buying her first home. She has received two residential mortgage offers: one with a two-year tracker rate and one with a five-year fixed rate and higher early repayment charges. She asks her solicitor, whose firm is not authorised by the Financial Conduct Authority to advise on mortgages, which offer she should accept.\n\nHow should the solicitor respond?","o":["Recommend the fixed-rate offer, because certainty of monthly payments is in her best interests.","Recommend whichever of the two offers has the lower early repayment charges overall.","Refuse to discuss either offer, as the solicitor may not comment on mortgage terms at all.","Recommend one of the offers only after reviewing her income and outgoings in detail.","Explain the terms of each offer, but refer her to a mortgage adviser for a recommendation."],"a":4,"e":"**Advising on which regulated mortgage contract to take is a regulated activity that needs Financial Conduct Authority authorisation**, so the solicitor must not recommend a product (A, B, D), however carefully the client's finances are reviewed. But **explaining the terms and conditions of offers the client already has** is part of the solicitor's normal work, so refusing to discuss them at all (C) goes too far. The best response is E: explain both offers and refer Ayesha to an independent mortgage adviser."},{"t":"exchange","p":"FLK2","q":"Omar Farouk is selling his flat to Leah Grant and buying a house from Priscilla Mensah. At 11am, Omar's solicitor and Priscilla's solicitor carry out Part I of Law Society Formula C on the purchase, agreeing a release deadline of 3pm that day. Omar's solicitor is waiting for Leah's solicitor to be ready to exchange on the sale.\n\nWhat has Priscilla's solicitor undertaken under Part I?","o":["To exchange contracts if Omar's solicitor telephones to release them by 3pm that day.","To exchange contracts at 3pm that day whether or not Omar's solicitor calls back.","To hold Priscilla's signed contract until 3pm, with no obligation to exchange at all.","To exchange only if Omar's solicitor has sent the deposit to her before 3pm that day.","To exchange if Omar's solicitor calls back by 3pm on that day or on any later day."],"a":0,"e":"Under **Part I of Formula C** each solicitor confirms it holds its client's signed contract, and the seller's solicitor **undertakes to exchange if the buyer's solicitor calls to release by the agreed deadline that day**, as in A; Part II is the call that actually creates the contract. This lets Omar's solicitor secure the purchase commitment before exchanging on the sale, without being bound to buy until it releases. Exchange is not automatic at the deadline (B), the commitment is real, not a mere holding arrangement (C), and it lapses if no call is made that day (E); the deposit is sent after exchange as the formula directs, not as a precondition (D)."},{"t":"exchange","p":"FLK2","q":"Contracts for the sale of a house incorporating the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition, 2018 revision) were exchanged on 25 November 2026, with completion fixed for 18 December 2026. The seller, Margaret Owusu, died suddenly on 2 December. Her executors say they are unlikely to obtain a grant of probate before February 2027.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the buyer's position?","o":["The contract is frustrated by Margaret's death, so the buyer may recover the deposit and walk away.","The contract ended on Margaret's death, but the buyer may claim its wasted costs from the estate.","The contract binds Margaret's estate, and if completion is late the buyer may serve a notice to complete.","The contract binds Margaret's estate, but the buyer must wait for the grant however long it takes.","The contract may be rescinded at once by the buyer, because completion cannot take place on time."],"a":2,"e":"**Death of a party after exchange does not end the contract**: it binds the deceased's personal representatives, who must complete, so A and B are wrong. If they cannot complete on 18 December, they are in breach: the buyer can claim **contractual compensation** for the delay and, if ready, able and willing, **serve a notice to complete** making time of the essence, as in C. The buyer need not wait indefinitely (D), but nor can it rescind before a notice to complete has expired (E), because time is not initially of the essence."},{"t":"pre-completion","p":"FLK2","q":"Arjun Patel is buying a registered freehold house from Brightwater Lettings Ltd, which has two directors and no company secretary. The seller's solicitor sends Arjun's solicitor a copy of the TR1 to show that it is ready for completion. It has been signed on the company's behalf by one director, Kofi Boateng, alone. There is no witness and the company's seal has not been used.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the transfer has been validly executed?","o":["It is validly executed, because any one director may sign a deed on behalf of a company.","It is validly executed, provided the board passed a resolution authorising Kofi to sign it.","It is not validly executed, because a company can execute a deed only under its common seal.","It is not validly executed: it needs a second director to sign, or a witness to Kofi's signature.","It is not validly executed, because a company secretary must sign alongside one of the directors."],"a":3,"e":"Under the **Companies Act 2006 s.44**, a company executes a document by its **common seal**, by the signatures of **two authorised signatories** (two directors, or a director and the secretary), or by **one director signing in the presence of a witness who attests**, so D is right. One director signing alone without a witness (A) is not enough, and a board resolution (B) does not change the statutory formalities. A seal is only one option (C), and as the company has no secretary a second director's signature will do (E)."},{"t":"pre-completion","p":"FLK2","q":"A seller is selling a freehold shop, let to a tenant at a rent of £36,500 a year, for £350,000 under the Standard Commercial Property Conditions (3rd edition, 2018 revision). The buyer paid a 10% deposit on exchange. Before completion, the seller received the rent of £9,200 for the quarter from 1 October to 31 December in advance. Completion takes place on 15 November. Rent accrues at an equal daily rate of £100. A special condition provides that the seller is treated as owning the property for the whole of the completion day.\n\nWhat sum should the completion statement show as due from the buyer on completion?","o":["£305,800","£310,400","£310,300","£319,600","£345,400"],"a":1,"e":"The price less the £35,000 deposit is £315,000. **Under the special condition the seller owns the property for the whole of the completion day**, so the buyer is entitled to the rent for 16 November to 31 December (46 days × £100 = £4,600), and because the seller received it in advance it is **credited to the buyer**, giving **£310,400** (B). C wrongly gives the buyer the completion day as well (47 days), A credits the whole quarter, D adds the apportionment instead of deducting it, and E forgets to deduct the deposit."},{"t":"completion","p":"FLK2","q":"On 2 June 2026, Eleni Christou completed her purchase of an unregistered freehold cottage in Cumbria by a conveyance from the seller. Through an oversight, her solicitor never sent an application for first registration to HM Land Registry. The omission was discovered on 20 August 2026.\n\nWhat is the position regarding the legal estate in the cottage?","o":["The legal estate has revested in the seller, who holds it on a bare trust for Eleni.","Eleni keeps the legal estate, but she is liable to a fixed penalty from HM Land Registry.","Eleni keeps the legal estate, but her search priority has been lost to any later entries.","The sale is void, so the seller must repay the price and Eleni must return the cottage.","Eleni's interest in the cottage has ended, leaving her only a claim against her solicitor."],"a":0,"e":"A conveyance of unregistered freehold land on sale triggers **compulsory first registration, which must be applied for within 2 months of completion** (Land Registration Act 2002 s.6). If it is not, **the transfer becomes void as regards the legal estate, which revests in the seller on a bare trust for the buyer** (s.7), as in A; Eleni remains owner in equity, so E is wrong, and a fresh conveyance will be needed. The sale itself is not undone (D), and the consequence is not a penalty (B) or a lost search priority (C)."},{"t":"completion","p":"FLK2","q":"The solicitors acting on the sale of a house have agreed to use the Law Society Code for Completion by Post. On completion day, the buyer's solicitor sends the completion money by CHAPS, and it reaches the seller's solicitor's client account at 11am. The seller's solicitor then emails to say that it will charge the buyer's solicitor £150 plus VAT for acting as its agent, and will send the dated transfer once that fee is paid.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the seller's solicitor's position under the Code?","o":["It acts for both the seller and the buyer on completion, so it may charge each of them a reasonable fee.","It acts only for the seller, so the buyer's solicitor must attend in person if it wants the documents.","It may charge the fee, because acting as the buyer's solicitor's agent falls outside its own retainer.","It must complete now, but may hold the transfer until the buyer's solicitor has paid the agency fee.","It acts as the buyer's solicitor's agent without charge, so it should complete and send the documents."],"a":4,"e":"Under the **Code for Completion by Post the seller's solicitor acts as the buyer's solicitor's agent for completion, without charge**, following its instructions, so E is right and A and C are wrong. Once the money arrives it should complete, date the transfer, report completion, and **send the transfer and other documents to the buyer's solicitor the same day**, so withholding them for a fee (D) breaches the Code. It does not act for the buyer as a client, but the point of the Code is that personal attendance (B) is unnecessary."},{"t":"delayed-completion","p":"FLK2","q":"Ruth Abara exchanged contracts to purchase a terraced house in Salford for £360,000 on the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition, 2018 revision), paying £36,000 on exchange. The agreed interest rate in the contract is 7% a year. Ruth sent the draft transfer 3 days late, which is a period of default on her part. The seller's tenant would not move out, so completion slipped from 6 November to 13 November. Use a 365-day year.\n\nWho must pay contractual compensation, and how much?","o":["The seller pays Ruth £483.29.","The seller pays Ruth £248.55.","The seller pays Ruth £276.16.","The seller pays Ruth £434.96.","Ruth pays the seller £186.41."],"a":2,"e":"Under **SC 7.2 the party whose total period of default is greater pays compensation for the period by which it exceeds the other's**: the seller's 7 days less Ruth's 3 days gives 4 days. **When the seller pays, interest is on the full price** (the deposit is deducted only when the buyer pays), so £360,000 × 7% × 4/365 = **£276.16** (C). A ignores Ruth's own default, B and D wrongly deduct the deposit, and E makes the party with the shorter default pay."},{"t":"delayed-completion","p":"FLK2","q":"Aldgate Print Ltd agreed to buy a freehold industrial unit for £900,000 under a contract incorporating the Standard Commercial Property Conditions (3rd edition, 2018 revision) without amendment. The seller completed 10 days late because it had not cleared its equipment from the unit. As a result, Aldgate Print Ltd had to pay its current landlord an extra £6,000 to stay on in its old premises.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Aldgate Print Ltd's remedy for the delay?","o":["It may claim contractual compensation at the contract rate on the full price for the 10 days.","It may claim contractual compensation at the contract rate on the price less the deposit.","It has no remedy, because time was not of the essence of the completion date.","It has no contractual compensation, but may claim damages for the losses it can prove.","It may rescind the contract and recover the deposit, because the seller completed late."],"a":3,"e":"Under **SCPC 10.3 contractual compensation for late completion is payable only by the buyer**, so a late seller pays none (A, B), unlike under the residential SCS. But late completion is still a **breach of contract**, so the buyer may claim **common law damages** for losses it proves, such as the extra £6,000, subject to remoteness and mitigation, as in D; time not being of the essence (C) prevents termination, not damages. Rescission (E) is available only after a notice to complete has been served and ignored."},{"t":"contract","p":"FLK2","q":"Jonah Clarke exchanged contracts on 1 October to buy a house for £280,000, under the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition, 2018 revision) without amendment, with completion on 29 October. On 15 October a storm tore off the roof, and the house cannot be lived in until the roof is replaced. Jonah had not insured the house. The seller has made a claim on her own buildings insurance.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Jonah's position?","o":["He may rescind the contract, because the damage has made the house unusable for its purpose as a home.","He must complete at the full price, because the property has been at his risk since exchange.","He must complete, but the price is reduced by the cost of repairing the damage to the roof.","He must complete at the full price, but is entitled to the proceeds of the seller's insurance.","He may delay completion until the seller has repaired the roof at her own expense."],"a":1,"e":"Under **SC 5.1.1** of the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition) the **property is at the buyer's risk from exchange**, so the storm damage does not let Jonah escape the contract or reduce the price, and he must complete at the full price, as in B. Rescission because the property has become unusable (A) and a seller who keeps the risk until completion are the old 4th edition rules, which the 5th edition reversed. The seller need not insure the property (SC 5.1.2) and **s.47 of the Law of Property Act 1925 is excluded** (SC 5.1.6), so Jonah has no claim to the seller's insurance money (D). Nothing in the SCS reduces the price for the repair cost (C) or lets a buyer delay completion until repairs are done (E). This is why a buyer should insure from exchange."},{"t":"contract","p":"FLK2","q":"Bethan Morgan bought an unregistered cottage in Powys from the executors of the late Iris Lloyd, who had bought it in 1995. The contract and conveyance were made with limited title guarantee. After completion, Bethan discovered that in 2015 Iris had granted her neighbour a legal easement by deed to run a drain under the garden. The executors genuinely knew nothing about it, and it was not disclosed.\n\nCan Bethan sue the executors on the covenants for title?","o":["No, because limited title guarantee covers only incumbrances the executors created or knew of.","Yes, because every title guarantee implies that the property is free from all third-party rights.","Yes, because limited title guarantee covers every incumbrance created since the last sale for value.","No, because covenants for title merge with the contract and cannot be enforced after completion.","Yes, but only if the executors could reasonably have discovered the easement before the sale."],"a":0,"e":"With **limited title guarantee** the seller covenants only that it **has not itself incumbered the property and is not aware that anyone else has done so since the last disposition for value** (LP(MP)A 1994 s.3(3)), so the executors, who neither granted the easement nor knew of it, are not in breach, as in A. B overstates any title guarantee, E describes the wider **full title guarantee** covenant (which excludes only incumbrances the seller does not and could not reasonably know about), and C misstates the limited covenant. Covenants for title are designed to be enforced after completion (D), which is why personal representatives normally give only limited title guarantee."},{"t":"lease-contents","p":"FLK2","q":"Hartley Analytics Ltd holds a full repairing lease of a whole office building. The landlord must insure against defined 'Insured Risks', which do not include terrorism because cover was unavailable. Rent is suspended only if the building is damaged by an Insured Risk, and the tenant's repairing covenant excludes only damage by an Insured Risk. The lease says nothing else about uninsured risks. The building has been badly damaged by a terrorist explosion.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the tenant's position?","o":["Rent is suspended, because the landlord must reinstate damage however it was caused.","Rent is suspended, and the lease ends automatically if the building is not rebuilt in 3 years.","Rent remains payable, but the landlord must rebuild the building at its own expense.","The lease is frustrated, so neither party has any further obligations under it.","Rent remains payable, and the tenant's repairing covenant extends to the damage."],"a":4,"e":"**Rent suspension and the exclusion from the tenant's repairing covenant usually apply only to damage by an insured risk**, so damage by an uninsured risk falls outside both and the tenant must keep paying rent and, under a full repairing covenant, repair, as in E. That is why tenants negotiate an **uninsured risks** clause requiring the landlord to rebuild or giving a right to terminate; this lease has none, so A, B and C are wrong. Frustration of a lease (D) is extremely rare and unlikely where the lease has allocated the risk of damage."},{"t":"lease-dealings","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2015 a landlord granted a 15-year lease of a warehouse to Orchard Print Ltd. The lease contains a covenant not to assign without the landlord's consent, such consent not to be unreasonably withheld. In 2024 Orchard Print Ltd assigned the lease to Vale Packaging Ltd without asking for consent, and the landlord took no action at the time. Vale Packaging Ltd has not paid the rent due on 29 September 2026. On 10 October 2026 the landlord served a notice under s.17 of the Landlord and Tenant (Covenants) Act 1995 on Orchard Print Ltd.\n\nCan the landlord recover the unpaid rent from Orchard Print Ltd?","o":["No, because the lease was granted after 1995, so Orchard Print Ltd was released when it assigned.","No, because by taking no action the landlord is treated as having consented to the assignment.","Yes, because the assignment was made in breach of covenant, so Orchard Print Ltd was not released.","Yes, but only if Orchard Print Ltd gave an authorised guarantee agreement when it assigned.","No, because an assignment without consent is void, so Vale Packaging Ltd is a trespasser."],"a":2,"e":"For a lease granted after 1995 the tenant is released on a lawful assignment, but **an assignment in breach of covenant is an excluded assignment, so the outgoing tenant is not released** (LTCA 1995 s.11) and remains liable until the next lawful assignment, as in C. The s.17 notice was served within **6 months** of the rent falling due, so the claim is in time. A ignores the excluded assignment rule, an authorised guarantee agreement (D) is irrelevant where there was no release, the landlord's inaction is not consent (B), and an assignment without consent still transfers the lease (E) although it is a breach."},{"t":"lease-remedies","p":"FLK2","q":"Moorfield Properties Ltd is the landlord of a shop let on a 15-year lease with 6 years left. The tenant, which is solvent, has ignored repeated requests to repair the shopfront and roof. The lease allows the landlord, after giving notice, to enter, carry out repairs the tenant has failed to do, and recover the cost from the tenant as a debt. The repairs will cost £30,000, but the landlord's surveyor says the disrepair reduces the value of the freehold by only £4,000.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate remedy for the landlord?","o":["Sue for damages for breach of the repairing covenant, which will recover the full £30,000.","Serve a s.146 notice and claim damages, subject to the tenant's right to serve a counter-notice.","Seek an order for specific performance requiring the tenant to carry out the repairs.","Do the work under the clause after notice, and recover the £30,000 cost as a debt.","Forfeit the lease at once by peaceable re-entry, without serving any notice, and re-let."],"a":3,"e":"A **self-help (Jervis v Harris) clause** lets the landlord do the repairs and **recover the cost as a debt, not damages**, so neither the **s.18(1) cap** on damages at the diminution in value of the reversion nor the **Leasehold Property (Repairs) Act 1938** applies, making D the best route to the full £30,000. A damages claim (A, B) would be capped at about £4,000 and, as the lease was for 7+ years with 3+ left, subject to the 1938 Act counter-notice procedure. Specific performance against a tenant (C) is exceptional, and forfeiture for disrepair (E) needs a s.146 notice first."},{"t":"lease-remedies","p":"FLK2","q":"A landlord learnt on 1 September 2026 that its tenant had underlet the premises, in breach of an absolute covenant against underletting. The lease contains a forfeiture clause. On 15 September the landlord's managing agent sent the tenant its usual demand for the rent due on 29 September, marked 'without prejudice', and the tenant paid it. The landlord now wants to forfeit the lease because of the underletting.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the landlord's position?","o":["It may forfeit, because the rent demand was marked 'without prejudice'.","It cannot forfeit, as demanding rent after learning of the breach waived it.","It may forfeit, because the breach continues for as long as the underlease exists.","It may forfeit, but only after a s.146 notice requiring the underlease to be ended.","It cannot forfeit, because forfeiture is available only where rent is unpaid."],"a":1,"e":"A landlord **waives the right to forfeit if, knowing of the breach, it demands or accepts rent falling due after the breach**, because this recognises the lease as continuing, and **'without prejudice' wording does not prevent waiver** (A), so B is right. An unlawful underletting is a **once-and-for-all breach**, so the waiver is permanent, unlike a continuing breach such as disrepair where the right revives (C); a s.146 notice (D) cannot revive a waived right. Forfeiture is available for breaches other than non-payment of rent (E), subject to a s.146 notice."},{"t":"lta1954","p":"FLK2","q":"Hollis Optics Ltd holds a 10-year lease of a shop, protected by Part II of the Landlord and Tenant Act 1954, expiring on 25 March 2027. On 1 May 2026 it served a valid s.26 request on its landlord, proposing a new tenancy starting on 1 April 2027. The landlord did not respond. On 15 July 2026 the landlord wrote to say that it intends to occupy the shop for its own business and will oppose renewal.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the landlord's ability to oppose a new tenancy?","o":["It cannot oppose renewal, because it did not serve a counter-notice within 2 months of the request.","It may oppose under ground (g) at any time before the court hears the tenant's application.","It may oppose under ground (g), provided it has owned its interest for more than 5 years.","It need not oppose, because only a landlord may start the statutory renewal process.","It may oppose by serving a hostile s.25 notice in reply to the tenant's s.26 request."],"a":0,"e":"A landlord who receives a **s.26 request** and wishes to oppose renewal must serve a **counter-notice within 2 months** stating its grounds; **if it does not, it cannot later oppose** the grant of a new tenancy, as in A. The deadline here was 1 July 2026, so the July letter is too late, whatever the landlord's length of ownership (B, C). A tenant with a fixed term of more than one year may start the process by s.26 request (D), and once a s.26 request has been served the landlord cannot serve a s.25 notice (E)."},{"t":"lta1954","p":"FLK2","q":"Kemal Aydin has run a barber's shop from the same unit under four successive fixed-term leases, each for 6 months, the first starting on 1 January 2025 and the latest ending on 31 December 2026. None of the leases was contracted out of the Landlord and Tenant Act 1954 or contains any provision for renewal or extension. The landlord says Kemal must leave on 31 December 2026 because none of his leases has exceeded 6 months.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Kemal's current tenancy is protected by Part II of the 1954 Act?","o":["It is not protected, because each of his tenancies is for a fixed term of 6 months or less.","It is not protected, because a series of short lettings is treated in law as a licence.","It is protected, but only if the parties have agreed in writing that the Act is to apply.","It is not protected, unless Kemal has been in occupation for a period of more than 5 years.","It is protected, because Kemal has now been in occupation for a period of more than 12 months."],"a":4,"e":"A fixed-term tenancy of **6 months or less is excluded** from the Act, **unless it provides for renewal or extension beyond 6 months, or the tenant (with any predecessor in the same business) has already been in occupation for more than 12 months** (LTA 1954 s.43(3)). Kemal has occupied since January 2025, so his current tenancy is protected, as in E, and A ignores this exception. A lease does not become a licence by being short (B), protection arises automatically without agreement (C), and the 5-year period (D) relates to excluding compensation, not to whether the Act applies."},{"t":"planning","p":"FLK2","q":"Priya Nair is buying a detached house in Hampshire, England, in October 2026. The seller built a two-storey side extension without planning permission. Evidence shows the extension was substantially completed in June 2021, and the local planning authority has never taken any action about it. There is no suggestion of concealment.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the authority can still take enforcement action against the extension?","o":["It can enforce until June 2031, because a 10-year limit applies to all operational development.","It can enforce at any time, because there is no time limit for building without permission.","It cannot enforce only if the seller obtained a certificate of lawfulness within 4 years.","It cannot enforce, because the extension was substantially completed before 25 April 2024.","It can enforce, because the 4-year limit was abolished in England for all existing breaches."],"a":3,"e":"Building without permission is **operational development**. In England the limit is now 10 years, but **the old 4-year limit still applies where the works were substantially completed before 25 April 2024**, so this extension became **immune** in June 2025 and the authority cannot enforce, as in D. The 10-year rule (A) applies only to works completed on or after that date, so the abolition was not retrospective (E), and there is a time limit (B). A certificate of lawfulness (C) is useful evidence of immunity, but immunity does not depend on obtaining one."},{"t":"tax","p":"FLK2","q":"Seabrook Insurance Ltd, an insurer whose supplies are all exempt from VAT, is acquiring a new office block in Brighton for its head office at £1,200,000. The developer that built it finished construction 20 months ago and has never opted to tax. The sale contract uses the Standard Commercial Property Conditions (3rd edition, 2018 revision) unamended and is silent on VAT.\n\nWhat is the VAT position on the acquisition?","o":["No VAT is payable, because the developer has not opted to tax the building.","VAT of £240,000 is payable on top of the price, and the insurer cannot recover it.","No VAT is payable, because the first sale of a new building by its developer is zero-rated.","VAT is payable, but it is included within the £1,200,000 because the contract is silent.","VAT of £240,000 is payable on top of the price, and the insurer can reclaim it from HMRC."],"a":1,"e":"**The sale of the freehold of a new commercial building (less than 3 years old) is compulsorily standard-rated**, whether or not the seller has opted to tax (A), so VAT at 20% (£240,000) is charged. **Under the SCPC the price is exclusive of VAT**, so it is added on top (D is the SCS rule), and as the insurer makes only exempt supplies it cannot recover the input VAT (E), making B right. Zero-rating (C) applies to the first sale of a new dwelling (or certain residential or charitable-use buildings) by the person constructing it, not to an office."}],"property-p3":[{"t":"transaction-overview","p":"FLK2","q":"A solicitor acts for Tobias Lindqvist, who is buying a terraced house in Hull. The seller, Doreen Achterberg, has decided not to instruct a solicitor or licensed conveyancer and is dealing with the sale herself. She telephones Tobias's solicitor and asks him to explain what a special condition in the draft contract means for her and whether she should agree to it.\n\nHow should the solicitor respond?","o":["Explain the special condition fully and advise her whether to accept it, because she has no adviser of her own.","Refuse to speak to her at all and insist that every communication is made in writing through the estate agent.","Say that he acts only for Tobias, take no unfair advantage of her, and suggest she gets her own advice.","Agree to act for her as well on the sale, provided both she and Tobias give informed consent in writing.","Answer her question in a way that favours Tobias, because he owes duties only to his own client."],"a":2,"e":"Under the SRA Code (para 1.2) a solicitor **must not take unfair advantage of an unrepresented party**. He should explain that he **acts only for his own client**, so he cannot advise her, and recommend that she takes independent advice. Advising her on whether to accept the clause (option A) would amount to acting for her against Tobias's interests, and acting for both seller and buyer is almost never allowed because their interests conflict. Answering in a slanted way would be taking unfair advantage, and refusing all contact is unnecessary."},{"t":"transaction-overview","p":"FLK2","q":"A local estate agent has offered to recommend a firm of solicitors to its sellers in return for the firm paying the agent £200 for each sale instructed. The firm agrees. Graham Nwosu, who is selling his flat in Sheffield through the agent, is recommended to the firm and instructs it. The matter does not involve personal injury or legal aid.\n\nWhat must the firm do in relation to the referral arrangement?","o":["Refuse Graham's instructions, because solicitors are prohibited from paying for any referral of a client.","Pay the agent but keep the arrangement confidential, because it is a commercial matter between the firm and agent.","Tell Graham about the arrangement only if he asks how the firm came to be recommended to him.","Pass the £200 cost on to Graham as a disbursement, so that he bears the cost of his own referral.","Inform Graham of the financial arrangement with the agent and ensure its advice to him stays independent."],"a":4,"e":"Referral fees are permitted in conveyancing (the statutory ban applies to personal injury work), but under the SRA Code (para 5.1) the firm must ensure that the **client is informed of any financial arrangement** with an introducer, and its advice must remain **independent** and in the client's best interests. Keeping the arrangement confidential, or disclosing it only if asked, does not meet that duty. Charging the fee to Graham as a disbursement would be misleading, because it is the firm's own business cost, not a payment made on his behalf."},{"t":"title","p":"FLK2","q":"Dennis and Marion Haughton are the registered proprietors of a house in Stockport. The proprietorship register contains a Form A restriction. Marion died last year, leaving all her property by will to their daughter, Kerry. Dennis is now selling the house to Abdul Karim, and the official copies still show both names.\n\nWhat should Abdul's solicitor require before completion?","o":["That a second trustee is appointed to join Dennis in the transfer, so that the price is paid to two trustees.","A certified copy of Marion's death certificate, so that Dennis can sell alone as the surviving joint owner.","Kerry's written consent to the sale, because she is entitled to Marion's beneficial share under the will.","A statutory declaration by Dennis that he is now solely and beneficially entitled to the house.","An assent of Marion's share by her executors in favour of Dennis, followed by a transfer by Dennis alone."],"a":0,"e":"A **Form A restriction** shows the owners held as **tenants in common**, so Marion's beneficial share passed under her will to Kerry rather than to Dennis by survivorship. A sole surviving proprietor cannot give a valid receipt for capital money, so a **second trustee must be appointed** and the price paid to two trustees, which **overreaches** Kerry's interest (detaches it from the land so it attaches to the sale money). A death certificate alone works only for beneficial joint tenants with no restriction, and Kerry's consent does not satisfy the restriction or overreach her interest."},{"t":"title","p":"FLK2","q":"Chloe Barnard is selling an unregistered freehold cottage in Herefordshire. In November 2026 her solicitor sends an epitome of title containing: (1) a 2001 deed of gift of the cottage from Ann Davies to her son, Ben Davies; (2) a 2009 conveyance on sale from Ben Davies to Chloe; and (3) a 2012 legal mortgage by Chloe to a bank, with a receipt endorsed on it.\n\nWhich document should the buyer's solicitor accept as the root of title?","o":["The 2001 deed of gift, because it is the oldest document in the epitome and is more than 15 years old.","The 2012 legal mortgage, because a mortgage that has been discharged is the strongest form of root.","None of them, because the seller must show a chain of title going back at least 30 years.","The 2009 conveyance on sale, because it is a dealing for value that is at least 15 years old.","The 2001 deed of gift together with the 2009 conveyance, because two roots are needed where a gift appears."],"a":3,"e":"The epitome must start with a **good root of title at least 15 years old**: a document dealing with the whole legal and equitable interest, describing the property adequately and casting no doubt on the title, typically a **conveyance on sale** or legal mortgage. The 2009 conveyance is 17 years old and was a sale, so the buyer had every reason to investigate title then. A gift is a poor root because the donee normally does not investigate title, the 2012 mortgage is under 15 years old, and the 30-year period was cut to 15 years in 1969."},{"t":"searches","p":"FLK2","q":"Harriet Quaye is buying a farmhouse in a Lincolnshire village. The registered title shows that the seller bought the property for value in 2018 and was registered as proprietor. There is no notice of chancel repair liability on the register. The farmhouse stands next to the parish church, and Harriet's surveyor warns that the church chancel needs major work.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the risk to Harriet of chancel repair liability?","o":["She will be liable if the land was once rectorial land, because chancel repair liability is an overriding interest.","An unprotected liability cannot bind her, because the land has been sold for value and registered since October 2013.","She will be liable only if the liability is registered as a local land charge and shown on the LLC1 search.","An unprotected liability will bind her for the first 10 years after completion, so she must buy indemnity insurance.","She will be liable only if she goes into actual occupation of the farmhouse after completion."],"a":1,"e":"Since **13 October 2013** chancel repair liability is **no longer an overriding interest**. It binds a buyer of registered land only if it is **protected by a notice**, and a registered disposition for value since that date takes free of an unprotected liability. The seller's 2018 purchase was such a disposition, and there is still no notice, so the liability cannot bind Harriet. It is not a local land charge, and actual occupation is irrelevant because the liability attaches to ownership."},{"t":"searches","p":"FLK2","q":"Before exchange, Kofi Boateng's solicitor obtained an official search of the local land charges register (LLC1) for a house in Northampton, and it revealed no entries. Kofi completed the purchase. Two months later the council told him that it had made a tree preservation order over an oak tree in the garden a year before the search, but had failed to register it. Kofi has had to abandon his plans to build a garage where the tree stands and the house is worth less.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Kofi's position?","o":["The order does not bind him, because a local land charge that was not registered is void against a buyer for value.","The order binds him, and he has no remedy because the risk of an unregistered charge lies with the buyer.","The order binds him, but he may claim compensation for his loss because his search did not reveal it.","The order does not bind him, because the council is prevented from enforcing anything it failed to register.","The order binds him, and his only remedy is a claim against the seller for failing to disclose the tree."],"a":2,"e":"A **local land charge binds a buyer even if it was not registered**, so the tree preservation order (an order protecting trees from being cut down or lopped without consent) is enforceable against Kofi. However, a buyer who made an official search that failed to reveal an existing but unregistered charge can claim **compensation** for loss suffered (Local Land Charges Act 1975 s.10). Treating the charge as void wrongly applies the land charges rule for unregistered land, and a claim against the seller is not the remedy: the statute protects the searching buyer through s.10 compensation."},{"t":"finance-lender","p":"FLK2","q":"A solicitor acts for Imran Chaudhry and his lender on Imran's purchase of a house for £350,000 with a residential mortgage of £280,000. The lender has instructed the solicitor on the terms of the UK Finance Mortgage Lenders' Handbook. Imran tells the solicitor that his widowed mother, aged 68, is giving him £40,000 towards the price and will move into the house with him.\n\nWhat should the solicitor do?","o":["Nothing further, because a gift from a close relative does not affect the lender's security in any way.","Insist that Imran's mother becomes a joint borrower, because anyone who contributes to the price must be.","Apply for a restriction on the title to protect his mother's share, without informing the lender.","Ask Imran's mother to give the solicitor a personal guarantee of the mortgage before completion.","Check the source of the gift, obtain a gifted deposit letter and the mother's signed consent to the mortgage."],"a":4,"e":"A contributor who lives in the property could later claim a **beneficial interest** binding the lender. The lender will require a **gifted deposit letter** confirming that the money is a gift and gives her **no interest**, and an **occupier's consent** (a signed waiver of any rights against the lender) from anyone aged **17 or over** who will live there but is not a borrower; the source of funds must also be checked for money laundering purposes. She need not be a joint borrower, and protecting her share while concealing it from the lender would breach the solicitor's duty to the lender."},{"t":"contract","p":"FLK2","q":"Margot Leclerc exchanged contracts to buy a bungalow in Shropshire on the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition, 2018 revision) without amendment. The particulars described the property as including a narrow strip of land beside the garage. Before completion, Margot discovers that the strip, which is about 4% of the plot, is in fact owned by the neighbour. The seller honestly believed that it was part of the bungalow, and Margot can still park and use the garage.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Margot's remedies?","o":["She must complete, but she may claim damages for the error in the description of the property.","She may rescind the contract and recover her deposit, because the property does not match its description.","She must complete, and she has no remedy, because the buyer accepted the description on exchange.","She may refuse to complete until the seller buys the strip from the neighbour at the seller's expense.","She may rescind the contract, but she loses her deposit because the seller acted honestly."],"a":0,"e":"Under **SC 7.1**, if a plan or statement in the contract is inaccurate, the buyer is entitled to **damages**, but may **rescind** only if the error results from **fraud or recklessness**, or if the buyer would be obliged to accept property **differing substantially** (in quantity, quality or tenure) from what the contract led her to expect. The seller was honest, and a small strip that does not affect use of the bungalow is not a substantial difference, so Margot must complete and claim damages. Option C is wrong because SC 7.1 expressly gives a remedy for misdescription, and option D is wrong because the seller cannot be made to acquire land owned by a third party."},{"t":"contract","p":"FLK2","q":"Pemberton Homes Ltd wants to buy a paddock in Wiltshire for residential development, but only if it can obtain planning permission for eight houses. The landowner is willing to exchange now on a contract conditional on planning permission. The buyer's draft simply says that the contract is 'subject to the buyer obtaining satisfactory planning permission'.\n\nWhich of the following is the most important change the landowner's solicitor should seek?","o":["Replace the condition with a clause making the buyer's deposit refundable in all circumstances.","Delete the condition, because a contract for the sale of land cannot lawfully be made conditional.","Make the condition subject to the landowner's approval of each planning application before it is submitted.","Define the permission required, set out who must apply and when, and add a long-stop date for satisfying it.","Add a clause allowing the buyer to waive the condition at any time before completion takes place."],"a":3,"e":"A conditional contract is lawful, but the condition must be **precise**: it should **define the permission** required (e.g. for eight houses, free of onerous conditions), state **who must apply, by when** and whether appeals must be pursued, and include a **long-stop date** after which either party can withdraw. Vague wording such as 'satisfactory planning permission' risks the contract being **void for uncertainty** and could leave the landowner bound indefinitely. Approval of applications or a waiver clause may be useful extras, but neither fixes the uncertainty or the open-ended timescale."},{"t":"exchange","p":"FLK2","q":"Wren Logistics Ltd has exchanged contracts to buy a registered freehold yard in Doncaster. Because the seller needs time to relocate, completion is fixed for nine months after exchange. The buyer's solicitor is worried that the seller might charge or sell the yard to someone else in the meantime.\n\nWhat should the buyer's solicitor do to protect the buyer?","o":["Make an official search with priority (OS1) now, which will protect the buyer until completion.","Apply to enter a notice of the contract in the charges register of the seller's title.","Register a Class C(iv) land charge against the seller's name at the Land Charges Department.","Apply for a Form A restriction on the seller's title, preventing any sale by a sole proprietor.","Lodge a caution against first registration with HM Land Registry, naming the buyer as cautioner."],"a":1,"e":"After exchange the buyer has an **estate contract**, an equitable interest in the land. Where completion will be **delayed**, it should be protected by a **notice in the charges register** (registered land), so that it binds anyone who later acquires an interest in the yard. An OS1 search gives priority for only **30 working days** and is a pre-completion step; a Class C(iv) land charge and a caution against first registration are for **unregistered** land; and a Form A restriction concerns co-owners holding as tenants in common."},{"t":"pre-completion","p":"FLK2","q":"A solicitor acts for Lerato Dlamini and her lender on her purchase, with a mortgage, of a registered freehold flat in Brighton from Gary Fenton. Contracts have been exchanged and completion is in eight working days.\n\nWhich pre-completion searches should the solicitor make?","o":["A bankruptcy-only search (K16) against Gary Fenton and an official search of the whole (OS1) against the title.","A full search of the Land Charges register (K15) against Gary Fenton and a bankruptcy-only search against Lerato.","An official search of the whole (OS1) against the title and a bankruptcy-only search (K16) against Lerato.","An official search of the whole (OS1) against the title only, because no bankruptcy search is needed in registered land.","A bankruptcy-only search (K16) against both Gary Fenton and Lerato, with no search of the register."],"a":2,"e":"For registered land the buyer makes an **OS1** search against the seller's title, which reveals any new entries (including a bankruptcy entry against the seller) and gives **30 working days' priority**. Because the solicitor also acts for the lender, a **K16 bankruptcy-only search must be made against the borrower**, Lerato, to show she is not bankrupt or subject to a petition. A bankruptcy search against the seller is unnecessary in registered land, and a K15 search is used for **unregistered** land."},{"t":"completion","p":"FLK2","q":"Contracts were exchanged on the purchase of a house in Cambridge for £475,000, with completion fixed for 30 November 2026. The seller had already moved out, and with the seller's agreement the buyer, Esther Mwangi, moved in and took possession on 2 November 2026 to start redecorating. Completion took place on 30 November as planned.\n\nBy when must Esther's SDLT return be delivered and the tax paid?","o":["By 14 December 2026, being 14 days after completion.","By 30 December 2026, being 30 days after completion.","By 14 November 2026, being 14 days after exchange.","By 2 December 2026, being 30 days after possession.","By 16 November 2026, being 14 days after possession."],"a":4,"e":"The SDLT return must be delivered and the tax paid **within 14 days after the effective date**. The effective date is normally completion, but it is **earlier if the contract is substantially performed**, for example when the **buyer takes possession** before completion. Esther took possession on 2 November, so the deadline was 16 November. The 30-day period applies to Land Transaction Tax in Wales, and exchange is not the effective date."},{"t":"delayed-completion","p":"FLK2","q":"Nkechi Obi agreed to sell her house to Ross Gallagher for £420,000 on the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition, 2018 revision). Ross paid a 10% deposit of £42,000 on exchange. He failed to complete on the completion date, and Nkechi, who was ready, able and willing to complete, served a valid notice to complete. Ross has failed to complete within the period of the notice. House prices have fallen and Nkechi expects to resell for about £370,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Nkechi's remedies?","o":["She may rescind, keep the deposit, resell the house and claim damages for any loss exceeding the deposit.","She may rescind and keep the deposit, but keeping the deposit means she cannot claim any further damages.","She may only claim compensation at the contract rate, because the contract continues until Ross completes.","She must return the deposit before rescinding, and then sue Ross for the whole of her loss on the resale.","She must serve a second notice to complete before she can rescind or forfeit the deposit."],"a":0,"e":"Once a notice to complete has been served, **time is of the essence**. Where the **buyer fails to comply**, **SC 7.4** allows the seller to **rescind**, **forfeit and keep the deposit** with accrued interest, **resell** the property and claim damages, giving credit for the deposit kept. Her expected loss of about £50,000 plus costs exceeds the £42,000 deposit, so she may recover the shortfall. She need not return the deposit or serve a second notice, and she is not confined to contract-rate compensation."},{"t":"lease-contents","p":"FLK2","q":"Fernhill Crafts Ltd holds a lease of a shop in Harrogate which contains a covenant 'not to use the premises other than as an art gallery without the landlord's consent'. The lease says nothing about the landlord acting reasonably. The tenant wants to change the use to a café, which needs no structural alteration and is permitted under planning law. The landlord says it will consent only if the tenant pays it £8,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the landlord's position?","o":["It must give consent unless refusal is reasonable, and it may require the £8,000 as a condition.","It must give consent unless refusal is reasonable, and it cannot require any payment at all.","It may refuse consent for any reason, and it may lawfully insist on the £8,000 as its price.","It may refuse consent even unreasonably, but it cannot demand the £8,000 as a fine for consent.","It cannot refuse consent, because the new use is permitted under planning law."],"a":3,"e":"For a **qualified user covenant**, **LTA 1927 s.19(3)** provides that, where the change involves no structural alteration, the landlord **may not charge a fine** (a payment) for its consent, although it may ask for a reasonable sum for any loss in value of its reversion and its legal costs. Unlike covenants against assignment or improvements, there is **no implied term that consent cannot be unreasonably withheld**. So the landlord may refuse, even unreasonably, but cannot demand £8,000 as the price of consent. Planning permission does not oblige a landlord to consent."},{"t":"lease-contents","p":"FLK2","q":"Ashworth Media Ltd is negotiating a 10-year lease of a whole Victorian office building. The landlord's draft contains a tenant's covenant 'to keep the premises in good and substantial repair'. The tenant's surveyor reports that the roof leaks and several window frames are rotten at the start of the term.\n\nWhat is the most important protection the tenant's solicitor should seek?","o":["A covenant by the landlord to repair the roof and window frames whenever they fail during the term.","A schedule of condition, limiting the tenant's duty to the condition it records.","An express statement that the tenant's improvements will be disregarded on every rent review.","A tenant's break clause conditional only on the tenant giving vacant possession at the break date.","A rent-free period equal to the time the tenant estimates it will take to repair the building."],"a":1,"e":"A covenant to **keep** premises in repair includes an obligation to **put** them into repair first, so without protection the tenant would have to fix the existing leaking roof and rotten frames. The usual answer is a **schedule of condition** (a photographic record of the state of the premises at the start), with the tenant obliged only to keep them in **no better condition** than shown. A landlord covenant to repair a whole building let to one tenant is unlikely to be agreed, and the other options do not limit the repair liability."},{"t":"lease-dealings","p":"FLK2","q":"Bramwell Optics Ltd has agreed to sell the lease of its shop to Kaur Opticians Ltd under a contract incorporating the Standard Commercial Property Conditions (3rd edition, 2018 revision) without amendment. The lease requires the landlord's consent to assign. Completion was fixed for 1 February 2027. On that date the landlord still has not decided whether to consent, although both parties have complied with their obligations.\n\nWhat is the effect under the contract?","o":["Either party may rescind by notice, because consent was not given 3 working days before completion.","The buyer may complete without consent and the seller must indemnify it against any forfeiture.","Completion is postponed until 5 working days after the seller tells the buyer consent is given.","The contract is automatically void, and the seller must return the deposit to the buyer with interest.","Completion is postponed indefinitely, and neither party may rescind while the application is pending."],"a":2,"e":"Under **SCPC 11.3**, if the landlord's consent has not been obtained by the completion date, **completion is postponed until 5 working days after the seller notifies the buyer that consent has been given**. Either party may rescind only if consent is still not given **6 months after the original completion date** (here 1 August 2027). The 3-working-day right to rescind is the **SCS 8.3** rule for residential sales, and the contract is neither void nor postponed without limit."},{"t":"lease-dealings","p":"FLK2","q":"On 3 July 2026 a landlord granted Delacroix Dental Ltd a 12-year lease of a surgery out of the landlord's registered freehold title. The lease was properly executed as a deed and the tenant moved in. Through an oversight, the tenant's solicitor has not yet applied to HM Land Registry. It is now November 2026.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the tenant's position?","o":["The lease is void, because it was not registered within 2 months of the grant.","The tenant holds a legal lease, because a deed was used and the tenant is in occupation.","The tenant must ask the landlord to grant a fresh lease, because the time for registration has passed.","The lease takes effect as a legal lease protected as an overriding interest, so no application is needed.","The tenant does not yet hold the legal estate, but its solicitor can still apply to register the lease now."],"a":4,"e":"A lease granted for **more than 7 years** out of a registered title is a registrable disposition, and **the legal estate does not pass until it is registered** (LRA 2002 s.27); until then the tenant has only an equitable interest. There is **no fixed deadline** for registering a disposition of registered land, so the solicitor should apply now (ideally protected by an OS1 priority period). The **2-month** limit applies to **first registration** of unregistered land, and only leases of **7 years or less** override as legal leases."},{"t":"lease-remedies","p":"FLK2","q":"Quayside Leisure Ltd holds a 15-year lease of a gym, granted in 2020, which contains a forfeiture clause. The tenant is in breach of its covenant to repair the roof and external walls. The landlord's solicitor is preparing a notice under s.146 of the Law of Property Act 1925, as a first step towards forfeiture.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the notice must contain?","o":["The breach, a requirement to remedy it and pay compensation, and the tenant's right to serve a counter-notice in 28 days.","The breach and a requirement to remedy it, with a warning that the landlord will forfeit by peaceable re-entry after 14 days.","The breach, a requirement to remedy it and to pay compensation, and notice that the tenant may apply for relief within 6 months.","The breach and the cost of the works, because a notice for disrepair need not ask the tenant to remedy the breach.","The breach, a demand for the cost of the works as a debt, and notice that no court permission is required."],"a":0,"e":"A **s.146 notice** must specify the **breach**, require it to be **remedied** if capable of remedy, and require **compensation** if the landlord wants it. Because the lease was granted for **7 years or more** and has **3 years or more** left, the **Leasehold Property (Repairs) Act 1938** also requires the notice to tell the tenant of its right to serve a **counter-notice within 28 days**; if it does, the landlord needs the **court's permission** to forfeit. A demand for the cost as a debt is the self-help route where the landlord does the work itself, not a s.146 notice."},{"t":"lta1954","p":"FLK2","q":"Greystone Properties Ltd let a restaurant to Amara Kitchens Ltd on a 10-year lease, protected by Part II of the Landlord and Tenant Act 1954, which expires on 29 September 2027. On 1 August 2026 the landlord served a notice under s.25, in the prescribed form, opposing renewal on ground (f) and specifying 29 September 2027 as the termination date.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the notice?","o":["It is valid, because the termination date specified is not earlier than the date the lease expires.","It is valid, but the tenant must apply to court for a new tenancy within 2 months of receiving it.","It is invalid, because a landlord must wait for the tenant's s.26 request before opposing renewal.","It is invalid, because it was given more than 12 months before the termination date it specifies.","It is invalid, because a landlord relying on ground (f) must specify a date after the contractual expiry."],"a":3,"e":"A **s.25 notice** must be given **not less than 6 nor more than 12 months before the termination date** it specifies, and that date cannot be earlier than the contractual expiry date. From 1 August 2026 to 29 September 2027 is almost 14 months, so the notice is **invalid** even though the date itself is acceptable. The landlord need not wait for a s.26 request, and the tenant's deadline for applying to court is the termination date in the notice, not 2 months after service."},{"t":"lta1954","p":"FLK2","q":"Marlowe Leisure Ltd bought the freehold of a shop on 1 March 2022, subject to a protected business tenancy that had been in place for many years and expires on 31 May 2027. In October 2026 Marlowe served a valid hostile s.25 notice specifying 31 May 2027 as the termination date, opposing renewal on ground (g) because it intends to run its own business from the shop. The tenant argues that Marlowe bought the freehold less than five years ago.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Marlowe can rely on ground (g)?","o":["It cannot, because it bought its interest less than five years before the date it served the s.25 notice.","It can, because the five years run back from the termination date, and it bought before then.","It cannot, because a landlord that bought subject to a protected tenancy may never rely on its own occupation.","It can, because the five-year rule applies only to landlords that are individuals, not to companies.","It cannot, because ground (g) requires the landlord to have owned the reversion for at least ten years."],"a":1,"e":"Under the **five-year rule** (LTA 1954 s.30(2)), a landlord cannot rely on ground (g) if it **purchased its interest within the five years ending with the termination of the current tenancy**, and the premises were let on a protected tenancy throughout. The period runs back from the termination date (31 May 2027), so it began on 31 May 2022, after Marlowe bought on 1 March 2022. Marlowe is therefore not caught, although it bought less than five years before serving the notice. The rule applies to companies as well, and there is no ten-year requirement."},{"t":"planning","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2023 Leila Haddad took a lease of a café in Kent, England. The planning permission for the café contains a condition that it must not open to customers outside 8am to 8pm. Since early 2024 Leila has stayed open until 11pm. The local planning authority has now served on her a breach of condition notice requiring her to comply with the condition within 28 days.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Leila's position?","o":["She may appeal to the Secretary of State within 28 days, and the appeal suspends the notice until it is decided.","She may ignore the notice until an enforcement notice is served, because a breach of condition notice is only a warning.","She has no right of appeal against the notice, and failing to comply within the period is a criminal offence.","The breach is immune from enforcement, because more than 4 years have passed since the café opened in 2023.","She may apply for a lawful development certificate, which automatically suspends the notice until decided."],"a":2,"e":"There is **no right of appeal against a breach of condition notice**, and failure to comply within the stated period is a **criminal offence**. This is why an authority may prefer it to an **enforcement notice**, which can be appealed and is suspended by an appeal. A breach of condition falls within 'any other breach', with a **10-year** enforcement period, and in any event the breach began only in 2024. Applying for a certificate does not suspend the notice."},{"t":"planning","p":"FLK2","q":"Thornbury Developments Ltd obtained outline planning permission for twelve houses on a site in Oxfordshire, England, on 10 March 2025. The permission contains the standard statutory time conditions. The final reserved matter was approved on 1 December 2026. The company asks by when it must begin the development.\n\nWhich of the following is the latest date by which development must begin?","o":["10 March 2028, being 3 years from the grant of the outline permission.","1 December 2029, being 3 years from the approval of the final reserved matter.","10 March 2030, being 5 years from the grant of the outline permission.","10 March 2030, being the later of 5 years from the outline permission and 2 years from final approval.","1 December 2028, being 2 years from the approval of the final reserved matter."],"a":4,"e":"For an **outline permission** in England, applications for approval of **reserved matters** must be made within **3 years** of the grant, and development must **begin within 2 years of the final approval of the last reserved matter** (TCPA 1990 s.92(2)(b)). Final approval was on 1 December 2026, so development must start by 1 December 2028. The 3-year date is the deadline for reserved matters applications, and the 'later of 5 years and 2 years' formula is the old rule, not the current law in England."},{"t":"tax","p":"FLK2","q":"Oliver Brennan owns the house in Leeds that he lives in. He is buying a new house in York for £450,000 to be his main residence, completing before he has sold his Leeds house, which he will put on the market straight away. Use the following SDLT residential rates: up to £125,000, 0%; £125,001 to £250,000, 2%; £250,001 to £925,000, 5%. The higher rates for additional dwellings add 5% to each band.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Oliver's SDLT position?","o":["He must pay £35,000, but he can reclaim £22,500 if he sells the Leeds house within 36 months.","He must pay £12,500, because he is replacing his main residence and the higher rates do not apply.","He must pay £35,000, and none of it can be reclaimed, because he owned two dwellings at completion.","He must pay £35,000, but he can reclaim £22,500 if he sells the Leeds house within 12 months.","He must pay £12,500, provided he gives an undertaking to sell the Leeds house within 36 months."],"a":0,"e":"The **higher rates** apply because at the end of the day of purchase Oliver owns **two dwellings** and has **not yet disposed of** his old main residence: 5% on £125,000 (£6,250) + 7% on £125,000 (£8,750) + 10% on £200,000 (£20,000) = £35,000. If he **sells the old main residence within 36 months**, he can claim a **refund** of the surcharge, leaving the standard £12,500 (0% + £2,500 + £10,000), so £22,500 is reclaimable. He cannot avoid the higher rates at completion by promising to sell, and the refund window is 36 months, not 12."},{"t":"tax","p":"FLK2","q":"Castleford Estates Ltd has opted to tax an office building in Leeds that is let to three tenants. It is selling the building, with the benefit of the leases, to Penrose Investments Ltd, a VAT-registered property investment company that will continue to let it. The parties want the sale to be treated as a transfer of a going concern so that no VAT is charged on the price.\n\nWhat must Penrose do to achieve this?","o":["Register for VAT within 30 days after completion and send HMRC a copy of each of the leases.","Nothing, because the sale of a let building is automatically outside the scope of VAT.","Ask the seller to revoke its option to tax before completion, so that the sale becomes exempt.","Opt to tax the building itself and notify HMRC of its option before the time of the supply.","Opt to tax the building and notify HMRC within 30 days after completion of the purchase."],"a":3,"e":"The sale of a let building with the tenancies can be a **transfer of a going concern (TOGC)**, outside the scope of VAT, if the buyer continues the letting business and is VAT-registered. Because the seller has **opted to tax**, the **buyer must also opt and notify HMRC before the tax point** (normally completion), and confirm that its option will not be disapplied. Notifying within 30 days after completion is too late for TOGC purposes, the treatment is not automatic, and the seller's option is generally irrevocable after the 6-month cooling-off period."}],"tort-p1":[{"t":"negligence","p":"FLK1","q":"Gemma, who holds a current first-aid certificate, is waiting at a bus stop when an elderly man she has never met collapses beside her, apparently with a heart attack. Not wanting to be late for a job interview, she boards her bus without giving first aid or calling for help. Other people call an ambulance ten minutes later, and the delay leaves the man with permanent heart damage. He wants to sue Gemma in negligence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Gemma's position?","o":["She is not liable, because there is no general duty to help a stranger and nothing brings her within an exception.","She is liable, because it was reasonably foreseeable that the man would be harmed if she did nothing to help.","She is liable, because the court will apply the Caparo factors and find proximity from her presence at the scene.","She is not liable, because only paid medical professionals on duty owe a duty to help people who fall ill.","She is liable, because her first-aid training made her the person best placed to prevent the harm."],"a":0,"e":"The law imposes **no general duty to act or to rescue**: a pure omission (a failure to act) gives rise to liability only if an exception applies, such as an **assumption of responsibility**, control over the claimant, a special relationship, or the defendant **creating the danger**. None applies to Gemma, however morally blameworthy her conduct. Foreseeability of harm is not enough to create a duty to act, and her first-aid training does not by itself create one (Stovin v Wise; Robinson v Chief Constable of West Yorkshire)."},{"t":"negligence","p":"FLK1","q":"Dr Okafor, an orthopaedic surgeon, recommends a wrist operation to Hannah, a professional violinist. Hannah tells him she is worried about anything that could affect her playing. The operation carries a 1% risk of permanent nerve damage causing loss of fine finger movement. Dr Okafor does not mention this risk, and a responsible body of hand surgeons would not mention a risk that small. The risk materialises. Hannah says that, if told, she would have postponed the operation to get a second opinion.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Dr Okafor breached his duty of care?","o":["He did not breach his duty, because a responsible body of hand surgeons would also have chosen not to mention the risk.","He did not breach his duty, because a 1% risk is too small for any reasonable patient to regard as significant.","He breached his duty, because he should have warned of a risk he knew or ought to have known Hannah would treat as significant.","He breached his duty, because a surgeon must disclose every known risk of an operation, however small it may be.","He breached his duty only if Hannah can show the operation itself was performed below the standard of a competent surgeon."],"a":2,"e":"Advice on risks is governed by the **Montgomery** test, not Bolam: a doctor must take reasonable care to ensure the patient knows of any **material risk**, meaning one a reasonable person in the patient's position would think significant, or one the doctor **knows or should know this particular patient** would think significant. Hannah's profession and stated concern make the 1% risk material to her, so the support of a responsible body of surgeons (the Bolam test, which governs diagnosis and treatment) is no answer. There is no duty to disclose every risk, and the quality of the surgery itself is irrelevant to a failure-to-warn claim."},{"t":"negligence","p":"FLK1","q":"Priya is walking along a pavement past a builders' merchant. A pallet of roof tiles being lifted by a forklift truck at the edge of the merchant's yard slides off the forks, falls over the low boundary wall and strikes her. Only the merchant's employees operate the forklift. Priya has no idea why the pallet fell, and the merchant has offered no explanation.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Priya can establish breach of duty?","o":["She cannot succeed, because a claimant must always identify the specific careless act that caused the harm.","She can succeed only if the merchant is convicted of an offence arising from the same incident.","The burden of proof shifts to the merchant, which must prove on the balance of probabilities that it was not negligent.","The merchant is strictly liable, because loads falling from vehicles onto a highway are treated as inherently dangerous.","The court may infer negligence from the facts, unless the merchant offers a plausible explanation consistent with care."],"a":4,"e":"Under **res ipsa loquitur** (\"the thing speaks for itself\"), where the thing that caused the harm was under the defendant's **control**, the accident would not ordinarily happen without negligence, and the cause is unknown, the court **may infer** breach. It is only an evidential inference: it does **not reverse the burden of proof**, and the defendant can answer it with a reasonable explanation showing it took care. It does not create strict liability, and a criminal conviction is one way of proving breach, not a requirement (Scott v London and St Katherine Docks)."},{"t":"negligence","p":"FLK1","q":"Crews from the local fire and rescue service attend a fire at Delroy's warehouse. On arrival, the officer in charge orders the warehouse's working sprinkler system to be switched off because he wrongly believes it is hindering his crews. As a result, the fire spreads and destroys the whole building. Had the sprinklers stayed on, the fire would have been contained to one room.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the fire service's liability to Delroy in negligence?","o":["It is not liable, because fire services are immune from negligence claims arising from operational decisions.","It is liable, because by switching off the sprinklers it made Delroy's position worse rather than merely failing to help.","It is not liable, because a fire service owes no duty to the owner of a burning building, even once it has arrived.","It is liable, because its statutory duty to fight fires means it owes a duty of care to every property owner in its area.","It is not liable, because the fire was started by someone else and the service merely failed to put it out."],"a":1,"e":"A public body is generally in the same position as a private person: it owes no duty merely to **confer a benefit** by preventing harm, but it does owe a duty not to **make things worse** by a positive act. Switching off a working sprinkler system was a positive act that created a danger which would not otherwise have arisen, so a duty is owed (Capital & Counties plc v Hampshire CC). Arriving and fighting a fire ineffectively would not by itself create a duty, but that is not this case. There is no blanket immunity, and a statutory function does not on its own create a duty to every property owner."},{"t":"negligence","p":"FLK1","q":"Two 13-year-old schoolgirls, Ama and Chloe, are play-fencing with plastic rulers during break, a game that is common in their school playground. Ama's ruler snaps and a fragment enters Chloe's eye, causing serious injury. An adult would have recognised that brittle rulers could snap and cause injury, but there is no evidence that the girls were playing roughly. Chloe sues Ama in negligence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the standard of care Ama must meet?","o":["That of a reasonable adult, because the standard of care in negligence is objective and does not vary with age.","That of Ama herself, judged by her own maturity, understanding and experience of playground games.","That of a reasonable adult, reduced by whatever discount the court considers fair to reflect her youth.","That of an ordinarily careful and reasonable 13-year-old in the same circumstances as Ama.","No standard applies, because a child under 14 cannot be liable in negligence for injuring a playmate."],"a":3,"e":"A child defendant is judged by the standard of an **ordinarily careful and reasonable child of the same age** (Mullin v Richards), not that of an adult. The test is still **objective**: it is not based on Ama's own personal maturity or experience. There is no minimum age for liability in tort, but on these facts a reasonable 13-year-old playing a common game would probably not foresee the injury, so Ama is unlikely to be in breach."},{"t":"causation-remoteness","p":"FLK1","q":"Bilal, aged 14, falls from a climbing frame and is taken to A&E. The doctor negligently fails to X-ray his hip, and a fracture is not found until five days later. Bilal develops a permanent deformity of the hip. Expert evidence is that, even with prompt treatment, there was a 75% chance that the deformity would have developed anyway.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Bilal's claim against the hospital trust for the deformity?","o":["He recovers damages for the lost chance, valued at 25% of full damages for the deformity.","He recovers full damages, because the doctor's negligence materially increased the risk of deformity.","He recovers nothing for it, because he cannot prove on balance that the delay caused the deformity.","He recovers 75% of full damages, because that reflects the risk the hospital failed to reduce.","He recovers full damages, because breach is proved and harm followed the doctor's failure to act."],"a":2,"e":"In personal injury the claimant must prove on the **balance of probabilities** that the breach caused the harm, and a lost chance of a better medical outcome is **not** recoverable in itself (Gregg v Scott; Hotson). There was a 75% chance the deformity would have happened anyway, so on balance it was caused by the fall, not the delay, and the claim for it fails entirely (Hotson v East Berkshire AHA, on similar facts). Percentage awards for a lost chance are available only for **financial** loss depending on what a third party would have done, and the material-increase-in-risk exception is confined to mesothelioma. Proving breach is not enough: causation must also be shown, though Bilal could recover for any extra pain the delay itself caused."},{"t":"causation-remoteness","p":"FLK1","q":"Ruth's ankle is badly sprained when she is knocked over by a cyclist riding negligently on the pavement. Her doctor tells her not to put weight on the ankle for six weeks. Two weeks later, while still on crutches and alone at home, Ruth climbs a stepladder to clear leaves from a gutter. The ankle gives way and she falls, breaking her hip.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the cyclist's liability for the broken hip?","o":["He is not liable for it, because Ruth's unreasonable decision to climb the ladder broke the chain of causation.","He is liable for it, because the hip would not have been broken but for the weakness caused by the sprain.","He is liable for it, because a defendant must take the claimant as he finds her, including her later conduct.","He is liable for it, but damages are reduced for Ruth's contributory negligence in climbing the ladder.","He is not liable for it, because a broken hip is a different type of injury from a sprained ankle."],"a":0,"e":"A claimant's own later conduct breaks the **chain of causation** (a **novus actus interveniens**, or new intervening act) if it is **wholly unreasonable** in the circumstances (McKew v Holland & Hannen & Cubitts: going down steep stairs unaided with an injured leg). Climbing a ladder alone, against medical advice and while on crutches, is likely to be that unreasonable, so the cyclist is liable only for the sprain. But-for causation is satisfied but is not enough on its own, and the eggshell skull rule concerns the claimant's existing characteristics, not her later choices. A reduction for contributory negligence would be the answer only if her conduct were careless but not so unreasonable as to break the chain."},{"t":"causation-remoteness","p":"FLK1","q":"A delivery driver negligently reverses into Faisal in a car park, causing a deep cut to his leg. A person in normal health would have recovered fully within a few weeks. Faisal, however, has a rare blood-clotting disorder that nobody could have known about. The wound bleeds heavily, he needs several transfusions and a month in hospital, and he is left with permanent damage to the leg.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the driver's liability?","o":["He is liable only for the injury a healthy person would have suffered, because the disorder was unforeseeable.","He is not liable for the permanent damage, because the disorder is an intervening event breaking the chain.","He is liable for all the harm only if a reasonable driver would have known about Faisal's disorder.","He is liable for all the harm, because a physical injury was foreseeable and he must take Faisal as he finds him.","He is liable for all the harm, but damages are reduced because Faisal had not disclosed his disorder."],"a":3,"e":"Under the **eggshell skull rule**, once harm of a foreseeable **type** (here physical injury) is caused, the defendant is liable for its full extent even if it is much worse because of the claimant's pre-existing weakness (Smith v Leech Brain). Under the remoteness test only the type of harm must be foreseeable, not its extent or the precise way it occurs. A pre-existing condition is not an intervening act, and there is no reduction because Faisal was under no duty to warn anyone of it."},{"t":"economic-loss","p":"FLK1","q":"Contractors working on a road negligently cut through an underground electricity cable owned by the electricity company. Power to Bethany's bakery is cut off for 12 hours. A batch of bread in her ovens is spoiled: the ingredients and labour in it cost £600, and she would have made a profit of £200 on selling it. She also cannot bake the four further batches she had planned for that day, losing £800 profit.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Bethany can recover from the contractors in negligence?","o":["£600 only, because the cost of the spoiled bread is the only loss involving physical damage to her property.","£800, being the cost of the spoiled batch and the profit on it, but not the profit on the further batches.","£1,600, because all of her losses were a reasonably foreseeable consequence of cutting the cable.","Nothing, because the cable belonged to the electricity company, so all of her loss is purely economic.","£1,000, being the profit on the spoiled batch and the further batches, but not the cost of the spoiled bread."],"a":1,"e":"Physical damage to the claimant's property, and economic loss **consequential** on that damage, are recoverable in negligence, but **pure economic loss** caused by a careless act is not (Spartan Steel v Martin). The spoiled batch (£600) is physical damage and the £200 profit on it flows from that damage, so she recovers £800. The £800 profit on batches never made results only from the interruption of supply and is pure economic loss, however foreseeable. Ownership of the cable does not matter, because her own bread was physically damaged."},{"t":"economic-loss","p":"FLK1","q":"Grace is negotiating to buy all the shares in a small engineering company. She tells the company's accountants, Patel & Co, that she will decide whether to proceed, and at what price, on the strength of the year's accounts, which they are finalising. Patel & Co carelessly overstate the company's stock by £300,000. Grace relies on the accounts and pays too much for the shares. No disclaimer was given.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Patel & Co owed Grace a duty of care?","o":["No, because accountants owe a duty only to the company that engaged them and never to outsiders.","No, because Grace's loss is purely economic and so cannot be recovered in the tort of negligence.","No, because accounts are prepared for the shareholders as a body, so Grace's reliance was unreasonable.","Yes, because it was reasonably foreseeable that a buyer of the shares might read and rely on the accounts.","Yes, because they knew Grace would rely on the accounts for this purchase, and her reliance was reasonable."],"a":4,"e":"Pure economic loss caused by a negligent statement is recoverable where there is a **special relationship**: the defendant **assumed responsibility**, knowing the statement would be communicated to the claimant (or an identifiable class) for a **specific transaction** and that the claimant would reasonably rely on it (Hedley Byrne v Heller; Caparo v Dickman). Patel & Co knew who Grace was and why she needed the accounts, so a duty is owed. Mere foreseeability that some buyer might read the accounts would not be enough, and the point that accounts are prepared for shareholders as a body only defeats a claimant whose specific reliance the accountant did not know about."},{"t":"psychiatric-harm","p":"FLK1","q":"Isaac, aged 58, is seen by a cardiologist who negligently fails to diagnose his serious heart disease. Six months later Isaac collapses at home from a cardiac arrest and dies in front of his wife, Ngozi, who tries to resuscitate him. Ngozi develops post-traumatic stress disorder. A correct diagnosis would have led to treatment that would have prevented the collapse.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ngozi's claim against the cardiologist as a secondary victim?","o":["It succeeds, because she has a presumed close tie of love and affection and saw her husband die with her own eyes.","It fails, because Isaac's death resulted from an untreated illness rather than from an accident caused by negligence.","It fails, because she was not present when the cardiologist negligently failed to diagnose Isaac's heart disease.","It succeeds, because the collapse was a sudden and shocking event and her psychiatric illness was foreseeable.","It fails, because a spouse must prove an especially close bond of love before claiming as a secondary victim."],"a":1,"e":"A secondary victim can recover only for illness caused by witnessing an **accident** (an external, unexpected event causing injury) or its immediate aftermath; witnessing a death or medical crisis caused by a **failure to diagnose or treat** a disease is not enough (Paul v Royal Wolverhampton NHS Trust, 2024). Ngozi therefore has no claim, even though she saw the death and has a close tie. Presence is judged by reference to the accident, not the negligent act, so option C gives the wrong reason, and spouses have a **presumed** close tie, so option E is wrong."},{"t":"psychiatric-harm","p":"FLK1","q":"Lorna is walking over a zebra crossing when a van driven negligently swerves towards her. She jumps back and the van misses her by a few centimetres before hitting a lamp post. She suffers no physical injury but develops a recognised anxiety disorder, which is unusually severe because she has a history of depression.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Lorna's claim against the van driver?","o":["She must satisfy the control mechanisms for secondary victims, because she suffered no physical injury.","She cannot recover, because a person of ordinary fortitude would not develop an illness from a near miss.","She can recover only for the illness a person without a history of depression would have suffered.","She cannot recover, because damages for psychiatric harm require a physical injury as well as a mental one.","She is a primary victim, and can recover because physical injury to her was foreseeable."],"a":4,"e":"A **primary victim** is someone within the range of foreseeable **physical danger**. If **physical injury** to them was foreseeable, they can recover for a recognised psychiatric illness even though no physical injury occurred and psychiatric harm itself was not foreseeable (Page v Smith). The ordinary fortitude requirement and the Alcock control mechanisms apply only to **secondary victims** (witnesses of harm to others). Under the eggshell skull rule, Lorna recovers for the full illness despite her history of depression."},{"t":"employers-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"Kwame works as a joiner for a building firm. The firm bought a batch of chisels from a reputable national supplier. Because of a manufacturing fault that could not have been detected on inspection, the blade of Kwame's chisel shatters while he is using it properly, and a fragment injures his eye. The manufacturer has since gone into liquidation.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the building firm's liability to Kwame?","o":["It is liable, because the defect in the equipment it provided is deemed to be its own negligence.","It is not liable, because it took reasonable care by buying the chisels from a reputable supplier.","It is not liable, because Kwame's claim lies only against the manufacturer under the 1987 Act.","It is liable only if Kwame proves that it failed to inspect the chisels before issuing them.","It is liable for half his loss, with the rest recoverable from the supplier as a joint defendant."],"a":0,"e":"Under the **Employers' Liability (Defective Equipment) Act 1969 s.1**, where an employee is injured in the course of employment by a defect in equipment provided by the employer for its business, and the defect is due wholly or partly to the **fault of a third party** (here the manufacturer), the injury is **deemed** to be caused by the employer's negligence. Choosing a reputable supplier is therefore no answer, and Kwame need not prove a failure to inspect. The firm may seek a contribution or indemnity from others at fault, but that does not reduce what Kwame recovers from it."},{"t":"employers-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"Selin is an experienced roofer employed by a roofing company. The company supplies safety harnesses and keeps them in the van, but site supervisors never tell workers to wear them, and most of the crew, including Selin, routinely work without them. Selin falls from a roof while not wearing a harness and is badly injured. A harness would have prevented the fall.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome of Selin's claim against her employer?","o":["The employer is not liable, because it discharged its duty by making suitable harnesses available on site.","The employer is not liable, because Selin voluntarily accepted the risk of working without a harness.","The employer is liable, but Selin's damages are likely to be reduced for her contributory negligence.","The employer is liable in full, because an employee's carelessness cannot reduce damages for a work accident.","The employer is liable only if it was in breach of a health and safety regulation on working at height."],"a":2,"e":"An employer's non-delegable duty to provide a **safe system of work** includes taking reasonable steps to **instruct, encourage and supervise** the use of safety equipment, not merely making it available, especially where the risk is serious and workers habitually ignore it (Bux v Slough Metals). Selin's employer did nothing to ensure harnesses were worn, so it is in breach. **Volenti** (consent to the risk) rarely succeeds against an employer, but her choice not to use a harness she knew was available is **contributory negligence**, which reduces her damages. A breach of regulations is not required for a common law negligence claim."},{"t":"vicarious-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"Owen works in the payroll department of a large retailer. After being disciplined for lateness, he decides to take revenge on the company. One weekend, at home, he uses a copy of staff salary data that he made at work to post the names and salaries of 5,000 colleagues on a public website. The colleagues wish to sue the retailer for Owen's misuse of their private information.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the retailer is vicariously liable?","o":["It is liable, because Owen's job gave him access to the data and so created the opportunity for the wrong.","It is liable, because there was an unbroken sequence of events between Owen's work tasks and the disclosure.","It is not liable, because an employer can never be vicariously liable for an employee's deliberate wrongdoing.","It is not liable, because Owen was pursuing a personal vendetta rather than acting on the retailer's business.","It is not liable, because Owen's wrong was committed at the weekend and away from the retailer's premises."],"a":3,"e":"Vicarious liability requires a **close connection** between the wrong and what the employee was authorised to do, so that it can fairly be regarded as done in the course of employment. An employee pursuing a **personal vendetta** against the employer, rather than (however wrongly) furthering the employer's business, is not acting in the course of employment (WM Morrison Supermarkets v Various Claimants, 2020). Mere **opportunity** created by the job is not enough; deliberate wrongs can attract vicarious liability (as in Mohamud), and time and place are relevant but not decisive."},{"t":"vicarious-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"A gym engages Callum, a freelance fitness instructor, to run two classes a week. Callum runs his own fitness business, teaches at several other gyms, decides the content of his classes, carries his own insurance and invoices the gym monthly. During one class he negligently tells a member, Adaeze, to lift a weight far too heavy for her, and she injures her back.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the gym is vicariously liable for Callum's negligence?","o":["It is liable, because Callum was carrying out an activity that formed an integral part of the gym's business.","It is liable, because Adaeze was a member of the gym and the class took place on the gym's premises.","It is liable, because the gym chose Callum and was free to end the arrangement with him at any time.","It is not liable, because Callum was not paid a salary and so cannot be treated as an employee.","It is not liable, because Callum was in business on his own account and was not akin to an employee."],"a":4,"e":"The first stage of vicarious liability requires employment or a relationship **akin to employment**; a genuine **independent contractor** carrying on business on their own account does not qualify (Barclays Bank v Various Claimants, 2020). Callum has his own business, other clients, control over his work and his own insurance, so he is an independent contractor. Integration of the activity into the gym's business is relevant but cannot outweigh these features, and the method of payment is not decisive either way."},{"t":"defences","p":"FLK1","q":"Darius and Liam break into a warehouse at night and steal power tools. They flee in a car driven by Liam, who drives at 80 mph through a residential area to escape the police. Liam loses control and crashes, and Darius, his passenger, is seriously injured. Darius sues Liam in negligence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the most likely outcome?","o":["Darius succeeds in full, because the Road Traffic Act 1988 prevents a driver relying on any defence against a passenger.","Darius fails, because his injury arose directly from a joint criminal enterprise in which he was a participant.","Darius succeeds, but his damages are reduced for contributory negligence in getting into Liam's car.","Darius fails, because by joining in the escape he voluntarily accepted the risk of Liam's careless driving.","Darius succeeds, because illegality is a defence only where the claimant's own crime was the more serious."],"a":1,"e":"The defence of **illegality** (ex turpi causa) bars a claim where the injury arises from a **joint criminal enterprise** between claimant and defendant, because allowing it would undermine the coherence of the law (Pitts v Hunt; Patel v Mirza). Darius was injured during the getaway from a burglary they committed together, so his claim is likely to fail. **Road Traffic Act 1988 s.149** stops a driver relying on **volenti** (consent) against a passenger, so option D's reasoning is wrong, but it does not exclude illegality or contributory negligence, so option A overstates it."},{"t":"defences","p":"FLK1","q":"Rhianna is crossing a road while reading her phone and steps out between parked cars without looking. Sanjay, who is driving at 45 mph in a 30 mph zone, cannot stop and hits her. The court finds Sanjay negligent, assesses Rhianna's full damages at £120,000, and finds that she was 25% responsible for her injuries. Sanjay argues that because Rhianna was partly to blame her claim should fail.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Rhianna recovers?","o":["Nothing, because her own carelessness was a cause of the accident and so defeats her claim entirely.","£60,000, because where both parties are at fault the damages are divided equally between them.","£120,000, because Sanjay's speeding was the dominant cause and so no reduction is made.","£90,000, because her damages are reduced to the extent the court thinks just and equitable.","£30,000, because her damages are limited to the share of the fault attributed to her by the court."],"a":3,"e":"Under the **Law Reform (Contributory Negligence) Act 1945 s.1**, a claimant's own fault does not defeat the claim; instead damages are **reduced** to the extent the court thinks **just and equitable** having regard to the claimant's share in responsibility for the damage. A 25% reduction of £120,000 leaves £90,000. Equal division applies only where the court finds the parties equally to blame, a reduction is still made even where the defendant's fault was greater, and she recovers the 75% not attributed to her, not the 25% share (£30,000) that was."},{"t":"remedies","p":"FLK1","q":"Jasmine, aged 19 and unmarried, is killed by a negligent lorry driver. She had lived with her boyfriend, Ethan, for 18 months. Her parents, who are married to each other, and her 16-year-old brother are devastated by her death.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who is entitled to bereavement damages under the Fatal Accidents Act 1976?","o":["No one, because Jasmine was an adult and Ethan had not lived with her for at least two years.","Her parents, who share the fixed sum equally because they lost a child who had never married.","Ethan alone, because he was living with Jasmine as her partner in the same household when she died.","Her parents and Ethan, who divide the fixed sum equally between the three of them.","Her parents and her brother, as close family members who lived in the same household."],"a":0,"e":"**Bereavement damages** (a fixed sum, currently **£15,120**) are available only to the deceased's spouse or civil partner, a cohabiting partner of at least **two years**, and the parents of a deceased **unmarried minor** (under 18) (**Fatal Accidents Act 1976 s.1A**). Jasmine was 19, so her parents cannot claim, and Ethan had lived with her for only 18 months. Siblings are never entitled, though family members who depended on Jasmine financially could still claim loss of dependency."},{"t":"remedies","p":"FLK1","q":"Mohammed is injured at work through his employer's negligence and cannot work for a year. During that time he receives £6,000 in Universal Credit, £4,000 from a personal accident insurance policy he took out and paid for himself, and £1,500 raised for him by colleagues in a charity collection. His damages are assessed at £20,000 for pain, suffering and loss of amenity (PSLA) and £25,000 for lost earnings.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the deductions from his damages?","o":["The £6,000 is deducted from the PSLA award, and the insurance and charity payments from lost earnings.","All three payments are deducted from lost earnings, because otherwise Mohammed would be compensated twice.","Only the £6,000 is deducted, from lost earnings, and the employer repays it to the Compensation Recovery Unit.","Only the £4,000 is deducted, because insurance paid for the same injury would otherwise mean double recovery.","None of the payments is deducted, because each came from a source other than the defendant employer."],"a":2,"e":"Recoverable **state benefits** such as Universal Credit are deducted under the **Social Security (Recovery of Benefits) Act 1997**, but only from the matching head of loss (here lost earnings) and **never from PSLA**; the defendant repays them to the state through the Compensation Recovery Unit. Payments under **private insurance** the claimant paid for himself are not deducted, so he keeps the benefit of his own prudence (Bradburn v Great Western Railway). **Charitable** payments are not deducted either, because that would discourage generosity."},{"t":"occupiers-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"The owners of a small hotel engage a long-established, properly insured firm of electricians to rewire the guest bedrooms. The firm leaves a socket with live wires behind a loose faceplate, a fault that only someone with electrical training would have noticed. The hotel manager looks round each room when the work is finished. A guest, Yusuf, receives a serious electric shock from the socket.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the hotel's liability to Yusuf under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1957?","o":["It is liable, because an occupier's duty to visitors is non-delegable and cannot be passed to a contractor.","It is liable, because it should have employed a second electrician to inspect and certify the work.","It is not liable, because Yusuf's claim lies under the 1984 Act against the electricians instead.","It is not liable, because it chose a competent contractor and could not reasonably detect the fault.","It is not liable, because an occupier is never responsible for dangers created by a contractor's work."],"a":3,"e":"Under **Occupiers' Liability Act 1957 s.2(4)(b)**, an occupier is not liable for damage caused by the faulty work of an **independent contractor** if it acted reasonably in entrusting the work to the contractor, took reasonable steps to satisfy itself that the contractor was **competent**, and, where appropriate, checked that the work was properly done. Where the work is **technical**, such as electrics, the occupier is not expected to spot defects a layperson could not see (Haseldine v Daw), unlike obvious hazards. An occupier is not automatically protected, since it must choose and check reasonably, and Yusuf's remedy is a negligence claim against the electricians; the 1984 Act concerns non-visitors."},{"t":"occupiers-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"A builders' merchant's yard is fenced, but there is a gap in the fence that local people regularly use as a shortcut in the evenings after the yard has closed. The merchant knows this. Its staff dig a deep trench across the path the walkers use and leave it unlit and unfenced overnight. That evening Kelechi takes the shortcut, falls into the trench, breaks his wrist and smashes his phone.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Kelechi's claim against the merchant?","o":["He can claim for the broken wrist under the 1984 Act, but cannot claim for the phone.","He can claim for both under the 1957 Act, because the merchant tolerated the shortcut.","He can claim for both under the 1984 Act, because the merchant knew of the trespassers.","He cannot claim for either, because no duty is owed to an adult trespasser.","He cannot claim for either, because he entered private land at his own risk."],"a":0,"e":"A trespasser is owed a duty under the **Occupiers' Liability Act 1984 s.1(3)** if the occupier is aware of the danger, knows or has reasonable grounds to believe the trespasser is or may come into its vicinity, and the risk is one against which it may reasonably be expected to offer some protection; an unlit trench across a path the merchant knew was used in the evenings meets all three. However, the 1984 Act covers only **personal injury**, so damage to **property** such as the phone is not recoverable (s.1(8)). Mere toleration of trespass is not implied permission, so Kelechi was not a visitor under the 1957 Act."},{"t":"occupiers-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"Abena is staying at a hotel. Her booking confirmation and a notice by the lifts both state that the rooftop terrace closes at 10 pm and that guests must not use it after that time. At midnight Abena finds the terrace door unlocked, goes up to look at the view, and trips over a hosepipe left across the unlit terrace, injuring her knee.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the duty the hotel owed Abena when she was injured?","o":["The common duty of care under the 1957 Act, because she had entered the hotel lawfully as a guest.","No duty at all, because a guest who disobeys a notice takes full responsibility for whatever happens.","Only the more limited duty under the 1984 Act, because she was there outside the time she was permitted.","The common duty of care under the 1957 Act, but with damages reduced for her contributory negligence.","The 1957 Act duty, because leaving the door unlocked gave her implied permission to go up."],"a":2,"e":"A visitor's permission may be limited by **area**, time or purpose, and a visitor who goes beyond those limits becomes a **trespasser** for that excursion (The Calgarth: an invitation to use the staircase is not an invitation to slide down the banisters). Abena was clearly told the terrace was closed after 10 pm, so at midnight she was a trespasser there and the hotel owed her only the duty under the **Occupiers' Liability Act 1984**, if the s.1(3) conditions are met. An unlocked door does not override an express prohibition or amount to implied permission, and a trespasser is not owed no duty at all, so options B and E are wrong."},{"t":"product-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"Bronwen buys a new electric heater for her flat. A manufacturing defect causes it to overheat and catch fire. She suffers burns to her arm, the heater itself (price £90) is destroyed, and a rug in her living room worth £220 is ruined. She wants to sue the manufacturer under Part 1 of the Consumer Protection Act 1987.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what she can recover under the Act?","o":["Her burns, the rug and the heater, because all were caused by the defect and she need not prove fault.","Her burns and the rug, because together the injury and the property damage exceed £275.","Her burns and the heater, because the heater was the defective product and was her private property.","The rug and the heater only, because the combined property damage of £310 exceeds £275.","Her burns only, because the heater itself is excluded and the rug alone does not exceed £275."],"a":4,"e":"The Act covers **death and personal injury** and damage to private property, but not damage to the **defective product itself** (s.5(2)), and property damage is recoverable only if the claim **exceeds £275** (s.5(4)). The heater is excluded, and the rug at £220 is below the threshold; the threshold applies to the property damage alone, so the injury cannot be added to reach it. Bronwen can claim for the heater in contract against the seller, and for the rug in negligence (which has no threshold) if she can prove fault."},{"t":"product-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"Hamza buys an unbranded electric scooter from a small independent shop. Neither the scooter nor its packaging shows any manufacturer's name or mark. A defect in the battery causes it to explode, injuring Hamza. Two weeks later he writes to the shop asking who made or imported the scooter. The shop does not reply for six months and then says it cannot remember.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the shop's liability to Hamza under the Consumer Protection Act 1987?","o":["It is not liable, because the Act imposes liability only on producers, own-branders and importers, not retailers.","It is liable, because it failed within a reasonable time to identify the producer or importer when asked.","It is liable, because any business that supplies a defective product to a consumer is strictly liable under the Act.","It is not liable, because Hamza's claim against the shop lies only in contract for breach of satisfactory quality.","It is liable only if Hamza can prove that the shop knew or ought to have known about the defective battery."],"a":1,"e":"A mere **supplier**, such as a retailer, is liable under **Consumer Protection Act 1987 s.2(3)** only if the injured person asks it, within a reasonable time after the damage, to **identify** the producer, own-brander or importer, it is not reasonably practicable for the claimant to identify them, and the supplier **fails** within a reasonable time to identify them or its own supplier. Hamza asked promptly about an unbranded product and got no useful answer, so the shop is liable without proof of fault. A retailer is **not** liable simply for selling a defective product, which is why option C is wrong."},{"t":"nuisance-rylands","p":"FLK1","q":"Erin owns a house next to a recycling depot that runs noisy machinery through the night. Living with her are her 24-year-old son and a lodger who rents a room but has no exclusive right to occupy it. All three suffer badly disturbed sleep.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who can sue the depot's operator in private nuisance?","o":["Only Erin, because a claim in private nuisance requires a proprietary interest in the land affected.","Erin and her son, because both of them live in the house as members of the same family.","Erin and the lodger, because each of them owns or pays for their right to occupy the house.","All three of them, because each suffered the same interference with the enjoyment of the home.","None of them, because noise causing loss of sleep is not physical damage to the land itself."],"a":0,"e":"Only a person with a **proprietary interest** in the affected land (such as an owner or tenant) or **exclusive possession** of it can sue in private nuisance, because the tort protects the use and enjoyment of land rather than personal comfort as such (Hunter v Canary Wharf). Family members and lodgers without exclusive possession have no standing, however much they suffer. Interference with comfort and enjoyment, such as noise and loss of sleep, is actionable without physical damage."},{"t":"nuisance-rylands","p":"FLK1","q":"A motocross track has operated on farmland for 15 years under planning permission granted by the local council. Three years ago Gabriel bought a cottage half a mile away. The noise of weekend races is so loud that he cannot use his garden. The track's owners argue that Gabriel moved there knowing about the track, that it has planning permission, and that many local people enjoy it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the track owners' position?","o":["They have a complete defence, because Gabriel came to the nuisance knowing the track was already there.","None of their points is a defence, though they may affect whether the court grants an injunction or damages.","They have a complete defence, because the planning permission authorises the noise caused by the track.","They have a complete defence, because the track has run long enough to acquire a right by prescription.","None of their points is a defence, and the court must grant an injunction stopping the races altogether."],"a":1,"e":"**Coming to the nuisance** is not a defence, nor is the **public benefit** of the activity, and **planning permission** does not in itself authorise a nuisance (Coventry v Lawrence). **Prescription** requires 20 years of continuous actionable nuisance, so 15 years is not enough. These matters can, however, be relevant to the court's wide discretion to award **damages in lieu of an injunction** (money instead of an order to stop), and an injunction is never automatic because it is an equitable remedy."},{"t":"nuisance-rylands","p":"FLK1","q":"A specialist chemicals company stores thousands of litres of a toxic solvent in tanks at its rural site. A valve fails, through no fault of the company, and the solvent seeps through the ground onto Ifeoma's neighbouring smallholding, killing her vegetable crops. Ifeoma is also made ill by fumes from the solvent.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the company's liability to Ifeoma under the rule in Rylands v Fletcher?","o":["It is not liable, because the escape happened without any fault or negligence on the company's part.","It is liable for her crops and her illness, because liability under the rule is strict once there is an escape.","It is liable for her crops but not for her illness, because the rule does not cover personal injury.","It is not liable, because storing chemicals on an industrial site is a natural use of that kind of land.","It is liable for her illness but not her crops, because the crops were not brought onto the company's land."],"a":2,"e":"The rule imposes **strict liability** (no proof of fault) where the defendant brings onto its land something likely to do mischief if it escapes, in the course of a **non-natural** (extraordinary and unusual) use, and it **escapes** and causes foreseeable damage of the relevant type. Bulk storage of toxic chemicals is a classic non-natural use (Cambridge Water v Eastern Counties Leather), so the company is liable for the crops without fault. But the rule protects interests in land, so **personal injury is not recoverable** (Transco v Stockport MBC), and Ifeoma must claim for her illness in negligence."}],"tort-p2":[{"t":"negligence","p":"FLK1","q":"Tomasz is teaching his friend Aisha to drive in her own car. She holds a provisional licence, and before the first lesson Tomasz checked that her insurance covered him as a passenger. On her second lesson Aisha panics at a roundabout, presses the accelerator instead of the brake and hits a wall. Tomasz breaks his collarbone. Aisha says she did her best for a beginner and that Tomasz knew she was a learner.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the standard of care against which Aisha's driving will be judged?","o":["That of a reasonable learner driver who has had the same number of lessons as Aisha.","That of a reasonable learner driver, because Tomasz knew she was inexperienced.","That of a reasonably competent qualified driver, whatever her inexperience.","That of Aisha herself, judged by whether she drove as carefully as she could.","None, because Tomasz accepted the risk of her driving by agreeing to teach her."],"a":2,"e":"A learner is judged by the **objective standard of a reasonably competent qualified driver**, with no allowance for inexperience, so C is right (Nettleship v Weston). A and B are wrong because the standard does not drop for beginners, even against a passenger who knows the driver is learning. E fails because checking the insurance shows Tomasz did not agree to bear the risk of her negligence, and in any event **volenti** (voluntary acceptance of risk) cannot be relied on against a car passenger under the Road Traffic Act 1988 s.149."},{"t":"negligence","p":"FLK1","q":"Dr Adeleke, an obstetrician, decides to deliver Marta's baby using forceps rather than by emergency caesarean section. The baby suffers a shoulder injury. At trial, experts agree that a responsible body of obstetricians would have used forceps in the same situation, while another respected body would have performed a caesarean. The defence experts explain clearly how they weigh the risks of each method, and the judge finds their reasoning sound.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Dr Adeleke breached his duty of care?","o":["He breached it, because a respected body of obstetricians would have chosen a caesarean.","He breached it, because the baby's injury shows that the chosen method was unsafe.","He did not breach it, because a doctor is judged by the standard of his own experience.","He breached it, because the court must prefer the more cautious of two medical views.","He did not breach it, because a responsible body of opinion with a logical basis supports him."],"a":4,"e":"Under the **Bolam test**, a doctor is not negligent if he acted in accordance with a practice accepted as proper by a **responsible body of medical opinion**, provided that opinion can **withstand logical analysis** (Bolitho), so E is right. A and D are wrong because the existence of a different respected view does not make his choice negligent, and the court does not simply pick the more cautious one. B relies on hindsight from the outcome, and C is wrong because the standard is that of a reasonably competent obstetrician, not of the individual doctor."},{"t":"negligence","p":"FLK1","q":"Two police officers chase a suspected shoplifter through a crowded outdoor market. Although they can see Mrs Adeyemi, aged 70, standing at a stall right beside the suspect, they rugby-tackle him there. All three fall onto her and she breaks her hip. The police force argues that it owes no duty of care because the officers were catching a suspect in the course of their police work.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the officers owed Mrs Adeyemi a duty of care?","o":["A duty was owed, because the officers' own positive act foreseeably caused her physical injury.","No duty was owed, because the police owe no duty of care while they are catching suspects.","No duty was owed, because the harm was caused by the shoplifter, who was a third party.","A duty was owed only if a court decides it is fair, just and reasonable to create a new one.","No duty was owed, because a duty would make officers overly cautious in arresting suspects."],"a":0,"e":"Where a defendant's **positive act** foreseeably causes **physical injury**, an established duty of care applies, and the police are subject to the same ordinary principles as anyone else, so A is right (Robinson v Chief Constable of West Yorkshire). B and E describe a supposed blanket police immunity that does not exist; the police generally owe no duty for **omissions** (failing to protect someone from a third party), but this was their own act. D is wrong because the **Caparo** factors are only needed for novel situations, not an established category such as physical injury caused by a positive act."},{"t":"negligence","p":"FLK1","q":"A village football club's pitch is surrounded by a fence five metres high. In its 25-year history, balls have cleared the fence and landed in the lane beyond only about three times, and nobody has ever been hurt. One Saturday a ball is kicked over the fence and strikes Brenda, who is walking along the lane, breaking her glasses and cutting her face. Raising the fence would have been possible.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the club was in breach of its duty of care to Brenda?","o":["It was in breach, because it was foreseeable that a ball might leave the ground and hit someone.","It was in breach, because a higher fence could have been built to prevent balls leaving the ground.","It was not in breach, because sporting clubs cannot be liable where the activity has social value.","It was not in breach, because the risk of injury was so small that a reasonable club could ignore it.","It was in breach, because a club must ensure that no ball leaves its ground during a match."],"a":3,"e":"Breach is judged by balancing factors, and where the **likelihood of harm is very small**, a reasonable person may be justified in taking no further precautions, so D is right (Bolton v Stone). A is wrong because foreseeability alone is not enough: the risk must be one a reasonable person would not ignore. B overlooks that the court weighs the cost of a precaution against the size of the risk, E wrongly imposes strict liability, and C overstates the Compensation Act 2006 s.1, which lets the court **consider** the value of an activity but gives no immunity."},{"t":"causation-remoteness","p":"FLK1","q":"Gareth worked for three employers in turn, each of which negligently exposed him to asbestos dust: Alpha Ltd for ten years, Beta Ltd for five years and Gamma Ltd for two years. He has now been diagnosed with mesothelioma. Medical evidence cannot say which period of exposure triggered the disease. Alpha Ltd and Beta Ltd have been dissolved and no insurer for them can be traced. Gamma Ltd is solvent.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Gamma Ltd's liability to Gareth?","o":["It is liable for a share of his damages proportionate to its two years of exposure.","It is liable for all his damages, and may seek contributions from others responsible.","It is not liable, because Gareth cannot prove that its asbestos caused his disease.","It is liable only if it is shown that its exposure was the most likely cause of the disease.","It is liable for one third of his damages, because it was one of three negligent employers."],"a":1,"e":"For mesothelioma, a defendant who **materially increased the risk** of the disease by negligent exposure is liable even though but-for causation cannot be proved (Fairchild), and under the **Compensation Act 2006 s.3** each such person is liable **jointly and severally for the whole** of the damage, so B is right. A reflects the proportionate approach of Barker v Corus, which Parliament reversed for mesothelioma, and E is a similar error. C and D apply the ordinary but-for test, which is displaced in mesothelioma cases."},{"t":"causation-remoteness","p":"FLK1","q":"Rosa, a postwoman aged 45, is knocked off her bicycle by a van driver who is driving negligently. Her knee injury means she can no longer do delivery rounds and must take a lower-paid office job. Two years later Rosa is diagnosed with rheumatoid arthritis, which is unconnected with the accident. Medical evidence is that the arthritis would by itself have forced her to give up delivery work from the date of diagnosis.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the van driver's liability for Rosa's lost earnings?","o":["He is liable only up to the date the arthritis would have ended her delivery work anyway.","He is liable for the rest of her working life, because later events never reduce his liability.","He is not liable for any lost earnings, because the arthritis is the real cause of her loss.","He is liable for the rest of her working life, but with damages reduced by half for the arthritis.","He is liable only up to the date of trial, because future losses are too speculative."],"a":0,"e":"Where a **later natural illness** would have caused the same loss anyway, it is one of the **vicissitudes of life** and limits the first wrongdoer's liability to the period before it took effect, so A is right (Jobling v Associated Dairies). B states the rule for a **later tort** by another person (Baker v Willoughby), which does not apply to illness. C ignores the two years in which the driver's negligence alone caused her loss, and D and E describe reductions the law does not make."},{"t":"causation-remoteness","p":"FLK1","q":"Gas engineers digging a trench near a public playground leave a lit blowtorch and a gas canister unattended beside it, with only warning tape around the hole. Kai, aged 11, picks up the blowtorch and drops it into the trench. Gas that had leaked from a damaged pipe in an unusual way ignites in a sudden explosion, and Kai suffers serious burns. Expert evidence is that an explosion could not have been foreseen, but that a child being burned by the blowtorch flame was foreseeable.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the gas company is liable for Kai's burns?","o":["It is not liable, because the explosion that caused the burns was not foreseeable.","It is not liable, because Kai's interference with the equipment broke the chain of causation.","It is liable, because burns were a foreseeable type of harm even if the explosion was not.","It is liable only for the burns Kai would have suffered from the flame without the explosion.","It is liable, because the burns were a direct consequence of leaving the blowtorch unattended."],"a":2,"e":"Remoteness requires only that the **type** of harm was reasonably foreseeable; the **precise manner** in which it occurred and its **extent** need not be, so C is right (Hughes v Lord Advocate). A focuses wrongly on the unforeseeable explosion, and D wrongly limits recovery to the expected extent of harm. B fails because meddling by children with equipment left near a playground was the very risk the engineers should have guarded against, and E applies the old directness test, replaced by the reasonable foreseeability test (The Wagon Mound)."},{"t":"economic-loss","p":"FLK1","q":"Harold, a widower, instructs his solicitor, Ms Kaur, to change his will so as to leave £50,000 each to his two nieces, Asha and Bea, whom his existing will leaves out. Despite two reminders from Harold, Ms Kaur does nothing for six weeks, and Harold dies before a new will is drawn up. His estate passes under the old will to a charity. The nieces want to sue Ms Kaur in negligence for the legacies they have lost.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Ms Kaur owed the nieces a duty of care?","o":["She did not, because only the client or his estate can sue a solicitor for negligence.","She did not, because the nieces never relied on anything that she said or did.","She did not, because their loss is purely financial and arose from a failure to act.","She did, but they can sue only if the estate first brings its own claim against her.","She did, because she assumed responsibility for a service on which their gifts depended."],"a":4,"e":"The **Hedley Byrne** principle extends to the careless performance of **services**: a solicitor instructed to prepare a will owes a duty of care to the **intended beneficiaries**, even though they are not clients and have not relied on the solicitor, so E is right (White v Jones). A and B are wrong for that reason, and C is wrong because pure economic loss is recoverable where responsibility for a service was **assumed**. D is wrong because the estate has suffered no loss, which is exactly why the law lets the intended beneficiaries sue."},{"t":"economic-loss","p":"FLK1","q":"Rukhsana is buying a small flat for £165,000 with a mortgage. Her lender instructs a valuer, and Rukhsana pays the valuation fee. The report, which the lender sends to her, contains a notice saying the valuer accepts no responsibility to the buyer. Like most buyers of modest homes, she relies on it and does not commission her own survey. The valuer carelessly fails to notice obvious subsidence, and the flat is worth only £120,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Rukhsana can recover her loss from the valuer?","o":["She cannot, because the notice shows that the valuer did not assume responsibility to her.","She probably can, because reliance was foreseeable and the notice is unlikely to survive statute.","She cannot, because the valuer was instructed by the lender and owed duties only to its client.","She probably can, but only because she paid the valuation fee and so was the valuer's client.","She cannot, because a buyer is expected to obtain her own survey before buying a property."],"a":1,"e":"A valuer instructed by a lender owes a **duty to the buyer** of a modest home where it knows the buyer will probably rely on the valuation without an independent survey, and a business's notice excluding negligence liability is controlled by statute (now the **fairness** test of the Consumer Rights Act 2015 for a consumer), so B is right (Smith v Eric S Bush). A is wrong because a disclaimer cannot escape statutory control simply by saying no duty arises. C and D wrongly treat a contract with the valuer as essential, and E ignores the practice of modest buyers relying on the lender's valuation."},{"t":"economic-loss","p":"FLK1","q":"Kwabena leaves his job at an insurance broker. His former manager writes a reference to a prospective employer saying that Kwabena was suspected of mishandling client money. The manager honestly believes this, but carelessly fails to check records that would have shown the suspicion had been investigated and found to be groundless. The prospective employer withdraws its job offer. A defamation claim would fail because the manager was not malicious.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Kwabena's claim in negligence against his former employer?","o":["It fails, because a reference is written for its recipient, so only the recipient is owed a duty.","It fails, because the manager honestly believed that what he wrote in the reference was true.","It fails, because the only remedy for a damaging statement about a person lies in defamation.","It succeeds, because the writer of a reference owes a duty to take care to its subject as well.","It succeeds, but only if Kwabena can show that the manager wrote the reference out of malice."],"a":3,"e":"The writer of a reference owes a **duty of care to its subject** (the employee) to take reasonable care in preparing it, so a carelessly prepared reference that costs the subject a job can found a negligence claim, making D right (Spring v Guardian Assurance). B is wrong because honesty is no answer to **carelessness** in checking the facts, and E confuses negligence with the malice needed to defeat privilege in defamation. C and A are wrong because negligence provides a separate route, and the duty to the recipient exists alongside the duty to the subject."},{"t":"psychiatric-harm","p":"FLK1","q":"Mei's eight-year-old son is knocked down by a car being driven negligently outside his school. The school telephones Mei, and she reaches the hospital emergency department about 90 minutes after the accident. She sees her son before he has been treated, still covered in blood, screaming in pain and with his leg badly broken. Mei develops post-traumatic stress disorder. The driver's insurers say she cannot claim because she was not at the scene.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Mei's claim against the driver?","o":["It is likely to succeed, because she saw the immediate aftermath of the accident herself.","It is likely to fail, because she was not present when the accident itself happened.","It is likely to fail, because she first learned of the accident in a telephone call.","It is likely to succeed, but only if she proves a close tie of love and affection with her son.","It is likely to fail, because the hospital is a different place from the scene of the accident."],"a":0,"e":"A **secondary victim** (someone harmed by witnessing injury to another) must satisfy the **Alcock controls**, but proximity can be met by coming upon the **immediate aftermath** and perceiving it with one's **own unaided senses**, as Mei did when she saw her son untreated at the hospital within a short time, so A is right (McLoughlin v O'Brian). B and E wrongly require presence at the scene itself. C is wrong because the phone call only brought her to the aftermath, which she then saw for herself, and D is wrong because a close tie is **presumed** between parent and child."},{"t":"psychiatric-harm","p":"FLK1","q":"Bayo, an off-duty nurse, stops at the scene of a motorway pile-up caused by a lorry driver's negligence. By the time he arrives the vehicles are stationary and the fire service has made the scene safe. For an hour he helps tend injured strangers, some of whom die. He was never in any physical danger and did not reasonably think he was. Bayo develops post-traumatic stress disorder and sues the lorry driver.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Bayo's claim?","o":["It succeeds, because rescuers are a special category to whom a duty of care is always owed.","It succeeds, because his illness was foreseeable in a person of reasonable fortitude.","It fails, because he was not in physical danger and had no close tie with the victims.","It succeeds, because his physical presence at the scene makes him a primary victim.","It fails, because a medical professional is expected to be able to cope with such scenes."],"a":2,"e":"Rescuers have **no special category** for psychiatric harm: a rescuer recovers only as a **primary victim** (in physical danger, or reasonably believing so) or by satisfying the secondary victim controls, which include a **close tie of love and affection** with the person harmed, so C is right (White v Chief Constable of South Yorkshire). A states the old view that White rejected, and D is wrong because presence alone does not make someone a primary victim. B is not enough without the other controls, and E is not the legal reason the claim fails."},{"t":"psychiatric-harm","p":"FLK1","q":"Fiona is a housing officer for a council. After a restructuring her caseload doubles. She tells her manager in writing that she cannot cope, and her GP then signs her off work for six weeks with work-related stress. On her return the manager promises her an assistant and a reduced caseload, but nothing changes. Four months later Fiona suffers a breakdown and is diagnosed with clinical depression.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the council's liability to Fiona?","o":["It is not liable, because Fiona cannot satisfy the controls that apply to secondary victims.","It is liable, because after her first absence further harm to her was reasonably foreseeable.","It is not liable, because an employer may assume that its staff can cope with their workload.","It is not liable, because her illness was not caused by a sudden and shocking event.","It is liable, because an employer is responsible for any illness that work contributes to."],"a":1,"e":"An employer owes a duty to take reasonable care for an employee's **psychiatric health**; the key question is whether harm to **this employee** was **reasonably foreseeable**, and once she had been off with work-related stress and complained, it plainly was, so B is right (Walker v Northumberland CC; Hatton v Sutherland). C states the starting assumption, which no longer applies once the employer is on notice of a problem. A and D import secondary victim rules that do not apply to stress at work, and E overstates the duty, which is to take reasonable care, not to guarantee health."},{"t":"employers-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"For two years Darren, a warehouse operative, has been known for pranks such as pulling chairs from under colleagues. His supervisors have spoken to him informally several times but taken no further action. One day Darren lets off an air horn behind his colleague Lucy while she is standing on a stepladder. She falls and breaks her wrist. Lucy wants to sue her employer for breach of its own duty to her.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the employer's primary liability to Lucy?","o":["It is not liable, because Darren's prank was outside the course of his employment.","It is not liable, because its informal warnings were a reasonable response to his conduct.","It is liable only vicariously, because the employer itself did nothing wrong.","It is not liable, because an employer cannot foresee deliberate misconduct by staff.","It is liable, because it knew of Darren's behaviour and failed to take effective steps."],"a":4,"e":"An employer owes each employee a **personal, non-delegable duty** that includes providing **competent fellow employees**; where it knows an employee is a source of danger and does not deal with it effectively, it is in breach, so E is right (Hudson v Ridge Manufacturing). A confuses this with the course-of-employment test for **vicarious liability**, which is irrelevant to the employer's own breach, and C is wrong for the same reason. B and D fail because two years of known pranks made harm foreseeable and mere informal words were not enough."},{"t":"employers-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"Sunil works as a laboratory technician for a testing company. He has sight in only one eye following a childhood accident, and his employer knows this. His work involves pouring a mildly corrosive liquid. Small splashes happen occasionally, and for workers with two good eyes the risk of serious harm is low, so the company does not issue safety goggles. A splash enters Sunil's good eye and he loses his sight completely.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the employer breached its duty to Sunil?","o":["It did not, because it took the same precautions as for every other technician.","It did not, because the risk of a splash causing serious harm was very small.","It did, because an employer must provide every safety item that is available.","It did, because the consequences of an eye injury were far graver for Sunil.","It did not, because Sunil accepted the risks of the work by taking the job."],"a":3,"e":"The duty is owed to **each employee individually**, and the **seriousness of the harm** to a particular claimant is a factor in breach: total blindness was a far graver consequence for a one-eyed worker, so a reasonable employer would have given him goggles, making D right (Paris v Stepney BC). A and B ignore that the employer knew of his special vulnerability. C overstates the duty, which is to take reasonable care, and E is wrong because an employee's knowledge of a risk is not consent to it."},{"t":"vicarious-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"Lewis is employed by Citypark Ltd as a car park attendant. His job includes checking tickets and dealing with drivers' questions. He argues with Jerome, a driver, about a parking charge. As Jerome drives towards the exit, Lewis follows him on foot, shouts that he must never use the car park again and, at the barrier, punches him through the open car window. Citypark had told its staff never to use violence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Citypark is vicariously liable for the assault?","o":["It is not liable, because a criminal assault is outside any employee's job.","It is not liable, because it had expressly forbidden its staff to use violence.","It is liable, because the assault flowed unbroken from his job of dealing with drivers.","It is not liable, because Lewis was acting out of his own personal anger at Jerome.","It is liable only if it was negligent in recruiting or supervising Lewis."],"a":2,"e":"Vicarious liability requires a relationship of employment and a **close connection** between the tort and the employee's authorised activities; Lewis's job was to deal with drivers, and the attack was an **unbroken sequence** starting with that interaction, in which he purported to act on Citypark's business, so C is right (Mohamud v WM Morrison). D is the tempting answer, but this was not a **personal vendetta** unconnected with the job, unlike the Morrison data-leak case. A and B fail because deliberate and prohibited wrongs can still be closely connected, and E confuses vicarious liability, which is strict, with primary liability."},{"t":"vicarious-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"Paolo is employed by a courier firm as a van driver. A sign in the cab, and his contract, forbid him to carry passengers. While on his delivery round he gives a lift to his cousin, Lucia, who is going to a party in a town on his route. Paolo drives carelessly and crashes, and Lucia is injured. She has no connection with the firm or its deliveries.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the courier firm is vicariously liable to Lucia?","o":["It is not liable, because giving Lucia a lift was forbidden and served only Paolo's own purposes.","It is liable, because Paolo was doing his authorised job of driving, merely in a forbidden manner.","It is liable, because an employer's prohibition is never relevant to whether it is liable.","It is not liable, because Lucia consented to the risk of injury by getting into the van.","It is liable, because the accident happened in the firm's van during Paolo's working hours."],"a":0,"e":"A prohibited act done for the **employee's own purposes** falls outside the course of employment, so A is right (Twine v Bean's Express). The key question is **whose purposes** the act served; had Lucia been helping with the deliveries, the firm would probably be liable despite the ban (Rose v Plenty), which is why B and C are tempting but wrong here. D is wrong because **volenti** cannot be used against a car or van passenger (Road Traffic Act 1988 s.149), and E relies on time and place alone, which do not establish a close connection."},{"t":"vicarious-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"Hartley Plant Hire Ltd hires out a mechanical digger, together with its own employee Sam to operate it, to Okoro Construction for a week. Okoro's site manager tells Sam where to dig, but Sam alone decides how to operate the machine. Hartley continues to pay Sam and alone can dismiss him, and the hire contract says nothing about liability. Sam negligently swings the digger's bucket and injures Ruth, who is walking past on the pavement.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who is vicariously liable to Ruth?","o":["Okoro Construction, because its site manager directed where Sam was to dig.","Hartley Plant Hire, because it remains liable unless control of how Sam worked passed.","Both companies equally, because both had some control over what Sam was doing.","Neither company, because Sam was working for Okoro as an independent contractor.","Okoro Construction, because the digging was being done for the benefit of its business."],"a":1,"e":"Where an employee is lent with equipment, the **general (lending) employer** is presumed to remain vicariously liable, and the presumption is displaced only if it shows that control over **how** the work was done passed to the hirer, so B is right (Mersey Docks and Harbour Board v Coggins & Griffith). A and E are wrong because telling Sam **where** to dig is not control over **how** he operated the machine. C is wrong because dual vicarious liability is exceptional and not automatic, and D is wrong because Sam remained Hartley's employee throughout."},{"t":"defences","p":"FLK1","q":"Ellie accepts a lift home from a party with her friend Marcus, knowing he has drunk five pints of beer. She does not put on her seatbelt. Marcus loses control of the car and crashes it, and Ellie is injured. Medical evidence is that wearing a seatbelt would have made her injuries less severe but would not have prevented them. Marcus's insurers argue that Ellie voluntarily accepted the risk of injury.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of Ellie's conduct on her claim against Marcus?","o":["Her claim fails completely, because she voluntarily accepted the risk of a drunk driver.","Her claim fails completely, because travelling with a drunk driver is an illegal act.","Voluntary acceptance of risk does not apply, and her damages are reduced only for the seatbelt.","Her damages are reduced by 100%, because she was fully aware of all the risks involved.","Voluntary acceptance of risk does not apply, but her damages are reduced for both failings."],"a":4,"e":"Under the **Road Traffic Act 1988 s.149**, a passenger's willing acceptance of risk does not remove the driver's liability, so **volenti** fails, but the court reduces damages for **contributory negligence** both for getting in with a driver she knew was drunk and for not wearing a seatbelt (typically 15% where a belt would have made injuries less severe), so E is right. A is barred by statute, and C forgets the reduction for accepting the lift. B fails because being a passenger is not the kind of serious joint crime that triggers the **illegality** defence, and D is wrong because a 100% reduction is not possible."},{"t":"defences","p":"FLK1","q":"Kieran plays for a Sunday league football team. Several seconds after Kieran has passed the ball, Dominic, an opposing player, launches a reckless two-footed tackle at Kieran's legs and breaks his ankle. The referee sends Dominic off. Kieran sues Dominic, who argues that by choosing to play a contact sport Kieran consented to the risk of being injured.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Dominic's defence?","o":["It succeeds, because every player accepts the risk of injury in a contact sport.","It succeeds, because fouls are an ordinary and expected part of a football match.","It is unnecessary, because players owe each other no duty of care during a game.","It fails, because a player consents only to the ordinary risks of a game properly played.","It partly succeeds, so Kieran's damages are reduced by half for choosing to play."],"a":3,"e":"A sports participant consents to the **ordinary risks of the game played within its rules and spirit**, but not to **reckless or deliberate foul play**, so the defence of **volenti** (voluntary acceptance of risk) fails and D is right (Condon v Basi). A and B overstate the scope of consent, since a late, reckless tackle goes well beyond the inherent risks of football. C is wrong because players do owe each other a duty of care judged in the circumstances of the game, and E confuses consent with contributory negligence, for which there is no evidence."},{"t":"remedies","p":"FLK1","q":"Ayaan was born on 14 February 2010. On 20 May 2019, aged nine, he was knocked off his scooter by a cyclist riding negligently and suffered a fractured skull. His parents never started proceedings on his behalf. Ayaan now wants to sue the cyclist himself for his personal injury.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the latest date by which Ayaan must issue proceedings without needing the court to exercise its discretion?","o":["20 May 2022, being three years from the date of the injury.","20 May 2025, being six years from the date of the injury.","14 February 2031, being three years from his 18th birthday.","14 February 2028, being the date of his 18th birthday.","14 February 2034, being six years from his 18th birthday."],"a":2,"e":"The limitation period for **personal injury** is **3 years** (Limitation Act 1980 s.11), but under s.28 time does not run against a **child** until they reach **18**, so Ayaan has until three years after his 18th birthday, making C right. A ignores the suspension of time while he is a child. B and E wrongly apply the **6-year** period that governs property damage and economic loss, and D gives only the date on which time starts to run."},{"t":"remedies","p":"FLK1","q":"Kemi's car, which has a market value of £4,000, is badly damaged by a negligent driver. Repairs would cost £6,500, but Kemi wants it repaired because she is fond of it. While waiting for the matter to be resolved she hires a luxury sports car at £300 a day, although a car similar to her own was available to hire at £40 a day. Kemi could easily have afforded the cheaper hire.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the damages Kemi can recover for the car and the hire?","o":["£6,500 for the repairs, plus the full cost of the sports car hire.","£4,000 for the car, plus hire at a reasonable rate for a similar car.","£6,500 for the repairs, plus hire at a reasonable rate for a similar car.","£4,000 for the car only, because hire is not recoverable when a car is written off.","£4,000 for the car, plus the full cost of hire, because she acted in good faith."],"a":1,"e":"Where repair would be **uneconomic**, damages for damaged property are measured by the loss in value, here the car's **market value** of £4,000; reasonable **hire charges** for loss of use are recoverable, but the claimant must **mitigate** her loss, so only the cost of hiring a comparable car is allowed, making B right. A and C wrongly award repair costs well above the car's value. D is wrong because loss of use is a recognised head of loss, and E is wrong because good faith does not excuse an unreasonable failure to mitigate."},{"t":"occupiers-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"A council is regrading part of a public recreation ground and has dug a shallow but steep-sided excavation, which is partly fenced. Families regularly use the rest of the ground. Leo, aged four, is allowed by his mother to go to the recreation ground on his own while she stays at home. He wanders into the excavation, falls and breaks his arm. The danger would be obvious to an adult or an older child.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the council's liability to Leo under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1957?","o":["It is probably not liable, because it may expect parents not to let very young children go there alone.","It is liable, because an occupier must be prepared for children to be less careful than adults.","It is liable, because the excavation was an allurement that was bound to attract young children.","It is not liable, because Leo was a trespasser who had no permission to enter the excavation.","It is not liable, because the danger was obvious and occupiers need not protect against obvious risks."],"a":0,"e":"Although an occupier must be prepared for children to be **less careful than adults** (s.2(3)(a)), it is entitled to expect that **parents** will not allow **very young children** to wander unaccompanied into danger, so A is right (Phipps v Rochester Corporation). B states the general rule but ignores the parental-responsibility point, which decides the case. C overstates the allurement idea, D is wrong because Leo was a visitor to an open recreation ground, and E applies an obvious-risk principle that is weak in the case of a four-year-old."},{"t":"occupiers-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"A restaurant owner calls in Hamid, a gas engineer, to repair a commercial oven, telling him that it seems to be leaking gas. Hamid does not isolate the gas supply before starting work, and a flare of gas burns his hand. On his way out he steps on a cellar hatch that the owner knew had been loose for weeks; it gives way and Hamid breaks his leg. He sues the owner under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1957.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the owner's liability?","o":["It is liable for both injuries, because it owed Hamid the common duty of care as a visitor.","It is liable for neither injury, because a skilled visitor is expected to guard against all risks.","It is liable for the burn but not the leg, because it knew the oven was leaking gas.","It is liable for neither injury, because Hamid was a contractor rather than a customer.","It is liable for the leg but not the burn, because only the burn was a risk of Hamid's trade."],"a":4,"e":"An occupier may expect a person exercising their **calling** to guard against **special risks ordinarily incident to it** (s.2(3)(b)), so the gas flare, a risk of Hamid's trade of which he had been warned, is not the owner's responsibility (Roles v Nathan); but a loose hatch is an ordinary danger that has nothing to do with gas work, so E is right. B wrongly extends the skilled visitor rule to all risks, and A ignores it altogether. C has the position the wrong way round, and D is wrong because a contractor invited to do work is a lawful visitor."},{"t":"product-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"In 2021 a company launched a new waterproofing spray for outdoor clothing. In 2022 Ola used it in a small room and suffered lung damage. Not until 2025 did published research reveal, for the first time anywhere, that one of the spray's ingredients can cause lung damage when inhaled in an enclosed space. Before then, no scientific or technical literature had identified any such risk. Ola sues the company under the Consumer Protection Act 1987.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the company's position?","o":["It is liable, because liability under the Act is strict and the state of knowledge is irrelevant.","It is liable, because the only defences under the Act relate to compliance with legal requirements.","It is not liable, because it took all reasonable care when testing the spray before its launch.","It may escape liability, because the defect could not have been discovered when the spray was supplied.","It is not liable, because Ola did not buy the spray directly from the company that produced it."],"a":3,"e":"The **development risks defence** (s.4(1)(e)) applies where the **state of scientific and technical knowledge** when the producer supplied the product was not such that a producer of such products might be expected to **discover the defect**; no literature identified the risk until 2025, long after the spray was supplied, so D is right. A is wrong because, although liability is strict, the Act provides this defence, and B is wrong because compliance with legal requirements is only one of several defences. C applies the negligence standard of reasonable care, which is not the test for the defence, and E is wrong because the Act does not depend on any contract with the producer."},{"t":"product-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"In March 2015 a manufacturer supplied a batch of dehumidifiers to retailers. Ferdinand bought one of them second-hand in 2024. In September 2026 a design defect causes it to overheat and catch fire, burning his hands. He consults a solicitor two weeks later and wants to sue the manufacturer.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ferdinand's options?","o":["He can sue under the Consumer Protection Act 1987, because he is within three years of the injury.","His claim under the 1987 Act is extinguished, but he may sue in negligence if he can prove fault.","He can sue under the 1987 Act, because the court can extend time for a personal injury claim.","He has no claim at all, because he bought the dehumidifier second-hand rather than new.","He has no claim at all, because the ten-year time limit also applies to claims in negligence."],"a":1,"e":"A claim under the **Consumer Protection Act 1987** is extinguished after an absolute **10-year longstop** from when the producer put the product into circulation, and the court's s.33 discretion cannot override it; but a claim in **negligence** is unaffected and runs for 3 years from the injury, so B is right. A ignores the longstop, and C is wrong because s.33 cannot extend it. D fails because neither route depends on buying the product new, and E wrongly applies the Act's longstop to negligence."},{"t":"nuisance-rylands","p":"FLK1","q":"Ravi teaches the violin from his semi-detached house on weekday evenings. His neighbour, Colin, dislikes the sound and, to drive the pupils away, starts banging metal trays loudly against the party wall whenever a lesson begins, stopping as soon as it ends. Several pupils have left. Colin says he is entitled to make whatever noise he likes inside his own home.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Colin's noise is a private nuisance?","o":["It is not, because a landowner may make noise on his own land whatever his motive.","It is not, because the noise lasts only as long as each lesson and stops afterwards.","It is, because the malicious purpose of the noise makes it an unreasonable interference.","It is not, because teaching music from home is an abnormally sensitive use of land.","It is, but only if the noise exceeds the limits set by the local authority's licence."],"a":2,"e":"Whether an interference is **unreasonable** depends on factors including the defendant's **malice**: noise made deliberately to annoy a neighbour is likely to be a nuisance even if similar noise made for a genuine purpose would not be, so C is right (Christie v Davey; Hollywood Silver Fox Farm v Emmett). A is wrong because motive is relevant in nuisance. B is weak because the noise is repeated whenever lessons happen, D is wrong because ordinary music teaching is not abnormally sensitive, and E invents a statutory threshold for private nuisance."},{"t":"nuisance-rylands","p":"FLK1","q":"A hotel is supplied with water through ordinary pipes for its kitchens and guest bathrooms. Without any negligence on the hotel's part, a pipe bursts overnight and water escapes onto the neighbouring property, flooding Jade's shop and damaging its fittings. Jade wants to sue the hotel under the rule in Rylands v Fletcher because she cannot prove that the hotel was negligent.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Jade's claim under that rule?","o":["It fails, because supplying water through ordinary pipes is not an extraordinary use of land.","It succeeds, because water was brought onto the hotel's land and then escaped from it.","It succeeds, because the rule imposes strict liability for every escape of water.","It fails, because the rule applies only to escapes of chemicals and explosives.","It succeeds, because the hotel brought the water onto its land for its own business purposes."],"a":0,"e":"The rule in Rylands v Fletcher requires a thing creating an **exceptionally high risk** if it escapes, and a **non-natural** (extraordinary and unusual) use of land; piping an ordinary water supply to a building meets neither, so A is right (Transco v Stockport MBC). B, C and E each rely on the bringing onto land and escape, which are necessary but not sufficient, and C overstates the rule. D is wrong because large accumulations of water can fall within the rule, as in Rylands itself."}],"tort-p3":[{"t":"negligence","p":"FLK1","q":"Tunde, a retired accountant, replaces the wooden handrail on the steps up to his front door himself, using brackets and screws bought from a DIY store and following the manufacturer's instructions. A month later his friend Marisol, who has come for lunch, leans on the rail. One bracket pulls out of the brickwork and she falls, breaking her arm. A professional joiner would have used longer masonry fixings, but a careful amateur following the instructions would not have known to do so.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the standard of care Tunde owed Marisol?","o":["That of a professional joiner, because anyone who carries out building work must show a joiner's skill.","That of a householder with no practical experience, because he is a retired accountant.","That of a reasonably competent amateur doing that kind of job, which he appears to have met.","No standard at all, because a householder owes no duty to a social guest for work on his home.","That of a professional joiner, reduced because he did the work for himself without payment."],"a":2,"e":"A person who does a skilled job as an amateur is judged by the standard of a **reasonably competent amateur doing that task**, not a professional (Wells v Cooper), so C is right and, on these facts, Tunde met it. A and E wrongly apply a professional standard, and the law does not scale it down for unpaid work. B is wrong because the test is objective and tied to the task, not to Tunde's own background, and D is wrong because Tunde, as occupier, owes a guest the common duty of care (the duty under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1957 to take reasonable care for a visitor's safety)."},{"t":"negligence","p":"FLK1","q":"Desmond, a delivery driver, feels light-headed and notices his vision blurring about twenty minutes into his shift. He assumes he is just tired and keeps driving. Ten minutes later he suffers a stroke at the wheel, mounts the pavement and injures Ruth, a pedestrian. Desmond had no previous history of stroke.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Desmond breached his duty to Ruth?","o":["He did not, because at the moment of impact the stroke had removed all control over the van.","He did not, because he had no history of stroke and could not have known what was happening.","He did, because a driver who falls ill at the wheel is strictly liable for any harm that results.","He did, because once he knew something was wrong a reasonable driver would have stopped.","He did, but only if Ruth can show that a stroke was a likely result of the symptoms he felt."],"a":3,"e":"An illness excuses a driver only if it **entirely removed control without the driver knowing** of it; a driver who **feels unwell and carries on** is judged against a reasonable driver who would have stopped (Roberts v Ramsbottom), so D is right. A and B would be right only for an unknown condition with no warning signs, which is not the case here because Desmond had symptoms. C is wrong because liability is fault-based, not strict, and E is wrong because Desmond did not need to diagnose a stroke: it is enough that he knew he was not fit to drive."},{"t":"negligence","p":"FLK1","q":"A county council runs a mountain rescue team. The team receives a call that a climber has fallen and is bleeding heavily on a hillside three miles away. The team's specialist vehicle is out of service, so the team leader loads a heavy stretcher winch into the back of an ordinary pick-up truck, which has no way of securing it. On the rough track the winch slides and crushes the foot of Nadia, a team member riding in the back.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the council was in breach of its duty to Nadia?","o":["It probably was not, because the need to save life justified a risk that would otherwise be unreasonable.","It was, because an employer's duty to provide safe equipment for its staff is an absolute one.","It was not, because emergency services owe no duty of care to their own staff during a rescue.","It was, because the risk of an unsecured winch sliding on a rough track was plainly foreseeable.","It was not, because Nadia accepted the risk by agreeing to ride in the back of the truck."],"a":0,"e":"In deciding breach the court weighs the risk against the **social value of the defendant's purpose**, and **saving life can justify taking greater risks** (Watt v Hertfordshire CC), so A is right. D is tempting, but foreseeability alone does not establish breach: the question is whether a reasonable person would have taken the risk in the emergency. B is wrong because the employer's duty is one of reasonable care, not absolute, C is wrong because a duty plainly exists, and E is wrong because consent (volenti) needs real agreement to run the legal risk, which an employee doing her job does not give."},{"t":"negligence","p":"FLK1","q":"Callum pleads guilty in the magistrates' court to careless driving after his car hit Efua's car at a junction. Efua sues him for her injuries. In his defence Callum says he pleaded guilty only to avoid the cost of a trial and that Efua in fact pulled out in front of him.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the conviction in Efua's civil claim?","o":["It is inadmissible, because a criminal conviction cannot be relied on in civil proceedings.","It proves negligence conclusively, so Callum is not allowed to dispute liability at all.","It is admissible only if Callum was convicted after a trial rather than on a guilty plea.","It changes nothing, because Efua must still prove the offence to the criminal standard.","It is admissible, and Callum is taken to have committed the offence unless he proves otherwise."],"a":4,"e":"Under the **Civil Evidence Act 1968 s.11**, a conviction is **admissible** in civil proceedings and the person convicted is **taken to have committed the offence unless they prove otherwise**, so E is right. In practice this puts the burden on Callum to disprove carelessness. B goes too far because the presumption can be rebutted, C is wrong because a guilty plea counts just as much as a conviction after trial, and A and D misstate the rule entirely (the civil standard of proof is the balance of probabilities)."},{"t":"causation-remoteness","p":"FLK1","q":"Imogen's company buys a rival business. Its solicitors negligently fail to advise it to ask the seller for an indemnity against a pending tax dispute. The dispute later costs the company £200,000. The evidence shows a real and substantial chance, assessed at 40%, that the seller would have agreed to give the indemnity if asked.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the damages the company is likely to recover from the solicitors?","o":["Nothing, because it cannot prove on the balance of probabilities that the seller would have agreed.","£80,000, being the value of the lost chance of persuading the seller to give the indemnity.","£200,000, because the solicitors' negligence materially increased the risk of the loss.","£100,000, because where the evidence is uncertain the court divides the loss equally.","£200,000, because a 40% chance is real and substantial, so the whole loss is proved."],"a":1,"e":"Where a financial loss depends on what a **third party** would have done, the claimant need only show a **real and substantial chance**, and damages are a **percentage of the full loss** reflecting that chance (Allied Maples v Simmons & Simmons): 40% of £200,000 is £80,000, so B is right. A applies the all-or-nothing balance of probabilities test used for personal injury loss-of-chance claims, and E wrongly treats proof of a real chance as proof of the whole loss. C borrows the material increase in risk exception, which is confined to mesothelioma, and D has no basis in law."},{"t":"causation-remoteness","p":"FLK1","q":"Kofi's leg is fractured when a scaffolding pole falls on him because of a builder's negligence. At hospital a junior doctor sets the fracture slightly out of alignment. This is an error that a competent doctor should not have made, but it is not an unusual one. As a result Kofi needs a second operation and his recovery takes four months longer.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the builder's liability for the extra four months?","o":["It is not liable, because any negligent medical treatment breaks the chain of causation.","It is not liable, because the doctor's error rather than the pole was the but-for cause of the delay.","It is liable, because ordinary errors in treatment are foreseeable and do not break the chain.","It is liable only if Kofi first sues the hospital and fails to recover the extra loss from it.","It is liable for half, because the builder and the doctor each caused an equal part of the harm."],"a":2,"e":"A later act breaks the **chain of causation** (a novus actus interveniens) only if it is something unforeseeable or so serious that it eclipses the original wrong; **ordinary negligent medical treatment** of an injury is a foreseeable consequence and does **not** break the chain, so C is right. Only a gross error would do so, which is why A is too wide. B is wrong because the pole remains a but-for cause (without it there would have been no treatment at all), D invents a precondition, and E is wrong because the builder is liable for the whole of this loss, though it may claim a contribution from the hospital."},{"t":"causation-remoteness","p":"FLK1","q":"Owain, a bricklayer, is left with a permanently stiff right arm when a motorist negligently crashes into him. He can no longer lay bricks and takes lower-paid work. A year later, before his claim comes to trial, he is attacked by a robber who stabs him in the same arm, which then has to be amputated. The robber has no money.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the motorist's liability for Owain's lost earnings after the attack?","o":["The motorist remains liable for the effects of the stiff arm as if the attack had not happened.","The motorist's liability ends at the date of the attack, because the amputation overtook the injury.","The motorist is liable for all losses including the amputation, because the attack was foreseeable.","The motorist's liability is halved, because two wrongdoers now share responsibility for the arm.","The motorist's liability ends at the attack unless the robber is traced and turns out able to pay."],"a":0,"e":"A **later tort does not reduce the first wrongdoer's liability**: the motorist must go on compensating for the original disability as if the attack had not happened (Baker v Willoughby), so A is right. Otherwise Owain would fall between two stools, because the robber is liable only for making an already damaged arm worse. B describes the rule for a later natural illness (which does cut off liability), not a later tort, C is wrong because a deliberate criminal attack is not a foreseeable result of the crash, and D and E have no basis in law."},{"t":"economic-loss","p":"FLK1","q":"In 2023 Leon buys a five-year-old house from its first owner. In 2026 serious cracks appear because the developer that built the house negligently laid inadequate foundations. No one has been hurt and none of Leon's belongings has been damaged, but the house needs £45,000 of underpinning to make it safe. Leon has no contract with the developer.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Leon's position in the tort of negligence against the developer?","o":["He can recover £45,000, because the defective foundations have physically damaged the house.","He can recover £45,000, because the developer owed a duty to all future owners of the house.","He can recover only if he proves the developer knew that he personally would rely on its work.","He cannot recover, because the cost of curing a defect in the house itself is pure economic loss.","He cannot recover, because claims for defective buildings must be made within three years of purchase."],"a":3,"e":"The cost of repairing a **defect in the thing itself** (here the house), where no person and no **other** property has been damaged, is **pure economic loss** (financial loss not flowing from physical damage to the claimant's person or other property) and is not recoverable in negligence (Murphy v Brentwood DC), so D is right. A is the tempting wrong answer: cracks in the house are the defect showing itself, not damage to other property. B and C misstate the law, and E invents a limitation rule; Leon's possible route is a statutory claim under the Defective Premises Act 1972, not negligence."},{"t":"economic-loss","p":"FLK1","q":"At a neighbour's barbecue, Shirin mentions that she is thinking of putting her savings into a new investment fund. Victor, another guest who works as an independent financial adviser, says between mouthfuls that \"it sounds very safe, I'd go for it\". He says nothing more and is not asked anything further. Shirin invests £20,000 and loses it all when the fund collapses.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Victor owed Shirin a duty of care in giving that advice?","o":["He did, because he has professional expertise and it was foreseeable that Shirin would act on his view.","He did, because a financial adviser owes a duty to anyone who relies on his views about investments.","He did not, because liability for a careless statement arises only where the adviser is paid for it.","He did not, because Shirin's loss is purely financial and so can never be recovered in negligence.","He did not, because a casual remark at a social event is not normally an assumption of responsibility."],"a":4,"e":"A duty for careless statements causing pure economic loss requires a **special relationship** in which the defendant **assumes responsibility** and reasonable reliance is expected; advice given **casually on a social occasion** does not normally qualify, so E is right. A and B wrongly treat expertise plus foreseeable reliance as enough, ignoring the context in which the remark was made. C is wrong because payment is not essential (a friend who takes on a task can owe a duty: Chaudhry v Prabhakar), and D is wrong because pure economic loss is recoverable under the Hedley Byrne principle where a special relationship exists."},{"t":"psychiatric-harm","p":"FLK1","q":"Gloria, aged 70, is walking her eight-year-old grandson, Jayden, home from school when a car driven negligently mounts the pavement a few metres ahead and hits him. Gloria is not in any danger herself. Jayden is seriously injured, and Gloria develops post-traumatic stress disorder. She has looked after Jayden every weekday since he was a baby.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Gloria's prospects of recovering damages for her post-traumatic stress disorder?","o":["She will recover, because a grandparent is presumed to have a close tie of love with a grandchild.","She can recover if she proves a close tie of love and affection, which the facts suggest she can.","She cannot recover, because only parents, spouses and children can claim as secondary victims.","She cannot recover, because she was not herself in any physical danger at the time.","She will recover as a primary victim, because she was present at the scene of the accident."],"a":1,"e":"Gloria is a **secondary victim** (a witness to harm to another, not in danger herself), so she must satisfy the control mechanisms; she saw the accident with her own senses, but the **close tie of love and affection is presumed only for parents and children, spouses and engaged couples**, so as a grandparent she must **prove** it, as B states. A wrongly extends the presumption, and C wrongly closes the class altogether. D is wrong because not being in danger only stops her being a primary victim, which is also why E is wrong."},{"t":"psychiatric-harm","p":"FLK1","q":"Marcus and his wife, Ines, are in their car when a negligently driven bus crashes into it. Ines is badly injured. Marcus is unhurt but feared for his life at the time. In the months that follow he feels intense distress, anger and sadness, and has trouble sleeping. A psychiatrist reports that these are normal, if severe, reactions and that Marcus has no recognised psychiatric illness.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Marcus's claim against the bus company for his mental distress?","o":["It succeeds, because he was a primary victim who was in physical danger himself.","It succeeds as a secondary victim claim, because he saw his wife injured with his own eyes.","It fails, because damages are not recoverable for distress short of a recognised illness.","It fails, because a person who is physically unhurt can never recover for mental harm.","It succeeds in part, because severe distress is recoverable at a reduced rate."],"a":2,"e":"Whatever kind of victim the claimant is, there is a threshold requirement of a **recognised psychiatric illness**; ordinary grief, fear, anger and distress, however severe, are not enough, so C is right. A and B are tempting because Marcus would otherwise qualify as a primary victim (he was in danger) and may meet the secondary victim controls, but neither status helps without a recognised illness. D is wrong because primary victims can recover for psychiatric harm without physical injury, and E describes no rule of law."},{"t":"employers-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"Rashid is employed by Northway Engineering, which sends him to work for a month on a client's production line. The client's foreman, who directs the work day to day, tells the team to clear jams on a conveyor without first switching it off. Rashid's hand is caught in the conveyor and badly injured. Northway had carefully checked beforehand that the client was a reputable, well-run business.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Northway's liability to Rashid?","o":["It is liable, because its duty to provide a safe system of work is non-delegable and was not met.","It is not liable, because only the client had control over the system of work on the line.","It is not liable, because it acted reasonably in choosing a reputable client to supervise the work.","It is liable only vicariously, and only if the foreman can be treated as Northway's own employee.","It is liable only if Rashid had earlier complained to Northway about the unsafe practice."],"a":0,"e":"An employer's duty to take reasonable care for an employee's safety, including providing and operating a **safe system of work**, is **personal and non-delegable**: it may delegate the task, but if the person it delegates to fails to operate a safe system, the employer is in breach (McDermid v Nash Dredging), so A is right. C is the tempting wrong answer, because choosing carefully is a defence for occupiers using contractors, but not for an employer's own duty to its staff. B and D wrongly focus on who had day-to-day control, and E invents a precondition."},{"t":"employers-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"In March 2026 Bella, a warehouse worker, injures her back lifting heavy boxes. Her employer had not carried out the manual handling risk assessment that health and safety regulations require. Bella wants to sue her employer for her injury.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Bella must put her claim?","o":["She can sue for breach of statutory duty without proving fault, because the regulations impose strict duties.","She must prove common law negligence, though the breach of the regulations is evidence of the expected standard.","She has no civil claim, because breaches of health and safety regulations are dealt with only by prosecution.","She can sue for breach of statutory duty, but only if her employer has been convicted of the breach.","She must prove negligence, and the breach of the regulations is irrelevant to whether her employer was careless."],"a":1,"e":"For breaches on or after **1 October 2013**, breach of health and safety **regulations is not actionable** in a civil claim (Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974 s.47, as amended by the Enterprise and Regulatory Reform Act 2013), so Bella must prove **common law negligence**, using the regulations as **evidence of what a reasonable employer would do**, as B states. A describes the pre-2013 position, and D is wrong for the same reason. C is wrong because a negligence claim remains open, and E is wrong because the regulations remain highly relevant to the standard of care."},{"t":"employers-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"A logistics company hires Gavin as a forklift driver after obtaining two satisfactory references and checking his licence. Nothing in his history suggests violence. On his third day, during an argument over a parking space, Gavin deliberately pushes his colleague Priti down a flight of stairs, injuring her. Priti brings a claim against the company alleging that it breached its own duty of care to her.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the company's liability to Priti on that basis?","o":["It is not liable, because it had no reason to know that Gavin posed a danger to colleagues.","It is liable, because its duty to provide competent fellow employees is non-delegable.","It is liable, because an employer guarantees that its staff will not injure one another at work.","It is not liable, because an employer owes no duty at all in respect of deliberate acts by its staff.","It is liable, because the assault took place on its premises and during working hours."],"a":0,"e":"The employer's duty to provide **competent fellow employees** is a duty of **reasonable care**: it is liable for a colleague's dangerous conduct only if it **knew or ought to have known** of the danger and failed to act (contrast Hudson v Ridge Manufacturing, where the employer knew of years of horseplay), so A is right. B and C turn a duty of reasonable care into a guarantee, and E treats time and place as decisive when they are not. D is wrong because a duty does exist (an employer who knows of a violent employee must act); the vicarious liability route is also weak here because the assault arose from a purely personal dispute."},{"t":"vicarious-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"Prisoners at a prison work in the prison laundry for a small weekly payment set by the prison. Ade, a prisoner, carelessly leaves a heavy trolley at the top of a ramp. It rolls down and injures Mrs Lowe, the civilian laundry supervisor employed by the Ministry of Justice. She sues the Ministry in respect of Ade's negligence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the Ministry is vicariously liable for Ade's negligence?","o":["It is not, because Ade is not an employee and receives only a nominal payment.","It is not, because the Ministry makes no profit from the work the prisoners carry out.","It is, because his work is integral to the Ministry's activities and is akin to employment.","It is not, because a prisoner works under compulsion rather than under a contract.","It is, because the Ministry answers for the acts of everyone it holds in its prisons."],"a":2,"e":"Vicarious liability can arise from a relationship **akin to employment**, where the wrongdoer carries out activities **integral to the defendant's business, for its benefit**, and the defendant **created the risk** by assigning those activities to him (Cox v Ministry of Justice), so C is right; Ade's negligence while doing that work satisfies the close connection test. A, B and D list features (no contract, low pay, no profit, compulsion) that do not prevent the relationship qualifying. E is far too wide: the Ministry is not liable for everything a prisoner does, only for torts closely connected with the work assigned."},{"t":"vicarious-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"Fenwick Furnishings employs Greg to deliver and assemble flat-pack wardrobes. His written instructions say he must never accept help from members of the public. On a busy day Greg pays Jack, a 15-year-old from his street, £10 to help him carry boxes up to customers' flats. While Greg is carelessly reversing the delivery lorry, Jack, who is guiding him back, is struck and injured.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Fenwick's position if Jack sues it?","o":["It is not liable, because Greg did something that his employer had expressly forbidden.","It is not liable, because Jack had no permission from Fenwick to be helping on its round.","It is liable only if it knew that Greg was in the habit of using unofficial helpers.","It is liable, because Greg's prohibited act was done in carrying out Fenwick's deliveries.","It is not liable, because Jack was too young to be lawfully taken on as a helper."],"a":3,"e":"An express prohibition is **not a defence** where the employee does a forbidden act **for the employer's purposes**, in the course of doing his job; here Greg's careless driving and use of Jack were part of making Fenwick's deliveries (Rose v Plenty), so D is right. A is the tempting wrong answer: a prohibition matters only where it limits the job itself or the employee is pursuing his own ends, such as giving a friend a lift. B, C and E do not affect whether Greg's tort was closely connected with his employment."},{"t":"defences","p":"FLK1","q":"A landlord negligently leaves faulty wiring in a block of flats, and a fire breaks out. Tariq, who lives next door, sees a young child at a first-floor window, runs into the smoke-filled stairwell and carries her out. He suffers serious burns. The landlord argues that Tariq knowingly ran into the fire, so that he has no claim or, at least, his damages should be heavily reduced.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the landlord's arguments?","o":["Consent succeeds, because Tariq fully understood the danger and chose to run into the fire.","Consent fails, and his damages are unlikely to be reduced, because he acted reasonably to save a life.","Consent fails, but his damages will be reduced by at least half for entering a burning building.","Consent succeeds, because the landlord owed no duty to someone who did not live in the block.","Consent fails, but the claim fails anyway because Tariq's own choice broke the chain of causation."],"a":1,"e":"**Rescuers are not treated as consenting** (volenti non fit injuria) to a danger the defendant created, because they act under a moral compulsion, and they are **rarely held contributorily negligent** unless they act with foolhardy disregard for their own safety (Haynes v Harwood), so B is right. A confuses knowledge of a risk with free agreement to it, and C wrongly treats a reasonable rescue as careless. D is wrong because a rescuer is a foreseeable claimant to whom a duty is owed in their own right, and E is wrong because a reasonable rescue does not break the chain of causation."},{"t":"defences","p":"FLK1","q":"Hollie suffers a severe head injury in a crash caused by a negligent lorry driver. The injury changes her personality. Eighteen months later she seriously assaults a stranger, pleads guilty to causing grievous bodily harm with intent and is sentenced to six years' imprisonment. She sues the lorry driver's employer for damages for the head injury, her lost earnings before her imprisonment and the earnings she will lose while in prison.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the defence of illegality on her claim?","o":["It bars the whole claim, because Hollie has been convicted of a serious criminal offence.","It has no effect, because her offence was committed after, and as a result of, the negligence.","It reduces all her damages in proportion to the degree of her criminal responsibility.","It bars her claim only if the lorry driver was also involved in the assault in some way.","It bars the earnings lost through her imprisonment, but not the injury and earlier losses."],"a":4,"e":"Under the **illegality** defence a claimant cannot recover for losses flowing from a **sentence imposed for her own crime**, because that would be inconsistent with the criminal court's finding that she was responsible (Gray v Thames Trains), but losses **unconnected with the crime** remain recoverable, so E is right. A goes too far, since the head injury and the earnings lost before the assault do not depend on her offence. B ignores the policy behind the defence, C wrongly applies a contributory negligence-style reduction to a complete defence, and D confuses this with the joint criminal enterprise category."},{"t":"defences","p":"FLK1","q":"Sean and Pete spend the afternoon drinking heavily together in a flying club bar. Pete, who is obviously very drunk, then insists on taking his light aircraft up, and Sean agrees to go as his passenger and helps him start the engine. The plane crashes on take-off and Sean is badly injured. Sean sues Pete in negligence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Pete's best defence?","o":["Voluntary acceptance of risk, which is likely to defeat the claim completely on these facts.","None, because statute prevents a passenger's acceptance of risk from negating the pilot's liability.","Contributory negligence only, which will reduce Sean's damages by about 20%.","Illegality, because Sean was helping Pete to commit an offence and that bars every claim.","Voluntary acceptance of risk, but only if Sean signed a written waiver before the flight."],"a":0,"e":"**Volenti** (voluntary acceptance of risk) is a complete defence where the claimant, knowing the nature and extent of the risk, freely agreed to it; flying with an obviously drunk pilot is so glaringly dangerous that agreement can be implied from conduct (Morris v Murray), so A is right. B is the trap: the **Road Traffic Act 1988 s.149** bar applies only to passengers in **vehicles requiring compulsory motor insurance**, not to aircraft. C would be the likely answer for a car passenger, D overstates illegality, which turns on policy and proportionality, and E is wrong because agreement can be implied without any written waiver."},{"t":"remedies","p":"FLK1","q":"Naomi is a passenger on her husband Felix's motorbike when he rides negligently and crashes. Her injuries leave her needing help with washing and dressing for a year. Felix gives up his job to care for her, and Naomi's sister also helps every weekend. Naomi sues Felix, whose insurer will meet any award.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Naomi's claim for the value of the care she received?","o":["She can recover the value of both Felix's and her sister's care, holding it on trust for each of them.","She can recover nothing for care, because neither of the carers charged her for the help given.","She can recover the value of her sister's care, held on trust for her sister, but not Felix's care.","She can recover the value of Felix's care only, because he gave up paid work to provide it.","She can recover the value of all the care, but only as part of her damages for loss of amenity."],"a":2,"e":"The value of **gratuitous care** (care given free by family or friends) is recoverable and held **on trust for the carer**, but **not where the carer is the defendant**, who would simply be paying himself (Hunt v Severs), so C is right. A is the tempting wrong answer because it ignores that Felix is the defendant, and D gets the position the wrong way round. B is wrong because free care is still recoverable, and E is wrong because care is a pecuniary head of loss, not part of damages for pain, suffering and loss of amenity."},{"t":"remedies","p":"FLK1","q":"Chidi, aged 30, suffers a skull fracture in a workplace accident for which his employer admits liability. He has recovered well, but medical evidence is that there is a 5% chance that he will develop epilepsy at some point in the future as a result of the injury. His solicitor is concerned that a single once-and-for-all award will undervalue the claim if epilepsy does develop.\n\nWhich of the following is the most suitable way for the court to deal with the risk of epilepsy?","o":["Award full damages as though he will develop epilepsy, because the risk arises from the employer's fault.","Adjourn the trial until it becomes clear whether or not Chidi develops epilepsy.","Award 5% of the damages for epilepsy now, with no right to come back to court later.","Order interim payments until Chidi reaches an age at which the risk of epilepsy ends.","Award provisional damages on the basis that he will not develop epilepsy, with a right to return if he does."],"a":4,"e":"Where there is a **chance** that the claimant will develop a **serious disease or serious deterioration**, the court can award **provisional damages** on the assumption it will not happen, allowing the claimant to apply for more if it does (**Senior Courts Act 1981 s.32A**), so E is right. C describes the usual lump-sum approach, which is exactly the risk of under-compensation the solicitor wants to avoid. A over-compensates, B would leave the claim unresolved indefinitely, and D misuses interim payments, which are payments on account before trial."},{"t":"occupiers-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"The Mensah family are having their house extended and move out for three months while a building firm, Kestrel Builders, takes over the site. Kestrel keeps the keys and controls who comes and goes, but Mr Mensah visits twice a week to check progress and still arranges for some repairs to the existing structure. A surveyor visiting at Kestrel's invitation falls through an unguarded hole in the first floor.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who may owe the surveyor a duty under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1957?","o":["Only Mr Mensah, because an occupier for the purposes of the Act must own the premises.","Only Kestrel, because an owner stops being an occupier as soon as he moves out.","Neither of them, because a site under construction is not premises within the Act.","Both may be occupiers, each owing a duty that matches its degree of control.","Only Kestrel, but only if the surveyor had paid it a fee to come onto the site."],"a":3,"e":"An **occupier** is anyone with a **sufficient degree of control** over premises, and there can be **more than one occupier** at once, each owing a duty matching its degree of control (Wheat v Lacon), so D is right. A is wrong because ownership is neither necessary nor sufficient, and B is wrong because an owner who keeps some control can remain an occupier after moving out. C is wrong because the Act applies to any fixed or movable structure, including a building site, and E is wrong because a lawful visitor need not pay to be owed the common duty of care."},{"t":"occupiers-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"A private estate charges visitors to walk its woodland trail. One small footbridge over a stream has rotten planks. At each end of the bridge the estate puts up a large, clear sign reading \"Danger: bridge unsafe. Use the stepping-stone crossing 20 metres downstream\", and the stepping stones are safe and easy to use. Yusuf, an adult visitor, reads the sign but decides to use the bridge anyway, falls through it and injures his back.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the estate is liable to Yusuf?","o":["It is liable, because a warning sign on its own can never discharge the common duty of care.","It is not liable, because the warning, with a safe alternative route, enabled him to be reasonably safe.","It is liable, because it knew the bridge was dangerous and chose not to repair it.","It is not liable, because a visitor who has paid for entry cannot sue an occupier for injury.","It is liable, but his damages will be reduced by 25% because he chose to ignore the sign."],"a":1,"e":"Under the **Occupiers' Liability Act 1957 s.2(4)(a)**, a warning discharges the common duty of care if, **in all the circumstances, it was enough to enable the visitor to be reasonably safe**; a clear, specific sign pointing to a safe alternative crossing met that test, so B is right. A overstates the rule: a warning may be insufficient where, for example, there is no alternative route, but it can be enough. C is wrong because an occupier may deal with a danger by warning rather than repair, D has no basis, and E wrongly assumes a breach that the warning prevented."},{"t":"occupiers-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"A water company owns a disused flooded quarry, fenced and signed \"Private property: deep cold water, no swimming\". It knows that young people sometimes climb the fence on summer evenings. Kyle, aged 19, climbs the fence one evening, jumps into the water from a rock and suffers cold-water shock, nearly drowning. There is nothing unusual or hidden about the water.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the company is liable to Kyle under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1984?","o":["It is liable, because it knew that trespassers used the quarry and did not make the fence impassable.","It is liable, but his damages will be reduced for contributory negligence because he ignored the signs.","It is not liable, because no duty is ever owed to an adult trespasser under the 1984 Act.","It is not liable, because the risk was obvious and came from his own activity, not the state of the land.","It is not liable, because the signs excluded all liability to anyone entering without permission."],"a":3,"e":"Under the 1984 Act an occupier is not generally required to protect an adult from an **obvious risk** that arises from the claimant's **own chosen activity** rather than from any danger due to the **state of the premises** (Tomlinson v Congleton BC), so D is right. A and B assume a breach of duty, but knowledge of trespassers only goes to whether a duty arises and does not oblige the occupier to guard against obvious risks. C is wrong because adult trespassers can be owed a duty where the s.1(3) conditions are met, and E overstates the effect of the signs, which warn rather than exclude."},{"t":"product-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"Brightside Ltd buys hairdryers from a manufacturer in Vietnam, ships them to the United Kingdom and sells them to independent chemists. Zainab buys one from her local chemist. A wiring defect causes it to burst into flames while she is using it, burning her face. The Vietnamese manufacturer has no assets or presence in the United Kingdom.\n\nUnder the Consumer Protection Act 1987, which of the following best describes who Zainab can sue?","o":["Brightside, as the importer of the product into the United Kingdom, without proving fault.","Only the chemist, because it is the business that sold the hairdryer to her.","Only the manufacturer, because the Act makes only the maker of a product liable.","Brightside, but only if she proves it was careless in failing to test the hairdryers.","Nobody, because the Act does not apply to products made outside the United Kingdom."],"a":0,"e":"Under the **Consumer Protection Act 1987 s.2(2)(c)**, a business that **imports a product into the United Kingdom** to supply it to others is liable for its defects in the same way as the producer, and liability is **strict** (no proof of fault), so A is right. D wrongly adds a fault requirement, which belongs to a negligence claim. B is wrong because a mere supplier is liable only if it fails to identify the producer or importer when asked, C ignores the importer and own-brander, and E is wrong because the importer rule exists precisely to cover foreign-made goods."},{"t":"product-liability","p":"FLK1","q":"Moses runs a café and buys a commercial-grade coffee machine for it. A design defect causes the machine to leak scalding water, which warps the café's wooden serving counter (repair cost £1,800) and burns Moses's hand. Moses wants to sue the manufacturer under the Consumer Protection Act 1987.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Moses can claim under the Act?","o":["Damages for the counter and his burns, because the property damage exceeds the £275 threshold.","Nothing at all, because the machine was bought for business rather than private purposes.","Damages for his burns, but not the counter, which is property not intended for private use.","Damages for the counter only, because claims for personal injury must be brought in negligence.","Damages for the counter, his burns and the cost of replacing the coffee machine itself."],"a":2,"e":"The 1987 Act covers **personal injury** whatever the setting, but **property damage** only to property **ordinarily intended for private use** and intended by the claimant mainly for private use, and above **£275** (s.5), so C is right. A is the tempting wrong answer: the £275 threshold is irrelevant once the property is business property, and Moses must claim for the counter in negligence, proving fault. B wrongly excludes the personal injury claim, D gets the position the wrong way round, and E is wrong because damage to the defective product itself is never recoverable under the Act."},{"t":"nuisance-rylands","p":"FLK1","q":"Agnieszka has run a plant nursery for ten years on the edge of a long-established industrial estate. A new metal-finishing works opens next door, and its fumes kill several hundred of her shrubs and stain her greenhouse glass. The shrubs are ordinary garden varieties. The works argues that fumes are only to be expected in an industrial area.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the relevance of the industrial character of the area to her claim in private nuisance?","o":["It is a complete defence, because Agnieszka chose to run a nursery beside an industrial estate.","It is irrelevant, because the fumes have caused physical damage to her property, not mere discomfort.","It means she must prove the works was negligent before she can succeed in private nuisance.","It makes her an abnormally sensitive user, so she can recover only if hardier plants would be harmed.","It is decisive, because every interference must be judged by the standards of the locality."],"a":1,"e":"The **character of the locality** is relevant to nuisance causing **discomfort or inconvenience**, but **not** where the interference causes **material physical damage** to the claimant's property (St Helen's Smelting Co v Tipping), so B is right. E overstates the role of locality, and A is wrong because locality is never a complete defence. C is wrong because private nuisance turns on whether the interference is unreasonable, not on negligence, and D is wrong because ordinary garden shrubs are not an abnormally sensitive use."},{"t":"nuisance-rylands","p":"FLK1","q":"For six weeks a haulage company parks lines of lorries along a narrow high street while waiting to load at its depot, blocking the road to through traffic and forcing shoppers to make long detours on foot. Rhys, who owns a bakery on the high street, loses about £6,000 in trade because customers cannot reach his shop. Other local residents have simply been inconvenienced.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Rhys's claim against the haulage company?","o":["It fails, because obstructing a highway is a crime that only the police or the council can pursue.","It fails, because loss of trade is pure economic loss and is never recoverable in nuisance.","It can succeed in private nuisance, because that tort does not require any interest in land.","It can succeed under the rule in Rylands, because the lorries were brought onto the road and escaped.","It can succeed in public nuisance, because he has suffered special damage beyond that of the public."],"a":4,"e":"Obstructing a highway is a **public nuisance**, and an individual can sue in tort if they suffer **special damage** over and above that suffered by the public generally, which can include **loss of trade**, so E is right. A is wrong because, although public nuisance is also a crime, an individual with special damage has a civil claim. B is wrong because economic loss such as lost trade is recoverable in public nuisance, C misstates private nuisance (which does require a proprietary interest), and D is wrong because Rylands needs an escape of a dangerous thing from the defendant's land in the course of an extraordinary use."}],"trusts-p1":[{"t":"certainties","p":"FLK2","q":"Basil, who died in 2025, left a will directing his trustees \"to allow my companion Imelda to live in my flat in Bath for the rest of her life, and to pay her a reasonable income from my investment portfolio\". The residuary beneficiary, Basil's nephew, argues that the words \"a reasonable income\" are too vague and that the gift of income fails.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the gift of income to Imelda?","o":["It fails, because the amount would depend on the trustees' personal opinion.","It fails for uncertainty of subject matter, and the income goes on resulting trust.","It is valid, because the court can measure a reasonable income objectively.","It is valid, and the trustees may pay her whatever sum they personally think fit.","It fails, because a fixed trust must state the exact amount each person receives."],"a":2,"e":"Certainty of subject matter requires that the court can identify what each beneficiary takes, but this can be met by an **objective yardstick**: a direction to pay a **reasonable income** is valid because the court can quantify it by reference to the beneficiary's circumstances and standard of living (Re Golay's Will Trusts). It does not depend on the trustees' subjective views, which is why the answers based on the trustees' personal opinion are wrong. No exact figure is required where an objective standard is given, so there is nothing to fall on resulting trust."},{"t":"certainties","p":"FLK2","q":"Fenella transferred £240,000 to trustees \"for such of the persons who have worked at my bakery at any time since it opened in 1985 as my trustees in their absolute discretion select\". The staff records for 1985 to 2005 were destroyed in a flood, so the trustees cannot draw up a full list of everyone who has worked there. For most people who apply, however, they can tell whether or not that person worked at the bakery.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the validity of this trust?","o":["It fails, because the trustees are unable to draw up a complete list of the class.","It fails for evidential uncertainty, and the fund is held on resulting trust for Fenella.","It is valid, but the fund must be divided equally among all the former staff identified.","It is valid, because it is possible to say whether any given person is or is not in the class.","It fails, because a class of every worker since 1985 is too wide to be administered."],"a":3,"e":"This is a **discretionary trust**, so certainty of objects requires only the **'is or is not' test**: it must be possible to say of any given person whether they are within the class (McPhail v Doulton). 'Persons who have worked at my bakery' is **conceptually certain**, and the lost records create only **evidential** difficulty, which does not invalidate the trust: a person who cannot prove they are in the class is simply treated as outside it (Re Baden's Deed Trusts (No 2)). The complete list test applies to **fixed** trusts, and a class of the bakery's former workers is nowhere near so wide as to be administratively unworkable."},{"t":"formalities","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2023 Cecily made a will leaving £50,000 \"to my friend Lionel absolutely\". In 2025 she told Lionel that she wanted him to hold the £50,000 for her former carer, Marisol, after her death, and Lionel agreed to do so. Nothing was put in writing. Cecily died in 2026. Lionel now says the will gives him the money outright and he intends to keep it. Cecily's residuary estate goes to her son.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who is entitled to the £50,000?","o":["Lionel, because the will leaves the money to him absolutely and nothing was in writing.","Marisol, because the trust was communicated to and accepted by Lionel before Cecily's death.","Cecily's son, because the trust was not communicated to Lionel before the will was made.","Cecily's son, because Lionel holds the money on resulting trust for her residuary estate.","Marisol, but only if Lionel confirms the arrangement in a document he has signed."],"a":1,"e":"This is a **fully secret trust**: the will gives the property to Lionel apparently outright, but he agreed during Cecily's lifetime to hold it for Marisol. Equity enforces it outside the Wills Act formalities to prevent fraud, provided there was **intention**, **communication** and **acceptance** at any time **before the testator's death** (Ottaway v Norman). The rule that communication must come **before or at the time of the will** applies only to **half-secret** trusts (where the will itself says the gift is on trust), so the 2025 communication is effective and no writing is needed."},{"t":"constitution","p":"FLK2","q":"Niamh lent her nephew Declan £15,000 in 2022. In 2024 she told him: \"Forget about the loan, I don't want it back.\" She never signed any document releasing the debt, and Declan gave nothing in return. Until her death in 2026 she told several friends that Declan's debt had been written off. Her will appoints Declan and Niamh's sister Bridget as executors and leaves her residuary estate to Bridget, who says that Declan must repay the £15,000 to the estate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Declan must repay the loan?","o":["He must repay, because a promise to release a debt is ineffective without a deed or consideration.","He must repay, because he is only one of the two executors appointed in Niamh's will.","He need not repay, because her words amounted to a valid donatio mortis causa of the debt.","He must repay, because an executor cannot take a personal benefit from the estate he administers.","He need not repay, because her continuing intention to forgive is perfected by his becoming executor."],"a":4,"e":"Under **Strong v Bird**, an imperfect lifetime gift (including the release of a debt) is perfected where the donor intended an **immediate** gift, that intention **continued until death**, and the donee is appointed **executor**, so that the legal title vests in them. It is enough that Declan is **one of several** executors. Although a release would normally need a deed or consideration, that is exactly the gap this exception fills; a **donatio mortis causa** (a gift made in contemplation of imminent death) does not fit, because Niamh was not contemplating death."},{"t":"constitution","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2023, the day before a major heart operation, Ruth handed her nephew Alun the only key to a locked box containing her building society passbook, saying: \"If I don't come through this, the money in that account is yours.\" The operation was a success and Ruth made a full recovery. She never asked for the key back. In 2025 she was killed in a road accident. Her will leaves her whole estate to her friend Joyce.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who is entitled to the money in the account?","o":["Joyce, because the gift lapsed when Ruth recovered from the operation she had feared.","Alun, because Ruth handed over dominion of the passbook and never revoked the gift.","Alun, because Ruth died within two years of making the gift to him.","Alun, because Ruth's intention to benefit him continued unchanged until her death.","Joyce, because money in a building society account cannot be given by delivering a passbook."],"a":0,"e":"A **donatio mortis causa** (a gift made in contemplation of death) requires the donor to contemplate **death in the near future from a specific cause**, to make the gift **conditional on dying** from it, and to part with **dominion** (control) over the property or the means of getting it. Such a gift is revocable and **lapses automatically if the donor recovers** from the contemplated peril; Ruth's later death in an accident is not the death she contemplated. Delivering a passbook can be enough dominion for a building society account, and the continuing-intention rule from Strong v Bird does not help Alun, because he is not Ruth's executor."},{"t":"beneficial-entitlement","p":"FLK2","q":"Trustees hold 600 of the 1,000 issued shares in Oakhurst Ltd, a private family company, on trust for three siblings, Nadia, Ewan and Ffion, in equal shares absolutely. All three are adults with full capacity. Nadia asks the trustees to transfer 200 of the shares to her now. Ewan and Ffion want the trust to continue, because the trust's 60% stake gives control of the company, and splitting off Nadia's shares would leave a 40% minority stake worth much less per share.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Nadia's position?","o":["The trustees may refuse, because dividing the holding would harm the other siblings' interests.","She can insist, because she is an adult with capacity and is absolutely entitled to a third share.","She can insist, but only if she compensates the others for the loss of the controlling stake.","She cannot insist, because a beneficiary under a fixed trust has only a hope of benefit.","She can insist, but only once the court approves under the Variation of Trusts Act 1958."],"a":0,"e":"Under the **rule in Saunders v Vautier**, a single adult beneficiary with a **vested share** can normally call for that share where the property is easily divisible, such as listed shares or cash. But where splitting the property would **damage the interests of the other beneficiaries**, as with a house or a **controlling block of private company shares**, the trustees may refuse to hand over the share in specie (Stephenson v Barclays Bank). All three siblings acting together could end the trust; Nadia's fixed one-third share is a real proprietary interest, not a mere hope, and the Variation of Trusts Act 1958 is relevant only where some beneficiaries cannot consent."},{"t":"purpose-charitable","p":"FLK2","q":"Bernadette's will leaves £30,000 to the parish priest of St Columba's Roman Catholic Church \"for the saying of masses for the repose of my soul\". The masses at St Columba's are always celebrated in public and are open to anyone who wishes to attend. Bernadette's residuary beneficiary argues that the gift is a non-charitable purpose trust and fails.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the gift?","o":["It fails, because a gift for the benefit of one person's soul is a purely private purpose.","It is charitable, as advancing religion, because the masses are said in public.","It is valid only as a non-charitable purpose trust, limited to a 21-year perpetuity period.","It fails, because a religious service cannot be shown to be for the public benefit.","It is a gift to the priest personally, who may use the money as he chooses."],"a":1,"e":"A gift for the saying of masses is **charitable** as the **advancement of religion** (Charities Act 2011 s.3(1)(c)) where the masses are celebrated **in public**, because the public benefit lies in the edification of those who attend (Re Hetherington). Masses said **in private** would not satisfy public benefit, but even they can be valid as one of the **anomalous** non-charitable purpose trusts; here, though, the trust is fully charitable, so no perpetuity limit applies. The words 'for the saying of masses' impose a purpose, so the priest does not take the money for himself."},{"t":"purpose-charitable","p":"FLK2","q":"The Greenacre Allotment Society is an unincorporated association whose rules say nothing about what happens to its property if it is wound up. Its funds of £24,000 came from members' annual subscriptions and from fundraising events. In 2026 the 40 current members voted to dissolve the society. Several people who were members in earlier years, and who paid subscriptions then, claim a share of the £24,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the £24,000 should be distributed?","o":["To the Crown as ownerless property, because the society no longer exists.","Among all past and present members, in proportion to the subscriptions each paid.","Among the 40 current members, in proportion to how long each has been a member.","Cy-près to a charitable body with gardening purposes similar to the society's.","Equally among the 40 people who were members when the society was dissolved."],"a":4,"e":"Under the **contract-holding theory**, the property of an **unincorporated association** (a club bound by rules that cannot own property itself) belongs to its **current members**, subject to their contract under the rules. On dissolution, where the rules are silent, the surplus is shared **equally among the members at the date of dissolution** (Re Bucks Constabulary Widows' and Orphans' Fund Friendly Society (No 2)). Former members gave up their rights when they left, so there is no resulting trust for them, the property is not ownerless because the members own it, and cy-près applies only to charitable funds."},{"t":"resulting-trusts","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2010 Wilfred transferred £200,000 to trustees \"on trust to pay the income to my sister Olwen for the rest of her life\". The trust deed said nothing about what should happen to the capital after Olwen's death. Olwen died in 2026, leaving a will giving all her property to her son, Gethin. Wilfred is still alive.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who is entitled to the £200,000?","o":["Gethin, under Olwen's will.","The trustees, beneficially.","Wilfred, on a resulting trust.","The Crown, as bona vacantia.","Wilfred's children, equally."],"a":2,"e":"Where a settlor fails to dispose of the **whole beneficial interest**, the part not disposed of returns to the settlor on an **automatic resulting trust** (Vandervell v IRC). Olwen had only a **life interest**, which ended on her death, so nothing passes under her will to Gethin. The trustees were never meant to benefit, and the property is not ownerless (bona vacantia) because Wilfred is alive and entitled to it."},{"t":"resulting-trusts","p":"FLK2","q":"Twenty-five parents each contributed money, recorded by the organiser, Delroy, to a fund held by him \"solely to pay the travel costs of the under-14 team of Hartley Juniors Football Club to a tournament in Canada\". In total £9,000 was raised. Before any money was spent, the tournament was cancelled and will not be rearranged. The club's committee says the money should go into the club's general funds.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who is entitled to the £9,000?","o":["The club, to be used for its general purposes.","The players in the under-14 team, in equal shares.","The Crown, as bona vacantia (ownerless property).","The parents, in proportion to their contributions.","Delroy, as the legal owner of the whole fund."],"a":3,"e":"Where money is given for a **specific purpose** that **fails**, and the contributors did not intend an out-and-out gift, the fund is held on **automatic resulting trust** for the contributors, **in proportion** to what each gave (Re Abbott Fund Trusts). The parents gave 'solely' for the travel costs, so neither the club nor the players have any claim. Bona vacantia applies only where contributors parted with their money **out and out** (for example anonymous collections), which does not fit recorded contributions, and Delroy is a trustee who cannot benefit."},{"t":"family-home","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2015 Owen bought a house for £300,000 in his sole name. Before the purchase, he and his partner Talia expressly agreed that she would \"own part of the house\", but they never discussed how much. Talia paid £30,000 towards the deposit, later paid half the mortgage instalments for five years, and paid for a new roof. They have now separated. Owen accepts that Talia has some share but disputes its size.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Talia's share will be decided?","o":["The court will fix a fair share in light of their whole course of dealing over the property.","Her share will be 10%, strictly in proportion to her contribution to the purchase price.","Her share will be one half, because equality follows from any common intention to share.","She has no share, because the agreement was not in writing signed by Owen.","She is entitled only to repayment of her £30,000, as a loan she made to Owen."],"a":0,"e":"Where the home is in one partner's **sole name**, the claimant must first show a **common intention to share** plus **detrimental reliance**; here there was an express agreement and substantial contributions. If the parties never agreed the size of the share, the court **infers or, if necessary, imputes** a fair share, having regard to the **whole course of dealing** between them in relation to the property (Oxley v Hiscock; Jones v Kernott). A purely arithmetical resulting trust ignores her mortgage payments and the roof; there is no presumption of equality in sole-name cases, and constructive trusts need no writing (LPA 1925 s.53(2))."},{"t":"family-home","p":"FLK2","q":"Lewis and his wife Joanne are the registered owners of a house. Lewis's mother, Maud, contributed £80,000 towards the purchase price and has lived in the house with them ever since, so she has a beneficial interest under a trust. Without telling Maud, Lewis and Joanne sold the house to Kamal, who paid the purchase price to both of them. Kamal now seeks possession, and Maud argues that her interest binds him because she was in actual occupation.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Maud's position against Kamal?","o":["Her interest binds Kamal as an overriding interest, because she was in actual occupation.","Her interest binds Kamal, because he should have inspected the house and asked who lived there.","Her interest was overreached by payment to two trustees, so it now attaches to the sale money.","Her interest binds Kamal, because she did not give her written consent to the sale.","Her interest binds Kamal only for the £80,000 she contributed, not any increase in value."],"a":2,"e":"A beneficial interest under a trust of land can bind a buyer as an **overriding interest** if the beneficiary is in **actual occupation**, but not if it has been **overreached**. **Overreaching** happens when the buyer pays the purchase money to **at least two trustees** (or a trust corporation): the beneficiary's interest is then detached from the land and attaches to the **proceeds of sale** (City of London Building Society v Flegg). Kamal paid both Lewis and Joanne, so actual occupation, inspection and Maud's lack of consent are irrelevant; her remedy is against Lewis and Joanne and the money."},{"t":"fiduciary-duties","p":"FLK2","q":"Edmund and Farah are the trustees of a trust that owns Ashcombe Farm. The owner of an adjoining field offered to sell it to them, as owners of the farm, for £100,000. The trustees decided that the trust could not afford it. Edmund then bought the field for himself with his own money, with Farah's agreement. The beneficiaries were not told. The field has since received planning permission and is now worth £200,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the beneficiaries' position?","o":["Edmund may keep the field, because the trust could not have afforded to buy it.","Edmund holds the field on trust for them, because the opportunity came to him as a trustee.","Edmund may keep the field, because his co-trustee agreed to the purchase.","Edmund must account only if the beneficiaries can show that the trust suffered a loss.","Edmund may keep the field, because he paid the full price out of his own money."],"a":1,"e":"Under the strict **no profit rule**, a trustee must not make a profit from an opportunity that came to them **because of their position**, unless the beneficiaries give **fully informed consent** or the trust instrument authorises it (Regal (Hastings) v Gulliver; Boardman v Phipps). The rule applies even where the trust **could not have taken the opportunity** itself and the trustee acted honestly with their own money. A co-trustee cannot authorise the profit, and no loss to the trust need be shown; Edmund holds the field, including its increase in value, for the beneficiaries."},{"t":"fiduciary-duties","p":"FLK2","q":"Imogen and Jack, the trustees of a family trust, sold a commercial unit belonging to the trust to Harbourline Ltd for its full market value, after open marketing and an independent valuation. Imogen owns 80% of the shares in Harbourline Ltd. The trust deed says nothing about purchases by trustees, and the beneficiaries were not consulted. Two years later the unit has risen sharply in value, and the beneficiaries want the sale set aside.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the sale can be set aside?","o":["It cannot, because the unit was sold at full value after open marketing.","It cannot, because Harbourline Ltd is a separate legal person from Imogen.","It cannot, because Jack, an independent co-trustee, agreed to the sale.","It can, but only if the beneficiaries prove the price was below market value.","It can be set aside at the beneficiaries' option, however fair the price was."],"a":4,"e":"Under the **self-dealing rule**, a sale of trust property to a trustee is **voidable** at the option of any beneficiary, **however fair** the price and however honest the process, unless authorised by the trust instrument, the beneficiaries' fully informed consent or the court (Tito v Waddell (No 2); Kane v Radley-Kane). The rule cannot be avoided by buying through a **company the trustee controls**, because Imogen is in substance on both sides of the transaction. A co-trustee's agreement is no substitute for authorisation, and the beneficiaries do not need to show an undervalue."},{"t":"trustees-appointment","p":"FLK2","q":"Godfrey's will appoints Priyanka and Leon as the trustees of a trust holding several let houses. After Godfrey's death, Leon collected the rents for four months, instructed a letting agent and opened a trust bank account. He now finds the work too time-consuming and tells Priyanka that he wishes to disclaim the trusteeship. There is no one willing to replace him.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Leon's position?","o":["He cannot disclaim, having accepted the trust by conduct, and can leave only by retiring.","He can disclaim at any time by deed, because no one can be forced to act as a trustee.","He can disclaim the management of the let houses but remain a trustee for other purposes.","He can disclaim, but only with the court's permission, because the trust holds land.","He ceases to be a trustee as soon as he notifies Priyanka in writing that he disclaims."],"a":0,"e":"A person named as trustee can **disclaim** (refuse the office), but only **before accepting** it, and acceptance can be shown by **conduct**, such as collecting rents and managing the property. A disclaimer must also be of the **whole** trust, not part of it. Having accepted, Leon can leave only by **retirement** (with a replacement under TA 1925 s.36, or without one under s.39 if at least two persons or a trust corporation will remain), by removal or by death; since only Priyanka would remain and no one will replace him, s.39 retirement is not yet available."},{"t":"trustee-duties-powers","p":"FLK2","q":"Under a will trust that arose in 2019, trustees hold a fund for Grace contingently on her reaching 25. When Grace was 21, the trustees used their statutory power of advancement to pay £40,000 into her bank account \"to enable her to pay the deposit on a flat\". They knew that her father was pressing her for money for his struggling business, but took no steps to check how the money was used. Grace handed it all to her father, whose business failed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the trustees acted in breach of trust?","o":["They are not in breach, because they may advance up to the whole of Grace's presumptive share.","They are not in breach, because once paid to an adult beneficiary the money was hers to use as she wished.","They are in breach, because capital cannot be advanced to a beneficiary before she satisfies the contingency.","They are in breach, because they had to take reasonable steps to see the money was used for the stated purpose.","They are not in breach, because the trustees' decision to advance capital cannot be questioned by anyone."],"a":3,"e":"TA 1925 s.32 allows trustees to advance capital for a beneficiary's **advancement or benefit**, including to a **contingent** beneficiary and (for a trust arising after 1 October 2014) up to the **whole** presumptive share. But where trustees advance money **for a stated purpose**, they must take **reasonable steps to ensure it is applied for that purpose**; paying it over when they knew of the father's pressure, and doing nothing more, is a breach (Re Pauling's Settlement Trusts). The size of the advance was within the power, which is why the presumptive-share answer misses the point."},{"t":"trustee-duties-powers","p":"FLK2","q":"The Fernbrook Wetlands Trust is a charity whose purposes are the protection of the natural environment and the reduction of climate change. After taking investment advice, its trustees adopt a policy of excluding fossil fuel companies from the portfolio. They assessed the likely reduction in returns as modest, and weighed it against the conflict between such investments and the charity's purposes and the risk of losing supporters. One trustee objects that they must always seek the highest possible return.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the policy is permitted?","o":["It is not permitted, because trustees must always seek the best return and ignore all non-financial factors.","It is permitted, because charity trustees may exclude conflicting investments after weighing the cost.","It is permitted only if every donor to the charity has agreed to the exclusion of fossil fuel companies.","It is permitted only if the charity's governing document expressly authorises ethical investment.","It is not permitted unless the Charity Commission has first given its consent to the exclusion policy."],"a":1,"e":"Trustees' investment powers must normally be used in the **best financial interests** of the beneficiaries, putting personal, political or moral views aside (Cowan v Scargill). However, **charity trustees** may exclude investments that **conflict with the charity's purposes**, provided they reasonably **weigh the likely financial effect** against the conflict and other relevant factors such as reputational risk (Butler-Sloss v Charity Commission). Neither donor agreement, an express clause nor Charity Commission consent is required where trustees have taken advice and carried out that balancing exercise."},{"t":"breach-of-trust","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2017 Bethan, the sole trustee of her late father's trust, which was for his children equally, distributed the £300,000 fund equally between herself and her brother, keeping £150,000. She honestly and reasonably believed, after making proper enquiries, that they were his only children. In 2026 their half-sister Nerys, an adult throughout, whose existence nobody had known about, sued Bethan, who still has the £150,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the amount Nerys can recover from Bethan?","o":["£150,000, everything that Bethan received and still holds.","£100,000, the full value of Nerys's proper one-third share.","Nothing, because more than six years have passed since 2017.","Nothing, because Bethan acted honestly and reasonably throughout.","£50,000, the excess Bethan received over her own proper share."],"a":4,"e":"Ordinarily there is **no limitation period** for recovering trust property **still held** by a trustee (Limitation Act 1980 s.21(1)(b)). But under **s.21(2)**, where a **trustee who is also a beneficiary** acted **honestly and reasonably** in distributing the fund, her liability after the six-year period (s.21(3), running from the 2017 distribution, as Nerys has been an adult throughout and there was no fraud or concealment) is **limited to the excess over her proper share**: she received £150,000, her proper share was £100,000, so she is liable for £50,000. Nerys must look to her brother for the rest of her share; court relief under TA 1925 s.61 is discretionary and would not wipe out a claim to money Bethan still holds."},{"t":"breach-of-trust","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2022 Graham, then the sole trustee of a family trust, lent £70,000 of trust money, without security and without authority, to his brother's business. In 2024 Priya was appointed as a second trustee. She never looked at the trust records or asked about the trust's assets. At that time the loan could have been recovered in full, but the business has now become insolvent and the money is lost.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Priya's liability to the beneficiaries?","o":["She is not liable, because a trustee is never responsible for anything that happened before her appointment.","She is liable for the 2022 loan itself, because on appointment she became jointly liable with Graham for it.","She is liable for the loss caused by her own failure to investigate and pursue the loan after her appointment.","She is not liable, because only Graham, who made the unauthorised loan, can be sued for the loss.","She is not liable, because as an unpaid trustee who acted honestly she is automatically relieved by the court."],"a":2,"e":"A new trustee is **not liable for breaches committed before her appointment**, but on appointment she must **get in and safeguard the trust property** and investigate past dealings, taking steps to remedy any breach she discovers. Priya's own **failure to investigate and recover the loan** while it was recoverable is a breach in itself, and it caused the loss, so she is liable for that loss, not for Graham's original breach as such. Relief under TA 1925 s.61 is never automatic: the trustee must show she acted honestly **and reasonably**, and doing nothing at all is not reasonable."},{"t":"strangers","p":"FLK2","q":"Dariusz runs a company formation business. A client, Mr Hale, asked him to receive £600,000 into his company's client account and immediately send it on to several overseas companies, for a fee of £5,000. Dariusz suspected that the money might be investors' funds that Hale was misappropriating, but deliberately asked no questions so that he would not find out. In fact Hale was a trustee and the money belonged to the trust. Dariusz says he never knew that a trust existed or what its terms were.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Dariusz's liability to the beneficiaries?","o":["He is not liable, because he did not know that a trust existed or what its terms were.","He is liable, but only to repay the £5,000 fee that he received for his services.","He is liable for knowing receipt, because the trust money passed through his company's account.","He is liable for dishonest assistance, as deliberately ignoring his suspicion can be dishonest.","He is not liable, unless he himself realised that honest people would regard his conduct as dishonest."],"a":3,"e":"**Dishonest assistance** requires a breach of trust, assistance by the defendant, and **dishonesty**, judged objectively: the court identifies what the defendant actually knew or suspected and asks whether **ordinary decent people** would regard the conduct as dishonest (Ivey v Genting; Barlow Clowes v Eurotrust). **Deliberately avoiding enquiry** to avoid confirming a suspicion counts, and the defendant need not know the details of the trust. Liability is for the **whole loss**, not just his fee; money passing through an account on another's instructions is not **receipt for his own benefit**, and the old requirement that the defendant realise others would think it dishonest no longer applies."},{"t":"remedies-tracing","p":"FLK2","q":"Gwen, the sole trustee of a family trust, used £20,000 of trust money, and nothing else, to buy a painting. She then gave the painting to her son, Hamish, as a birthday present. Hamish had no idea where the money for it had come from. The painting is now worth £70,000. Gwen has been declared bankrupt.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the beneficiaries' best claim?","o":["They can claim only £20,000 as unsecured creditors in Gwen's bankruptcy.","They can claim the painting itself from Hamish, including the increase in its value.","They can claim nothing from Hamish, because he received the painting in good faith.","They can claim a charge over the painting for £20,000, but not the increase in value.","They can claim £20,000 from Hamish personally for knowing receipt of trust property."],"a":1,"e":"Where trust money alone is used to buy an asset, the beneficiaries can **trace** into it and **claim the asset itself, including any increase in value** (or take a charge over it if that is better). A proprietary claim can be made against anyone holding the traced property except a **bona fide purchaser for value without notice**; Hamish is an **innocent volunteer** (he gave nothing), so his good faith does not protect him. He is not personally liable for **knowing receipt**, because he had no knowledge making it unconscionable to keep the painting, but the proprietary claim does not depend on his knowledge."},{"t":"remedies-tracing","p":"FLK2","q":"On 1 March Hugo, a trustee, bought a vintage car for £60,000 using a short-term loan from his bank, intending from the outset to repay the loan with trust money. On 10 March he took £60,000 from the trust account and used it to pay off the loan in full. Hugo is now bankrupt, and the car is worth £75,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the beneficiaries can claim the car?","o":["They can, because the loan and its repayment with trust money formed one co-ordinated scheme.","They cannot, because trust money can never be traced into an asset bought before it was taken.","They cannot, because paying off a debt dissipates the trust money, leaving nothing to trace.","They cannot, but they can recover the £60,000 from the bank as a recipient of trust money.","They can, but only for £60,000, because any increase in value belongs to Hugo's creditors."],"a":0,"e":"Normally trust money cannot be traced into an asset acquired **before** the money was misappropriated, and using trust money to pay off a debt usually **dissipates** it. But **backwards tracing** is allowed where there is a **co-ordinated scheme** linking the acquisition of the asset with the later misuse of trust money, such as buying on credit **intending** to repay with trust money (Federal Republic of Brazil v Durant International, a Privy Council decision treated as the leading modern authority). The claimant must show that co-ordination on the facts; here Hugo intended from the outset to use trust money and did so ten days later, so the link is clear. Because trust money alone paid for the car, the claim is to the car itself and not merely a charge for £60,000. The bank received the money in repayment of a debt, so it gave **value** in good faith and has no liability; the beneficiaries can claim the car, including its increase in value."}],"trusts-p2":[{"t":"certainties","p":"FLK2","q":"Marguerite died in 2026. Her will leaves her investment portfolio, worth £180,000, \"to my husband Ferdinand, and I desire that he will make such provision for our godchildren as he thinks fit\". Nothing else in the will refers to the godchildren. Ferdinand has told the godchildren that he intends to keep the whole portfolio for himself.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the godchildren's position?","o":["They are entitled to the portfolio in equal shares, because the will names them as the people to be provided for.","They are objects of a discretionary trust, because Ferdinand must choose how much each of them receives.","They have no claim, because the words used express only a wish and Ferdinand takes the portfolio outright.","They have no claim, because the will fails to identify how much of the portfolio is to be held for them.","They can claim a fair share, because Ferdinand holds the portfolio on a secret trust in their favour."],"a":2,"e":"**Precatory words** (words of wish or hope, such as \"I desire\") **do not create a trust unless the document read as a whole shows an intention to impose an obligation**. Nothing else in the will points to an obligation, so Ferdinand takes the portfolio as an outright gift. A discretionary trust (B) would need certainty of intention in the first place, and the problem is not uncertainty of subject matter (D) but the absence of any intention to create a trust; there is no secret trust (E) because no obligation was communicated to and accepted by Ferdinand."},{"t":"formalities","p":"FLK2","q":"Lowell is the sole beneficiary of 10,000 shares in Fenwick plc, which Calder Nominees Ltd holds for him on a bare trust (a trust where the trustee simply holds the property to the beneficiary's order). Lowell telephoned Calder Nominees and told it to transfer the shares to his niece, Martha, as a present. Calder Nominees executed a stock transfer form and Martha was registered as the owner of the shares. Nothing was signed by Lowell. Lowell has since died, and his executors claim that the shares belong to his estate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who is entitled to the shares?","o":["Martha, because directing a transfer of legal title, intending the beneficial interest to pass, needs no writing.","Lowell's estate, because a disposition of an existing equitable interest is void unless it is in writing signed by Lowell.","Lowell's estate, because Martha gave no consideration and equity will not assist a volunteer to complete a gift.","Martha, but only because Calder Nominees signed a stock transfer form which counts as writing signed by Lowell's agent.","Calder Nominees, which holds the shares on a resulting trust for Lowell's estate until the position is resolved."],"a":0,"e":"Where a bare trust beneficiary directs the trustee to **transfer the legal title to a third party, intending the beneficial interest to pass with it, LPA 1925 s.53(1)(c) does not apply** and no signed writing by the beneficiary is needed (Vandervell v IRC). Martha has both the legal title and the beneficial interest. B would be right if Lowell had told the nominee to hold the shares for Martha while keeping the legal title (an oral direction of that kind is void); the gift is complete because legal title has passed, so the volunteer point in C does not arise, and D is wrong because the reason is not that the nominee's form counted as Lowell's writing."},{"t":"formalities","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2023 Bronwen told her son, Cai, \"From today I hold Gwynfa Cottage on trust for you.\" Bronwen owns the cottage, which is registered land, in her sole name. In 2025 she sent Cai a letter, which she signed, saying: \"As I told you in 2023, I hold Gwynfa Cottage on trust for you.\" Bronwen now wishes to sell the cottage and keep the proceeds, arguing that there was never a valid trust.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Cai's position?","o":["He has no enforceable interest, because the signed writing was made two years after the oral declaration.","He has no enforceable interest, because a declaration of trust of land must be made by deed.","He has an enforceable interest, but only from 2025, when the trust was first put into signed writing.","He has no enforceable interest, because Bronwen's letter was not signed in the presence of a witness.","He has an enforceable interest, because the signed letter evidences the trust declared orally in 2023."],"a":4,"e":"**A declaration of trust of land must be manifested and proved by signed writing (LPA 1925 s.53(1)(b))**, which is an evidential requirement: the writing need not be made at the same time as the declaration. Bronwen's signed letter proves the trust, so Cai can enforce it, and the trust dates from the oral declaration in 2023, not from the letter (so C is wrong). No deed (B) or witness (D) is needed; any signed writing by the person able to declare the trust will do."},{"t":"constitution","p":"FLK2","q":"Ophelia owned 5,000 shares in Kestrel Ltd, a private company. In March 2026 she signed a stock transfer form transferring the shares to her daughter, Saffron, as a gift, and handed the form and the share certificate to Saffron. Saffron posted them to the company the same day. Ophelia died a week later, before the directors met to register the transfer. Her will leaves her residuary estate to her nephew, Raymond.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position regarding the shares?","o":["The shares form part of Ophelia's residuary estate, because legal title had not passed to Saffron at her death.","Saffron is entitled to the shares in equity, and Ophelia's executors hold the legal title on trust for her.","Saffron is entitled to the shares only if Ophelia's executors choose to complete the registration of the transfer.","The shares form part of Ophelia's residuary estate, because Saffron gave no consideration for the transfer.","Saffron is entitled to the shares, because Ophelia made a valid gift in contemplation of her impending death."],"a":1,"e":"**A gift is complete in equity once the donor has done everything in her power to transfer the property**, even if a third party, here the company's directors, must still register the transfer (Re Rose). Ophelia signed the form and handed over the certificate, so from that moment she, and after her death her executors, held the legal title on trust for Saffron. A and D overlook this exception to the rule that equity will not assist a volunteer, the executors have no choice in the matter (C), and nothing suggests Ophelia contemplated imminent death, so this is not a donatio mortis causa (E)."},{"t":"constitution","p":"FLK2","q":"Tomasz wanted to settle his 2,000 shares in Larkspur plc on trust for his grandchildren. He appointed his friends Imani and Dafydd as trustees and wrote to them, in a letter he signed: \"I am transferring my Larkspur shares to you, to hold for my grandchildren.\" He never signed a stock transfer form or took any other step to transfer the shares. Tomasz died in 2026. His will appoints his wife, Ula, as sole executor and leaves everything to her.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position regarding the shares?","o":["The grandchildren are entitled, because Tomasz did everything in his power to transfer the shares to the trustees.","The grandchildren are entitled, because Tomasz's letter shows that he held the shares on trust for them himself.","The grandchildren are entitled, because they can enforce Tomasz's written promise to transfer the shares.","Ula takes the shares under the will, because the trust was never constituted and equity will not perfect it.","Ula takes the shares, but she must hold them on trust for the grandchildren because she is Tomasz's executor."],"a":3,"e":"**Equity will not perfect an imperfect gift, and a failed transfer to trustees will not be treated as a declaration by the settlor of himself as trustee** (Milroy v Lord). Tomasz intended to transfer the shares to Imani and Dafydd, not to hold them himself (so B is wrong), and he did not do everything in his power because he never signed a transfer form (so A is wrong). The grandchildren are volunteers who cannot enforce a gratuitous promise (C), and the rule that perfects a gift when the intended recipient becomes executor does not help, because Ula was not the intended recipient (E)."},{"t":"beneficial-entitlement","p":"FLK2","q":"Under his late aunt's will trust, £200,000 is held on trust for Tobias \"if he reaches 30, but if he dies before reaching that age, for the Hollin Bird Sanctuary\", a registered charity. Tobias is 24, has full capacity and wants the trustees to hand the whole fund to him now so that he can buy a flat. The trustees have refused.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Tobias's position?","o":["He cannot call for the fund alone, but he could do so with the agreement of the Hollin Bird Sanctuary.","He can call for the fund now, because he is the only person named to take it if the condition is met.","He can call for the fund now, because he is an adult with full capacity and the trust is for his benefit.","He cannot call for the fund under any circumstances until he reaches 30, because that is what the will says.","He can call for half of the fund now, because his interest and the charity's interest are of equal value."],"a":0,"e":"Under **the rule in Saunders v Vautier, the beneficiaries can end a trust only if together they are absolutely entitled and all are adults of full capacity**. Tobias's interest is contingent on reaching 30, and the charity takes if he dies first, so he alone is not absolutely entitled (B and C are wrong). Together, he and the charity hold the entire beneficial interest and could call for the fund, so the trustees' refusal is not the last word (D is wrong); there is no rule entitling him to half (E)."},{"t":"beneficial-entitlement","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2019 Viktor transferred £100,000 to trustees \"on trust for my granddaughter Petra absolutely, the capital to be paid to her when she reaches the age of 25\". There is no gift over if Petra dies before 25. Petra died in 2026, aged 22, leaving a will that gives everything to her partner, Jonas. Viktor's son, who is entitled to Viktor's residuary estate, claims that the fund should now come back to Viktor's estate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who is entitled to the trust fund?","o":["Viktor's estate, because Petra's interest was contingent on reaching 25 and that condition can no longer be met.","Viktor's estate, because the trust has failed and the fund is held on a resulting trust for the settlor.","The trustees, who may now distribute the fund among Viktor's other relatives as they think fit.","Jonas, but only for the income of the fund until the date on which Petra would have reached 25.","Jonas, because Petra's interest was vested and only the payment of the capital was postponed."],"a":4,"e":"**A gift to a beneficiary \"absolutely\" with only the payment postponed to a stated age gives a vested interest, which passes on the beneficiary's death to her estate**. Petra's interest was not conditional on reaching 25 (so A is wrong): the age affected only when she would receive the capital, so on her death the fund passes under her will to Jonas absolutely, and nothing fails to create a resulting trust (B). Indeed, as an adult absolutely entitled, Petra could have called for the fund at 18 under the rule in Saunders v Vautier."},{"t":"purpose-charitable","p":"FLK2","q":"Ilse, the founder of Marlowe Engineering Ltd, died in 2026. Her will leaves a building to trustees \"to be used for a period of 21 years as a gym and social club for the employees of Marlowe Engineering Ltd and, at the trustees' discretion, for other local residents\". The company has about 300 employees. Ilse's son, who is entitled to her residuary estate, argues that the trust is void.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the validity of the trust?","o":["It is void, because it is a trust for a non-charitable purpose and there is no one who can enforce it.","It is a valid charitable trust, because it promotes amateur sport and healthy recreation in the locality.","It is valid, because it directly and tangibly benefits an ascertainable group of people who can enforce it.","It is void, because the employees benefit only indirectly from a purpose and have no interest in the building.","It is valid, because it is one of the anomalous purpose trusts that equity allows to stand for 21 years."],"a":2,"e":"A trust expressed as a purpose is valid where it **directly and tangibly benefits ascertainable individuals who have standing to enforce it** (Re Denley), provided it is limited to the perpetuity period, as this one is. The employees benefit directly from using the gym and club, so the beneficiary principle is satisfied and A and D are wrong. It is not charitable (B) because a class defined by employment with one company lacks public benefit, and the anomalous exceptions (E) are limited to cases such as animals, graves and private masses."},{"t":"purpose-charitable","p":"FLK2","q":"Penrose Logistics plc has 40,000 employees across the UK. Its founder, Callum, leaves £2 million in his will on trust \"to fund university scholarships for the children of present and former employees of Penrose Logistics plc\". His executors want to know whether the trust is charitable, as this affects inheritance tax.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the trust is charitable?","o":["It is charitable, because a class of beneficiaries as large as this counts as a section of the public.","It is not charitable, because a class defined by a link to one employer is not a section of the public.","It is charitable, because a trust for the advancement of education is presumed to be for the public benefit.","It is not charitable, because scholarships to attend university do not count as the advancement of education.","It is charitable, because the exception allowing gifts to employees for the relief of poverty applies to it."],"a":1,"e":"**A class defined by a personal nexus, such as employment by a particular company, is not a section of the public, however large it is** (Oppenheim v Tobacco Securities Trust), so the trust fails the public benefit test. Size does not cure this (A), and since the Charities Act 2006 (now Charities Act 2011 s.4(2)) there is **no presumption** of public benefit for any purpose (C). Funding university study is clearly educational (D), and the special poverty exception that allows employee classes does not extend to education (E)."},{"t":"resulting-trusts","p":"FLK2","q":"Quentin and his friend Rosalind bought 8,000 shares in Tamworth Holdings plc for £80,000. Quentin paid £60,000 and Rosalind paid £20,000, but the shares were registered in Rosalind's sole name. There was no discussion about who would own the shares, nothing was put in writing, and there is no evidence that Quentin intended a gift or a loan. Rosalind now claims that the shares are entirely hers.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the beneficial ownership of the shares?","o":["Rosalind owns all the shares beneficially, because she is the registered legal owner and nothing was in writing.","Quentin and Rosalind own the shares beneficially in equal shares, because they bought them together.","Quentin owns all the shares beneficially, because he paid most of the purchase price and the shares were bought for him.","Rosalind holds the shares on a resulting trust for herself and Quentin in proportion to what each paid.","Rosalind owns all the shares beneficially, because Quentin's payment is presumed to be a gift to her."],"a":3,"e":"Where a person **contributes to the purchase price of property put in another's name, a resulting trust is presumed in proportion to the contribution**, unless there is evidence of a contrary intention. Rosalind holds the shares for Quentin as to three-quarters and herself as to one-quarter. Resulting trusts need no writing (LPA 1925 s.53(2)), so A is wrong; equality (B) is the starting point for a family home in joint names, not for an investment like this; and no presumption of advancement (gift) applies between friends (E)."},{"t":"family-home","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2018 Kwame and Lucy, an unmarried couple, bought a house for £320,000 in their joint names. The transfer contains no declaration of their beneficial interests and they made no other agreement about shares. Kwame paid 70% of the £40,000 deposit from his savings. The mortgage has been paid from their joint bank account, into which both paid their salaries, and they shared all household bills from that account. They have now separated.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the beneficial ownership of the house?","o":["They own it equally, because nothing in the facts displaces the presumption that joint owners share equally.","They own it in proportion to their contributions to the deposit, because that is the only unequal payment.","Kwame owns 70% and Lucy 30%, because a resulting trust arises in proportion to the deposit paid.","The court will divide the house in whatever shares it considers fair, considering all their circumstances.","Kwame owns it all beneficially unless Lucy can prove an express agreement that she would share."],"a":0,"e":"For a home bought by a couple in **joint names with no express declaration, the starting point is that they own it beneficially in equal shares, and the party claiming otherwise must show an unusual case** (Stack v Dowden). An unequal contribution to the deposit is not enough where, as here, the couple pooled their finances and paid the mortgage and bills jointly, so B and C are wrong. The court looks for the parties' intention, not what it thinks fair (D), and Lucy needs no express agreement (E) because she is a joint legal owner, which is the basis of the equal-shares presumption."},{"t":"fiduciary-duties","p":"FLK2","q":"Saoirse, a solicitor, and her brother-in-law, Dev, a retired teacher, are the trustees of a family trust. The trust deed contains no provision about remuneration. The trust is not a charity. Dev has agreed in a signed letter that Saoirse may charge for her work on the trust. Acting in the course of her practice as a solicitor, Saoirse has spent many hours managing the trust, including some routine administrative tasks that a lay trustee could have done.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Saoirse's entitlement to be paid?","o":["She can recover only her out-of-pocket expenses, because the trust deed contains no charging clause.","She can be paid only if all the adult beneficiaries consent, because payment would be a profit from her office.","She is entitled to reasonable remuneration for all her services, including those a lay trustee could provide.","She is entitled to reasonable remuneration, but only for work that needed her skills as a solicitor.","She can be paid only if the court authorises remuneration under its inherent jurisdiction."],"a":2,"e":"Under **TA 2000 s.29, a trustee acting in a professional capacity who is not a sole trustee is entitled to reasonable remuneration if each other trustee agrees in writing**, where the trust instrument is silent and the trust is not charitable. Dev has agreed in writing, so Saoirse can charge, and the statute expressly covers services that a lay trustee could provide (so D is wrong). A charging clause (A), the beneficiaries' consent (B) and a court order (E) are other routes to payment, but none is needed here."},{"t":"fiduciary-duties","p":"FLK2","q":"Trustees of a family trust hold 40% of the shares in Hatherley Ltd. Using the votes attached to the trust's shares, the trustees appointed one of themselves, Mehmet, as a director of the company. Mehmet receives director's fees of £30,000 a year. The trust deed says nothing about directors' fees. Mehmet works hard for the company and the trust's shares have risen in value.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Mehmet's position regarding the fees?","o":["He can keep the fees, because he earned them by working for the company and not for the trust.","He can keep the fees, because the trust has made no loss and its shares have risen in value.","He can keep the fees, but only if his work for the company was carried out honestly and in good faith.","He must account for any part of the fees that exceeds a fair rate of pay for the work he does.","He must account to the trust for the fees, as he got the directorship by using the trust's votes."],"a":4,"e":"**A trustee who obtains a benefit by using his position as trustee must account for it to the trust** (the no profit rule). Mehmet became a director only because the trustees used the trust's votes, so the fees are an unauthorised profit from his office (Re Macadam); it would be different if he had become a director independently of the trust's shareholding. The rule is strict: it does not matter that the trust has made no loss (B) or that he acted honestly (C), and he must account for the whole profit, not only an excess (D)."},{"t":"trustee-duties-powers","p":"FLK2","q":"Lorcan died in 2020. His will leaves his residuary estate to trustees on trust for his granddaughter, Ffion, \"if she reaches the age of 25\". The will says nothing about the income of the fund and does not exclude any statutory powers. Ffion has just turned 18. The trustees have been accumulating the income since Lorcan's death.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ffion's entitlement now?","o":["She is entitled to the capital of the fund now, because she has reached the age of majority.","The trustees must now pay her the income of the fund until she reaches 25 or dies before that age.","The trustees may pay her income if they think fit, but must otherwise go on accumulating it until she is 25.","She has no entitlement until she reaches 25, because her interest is contingent until then.","She is entitled to the accumulated income now, but has no right to future income until she is 25."],"a":1,"e":"Under **TA 1925 s.31(1)(ii), once a beneficiary with a contingent interest reaches 18, the trustees must pay them the income of their share until the interest vests or fails**, provided the gift carries the intermediate income and s.31 is not excluded. A contingent gift of residue carries the intermediate income, so Ffion is entitled to the income now, though not the capital (A). The discretion in C applies only while a beneficiary is a minor, and the past accumulations are added to capital, so Ffion takes them only if she reaches 25 (E)."},{"t":"trustee-duties-powers","p":"FLK2","q":"The trustees of a £1.5 million family trust appointed Abernethy Asset Management to manage the trust's investments on a discretionary basis, so that it could buy and sell without consulting them. They signed a written agreement with the manager after checking its record and fees, but never gave it any written statement of how the investments should be managed. The manager invested heavily in a single volatile sector, which the trustees had wanted to avoid, and the fund lost £200,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the trustees' position?","o":["They are not liable, because trustees are entitled to delegate investment decisions to an authorised manager.","They are not liable, because they used reasonable care in choosing the manager and agreeing its terms.","They are liable, because trustees may never delegate their discretions to anyone else.","They are in breach of trust, because they failed to give the manager a written policy statement.","They are liable, because the trust deed did not expressly authorise them to delegate asset management."],"a":3,"e":"Trustees may **collectively delegate asset management to an agent (TA 2000 s.11), but where the agent acts on a discretionary basis they must give it a written policy statement to guide how it exercises its functions (s.15)**. Failing to give one is a breach, regardless of the care taken in choosing the manager (B). Delegation itself was permitted by statute without any express clause (C and E), but the power to delegate (A) comes with the safeguards in s.15."},{"t":"breach-of-trust","p":"FLK2","q":"Under a trust set up in 2012, the income of the fund is payable to Ingrid for life, with the capital to her son, Conrad, on her death. In 2016 the trustees negligently invested £300,000 of the fund in an unauthorised investment that lost most of its value. They did not act fraudulently and kept none of the money. Ingrid died in 2024. Conrad, who learned of the loss only after her death, now wants to sue the trustees in 2026.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Conrad's claim is time-barred?","o":["It is time-barred, because more than six years have passed since the breach took place in 2016.","It is time-barred, because Conrad must sue within six years of first being a beneficiary of the trust.","It is not time-barred, because time ran against Conrad only once his interest fell into possession.","It is not time-barred, because no limitation period applies to any action for breach of trust.","It is not time-barred, because time runs only from when Conrad discovered the breach in 2024."],"a":2,"e":"The ordinary limitation period for breach of trust is six years from the breach (Limitation Act 1980 s.21(3)), but **for a beneficiary with a future interest, time does not start to run until the interest falls into possession**. Conrad's remainder fell into possession on Ingrid's death in 2024, so he has until 2030 and A is wrong. There is no limitation period only for fraud or where the trustee kept trust property (D), and the discoverability rule in E is not the reason, because time had not started to run at all."},{"t":"breach-of-trust","p":"FLK2","q":"Trustees hold a fund for three adult siblings, Felix, Gemma and Hari, in equal shares. Felix, who has full knowledge of the terms of the trust, persuaded the trustees to invest £150,000 of the fund in his friend's speculative start-up, an investment the trust deed does not authorise. The start-up has failed and the money is lost. Gemma and Hari did not know about the investment. All three siblings now want to sue the trustees.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the trustees' liability?","o":["They are liable to Gemma and Hari, but Felix cannot sue them for a breach he instigated with full knowledge.","They are liable to all three siblings, because a beneficiary cannot authorise a breach of the trust deed.","They are not liable to anyone, because one of the beneficiaries asked them to make the investment.","They are liable to all three siblings, but Felix can recover only half of the loss to his share.","They are not liable to Gemma and Hari, because the loss falls on Felix's share of the fund alone."],"a":0,"e":"**An adult beneficiary of full capacity who instigates or consents to a breach of trust with full knowledge cannot later sue the trustees for it**, but this does not affect the rights of beneficiaries who did not consent. So the trustees remain liable to Gemma and Hari (C and E are wrong), while Felix has no claim (B and D are wrong). The court may also **impound** Felix's interest (use it to indemnify the trustees) under TA 1925 s.62."},{"t":"trustees-appointment","p":"FLK2","q":"Halima died in 2026. Her will leaves her farm \"to Agnieszka, Babajide, Ceri, Duncan and Eleri as trustees\" on trust for her grandchildren. Agnieszka immediately signed a deed of disclaimer, refusing to act, before doing anything in relation to the trust. The other four named trustees are all adults of full capacity and are willing to act.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who are the trustees of the farm?","o":["Agnieszka, Babajide, Ceri and Duncan, because only the first four named in the will can be trustees of land.","All five named persons, because a person named as a trustee in a will cannot refuse the office.","Babajide, Ceri, Duncan and Eleri, but only once a court order is made confirming their appointment.","Babajide and Ceri only, because a trust of land must have exactly two trustees to give a valid receipt.","Babajide, Ceri, Duncan and Eleri, because they are the first four named who are able and willing to act."],"a":4,"e":"**A trust of land can have no more than four trustees, and where more are named, only the first four who are able and willing to act become trustees (TA 1925 s.34)**. Agnieszka validly disclaimed before accepting the office, which anyone named as trustee may do (so B is wrong), so she is passed over and the next four named take the legal title automatically, without a court order (C). A is wrong because it counts Agnieszka, who is unwilling to act, and two trustees are the minimum for a valid receipt for capital money, not a maximum (D)."},{"t":"trustees-appointment","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2015 Ignatius transferred investments to Nell and Osric on trust for his three adult grandchildren equally. The trust deed gives Ignatius the power to appoint new trustees. Osric now wants to step down, and the grandchildren, who are all of full capacity, want their cousin Kemi to replace him. Ignatius, who is alive and well, refuses to appoint Kemi and has proposed someone else. The deed does not exclude any statutory powers.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the grandchildren's position?","o":["They can direct the trustees in writing to appoint Kemi, because together they are absolutely entitled to the fund.","They cannot direct the appointment of Kemi while Ignatius holds the power, but together they could end the trust.","They can apply to court to have Kemi appointed, because the court must follow the wishes of all the beneficiaries.","They can direct the appointment of Kemi, provided Nell agrees to act alongside her as a trustee.","They have no way of influencing who becomes a trustee, because only Ignatius can appoint new trustees."],"a":1,"e":"**Beneficiaries can direct trustees to appoint a named person under TLATA 1996 s.19 only if no one is nominated by the trust instrument to appoint new trustees**, they are all adults of full capacity and together absolutely entitled. Ignatius is nominated, so s.19 is not available (A and D are wrong). However, as adults absolutely entitled, the grandchildren could end the trust altogether under the rule in Saunders v Vautier, so E overstates the position, and the court is not bound to follow the beneficiaries' wishes (C)."},{"t":"strangers","p":"FLK2","q":"Rhodri, the sole trustee of a family trust, took £25,000 from the trust account in breach of trust and gave it to his adult daughter, Hannah, as a present for her 30th birthday. Hannah had no reason to suspect that the money came from the trust, as Rhodri was a successful businessman. She spent the whole sum on a round-the-world trip. Rhodri is now bankrupt and the beneficiaries are considering a claim against Hannah for knowing receipt.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Hannah's liability for knowing receipt?","o":["She is liable to repay £25,000, because she received trust property for her own benefit and gave nothing in return.","She is liable to repay £25,000, because liability for receiving trust property is strict and does not depend on fault.","She is liable only if she was dishonest by the standards of ordinary decent people when she spent the money.","She is not liable, because her knowledge did not make it unconscionable for her to keep the money.","She is not liable, because she received the money from her father rather than directly from the trust account."],"a":3,"e":"**Liability for knowing receipt requires the recipient's knowledge to make it unconscionable for them to keep the benefit of the receipt** (BCCI v Akindele). Hannah received the money beneficially, but she had no reason to suspect a breach of trust, so she is not personally liable (A and B are wrong). Dishonesty (C) is the test for dishonest assistance, not knowing receipt, and it does not matter that the money came through Rhodri (E); since she has spent it on a holiday, there is also nothing left to trace."},{"t":"strangers","p":"FLK2","q":"Bastian, a trustee, wanted to use £400,000 of trust money to pay his personal gambling debts. He asked Nadine, a solicitor, to receive the money into her firm's client account and pay it on to his creditors. Nadine knew the money belonged to the trust and that Bastian had no right to use it in this way, but she wanted to keep him as a client. She made the payments and charged only her usual fee of £500. Bastian is now bankrupt.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Nadine's liability to the beneficiaries?","o":["She is liable only for £500, because that is the only benefit she received from the breach of trust.","She is liable for knowing receipt of £400,000, because the money passed through her firm's client account.","She is liable for dishonest assistance for the full £400,000 loss, jointly and severally with Bastian.","She is not liable, because she did not receive the money for her own benefit and acted on her client's instructions.","She is not liable, because only a trustee can be liable to beneficiaries for a breach of trust."],"a":2,"e":"**A person who dishonestly assists a breach of trust is personally liable, jointly and severally with the trustee, for the whole loss, whether or not they received anything**. Nadine knew the payments were a misapplication of trust money, which honest people would regard as dishonest, so she is liable for the £400,000 (A is wrong). She received the money only as an agent (a ministerial receipt), not for her own benefit, so knowing receipt does not apply (B), but that does not protect her from liability for assistance (D and E)."},{"t":"remedies-tracing","p":"FLK2","q":"Ravi, a trustee, paid £10,000 of trust money into his personal bank account, which already held £4,000 of his own money. He then withdrew £11,000 and spent it on a cruise, leaving a balance of £3,000. A month later he paid his salary of £8,000 into the account, bringing the balance to £11,000. He made no other payments in or out. Ravi is now bankrupt.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the maximum amount the beneficiaries can claim from the account as their property?","o":["£3,000, because that was the lowest balance in the account after the trust money was paid in.","£10,000, because the trust money was paid in and there is now more than that in the account.","£6,000, because the trust money is treated as spent first and Ravi's money remains in the account.","£11,000, because a trustee who mixes trust money with his own must lose the whole of the mixed fund.","£7,000, because the £11,000 withdrawal is shared between the two funds in proportion to their sizes."],"a":0,"e":"A trustee is presumed to spend **his own money first** (Re Hallett), so the £11,000 withdrawal used Ravi's £4,000 and £7,000 of trust money, leaving £3,000 of trust money. Under **the lowest intermediate balance rule, the beneficiaries cannot claim more from the account than the lowest balance after the trust money went in**, and Ravi's later salary payment is not treated as repaying the trust unless he intended it to (Roscoe v Winder), so B and D are wrong. C reverses the Hallett presumption, and E applies a proportionate approach that is not used against a wrongdoing trustee."}],"trusts-p3":[{"t":"certainties","p":"FLK2","q":"Rupert owns 2,400 ordinary shares in Calloway Brewing plc, all of the same class. In June 2026, at a meeting with his long-serving office manager, Delphine, he said, in front of two witnesses: \"From today I hold 300 of my Calloway shares on trust for you.\" He did not say which 300 shares he meant and nothing was put in writing. Rupert died in September 2026, and his executors refuse to recognise Delphine's claim.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Delphine has an enforceable interest in the shares?","o":["She has none, because a trust of part of a holding of shares fails unless the shares are first separated.","She has none, because a declaration of trust of shares in a company must be made in signed writing.","She has one, because the shares are identical and it does not matter which 300 of them are held for her.","She has one, but only if Rupert's executors choose to identify 300 particular shares as hers.","She has none, because she gave no consideration and equity will not assist a volunteer."],"a":2,"e":"**Certainty of subject matter is satisfied for a trust of part of a holding of identical intangible property, such as shares of one class, without segregation** (Hunter v Moss), because it makes no difference which shares are held on trust. The rule that goods taken from a bulk must be separated applies only to tangible property, so A is wrong. Only declarations of trust of land need signed writing, so B is wrong. E is wrong because Rupert declared himself trustee, which needs no transfer, so no question of perfecting a gift to a volunteer arises."},{"t":"certainties","p":"FLK2","q":"Edwina's will, which took effect on her death in 2026, leaves £500,000 to trustees \"to divide equally among all my first cousins\". Edwina came from a very large family that emigrated widely, and the trustees, after extensive genealogical research, have identified 43 first cousins. The researchers report that there were almost certainly other first cousins, but that the records needed to find them no longer exist, so it will never be possible to say how many there are.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the validity of the gift?","o":["It fails, because the trustees cannot draw up a complete list of all the first cousins.","It is valid, because it can be said of any given person whether they are a first cousin.","It is valid, and the trustees should divide the fund equally among the 43 cousins found.","It fails, because the description \"first cousins\" is conceptually uncertain.","It is valid, and the trustees may pay into court the shares of any cousins not found."],"a":0,"e":"A gift \"equally\" among a class is a **fixed trust, which is valid only if a complete list of every beneficiary can be drawn up** (IRC v Broadway Cottages), because the trustees must know how many shares to divide the fund into. B states the is-or-is-not test, which applies to discretionary trusts, not fixed trusts. D is wrong because \"first cousins\" is a clear concept: the problem is evidential, as the class cannot all be identified. C and E would ignore the settlor's direction that all the cousins share equally."},{"t":"formalities","p":"FLK2","q":"Trustees hold 20,000 shares in Westbury plc on a bare trust (a trust where the trustees simply hold for the beneficiary absolutely) for Anouk, who is 34. In February 2026 Anouk telephoned the trustees and told them that from then on they were to hold the shares on trust for her nephew, Teodor, and the trustees agreed to do so. No document was signed by Anouk. Anouk has now fallen out with Teodor and says the shares are still hers.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who is beneficially entitled to the shares?","o":["Teodor, because Anouk made a valid oral declaration of trust of personal property in his favour.","Teodor, because the trustees agreed to Anouk's instruction and so are bound by it.","Teodor, because writing is needed only for dispositions of interests in land.","Anouk, because a trust of shares can be created only by a transfer of the legal title.","Anouk, because a disposition of an existing equitable interest must be in signed writing."],"a":4,"e":"**A disposition of a subsisting equitable interest must be in writing signed by the person disposing of it (LPA 1925 s.53(1)(c))**, and an oral direction to trustees to hold for someone else is such a disposition (Grey v IRC), so it is void. A is wrong because Anouk was not declaring a new trust of property she owned outright; she was passing on her existing equitable interest. C confuses s.53(1)(c), which covers all property, with s.53(1)(b), which covers declarations of trust of land. B is wrong because the trustees' agreement cannot cure the lack of writing."},{"t":"constitution","p":"FLK2","q":"Harjit owned £400,000 in a deposit account in his sole name. In April 2026, while seriously ill, he signed a deed setting up the Harjit Family Trust, for the education of his grandchildren, naming himself, his daughter Simran and his friend Clive as its three trustees. At the signing he said: \"I now give all my money in the deposit account to the trust.\" He died a week later, before any money was moved or the account was put into the trustees' joint names. His will leaves everything to a charity.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who is entitled to the money in the account?","o":["The charity, because the legal title to the money never reached all three trustees.","The trustees, because Harjit was one of them and it would be unconscionable to deny the gift.","The charity, because Harjit did not declare himself sole trustee of the money for the trust.","The trustees, because Harjit had done everything in his power, leaving only registration by a third party.","The charity, because the gift to the trust was conditional on Harjit's death and so took effect only by will."],"a":1,"e":"**Where a donor declares an immediate gift to a trust of which they are one of the trustees, the gift is effective even though the property is vested in the donor alone; equity will not let the donor, or their estate, deny it** (Choithram v Pagarani). Harjit's personal representatives must transfer the money to all the trustees. D describes the Re Rose principle, which does not fit because Harjit had not done everything needed to transfer the account. A and C reflect the general rule that equity will not perfect an imperfect gift, which this exception displaces."},{"t":"constitution","p":"FLK2","q":"Benedict kept a valuable first edition of a novel in a glass case at his home. At his granddaughter Mabli's 18th birthday party in 2025 he took the book out, put it into her hands and said: \"This is yours, but I'll keep it in the case here for now because it's safer.\" He had let go of it, and Mabli held it while he spoke. With her agreement, he then took it back and replaced it in the case. Benedict died in 2026, leaving his whole estate to his son. Mabli was not appointed executor.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who is entitled to the book?","o":["Mabli, because Benedict's words show that he intended to hold the book on trust for her.","The son, because a gift of a chattel worth a large sum can be made only by deed.","Mabli, because a gift made in contemplation of death takes effect when the donor dies.","Mabli, because Benedict delivered the book to her with an intention to give it to her.","The son, because equity will not treat a failed gift as a declaration of trust."],"a":3,"e":"**A gift of a chattel is made by delivery to the donee with an intention to give, or by deed**: letting go of the book into Mabli's hands while saying \"This is yours\" was a completed gift, and Benedict then kept it with her consent as a mere custodian. E states a correct rule (Richards v Delbridge), but it matters only where the gift is incomplete, which this one is not. A is wrong because Benedict intended an outright gift, not to make himself a trustee. C is wrong because there was no contemplation of impending death, and B is wrong because delivery is an alternative to a deed."},{"t":"beneficial-entitlement","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2018 Clementine transferred £360,000 to trustees to hold on a discretionary trust, under which the trustees may pay the income and capital to such one or more of her three children, Arlo, Bea and Cyrus, as they think fit, for 80 years. The class cannot be added to. All three children are now adults of full capacity. They have agreed among themselves to split the fund equally and have asked the trustees to transfer it to them now. The trustees would prefer to keep the trust running.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The children can together end the trust and require the trustees to transfer the fund as they direct.","The trustees may refuse, because each child has only a hope of receiving something under the trust.","The children can end the trust, but only with the consent of Clementine as the settlor.","The trustees may refuse, because the trust has a fixed period of 80 years that has not ended.","The children can end the trust, but only if the court approves a variation on their behalf."],"a":0,"e":"**All the beneficiaries of a discretionary trust, if together they are adult, of full capacity and absolutely entitled to the whole fund, can end the trust and call for the property** (Saunders v Vautier, applied to discretionary trusts in Re Smith). B is wrong because, although no one child can demand anything alone, together the closed class owns the whole beneficial interest. D is wrong because the beneficiaries' collective right overrides the settlor's chosen duration. C and E are wrong because neither the settlor's consent nor the court's approval is needed where all beneficiaries are adult and agree."},{"t":"purpose-charitable","p":"FLK2","q":"Marcus died in 2026. His will leaves £150,000 to trustees \"to campaign for, and to lobby Parliament to pass, a law making it compulsory for all cyclists in England to wear helmets\". The will contains no other provision for this money, and the residue of his estate goes to his partner, Ines. Marcus had long believed such a law would save many lives. The trustees ask whether they can carry out the gift.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the validity of the gift?","o":["It is charitable, because it aims to advance health and to save lives.","It is charitable, because campaigning by charities is permitted if it furthers their purposes.","It fails, because its purpose of changing the law is political and it is not a valid private trust.","It is valid as a private purpose trust, because the trustees are willing to carry it out.","It fails as a charity, but the court will apply the money cy-près to a road safety charity."],"a":2,"e":"**A trust whose main purpose is to change the law is political and not charitable** (National Anti-Vivisection Society v IRC), because the court cannot judge whether the change would be for the public benefit. It cannot take effect as a private purpose trust either, because it has no beneficiaries to enforce it and falls within none of the anomalous exceptions, so D is wrong and the money falls into residue for Ines. B is wrong because campaigning is acceptable only where it is ancillary to a charitable purpose, not the purpose itself. E is wrong because cy-près applies only to a gift that was charitable in the first place."},{"t":"resulting-trusts","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2021 Gideon used £90,000 of his own money to buy 5,000 shares in Felsham plc, which he had registered in the sole name of his adult daughter, Portia. Nothing was said or written about why he did this, and no other evidence of his intention exists. Gideon and Portia have since fallen out, and Gideon now claims that Portia holds the shares on trust for him. Portia says the shares were a gift.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who is beneficially entitled to the shares?","o":["Gideon, because the presumption of advancement has been abolished by the Equality Act 2010.","Gideon, because a person who provides the purchase money is always presumed to keep the beneficial interest.","Gideon, because Portia was an adult when the shares were bought in her name.","Portia, because a transfer of shares is presumed to be a gift whoever paid for them.","Portia, because a father's purchase in his child's name is presumed to be a gift to the child."],"a":4,"e":"**The presumption of advancement still applies where a father provides money for property put in his child's name**, so the transfer is presumed to be a gift unless the father proves otherwise, and Gideon has no evidence to rebut it. A is a common trap: **Equality Act 2010 s.199 would abolish the presumption but has never been brought into force**. B is wrong because the presumption of a resulting trust is displaced by the presumption of advancement in this relationship, and C is wrong because the child's age does not prevent the presumption arising. D overstates the position: between strangers, a purchase in another's name raises a presumed resulting trust."},{"t":"resulting-trusts","p":"FLK2","q":"Ottilie runs a small catering firm. A former colleague, Wilhelmina, who owns Dunmore Joinery Ltd, rang her in a panic because the company could not meet its staff costs. Ottilie agreed to advance £45,000, and a letter signed by both of them stated that the money must sit in a fresh bank account and be applied solely towards the September 2026 payroll, and for nothing else. Dunmore Joinery Ltd opened that account and Ottilie paid the money in. Before any payroll was run, the company entered insolvent liquidation, and the liquidator now proposes to share the £45,000 among everyone owed money.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ottilie's position?","o":["She is an unsecured creditor and will receive only the same dividend as the other creditors.","She is entitled to the £45,000 back, because the company held it on trust for the agreed purpose.","She is entitled to the £45,000 back, but only if the loan was secured by a registered charge.","The employees are entitled to the £45,000, because the money was held on trust for them.","She is entitled to the £45,000 back, but only after the company's preferential creditors are paid."],"a":1,"e":"**Where money is lent for a specific purpose on terms that it is not at the borrower's free disposal, the borrower holds it on a Quistclose trust; if the purpose fails, it is held on resulting trust for the lender**, outside the borrower's insolvent estate (Barclays v Quistclose). So Ottilie recovers it in full, not merely as a creditor, which rules out A and E. C is wrong because the trust gives her a proprietary claim without any registered charge. D is wrong because the purpose trust benefits the lender; the employees had no beneficial interest in the money itself."},{"t":"family-home","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2017 Siobhan and Ravi, an unmarried couple, bought a flat for £280,000 in their joint names. The transfer to them, which both signed, declared that they held the flat \"on trust for ourselves as tenants in common in equal shares\". Siobhan paid £100,000 towards the price from an inheritance and Ravi paid nothing towards the deposit; they paid the mortgage equally. They have now separated, and Siobhan claims she should have a larger share because of her contribution.\n\nWhich of the following best describes their beneficial shares in the flat?","o":["Siobhan will have a larger share, because the court looks at the whole course of dealing between them.","Siobhan will have a larger share, under a resulting trust reflecting what each contributed to the price.","They own the flat in equal shares, because the express declaration in the transfer is conclusive.","They own the flat in equal shares, but only because they contributed equally to the mortgage.","Siobhan will have a larger share, because her inheritance was her separate property."],"a":2,"e":"**An express declaration of the beneficial interests in the transfer, signed by the parties, is conclusive** in the absence of fraud, mistake or a later agreement (Goodman v Gallant), so the court will not investigate who paid what. A describes the approach used when there is no express declaration (Stack v Dowden), which does not apply here. B is wrong because a resulting trust can arise only where the beneficial interests have not been expressly declared. D gives the right answer for the wrong reason, since the mortgage payments are irrelevant once there is an effective declaration."},{"t":"family-home","p":"FLK2","q":"From 2004 to 2026 Ewan worked on his uncle Lachlan's farm for well below the market wage. Throughout that time Lachlan told him repeatedly, in front of others: \"Don't worry about the money, the farmhouse and land will be yours when I'm gone.\" Relying on this, Ewan turned down a well-paid job elsewhere and never built up savings or a pension. Lachlan died in 2026, leaving a will made in 2020 that gives the farm to a neighbour. There is no written agreement.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ewan's claim?","o":["He can claim through proprietary estoppel, and the court will decide what remedy fairly satisfies the equity.","He has no claim, because a promise about a future gift by will is not binding until death and can be revoked.","He has no claim, because a contract for an interest in land must be in writing signed by both parties.","He can claim the farm under a common intention constructive trust, in a share fixed by his contributions.","He can claim only the wages he lost, as damages for breach of an oral agreement with Lachlan."],"a":0,"e":"**Proprietary estoppel arises where an owner gives an assurance about property, the claimant relies on it to their detriment, and it would be unconscionable to go back on it**; assurances about inheriting property can found an estoppel where they were meant to be relied on (Thorner v Major). The court then decides **what remedy is needed to satisfy the equity and remove the unconscionability**, usually starting from the promise made (Guest v Guest), which may be the farm itself or a lesser award. B is wrong because a repeated assurance that has been relied on can bind even though wills are revocable. C is wrong because estoppel does not depend on a contract, and D is wrong because nothing suggests a common intention that Ewan already owned a share; Lachlan promised a future gift. E is wrong because the claim is equitable and the remedy is not limited to lost wages."},{"t":"fiduciary-duties","p":"FLK2","q":"Nkechi is one of two trustees of her late father's will trust, under which her brother, Obi, has a one-third share of a fund invested in property. Obi, who is 40 and in debt, offered to sell his beneficial interest to Nkechi. Before agreeing, Nkechi told Obi everything she knew about the trust properties, including an offer the trustees had just received for one of them. She paid him a price fixed by an independent valuer. Obi now regrets the sale and wants to set it aside.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Obi can set aside the sale?","o":["He can, because any purchase by a trustee from the trust is voidable however fair the price.","He can, because a trustee may never buy a beneficiary's interest without the court's consent.","He cannot, because Obi is an adult and the sale was made under a binding contract.","He cannot, because Nkechi made full disclosure, took no advantage and paid a fair price.","He cannot, because the sale concerned his own interest and not trust property."],"a":3,"e":"Where a trustee buys a beneficiary's interest from the beneficiary, the **fair-dealing rule** applies: **the sale stands if the trustee shows that they took no advantage of their position, made full disclosure and paid a fair price** (Tito v Waddell (No 2)). Nkechi did all three. A describes the stricter self-dealing rule, which applies when a trustee buys trust property itself. B is wrong because court consent is not needed where the fair-dealing conditions are met, and C is wrong because a contract does not by itself protect a fiduciary who failed to deal fairly. E is the most tempting wrong answer: the fact that it was Obi's own interest means fair-dealing applies, but the sale stands only because Nkechi proved its fairness, not automatically."},{"t":"fiduciary-duties","p":"FLK2","q":"Florian and Wren are the trustees of a family trust that owns several let houses. Florian arranged the buildings insurance for all the houses through Harcourt Brokers, which charged the trust a normal market premium. Without telling Wren or the beneficiaries, Harcourt Brokers paid Florian personally a commission of £6,000 for bringing it the business. The trust deed does not allow trustees to keep commissions. Florian says the trust lost nothing because the premium was fair.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Florian's position?","o":["He may keep the £6,000, because the trust paid a fair premium and suffered no loss.","He must repay the £6,000, but only if the beneficiaries prove he acted dishonestly.","He may keep the £6,000, because commissions are part of a trustee's reasonable remuneration.","He holds the £6,000 on constructive trust for the trust and must account for it.","He must pay the trust damages equal to any extra premium the trust paid because of the commission."],"a":3,"e":"**A fiduciary must not make an unauthorised profit from their position, and a secret commission is held on constructive trust for the principal from the moment it is received** (FHR European Ventures v Cedar Capital). A is wrong because liability does not depend on the trust having suffered loss. B is wrong because the rule is strict and applies even to an honest trustee. C is wrong because a trustee is entitled to remuneration only where authorised by the trust deed, statute or the court, and E is wrong because the remedy is to give up the profit, not to compensate for loss."},{"t":"trustees-appointment","p":"FLK2","q":"Under a 2012 settlement, Hollis and Juno were appointed trustees of a fund for the settlor's grandchildren. The settlement gives no one a power to appoint new trustees, and the settlor has died. Juno died in 2024, and Hollis continued alone until he died in March 2026. Hollis's will appoints his son, Aksel, as his executor, and Aksel has obtained probate. The grandchildren are aged 6, 9 and 15. The trust property needs to be managed by new trustees.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who may appoint new trustees out of court?","o":["The grandchildren's parents, acting on behalf of the minor beneficiaries.","Aksel, as the personal representative of the last surviving trustee, by a written appointment.","No one, because once all the trustees have died only the court can appoint new trustees.","Aksel, but only because Hollis's will expressly gave him the power to appoint new trustees.","The beneficiaries, by giving a written direction to Aksel under the statutory scheme."],"a":1,"e":"**Under TA 1925 s.36(1), where there is no person nominated to appoint and no surviving or continuing trustee, the personal representatives of the last surviving trustee may appoint new trustees in writing.** So Aksel may act, and C is wrong because the court is needed only if there is no one able and willing. D is wrong because the power comes from statute, not from Hollis's will. E is wrong because a direction under TLATA 1996 s.19 requires all the beneficiaries to be adult and of full capacity, which these grandchildren are not; A is wrong because parents have no such power."},{"t":"trustee-duties-powers","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2011 Wendell transferred £200,000 to trustees on trust for his only grandson, Silas, contingently on Silas reaching 30. There is no prior interest and no other beneficiary, and the trust deed does not alter the trustees' statutory powers. Silas is now 23 and wants £150,000 of the capital to buy a share in a veterinary practice where he has qualified to work. The fund is still worth £200,000 and nothing has previously been advanced.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the maximum the trustees can advance to Silas under their statutory power?","o":["£200,000, because the statutory power extends to the whole of the beneficiary's presumptive share.","£150,000, because the trustees may advance whatever sum the beneficiary reasonably needs.","£50,000, because the statutory power is limited to one quarter of the capital of the fund.","Nothing, because the statutory power cannot be used while the beneficiary's interest is contingent.","£100,000, because for this trust the statutory power is limited to one half of his presumptive share."],"a":4,"e":"Under the statutory power of advancement (**TA 1925 s.32**), **for trusts created before 1 October 2014 the trustees may advance up to one half of the beneficiary's presumptive share**; the power to advance the whole share applies only to trusts created on or after that date. Silas's presumptive share is the whole £200,000, so the limit is £100,000. A states the post-2014 rule, which does not apply to a 2011 trust. B is wrong because the statutory power is capped by a fraction of the presumptive share, not by what the beneficiary needs, and C invents a one-quarter limit. D is wrong because s.32 expressly extends to contingent interests, and buying into a practice is a classic advancement."},{"t":"trustee-duties-powers","p":"FLK2","q":"Leontyne and Paddy, two retired teachers, are unpaid trustees of a £600,000 family trust for their nieces. The trust deed gives them a wide power to invest as if they were absolutely entitled and says nothing else about investment. In 2025, without taking any advice, they invested the whole fund in shares of Varden Technologies plc, a single listed company that Paddy had read about and believed would grow quickly. The shares have since lost half their value.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the trustees are in breach of trust?","o":["They are not, because the deed allowed them to invest as if they were absolutely entitled.","They are not, because as unpaid lay trustees they are judged only by their own personal standards.","They are, because they failed to consider the need to diversify and failed to take proper advice.","They are, because trustees may not invest in the shares of a single listed company.","They are not, because they honestly believed that the investment was in the trust's best interests."],"a":2,"e":"Even under a wide express power, **trustees must have regard to the standard investment criteria, including the need for diversification, and must obtain and consider proper advice unless they reasonably conclude it is unnecessary (TA 2000 ss.4 and 5)**; putting a £600,000 fund into one company without advice breaches both. A is wrong because these duties apply to any investment power, whether statutory or express. B is wrong because the statutory duty of care is objective, set at least at the level of a reasonable person in the trustees' position. D overstates the rule, as a single-company investment is not banned outright, and E is wrong because honesty is no defence to a breach of these duties."},{"t":"breach-of-trust","p":"FLK2","q":"Ashwin, an unpaid lay trustee, held £80,000 on trust for his godson until the godson reaches 25. In 2024 a man Ashwin met at his golf club persuaded him to invest the whole fund in an unregulated overseas property scheme promising 20% a year. Ashwin did not check the scheme or take any advice, but he honestly believed it was a good investment. The scheme collapsed and the money was lost. The godson's parents, on his behalf, are suing Ashwin, who asks the court to excuse him.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the court is likely to relieve Ashwin of liability?","o":["It is not, because although he acted honestly, he did not act reasonably.","It is, because he acted honestly and received no payment for acting as trustee.","It is, because a lay trustee is liable only for losses caused by dishonesty.","It is not, because the court has no power to excuse an unauthorised investment.","It is, because the trust deed contained no clause making him liable for negligence."],"a":0,"e":"**Under TA 1925 s.61 the court may relieve a trustee who acted honestly and reasonably and ought fairly to be excused**; all the conditions must be met. Investing the whole fund in an unregulated scheme on a stranger's word, without checks or advice, was not reasonable, so honesty alone is not enough. B is the tempting wrong answer: being unpaid makes relief more likely, but it cannot replace the requirement of reasonableness. D is wrong because s.61 extends to any breach of trust, including an unauthorised investment. C and E are wrong because trustees are liable for negligent breaches unless an exemption clause protects them, and there is no such clause here."},{"t":"breach-of-trust","p":"FLK2","q":"Gwendolyn and Laszlo are the trustees of a trust for Gwendolyn's nephews. In 2025 Laszlo, who managed the trust's accounts, transferred £120,000 from the trust account to his own account and spent it on his business, which then failed. Gwendolyn carelessly signed off the trust accounts each year without checking them, and so did not notice the transfer for eleven months. The beneficiaries have sued Gwendolyn alone for the full £120,000, as Laszlo has few assets.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Gwendolyn's position?","o":["She is liable only for half of the loss, because liability is divided equally between trustees.","She is not liable, because only the trustee who took the money is liable for its loss.","She is liable for the whole loss and has no claim against Laszlo, as both were in breach.","She is liable for the whole loss, but can claim a full indemnity from Laszlo.","She is liable only for losses caused after she should have noticed the transfer."],"a":3,"e":"**Trustees in breach are jointly and severally liable, so the beneficiaries may recover the whole loss from any one of them**; Gwendolyn's careless failure to supervise made her liable too. But under the **Civil Liability (Contribution) Act 1978** the court orders a just and equitable contribution, which can be a **full indemnity where the other trustee took the money for himself**. A is wrong because liability is not divided as against the beneficiaries. C is wrong because a trustee in breach can still claim contribution, and B is wrong because a passive trustee who fails to supervise is not protected. E is wrong because she is liable for the whole loss caused by the breach, not just for losses after she should have noticed."},{"t":"strangers","p":"FLK2","q":"Tobiah, the sole trustee of a family trust, transferred £50,000 of trust money to his girlfriend, Esme, so that she could buy a sports car. Esme had seen the trust papers and knew that the money belonged to the trust and that Tobiah had no right to give it to her. She bought the car, crashed it a month later and it was written off without insurance. She now has no traceable assets derived from the money. Tobiah is bankrupt.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Esme's liability to the beneficiaries?","o":["She is not liable, because the trust property has been destroyed and cannot be traced.","She is personally liable for the £50,000, because she received trust property knowing of the breach.","She is liable only if she helped Tobiah plan the breach before the money was transferred.","She is not liable, because she gave nothing in return and so is treated as an innocent volunteer.","She is liable for the £50,000, but only if the beneficiaries prove she acted dishonestly."],"a":1,"e":"**A person who beneficially receives trust property transferred in breach of trust, with knowledge that makes it unconscionable to keep it, is personally liable for knowing receipt** (BCCI v Akindele), to the value received, even if the property has since been lost. Esme's actual knowledge plainly meets the test. A confuses this personal claim with a proprietary tracing claim, which does fail here. C describes dishonest assistance, which is a separate route, and E is wrong because knowing receipt requires unconscionable knowledge, not proof of dishonesty. D is wrong because a volunteer who knew of the breach is not innocent."},{"t":"strangers","p":"FLK2","q":"Celestine, an elderly trustee, had a trust fund of £300,000. Her financial adviser, Morgan, told her that it was a safe and permitted investment to lend the whole fund to Altair Ventures Ltd, and she honestly believed him. In fact Morgan secretly controlled Altair Ventures Ltd, knew that the loan was outside Celestine's powers as trustee and knew the company could not repay it. The company has collapsed and the money is lost. Morgan received none of the money himself.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Morgan's liability to the beneficiaries?","o":["He is not liable, because he did not receive any of the trust money for his own benefit.","He is not liable for dishonest assistance, because Celestine herself acted honestly.","He is liable only for any fee he earned for giving the advice to Celestine.","He is liable only if Celestine is unable to make good the loss herself.","He is liable for dishonest assistance, as only he, not the trustee, needs to be dishonest."],"a":4,"e":"**Dishonest assistance requires that the defendant dishonestly helped a breach of trust; the trustee need not have been dishonest** (Royal Brunei Airlines v Tan). Morgan knew the facts and his conduct was dishonest by the standards of ordinary decent people, so he is liable, jointly and severally with Celestine, for the £300,000 loss. B is the tempting wrong answer, reflecting the old view rejected in that case. A is wrong because, unlike knowing receipt, dishonest assistance needs no receipt of property, D is wrong because his liability is joint and several, not secondary, and C is wrong because he must make good the whole loss, not merely give up his fee."},{"t":"remedies-tracing","p":"FLK2","q":"Barnaby, a trustee, paid £40,000 of trust money into his personal bank account, which already held £40,000 of his own money. The next day he withdrew £40,000 and bought shares in Pellston plc. A week later he withdrew the remaining £40,000 and spent it on a holiday and gambling. The Pellston shares are now worth £70,000. Barnaby has no other assets and his creditors are pressing for payment.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the beneficiaries' best claim?","o":["They can claim nothing, because the trust money was spent on the holiday and gambling.","They can claim only £20,000 of the shares' value, as half of each withdrawal was trust money.","They can claim the shares, because they can treat the first withdrawal as made with trust money.","They can claim the shares, but their claim is limited to £40,000 of the shares' value.","They can claim the balance of Barnaby's bank account and nothing else."],"a":2,"e":"Where a trustee mixes trust money with their own, the beneficiaries may **elect for the treatment most favourable to them: they may treat the first withdrawal as trust money and claim the asset bought with it** (Re Oatway), rather than relying on the presumption that the trustee spends his own money first (Re Hallett). Since the rest was dissipated, they claim the Pellston shares, including the increase in value, so D is wrong. A wrongly applies the Re Hallett presumption against the beneficiaries. B applies proportionate sharing, which is not forced on beneficiaries against a wrongdoing trustee, and E is wrong because the account balance is now nil."},{"t":"remedies-tracing","p":"FLK2","q":"Kazimir, a trustee, bought a flat for £200,000 using £50,000 of trust money and £150,000 of his own savings. He knew he had no right to use the trust money. The flat is now worth £320,000, and Kazimir is insolvent. The beneficiaries want to recover as much as possible from the flat ahead of Kazimir's unsecured creditors.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the beneficiaries can claim?","o":["A one-quarter share of the flat, worth £80,000, or alternatively a lien over it for £50,000.","Only a lien over the flat for £50,000, because the trustee contributed most of the price.","Only a personal claim for £50,000, ranking alongside Kazimir's other unsecured creditors.","The whole flat, because the trustee's own money is presumed to have been spent elsewhere.","A one-quarter share of the flat, but only if its value has risen since the purchase."],"a":0,"e":"**Where trust money and a trustee's own money are used to buy an asset, the beneficiaries can choose either a proportionate share of the asset or a lien (charge) over it to secure the amount of trust money used** (Foskett v McKeown). Trust money paid a quarter of the price, so a quarter share gives them £80,000, more than a £50,000 lien; as a proprietary claim, it ranks ahead of the unsecured creditors. B and C wrongly limit them to less valuable remedies. D is wrong because Kazimir's own money demonstrably went into the flat too, and E is wrong because the choice is always available, though a lien is better only if the asset falls in value."}],"wills-p1":[{"t":"validity","p":"FLK2","q":"In January, Margaret, aged 84, met her solicitor alone and gave clear, detailed instructions for a new will, describing her house, her savings and her three grandchildren, whom she wished to benefit. Two weeks later, before the will was signed, she suffered a stroke. At the signing in hospital she could no longer follow the individual clauses when they were read to her, but she said she knew this was the will she had asked her solicitor to prepare and wished to sign it. The will follows her instructions exactly. Her son, who receives less than under her previous will, says the will is invalid.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the validity of Margaret's will?","o":["It is invalid, because the testator must understand each clause of the will at the moment it is signed.","It is likely to be valid, as she had capacity when instructing and knew the will followed those instructions.","It is likely to be valid, because the Mental Capacity Act 2005 presumes capacity and her son cannot rebut it.","It is invalid, because capacity is tested only at execution and instructions given earlier are irrelevant.","It is valid only if a doctor attended the signing and recorded that she had testamentary capacity."],"a":1,"e":"Capacity is normally judged at execution, but where the testator had capacity when giving instructions, the will is still valid if at execution they understood they were **signing a will prepared in accordance with those instructions** and it does follow them (Parker v Felgate). So Margaret need not have followed each clause at the signing. The challenged-will test is the common law one, not the **Mental Capacity Act 2005**, and the **golden rule** (a doctor witnessing) is good practice rather than a legal requirement."},{"t":"validity","p":"FLK2","q":"Ronald, a retired engineer aged 88, signed a will leaving his entire estate to his accountant, Leon, who drafted the will on his own computer, arranged the signing and chose the two witnesses. Ronald's previous will, made five years earlier, divided everything between his two nieces. There is no evidence that Leon threatened or pressured Ronald, and the will appears properly signed and witnessed with a full attestation clause. The nieces wish to dispute the will.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who must prove what if the will is disputed?","o":["The nieces must prove that Ronald did not know and approve the will, because it was duly executed.","Leon must disprove undue influence, because a presumption of undue influence arises from his role.","The nieces must prove undue influence, and if they cannot, the will is admitted to probate without more.","Leon must positively prove that Ronald knew and approved the will, and the nieces must prove any undue influence.","Leon must prove the will was duly executed, because the presumption of due execution does not apply."],"a":3,"e":"Knowledge and approval is normally **presumed** from capacity and due execution, but a beneficiary who **prepared the will and takes a substantial benefit** creates **suspicious circumstances**, so the propounder (Leon) must positively prove that Ronald knew and approved its contents. **Undue influence is never presumed for wills**: the nieces must prove it, and it must be the only reasonable explanation of the facts. The attestation clause means due execution is still presumed, so that is not where the burden lies."},{"t":"formalities","p":"FLK2","q":"Gwen signed her will at her dining table while her friends Hamza and Isla were both sitting beside her watching. Hamza then signed as a witness. Before Isla could sign, Gwen went out to the garden to speak to a delivery driver and was away for ten minutes, out of sight of the dining room. Isla signed the will while Gwen was outside and left before Gwen returned. Nothing further was done. Neither witness benefits under the will.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the validity of Gwen's will?","o":["It is invalid, because Isla did not sign or acknowledge her signature in Gwen's presence.","It is valid, because Gwen signed while both witnesses were present at the same time.","It is valid, because the witnesses do not need to sign in each other's presence.","It is invalid, because both witnesses must sign while each other and the testator are present.","It is valid, provided Isla swears an affidavit of due execution describing how the will was signed."],"a":0,"e":"Under **Wills Act 1837 s.9(d)**, **each witness must sign, or acknowledge their signature, in the presence of the testator**. Gwen's signature satisfied s.9(c) because both witnesses were present together, but Isla signed while Gwen was out of sight and never acknowledged her signature to Gwen afterwards, so the will fails. Witnesses need not sign in front of each other, and an affidavit of due execution only proves what happened; it cannot cure a failure to comply with s.9."},{"t":"executors","p":"FLK2","q":"Teodor's will appoints his brother Marek and his daughter Zofia, aged 16, as executors. Since Teodor's death a month ago, Marek has collected rent from Teodor's tenant, paid Teodor's credit card bill from the rent and closed one of his bank accounts. Marek now tells the firm that he finds the role stressful and wants to give it up entirely. Nobody has yet applied for a grant.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Marek's position?","o":["He may renounce probate, because an executor can renounce at any time before a grant is issued.","He may renounce, and a grant of letters of administration with will annexed will issue to Zofia's guardian.","He must be joined by a second adult applicant, because a minority interest requires two personal representatives.","He and Zofia may apply for probate together, because a minor can be appointed and act jointly with an adult.","He cannot renounce, and he may apply for probate himself with power reserved to Zofia."],"a":4,"e":"An executor **cannot renounce once they have intermeddled**, and collecting rent, paying debts and closing accounts are acts of an executor, so Marek has accepted office. A **minor cannot take a grant**, so Marek proves alone with **power reserved** to Zofia, who can apply for double probate at 18. The rule requiring two personal representatives where a minority interest arises applies to **administrators**, not executors, so a sole executor can take probate."},{"t":"alterations-revocation","p":"FLK2","q":"Fenella's will, properly executed in 2022, left \"£15,000 to my godson Rhys\". In 2024 she stuck a strip of paper over \"£15,000\" and wrote \"£25,000\" on the strip, adding her initials but no witness signatures. The original figure can be read only by removing the strip. A letter Fenella wrote at the time says she wanted to \"increase Rhys's gift\". She has died and the will contains no other alterations.\n\nHow much is Rhys most likely to receive?","o":["£25,000, because Fenella initialled the alteration and her intention is clear from the letter.","Nothing, because the original figure is not apparent and has been revoked by obliteration.","£15,000, because she meant to revoke the original amount only if the new amount took effect.","£25,000, because an alteration on the face of a will is presumed to have been made before execution.","Nothing, because an invalid alteration to a legacy revokes the whole of that gift."],"a":2,"e":"An alteration made after execution is valid only if **executed like a will** (Wills Act 1837 s.21), so the unwitnessed £25,000 is ineffective; unattested alterations are in fact **presumed made after** execution. Words that are not apparent by natural means are normally revoked, but where the testator intended to revoke them **only if the new words took effect**, **conditional revocation** applies and the original words stand, read by any means (Re Itter). Fenella plainly wanted Rhys to have more, not nothing, so he takes £15,000."},{"t":"alterations-revocation","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2022, when she was single and not in a relationship, Ngozi made a will leaving her estate equally to her sister Ada and her friend Bisi. The will includes the clause: \"This will is made in contemplation of marriage and shall not be revoked by any future marriage.\" In 2025 Ngozi met and married Callum. She died in 2026 without making a new will, survived by Callum, her parents and Ada. She had no children.\n\nWho is entitled to Ngozi's estate?","o":["Callum takes the whole estate, because the will was revoked by the marriage and he takes everything on intestacy.","Ada and Bisi take equal shares, because the will states that it shall not be revoked by marriage.","Callum takes the statutory legacy and half the rest, with the balance going to Ngozi's parents.","Callum and Ngozi's parents share the estate equally, because the will was revoked by the marriage.","Ada and Bisi take equal shares, subject to Callum's right to apply under the 1975 Act within six months."],"a":0,"e":"Marriage **automatically revokes** an earlier will unless it appears from the will that the testator was **expecting to marry a particular person** and intended the will not to be revoked by that marriage (Wills Act 1837 s.18(3)). A general clause about any future marriage, made before Ngozi had even met Callum, does not qualify, so she died intestate. A surviving spouse where there are **no issue takes the whole estate**; parents and siblings take nothing, and the statutory legacy applies only where there are issue."},{"t":"interpretation","p":"FLK2","q":"Leila's will leaves \"£10,000 to Tobias, to be paid from my Nationwide savings account\", her car (worth £8,000) to Una, a legacy of £30,000 to Victor and the residue to Wilf. At her death the Nationwide account holds only £4,000. After paying all debts and expenses, the estate, including the car and the Nationwide account, is worth £39,000.\n\nHow much will Tobias receive?","o":["£4,000","£7,750","£4,500","£8,500","£10,000"],"a":3,"e":"A **demonstrative** gift is treated as **specific** so far as the named fund exists and as a **general** gift for any shortfall. So Tobias's £4,000 from the account ranks with Una's car and is paid in full (£12,000 in all), leaving £27,000 for general legacies totalling £36,000 (Victor's £30,000 and Tobias's £6,000 balance), which **abate rateably** to 75%. Tobias receives £4,000 plus £4,500, so £8,500; residue abates first and Wilf takes nothing. Treating the whole gift as general (£7,750) or as adeemed beyond the fund (£4,000) ignores how demonstrative gifts work."},{"t":"interpretation","p":"FLK2","q":"Cordelia, aged 74, and her nephew Desmond, aged 41, were killed when the light aircraft in which they were travelling crashed. It cannot be established which of them died first. Cordelia's will leaves her residuary estate \"to my nephew Desmond\" with no survivorship clause or gift over. Desmond's will leaves his whole estate to his wife, Elspeth. Desmond has two children.\n\nWhat happens to Cordelia's residuary estate?","o":["It passes on partial intestacy, because Desmond cannot be shown to have survived Cordelia.","It passes into Desmond's estate and then to Elspeth under his will.","It passes directly to Desmond's two children, because the gift is saved by statute.","It is divided equally between Cordelia's estate and Desmond's estate.","It passes to Elspeth directly, as Desmond's substitute under Cordelia's will."],"a":1,"e":"Where it is **uncertain who died first**, the **younger is presumed to have survived the elder** (Law of Property Act 1925 s.184), so Desmond is treated as surviving Cordelia and her gift to him does not lapse. It falls into Desmond's estate and passes under his will to Elspeth. The rule saving gifts to predeceased issue (Wills Act 1837 s.33) applies only to the testator's own **children and remoter descendants**, and a nephew is not one, so his children take nothing directly."},{"t":"intestacy","p":"FLK2","q":"Kwame died intestate in August 2026, survived by his wife, Adjoa, and their two adult children. He and Adjoa owned their home, worth £500,000, as beneficial joint tenants. In his sole name Kwame had savings and investments worth £522,000 after paying his debts and funeral and administration expenses. He also owned personal chattels worth £20,000. The statutory legacy is £322,000.\n\nHow much will each child receive from Kwame's estate?","o":["£175,000","£112,500","£100,000","Nothing","£50,000"],"a":4,"e":"The home held as **joint tenants** passes to Adjoa by **survivorship** and is outside the intestacy. Adjoa takes the **personal chattels**, the **statutory legacy of £322,000** and **half of the remaining £200,000**; the other £100,000 passes to the children on the statutory trusts, so £50,000 each, absolutely as they are adults. Including the house, or giving the children the whole balance after the statutory legacy, overstates their shares."},{"t":"intestacy","p":"FLK2","q":"Ignatius died intestate in 2026. He never married or formed a civil partnership and had no children. Both his parents died before him. He is survived by his paternal grandmother, his half-brother Jude (they share a mother) and the two children of his sister Hester, who shared both his parents and died before him: Kit, aged 22, and Lily, aged 15.\n\nWho is entitled to Ignatius's estate?","o":["Jude, Kit and Lily share it equally, each taking one-third absolutely.","His grandmother takes it all, because grandparents rank before nephews and nieces.","Kit and Lily share it equally, with Lily's share held on trust until she is 18.","Jude takes half, and Kit and Lily share the other half that Hester would have taken.","Jude takes it all, because siblings take before the children of a sibling."],"a":2,"e":"With no spouse, issue or parents, the estate passes to **brothers and sisters of the whole blood** on the statutory trusts, and the **issue of a predeceased whole-blood sibling take that sibling's share by representation**. Because Hester's children make up the whole-blood class, **half-blood siblings** like Jude (a later class) and **grandparents** take nothing. Under the **statutory trusts** Lily's share is contingent on reaching 18."},{"t":"outside-estate","p":"FLK2","q":"Rosalind has died leaving a will that gives everything to her partner, Sanjay, whom she never married. Her assets were: a flat she owned with her brother as tenants in common in equal shares; a life policy written in trust for her daughter; a lump sum death benefit from her workplace pension, payable at the scheme trustees' discretion, with an expression of wishes naming Sanjay; and a bank account held jointly with her daughter, into which both paid their earnings.\n\nWhich asset will pass to Sanjay under Rosalind's will?","o":["Her half share of the flat.","The proceeds of the life policy.","The pension lump sum.","The money in the joint bank account.","Her half of the joint account and the flat."],"a":0,"e":"A share held as a **tenant in common** passes **under the will or intestacy**, so Rosalind's half of the flat goes to Sanjay (and a grant is needed to deal with it). A life policy **written in trust** belongs to the trust beneficiary, a **discretionary pension** benefit is paid by the trustees, who are guided but not bound by an expression of wishes, and a genuinely joint account passes to the other holder by **survivorship**. None of those is governed by the will."},{"t":"grants","p":"FLK2","q":"Ottilie's valid will appoints her friend Perpetua as sole executor and leaves a legacy of £5,000 to her neighbour and the residue equally to her two sons, Quentin, aged 30, and Rufus, aged 16. Perpetua died a year before Ottilie, and the will appoints no substitute executor. Quentin wishes to administer the estate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the grant that should be obtained?","o":["Quentin may apply alone for a grant of probate, as the residuary beneficiary with a vested interest.","Quentin may apply alone for letters of administration, because the will no longer has an executor.","Quentin and the neighbour have equal priority, because both are beneficiaries named in the will.","Quentin may apply for letters of administration with will annexed, but a second administrator is needed.","Quentin may apply alone for letters of administration with will annexed, as he is the only adult son."],"a":3,"e":"**Probate issues only to executors**; where no executor can act, the grant is **letters of administration with will annexed**, and under **NCPR r.20** a residuary beneficiary ranks above a pecuniary legatee, with a vested interest preferred to a contingent one. Because Rufus is a minor, a **minority interest** arises, so the grant must go to **at least two administrators** or a trust corporation (Senior Courts Act 1981 s.114(2)). Simple letters of administration apply only where there is no valid will."},{"t":"grants","p":"FLK2","q":"Barnaby, a widower, died in May 2026. His executor, Cressida, has established that the estate is not an excepted estate and that IHT is payable. The estate consists of Barnaby's house in his sole name, £180,000 in a bank account and a portfolio of quoted shares. Cressida asks whether she can obtain probate first and then pay the IHT out of the estate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the correct procedure?","o":["She may apply for probate now and must pay all of the tax within six months of the end of the month of death.","She must send an IHT400 and pay the tax on the cash and shares first, but may pay the house tax by instalments.","She must send an IHT400 and pay all of the tax, including that on the house, before applying for probate.","She need only give the estate values in the probate application, because the IHT205 has been abolished.","She must pay the tax on the house first, because tax on land can never be deferred until after the grant."],"a":1,"e":"For a **non-excepted estate**, the PRs must deliver an **IHT400** and **pay the IHT on non-instalment property** (cash and quoted shares) before applying, then wait 20 working days for HMRC to notify the Probate Registry. **Land is instalment property**, so the tax on the house can be paid by **10 annual instalments** and need not be paid before the grant. Giving values only in the application is for excepted estates, and the IHT205 point is irrelevant once the estate is not excepted."},{"t":"iht-funding-incidence","p":"FLK2","q":"Imelda left a will giving her holiday cottage in Cornwall to her goddaughter Juno \"subject to payment of any inheritance tax attributable to it\", a cash legacy of £40,000 to her friend Marisol, and her residuary estate in equal halves to a registered UK animal charity and her brother Lorcan. After applying her nil rate band, a substantial amount of IHT is payable on her death estate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the burden of the IHT falls?","o":["Juno and Marisol bear tax on their gifts, and the charity and Lorcan share the rest equally.","All the tax comes out of residue, so the charity and Lorcan bear it in equal shares.","Lorcan bears all the tax, because the charity is exempt and the other gifts are specific.","Juno, Marisol and Lorcan bear the tax rateably by the value of what each receives.","Juno bears the tax on the cottage, Marisol takes free of tax, and Lorcan's half bears the rest."],"a":4,"e":"By default IHT on UK free estate is a **testamentary expense paid from residue** (IHTA 1984 s.211), so Marisol's legacy is received tax-free, but a will can make a gift bear its own tax, and Juno's cottage is expressly **subject to tax**. **Exempt gifts never bear tax** (s.41), so residue is divided first and the remaining tax falls entirely on Lorcan's **non-exempt** half, not on the charity's half (Re Ratcliffe)."},{"t":"administration","p":"FLK2","q":"Percival, a sole trader, has died. His executors have £45,000 in the estate after his secured lender has sold his mortgaged property. The claims are: reasonable funeral, testamentary and administration expenses of £10,000; arrears of wages owed to his employees, all within the preferential limits, of £15,000; his own unpaid income tax of £12,000; trade creditors of £28,000; and a loan from his wife of £5,000.\n\nHow much will HMRC receive for Percival's unpaid income tax?","o":["£12,000","£5,333","£6,000","£7,636","Nothing"],"a":2,"e":"In an **insolvent estate**, the order is: funeral, testamentary and administration expenses; **preferential debts** (employees' wages within the limits); **ordinary unsecured debts**; interest; then **deferred debts** such as a spouse's loan. After paying £10,000 and £15,000, £20,000 remains for ordinary debts of £40,000 (his own income tax and the trade creditors), which **abate rateably** at 50%, so HMRC receives £6,000. The deceased's **own income tax is an ordinary debt** with no Crown preference, so it is not paid in full (£12,000). Ranking the wife's loan as an ordinary debt (£5,333) or the wages as ordinary rather than preferential (£7,636) gets the order wrong, and ordinary creditors do receive a dividend, so 'nothing' is wrong."},{"t":"administration","p":"FLK2","q":"The executors of Hortense's estate placed notices under section 27 of the Trustee Act 1925 in The London Gazette and a local newspaper, allowing three months for claims. Before the notices were placed, they had found among her papers an unpaid invoice from a builder, Ibrahim, for £9,000, but he did not respond to the notices. Four months after the notices, the executors distributed the whole estate to the residuary beneficiaries. Ibrahim now demands payment.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the executors' position?","o":["They are personally liable to Ibrahim, because they knew of his claim before distributing.","They are protected, because Ibrahim did not respond within the time allowed by the notices.","They are protected, and Ibrahim's only remedy is to follow the assets into the beneficiaries' hands.","They are protected, because they waited until after the executor's year before distributing.","They are liable only if Ibrahim can show they acted dishonestly in distributing the estate."],"a":0,"e":"Section 27 notices protect PRs only against claims they **did not know about** when they distribute; **known debts must be paid or provided for** whether or not the creditor responds. Distributing without paying Ibrahim is a **devastavit**, making the executors personally liable to him. Following the assets is the remedy of a late creditor the PRs did not know about, and the executor's year only means PRs cannot be forced to distribute earlier; it gives no protection against a known creditor."},{"t":"family-provision","p":"FLK2","q":"Saoirse and her husband Tadhg separated informally four years ago and lived apart, but never divorced or obtained any court order. Tadhg has died, and his will, made last year, leaves his entire estate of £900,000 to his new partner, Una, with whom he had lived for 18 months. Saoirse, aged 58, has modest savings and rents a small flat. She wishes to make a claim under the Inheritance (Provision for Family and Dependants) Act 1975.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Saoirse's claim?","o":["She can claim only for her maintenance, because she and Tadhg had separated.","She can claim as a spouse, and is not limited to provision needed for her maintenance.","She cannot claim, because a spouse who has lived apart from the deceased for over two years loses the right to apply.","She can claim as a spouse, but Una's claim as a cohabitant must be satisfied first.","She can claim, but only within six months of Tadhg's death, whether or not a grant has been issued."],"a":1,"e":"A spouse who is merely **separated** (not judicially separated or divorced) applies under s.1(1)(a) and benefits from the **surviving spouse standard**: such provision as is **reasonable in all the circumstances, whether or not required for maintenance**, cross-checked against what she might have received on divorce. Only a **judicially separated** spouse falls to the maintenance standard. Una lived with Tadhg for under two years, so she is not a qualifying cohabitant in any event, and the **time limit runs from the grant**, not from death."},{"t":"iht-lifetime","p":"FLK2","q":"Wilhelmina made no gifts in the tax year 2025/26. In the tax year 2026/27 she gave £200 to her niece in May, £10,000 to her son in June and £340,000 to the trustees of a discretionary trust in December. She had made no other lifetime gifts. The trustees agreed to pay any IHT due on the gift to the trust. Assume a nil rate band of £325,000 and a lifetime rate of 20%.\n\nHow much lifetime IHT is payable on the gift to the trust?","o":["£1,800","£2,250","£3,750","£3,000","£3,800"],"a":3,"e":"The £200 gift is covered by the **small gifts exemption**. Exemptions are applied to gifts **in date order**, so the **annual exemptions** for 2026/27 and the carried-forward 2025/26 exemption (£6,000) are used up by the earlier £10,000 gift to her son, which is a **PET** and is **ignored when calculating lifetime tax** on the later LCT. The trust gift is therefore £340,000; the excess over the nil rate band is £15,000, taxed at 20% = £3,000, with no grossing up because the **trustees pay**. Grossing up (25%) applies only where the donor pays."},{"t":"iht-lifetime","p":"FLK2","q":"In August 2021, Xavier gave his son Yorick £400,000 in cash. Xavier had made no other lifetime gifts. Xavier died in October 2026, leaving his estate to his daughter. Assume a nil rate band of £325,000 at death, a death rate of 40%, and taper relief of 40% of the full tax being payable where death occurs between 5 and 6 years after the gift.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the IHT position on the gift to Yorick?","o":["Tax of £11,040 is payable by Xavier's executors out of the estate passing to his daughter.","Tax of £27,600 is payable by Yorick, because taper relief reduces only the value of the gift.","Tax of £16,560 is payable by Yorick after taper relief.","Tax of £12,000 is payable by Yorick, because annual exemptions do not apply to failed PETs.","Tax of £11,040 is payable by Yorick after taper relief."],"a":4,"e":"The gift is a **failed PET** because Xavier died within 7 years. Two **annual exemptions** (2021/22 and 2020/21) reduce it to £394,000; the excess over the nil rate band is £69,000, giving tax of £27,600 at 40%, and **taper relief** (5 to 6 years) leaves 40% payable: £11,040. **Taper relief reduces the tax, not the value**, and the **donee** is primarily liable for tax on a failed PET; the executors become liable only if it is unpaid 12 months after the end of the month of death."},{"t":"iht-death-reliefs","p":"FLK2","q":"Zelda, a widow, died in 2026. Her net estate, after debts and funeral expenses, is worth £2,100,000 and includes her home, worth £700,000, which she leaves to her daughter; the rest passes to her daughter too. Her husband died in 2015, leaving his whole estate to Zelda, so none of his nil rate band was used, and the PRs claim all transferable allowances. Zelda made no lifetime gifts. Assume a nil rate band of £325,000, a residence nil rate band of £175,000 tapered by £1 for every £2 over £2,000,000, and a death rate of 40%.\n\nHow much IHT is payable on Zelda's estate?","o":["£440,000","£480,000","£460,000","£580,000","£660,000"],"a":2,"e":"Zelda has a nil rate band of £650,000 (her own plus her husband's **transferable NRB**) and a maximum residence nil rate band of £350,000 (including the **transferred RNRB**, available even though he died before it was introduced). Her estate exceeds £2 million by £100,000, so the total RNRB is **tapered by £50,000** to £300,000. Taxable estate: £2,100,000 − £950,000 = £1,150,000, at 40% = £460,000. Ignoring the taper gives £440,000, and tapering each of the two RNRBs separately wrongly gives £480,000."},{"t":"iht-death-reliefs","p":"FLK2","q":"Anselm died in July 2026. His assets included: a share in a trading partnership he joined ten years ago, where the partnership agreement gives the surviving partners an option to buy a deceased partner's share; unquoted shares, held for ten years, in a company whose business is letting residential flats; shares listed on AIM in a trading company, held for five years; unquoted shares in a trading company bought 15 months before he died; and a warehouse he owned personally and let to his partnership. The total value of his business assets is well below £2.5 million.\n\nWhich asset is most likely to qualify for business property relief at 100%?","o":["The partnership share.","The shares in the letting company.","The AIM-listed shares.","The unquoted trading company shares.","The warehouse used by the partnership."],"a":0,"e":"An **interest in a business**, such as a partnership share, qualifies for **100% BPR** if owned for 2 years; an **option** to buy is not a **binding contract for sale**, so relief is not lost. A business that **mainly holds investments** (letting flats) gets no relief, **AIM shares** get 50% from 6 April 2026, land owned personally and used by the deceased's partnership gets 50%, and the trading shares fail the **2-year ownership** condition."},{"t":"income-cgt-administration","p":"FLK2","q":"The executors of Bartholomew's estate hold quoted shares that have risen in value by £20,000 since his death. The residue is left equally to his four grandchildren, all adults, who are basic rate taxpayers with no other capital gains this year. The executors ask whether they should sell the shares and distribute the cash or transfer the shares to the grandchildren.\n\nWhich of the following is the best advice on the capital gains tax position?","o":["Transferring the shares is a disposal by the executors at market value, so the tax is the same either way.","Transferring the shares is not a disposal, and each grandchild acquires at probate value and can use their own annual exempt amount.","Selling is better, because the grandchildren would take the shares at Bartholomew's original cost and so face a larger gain.","Selling is better, because executors pay capital gains tax at a lower rate than basic rate taxpayers.","Transferring the shares is better, because the grandchildren will never pay capital gains tax on inherited assets."],"a":1,"e":"When PRs transfer an asset to a beneficiary **in specie** there is **no disposal** for CGT, and the beneficiary is treated as acquiring it **at probate value at the date of death** (TCGA 1992 s.62(4)). Each grandchild can then sell and use their **own annual exempt amount** and rates, whereas the executors would have a single annual exempt amount and pay at the trustees' and PRs' rate. The grandchildren are taxed on later sales, but only on growth since death, not from the deceased's original cost."}],"wills-p2":[{"t":"validity","p":"FLK2","q":"Gloria, aged 79, has full mental capacity. Her son Lionel, who visits her daily and does her shopping, repeatedly told her over several months that he deserved her house because he had looked after her, and on one occasion broke down in tears asking her to change her will. Lionel drove Gloria to a solicitor's office and waited in the car while she gave instructions alone. Her new will, properly signed and witnessed, leaves the house to Lionel and the rest of her estate equally to Lionel and his sister Hattie. Gloria has died, and Hattie says the will was obtained by undue influence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome of Hattie's challenge?","o":["The will is likely to stand, because Hattie must prove coercion and appeals to affection and gratitude are not enough.","The will is likely to fail, because Lionel's position of trust raises a presumption of undue influence that he cannot rebut.","The will is likely to fail, because Lionel arranged the appointment and takes a benefit, so undue influence is presumed.","The will is likely to stand only if Lionel shows that a doctor assessed Gloria's capacity when she signed it.","The will is likely to stand except for the gift of the house, which is void because Lionel procured it."],"a":0,"e":"**Undue influence in a will means coercion that overbears the testator's own wishes; persuasion, including emotional appeals to affection or gratitude, is permitted.** **No presumption of undue influence applies to wills**, so Hattie (the challenger) must prove it, and coercion must be the only reasonable explanation of the facts. Lionel's involvement might at most raise suspicious circumstances requiring proof of knowledge and approval, which Gloria's giving instructions alone would satisfy. A doctor's assessment (the \"golden rule\") is good practice, not a legal requirement, and there is no rule voiding only the gift to the person who persuaded the testator."},{"t":"formalities","p":"FLK2","q":"Elspeth signed her will at her kitchen table in the presence of three people who were all in the room together: her neighbours Corin and Delyth, and Omar, the husband of her friend Nadia. Corin, Delyth and Omar each then signed the will as witnesses while Elspeth watched. The will leaves £20,000 to Nadia and the residue to Elspeth's nephew. Elspeth has now died.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the gift to Nadia?","o":["It is void, because Nadia's husband witnessed the will, but the rest of the will is valid.","It fails, because the will is invalid where a beneficiary's spouse acts as one of the witnesses.","It is valid, because the will was also witnessed by two other people who take nothing under it.","It is void unless Nadia and Omar married after the will was signed, which they did not.","It is valid only if Omar signs a statement disclaiming any benefit from the gift to his wife."],"a":2,"e":"Under **s.15 Wills Act 1837** a gift to a witness or to a witness's spouse is normally void, but under **Wills Act 1968 s.1 the gift is saved if the will is also signed by at least two other witnesses who do not benefit**: the beneficiary's spouse is then disregarded as a supernumerary (extra) witness. Corin and Delyth are two independent witnesses, so Nadia's gift takes effect. The option treating the gift as void overlooks the 1968 Act, and s.15 never invalidates the whole will. There is no procedure for a witness to disclaim a spouse's gift to save it."},{"t":"formalities","p":"FLK2","q":"Philippa has severe arthritis and cannot hold a pen. Her solicitor read her new will aloud to her and she confirmed it was what she wanted. With her solicitor and a trainee solicitor both present, Philippa asked her son Rory to sign her name on the will for her, and Rory did so in front of her. The solicitor and the trainee then signed as witnesses in Philippa's presence. Rory did not sign as a witness. The will leaves the residue of her estate to Rory.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position after Philippa's death?","o":["The will is invalid, because a will must be signed by the testator personally unless they are on actual military service.","The will is valid, but Rory's gift is void because he signed the will and so is treated in the same way as a witness.","The will is invalid, because the person who signs for the testator must be independent and take nothing under the will.","The will is valid only if Philippa also made a mark, such as a cross, beside the signature that Rory wrote for her.","The will is validly executed and Rory's gift is effective, though proof that Philippa knew and approved it may be needed."],"a":4,"e":"**s.9 Wills Act 1837 allows the will to be signed by another person in the testator's presence and by their direction**, and here the signature was made before two witnesses present together, who then signed in Philippa's presence. **s.15 only makes void gifts to a witness or a witness's spouse**, so Rory, who signed for Philippa but did not witness, keeps his gift. Because someone signed on her behalf, the probate registry will want the attestation clause or an affidavit to show she knew and approved the contents (NCPR r.13). No rule requires the signer to be independent or the testator to add a mark."},{"t":"executors","p":"FLK2","q":"Matthias's will appoints Priya, a partner in a firm of solicitors, as his sole executor. The will contains no clause allowing an executor to charge for professional services. Priya has taken out probate and is administering the estate, which includes a property portfolio and a family company. She has spent many hours on the administration and would like to charge her firm's normal hourly rates.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Priya can charge for her work?","o":["She can charge reasonable fees, because the Trustee Act 2000 gives any professional executor that right.","She cannot charge, because she is the sole executor and the will contains no charging clause.","She can charge reasonable fees, but only for the work she has done since the grant of probate was issued.","She can charge reasonable fees, provided they are approved by the probate registrar when she files the accounts.","She can charge reasonable fees, but only out of the income of the estate and not out of its capital."],"a":1,"e":"**A professional executor needs authority to charge.** Without a charging clause, **Trustee Act 2000 s.29** (applied to PRs) allows a trust corporation to charge, and allows a professional who is **not a sole** PR to charge only if **each other PR agrees in writing**. Priya is the sole executor and not a trust corporation, so s.29 does not help her (absent the agreement of all the beneficiaries or the court's authority), which is why a will appointing a sole professional executor should include a charging clause. The other options describe limits or approvals that the statute does not contain."},{"t":"executors","p":"FLK2","q":"Henrietta's valid will appoints all five of her adult children as her executors. She has died, and all five children are willing to act and none of them has done anything in the estate yet. They would like to apply together so that each of them has an equal say in the administration.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the grant can be taken?","o":["All five can take probate together, because there is no limit on the number of executors who can prove.","Only two of them can take probate, because a grant can never be issued to more than two personal representatives.","No more than four of them can take probate, and the fifth must formally renounce before the grant is issued.","No more than four of them can take probate; the fifth can have power reserved and prove later if a vacancy arises.","All five can take probate together, provided the probate registrar gives permission because they are all adults."],"a":3,"e":"**A will can appoint any number of executors, but no more than four can take a grant in respect of the same property (Senior Courts Act 1981 s.114(1)).** The others need not renounce (formally give up the right to act): they can have **power reserved**, keeping the right to prove later, for example if a proving executor dies, and notice of the application is normally given to them (NCPR r.27). The rule about **two** personal representatives is a minimum for administrators where a minority or life interest arises, not a maximum."},{"t":"alterations-revocation","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2019 Rashid made a valid will leaving his entire estate \"to my wife Leanne, but if she dies before me, to my sister Mariam\". The will appoints Leanne as executor and, if Leanne dies before him, Mariam as executor. Rashid and Leanne divorced, and the final order was made in March 2026. Rashid died in September 2026 without having changed his will. Leanne is still alive. The will shows no intention about what should happen on a divorce.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of Rashid's will?","o":["The will was revoked by the divorce, so the estate passes under the intestacy rules.","Mariam takes the whole estate and is the person entitled to act as executor.","Leanne takes the whole estate, because she is alive and the will was not revoked.","Mariam takes the whole estate, but Leanne remains entitled to act as executor.","The estate passes on partial intestacy, because Leanne did not in fact die first."],"a":1,"e":"**Divorce does not revoke a will, but under s.18A Wills Act 1837 the former spouse is treated as having died on the date of the final order**, both for gifts to them and for their appointment as executor, unless the will shows a contrary intention. So the substitutional gift and the substitute appointment of Mariam both take effect. It is **marriage**, not divorce, that revokes a will, and the deemed death applies even though Leanne is actually alive, which is why the partial intestacy option is wrong."},{"t":"interpretation","p":"FLK2","q":"Penelope, a widow, made a valid will leaving her residuary estate \"equally between my son Hamish and my nephew Callum\". There is no substitution clause or survivorship wording. Hamish died in 2024, survived by his daughter Isla, aged 20. Callum died in 2025, survived by his two sons. Penelope died in 2026. Isla and both of Callum's sons are alive.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Penelope's residuary estate passes?","o":["Isla takes Hamish's half, and Callum's two sons share Callum's half equally.","Both halves lapse, so the whole residue passes under the intestacy rules.","Both gifts lapse, but Isla takes the whole residue as Penelope's only descendant.","Callum's two sons share the whole residue, as the gift to Hamish has lapsed.","Isla takes Hamish's half, and Callum's half passes under the intestacy rules."],"a":4,"e":"**Under s.33 Wills Act 1837 a gift to the testator's child or remoter descendant who dies first leaving issue alive at the testator's death does not lapse but passes to that issue**, so Isla takes Hamish's half. **s.33 does not apply to a nephew**, so Callum's gift lapses (fails because he died first), and a lapsed share of **residue** cannot fall into residue, so it passes on **partial intestacy**. The tempting option giving Callum's sons his share wrongly extends s.33 beyond the testator's own descendants."},{"t":"intestacy","p":"FLK2","q":"Ifeoma and her husband Chidi separated two years ago. Ifeoma started divorce proceedings and a conditional order was made in June 2026, but no final order had been made when Ifeoma died intestate in August 2026. They had no children. Ifeoma is survived by Chidi and by her mother, Ngozi. Her net estate is worth £700,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Ifeoma's estate passes?","o":["Chidi takes the whole estate, because he was still her husband when she died.","Ngozi takes the whole estate, because the conditional order ended Chidi's rights.","Chidi takes the statutory legacy and half the rest, and Ngozi takes the other half.","Chidi and Ngozi share the estate equally, because the spouses had separated.","Ngozi takes the whole estate, because Chidi has lived apart from Ifeoma for two years."],"a":0,"e":"**A spouse remains entitled on intestacy until the final order of divorce; separation and a conditional order have no effect.** **Where the intestate leaves a spouse but no issue, the spouse takes the whole estate**, so parents take nothing however large the estate. The statutory legacy and half the residue applies only where there are **issue** (descendants), not parents. Only a judicially separated spouse is treated as having died first, which is not this case; Ngozi's only route would be a claim under the 1975 Act, if she qualified."},{"t":"intestacy","p":"FLK2","q":"Rosemary, a widow, died intestate in 2026 with a net estate of £600,000. She had two children: her daughter Moira, aged 45, and her son Neville, who died in 2022 leaving two children, Orla, aged 19, and Piers, aged 14. Rosemary also brought up her late husband's son Quentin from the age of six, but never adopted him. All of them survived Rosemary.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Rosemary's estate is distributed?","o":["Moira, Orla, Piers and Quentin each take £150,000, with Piers's share held on trust until he is 18.","Moira and Quentin each take £300,000, because Neville's share lapsed when he died before Rosemary.","Moira takes £300,000, Orla takes £150,000 absolutely, and Piers's £150,000 is held on trust until he is 18.","Moira, Orla and Piers each take £200,000, with Piers's share held on trust until he is 18.","Moira, Orla and Quentin each take £200,000 absolutely, and Piers takes nothing until he is 18."],"a":2,"e":"With no spouse, the estate passes to **issue on the statutory trusts**, and **a child who died before the intestate is represented by their own children, who share that child's part equally (per stirpes)**. So Moira takes half and Neville's half is split between Orla and Piers, with Piers's share **contingent** on reaching 18. **Stepchildren who were not adopted take nothing on intestacy**, so Quentin's only route is a claim under the 1975 Act as a child of the family. Dividing equally between the three grandchildren and child ignores the per stirpes (by branch) rule."},{"t":"outside-estate","p":"FLK2","q":"Kamala and her brother Vikram bought a house together in 2015 as beneficial joint tenants. In 2025, after a family disagreement, Kamala's solicitor served written notice on Vikram stating that Kamala wished to sever the joint tenancy; Vikram did not reply. In 2026 Kamala made a will leaving \"my share of the house\" to her daughter Sunita and died shortly afterwards.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what happens to Kamala's interest in the house?","o":["It passes to Vikram by survivorship, because a will cannot sever a joint tenancy or override it.","It passes to Vikram by survivorship, because the notice was ineffective without his written agreement.","Her half share passes to Sunita outside the will, so her executors do not need a grant to deal with it.","Her half share passes under her will to Sunita, and her executors will need a grant to deal with it.","It passes to Vikram by survivorship, but Sunita can claim it from him under the 1975 Act as of right."],"a":3,"e":"**A beneficial joint tenancy can be severed during life by written notice to the other joint tenant (Law of Property Act 1925 s.36(2))**, and the other co-owner's agreement is not needed. Once severed, the co-owners hold as **tenants in common**, so Kamala's half share passes **under her will** and her PRs need a grant to deal with it. It is true that a will cannot sever, but the severance here happened in Kamala's lifetime. Survivorship (the automatic passing of a joint tenant's interest to the survivor) therefore does not apply."},{"t":"outside-estate","p":"FLK2","q":"Walter, aged 90, added his niece Freya's name to his bank account last year so that she could pay his bills and buy his groceries while he was in hospital. All the money in the account came from Walter's pension and savings, and Freya never paid anything in or used the money for herself. Walter has died, and his will leaves everything to his two sons. The account holds £38,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who is likely to be entitled to the money in the account?","o":["Freya alone, because the money in a joint account always passes to the survivor by survivorship.","The sons under the will, because Freya probably holds the money on a resulting trust for Walter's estate.","Freya and Walter's estate in equal shares, because the account was held in their joint names.","Freya alone, because the bank will release a joint account balance to the survivor without a grant.","The sons under the will, because survivorship applies only to land and not to money in bank accounts."],"a":1,"e":"A joint account normally passes to the survivor by **survivorship**, but **where one person provided all the money and added the other's name only for convenience, a presumed resulting trust means the money still belongs beneficially to the person who paid it in**. Walter funded the account and Freya's name was added only to help with bills, so the money forms part of his estate and passes to his sons. That the bank may pay Freya on production of the death certificate does not decide who is beneficially entitled, and survivorship can apply to money as well as land."},{"t":"grants","p":"FLK2","q":"Ewan, who was divorced, died intestate in 2026. His net estate is worth £420,000. He is survived by his former wife, Mhairi, and their two children, Kirsty, aged 22, and Lachlan, aged 15. Kirsty wants to deal with the estate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Kirsty's position?","o":["She may apply alone for letters of administration, as she has the first right to a grant.","She may apply for a grant of probate together with one other adult as a co-executor.","She cannot apply, because Mhairi as Ewan's former wife has the first right to a grant.","She cannot apply until Lachlan is 18, as both children have an equal right to a grant.","She may apply for letters of administration but must be joined by a second administrator."],"a":4,"e":"On intestacy priority to a grant follows **NCPR r.22**; a **former spouse has no right after the final order**, so Ewan's **children** come first and Kirsty, as an adult, can apply for **letters of administration**. However, **at least two individuals (or a trust corporation) must take a grant of administration where a minority interest arises (Senior Courts Act 1981 s.114(2))**, and Lachlan's share is held on the statutory trusts until he is 18. **Probate** is only for executors appointed by a will, and a minor's interest does not stop an adult with equal priority from applying."},{"t":"iht-funding-incidence","p":"FLK2","q":"Leopold owned a house with his brother Casimir as beneficial joint tenants, and Casimir became sole owner on Leopold's death. Leopold's will leaves a legacy of £25,000 to his friend Ottoline and the residue of his estate to his daughter Philippa. The will says nothing about who bears inheritance tax. Inheritance tax is payable on Leopold's estate, part of which is attributable to his half share of the house.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who bears the inheritance tax attributable to Leopold's half share of the house?","o":["Philippa, because all the tax on the estate is a testamentary expense paid from residue.","Ottoline and Philippa rateably, because the legacy and the residue share the burden of tax.","Casimir, because property passing by survivorship bears its own share of the tax.","No one, because a joint tenant's share passing by survivorship is outside the estate for tax.","Leopold's executors personally, because they are the only people liable to pay HMRC on death."],"a":2,"e":"**The default rule that IHT is paid from residue (IHTA s.211) applies only to UK free estate vesting in the PRs; property outside the free estate, such as a joint tenant's share passing by survivorship, bears its own tax.** Leopold's half share is still **included** in his estate for IHT, even though it passes to Casimir outside the will, so the tax attributable to it falls on Casimir. The PRs and Casimir are both liable to HMRC for it, but liability (who must pay HMRC) is different from **incidence** (whose inheritance is reduced in the end). Ottoline's pecuniary legacy is received free of tax."},{"t":"iht-funding-incidence","p":"FLK2","q":"In March 2025 Agnieszka gave her nephew Tomasz £400,000 in cash. She had made no other gifts. She died in October 2026, leaving her whole estate, worth £600,000, to her husband. The inheritance tax on the gift to Tomasz, after deducting two annual exemptions and the nil rate band, is £27,600.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who is liable to pay the £27,600 to HMRC?","o":["Tomasz primarily; Agnieszka's PRs are liable only if it is unpaid 12 months after the end of the month of death.","Agnieszka's PRs, from residue, because tax on a lifetime gift is a testamentary expense of the estate.","Agnieszka's husband, because he is the residuary beneficiary and the tax reduces the residuary estate.","No one, because the whole estate passes to her husband and the spouse exemption covers the gift as well.","Tomasz and Agnieszka's PRs equally, because the tax arises on a gift made within seven years of death."],"a":0,"e":"A lifetime gift to an individual is a **PET** (potentially exempt transfer), which **fails** and becomes chargeable if the donor dies within 7 years. **The donee of a failed PET is primarily liable for the tax; the PRs become liable only if it remains unpaid 12 months after the end of the month of death**, and only up to the estate they hold. The rule that tax is paid from residue (IHTA s.211) applies only to the **free estate**, not to lifetime gifts, and the spouse exemption protects only what passes to the husband, not the earlier gift to Tomasz."},{"t":"administration","p":"FLK2","q":"Aurelia died 14 months ago. Her will leaves her residuary estate equally to her three nephews. Her executors have paid all debts, expenses and tax, and are ready to distribute about £90,000 to each nephew. Before doing so, a search carried out by the executors against each nephew reveals that one of them, Fergus, was made bankrupt eight months ago and has not been discharged. Fergus has asked the executors to pay his share into his personal bank account.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the executors should do with Fergus's share?","o":["Pay it to Fergus as he asks, because his share was given to him personally by the will.","Divide it between the other two nephews, because a bankrupt cannot take under a will.","Pay it into court, because executors cannot distribute to a beneficiary who is bankrupt.","Pay it to Fergus's trustee in bankruptcy, because his entitlement vests in the trustee.","Hold it on trust until Fergus is discharged, and then pay the whole share to Fergus."],"a":3,"e":"**A bankrupt beneficiary's entitlement vests in their trustee in bankruptcy, so the PRs must pay the trustee, not the beneficiary.** That is why PRs carry out a **bankruptcy search** (for example of the Individual Insolvency Register) against each beneficiary before paying. **A PR who pays a bankrupt beneficiary directly is personally liable to the trustee in bankruptcy** for the loss. Bankruptcy does not make the gift fail or pass to the other beneficiaries, and there is no need to pay into court or wait for discharge."},{"t":"family-provision","p":"FLK2","q":"Beatrix and Lucian divorced in 2015. On the divorce the court made a financial order giving Beatrix a lump sum on a clean break basis, which included an order that neither of them could apply against the other's estate under the Inheritance (Provision for Family and Dependants) Act 1975. Beatrix has not remarried. Lucian has died, leaving his estate of £1.2 million to his second wife. Beatrix, now in poor health and short of money, asks whether she can claim against Lucian's estate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Beatrix's position?","o":["She can claim as a former spouse who has not remarried, and the court will apply the maintenance standard.","She can claim as a former spouse who has not remarried, and the court will apply the surviving spouse standard.","She can claim, because a 1975 Act claim can be barred only by the deceased's will and not by a court order.","She can claim, but only if she shows that Lucian was paying her maintenance immediately before his death.","She cannot claim, because the order made on the divorce bars her from applying under the 1975 Act."],"a":4,"e":"A **former spouse who has not remarried** is normally within **s.1(1)(b)** of the 1975 Act, but **a clean break order made on divorce can bar a former spouse from applying under the Act**, and that is what happened here. Without the bar, Beatrix would have been limited to the **maintenance standard** (what is reasonable for day-to-day living costs), not the generous surviving spouse standard, which is reserved for current spouses and civil partners. A will cannot itself exclude a 1975 Act claim; the Act exists to override wills."},{"t":"family-provision","p":"FLK2","q":"Desmond died in January 2026. He and his second wife, Carys, owned their home as beneficial joint tenants, and it passed to her on his death. His will leaves the rest of his estate, about £40,000, to Carys. Desmond's adult son from his first marriage, Ivor, has a serious disability and depended on regular payments from Desmond. Carys obtained a grant of probate in May 2026, and Ivor issued a claim under the Inheritance (Provision for Family and Dependants) Act 1975 in September 2026.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ivor's claim?","o":["It is out of time, because a claim must be brought within six months of the date of death.","It is in time, and the court may treat Desmond's severable share of the home as part of his net estate.","It is in time, but the court can make an order only out of the £40,000 that passed under the will.","It is in time, and the court will apply the surviving spouse standard because Ivor was dependent.","It is out of time, because property passing by survivorship can be claimed only within six months of death."],"a":1,"e":"**A 1975 Act claim must be made within 6 months from the date of the grant**, not the death, so a claim in September after a May grant is in time. **Under s.9 the court may treat the deceased's severable share of property held as joint tenants as part of the net estate**, and since 2014 there is no separate time limit for doing so. As an adult child, Ivor is limited to the **maintenance standard**, although his disability and dependence are relevant s.3 factors; the surviving spouse standard applies only to a spouse or civil partner."},{"t":"iht-lifetime","p":"FLK2","q":"Ruth made no gifts in the tax year 2025/26. In the tax year 2026/27 she made these gifts: in July, £20,000 to her son Simeon on the occasion of, and conditional on, his marriage; in August, £400 to her niece Talitha; and in December, £150 to each of her ten grandchildren. She made no other gifts.\n\nWhat is the total value of the potentially exempt transfers made by Ruth in 2026/27?","o":["£9,150","£12,400","£9,400","£11,900","£10,900"],"a":2,"e":"Specific exemptions come first: the **marriage exemption for a parent is £5,000** and **each £150 gift is covered by the £250 small gifts exemption**. Simeon's gift is then reduced by the **annual exemption for 2026/27 and the unused £3,000 carried forward from 2025/26**: £20,000 − £5,000 − £6,000 = £9,000. **A gift over £250 gets no small gifts exemption at all** and the annual exemptions are used up, so Talitha's £400 is a PET in full, giving **£9,400**. £9,150 wrongly exempts £250 of Talitha's gift, and £12,400 forgets the one-year carry forward."},{"t":"iht-death-reliefs","p":"FLK2","q":"Florian, who was never married and made no lifetime gifts, died in 2026. He lived in a rented flat. His net estate, after debts and funeral expenses, is £825,000. His will leaves £50,000 to a registered UK charity and the residue to his friend Gideon. Assume a nil rate band of £325,000, a death rate of 40%, and a reduced rate of 36% where at least 10% of the baseline amount is left to charity.\n\nHow much inheritance tax is payable on Florian's estate?","o":["£162,000","£180,000","£200,000","£99,000","£279,000"],"a":0,"e":"The charity gift is exempt, leaving £825,000 − £50,000 − £325,000 = £450,000 chargeable. **The baseline amount is the estate after exemptions, reliefs and the nil rate band, with the charitable gift added back**: £450,000 + £50,000 = £500,000, and 10% of that is £50,000, so the gift qualifies and the tax is £450,000 × 36% = **£162,000**. £180,000 applies the 40% rate (as if 10% of the whole estate were needed) and £200,000 also ignores the charity deduction. £99,000 wrongly deducts the residence nil rate band, which needs a home passing to direct descendants."},{"t":"iht-death-reliefs","p":"FLK2","q":"Gareth, who was never married and made no lifetime gifts, died in August 2026. He lived in a rented cottage. He owned a farming business worth £3,300,000, all of which meets the conditions for 100% agricultural or business property relief, and £400,000 in cash. He left everything to his nephew. Assume a nil rate band of £325,000, a death rate of 40%, and a £2,500,000 allowance for 100% relief.\n\nHow much inheritance tax is payable on Gareth's estate?","o":["£30,000","£350,000","£490,000","£190,000","£690,000"],"a":3,"e":"**For deaths on or after 6 April 2026, 100% APR and BPR applies only to the first £2.5 million of combined qualifying property; qualifying property above that gets 50% relief.** The excess of £800,000 is relieved by half, leaving £400,000 chargeable, plus £400,000 cash: £800,000 − £325,000 = £475,000 × 40% = **£190,000**. £30,000 gives 100% relief on the whole business, £350,000 gives no relief at all on the excess, and £490,000 uses the original £1 million allowance that was abandoned before enactment. There is no transferable allowance because Gareth never married, and no residence nil rate band because he left no home."},{"t":"income-cgt-administration","p":"FLK2","q":"During the tax year 2026/27, the PRs of Esther's estate receive £1,400 of bank interest. That is the estate's only income in the year. They pay the net income to the sole residuary beneficiary, Jonah, a higher rate taxpayer. Assume a basic rate for savings income of 20% and a higher rate of 40%.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the income tax position on the interest?","o":["The PRs pay no tax, because the interest is within a personal savings allowance of £1,000.","The PRs pay £280 on the whole £1,400, and Jonah pays further tax with credit for the £280.","The PRs pay £180 on the £900 above £500, and Jonah pays further tax with credit for the £180.","The PRs pay £560 at the higher rate, because the income is paid to a higher rate taxpayer.","The PRs pay no tax, because their personal allowance covers the whole £1,400 of interest."],"a":1,"e":"**PRs pay income tax at the basic rate appropriate to the income, with no personal allowance, personal savings allowance or dividend allowance, and never at the higher rate.** The **£500 de minimis** rule removes tax only where total estate income is £500 or less; above that, the **whole** amount is taxed, so the PRs pay 20% of £1,400 = £280. Jonah includes the gross income in his own return using the R185 (the statement of estate income) and pays the difference between the higher and basic rates, with credit for the tax the PRs paid."},{"t":"income-cgt-administration","p":"FLK2","q":"Divya's father died in March 2025, leaving her £200,000. Divya is wealthy, and in November 2026 she signed a deed of variation, for no payment, redirecting the £200,000 to her two children, aged 8 and 11, to be held for them absolutely. The deed contains statements that the IHT and CGT read-back provisions are to apply. The money is invested and produces about £6,000 of interest a year.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the tax effect of the variation?","o":["For IHT it is treated as made by her father, but the interest is taxed as Divya's income.","Divya makes a potentially exempt transfer of £200,000, which fails if she dies within seven years.","It is not read back for any tax, because it was made more than twelve months after the death.","For IHT it is treated as made by her father, and the interest is taxed as the children's income.","It is not read back for IHT, because a deed of variation cannot benefit the beneficiary's own children."],"a":0,"e":"**A variation made in writing within 2 years of death, signed by the original beneficiary, not for consideration and containing the required statement, is read back to the death for IHT (IHTA s.142) and CGT**, so Divya makes no PET. **There is no read-back for income tax**: Divya is treated as the settlor, and because the money benefits her own minor, unmarried children and the income exceeds £100 a year, the interest is taxed as **her** income. The time limit is 2 years, not 12 months, and redirecting to the beneficiary's own children (skipping a generation) is a common, permitted use."}],"wills-p3":[{"t":"validity","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2019 Harold made a valid will leaving his estate equally to his son Ewan and his daughter Freya. In 2025, while suffering from a paranoid illness, he came to believe, without any basis, that Freya was stealing from him and putting poison in his food. Shortly afterwards he made a new will, properly signed and witnessed, which begins with a clause revoking all earlier wills and leaves everything to Ewan. When he made it, Harold could describe his property accurately and named both his children, but told his solicitor that Freya was \"trying to kill me for my money\". Nobody pressured him. Harold has now died.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Harold's estate will pass?","o":["Ewan takes everything, because Harold understood what he owned and who his children were when he signed the 2025 will.","Ewan takes everything under the 2025 will, but Freya may apply for reasonable provision for her maintenance from the estate.","Ewan and Freya share it equally under the 2019 will, because the 2025 will is invalid and its revocation clause fails with it.","Ewan and Freya share it equally under the intestacy rules, because the 2025 will is invalid and it revoked the 2019 will.","Ewan takes everything unless Freya can prove that Ewan put pressure on Harold to cut her out of the will."],"a":2,"e":"A testator lacks testamentary capacity if a **disorder of the mind poisons their affections** and causes the gift, even if they know their property and family (Banks v Goodfellow). Harold's delusion about Freya directly caused him to cut her out, so the whole 2025 will, **including its revocation clause**, is invalid and the **earlier valid will** applies. Option A looks only at two limbs of the test, option D wrongly assumes the invalid will revoked the 2019 will, and option E confuses capacity with undue influence, which is not needed here."},{"t":"validity","p":"FLK2","q":"Ines instructed her solicitor to prepare a will leaving \"£50,000 to my nephew Jamal\" and the residue to a cancer charity. The solicitor's attendance note records the figure of £50,000 clearly. When the will was typed, a secretary left off a nought, so the signed will gives Jamal £5,000. Ines signed it without noticing the error, and it was properly witnessed. Ines died eight months ago, and the executors obtained probate three months ago. Jamal has just discovered the mistake.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Jamal's position?","o":["He can apply to the court to rectify the will to £50,000, as it fails to carry out Ines's intentions because of a clerical error.","He cannot change the figure, because Ines signed the will and is presumed to have known and approved its contents.","He can ask the court to interpret the figure of £5,000 as meaning £50,000, using the attendance note as evidence of intention.","He cannot apply to rectify the will, because an application must be made within six months of the date of death.","He cannot change the figure, and his only remedy is to apply for reasonable provision from the estate as a dependant."],"a":0,"e":"The court can **rectify** a will that fails to carry out the testator's intentions because of a **clerical error** (Administration of Justice Act 1982 s.20), and the application must normally be made within **six months of the grant**, not of death, so Jamal is in time (option D is wrong). Option C is wrong because \"£5,000\" is not ambiguous, so the s.21 rules on extrinsic evidence for interpretation do not help; the remedy is to correct the words. Option B is wrong because signing a will does not stop the court correcting a typing slip, and option E is wrong because Jamal was not being maintained by Ines."},{"t":"formalities","p":"FLK2","q":"Kalinda had never made a will. She signed a homemade will at her kitchen table while her neighbour Jack watched, and Jack then signed as a witness in her presence. Jack went home. An hour later her friend Kemal called round. Kalinda showed Kemal the will, pointed to her signature and said \"That's my signature on my will, please sign it too\". Kemal signed as a witness while she watched. The will has a standard attestation clause. Kalinda has now died.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the validity of the will?","o":["It is valid, because each witness signed the will in Kalinda's presence and she watched them both sign.","It is valid, because Kalinda was entitled to acknowledge her signature to Kemal instead of signing again in front of him.","It is valid, because the attestation clause raises a presumption of due execution that cannot be displaced by the facts.","It is invalid, because the two witnesses did not sign the will in each other's presence.","It is invalid, because Kalinda never signed or acknowledged her signature while both witnesses were present together."],"a":4,"e":"Under **Wills Act 1837 s.9(c)** the testator's signature must be **made or acknowledged in the presence of two witnesses present at the same time**. Kalinda signed before Jack alone and acknowledged to Kemal alone, so the will is invalid and her estate passes on intestacy. Option B is the tempting trap: an acknowledgment is enough only if both witnesses are present together when it is made. Option D gives the wrong reason, because the witnesses do not need to sign in front of each other, and option C is wrong because the presumption of due execution is rebutted by evidence of what actually happened."},{"t":"executors","p":"FLK2","q":"Agatha's will appointed her friend Bernard as sole executor. Bernard obtained probate in 2025 and began administering the estate, but he died in 2026 before he had sold Agatha's shares or distributed her residue. Bernard's own will appoints his daughter Carys as his sole executor, and Carys has now obtained probate of Bernard's will. Agatha's residuary beneficiaries want to know who will finish administering Agatha's estate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Carys must apply for a grant of letters of administration de bonis non to complete the administration of Agatha's estate.","Carys is now also Agatha's executor, by the chain of executorship, and needs no further grant to complete the administration.","Agatha's residuary beneficiaries must apply for letters of administration with will annexed, because Bernard's death ended his office.","Carys can deal with Agatha's estate only if she first obtains a grant of double probate of Agatha's will.","Carys can complete the administration only if Agatha's residuary beneficiaries give their written consent to her acting."],"a":1,"e":"Where the **sole or last surviving proving executor** dies, their own executor, once they prove that executor's will, automatically becomes executor of the original testator too (**Administration of Estates Act 1925 s.7**, the **chain of executorship**). Bernard had proved Agatha's will and Carys has proved Bernard's, so the chain is unbroken and Carys needs no new grant. A grant **de bonis non** (option A) is needed only if the chain is broken, for example if Bernard had died intestate. Double probate (option D) is for an executor who had power reserved, which is not this situation."},{"t":"alterations-revocation","p":"FLK2","q":"Lionel kept his only will, which leaves everything to his partner Mira, at the home of his sister Maud. After an argument with Mira, Lionel phoned Maud from his own house, twenty miles away, and told her to burn the will. Maud burnt it in her garden that evening. Lionel never made another will and died two months later, having told friends that he \"had dealt with his will\". His solicitor has kept a copy of the will.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of what happened?","o":["The will was revoked, because Maud destroyed it on Lionel's direction and he intended to revoke it.","The will was revoked, because Lionel ratified Maud's act by later telling friends he had dealt with his will.","The will was revoked, because the original cannot be found and is presumed to have been destroyed by Lionel.","The will was not revoked, because Maud did not burn it in Lionel's presence, and its contents can be proved from the copy.","The will was not revoked, because a will can be revoked only by a later will or by a properly executed written declaration."],"a":3,"e":"Destruction by another person revokes a will only if done **in the testator's presence and by their direction** (**Wills Act 1837 s.20**), and the testator cannot later ratify destruction done elsewhere, so options A and B fail. The will therefore stands and can be proved from the **copy**, with a registrar's order. Option C is wrong because the presumption of revocation applies only to a will last known to be in the testator's own possession, and here we know what happened to it. Option E is wrong because destruction with intention to revoke is a valid method if properly carried out."},{"t":"alterations-revocation","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2020 Nerys made a will leaving £10,000 to her friend Hugo and the residue to her niece. The will was signed in the presence of Hugo and another friend, Pip, who both signed as witnesses. In 2023 Nerys made a properly executed codicil, witnessed by two neighbours who take nothing under the will or codicil. The codicil replaced the executor named in the will and stated: \"In all other respects I confirm my will dated 2020.\" Nerys has now died.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Hugo's entitlement?","o":["He takes the £10,000, because the codicil republished the will at a date when it was not witnessed by him.","He takes nothing, because the gift was void when the will was signed and a codicil cannot cure a void gift.","He takes the £10,000 only if he can show that two other independent witnesses were present when the will was signed.","He takes nothing, because the gift to him fell into residue when the will was made and passed to the niece.","He takes the £10,000 only if the codicil expressly repeats the legacy to him in its own terms."],"a":0,"e":"A codicil that **confirms** a will **republishes** it, so the will is treated as made on the date of the codicil (**Wills Act 1837 s.34**). Hugo witnessed the will but not the codicil, so at the republished date the **s.15** rule no longer bites and his gift is saved. Option B overlooks republication, and option D misdescribes when s.15 operates. Option C refers to the Wills Act 1968 exception, which is not needed here because Hugo did not witness the codicil, and option E is wrong because general confirmation is enough without repeating the gift."},{"t":"interpretation","p":"FLK2","q":"Rhoda's will, made in 2018, leaves \"my cottage in Whitby to my nephew Selim\" and the residue of her estate to her daughter Tamsin. In August 2026 Rhoda exchanged contracts to sell the cottage for £280,000. She died suddenly in September 2026, and her executors completed the sale in October 2026. The will contains no provision about the sale of the cottage.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Selim's entitlement?","o":["He takes the cottage, because Rhoda still owned it at her death, so the sale cannot proceed without his agreement.","He takes the £280,000 sale price, because the proceeds represent the cottage, which Rhoda still owned at her death.","He takes nothing, because the gift adeemed when Rhoda contracted to sell, and the sale price falls into residue for Tamsin.","He takes the £280,000 as a demonstrative gift, payable out of the sale proceeds in priority to the residue.","He takes nothing, because the gift lapsed when contracts were exchanged, and the cottage passes on partial intestacy."],"a":2,"e":"A **specific gift adeems** if the property is not in the estate at death, and where the testator has **contracted to sell** before death but completion takes place after death, the gift adeems and the sale money goes to **residue**. The beneficiary does not take the proceeds unless the will says so, so options B and D are wrong. Option A ignores the binding contract, which the executors must complete. Option E is wrong because **lapse** is about a beneficiary dying before the testator, and the money passes under the residuary gift to Tamsin, not on intestacy."},{"t":"interpretation","p":"FLK2","q":"Ulrika, a widow, made a will leaving £20,000 to her daughter Ffion and the residue \"to my son Gareth\". There is no substitution clause. Gareth was convicted of murdering Ulrika. Gareth has one child, Hana, aged 12, who was alive when Ulrika died. Ffion is also alive.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Ulrika's residuary estate will pass?","o":["Gareth takes the residue, but it is held on trust for him because he is in prison.","Gareth takes nothing, and the court can decide how much of the residue, if any, he may keep despite the conviction.","Gareth takes nothing, and the residue passes on partial intestacy, shared equally between Ffion and Hana.","Gareth takes nothing, and the residue passes on partial intestacy to Ffion alone, as Ulrika's only surviving child.","Gareth takes nothing, and the residue passes to Hana, because Gareth is treated as having died before Ulrika."],"a":4,"e":"Under the **forfeiture rule** a killer cannot benefit, and the killer is **treated as having died before the testator** (**Wills Act 1837 s.33A**). A gift to the testator's **child** who is treated as predeceasing, leaving **issue alive at the testator's death**, passes to that issue under **s.33**, so Hana takes the whole residue (held for her until she is 18). Option B is wrong because the court cannot modify the rule in a case of **murder**. Options C and D wrongly treat the residuary gift as failing, which overlooks s.33."},{"t":"intestacy","p":"FLK2","q":"Bartosz died intestate on 1 March 2026. His wife, Celina, was injured in the same accident and died in hospital on 20 March 2026. They had no children. Bartosz is survived by both his parents. Celina left a valid will giving her whole estate to her sister, Dorota. Bartosz's net estate, which includes no jointly owned property, is worth £400,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who will inherit Bartosz's estate?","o":["Celina's estate takes all of it, so it passes to Dorota under Celina's will.","Celina's estate takes the personal chattels and £322,000, and Bartosz's parents take the rest.","Bartosz's parents and Celina's estate take half each, because Celina survived Bartosz but died shortly after.","Bartosz's parents take all of it equally, because Celina did not survive Bartosz by 28 days.","Bartosz's parents take all of it, because the younger of the two is presumed to have survived the elder."],"a":3,"e":"A spouse inherits on intestacy only if they **survive the intestate by 28 days** (**Administration of Estates Act 1925 s.46(2A)**); otherwise the estate is distributed as if the spouse had **died first**. Celina died 19 days after Bartosz, so with no issue his **parents** take the whole estate equally and absolutely. Option A would be right only if Celina had survived by 28 days, and option B applies the statutory legacy, which is used only where there are issue. Option E is wrong because the order of deaths is known here, so the commorientes presumption does not arise."},{"t":"intestacy","p":"FLK2","q":"Kelechi's valid will gives all his personal chattels to his wife, Lerato, £50,000 to his adult daughter, Imani, and the residue to his friend Jasper. Jasper died before Kelechi, and the will contains no substitute gift. Kelechi died in 2026, survived by Lerato and Imani, who is his only child. After paying all debts, expenses and tax, and after the chattels and the £50,000 legacy, the residue is worth £522,000. Assume a statutory legacy of £322,000.\n\nHow much will Imani receive from Kelechi's estate in total?","o":["£50,000","£150,000","£100,000","£311,000","£261,000"],"a":1,"e":"The lapsed gift of residue passes on **partial intestacy**: Lerato takes the **statutory legacy** of £322,000 plus half the remaining £200,000, and Imani takes the other half, £100,000. There is **no hotchpot** on intestacy, so Imani keeps her £50,000 legacy as well, a total of **£150,000**. Option C forgets the legacy (or wrongly deducts it from her share), option A assumes Lerato takes all the undisposed residue, and options D and E ignore the statutory legacy."},{"t":"outside-estate","p":"FLK2","q":"Mustafa died in 2019. His will left £400,000 to trustees to pay the income to his wife, Nadia, for her life, and after her death to hold the fund for his son from his first marriage, Owen. Nadia has now died. Her own will leaves her whole estate to her niece, Parveen. The trust fund is still worth £400,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position on the £400,000 trust fund?","o":["It passes to Parveen under Nadia's will, because Nadia was entitled to the fund for her lifetime.","It passes to Owen and is not part of Nadia's estate for inheritance tax, because she never owned the capital.","It passes to Owen, and Nadia's executors must pay any inheritance tax on it out of the property passing to Parveen.","It passes to Parveen, but Owen can claim it from her because Mustafa's will said the fund was to go to him.","It passes to Owen under the trust, but counts as part of Nadia's estate for IHT, with the tax paid by the trustees."],"a":4,"e":"Trust property in which the deceased had a life interest passes under the **trust's own terms** to the person entitled next (Owen), not under the life tenant's will, so options A and D are wrong. A life interest created by a will that took effect on death is an **immediate post-death interest**, a qualifying interest in possession, so the fund is **treated as part of Nadia's estate for IHT** (option B is wrong). The **trustees** are liable for, and the fund bears, the tax attributable to it, not Parveen's share of the free estate (option C)."},{"t":"grants","p":"FLK2","q":"Pradeep, a long-term UK resident, died in 2026. His will leaves his whole estate to his wife, Rani, who is also a long-term UK resident. His estate has a gross value of £1,600,000, including the family home in his sole name, and he has debts of £20,000. He made no lifetime gifts, had no interest in any trust and owned no foreign assets. Rani, his executor, wants to apply for probate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what she must do about inheritance tax before applying?","o":["She need not send an IHT400, as it is an exempt excepted estate, and gives the values in the probate application.","She must send an IHT400, because the gross value of the estate is more than the nil rate band of £325,000.","She must send an IHT400 to claim the spouse exemption, then wait 20 working days before applying for probate.","She must send an IHT205 short form, because no inheritance tax is payable but the estate exceeds the nil rate band.","She need not send any IHT form, because no inheritance tax is payable on a death where everything passes to a spouse."],"a":0,"e":"An **exempt excepted estate** has a gross value of no more than **£3 million**, and after deducting debts and only the **spouse** and/or **charity** exemptions the chargeable estate is within the nil rate band; here it is nil, and there are no gifts, trusts or foreign assets to disqualify it. So **no IHT400** is needed and Rani simply gives the values in the probate application. Option B applies the low value test only, and option C describes the procedure for a non-excepted estate. Option D is wrong because the IHT205 was abolished for deaths from 1 January 2022, and option E gives the wrong reason, as a spouse estate over £3 million would still need an IHT400."},{"t":"grants","p":"FLK2","q":"Quinton's valid will appoints his accountant as sole executor, leaves a legacy of £10,000 to his friend Rosa, and leaves his residuary estate \"to my brother Silas, to hold on trust for my three grandchildren in equal shares\". All three grandchildren are adults. The accountant has validly renounced probate. Silas, Rosa and the eldest grandchild are each willing to apply for a grant.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who is entitled to apply first, and for what grant?","o":["The grandchildren, for a grant of probate, because they are the people beneficially entitled to the residue.","Silas, for letters of administration with will annexed, because he is the residuary legatee holding in trust.","Silas, for a grant of probate, because the will appoints him to hold the residue on trust and so makes him an executor.","Rosa, for letters of administration with will annexed, because she is the only beneficiary given a fixed legacy.","The grandchildren, for letters of administration with will annexed, because they rank ahead of a trustee of residue."],"a":1,"e":"Where there is a will but no proving executor, priority follows **Non-Contentious Probate Rules 1987 r.20**: after the executor comes **(b) a residuary legatee holding in trust** for others, then (c) other residuary beneficiaries, and only at (e) other legatees such as Rosa. Silas therefore ranks first, ahead of the grandchildren (option E). **Probate goes only to executors**, so anyone else takes **letters of administration with will annexed**, which rules out options A and C; a trustee of residue is not appointed executor merely by holding the residue on trust."},{"t":"iht-funding-incidence","p":"FLK2","q":"Yolanda died on 10 May 2026. Her estate included a house she had always let to tenants and never lived in, which does not qualify for any relief, together with cash and quoted shares. Inheritance tax was payable. Her PRs paid the tax on the cash and shares in full and chose to pay the tax on the let house by instalments, paying the first instalment on time on 30 November 2026. In 2028 the PRs sold the let house to raise money for the beneficiaries.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position on the inheritance tax on the let house?","o":["The PRs may continue paying the remaining instalments as scheduled, because the election for instalments cannot be withdrawn.","The PRs may continue paying the remaining instalments, which carry no interest because land always qualifies for interest-free instalments.","All the outstanding tax on the house became payable on the sale, but no interest is due because each instalment was paid on time.","All the outstanding tax on the house became payable on the sale, and interest has been running on the whole outstanding balance.","Only the next instalment became payable on the sale, and the PRs may pay the rest when the estate is finally distributed."],"a":3,"e":"Tax on **land** can be paid in **10 yearly instalments**, but if the instalment property is **sold**, **all the outstanding tax** on it becomes payable immediately, so options A, B and E are wrong. Land that does not qualify for agricultural or business property relief carries **interest on the whole outstanding balance** of tax, even when each instalment is paid on time; that is why option C is wrong. Interest-free instalments apply only to property such as businesses, qualifying shareholdings and APR or BPR property."},{"t":"administration","p":"FLK2","q":"Tomasz's will leaves his residuary estate equally to his three children, Ursula, Viktor and Wiktoria. Ursula went to work abroad in 2013 and has not contacted anyone since. The family have tried to trace her through her last employer, social media and a tracing agent, without success. Nobody knows whether she is alive. Tomasz died 18 months ago, and the executors have paid all debts, expenses and tax. Viktor and Wiktoria are pressing for the residue to be distributed.\n\nWhich of the following best protects the executors if they distribute the residue?","o":["Placing notices under section 27 of the Trustee Act 1925 and distributing after the time for claims has passed.","Distributing the residue in thirds now, because the executor's year has ended and Ursula has not made any claim.","Applying to the court for an order allowing them to distribute on the basis that Ursula died before Tomasz.","Paying Ursula's third to Viktor and Wiktoria, because a person missing for over seven years is presumed to be dead.","Paying Ursula's third to the Crown as ownerless property, because nobody is able to give a receipt for it."],"a":2,"e":"Where a beneficiary is **known to exist but cannot be found**, the executors should obtain a **Benjamin order**, authorising distribution as if Ursula **died before Tomasz**; if she reappears she can recover from the recipients but **not from the executors**. Option A is the tempting distractor, but **s.27 notices** protect only against claims the PRs **do not know about**, and they know of Ursula's entitlement. The end of the **executor's year** (option B) only means the PRs cannot be forced to distribute earlier, and options D and E give no protection without a court order."},{"t":"administration","p":"FLK2","q":"Xander's will leaves \"my house to my son Vernon\" and the residue of his estate to his daughter Wren. The will says nothing about debts. At his death the house is worth £400,000 and is subject to a mortgage of £150,000. The rest of his estate is worth £300,000. He also owed £20,000 on credit cards, and his reasonable funeral expenses were £5,000. Ignore inheritance tax and administration expenses.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Vernon and Wren will receive?","o":["Vernon takes the house free of the mortgage, and Wren receives £125,000.","Vernon takes the house subject to the mortgage, and Wren receives £275,000.","Vernon takes the house subject to the mortgage and a share of the other debts, and Wren receives about £284,000.","Vernon takes the house free of the mortgage, and Wren receives £150,000 because the other debts are paid from the house.","Vernon takes the house subject to the mortgage, and Wren receives £300,000 because a specific gift bears the estate's debts."],"a":1,"e":"A **secured debt** is paid primarily from the **property charged** with it (**Administration of Estates Act 1925 s.35**), unless the will shows a contrary intention, so Vernon takes the house **subject to the £150,000 mortgage**. **Unsecured debts and funeral expenses** are paid in the statutory order, which uses **residue** before property specifically given, so Wren's £300,000 bears the £25,000, leaving **£275,000**. Option A would be right only if the will gave the house \"free of mortgage\", and options C and E wrongly put the unsecured debts on the specific gift."},{"t":"family-provision","p":"FLK2","q":"Zainab lived with her partner, Ahmed, as if they were married, in his flat for two years and five months. They never married. For the last seven weeks of his life Ahmed was in hospital, and Zainab visited him daily while continuing to live in the flat. Ahmed's will, made before they met, leaves his entire estate to his sister. Probate was granted two months ago. Zainab has a modest income and nowhere else to live.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Zainab's position under the Inheritance (Provision for Family and Dependants) Act 1975?","o":["She cannot apply as a cohabitant, because she and Ahmed were not living in the same household when he died.","She can apply as a cohabitant, and the court may award her such provision as is reasonable whether or not it is needed for her maintenance.","She cannot apply, because her claim had to be made within six months of Ahmed's death and the time has nearly run out.","She can apply as a cohabitant, but only for such provision as is reasonable for her maintenance.","She can apply only if she shows that Ahmed was paying a substantial part of her living costs immediately before he died."],"a":3,"e":"A **cohabitant** can apply if they lived with the deceased in the **same household as if married** for the **whole of the two years immediately before the death**, and a temporary absence such as a **hospital stay** does not end the household, so option A is wrong and option E is unnecessary. Only spouses and civil partners get the generous **surviving spouse standard** (option B); a cohabitant is limited to the **maintenance standard**. The time limit is **six months from the grant**, not death, so she is in time (option C)."},{"t":"iht-lifetime","p":"FLK2","q":"In May 2020 Rosalind settled £150,000 on a discretionary trust for her wider family; after deducting annual exemptions, this was a chargeable transfer of £144,000, on which no tax was payable. In January 2027 she transferred quoted shares worth £200,000 to the trustees of a second discretionary trust, agreeing to bear any inheritance tax on that transfer herself. Her annual exemptions for 2025/26 and 2026/27 are unused, and she has made no other lifetime gifts. Assume a nil rate band of £325,000, an annual exemption of £3,000 and a lifetime rate of 20%.\n\nHow much lifetime inheritance tax is payable on the January 2027 transfer?","o":["£3,250","£2,600","Nil","£4,750","£4,000"],"a":0,"e":"The 2020 settlement is a chargeable transfer made within the **seven years** before the new one, so it is **cumulated** and leaves only £181,000 of the nil rate band. The shares less two annual exemptions give £194,000, which is £13,000 above the available band, and because **the settlor bears the tax** the transfer is **grossed up**, so the tax is **20/80 (25%)** of the excess: **£3,250**. Option B (£2,600) is the tax if the trustees paid, option C forgets **cumulation** with the 2020 trust, and options D and E ignore both or one of the annual exemptions."},{"t":"iht-lifetime","p":"FLK2","q":"In 2017 Celestine, a widow, transferred her house, then worth £300,000, into the sole name of her son Dmitri as an outright gift. She continued to live there alone, rent-free, until her death in October 2026. Her circumstances did not change after the gift. At her death the house was worth £450,000. She made no other lifetime gifts. Her will leaves her estate to her daughter.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the inheritance tax treatment of the house on Celestine's death?","o":["It is ignored, because the gift to Dmitri was a potentially exempt transfer made more than seven years before her death.","It is taxed as a failed potentially exempt transfer of £300,000, because she continued to benefit from the house.","It is treated as part of her estate at £300,000, its value when she gave it to Dmitri, with tax paid by her PRs.","It is ignored, because Dmitri became the legal owner of the house in 2017 and it passed to him outside her estate.","It is treated as part of her estate at £450,000, its value at her death, with Dmitri primarily liable for the tax on it."],"a":4,"e":"Because Celestine kept living in the house rent-free, the gift was a **gift with reservation of benefit**, so the house is **treated as still part of her estate** at death and taxed at its **value at death** (**Finance Act 1986 s.102**). The **donee**, Dmitri, is primarily liable for the tax on it, with the PRs liable only if it is unpaid. Options A and D are the tempting traps: surviving seven years makes the PET itself exempt, but does not take reserved property out of the estate. Options B and C use the value at the date of the gift, which is wrong for property caught by the reservation rules."},{"t":"iht-death-reliefs","p":"FLK2","q":"Emeka, who never married, died in August 2026. His net estate, after debts and funeral expenses, is worth £900,000. He leaves his home, worth £400,000 with no mortgage, which he lived in until his death, to his stepdaughter Fola, and the rest of his estate to his brother. In 2023 he gave his nephew £100,000, which after deducting annual exemptions was a potentially exempt transfer of £94,000; he made no other lifetime gifts. Assume a nil rate band of £325,000, a residence nil rate band of £175,000 and a death rate of 40%.\n\nHow much inheritance tax is payable on Emeka's death estate?","o":["£267,600","£160,000","£197,600","£235,200","£230,000"],"a":2,"e":"The failed PET of £94,000 uses part of the nil rate band, leaving **£231,000** for the death estate. The home passes to a **stepchild**, who counts as a **direct descendant**, so the full **residence nil rate band** of £175,000 applies, and the RNRB is **not reduced by lifetime gifts**. Tax is (£900,000 − £231,000 − £175,000) × 40% = **£197,600**. Option A wrongly denies the RNRB to a stepchild, option B ignores the lifetime gift, and option D reduces the RNRB by the gift as well, and option E ignores both the gift and the RNRB."},{"t":"iht-death-reliefs","p":"FLK2","q":"Farida's brother died in February 2024, leaving her £300,000. Inheritance tax was charged on his estate, including on the gift to her. Farida died in May 2026, about two years and three months later, leaving her estate to her partner, whom she never married. Inheritance tax is payable on Farida's estate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of quick succession relief on Farida's estate?","o":["No relief is available, because the inheritance tax on the gift to Farida was paid by her brother's estate, not by her.","Her estate's taxable value is reduced by 60% of the £300,000 she inherited from her brother.","The tax on her estate is reduced by 80% of the tax charged on the earlier transfer, because she died within three years.","The tax on her estate is reduced by 60% of the earlier tax attributable to the net increase in her estate.","No relief is available, because quick succession relief applies only where the second death occurs within two years."],"a":3,"e":"**Quick succession relief** (**IHTA 1984 s.141**) applies where the deceased's estate was increased by a chargeable transfer within **five years** before death. It reduces the **tax** (not the value) on the later death by a percentage of the tax charged on the earlier transfer that is **attributable to the net increase** in the deceased's estate: **60%** where the later death is **2 to 3 years** after the earlier transfer. Option B applies the percentage to the value inherited, and option C uses the 80% band, which is for deaths between 1 and 2 years. Option A is wrong because the relief does not depend on who paid the earlier tax, and option E confuses the bands with the five-year limit."},{"t":"income-cgt-administration","p":"FLK2","q":"Gwendolyn died in 2026. Her son Ravi had lived with her in her house as his only home for ten years before her death and continued to live there afterwards. Her will leaves her residuary estate, which includes the house, 80% to Ravi and 20% to her daughter Sunita. The PRs sold the house eight months after the death for £40,000 more than its probate value, after allowable costs.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the capital gains tax position on the sale of the house?","o":["The PRs can claim main residence relief, as Ravi lived there before and after the death and takes at least 75% of the proceeds.","No capital gains tax can arise, because the house was Gwendolyn's main residence and death is not a disposal for capital gains tax.","The PRs must pay capital gains tax on the £40,000 gain, less their annual exempt amount, because main residence relief applies only to individuals.","Ravi must pay capital gains tax on 80% of the gain and Sunita on 20%, because they were entitled to the residue when the house was sold.","The PRs can claim main residence relief only on 80% of the gain, reflecting Ravi's share, and must pay tax on the rest."],"a":0,"e":"The PRs are taxed on any gain **since death**, so option B is wrong; but under **TCGA 1992 s.225A** they can claim **main residence relief** if, both immediately before and after the death, the house was the only or main residence of individuals entitled to **75% or more** of the net proceeds. Ravi meets both conditions with an 80% entitlement, so the **whole gain** is exempt, not just his share (option E). Option D is wrong because a sale by the PRs during the administration is their disposal, not the beneficiaries'."}]};
;
window.SQE_MOCKS={"blp":[{"t":"business-structures","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Asif subscribed for 2,000 £1 ordinary shares in Fernhill Ltd at an issue price of £2.50 per share. He has so far paid £3,000 towards them, and the company has not called for the balance. Fernhill Ltd has now gone into insolvent liquidation owing its creditors £400,000.\n\nWhat is the maximum amount the liquidator can require Asif to contribute as a shareholder?","o":["Nothing, because he has already paid more than the nominal value of his shares.","£5,000","£400,000, less any amount recovered from the other shareholders.","£2,000","£3,000, being the premium element of the issue price."],"a":3,"e":"A shareholder's liability is **limited to the amount unpaid on his shares**, which includes any **premium** he agreed to pay, not just the nominal value. Asif agreed to pay £5,000 (2,000 × £2.50) and has paid £3,000, so he can be required to pay the remaining **£2,000** and nothing more towards the company's debts. It is the **company**, not the shareholder, that is liable for its own debts without limit."},{"t":"business-structures","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Ravi is the sole director and shareholder of Ravi's Print Ltd. When the company opened a bank overdraft, Ravi signed a personal guarantee of the overdraft in favour of Eastgate Bank plc. He gave no guarantee to the company's paper supplier, Pulp Supplies Ltd. The company has ceased trading owing £60,000 on the overdraft and £25,000 to Pulp Supplies Ltd, and has no assets. There is no suggestion of fraud or wrongful trading.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ravi's personal liability?","o":["He is liable to both creditors, because as sole shareholder and director the court will pierce the corporate veil.","He is liable to neither creditor, because the company is a separate legal person and his liability is limited.","He is liable to Eastgate Bank plc under his guarantee, but not to Pulp Supplies Ltd.","He is liable to Pulp Supplies Ltd but not to the bank, because the bank should have taken security over the company's assets.","He is liable to both creditors, because limited liability does not protect the only shareholder of a company."],"a":2,"e":"Ravi's Print Ltd is a **separate legal person** whose debts are its own, and owning all the shares and being sole director does not justify **piercing the veil** (Salomon). Ravi is liable to the bank because of a **separate contract**, his **personal guarantee**, not because limited liability has been lost. Without a guarantee or a statutory claim such as wrongful trading, the supplier has no claim against him."},{"t":"business-structures","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Cobalt Holdings plc was incorporated as a public company last month. Before Companies House issued it with a trading certificate, its directors signed a £90,000 contract with Vega Fit-Outs Ltd for the refurbishment of its offices. Vega completed the work, but the company has failed to pay despite Vega's written demand for payment four weeks ago.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Vega's position?","o":["The contract is void because the company had no trading certificate, so Vega can recover only a reasonable sum for its work.","The contract is valid, but Vega can claim only against the company, because the directors acted as its agents.","The contract is valid, and because the company has failed to pay within 21 days of being called on to do so, the directors are jointly and severally liable to indemnify Vega.","The shareholders are personally liable to Vega, because the company was not entitled to do business.","The contract binds the company only if it ratifies the contract after obtaining its trading certificate."],"a":2,"e":"A company **formed as a plc** must not do business or borrow until it has a **trading certificate** (CA 2006 s.761). If it does, the transaction **remains valid**, but if the company fails to meet its obligations within **21 days** of being called on, the **directors are jointly and severally liable** to indemnify the other party (s.767). The shareholders' limited liability is unaffected, and no ratification is needed because the contract was never void."},{"t":"formation","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"On 2 March, Hannah signed a three-year equipment lease with Ardent Leasing Ltd 'for and on behalf of Hannah's Kitchens Ltd'. The company was incorporated on 9 March. At its first board meeting the company resolved to 'adopt and ratify' the lease, and it made two monthly payments. It has now stopped paying, and Ardent Leasing Ltd has sued Hannah personally. Ardent has never agreed to release Hannah.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Hannah's position?","o":["Hannah is not liable, because signing expressly on behalf of the company amounts to an agreement excluding her personal liability.","Hannah is not liable, because the board resolution ratifying the lease transferred liability to the company.","Hannah is personally liable, because the contract took effect as a contract with her and the company could not ratify a contract made before it existed.","Hannah is not liable, because the company's two payments amounted to a novation releasing her.","Hannah is liable only for payments that fell due before the company was incorporated."],"a":2,"e":"Under **CA 2006 s.51**, a contract made for a company **before incorporation** takes effect as a contract with the **person who made it**, who is **personally liable** unless there is a clear agreement to the contrary; signing 'for and on behalf of' the company is not enough (Phonogram v Lane). A company **cannot ratify** a contract made before it existed. Liability shifts only through a **novation**, which needs Ardent's agreement to release Hannah."},{"t":"formation","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"On 3 March, the shareholders of Tidewater Ltd passed a special resolution to change its name to Seabright Ltd. The resolution and form NM01 were filed on 6 March, and Companies House issued a certificate of incorporation on change of name on 12 March. A customer with a two-year supply contract made with Tidewater Ltd claims that the contract came to an end when the company changed its name.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The change of name took effect on 3 March, and the supply contract continues because the company remains the same legal person.","The change of name took effect on 12 March, and the supply contract must be novated to Seabright Ltd to remain binding.","The change of name took effect on 12 March, and the supply contract continues because the company remains the same legal person.","The change of name took effect on 6 March, and the customer may treat the contract as terminated.","The change of name is invalid, because it required an ordinary resolution and the consent of the company's customers."],"a":2,"e":"A change of name is made by **special resolution** (s.77) and takes effect from the date of the **new certificate of incorporation on change of name** (s.81), here **12 March**. The company remains the **same legal person**, so existing contracts and legal proceedings are unaffected and no novation is needed. Neither the date of the resolution nor the date of filing is the effective date."},{"t":"formation","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Larch Ltd, a private company incorporated in 2019, is reviewing its compliance with the Companies House reforms made under the Economic Crime and Corporate Transparency Act 2023.\n\nWhich of the following statements about Larch Ltd's obligations as at September 2026 is correct?","o":["It may elect to keep its register of members on the central register at Companies House instead of keeping its own.","It must keep its own register of directors at its registered office for inspection by members.","It may use a PO box as its registered office address, provided documents are collected regularly.","It must keep its own register of members, but it no longer keeps its own register of directors or register of people with significant control.","It need not provide Companies House with a registered email address unless it has more than one director."],"a":3,"e":"Since **18 November 2025** companies no longer keep their own registers of **directors**, directors' residential addresses, secretaries or **PSCs**; that information is held only at Companies House. The option to keep the **register of members** centrally was **abolished on 26 January 2026**, so every company must keep its own. Since March 2024 every company must have an **appropriate registered office address** (a PO box is not appropriate) and a **registered email address**."},{"t":"directors","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Nadine is a director of Arcwell Ltd, a private company incorporated in 2015 whose articles say nothing about directors' conflicts of interest. A potential customer offers Nadine a consultancy contract personally, making clear that it will never contract with Arcwell. Nadine discloses the full details to the board. The other three directors, none of whom has any interest in the matter, resolve to authorise her to accept the contract; Nadine does not vote and is not counted in the quorum.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Nadine's position if she takes the contract?","o":["She breaches her duty to avoid conflicts of interest, because the duty applies even where the company could not take the opportunity and the board cannot authorise it.","She does not breach her duty to avoid conflicts of interest, because the matter has been validly authorised by the independent directors.","She does not breach any duty, simply because the customer would never have contracted with Arcwell.","She can take the contract only if the shareholders pass a special resolution authorising it.","She breaches her duty to declare an interest in a proposed transaction with the company."],"a":1,"e":"Under **s.175** it does not matter that the company could not have taken the opportunity itself (Cooley), so that fact alone is no answer. However, in a **private company** formed after 1 October 2008, the **directors may authorise** a conflict unless the constitution prohibits it, provided the conflicted director does **not count in the quorum or vote** (s.175(5)-(6)). **s.177** concerns transactions **with the company**, which this is not."},{"t":"directors","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Owen is the purchasing director of Gristmill Ltd, which has unamended Model Articles. A supplier hoping to win a large contract from the company offers him a free week at a luxury villa for his family, worth £8,000. Owen tells the board, and the other directors pass a board resolution authorising him to accept the holiday.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the board resolution?","o":["It is effective, because the board of a private company may authorise any conflict of interest unless the articles prohibit it.","It is effective, provided that Owen did not vote on the resolution.","It is unnecessary, because the duty not to accept benefits from third parties covers only payments of money, not hospitality.","It is ineffective, because acceptance of a benefit from a third party cannot be authorised by the board; it needs the approval of the shareholders.","It is ineffective, because only the court can authorise a director to accept a benefit from a third party."],"a":3,"e":"**s.176** prohibits a director from accepting a **benefit from a third party** conferred because he is a director, unless acceptance cannot reasonably be regarded as likely to give rise to a conflict; a valuable holiday from a supplier seeking a contract plainly can. Unlike a s.175 conflict, a s.176 benefit **cannot be authorised by the board**: approval must come from the **members** (or the articles). Benefits include gifts and hospitality as well as money."},{"t":"directors","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"The board of Kestrel Ltd approves a new service contract for its managing director, Imran, guaranteeing him employment for four years, with no right for the company to terminate it during that period except for gross misconduct. No resolution of the shareholders is sought. Eighteen months later, the company wishes to end the contract.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The whole service contract is void, so Imran has been working without a contract.","The contract is fully binding for four years, because it was approved by the board.","The guaranteed-term provision is void, and the company can end the contract by giving reasonable notice.","The company can end the contract only by paying Imran his full salary for the remaining term.","The guaranteed term is binding only if the shareholders ratify it by special resolution before the company tries to end it."],"a":2,"e":"A director's service contract with a **guaranteed term of more than 2 years** needs **prior approval by ordinary resolution** of the members (s.188). Without it, the **guaranteed-term provision is void** and the contract is treated as terminable on **reasonable notice** (s.189); the rest of the contract survives. Board approval alone is not enough, and the Act requires prior approval, not later ratification by special resolution."},{"t":"decision-making","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"At 10am on Thursday 1 October 2026, the directors of Pellow Ltd, a private company with unamended Model Articles, send notice by email to all its members of a general meeting to pass an ordinary resolution. The members will not agree to short notice. Assume there are no bank holidays in October 2026.\n\nWhat is the earliest date on which the general meeting can validly be held?","o":["Thursday 15 October 2026","Friday 16 October 2026","Tuesday 20 October 2026","Monday 19 October 2026","Thursday 22 October 2026"],"a":2,"e":"Notice sent electronically is **deemed received 48 hours after it was sent**, ignoring days that are not working days (s.1147), so notice sent on Thursday morning is received on Monday 5 October. A private company general meeting needs **14 clear days' notice** (s.307), and **clear days exclude** both the day of deemed receipt and the day of the meeting (s.360). The 14 clear days therefore run from 6 to 19 October, and the earliest date is **Tuesday 20 October**; 21 days' notice is needed only for a plc AGM."},{"t":"decision-making","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Quayside Ltd, a private company, circulates a written special resolution to amend its articles to all four of its shareholders, each share carrying one vote. Ffion (45%) and Gareth (25%) sign to signify their agreement. Huw (20%) and Iris (10%) do not respond at all. The articles say nothing about the lapse period, and 28 days have now passed since circulation.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the outcome?","o":["The resolution has not been passed and has lapsed, because a special written resolution needs the agreement of at least 75% of the total voting rights of eligible members.","The resolution has been passed, because no shareholder voted against it.","The resolution has been passed, because 100% of the shareholders who responded agreed to it.","The resolution has been passed as an ordinary resolution, because more than 50% of the voting rights agreed.","The resolution has not been passed, because a written resolution to amend the articles must be agreed unanimously."],"a":0,"e":"A **written resolution** is passed only when members holding the required majority of the **total voting rights of all eligible members** have agreed, not a majority of those who respond. A **special** written resolution needs at least **75%**, and Ffion and Gareth hold only 70%; silence by Huw and Iris effectively counts against. The resolution **lapses** if not passed within **28 days** of circulation (s.297), and it cannot be converted into an ordinary resolution."},{"t":"decision-making","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Sorrel Ltd, a private company not associated with any public company, proposes to lend £30,000 to its director, Kieran, so that he can buy a new family car. The other directors support the loan.\n\nWhich of the following is required before the company makes the loan?","o":["A special resolution of the shareholders approving the loan.","An ordinary resolution of the shareholders approving the loan.","A board resolution only, because the loan is below £50,000.","No approval, because loans to directors of private companies are exempt.","A board resolution only, provided Kieran declares his interest under s.177."],"a":1,"e":"A company may not make a **loan to a director** without approval by **ordinary resolution** of the members (s.197). The exceptions cover **minor loans** up to **£10,000** in total and loans of up to **£50,000** to meet **expenditure on company business**; a loan for a personal car fits neither. Without approval the loan is voidable and Kieran must account for any gain and indemnify the company against loss."},{"t":"shareholders-minority","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Petra holds 10% of the shares in Hollin Ltd. She believes that Marcus, a director who holds 30% of the shares, negligently caused the company to lose £200,000 by failing to check a new supplier's credentials. At a properly convened general meeting, the holders of the remaining 60% of the shares, none of whom is connected with Marcus, vote to ratify his negligence; Petra votes against and Marcus's votes are disregarded. Petra then applies for permission to continue a derivative claim against Marcus.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court will deal with the application?","o":["The court may give permission, and any damages recovered will be paid to Petra in proportion to her shareholding.","The court must give permission if Petra shows a prima facie case, because negligence cannot be ratified.","The court may give permission only if Petra first obtains the consent of the board.","The court must refuse permission, because the negligence has been validly ratified.","The court must refuse permission, because a shareholder can never bring a claim for a loss suffered by the company."],"a":3,"e":"The members can **ratify** a director's negligence by **ordinary resolution**, disregarding the votes of the director and any connected persons (s.239); here the resolution passes on the independent 60%. Where the act has been **ratified**, the court **must refuse** permission to continue a **derivative claim** (s.263(2)). If a derivative claim did proceed, any recovery would belong to the **company**, not to Petra."},{"t":"shareholders-minority","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Bernard held 30% of the shares in Garnet Ltd, which has unamended Model Articles. He died in July, and his executor, Celia, has produced the grant of probate to the company but has not yet asked to be registered as a shareholder. The company has called a general meeting for next week to pass a special resolution, and Celia wants to attend and vote Bernard's shares against it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Celia's position?","o":["She can vote the shares at the meeting, because they passed to her automatically on Bernard's death.","She can vote the shares, because producing the grant of probate makes her a member of the company.","She can never be registered as holder of the shares and can only transfer them to a beneficiary.","The shares were cancelled on Bernard's death, so no votes can be cast in respect of them.","She cannot attend and vote in respect of the shares until she is registered as their holder, although she is entitled to any dividend paid on them."],"a":4,"e":"On death, shares pass by **transmission** to the **personal representatives**. Under the **Model Articles** (arts 27-29) a transmittee may choose to **become the registered holder** or to **transfer** the shares, subject to the directors' power to refuse registration, but until registered she has the holder's rights such as dividends **without** the right to **attend or vote** at general meetings (art.27(3)). Membership depends on **entry in the register of members**, not on the grant of probate."},{"t":"shareholders-minority","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Linnet Ltd has 10,000 preference shares in issue carrying a fixed dividend of 7% a year. To reduce the dividend to 4%, the company obtains the written consent of the holders of 8,000 of the preference shares. Rhiannon, who holds the other 2,000 preference shares, did not consent. The articles contain no provision on varying class rights.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Rhiannon's position?","o":["The variation is invalid, because varying class rights requires the consent of every holder of that class.","The variation is effective, and she has no remedy because the holders of at least 75% of the class consented.","The variation is invalid, because it also requires a special resolution of the ordinary shareholders.","The variation is effective, but she may apply to the court within 21 days to have it cancelled, because she holds at least 15% of the class and did not consent.","The variation is effective, but she may require the company to buy her preference shares at their nominal value."],"a":3,"e":"Class rights may be varied with the **written consent of holders of at least 75%** in nominal value of the class, or a special resolution at a separate class meeting (s.630), so the 80% consent is enough. But holders of **at least 15%** of the class who did not consent may apply to court within **21 days** to have the variation **cancelled** (s.633); Rhiannon holds 20%. Unanimity is not required, and there is no statutory right to be bought out."},{"t":"partnerships","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Frank retired from Oakfield Builders, a general partnership, on 31 March. The firm placed a notice of his retirement in the London Gazette in April and wrote to its existing customers and suppliers telling them he had left, but overlooked Timber Direct Ltd, a long-standing supplier. In June the firm bought materials on credit from Timber Direct Ltd and from Brickworks Ltd, a supplier it had never dealt with before and which had never heard of Frank. The firm cannot pay either debt.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Frank's liability for the June debts?","o":["He is liable to neither supplier, because the Gazette notice was notice to everyone.","He is liable to both suppliers, because a retiring partner remains liable until the firm's creditors agree to release him.","He is liable to Brickworks Ltd only, because Timber Direct Ltd should have checked the Gazette.","He is liable to neither supplier, because the debts were incurred after he retired.","He is liable to Timber Direct Ltd, but not to Brickworks Ltd."],"a":4,"e":"Under **PA 1890 s.36**, anyone who **dealt with the firm before** a change in its membership may treat apparent partners as still partners until they receive **actual notice**; a Gazette notice protects a retiring partner only against those who had **not** dealt with the firm before. Timber Direct Ltd had no actual notice, so Frank is liable to it, but Brickworks Ltd had never dealt with the firm or known him as a partner, so it cannot hold him liable (s.36(3)). The need for a **novation** concerns debts incurred **before** retirement (s.17)."},{"t":"partnerships","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Gail, Hamid, Isla and Jon have run a design business as a partnership for six years, with no written agreement and no fixed term. Gail, Hamid and Isla are unhappy with Jon's conduct and want to expel him while continuing the business together.\n\nWhich of the following best advises them?","o":["They can expel Jon by majority decision, because ordinary matters connected with the business are decided by a majority.","They can expel Jon only by court order, which will be granted automatically following a majority vote.","They can neither expel Jon nor end the partnership without his consent, because any change to the partnership needs unanimity.","They can expel Jon by giving him reasonable notice, because the partnership has lasted more than five years.","They cannot expel Jon, because no express power of expulsion has been agreed, but any partner may dissolve the partnership by notice because it is a partnership at will."],"a":4,"e":"Under **PA 1890 s.25**, a majority **cannot expel** a partner unless an **express power** has been agreed. Because there is **no fixed term**, it is a **partnership at will**, which **any partner** may dissolve by notice (ss.26 and 32(c)); the other three could then carry on the business in a new partnership. Majority decisions are limited to ordinary matters, and dissolution by the court under s.35 is discretionary, not automatic."},{"t":"partnerships","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Kellaway LLP has gone into insolvent liquidation owing £500,000 to trade creditors. Eighteen months before the winding up began, Rhys, a member, withdrew £80,000 from the LLP when he knew that it was unable to pay its debts. One of the trade creditors wants Rhys to pay its debt personally.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The trade creditor cannot sue Rhys for the LLP's debt, but the liquidator can apply to the court for Rhys to contribute in respect of the withdrawal.","The trade creditor can sue Rhys personally, because each member of an LLP is an agent of the other members.","The trade creditor can sue Rhys personally for the whole debt, because he withdrew money while the LLP was insolvent.","Neither the trade creditor nor the liquidator has any claim against Rhys, because LLP members have limited liability.","The liquidator can recover the withdrawal only if it was made within 6 months before the winding up."],"a":0,"e":"An **LLP** is a separate legal person liable for its own debts; members are **agents of the LLP**, not of each other, and are not personally liable to its creditors. However, under **IA 1986 s.214A** the court can order a member to contribute where he made **withdrawals within 2 years** before the winding up while knowing or having reasonable grounds to believe the LLP was, or would become, unable to pay its debts. Any recovery goes into the LLP's assets for its creditors generally."},{"t":"equity-finance","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Willowmere Ltd was incorporated in 2016 with unamended Model Articles and has one class of ordinary shares. It has agreed to buy a warehouse from Ashok, who is not a shareholder or director, in exchange for the company allotting him 50,000 new ordinary shares. The existing shareholders are content with the deal.\n\nWhich shareholder resolution(s) must the company pass before the directors allot the shares?","o":["None, because the directors have authority to allot under s.550 and pre-emption rights do not apply to an allotment for non-cash consideration.","An ordinary resolution under s.551 giving the directors authority to allot.","A special resolution disapplying pre-emption rights.","An ordinary resolution giving authority to allot and a special resolution disapplying pre-emption rights.","A special resolution amending the articles to permit an allotment to a non-member."],"a":0,"e":"In a **private company with one class of shares**, the directors may allot more shares of that class under **s.550** unless the articles prohibit it, and the Model Articles do not. **Statutory pre-emption** under s.561 applies only to allotments of equity securities **wholly for cash**, so an allotment in exchange for the warehouse is outside it. The board can simply resolve to allot and then file form **SH01** within one month."},{"t":"equity-finance","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Brackley Ltd, a private company, wishes to buy back all the shares held by Elaine, who owns 40% of its shares and is retiring. The purchase will be funded from distributable profits. At a general meeting to approve the buyback contract, Elaine (40%) and Ben (25%) vote in favour and Colin (35%) votes against. Each share carries one vote and the vote is taken on a poll.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the outcome?","o":["The resolution is passed, because 65% of the votes cast were in favour.","The resolution is not effective, because it would not have been passed without Elaine's votes.","The resolution is not effective, because approval of a buyback contract requires a special resolution.","The resolution is passed, because Elaine remains a member until completion and is entitled to vote.","The resolution is not effective, because a buyback contract can be approved only by written resolution."],"a":1,"e":"An off-market buyback contract must be approved in advance by **ordinary resolution** (s.694). At a meeting, the resolution is **not effective if it would not have been passed without the seller's votes** on the shares being bought (s.695(3)). Leaving out Elaine's 40%, Ben's 25% in favour is outweighed by Colin's 35% against, so the approval fails."},{"t":"equity-finance","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Dorian subscribed for 20% of the shares in Elmstead Ltd, an unquoted trading company, four years ago at £1 per share, and has been a director throughout. He is retiring, and the company has agreed to buy back all his shares for £6 per share. The buyback is for the benefit of the company's trade, and Dorian is UK resident.\n\nHow will the buyback payment be taxed in Dorian's hands?","o":["Under capital gains tax, because the buyback benefits the company's trade and he is selling his entire holding.","As a distribution, so the amount above the £1 per share he subscribed is taxed as dividend income.","Under capital gains tax at the business asset disposal relief rate, because he has been a director holding at least 5% for more than 2 years.","As employment income, because he is a retiring director.","It is not taxable, because a buyback is a return of the shareholder's own capital."],"a":1,"e":"A buyback payment is treated as a **distribution**, taxed as **dividend income** on the excess over the original subscription price, unless **all** the conditions for **capital treatment** are met. One condition is that the seller has **owned the shares for at least 5 years**; Dorian has held them for only four, so capital treatment (and with it CGT and any BADR) is unavailable even though the trade-benefit and reduction-in-interest conditions are met. Meeting the BADR conditions does not by itself displace income treatment."},{"t":"debt-security","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"On 1 February, Holm Ltd granted Northway Bank plc a floating charge over all its assets, containing a negative pledge prohibiting any later charge ranking ahead of it. On 1 May, Holm Ltd granted Castle Finance Ltd a fixed charge over its factory. Before lending, Castle Finance's loan officer read the Northway debenture, including the negative pledge. Both charges were registered within 21 days. Holm Ltd is now in liquidation.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the priority of the two charges over the factory?","o":["Castle Finance Ltd ranks first, because a fixed charge always takes priority over an earlier floating charge.","Castle Finance Ltd ranks first, because a negative pledge is void as an unlawful restriction on the company's power to borrow.","Castle Finance Ltd ranks first, because the floating charge did not crystallise until the liquidation.","The two charges rank equally and share the proceeds of the factory rateably.","Northway Bank plc ranks first, because Castle Finance Ltd had actual notice of the negative pledge."],"a":4,"e":"A **later fixed charge** normally takes priority over an **earlier floating charge**, because the floating charge leaves the company free to deal with its assets, including by granting fixed charges. The exception is where the floating charge contains a **negative pledge** and the later chargee has **actual notice** of it, as Castle Finance did, so Northway keeps priority. Crystallisation on liquidation does not alter the priority already fixed by these rules."},{"t":"debt-security","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Juniper Ltd's debenture in favour of Ridge Bank plc grants a 'fixed charge' over the company's book debts. Juniper must pay all money collected from its customers into its ordinary current account with Ridge Bank, from which it is free to make payments for its business without the bank's consent. Juniper Ltd has gone into insolvent liquidation.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the bank's charge over the book debts?","o":["It is a fixed charge, because the debenture describes it as fixed and the parties' label is decisive.","It is a fixed charge, because the money must be paid into an account with the chargee bank.","It is a floating charge, but it ranks ahead of the preferential creditors because it was created first.","It is void, because a charge over book debts can never be a fixed charge.","It is a floating charge, so the book debts will be applied to the liquidation expenses, the preferential creditors and the prescribed part before the bank is paid."],"a":4,"e":"Whether a charge is fixed or floating depends on its **substance**, especially the **lender's control** over the assets, not the label. Because Juniper can **freely use** the proceeds of its book debts, the charge is **floating** (Spectrum Plus); a fixed charge would need a **blocked account**. Floating charge assets are applied first to **expenses**, **preferential creditors** and the **prescribed part**."},{"t":"distributions-accounts","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"The directors of Hazel Ltd, which has unamended Model Articles and ample distributable profits, recommend a final dividend of £20,000. At the general meeting, the shareholders want to declare a final dividend of £35,000 instead.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The shareholders can declare a final dividend of no more than £20,000, the amount recommended by the directors.","The shareholders can declare £35,000 by ordinary resolution, because declaring a final dividend is a matter for the members.","The shareholders can declare £35,000, but only by special resolution.","The shareholders cannot declare any dividend, because final dividends are decided by the directors alone.","The shareholders can declare £35,000 provided it does not exceed the company's distributable profits."],"a":0,"e":"Under **Model Articles art.30**, a **final dividend** is **recommended by the directors** and **declared by ordinary resolution**, but the members **cannot declare more than the amount recommended**; they may declare £20,000, a smaller sum or nothing. Having enough distributable profits is necessary but not sufficient. It is **interim** dividends that the directors decide alone."},{"t":"distributions-accounts","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Quince Ltd's shareholders are its two directors, Sam and Tara (40% each), and an outside investor, Uma (20%), who takes no part in management and knows nothing of the company's finances. On their accountant's advice, Sam and Tara declared and paid a dividend at a time when, as they knew, the company had accumulated realised losses and no distributable profits; they did not realise that this made the dividend unlawful. The company is now in liquidation.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the liability to repay the dividend?","o":["None of the shareholders must repay, because Sam and Tara did not know the law and relied on professional advice.","All three shareholders must repay the dividends they received, because an unlawful dividend is void.","Only Uma must repay, because directors who act on an accountant's advice are automatically excused.","Sam and Tara must repay the dividends they received and are liable as directors for the whole dividend, but Uma does not have to repay hers.","Sam and Tara must repay only the dividends they received, and they have no further liability as directors."],"a":3,"e":"A shareholder must repay an unlawful distribution if he **knew or had reasonable grounds to believe** it contravened the rules (s.847); knowing the **facts** is enough and **ignorance of the law is no defence** (It's a Wrap v Gula). Uma had no such knowledge or grounds, so she keeps her dividend. As **directors**, Sam and Tara are also **jointly and severally liable** to repay the whole unlawful distribution, subject to any relief the court may give under s.1157."},{"t":"corporate-insolvency","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"The directors of Pinecroft Ltd, a profitable company with no borrowing, want to close it down because its shareholders are retiring, and to return its surplus assets to the shareholders.\n\nWhich of the following is required to begin the appropriate procedure?","o":["A statutory declaration of solvency by the directors, made within the 5 weeks before a special resolution of the members to wind up voluntarily.","A special resolution of the members, followed by a decision of the creditors appointing a liquidator.","A winding-up petition presented to the court by the directors on the ground that it is just and equitable.","An ordinary resolution of the members, with a statement of affairs prepared by the directors.","A declaration by the directors that the company will pay its debts within 3 years, filed with an ordinary resolution."],"a":0,"e":"A solvent company is closed by a **members' voluntary liquidation**, which needs a **statutory declaration of solvency** by the directors that the company will pay its debts in full, with interest, within a stated period **not exceeding 12 months**, made in the **5 weeks before** the members' **special resolution** to wind up (IA 1986 ss.84 and 89). The **members** then appoint the liquidator. A creditors' decision on the liquidator and a statement of affairs belong to a **creditors' voluntary liquidation**."},{"t":"corporate-insolvency","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Ashby Ltd's creditors vote on a proposed company voluntary arrangement. Creditors holding 78% in value of the debts voted approve it; Fulton Supplies Ltd, an unsecured trade creditor, votes against. Unconnected creditors voting against hold 30% in value of the unconnected creditors' votes. The members approve the CVA. Delmont Bank plc, which holds a fixed charge over the company's premises, has not consented to the CVA's proposed restriction on enforcing its security.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the CVA?","o":["It binds Fulton Supplies Ltd, but it cannot affect Delmont Bank plc's right to enforce its security.","It binds neither Fulton Supplies Ltd nor Delmont Bank plc, because neither agreed to it.","It binds both, because it was approved by more than 75% in value of the creditors voting.","It binds Delmont Bank plc but not Fulton Supplies Ltd, because secured creditors are bound once the members approve.","It has not been approved, because an unsecured creditor voted against it."],"a":0,"e":"A CVA is approved if at least **75% in value** of creditors voting support it and no more than **50% in value of the unconnected creditors** voting oppose it; both tests are met. It then **binds every unsecured creditor entitled to vote**, including those who voted against (s.5). But it **cannot affect a secured creditor's right to enforce its security** (or the priority of preferential debts) **without that creditor's consent** (s.4(3))."},{"t":"corporate-insolvency","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"An administrator has been appointed over Tamworth Glass Ltd. She concludes that she can sell the company's business and assets as a going concern, which would give creditors a much better return than an immediate liquidation, but would leave the company itself as an empty shell.\n\nIn which circumstances may the administrator pursue the sale?","o":["Only if all of the company's secured creditors agree to the sale in writing.","Whenever the sale would produce a better result for the secured creditors than rescuing the company.","Only if the creditors first approve her proposals at a decision procedure held within 2 weeks of her appointment.","In any case, because the administrator's main objective is to realise property for the floating charge holder.","Only if she thinks that rescuing the company as a going concern is not reasonably practicable, or that the sale would achieve a better result for the creditors as a whole than rescuing the company."],"a":4,"e":"An administrator must pursue the statutory objectives **in order**: first **rescuing the company as a going concern**; then, if she thinks rescue is **not reasonably practicable** or that objective (b) would achieve a **better result for the creditors as a whole** than rescue (para.3(3)), achieving a **better result for the creditors as a whole** than a winding up (for example by a going-concern sale of the business); and only then realising property for secured or preferential creditors (Sch.B1 para.3). She acts in the interests of the **creditors as a whole**, not mainly for a floating charge holder."},{"t":"insolvency-clawback-priority","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"In January, the accountant of Ember Ltd told its two directors that the company was insolvent and that there was no realistic prospect of recovery. The directors, honestly believing that a large new order might turn things round, continued to trade and take credit from suppliers for a further nine months without taking advice from an insolvency practitioner. The company then went into insolvent liquidation with a much larger deficiency than in January.\n\nWhich claim is the liquidator most likely to bring against the directors?","o":["Fraudulent trading, seeking an order that the directors contribute to the company's assets.","Wrongful trading, seeking an order that the directors contribute to the company's assets.","An application to pierce the corporate veil so that the directors become liable for all the company's debts.","A claim that the company gave a preference to the suppliers who gave credit.","A claim that buying goods on credit was a transaction at an undervalue."],"a":1,"e":"**Wrongful trading** (IA 1986 s.214) applies where a director **knew or ought to have concluded** that there was **no reasonable prospect** of avoiding insolvent liquidation and did not take **every step** to minimise loss to creditors; **no dishonesty** is needed, so honest optimism is no defence. **Fraudulent trading** requires **intent to defraud**, which the facts do not show. The remedy is a **contribution** to the company's assets, not veil piercing."},{"t":"insolvency-clawback-priority","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Larkspur Ltd is in insolvent liquidation. Its bank holds a floating charge created in 2022 and is owed £500,000. After payment of the liquidation expenses and the preferential creditors, £250,000 of floating charge realisations remain.\n\nHow much must be set aside for the unsecured creditors as the prescribed part?","o":["£50,000","£53,000","£125,000","£5,000","£48,000"],"a":1,"e":"The **prescribed part** (IA 1986 s.176A) is **50% of the first £10,000** of the net floating charge realisations plus **20% of the remainder**: £5,000 + 20% of £240,000 (£48,000) = **£53,000**. The cap of **£800,000** for floating charges created on or after 6 April 2020 is not reached. The bank takes the remaining £197,000 towards its debt and cannot share in the prescribed part for its shortfall."},{"t":"insolvency-clawback-priority","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Four months before Grebe Ltd went into insolvent liquidation, and while it was unable to pay its debts, it paid £30,000 of arrears in full to Halcyon Components Ltd, its only supplier of a key part. Halcyon, which is not connected with Grebe, had refused to make further deliveries unless the arrears were cleared, and the directors paid solely so that the company could keep trading. Other creditors remain unpaid.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the payment can be set aside as a preference?","o":["It is a preference, because it was made within 6 months of the liquidation while the company was insolvent and put Halcyon in a better position.","It is a preference, because a desire to prefer is presumed whenever a creditor is paid in full shortly before liquidation.","It is not a preference, because a payment to an unconnected creditor can be challenged only if made within 3 months.","It is a transaction at an undervalue, because the company received nothing in return for the payment.","It is unlikely to be a preference, because the company was influenced by commercial pressure rather than by a desire to improve Halcyon's position."],"a":4,"e":"A preference (IA 1986 s.239) can be set aside only if the company was **influenced by a desire** to improve the creditor's position. Where it acts under **genuine commercial pressure**, such as paying to keep a vital supplier, there is no such desire (Re MC Bacon). The **6-month** time limit for unconnected creditors is met but is not enough on its own, desire is **presumed** only for **connected** persons, and paying a genuine debt is not a transaction at an undervalue."},{"t":"personal-insolvency","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Declan was made bankrupt on his own application in February 2026. In September 2026, before his discharge, his aunt died and left him £40,000. He notified his trustee in bankruptcy within 21 days.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position of the inheritance?","o":["It belongs to Declan, because only property he owned when the bankruptcy order was made forms part of his estate.","It vests in the trustee automatically, without any notice, because it was received before discharge.","The trustee can claim it for the bankruptcy estate by giving written notice within 42 days of learning of it.","It belongs to Declan unless the trustee obtains an income payments order before his discharge.","It can be claimed by the trustee, but returns to Declan when he is discharged in February 2027."],"a":2,"e":"Property acquired by a bankrupt **after** the bankruptcy order but **before discharge** is **after-acquired property** (IA 1986 s.307). It does not vest automatically, but the trustee can claim it by **written notice within 42 days** of first learning of it. Once claimed it stays in the estate despite discharge, and an income payments order deals with surplus income, not capital such as an inheritance."},{"t":"personal-insolvency","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Imelda is owed £4,200 by Kyle, a self-employed plumber, for unpaid rent on a lock-up garage. The debt is undisputed and unsecured. She served a statutory demand on Kyle five weeks ago, and he has neither paid nor applied to set it aside. No other creditor is taking action against him.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Imelda can present a bankruptcy petition against Kyle?","o":["She can, because Kyle failed to comply with the statutory demand within 3 weeks.","She can, because there is no minimum debt for a creditor's bankruptcy petition.","She cannot, because a creditor must obtain a court judgment before presenting a bankruptcy petition.","She cannot, because the debt is below the bankruptcy level of £5,000.","She can, but only by applying to the Adjudicator rather than to the court."],"a":3,"e":"A creditor's petition requires a **liquidated, unsecured debt** of at least the **bankruptcy level of £5,000**, alone or together with other petitioning creditors (IA 1986 s.267). An unpaid statutory demand shows inability to pay, but it does not cure a debt below the threshold. A judgment is not required, and the **Adjudicator** deals only with a **debtor's own application**, for which there is no minimum debt."},{"t":"income-tax","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Leanne is a director of Moorland Ltd. In 2026/27 her only income is a salary of £30,000 and dividends from the company of £20,000. Assume a personal allowance of £12,570, a basic rate band of £37,700, a dividend allowance of £500, and dividend tax rates of 10.75% (basic rate) and 35.75% (higher rate).\n\nWhat is Leanne's income tax liability on her dividends?","o":["£2,150.00","£1,706.25","£744.98","£6,971.25","£2,096.25"],"a":4,"e":"Income is taxed in the order **non-savings, then savings, then dividends**, and the **personal allowance** is set against non-savings income first, leaving taxable salary of £17,430 and £20,270 of the basic rate band. The first **£500** of dividends is taxed at **0%** (but still uses the band), and the remaining £19,500 falls within the basic rate band at **10.75%**, giving **£2,096.25**. The 8.75% rate applied only before 6 April 2026."},{"t":"income-tax","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Cara and Dan are partners in a consultancy. For the accounting period, the firm's trading profit before any payments to partners is £150,000. The partnership agreement provides that Cara receives a 'salary' of £30,000, that each partner receives interest of 5% a year on capital (Cara has £100,000 of capital and Dan £20,000), and that the remaining profit is shared 60% to Cara and 40% to Dan.\n\nOn what amount of the firm's profit will Cara be taxed for this period?","o":["£68,400","£103,400","£90,000","£98,400","£75,000"],"a":1,"e":"A partnership is tax transparent: each partner is taxed on their **share of the firm's profit**, and partners' **'salaries' and interest on capital are not deductible expenses** of the firm but part of the profit allocation. Cara is allocated £30,000 + £5,000 interest + 60% of the remaining £114,000 (£68,400) = **£103,400**, and Dan the other £46,600. Profit is shared under the agreement, not equally, because the partners have agreed otherwise."},{"t":"income-tax","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Priyanka's employment with Halberd Ltd is terminated. Her contract allows the company to make a payment in lieu of notice, and it pays her £10,000 under that clause, equal to her pay for the full notice period. It also pays her £45,000 as a genuine non-contractual compensation payment for the loss of her employment.\n\nHow much of these payments is taxable as employment income?","o":["£25,000","£15,000","£55,000","£45,000","Nothing"],"a":0,"e":"A genuine **non-contractual** compensation payment for loss of employment is **tax-free up to £30,000**, with the excess taxed as earnings, so £15,000 of the £45,000 is taxable. A **contractual** payment in lieu of notice is taxed **in full** as earnings. The taxable total is £10,000 + £15,000 = **£25,000**."},{"t":"cgt","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Bethan, a sole trader, sells the freehold workshop she has used in her trade for £500,000, realising a chargeable gain of £180,000. Fourteen months later she buys a larger freehold workshop for use in her trade for £420,000, and claims rollover relief.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the claim?","o":["The whole gain of £180,000 is rolled over, giving the new workshop a base cost of £240,000.","No relief is available, because she did not reinvest the whole of the sale proceeds.","£100,000 of the gain is taxed now and £80,000 is rolled over, giving the new workshop a base cost of £340,000.","£80,000 of the gain is taxed now and £100,000 is rolled over, giving the new workshop a base cost of £320,000.","No relief is available, because the new workshop was bought more than 12 months after the sale."],"a":3,"e":"Rollover relief defers a gain on a qualifying asset, such as land and buildings used in the trade, where the proceeds are reinvested in a qualifying asset within **1 year before to 3 years after** the disposal. Where not all the **proceeds** are reinvested, the amount **not reinvested** (£500,000 − £420,000 = £80,000) is taxed now. The remaining £100,000 of the gain is **deducted from the base cost** of the new workshop (£420,000 − £100,000 = **£320,000**)."},{"t":"cgt","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Joan has run a florist's business as a sole trader for ten years. She sells the freehold shop from which she has traded, realising a chargeable gain of £150,000, and moves the business, which she continues to run, to rented premises nearby. She does not intend to buy another property. She is a higher rate taxpayer and has made no other disposals in 2026/27.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the tax treatment of the gain?","o":["Business asset disposal relief is available because she has owned the shop and run the business for more than 2 years, so the gain is taxed at 18%.","The gain is exempt, because the shop was a business asset used in her trade.","The gain is deferred automatically under incorporation relief until she sells the business.","Business asset disposal relief is available, so the gain is taxed at 14%.","Business asset disposal relief is not available, because she has sold an individual asset while continuing the business, so the gain above the annual exempt amount is taxed at 24%."],"a":4,"e":"For a sole trader, **BADR** requires a disposal of the **whole or part of the business** (or of business assets within 3 years after it ceases), not the sale of an **individual asset** while trading continues (McGregor v Adcock). Joan's gain, less the **£3,000 annual exempt amount**, is therefore taxed at **24%** as a higher rate taxpayer. Rollover relief would require reinvestment, which she does not intend, and the BADR rate for 2026/27 disposals is 18%, not 14%."},{"t":"corporation-tax","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Fernlea Ltd has no associated companies. For its 12-month accounting period ending 31 March 2027 its taxable total profits are £100,000. Assume a small profits rate of 19% (profits up to £50,000), a main rate of 25% (profits over £250,000), and marginal relief of 3/200 × (£250,000 − taxable total profits).\n\nWhat is Fernlea Ltd's corporation tax liability?","o":["£25,000","£19,000","£22,750","£22,000","£23,500"],"a":2,"e":"Profits between **£50,000 and £250,000** are taxed at the **main rate of 25% less marginal relief**, not in slices. Tax at 25% is £25,000 and marginal relief is 3/200 × (£250,000 − £100,000) = £2,250, giving **£22,750**. Taxing the first £50,000 at 19% and the rest at 25% (£22,000) is a common error."},{"t":"corporation-tax","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Gannet Ltd is a close company with a 31 March year end. In June 2026 it lent £40,000 to Harriet, a director who owns 60% of its shares, to help her buy a holiday home. The loan is still outstanding on 1 January 2028. Assume the relevant rate for loans made on or after 6 April 2026 is 35.75%.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the tax consequences for the company?","o":["The company must pay tax of £14,300 under s.455, which is a permanent charge that is never refunded.","There is no charge, because loans to participators of up to £50,000 are exempt.","The company must pay tax of £14,300 under s.455, which will be repaid to it if and when Harriet repays the loan.","The company can deduct the £40,000 loan in computing its trading profits.","There is no charge on the company, but Harriet is immediately taxed on £40,000 as dividend income."],"a":2,"e":"A **close company** loan to a **participator** that is still outstanding **9 months** after the end of the accounting period in which it was made triggers a temporary **s.455** charge on the company: 35.75% × £40,000 = **£14,300**. The charge is **refunded** when the loan is repaid, released or written off. Harriet is taxed on the amount as a dividend only if the loan is **written off**, and the £15,000 exception applies only to full-time directors owning no more than 5%."},{"t":"vat-iht-bpr","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Meera opened a bookshop on 1 September 2025. Almost all her sales are of books, which are zero-rated. At the end of July 2026 her sales for the previous 12 months total £93,000. She is not registered for VAT and assumes she need not register because she charges no VAT on books. Assume the VAT registration threshold is £90,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Meera's position?","o":["She must notify HMRC by 30 August 2026 and will be registered from 1 September 2026.","She need not register, because zero-rated supplies do not count towards the registration threshold.","She need not register, because her turnover has not exceeded the threshold in a single tax year.","She must notify HMRC by 30 August 2026 and will be registered from 1 August 2026.","She must register immediately and account for VAT on all her sales since she opened."],"a":0,"e":"**Zero-rated** supplies are **taxable supplies** at 0%, so they **count** towards the **£90,000** threshold, unlike exempt supplies. Under the **historic test**, once taxable turnover for the **previous 12 months** exceeds the threshold at the end of a month, the trader must **notify HMRC within 30 days** and is registered from the **first day of the second month** after that month, here 1 September 2026. The test uses a rolling 12 months, not the tax year."},{"t":"vat-iht-bpr","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Rowena had been one of three equal partners in a trading partnership for 15 years. The partnership agreement provides that on a partner's death the surviving partners must buy, and the deceased partner's personal representatives must sell, the deceased's share at market value. Rowena died in October 2026 owning her partnership share, worth £900,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the availability of business property relief on her partnership share?","o":["100% relief is available, because an interest in a trading business owned for more than 2 years qualifies.","No relief is available, because at her death the share was subject to a binding contract for sale.","50% relief is available, because the share is subject to an agreement for sale at market value.","100% relief is available because the share is worth less than the £2.5 million allowance.","No relief is available, because a partnership share is not relevant business property."],"a":1,"e":"An **interest in a business**, such as a partner's share, normally qualifies for **100% BPR** (within the £2.5m allowance) if owned for **2 years**. But no relief is given where, at the time of the transfer, the property is subject to a **binding contract for sale** (IHTA 1984 s.113), which an automatic buy-sell clause creates. Partnership agreements usually use **options** (cross-options) instead, so that BPR is preserved."}],"dr":[{"t":"dr-options","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Tidewater Marine Ltd and Penrose Engineering Ltd signed a supply contract containing a clause referring all disputes to arbitration in London. Tidewater issued a claim in the High Court for breach of contract. Penrose filed an acknowledgment of service and, two weeks later, filed and served a full defence on the merits. Penrose's new solicitor now wants the court proceedings stopped so the dispute can be arbitrated.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The court is unlikely to grant a stay, because by serving a defence on the merits Penrose has taken a step to answer the substantive claim.","The court will stay the claim provided Penrose applies within 28 days of service of the claim form.","The court will stay the claim only if Tidewater consents, because Tidewater chose to litigate.","The court will refuse a stay because an arbitration clause is enforceable only if it is signed as a separate agreement.","The court must stay the claim, because a stay under s.9 Arbitration Act 1996 is mandatory whenever there is a valid arbitration clause."],"a":0,"e":"A s.9 stay is mandatory only if the applicant acknowledges service and applies **before taking any step to answer the substantive claim**; serving a defence on the merits is such a step, so the right to a stay is lost. The mandatory nature of s.9 does not help a party who has already submitted to the court's process. There is no 28-day window from service and no requirement for the claimant's consent. An arbitration clause in the main contract is valid if in writing; it need not be a separate signed document."},{"t":"dr-costs","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"In a multi-track claim, the claimant applied successfully for an order for specific disclosure. The judge ordered \"claimant's costs in the case\". At trial, the claimant lost and was ordered to pay the defendant's costs of the action.\n\nWhat is the effect of the interim costs order on the costs of the disclosure application?","o":["The claimant recovers its costs of the application whatever the outcome of the trial.","Neither party recovers the costs of that application from the other.","The claimant must pay the defendant's costs of the application as part of the costs of the action.","The defendant must pay the claimant's costs of the application, because the claimant won the application.","The trial judge decides the costs of the application afresh, because the order was in effect \"costs reserved\"."],"a":1,"e":"Under **\"claimant's costs in the case\"** the claimant recovers its costs of the application only if it wins at the end; if it loses, it does **not** pay the defendant's costs of that application. So here each side bears its own costs of the application. Recovery whatever the outcome describes \"costs in any event\"; paying the defendant's costs describes plain \"costs in the case\". \"Costs reserved\" is a different order postponing the decision."},{"t":"dr-disclosure","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Hartwell Ltd and Osprey Foods Ltd exchanged emails marked \"without prejudice\" in an attempt to settle a pending contract claim. Hartwell says that Osprey's last email accepted its offer of £45,000 and that a binding settlement was made. Osprey denies that any settlement was reached and wants the claim to go to trial. Hartwell applies to the court to enforce the alleged settlement.\n\nCan the court look at the without prejudice emails?","o":["No, because without prejudice communications can never be shown to the court unless both parties consent.","Yes, but only after liability has been decided at trial.","Yes, because the label \"without prejudice\" has no legal effect between commercial parties.","No, because only an accepted Part 36 offer can produce a binding settlement once proceedings have started.","Yes, because without prejudice material may be considered to decide whether a settlement was actually reached and on what terms."],"a":4,"e":"Without prejudice communications are normally inadmissible, but an established exception allows the court to look at them to decide **whether a settlement was concluded and its terms**. The protection is not absolute, so a refusal to look at all is wrong, and the court need not wait until after trial. The label does matter where there is a genuine attempt to settle. Any offer, not just a Part 36 offer, can be accepted to form a binding contract of settlement."},{"t":"dr-evidence","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"In a negligence claim, the defendant has served a hearsay notice stating that it will rely on the witness statement of Mr Okafor, an eyewitness who has moved to Australia and will not attend the trial. The claimant's solicitor believes Mr Okafor's account is inaccurate and wants his evidence tested.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate step for the claimant's solicitor to take?","o":["Serve a notice to admit facts no later than 21 days before trial.","Object at trial, when the judge must exclude the statement because Mr Okafor has not been called.","Apply to exclude the statement, because hearsay is inadmissible where the maker is still alive.","Take no action, because the statement cannot be relied on unless Mr Okafor attends trial.","Apply within 14 days of service of the hearsay notice for permission to call Mr Okafor and cross-examine him on his statement."],"a":4,"e":"Hearsay is **admissible** in civil proceedings (Civil Evidence Act 1995 s.1), so it cannot simply be excluded. The other party's protection is to apply for **permission to call the maker for cross-examination within 14 days** of service of the hearsay notice (s.3, CPR 33.4), or to attack credibility on notice. Doing nothing leaves the statement in evidence, with the court only weighing the fact that it was untested. A notice to admit facts is used to get facts admitted, not to challenge a witness."},{"t":"dr-parties-law","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Studio Verde Srl, a design consultancy with its central administration in Milan, agreed to provide branding and marketing design services to Larkspur Retail Ltd, an English company. The contract has no choice of law clause and nothing in its terms or circumstances points to a chosen law. Larkspur has sued Studio Verde in the High Court in London for poor performance.\n\nWhich law is most likely to govern the contract?","o":["Italian law, because it is a contract for the provision of services and the service provider is habitually resident in Italy.","Whichever law the English court considers more appropriate under the forum non conveniens doctrine.","English law, because the services were to be received and paid for in England.","English law, because the claim is being heard by an English court.","English law, because Larkspur is the weaker party and its home-law protections apply."],"a":0,"e":"Under **Rome I Art 4(1)(b)**, with no choice of law a contract for services is governed by the law of the **service provider's habitual residence**, here Italy (place of central administration). Jurisdiction and applicable law are separate questions, so an English court can apply Italian law. Where the services are received is not the test, and the consumer rule in Art 6 does not apply between two businesses. Forum non conveniens concerns which court hears a case, not which law applies."},{"t":"dr-case-management","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Calloway plc is the claimant in a £400,000 multi-track claim. The first case management conference is listed for 30 June. Because of an oversight, Calloway's solicitors file and exchange its costs budget (Precedent H) on 28 June. The defendant filed its budget on time.\n\nWhat is the consequence for Calloway, unless the court orders otherwise?","o":["Calloway's costs will be assessed on the indemnity basis, because the defendant had no time to consider the budget.","Calloway will be limited to fixed recoverable costs for the intermediate track.","Calloway will be treated as having filed a budget comprising only the applicable court fees, and must apply for relief from sanctions.","Calloway's claim will be struck out automatically for failure to comply with a practice direction.","There is no sanction, because a costs budget only has to be filed before the first CMC begins."],"a":2,"e":"For claims above £50,000 the budget must be filed **at least 21 days before the first CMC**; a party that fails to file on time is **treated as having filed a budget comprising only court fees** (CPR 3.14), so it must seek relief under Denton. The sanction does not strike out the claim. Fixed recoverable costs belong to the fast and intermediate tracks, not this multi-track case. The indemnity basis would favour Calloway, not punish it."},{"t":"dr-issue-service","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Before proceedings were issued, Brennan & Co, solicitors for Marlow Developments Ltd, wrote to the claimant's solicitors confirming that they were authorised to accept service of any claim form on Marlow's behalf. The claimant then issued a claim and its process server handed the claim form, with the particulars of claim, to Marlow's managing director at Marlow's head office.\n\nHas the claim form been validly served?","o":["No, because personal service cannot be used on a company, only on an individual.","Yes, because Marlow actually received the claim form and so suffered no prejudice.","Yes, because personal service on a person holding a senior position in a company is always a permitted method.","No, because a company may only be served at its registered office.","No, because the claim form had to be served on Brennan & Co once they had confirmed in writing that they were authorised to accept service."],"a":4,"e":"Where a defendant's solicitor has notified the claimant **in writing** that it is **authorised to accept service**, the claim form **must** be served on the solicitor (CPR 6.7), and service on the defendant itself is invalid. Personal service on a senior officer is normally permitted, which makes it tempting, but CPR 6.7 overrides it. Actual receipt does not cure invalid service. A company can be served at its principal office or registered office and personally through a senior person, so the other two reasons are wrong."},{"t":"dr-interim","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Imogen is about to sue her former business partner, Rhys, for £600,000 diverted from their company. She has strong documentary evidence. She has learned that over the last fortnight Rhys has transferred large sums to accounts overseas and has put his house on the market. She is worried he will move his remaining assets as soon as he hears of the claim.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate step for Imogen to take?","o":["Apply on notice for an interim payment of a reasonable proportion of the likely damages.","Issue the claim and apply for summary judgment before seeking any other order.","Apply without notice for a freezing injunction, supported by an affidavit giving full and frank disclosure, and offer a cross-undertaking in damages.","Apply without notice for a search order permitting entry to Rhys's home.","Apply for an order requiring Rhys to attend court to be questioned about his assets."],"a":2,"e":"A **freezing injunction** stops a respondent dissipating assets; Imogen must show a good arguable case, assets and a **real risk of dissipation**, and it is usually sought **without notice** on **affidavit** with a **cross-undertaking**. An interim payment on notice would alert Rhys and needs one of its specific conditions. A search order preserves evidence, not assets. Summary judgment is too slow to protect the assets, and an order to obtain information is a post-judgment enforcement tool."},{"t":"dr-enforcement","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Grafton Supplies Ltd has a £30,000 judgment against Kemal, a self-employed web designer. Kemal rents his flat, owns no land and has only basic household goods and a laptop. His business current account, in his sole name, regularly holds around £40,000 after customers pay him in the middle of each month.\n\nWhich method of enforcement is most likely to be effective?","o":["A third party debt order against the bank, timed so that the interim order is served when the account is in credit.","An attachment of earnings order directed to Kemal's customers.","A writ of control executed by a High Court Enforcement Officer.","A charging order followed by an order for sale.","A warrant of control executed by County Court bailiffs."],"a":0,"e":"A **third party debt order** captures money owed to the debtor by a third party, typically a **bank account in credit**, and catches the balance **when the interim order is served**, so timing matters. Attachment of earnings is available only against **employed** debtors, not the self-employed. A charging order needs land or securities, which Kemal does not have. Taking control of goods is pointless where the goods are basic household items or exempt tools of trade such as a laptop used for work."},{"t":"dr-trial","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"The trial of a fast track claim is fixed for Monday 9 March. On Thursday 5 March, the claimant's solicitor learns that Lena, a former employee of the claimant and an important witness of fact, has decided she will not come to court. Lena is not a party to the claim.\n\nWhat should the claimant's solicitor do to compel Lena's attendance?","o":["Nothing can be done, because a witness summons can only be issued against a party.","Seek the court's permission to issue a witness summons, because the trial is less than 7 days away, ask for a direction that it binds although served late, and offer Lena conduct money.","Do nothing, because Lena's witness statement will automatically be read as hearsay without notice.","Issue a witness summons without permission; it will be binding if served at least 24 hours before the trial.","Apply for an adjournment, which the court must grant as of right where a witness refuses to attend."],"a":1,"e":"The court's **permission** is needed to issue a witness summons **less than 7 days before the trial**, and a summons is binding only if served **at least 7 days** before attendance unless the court directs a shorter period; **conduct money** must be offered. There is no 24-hour rule. Using the statement as hearsay requires notice and loses the chance to have Lena cross-examined. Adjournments are discretionary, and a summons can be issued against any person, not just parties."},{"t":"dr-statements-of-case","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Holloway, a homeowner, sued Ashdown Builders Ltd for defective roof works. Ashdown filed its defence three weeks ago. Ashdown now realises that the work was done by its roofing subcontractor, Slate & Co, which is not a party, and wants to claim an indemnity from Slate & Co in the same proceedings.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Ashdown needs to do?","o":["Ashdown must ask Holloway to amend the particulars of claim to add Slate & Co as a second defendant.","Ashdown needs Holloway's written consent before it can bring any claim against Slate & Co.","Ashdown must start separate proceedings, because a Part 20 claim cannot be brought against a non-party.","Ashdown needs the court's permission to bring an additional claim against Slate & Co, because it was not issued before or at the same time as the defence.","Ashdown may issue the additional claim without permission, because it is within 28 days of filing its defence."],"a":3,"e":"An **additional claim against a non-party** can be made without permission only if issued **before or at the same time as** the defence; later, the **court's permission** is needed (Part 20). A 28-day period applies only to a contribution notice against a **co-defendant** added later (28 days after that defendant files its defence), not to a non-party. The claimant's consent is not required, and Part 20 exists precisely to bring third parties in. Ashdown cannot force the claimant to sue Slate & Co."},{"t":"dr-responding","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Ferris Ltd's claim form was deemed served on Ingleby Ltd on 2 February 2026. The particulars of claim were served separately and deemed served on 10 February 2026. Ingleby filed an acknowledgment of service on 20 February 2026, stating that it intends to defend the whole claim. There has been no agreement to extend time.\n\nBy what date must Ingleby file its defence?","o":["24 February 2026.","6 March 2026.","10 March 2026.","7 April 2026.","2 March 2026."],"a":2,"e":"Time for a defence runs from **deemed service of the particulars of claim**, not the claim form; where an acknowledgment of service is filed, the defence is due **28 days** after service of the particulars, here 10 March 2026. Counting 28 days from the claim form gives the wrong start date, and 14 days applies only where no acknowledgment of service is filed. Time does not run from the date of the acknowledgment. A further 28 days is available only by agreement notified to the court, which there was not."},{"t":"dr-limitation","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Ruby was born on 10 June 2007. On 3 March 2019, when she was 11, she was injured when a defective climbing frame collapsed at a leisure centre. She knew straight away that she was hurt and who ran the centre. No proceedings have yet been issued on her behalf.\n\nWhat is the last date on which proceedings for her personal injury claim can be issued without relying on the court's discretion?","o":["10 June 2025.","3 March 2025.","10 June 2031.","10 June 2028.","3 March 2022."],"a":3,"e":"Time does not run while a claimant is a **child** (LA 1980 s.28); the **3-year** personal injury period starts on her **18th birthday** (10 June 2025), so she has until her **21st birthday**, 10 June 2028. Counting 3 years from the accident ignores the disability rule, and 10 June 2025 is merely when time starts. 10 June 2031 is her 24th birthday, which is the end of the 6-year period for contract or ordinary tort claims, not personal injury."},{"t":"dr-appeals","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"After a two-day trial in the County Court, the judge gave judgment for the claimant on 2 June. The judge did not direct any different period for appealing. The defendant's solicitors received the sealed order on 16 June. The parties' solicitors then agreed in correspondence that the defendant could file any appellant's notice by 14 July.\n\nBy when must the defendant file its appellant's notice?","o":["By 7 July, being 21 days after the sealed order was received.","By 14 July, because the parties have agreed an extension in writing.","By 16 June, being 14 days after the decision.","By 23 June, being 21 days after the decision, unless the appeal court extends time.","By 30 June, being 28 days after the decision."],"a":3,"e":"The appellant's notice must be filed **within 21 days after the date of the decision** (CPR 52.12) unless the lower court directs otherwise; time runs from the decision, not receipt of the sealed order. The parties **cannot extend** this time by agreement: only the court can. 28 days and 14 days are not the relevant periods (14 days is the time for serving the sealed notice and for a respondent's notice)."},{"t":"dr-options","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Partway through a High Court claim, Ferncliff Ltd and Larch Distribution Ltd agree to settle. The terms include a payment of £120,000, a new three-year supply agreement between them and a 10% discount on future orders. Both companies want the terms kept out of the public court record, but they want to be able to enforce them quickly if either side defaults.\n\nWhich is the most appropriate way to record the settlement?","o":["A notice of discontinuance filed by Ferncliff, with the terms recorded in a letter.","Acceptance of a Part 36 offer, which automatically records all the terms in a court order.","A judgment entered by the court on the terms agreed, after a short trial.","A consent order setting out all the agreed terms in the body of the order.","A Tomlin order staying the claim on the terms set out in a confidential schedule, with liberty to apply."],"a":4,"e":"A **Tomlin order** stays the claim on terms in a **confidential schedule**, with **liberty to apply** to enforce them without a new claim, and suits terms a court could not itself order, such as a new supply agreement. A consent order is enforceable but its terms appear in the public order. Discontinuance would leave Ferncliff liable for costs and the terms enforceable only by a new claim. A Part 36 offer from a defendant must be for a single sum of money, so it cannot carry these terms."},{"t":"dr-costs","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Darius brought a personal injury claim against Halberd Retail Ltd following a fall in one of its shops. At trial, the judge dismissed the claim and found on the balance of probabilities that Darius had grossly exaggerated his injuries, so that the claim was fundamentally dishonest. Darius recovered nothing. The judge ordered him to pay Halberd's costs of £35,000.\n\nCan Halberd enforce the costs order?","o":["Only if the claim was also struck out as an abuse of process.","Yes, with the court's permission, it may enforce the order in full, because qualified one-way costs shifting protection is lost where the claim is fundamentally dishonest.","Yes, but only on the standard basis and only after a detailed assessment lasting more than one day.","Only up to the amount of damages Darius recovered, which is nothing.","No, because a claimant in a personal injury claim can never be made to pay the defendant's costs."],"a":1,"e":"**QOCS** normally limits enforcement of costs against a PI claimant to the total of damages, interest and costs awarded in their favour, but protection is **lost** where the claim is found **fundamentally dishonest**, and the order may then be enforced in full with permission. PI claimants can be ordered to pay costs; QOCS only restricts enforcement. The cap to damages is the normal rule that no longer applies. Strike-out is a separate route to losing protection, not an extra requirement."},{"t":"dr-disclosure","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"In 2024, before any dispute arose, Melford Holdings Ltd asked its accountants for written advice on the tax and legal consequences of a supply contract it was negotiating. Melford is now being sued under that contract, and the accountants' advice is relevant to the issues. Melford wants to withhold it from inspection.\n\nIs the accountants' advice privileged?","o":["Yes, because accountants owe their clients a professional duty of confidence.","Yes, because the advice is confidential and commercially sensitive.","Yes, under legal advice privilege, because the advice concerned the legal consequences of the contract.","No, because legal advice privilege covers only communications with lawyers, and no litigation was in contemplation when the advice was given.","Yes, under litigation privilege, because any commercial contract carries a risk of later disputes."],"a":3,"e":"**Legal advice privilege** protects confidential communications with **lawyers** only; it does not extend to non-lawyers such as accountants, even if they advise on legal matters. **Litigation privilege** requires litigation to be **reasonably in contemplation**, not a mere general risk. Confidentiality and professional duties of confidence are not grounds of privilege, so the advice must be disclosed and made available for inspection."},{"t":"dr-evidence","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Paolo was injured when a car driven by Grant mounted the pavement. Grant was later convicted in the magistrates' court of careless driving arising out of the incident. Paolo has sued Grant in negligence and his particulars of claim plead the conviction. Grant still insists that a sudden mechanical failure, not his driving, caused the accident.\n\nWhat is the effect of the conviction in the civil claim?","o":["The conviction is inadmissible, because it is merely the opinion of the magistrates.","The conviction is conclusive and Grant cannot dispute that he drove carelessly.","Paolo must still prove Grant's negligence beyond reasonable doubt, as the conviction was in a criminal court.","Grant is taken to have committed the offence unless he proves on the balance of probabilities that he did not.","The conviction is admissible only if Grant pleaded guilty in the magistrates' court."],"a":3,"e":"Under **Civil Evidence Act 1968 s.11**, a conviction is admissible to prove the offence was committed, and the convicted person is **taken to have committed it unless he proves otherwise**, so the burden shifts to Grant on the balance of probabilities. It is not conclusive, so Grant can still try to prove the mechanical failure. The civil standard is the balance of probabilities, and s.11 applies whether the conviction followed a guilty plea or a trial."},{"t":"dr-parties-law","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Maisie, aged 15, was injured while a passenger in a car that crashed. The driver's insurer accepts liability and, before any proceedings have been issued, offers £18,000 in full settlement. Maisie's mother thinks the offer is fair and wants to accept it on Maisie's behalf.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The settlement will not be binding unless it is approved by the court.","Maisie's mother can accept the offer as her parent, and the settlement will then be binding.","Maisie can accept the offer herself, because she is over 14.","Court approval is needed only if the settlement exceeds £25,000.","No settlement can be made until Maisie reaches 18."],"a":0,"e":"Any settlement of a claim by or on behalf of a **child** is **not binding unless approved by the court** (CPR 21.10), even if made before proceedings are issued; approval is usually sought by a Part 8 claim. A parent cannot bind the child alone, and a child cannot settle her own claim. There is no need to wait until 18 and no value threshold for approval."},{"t":"dr-case-management","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Bellamy Ltd claims £70,000 from a former supplier for breach of contract. The issues are straightforward and each side wants one expert accountant, but because of the number of factual witnesses both parties estimate that the trial will last five days. The claim was issued in 2026.\n\nTo which track is the claim most likely to be allocated?","o":["The intermediate track, because the claim is worth between £25,000 and £100,000.","The small claims track, because the parties could be ordered to mediate.","The multi-track, because the trial is likely to last more than three days.","The fast track, because the issues are straightforward.","The intermediate track, because each party will call no more than two experts."],"a":2,"e":"The **intermediate track** is for claims up to £100,000 only where the **trial will last no more than 3 days** and the other conditions are met; a claim that fails any limit **moves up** to the **multi-track**. The value and expert numbers alone fit the intermediate track, which is the tempting answer, but the five-day trial takes it out. The fast track is limited to £25,000 and one-day trials, and the small claims track to £10,000."},{"t":"dr-issue-service","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"The defendant's solicitors have confirmed in writing that they will accept service of documents by email at a stated address. The claimant served the claim form earlier and now serves the particulars of claim separately by email, sent at 4.45pm on Friday 6 November 2026. There are no bank holidays that month.\n\nWhen are the particulars of claim deemed served?","o":["Tuesday 10 November 2026.","Sunday 8 November 2026.","Wednesday 11 November 2026.","Monday 9 November 2026.","Friday 6 November 2026."],"a":3,"e":"Particulars served separately are an \"other document\", so **CPR 6.26** applies: an email sent **before 4.30pm** on a business day is served that day, otherwise on the **next business day**. Sent at 4.45pm on a Friday, they are deemed served on Monday 9 November. The \"second business day after the step\" rule (giving Tuesday) applies only to the **claim form** (CPR 6.14). A Sunday is never a business day."},{"t":"dr-interim","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Corrin Ltd sues Vale Hotels Ltd for £90,000 for unpaid catering services and applies for summary judgment. Vale's only defence is that Corrin's director orally agreed to waive half the price. Vale has a witness statement from its manager supporting this, but the contemporaneous emails make the alleged agreement look unlikely. The judge thinks it is possible that the defence will succeed, but improbable.\n\nWhich order is the judge most likely to make?","o":["An interim payment order for £90,000.","An order striking out Vale's defence as disclosing no reasonable grounds for defending the claim.","A conditional order requiring Vale to pay a sum into court, failing which its defence will be struck out.","Dismissal of the application with no conditions, with costs in any event to Vale.","Summary judgment for Corrin for the full £90,000."],"a":2,"e":"Where it appears **possible** that a claim or defence may succeed but **improbable**, the court may make a **conditional order**, for example requiring a payment into court, failing which the statement of case is struck out. Summary judgment needs **no real prospect** of success, which a possible defence does have. Striking out on the pleadings is wrong because the defence discloses a recognised legal basis. An interim payment is limited to a reasonable proportion of the likely judgment, not the full sum."},{"t":"dr-enforcement","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Lyndon Ltd obtained a £22,000 County Court judgment against Ravensworth Trading Ltd, which has not paid. Lyndon has no information about what assets Ravensworth has, and searches have revealed nothing useful.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate first step for Lyndon to take?","o":["Instruct a High Court Enforcement Officer to take control of goods at the director's home.","Apply for an order requiring an officer of Ravensworth, such as a director, to attend court to be questioned on oath about the company's means.","Apply for a third party debt order against the personal bank account of Ravensworth's managing director.","Apply for an attachment of earnings order against Ravensworth.","Apply for a charging order over the managing director's house."],"a":1,"e":"Where the creditor does not know what the debtor owns, the first step is an **order to obtain information** (CPR 71); for a company, an **officer** is ordered to attend court and answer questions on oath and produce documents. Attachment of earnings applies only to individuals in employment. The company is a separate legal person, so its director's personal bank account, goods or house cannot be used to satisfy the company's debt."},{"t":"dr-trial","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"At trial, Nadia, the claimant's witness, was cross-examined about the speed of a delivery van and said it was \"going quickly\". The claimant's advocate now wants to re-examine Nadia to clarify that answer.\n\nWhich of the following questions is the most appropriate for the claimant's advocate to ask?","o":["\"You told my friend the van was 'going quickly'. What did you mean by that?\"","\"Can you now tell the court about the defendant's poor driving record?\"","\"You'd agree the van driver wasn't looking at the road, wouldn't you?\"","\"You meant that the van was doing well over 30 miles an hour, didn't you?\"","\"So the defendant was obviously speeding, wasn't he?\""],"a":0,"e":"In **re-examination** the advocate questions their **own** witness, so **leading questions** are not allowed, and questions must be limited to **matters arising from cross-examination**. An open question asking what the witness meant clarifies the answer properly. The questions suggesting the answer are leading. Raising the driver's record introduces a **new topic** not arising from cross-examination."},{"t":"dr-pre-action","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Hollins Ltd believes its former accountants negligently prepared its accounts. Its solicitors sent a letter of claim complying with the Professional Negligence Pre-Action Protocol on 1 March. The accountants' solicitors acknowledged it on 15 March. Limitation is not an issue.\n\nBy when should the accountants send their letter of response?","o":["Within 14 days of the acknowledgment, by 29 March.","Within 3 months of the acknowledgment, by 15 June.","Within 3 months of the letter of claim, by 1 June.","Within 4 months of the letter of claim, by 1 July.","Within 30 days of the letter of claim, by 31 March."],"a":1,"e":"Under the **Professional Negligence PAP**, the professional acknowledges within **21 days** and sends a **letter of response within 3 months of the acknowledgment**. Fourteen days reflects the general PD-PAC response in a simple case, 4 months reflects the Clinical Disputes protocol, and 30 days reflects the Debt Claims protocol. The 3 months runs from acknowledgment, not from the letter of claim."},{"t":"dr-where-to-start","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Wren was injured at work. Her solicitor values her personal injury claim at £60,000 on full liability, but thinks the employer is likely to argue that she was 25% to blame, which would reduce her damages to £45,000. The case raises some complex medical issues.\n\nWhich of the following best describes where the claim may be started?","o":["It may be started in the High Court if Wren believes it ought to be dealt with by a High Court judge, because possible contributory negligence is ignored in valuing the claim.","It must be started in the County Court, because its likely value after contributory negligence is below £50,000.","It must be started in the County Court, because personal injury claims below £100,000 cannot start in the High Court.","It must be started in the High Court, because all claims involving complex medical issues go to the King's Bench Division.","It must be started in the High Court, because it is a personal injury claim worth more than £50,000."],"a":0,"e":"For the court thresholds, value **disregards any possible finding of contributory negligence** (CPR 16.3(6)), so the claim is worth £60,000, above the **£50,000 personal injury threshold**. It **may** then start in the High Court if value, complexity or public importance justify a High Court judge, but is not obliged to. The £100,000 figure is the non-PI threshold. Complexity alone never forces a claim into the High Court."},{"t":"dr-statements-of-case","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"In a road traffic claim, the defendant's defence denies negligence and alleges that the claimant was contributorily negligent by not wearing a seatbelt. The claimant files a directions questionnaire but does not file a reply.\n\nWhat is the effect of the claimant not filing a reply?","o":["The claimant may challenge the allegation at trial only with the court's permission.","The claimant is taken to admit the allegation of contributory negligence.","The defendant may obtain default judgment on the issue of contributory negligence.","The claimant's claim will be struck out unless a reply is filed within 14 days.","It is not an admission, and the defendant must still prove the allegation of contributory negligence at trial."],"a":4,"e":"A **reply is optional**, and a claimant who does not file one is **not taken to admit** matters raised in the defence (CPR 16.7). The defendant bears the burden of proving its defence of contributory negligence. Default judgment is available on a **counterclaim** if no defence to it is filed, which is a different situation. There is no strike-out sanction or permission requirement for failing to reply."},{"t":"dr-responding","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Wilmot Ltd sued Fenby Ltd for breach of contract. After Fenby filed its defence, Wilmot decided it could not afford to continue and filed and served a notice of discontinuance of the whole claim. No interim injunction or interim payment had been made. A year later, still within the limitation period, Wilmot wants to sue Fenby again on the same facts.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Wilmot's position?","o":["Wilmot needed the court's permission to discontinue, because Fenby had already filed a defence.","Wilmot may issue a new claim on the same facts without permission, provided it is within limitation.","Each party bears its own costs of the discontinued claim, unless Fenby shows that the claim was hopeless.","Wilmot could only discontinue with Fenby's written consent.","Wilmot is normally liable for Fenby's costs up to the date the notice was served, and needs the court's permission to bring a new claim on the same facts."],"a":4,"e":"A claimant can discontinue **at any time** without permission unless an interim injunction or interim payment is involved. Unless the court orders otherwise, it must pay the **defendant's costs** up to service of the notice (CPR 38.6). If it discontinues **after a defence has been filed**, it needs the **court's permission** to bring another claim arising out of the same or substantially the same facts (CPR 38.7), even within limitation. The defendant's consent is not needed."},{"t":"dr-options","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"A dispute between Kestrel Shipping Ltd and Ardent Fuels Ltd was decided by an arbitral tribunal seated in London under the LCIA Rules, with English law governing. The award was published three weeks ago. Kestrel's lawyers believe the tribunal made a clear error in its interpretation of an English statute, but accept that the hearing was conducted fairly.\n\nWhat is the best advice to Kestrel?","o":["Kestrel may appeal to the High Court as of right within 28 days of the award.","An appeal on a point of law is unlikely to be available, because choosing the LCIA Rules usually excludes the right of appeal under s.69 Arbitration Act 1996.","Kestrel may appeal on the point of law at any time within six months with the court's leave.","Kestrel may ask the court to rehear the whole dispute on the facts and the law.","Kestrel may challenge the award under s.68 for serious irregularity, because an error of law is a serious irregularity."],"a":1,"e":"An appeal on a point of law under **s.69** needs agreement or leave and **can be excluded by agreement**; choosing institutional rules such as the **LCIA Rules usually excludes** it. An error of law is not a **serious irregularity** under s.68, which concerns matters such as unfairness or excess of powers, and the hearing here was fair. There is no appeal as of right and no rehearing of facts. Any challenge must be brought within **28 days**, not six months."},{"t":"dr-costs","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"On 2 February, the defendant in a multi-track claim made a valid Part 36 offer to pay £40,000 in settlement of the whole claim, with a relevant period of 21 days. The claimant did nothing until 6 April, when it served written notice accepting the offer. The court sees nothing to make the usual order unjust.\n\nWhat is the likely costs consequence?","o":["The defendant pays the claimant's costs up to 6 April on the indemnity basis.","The claimant cannot accept the offer, because the relevant period has expired.","Each party bears its own costs, because the offer was accepted late.","The claimant recovers its costs up to the end of the relevant period, but must pay the defendant's costs from then until 6 April.","The claimant recovers all its costs up to 6 April on the standard basis."],"a":3,"e":"A Part 36 offer can be accepted **after the relevant period** unless withdrawn. On **late acceptance** of a defendant's offer, the claimant normally gets its costs up to the **end of the relevant period** and pays the **defendant's costs from then until acceptance** (CPR 36.13). Full costs to acceptance apply only to acceptance within the relevant period. Indemnity costs are a consequence of a claimant beating its own offer at trial, not of late acceptance."},{"t":"dr-disclosure","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Jacob, a trainee solicitor acting for the claimant, receives copies of documents from the defendant following inspection. Among them is a letter from the defendant's solicitors to the defendant, headed \"Privileged and confidential: legal advice\", setting out the weaknesses in the defendant's case. It is obvious that it was included by mistake.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate course of action for Jacob's firm?","o":["Attach the letter to the trial bundle, because any document disclosed under Part 31 may be used at trial.","Use the letter freely, because privilege was waived when the defendant disclosed it.","Not use the letter, tell the defendant's solicitors about it and return it, because an inadvertently disclosed privileged document may be used only with the court's permission.","Keep the letter and send a full summary of it to the client, because the firm must tell the client all material information.","Read the letter carefully and use it to prepare cross-examination of the defendant's witnesses."],"a":2,"e":"Where a privileged document is **inadvertently** made available for inspection, the receiving party may use it **only with the court's permission** (CPR 31.20), and the court will usually restrain use where the mistake would have been **obvious**. A solicitor receiving such a document should return it and tell the other side, consistent with the duty not to take unfair advantage. Mistaken disclosure is not a deliberate waiver. The duty to inform the client does not extend to using material the firm is not entitled to use."},{"t":"dr-evidence","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"In a subsidence claim, each party was given permission to rely on a structural engineer. The court directed the experts to discuss the case. Their joint statement records that they agree the damage was caused by tree roots on the defendant's land. The defendant's solicitor is unhappy because this undermines the defendant's case, and had never agreed to be bound by the experts' conclusions.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the experts' agreement?","o":["The agreement binds both parties, because the court directed the discussion.","The defendant is bound, because experts owe their overriding duty to the court rather than to the parties.","The agreement does not bind the defendant unless the parties have expressly agreed to be bound by it.","The joint statement is inadmissible at trial, because everything said in the discussion is without prejudice.","The defendant may replace its expert without permission and ignore the joint statement."],"a":2,"e":"Under **CPR 35.12**, an agreement reached between experts in a discussion **does not bind the parties unless they expressly agree** to be bound. The experts' overriding duty to the court explains why the joint statement may still carry great weight, but it does not make it binding. The **content of the discussion** cannot be referred to without agreement, but the joint statement itself is used at trial. Changing expert needs permission, usually on terms of disclosing the first report."},{"t":"dr-parties-law","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Tamsin's company, Bluebell Interiors Ltd, is owed £75,000 by a furniture supplier, Corran Ltd, for defective goods. Tamsin has just learned that Corran entered administration last week.\n\nWhat must Bluebell do before it can start a claim against Corran?","o":["Obtain the administrator's consent or the court's permission, because a moratorium applies.","Wait until Corran is dissolved and then sue its directors personally.","Obtain the consent of the Secretary of State for Business and Trade.","Obtain the consent of Corran's directors, who remain in control of litigation.","Nothing, because administration does not affect claims by creditors."],"a":0,"e":"When a company enters **administration**, a **moratorium** applies: no legal process can be started or continued against it without the **administrator's consent** or the **court's permission**. Administration therefore does affect creditors' claims. No ministerial consent is involved, and the directors' management powers are largely displaced by the administrator. A company's separate personality means its directors are not personally liable for its debts merely because it fails."},{"t":"dr-case-management","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"In a multi-track claim, the court ordered standard disclosure by 1 May. The trial is fixed for December. On 24 April, the solicitors for both parties agree in writing that the deadline for disclosure will be extended to 22 May. Neither side has told the court.\n\nIs the agreed extension effective?","o":["Yes, because parties may agree in writing to extend a time limit by up to 28 days without the court, provided it does not put any hearing date at risk.","No, because agreed extensions are limited to 14 days.","Yes, because parties may agree any extension they wish provided both consent in writing.","No, because any extension of a time limit in a court order requires a court order.","Yes, but only once the court has been notified and has approved the agreement."],"a":0,"e":"Under **CPR 3.8(4)** the parties may, by **prior written agreement**, extend a time limit by **up to 28 days** without applying to the court, provided the extension does **not put at risk any hearing date**. This 21-day extension, made before the deadline with trial months away, is within that rule. Extensions longer than 28 days, or which risk hearing dates, need the court. No court approval is required for an extension within the rule."},{"t":"dr-issue-service","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"A van driven by Owen crashed into Priya's shop front, causing £40,000 of property damage. Priya issued and served a negligence claim against Owen just before the six-year limitation period expired. Priya's solicitors have now discovered that Owen was driving in the course of his employment with Speedway Couriers Ltd and want to add Speedway as a second defendant. The limitation period has now expired.\n\nCan Speedway be added as a defendant?","o":["Yes, because the original claim against Owen was issued within the limitation period.","Yes, if the court disapplies the limitation period under s.33 Limitation Act 1980.","Yes, because Speedway is vicariously liable and so is treated as the same party as Owen.","Yes, with permission, because adding Speedway is desirable so all matters in dispute can be resolved.","No, because the addition is not necessary: Speedway is not being substituted for a party named by mistake, and the claim can properly be carried on against Owen."],"a":4,"e":"After limitation has expired, a party may be added only if the period was current when proceedings started **and** the addition is **necessary** (CPR 19.6): substituting a party named by **mistake**, where the claim **cannot properly be carried on** without the new party, or after death or bankruptcy. The claim against Owen can proceed on its own, so none applies. Mere desirability is the test before limitation expires. **s.33** discretion is only for **personal injury** claims, not property damage."},{"t":"dr-interim","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Ellis was badly injured in an industrial accident and is suing Morley Fabrications Ltd. Morley denies liability, but the court is satisfied that if the claim went to trial Ellis would obtain judgment for a substantial sum. The likely full value of the claim is £200,000, but the court considers it likely that Ellis will be found 50% contributorily negligent. Ellis applies for an interim payment.\n\nWhat is the maximum interim payment the court can order?","o":["Exactly £100,000, because the court must award the full likely judgment.","Nothing, because Morley has not admitted liability.","Nothing until after trial, because contributory negligence is disputed.","Up to £200,000, the likely full value of the claim.","No more than a reasonable proportion of about £100,000, the likely judgment after contributory negligence."],"a":4,"e":"An interim payment may be ordered where the court is satisfied the claimant **would obtain judgment for a substantial sum**, even if liability is denied. The amount must be **no more than a reasonable proportion of the likely amount of the final judgment**, taking account of **contributory negligence**, so the ceiling is a proportion of about £100,000. The full value ignores contributory negligence, and the court does not award the whole likely judgment in advance."},{"t":"dr-enforcement","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Ashby obtained an £8,000 County Court judgment against Nell, which she has not paid. Nell owns, outright, a valuable vintage sports car that she keeps for weekends. She also uses a van for her gardening business, which she is buying on hire purchase. Ashby wants to enforce by taking control of goods.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate course of action?","o":["Apply for a warrant of control in the County Court to seize both vehicles.","Apply for a writ of control and seize the van, because vehicles used in a business can always be taken.","Apply for an attachment of earnings order against Nell's gardening business.","Apply for a warrant of control in the County Court to seize the van only.","Transfer the judgment to the High Court for a writ of control executed by a High Court Enforcement Officer, targeting the vintage car rather than the van."],"a":4,"e":"A judgment of **£5,000 or more** to be enforced by taking control of goods must be enforced in the **High Court** by **writ of control**, so the County Court judgment is transferred. The van is on **hire purchase**, so it belongs to the finance company (third-party goods), and in any event a work vehicle may be exempt up to £1,350. The vintage car, owned outright and not needed for work, is the right target. Attachment of earnings is not available against the self-employed."},{"t":"dr-trial","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Harlan, the defendant in a County Court claim, did not attend the trial. He had decided to go to a business meeting abroad because he assumed the trial would be adjourned, although no adjournment had been sought or granted. The judge gave judgment for the claimant in his absence. Harlan applied to set the judgment aside two days after he returned, and has a defence with a reasonable prospect of success.\n\nIs the court likely to set the judgment aside?","o":["Yes, provided he pays the claimant's costs thrown away.","No, because Harlan had no good reason for not attending, and all three conditions must be met.","Yes, because he acted promptly and has a reasonable prospect of success.","No, because his only remedy is to appeal to a Circuit Judge.","Yes, because a judgment given in a party's absence must always be set aside on request."],"a":1,"e":"Under **CPR 39.3**, a judgment given in a party's absence may be set aside **only if** the applicant acted **promptly**, had a **good reason** for not attending, **and** has a **reasonable prospect of success**. A voluntary decision to go to a business meeting on an unfounded assumption is not a good reason, so the application fails despite the other two conditions. There is no automatic right to set aside, and paying costs cannot make up for a missing condition."},{"t":"dr-limitation","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Under a written contract, Holt Ltd supplied goods to Wexley Ltd, with payment of £50,000 due on 1 March 2019. Wexley did not pay. On 10 January 2024, Wexley's finance director sent Holt a signed letter acknowledging that the £50,000 was owed and asking for more time to pay. No payment has been made. It is now November 2026.\n\nIs a claim for the debt time-barred?","o":["No, because the written, signed acknowledgment made before the period expired restarted the six-year period from 10 January 2024.","Yes, because the six-year period expired on 1 March 2025.","No, but the acknowledgment extended the period only by one year from 1 March 2025.","Yes, because an acknowledgment restarts time only if it contains a promise to pay by a fixed date.","Yes, because an acknowledgment of a debt is effective only if made by deed."],"a":0,"e":"For a debt, a **written, signed acknowledgment** (or part payment) by the debtor, made **before** the period expires, **restarts** the limitation period from that date (LA 1980 ss.29-30). The original six years would have run to 1 March 2025, but the January 2024 acknowledgment means the claim is in time until January 2030. No deed or promise to pay by a fixed date is needed, and the acknowledgment restarts the full period rather than adding one year."},{"t":"dr-appeals","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"A District Judge in the County Court gave judgment against Corbin after a small claims hearing. Corbin appealed with permission, and a Circuit Judge dismissed the appeal. Corbin wants to appeal again, arguing that the Circuit Judge misapplied the law.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Corbin's position?","o":["Corbin may appeal to the Court of Appeal if the Circuit Judge grants permission on the basis of a real prospect of success.","Any further appeal is a second appeal to the Court of Appeal, for which only the Court of Appeal can give permission, and it must raise an important point of principle or practice or have some other compelling reason.","Corbin may appeal to a High Court Judge if the Circuit Judge grants permission on the basis of a real prospect of success.","No further appeal is possible in a small claims track case.","Corbin may appeal directly to the Supreme Court because the case raises a point of law."],"a":1,"e":"An appeal from a decision made on a first appeal is a **second appeal**, which always goes to the **Court of Appeal**, and **only the Court of Appeal** can grant permission (CPR 52.7). The test is stricter: a real prospect of success **and** an **important point of principle or practice**, or some other compelling reason. The Circuit Judge cannot grant permission, and a High Court Judge is not the destination. The Supreme Court hears appeals from the Court of Appeal."},{"t":"dr-pre-action","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Quarry Stone Ltd sent Delph Paving Ltd a detailed letter before claim under the Practice Direction on Pre-Action Conduct, enclosing its key documents. Delph ignored the letter and all later correspondence, and Quarry had to issue proceedings. At trial, Quarry wins its £150,000 claim.\n\nWhich order may the court make to reflect Delph's failure to comply with pre-action conduct?","o":["An order that the claim be stayed so the pre-action steps can now be taken.","No order, because compliance with the Practice Direction is voluntary for commercial parties.","An order that Delph pay interest on the damages at a higher rate, up to 10% above base rate, for a period, and costs on the indemnity basis.","An order depriving Quarry of interest on its damages for the pre-action period.","An order striking out Delph's defence automatically for failure to respond."],"a":2,"e":"For substantial non-compliance by a **defendant**, the court may order it to pay **interest at a higher rate**, up to **10% above base rate**, and costs on the **indemnity basis** (PD-PAC). Depriving a party of interest is the sanction for a non-compliant **claimant**, not the innocent one. Striking out is not automatic and is a last resort. A stay to complete the steps makes no sense after trial, and the Practice Direction is not optional."},{"t":"dr-where-to-start","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Morwen Ltd issued a claim in the High Court for a straightforward unpaid debt of £70,000. The claim involved no complex issues and no wider public importance. Morwen won at trial and the judge is now dealing with costs.\n\nWhat is the most likely consequence of Morwen starting the claim in the High Court?","o":["There is no consequence, because the High Court has unlimited jurisdiction in money claims.","Morwen must pay the defendant's costs of the whole action.","The judgment is a nullity and Morwen must reissue the claim in the County Court.","The court may reduce Morwen's recoverable costs by up to 25%.","The claim should have been struck out as soon as it was issued."],"a":3,"e":"A money claim worth **£100,000 or less** must start in the **County Court**, but a claim started in the wrong court is **not invalid**; it can be transferred or allowed to continue. The sanction is that the court may **reduce the claimant's recoverable costs by up to 25%** (Senior Courts Act 1981 s.51(8)-(9)). Striking out or nullity is wrong, and a winning claimant is not made to pay all the loser's costs for this alone."}],"ls":[{"t":"sra-regulatory-role","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Imogen is a solicitor at a traditional (non-ABS) firm. An SRA investigation finds that she deliberately backdated a document and lied to a client about why a deadline had been missed. The SRA considers that her conduct is so serious that she should no longer be able to practise as a solicitor.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how that outcome can be achieved?","o":["The SRA can strike her off the roll itself, because it is the independent regulator of solicitors.","The Legal Ombudsman can order that she be struck off, because the matter began with a client complaint.","The SRA can impose an unlimited fine on her, which automatically ends her right to practise.","The Law Society can strike her off, as the approved regulator for solicitors under the Legal Services Act 2007.","The SRA must bring proceedings before the Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal, which (with the High Court) alone has the power to strike her off the roll."],"a":4,"e":"Only the **Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal** (or the High Court) can **strike off or suspend** a solicitor, so the SRA must refer serious cases such as dishonesty to the SDT. The SRA's own powers stop at warnings, rebukes, conditions and fines (capped at **£25,000** for individuals at traditional firms outside economic crime cases). The Legal Ombudsman deals with poor service, not misconduct, and the Law Society has delegated its regulatory functions to the SRA."},{"t":"sra-regulatory-role","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"A trainee solicitor is preparing a training session on the Legal Services Act 2007. She wants to give an example of a professional principle under s.1(3) of the Act, which binds every authorised person, rather than a regulatory objective.\n\nWhich of the following should she choose?","o":["Keeping the affairs of clients confidential.","Promoting competition in the provision of legal services.","Improving access to justice.","Promoting the prevention and detection of economic offences.","Acting in a way that encourages equality, diversity and inclusion."],"a":0,"e":"The **professional principles** in s.1(3) are duties on authorised persons: independence and integrity, proper standards of work, acting in clients' best interests, the duty to the court, and **keeping clients' affairs confidential**. Promoting competition, improving access to justice and preventing economic offences are **regulatory objectives**, which are aims for the regulators. Encouraging equality, diversity and inclusion is one of the SRA's own seven Principles, a separate list."},{"t":"sra-regulatory-role","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Darren is a paralegal at Wren Solicitors LLP. He is not an authorised person for the conduct of litigation. In a debt claim, he drafts and issues the claim form and serves it on the defendant. Ayesha, the solicitor with conduct of the matter, retains responsibility for the claim, reviews his work at key points and has proper supervision and control arrangements in place, but does not approve each step personally.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position as at September 2026?","o":["Darren is committing a criminal offence, because an unauthorised person can never carry out tasks amounting to the conduct of litigation, even under supervision.","Darren's actions are lawful only if Ayesha personally approves every step before he takes it.","Darren's actions are lawful because issuing and serving a claim form is not a reserved legal activity.","Darren may lawfully carry out these tasks on Ayesha's behalf, because she retains responsibility for the litigation and supervises him appropriately.","Darren's actions are lawful only because the claim is a debt claim, which falls outside the conduct of litigation."],"a":3,"e":"The Court of Appeal held in 2026 that an unauthorised person may carry out tasks amounting to the **conduct of litigation for and on behalf of an authorised individual**, provided that individual **retains responsibility** and has appropriate **supervision, management and control** arrangements; step-by-step approval is not required (Mazur). The 2025 High Court view that supervised staff could only support, not conduct, litigation was reversed. Issuing and serving proceedings is still conduct of litigation, a reserved activity, whatever the type of claim."},{"t":"sra-regulatory-role","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Gerald, a retired engineer with no legal qualifications, helps his niece Pippa buy her first flat. As a favour, and without charging anything, he prepares the TR1 transfer and lodges the application to register it at HM Land Registry.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Gerald's position under the Legal Services Act 2007?","o":["He has committed an offence, because preparing a land transfer is a reserved instrument activity whoever does it.","He has not carried on a reserved legal activity, because conveyancing is not one of the six reserved legal activities.","He has committed an offence unless a solicitor checked the transfer before it was lodged.","He has not committed an offence, because he is exempt as he acted without any fee, gain or reward.","He has not committed an offence, but only because Pippa is a member of his family."],"a":3,"e":"Preparing a land transfer and lodging it at HM Land Registry are **reserved instrument activities**, but a person who carries them on **otherwise than for, or in expectation of, any fee, gain or reward** is an **exempt person** (LSA 2007 Sch 3 para 3), so Gerald's unpaid help is lawful. Had he charged, he would commit an offence under s.14 by carrying on a reserved activity when not entitled. The family relationship is irrelevant; what matters is the absence of payment."},{"t":"pii-other-providers","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Rosa practises as a sole practitioner through an SRA-authorised recognised sole practice. She has professional indemnity insurance on the minimum terms only. She negligently misses a limitation date and her client, Keith, suffers a loss of £2.6 million.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Keith's loss will be met?","o":["The insurer must pay up to £3 million, so Keith's whole loss is covered.","The SRA Compensation Fund must pay the balance above the insurance limit, because Keith is an individual.","The insurer must pay the full £2.6 million, because minimum terms cover is unlimited for sole practitioners.","The insurer must pay up to £2 million plus defence costs, and Keith can pursue Rosa personally for the balance.","Keith can claim only from the SRA Compensation Fund, because negligence by a sole practitioner is not covered by indemnity insurance."],"a":3,"e":"Minimum cover is **£2 million** per claim for sole practitioners and partnerships, and **£3 million** for firms that are LLPs or companies; defence costs are paid on top. Rosa has unlimited personal liability, so Keith can sue her for the remaining £600,000. The **Compensation Fund** covers **dishonesty or failure to account**, not negligence, and is a discretionary last resort."},{"t":"pii-other-providers","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Halden Engineering plc, which has an annual turnover of £40 million, paid £1.5 million into the client account of a small law firm to fund a property purchase. A partner stole the money and the firm has collapsed. Halden asks whether it can apply to the SRA Compensation Fund.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Halden is entitled to a payment as of right, because the loss was caused by a solicitor's dishonesty.","Halden can recover from the Fund only if it shows that the partner was negligent rather than dishonest.","Halden is eligible, because there is no restriction on who can apply, but any grant is capped at £2 million.","Halden is unlikely to be eligible, because the Fund is generally available only to individuals and to businesses, charities and trusts below a £2 million turnover, income or asset threshold.","Halden must claim from the Legal Ombudsman, which can award up to £1.5 million for dishonesty."],"a":3,"e":"The **Compensation Fund** covers losses from **dishonesty** or **failure to account**, but it is **discretionary** and broadly limited to **individuals and smaller businesses, charities and trusts** (turnover, income or assets below **£2 million**); large businesses are expected to protect themselves. No one has a right to a payment, and theft, not negligence, is the Fund's territory. The Legal Ombudsman deals with service complaints and its awards are capped at £50,000."},{"t":"pii-other-providers","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Thorne & Co's professional indemnity insurance expired on 30 September. Despite approaching several participating insurers, the firm has been unable to obtain a new policy. It is now 15 November and no cover has been arranged.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's position?","o":["The firm is in the extended policy period and may continue to accept new instructions until the end of December.","The firm is in the cessation period: it must not take on new instructions, and if it has no cover by the end of that period it must close.","The firm's old policy lapsed completely on 30 September, so it should have stopped practising on that date.","The firm may carry on as normal for six years while it looks for cover, because run-off cover applies.","The firm may continue practising if it buys insurance below the minimum terms, provided it tells its clients."],"a":1,"e":"If a firm fails to renew, the old policy continues for an **extended policy period of 30 days**, followed by a **cessation period of 60 days** (90 days in all). By 15 November the firm is in the **cessation period**, during which it must not take on new work, and it must close if it has no cover by the end. **Run-off cover** (6 years) applies only where a firm closes without a successor, and an SRA firm cannot practise with cover below the minimum terms."},{"t":"equality-act","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Marta, an associate at Fenwick LLP, gave evidence in support of a colleague's sex discrimination claim against the firm in the employment tribunal. Marta makes no allegation that she has been discriminated against herself. Shortly afterwards, she is told she will not be considered for promotion this year because she 'is not a team player'. Her performance reviews are excellent.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Marta's likely claim?","o":["Harassment, because the comment about not being a team player was unwanted conduct.","Direct sex discrimination, because the original claim concerned sex discrimination.","Victimisation, because she has been subjected to a detriment for doing a protected act.","Indirect discrimination, because the promotion criteria apply to all associates.","No claim, because Marta does not allege that she was treated less favourably because of her own protected characteristic."],"a":2,"e":"**Victimisation** (s.27) is subjecting a person to a **detriment** because they did a **protected act**, which includes **giving evidence** in proceedings under the Act. It does not depend on the victim's own protected characteristic, so Marta's lack of a personal discrimination complaint does not matter. Harassment needs unwanted conduct related to a protected characteristic, and nothing suggests her own sex or a neutral rule caused the decision."},{"t":"equality-act","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Jonah, a legal executive at Calder Solicitors, was diagnosed with multiple sclerosis two months ago and told the firm at once. He has since had several days off for hospital appointments and treatment. The firm gives him a formal warning under its attendance policy, which applies to all staff, saying that his level of absence is unacceptable.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's position?","o":["Jonah is not yet disabled, because his condition has not lasted for 12 months.","The warning cannot be discriminatory, because the attendance policy applies equally to all staff.","Jonah is disabled from diagnosis, and the warning is likely to be discrimination arising from disability unless the firm shows it is a proportionate means of achieving a legitimate aim.","The warning is direct disability discrimination, which the firm cannot justify in any circumstances.","The firm has a complete defence, because it did not intend to treat Jonah unfavourably."],"a":2,"e":"**Multiple sclerosis** (like cancer and HIV) is a disability **from diagnosis**, so the 12-month test does not apply. Warning Jonah for absences caused by his treatment is **unfavourable treatment because of something arising in consequence of his disability** (s.15), which the firm can defend only by showing a **proportionate means of achieving a legitimate aim**; it knew of the disability, so the lack-of-knowledge defence is unavailable. Applying the policy to everyone does not prevent a s.15 claim, and motive is irrelevant."},{"t":"equality-act","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Beacon Law LLP has very few trainees from Black and minority ethnic backgrounds compared with the local population. In its trainee recruitment, two final candidates, Kwame and Oliver, are judged to be exactly equally qualified. The firm wants to offer the place to Kwame, who is Black, because people of his ethnic background are under-represented among its trainees. It has no policy of automatically preferring candidates from under-represented groups.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the lawfulness of the firm's proposal?","o":["It is likely to be lawful positive action, because the candidates are equally qualified and preferring Kwame is a proportionate way to address under-representation.","It is unlawful direct race discrimination against Oliver, because an employer may never take race into account in recruitment.","It would be lawful even if Oliver were better qualified, because the group is under-represented.","It is lawful only if the firm sets a quota of trainee places reserved for minority ethnic candidates.","It is lawful only if Kwame can show that he has personally suffered disadvantage because of his race."],"a":0,"e":"The Equality Act allows **positive action** (ss.158-159), including the **tie-breaker**: where candidates are **equally qualified**, an employer may prefer one from an **under-represented** group if this is proportionate and not an automatic policy. Preferring a less-qualified candidate, or reserving places by **quota**, is unlawful positive discrimination. The individual need not prove personal disadvantage; the test looks at the group's under-representation."},{"t":"aml-framework-cdd","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Sefton Solicitors is reviewing which of its current matters fall within the scope of the Money Laundering, Terrorist Financing and Transfer of Funds (Information on the Payer) Regulations 2017.\n\nWhich of the following matters is most likely to be within scope?","o":["Acting for a dentist on the sale of her dental practice business to a corporate buyer.","Defending a client in the Crown Court on a charge of fraud.","Representing an employee in an unfair dismissal claim in the employment tribunal.","Drafting a will for a client who owns several investment properties.","Acting for a mother in a contested child arrangements application."],"a":0,"e":"The MLR 2017 apply to legal professionals when they **participate in financial or real property transactions**, including **buying and selling business entities**, so the sale of the dental practice is in scope. Criminal defence, employment litigation, will drafting and family disputes not involving property transfers are generally **outside** the Regulations. The **POCA principal offences** still apply to all of these matters."},{"t":"aml-framework-cdd","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Gwen Morley, a serving member of the UK House of Commons, instructs Abbott Solicitors to act on her purchase of a house in her constituency for £650,000. There are no other risk factors. A trainee suggests that, since January 2024, UK politicians no longer need to be treated as politically exposed persons.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the due diligence the firm must apply?","o":["Enhanced due diligence, including senior management approval and measures to establish her source of wealth and source of funds, although as a domestic PEP she may be treated as presenting a lower starting level of risk than a foreign PEP.","Simplified due diligence, because a UK MP is a public figure whose identity is easily verified.","Standard customer due diligence only, because domestic PEPs are no longer politically exposed persons.","No customer due diligence, because the transaction is a residential purchase by an individual.","Enhanced due diligence only if she has held office for less than 12 months."],"a":0,"e":"An **MP** holds a prominent public function, so Gwen is a **PEP** and **enhanced due diligence** applies: **senior management approval**, measures to establish **source of wealth and source of funds**, and enhanced ongoing monitoring. Since January 2024 a **domestic PEP** is treated as a **lower starting level of risk** than a foreign PEP, which affects how extensive the EDD is, not whether it applies. The 12-month rule concerns how long someone remains a PEP after leaving office."},{"t":"aml-framework-cdd","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Northgate Solicitors is instructed by Coralline plc, a company whose shares are admitted to trading on a regulated market, to act on its acquisition of an office building. The firm's risk assessment rates the matter as low risk and there is no suspicion of money laundering. A trainee proposes carrying out no customer due diligence at all.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the correct approach?","o":["The firm need carry out no customer due diligence, because listed companies are exempt from the Regulations.","The firm must apply enhanced due diligence, because the transaction involves commercial property.","The firm must identify and verify every shareholder holding more than 25% before acting, as for any private company.","The firm may rely on Coralline plc's own internal anti-money laundering checks without further enquiry.","The firm may apply simplified due diligence, reducing the extent and timing of its checks, but must still identify the client and carry out ongoing monitoring."],"a":4,"e":"Where a relationship is assessed as **low risk**, for example a company listed on a regulated market, the firm may apply **simplified due diligence** (reg 37). SDD is **reduced CDD, not no CDD**: the client must still be identified and the relationship monitored, and SDD is lost if any suspicion arises. Nothing here triggers EDD, and reliance is permitted only on certain regulated third parties who agree, not on the client itself."},{"t":"aml-framework-cdd","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Laurel & Co completed the sale of a client's commercial unit in March 2026, which ended its business relationship with the client. The office manager proposes to destroy the customer due diligence documents and transaction records on the file in March 2027 to save storage costs.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's obligation?","o":["The records may be destroyed in March 2027, because they need be kept for only 12 months after completion.","The records must be kept indefinitely, because they relate to a property transaction.","The records may be destroyed at any time once the matter is completed, provided the client consents.","The records must be kept for 2 years after completion and may then be destroyed.","The records must be kept for 5 years after the end of the business relationship, so they must not be destroyed in March 2027."],"a":4,"e":"Under **reg 40** of the MLR 2017, CDD records and supporting transaction records must be kept for **5 years** after the **end of the business relationship** or completion of the occasional transaction. Destroying them after one year would breach the Regulations, which is a **criminal offence** as well as a regulatory matter. The client cannot consent away this duty, and there is no requirement to keep them indefinitely."},{"t":"poca-offences-reporting","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"The MLRO of Pemberton LLP makes a suspicious activity report to the National Crime Agency and requests a defence against money laundering so that a client's share purchase can complete. On the fourth working day after the report, the NCA notifies the firm that consent is refused. The client is pressing to complete.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's position?","o":["The firm may proceed once 7 working days have passed from the report, because consent is then deemed to be given.","The firm must not proceed during the 31-day moratorium period running from the refusal; if that period ends without an extension or further action, it may then proceed.","The firm may never proceed with the transaction, because consent has been refused.","The firm may proceed immediately if the client provides a written explanation of the source of the funds.","The firm must not proceed for a fixed period of 186 days from the refusal."],"a":1,"e":"If the NCA **refuses** consent within the 7-working-day notice period, a **moratorium of 31 days** runs from the refusal, during which the firm **must not proceed**. If nothing further happens (for example, no court extension), the firm may go ahead at the end of it. Deemed consent arises only where the NCA does not refuse within the notice period, and **186 days** is the maximum total of court extensions beyond the initial 31 days, not a fixed period."},{"t":"poca-offences-reporting","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Ifeoma is a criminal defence solicitor; her firm does no transactional work and is outside the regulated sector. She represents Liam, who is charged with a large benefit fraud, and he pays her firm's normal fixed fee for the trial. Ifeoma suspects that the money Liam used comes from the fraud. Her work consists solely of defending him at trial.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ifeoma's position?","o":["She has a defence to the offence of acquiring or possessing criminal property, because her firm received the fee for adequate consideration in the form of genuine legal services.","She commits the s.330 failure to disclose offence unless she reports her suspicion to the NCA.","She must stop acting for Liam immediately, because accepting any money suspected to be criminal property is always an offence.","She commits the tipping off offence by continuing to act for Liam.","She must tell Liam that she suspects his money is criminal property and ask him to pay from another source."],"a":0,"e":"Receiving criminal property can be the **s.329** offence, but there is a defence where it was acquired for **adequate consideration**, such as **normal fees for genuine legal work**, provided the services do not help the client's criminal conduct. **s.330** and **tipping off** (s.333A) apply only in the **regulated sector**, which criminal defence work is not. Raising her suspicion with Liam is unnecessary and could risk prejudicing an investigation."},{"t":"poca-offences-reporting","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Ruth, a family law solicitor whose firm is outside the regulated sector, learns that the police have begun a money laundering investigation into her client, Owen. Owen asks her to shred file notes recording what he told her about hidden offshore accounts, and she does so, knowing that the notes would be relevant to the investigation.\n\nWhich offence has Ruth most likely committed?","o":["Tipping off under s.333A.","Failure to disclose under s.330.","No offence, because her firm is outside the regulated sector.","Prejudicing an investigation under s.342, by destroying documents relevant to the investigation.","No offence, because the file notes were covered by legal professional privilege."],"a":3,"e":"**s.342** applies to **anyone**, in or outside the regulated sector, who knowing or suspecting that an investigation is under way **falsifies, conceals or destroys** relevant documents (or makes a prejudicial disclosure). **Tipping off** (s.333A) and **failure to disclose** (s.330) are regulated-sector offences, so they do not fit Ruth's position. Privilege may affect whether documents must be produced, but it never permits a solicitor to destroy them to defeat an investigation."},{"t":"financial-services","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Bridget asks her solicitor, Tom, whether she should sell her holiday cottage in Cornwall now or wait until next year, and what price she should accept. Tom's firm is not authorised by the Financial Conduct Authority.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Tom can advise her?","o":["Tom may advise her only under the Part 20 exemption, because advice on the merits of selling a particular asset is a regulated activity.","Tom must refer her to an independent financial adviser, because the advice concerns the merits of selling a particular asset.","Tom may advise her only because the advice is excluded as a necessary part of other professional services.","Tom may not advise her, because only FCA-authorised firms can advise on the sale of assets of significant value.","Tom may advise her, because land is not a specified investment, so the advice is not a regulated activity under FSMA 2000."],"a":4,"e":"A regulated activity requires a **specified investment**, and **land and buildings** are **not** specified investments (unlike shares, units in collective investment schemes or contracts of insurance). Advising on selling the cottage is therefore outside FSMA altogether, and no exclusion or **Part 20** exemption is needed. Arranging a **mortgage** for a purchase, by contrast, could be regulated."},{"t":"financial-services","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Quill Solicitors agrees that Mr Harding may pay its £4,800 bill in 12 equal monthly instalments over the next year. The firm proposes to add a charge of 2% a year to reflect the delay in payment. The firm does not have FCA permission for consumer credit.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the arrangement?","o":["It is exempt, because the credit is repayable in no more than 12 instalments within 12 months.","It is likely to be a regulated credit agreement requiring authorisation, because instalment arrangements are exempt only if no interest or other charges are made.","It is exempt, because legal fees are not a specified investment under FSMA 2000.","It is permitted under the Part 20 exemption, because the credit is incidental to the firm's legal services.","It is exempt provided the interest rate does not exceed the Bank of England base rate."],"a":1,"e":"Allowing a client to pay fees by instalments can be providing **credit**, a regulated activity. It is **exempt** only where repayment is in **12 or fewer instalments within 12 months** with **no interest or other charges**, so adding a 2% charge takes the arrangement outside the exemption. Dropping the charge would bring this 12-instalment arrangement within the exemption."},{"t":"financial-services","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Fairbank Solicitors is not FCA-authorised but is on the FCA Register as an exempt professional firm. It arranges after-the-event insurance for a client in a contract claim, and the insurer pays the firm a commission of £350.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the firm must do with the commission to rely on the Part 20 exemption?","o":["Keep the commission, because commission from an insurer is not a reward for a regulated activity.","Account to the client for the commission, unless the client agrees that the firm may keep it after being told about it.","Pay the commission to the SRA Compensation Fund.","Keep the commission, provided it is shown on the firm's bill as a disbursement.","Refuse the commission, because exempt firms may never receive commission from third parties."],"a":1,"e":"One condition of the **Part 20 exemption** (FSMA s.327) is that the firm receives **no pecuniary reward** from anyone other than its client for the activity **unless it accounts to the client** for it. In practice the firm passes the commission on, or keeps it only with the client's **informed agreement**. Exempt firms are not barred from receiving commission, but must deal with it this way and record it under the Conduct of Business Rules."},{"t":"funding-private-cfa-dba","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Helena brings a commercial contract claim under a damages-based agreement providing for payment of 30% of the sums recovered. She wins £200,000, and the court orders the defendant to pay £35,000 towards her costs, which the defendant pays.\n\nHow much of her damages will Helena have to pay to her solicitors under the DBA?","o":["£60,000","£35,000","£25,000","£95,000","£50,000"],"a":2,"e":"The DBA payment is **30% of £200,000 = £60,000**, within the **50%** cap for ordinary civil claims. **Recovered costs are set off** against the DBA payment, so the £35,000 paid by the defendant goes towards it and Helena pays only the **balance of £25,000** from her damages. Adding the recovered costs on top, or ignoring the set-off, misstates how a DBA works."},{"t":"funding-private-cfa-dba","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Sunita wants to start financial remedy proceedings following her divorce. She cannot afford hourly rates and asks her solicitor to act under a conditional fee agreement, with a success fee payable if she obtains a good outcome.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["A CFA can be used if it is in writing and the success fee does not exceed 100% of base costs.","A CFA cannot be used, because conditional fee agreements are not permitted in family proceedings.","A CFA can be used, and any success fee will be recoverable from her former husband.","A CFA cannot be used, but a damages-based agreement capped at 50% of her award can be.","A CFA can be used if the success fee is limited to 25% of any lump sum she receives."],"a":1,"e":"Under the **Courts and Legal Services Act 1990 s.58**, a CFA **must not relate to criminal or family proceedings**, so it is unavailable for financial remedy proceedings however it is drafted. **DBAs** are likewise not allowed in family proceedings. The 100% and 25% caps apply only where a CFA is permitted, and success fees have not been recoverable from opponents since April 2013."},{"t":"funding-private-cfa-dba","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Arun wins a commercial claim. He instructed his solicitors under a CFA providing for a 60% success fee on a win, with Arun liable for the full base costs if he wins. His solicitors' base costs are £40,000. The court orders the defendant to pay his costs, which are assessed at £32,000 and paid.\n\nHow much will Arun have to pay his solicitors in addition to the costs recovered from the defendant?","o":["£24,000","£8,000","£32,000","£64,000","Nothing, because the defendant has paid his costs"],"a":2,"e":"On a win Arun owes his solicitors **base costs of £40,000 plus a success fee of 60% of base costs (£24,000)**, a total of £64,000. The defendant's £32,000 goes towards this, but the **success fee is not recoverable from the opponent** (LASPO s.44) and the client remains **liable for any shortfall** in base costs, so Arun pays £8,000 + £24,000 = **£32,000**. The 25% of damages cap applies only to personal injury claims."},{"t":"funding-legal-aid-tpf-lei","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Five clients on low incomes ask Brook Law Centre, which holds a civil legal aid contract, whether legal aid is available for their cases.\n\nWhich client's case is most likely to be within the scope of civil legal aid?","o":["Gemma, who wants to claim damages for a broken wrist suffered in a road traffic accident.","Faisal, who has been refused help by his local authority on a homelessness application and wants to challenge the decision.","Harry, who wants to sue a builder for breach of contract over defective work on his kitchen.","Irina, who wants to bring an unfair dismissal claim against her former employer.","Jake, who wants to apply for a child arrangements order where there is no evidence of domestic or child abuse."],"a":1,"e":"Under **LASPO Schedule 1**, **housing** matters including **homelessness** are within scope. Most **personal injury**, **contract** and **employment** claims (other than discrimination), and **private family** cases without evidence of **domestic or child abuse**, are **out of scope**, so legal aid is unavailable however poor the client. Scope should be checked before the means and merits tests."},{"t":"funding-legal-aid-tpf-lei","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Lorna had civil legal aid in a property dispute in which she recovered £60,000. Her legal aid costs came to £25,000, of which the other party has been ordered to pay, and has paid, £10,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the statutory charge?","o":["The Legal Aid Agency can recover the full £25,000 from the £60,000, ignoring the costs paid by the other party.","The statutory charge does not apply, because the other party has paid some of her costs.","The Legal Aid Agency has a first charge on the £60,000 to recover the £15,000 of legal aid costs not recovered from the other party.","Lorna must repay £15,000 out of her own savings, but the £60,000 is protected.","The statutory charge applies only if Lorna loses, to cover the other party's costs."],"a":2,"e":"The **statutory charge** (LASPO s.25) gives the LAA a **first charge** on money or property **recovered or preserved** in the proceedings, to repay legal aid costs **not recovered from the opponent**: here £25,000 − £10,000 = **£15,000**. Clients must be warned at the outset, because the charge can take a large part of what they win. A losing client is instead protected by **costs protection** under s.26."},{"t":"funding-legal-aid-tpf-lei","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Orbis Ltd is bringing a £30 million commercial claim. A litigation funder agrees to pay its legal costs in return for three times the amount it invests, payable only out of the proceeds if the claim succeeds; the return is capped so that it can never exceed 40% of the damages. Orbis's directors ask whether the funding agreement is enforceable as at September 2026.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["It is unenforceable, because any litigation funding agreement whose return is linked in any way to damages is a damages-based agreement.","It is unenforceable, because legislation passed in 2025 banned third-party litigation funding.","It is enforceable only if the funder is authorised by the Financial Conduct Authority.","It is enforceable only if Orbis also enters into a conditional fee agreement with its solicitors.","It is likely to be enforceable, because the funder's return is calculated as a multiple of its investment rather than as a share of damages, so it is not a damages-based agreement."],"a":4,"e":"A funding agreement under which the funder takes a **percentage of damages** is a **DBA** and unenforceable unless it complies with the DBA Regulations (PACCAR), but one calculated as a **multiple of the investment** is not, even where the return is **capped by reference to damages** (Sony v Neill). The proposed legislation to reverse PACCAR has **not** been enacted, and funders are **self-regulated** rather than FCA-authorised. No CFA is needed for the funding agreement to be valid."},{"t":"funding-legal-aid-tpf-lei","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Nadia brings a clinical negligence claim against an NHS trust, funded by a CFA and after-the-event insurance taken out in 2026. The ATE premium of £9,000 includes £3,000 attributable to cover for the cost of expert reports on liability and causation. She wins.\n\nHow much of the ATE premium can Nadia recover from the trust?","o":["£9,000","Nothing","£3,000","£6,000","£4,500"],"a":2,"e":"Since April 2013 an **ATE premium is not recoverable** from the losing opponent, so it normally comes out of the client's damages. The exception is **clinical negligence**, where the part of the premium covering the cost of **expert reports on liability and causation** remains recoverable. Nadia can therefore recover **£3,000** and must bear the other £6,000 herself."},{"t":"transparency-client-care","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Holt & Partners offers each of the following services and does not publish any price information on its website.\n\nFor which service must the firm publish costs information under the SRA Transparency Rules?","o":["Acting for claimants in personal injury claims.","Acting on the purchase and sale of commercial property.","Acting for clients in contested probate disputes.","Defending employment tribunal claims for unfair or wrongful dismissal brought against business clients.","Acting for clients in divorce and financial remedy proceedings."],"a":3,"e":"Price information is required only for the **listed services**, which for businesses include **defending employment tribunal claims for unfair or wrongful dismissal** (alongside debt recovery up to £100,000 and licensing applications). **Personal injury**, **commercial property**, **contested probate** and **family** work are not on the list. The firm must still publish its **complaints procedure** and regulatory information whatever services it offers."},{"t":"transparency-client-care","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Clara complained to her solicitors, Vance LLP, about delays on her probate matter three weeks ago. The firm acknowledged the complaint and says it is investigating, but has not yet sent a final response. Clara wants to take her complaint to the Legal Ombudsman now.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Clara can go to the Legal Ombudsman at once, because she has already complained to the firm.","Clara must wait 6 months from her complaint before going to the Legal Ombudsman.","Clara must take her complaint to the SRA, because the Legal Ombudsman deals only with misconduct.","Clara cannot use the Legal Ombudsman, because complaints about delay are not within its remit.","The Legal Ombudsman will not normally accept the complaint until 8 weeks have passed since she complained or the firm has given its final response."],"a":4,"e":"A client must first use the firm's **complaints procedure**, and the firm has up to **8 weeks** to resolve it. The **Legal Ombudsman** normally accepts a complaint only after **8 weeks** or once the firm has given its **final response**; the client then has **6 months** from the final response (and 1 year from the problem) to go to LeO. LeO deals with **service** issues such as delay, while the SRA deals with conduct."},{"t":"transparency-client-care","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Mr Dawson, a consumer, instructs Penrose Solicitors by email to act on his lease extension. He never meets anyone at the firm. The firm starts work the next day without giving him any information about cancellation and without asking whether he wants work to begin straight away. Ten days later, after substantial work has been done, he cancels.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Mr Dawson can cancel, and is unlikely to have to pay for the work done, because he did not expressly request that work start within the cancellation period.","Mr Dawson cannot cancel, because legal services are excluded from the Consumer Contracts Regulations 2013.","Mr Dawson can cancel, but must pay for all work done up to cancellation.","Mr Dawson cannot cancel, because the firm had already begun work before he cancelled.","Mr Dawson can cancel only if the firm agrees, because he has received a benefit from the work."],"a":0,"e":"A contract made **at a distance** (by email, phone or online) with a **consumer** carries a **14-day right to cancel**, and the firm must give pre-contract information including the cancellation right. A consumer pays for work done during the cancellation period only if he **expressly requested** that work start within it after being properly informed; neither happened here. Because no cancellation information was given, the cancellation period is also **extended** (by up to 12 months)."}],"contract":[{"t":"offer-acceptance","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Harlow Auctions advertises an auction of farm machinery and states in the catalogue that every lot will be sold 'without reserve'. At the auction, Nell bids £1,200 for a tractor worth about £9,000. No one else bids. The auctioneer refuses to bring the hammer down, saying the bid is far too low, and withdraws the tractor from the sale.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Nell's position?","o":["She has no remedy, because her bid was only an offer, which the auctioneer was free to reject before the fall of the hammer.","She has a binding contract of sale with the owner of the tractor at £1,200, because the catalogue was an offer to sell to the highest bidder.","She can obtain specific performance against the owner, compelling the sale of the tractor to her for £1,200.","She can claim damages from the auctioneer for breach of its promise to sell to the highest genuine bidder at an auction without reserve.","She can recover only her travel costs, because the auction catalogue was an invitation to treat."],"a":3,"e":"At an ordinary auction each bid is an **offer** accepted on the fall of the hammer, so no contract of sale exists until then. But where an auction is advertised **without reserve**, the auctioneer impliedly makes a **collateral unilateral promise** to sell to the **highest genuine bidder**, which Nell accepted by bidding (Barry v Davies). Withdrawing the lot breaches that promise, so she can claim **damages** from the auctioneer, measured by the difference between her bid and the tractor's value. There is no contract of sale with the owner, so specific performance is not available."},{"t":"offer-acceptance","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"On 1 May, Bexley Ltd sends Corran Ltd a purchase order for 500 steel brackets on Bexley's standard terms, which give the buyer 12 months to claim for defects. On 3 May, Corran replies with an 'order acknowledgement' confirming the price and quantity but stating that the sale is on Corran's own standard terms, which limit claims for defects to 30 days and say they override any other terms. Bexley does not respond. On 10 May, Corran delivers the brackets and Bexley accepts them without comment.\n\nWhose terms, if any, govern the contract?","o":["Bexley's terms, because it made the first offer and Corran's acknowledgement confirmed the order.","Bexley's terms, because silence cannot amount to acceptance of a counter-offer.","Neither party's terms, because the parties never agreed whose terms applied, so there is no contract.","Corran's terms, but only the parts of them that do not conflict with Bexley's terms.","Corran's terms, because Bexley accepted Corran's counter-offer by taking delivery."],"a":4,"e":"On the traditional **offer and counter-offer** analysis (Butler Machine Tool; Tekdata), Bexley's order was an offer, and Corran's acknowledgement on different terms was a **counter-offer** that destroyed it. Bexley did not merely stay silent: it **accepted by conduct** when it took delivery without objection, so Corran's terms (the **last shot**) govern. A contract plainly exists, because both sides performed, and the courts do not blend the two sets of terms."},{"t":"consideration","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Fenton owes his supplier, Grace, £10,000, payable on 30 June. In May, Grace, who has cash-flow problems, asks Fenton whether he would pay her £8,000 on 1 June in full and final settlement. Fenton agrees and pays £8,000 on 1 June. In August, Grace sues Fenton for the remaining £2,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Grace can recover the £2,000?","o":["She can recover it, because payment of a smaller sum is never consideration for a promise to forgive the rest of a debt.","She can recover it, because promissory estoppel only suspends a creditor's rights and she has now given notice.","She cannot recover it, because Fenton paid early at her request, which is good consideration for her promise to accept less.","She cannot recover it, because Grace obtained a practical benefit from receiving the money early.","She can recover it, because Fenton did nothing more than he was already obliged to do."],"a":2,"e":"Under the rule in **Pinnel's Case** and **Foakes v Beer**, paying part of a debt on the due date is not consideration for a promise to forgive the rest. But payment **earlier than the due date at the creditor's request** is an exception: the early payment is something different and is **good consideration**, so Grace cannot sue for the balance. Practical benefit (Williams v Roffey) does not apply to promises to accept less, and promissory estoppel does not need to be relied on because there is a binding agreement."},{"t":"intention-certainty-capacity","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Oscar, aged 17, is starting an outdoor apprenticeship in November and owns only a thin jacket. He orders a warm waterproof work coat from a clothing shop for £400 on credit. A coat of that kind would usually sell for about £250. Oscar takes the coat, wears it daily at work, but then refuses to pay anything.\n\nWhat, if anything, can the shop recover from Oscar?","o":["The full contract price of £400.","A reasonable price for the coat, likely to be about £250.","Nothing, because a contract is never enforceable against a minor.","Nothing, but the court may order Oscar to return the coat.","The contract price of £400, but only if an adult guaranteed the contract."],"a":1,"e":"Contracts are generally not enforceable against **minors**, but a minor must pay for **necessaries**: goods suitable to the minor's **condition in life** and **actual requirements** at the time. A warm work coat for someone who owns only a thin jacket and is starting an outdoor job is likely to be a necessary. However, the minor need only pay a **reasonable price** (Sale of Goods Act 1979 s.3), not the contract price, so the shop can recover about £250. A guarantee would make the adult guarantor liable, not Oscar."},{"t":"privity-third-parties","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Ella pays Bloom Florists £600 under a written contract by which Bloom agrees to deliver a bouquet every week for a year 'to my grandmother, Rose Hill, at 4 Elm Road'. The contract says nothing else about Rose's rights. Rose telephones Bloom after the first delivery to say she is delighted and accepts the arrangement. Two months later, Ella and Bloom agree to cancel the contract and Bloom refunds Ella part of the price. Rose did not agree to this.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Rose's position?","o":["Rose cannot enforce the contract, because she is not a party to it and gave no consideration.","Rose cannot enforce the contract, because the contract did not expressly state that she could enforce it.","Rose can enforce the contract only if she can show that she relied on the deliveries to her detriment.","Rose can enforce it, because she communicated her assent to Bloom before the contract was cancelled.","Rose cannot enforce the contract, because her assent was given by telephone rather than in writing."],"a":3,"e":"Under the **Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999 s.1(1)(b)**, a third party may enforce a term that **purports to confer a benefit** on them, provided they are **expressly identified** (s.1(3)) and nothing shows the parties did not intend it to be enforceable. Under **s.2(1)(a)**, once the third party has **communicated assent** to the promisor, the parties cannot cancel or vary the contract to remove her right without her **consent**, and assent may be given by words or conduct. No express right is needed under s.1(1)(b), and reliance is an alternative to assent, not an extra requirement."},{"t":"express-terms","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"A 99-year lease of a holiday flat, granted in 2000, requires the tenant to pay a service charge of £200 in the first year, 'increasing by 12% each year on a compound basis'. The service charge is now far higher than the actual cost of the services, and will become enormous in later years. The tenant argues that no sensible landlord and tenant could have intended this, so the clause should be read as allowing only an increase in line with the actual costs.\n\nHow is the court most likely to interpret the clause?","o":["According to the natural meaning of its clear words, even though the bargain has turned out badly for the tenant.","In line with commercial common sense, limiting the increase to the actual cost of services, because the literal result is absurd.","By reference to what the parties said during negotiations about the expected cost of services.","Against the landlord as the drafting party, so the increase is treated as 12% simple rather than compound.","By reference to how the parties have operated the charge since 2000."],"a":0,"e":"The court interprets a contract by asking what a reasonable person with the parties' **background knowledge** would understand the words to mean, but the **natural and ordinary meaning** of clear words is the most important factor. **Commercial common sense** cannot be used to rewrite clear language simply because the bargain has turned out badly (Arnold v Britton). **Pre-contract negotiations** and **subsequent conduct** are inadmissible to interpret a contract, and contra proferentem applies only to genuine ambiguity, which this clause does not have."},{"t":"implied-terms","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Ian, a teacher, sells his eight-year-old car privately to his neighbour, Jo, for £4,000. He describes it accurately as 'a 2018 Ford Focus, one owner'. He says nothing about its condition, and Jo does not ask. A week after the sale, the gearbox fails because of wear that neither of them knew about, and repairs will cost £1,800.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Jo's position?","o":["She has no claim, because a private seller gives no implied term as to satisfactory quality.","She can reject the car, because it was not of satisfactory quality under the Consumer Rights Act 2015.","She can claim damages under the Sale of Goods Act 1979, because the car was not of satisfactory quality.","She can reject the car, because it did not correspond with its description.","She can claim damages, because Ian impliedly warranted that the car was fit for driving."],"a":0,"e":"A private sale between two individuals is governed by the **Sale of Goods Act 1979**, not the Consumer Rights Act, which applies only to **trader-to-consumer** contracts. Under the SGA, the implied terms as to **satisfactory quality** (s.14(2)) and **fitness for purpose** (s.14(3)) apply only where the seller sells **in the course of a business**. A private seller implies terms only as to **title**, **description** and **sample**. The car matched its description, so Jo has no claim and the principle of caveat emptor applies."},{"t":"exemption-clauses","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Lucy, a consumer, books a hair-colouring appointment at Glow Salon. Before the treatment she signs a form stating: 'Glow Salon accepts no liability for any injury to clients, however caused, including by the negligence of its staff.' The stylist negligently leaves a chemical on Lucy's scalp for far too long, causing painful burns.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Glow Salon can rely on the clause?","o":["It can rely on it, because Lucy signed the form and is bound by its terms.","It can rely on it only if the clause is fair and was brought to Lucy's attention.","It cannot rely on it, because a trader cannot exclude or restrict liability to a consumer for personal injury caused by negligence.","It can rely on it only if it is reasonable under the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977.","It cannot rely on it, because Lucy is treated as having voluntarily accepted the risk only if she read the clause."],"a":2,"e":"Under the **Consumer Rights Act 2015 s.65**, a trader **cannot exclude or restrict** liability for **death or personal injury** resulting from **negligence**, by any contract term or notice. The clause is therefore **not binding** on Lucy, regardless of signature, fairness or prominence. UCTA no longer applies to consumer contracts, and under s.65 agreeing to or knowing of such a term is not itself acceptance of the risk."},{"t":"misrepresentation","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Victor is selling a small shop unit that is let to a tenant. During negotiations, the buyer, Amina, asks about the tenant. Victor replies that the tenant is 'a model tenant, no trouble at all'. Victor knows that the tenant is three months behind with the rent and has ignored several letters. Relying on Victor's reply, Amina buys the unit. The tenant soon leaves owing a large sum.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Victor's statement?","o":["It is not actionable, because it was a statement of opinion rather than fact.","It is actionable, because Victor's opinion implied that he knew facts justifying it, which was untrue.","It is not actionable, because it was mere sales talk of the kind no reasonable buyer would rely on.","It is not actionable, because Amina could have checked the rent records herself.","It is actionable only if Victor's statement was included as a term of the written contract."],"a":1,"e":"An honest opinion where both parties are equally placed is not a misrepresentation, but an opinion given by someone with **superior knowledge** carries an implied statement that the maker **knows facts justifying it** (Smith v Land and House Property Corp). Victor knew the tenant was in arrears, so the implied statement of fact was false. The statement was specific enough not to be mere puff, it is no defence that Amina could have checked (Redgrave v Hurd), and a misrepresentation need not become a term to be actionable."},{"t":"misrepresentation","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"In 2021, Rhys buys an antique clock from Tilly's Antiques for £6,000 after the dealer tells him, honestly and on the basis of a reputable expert's certificate, that it was made in the 1780s. The statement is not a term of the contract. In 2025, when Rhys has the clock valued for insurance, he discovers that it was made in the 1890s and is worth £1,500. He immediately tells the dealer he wants to return it and get his money back.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Rhys's position?","o":["He is unlikely to have any remedy: rescission is probably barred by lapse of time, and the dealer had reasonable grounds for its belief.","He can rescind, because for a misrepresentation time runs only from when the representee discovers the truth.","He can claim damages under s.2(1) of the Misrepresentation Act 1967, because the dealer cannot rely on the expert's certificate.","He can rescind, because the parties can still be restored to their original positions by returning the clock and the price.","He can claim damages for deceit, because the statement turned out to be false."],"a":0,"e":"Rescission of a contract for **non-fraudulent** misrepresentation can be barred by **lapse of time**, which may run from the **date of the contract** rather than from discovery (Leaf v International Galleries); four years is likely to be too long. The dealer honestly relied on a reputable expert, so it can probably prove **reasonable grounds** for its belief, defeating a claim under **s.2(1)**. Without fraud there is no claim in deceit, and the s.2(2) power to award damages in lieu of rescission is generally treated as needing a right to rescind that still exists. Restitution being physically possible does not overcome the lapse of time."},{"t":"mistake-duress-ui-illegality","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"A fraudster sets up an email address almost identical to that of Brightline Ltd, a well-known company that is an established customer of Comptek Ltd. Posing as Brightline's purchasing manager, the fraudster emails Comptek to order 20 laptops on credit, asking for delivery to a 'new Brightline warehouse'. Comptek, believing it is dealing with Brightline, delivers the laptops. The fraudster sells them to Dev, a second-hand dealer who buys in good faith and pays a fair price, then disappears.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Comptek can recover the laptops from Dev?","o":["Comptek cannot recover them, because the contract with the fraudster was voidable for fraud and Dev bought in good faith before it was rescinded.","Comptek cannot recover them, because a party who deals at a distance is presumed to intend to deal with whoever sent the order.","Comptek can recover them only if it can show Dev was careless in buying them.","Comptek can recover them, because the contract with the fraudster is void, so no ownership passed.","Comptek cannot recover them, because its own carelessness in not checking the email address prevents it relying on mistake."],"a":3,"e":"Where the parties deal **at a distance in writing** and the seller intends to contract only with a **specific, existing person** whom the rogue impersonates, the contract with the rogue is **void** for mistake as to identity (Cundy v Lindsay). A void contract passes **no ownership**, so the rogue had nothing to pass to Dev, and Comptek can recover the laptops despite Dev's good faith. The 'voidable' outcome, protecting an innocent buyer before rescission, applies mainly to **face-to-face** dealings. Dev's good faith and Comptek's lack of checks do not change the outcome."},{"t":"discharge-frustration","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Prime Kitchens agrees to supply and fit a new kitchen for Raj for £20,000, payable on completion. Prime finishes the work, but two cupboard doors are misaligned and a drawer sticks. It would cost £600 to put these defects right. Raj refuses to pay anything, arguing that Prime has not completed the work it promised.\n\nWhat is Prime most likely to be entitled to recover?","o":["Nothing, because payment was due only on completion of the work as promised.","£19,400, the price less the cost of remedying the defects.","£20,000, because the defects are too minor to affect its entitlement.","A reasonable sum for the work done, assessed on a quantum meruit.","£10,000, because the court will split the price where performance is imperfect."],"a":1,"e":"Under the **entire obligations** rule, a party who has not completed performance is generally not entitled to payment, but where the work is **substantially performed** with only minor defects, the claimant recovers the **contract price less the cost of remedying the defects** (Hoenig v Isaacs). The defects here are minor relative to a £20,000 kitchen, so Prime recovers £19,400. Raj is not entitled to withhold the whole price, and quantum meruit applies where there is no right to the contract price, such as where partial performance is accepted."},{"t":"damages","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Mercer Fabrics contracts to supply Lane Ltd, a clothing manufacturer, with 12 monthly deliveries of cloth on 60 days' credit. After the first delivery, Mercer wrongfully refuses to continue on credit, but offers to make the remaining deliveries at the contract price if Lane pays cash on each order. Lane, which could easily afford to pay cash, refuses and buys the cloth elsewhere at a higher market price.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Lane's claim for the extra cost of buying elsewhere?","o":["Lane can recover the full extra cost, because it is never required to accept an offer from the party in breach.","Lane can recover the full extra cost, because the burden is on Lane to show it acted reasonably and buying elsewhere was reasonable.","Lane cannot recover the extra cost, because it failed to mitigate its loss by refusing Mercer's reasonable offer to supply for cash.","Lane can recover only nominal damages, because Mercer did not refuse to deliver.","Lane can recover the full extra cost, because a party need not mitigate until the end of the contract period."],"a":2,"e":"The innocent party cannot recover loss it could have avoided by taking **reasonable steps** to mitigate. Refusing a **reasonable offer** from the party in breach can be a failure to mitigate (Payzu v Saunders): Lane could easily have paid cash and obtained the cloth at the contract price. The **burden** is on the **defendant** to prove failure to mitigate, and Mercer can show it on these facts. Lane may still recover any genuine loss caused by losing the credit terms, such as financing costs."},{"t":"equitable-remedies-guarantees","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Marco, a celebrated chef, signs a two-year contract with The Larder, a London restaurant, agreeing to work as its head chef and 'not to cook professionally at any other restaurant in London' during that period. Six months later, he leaves to become head chef at a rival restaurant across the road. Marco could easily find well-paid work as a chef outside London.\n\nWhich order is The Larder most likely to obtain?","o":["Specific performance compelling Marco to return to work as its head chef.","An injunction restraining Marco from working as a chef anywhere for the rest of the contract period.","No order, because the court will never grant an injunction in a contract for personal services.","A mandatory injunction requiring Marco to return to work for The Larder.","An injunction restraining Marco from working as a chef at any other London restaurant during the contract period."],"a":4,"e":"The court will not order **specific performance** of a contract of **personal service**, and will not grant an injunction that would in practice **compel** the defendant to work for the claimant. But it will usually enforce an **express negative stipulation** by a **prohibitory injunction** where the defendant can still earn a living in other ways (Lumley v Wagner; Warner Bros v Nelson). Marco can work as a chef outside London, so an injunction limited to the terms of his promise is likely. An injunction going wider than his promise would not be granted."},{"t":"offer-acceptance","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Wellspring Charity publishes a notice offering £2,000 to the first person who completes a 100-mile sponsored walk along a canal by the end of the month. Hamid sees the notice and sets off. When he has walked 60 miles, Wellspring publishes a further notice, which Hamid sees, withdrawing the offer. Hamid completes the walk anyway, the first person to do so.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Hamid is entitled to the £2,000?","o":["He is not entitled to it, because an offer can be revoked at any time before acceptance and he had not completed the walk.","He is entitled to it, because the offer could not be revoked once he had started the walk.","He is not entitled to it, because he did not communicate his acceptance to Wellspring before starting.","He is entitled to it only if he had told Wellspring that he was taking part before the offer was withdrawn.","He is not entitled to it, because the notice was only an invitation to treat."],"a":1,"e":"A notice promising payment for completing an act is a **unilateral offer**, accepted by **performing** the act; no prior communication of acceptance is needed. Although an offer can normally be revoked at any time before acceptance, a unilateral offer **cannot be revoked once the offeree has started performing**: the offeree must be given a reasonable opportunity to complete (Errington v Errington; Daulia v Four Millbank Nominees). Hamid had started and then completed the walk, so he is entitled to the £2,000."},{"t":"consideration","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Ben worked for Ava's company for 20 years before retiring. A month after he retires, Ava writes to him: 'To help you while you retrain, I will pay you £400 a month for the next year.' Ben did not ask for anything and gives nothing in return. Relying on the letter, he stops looking for paid work and enrols on an unpaid course. After three months, Ava stops paying. Ben wants to sue her for the remaining nine payments.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ben's position?","o":["He cannot enforce it, because he gave no consideration and estoppel cannot found a claim.","He can enforce the promise under promissory estoppel, because he relied on it by stopping his job search.","He can enforce the promise, because his 20 years of service were good consideration for it.","He can enforce the promise, because Ava's letter was in writing and signed.","He can enforce the promise, because enrolling on the course was an act done at Ava's request."],"a":0,"e":"A promise is enforceable only if supported by **consideration** or made by **deed**. Ben's 20 years of service was **past consideration**: it was not done in return for the promise, nor at Ava's request on the understanding that this payment would follow. **Promissory estoppel** is a **shield, not a sword** (Combe v Combe): it can stop a party enforcing strict rights in an existing relationship but cannot create a new cause of action. A signed letter is not a deed, and Ava did not ask Ben to enrol on the course."},{"t":"intention-certainty-capacity","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Linnet Bank lends £2 million to Pyke Ltd. As part of the arrangement, Pyke's parent company, Pyke Holdings plc, writes to the bank: 'It is our policy to ensure that Pyke Ltd is at all times in a position to meet its liabilities to you.' Pyke Holdings had refused to give a formal guarantee. Two years later, Pyke Holdings changes its policy and Pyke Ltd goes into liquidation owing the bank £1.5 million.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Pyke Holdings is liable to the bank?","o":["It is liable, because in a commercial context there is a strong presumption that the parties intended to create legal relations.","It is not liable, because the letter was a statement of its present policy, not a contractual promise about its future conduct.","It is liable, because the letter was a guarantee of Pyke Ltd's debts.","It is not liable, because a letter of comfort can never have contractual effect.","It is liable, because the bank relied on the letter in making the loan."],"a":1,"e":"In a commercial context, intention to create legal relations is **presumed**, but the question first is whether the words amount to a **contractual promise** at all. A **letter of comfort** that merely states the parent's **present policy**, especially after it has refused a guarantee, is not a promise about future conduct, so there is nothing to enforce (Kleinwort Benson v Malaysia Mining Corp). A letter of comfort is not automatically non-binding: one containing a clear promise about the future could be enforced. Reliance alone cannot turn a statement of policy into a contract."},{"t":"privity-third-parties","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Northgate Developments engages MainBuild Ltd to construct an office block. Clause 18 of their contract states: 'Northgate shall not bring any claim in negligence against any subcontractor engaged by MainBuild in connection with the works.' MainBuild engages Brickworks Ltd as its masonry subcontractor. Brickworks negligently damages the building's facade, and Northgate sues Brickworks in negligence for the cost of repair.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Brickworks can rely on clause 18?","o":["Brickworks cannot rely on clause 18, because it is not a party to the contract between Northgate and MainBuild.","Brickworks cannot rely on clause 18, because it is not named in the contract.","Brickworks can rely on it, because the clause purports to benefit an identified class of which it is a member.","Brickworks cannot rely on clause 18, because the 1999 Act allows third parties to enforce positive rights but not to rely on exclusion clauses.","Brickworks can rely on clause 18 only if MainBuild first assigns the benefit of the clause to it."],"a":2,"e":"Under the **1999 Act s.1(1)(b)**, a third party can enforce a term that **purports to confer a benefit** on it, and under **s.1(6)** 'enforcing' includes **relying on an exclusion or limitation clause**. Under **s.1(3)**, the third party may be identified as a **member of a class** ('any subcontractor'), so it need not be named or even exist at the time of the contract. No assignment is needed: the Act gives Brickworks the right directly. (Because the clause restricts Brickworks' own business liability for negligence, it will still have to pass the UCTA s.2(2) reasonableness test; s.7(2) disapplies s.2(2) only where a third party sues to enforce a contractual duty.)"},{"t":"express-terms","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Kim, a teacher, visits Swift Motors, a used car dealer, to buy a van for her weekend catering business. The salesman tells her, 'This van has done only 30,000 miles since its engine was replaced.' Kim says the low mileage is the main reason she is interested. She signs a short order form that does not mention the mileage. In fact the replacement engine has done 90,000 miles, and the van is worth much less than she paid.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the status of the salesman's statement?","o":["It is a mere representation, because it was not included in the written order form.","It is a mere representation, because Kim could have had the van inspected by a mechanic.","It is likely to be a term, because a dealer made it on a point Kim said was important to her.","It is a mere sales puff, because statements about mileage are a matter of estimate.","It is a term only if the salesman knew the statement was false."],"a":2,"e":"Whether a statement is a **term** or a **representation** depends on the parties' intention, judged **objectively**. Important factors include the **importance** of the statement to the recipient and whether the maker had **special knowledge**: a statement by a **dealer** to a buyer, on a point the buyer makes clear is vital, is likely to be a term (Dick Bentley v Harold Smith; contrast Oscar Chess v Williams, where a private seller spoke to a dealer). Its omission from a short order form is not decisive, and whether the salesman knew it was false goes to misrepresentation, not to whether it was a term."},{"t":"express-terms","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Under a contract for the sale of 5,000 tonnes of wheat, the buyer, Arden Grain, must give the seller, Corvex, 'at least 15 days' notice of the readiness of the vessel' to load at the port. The contract does not say what happens if this obligation is broken. Arden gives notice only 12 days before the vessel arrives. Corvex suffers no real loss from the late notice but terminates the contract because market prices have risen.\n\nWas Corvex entitled to terminate the contract?","o":["No, because the clause is an innominate term and the breach did not deprive Corvex of substantially the whole benefit of the contract.","No, because the clause is a warranty, so Corvex can only claim damages.","No, because a party cannot terminate for a breach that causes it no loss.","Yes, but only because the contract was for the sale of goods, so every term is a condition.","Yes, because a time clause of this kind in a commercial contract is likely to be treated as a condition, so any breach allows termination."],"a":4,"e":"Previous case law may classify a type of term. **Time clauses** in commercial contracts, such as notice periods in sale of goods contracts, are usually treated as **conditions**, because certainty is vital to traders and the other party needs to know where it stands (Bunge Corp v Tradax Export). Breach of a **condition** allows termination **however minor its consequences**, and Corvex's motive is irrelevant. Not every term in a sale of goods contract is a condition; many are innominate."},{"t":"implied-terms","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Freya, a consumer, buys a laptop from TechWorld for £1,000. It is delivered on 1 February. On 10 May it starts shutting down repeatedly because of a faulty component. Freya asks TechWorld to repair it, and TechWorld returns it on 1 June, but the fault reappears within a week. On 10 June Freya tells TechWorld she wants to give it back and get her money back.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Freya's rights under the Consumer Rights Act 2015?","o":["She cannot reject the laptop, because more than 30 days have passed since delivery.","She can exercise the final right to reject, but her refund will be reduced to reflect her use of the laptop since February.","She must allow TechWorld a second attempt at repair before she can reject the laptop.","She can claim a price reduction only, because she has used the laptop for more than three months.","She can exercise the final right to reject and is entitled to a full refund with no deduction for her use of the laptop."],"a":4,"e":"After the **30-day** short-term right to reject has expired, the consumer's next remedy is **repair or replacement**. If **one** repair or replacement fails, the consumer can claim a **price reduction** or exercise the **final right to reject** (s.24). On final rejection, the refund may be reduced for use, but **no deduction** is allowed if the right is exercised **within six months of delivery** (other than for motor vehicles). Freya rejects on 10 June, within six months of 1 February, so she gets a full refund."},{"t":"exemption-clauses","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Hadron plc, a large manufacturer, buys industrial pumps from Pumpco Ltd under a contract negotiated over several months by both companies' lawyers. In return for a 10% discount, Hadron agreed to a clause limiting Pumpco's liability for defective pumps to the price paid (£300,000). Hadron could readily have insured against losses from pump failure. The pumps are not of satisfactory quality and Hadron suffers losses of £2 million.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Pumpco can rely on the limitation clause?","o":["It cannot rely on it, because liability for breach of the implied term as to satisfactory quality can never be excluded or limited.","It can rely on it without any test of reasonableness, because the contract was negotiated rather than made on standard terms.","It can probably rely on it, because the clause is likely to satisfy the reasonableness test on these facts.","It cannot rely on it, because a limitation to the contract price is always unreasonable where the loss is much greater.","It can rely on it only if Hadron, as the party challenging the clause, proves that it is unreasonable."],"a":2,"e":"In a business sale, **UCTA s.6(1A)** allows liability for breach of the implied terms as to **description, quality, fitness and sample** to be excluded or restricted only if the term is **reasonable**, and this applies whether or not the contract is on standard terms. Under **s.11**, reasonableness is judged at the **time of contracting**, and the **burden** is on the party relying on the clause, here Pumpco. Factors such as **equal bargaining power**, legal advice, an **inducement** (the discount) and the customer's ability to **insure** point strongly towards reasonableness (Watford Electronics v Sanderson). Only the implied term as to **title** cannot be excluded at all."},{"t":"misrepresentation","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"In January, Dr Price, who is selling her dental practice, tells a prospective buyer, Dr Moss, that the practice's annual takings are about £400,000. This is true at the time. In March, Dr Price falls ill and many patients leave, so that by June the takings have fallen to an annual rate of £200,000. Dr Price says nothing further, and in June Dr Moss buys the practice relying on the January figure.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Dr Price's position?","o":["She is not liable, because her statement was true when she made it.","She is not liable, because there is no general duty to disclose facts to the other party.","She is not liable, because Dr Moss should have checked the up-to-date accounts before buying.","She is liable, because she should have corrected a statement that became false before the contract.","She is liable only if the January figure was repeated in the written contract."],"a":3,"e":"Silence is not generally a misrepresentation, but there are exceptions. A statement that was **true when made** but becomes **false before the contract** is concluded must be **corrected**, because it is treated as a continuing representation (With v O'Flanagan). Dr Price's failure to update Dr Moss is therefore a misrepresentation that induced the contract. It is no defence that Dr Moss could have discovered the truth (Redgrave v Hurd), and a misrepresentation need not be repeated in the contract."},{"t":"mistake-duress-ui-illegality","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Laura sells a painting to Simon for £30,000. Both believe it is by a well-known Victorian artist, although Laura makes no statement or promise about who painted it, and the contract says nothing on the point. Neither of them has the painting checked. A year later, an expert establishes that it is a skilful copy painted in the 1950s, worth about £2,000. Simon argues that the contract is void for common mistake.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the contract?","o":["The contract is valid, because the mistake goes only to the painting's quality.","The contract is void, because both parties shared a fundamental mistake about the painting.","The contract is void, because the painting was worth far less than the price paid.","The contract is voidable for misrepresentation, so Simon can rescind it.","The contract is void, because the subject matter of the contract did not exist."],"a":0,"e":"A common mistake as to **quality** makes a contract void only if it makes the subject matter **essentially and radically different** from what the parties believed, a test that is almost never satisfied (Bell v Lever Brothers; Great Peace Shipping). The parties contracted for and received this particular painting; a mistake about its attribution and value goes to quality, not identity. Laura made no statement, so there is no misrepresentation. The painting existed, so this is not a case of res extincta."},{"t":"mistake-duress-ui-illegality","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"During negotiations, Kestrel Wool Ltd and Fenwick Textiles discuss prices for wool exclusively 'per tonne', which is also the usual practice in their trade. Kestrel then emails Fenwick: 'We offer you 20 tonnes of merino wool at £18 per kilo.' The market price is about £18,000 per tonne, so the email offers the wool at about one-thousandth of what the parties had discussed. Fenwick, realising the mistake, immediately replies, 'We accept.'\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether there is a contract at £18 per kilo?","o":["There is no contract on those terms, because Fenwick knew that Kestrel was mistaken about the terms of its own offer.","There is a contract at £18 per kilo, because the court looks only at the objective words of the offer and acceptance.","There is a contract at £18 per kilo, because Kestrel's mistake was its own fault.","There is a contract at £18,000 per tonne, because the court will rectify the email to reflect the parties' negotiations.","There is a contract at £18 per kilo, but Kestrel can rescind it for misrepresentation."],"a":0,"e":"Where one party is mistaken about the **terms** of the contract and the other party **knows** (or ought to know) of the mistake, there is **no contract on those terms** (Hartog v Colin and Shields). Fenwick could not reasonably have believed Kestrel meant to sell at a thousandth of the negotiated price, and it knew the offer was a mistake. Snapping up an offer known to be mistaken does not create a binding contract. Rectification does not create a contract at a different price where none was agreed, and Fenwick made no misrepresentation."},{"t":"discharge-frustration","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Ardent Exports agrees to sell 1,000 tonnes of fertiliser to a buyer in Hamburg, shipment from Mumbai during March. Both parties assume the goods will be shipped via the Suez Canal, but the contract does not specify a route. In February, the canal is closed indefinitely. The only alternative route, around the Cape of Good Hope, will double Ardent's shipping costs and take three weeks longer, although the fertiliser will not deteriorate. Ardent claims the contract is frustrated.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the contract?","o":["It is frustrated, because the route both parties contemplated has become impossible.","It is not frustrated, because the longer route makes performance more costly, not radically different.","It is frustrated, because doubling the cost of performance makes it commercially unreasonable to require performance.","It is frustrated, because the closure of the canal was not the fault of either party.","It is not frustrated, because frustration applies only where the subject matter of the contract is destroyed."],"a":1,"e":"Frustration requires an unforeseen event, not the fault of either party, which makes performance **impossible, illegal or radically different** from what was contemplated. **Increased cost, delay or difficulty** is not enough (Tsakiroglou v Noblee Thorl; Davis Contractors v Fareham UDC). Since no route was a term of the contract and the goods can still be delivered by the Cape route without deteriorating, performance is not radically different. Lack of fault is necessary but not sufficient, and frustration is not limited to destruction of the subject matter."},{"t":"damages","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Bolt Engineering contracts to sell and install a large printing press for Inkwell Printers, knowing that Inkwell needs it urgently to expand its business. Bolt is not told about any particular orders. Bolt delivers the press five months late. During the delay, Inkwell loses (i) the ordinary profits it would have made from extra printing work, and (ii) an exceptionally lucrative contract to print a national magazine, which it had secured after the contract with Bolt was signed.\n\nWhich of these losses can Inkwell recover from Bolt?","o":["Both losses, because both were caused by Bolt's late delivery.","Neither loss, because loss of profits is never recoverable for late delivery of machinery.","Only the magazine contract, because it was a specific, identifiable loss.","Only the ordinary lost profits, because the magazine contract was not in contemplation when the contract was made.","Both losses, because Bolt knew Inkwell needed the press urgently and so could foresee any lost business."],"a":3,"e":"Under **Hadley v Baxendale**, damages are recoverable for loss arising **naturally** from the breach (limb 1) or loss in the **reasonable contemplation of both parties** at the time of contracting because of **special circumstances** communicated (limb 2). Bolt knew the press was needed urgently, so **ordinary lost profits** were within its contemplation, but an **exceptionally lucrative** contract it was not told about was not (Victoria Laundry v Newman Industries). Remoteness is judged at the **time the contract was made**, and the magazine contract was secured only afterwards."},{"t":"equitable-remedies-guarantees","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Brickworks Ltd wants to buy materials on credit from Supply Hub. Supply Hub insists on personal backing from Brickworks' director, Sam Reid. Sam sends Supply Hub an email saying: 'If Brickworks Ltd fails to pay any of your invoices, I will personally pay them. Regards, Sam Reid.' Supply Hub then supplies materials worth £40,000. Brickworks goes into liquidation without paying, and Sam refuses to pay, saying he never signed anything.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Sam's liability?","o":["Sam is not liable, because a guarantee must be signed by hand on a formal document.","Sam is liable, because his promise is an indemnity, which does not need to be in writing.","Sam is not liable, because there was no consideration for his promise.","Sam is liable only if Supply Hub can show it relied on his promise to its detriment.","Sam is liable, because his email is a guarantee signed with his typed name."],"a":4,"e":"'If Brickworks fails to pay, I will' is a **guarantee**: a **secondary** liability to answer for another's debt. Under the **Statute of Frauds 1677 s.4**, a guarantee must be evidenced in **writing signed** by the guarantor, but the writing can be informal, and an email signed with a **typed name** can satisfy the requirement (Golden Ocean Group v Salgaocar Mining). There is consideration in Supply Hub supplying goods on credit to Brickworks at Sam's request. Because it is a guarantee, not an indemnity, the writing requirement matters, but it is met."},{"t":"offer-acceptance","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Juno grants Kai an option to buy her workshop for £250,000. The option agreement states that it must be exercised 'by notice in writing to Juno at her business address' by 30 September. On 25 September, Kai posts a properly addressed and stamped letter exercising the option. The letter is lost in the post and never reaches Juno. On 2 October, Juno sells the workshop to someone else.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Kai validly exercised the option?","o":["He did, because under the postal rule acceptance took effect when his letter was properly posted.","He did, because a letter lost in the post is treated as delivered once it has been properly posted.","He did not, because the option required the notice actually to reach Juno.","He did not, because an option can only be exercised by personal delivery.","He did, because Juno had no right to sell to someone else while the option was outstanding."],"a":2,"e":"The **postal rule** makes an acceptance effective on posting, but it is **excluded** where the offer, expressly or impliedly, requires **actual receipt**. A requirement of 'notice in writing to' the offeror means the notice must actually reach her (Holwell Securities v Hughes). Kai's letter never arrived, so the option was not exercised in time and Juno was free to sell. Delivery by post would have been fine had it arrived; personal delivery was not required."},{"t":"consideration","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Lyra, a singer, has a contract with a promoter to perform at a concert at the Rowan Hall. Shortly before the concert, the owner of the Rowan Hall, worried about ticket refunds if she pulls out, promises Lyra a £1,000 bonus if she performs as planned. Lyra performs, but the hall owner refuses to pay the bonus, arguing that Lyra was already bound to perform.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Lyra can enforce the hall owner's promise?","o":["She cannot enforce it, because she did nothing more than she was already contractually bound to do.","She can enforce it only if the hall owner obtained a practical benefit and there was no economic duress.","She cannot enforce it, because the hall owner was not a party to her contract with the promoter.","She can enforce it only if she can show that she had doubts about performing before the promise was made.","She can enforce it, because performing a contractual duty owed to a third party is good consideration for a new promise."],"a":4,"e":"Performing an existing duty owed **to the promisor** is traditionally not consideration, but performing (or promising to perform) a contractual duty owed to a **third party** **is** good consideration for a new promise (Shadwell v Shadwell; The Eurymedon). Lyra's duty was owed to the promoter, not the hall owner, so her performance was consideration for the hall owner's promise. The Williams v Roffey practical benefit test is needed only where the existing duty is owed to the promisor himself."},{"t":"intention-certainty-capacity","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Haven Coaches agrees to buy all the diesel it needs for three years from Penn Fuels 'at a price to be agreed by the parties from time to time'. The contract adds that 'any dispute arising under this agreement shall be referred to arbitration'. The parties operate the agreement without difficulty for two years. Penn then finds a more profitable customer and tells Haven that there was never a binding contract because the price was not agreed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the agreement?","o":["It is not binding, because an agreement to agree the price in future is too uncertain to be enforced.","It is not binding, because the parties had no intention to create legal relations.","It is binding, but Penn can withdraw from it at any time on reasonable notice.","It is likely to be binding, because the arbitration clause provides machinery for fixing the price.","It is not binding, because the price is an essential term that a court can never fill in."],"a":3,"e":"An **agreement to agree** an essential term such as the price is usually not binding, but the court will uphold an agreement where there is **machinery for fixing the term**, such as an **arbitration clause** (Foley v Classique Coaches). The fact that the parties have **acted on the agreement** for two years also shows they thought they had a deal, and the courts try hard to uphold such agreements. A commercial agreement is presumed to be intended to create legal relations."},{"t":"privity-third-parties","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Marina Holdings is planning to repaint its boat sheds. Coastal Paints Ltd's sales director tells Marina that Coastal's paint will 'last at least five years in a marine environment'. Relying on this, Marina instructs its painting contractor to buy Coastal's paint for the job, and the contractor buys it from Coastal. The paint starts peeling after one year and the sheds have to be repainted.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Marina's claim against Coastal?","o":["Marina has no claim against Coastal, because the contract of sale was between Coastal and the contractor.","Marina can sue Coastal under the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999, because it was the intended user of the paint.","Marina can sue Coastal only in negligence, for the cost of repainting.","Marina can sue its contractor, but not Coastal, for breach of the implied term as to quality.","Marina can sue Coastal on a collateral contract, supported by Marina's instruction to buy Coastal's paint."],"a":4,"e":"A **collateral contract** can exist alongside the main contract where one party's promise is given in return for the other **causing the main contract to be made** (Shanklin Pier v Detel Products). Coastal promised the paint would last five years, and Marina provided consideration by instructing its contractor to buy Coastal's paint, so Marina can sue on that promise. The 1999 Act does not help because the sale contract did not identify Marina or purport to benefit it. A negligence claim for paint that has failed only itself would be for pure economic loss."},{"t":"express-terms","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Prop Hire Ltd delivers 40 vintage props to Stagecraft Ltd, a theatre company, which had ordered them by phone for a production. The delivery note, which Stagecraft's manager signs only to confirm receipt, contains nine conditions in small print. Condition 7 states that a fee of £100 per prop per day is payable for late return, far more than the usual charge in the trade of about £5. Nothing is done to draw attention to it. Stagecraft returns the props two weeks late and Prop Hire claims £56,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether condition 7 is part of the contract?","o":["It is probably not incorporated, because it is onerous and was not fairly brought to Stagecraft's attention.","It is part of the contract, because Stagecraft's manager signed the delivery note.","It is part of the contract, because businesses are presumed to read all the conditions on delivery documents.","It is part of the contract, but the fee is unenforceable as a penalty.","It is not part of the contract, because a delivery note can never contain contractual terms."],"a":0,"e":"An unsigned term is incorporated only if **reasonable notice** of it is given, and **the more onerous or unusual the term, the more clearly it must be drawn to the other party's attention** (Interfoto v Stiletto; the 'red hand' rule). A fee of £100 per prop per day, twenty times the usual rate, is onerous, and nothing was done to highlight it. Signing to acknowledge receipt after the contract was made by phone does not incorporate terms retrospectively. If condition 7 is not incorporated, Prop Hire can recover only a reasonable charge, so the penalty rule does not arise."},{"t":"implied-terms","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Orbit Ltd hires specialist audio equipment from Soundbase Ltd under a two-year contract, paying an annual fee of £24,000 in advance each January. The contract allows Orbit to terminate on one month's notice at any time but says nothing about refunds. Orbit gives notice ending the contract on 31 March, and asks for a refund of the fee for April to December. A refund would be reasonable and fair, and Orbit argues that a term should be implied requiring it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether a term requiring a refund will be implied?","o":["It will be implied, because a reasonable person would think it fair that Orbit should not pay for months it does not use.","It will be implied, because the parties would have agreed to it had it been suggested to them.","It will not be implied, because the contract works without it, and it is not enough that the term would be reasonable or fair.","It will be implied in law, because a refund term is a standard incident of all hire contracts.","It will be implied, because the court must resolve any gap in a contract in favour of the paying party."],"a":2,"e":"A term is implied in fact only if it is **necessary for business efficacy** or **so obvious that it goes without saying**; it is **not enough** that it would be **reasonable or fair**, or that the parties would have agreed to it if asked (Marks and Spencer v BNP Paribas). The contract works perfectly well without a refund term, since the parties allocated the fee annually in advance and allowed termination on notice. There is no rule that refund terms are a standard incident of all hire contracts, and courts do not fill gaps simply in favour of one side."},{"t":"exemption-clauses","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Ruth, a consumer, joins PowerFit gym on a 12-month contract at £50 a month, using the gym's standard terms. One term, in the same small print as the rest of the contract, states that a member who cancels early, for any reason, must immediately pay all the remaining monthly fees for the 12-month period. Ruth moves to another city after two months and cancels. PowerFit demands £500.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court will assess this term?","o":["It cannot be assessed for fairness, because it concerns the price payable for the gym membership.","It is automatically void, because terms requiring payment on cancellation are on the Consumer Rights Act 2015 blacklist.","It is valid, because Ruth agreed to the term when she signed up.","It must be tested for reasonableness under the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977.","It can be assessed for fairness, and as a disproportionate cancellation charge it may be unfair and not binding."],"a":4,"e":"Under **CRA 2015 s.62**, a term is **unfair** if, contrary to **good faith**, it causes a **significant imbalance** in the parties' rights to the consumer's detriment, and an unfair term is **not binding** on the consumer. **Schedule 2 Part 1** (the **grey list**) includes terms requiring a consumer who cancels to pay a **disproportionately high** sum. The **core terms exemption** (s.64) covers only the main subject matter and the adequacy of the price, applies only if the term is **transparent and prominent**, and never covers a grey-list term. The term is not blacklisted, and UCTA does not apply to consumer contracts."},{"t":"misrepresentation","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Quentin is buying a haulage business from Hale Transport. Hale's director tells him the business makes annual profits of £300,000. Quentin instructs his own accountants to examine the books in detail. They report that the figure is broadly accurate, and Quentin buys the business relying on their report. In fact, the profits are only £150,000, which the accountants carelessly failed to spot.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Quentin's claim for misrepresentation against Hale?","o":["It is likely to succeed, because it is no defence that the representee could have discovered the truth.","It is likely to succeed, because the statement was a material one that would have induced a reasonable person to buy.","It is likely to fail, because a statement about profits is an opinion rather than a statement of fact.","It is likely to fail, because Quentin relied on his own accountants rather than on Hale's statement.","It is likely to succeed, but only if Hale's director knew the figure was false."],"a":3,"e":"A misrepresentation must have **induced** the contract. There is no inducement where the representee **relied on its own investigations** or advisers instead of the statement (Attwood v Small). This differs from the situation where the representee **could** have checked but did not, which is no defence (Redgrave v Hurd). Quentin actually had the figures checked and relied on his accountants' report, so his remedy, if any, is against them in negligence; a profit figure is a statement of fact, so opinion is not the problem."},{"t":"mistake-duress-ui-illegality","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Nina sells her bakery business in a market town, including its goodwill, to Owen for £150,000. In the sale agreement, she promises not to open or work in a bakery within two miles of the shop for three years. Eighteen months later, Nina opens a new bakery half a mile away and many of her old customers follow her. Nina argues that her promise is void as a restraint of trade.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Nina's promise?","o":["It is void, because any promise restricting a person's freedom to work is contrary to public policy.","It is void, because its only purpose is to protect Owen from competition.","It is enforceable only if Owen can show that Nina is using confidential recipes from the business.","It is likely to be enforceable, because it reasonably protects the goodwill Owen bought.","It is void, because restraints are enforceable only in employment contracts."],"a":3,"e":"A restraint of trade is **void** as contrary to public policy **unless** it protects a **legitimate interest** and is **reasonable** in scope. The **goodwill** of a business that has been sold is a recognised legitimate interest, because the buyer pays for the customer connection, and restraints in business sales are viewed more favourably than those in employment. A two-mile, three-year restriction on a local bakery is likely to be reasonable. The restraint protects the goodwill Owen paid for, not mere freedom from competition."},{"t":"discharge-frustration","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Fable Press commissions Zara to write a book on the history of tea for its new 'Food Heritage' series, for a fee of £8,000 payable on delivery of the completed manuscript. After Zara has completed most of the research and written half the book, Fable Press decides to abandon the series and tells her it will not publish or pay for the book.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Zara's position?","o":["She can recover nothing, because payment was due only on delivery of the completed manuscript.","She can claim a reasonable sum for her work, because Fable Press prevented her from completing it.","She can recover the full £8,000 by completing the book and delivering it anyway.","She can recover half of the fee, because she had substantially performed the contract.","She can recover nothing, because the abandonment of the series frustrated the contract."],"a":1,"e":"Under the **entire obligations** rule, payment due only on completion is normally not earned by partial performance. But where the other party **prevents** completion, the claimant can claim a **quantum meruit** for work done, or damages for breach (Planché v Colburn). Writing half a book is not substantial performance, and a party's own decision to abandon a project is not a frustrating event. Completing the work regardless would require Fable's cooperation in accepting delivery, and Zara is unlikely to have a legitimate interest in doing so."},{"t":"discharge-frustration","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Skyline Cruises agrees to hire a boat to Pip for a day, with crew, for £3,000, 'for the purpose of viewing the Seaview Air Show and for a day's cruise around the bay'. Two days before the event, the air show is cancelled because of a dispute with the organisers. The bay is still open and the boat and crew are available. Pip claims the contract is frustrated.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the contract?","o":["It is not frustrated, because the cruise around the bay can still take place.","It is frustrated, because the main purpose of the hire, viewing the air show, can no longer be achieved.","It is frustrated, because the cancellation was outside the control of both parties.","It is not frustrated, because frustration never applies to contracts for the hire of a boat.","It is frustrated, but Skyline can keep the whole fee to cover its expenses."],"a":0,"e":"A contract can be frustrated where an event that is the **sole foundation** of the contract does not happen (Krell v Henry). But where the contract has **other purposes** that can still be performed, it is not frustrated (Herne Bay Steam Boat Co v Hutton). Here the hire was also for a day's cruise around the bay, which is still possible, so the contract has not become radically different. Lack of fault is necessary but not sufficient, and there is no rule excluding boat hire contracts from frustration."},{"t":"damages","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Halcyon Homes, a developer, engages Turret Construction to build a block of flats under a contract negotiated by both companies with legal advice. The contract provides that Turret must pay £5,000 for each week of delay in completion. When the contract was made, Halcyon's likely losses from delay, including lost rents and finance costs, were estimated at about £4,000 to £6,000 a week. Turret finishes four weeks late. Halcyon's actual loss turns out to be only £2,000 a week.\n\nWhat can Halcyon recover from Turret for the delay?","o":["£20,000, because the clause is a valid liquidated damages clause, not a penalty.","£8,000, because a liquidated damages clause cannot be enforced where it exceeds the actual loss.","£8,000, because the clause is a penalty and Halcyon can recover only its actual loss.","Nothing, because a penalty clause makes the whole contract unenforceable.","£20,000, but only if Halcyon proves its actual loss was at least that amount."],"a":0,"e":"A clause fixing a sum payable on breach is a **penalty** only if it imposes a detriment **out of all proportion** to the innocent party's **legitimate interest** in performance (Cavendish v Makdessi; ParkingEye v Beavis). £5,000 a week was in line with the estimated losses at the time of contracting, and in a **negotiated** contract between advised parties there is a strong presumption that the parties are the best judges of what is legitimate. A valid clause entitles the innocent party to the **agreed sum** without proving loss, even if actual loss turns out to be lower."},{"t":"damages","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Elsa contracts with PoolPro Ltd to build a swimming pool in her garden with a maximum depth of 2.3 metres, as she wants to be able to dive. PoolPro builds the pool with a maximum depth of 1.9 metres. The pool is safe for diving, and its shallower depth makes no difference to the value of Elsa's property. The only way to achieve the specified depth would be to demolish and rebuild the pool at a cost of £45,000.\n\nWhat is Elsa most likely to recover from PoolPro?","o":["The £45,000 cost of rebuilding, because she is entitled to be put in the position she would have been in had the contract been performed.","A modest sum for loss of amenity, but not the £45,000 cost of rebuilding, because that would be out of all proportion to the benefit of curing the defect.","Nothing, because the pool is safe and there is no difference in the value of her property.","The £45,000 cost of rebuilding, because she has a personal preference for a deeper pool.","The full price she paid for the pool, because PoolPro did not provide what she contracted for."],"a":1,"e":"Expectation damages may be measured by **difference in value** or **cost of cure**, but cost of cure will not be awarded where it is **wholly unreasonable** or **out of all proportion** to the benefit of curing the breach (Ruxley Electronics v Forsyth). Rebuilding at £45,000 for a pool that is safe and no less valuable would be disproportionate. However, Elsa did not get the specific thing she bargained for, so she may recover a modest sum for **loss of amenity**. She received a usable pool, so she cannot recover the whole price."},{"t":"equitable-remedies-guarantees","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Brookside Shopping Centre lets its largest unit to Valumart, a supermarket chain, on a 25-year lease containing a covenant by Valumart to keep the supermarket open for retail trade during normal business hours throughout the lease. Ten years into the lease, the store is making a loss and Valumart closes it. Other tenants complain that their trade has fallen. Brookside seeks an order compelling Valumart to reopen and run the store for the rest of the lease.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the court is likely to grant specific performance?","o":["It is likely to do so, because damages for the loss of trade in the centre would be difficult to calculate.","It is unlikely to, because the order would require constant supervision of a business over many years.","It is likely to do so, because specific performance is available as of right for breach of a covenant in a lease of land.","It is unlikely to do so, because a covenant to keep a shop open is void as a restraint of trade.","It is likely to do so, because Valumart deliberately broke the covenant to save money."],"a":1,"e":"**Specific performance** is **discretionary** and is refused where an order would require the court to supervise a **continuing activity** over a long period, such as running a business (Co-operative Insurance v Argyll Stores). Repeated applications to enforce such an order, and forcing a business to trade at a loss, make the remedy unsuitable, so Brookside is left to **damages**. Contracts involving land do not give an automatic right to specific performance of every covenant, and the defendant's deliberate breach does not overcome the supervision bar."}],"tort":[{"t":"negligence","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"A fire breaks out in a warehouse owned by Calder Storage Ltd. The fire service attends promptly. The senior fire officer, wrongly believing that water from the sprinklers is making the fire harder to tackle, orders the building's working sprinkler system to be turned off. The fire then spreads rapidly and destroys the whole warehouse. Expert evidence shows that, had the sprinklers stayed on, most of the building would have been saved.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the fire service owed Calder Storage a duty of care?","o":["No duty was owed, because the fire service owes no duty to property owners in the way it chooses to fight a fire.","A duty was owed, because by attending the fire the fire service assumed responsibility for saving the building.","No duty was owed, because public authorities exercising statutory functions are immune from claims in negligence.","A duty was owed, because turning off the sprinklers was a positive act that made the damage worse.","A duty was owed only if the court, applying the three Caparo factors afresh, decides it would be fair, just and reasonable to impose one."],"a":3,"e":"Public bodies follow the same rules as private individuals: there is generally **no duty to confer a benefit**, but there is liability for **making things worse by a positive act**. Turning off a working sprinkler system is a positive act that increased the damage, so a duty was owed (Capital & Counties v Hampshire CC). Merely attending a fire does not amount to an assumption of responsibility, and there is no blanket immunity for the emergency services. Caparo factors are not needed where the case falls within the established principle that a positive act causing physical damage gives rise to a duty."},{"t":"negligence","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Dr Okafor, an eye surgeon, recommends laser surgery to Hannah, a commercial airline pilot. Hannah tells him that her career depends on her night vision and that anything affecting it would be disastrous for her. The procedure carries a 1% risk of permanent glare when looking at lights at night. Dr Okafor does not mention this risk, and a responsible body of eye surgeons would not normally mention a risk that small. The surgery is performed with all due skill, but Hannah develops permanent night glare and loses her licence. She says she would not have had the surgery had she known of the risk.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Dr Okafor was in breach of duty?","o":["He was not in breach, because a responsible body of eye surgeons would not have mentioned the risk.","He was probably in breach, because he knew this patient would regard the risk as significant, making it material.","He was not in breach, because a risk of 1% is too small to be material in law.","He was in breach only if the practice of not mentioning the risk cannot withstand logical analysis.","He was not in breach, because the surgery itself was performed with all due skill and Hannah consented to it."],"a":1,"e":"Advice about the risks of treatment is governed by **Montgomery**, not Bolam: a doctor must disclose any **material risk**, meaning one a reasonable person in the patient's position would think significant, **or one the doctor knows (or should know) this particular patient would think significant**. Hannah told him her night vision was vital to her career, so the 1% risk was material to her. The responsible body of opinion test (Bolam) and the logical analysis gloss (Bolitho) apply to diagnosis and treatment, not to what risks must be disclosed, and there is no fixed percentage threshold. Skilful surgery does not cure a failure to warn."},{"t":"negligence","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Ravi is driving his van when he collides with a motorcyclist, Ellen, who suffers a broken leg. Ravi pleads not guilty to a charge of driving without due care and attention but is convicted by the magistrates' court. Ellen now brings a negligence claim against Ravi. Ravi intends to argue in the civil proceedings that he was driving carefully and that Ellen pulled out in front of him.\n\nWhat effect does Ravi's conviction have on Ellen's negligence claim?","o":["The conviction is irrelevant, because the criminal and civil courts apply different standards of proof.","The conviction is conclusive, so Ravi cannot dispute breach and Ellen need prove nothing further to obtain damages.","The conviction is admissible only because the criminal court heard evidence from Ellen herself.","It is admissible, and Ravi will be taken to have driven carelessly unless he proves otherwise.","The conviction could be relied on only if Ravi had pleaded guilty, rather than being convicted after a trial."],"a":3,"e":"Under the **Civil Evidence Act 1968 s.11**, a conviction by a UK court is admissible in civil proceedings, and the person convicted is **taken to have committed the offence unless they prove otherwise**. In practice the burden shifts to Ravi to disprove careless driving. It is not conclusive, and it applies whether the conviction followed a guilty plea or a trial. Ellen still has to prove the remaining elements, such as causation and damage."},{"t":"causation-remoteness","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Ola visits her GP, Dr Hart, with severe pain and swelling in her knee. Dr Hart negligently diagnoses a sprain and fails to refer her to hospital. Three weeks later Ola is diagnosed with a bone infection and is left with a permanent limp. Expert evidence shows that, even with an immediate referral, Ola would have had only a 40% chance of avoiding the limp.\n\nWhat, if anything, can Ola recover from Dr Hart for the limp?","o":["40% of the full damages for the limp, reflecting the chance she lost.","Full damages for the limp, because the delay materially increased the risk of her developing it.","Nothing, because she cannot show on the balance of probabilities that the negligent delay caused the limp.","Full damages for the limp, because Dr Hart was clearly in breach of duty.","60% of the full damages for the limp, reflecting the share of the outcome caused by the infection itself."],"a":2,"e":"In **personal injury**, causation is **all or nothing** on the **balance of probabilities**. Since Ola had only a 40% chance of a good outcome even with proper care, it is more likely than not that she would have been left with the limp anyway, so she recovers nothing for it (Hotson; Gregg v Scott). Damages for a percentage lost chance are available only in financial loss cases that depend on what a third party would have done. The material increase in risk exception is confined to mesothelioma, and a breach alone does not prove causation."},{"t":"economic-loss","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Norbank lends £800,000 on the security of an office building after Grafton & Co, valuers instructed by Norbank, negligently value it at £1,000,000. Its true value at the time was £800,000. The borrower defaults. By the time Norbank sells the building, a general fall in the property market has reduced its value to £550,000, leaving Norbank with a shortfall of £250,000 on the loan. Norbank would not have lent at all had it known the true value.\n\nWhat is the maximum Norbank can recover from Grafton & Co?","o":["£250,000, because Norbank would not have lent at all but for the negligent valuation.","Nothing, because the loss is pure economic loss and no duty is owed for it.","Nothing, because the fall in the market was a new intervening act breaking the chain of causation.","£450,000, being the difference between the negligent valuation and the sale price.","£200,000, the loss attributable to the overvaluation itself, excluding the effect of the market fall."],"a":4,"e":"A valuer instructed by a lender owes it a duty under the **Hedley Byrne** principle, so the claim is not defeated just because the loss is purely financial. But under the **scope of duty** principle (SAAMCO; Manchester Building Society v Grant Thornton), the adviser is liable only for loss caused by the risk the duty guarded against: the consequences of the valuation being wrong. That is capped at the £200,000 overvaluation, not the extra loss caused by the falling market. The but-for answer of £250,000 ignores this limit, and a market fall is not treated as a new intervening act."},{"t":"psychiatric-harm","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Lewis, an off-duty paramedic, is driving on a motorway when a lorry driver negligently causes a multi-vehicle pile-up ahead of him. Lewis stops and spends two hours treating badly injured strangers who have been moved onto the hard shoulder, well away from the traffic. He is never in any physical danger and does not believe he is. He later develops post-traumatic stress disorder.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Lewis's claim against the lorry driver for his psychiatric illness?","o":["It is likely to succeed, because rescuers are a special category who are always owed a duty for psychiatric harm.","It is likely to succeed, because he witnessed the immediate aftermath of the accident with his own unaided senses.","It is likely to fail, because PTSD is not a recognised psychiatric illness.","It is likely to succeed, because a lorry driver owes a duty to every road user who is affected by an accident.","It is likely to fail, because he was never in danger and had no close tie with any of the injured."],"a":4,"e":"Rescuers have **no special category** for psychiatric harm (White v Chief Constable of South Yorkshire). A rescuer recovers only as a **primary victim** if they were in physical danger or reasonably believed they were, which Lewis was not. As a **secondary victim** he must satisfy all the **Alcock** controls, and he fails the first: he had no **close tie of love and affection** with the strangers. Being present at the aftermath is not enough on its own, and PTSD is a recognised psychiatric illness."},{"t":"employers-liability","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Carla works on a grinding machine at Steelform Ltd. Steelform provides safety goggles and keeps them in a cupboard, but gives no instruction about wearing them. The foreman knows that most of the workforce, including Carla, never wear them, and does nothing about it. A metal fragment flies off the grinder into Carla's eye, causing serious injury. Goggles would have prevented it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Steelform's liability to Carla?","o":["Steelform is not liable, because it satisfied its duty by providing suitable goggles.","Steelform is not liable, because Carla voluntarily accepted the risk by choosing not to wear the goggles.","Steelform is likely to be liable for an unsafe system of work, with damages reduced for contributory negligence.","Steelform is liable in full, because an employer's duty to employees is strict.","Steelform is liable only if Carla can show a breach of health and safety regulations giving rise to a civil claim."],"a":2,"e":"An employer's non-delegable duty includes a **safe system of work**, which means not just providing equipment but **instructing, encouraging and supervising** its use (Bux v Slough Metals). Leaving goggles in a cupboard while the foreman ignored widespread non-use is a breach. Carla's failure to wear them is **contributory negligence**, reducing damages under the 1945 Act. Volenti rarely succeeds against employees, the duty is one of reasonable care rather than strict, and since 2013 breach of health and safety regulations does not by itself give a civil claim."},{"t":"vicarious-liability","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"FitZone plc, a national gym chain, engages Mr Barr, a self-employed physiotherapist, to carry out injury assessments on gym members one afternoon a week. Mr Barr runs his own private clinic, has many other clients, sets his own fees and carries his own professional insurance. During an assessment at a FitZone gym, he carelessly performs a manipulation on a member, Priya, injuring her back.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether FitZone is vicariously liable for Mr Barr's negligence?","o":["FitZone is vicariously liable, because the negligence occurred on its premises during work it had arranged.","FitZone is vicariously liable, because the assessments were an integral part of the services it offers members.","FitZone is not vicariously liable, because the manipulation was not closely connected with the work Mr Barr was engaged to do.","FitZone is not vicariously liable, because Mr Barr was running his own independent business.","FitZone is vicariously liable, because it is better able to pay compensation than Mr Barr."],"a":3,"e":"Vicarious liability requires at **stage 1** a relationship of **employment or one akin to employment**. A professional who runs **their own independent business**, with other clients and their own insurance, is an independent contractor (Various Claimants v Barclays Bank), so the claim fails at stage 1. The place where the tort happened and FitZone's ability to pay do not create the relationship. The manipulation was plainly connected with the assessment work, so stage 2 is not the problem; Priya should sue Mr Barr personally."},{"t":"defences","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Kayla and her friend Dom, a private pilot, spend the afternoon drinking heavily together at a party. Kayla knows exactly how much Dom has drunk. Although she has had several drinks herself, she is not so drunk that she cannot understand what she is doing. She then agrees to go up with Dom in his light aircraft and helps him prepare it for take-off. Shortly after take-off, Dom crashes the aircraft because of his drunkenness and Kayla is seriously injured.\n\nWhich defence is most likely to defeat Kayla's negligence claim against Dom?","o":["Illegality, because flying an aircraft while drunk is a criminal offence and Kayla took part in the drinking.","Volenti, because she knowingly accepted the risk, and the statutory bar for passengers does not extend to aircraft.","Contributory negligence, reducing her damages by 100%, because she was entirely responsible for her own injury.","None, because the Road Traffic Act 1988 prevents a defendant relying on volenti against a passenger.","Volenti non fit injuria, but only if Kayla signed a written agreement accepting the risk."],"a":1,"e":"**Volenti** is a complete defence where the claimant, with full knowledge of the risk, freely agreed to accept it, and agreement can be **implied from conduct** (Morris v Murray). Kayla knew how drunk Dom was, understood the risk and helped prepare the flight. **RTA 1988 s.149** stops volenti being used against passengers only in **vehicles requiring compulsory motor insurance**, not aircraft. Contributory negligence can never be 100%, and illegality is not engaged because the crime was Dom's, not a joint criminal enterprise."},{"t":"remedies","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Mia is injured at work through her employer's negligence and is off work for six months. During that time her employer pays her contractual sick pay of £6,000. She also receives £8,000 under a personal accident insurance policy she took out and paid for herself, and £1,500 raised for her by a local charity. Her net loss of earnings for the period is £12,000.\n\nWhich of these receipts will be deducted from her damages for loss of earnings?","o":["Only the £6,000 sick pay.","The sick pay and the insurance payment, but not the charity money.","All three receipts, because damages must not over-compensate Mia.","None of them, because each was paid for a reason unconnected with the tort.","Only the insurance payment, because it was paid specifically because of the injury."],"a":0,"e":"Damages compensate, so receipts that **replace earnings**, such as **sick pay** from the employer, are deducted. But **private insurance** the claimant paid for is **not** deducted (Bradburn v Great Western Railway), because she should not lose the benefit of her own prudence. **Charitable payments** are also **not** deducted, because deducting them would discourage generosity. So only the £6,000 sick pay reduces her award."},{"t":"occupiers-liability","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Westgate Primary School hires a reputable and properly insured contractor to clear snow and grit the steps at the main entrance before parents arrive each morning. One morning the contractor's worker leaves the steps covered in ice without gritting them. The head teacher walks past and could plainly see that no grit had been laid, but does nothing. Shortly afterwards a parent, Jane, slips on the steps and breaks her wrist.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the school's liability to Jane under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1957?","o":["The school is likely to be liable, because it could easily have checked that the gritting had been done.","The school is not liable, because it acted reasonably in entrusting the work to a competent and insured contractor.","The school is not liable, because Jane is a visitor and the danger of ice in winter is obvious.","The school is liable only under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1984, because Jane was not a pupil.","The school is not liable, because the contractor's employee was the only person at fault."],"a":0,"e":"Under **OLA 1957 s.2(4)(b)**, an occupier escapes liability for an independent contractor's faulty work only if it acted reasonably in entrusting the work, took reasonable steps to check the contractor's **competence**, **and** took reasonable steps, where the nature of the work allows, to check the work was **properly done**. Checking that steps are gritted needs no expertise (contrast specialist lift repairs), so the school failed the third requirement (Woodward v Mayor of Hastings). Jane is a lawful visitor, so the 1957 Act applies, and ice on an entrance step used by all parents is not something she can be expected simply to avoid."},{"t":"occupiers-liability","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Rosa is shopping at a large DIY store. Looking for a toilet, she pushes open a door clearly marked 'Staff only – no customers beyond this point' and walks along a corridor. She falls down an unlit flight of steps into a storage cellar, breaking her ankle and smashing her mobile phone. The store's managers know that customers sometimes wander through that door.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Rosa's claim against the store?","o":["Her claim is governed by the Occupiers' Liability Act 1957, because she entered the store lawfully as a customer.","The 1984 Act applies; her ankle may be recoverable if the statutory conditions are met, but her phone is not.","Her claim is governed by the Occupiers' Liability Act 1984, under which she can recover for both her ankle and her phone.","She has no claim, because a trespasser is owed no duty by an occupier.","Her claim is governed by the Occupiers' Liability Act 1957, but her damages will be reduced because she ignored the sign."],"a":1,"e":"A visitor who goes beyond the **area** of their permission becomes a **trespasser** for that excursion, so the **1984 Act** governs Rosa's fall. A duty arises only if the three conditions in **s.1(3)** are met (awareness of the danger, knowledge she may come into its vicinity, and a risk against which some protection is reasonably expected). Even then, the 1984 Act covers **personal injury only** (s.1(8)), so her phone is not recoverable. Trespassers are owed a duty in the right circumstances, so the claim is not automatically barred."},{"t":"product-liability","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Sana buys a new dishwasher for her home from an electrical retailer. It was manufactured in the UK by Clean-Tech Ltd. Because of a manufacturing defect, it catches fire. Sana suffers burns to her hands. The fire destroys the dishwasher (worth £450), damages her kitchen units (£1,800 to replace), and destroys a laptop (£900) that she uses almost entirely for her self-employed accountancy business.\n\nWhich losses can Sana recover from Clean-Tech under Part 1 of the Consumer Protection Act 1987?","o":["Her burns, the kitchen units and the laptop, but not the dishwasher.","Her burns, the kitchen units, the laptop and the dishwasher.","Her burns and the kitchen units only.","Her burns only, because property damage is not recoverable under the Act.","The kitchen units and the dishwasher only, because personal injury must be claimed in negligence."],"a":2,"e":"Under **CPA 1987 s.5**, the producer is liable for **death or personal injury**, and for damage to **other property** that is **ordinarily intended for private use** and intended by the claimant **mainly for private use**, provided the property claim exceeds **£275**. The **defective product itself** is excluded (s.5(2)), so the dishwasher cannot be claimed. The laptop is used **mainly for business**, so it is excluded too. The burns and the kitchen units are recoverable."},{"t":"nuisance-rylands","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Bella runs a café on a busy pedestrian street. For four months, a building contractor working on a nearby office block, without any licence or lawful authority, keeps a large skip and scaffolding that block most of the street. Footfall drops sharply and Bella's takings fall by £15,000. Other people using the street are inconvenienced but suffer no particular loss.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Bella's position in public nuisance?","o":["She can sue, because her lost trade is special damage beyond the inconvenience suffered by the public.","She cannot sue, because only the Attorney General or the local authority can bring proceedings for public nuisance.","She cannot sue, because a claim in public nuisance requires a proprietary interest in land affected by the nuisance.","She cannot sue, because pure economic loss is never recoverable in public nuisance.","She can sue, but only if she can show that she suffered personal injury as a result of the obstruction."],"a":0,"e":"Obstructing the highway is a classic **public nuisance**. An individual can sue if they suffer **special damage** beyond that suffered by the public at large, and that can include **economic loss** such as lost trade, as well as personal injury. **No proprietary interest** is needed for public nuisance; that is a requirement of private nuisance. The Attorney General or a local authority can also act, but that does not prevent an individual with special damage from suing."},{"t":"negligence","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Josh, aged 12, is playing football with friends on a grassed area of a public park where ball games are permitted. He takes a hard shot at an improvised goal, misses, and the ball strikes Edna, an elderly woman walking along a path nearby, breaking her glasses and injuring her eye. A careful adult would have realised there was a risk of hitting someone on the path, but an ordinary 12-year-old playing football would not have thought about it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the standard of care that applies to Josh?","o":["The standard of a reasonable adult, because the law applies one objective standard to everyone.","No standard, because a child under 14 cannot be liable in negligence.","That of a reasonable 12-year-old, so he is unlikely to be in breach.","The standard of a child of Josh's own intelligence and experience, judged subjectively.","The standard of a reasonable adult, but his parents rather than Josh will be liable for any breach."],"a":2,"e":"A child is judged against a **reasonable child of the same age**, not an adult (Mullin v Richards). The standard remains **objective** for children of that age, so it does not depend on Josh's personal intelligence. There is no minimum age for liability in negligence. Parents are not automatically liable for their children's torts; they are liable only for their own negligence, for example in supervision."},{"t":"negligence","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Tariq, an office worker who has attended a basic first aid course, is in a shopping centre when an elderly man, Mr Hollis, collapses and stops breathing. No one else steps in. Tariq performs chest compressions in the way he was taught, and Mr Hollis is revived by paramedics a few minutes later. However, two of his ribs were broken by the compressions, and he sues Tariq in negligence.\n\nHow will the court approach the question of whether Tariq was negligent?","o":["It will dismiss the claim, because the Social Action, Responsibility and Heroism Act 2015 gives anyone helping in an emergency complete immunity from liability.","It will judge Tariq by the standard of a reasonably competent paramedic, because he took on a medical task.","It will find Tariq liable, because by choosing to intervene he assumed responsibility for any harm caused.","It will apply a lower standard of care to Tariq because he was a volunteer with only basic training.","It will apply the ordinary reasonable person standard, but must consider that he was acting heroically in an emergency."],"a":4,"e":"Under the **SARAH Act 2015 s.4**, when deciding whether a person was negligent the court **must consider** whether they were **acting heroically by intervening in an emergency** to help someone in danger. These are factors to weigh in applying the ordinary reasonable person standard; they are **not a defence** or immunity, and they **do not lower the standard**. An amateur doing a task in an emergency is judged by a reasonable person with his training, not a professional. A volunteer rescuer is generally liable only if they make things worse through carelessness, and properly performed CPR that breaks ribs is unlikely to be careless."},{"t":"negligence","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Gwen hires Lockfast, a locksmith, to change the locks on her house while she is at work. Gwen gives the Lockfast technician her keys and asks him to lock up when he has finished. Halfway through the job he goes to a builders' merchant for an hour to buy a part, leaving the front door closed but unlocked. While he is away, a burglar walks in and steals Gwen's jewellery.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Lockfast is liable for the loss of the jewellery?","o":["Lockfast is not liable, because there is no general duty to prevent a third party from causing harm to another.","Lockfast is likely to be liable, because its technician had assumed responsibility for securing the house.","Lockfast is not liable, because the burglar's deliberate criminal act broke the chain of causation.","Lockfast is not liable, because the theft caused pure economic loss.","Lockfast is liable only if the burglar had been seen near the house before the technician left."],"a":1,"e":"The general rule is that there is **no duty to prevent a third party** from causing harm. An exception applies where the defendant has **assumed responsibility** for protecting the claimant or her property (Stansbie v Troman). Here the technician was entrusted with the house and keys, so guarding against the very risk of burglary was part of his duty, and the burglary cannot break the chain of causation when it is the very thing the duty was meant to prevent. The loss of jewellery is physical loss of property, not pure economic loss, and no prior sighting of the burglar is needed."},{"t":"causation-remoteness","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"In 2022, Frank injures his back at work through his employer's negligence and has to give up his job as a warehouse supervisor. In 2024, he is diagnosed with a degenerative spinal disease that has nothing to do with the accident. Medical evidence shows that, from the end of 2025, the disease would have made him unable to work in any event.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the employer's liability for Frank's future loss of earnings?","o":["The employer is liable for lost earnings for the rest of Frank's working life, because a later event cannot reduce the first wrongdoer's liability.","The employer is not liable for any lost earnings, because the disease broke the chain of causation.","The employer is liable for lost earnings only up to the end of 2025, because after that the unrelated disease would have stopped Frank working anyway.","The employer is liable for half of Frank's lost earnings after 2025, because the accident and the disease are concurrent causes.","The employer is liable for lost earnings for the rest of Frank's working life, because of the eggshell skull rule."],"a":2,"e":"Where a **later natural illness** would have caused the same loss, the damages for the original tort are **reduced** to reflect that the claimant would have lost his earnings anyway from that point (Jobling v Associated Dairies). This contrasts with a **later tort**, which does not reduce the first wrongdoer's liability (Baker v Willoughby). The disease does not wipe out liability for the period before it would have taken effect. The eggshell skull rule concerns pre-existing vulnerabilities that make a foreseeable injury worse, not unrelated later illnesses."},{"t":"causation-remoteness","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Mark negligently knocks a cyclist, Ade, off his bike, breaking his arm. At hospital, the junior doctor treating Ade makes a careless error in setting the fracture, of a kind that unfortunately occurs from time to time in busy emergency departments. As a result, Ade's arm heals badly and he is left with a permanent weakness he would not otherwise have had.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Mark's liability for the permanent weakness?","o":["Mark is not liable for the permanent weakness, because any negligent act by a third party breaks the chain of causation.","Mark is liable for the permanent weakness, because ordinary negligent medical treatment of an injury he caused does not break the chain of causation.","Mark is not liable for the permanent weakness, because it was not a foreseeable type of harm.","Mark is liable for the permanent weakness only if the doctor cannot pay, because the doctor's negligence was the more recent cause.","Mark is not liable for the permanent weakness, because Ade must sue the hospital trust for all harm after he was admitted."],"a":1,"e":"A third party's act breaks the chain of causation only if it is **free, deliberate and informed**, or so unreasonable that it eclipses the original wrong. **Negligent medical treatment** of an injury the defendant caused usually does **not** break the chain unless it is **grossly** negligent or palpably wrong, because imperfect treatment is a foreseeable consequence of causing injury. An ordinary error in setting a fracture is not gross. Further harm to an injured arm is plainly of a foreseeable type, and Ade may also have a claim against the hospital, but that does not remove Mark's liability."},{"t":"economic-loss","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Nadia left her job at Harbour Insurance Ltd to move to a competitor, Alton Ltd, which offered her a post subject to a satisfactory reference. Harbour's HR manager writes a reference stating that Nadia 'was dismissed for misconduct'. In fact Nadia resigned and was never disciplined; the manager had carelessly confused her file with another employee's. Alton withdraws its job offer, and Nadia is out of work for six months.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Nadia's claim in negligence against Harbour?","o":["Harbour owed no duty to Nadia, because a reference is relied on by the recipient, so only Alton could sue.","Harbour owed no duty to Nadia, because her loss is pure economic loss and no physical damage was caused.","Nadia's only possible claim is in defamation, because the reference damaged her reputation.","Harbour owed Nadia a duty only if it had expressly promised her a favourable reference.","Harbour owed Nadia a duty of care in preparing the reference, so she can recover her lost earnings."],"a":4,"e":"An employer who gives a reference owes a **duty of care to the subject** of the reference (Spring v Guardian Assurance), because it assumes responsibility for preparing it carefully and the employee relies on it being accurate. This is an application of the extended **Hedley Byrne** principle, so the fact that the loss is purely financial does not prevent recovery. A claim in negligence is available alongside any claim in defamation, and no express promise of a good reference is required."},{"t":"psychiatric-harm","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Yusuf is driving slowly in traffic when Kim negligently drives into the back of his car. It is a minor collision and Yusuf suffers no physical injury at all. However, the shock of the crash causes a recurrence of a serious anxiety disorder from which Yusuf had suffered years before, and he is unable to work for two years. A person of ordinary fortitude would not have suffered any psychiatric illness from such a minor collision.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Kim's liability for Yusuf's psychiatric illness?","o":["Kim is liable, because Yusuf was a primary victim and she must take him as she finds him.","Kim is not liable, because psychiatric illness to a person of ordinary fortitude was not foreseeable.","Kim is not liable, because Yusuf suffered no physical injury.","Kim is liable only if Yusuf can satisfy the Alcock control mechanisms.","Kim is not liable, because a recurrence of an existing illness is not a recognised psychiatric illness."],"a":0,"e":"Yusuf was directly involved in the collision, so he is a **primary victim**. For a primary victim, the test is whether **personal injury (physical or psychiatric)** was foreseeable, and once it was, the **eggshell skull** rule applies to his mental vulnerability (Page v Smith). The person of ordinary fortitude test and the Alcock controls apply only to **secondary victims**. No physical injury is required, and a recurrence of a diagnosed anxiety disorder is a recognised psychiatric illness."},{"t":"employers-liability","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"In March 2025, Owen, a warehouse operative, injures his back lifting a heavy crate on his own. His employer had not carried out the risk assessment for this task required by the Manual Handling Operations Regulations 1992, which would have identified that a two-person lift was needed. Owen's solicitor is advising on how to frame his claim.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Owen's position?","o":["He must prove negligence, but the breach of the Regulations is strong evidence of the required standard.","He can sue for breach of statutory duty, and need not prove any fault, because the Regulations impose strict duties.","He has no claim, because the Regulations exclude any civil liability for breach.","He can sue only the Health and Safety Executive, which enforces the Regulations.","He must prove negligence, and the Regulations are irrelevant to whether his employer was in breach."],"a":0,"e":"Since **1 October 2013** (Enterprise and Regulatory Reform Act 2013 s.69, amending HSWA 1974 s.47), breach of health and safety **regulations** is **not actionable** as breach of statutory duty unless the regulations say otherwise. Owen must therefore prove **common law negligence**, such as failing to provide a **safe system of work**. The Regulations remain highly relevant as **evidence of the standard** a reasonable employer would meet. His claim is against his employer, not the regulator."},{"t":"vicarious-liability","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Dan and Sol are delivery drivers employed by Rapid Parcels Ltd. Halfway through their afternoon round, instead of continuing to their next drop, they drive the company van 20 miles in the opposite direction to visit a friend for an hour. On the way there, Dan, who is driving, carelessly hits a pedestrian, Lily.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Rapid Parcels is vicariously liable to Lily?","o":["Rapid Parcels is vicariously liable, because Dan was driving the company van during working hours.","Rapid Parcels is vicariously liable, because an employer cannot avoid liability by showing that it prohibited the employee's conduct.","Rapid Parcels is not vicariously liable, because there is no employment relationship between Rapid Parcels and Lily.","Rapid Parcels is unlikely to be vicariously liable, because Dan was on a frolic of his own, wholly unconnected with his work, when he injured Lily.","Rapid Parcels is vicariously liable, because careless driving is an unauthorised way of doing an authorised act."],"a":3,"e":"Vicarious liability requires the tort to be **closely connected** with what the employee was authorised to do. A detour for a **purely personal purpose**, abandoning the employer's business, is a **frolic of his own** (Hilton v Thomas Burton), so the connection is lost even though the van and working hours were the employer's. Careless driving while actually delivering would be an unauthorised way of doing an authorised act, but Dan was not delivering. The relevant relationship is between employer and tortfeasor, not employer and victim."},{"t":"defences","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Fern is a passenger on a single-decker bus. The driver, who is speeding, negligently loses control on a bend and the bus careers towards a ditch. Reasonably believing the bus is about to crash, Fern jumps off through the open door and breaks her leg. In fact, the driver regains control and the bus stops safely without anyone else being hurt.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the bus company's likely response to Fern's claim?","o":["Fern's claim will fail, because her own decision to jump broke the chain of causation.","Fern's damages will be reduced for contributory negligence, because with hindsight she would not have been hurt had she stayed on the bus.","Fern's claim will fail, because she voluntarily accepted the risk of injury by jumping.","Fern's damages will be reduced by 25%, the standard reduction for passengers who contribute to their own injuries.","Fern's damages are unlikely to be reduced, because she reacted reasonably in the agony of the moment to a danger created by the driver's negligence."],"a":4,"e":"A claimant who reacts **reasonably** to a sudden danger created by the defendant is **not at fault**, even if with hindsight the choice turns out to be wrong (the **agony of the moment** principle, Jones v Boyce). Her reasonable reaction does not break the chain of causation, which requires conduct that is wholly unreasonable. Volenti requires free agreement to run a risk, not a reaction to danger. The 25% figure relates to seatbelt cases where wearing a belt would have prevented the injury, and has no application here."},{"t":"remedies","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Grant, aged 45, is killed in a road accident caused by Paula's negligence. He was unmarried. He leaves his partner, Zoe, with whom he had lived for the last 18 months, his son, Luke, aged 22, and his parents, with whom he was very close. All of them are devastated by his death.\n\nWhich of these people can claim bereavement damages under the Fatal Accidents Act 1976?","o":["Zoe only, because she was living with Grant as his partner at the time of his death.","None of them.","Luke only, because he is Grant's child.","Grant's parents, who will share the award equally.","Zoe and Luke, who will share the award equally."],"a":1,"e":"**Bereavement damages** (FAA 1976 s.1A) are a fixed sum available only to a narrow list: the deceased's **spouse or civil partner**, a **cohabiting partner of at least two years**, and the **parents of an unmarried minor**. Zoe had lived with Grant for only 18 months, so she does not qualify. Luke is an **adult child**, and Grant's parents qualify only where the deceased was a **minor**. However close they were, none of them can claim, though Luke and Grant's parents may claim for **loss of dependency** if they were financially dependent on him. Zoe cannot: a cohabitant is a dependant only after **two years** living together (s.1(3)(b))."},{"t":"occupiers-liability","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Kris, a self-employed electrician, is called to Harbour House, an office building, to repair a faulty light fitting in the roof space. The building manager knows that several floorboards in the roof space are rotten but says nothing about it. While Kris is working on the light fitting, a rotten floorboard gives way and he falls through the ceiling, injuring his back.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the occupier's liability to Kris?","o":["The occupier is not liable, because a skilled visitor is expected to appreciate and guard against all risks at the place where he works.","The occupier is not liable, because Kris is an independent contractor rather than a visitor.","The occupier is likely to be liable, because rotten floorboards are not a risk incidental to an electrician's trade.","The occupier is liable only under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1984, because Kris was in a part of the building not open to the public.","The occupier is not liable, because Kris voluntarily accepted the risks of working in a roof space."],"a":2,"e":"Under **OLA 1957 s.2(3)(b)**, an occupier may expect a person working in their calling to **appreciate and guard against special risks ordinarily incident to it**, but this covers only the risks of **the visitor's own trade**. An electrician is expected to deal with electrical hazards, not a rotten floorboard, so the occupier still owes the full **common duty of care** for that danger, which it breached by giving no warning. Kris was invited into the roof space to work, so he is a **visitor** under the 1957 Act, and merely knowing that roof spaces can be hazardous is not willing acceptance of this particular risk."},{"t":"product-liability","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Tom buys an electric kettle from Reg, who runs a market stall selling household goods. The kettle has no brand name or other marking. It is defective and explodes, scalding Tom's arm. Tom has no way of finding out who made or imported it. Within a few days of the injury, Tom asks Reg in writing to tell him who supplied the kettle to him. Reg ignores the request.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Reg's liability to Tom under Part 1 of the Consumer Protection Act 1987?","o":["Reg is liable as a supplier, because Tom asked him within a reasonable time to identify the producer or his own supplier and he failed to do so.","Reg is not liable, because only the producer of a defective product can be liable under the Act.","Reg is liable, because every person who supplies a defective product in the course of business is strictly liable under the Act.","Reg is not liable, because he did not hold himself out as the producer of the kettle.","Reg is liable only if Tom proves that Reg was negligent in selling the kettle."],"a":0,"e":"A mere **supplier** such as a retailer is not normally liable under the CPA. But under **s.2(3)**, a supplier becomes liable if the injured person **asks** it, within a reasonable time, to identify the producer, own-brander or importer, it is not reasonably practicable for the claimant to identify them, and the supplier **fails** within a reasonable time to identify them or its own supplier. Reg ignored Tom's request, so he is liable. Liability under the Act is strict, so Tom need not prove negligence, and the own-brander route is irrelevant here."},{"t":"nuisance-rylands","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Sparkle Ltd manufactures fireworks at a factory on the edge of a town, storing large quantities of explosive materials there. Without any negligence by Sparkle, an explosion occurs inside the factory. A safety inspector visiting the factory is injured inside the building. Debris from the explosion lands on a neighbouring house owned by Alma, smashing its roof and windows. Alma's lodger, Ben, is injured by falling glass.\n\nWhich claim under the rule in Rylands v Fletcher is most likely to succeed?","o":["The inspector's claim for his injuries.","Ben's claim for his injuries.","Alma's claim for the damage to her house and Ben's claim for his injuries.","Alma's claim for the damage to her house.","None, because Sparkle was not negligent."],"a":3,"e":"**Rylands v Fletcher** imposes **strict liability**, so the lack of negligence does not matter. Storing large quantities of explosives is a **non-natural use** carrying an exceptionally high risk, and debris landing on Alma's land is an **escape**. Only a person with an **interest in the land** affected can sue, and **personal injury is not recoverable** (Transco), so Ben, a lodger with an injury, cannot claim under the rule. The inspector was injured **inside** the factory, so there was no escape (Read v J Lyons)."},{"t":"negligence","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Callum, a strong swimmer, is sunbathing on a quiet beach when he sees a young child he does not know struggling in the sea a few metres from the shore. He could easily have waded in and pulled the child out without any risk to himself, but he does nothing and carries on reading. The child drowns. There is no lifeguard on duty, and Callum has no connection with the child or the child's family.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Callum is liable in negligence to the child's estate?","o":["He is liable, because it was reasonably foreseeable that the child would drown if he did nothing.","He is liable, because he could have rescued the child without any risk to himself.","He is not liable, because the child's parents were responsible for supervising the child.","He is not liable, because there is no general duty to rescue and no exception applies.","He is liable only if a court, applying the Caparo factors, considers it fair, just and reasonable to impose a duty."],"a":3,"e":"The law distinguishes making things worse from **failing to make things better**. There is **no general duty to rescue**, however easy the rescue and however foreseeable the harm, unless an exception applies, such as an **assumption of responsibility** (e.g. a lifeguard on duty), **control** over the danger or the person, a **special relationship** (e.g. a parent), or **creating the danger**. Callum falls within none of these. Foreseeability alone does not create a duty to act, and whether the parents were also at fault does not explain why Callum owes no duty."},{"t":"negligence","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"In 2019, a dentist, Dr Chen, sterilised her instruments using a method that was then accepted throughout the profession as safe. In 2023, new research showed for the first time that the method could fail to kill a rare bacterium. A patient treated in 2019, Iris, had been infected by that bacterium from Dr Chen's instruments and became seriously ill.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Dr Chen was in breach of duty?","o":["She was not in breach, because she is judged by what was known at the time, not with hindsight.","She was in breach, because the method has now been shown to be unsafe.","She was in breach, because a dentist guarantees that instruments used on patients are free from infection.","She was not in breach, because the infection was too remote a consequence of her treatment.","She was not in breach, because Iris consented to the treatment."],"a":0,"e":"The standard of care is judged by the state of knowledge **at the time** of the alleged breach, **not with hindsight** (Roe v Minister of Health). In 2019 the method was accepted across the profession, and the risk was unknown, so Dr Chen acted as a reasonably competent dentist would have done. A professional promises reasonable care and skill, not a guarantee of a good result. Remoteness and consent are not the reasons she escapes liability."},{"t":"causation-remoteness","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Ella works in a commercial kitchen. Her employer negligently fails to repair a leaking pan handle, and boiling water spills onto Ella's hand, causing a burn that would normally heal within a few weeks. Because Ella has a rare and undiagnosed skin disorder, the burn becomes badly infected and part of one finger has to be amputated. No one could have foreseen such a serious outcome.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the employer's liability?","o":["The employer is liable only for the ordinary burn, because the amputation was not reasonably foreseeable.","The employer is not liable at all, because Ella's skin disorder was the real cause of her injury.","The employer is liable for the amputation only if it knew about Ella's skin disorder.","The employer is liable for half of Ella's loss, because her injury had two causes.","It is liable for everything, including the amputation, because a burn was a foreseeable type of harm."],"a":4,"e":"Under the **eggshell skull** rule, if the **type** of injury is foreseeable, the defendant is liable for its **full extent**, even if a pre-existing weakness makes it far worse than anyone could have expected (Smith v Leech Brain). A burn was plainly foreseeable, so the employer is liable for the amputation too. Remoteness requires only the type of harm to be foreseeable, not its extent, and the employer need not have known of the condition. There is no apportionment simply because the claimant was unusually vulnerable."},{"t":"economic-loss","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"In 2021, Solid Build Ltd builds a garage extension for Adam, negligently laying inadequate foundations. In 2024, Adam sells the house to Kate. Shortly afterwards, cracks appear in the garage walls because of the foundations, and Kate is advised that the garage must be underpinned at a cost of £25,000 to stop it deteriorating further. No one has been injured and nothing else has been damaged. Kate has no contract with Solid Build.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Kate's claim in negligence against Solid Build for the cost of the repairs?","o":["It is likely to succeed, because the cracks are physical damage to Kate's property.","It is likely to succeed, because a builder owes a duty of care to all subsequent owners of the building.","It is likely to fail, because Kate bought the house after the work was done, so she was not a foreseeable claimant.","It is likely to succeed, because she has suffered consequential economic loss flowing from physical damage.","It is likely to fail, because damage to the building itself is pure economic loss."],"a":4,"e":"Where a building or product is defective but has damaged **only itself**, the cost of repair or the loss in value is treated as **pure economic loss**, not property damage (Murphy v Brentwood DC). No duty is owed in negligence for pure economic loss caused by a negligent act, so Kate's claim fails. Had the defect injured someone or damaged **other property**, that would be recoverable. Kate's remedies, if any, lie elsewhere, for example in contract against the seller if there was a relevant term."},{"t":"psychiatric-harm","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Lena and her adult brother, Max, go to an outdoor concert. Lena is watching from the far side of the arena, well away from any danger, when a negligently erected lighting rig collapses on the area where Max is standing. She sees it fall and runs over to find him trapped and badly injured. She develops post-traumatic stress disorder.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Lena's claim against the concert organiser as a secondary victim?","o":["She will succeed, because a close tie of love and affection is presumed between siblings.","She must prove a close tie with Max, which is not presumed for siblings.","She will fail, because only parents, children and spouses can ever claim as secondary victims.","She will fail, because she was not herself in danger, so she can only be a primary victim.","She will fail, because PTSD caused by seeing injury to a sibling is not reasonably foreseeable."],"a":1,"e":"A secondary victim must satisfy all the **Alcock** controls. A **close tie of love and affection** is **presumed** only for parents and children, spouses and engaged couples; **siblings must prove** an especially close bond, but they are not excluded altogether. Lena saw the **accident** with her **own unaided senses** and came to the **immediate aftermath**, so proximity and perception are likely satisfied. Not being in danger makes her a secondary, not a primary, victim."},{"t":"employers-liability","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Brightway Engineering Ltd sends one of its employees, Ivan, to work for three months on a client's construction site. The client's site manager directs how the work is done and adopts an unsafe method of lifting steel beams. Ivan is injured as a result. Brightway argues that it had handed control of the work to an experienced client and was not at fault.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Brightway's liability to Ivan?","o":["Brightway is not liable, because it reasonably entrusted the work to a competent and experienced client.","Brightway is not liable, because only the occupier of the site owes Ivan a duty.","Brightway is liable only if it knew that the client's lifting method was unsafe.","Brightway is liable, because its duty to provide a safe system of work is non-delegable.","Brightway is liable only vicariously, for the negligence of the client's site manager."],"a":3,"e":"An employer owes each employee a **personal, non-delegable duty** to take reasonable care for their safety, including a **safe system of work**. It can delegate the **task** but not the **responsibility**, so if the person it delegates to fails to take care, the employer is itself in breach (McDermid v Nash Dredging). This is **primary** liability, not vicarious liability, and does not depend on Brightway knowing of the unsafe method. The client may also be liable, but that does not relieve Brightway."},{"t":"vicarious-liability","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Vic is employed as a door supervisor at a nightclub, with the job of keeping order and removing troublesome customers using no more force than is reasonable. He asks a rowdy customer, Jordan, to leave. Jordan swears at him on the way out. Vic follows him into the street outside the club and, still angry, punches him repeatedly, breaking his jaw. The club's rules forbid staff from using excessive force.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the nightclub is vicariously liable to Jordan?","o":["The nightclub is not vicariously liable, because the assault happened outside the club's premises.","The nightclub is not vicariously liable, because it expressly prohibited staff from using excessive force.","The nightclub is likely to be liable, because the assault was closely connected with Vic's job.","The nightclub is not vicariously liable, because an intentional assault can never be within the course of employment.","The nightclub is not vicariously liable, because Vic acted out of a personal grudge against Jordan."],"a":2,"e":"Under **stage 2**, the question is whether the tort was **so closely connected** with what the employee was authorised to do that it is fair to hold the employer liable. Vic's job was to deal with customers and remove them, using force where needed; continuing the confrontation was an unbroken sequence connected with that role (compare Mohamud v WM Morrison). An express prohibition is not a defence where the act is an improper way of doing the job, intentional wrongs can be within the course of employment, and the location just outside the club does not break the connection. This was not a pre-existing personal vendetta."},{"t":"defences","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Dennis keeps valuable tools in a shed on his allotment, which has been broken into several times. One night he hides inside the shed with a shotgun. When he hears someone trying to force the door, he fires through a gap in the door without warning, injuring Wes, a burglar. Wes sues Dennis in negligence for his injuries.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome of Wes's claim?","o":["Wes's claim will fail, because he was injured while committing a crime, so it is barred by illegality.","Wes's claim is likely to succeed, but his damages are likely to be reduced substantially for contributory negligence.","Wes's claim will fail, because by breaking into the shed he voluntarily accepted the risk of injury.","Wes's claim will succeed in full, because Dennis used unreasonable force.","Wes's claim will fail, because an occupier owes no duty of care to a burglar."],"a":1,"e":"Illegality does not automatically bar a claim just because the claimant was doing something criminal; applying a proportionate, policy-based approach, the courts have allowed a burglar shot by an occupier to recover (Revill v Newbery). Firing a shotgun through a door without warning was negligent, and an occupier does owe a duty to trespassers in the right circumstances. Wes's own criminal conduct contributed to his injury, so his damages are likely to be **reduced for contributory negligence** (by two-thirds in Revill). Volenti requires real agreement to run the risk of the defendant's carelessness, which a burglar does not give."},{"t":"remedies","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Ruby was born on 5 May 2012. On 1 September 2022 she was knocked down by a negligent driver and broke her leg. Her injury and its cause were obvious at once. No claim has yet been issued on her behalf.\n\nWhat is the latest date by which Ruby can issue proceedings for her personal injury without needing the court to exercise its discretion to extend time?","o":["1 September 2025","5 May 2030","1 September 2028","5 May 2036","5 May 2033"],"a":4,"e":"The limitation period for **personal injury** is **3 years** (Limitation Act 1980 s.11). But under **s.28**, time does **not run against a child** until they reach **18**. Ruby turns 18 on 5 May 2030, so the 3-year period runs from then and expires on **5 May 2033**, her 21st birthday. 1 September 2025 would apply to an adult claimant, and 5 May 2030 ignores the 3 years that run after her 18th birthday."},{"t":"remedies","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Sophie suffers a serious head injury in a car accident caused by Neil's negligence. She makes a good recovery, but medical evidence shows that there is a small but real chance that she will develop epilepsy at some point in the future as a result of the injury. If she does, her condition will be significantly worse. Her claim is about to be tried.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court can deal with the risk of epilepsy?","o":["The court may award provisional damages, assessed on the basis that she will not develop epilepsy, with the right to apply for further damages if she does.","The court must award a lump sum now that includes a discounted amount for the chance of epilepsy, because damages are assessed once and for all.","The court must ignore the risk of epilepsy, because it is less likely than not to happen.","The court must postpone the trial until it is clear whether she will develop epilepsy.","The court may order periodical payments, which will increase automatically if she develops epilepsy."],"a":0,"e":"Where there is a **chance** that the claimant will develop a **serious disease** or suffer a **serious deterioration** in future, the court can award **provisional damages**: damages now on the assumption that the deterioration will not happen, with a right to return to court for more if it does. This avoids the unfairness of a once-and-for-all lump sum that either over- or under-compensates. Periodical payments deal with ongoing future losses such as care, not with uncertain future deterioration, and the court will not delay trial indefinitely."},{"t":"occupiers-liability","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Barwell Ltd owns a large patch of waste ground. It knows that local teenagers often take a short cut across it, and it inspects the land regularly. One night, without Barwell's knowledge, fly-tippers dig a deep hole near the path to bury rubbish and leave it uncovered. Early the next morning, before anyone from Barwell has visited the land, Ali, aged 15, takes the short cut, falls into the hole and is injured.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Barwell's liability to Ali under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1984?","o":["Barwell is liable, because it knew that trespassers regularly used the land.","Barwell is not liable, because it neither knew nor had reason to believe the hole existed.","Barwell is liable, because an occupier must be prepared for children to be less careful than adults.","Barwell is not liable, because trespassers are owed a duty only if they are under the age of 14.","Barwell is liable, because a deep hole is a danger due to the state of the premises."],"a":1,"e":"Under **OLA 1984 s.1(3)**, a duty to a trespasser arises only if **all three** conditions are met, the first being that the occupier is **aware of the danger** or has **reasonable grounds to believe** it exists. The hole was dug overnight without Barwell's knowledge and before any inspection could reveal it, so condition (a) fails (compare Rhind v Astbury Water Park). Knowing that trespassers use the land satisfies only condition (b). The extra care owed to children is relevant only once a duty exists, and there is no age limit for trespassers."},{"t":"product-liability","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"In 2014, Liftmaster Ltd manufactures a ladder and supplies it to a DIY retailer, which sells it to Hugh shortly afterwards. In 2025, a weld on the ladder, defective since manufacture, fails and Hugh falls, breaking his arm. Hugh consults a solicitor two months after his fall.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Hugh's claims against Liftmaster?","o":["He can sue under the Consumer Protection Act 1987, because he has brought his claim within 3 years of his injury.","He has no claim, because both his statutory and negligence claims are time-barred.","He can sue under the Consumer Protection Act 1987 if the court exercises its discretion to extend the 10-year period.","His statutory claim is extinguished by the 10-year longstop, but he can still sue in negligence.","He can sue only the retailer, because Liftmaster's liability ended when it supplied the ladder."],"a":3,"e":"A CPA claim is subject to an **absolute 10-year longstop** from when the producer **put the product into circulation**, after which the right is **extinguished**, and the court's s.33 discretion cannot override it. Liftmaster supplied the ladder in 2014, so the CPA claim is gone. A claim in **negligence** for personal injury runs for **3 years** from the injury, so it is in time, but Hugh must prove that Liftmaster failed to take reasonable care. A contract claim would lie only against the retailer, and it is likely to be out of time anyway."},{"t":"nuisance-rylands","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"For 30 years a motor racing circuit has operated near a village with planning permission. Last year, Priti bought a house close to the circuit, knowing about it. She finds the noise on race days intolerable and sues the circuit's operator in private nuisance, seeking an injunction. The operator argues that it has planning permission, that Priti came to the nuisance, and that the circuit provides valuable local employment.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the operator's arguments?","o":["None is a defence in itself, though planning permission and public benefit may affect locality and remedy.","Planning permission is a complete defence, because the activity has been authorised by the planning authority.","Priti has no claim, because she came to the nuisance by buying a house near an established circuit.","The operator has acquired a right to make the noise by 20 years' prescription, so the claim must fail.","The public benefit of the circuit is a complete defence to a claim in private nuisance."],"a":0,"e":"**Coming to the nuisance** is **not a defence**, nor is the **public benefit** of the activity, nor is **planning permission** in itself (Coventry v Lawrence). Planning permission may, however, change the **character of the locality**, and public benefit is relevant to the **remedy**: the court may award **damages in lieu** of an injunction. Prescription needs 20 years of continuous **actionable** nuisance against the claimant's land, running only from when the activity became a nuisance to that land, which is very hard to prove for intermittent race-day noise."},{"t":"nuisance-rylands","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Gareth owns a steep hillside field above a row of cottages. Over several years, a natural bank of earth on the field has been slipping towards the boundary, and the owner of the cottage below, Mabel, has written to Gareth twice warning him that it might collapse. Gareth could have shored it up at modest cost, but does nothing. After heavy rain, part of the bank collapses onto Mabel's garden, destroying her shed and fence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Gareth's liability to Mabel?","o":["Gareth is not liable, because an occupier is never responsible for hazards arising naturally on its land.","Gareth is strictly liable under the rule in Rylands v Fletcher, because the earth escaped from his land.","Gareth is likely to be liable in private nuisance for failing to deal with a known natural hazard.","Gareth is not liable, because the collapse was caused by an act of God.","Gareth is liable only in negligence, because private nuisance requires a continuing state of affairs."],"a":2,"e":"An occupier who **knows or ought to know** of a nuisance arising **naturally** on its land must take **reasonable steps** to prevent it harming neighbours (Leakey v National Trust; Goldman v Hargrave). This is a **measured duty**, taking account of the occupier's resources, and here the fix was cheap and Gareth had been warned. **Rylands** does not apply because the earth was not **brought onto** the land. Heavy rain is not an act of God, and a slipping bank is a continuing state of affairs that can found a claim in private nuisance."}],"legalsys":[{"t":"courts-judiciary","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Several ministers are angered by a series of rulings against the government by Mrs Justice Ashworth, a High Court judge. The Secretary of State for Justice proposes that the government should remove her from office so that a judge more sympathetic to government policy can be appointed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the legal position?","o":["The Lord Chancellor may dismiss her after consulting the Lord Chief Justice.","She holds office during good behaviour and can be removed only by the monarch on an address presented by both Houses of Parliament.","The Prime Minister may advise the monarch to dismiss her, and the monarch must act on that advice by convention.","The Judicial Appointments Commission may remove her because it appointed her.","The House of Commons alone may remove her by passing a resolution."],"a":1,"e":"Senior judges hold office **during good behaviour** and can be removed only by the monarch on an **address presented by both Houses** (s.11(3) Senior Courts Act 1981), a key protection of **judicial independence**. Ministers have no power to dismiss a High Court judge, and the convention of acting on ministerial advice does not override the statute. The JAC selects judges but cannot remove them, and one House acting alone is not enough."},{"t":"sovereignty-rule-of-law","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"A campaign group says the (fictional) Coastal Development Act 2025 is invalid because the minister promoting the Bill misled the House of Commons about its environmental effects, and because the Commons failed to follow its own standing orders at committee stage. The Act received Royal Assent. The group asks the High Court to declare it invalid.\n\nHow is the court most likely to respond?","o":["It will refer the question of the Act's validity to the Supreme Court.","It will quash the Act for procedural impropriety, because the Commons broke its own rules.","It will declare the Act invalid, because Parliament was misled about its effects.","It will refuse to investigate how the Act was passed, and the Act remains valid.","It will make a declaration of incompatibility, which suspends the Act until Parliament reconsiders it."],"a":3,"e":"Under the **enrolled Act rule**, once an Act has been passed and received Royal Assent the courts will **not investigate the parliamentary process**, even if Parliament was misled; this follows from **parliamentary sovereignty** and Article 9 of the Bill of Rights (Pickin). Courts cannot quash or declare invalid an Act for misleading statements or breach of standing orders. A declaration of incompatibility concerns Convention rights and never suspends an Act."},{"t":"statutory-interpretation","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"The (fictional) Exotic Animals Act 2024 makes it an offence to keep \"lions, tigers, leopards and other animals\" without a licence from the local authority. Yusuf, who keeps a pet rabbit in his garden without a licence, is prosecuted under the Act.\n\nApplying the rules of language, how is a court most likely to interpret \"other animals\"?","o":["As excluding every animal not expressly named, so that only lions, tigers and leopards are covered.","As limited to animals kept indoors, because the listed animals are usually kept in enclosures.","As covering the rabbit, because the purpose of the Act is to protect animal welfare.","As covering every animal, because the ordinary meaning of \"animals\" includes rabbits.","As limited to animals of the same kind as those listed, such as dangerous wild cats, so the rabbit is not covered."],"a":4,"e":"Under the **ejusdem generis** rule, general words following a list of at least two **specific** words are limited to things of the **same kind** as the list, here dangerous wild animals, so a pet rabbit is not covered. The literal meaning would produce an outcome Parliament plainly did not intend. Expressio unius would make the words \"other animals\" pointless. The Act's evident purpose concerns dangerous animals, not general welfare, and the list gives no indication about keeping animals indoors."},{"t":"parliament-legislation-privilege","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"The Secretary of State makes regulations under a parent Act which provides that they are subject to annulment in pursuance of a resolution of either House of Parliament. The regulations are laid before both Houses. Twenty-five days later, the House of Lords passes a motion that the regulations be annulled. The House of Commons takes no action.\n\nWhat is the effect of the Lords' resolution?","o":["It requires the courts to quash the regulations as ultra vires.","It is effective: under the negative procedure either House may resolve to annul within 40 days, so no further proceedings may be taken under the regulations.","It delays the regulations for one year, after which they take effect under the Parliament Acts.","It has no effect, because the regulations needed the approval of both Houses and so never came into force.","It has no effect, because only the House of Commons can annul delegated legislation."],"a":1,"e":"Under the **negative procedure**, an instrument stays law unless **either House** resolves to annul it **within 40 days** (Statutory Instruments Act 1946 s.5); after such a resolution **no further proceedings** may be taken under it and it may be revoked. The Lords therefore has a legal power here, even if it rarely uses it. Approval by both Houses describes the affirmative procedure. The Parliament Acts do not apply to delegated legislation, and annulment by Parliament is not a judicial quashing."},{"t":"crown-executive-prerogative","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"An Act gives the Home Secretary power to revoke a licence where she is satisfied that the holder is not a fit and proper person. A senior civil servant in the Home Office, acting in the Home Secretary's name, revokes Shona's licence. The Home Secretary never saw the file. Nothing in the Act says that the Home Secretary must take the decision personally. Shona argues that the decision is unlawful because it was not taken by the minister.\n\nIs Shona's argument likely to succeed?","o":["Yes, because a statutory power given to a minister can never be exercised by anyone else.","Yes, unless the Home Secretary later ratifies the decision in writing.","No, because officials may exercise a minister's powers in the minister's name, as the minister remains accountable to Parliament for them.","Yes, because the civil servant fettered the Home Secretary's discretion.","No, but only because Parliament approved the delegation by affirmative resolution."],"a":2,"e":"Under the **Carltona principle**, civil servants may exercise a **minister's** powers in the minister's name, because the minister cannot take every decision personally and remains **accountable to Parliament**; this is an exception to the rule against delegation. It does not apply where the statute requires a **personal** decision, which this Act does not. No ratification or parliamentary approval is needed. Fettering concerns rigid policies, not who takes the decision."},{"t":"devolution-wales","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Senedd Cymru passes an Act requiring large supermarkets to display calorie information on all hot food sold. Section 1 states that the duty applies to every large supermarket \"in England and Wales\". The Act's extent section provides that it extends to England and Wales.\n\nWhich of the following best describes section 1?","o":["It is outside the Senedd's legislative competence so far as it applies to supermarkets in England, and to that extent it is not law.","It is valid until the UK Parliament passes an Act repealing it.","It is valid in full, because under the reserved powers model the Senedd may legislate on anything that is not reserved.","It is valid in full, because England and Wales form a single legal jurisdiction.","It is valid in full unless the Secretary of State for Wales made a prohibition order within four weeks of the Bill being passed."],"a":0,"e":"A provision of a Senedd Act is outside competence, and **not law**, if it **applies otherwise than in relation to Wales** (s.108A(2)(b) GoWA 2006). Extending to England and Wales is permitted because there is a single jurisdiction, but **application** must be confined to Wales. The reserved powers model removes the list of subjects but keeps the territorial limits. A s.114 order or Westminster repeal is not needed for an out-of-competence provision to be ineffective."},{"t":"public-order","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"A campaign group plans weekly marches through Manchester city centre for the next two months. The Chief Constable of Greater Manchester Police reasonably believes that the marches will cause serious disruption to the life of the community, but not serious public disorder. He wants to ban all marches in the city centre for one month.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the Chief Constable's powers?","o":["He may apply to the council for a ban of up to three months, because serious disruption to the life of the community is a ground for a ban.","He may ban the marches only if the organisers failed to give six clear days' notice.","He may make an order banning the marches himself, because he is the chief officer of police.","He may ban the marches because he must take cumulative disruption from repeated protests into account.","He cannot obtain a ban, because a ban is available only where conditions would not be sufficient to prevent serious public disorder; he may instead impose conditions."],"a":4,"e":"A ban on processions under **s.13 POA 1986** is available only where the chief officer reasonably believes conditions under s.12 will **not be sufficient to prevent serious public disorder**; outside London he must apply to the **council**, which needs the **Home Secretary's consent**. Serious disruption justifies **conditions** under s.12, not a ban. The duty to consider cumulative disruption applies to conditions and creates no power to ban. Failure to give notice is an offence by the organisers, not a ground for a ban."},{"t":"judicial-review-procedure","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"The Secretary of State grants a licence for a large incinerator. Greenshore, a national environmental charity with expertise in air quality, wants to challenge the decision. It argues (1) that the minister failed to consider statutory air quality factors and (2) that the decision breaches the Article 8 rights of people living near the site. Greenshore itself owns no property near the site.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Greenshore's position?","o":["It can rely on Article 8, but has no standing for the first ground because that ground concerns only local residents.","It cannot bring any claim, because it is not directly affected by the decision.","It can bring both grounds, because a charity with expertise satisfies both the sufficient interest and the victim tests.","It is likely to have sufficient interest to bring judicial review on the first ground, but cannot rely on Article 8 under the Human Rights Act because it is not a victim.","It can bring the first ground only if it obtains the written consent of the residents."],"a":3,"e":"For ordinary judicial review the claimant needs a **sufficient interest** (s.31(3) SCA 1981), and courts take a generous approach to expert **pressure groups** raising important issues where no one else may challenge. A claim under the HRA is narrower: the claimant must be a **victim**, directly affected (s.7 HRA), which a campaign group without local property is not. So the domestic ground can proceed but the Article 8 ground cannot be brought by Greenshore. Residents' consent is not a standing requirement."},{"t":"judicial-review-grounds","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Under a statute, Brackley Borough Council may revoke street market licences \"for the proper management of the market\". After a group of traders signed a petition criticising the council leader, the council revoked their licences. Internal emails show that the council's only reason was to punish them for the petition.\n\nOn which ground is a judicial review challenge most likely to succeed?","o":["No ground, because the statute gives the council a broad discretion.","Legitimate expectation, because the traders expected their licences to continue indefinitely.","Irrationality, because no reasonable council could have revoked any licences.","Illegality, because the power was used for an improper purpose.","Procedural impropriety, because the council leader was automatically disqualified for bias."],"a":3,"e":"A power given for one purpose must not be used for another; using a power to manage a market to **punish critics** is an **improper purpose**, a form of **illegality**. Irrationality has a very high threshold and is not needed where the purpose is plainly wrong. There is no financial interest giving automatic disqualification, and no clear promise founding a legitimate expectation. Even a broad discretion must be used to promote the purpose of the Act."},{"t":"human-rights-act","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Police officers suspect that Darren has information about a planned terrorist attack. To make him talk, they deprive him of sleep for several days and keep him in a freezing cell, conduct which reaches the minimum level of severity for inhuman treatment. The force argues that its actions were justified because they were necessary to protect the public and proportionate to the threat.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position under the Human Rights Act 1998?","o":["There is no breach if the force can show the treatment was a proportionate means of protecting public safety.","There is no breach, because the UK can derogate from Article 3 in a public emergency.","The treatment breaches Article 3, because the prohibition of torture and inhuman or degrading treatment is absolute and cannot be justified.","There is no breach, because Article 3 may be restricted in the interests of national security.","There is a breach of Article 5 only, because Article 3 does not apply to detention."],"a":2,"e":"**Article 3** is an **absolute** right: once treatment reaches the minimum level of severity, there is a breach whatever the reason, and **no proportionality** or public interest justification is possible. Qualified rights such as Articles 8-11 can be restricted for national security, but Article 3 cannot. It applies fully to people in detention. **No derogation** from Article 3 is permitted, even in an emergency."},{"t":"precedent","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"A district judge in the County Court is deciding a contract claim. The defendant relies on a Court of Appeal decision holding that an agreement between a cohabiting couple about household spending was not intended to be legally binding. In the present case, the couple had already separated and had moved to different homes when they made their written agreement about paying the mortgage.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the district judge may do?","o":["Distinguish the Court of Appeal decision on the basis that the material facts are different.","Nothing, because the County Court must follow the Court of Appeal decision whatever the facts.","Overrule the Court of Appeal decision if she considers it wrongly decided.","Refuse to follow it on the ground that it was decided per incuriam.","Refer the question to the Court of Appeal, which must decide it before the claim can continue."],"a":0,"e":"**Distinguishing** is available to **any** court, however low: where the **material facts** differ, the earlier ratio does not apply. Here the parties' separation is a material difference. A lower court is bound by the ratio of a higher court where the facts are materially the same, so it cannot overrule it, and the per incuriam exception lets the Court of Appeal depart from its own decisions, not lower courts depart from higher ones. There is no reference procedure of the kind described."},{"t":"legislation-structure","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"An Act of Senedd Cymru contains no provision about when it comes into force. It receives Royal Assent on Thursday 14 May 2026, when the Letters Patent signifying Royal Assent are notified to the Clerk of the Senedd.\n\nWhen does the Act come into force?","o":["At the end of the period of two months beginning with the day of Royal Assent.","At the beginning of Friday 15 May 2026.","On a day appointed by commencement regulations made by the Welsh Ministers.","At the beginning of Thursday 14 May 2026.","When it is published on legislation.gov.uk in both English and Welsh."],"a":1,"e":"Under **s.30 Legislation (Wales) Act 2019**, an Act of the Senedd that says nothing about commencement comes into force at the **beginning of the day after** Royal Assent. The **start of the day of Royal Assent** is the Westminster default rule under s.4 Interpretation Act 1978. Commencement regulations and fixed periods apply only where the Act provides for them, and publication has no bearing on commencement."},{"t":"courts-judiciary","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Callum was convicted by a bench of lay magistrates of a summary-only offence after a trial. He believes the magistrates were wrong to prefer the prosecution witness's account of the facts to his own, and he wants the evidence heard again by a different court.\n\nTo which court should Callum appeal against his conviction?","o":["The Crown Court.","The County Court.","The Court of Appeal (Criminal Division).","The Administrative Court, by way of judicial review.","The Supreme Court."],"a":0,"e":"The **Crown Court** is a trial court for indictable cases but also hears **appeals from magistrates' courts**, rehearing the evidence, which suits an appeal on the facts. The County Court is a civil court. The Court of Appeal (Criminal Division) hears appeals from the Crown Court, not directly from magistrates. Judicial review concerns the legality of a decision, not a rehearing of the facts, and the Supreme Court hears only points of law of general public importance on further appeal."},{"t":"sovereignty-rule-of-law","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"The (fictional) Infrastructure Planning Act 2026 contains a provision that is inconsistent with a section of the Government of Wales Act 2006 governing the Senedd's powers. The 2026 Act does not mention the 2006 Act and contains no express words of repeal. A party argues that the relevant section of the 2006 Act has been impliedly repealed.\n\nHow is the court most likely to approach this argument?","o":["It must apply the 2006 Act, because devolution legislation can be repealed only after a referendum in Wales.","It must apply the later Act, because a later Act always impliedly repeals an inconsistent earlier Act.","It must declare the 2026 Act invalid to the extent of the inconsistency.","It is likely to hold that the 2006 Act, as a constitutional statute, cannot be impliedly repealed, so express or unmistakably clear words would be needed.","It must refer the question to Senedd Cymru, as the inconsistency concerns devolved powers."],"a":3,"e":"Ordinary statutes are subject to **implied repeal**, but **constitutional statutes**, such as the devolution Acts and the HRA, can be repealed only **expressly** or by **unmistakably clear words** (Thoburn). The general rule that a later Act prevails is therefore not decisive here. The statutory referendum requirement (s.A1(3) GoWA 2006) concerns only abolishing the Senedd and Welsh Government, not changing the Senedd's powers, so it does not decide this question. Courts cannot declare an Act of Parliament invalid, and the Senedd has no role in deciding the question."},{"t":"statutory-interpretation","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"The (fictional) School Safety Act 2025 makes it an offence \"to be in possession of a bladed article on school premises\". The Act says nothing about any mental element. Freya, a parent collecting her child, was found with a craft knife in her handbag that her teenage son had slipped into it without her knowledge.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how a court is likely to approach the offence?","o":["It will treat the offence as strict liability, because the Act is silent about any mental element.","It will refuse to interpret the Act and leave the matter to Parliament.","It will apply the literal rule, so Freya is guilty because she physically had the knife.","It will apply the mischief rule, so Freya is guilty because the Act aims to keep knives out of schools.","It will presume that mens rea is required unless the Act rebuts the presumption by clear words or necessary implication."],"a":4,"e":"There is a strong **presumption that mens rea is required** for criminal offences, which applies **unless the Act rebuts it** by clear words or **necessary implication** (Sweet v Parsley). Silence does not make an offence strict liability; it is the starting point for the presumption. A purely literal or mischief-based reading would ignore the presumption. Courts must interpret the Act; they cannot decline to do so."},{"t":"parliament-legislation-privilege","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Felicity, the chief executive of a technology company, gives oral evidence to a House of Commons select committee inquiry. During her evidence, she accuses a rival company of fraud. The rival company wants to sue Felicity for defamation over what she said to the committee.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The claim may proceed, because parliamentary privilege protects only MPs and peers, not witnesses.","The claim will fail only if Felicity proves she honestly believed her accusation.","The claim will fail, because evidence given to a parliamentary committee is part of proceedings in Parliament and is absolutely privileged.","The claim may proceed if the committee's chair gives permission.","The claim may proceed, because select committee hearings are not debates on the floor of the House."],"a":2,"e":"Under **Article 9 of the Bill of Rights 1689**, proceedings in Parliament cannot be questioned in any court; **proceedings** include **committee** proceedings and **evidence given to committees**, so the immunity is **absolute** and covers witnesses as well as members. It does not depend on honest belief, which is relevant only to defences such as qualified privilege. Committee hearings are proceedings just as much as debates, and no one can give permission to waive Article 9 in this way."},{"t":"crown-executive-prerogative","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"The UK is party to a treaty giving foreign investors certain protections. An Act of Parliament gave those protections effect in domestic law and created rights that investors can enforce in UK courts. The government now proposes to withdraw from the treaty using prerogative powers. The withdrawal would mean that the statutory rights could no longer be relied on.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the legal position?","o":["The government may withdraw, but must pay compensation to the investors affected.","The government may withdraw only with the consent of the devolved legislatures.","The government may withdraw using the prerogative, because the conduct of foreign affairs is non-justiciable.","The government cannot use the prerogative in a way that removes rights created by Parliament; an Act of Parliament is needed.","The government may withdraw, provided it lays the decision before Parliament for 21 sitting days."],"a":3,"e":"The prerogative cannot be used to **change domestic law** or **remove rights** that Parliament has created; that requires an **Act of Parliament** (Miller 1). Although treaty-making is usually non-justiciable, the courts decide the **limits** of a prerogative power, including whether it can override statutory rights. The 21-sitting-day procedure under CRaG 2010 concerns ratifying treaties. Compensation and devolved consent do not answer the need for statutory authority."},{"t":"devolution-wales","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Senedd Cymru passes a Bill reforming the law on renting homes in Wales. The UK Government accepts that the Bill is within the Senedd's legislative competence, but has reasonable grounds to believe that it would have an adverse effect on the operation of the law as it applies in England. The Bill was passed two weeks ago.\n\nWhat action is available to the UK Government?","o":["The Secretary of State for Wales may make an order prohibiting the Clerk from submitting the Bill for Royal Assent.","No action is possible, because the Bill is within the Senedd's competence.","The Attorney General may refer the Bill to the Supreme Court, which will strike it down because of its effect on England.","The UK Government may ask the monarch to refuse Royal Assent on the advice of UK ministers.","The Presiding Officer may withdraw the Bill because it affects the law in England."],"a":0,"e":"Within four weeks of a Bill being passed, the **Secretary of State for Wales** may make an order under **s.114 GoWA 2006** prohibiting it from being submitted for Royal Assent where there are reasonable grounds to believe it would have an **adverse effect** on a **reserved matter** or on the **operation of the law as it applies in England**, or would be incompatible with international obligations or the interests of defence or national security. A Supreme Court reference under s.112 concerns **competence**, which is accepted here. The Presiding Officer cannot withdraw a passed Bill, and Royal Assent is not refused by convention."},{"t":"public-order","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Agnes, a street preacher, preaches peacefully in a town square. A group of onlookers become angry and some start threatening to attack her. PC Morris, fearing imminent violence, arrests Agnes to prevent a breach of the peace. He takes no action against the people making the threats.\n\nIs the arrest of Agnes likely to be lawful?","o":["Yes, because any arrest to prevent a breach of the peace is lawful if violence is imminent.","Yes, because Agnes's preaching was the cause of the threatened violence.","Yes, because Agnes committed the criminal offence of breach of the peace.","No, because breach of the peace powers apply only on private premises.","No, because police action should normally be taken against those threatening violence, not against a person acting lawfully, unless that is necessary in an extreme case."],"a":4,"e":"Police may act to prevent an **imminent breach of the peace**, but action should normally be taken against the people **threatening violence**, not someone acting **lawfully**; acting against the lawful speaker is justified only in **extreme** cases where it is necessary (Redmond-Bate). Imminence alone is therefore not enough. **Breach of the peace is not a criminal offence**. The powers apply in public places and, in some cases, on private premises."},{"t":"judicial-review-procedure","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Hamza filed a claim for judicial review of a council's decision to refuse him a taxi licence. On 3 March, he was served with an order of a High Court judge refusing permission on the papers. The judge did not certify the claim as totally without merit.\n\nWhat is Hamza's best next step?","o":["Request reconsideration at an oral hearing within 14 days of service of the refusal.","File a fresh claim for judicial review on the same grounds.","Request that the decision be reconsidered at an oral hearing, within 7 days of service of the refusal.","Appeal to the Court of Appeal within 21 days of the refusal.","Nothing, because a refusal of permission on the papers is final."],"a":2,"e":"Where permission is refused **on the papers**, the claimant may ask for the decision to be **reconsidered at an oral hearing**, within **7 days** of service of the refusal (CPR 54.12), unless the claim is certified **totally without merit**. An appeal to the Court of Appeal is the route only where oral renewal is not available or has failed. A fresh claim would be an abuse and out of time, and the period for renewal is 7 days, not 14."},{"t":"judicial-review-grounds","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Northdale County Council has a statutory duty to consult before closing public libraries. It publishes a consultation paper that presents only the option of closing three libraries, without mentioning the alternatives it had considered, such as reduced opening hours. It allows ten days for responses, and internal minutes show the closures had already been finally agreed before the consultation began.\n\nOn which ground is a challenge most likely to succeed?","o":["Irrationality, because closing libraries is a decision no reasonable council could reach.","No ground, because the council did carry out a consultation.","Procedural impropriety, because the consultation did not take place at a formative stage and did not give adequate information or time.","Illegality, because the council fettered its discretion by adopting a policy.","Legitimate expectation, because residents expected the libraries to stay open."],"a":2,"e":"Where there is a duty to consult, consultation must meet the **Gunning principles**: it must be at a **formative stage**, give **sufficient reasons** and information (including alternatives where fairness requires), allow **adequate time** and be **conscientiously considered** (Moseley). A predetermined decision, a single option and ten days fail these, so this is **procedural impropriety**. Closing libraries is not irrational in itself. Merely going through the motions of consultation is not enough."},{"t":"human-rights-act","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"In a possession claim in the County Court, the defendant tenant argues that a provision of a 1988 Act on which the landlord relies is incompatible with his Article 8 rights. He asks the district judge to make a declaration of incompatibility.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the district judge's position?","o":["The district judge may disapply the provision if it is incompatible with Article 8.","The district judge may make a declaration of incompatibility, which will prevent the landlord relying on the provision.","The district judge must ignore Article 8, because the 1988 Act was passed before the Human Rights Act 1998.","The district judge must adjourn the claim and refer the question to the European Court of Human Rights.","The district judge cannot make a declaration of incompatibility, but must read the provision compatibly with Article 8 so far as it is possible to do so."],"a":4,"e":"Only the **higher courts** (High Court and above) can make a **declaration of incompatibility** under s.4, so the County Court cannot. Every court must, however, read legislation compatibly with Convention rights **so far as it is possible** under **s.3**, which applies to Acts **whenever passed**. No court can disapply an Act of Parliament, and even a declaration would not affect the provision's validity or the parties. There is no reference procedure to Strasbourg."},{"t":"precedent","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"A High Court judge is deciding a claim in negligence. A Court of Appeal decision directly in point holds that a duty of care is owed in such cases. In a later Supreme Court decision on a different issue, one Justice remarked, in a passage not necessary to the decision, that he doubted whether a duty should be owed in such cases.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position of the High Court judge?","o":["The judge must refer the conflict to the Supreme Court for a ruling.","The judge is bound to follow the Court of Appeal's ratio, and the Supreme Court Justice's remarks are persuasive only.","The judge is bound by neither, because a single High Court judge is not bound by appellate decisions on negligence.","The judge may choose freely between the two, because both courts rank above the High Court.","The judge must follow the Supreme Court Justice's remarks, because the Supreme Court is the highest court."],"a":1,"e":"Only the **ratio decidendi** of a higher court binds; remarks not necessary to the decision are **obiter dicta** and are **persuasive only**, even when made in the Supreme Court. The Court of Appeal's ratio directly in point binds the High Court. A High Court judge has no free choice between binding and persuasive authority, and there is no reference procedure to the Supreme Court."},{"t":"eu-law-brexit","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"In 2026, Kerrigan Ltd challenges a provision of assimilated direct legislation (a converted EU regulation) in the High Court. It argues that the provision breaches the EU general principle of equal treatment and should be disapplied.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The argument may succeed, because the principle of supremacy of EU law still applies to assimilated law.","The argument fails, because the general principles of EU law no longer form part of domestic law from 1 January 2024.","The argument may succeed, but only in the Court of Appeal or Supreme Court.","The argument may succeed, because the regulation was made before the end of 2020 and so remains subject to EU general principles.","The High Court must refer the question to the Court of Justice of the European Union."],"a":1,"e":"The **Retained EU Law (Revocation and Reform) Act 2023** abolished the **general principles of EU law** as part of domestic law, and the **supremacy** of EU law, from **1 January 2024**, so they cannot be used to challenge or disapply assimilated law. The date the regulation was made is irrelevant. The higher courts' power to depart from assimilated case law is a different point. UK courts can no longer refer questions to the CJEU outside narrow Withdrawal Agreement routes."},{"t":"courts-judiciary","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Gwen is representing herself in a County Court claim against her former landlord. At the hearing she brings her friend Rob, who is not a lawyer, and asks the judge to let Rob put her case and question the landlord's witness, because she is nervous.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Rob's position?","o":["Rob may address the court only if Gwen signs a written authority appointing him her agent.","Rob may address the court and question witnesses as of right, because Gwen is a litigant in person.","Rob commits a criminal offence if he sits beside Gwen and advises her during the hearing.","Rob may not attend at all, because only qualified lawyers may assist a party in court.","Rob may sit with Gwen, take notes and quietly advise her, but has no right of audience; the court may exceptionally give him permission to address it."],"a":4,"e":"A **litigant in person** may always address the court in their own case. A **McKenzie friend** may sit with them, take notes and quietly advise, but has **no right of audience**; the court has a discretion to allow a lay person to address it **exceptionally**. A signed authority cannot confer a right of audience, which is a **reserved legal activity**. Quiet assistance is not exercising a right of audience, so it is neither forbidden nor an offence."},{"t":"sovereignty-rule-of-law","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"A former Cabinet minister plans to publish memoirs describing in detail the disagreements between ministers during Cabinet discussions twelve years ago. The government seeks an injunction to stop publication, relying on the convention of collective ministerial responsibility.\n\nHow is the court most likely to respond?","o":["It will not enforce the convention itself, and although Cabinet confidentiality can be protected by the law of confidence, an injunction is unlikely after so long because publication would no longer harm collective responsibility.","It will grant a declaration that the minister acted unlawfully but no injunction.","It will grant the injunction, because breach of a constitutional convention is unlawful.","It will refuse to hear the claim, because courts can never take account of conventions.","It will grant the injunction, because Cabinet discussions are confidential permanently."],"a":0,"e":"Courts **do not enforce conventions** as such, but they may **recognise** them when deciding a legal issue; collective responsibility can make Cabinet discussions **confidential**, protectable through the law of confidence (Jonathan Cape). Confidentiality lasts only so long as disclosure would cause harm, so after twelve years an injunction is unlikely. Breaching a convention is not in itself unlawful, and the courts can take conventions into account."},{"t":"statutory-interpretation","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"The (fictional) Emergency Services Act 2024 makes it an offence to park a vehicle \"in the vicinity of\" a fire station so as to obstruct fire engines. Hal parks his car inside the fire station's yard, blocking the engines. He argues that \"in the vicinity of\" means near the fire station, not inside it, so he has committed no offence.\n\nWhich approach would most naturally lead the court to convict Hal?","o":["The presumption against retrospective effect, because Hal's parking occurred after the Act came into force.","The expressio unius rule, because the Act mentions only the vicinity of fire stations.","The golden rule, choosing the meaning of \"in the vicinity of\" that includes \"in\" to avoid an absurd result.","The ejusdem generis rule, because fire stations are of the same kind as other emergency premises.","The literal rule, giving \"in the vicinity of\" its ordinary meaning of \"near\"."],"a":2,"e":"Under the **golden rule** (narrow approach), where words can bear more than one meaning the court chooses the one that avoids an **absurd** result; it would be absurd for obstruction near a fire station to be an offence but not obstruction inside it. The literal rule is the approach that would favour Hal. Ejusdem generis and expressio unius concern lists of words, which do not appear here. The presumption against retrospectivity has nothing to do with the meaning of these words."},{"t":"parliament-legislation-privilege","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"The government introduces a Bill in the House of Commons. Having taken legal advice, the minister in charge cannot say that the Bill's provisions are compatible with the Convention rights, but the government wants it to proceed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The Bill cannot proceed unless the minister makes a statement of compatibility.","The Bill may proceed only if it is passed under the Parliament Acts without the Lords' consent.","Before second reading the minister must make a written statement that, although unable to make a statement of compatibility, the government wishes the House to proceed with the Bill.","The Bill must be referred to the Supreme Court for a ruling on compatibility before second reading.","The Bill may proceed, but if enacted the courts will treat it as invalid to the extent of any incompatibility."],"a":2,"e":"Under **s.19 HRA 1998**, before second reading of a government Bill in each House the minister must make a written **statement of compatibility** or a statement that, although unable to do so, the **government wishes the House to proceed**. The Bill can therefore continue. There is no pre-legislative reference to the Supreme Court, and if enacted the Act is fully **valid**: the courts could at most make a declaration of incompatibility. The Parliament Acts have nothing to do with compatibility."},{"t":"crown-executive-prerogative","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"The Prime Minister asks the King to dissolve Parliament, and the King does so. A campaign group believes the Prime Minister's real reason was to avoid a critical select committee report and applies for judicial review, arguing that the advice was given for an improper purpose and frustrated Parliament's functions.\n\nHow is the court likely to respond?","o":["It will consider the claim, because the exercise of all prerogative powers is now justiciable.","It will decline to consider the claim, because statute provides that a court may not question the exercise of the power to dissolve Parliament or its limits.","It will quash the dissolution if the Prime Minister cannot give a reasonable justification.","It will refer the question to the House of Commons Committee of Privileges.","It will review the decision in the same way as the advice to prorogue Parliament for five weeks was reviewed."],"a":1,"e":"The **Dissolution and Calling of Parliament Act 2022** revived the prerogative to dissolve Parliament, and **s.3** is an **ouster clause**: a court may not question the exercise or purported exercise of the dissolution power, or its **limits or extent**. The prorogation challenge in Miller 2 concerned a different prerogative with no ouster clause, which makes that analogy tempting but wrong. Not every prerogative is justiciable, and the Committee of Privileges has no role here."},{"t":"devolution-wales","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Senedd Cymru passes a Bill setting a minimum hourly wage, higher than the national minimum wage, that all employers in Wales must pay their workers. The Bill applies only in relation to Wales and extends only to England and Wales.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the Bill?","o":["It is within competence, but will become invalid if the UK Parliament later legislates on wages.","It is within competence, because under the reserved powers model the Senedd may legislate on any matter affecting Wales.","It is within competence, because it applies only in relation to Wales.","It is within competence unless the Secretary of State for Wales makes an order within four weeks.","It is outside the Senedd's legislative competence, because its purpose relates to the reserved matter of employment rights, including the national minimum wage."],"a":4,"e":"A provision is outside competence if it **relates to a reserved matter** in **Schedule 7A** (s.108A(2)(c) GoWA 2006), judged by its **purpose** and effect; **employment rights**, including the **National Minimum Wage Act 1998**, are reserved. Applying only to Wales satisfies the territorial tests but cannot save a provision on a reserved matter. The reserved powers model allows legislation only on matters that are **not** reserved. Competence does not depend on a s.114 order or later Westminster legislation."},{"t":"public-order","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Owain protests alone outside a GP surgery every morning, using a megaphone to shout slogans about vaccines. The noise regularly prevents staff from hearing patients and disrupts appointments. The senior police officer reasonably believes the noise may result in serious disruption to the surgery's activities.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the police powers?","o":["The officer must arrest Owain for the offence of breach of the peace.","The police have no power, because a public assembly requires at least two people.","The chief officer may apply to the council for an order banning Owain's protest.","The officer may impose conditions only if Owain failed to give six clear days' written notice.","The officer may impose conditions on Owain's one-person protest, such as limiting the use of the megaphone."],"a":4,"e":"The PCSCA 2022 introduced **s.14ZA POA 1986**, allowing conditions on a **one-person protest** where its **noise** may cause **serious disruption** to the activities of an organisation nearby or have a significant impact on people. It is true that an assembly needs two or more people, which is why s.14ZA was created. Bans under s.13 apply only to processions. No advance notice is required for static or one-person protests, and breach of the peace is not a criminal offence."},{"t":"judicial-review-procedure","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"On 10 February, Mereside Council decided to withdraw funding from a community centre run by Hollybank Trust. The trust first learned of the decision on 1 March. After pre-action correspondence, the council's solicitors agreed in writing that the council would not take any time point if the trust issued its claim by 31 May. The trust filed its claim for judicial review on 28 May.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position on time?","o":["The claim is in time, because a three-month limit is replaced by a one-year limit where a charity is involved.","The claim is out of time unless the court extends time, because time ran from the decision on 10 February and the parties cannot extend it by agreement.","The claim is in time, because the council agreed in writing to extend time to 31 May.","The claim is in time, because it was filed within three months of the trust learning of the decision.","The claim is in time, because time stops running while the pre-action protocol is followed."],"a":1,"e":"A judicial review claim must be brought **promptly** and in any event **within three months** of when the grounds **first arose**, normally the date of the **decision** (CPR 54.5), here 10 February, so the deadline was 10 May. Time does not run from when the claimant found out. The parties **cannot extend** time by agreement, and **pre-action correspondence does not stop time running**; only the court can extend time. There is no special one-year period for charities."},{"t":"judicial-review-grounds","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"A (fictional) Act sets up a Relocation Compensation Board and provides that \"any determination of the Board shall not be called in question in any court of law\". The Board rejects Ingrid's claim after misinterpreting the statutory eligibility rules, an error of law. Ingrid applies for judicial review.\n\nHow is the court most likely to treat the ouster clause?","o":["It will not prevent review, because a decision based on an error of law is not a true determination but only a purported one.","It will prevent review of errors of law, but not of procedural unfairness.","It will prevent review completely, because Parliament is sovereign and the words are clear.","It will prevent review unless Ingrid applies within six weeks.","It will prevent review, but the court may make a declaration of incompatibility."],"a":0,"e":"Courts read **total ouster clauses** very narrowly, because access to the courts is fundamental to the **rule of law**: a decision tainted by an **error of law** is a **purported determination**, a nullity, which the clause does not protect (Anisminic). Sovereignty does not lead the court to apply the clause literally. Time-limited ousters are upheld, but this clause contains no time limit. A declaration of incompatibility concerns Convention rights and is irrelevant here."},{"t":"human-rights-act","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"A local housing authority adopts a policy of publishing on its website the names and addresses of all its tenants who are more than one month behind with their rent, to encourage prompt payment. Leila, a tenant two months in arrears because of illness, complains that the policy breaches her right to respect for private life under Article 8.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome?","o":["The policy is likely to be disproportionate, because less intrusive measures could achieve the aim of recovering rent.","There is a breach, because Article 8 is an absolute right.","There is no breach, because the authority's policy was published and so was prescribed by law.","There is no breach, because Article 8 does not apply to publication of financial information.","There is no breach, because recovering rent is a legitimate aim."],"a":0,"e":"Article 8 is a **qualified** right: an interference is lawful only if **prescribed by law**, for a **legitimate aim** and **proportionate**, applying the four questions including whether a **less intrusive measure** could have been used. Recovering rent is a legitimate aim, but publicly naming every tenant in arrears goes further than necessary when ordinary recovery methods exist. A legitimate aim or a legal basis alone is not enough, and Article 8 is qualified, not absolute."},{"t":"legislation-structure","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"In a dispute about a (fictional) Act of Parliament, one party argues that a provision in Schedule 4 to the Act has less legal force than the numbered sections in the body of the Act. It also argues that the Explanatory Notes published with the Act give a different meaning which the court must follow.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Both the Schedule and the Explanatory Notes are part of the Act and have equal force.","The Schedule has less force than the sections, and the Explanatory Notes bind the court.","The Schedule is part of the Act only if a section expressly says it has the force of law, and the Explanatory Notes bind the court where the Act is ambiguous.","The Schedule is as much part of the Act as the sections, while the Explanatory Notes are not part of the Act and may be used only as an aid to context.","Neither the Schedule nor the Explanatory Notes are part of the Act."],"a":3,"e":"**Schedules** are **part of the Act** and have the same legal force as the sections; a section in the body introduces each Schedule. **Explanatory Notes** are written by the government department, are **not enacted** and have no legal force, though courts may use them as an **external aid** to context and purpose. They never bind the court, even where the Act is ambiguous."},{"t":"eu-law-brexit","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"In a commercial claim, the High Court must interpret a provision of assimilated law derived from an EU regulation. A party relies on a 2023 judgment of the Court of Justice of the European Union interpreting the identical provision of the EU regulation.\n\nWhat is the status of the 2023 CJEU judgment in the High Court?","o":["The High Court must refer the question of interpretation to the CJEU.","It is binding unless the Court of Appeal or Supreme Court has departed from it.","The High Court must ignore it, because CJEU decisions have no relevance after Brexit.","It is not binding, but the High Court may have regard to it if it is relevant.","It is binding, because it interprets a provision of assimilated law."],"a":3,"e":"Under **s.6 EUWA 2018**, UK courts are **not bound** by CJEU decisions made **after 31 December 2020**, but **may have regard** to them if relevant. Only pre-2021 CJEU decisions are **assimilated case law** binding the High Court and lower courts. UK courts can no longer refer questions to the CJEU outside narrow Withdrawal Agreement routes, and a later decision can still be considered, so it need not be ignored."}],"wills":[{"t":"validity","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Harold, aged 81, made a will last year leaving his estate equally to his two daughters and nothing to his son, Jack, explaining in a signed note that he had already given Jack large sums to buy a house. At the time Harold firmly believed that his neighbours were monitoring him through his television, and his GP records this as a fixed delusion. When giving instructions he described his house, savings and shares accurately. Jack wants to challenge the will.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the validity of Harold's will?","o":["It is invalid, because a person suffering from a fixed delusion cannot satisfy the test for testamentary capacity.","Its validity depends on whether Harold met the Mental Capacity Act 2005 test, which governs all challenges to wills.","It is valid, because the delusion did not influence the gifts and Harold understood his act, his property and the claims on him.","It is valid only if a medical practitioner witnessed the will and recorded that Harold had capacity.","It is presumed invalid, because cutting out one child raises a presumption of undue influence by the others."],"a":2,"e":"The **Banks v Goodfellow** test governs capacity: the testator must understand the nature and effect of making a will, the extent of their property and the claims they ought to consider, with **no disorder of the mind poisoning their affections or perverting their judgment**. A delusion matters only if it **influences the dispositions**; Harold's belief about his neighbours had nothing to do with his gifts, and he gave a rational reason for excluding Jack. The Mental Capacity Act 2005 test does not replace the common law test for a challenged will, the **golden rule** is good practice rather than a legal requirement, and undue influence is **never presumed** for wills."},{"t":"formalities","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Ruth asked her neighbours, Pat and Sam, to witness her will at her kitchen table. With both of them watching, Ruth signed the will and Pat then signed as a witness. Before Sam could sign, Ruth went upstairs to take a phone call; while she was out of the room, Sam signed beneath Pat's signature and went home. Ruth died suddenly that evening. The will contains a standard attestation clause.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the will was validly executed?","o":["It was validly executed, because Ruth signed in the presence of both witnesses present at the same time.","It was validly executed, because witnesses do not need to sign in each other's presence.","It was not validly executed, because Pat and Sam did not sign while both were in the room together.","It was not validly executed, because Sam neither signed nor acknowledged his signature in Ruth's presence.","It was validly executed, because the attestation clause raises a presumption of due execution that cannot be displaced."],"a":3,"e":"Under **Wills Act 1837 s.9(d)** each witness must sign, or acknowledge their signature, **in the presence of the testator**. Ruth's own signature satisfied s.9(c) because both witnesses were present together, but Sam signed after she had left the room and she died before he could acknowledge his signature to her, so the will fails. Witnesses need not sign in front of each other, but that does not cure the absence of the testator. The presumption of due execution from an attestation clause is **rebuttable** by evidence of what actually happened."},{"t":"executors","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Tessa's will appoints her daughter Una, aged 16, and her friend Vince as executors. It leaves her estate on trust for Una until she is 25. Tessa has just died. Vince is willing to act and wants to obtain a grant as soon as possible.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the grant that can be obtained?","o":["Vince cannot act alone, because a trust for a minor means at least two personal representatives must take the grant.","Vince and Una's guardian must take letters of administration with will annexed until Una is 18.","Una's appointment is void because she was a minor when the will was made, so Vince takes probate as sole executor.","Vince must apply for letters of administration with will annexed, because one of the executors is a minor.","Vince can take probate alone, with power reserved to Una to prove when she reaches 18."],"a":4,"e":"A minor cannot take a grant, but where there is an adult co-executor, that executor **takes probate with power reserved** to the minor, who can apply at 18. The rule that **at least two** personal representatives are needed where a **minority or life interest** arises applies to **administrators**, not executors (Senior Courts Act 1981 s.114). Letters of administration with will annexed for the minor's use and benefit are needed only where the minor is the **sole** executor, and a minor's appointment is not void."},{"t":"alterations-revocation","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Walter kept the original of his will at his daughter Xena's house. After an argument with his son, Walter telephoned Xena from his own home and told her to burn the will, saying he wanted to 'start again'. Xena burned it in her fireplace while still on the phone to him. Walter died a week later without making a new will. A photocopy of the will survives.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of Xena's actions?","o":["The will is revoked, because Xena destroyed it at Walter's direction and he intended to revoke it.","The will is revoked, because a will that cannot be found after death is presumed to have been destroyed with intention to revoke.","The will is not revoked, because only the testator personally can revoke a will by destruction.","The will is not revoked, because it was not destroyed in Walter's presence, and its contents can be proved from the photocopy.","The will is revoked, because Walter ratified the destruction by not making a new will before he died."],"a":3,"e":"Revocation by destruction under **Wills Act 1837 s.20** needs burning, tearing or otherwise destroying with intention to revoke, by the testator or by someone else **in his presence and by his direction**. Being on the telephone is not presence, so Xena's act did not revoke the will, and a testator cannot later ratify a destruction done out of his presence. Another person can destroy a will for the testator if both requirements are met, and the presumption of revocation does not arise because the will was **not in Walter's possession** and how it was destroyed is known, so the will can be proved from the copy."},{"t":"interpretation","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Yvette's will leaves '£10,000 to my nephew Ahmed, to be paid from my account at Halifax', '£5,000 to my friend Bella', and the residue to her son Carl. When Yvette died, the Halifax account held £4,000. Her estate is easily large enough to pay all debts, expenses and legacies.\n\nHow much will Ahmed receive, and from where?","o":["£4,000 only, because the gift has adeemed to the extent that the account fell short.","£10,000: £4,000 from the Halifax account and the remaining £6,000 from the general estate.","Nothing, because the account no longer held enough to pay the gift, so the gift has adeemed.","£10,000, all paid from residue, with the £4,000 in the Halifax account passing to Carl.","£9,000, because Ahmed's gift abates rateably with Bella's legacy."],"a":1,"e":"A gift of money **payable from a named fund** is a **demonstrative** gift. It is treated as specific so far as the fund exists, and any **shortfall is paid as a general legacy** from the rest of the estate, so it **cannot adeem**. Only specific gifts adeem, and abatement does not arise because the estate can pay everything in full."},{"t":"intestacy","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Dora died intestate last month. Three years ago the court made a judicial separation order between Dora and her husband, Ed, which was still in force when she died; they never divorced. Dora had no children. She is survived by Ed, by her mother and father, and by her brother Finn.\n\nWho is entitled to Dora's estate?","o":["Ed, because a spouse with no issue takes the whole estate.","Ed takes the personal chattels and the statutory legacy, with the balance to her parents.","Her mother, father and Finn in equal shares on the statutory trusts.","Finn, on the statutory trusts, because he is her closest relative of her own generation.","Her mother and father in equal shares absolutely."],"a":4,"e":"A spouse who is **judicially separated** at the date of death is treated as having **died before** the intestate (Matrimonial Causes Act 1973 s.18(2)), so Ed takes nothing on intestacy. With no spouse and no issue, the estate passes to the **parents in equal shares absolutely**, ahead of brothers and sisters (AEA 1925 s.46). Parents do not take on the statutory trusts, siblings take only if neither parent survives, and Ed's only route is a claim under the 1975 Act."},{"t":"outside-estate","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Gail and her sister Hannah bought a house together and hold both the legal title and the beneficial interest as joint tenants. Last year Gail made a will leaving 'my half share in the house' to her son Ian, and the next day she sent Hannah a signed letter saying that she wished to hold her share separately from then on. Hannah received the letter but did not reply. Gail has now died; Hannah is still alive.\n\nWho is entitled to Gail's interest in the house?","o":["Ian, because Gail's letter severed the joint tenancy, so her half share passes under her will.","Hannah, by survivorship, because a will cannot sever a beneficial joint tenancy.","Hannah, because severance by notice is effective only if the other joint tenant agrees to it.","Hannah, because severance takes effect only when a restriction is entered on the register.","Ian and Hannah equally, because the will and the right of survivorship each apply to half of Gail's share."],"a":0,"e":"A beneficial joint tenancy of land can be **severed by written notice** to the other joint tenant (**LPA 1925 s.36(2)**); the other's agreement is not needed, and entering a restriction protects the position but is not what severs. Once severed, Gail and Hannah held as **tenants in common**, so Gail's half share passes **under her will** to Ian. A will on its own cannot sever, which is why the separate lifetime notice is decisive here."},{"t":"grants","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Jasmine's will appoints her cousin Kofi as sole executor and leaves her residuary estate to 'my trustees, Lara and Mo, to hold on trust for my grandchildren'. It also leaves a pecuniary legacy to her friend Nell. Kofi has signed a renunciation. Jasmine's grandchildren are all adults.\n\nWho has the first right to apply for a grant, and which grant will be issued?","o":["Lara and Mo, as the trustees named in the will, for a grant of probate.","The grandchildren, as the people beneficially entitled to residue, for letters of administration with will annexed.","Nell, as a legatee, for letters of administration with will annexed.","Lara and Mo, as residuary legatees holding in trust, for letters of administration with will annexed.","Any of the grandchildren, for simple letters of administration, because the executor has renounced."],"a":3,"e":"Where there is a will but no proving executor, priority follows **NCPR r.20**: after the executor comes a **residuary legatee or devisee holding in trust** for another person, then other residuary beneficiaries, then other legatees and creditors. Lara and Mo are therefore first in line, ahead of the grandchildren and Nell. **Probate is issued only to executors**, so anyone else takes **letters of administration with will annexed**; simple administration is only for a total intestacy."},{"t":"administration","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Olu is the executor of his late aunt's estate; she died a year ago. Her will leaves the residue equally to her two nephews, Paddy and Quinn. Olu has paid all the debts and is about to distribute £80,000 to each nephew. He has heard that Paddy was made bankrupt last month, but Paddy has asked for his share to be paid directly into his own bank account.\n\nWhat should Olu do to protect himself before paying Paddy's share?","o":["Place notices under section 27 of the Trustee Act 1925, which will protect him against any claim by Paddy's creditors.","Carry out a bankruptcy search against Paddy and, if he is bankrupt, pay his share to his trustee in bankruptcy.","Pay Paddy as requested, because a beneficiary's entitlement under a will is not affected by bankruptcy.","Apply for a Benjamin order authorising him to distribute on the basis that Paddy is not bankrupt.","Retain Paddy's share until his bankruptcy is discharged and then pay it to him with interest."],"a":1,"e":"A bankrupt beneficiary's entitlement **vests in the trustee in bankruptcy**, so a PR who pays the bankrupt directly is **personally liable** to the trustee for that sum. Olu should **search** the Individual Insolvency Register (or the Land Charges register) and pay any bankrupt beneficiary's share to the trustee in bankruptcy. Section 27 notices protect only against **unknown** claims, a **Benjamin order** deals with missing beneficiaries, and the share now belongs to the trustee rather than waiting for Paddy's discharge."},{"t":"iht-lifetime","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"In the 2026/27 tax year Glenys, a widow, made the following gifts, in this order: £2,500 to her granddaughter on her wedding day, conditional on the marriage taking place; £250 to each of her eight other grandchildren at Christmas; and £10,000 to her son in January 2027. She made no gifts at all in 2025/26. Assume an annual exemption of £3,000, a small gifts exemption of £250 per recipient and a marriage exemption of £2,500 for a gift by a grandparent.\n\nWhat is the value of the potentially exempt transfer made to her son?","o":["£4,000.","£7,000.","£10,000.","£6,500.","£8,500."],"a":0,"e":"Specific exemptions apply before the annual exemption: the wedding gift is fully covered by the **marriage exemption** for a grandparent, and each £250 Christmas gift by the **small gifts exemption**. The son's gift can then use the **current year's annual exemption plus the unused exemption carried forward from 2025/26**, £6,000 in total, leaving a PET of £4,000. Using only one year's annual exemption gives £7,000, and setting the annual exemption against the already exempt wedding or Christmas gifts wrongly reduces what is left for the son's gift."},{"t":"iht-death-reliefs","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Rashid died in October 2026. For 12 years he had been a partner in a firm of accountants. The partnership agreement provides that, on a partner's death, his personal representatives must sell and the surviving partners must buy his share at a valuation. His share is worth £900,000. Assume business property relief is available at 100% on the first £2.5 million of qualifying property.\n\nWhat business property relief is available on Rashid's partnership share?","o":["100%, because an interest in a business owned for at least two years qualifies in full within the £2.5 million allowance.","None, because the partnership agreement creates a binding contract for the sale of his share.","50%, because a partnership share qualifies only for the reduced rate of relief.","100%, but only if the surviving partners pay the purchase price within 12 months of death.","None, because a professional practice is treated as a business of making or holding investments."],"a":1,"e":"An interest in a business normally attracts **100% BPR** if owned for **two years**, but **no relief** is given where there is a **binding contract for sale** of the property at the time of the transfer. A clause obliging the PRs to sell and the survivors to buy is such a contract, so Rashid's share gets no relief; an **option** arrangement would have preserved it. A professional firm carries on a business rather than an investment activity, and partnership interests are not limited to 50% relief."},{"t":"validity","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Sian, aged 89 and housebound, signed a new will which her live-in carer, Toby, had typed from her handwritten notes and arranged to have witnessed by two of his friends. The will leaves Toby her flat; her previous will left everything to her nephew Umar. There is no evidence that anyone read the new will over to Sian. Umar wishes to challenge it. There is no doubt about Sian's capacity or that the will was properly signed and witnessed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the burden of proof?","o":["Toby must prove that he did not exert undue influence, because his relationship with Sian raises a presumption of it.","Umar must prove that Sian did not know and approve the contents, because knowledge and approval is presumed from due execution.","Toby must positively prove that Sian knew and approved the contents of the will, but Umar must prove any allegation of undue influence.","Umar must prove both lack of knowledge and approval and undue influence, because the will was properly executed.","The will is invalid unless Toby proves that Sian received independent legal advice before signing it."],"a":2,"e":"Knowledge and approval is normally **presumed** from capacity and due execution, but **suspicious circumstances**, such as a beneficiary preparing the will and arranging its execution, displace the presumption, so the **propounder must positively prove** knowledge and approval. **Undue influence is never presumed** for wills, whatever the relationship; the person alleging it must prove that coercion is the only reasonable explanation. Independent advice is one way to dispel suspicion, not a legal requirement for validity."},{"t":"formalities","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Viktor's will leaves £25,000 to his friend Leo and the residue to his daughter. The will was witnessed by two people: Viktor's neighbour and Mia, who was Leo's girlfriend at the time. Leo and Mia married a year after the will was signed. Viktor has now died without changing his will.\n\nWhat is the effect of Mia having witnessed the will?","o":["The gift to Leo is void, because Mia was married to him by the time Viktor died.","The will is invalid, because one of only two witnesses was connected to a beneficiary.","The gift to Leo is void unless the will was also signed by at least two other witnesses.","The gift to Leo is valid only if he and Mia were engaged when the will was signed.","The gift to Leo is valid, because Mia was not his spouse when the will was signed."],"a":4,"e":"**Wills Act 1837 s.15** makes a gift void only if it is to a witness or to a person who was the witness's **spouse or civil partner at the time the will was signed**. Leo and Mia married afterwards, so the gift is **saved**, whether or not they were engaged. In any event s.15 affects only the **gift**, never the validity of the will, and the 'two other witnesses' rule is a separate way of saving a gift that would otherwise be void."},{"t":"executors","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Wendy, a solicitor, is appointed sole executor of the will of her client, Xavier. The will contains no charging clause. Xavier's estate is large and complex, and Wendy expects to spend many hours administering it. The adult residuary beneficiaries have not been asked about fees.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Wendy's right to be paid for her professional time?","o":["She may charge reasonable remuneration under the Trustee Act 2000, because she acts in a professional capacity.","She may charge, but only for work that a lay executor could not have done.","She has no statutory right to charge, because she is a sole executor and the will contains no charging clause.","She may charge reasonable fees, provided she discloses them to the residuary beneficiaries in the estate accounts.","She may not charge for her time, and without a charging clause she cannot recover her expenses either."],"a":2,"e":"Without a charging clause, **Trustee Act 2000 s.29** (applied to PRs by s.35) lets a **trust corporation** charge, and lets a professional charge only if they are **not a sole** PR and **each other PR agrees in writing**. As sole executor Wendy has no statutory route, so she would need the agreement of all the adult beneficiaries or the court's authority. She can always recover **expenses properly incurred**, and disclosure in the estate accounts does not create a right to charge."},{"t":"alterations-revocation","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"In 2019 Yusra made a valid will leaving her entire estate to her brother Zain. In 2023 she married Bilal; the will says nothing about marriage. In 2025 she signed a properly witnessed codicil which replaced the executor named in the will and stated: 'In all other respects I confirm my will dated 2019.' Yusra has now died, survived by Bilal and Zain. She had no children.\n\nWho is entitled to Yusra's estate?","o":["Zain, because the codicil revived the will, which now takes effect as if made on the date of the codicil.","Bilal, because the will was revoked by the marriage and a codicil cannot revive a revoked will.","Bilal, because only the change of executor in the codicil is effective and the estate passes on intestacy.","Zain, because marriage revokes only gifts to people other than the new spouse that are made after the marriage.","Bilal and Zain equally, because the codicil revived the will only so far as it is consistent with the marriage."],"a":0,"e":"The 2019 will was **revoked by Yusra's marriage** (Wills Act 1837 s.18) because it was not made in contemplation of marrying Bilal. A revoked will can be **revived** by re-execution or by a **codicil showing an intention to revive it** (s.22); a codicil expressly **confirming** the will shows that intention, so the will is **republished** as at 2025 and Zain takes everything. Bilal's possible remedy is a claim under the 1975 Act, not an intestacy entitlement."},{"t":"interpretation","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Jack, aged 72, and his brother Ken, aged 68, were both killed when their car crashed; it is impossible to tell which of them died first. Jack's will leaves his entire estate to Ken, with no substitute beneficiary and no survivorship clause. Ken's will leaves his entire estate to his partner, Lily.\n\nWhat happens to Jack's estate?","o":["Jack's gift to Ken lapses, because it cannot be proved that Ken survived Jack, so Jack's estate passes on intestacy.","Jack's gift to Ken is saved by section 33 of the Wills Act 1837, so it passes to Ken's issue.","The brothers are presumed to have died at the same moment, so each estate is distributed as if the other had died first.","Jack's estate is divided equally between Ken's estate and Jack's relatives entitled on intestacy.","Ken is presumed to have survived Jack, so Jack's estate passes to Ken's estate and then to Lily under Ken's will."],"a":4,"e":"Where people die in circumstances making it **uncertain who died first**, the **younger is presumed to have survived the elder** (**LPA 1925 s.184**). Ken, the younger, is treated as surviving Jack, so Jack's gift to him takes effect and passes through Ken's estate to Lily; a survivorship clause would have prevented this. Section 33 applies only to gifts to the testator's **children or remoter descendants**, and the 28-day survival rule applies to spouses on **intestacy**, not to gifts by will."},{"t":"intestacy","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Ted died intestate on 1 March. His wife, Uma, died on 15 March from injuries suffered in the same accident. Ted and Uma had no children. Ted's parents are both dead. He is survived by his sister Vera, with whom he shared both parents, and Uma is survived by her mother. Ted's net estate is worth £400,000.\n\nWho is entitled to Ted's estate?","o":["Uma's estate, because a surviving spouse with no issue takes everything, so it passes to her mother.","Uma's estate takes the statutory legacy, and Vera takes the balance on the statutory trusts.","Uma's mother and Vera in equal shares, because both spouses died as a result of the same accident.","The Crown, because neither a spouse nor issue survived Ted by 28 days.","Vera, on the statutory trusts, because Uma is treated as having died before Ted."],"a":4,"e":"A spouse or civil partner must **survive the intestate by 28 days** to inherit (**AEA 1925 s.46(2A)**); otherwise the estate is distributed as if they had **died first**. Uma died 14 days after Ted, so he is treated as leaving no spouse and no issue, and with no parents the estate passes to his **sister of the whole blood** on the statutory trusts. Uma's relatives have no claim to Ted's estate, and the Crown takes only if no relative in the statutory list survives."},{"t":"iht-funding-incidence","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Abdul died in May 2026. His estate is not an excepted estate and IHT is payable. His assets include a holiday cottage that he let out (no reliefs apply), a 2% holding of shares in a listed company, and £300,000 in a building society account. His executors want to know which IHT they may pay by instalments.\n\nWhich of the following is correct?","o":["The tax on the cottage may be paid by ten annual instalments, but interest will run on the outstanding balance.","The tax on the cottage and on the listed shares may be paid by ten annual interest-free instalments.","The tax on the cottage may be paid by ten annual instalments, which will be interest-free if each is paid on time.","No tax may be paid by instalments, because all IHT must be paid before the grant is issued.","The tax on all three assets may be paid by instalments, because the estate is not an excepted estate."],"a":0,"e":"**Land** is instalment property, so the tax on the cottage can be paid by **ten equal yearly instalments** starting six months after the end of the month of death, and it need not be paid before the grant. Because the cottage does not qualify for APR or BPR, the instalments **carry interest on the whole outstanding balance**. A small holding of **listed** shares and cash are never instalment property, so the tax on them must be paid in full before the grant, for example through the Direct Payment Scheme."},{"t":"administration","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Cerys is the administrator of her late father's estate. He died intestate, a widower, leaving three adult children: Cerys, Dafydd and Elin. Elin went travelling in South America eight years ago and the family has not heard from her since; extensive enquiries through the embassy, a tracing agent and social media have failed to find her. Cerys wants to distribute the estate.\n\nWhich of the following would best protect Cerys from personal liability if Elin later reappears?","o":["Placing notices under section 27 of the Trustee Act 1925 and distributing after two months.","Distributing the estate equally between herself and Dafydd once the executor's year has passed.","Obtaining a court order authorising distribution on the basis that Elin died before her father without leaving issue.","Distributing on the basis that Elin is presumed dead, because she has been missing for more than seven years.","Waiting until six months after the grant, so that the protection given by the 1975 Act applies."],"a":2,"e":"Elin is a **known** beneficiary who cannot be found, so the right protection is a **Benjamin order**: the court authorises distribution on a stated basis, such as that Elin **predeceased** her father without issue, and the PR is protected if she reappears (although Elin could still recover from the other beneficiaries). **Section 27 notices** protect only against claims the PR **did not know about**, and neither the executor's year nor the 1975 Act time limit protects against a known beneficiary. Missing beneficiary insurance would be a cheaper alternative."},{"t":"family-provision","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Fergus married Gwen when her daughter, Hattie, was six. Fergus treated Hattie as his own child, paying for her upbringing and education, although he never adopted her. Gwen died ten years ago. Fergus has now died aged 70, leaving his whole estate to his brother. Hattie, aged 30, has a low income and is struggling to pay her rent. She is within the time limit.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Hattie's position under the Inheritance (Provision for Family and Dependants) Act 1975?","o":["She cannot apply, because only biological or adopted children of the deceased can claim as children.","She can apply as a person treated by Fergus as a child of the family, and the court will consider what is reasonable for her maintenance.","She can apply, and the court will award what is reasonable in all the circumstances, whether or not it is needed for her maintenance.","She can apply only if Fergus was maintaining her immediately before his death.","She cannot apply, because her status as a child of the family ended when her mother died."],"a":1,"e":"**Section 1(1)(d)** allows a claim by a person **treated by the deceased as a child of the family** in relation to a marriage in which the deceased stood in the role of a parent, typically a **stepchild**; the status does not end when the marriage ends. Everyone except a spouse or civil partner is judged by the **maintenance standard**, and the court will weigh s.3 factors such as the basis on which Fergus assumed responsibility for her and her own needs. She does not need to show dependency at death, which is a separate category under s.1(1)(e)."},{"t":"iht-death-reliefs","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Harry died in 2008, leaving £78,000 to his children and the rest of his estate to his wife, Iris. He had made no lifetime gifts. Iris has now died, without remarrying and without making any lifetime gifts, leaving everything to their children. Assume the nil rate band was £312,000 at Harry's death and is £325,000 at Iris's death, and ignore the residence nil rate band.\n\nWhat nil rate band is available to Iris's estate if the appropriate claim is made?","o":["£568,750.","£559,000.","£650,000.","£325,000.","£637,000."],"a":0,"e":"Harry used £78,000 of a £312,000 nil rate band, which is **25%**, so **75% was unused**, the gift to Iris being spouse-exempt. The unused **percentage** is applied to the **nil rate band at the second death** (**IHTA 1984 s.8A**): 75% of £325,000 is £243,750, giving Iris £568,750. Adding the unused cash figure of £234,000 gives the wrong answer, and the full uplift to £650,000 is available only if none of the first spouse's band was used."},{"t":"income-cgt-administration","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Jamal's executors have kept his estate's cash in a deposit account while they sell his house. In the current tax year the account has earned £620 of interest; the estate has no other income. Jamal's sole residuary beneficiary is his daughter, a basic rate taxpayer.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the executors' liability to income tax on the interest?","o":["They pay no income tax, because the interest is within the personal savings allowance.","They must pay income tax at the basic rate on £120, the excess over £500.","They pay no income tax, because estate income is taxed only on the beneficiary when it is distributed.","They must pay income tax at the basic rate on the whole £620.","They must pay income tax at the higher rate, because personal representatives have no allowances."],"a":3,"e":"PRs have **no personal allowance or personal savings allowance** and pay income tax at the **basic rate** only, never the higher rate. Under the **£500 de minimis rule** (from 6 April 2024), no tax is due if the estate's total income for the year is £500 or less, but once income **exceeds £500 the whole amount is taxable**, not just the excess. The daughter will receive the net income with an R185 and get credit for the tax the executors have paid."},{"t":"validity","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Kiran instructed her solicitor to draft a will leaving £50,000 to her godson, Liam, and the residue to charity. The solicitor's assistant made a typing error, and the will, which Kiran signed without noticing, leaves Liam £5,000. Kiran died four months ago and probate was granted to her executors last month. The solicitor's file note records the instructions clearly.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Liam's position?","o":["He can apply to the court to rectify the will for a clerical error, and should do so within six months of the grant.","He can apply to rectify the will, but must do so within six months of Kiran's death.","He cannot have the will changed, because Kiran signed it and is presumed to have known and approved its contents.","He can rely on the file note to read the gift as £50,000, because extrinsic evidence of intention is always admissible.","He cannot have the will rectified, but he can claim reasonable financial provision under the 1975 Act as a godchild."],"a":0,"e":"**Administration of Justice Act 1982 s.20** allows the court to **rectify** a will that fails to carry out the testator's intentions because of a **clerical error** or a failure to understand instructions. The application must be made within **six months of the grant** (the court can extend this), not six months from death. Extrinsic evidence of intention is admissible to **interpret** a will only where it is meaningless or ambiguous (s.21), and being a godchild is not a 1975 Act category."},{"t":"interpretation","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Mona's will leaves her residuary estate to her only son, Ned, with no substitute gift. Ned has been convicted of Mona's murder. Ned has a daughter, Ola, aged 12. Mona's only other relative is her sister.\n\nWho is entitled to Mona's residuary estate?","o":["Ned, if he applies under the Forfeiture Act 1982 within three months of his conviction.","Mona's sister, because the residuary gift fails and passes on partial intestacy.","Ola, but only when she reaches 25, because the forfeiture rule postpones any gift to the killer's family.","Mona's sister, because the forfeiture rule excludes the killer and anyone claiming through him.","Ola, because Ned is treated as having died before Mona and the gift passes to his issue."],"a":4,"e":"The **forfeiture rule** stops Ned benefiting, and under **Wills Act 1837 s.33A** a person precluded by the rule is **treated as having died immediately before the testator**. Ned is Mona's child treated as predeceasing her and **leaving issue alive at her death**, so **s.33** passes the gift to Ola. The court has no power to modify the forfeiture rule where the killing was **murder**, and nothing postpones Ola's gift beyond the usual rule that the PRs hold it until she can give a valid receipt."},{"t":"intestacy","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Abe, a widower, died intestate. He is survived by Bea, aged 30, whom he and his late wife adopted as a baby; by Col, aged 28, his late wife's son from her first marriage, whom Abe brought up but never adopted; and by Eve, aged 17, and Fin, aged 12, the children of Abe's son Dan, who died two years ago. Abe's net estate is worth £600,000.\n\nHow will Abe's estate be distributed?","o":["Bea and Col each take £200,000 absolutely, and Eve and Fin each take £100,000 on the statutory trusts.","Bea, Eve and Fin each take £200,000, with Eve's and Fin's shares held on trust until they reach 18.","Bea takes £300,000 absolutely, and Eve and Fin each take £150,000 on the statutory trusts until they reach 18.","Bea takes the whole estate absolutely, because grandchildren inherit only if no child of the intestate survives.","Bea takes £300,000 absolutely, and Eve and Fin each take £150,000 absolutely."],"a":2,"e":"The estate passes to Abe's **issue on the statutory trusts**: an **adopted** child counts as his child, but a **stepchild who was never adopted** takes nothing (Col's route is a 1975 Act claim). Dan's half passes **by representation** to his children equally. Their quarter shares are **contingent on reaching 18** (or earlier marriage or civil partnership)."},{"t":"outside-estate","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Ivo died in August 2026. He was a member of his employer's pension scheme, whose rules give the scheme trustees discretion to pay a lump sum death benefit to any of a wide class of his family and dependants. Ivo signed an expression of wishes form naming his partner, Jade. His will leaves his entire estate to his two children.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the lump sum?","o":["It passes to Ivo's children under his will, because the expression of wishes is not a valid testamentary document.","The scheme trustees decide who receives it, guided but not bound by the expression of wishes, and it is outside Ivo's estate for IHT.","Jade is entitled to it, because an expression of wishes binds the trustees in the same way as a will.","The scheme trustees decide who receives it, but it is included in Ivo's estate for IHT under the pension reforms.","It forms part of Ivo's estate and cannot be paid until his executors obtain a grant of probate."],"a":1,"e":"A discretionary pension death benefit passes **outside the estate**: the **scheme trustees** choose the recipient, and an **expression of wishes guides but does not bind** them, so neither the will nor a grant is relevant. For deaths **before 6 April 2027**, such benefits are generally **outside the IHT estate**. The Finance Act 2026 brings most unused pension funds and death benefits into IHT only for deaths on or after that date."},{"t":"grants","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Kwame's executors are administering his estate, which is not an excepted estate. It includes his house and £200,000 in bank accounts, and substantial IHT is payable. The executors want to know the correct order of steps for obtaining probate.\n\nWhich of the following is correct?","o":["Apply for probate first, and then pay all the IHT within six months of the end of the month of death.","Send HMRC the IHT400, pay the IHT due on non-instalment property, wait 20 working days, and then apply for probate.","Send HMRC the IHT400 and pay all the IHT, including the tax on the house, before applying for probate.","Give the estate values in the probate application, after which HMRC will calculate and collect the IHT.","Send HMRC the IHT400, wait six months for HMRC's response, and then apply for probate and pay the IHT."],"a":1,"e":"For a **non-excepted estate**, the PRs must deliver an **IHT400** and **pay the tax on non-instalment property** before applying, then **wait 20 working days** so HMRC can confirm the position to the Probate Registry. Tax on **instalment property** such as the house need not be paid before the grant. Only an **excepted estate** can simply give values in the probate application without an IHT400."},{"t":"iht-funding-incidence","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Lowri's will leaves her cottage to her niece Mair, saying nothing about tax, and the residue to her nephew Neil. She also held a building society account jointly with her sister Olwen, which passes to Olwen by survivorship. IHT is payable on Lowri's death, part of it attributable to the cottage and part to her share of the joint account.\n\nWho bears the IHT attributable to the cottage and to the joint account?","o":["Mair bears the tax on the cottage, and Olwen bears the tax on the joint account.","Neil bears the tax on both the cottage and the joint account, because all IHT is paid from residue.","Neil bears the tax on the cottage through the residue, and Olwen bears the tax on the joint account.","Mair bears the tax on the cottage, and Neil bears the tax on the joint account through the residue.","Mair, Neil and Olwen bear the tax rateably in proportion to the value each receives."],"a":2,"e":"Under **IHTA 1984 s.211**, IHT on **UK free estate** is a testamentary expense paid **out of residue** unless the will says otherwise, so a specific gift with no 'subject to tax' direction is received **tax-free** and Neil, as residuary beneficiary, bears that tax. Property **outside the free estate bears its own tax**, so Olwen, the surviving joint owner, bears the tax on Lowri's share of the account. There is no general rule apportioning the tax rateably among all recipients."},{"t":"administration","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Priya's will leaves her house, worth £350,000 and subject to a mortgage of £120,000, to her daughter Rani, and the residue (worth £400,000) to her son Sunil. The will says nothing about the mortgage or about debts generally. All other debts and expenses can easily be paid from residue.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the mortgage will be dealt with?","o":["The mortgage is paid from residue, so Rani takes the house free of the mortgage.","Rani takes the house subject to the mortgage, so her gift is worth £230,000.","The mortgage is shared between Rani and Sunil in proportion to the values of their gifts.","The mortgage is paid from residue only if Rani lived in the house with Priya.","Rani may choose either to take the house subject to the mortgage or to have it paid from residue."],"a":1,"e":"A debt **charged on a particular asset** is paid primarily from **that asset** (**AEA 1925 s.35**) unless the will shows a **contrary intention**, such as a gift 'free of mortgage'. Even a general direction to pay debts from residue would not be enough unless it referred to the charge, and here the will says nothing, so Rani takes the house subject to the £120,000 mortgage. Unsecured debts and expenses are paid from residue under the statutory order."},{"t":"family-provision","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Tomos and his wife, Ursula, separated informally three years ago but never started divorce proceedings. Tomos died recently, leaving his entire estate of £1.2 million to his brother. Ursula, aged 62, has modest savings and a part-time income. She issued a claim under the Inheritance (Provision for Family and Dependants) Act 1975 four months after the grant.\n\nWhich standard of provision will the court apply to Ursula's claim?","o":["Such provision as is reasonable for her maintenance, because they had separated before Tomos died.","Such provision as she would have received on divorce, which acts as the maximum award.","No provision, because a separated spouse may claim only if she was being maintained by Tomos at his death.","Such provision as is reasonable in all the circumstances, whether or not it is required for her maintenance.","Such provision as is reasonable for her maintenance, because she has some income of her own."],"a":3,"e":"A **spouse** who is not **judicially separated** is judged by the **surviving spouse standard**: such provision as is reasonable **whether or not required for maintenance** (s.1(2)(a)). Informal separation does not change the standard, though it is relevant under the s.3 factors together with her age, the length of the marriage and her contribution to the family. What she might have received on **divorce** is a **cross-check**, neither a floor nor a ceiling, and her claim is within six months of the grant."},{"t":"iht-lifetime","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"In 2017 Vera gave her house to her son Will. She continued to live there alone until she died in October 2026, paying Will £100 a month, although the market rent was about £1,500 a month. The house was worth £300,000 in 2017 and £450,000 at Vera's death.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the IHT position on the house?","o":["The house is ignored, because the gift was a potentially exempt transfer made more than seven years before death.","The gift is a failed potentially exempt transfer taxed on its 2017 value of £300,000.","The house is ignored, because Vera paid rent to Will for her continued occupation.","The house is treated as part of Vera's estate at death and taxed on its value of £450,000.","The house is treated as part of Vera's estate, but only at its 2017 value of £300,000."],"a":3,"e":"Under the **gift with reservation of benefit** rules (**Finance Act 1986 s.102**), property given away while the donor **continues to benefit** from it is treated as still in the donor's estate at death, at its **value at death**. Paying rent prevents a reservation only if it is **full market rent**, so £100 a month does not help. Surviving seven years made the original PET exempt but does not remove the reservation, and Will, as donee, is primarily liable for the tax."},{"t":"iht-death-reliefs","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Xander, who never married, died in 2026 leaving his home, worth £800,000, and other assets to his two daughters. His estate after debts is worth £2.2 million, and he made no lifetime gifts. Assume a residence nil rate band of £175,000, reduced by £1 for every £2 by which the estate exceeds £2 million.\n\nHow much residence nil rate band is available to Xander's estate?","o":["£75,000.","£175,000.","£100,000.","Nothing.","£87,500."],"a":0,"e":"The RNRB is available because a home Xander lived in passes to **direct descendants**, but it is **tapered** by £1 for every £2 by which the **net estate exceeds £2 million**. The excess is £200,000, so the band is reduced by £100,000 to £75,000. The taper uses the estate value **before** reliefs and exemptions, and the band disappears completely only at £2.35 million."},{"t":"income-cgt-administration","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Yasmin died 20 months ago, leaving a legacy of £200,000 to her wealthy son, Zac. Zac wants to redirect the whole legacy to his two children, aged 8 and 10, without being treated as making a gift himself for IHT. The executors still hold the legacy in an interest-bearing account.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of a deed of variation?","o":["It must be made within six months of the grant, and once made the children's income from the fund will be taxed as theirs.","It will be read back for IHT, but Zac will still make a potentially exempt transfer to his children.","If made in writing within two years of death with a statement under s.142, it is read back for IHT, but if the income exceeds £100 a year, all of it will be taxed as Zac's.","It will be effective for IHT only if the executors also sign it, whether or not more tax becomes payable.","It will be read back for all taxes, including income tax, provided it is signed by Zac and the executors."],"a":2,"e":"A **deed of variation** made in **writing within two years of death**, signed by the original beneficiary, not for consideration and containing a **statement** that s.142 applies, is **read back** for IHT as if Yasmin had left the money to her grandchildren, so Zac makes no PET. There is **no read-back for income tax**: Zac is treated as the **settlor**, so if income arising for his own minor, unmarried children is more than **£100 a year**, the **whole** of it is taxed as his. The PRs need join in the statement only where the variation increases the IHT payable."}],"accounts":[{"t":"framework-client-money","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Hart & Co acts for Mrs Bello in a dispute over a lease. On 3 June the firm delivered a bill for profit costs of £1,000 plus VAT of £200, together with counsel's fee of £600 (no VAT). The firm has not yet paid counsel. On 10 June Mrs Bello sends £1,800 to settle the bill in full.\n\nHow should the firm deal with the £1,800?","o":["Pay it all into client account, because counsel's fee has not yet been paid.","Pay £1,200 into the business account and the £600 for counsel's fee into client account until counsel is paid.","Pay it into client account and transfer counsel's fee to counsel within two working days.","Pay it into the business account, provided counsel's fee is paid within 14 days.","Pay it into business account, as it settles a bill already delivered, and pay counsel promptly."],"a":4,"e":"Money for fees and **unpaid disbursements** is client money only if received **before delivery of a bill** (r.2.1(d)). Here the bill, including counsel's fee, had already been delivered, so the whole £1,800 is **firm money** and goes to the **business account**, even though counsel is still unpaid. The firm must then pay counsel **promptly**. The two-working-day and 14-day periods come from the old 2011 rules and no longer apply."},{"t":"framework-client-money","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Dale Solicitors finished acting for Mr Kerr on a sale of land two years ago. A balance of £180 remains on his client ledger. The firm has written to his last known address and email and carried out an online tracing search, all without success. The tracing search cost the firm £40. The firm proposes to deduct the £40 and pay the remaining £140 to a charity.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["It may pay the full £180 to charity without SRA authorisation, but may not deduct its tracing costs.","The firm may deduct its £40 tracing costs and pay the remaining £140 to charity without SRA authorisation.","The firm must obtain the SRA's prior written authorisation before paying any residual balance to charity.","The firm may transfer the full £180 to its business account, because the client cannot be traced.","The firm must keep the £180 in client account until Mr Kerr contacts it, however long that takes."],"a":0,"e":"Withdrawing a residual balance needs SRA authorisation unless it falls within the SRA's **prescribed circumstances**, which allow a balance of **£500 or less** to be paid to **charity** after **reasonable steps** to trace the owner, with records kept for at least six years. One condition is that the firm must **not deduct its own costs**, such as tracing costs, so the whole £180 must go to the charity. The firm can never keep the money itself, and there is no need to hold it indefinitely."},{"t":"client-account-withdrawals","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Lyle & Co is about to send £150,000 of sale proceeds to its client, Mrs Abara, following completion of her sale. The day before payment, the firm receives an email that appears to come from Mrs Abara's usual email address, asking the firm to send the money to a different bank account from the one given in her signed instructions.\n\nWhat should the firm do?","o":["Send the money to the new account, because a withdrawal may be made on the client's instructions.","Send the money to the account originally given without telling Mrs Abara about the email.","Refuse to make any payment until the SRA has authorised the withdrawal.","Verify the change directly with Mrs Abara using contact details it already holds before paying.","Send half of the money to each account to limit the risk of loss."],"a":3,"e":"All withdrawals from client account must be **appropriately authorised and supervised** (r.5.2), which requires sensible controls such as **checking changes of bank details** to prevent payment fraud. A last-minute email changing bank details is a classic fraud warning sign, so the firm should confirm the change **directly with the client** by a trusted method before paying. If it paid a fraudster, the money would have been improperly withdrawn and the firm would have to **replace it immediately** from its own funds (r.6.1). SRA authorisation is not needed for an ordinary withdrawal on proper instructions."},{"t":"separation-mixed-payments","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Rook Solicitors holds £5,000 on account of costs for Mr Iqbal. On 1 March it delivered a bill to him for £2,000 plus VAT of £400, to be paid from the money held. The cashier decides to leave the £2,400 in client account for three months \"as a buffer\" in case the firm needs to pay other sums for Mr Iqbal.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The firm is not in breach, because extra money in client account gives clients greater protection.","The firm is not in breach, because it may choose when to transfer its costs after delivering a bill.","It is in breach, because the billed £2,400 became firm money, which must not be kept in client account.","The firm is in breach only if the £2,400 remains in client account for more than 14 days.","The firm is in breach, because it should not have delivered the bill without Mr Iqbal's approval of the costs."],"a":2,"e":"Client money and firm money must be **kept separate** (r.4.1), and this works both ways: **firm money must not be kept in client account**. Once a **bill** is delivered, the billed sum held in client account becomes the firm's money and should be transferred **promptly** to business account. Leaving it as a buffer can hide shortfalls and blur which money is protected. The rules say **promptly**, with no 14-day period, and do not require client approval before a bill is delivered."},{"t":"double-entry-basics","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Mill & Co acts for the executors of the late Mr Grey. A fee earner pays a £15 fee for the executors to swear a statement, using the firm's petty cash. The firm holds money for the estate in client account.\n\nWhich entries should the firm make?","o":["Debit the estate's ledger client column £15; credit petty cash £15.","Debit the estate's ledger client column £15; credit client cash £15.","Debit the estate's ledger business column £15; credit petty cash £15.","Debit petty cash £15; credit the estate's ledger business column £15.","Debit the estate's ledger business column £15; credit client cash £15."],"a":2,"e":"**Petty cash** is a float of the **firm's own money**, so a payment from it for a client is recorded in the **business** books: **debit the client ledger business column** (the client owes the firm) and **credit petty cash** (money has gone out). The fact that client money is held for the estate does not matter, because no client money has been used. Entries in the client column or client cash account would wrongly show client money being spent, and debiting petty cash would record money coming in."},{"t":"bills-vat-disbursements","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Royle Solicitors delivered a bill to Mr Faulk for profit costs of £1,500 plus VAT of £300. More than six months later, Mr Faulk has been made bankrupt and the firm decides to write off the debt as bad, claiming VAT bad debt relief.\n\nWhich entries record the write-off?","o":["Debit profit costs £1,500 and HMRC £300; credit Mr Faulk's ledger business column £1,800.","Debit bad debts £1,500 and HMRC £300; credit Mr Faulk's ledger business column £1,800.","Debit Mr Faulk's ledger business column £1,800; credit bad debts £1,500 and HMRC £300.","Debit bad debts £1,800; credit Mr Faulk's ledger client column £1,800.","Debit bad debts £1,800; credit profit costs £1,500 and HMRC £300."],"a":1,"e":"Writing off a **bad debt** clears what the client owes, so the firm **credits the client ledger business column** with the full £1,800. The fee was earned but will not be paid, so the debit goes to a **bad debts** expense account; where **VAT bad debt relief** is claimed, the VAT element is debited to **HMRC**. Debiting profit costs would treat it as an **abatement** (a reduction in the fee), and the client column is irrelevant because no client money is involved."},{"t":"interest-breaches","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Vaughan LLP holds £400,000 for the executors of an estate in a separate designated client deposit account. At the end of the quarter the bank credits £1,250 of interest directly to that account.\n\nWhich entries should the firm make?","o":["Debit interest payable £1,250; credit the estate's ledger business column £1,250.","Debit the estate's ledger client column £1,250; credit client cash (designated deposit account) £1,250.","Debit business cash £1,250; credit interest received £1,250.","Debit client cash (designated deposit account) £1,250; credit the estate's ledger client column £1,250.","Debit interest payable £1,250; credit the estate's ledger client column £1,250."],"a":3,"e":"Interest earned on a **separate designated client account** is credited by the bank **directly** to that account and belongs to the client from the start. The firm records it as money received for the client: **debit client cash** and **credit the client ledger client column**, with **no business entries**. The interest payable entries are used for a **general client account**, where the firm receives the interest itself and pays the client a fair sum from its own money. Crediting the firm's interest received account would treat the client's money as the firm's."},{"t":"probate-accounts","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Joanna, a partner at Teal Solicitors, is the sole executor of her late client Mr Penrose's estate. She receives a cheque for £40,000 from the deceased's building society, payable to her as executor. She proposes to pay it into her personal bank account until the estate is ready for distribution, because she is acting as executor rather than as a solicitor.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the £40,000 should be treated?","o":["It is client money held as executor, so it must be paid promptly into the firm's client account.","It is not client money, because she receives it in a personal capacity as executor.","It may be held in her personal account, provided the residuary beneficiaries agree to this in writing.","It is firm money, because Joanna is a partner and will charge the estate for her work.","It becomes client money only once the grant of probate has been issued."],"a":0,"e":"Money held by a firm's partner or employee as **trustee** or as holder of an office such as **executor** is **client money** under **r.2.1(c)**, and it must be paid **promptly** into **client account** (r.2.3). The exceptions, such as where the rules of the office conflict, or where the money stays in an estate account in the PRs' names that she operates as signatory (r.10), do not cover a personal account. Beneficiaries cannot authorise mixing estate money with her own, and the timing of the grant is irrelevant."},{"t":"conveyancing-accounts","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Stour Solicitors acts for Ms Ford and her lender on her house purchase. The lender sent the £180,000 mortgage advance to the firm the day before the agreed completion date. The seller has now delayed completion indefinitely. The lender's instructions state that if completion does not take place within five working days of receipt, the advance must be returned. Five working days have passed.\n\nWhat should the firm do?","o":["Return it to the lender, because it is held for the lender and its instructions require this.","Keep the advance in client account for Ms Ford until the seller is ready to complete the sale.","Transfer the advance to a separate ledger for Ms Ford so that it earns interest for her.","Ask Ms Ford whether she wishes the firm to return the advance or keep it.","Hold the advance as stakeholder for both Ms Ford and the seller until completion."],"a":0,"e":"Where the firm also acts for the lender, the **mortgage advance** is client money held **for the lender** until completion and may be used only as the lender's **instructions** allow. If completion is delayed and the lender's instructions require the advance to be **returned**, the firm must return it, as that is now the only proper purpose for holding it (r.5.1). Ms Ford cannot override the lender's instructions, and nothing makes the firm a stakeholder of the advance."},{"t":"records-reconciliation","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"At its five-weekly reconciliation, Ashby Solicitors' client bank statement shows a balance of £82,400. There are unpresented cheques of £5,600 and an outstanding lodgement of £2,300. The client cash book shows a balance of £79,100. The total of the client ledger balances is £79,900.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the result of the reconciliation?","o":["There is a surplus of £800 in client account, which belongs to the firm and should be transferred to business account.","There is a shortfall of £800, which must be investigated and any missing money replaced immediately.","There is no problem, because the adjusted bank balance agrees with the cash book.","There is a shortfall of £2,500, which is the difference between the bank statement and the client ledger total.","There is a shortfall of £3,300, which is the difference between the unpresented cheques and the outstanding lodgement."],"a":1,"e":"The bank balance is first **adjusted**: £82,400 less unpresented cheques of £5,600 plus the outstanding lodgement of £2,300 gives **£79,100**, which agrees with the cash book. But the **client ledger total** of £79,900 shows the firm owes clients £800 more than it holds, a **shortfall**. All three figures must agree in a **three-way reconciliation** (r.8.3), so the difference must be investigated promptly and any missing client money **replaced immediately** (r.6.1)."},{"t":"accountants-reports","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Cray Solicitors does mainly legal aid work. During its last accounting period, almost all client money it received came from the Legal Aid Agency, but it also held £500 on account of costs from one private client. The statement balance of its client account averaged £14,000 and never exceeded £60,000.\n\nIs the firm required to obtain an accountant's report for the period?","o":["No, because almost all its client money came from the Legal Aid Agency and the private sum was small.","No, because its maximum balance never exceeded £250,000.","No, because the private client's money was less than £10,000.","Yes, but only if the accountant finds that client money has been placed at risk.","Yes, because not all its client money came from the LAA and its average balance exceeded £10,000."],"a":4,"e":"The legal aid exemption applies only if **all** client money held in the period came from the **Legal Aid Agency** (r.12.2(a)), so a single private client's money on account takes the firm outside it. The low-balance exemption requires **both** an average balance of no more than **£10,000** **and** a maximum of no more than **£250,000** (r.12.2(b)); the average of £14,000 fails the first limb. The report must therefore be **obtained**, though under the rules in force it is sent to the SRA only if qualified to show money at risk."},{"t":"framework-client-money","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Pell & Co does both private and legally aided work. It receives a payment of £2,800 from the Legal Aid Agency in respect of its costs on a legally aided family matter.\n\nWhere may the firm pay the £2,800?","o":["Into client account only, because it is received before a bill has been delivered to the client.","Into client account only, because all money received in connection with a legally aided client's matter is client money.","Into its business account, because LAA payments for the firm's costs are an exception to the client account rule.","Into a separate designated client account in the legally aided client's name.","Into its business account, but only if the legally aided client agrees in writing."],"a":2,"e":"Client money must normally be paid promptly into client account (r.2.3), but one of the three exceptions is a payment from the **Legal Aid Agency for the firm's costs**, which may be paid straight into the **business account** (r.2.3(b)). No agreement with the client is needed for this exception. The other exceptions are money held as trustee or office-holder where client account would conflict with that office, and an **alternative arrangement agreed in writing** with the client or third party."},{"t":"client-account-withdrawals","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Hale & Co, an SRA-authorised firm in Bristol, wants to open an additional account for client money. It proposes to open the account at a bank branch in Jersey, which offers a better interest rate, and to call it the \"Hale & Co Deposit Account\".\n\nWhich of the following best describes the proposal?","o":["It complies, because the account is in the firm's name and is held at a bank.","It does not comply: client accounts must be at a branch in England and Wales and named with the word \"client\".","It does not comply only because of the location; the name is acceptable as it includes the firm's name.","It does not comply only because of the name; a bank branch in Jersey is acceptable if it pays clients better interest.","It complies, provided each client whose money is held there agrees in writing."],"a":1,"e":"Client money may only be held in a client account at a **branch or head office of a bank or building society in England and Wales** (r.3.1), so a Jersey branch is not allowed. The account name must include **both** the **name of the authorised body** and the word **\"client\"** (r.3.2), so \"Hale & Co Deposit Account\" also fails. A better interest rate does not change this, and an arrangement agreed with a client in writing is a separate exception from these account requirements."},{"t":"separation-mixed-payments","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Last week Ward Solicitors delivered a bill to Ms Lin for £1,500 plus VAT of £300. Ms Lin now sends a single payment of £3,000: £1,800 to pay the bill and £1,200 on account of future work. The cashier pays the whole £3,000 into the business account.\n\nWhat must the firm now do?","o":["Transfer £1,200 to client account immediately, because client money is in the business account.","Nothing, because Ms Lin has been told how her payment has been allocated.","Transfer £1,200 to client account within 14 days of receipt.","Nothing, because the money on account is safe in the firm's own account until it is needed for Ms Lin's matter.","Return £1,200 to Ms Lin and ask her to send it again as a separate payment."],"a":0,"e":"A **mixed payment** contains both firm money (the £1,800 for the delivered bill) and client money (the £1,200 on account), and must be **allocated promptly** to the correct accounts (r.4.2). Paying the whole sum into business account puts **client money in the business account**, breaching r.2.3 and r.4.1. The firm must **correct the breach promptly on discovery** by transferring £1,200 to client account immediately (r.6.1). There is no 14-day period, and returning the money is unnecessary."},{"t":"double-entry-basics","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"At the month end, the cashier at Elm Solicitors extracts the balances from the firm's accounts.\n\nWhich of the following balances indicates a breach of the SRA Accounts Rules?","o":["A debit balance on the business column of a client's ledger.","A debit balance on the client column of a client's ledger.","A credit balance on the business cash account.","A debit balance on the client cash account.","A credit balance on the profit costs account."],"a":1,"e":"The **client column** of a client ledger normally shows a **credit** balance, being money held for that client. A **debit** balance means more has been paid out for that client than was held, so **other clients' money** has been used, a breach of r.5.3. A debit on the business column simply means the client owes the firm money, and a credit on business cash means the firm's own account is overdrawn, which is allowed. A debit on client cash is the normal position (money in the bank), and profit costs normally has a credit balance."},{"t":"bills-vat-disbursements","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Bryn Solicitors acts for Mrs Shah on the sale of her house and holds the sale proceeds in client account. The estate agent's invoice for £4,000 plus VAT of £800 is addressed to Mrs Shah, who authorises the firm to pay it from the proceeds.\n\nWhich entries record the payment?","o":["Debit Mrs Shah's ledger client column £4,000 and HMRC £800; credit client cash £4,800.","Debit Mrs Shah's ledger business column £4,000 and HMRC £800; credit business cash £4,800.","Debit Mrs Shah's ledger business column £4,800; credit business cash £4,800.","Debit Mrs Shah's ledger client column £4,800; credit client cash £4,800.","Debit client cash £4,800; credit Mrs Shah's ledger client column £4,800."],"a":3,"e":"An invoice **addressed to the client** means the client received the supply, so the firm pays as the client's agent under the **agency method**. It may pay from **client account** where enough is held for that client, recording the **gross** amount: **debit the ledger client column £4,800, credit client cash £4,800**. The firm does **not reclaim** the VAT, which is the client's, so no HMRC entry is made; HMRC entries belong to the **principal method**, which must be paid from business account."},{"t":"bills-vat-disbursements","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Osborne LLP acts for the executors of the late Mrs Day. A valuer's invoice for valuing the deceased's jewellery is addressed to the firm and is for £500 plus VAT of £100. The firm holds £20,000 for the estate in client account but pays the invoice from its business account, using the principal method.\n\nWhich entries record the payment?","o":["Debit the estate's ledger client column £600; credit client cash £600.","Debit the estate's ledger business column £600; credit business cash £600.","Debit the estate's ledger client column £500 and HMRC £100; credit client cash £600.","Debit business cash £600; credit the estate's ledger business column £500 and HMRC £100.","Debit the estate's ledger business column £500 and HMRC £100; credit business cash £600."],"a":4,"e":"An invoice **addressed to the firm** is the firm's own debt, so it is a **principal method** disbursement and must be paid from the **business account**, even though client money is held for the estate. The firm reclaims the input VAT, so the entries are **debit the ledger business column with the net £500**, **debit HMRC £100** and **credit business cash £600**. The firm will later charge output VAT on the £500 as part of its own bill. Entries in the client column or client cash would mean client money was used for the firm's debt."},{"t":"interest-breaches","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Kemble Solicitors holds money for Mr Ortiz in its general client account. On completion of his matter the firm calculates, under its interest policy, that he is owed £340 interest, and sends him a cheque for that amount from its business account.\n\nWhich entries should the firm make?","o":["Debit client cash £340, credit Mr Ortiz's ledger client column £340; then debit Mr Ortiz's ledger client column £340, credit client cash £340.","Debit interest payable £340, credit Mr Ortiz's ledger business column £340; then debit Mr Ortiz's ledger business column £340, credit business cash £340.","Debit Mr Ortiz's ledger business column £340; credit interest received £340.","Debit interest payable £340, credit Mr Ortiz's ledger client column £340; then debit Mr Ortiz's ledger client column £340, credit client cash £340.","Debit business cash £340; credit interest payable £340."],"a":1,"e":"On a **general client account** the bank pays interest to the **firm**, which then pays the client a **fair sum** out of its own money, so the interest is a **firm expense**. First record what the firm owes: **debit interest payable, credit the client ledger business column**. Then record the payment from business account: **debit the ledger business column, credit business cash**. Client cash and client column entries are used only for a **separate designated client account**, or if the interest is transferred into client account to be held for the client."},{"t":"probate-accounts","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Rhys LLP acts for the executors of the late Mr Moss. To obtain the grant of probate, inheritance tax of £48,000 must be paid. The executors arrange for Mr Moss's bank to pay the tax directly to HMRC from his account under the direct payment scheme. The firm holds no money for the estate.\n\nWhich entries should the firm make for the inheritance tax payment?","o":["Debit the estate's ledger client column £48,000; credit client cash £48,000.","Debit the estate's ledger business column £48,000; credit business cash £48,000.","Debit client cash £48,000; credit the estate's ledger client column £48,000.","None, because the money is paid directly from the deceased's bank to HMRC and does not pass through the firm's accounts.","Debit client cash and credit the estate's ledger client column £48,000; then debit the estate's ledger client column and credit client cash £48,000."],"a":3,"e":"Under the **direct payment scheme**, the deceased's bank or building society pays the inheritance tax **straight to HMRC** from the deceased's account. The money is never held or received by the firm, so it is not client money in the firm's hands and there are **no entries** in the firm's books. Entries would be needed only if the money passed through the firm, for example a loan paid into client account and then sent to HMRC, or a payment from the firm's business account."},{"t":"conveyancing-accounts","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Crane Solicitors acts for Mrs Ellis on the sale of her house for £350,000. The £35,000 deposit paid on exchange is held by the firm as stakeholder. On completion the firm receives £315,000 from the buyer's solicitors. It then redeems Mrs Ellis's mortgage of £210,000, pays the estate agent's invoice addressed to her for £4,500 plus VAT of £900, and transfers its costs of £1,500 plus VAT of £300, which it billed before completion.\n\nHow much should the firm send to Mrs Ellis?","o":["£132,800","£97,800","£133,700","£134,600","£133,100"],"a":0,"e":"On completion the **stakeholder deposit** becomes the seller's, so Mrs Ellis is entitled to the full price of **£350,000** (£315,000 plus the £35,000 deposit). From this the firm pays the mortgage of £210,000, the estate agent's invoice **gross** at £5,400 (an **agency** disbursement, as the invoice is addressed to her) and its billed costs of £1,800 including VAT, leaving **£132,800**. Forgetting the deposit gives £97,800; leaving out the VAT on the agent's fee gives £133,700, leaving out the VAT on the firm's costs gives £133,100, and forgetting the firm's costs altogether gives £134,600. The balance must be sent to her **promptly** (r.2.5)."},{"t":"records-reconciliation","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Foxley Solicitors reconciles its client account every four weeks and the reconciliation is signed off by a partner. It obtains statements for its client account monthly, but obtains statements for its business account only once a quarter. It keeps all its accounting records for six years.\n\nWhich of the following practices breaches the SRA Accounts Rules?","o":["Reconciling the client account every four weeks rather than every month.","Having the reconciliation signed off by a partner rather than the COFA.","Obtaining business account statements only once a quarter.","Keeping accounting records for only six years.","Obtaining client account statements monthly rather than every five weeks."],"a":2,"e":"Statements must be obtained **at least every five weeks** for **all client accounts and business accounts** (r.8.2), so quarterly business account statements are a breach. Reconciling client accounts at least every **five weeks** is required (r.8.3), and four-weekly or monthly is more frequent, so it complies. Sign-off by **the COFA or a manager**, such as a partner, is acceptable, and records must be kept for **at least six years** (r.13.1)."},{"t":"joint-own-tpma","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Nadia, a partner at Quay Solicitors, is the Court of Protection deputy for Mr Ross. Until now she has operated Mr Ross's own bank account as signatory to pay his bills. She now transfers £15,000 from his account into the firm's client account to pay a large bill for building works at his home next month.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the SRA Accounts Rules apply to the £15,000?","o":["Only the rules on statements, reconciliation and the record of bills apply, because the money belongs to Mr Ross.","Only the rules on statements and the record of bills apply, because the money is held jointly with Mr Ross.","No Accounts Rules apply, because Nadia acts in a personal capacity as deputy.","It must be returned to Mr Ross's own account, because a deputy may not use the firm's client account.","It is client money held by the firm as deputy, so the main client money rules all apply to it."],"a":4,"e":"While money stays in the client's own account and the solicitor operates it as signatory, only **rule 10** applies: statements, reconciliation and the bills record. Once the deputy **moves the money into the firm's client account**, it is **client money** under **r.2.1(c)**, held as holder of a specified office or appointment such as **Court of Protection deputy**, and the **full rules** in Part 2 apply. The limited rule 9 regime is for joint accounts, which this is not."},{"t":"framework-client-money","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Fern Legal is a small firm that does not hold a client account. It has always relied on the rule allowing it to hold money on account of fees in its business account, and tells clients in advance where their money will be held. It now agrees to act on a house purchase and asks the buyer to send £5,500 before completion: £1,500 on account of its fees and £4,000 for Stamp Duty Land Tax.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["It may hold the whole £5,500 in its business account, because it has told the client in advance where and how the money will be held.","It may hold the whole £5,500 in its business account, because SDLT is a disbursement paid before a bill is delivered.","It may hold the SDLT money in its business account, provided it pays HMRC within 14 days of completion.","It must not hold the SDLT money outside a client account, as the exemption covers only fees and disbursements the firm is liable for.","It may hold the whole £5,500 in its business account, provided the client agrees in writing that no interest will be paid."],"a":3,"e":"A firm need not use a client account (r.2.2) only if the **only** client money it holds is for its **fees** and **unpaid disbursements for which the firm itself is liable**, such as counsel's or an expert's fee, and it tells the client in advance. **SDLT** is the **client's** liability, not the firm's, so money for it falls outside the exemption and must be held in a **client account**. Telling the client, agreeing about interest or the SDLT deadline cannot extend the exemption; the firm would need a client account or a TPMA for this money."},{"t":"client-account-withdrawals","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Garnet & Co holds £400 for Mr Lowe in client account. It must pay a court fee of £1,000 (no VAT) for him today. The firm pays £400 from client account and the remaining £600 from its business account.\n\nWhich entries record the payment?","o":["Debit Mr Lowe's ledger client column £1,000; credit client cash £1,000.","Debit Mr Lowe's ledger client column £1,000; credit client cash £400 and business cash £600.","Debit Mr Lowe's ledger business column £1,000; credit client cash £400 and business cash £600.","Debit client cash £400 and business cash £600; credit Mr Lowe's ledger client column £1,000.","Debit Mr Lowe's ledger client column £400, credit client cash £400; debit Mr Lowe's ledger business column £600, credit business cash £600."],"a":4,"e":"Client money may be withdrawn only if **sufficient funds are held for that specific client** (r.5.3), so only £400 can come from client account and the rest must be the firm's own money. Each part of a **split payment** is entered in its own set of books: **debit the ledger client column and credit client cash** for £400, and **debit the ledger business column and credit business cash** for £600. Debiting £1,000 to the client column would leave it **overdrawn**, and both entries of a single movement must be in the **same set of books**."},{"t":"separation-mixed-payments","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Ivy works at Blake Solicitors, which holds £3,000 on account of costs for Mr Noor. Having completed the work, Ivy sends Mr Noor an email setting out the work done and stating that the costs incurred are £900 plus VAT of £180. The next day the cashier transfers £1,080 from client account to business account. No formal bill has been sent.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the transfer?","o":["It is a breach, because only a formal bill of costs, not an email, allows money to be transferred from client account.","It is a breach, because Mr Noor has not confirmed that he agrees with the costs.","It complies, because an email setting out the costs is a written notification given before the transfer.","It complies only if a formal bill is sent within 14 days of the transfer.","It is a breach, because costs may be transferred only once the whole matter has ended."],"a":2,"e":"Before transferring client money to pay its costs, the firm must give the client a **bill of costs or other written notification of the costs incurred** (r.4.3), and transfer the **specific sum** identified. A written notification can be **less formal** than a bill, such as a letter or **email** setting out the costs, provided it is in writing and given **before** the transfer. The client's agreement is not required, and there is no rule that the matter must have ended. The notification must be kept in the firm's central record of bills (r.8.4)."},{"t":"double-entry-basics","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Ash Solicitors acts for Ms Webb. It receives £2,000 from her on account of costs. It then pays a court fee of £332 for her from client account and £50 of oath fees for her from petty cash. Finally it delivers a bill for profit costs of £800 plus VAT of £160. No other transactions have taken place.\n\nWhat are the balances on Ms Webb's ledger?","o":["Client column £1,668 credit; business column £1,010 debit.","Client column £1,668 credit; business column £960 debit.","Client column £1,618 credit; business column £960 debit.","Client column £708 credit; business column nil.","Client column £2,000 credit; business column £1,342 debit."],"a":0,"e":"The receipt is credited to the **client column** (£2,000) and the court fee paid from client account is debited to it, leaving **£1,668 credit**. The oath fees come from **petty cash**, which is firm money, so they are debited to the **business column** (£50), and the bill is debited there too (£960), giving **£1,010 debit**. No transfer has been made, so the bill is still unpaid; the firm can transfer £1,010 from client account now that a bill has been delivered (r.4.3)."},{"t":"bills-vat-disbursements","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Lowther LLP acts for a buyer client. It paid a Land Registry fee of £300 from client account and a local authority search fee of £150 from its business account (no VAT was charged by the local authority). It now delivers its bill for profit costs of £2,000, also listing both payments. VAT is at 20%.\n\nHow much VAT should the bill show?","o":["£400","£430","£460","£490","£30"],"a":1,"e":"VAT is charged on the firm's **profit costs** and on any **principal method** disbursement, which is treated as part of the firm's own supply. HMRC's view is that **property search fees** obtained and used by the solicitor in advising the client are part of the solicitor's supply, so VAT is charged on the £150: 20% of £2,150 is **£430**. The **Land Registry fee** is an **agency** disbursement and not a VAT supply, so no VAT is added to it."},{"t":"interest-breaches","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Caxton Ltd, a commercial client, asks Hollis LLP to hold £500,000 in client account for three months pending an acquisition. Before the money is received, the firm explains in writing the interest it would expect to pay under its policy and the approximate amount. Caxton Ltd then signs a letter agreeing that no interest will be paid, in return for a reduction in the firm's fees.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's position?","o":["The firm must still pay a fair sum of interest, because the duty to pay interest cannot be excluded.","The firm must pay interest at the full rate it receives from the bank, despite the agreement.","No interest is payable, but only because Caxton Ltd is a company rather than an individual.","The agreement is valid only if the money is placed in a separate designated client account.","No interest is payable, because Caxton Ltd gave informed consent in a written agreement."],"a":4,"e":"The firm must account for a **fair sum of interest** (r.7.1), but may agree a **different arrangement, including no interest**, by **written agreement** with the client, provided the client has **sufficient information to give informed consent** (r.7.2). Here Caxton Ltd was told the likely interest and signed an agreement, so the arrangement is effective. The rules never require the firm to pay all the interest it earns, and the exception applies to individuals and companies alike, whatever type of account is used."},{"t":"probate-accounts","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Penrith Solicitors acts for the executors of two unrelated estates. The Ahmed estate must pay inheritance tax of £30,000 before the grant can be obtained, but only £10,000 is held on its ledger. A partner suggests paying the full £30,000 from client account, using £20,000 held for the Brook estate for a week until the Ahmed estate's funds can be released.\n\nWhat is the best advice to the firm?","o":["It may use the Brook estate's money, provided the £20,000 is repaid to the Brook ledger within a week.","It may use the Brook estate's money, provided the Brook executors are told afterwards.","It must not use the Brook estate's money; the shortfall must be funded another way, such as from business account.","It may use the Brook estate's money, because inheritance tax must be paid before the grant can be issued.","It may use the Brook estate's money, because all client money is held in one general client account."],"a":2,"e":"Client money may be withdrawn for a client only if **sufficient funds are held for that specific client** (r.5.3). Using the Brook estate's money for the Ahmed estate, even for a short time, would **overdraw** the Ahmed ledger and use **another client's money**, a breach that would have to be replaced immediately. The firm may pay £10,000 from the Ahmed ledger and fund the rest from its **business account**, or the executors may arrange a **loan** or use the **direct payment scheme**. The urgency of the tax and pooling in a general client account do not change this."},{"t":"conveyancing-accounts","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Hurst Solicitors acts for Mr Coles on the sale of his house. On exchange it received a deposit of £40,000, which it holds as stakeholder and has recorded on a separate stakeholder ledger. Completion has now taken place.\n\nWhich entries record the release of the deposit to Mr Coles on completion?","o":["Debit client cash £40,000; credit Mr Coles's ledger client column £40,000.","Debit the stakeholder ledger client column £40,000; credit client cash £40,000.","Debit the stakeholder ledger client column £40,000; credit Mr Coles's ledger client column £40,000, with no cash entry.","Debit Mr Coles's ledger client column £40,000; credit the stakeholder ledger client column £40,000, with no cash entry.","Debit the stakeholder ledger business column £40,000; credit Mr Coles's ledger business column £40,000."],"a":2,"e":"A deposit held **as stakeholder** is held for both parties until **completion**, when it becomes the seller's. Moving it to the seller's ledger is an **inter-client transfer**: the money stays in client account, so there is **no cash entry**, just a **debit to the stakeholder ledger** and a **credit to the seller's ledger**, both in the **client columns**. Reversing the entries would increase the stakeholder balance, and the business columns are irrelevant because this is client money."},{"t":"conveyancing-accounts","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Tarn Solicitors acts for Mrs Okafor, who is selling her house and buying another in England. Contracts on both have been exchanged on the same day, with completion of both on the same date. The buyer of her house paid a £30,000 deposit to Tarn Solicitors. Both contracts incorporate the Standard Conditions of Sale unamended. Mrs Okafor asks the firm to use the £30,000 as the deposit on her purchase.\n\nMay the firm do so?","o":["No, because a deposit received by the seller's solicitor is always held as stakeholder until completion.","No, because the deposit belongs to the buyer until completion of Mrs Okafor's sale.","Yes, but only if the buyer of her house gives written consent.","Yes, because the seller may use it as the deposit on a related purchase of a home in England and Wales.","Yes, because the deposit becomes firm money on exchange of contracts."],"a":3,"e":"Under **Standard Condition 2.2.5**, if the seller is buying another home in England and Wales before completion, the seller may use all or part of the deposit as the **deposit on that purchase**. Only a deposit **not used in this way** is held **as stakeholder** (SC 2.2.6). The buyer's consent is not needed, as the contract already permits it. A deposit is client money, never firm money."},{"t":"joint-own-tpma","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Ryder Solicitors holds a £20,000 retention in a joint deposit account in the names of Ryder Solicitors and the buyer's solicitors, pending a planning issue. Separately, a partner of the firm is a signatory on an estate bank account in the names of the personal representatives of a deceased client, and uses it to pay estate expenses.\n\nWhich of these accounts must the firm reconcile at least every five weeks?","o":["Only the estate bank account operated as signatory.","Only the joint deposit account.","Both the joint deposit account and the estate bank account.","Neither account, because neither is the firm's client account.","Neither account, but only statements need be obtained for each."],"a":0,"e":"For a **joint account** (r.9), only **statements every five weeks** (r.8.2) and the **bills record** (r.8.4) apply, so no reconciliation is needed. Where the firm **operates a client's own account as signatory** (r.10), such as an estate account in the PRs' names, the firm must also **reconcile** at least every five weeks (r.8.3). Both accounts still count towards the duty to obtain an **accountant's report**."},{"t":"accountants-reports","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Selby Solicitors' accounting period ended on 31 December 2025. Its accountant finds that, during the period, a client's money was used to make a payment for another client, putting client money at risk. The shortfall was replaced as soon as it was found.\n\nWhat must the firm do with the accountant's report?","o":["Obtain the report by 30 June 2026, but it need not deliver it to the SRA because the shortfall was replaced.","Obtain the report and deliver it to the SRA by 30 June 2026.","Obtain the report and deliver it to the SRA by 31 March 2026.","Obtain the report by 31 December 2026 and keep it for inspection.","Obtain the report and deliver it to the SRA by 1 April 2027."],"a":1,"e":"A firm that held client money must **obtain** an accountant's report **within six months** of the end of the accounting period, and must **deliver** it to the SRA within the same period **if** it is **qualified** to show that client money **is, has been or is likely to be placed at risk** (r.12.1). Using one client's money for another put money at risk, so the report must reach the SRA by **30 June 2026**, even though the shortfall was replaced. The requirement for all reports to be submitted from **1 April 2027** was not in force at the examinable date."}],"trusts":[{"t":"certainties","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Gerald owns 80 bottles of the same vintage port, all stored together in his cellar. He signs a document declaring that he holds '20 bottles of my port' on trust for his niece, Hope, but he never separates or labels any bottles. Gerald has now been made bankrupt, and his trustee in bankruptcy claims all 80 bottles.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Hope's position?","o":["She is entitled to 20 bottles, because identical items forming part of a bulk need not be separated.","She is entitled to 20 bottles, because Gerald declared himself trustee and so no transfer was needed.","She has no claim, because a declaration of trust of chattels must be made by deed.","She has no claim to any bottles, because a trust of tangible goods requires the goods to be segregated.","She is entitled to a quarter share of the 80 bottles as a tenant in common with the trustee in bankruptcy."],"a":3,"e":"Certainty of subject matter requires the trust property to be identifiable, and for **tangible goods** taken from a larger bulk this means they must be **segregated** (Re London Wine). No bottles were ever separated, so no one can say which 20 are Hope's, **no trust arises** and the port belongs to Gerald's estate. The relaxed approach for identical **intangible** property such as shares of one class does not extend to physical goods, and a self-declaration of trust of chattels needs no formality."},{"t":"formalities","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Isla owns a holiday cottage. She telephones her son Jake and tells him that from now on she holds the cottage on trust for him. The next day her friend Kit, acting on Isla's oral instructions, writes and signs a letter to Jake confirming that Isla holds the cottage on trust for him. Isla herself signs nothing. Isla has now changed her mind.\n\nIs the trust enforceable by Jake?","o":["Yes, because Kit's letter is signed writing made on Isla's instructions.","Yes, because the writing may come after the oral declaration and need not be signed.","No, because the writing must be signed by Isla herself as the person able to declare the trust.","No, because a declaration of trust of land is void unless it is made by deed.","Yes, because an owner declaring herself trustee of her own land needs no writing at all."],"a":2,"e":"A lifetime declaration of trust of **land** must be **manifested and proved by writing signed by the person able to declare the trust** (**LPA 1925 s.53(1)(b)**). Unlike a disposition of an existing equitable interest under **s.53(1)(c)**, s.53(1)(b) makes no provision for an **agent** to sign, so Kit's letter is not enough and the trust is **unenforceable**. Writing made later can satisfy the section, but it must be signed by Isla, and no deed is needed."},{"t":"constitution","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Leon, who was terminally ill, executed a deed establishing the Leon Foundation, a charitable trust, with himself and three others as trustees. At the signing he said: 'I give all my shares in Mapp plc to the Foundation.' He died a week later without transferring the shares, which remained registered in his sole name. His will leaves everything to his sister.\n\nWho is entitled to the shares?","o":["Leon's sister, because the gift was incomplete and equity will not assist a volunteer.","Leon's sister, because a failed gift to trustees cannot be treated as a declaration of trust.","The trustees of the Foundation, because Leon was one of the trustees and cannot in conscience deny the gift.","The trustees of the Foundation, because Leon made a valid gift in contemplation of death.","The trustees of the Foundation, because Leon had done everything in his power to transfer the shares."],"a":2,"e":"Where a donor declares a gift to a trust of which **he is himself one of the trustees**, the gift is complete once the property is vested in **him as one trustee**; his conscience is affected and his estate must transfer the property to all the trustees (Choithram v Pagarani). So the general rule that **equity will not assist a volunteer** does not defeat the gift. It is not a 'done everything in his power' case, because Leon had signed no transfer, and there was no **delivery of dominion** for a gift in contemplation of death."},{"t":"beneficial-entitlement","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Nora's will left her estate on trust to pay the income to her husband, Owen, for life and then the capital to her nephew, Pip. Pip has since died, leaving a will giving everything to his partner, Quin. Owen is still alive, aged 80.\n\nWhat will happen to the trust capital when Owen dies?","o":["It will return to Nora's estate on resulting trust, because Pip did not survive Owen.","It will pass to Owen's estate, because the life tenant takes absolutely if the remainderman dies first.","It will pass to Pip's next of kin, because an interest in remainder cannot be left by will.","It will pass to Quin only if the court approves under the Variation of Trusts Act 1958.","It will pass to Quin under Pip's will, because Pip's interest was vested in interest."],"a":4,"e":"A gift of capital 'then to Pip' with **no condition** gives Pip an interest **vested in interest**: his right is certain and only its enjoyment is postponed until Owen's life interest ends. A vested interest is property that **passes on the beneficiary's death**, so it goes under Pip's will to Quin and falls into possession when Owen dies. Only a **contingent** interest, such as one requiring Pip to survive Owen, would have failed, leading to a resulting trust or gift over."},{"t":"remedies-tracing","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Rhys, the sole trustee of a family trust, took £20,000 of trust money and added £30,000 of his own to buy shares in a private company for £50,000 in his own name. The shares are now worth £100,000. Rhys has been made bankrupt and has no other significant assets.\n\nWhat is the beneficiaries' best claim?","o":["A personal claim against Rhys for £20,000 plus interest, as unsecured creditors.","A proprietary claim to a 40% share of the shares, now worth £40,000.","A lien over the shares to secure repayment of £20,000.","A proprietary claim to all the shares, because a wrongdoing trustee cannot rely on his own contribution.","Nothing, because the trust money was mixed with Rhys's money and can no longer be traced."],"a":1,"e":"Where trust money and a trustee's own money are used together to buy an asset, the beneficiaries may choose either a **proportionate share** of the asset, including any increase in value, or a **lien** securing the trust money (Foskett v McKeown). The trust provided 40% of the price, so a 40% share worth £40,000 is better than a £20,000 lien or a **personal** claim, which would rank only as an unsecured debt in Rhys's bankruptcy. Equitable tracing can follow money into a mixed purchase, but it does not give the beneficiaries the trustee's own contribution."},{"t":"resulting-trusts","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Seren lent £150,000 to Tidy Homes Ltd on express written terms that the money could be used only to buy a particular plot of land and must be kept in a separate account until completion. The money was paid into a separate account. Before the purchase completed, Tidy Homes went into liquidation, owing large sums to other creditors. The £150,000 is still in the account.\n\nWhat is Seren's position?","o":["She is an unsecured creditor for £150,000 and must share pari passu with the other creditors.","The company holds the money on trust for her, so she can recover it in full ahead of the other creditors.","She can recover the money only if she registered a charge over the account at Companies House.","The money passes to the liquidator, because a loan always transfers full ownership to the borrower.","She can recover the money only if the land purchase completes before the liquidation ends."],"a":1,"e":"Money lent for a **specific purpose**, on terms that it is **not at the borrower's free disposal**, is held on a **Quistclose trust**. When the purpose fails, the borrower holds the money on **resulting trust for the lender**, so it never became part of the company's assets available to its creditors. The separate account is good evidence of the shared intention, and no charge needs registering because Seren is claiming her own beneficial interest."},{"t":"family-home","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Uri and Vana, an unmarried couple, bought a house for £400,000. The transfer to them, signed by both, declares that they hold the property 'as tenants in common in equal shares'. Uri paid the whole £100,000 deposit and has paid two-thirds of the mortgage instalments since. They have now separated, and Uri claims a larger share.\n\nWhat are their beneficial shares likely to be?","o":["Equal shares, because the express declaration of trust in the transfer is conclusive.","Shares proportionate to their financial contributions, under a resulting trust.","Unequal shares reflecting the whole course of dealing between them, because they were not married.","Equal shares, unless Uri can show that they kept their finances separate throughout.","Uri takes the whole beneficial interest, because he provided the deposit."],"a":0,"e":"Where the purchase documents contain an **express declaration of the beneficial shares**, that declaration is **conclusive** unless set aside for fraud or mistake or later varied. The court does not look behind it at who paid what, so the couple hold in **equal shares**. The whole-course-of-dealing approach applies only where there is **no express declaration**, and resulting-trust shares based on contributions are not used for a couple's home."},{"t":"fiduciary-duties","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Wyn was managing director of Xeno Ltd for ten years before he was appointed a trustee of a family trust that holds 25% of Xeno's shares. Since then the trust's votes have been cast to re-elect him at each annual meeting, but the holders of the other 75% of the shares, who have no connection with the trust, have always voted for him and would re-elect him regardless. The trust deed says nothing about directors' fees.\n\nMust Wyn account to the trust for his director's fees?","o":["Yes, because any payment a trustee receives from a company in which the trust holds shares belongs to the trust.","Yes, because the trust's votes were cast in his favour, which creates a conflict of interest.","No, because he did not obtain or keep his directorship through the use of the trust's shareholding.","No, because his fees are payment for work done and the trust has suffered no loss.","Yes, unless he can show that the fees were reasonable for the work he did as a director."],"a":2,"e":"A trustee must account for directors' fees where the position was **obtained by using the trust's shareholding** (Re Macadam), because the profit then comes from the trusteeship. Wyn was a director long before becoming a trustee and would be re-elected **whatever the trust's votes**, so his fees do not derive from his position as trustee and he may keep them (Re Gee). Lack of loss to the trust, or the reasonableness of the fees, would not on their own excuse an unauthorised profit."},{"t":"trustees-appointment","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"A trust deed names Yara as the person with power to appoint new trustees. The trustees are Zak, Amir and Bryony. Yara has died. Bryony wishes to retire, and the trustees want her replaced by an accountant, Cai. All the beneficiaries are adults, but one of them opposes Cai's appointment.\n\nWho has power to appoint Cai in Bryony's place?","o":["Zak and Amir only, because a retiring trustee cannot take part in appointing her own replacement.","The beneficiaries, by a written direction under the Trusts of Land and Appointment of Trustees Act 1996.","Yara's personal representatives, because the power of appointment passes to them on her death.","Only the court, because the person nominated in the trust deed to appoint new trustees has died.","Zak, Amir and Bryony together, as the continuing trustees including the retiring trustee."],"a":4,"e":"Under **Trustee Act 1925 s.36(1)**, the power to replace a trustee belongs first to the **person nominated** in the trust instrument and, if there is none able and willing, to the **surviving or continuing trustees**, which **includes a retiring trustee** willing to act (s.36(8)). With Yara dead, Zak, Amir and Bryony can appoint Cai in writing, preferably by deed so the property vests under s.40. The personal representatives of the **last surviving trustee**, not of the nominated person, act only if no trustees remain, and a TLATA s.19 direction would need **all** the beneficiaries."},{"t":"trustee-duties-powers","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Under a will trust arising on his grandmother's death in 2022, the residue of her estate, now worth £120,000, is held for Dewi 'if he attains the age of 25', with a gift over to his sister if he dies before then. The will does not exclude the statutory powers. Dewi has just turned 18 and asks the trustees what he is now entitled to.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Dewi's entitlement?","o":["He is entitled to the whole fund now, because he is an adult with full capacity.","He is entitled to the income from the fund as it arises until he reaches 25 or dies, but not to the capital.","He is entitled to nothing until he reaches 25, although the trustees may pay him income at their discretion.","He is entitled to half the capital now, with the rest paid when he reaches 25.","He is entitled to the income only if the trustees consider it reasonable, having regard to his other income."],"a":1,"e":"Under **Trustee Act 1925 s.31(1)(ii)**, a beneficiary whose **contingent** interest carries the intermediate income becomes **entitled to that income** at **18**, until the interest vests or fails. Dewi cannot call for the capital, because his interest is contingent and his sister takes if he dies under 25, so Saunders v Vautier does not help him alone. Capital can be paid early only at the trustees' discretion under the **power of advancement**, not as of right."},{"t":"breach-of-trust","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"A trust deed excludes the trustees' liability for 'any loss howsoever caused, other than by actual fraud'. One trustee, Efa, invested £200,000 of trust money in a company run by her brother, knowing it was close to insolvency and that the investment was against the beneficiaries' interests, because she wanted to help him. She made no personal gain. The money has been lost.\n\nCan Efa rely on the exemption clause?","o":["No, because she acted dishonestly, and liability for actual fraud cannot be excluded.","Yes, because the clause covers any loss other than fraud, and she did not intend to profit herself.","Yes, because the clause excludes liability for gross negligence, which is what her conduct amounts to.","No, because a trust deed cannot exclude the statutory duty of care under the Trustee Act 2000.","No, because the beneficiaries did not consent to the clause when the trust was created."],"a":0,"e":"An exemption clause can exclude liability for negligence, even **gross negligence**, but **never for actual fraud**, meaning **dishonesty**: deliberately acting against the beneficiaries' interests or being recklessly indifferent to them (Armitage v Nurse). Efa knowingly made an investment she knew was against their interests, which is dishonest even though she sought no gain for herself. The Trustee Act 2000 duty of care can be excluded, and the beneficiaries' consent to the clause is irrelevant."},{"t":"certainties","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Fenella's will leaves £5 million to trustees 'to distribute among such of the residents of Greater Manchester as my trustees in their absolute discretion think fit'. Fenella had no particular connection with the area, where around 2.8 million people live. Her residuary beneficiary argues that the gift is void.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the gift?","o":["It is valid, because it can be said of any given person whether or not they are a resident.","It is void, because it is impossible to draw up a complete list of all the residents.","It is void, because the class is so wide that the discretionary trust is administratively unworkable.","It is valid, because evidential difficulty in identifying residents does not invalidate a discretionary trust.","It is void, because 'residents' is a conceptually uncertain description."],"a":2,"e":"A **discretionary trust** must satisfy the **is or is not test**, which 'residents of Greater Manchester' does, but it is still **void** if the class is so wide that it is **administratively unworkable** (ex p West Yorkshire Metropolitan County Council). A class of millions with no connection to the testator gives the trustees no sensible way to perform their duty to distribute. The complete list test applies only to **fixed** trusts, and 'residents' is a clear concept, so the problem is width, not conceptual or evidential uncertainty."},{"t":"constitution","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Gwil, who had been told he would die within weeks from cancer, handed his friend Hal the only key to a locked box containing his building society passbook, which the society required to be produced for any withdrawal. He said: 'If I don't get through this, the money in that account is yours.' Gwil died from the cancer a month later. His will leaves everything to his nephew.\n\nWho is entitled to the money in the building society account?","o":["The nephew, because Gwil did not sign anything transferring the account to Hal.","Hal, because Gwil made a valid gift in contemplation of death by handing over the means of control.","The nephew, because a gift that takes effect only on death must comply with the Wills Act 1837.","Hal, because Gwil had done everything in his power to transfer the account.","The nephew, because Hal is a volunteer and equity will not perfect an imperfect gift."],"a":1,"e":"A **donatio mortis causa** is valid where the donor **contemplates impending death** from an identified cause, makes the gift **conditional on death**, and **parts with dominion** over the property, for example by handing over the key to a box holding its **essential documents**. All three are met, so the gift takes effect outside the will on Gwil's death. A DMC is an established **exception** to the rule that equity will not assist a volunteer, and the Wills Act formalities do not apply to it."},{"t":"purpose-charitable","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Iolo's will leaves £30,000 'to the Llanwen Model Railway Club, to be used for the purposes of the club'. The club is an unincorporated association whose members can change its rules and decide by majority vote how its funds are spent, and nothing in the rules prevents them dividing its assets between themselves. The executors are unsure whether the gift is valid.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the gift?","o":["It is void, because it is a non-charitable purpose trust with no human beneficiary to enforce it.","It is void, because an unincorporated association cannot own property.","It is valid as a charitable trust for the advancement of amateur sport.","It is valid as a gift to the current members, to be held subject to the club's rules.","It is valid only if the club spends the money within a perpetuity period of 21 years."],"a":3,"e":"A gift to an unincorporated association is usually construed as an addition to the funds held by the **current members** under their **contract** with each other, the club's rules (Re Recher). Because the members **control** the property and could change the rules or divide the funds, the gift is valid and does not fail as a non-charitable purpose trust. The club itself cannot own property, which is exactly why the gift takes effect in favour of its members, and a private hobby club of this kind is not a charity."},{"t":"family-home","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Jac and Kim, an unmarried couple, bought a house in joint names in 2008 with no declaration of trust, sharing the deposit and the mortgage payments equally. In 2012 Jac moved out and bought his own flat. Since then Kim alone has paid the mortgage and all the outgoings and has brought up their children in the house, and Jac has contributed nothing. They now want the house sold.\n\nWhat is the most likely approach to their beneficial shares?","o":["Equal shares, because they bought in joint names and contributed equally at the time of purchase.","Shares in proportion to their total financial contributions, under a resulting trust.","Equal shares, because any later change of intention would need to be in writing signed by Jac.","Kim takes the whole beneficial interest, because Jac abandoned the property.","Unequal shares, because a later common intention can be inferred from their conduct, with a fair share imputed if the precise shares cannot be inferred."],"a":4,"e":"For a home in **joint names**, the starting point is **equal shares**, but that can be displaced by a different common intention, at purchase or **formed later**, inferred objectively from conduct (Jones v Kernott). Jac's departure and Kim's sole payment of everything for years point to a change of intention; if the actual shares cannot be inferred, the court may **impute** a **fair** share having regard to the **whole course of dealing**. A constructive trust needs **no writing** (LPA 1925 s.53(2)), and resulting-trust arithmetic is not used for a couple's home."},{"t":"fiduciary-duties","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Lyn is a trustee of a trust under which her adult nephew, Mal, has a one-third share of the capital. Mal, who needs money quickly, offers to sell Lyn his beneficial interest. Lyn tells Mal everything she knows about the trust's investments, obtains an independent valuation and pays Mal its full value, and Mal signs a written assignment. The other beneficiaries later object.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the purchase?","o":["It will stand, because Lyn can show she took no advantage of her position, made full disclosure and paid a fair price.","It is voidable at the option of any beneficiary, however fair the price, because a trustee must never buy trust property.","It is void, because a trustee cannot acquire any interest under a trust she administers.","It will stand only if the other beneficiaries consented to it in advance in writing.","It is voidable, because Mal's disposal of his equitable interest was not approved by the court."],"a":0,"e":"A trustee who buys a **beneficiary's interest** from that beneficiary is subject to the **fair-dealing rule**: the purchase stands if she shows she **took no advantage** of her position, made **full disclosure** and paid a **fair price**. That differs from the **self-dealing rule**, under which a trustee's purchase of **trust property** itself is voidable at any beneficiary's option, however fair. Lyn did all that was required, and the other beneficiaries' consent is not needed because she bought Mal's own interest."},{"t":"trustees-appointment","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Nia, Osian and Pryce are the trustees of a trust of investments. Nia wishes to retire, and no one is willing to replace her. Osian and Pryce are happy to continue as the only trustees and to consent to her retirement. The trust deed says nothing about retirement.\n\nHow can Nia retire?","o":["Only by appointing a replacement, because a trustee cannot retire without being replaced.","Only with the written consent of all the beneficiaries.","By giving written notice to Osian and Pryce, with no need for their consent.","By deed, with Osian and Pryce consenting by deed, because at least two persons will remain as trustees.","Only by applying to the court for an order discharging her from the trust."],"a":3,"e":"Under **Trustee Act 1925 s.39**, a trustee may retire **without a replacement** by **deed** if afterwards **at least two persons or a trust corporation** will remain as trustees and the co-trustees **consent by deed**. Osian and Pryce will remain and are willing to consent, so Nia needs no replacement, no beneficiary consent and no court order. A unilateral notice is not enough."},{"t":"trustee-duties-powers","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Rhian and Sion are trustees of a £2 million fund. After taking proper advice, they appointed a well-established, properly regulated discretionary fund manager under a written agreement, gave it a written policy statement and reviewed its performance every six months. Without their knowledge, an employee of the manager made an unauthorised and fraudulent trade that lost £100,000. The beneficiaries want to sue Rhian and Sion.\n\nAre Rhian and Sion personally liable for the loss?","o":["No, because a trustee is liable for an agent's default only if they failed to take reasonable care in appointing or reviewing the agent.","Yes, because trustees are vicariously liable for the acts of any agent they appoint.","Yes, because investment decisions are a function that trustees cannot delegate.","No, because the loss was caused by fraud, for which trustees are never liable.","Yes, because trustees must personally approve every investment made with trust money."],"a":0,"e":"Trustees may delegate **asset management** to a discretionary fund manager under **Trustee Act 2000 Part IV**, provided there is a written agreement and a written **policy statement**. Under **s.23**, a trustee is **not liable** for an agent's acts or defaults unless the trustee failed to meet the **duty of care** in appointing the agent or reviewing the arrangement. Rhian and Sion took advice, chose a reputable manager and reviewed it regularly, so they are not personally liable; it is decisions about **who benefits** that cannot be delegated."},{"t":"breach-of-trust","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Tegan, an unpaid lay trustee, was asked to distribute a small family trust fund among the settlor's grandchildren. She took advice from a solicitor, who wrongly told her that a step-grandchild was included in the class. She paid the step-grandchild £15,000 before the error came to light, and the money cannot now be recovered.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Tegan's position?","o":["She is in breach of trust, but the court may relieve her if she acted honestly and reasonably and ought fairly to be excused.","She is not in breach of trust, because she followed legal advice.","She is liable in full, because the court cannot relieve a trustee who has paid the wrong person.","She is not liable, because an unpaid lay trustee owes only a duty to act honestly.","She is liable in full, because relief is available only to trustees who are paid for their services."],"a":0,"e":"Paying the **wrong person** is a breach of trust even when done honestly. Under **Trustee Act 1925 s.61**, the court may relieve a trustee wholly or partly if she acted **honestly** and **reasonably** and **ought fairly to be excused**; an unpaid lay trustee who took and followed professional advice is the kind of trustee the court is most willing to relieve. Courts are slower to relieve paid professionals, not the reverse, and lay trustees still owe a duty of care."},{"t":"strangers","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Ursula, a trustee, transferred £50,000 of trust money to her friend Vaughan as a birthday present. Vaughan had no idea the money came from a trust and no reason to suspect anything. He spent £30,000 on a holiday and still has £20,000 in a separate savings account. The beneficiaries have now discovered the breach.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Vaughan's liability?","o":["He is liable for knowing receipt for the full £50,000, because he received trust property for his own benefit.","He is liable as a dishonest assistant for the full £50,000.","He has no liability at all, because he was a bona fide purchaser for value without notice.","He is liable for knowing receipt, but only for the £20,000 he still holds.","He is not liable for knowing receipt, but the beneficiaries can trace into and recover the £20,000 he still holds."],"a":4,"e":"**Knowing receipt** requires knowledge making it **unconscionable** to keep the benefit, so an **innocent volunteer** with no reason to suspect a breach is not personally liable. But Vaughan gave no value, so he is **not a bona fide purchaser**, and the beneficiaries' interest survives in the identifiable £20,000, which they can recover by a **proprietary** tracing claim. The £30,000 spent on a holiday has been **dissipated**, and dishonest assistance requires dishonesty."},{"t":"remedies-tracing","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Wil, a trustee, paid £10,000 of trust money into his personal current account, which then held £2,000 of his own money. Over the next month he spent money on living expenses until the balance fell to £3,000. He then paid in £8,000 of his salary, bringing the balance to £11,000. Wil is now bankrupt.\n\nHow much can the beneficiaries claim from the account by a proprietary claim?","o":["£10,000.","£11,000.","£8,000.","£3,000.","£1,000."],"a":3,"e":"Under **Re Hallett** a trustee is presumed to spend his own money first, so when the balance fell to £3,000 all of what remained was trust money. The beneficiaries cannot claim more than the **lowest intermediate balance** (Roscoe v Winder), and Wil's later salary payment is not treated as replacing trust money unless he intended to repay the trust. The other £7,000 has been dissipated on living expenses and can be pursued only by a **personal** claim in the bankruptcy."},{"t":"resulting-trusts","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"After a fire destroyed the Xu family's home, neighbours set up a fund 'to pay for the family's temporary accommodation until their house is rebuilt'. Forty named neighbours gave between £100 and £1,000 each. The house has been rebuilt and £6,000 remains in the fund. The Xu family claim the surplus.\n\nWho is entitled to the surplus?","o":["The Xu family, because the fund was raised for their benefit.","The Crown, as ownerless property, because the purpose has been fulfilled.","The named donors, on resulting trust, in proportion to their contributions.","It must be applied cy-près to a similar charitable purpose.","The trustees of the fund, who may keep it as payment for their work."],"a":2,"e":"Where money is given for a **specific purpose** that has been fulfilled leaving a **surplus**, the surplus is held on **automatic resulting trust** for the donors in proportion to their gifts (Re Abbott Fund Trusts), unless they intended an **out-and-out gift**. The fund was limited to accommodation costs, so the family were never given the money outright. The Crown takes as bona vacantia only where donors parted with their money out and out, as with anonymous collections, and cy-près needs a charitable trust, which a fund for one family is not."},{"t":"certainties","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Yestin's will leaves £100,000 on trust 'to be divided equally among my good friends'. Separately, it allows 'any of my good friends who wish to' to buy any of his watches at half their market value. The executors are unsure whether either provision is valid.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the two provisions?","o":["Both are valid, because any claimant can try to show that they were one of Yestin's good friends.","Both fail, because 'good friends' is too uncertain to identify anyone.","The trust to divide £100,000 is valid, but the option to buy the watches fails.","The trust is valid as a discretionary trust, because the executors can decide who were his good friends.","The trust to divide £100,000 equally fails, but the option to buy the watches is valid."],"a":4,"e":"A **fixed trust** ('equally') requires a **complete list** of beneficiaries, which cannot be drawn up for an uncertain concept such as 'good friends', so the £100,000 trust fails and results to the estate. A **gift to individuals subject to a condition** is different: it is enough that a particular claimant can be shown to qualify, so the watch option is valid (Re Barlow). The trust is not discretionary, because Yestin fixed equal shares and gave the executors no power to select."},{"t":"formalities","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Zara is the sole beneficiary under a bare trust of 5,000 shares in a listed company, which her brother Aled holds for her as nominee. Zara orally instructs Aled to transfer the shares to her son, Bryn, as a gift. Aled signs a stock transfer form and Bryn is registered as the owner. Zara signs nothing.\n\nWho owns the shares beneficially?","o":["Bryn, because the equitable interest passed with the legal title and no separate writing was needed.","Zara, because a disposition of an existing equitable interest is void unless it is in signed writing.","Zara, because Bryn is a volunteer and equity will not assist a volunteer.","Zara, because Bryn holds the shares on resulting trust for her as he provided no consideration.","Bryn, but only once Zara confirms the gift by deed."],"a":0,"e":"**LPA 1925 s.53(1)(c)** requires signed writing for a **disposition of a subsisting equitable interest**, but where a bare trust beneficiary directs the trustee to **transfer the legal title** to a third party, intending the beneficial interest to go with it, **no separate writing** is needed (Vandervell v IRC). The legal transfer was completed by registration, so Bryn owns the shares outright. Contrast an oral direction to the trustee to hold the shares for someone else from now on, which would be **void** without writing."},{"t":"constitution","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Carys decided to give her 2,000 shares in Dyfi Ltd, a private company, to her nephew Emyr. She signed a stock transfer form in his favour but kept it, with the share certificate, in her desk, intending to post them to the company later. She died suddenly a month afterwards. Her will leaves everything to her daughter, who is her executor.\n\nWho is entitled to the shares?","o":["Emyr, because Carys had signed the stock transfer form and so had done everything required of her.","Her daughter, because Carys had not done everything in her power to transfer the shares.","Emyr, because Carys intended an immediate gift and that intention continued until her death.","Emyr, because the failed gift takes effect as a declaration that Carys held the shares on trust for him.","Her daughter, unless the directors of Dyfi Ltd agree to register the transfer to Emyr."],"a":1,"e":"A gift of shares is complete in equity under **Re Rose** only once the donor has done **everything in her power**, typically handing the signed transfer and certificate to the donee or the company so that only registration by a third party remains. Carys kept the documents, so the gift was incomplete and the shares pass under her will (Zeital v Kaye). **Strong v Bird** could help only if Emyr were her executor, and a failed gift is **not** treated as a declaration of trust (Milroy v Lord)."},{"t":"beneficial-entitlement","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Under a trust created in 2015, trustees hold £600,000 on discretionary trust for 'such of Gethin, Heledd and Iestyn as the trustees select', in such shares as the trustees decide. No one else can benefit. All three are adults with full capacity, and they agree that they want the fund divided equally between them now. The trustees would prefer to keep the trust running.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the beneficiaries' position?","o":["They cannot end the trust, because the objects of a discretionary trust have only a hope of benefit.","Each of them can demand one-third of the fund, whether or not the others agree.","They can end the trust only with the court's approval under the Variation of Trusts Act 1958.","They can direct the trustees how to exercise their discretion, but cannot end the trust.","Acting together, they can end the trust and require the trustees to transfer the fund to them."],"a":4,"e":"Under **Saunders v Vautier**, beneficiaries who are all **adults with capacity** and **together absolutely entitled** can end the trust and call for the property. This applies to a discretionary trust where the class is **closed** and every object joins in (Re Smith), as here. Individually each has only a hope, so none can act alone, the Variation of Trusts Act is needed only where some beneficiaries cannot consent, and beneficiaries can end a trust but cannot direct how trustees use their discretion while it continues."},{"t":"family-home","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"For 15 years Jem gave up a well-paid job to care full-time for his elderly aunt, Kath, in her house, relying on her repeated assurances that 'this house will be yours when I'm gone'. Kath, who is still alive, has now changed her will and wants Jem to leave. The house is worth £500,000, and the court finds that Jem has established proprietary estoppel.\n\nHow will the court decide what remedy to give Jem?","o":["It must transfer the house to Jem immediately, because the remedy always fulfils the claimant's expectation in full.","It will award Jem only the market value of the care he provided, because estoppel compensates financial loss.","It cannot give Jem any remedy until Kath dies, because a will can always be revoked.","It will aim to remedy the unconscionability, starting from Jem's expectation but adjusting it, for example for early receipt, so that it is not out of all proportion to his detriment.","It will give Jem a share of the house in proportion to his financial contributions to it."],"a":3,"e":"Once proprietary estoppel is established, the court decides how to **satisfy the equity**, aiming to remedy the **unconscionability** of going back on the assurance. The **starting point is usually the claimant's expectation**, but the court can reduce it, for example by a **discount for early receipt** while the promisor is alive, and must make sure the remedy is not out of all proportion to the detriment (Guest v Guest). An assurance about a future inheritance can found an estoppel even though wills are revocable, and the remedy is flexible rather than tied to financial contributions."},{"t":"fiduciary-duties","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Lowri, acting as agent for Mabli in buying a hotel, secretly accepted a £200,000 commission from the seller. She used the money to buy shares, which are now worth £300,000. Lowri has since become insolvent.\n\nWhat is Mabli's best claim?","o":["A personal claim against Lowri for £200,000, ranking with her other unsecured creditors.","A claim for damages only, because a commission paid by a third party never belonged to Mabli.","A proprietary claim to the shares, because the commission was held on constructive trust for her from the moment Lowri received it.","A proprietary claim limited to £200,000, because Mabli cannot benefit from the rise in value of the shares.","No claim, unless Mabli can show that she paid a higher price for the hotel because of the commission."],"a":2,"e":"A **bribe or secret commission** received by a fiduciary is held on **constructive trust** for the principal as soon as it is received (FHR European Ventures v Cedar Capital). Mabli therefore has a **proprietary** claim and can trace into the shares, taking the **increase in value** and ranking ahead of Lowri's creditors. Under the strict no-profit rule she need not prove that she suffered any loss."},{"t":"trustee-duties-powers","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Under a will trust that took effect in 2010, £90,000 is held for Nest 'if she attains 30', with a gift over to her cousin if she does not. There is no prior life interest, the will does not modify the statutory powers, and nothing has been advanced to Nest before. Nest, aged 24, asks the trustees for capital to buy a flat.\n\nWhat is the maximum the trustees may advance to Nest under the statutory power?","o":["£90,000.","£45,000.","£30,000.","Nothing until she reaches 30, because her interest is contingent.","Nothing, unless her cousin consents in writing."],"a":1,"e":"The **statutory power of advancement** (Trustee Act 1925 s.32) allows capital to be applied for a beneficiary's **advancement or benefit**, such as buying a home, even though her interest is **contingent**. For trusts created **before 1 October 2014**, the limit is **one-half** of the presumptive share, here £45,000; the whole share is available only for trusts created or arising after that date. Consent is needed only from a person with a **prior** interest, and the cousin's gift over is a later interest."},{"t":"breach-of-trust","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Owain and Prys are trustees of a trust under which Rhodri, an adult, and his sister Sali each have a one-half share. Rhodri wrote to the trustees urging them to invest £100,000 in his friend's start-up company, and they did so, although it was not an authorised investment. The company has collapsed and the money is lost. Sali sues the trustees.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the trustees' position?","o":["They have a complete defence against Sali, because a beneficiary instigated the breach.","They are not liable to anyone, because Rhodri's written request authorised the investment.","They are liable to Sali, but the court may impound Rhodri's beneficial interest to indemnify them.","They are liable to Sali, and Rhodri can also sue them for his share of the loss.","They are liable to Sali, and their only recourse is to sue the company that received the money."],"a":2,"e":"A beneficiary who **instigates or consents** to a breach with full knowledge cannot sue for it, but that does not bar **other beneficiaries** such as Sali. Under **Trustee Act 1925 s.62**, where a beneficiary **instigated, requested or consented in writing** to a breach, the court may **impound** his interest to indemnify the trustees. Rhodri's request could not make an unauthorised investment authorised as against Sali."},{"t":"strangers","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Seb, a trustee, honestly but mistakenly believed that the trust deed allowed him to lend trust money to a property developer. He asked Tali, an accountant, to arrange the transfer. Tali had read the deed and knew the loan was not permitted, and she suspected the developer was insolvent, but she arranged it because Seb paid her a fee from his own money. The money has been lost. Tali says she did not think she was doing anything dishonest.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Tali's liability?","o":["She is not liable, because Seb himself acted honestly, so there was no dishonest breach to assist.","She is not liable, because she did not believe she was acting dishonestly.","She is liable for knowing receipt, because she was paid a fee for arranging the transfer.","She is liable for dishonest assistance, because her conduct, given what she knew, was dishonest by the standards of ordinary decent people.","She is not liable, because she never received any of the trust money."],"a":3,"e":"Dishonest assistance requires a **breach of trust**, **assistance** by the defendant and **dishonesty** by the defendant; the **trustee need not be dishonest** (Royal Brunei Airlines v Tan). Dishonesty is judged by the **Ivey** test: the court establishes what Tali actually knew or believed, then asks whether her conduct was dishonest by the **standards of ordinary decent people**, so her own view of her honesty is irrelevant. No receipt of trust property is needed, and a fee paid from Seb's own money is not a receipt of trust property."},{"t":"remedies-tracing","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Ula, a solicitor, wrongly paid £20,000 belonging to the Vance Trust and then £30,000 belonging to the Wynne Trust into a single current account containing no other money. She later withdrew and spent £25,000 on her own debts. £25,000 remains in the account, and both trusts claim it.\n\nHow is the remaining £25,000 most likely to be shared?","o":["All £25,000 to the Wynne Trust, because the Vance Trust's money was paid in first and so withdrawn first.","Pari passu: £10,000 to the Vance Trust and £15,000 to the Wynne Trust.","Equally: £12,500 to each trust.","All £25,000 to the Vance Trust, because its money was paid in first and so has priority.","£20,000 to the Vance Trust and £5,000 to the Wynne Trust."],"a":1,"e":"Where the money of two **innocent** beneficiaries is mixed, the traditional first-in, first-out rule in **Clayton's Case** would give everything to the Wynne Trust, but it is only a rule of convenience and is usually **displaced** where it would be **arbitrary or unjust**. Courts now generally share the balance **pari passu**, in proportion to each trust's contribution (Barlow Clowes v Vaughan), giving £10,000 and £15,000. Rules such as Re Hallett, which work against a wrongdoing trustee, do not decide priority between two innocent trusts."},{"t":"purpose-charitable","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Xanthe's will, made in 2015, leaves £40,000 'to the Abergwyn Hospice'. The hospice closed in 2023 and its work was not taken over by any other body. Xanthe died in 2026. Her will makes several other gifts to medical charities, and the residue goes to her nephew.\n\nWhat is the most likely outcome of the gift to the hospice?","o":["It can be applied cy-près to a similar charitable purpose if the court finds that Xanthe had a general charitable intention.","It must be applied cy-près, because property given to charity can never return to the donor's estate.","It lapses and falls into residue, because a gift to a named institution can never be applied cy-près.","It passes to the hospice's former trustees to use for charitable purposes of their choice.","It is held on resulting trust for the people who donated to the hospice during its lifetime."],"a":0,"e":"The hospice ceased to exist **before Xanthe's death**, so this is **initial failure**: the gift can be applied **cy-près** only if she showed a **general charitable intention** rather than an intention to benefit that institution alone; otherwise it **lapses** into residue. Her pattern of other gifts to medical charities is evidence of such an intention. Cy-près without any need for general charitable intention applies only to **subsequent** failure, after the gift has taken effect."}],"land":[{"t":"nature-of-land","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Crumb Ltd has held a 10-year lease of a high street unit, which it runs as a bakery. During the term it bolted two commercial ovens to the floor and screwed glass display counters to the walls. The lease ends next week and says nothing about the ovens or counters. The landlord says they have become part of the building and must be left behind.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Crumb Ltd's position?","o":["Crumb Ltd must leave the ovens and counters, because anything fixed to the building other than by its own weight becomes the landlord's property.","Crumb Ltd may remove the ovens and counters before the lease ends, provided it makes good any damage caused by removing them.","Crumb Ltd may remove the counters, which are chattels, but must leave the ovens, which are bolted to the floor and so are fixtures.","Crumb Ltd may remove the ovens and counters at any time within 12 months after the lease ends, without any obligation to repair the premises.","Crumb Ltd must leave the ovens and counters unless the landlord agrees in writing to their removal, because the lease is silent."],"a":1,"e":"Even if items have become fixtures, a tenant may remove **trade fixtures** it installed itself, provided it does so **before the lease ends** (or, if it stays on in possession as tenant after the term, while it remains in possession) and **makes good** the damage. The degree of annexation decides only whether an item is a fixture; it does not stop the tenant's right to remove trade fixtures, so splitting the ovens from the counters is wrong. There is no 12-month grace period and no need for the landlord's written agreement."},{"t":"nature-of-land","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Gwen owns the unregistered freehold of a farmhouse. By a deed that is validly signed, witnessed and delivered, she grants her neighbour Hari a right to drive across her yard to reach his barn 'for the rest of Hari's life'.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Hari's right?","o":["It is a legal easement, because it was created by a validly executed deed.","It is an equitable easement, because a right for the grantee's lifetime is not equivalent to a fee simple absolute in possession or a term of years absolute.","It is a legal easement, because easements are listed in s.1(2) of the Law of Property Act 1925 whatever their duration.","It is a legal life interest in the yard, because s.1 of the Law of Property Act 1925 allows life estates to exist at law.","It is a mere licence, because a right limited to one person's lifetime cannot be proprietary."],"a":1,"e":"Under **s.1(2)(a) LPA 1925** an easement can be legal only if it is for a period **equivalent to a fee simple absolute in possession or a term of years absolute**; a right for life is neither, so under **s.1(3)** it can only be **equitable**, however formally it was made. A deed satisfies the formality condition but cannot cure a right that is not capable of being legal. A life interest is never a legal estate, and the right still benefits Hari's barn, so it is a proprietary easement rather than a licence."},{"t":"nature-of-land","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Larch Developments Ltd is building a block of six flats and wants each buyer to be bound, and every later owner of each flat to be bound, by an obligation to contribute to the cost of repairing the roof. It is considering selling each flat freehold, with a covenant to contribute in each transfer protected by a notice on each title.\n\nWhich of the following is the best advice to Larch Developments Ltd?","o":["The obligation will bind later owners if the flats are sold freehold, provided each covenant is protected by a notice on the register.","The obligation will bind later owners if the flats are sold freehold, because s.79 of the Law of Property Act 1925 makes covenants bind successors.","The obligation will bind later owners if it is worded negatively, for example 'not to fail to contribute to the roof'.","The obligation will not bind later owners under any structure, so Larch should rely on suing the first buyers for ever.","The obligation will not bind later owners if the flats are sold freehold, so it should sell long leases or set the block up as commonhold."],"a":4,"e":"The **burden of a positive covenant does not run with freehold land**, so a contribution covenant in a freehold transfer binds only the original buyer; a notice cannot make binding something incapable of binding, and s.79 does not change that rule. **Leasehold covenants** (positive and negative) bind successors, and in **commonhold** the **commonhold community statement** binds every unit-holder, so either structure achieves Larch's aim. Rewording does not help, because the test is substance, and an obligation to pay is positive."},{"t":"formalities","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Imogen owns a registered workshop. She orally agrees to let it to Jamal for two years at the full market rent, with Jamal paying a lump sum of £3,000 for the grant. Jamal moves in the same day. Nothing is put in writing.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Jamal has a legal lease for two years?","o":["He does, because a lease of three years or less can be created orally.","He does, because the lease took effect in possession at the best rent reasonably obtainable.","He does not, because a premium was paid, so the exception for short leases does not apply and a deed was needed.","He does not, but he has an equitable lease for two years because he went into possession under an agreement for a lease.","He does not, because every lease must be made by deed to be legal."],"a":2,"e":"A lease can be legal without a deed under **s.54(2) LPA 1925** only if it is for **3 years or less**, takes effect **in possession**, at the **best rent** and **without a fine (premium)**; the £3,000 lump sum is a premium, so a **deed** was needed. Nor is there an equitable lease, because a contract to grant a lease outside s.54(2) must comply with **s.2 LP(MP)A 1989**, and an oral agreement is void. Saying every lease needs a deed ignores the s.54(2) exception."},{"t":"formalities","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Kaveh granted Lin an option to buy his field for £90,000 at any time within three years. The option agreement was in writing, contained all the agreed terms and was signed by both of them. Two years later Lin sent Kaveh a letter, signed only by her, stating that she exercised the option. Kaveh now says that no binding sale contract exists because he did not sign the letter.\n\nIs Kaveh correct?","o":["Yes, because the exercise creates the contract of sale, which must be signed by both parties.","Yes, because the exercise is a variation of the option, which must itself comply with s.2.","No, because only the grant of the option had to comply with s.2 of the Law of Property (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1989, and its exercise is a unilateral act.","No, because contracts for the sale of land need not be signed by the seller if the buyer has paid for the option.","No, because Lin's letter is a deed, and deeds do not need to be signed by both parties."],"a":2,"e":"An option is a contract, so its **grant** must comply with **s.2 LP(MP)A 1989**, but the **exercise** of a validly granted option is a **unilateral act** under a contract that already exists and need not be signed by the grantor (Spiro v Glencrown). The letter is not a variation of the terms, so the variation rule does not apply. There is no general exception for a buyer who has paid, and a signed letter is not a deed."},{"t":"formalities","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"For 25 years Mabel worked on her uncle Nigel's farm for very low wages. Nigel repeatedly told her, in front of neighbours, that 'the farm will be yours when I'm gone', and she turned down a well-paid job elsewhere because of it. Nigel has now made a will leaving the farm to a charity. He is still alive and in good health.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Mabel's likely position?","o":["She has no claim, because a promise to leave land by will must be in writing signed by both parties.","She can claim proprietary estoppel; the court will start from fulfilling the promise but has a discretion and may reduce the award, for example because she would receive it earlier than promised.","She is entitled to an order that the farm be transferred to her now, because the elements of proprietary estoppel are made out.","She has a beneficial interest under a common intention constructive trust, because she contributed her labour.","She can recover only the difference between her wages and a market wage, because estoppel never gives an interest in land."],"a":1,"e":"An **assurance**, **reliance** and **detriment** that make it **unconscionable** to go back on the promise give rise to **proprietary estoppel**, which is not defeated by the lack of writing. The court's starting point is to **fulfil the expectation**, but it has a **discretion** and may reduce the award, e.g. with a **discount for accelerated receipt** (Guest v Guest), so saying she must get the farm now is too absolute. A common intention constructive trust needs a shared intention that she has a **present** share, which was not the case here, and estoppel can give an interest in land, not just money."},{"t":"registered-land","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Hassan is the sole registered proprietor of a house. His brother Imran paid a third of the purchase price and lives there with him. Before exchange on a sale to Jade, Jade's solicitor wrote to Imran asking whether he claimed any interest in the house. Imran, who understood the question, replied in writing that he had none. The sale has been completed and Jade has been registered as proprietor. Imran refuses to leave.\n\nIs Jade bound by Imran's interest?","o":["Yes, because Imran was in actual occupation at completion, so his interest overrides.","Yes, because Jade's solicitor knew Imran lived at the house and so had notice of his interest.","No, because the price was paid to a single trustee, so Imran's interest was overreached.","No, because a beneficial interest under a trust can only bind a buyer if protected by a notice.","No, because Imran failed to disclose his interest when asked on reasonable inquiry, so it does not override."],"a":4,"e":"An occupier's interest normally overrides under **LRA 2002 Sch 3 para 2**, but not where the occupier was **asked on reasonable inquiry** and **failed to disclose** the right when he could reasonably have been expected to (para 2(b)). Overreaching needs payment to **two** trustees, so it did not happen here, and a trust interest cannot be protected by notice at all (it is protected by a restriction or by actual occupation). Notice in the old equitable sense is not the test under the LRA 2002."},{"t":"registered-land","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Olga, the registered proprietor of a house, granted her neighbour Pete an option to buy a strip of her garden. Pete never protected the option on the register and does not occupy the strip. Olga has now transferred the house to her son Quentin as a gift, and Quentin has been registered as proprietor.\n\nIs Quentin bound by Pete's option?","o":["No, because an unprotected option does not bind a later registered proprietor.","No, because an option is a personal contract that cannot bind anyone other than Olga.","Yes, but only if Quentin actually knew about the option when the house was transferred to him.","Yes, because Quentin did not give valuable consideration, so he takes subject to earlier interests whether or not they were protected.","No, because Pete is not in actual occupation, so his option cannot override."],"a":3,"e":"Only a registered disposition **for valuable consideration** takes priority over unprotected interests under **s.29 LRA 2002**; a **gift** recipient is bound by existing interests under the **basic priority rule in s.28**, protected or not. The other wrong options would be right only for a buyer for value: actual occupation and notice matter only when s.29 applies. Knowledge is irrelevant, and an option is an estate contract, a proprietary interest."},{"t":"registered-land","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"In June 2026 Rosa bought an unregistered freehold house from Sam and completed by a conveyance executed as a deed. Her solicitor has just realised, in mid-November 2026, that no application for first registration has been made.\n\nWhat is the effect of the failure to apply in time?","o":["Nothing, because the legal estate passed to Rosa on completion and registration is optional for unregistered land.","Rosa still holds the legal estate, but she will be fined by HM Land Registry for applying late.","The conveyance has become void as regards the legal estate, which has reverted to Sam to hold on a bare trust for Rosa.","The sale contract is rescinded, so Sam must return the purchase price to Rosa.","Rosa holds the legal estate on a bare trust for Sam until she applies for registration."],"a":2,"e":"A sale of unregistered land triggers **compulsory first registration**, and the application must be made within **2 months** (**s.6 LRA 2002**). If it is not, the transfer becomes **void as regards the legal estate**, which **reverts to the transferor** to hold on a **bare trust** for the transferee (**s.7**), and a new conveyance is needed at Rosa's cost. The contract is not rescinded, the trust runs in Rosa's favour, not Sam's, and there is no fixed fine instead of this consequence."},{"t":"unregistered-land","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Tara is buying an unregistered freehold house. The root of title is a 1990 conveyance on sale, which states that the property is sold subject to the covenants contained in a 1920 conveyance. Tara's solicitor did not ask for a copy of the 1920 conveyance. It contains a covenant not to use the property for any trade. Tara completed and now wants to run a shop from the house.\n\nIs Tara bound by the 1920 covenant?","o":["No, because the covenant was never registered as a Class D(ii) land charge.","No, because she had no actual knowledge of the covenant's contents.","Yes, because a pre-1926 restrictive covenant is governed by the doctrine of notice, and Tara has constructive notice of a covenant referred to in her title deeds.","No, because the buyer of unregistered land is entitled to see only the last 15 years of title, so older covenants cannot bind her.","Yes, because all restrictive covenants bind every later owner of the land as legal interests."],"a":2,"e":"Class **D(ii)** registration applies only to restrictive covenants made **after 1925**; a **pre-1926** covenant is governed by the **doctrine of notice**, binding everyone except a bona fide purchaser of a legal estate for value **without notice**. A buyer has **constructive notice** of what reasonable inquiries into the deeds would reveal, and the root expressly refers to the 1920 covenants, so Tara is bound. The 15-year root rule limits what the buyer can insist on seeing but does not free her from interests referred to in the title, and restrictive covenants are equitable, not legal."},{"t":"unregistered-land","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Uri is the sole legal owner of an unregistered house. His wife Vera contributed to the purchase price and has a beneficial interest. Uri mortgages the house to Wexford Bank, telling it he is single. The bank's valuer visits and sees women's clothes in the wardrobes and children's toys, and Uri says he is separated. The bank makes no further inquiries. Vera still lives there.\n\nIs Wexford Bank bound by Vera's interest?","o":["No, because Vera did not register her interest as a land charge.","No, because the loan money was paid to the legal owner, so her interest was overreached.","No, because she can protect her right to occupy only by registering a Class F land charge.","Yes, because beneficial interests under a trust always bind a lender of unregistered land.","Yes, because the bank had constructive notice of her interest, imputed to it through its valuer, and the money was paid to only one trustee."],"a":4,"e":"Beneficial interests under a trust are **not land charges**; they are **overreached** only if money is paid to **two trustees**, and here there was one, so the **doctrine of notice** applies. The signs seen by the bank's agent put it on inquiry, so it had **constructive notice imputed** through its valuer and is bound (Kingsnorth Finance v Tizard). A Class F charge protects statutory home rights, not a beneficial interest, and the interest would not bind a bona fide lender without notice, so it does not 'always' bind."},{"t":"unregistered-land","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Xena owns an unregistered freehold, with her title deeds held by Yew Bank under a first legal mortgage. She later grants a second legal mortgage by deed to Zest Loans, which does not take the deeds and does not register anything at the Land Charges Department. Xena then sells the house to Abe for its full value, and the sale proceeds pay off only Yew Bank.\n\nIs Abe bound by Zest Loans' mortgage?","o":["Yes, because it is a legal interest, and legal interests in unregistered land bind the world.","Yes, because it was created by deed and Abe could have discovered it by asking Xena.","No, but only if Abe did not know about the second mortgage when he bought.","No, because a legal mortgage not protected by deposit of deeds is a puisne mortgage, which is void against a purchaser for value unless registered as a Class C(i) land charge.","Yes, because Zest Loans' mortgage takes effect in equity and binds anyone except a purchaser without notice."],"a":3,"e":"Legal interests in unregistered land usually bind everyone, but the **puisne mortgage** (a legal mortgage **not protected by deposit of the title deeds**) is the exception: it must be registered as a **Class C(i)** land charge. If not, it is **void against a purchaser for value** of any interest in the land (**LCA 1972 s.4(5)**), and **actual knowledge is irrelevant**. So Abe takes free, whatever he knew, and the doctrine of notice does not apply to a registrable land charge."},{"t":"co-ownership","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Kwame and Lola are the registered proprietors of a house and hold it as beneficial joint tenants. After separating, they agree orally that Lola will buy Kwame's interest for £80,000, but before anything is put in writing Kwame is killed in an accident. His will leaves everything to his sister Mira.\n\nWho is entitled to the beneficial interest in the house?","o":["Lola and Mira equally, because the oral agreement severed the joint tenancy and Kwame's half share passed under his will.","Lola alone, because the agreement was not in writing and so could not sever the joint tenancy.","Lola alone, because a joint tenant's interest cannot be left by will.","Mira alone, because Kwame had agreed to sell and the price had not been paid.","Lola and Mira in shares reflecting what each of Lola and Kwame contributed to the purchase price."],"a":0,"e":"A beneficial joint tenancy can be severed by **mutual agreement** between all the joint tenants, and the agreement need **not** be a binding contract complying with s.2 LP(MP)A 1989 (Burgess v Rawnsley). On severance each takes an **equal** share, regardless of contributions, so Kwame's half became a tenancy in common share that passed under his will to Mira. It is true that a will alone cannot sever, but here severance happened before Kwame died."},{"t":"co-ownership","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Nina and Omar bought a house as beneficial tenants in common in equal shares and live there with their 12-year-old son. A creditor of Omar has obtained a charging order over Omar's beneficial share and has applied to court for an order for sale. Omar is not bankrupt. Nina opposes a sale.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court will decide the application?","o":["It must order a sale, because a secured creditor's interests outweigh all other considerations.","It must order a sale unless the circumstances are exceptional, because more than a year has passed since the charging order.","It cannot order a sale, because the creditor is not a trustee or beneficiary of the trust of land.","It will apply the factors in s.15 of the Trusts of Land and Appointment of Trustees Act 1996, including the son's welfare and the creditor's interests, and has a discretion whether to order a sale.","It will refuse a sale, because the house is still being used as a family home for a child under 18."],"a":3,"e":"A person with an interest in the property, including a **secured creditor** of a beneficiary, can apply under **s.14 TLATA 1996**, and the court weighs the **s.15** factors: the intentions of the creators, the purposes of the trust, the **welfare of any minor** in occupation and the **interests of secured creditors**. No factor automatically wins (Mortgage Corporation v Shaire), so options saying the court must or cannot order a sale are wrong. The 'exceptional circumstances' test belongs to **s.335A Insolvency Act 1986**, which applies only on **bankruptcy**."},{"t":"co-ownership","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Pam sold her flat and used the proceeds to pay a third of the price of a house bought in the names of her daughter Rhea and son-in-law Sid as registered proprietors. All three moved in. Rhea and Sid later mortgaged the house to Tor Bank, which paid the loan to both of them, without telling Pam. They have defaulted, and Tor Bank seeks possession.\n\nIs Tor Bank bound by Pam's beneficial interest?","o":["No, because the loan was paid to two trustees, so Pam's interest was overreached and attaches to the money instead.","Yes, because Pam was in actual occupation when the mortgage was made, so her interest overrides.","Yes, because Pam contributed to the purchase price and so has a beneficial interest under a resulting trust.","No, because Pam's interest was not protected by a notice on the register.","Yes, because a lender is bound by the interest of any occupier aged 17 or over who has not signed a consent."],"a":0,"e":"When capital money is paid to **at least two trustees**, beneficial interests are **overreached** (**LPA 1925 ss.2 and 27**): they are detached from the land and attach to the money, **even if the beneficiary is in actual occupation** (City of London BS v Flegg). Pam does have a beneficial interest, but overreaching means there is nothing left to override. Trust interests cannot be protected by notice in any event, and occupiers' consent forms are a lender's practice, not a rule that binds it."},{"t":"easements","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Uma sells a house to Victor. By the transfer deed she grants him the right to park up to four cars at any time on a strip of her retained land, which is just large enough for four cars. The right is properly registered. Uma's buyer of the retained land now says that Victor's right is not a valid easement.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the validity of Victor's right?","o":["It is a valid easement, because it was granted expressly by deed and registered.","It is probably not a valid easement, because it leaves the servient owner without any reasonable use of the strip.","It is a valid easement, because rights to park can always exist as easements.","It is not a valid easement, because a right to park can never accommodate the dominant land.","It is a valid easement, because the servient owner still owns the strip and can sell it."],"a":1,"e":"A right cannot be an easement if it effectively **ousts** the servient owner from possession or reasonable use of its land; a right to fill a small area with cars at all times leaves the owner with **no reasonable use** of it (Batchelor v Marlow). Express grant and registration cannot turn a right incapable of being an easement into one. Parking rights can be easements (e.g. one allocated space where the owner keeps control), so 'always' and 'never' are both wrong."},{"t":"easements","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"For 22 years Wyn has driven across his neighbour Xavi's yard, openly and without objection, to reach his garage. In the fifth year, Xavi wrote to Wyn: 'You may use my yard until I say otherwise.' Wyn carried on using it. Xavi has now sold to Yolanda, who has blocked the yard.\n\nDoes Wyn have an easement by prescription?","o":["Yes, because he has used the yard openly and continuously for more than 20 years.","Yes, because Xavi only tolerated the use and never objected to it.","No, because since Xavi's letter the use has been by permission, so there have not been 20 years' use as of right.","No, because a prescriptive easement can only arise from use for at least 40 years.","Yes, under the doctrine of lost modern grant, because permission does not matter under that method."],"a":2,"e":"Prescription requires **20 years' continuous use as of right**: without force, without secrecy and **without permission**. Once Xavi gave **permission** in writing, Wyn's use was no longer as of right, leaving only about 4 years of qualifying use, so no method of prescription succeeds. Mere toleration does not defeat prescription, but express permission does, and 20 years (not 40) is the general period."},{"t":"easements","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Zainab owned a large plot with a house at the front and a paddock at the back. She sold the paddock to Ade and kept the house; the transfer said nothing about rights of way. For years she had reached the back of her house along a surfaced track across the paddock, although she can also reach it, less conveniently, through her front garden. Ade has blocked the track.\n\nDoes Zainab have an implied right of way over the track?","o":["Yes, because the track was a continuous and apparent quasi-easement, necessary for reasonable enjoyment and in use at the time of the sale.","Yes, under s.62 of the Law of Property Act 1925, because the track was visible and in use when the paddock was conveyed.","No, because the quasi-easement rule and s.62 operate only in favour of a buyer, and there is no necessity because she has other access.","Yes, by necessity, because the track is the most convenient route to the back of the house.","Yes, because the track was used for more than 20 years before the sale."],"a":2,"e":"Zainab is the **seller** claiming a right over the land she sold, so she needs an implied **reservation**; the **quasi-easement rule** (Wheeldon v Burrows) and **s.62** imply easements only in favour of the **buyer** (grant). **Necessity** needs the retained land to be **unusable** without the right, and mere inconvenience is not enough when another access exists. Her own use before the sale was over her own land, so it could not count towards prescription."},{"t":"covenants","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Houses on the Birch Lane estate were sold in 2001 with a right for each owner to use the private estate road and a covenant by each buyer to contribute a fair share of its maintenance costs. Carys bought one of the houses in 2024 from the original buyer and gave no covenant herself. She drives along the estate road every day but refuses to pay towards resurfacing it.\n\nCan Carys be required to contribute?","o":["No, because the burden of a positive covenant never binds a successor in title, whatever the circumstances.","Yes, because the covenant was made on behalf of the buyer and her successors under s.79 of the Law of Property Act 1925.","Yes, because the burden of the covenant runs in equity once it is protected on the register.","No, because she did not give an indemnity covenant when she bought.","Yes, because she exercises a right under the deed linked to the payment, so she must accept the related burden for as long as she uses the road."],"a":4,"e":"The burden of a positive covenant does not run, but under the **mutual benefit and burden** principle a successor who **chooses to take a benefit** under the deed must accept a burden **linked to it** (Halsall v Brizell); paying for the road she uses is a reciprocal burden. Section 79 and registration do not make the burden of a **positive** covenant run in equity. The lack of an indemnity covenant affects only the chain-of-indemnity workaround, not this principle."},{"t":"covenants","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"In 2010 Dafydd sold part of his land to Eira, who covenanted not to build on it. The deed identified Dafydd's retained land but stated that the benefit of the covenant 'shall not pass to any successor in title of the covenantee unless expressly assigned'. In 2020 Dafydd sold the retained land to Ffion without assigning the benefit. Eira has since sold her land to Gethin, who is starting to build. The covenant is protected by a notice.\n\nCan Ffion enforce the covenant against Gethin?","o":["Yes, because s.78 of the Law of Property Act 1925 annexes the benefit to every part of the retained land automatically.","Yes, because the covenant is restrictive, touches and concerns the land and is protected by a notice.","Yes, because Ffion owns the land that the covenant was intended to benefit.","No, because the burden of a restrictive covenant cannot pass to a successor of the covenantor.","No, because the deed excluded automatic annexation and the benefit was not expressly assigned to her."],"a":4,"e":"Against a successor of the covenantor, the **benefit** must pass **in equity**, by annexation, assignment or a building scheme. **Statutory annexation under s.78 can be excluded** by express words (Roake v Chadha), and this deed did so, so without an **express assignment** Ffion has no benefit to enforce. The burden has run to Gethin under the equitable rules, but that is only half the test."},{"t":"covenants","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Hywel owns land with the benefit of a restrictive covenant against building on the neighbouring plot. A developer has built and sold three houses on that plot in breach of the covenant, and families now live in them. Hywel objected only after the houses were finished. He asks what he is most likely to recover.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the most likely outcome?","o":["The court must grant a mandatory injunction ordering the houses to be demolished, because the covenant has been breached.","The court is likely to refuse to order demolition and instead award damages in lieu, measured by what Hywel could reasonably have demanded for releasing the covenant.","Hywel can recover only nominal damages, because his land has not fallen in value.","Hywel can apply to the Upper Tribunal (Lands Chamber) for an order that the houses be demolished.","Hywel has no remedy, because a restrictive covenant can be enforced only before building work starts."],"a":1,"e":"Remedies against a successor are **equitable and discretionary**; a **mandatory injunction** to pull down finished, occupied houses is granted reluctantly, especially where the claimant delayed. The court can award **damages in lieu** (Senior Courts Act 1981 s.50), commonly assessed as a reasonable **release fee** (Wrotham Park Estate v Parkside Homes). The Upper Tribunal discharges or modifies covenants and does not order demolition, and the covenant is not unenforceable merely because building has finished."},{"t":"mortgages","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Iona's house is subject to a legal charge by deed in favour of Juniper Bank. The legal date for redemption has passed. Iona is one month behind with her interest payments and has breached no other term. The bank has not served a notice requiring repayment, and the mortgage does not vary s.103 of the Law of Property Act 1925. The bank has sold the house to Kai.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Kai's position?","o":["Kai obtains good title, because the power of sale had arisen, and Iona's remedy is damages against the bank.","Kai's purchase is void, because the power of sale had not become exercisable.","Kai takes the house subject to Iona's equity of redemption.","Kai obtains good title only if he made inquiries showing that the power of sale had become exercisable.","Kai's purchase is valid only if the bank first obtained a court order for sale."],"a":0,"e":"The power of sale **arises** once the mortgage money is due (the legal redemption date has passed) (**s.101**), but becomes **exercisable** only on one of the **s.103** events (3 months after a notice, **2 months' interest arrears**, or another breach), none of which applies. Under **s.104** a buyer need only check that the power has **arisen**; it gets good title, and the borrower's remedy is **damages** against the lender. No court order is needed for a statutory sale, and the sale overreaches the borrower's interest."},{"t":"mortgages","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Larkspur Ltd borrows £2 million from Mere Finance Ltd, secured by a legal charge over its warehouse. The charge, negotiated between the two companies at the same time as the loan, also grants Mere Finance an option to buy the warehouse at a fixed price at any time in the next ten years.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the option?","o":["It is valid, because both parties are commercial companies of equal bargaining power.","It is void, because an option for the lender to buy the mortgaged property granted as part of the mortgage is a clog on the equity of redemption.","It is valid, because collateral advantages in a mortgage are always enforceable if freely negotiated.","It is void only if the fixed price is below the market value of the warehouse.","It is valid until the loan is repaid, and ends automatically on redemption."],"a":1,"e":"Equity strikes down any term that is a **clog or fetter on the equity of redemption**; an **option for the lender to purchase** the mortgaged property granted **as part of the mortgage transaction** is void, because the borrower might never get its property back (Samuel v Jarrah Timber). Equality of bargaining power can save a postponement of redemption or a collateral advantage, but not an option of this kind. The position would differ only if the option were granted later in a separate, independent transaction."},{"t":"mortgages","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Nell is the registered proprietor of a house. On 1 March she grants a legal charge to Oak Bank, and on 3 March a second legal charge to Pine Loans. Neither lender made an official search with priority. Pine Loans applies to register its charge on 5 March; Oak Bank applies on 10 March. Both charges are now registered.\n\nWhich charge has priority?","o":["Oak Bank's charge, because it was created first.","Oak Bank's charge, because a first legal charge always ranks before later charges.","Pine Loans' charge, but only if it did not know of Oak Bank's charge.","The charges rank equally, because both were registered before either lender sought to enforce.","Pine Loans' charge, because registered charges rank in the order in which they are entered on the register."],"a":4,"e":"Under **LRA 2002 s.48**, registered charges rank in the **order in which they are entered on the register**, not the order of creation. Oak Bank could have protected itself with an **OS1** search giving a **30-working-day priority period**, but it did not. Knowledge is irrelevant to priority under the LRA 2002."},{"t":"leases-nature","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"In 2015 Quarry Estates let a yard to Alder Ltd 'until Quarry Estates requires the land for a car park', at a rent of £1,000 a month, which Alder has paid monthly ever since. Quarry Estates now wants Alder to leave, although it has no plans for a car park.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Alder Ltd's position?","o":["The term is uncertain, so it cannot take effect as agreed, but Alder has a monthly periodic tenancy which can be ended by proper notice.","Alder has a valid lease that lasts until Quarry Estates genuinely needs the land for a car park.","Alder has a lease for 90 years, because a lease for an uncertain term is converted into a 90-year lease.","Alder is a licensee who can be required to leave immediately, because the arrangement is void.","Alder has a tenancy at will, which ends automatically when the landlord changes its plans."],"a":0,"e":"The **maximum duration** of a lease must be **certain** at the start, so a term ending when the land is 'required' fails (Prudential Assurance v London Residuary Body). Because Alder went into possession paying a **periodic rent**, a **monthly periodic tenancy** is implied, which can be ended by notice (subject to any statutory protection). Conversion into a 90-year lease under s.149(6) applies to leases for life granted to individuals, not to a company."},{"t":"leases-nature","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Bryn is employed as the live-in caretaker of a block of flats. His contract requires him to live in a ground floor flat in the block so that he can deal with emergencies at night. He has the only key to the flat and pays a reduced rent deducted from his wages. His employment has ended, and the employer wants the flat back.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Bryn's occupation?","o":["He is a licensee, because he is a service occupier required to live there for the better performance of his duties.","He is a tenant, because he has exclusive possession for a periodic term at a rent.","He is a tenant, because the label given to the arrangement cannot change its legal effect.","He is a licensee, because he pays less than a market rent.","He is a tenant at sufferance, because his right to occupy depended on his employment."],"a":0,"e":"Exclusive possession at a rent usually creates a tenancy (Street v Mountford), but there is an exception for a **service occupier**: an employee **required** to live in the property **for the better performance of his duties** is a **licensee**. Paying less than a market rent does not of itself prevent a tenancy, because rent is not even essential. A tenancy at sufferance arises when a tenant holds over after a lease ends, which is not this case."},{"t":"leases-nature","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Coral holds the registered freehold of a shop. In 2023 she granted Dune Ltd a six-year lease of the shop by deed. Nothing about the lease was entered on Coral's title. Dune Ltd has closed the shop temporarily for refurbishment and nobody is occupying it. Coral has now sold and transferred the freehold to Elm Ltd, which has registered.\n\nIs Elm Ltd bound by Dune Ltd's lease?","o":["No, because the lease was not protected by a notice on Coral's title.","No, because Dune Ltd was not in actual occupation at the date of the transfer.","No, because a lease of more than three years must be registered with its own title to be legal.","Yes, because a legal lease granted for seven years or less overrides a registered disposition whether or not the tenant is in occupation.","Yes, but only because Elm Ltd could have discovered the lease by inspecting the shop."],"a":3,"e":"A lease of more than 3 years needs a **deed** to be legal, and a lease of **7 years or less** does not need registration, so Dune Ltd has a **legal** lease. Under **LRA 2002 Sch 3 para 1**, a legal lease granted for **7 years or less** overrides, with **no requirement of occupation** or of discoverability. Leases must be registered with their own title only when they are granted for more than 7 years."},{"t":"leases-covenants","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"In 2012 Fern Ltd took a 20-year lease of an office containing a covenant not to assign without the landlord's consent. In 2021 Fern assigned the lease to Gorse Ltd without asking for consent. Gorse has now failed to pay two quarters' rent. The landlord served notices under s.17 of the Landlord and Tenant (Covenants) Act 1995 on Fern within a month of each payment falling due.\n\nCan the landlord recover the arrears from Fern Ltd?","o":["No, because the lease was granted after 1995, so Fern was automatically released when it assigned.","No, because the assignment was in breach of covenant and so did not transfer the lease to Gorse.","Yes, because an original tenant remains liable on its covenants for the whole term of any lease.","Yes, because an assignment in breach of covenant is an excluded assignment, so Fern was not released from its covenants.","No, because Fern did not enter into an authorised guarantee agreement."],"a":3,"e":"For a **new lease** (granted on or after 1 January 1996) the outgoing tenant is released only on a **lawful** assignment (**LTCA 1995 s.5**); an assignment **in breach of covenant** is an **excluded assignment** (**s.11**), so Fern stays liable until the next lawful assignment. The assignment is still effective to vest the lease in Gorse, but it is a breach. Liability for the whole term applies to old leases, and Fern's liability here does not depend on an AGA; the s.17 notices were served in time."},{"t":"leases-covenants","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"In 2015 Holly Properties granted Iris Ltd a 15-year lease of a shop, with a landlord's covenant to repair the structure. In 2024 Holly sold the freehold reversion to Juno Ltd. Holly did not serve any notice on Iris about the sale. The roof has leaked since early 2025, and the structure is now in serious disrepair. Juno has become insolvent.\n\nCan Iris Ltd sue Holly Properties for breach of the repairing covenant?","o":["No, because the landlord's covenants passed to Juno, and Holly was automatically released on the sale.","Yes, because a landlord is not automatically released when it sells the reversion, and Holly did not apply to be released.","No, because Iris can sue only the landlord for the time being under privity of estate.","Yes, but only if the lease expressly made the repairing covenant personal to Holly.","No, because a landlord's covenant to repair does not touch and concern the land."],"a":1,"e":"Under the **LTCA 1995**, a landlord who sells the reversion is **not released automatically**; it must serve a notice before or within **4 weeks** of the assignment and is released only if the tenant does not object or the court declares release reasonable (**ss.6-8**). Holly served no notice, so it remains liable alongside Juno. A covenant expressed to be personal would not pass to Juno at all, and repair plainly relates to the property."},{"t":"leases-covenants","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Kestrel Ltd holds a 15-year lease of an industrial unit granted in 2016. The lease contains a qualified covenant against assignment and states that the landlord may refuse consent to an assignment if the proposed assignee's net profits are less than three times the annual rent. Kestrel asks for consent to assign to Lynx Ltd, a sound business whose profits are twice the rent. The landlord refuses in writing within a week, relying on that clause.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the landlord's refusal?","o":["It is unreasonable, because Lynx Ltd is financially sound and the landlord suffers no real harm.","It is unreasonable, because any condition attached to consent must satisfy the general test of reasonableness.","It is not unreasonable, because the landlord relied on a circumstance agreed in advance under s.19(1A) of the Landlord and Tenant Act 1927.","It is not unreasonable, because the covenant is absolute and the landlord may refuse for any reason.","It is unreasonable, because the landlord did not offer to consent subject to a rent deposit."],"a":2,"e":"For **qualifying (non-residential) leases granted on or after 1 January 1996**, the parties may agree in advance the **circumstances** in which consent to **assignment** may be withheld (**LTA 1927 s.19(1A)**); a refusal on those grounds is **not unreasonable**. The general reasonableness test under s.19(1)(a) is displaced to that extent, so arguments based on Lynx's soundness fail. The covenant is qualified, not absolute, and the landlord need not offer alternative security."},{"t":"leases-remedies","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Marsh Ltd's lease of a shop contains a qualified covenant against subletting and a right of re-entry. Marsh sublet the whole shop to Nettle Ltd without consent. The landlord's managing agent learned of the subletting in May. In June the agent sent Marsh the usual quarterly rent demand, marked 'without prejudice', and Marsh paid it.\n\nCan the landlord now forfeit for the unlawful subletting?","o":["Yes, because the demand was marked 'without prejudice', which prevents it from operating as a waiver.","Yes, because subletting without consent is a continuing breach, so a new right to forfeit arises every day.","Yes, because knowledge of the managing agent is not treated as the landlord's knowledge.","No, because demanding rent with knowledge of the breach waived the right to forfeit, and subletting is a once-and-for-all breach, so the waiver is permanent.","No, because a landlord can never forfeit a lease for breach of a covenant against subletting."],"a":3,"e":"A landlord **waives** the right to forfeit if, **knowing of the breach** (including through its **agents**), it does an unequivocal act treating the lease as continuing, such as **demanding or accepting rent** due after the breach; marking the demand 'without prejudice' does **not** prevent waiver. An unlawful subletting is a **once-and-for-all** breach, so once waived the right to forfeit for it is lost **permanently**, unlike a continuing breach such as disrepair. Waiver does not stop the landlord claiming damages."},{"t":"leases-remedies","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Olive Ltd's commercial lease contains a right of re-entry for non-payment of rent. Olive owes £18,000 of rent, and the landlord has started forfeiture proceedings for possession in the county court. Eight clear days before the hearing, Olive pays into court all the arrears and the landlord's costs.\n\nWhat is the effect of the payment?","o":["The proceedings end and Olive continues to hold the premises under the lease.","The court must still order possession, but Olive can then apply for relief within six months.","The payment has no effect, because the landlord elected to forfeit when it served the claim.","The court has a discretion whether to grant relief, taking account of the tenant's past conduct.","The lease ends, but Olive is entitled to a new lease on the same terms."],"a":0,"e":"In the county court, if the tenant pays into court **all the arrears and the landlord's costs** at least **5 clear days before the hearing**, the claim ends and the tenant continues to hold under the lease (**County Courts Act 1984 s.138(2)**). The landlord's election to forfeit on service is subject to this statutory relief. Applying for relief within six months after possession is the fallback if the tenant pays later."},{"t":"leases-remedies","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Pebble Ltd has three years left on its lease of an office. Its directors post the keys through the landlord's letterbox with a note saying they are leaving. The landlord accepts the keys, changes the locks and lets the office to a new tenant. No deed is signed. The landlord now sues Pebble for rent for the remainder of the term.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Pebble Ltd's position?","o":["The lease has ended by surrender by operation of law, so Pebble is not liable for rent falling due after the surrender.","The lease continues, because an express surrender must be made by deed.","The lease continues, because the landlord did not sign anything accepting the surrender.","The lease has ended by merger, because the landlord has taken back possession.","The lease has been forfeited, so Pebble remains liable for all the rent for the rest of the term."],"a":0,"e":"A lease can be **surrendered by operation of law** where the parties' **unequivocal conduct** is inconsistent with it continuing, such as the tenant returning the keys and the landlord **accepting** them and **re-letting**; no deed is needed (**LPA 1925 s.52(2)(c)**). The deed requirement applies only to an **express** surrender. Merger needs the tenant to acquire the reversion, and there was no forfeiture for breach."}],"property":[{"t":"transaction-overview","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Amara, a solicitor, acts for Bruno on his cash purchase of a flat. Shortly before exchange, £250,000 arrives in the firm's client account from an overseas company Bruno has never mentioned. Bruno cannot explain the payment convincingly. Amara reports her suspicion to the firm's money laundering reporting officer (MLRO), who makes a report to the National Crime Agency. Bruno telephones to ask why exchange has been delayed.\n\nWhat should Amara do?","o":["Tell Bruno that a report has been made, so that he can provide evidence to clear up the suspicion quickly.","Exchange contracts as planned, because the matter has now been reported to the MLRO.","Return the £250,000 to the overseas company and close the file without telling anyone.","Tell Bruno only that there is a delay, without revealing that a report has been made, and not proceed until the MLRO confirms she may do so.","Ask Bruno to explain the source of the money, warning him that otherwise the firm may need to report it."],"a":3,"e":"Telling the client, or anyone, that a report has been made where that is likely to prejudice an investigation is **tipping off** (**POCA 2002 s.333A**), so Amara must not reveal the report or warn Bruno that one may be made. She must also not proceed with the transaction until **consent** is obtained, or she risks the **arranging** offence (s.328). Returning the money without authority could itself be dealing with criminal property."},{"t":"transaction-overview","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Carys, a solicitor, acts for Dev on the sale of his house. To make completion run smoothly, she gives the buyer's solicitor an unconditional undertaking to redeem Dev's mortgage to Eden Bank on completion. She did not obtain an up-to-date redemption statement. After completion it emerges that the redemption figure is £12,000 more than the net sale proceeds, and Dev has no other money.\n\nWhat is Carys's position?","o":["She is not liable, because the shortfall is Dev's debt and Eden Bank must recover it from him.","She is not liable, because an undertaking is binding only if it was given in writing on the firm's headed paper.","She is personally bound by the undertaking and must make good the shortfall so that the mortgage is redeemed.","She is released from the undertaking, because it was given on her client's instructions.","She is liable only if the buyer's solicitor can show that it relied on the undertaking to its loss."],"a":2,"e":"An undertaking is a statement that a solicitor **will do something**, on which the recipient reasonably relies, and it binds the solicitor **personally**; under **para 1.3 of the SRA Code** it must be performed. Because Carys gave it **unconditionally**, she must make good the shortfall; any condition (such as receipt of sufficient funds) had to be written in when it was given. Undertakings bind whether or not they are in writing, and the client's instructions are no excuse."},{"t":"transaction-overview","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Fen and Gail have each made an offer to buy the same commercial unit, and both ask Harrow LLP to act for them. The seller is not a client of the firm. Harrow LLP considers that it could keep each client's information separate by using different teams.\n\nCan Harrow LLP act for both Fen and Gail?","o":["Yes, provided the firm uses separate teams in different offices, without needing the clients' consent.","No, because a firm can never act for two clients whose interests conflict in the same matter.","Yes, provided both give informed consent in writing, effective safeguards protect their confidential information, and the firm is satisfied it is reasonable to act for both.","Yes, because Fen and Gail have a substantially common interest in buying the unit.","No, unless the seller also agrees in writing to the firm acting for both buyers."],"a":2,"e":"A firm must not act for two clients where there is a conflict or significant risk of one, unless an exception in **para 6.2 of the SRA Code** applies; competing buyers fall within the exception for clients **competing for the same objective**. All the conditions must then be met: **informed consent** of both, **given or evidenced in writing**, **effective safeguards** for confidential information, and the firm being **satisfied it is reasonable** to act. Two rival bidders do not have a substantially common interest, and the seller's consent is irrelevant."},{"t":"title","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Iris is buying an unregistered freehold house in November 2026. The seller's epitome of title lists: (1) a 1971 assent by personal representatives; (2) a 1988 conveyance on sale to the seller; (3) a 1993 legal mortgage, discharged in 2009; and (4) a 2015 deed by which the seller granted a neighbour a right of way over the garden.\n\nWhich document should Iris's solicitor expect to be the root of title?","o":["The 1988 conveyance on sale.","The 1971 assent, because it is the oldest document in the epitome.","The 1993 legal mortgage, because a mortgage deals with the whole legal and equitable interest.","The 2015 deed of grant, because it is the most recent document dealing with the property.","None of them, because a root of title must be at least 40 years old."],"a":0,"e":"A good root of title must deal with the **whole legal and equitable interest**, describe the land, cast no doubt on the title and be **at least 15 years old** (**LPA 1925 s.44**, as amended in 1969). The 1988 conveyance on sale is over 15 years old and is the strongest kind of root, because a buyer paying value would have checked title. A mortgage and the grant of an easement do not deal with the whole interest, an assent is a weaker root and is not needed when a later good root exists, and the period is no longer 40 or 30 years. The land is still unregistered because none of these deeds triggered **compulsory first registration**: a sale did so everywhere only from December 1990, and a first legal mortgage, assent or gift only from April 1998."},{"t":"title","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Joss is buying a house in which he plans to run a dental practice. The charges register contains a 1982 covenant not to use the property other than as a private dwelling. Joss's solicitor has already written to the neighbour who appears to have the benefit of the covenant, and the neighbour has replied that she objects to any business use.\n\nWhat is the best advice to Joss?","o":["Take out restrictive covenant indemnity insurance before exchange, so he can proceed with the business use.","Seek a negotiated release from the neighbour or consider an application to the Upper Tribunal to discharge or modify the covenant, as indemnity insurance is unlikely to be available.","Proceed, because covenants made more than 40 years ago are no longer enforceable.","Proceed, because the seller's full title guarantee will cover any claim for breach of the covenant.","Proceed, because the neighbour's remedy is limited to damages for a breach of a positive covenant."],"a":1,"e":"**Restrictive covenant indemnity insurance** is normally unavailable once the person with the benefit has been **approached or has objected**, so the realistic options are a **release** (by deed) or an application under **LPA 1925 s.84** to the **Upper Tribunal (Lands Chamber)**, or not proceeding. Age alone does not make a covenant unenforceable. A title guarantee does not cover incumbrances disclosed to the buyer, and this is a restrictive covenant, enforceable by injunction."},{"t":"title","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Kemal is buying a registered freehold house. The proprietorship register names Lowri and Mair as joint proprietors and contains no restriction. Mair died last year, and Lowri is selling as the only surviving proprietor.\n\nWhat should Kemal's solicitor require?","o":["A certified copy of Mair's death certificate, so that Lowri can sell alone as the survivor.","The appointment of a second trustee, so that the purchase price is paid to two trustees.","A grant of probate to Mair's estate, and the signature of her personal representatives on the transfer.","A court order authorising Lowri to sell, because one of the proprietors has died.","A statutory declaration from Lowri that Mair left no will."],"a":0,"e":"The absence of a **Form A restriction** indicates that the joint proprietors held as **beneficial joint tenants**, so on Mair's death the whole legal and beneficial interest passed to Lowri by **survivorship**; she can sell alone, and the buyer needs only evidence of death to remove Mair from the register. Appointing a second trustee is needed where a Form A restriction shows a **tenancy in common**. Mair's estate has no interest in the house, so her personal representatives and will are irrelevant."},{"t":"searches","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Nia is buying a 1930s semi-detached house in a former colliery village in South Yorkshire. Her solicitor has ordered a local authority search and a drainage and water search.\n\nWhich further search is the solicitor most clearly required to make?","o":["A chancel repair search.","An index map search (SIM) at HM Land Registry.","A coal mining search (CON29M) made to the Mining Remediation Authority.","A commons registration enquiry on form CON29O.","A company search at Companies House."],"a":2,"e":"Where a property is in a **coal mining area**, the buyer should make a **CON29M** search to the **Mining Remediation Authority** (formerly the Coal Authority), which reveals past workings, mine entries and **subsidence** claims. An index map search is for **unregistered** land, a commons enquiry for land next to a green or common, and a company search where a party is a company. Nothing in the facts points to chancel repair liability."},{"t":"searches","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Owen bought a house after receiving a clear official local land charges search. Since completion, the council has told him that a tree preservation order affecting an oak in his garden was made before his purchase but, through the council's error, was never entered in the local land charges register. Owen wanted to fell the tree to build a garage.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Owen's position?","o":["He is bound by the tree preservation order, but he may claim compensation for his loss because the charge was not registered when he searched.","He is not bound by the tree preservation order, because it was not registered when he searched.","He is not bound, because he can rely on the seller's full title guarantee.","He is bound and has no remedy, because the search result is not guaranteed.","He is bound, but he may recover his loss from the seller for misrepresentation."],"a":0,"e":"A local land charge **binds a buyer even if it was not registered**, but a buyer who made an official search and suffers loss because the charge was not registered can claim **compensation** (**Local Land Charges Act 1975 s.10**). A title guarantee does not cover public law restrictions of this kind. Nothing suggests the seller made any untrue statement, so misrepresentation is not the obvious remedy."},{"t":"finance-lender","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Priya's solicitor acts for her and for her lender, Quay Bank, on her house purchase. Contracts were exchanged today, with completion fixed for 14 working days' time. Part 2 of the UK Finance Mortgage Lenders' Handbook states that Quay Bank needs the certificate of title at least 5 working days before completion.\n\nWhen should the solicitor send the certificate of title to Quay Bank?","o":["Before exchange, as soon as the mortgage offer is received.","On the morning of completion, once the seller's solicitor confirms it is ready.","After completion, with the application to register the charge.","At any time before completion, because the lender's notice period is only guidance.","After exchange, and no later than 5 working days before completion, so that the advance arrives before completion."],"a":4,"e":"The **certificate of title** confirms to the lender that the title is good and **requests the advance**, so it is sent **after exchange** (when the completion date is fixed and title investigation is finished) and **before completion**, within the lender's **Part 2** notice period. Sending it before exchange is premature, and on or after completion is too late for the funds to arrive. The Part 2 requirements are instructions the solicitor must follow, not guidance."},{"t":"finance-lender","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Rhian is buying a house with a mortgage from Sorrel Building Society, which follows the UK Finance Mortgage Lenders' Handbook. Her mother is giving her £40,000 towards the deposit and will move in with Rhian after completion. Rhian's solicitor acts for both Rhian and the lender.\n\nWhich of the following is the solicitor's best course of action?","o":["Say nothing to the lender, because a gift from a parent is a private family matter.","Tell the mother that she will acquire a share of the house in proportion to her gift.","Advise Rhian to take a larger mortgage so that the gift is not needed.","Tell the lender about the gift and obtain a letter from the mother confirming it is a gift with no interest in the property, together with her signed occupier's consent.","Register a restriction protecting the mother's interest after completion."],"a":3,"e":"A lender must be told about a deposit coming from a **third party**, and it will usually require a **gifted deposit letter** confirming the money is a gift and the giver has **no interest** in the property. An adult (aged **17 or over**) who will live there but is not a borrower must sign an **occupier's consent** postponing any rights to the lender. A solicitor must not give advice on which mortgage to take unless authorised by the FCA, and protecting an interest for the mother contradicts the basis of the gift."},{"t":"contract","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Tomos agreed to buy Una's house for £300,000 under the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition, 2018 revision). By agreement, he paid a deposit of only £15,000 on exchange, and there is no special condition about the deposit. Tomos failed to complete on the completion date, and Una, who is ready, able and willing to complete, has served a notice to complete.\n\nWhat must Tomos now do about the deposit?","o":["Pay a further £15,000 immediately, so that the deposit amounts to 10% of the price.","Nothing, because the deposit was fixed at £15,000 by agreement on exchange.","Pay a further £15,000, but only if he fails to complete within the 10 working days.","Pay a further £30,000, because the deposit must be doubled on a notice to complete.","Nothing, because Una can recover only her actual loss, not a larger deposit."],"a":0,"e":"Under the SCS a buyer who has paid **less than 10%** must, on receiving a **notice to complete**, **immediately** pay the balance of a **10%** deposit (**SC 6.8.3**). Here that is a further £15,000, making £30,000 (10% of £300,000), so that the full 10% is at stake if Tomos does not complete. The obligation arises on service of the notice, not later, and there is no doubling rule."},{"t":"contract","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Vale Ltd exchanged contracts to buy a warehouse from Wick Ltd on the Standard Commercial Property Conditions (3rd edition, 2018 revision), with no special conditions about risk or insurance. A week later, before completion, the warehouse was badly damaged by a flood. Wick Ltd had let its insurance lapse on exchange, and Vale Ltd had not insured.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Vale Ltd's position?","o":["Vale may rescind the contract, because the property has been damaged and is now unusable.","Vale must complete and pay the full price, because the property has been at its risk since exchange and Wick was not obliged to insure.","Vale must complete, but the price is reduced by the cost of repairing the flood damage.","Vale must complete, but it is entitled to any insurance money that Wick would have received.","Vale need not complete until Wick has repaired the damage, because the seller bears the risk until completion."],"a":1,"e":"Under **SCPC 8.1** the property is **at the risk of the buyer from the date of the contract**, and the seller **need not insure** unless the contract requires it (SCPC 8.2), so Vale must complete at the full price. The right to rescind for damage was in the old SCS 4th edition and is not the current rule. There is no price reduction for damage, and the buyer's statutory right to the seller's insurance money is excluded in any event."},{"t":"contract","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Yvonne is buying a house from the personal representatives of the late owner. The contract provides that the personal representatives will transfer with limited title guarantee. Yvonne asks what protection this gives her about third-party rights affecting the property.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the covenant implied by limited title guarantee?","o":["That the property is free from all charges and third-party rights other than those the sellers do not know about and could not reasonably know about.","That the property is free from all third-party rights of every kind, whether or not the sellers know of them.","That the sellers have not themselves charged or incumbered the property and are not aware that anyone else has done so since the last disposition for value.","That the sellers give no promise at all about the title, because limited title guarantee excludes all implied covenants.","That the sellers guarantee the physical condition of the property at the date of completion."],"a":2,"e":"With **limited title guarantee** the seller covenants that it has **not itself charged or incumbered** the property and is **not aware** that anyone else has done so **since the last disposition for value** (**LP(MP)A 1994 s.3(3)**). The wider covenant, freedom from incumbrances other than those the seller does not and could not reasonably know about, is implied by **full** title guarantee. Limited title guarantee still implies covenants (including the right to dispose), and no title guarantee covers physical condition."},{"t":"exchange","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Zara's solicitor acts for her on her purchase of a house. A week ago he sent the contract signed by Zara to the seller's solicitor, to be held to his order pending exchange. The deposit is ready in the client account. Both solicitors now agree on the telephone to exchange contracts.\n\nWhich Law Society formula for exchange of contracts are they using?","o":["Formula B, because each solicitor holds its own client's signed part of the contract.","Formula C, because the parties are exchanging by telephone.","Formula A, because the seller's solicitor holds both signed parts of the contract.","Formula B, because the buyer's solicitor will send the deposit on the day of exchange.","Formula C, because the seller's solicitor will send the seller's part to the buyer's solicitor."],"a":2,"e":"**Formula A** is used where **one solicitor**, usually the seller's, **holds both signed parts**; on the call that solicitor confirms it holds both, inserts the completion date and undertakes to send the seller's part to the buyer's solicitor that day. **Formula B** applies where each solicitor holds its own client's part. **Formula C** is the two-stage procedure for **chains**, with a release deadline, not simply any telephone exchange."},{"t":"exchange","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Ash exchanged contracts to sell his flat to Beth on the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition, 2018 revision), with completion fixed for four weeks later. Ash died suddenly a week after exchange. Beth still wants to buy the flat.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of Ash's death on the contract?","o":["The contract is automatically ended by Ash's death, and the deposit must be returned to Beth.","The contract becomes voidable, and Beth may choose whether to continue with it.","The contract is suspended until probate is granted, and Beth cannot claim for any delay.","The contract remains binding, but only if Ash had made a will appointing executors.","The contract remains binding, and Ash's personal representatives must complete the sale."],"a":4,"e":"The death of a party after exchange does **not** end the contract: the deceased's **personal representatives** are bound to complete it, although there may be practical delay while they obtain a grant. If they fail to complete on time, Beth can serve a **notice to complete** and claim compensation. The obligation does not depend on a will, since administrators are equally bound."},{"t":"pre-completion","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Cai is buying a leasehold flat. The seller paid the service charge of £2,920 for the year 1 January to 31 December 2026 in advance. The contract incorporates the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition, 2018 revision), and completion takes place on 21 October 2026. Assume the charge accrues at an equal daily rate over a 365-day year.\n\nHow will the service charge be dealt with on the completion statement?","o":["Cai will pay the seller an extra £576.","Cai will pay the seller an extra £568.","Cai will receive a credit of £568, reducing the amount he pays.","Cai will pay the seller an extra £2,352.","Cai will pay the seller an extra £560."],"a":1,"e":"Under **SC 6.3** the seller is treated as owning the property for the **whole of the completion day**, and sums accrue at an **equal daily rate** (£2,920 ÷ 365 = £8 a day). The seller paid in advance for 22 October to 31 December, which is 71 days (10 + 30 + 31), so the buyer repays £568, which is **added** to the completion money. £576 wrongly includes 21 October, a credit gets the direction wrong, and £2,352 is the seller's own share for 1 January to 21 October."},{"t":"pre-completion","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Dilys is selling her house. Because she was abroad, she signed the TR1 transfer while her friend watched and signed as witness over a live video call. The buyer's solicitor has just received the signed transfer.\n\nWhat should the buyer's solicitor do?","o":["Ask for the transfer to be signed again in the physical presence of a witness, because HM Land Registry will not accept a deed witnessed remotely.","Accept it, because the witness is not a party to the transfer.","Accept it, because electronic and video signatures are always valid for deeds.","Accept it, provided Dilys's friend also signs a statutory declaration confirming she watched.","Ask Dilys to sign the transfer again with two witnesses, because remote witnessing requires two."],"a":0,"e":"An individual executes a deed by signing **in the presence of a witness** who attests the signature (**LP(MP)A 1989 s.1**), and HM Land Registry requires the witness to be **physically present**; **video witnessing is not accepted** for registration. The friend's independence does not cure the absence of physical presence, and neither a statutory declaration nor a second remote witness solves the problem."},{"t":"completion","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Efa's solicitor acts for her on her purchase of a house, and the seller's solicitor has agreed to complete under the Law Society Code for Completion by Post.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the seller's solicitor's role on completion under the Code?","o":["It acts for both the seller and Efa on completion, and may charge Efa a fee for doing so.","It acts only for the seller and owes Efa's solicitor no duties until the transfer is registered.","It acts as the agent of Efa's solicitor for completion, without charge, and must follow her solicitor's instructions.","It acts as stakeholder of the transfer deed until Efa's lender confirms the mortgage is registered.","It acts as agent of Efa's lender, and must send the completion money to the lender on completion."],"a":2,"e":"Under the **Code for Completion by Post**, the **seller's solicitor acts as the buyer's solicitor's agent** for completion, **without charge**, and must follow the buyer's solicitor's instructions (for example, checking documents and releasing keys). It confirms its authority to receive the money and gives **undertakings** to redeem the seller's mortgages. It does not act for both parties, and it charges no agency fee."},{"t":"completion","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Ffion has completed her purchase of a registered freehold house. Her solicitor sent the completion money this morning, received the dated transfer, and will apply to HM Land Registry tomorrow, well within the priority period of her OS1 search.\n\nWho holds the legal estate in the house now, before registration?","o":["Ffion, because the legal estate passed to her when the transfer was dated on completion.","The seller, who holds it on a bare trust for Ffion until she is registered.","Ffion, because the legal estate passed to her on exchange of contracts.","HM Land Registry, which holds it pending registration.","Ffion's lender, which holds it until its charge is registered."],"a":1,"e":"In registered land, a transfer is a registrable disposition that operates at law only **when it is registered** (**LRA 2002 s.27(1)**), so during the 'registration gap' the **seller** holds the legal title on a **bare trust** for the buyer, who owns it in equity. Ffion is protected meanwhile by her **OS1 priority period**. In unregistered land the legal estate passes on completion, which is the tempting but wrong analogy."},{"t":"delayed-completion","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Gwyn agreed to buy Huw's house for £500,000 under the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition, 2018 revision) and paid a £50,000 deposit. The contract rate is 7.3% a year. Huw was 3 days late in approving the draft transfer, and Gwyn completed 7 days after the completion date because his funds were delayed. Assume a 365-day year.\n\nWhat compensation is payable?","o":["Gwyn must pay Huw £400.","Gwyn must pay Huw £630.","Huw must pay Gwyn £300.","Gwyn must pay Huw £360.","No compensation is payable, because both parties were in default."],"a":3,"e":"Under **SC 7.2** the party whose **total period of default is greater** pays compensation for the period by which its default **exceeds** the other's: Gwyn's 7 days less Huw's 3 days is **4 days**. Where the **buyer** pays, compensation is at the **contract rate** on the price **less the deposit**: £450,000 × 7.3% × 4/365 = £360. £400 wrongly uses the full price, and £630 ignores the seller's default."},{"t":"delayed-completion","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Iolo Ltd agreed to buy an office building from Jet Ltd under the Standard Commercial Property Conditions (3rd edition, 2018 revision), with no special conditions about late completion. Jet Ltd completed 8 working days late because it had not cleared its own tenant out of the building in time. Iolo Ltd, which was ready to complete throughout, wants compensation at the contract rate for the delay.\n\nWhich of the following is the best advice to Iolo Ltd?","o":["Iolo Ltd can claim compensation at the contract rate on the full price for the 8 days.","Iolo Ltd can claim compensation at the contract rate on the price less the deposit for the 8 days.","Iolo Ltd has no claim at all, because time was not of the essence for the completion date.","Iolo Ltd can rescind the contract and recover its deposit, because the seller completed late.","Iolo Ltd cannot claim contractual compensation, because under the SCPC only a buyer who completes late pays it, but it can claim common law damages for losses it proves."],"a":4,"e":"Under **SCPC 10.3**, contractual compensation for late completion is payable **only by the buyer**; a late **seller** pays none, so the buyer must claim **common law damages** and prove its loss. Completing late is still a **breach of contract** even though time was not of the essence (Raineri v Miles), so the buyer is not left without a remedy. Rescission requires a notice to complete that has not been complied with, and the SCS rule on relative fault does not apply to the SCPC."},{"t":"lease-contents","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Kite Ltd's lease of an office contains a tenant's break clause, exercisable on 25 March 2027 on condition that 'all sums due under this lease have been paid on or before the break date'. Kite served a valid break notice, gave vacant possession and paid all its rent on time, but it overlooked an invoice for £40 of interest due under the lease on a payment made a few days late last year.\n\nWhat is the effect of the unpaid interest?","o":["The break is effective, because the unpaid sum is trivial and the landlord has suffered no real loss.","The break is effective, because only the principal rent counts as a sum due under a lease.","The break is effective, but Kite must pay the £40 with further interest after the break date.","The break is effective, because the landlord must give the tenant a reasonable opportunity to pay before relying on a breach of condition.","The break is likely to fail, because break conditions are strictly applied and not all sums due under the lease were paid."],"a":4,"e":"Conditions in a break clause must be **strictly complied with**; a condition that **all sums due** are paid can be failed by even a **small shortfall**, so the lease continues. That is why a tenant negotiating a lease should limit break conditions to paying the **principal rent** and giving **vacant possession**. There is no de minimis exception and no implied right to a reminder."},{"t":"lease-contents","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Lark Ltd holds a lease of a shop containing a covenant 'not to change the use of the premises without the landlord's consent'. Lark wants to change from selling clothes to running a café. The landlord says it will consent only if Lark pays it £10,000, and otherwise refuses.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the landlord's position?","o":["It cannot require a payment for consent, but it is not obliged to act reasonably in deciding whether to consent.","It may require the payment, because a landlord is free to set any condition on consent to a change of use.","It must consent, because statute implies that consent to a change of use is not to be unreasonably withheld.","It cannot require the payment, and it must consent unless refusal is reasonable.","It cannot refuse consent, because the covenant is only qualified and not absolute."],"a":0,"e":"For a **qualified user covenant**, **LTA 1927 s.19(3)** prevents the landlord from charging a **fine** (payment) for consent, but, unlike alienation and improvements, there is **no implied term** that consent is not to be **unreasonably withheld** unless the lease says so. So the landlord can refuse, but cannot demand £10,000. The reasonableness requirement is implied only for assignment (s.19(1)(a)) and improvements (s.19(2))."},{"t":"lease-dealings","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Moss Ltd is granting a 10-year lease of a shop to Nook Ltd, with Nook's director, Owain, acting as guarantor. The lease has been engrossed in two parts, a lease and a counterpart, ready for completion.\n\nWho should sign each part?","o":["Nook Ltd signs the lease, which goes to Moss Ltd; Moss Ltd signs the counterpart, which goes to Nook Ltd.","Moss Ltd, Nook Ltd and Owain all sign both parts, and each party keeps a copy.","Only Moss Ltd signs, because a lease is a grant by the landlord and needs no signature from the tenant or guarantor.","Moss Ltd signs the lease and Owain signs the counterpart; Nook Ltd need not sign because the guarantor is bound.","Moss Ltd signs the lease, which goes to Nook Ltd; Nook Ltd and Owain sign the counterpart, which goes to Moss Ltd."],"a":4,"e":"On the grant of a lease, the **original lease** is **signed by the landlord** and handed to the **tenant**, and the **counterpart** is **signed by the tenant** (and any **guarantor**) and handed to the **landlord**. So each side holds a part signed by the other, which it can enforce. The tenant gives covenants, so its signature is needed, and the guarantor's signature does not replace it."},{"t":"lease-dealings","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"In 1992 Pine Estates granted a 35-year lease of a warehouse to Quill Ltd. In 2005 Quill assigned the lease to Rook Ltd with Pine's consent; in the licence to assign, Rook covenanted directly with Pine to pay the rent and observe the covenants for the rest of the term. In 2015 Rook assigned to Slate Ltd. Slate has now stopped paying rent, and Pine has served notices under s.17 of the Landlord and Tenant (Covenants) Act 1995 on Quill and Rook within a month of each sum falling due.\n\nFrom whom can Pine claim the arrears?","o":["From Slate only, because Quill and Rook were released when they assigned.","From Slate and Quill only, because an assignee is liable only while it holds the lease.","From Slate and Rook only, because Quill's liability ended when it assigned with consent.","From Slate, Quill and Rook, because Quill is liable for the whole term as original tenant and Rook gave a direct covenant for the rest of the term.","From Quill only, because the original tenant is the only party in privity of contract with the landlord."],"a":3,"e":"This is an **old lease** (granted before 1996): the **original tenant** remains liable for the **whole term** through **privity of contract**, and the current tenant is liable through privity of estate. An assignee is normally liable only while it holds the lease, but Rook's **direct covenant** in the licence created a contract with Pine for the **rest of the term**. The s.17 notices were served within 6 months, so Pine can claim from all three, though it cannot recover more than the arrears."},{"t":"lease-dealings","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Teal Ltd holds a lease of an office with 6 years and 3 months left to run. It agrees to let the whole office to Umber Ltd by a document described as an 'underlease', for a term expiring on the same day as Teal's own lease.\n\nWhat is the effect of the document?","o":["It creates a valid underlease, with Teal becoming Umber's landlord.","It is void, because a tenant cannot grant any interest in the whole of the premises.","It operates as an assignment of Teal's lease, because an underlease must be for a shorter term than the tenant's own lease.","It creates a valid underlease, but Teal's own lease is extended by one day.","It operates as a surrender of Teal's lease to the head landlord."],"a":2,"e":"An **underlease** must be for a term **shorter** than the tenant's own lease, even by a day; a 'sublease' of the whole for the **whole remainder** of the term leaves the tenant no reversion and **operates as an assignment**. Teal therefore drops out and Umber steps into its shoes, which also matters for the head landlord's consent under the alienation covenant. It is not a surrender, and the term of Teal's lease cannot be extended unilaterally."},{"t":"lease-remedies","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Vixen Ltd's written lease is of a ground floor shop together with a flat above it, which the shop manager lives in as her home with the landlord's permission. Vixen owes three months' rent. The landlord wants to use Commercial Rent Arrears Recovery (CRAR) to take control of the stock in the shop.\n\nCan the landlord use CRAR?","o":["Yes, because the arrears exceed 7 days' rent and the lease is in writing.","Yes, but only against goods in the shop, not in the flat.","Yes, after giving at least 7 clear days' notice of enforcement.","No, because CRAR is not available where any part of the let premises is lawfully used as a dwelling.","No, because CRAR can be used only after a court has given judgment for the arrears."],"a":3,"e":"CRAR is available only for **commercial premises**; it cannot be used if any part of the premises is let or **used as a dwelling** (other than in breach of the lease) (**TCEA 2007 s.75**). Here the flat is lawfully used as a home, so the arrears threshold, written lease and notice requirements never come into play. CRAR does not need a court judgment."},{"t":"lease-remedies","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Willow Ltd holds a protected business tenancy of a shop, not contracted out of the Landlord and Tenant Act 1954. It agrees in a signed letter with its landlord that it will surrender the lease in nine months' time in return for a payment. No warning notice was served and no declaration was made. Willow has now changed its mind.\n\nIs the agreement to surrender binding on Willow?","o":["Yes, because it is in writing and signed by both parties.","No, because an agreement to surrender a protected tenancy in the future is void unless the warning notice and declaration procedure has been followed.","Yes, because a tenant may always give up its security of tenure by agreement.","No, because a surrender of a lease can be made only by deed.","Yes, but the landlord must obtain a court order before Willow leaves."],"a":1,"e":"An **agreement to surrender** a protected business tenancy at a **future** date is **void** unless the **s.38A** procedure (a warning notice and the tenant's declaration) is followed first (**LTA 1954 s.38A(2)**). An **immediate** surrender needs no such procedure; the problem here is the statutory protection, not the form of the document, and signed writing alone cannot exclude the Act."},{"t":"lta1954","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Yew Ltd holds a 10-year protected business lease expiring on 30 June 2027. On 1 August 2026 it served a valid s.26 request on its landlord proposing a new tenancy starting on 1 July 2027. The landlord, which wants to redevelop, did nothing until December 2026, when it wrote saying it will oppose renewal on ground (f).\n\nCan the landlord oppose the grant of a new tenancy?","o":["No, because a landlord that does not serve a counter-notice within two months of a s.26 request cannot later oppose renewal.","Yes, because the landlord's intention to redevelop is judged at the date of the hearing.","Yes, because the landlord has given more than six months' notice before the proposed start date.","Yes, provided it applies to court for an order terminating the tenancy before 1 July 2027.","No, because a landlord can rely on ground (f) only in a s.25 notice."],"a":0,"e":"On receiving a **s.26 request**, a landlord that wishes to oppose must serve a **counter-notice** within **2 months**, stating its grounds; if it does not, it **cannot** later oppose renewal. The landlord here waited about four months, so it has lost the right to rely on ground (f). The 'intention at the hearing' rule matters only if the ground is available in the first place."},{"t":"lta1954","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Zinnia Ltd has run a florist's shop from premises let to it on a protected business tenancy for 9 years. Before that, the premises were let to a different company running a newsagent's for 8 years. The landlord has successfully opposed renewal on ground (g), its intention to occupy the premises for its own business. The rateable value of the holding is £25,000, and the multiplier is 1.\n\nHow much compensation is Zinnia Ltd entitled to?","o":["£50,000.","Nothing, because the landlord succeeded on a mandatory ground.","£12,500.","Nothing, because Zinnia Ltd has occupied for less than 14 years.","£25,000."],"a":4,"e":"A tenant refused a new lease only on the **no-fault grounds (e), (f) or (g)** is entitled to compensation of the **rateable value × the multiplier (1)**, here £25,000. **Double** compensation is payable only where the tenant, or its **predecessors in the same business**, occupied for the whole **14 years** before termination; the newsagent's earlier occupation was a different business, so it does not count. Compensation does not depend on 14 years' occupation, which only doubles it."},{"t":"lta1954","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Ivo runs a café from a unit he has occupied for 18 months under three successive six-month leases from the same landlord. None of the leases was contracted out of the Landlord and Tenant Act 1954, and none contains any provision for renewal. The current lease expires next month, and the landlord says Ivo must leave then because a six-month lease has no protection.\n\nIs the landlord correct?","o":["Yes, because a fixed-term tenancy of six months or less is always excluded from protection.","Yes, because the leases were never contracted out, so protection never arose.","No, because every business tenancy is protected unless the tenant agrees otherwise in a statutory declaration.","No, because the exclusion for short leases does not apply where the tenant has already been in occupation for more than 12 months.","Yes, because a tenant can request a new tenancy only if its lease was granted for more than one year."],"a":3,"e":"Fixed-term tenancies of **6 months or less** are excluded from Part II, **unless** the tenancy contains provision for renewal beyond 6 months or the tenant (with any predecessor in the same business) has already been in occupation for **more than 12 months** (**LTA 1954 s.43(3)**). Ivo has occupied for 18 months, so he is protected and his tenancy will continue under s.24. The one-year rule limits only the tenant's right to serve a s.26 request, not whether the tenancy is protected."},{"t":"planning","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Jade is buying the freehold of a shop in England that has been used for many years as a clothes shop within Use Class E. She plans to turn it into a hot food takeaway, with no external alterations.\n\nDoes Jade need planning permission?","o":["No, because both uses are commercial uses within Use Class E.","No, because she will make no external alterations, so there is no development.","No, because a change of use from a shop is always permitted development.","Yes, but only if the shop is in a conservation area.","Yes, because a hot food takeaway is a sui generis use, so the change is a material change of use."],"a":4,"e":"A **hot food takeaway** is **sui generis**, outside any use class, so moving to it from a Class E shop is a **material change of use** and therefore **development** needing permission. A change **within** Class E would not be development, but that is not this case. The absence of building works is irrelevant to a change of use, and there is no general permitted development right for this change."},{"t":"planning","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Kian owns a house in Wales. In 2021 he built a large two-storey rear extension without planning permission, and it was substantially completed in May 2021. He did nothing to conceal it. In November 2026 the local planning authority discovered the extension and is considering enforcement action.\n\nCan the authority take enforcement action against the extension?","o":["Yes, because operational development can be enforced against for 10 years after substantial completion.","No, because in Wales operational development becomes immune from enforcement four years after substantial completion.","Yes, because there is no time limit for enforcement against building works without planning permission.","Yes, because the four-year rule was abolished for all breaches occurring after 25 April 2024.","No, because enforcement action can only be taken within 12 months of completion of the works."],"a":1,"e":"In **Wales**, the time limit for enforcement against **operational development** remains **4 years from substantial completion**, so the extension became immune in May 2025. The **10-year** rule applies in **England** to works substantially completed on or after **25 April 2024**; the Levelling-up and Regeneration Act 2023 change did not extend to Wales. There is no concealment, which might otherwise prevent reliance on the time limit."},{"t":"tax","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Lara, a first-time buyer, is buying a house in England for £520,000 as her only home. Assume the following rates. Standard residential rates: 0% up to £125,000; 2% on £125,001 to £250,000; 5% on £250,001 to £925,000. First-time buyers' relief: 0% up to £300,000 and 5% on £300,001 to £500,000, available only where the price is not more than £500,000.\n\nHow much SDLT will Lara pay?","o":["£11,000.","£16,000.","£10,000.","£26,000.","Nothing, because first-time buyers pay no SDLT on their first home."],"a":1,"e":"First-time buyers' relief is **not available at all** where the price exceeds **£500,000**, so the **standard residential rates** apply on the **slice** system: 0% on £125,000, 2% on £125,000 (£2,500) and 5% on £270,000 (£13,500), totalling £16,000. £11,000 wrongly applies the relief to the whole price, and £10,000 applies it to only the first £500,000. £26,000 charges 5% on the whole price instead of slicing."},{"t":"tax","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Moor Insurance plc is buying the freehold of a newly built office block from the developer that constructed it. The building was completed 18 months ago. The developer has not opted to tax. Moor Insurance makes mainly exempt supplies.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the VAT position?","o":["The sale is exempt, because the developer has not opted to tax.","The sale is zero-rated, because it is the first sale of a new building by the person who constructed it.","The sale is outside the scope of VAT, because it is a transfer of a going concern.","VAT at the standard rate is chargeable on the price, because the sale of the freehold of a commercial building less than 3 years old is compulsorily standard-rated.","VAT is chargeable, but Moor Insurance can recover all of it from HMRC."],"a":3,"e":"The sale of the **freehold of a new commercial building** (less than **3 years** old) is **compulsorily standard-rated**, whether or not there is an option to tax. Zero-rating applies to the first sale of a new **dwelling** by its builder, not to offices, and a TOGC needs a property rental business to be transferred. Because Moor Insurance makes mainly **exempt** supplies, it **cannot recover** most of the VAT, so it is a real cost (and it increases the SDLT)."},{"t":"tax","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Nell, a higher-rate taxpayer, owns a buy-to-let flat in England that she has never lived in. She exchanged contracts to sell it on 20 March 2026, and completion took place on 10 April 2026. A chargeable gain arises.\n\nWhich of the following best describes when the disposal takes place for capital gains tax and when Nell must report it?","o":["The disposal takes place on 20 March 2026, in the 2025/26 tax year, and she must report and pay the tax within 60 days of 10 April 2026.","The disposal takes place on 10 April 2026, in the 2026/27 tax year, and she must report and pay the tax within 60 days of that date.","The disposal takes place on 20 March 2026, and she must report and pay the tax within 60 days of that date.","The disposal takes place on 10 April 2026, and she need only report it in her self-assessment return for that year.","There is no disposal for capital gains tax, because Private Residence Relief applies to residential property."],"a":0,"e":"For CGT the time of disposal under an unconditional contract is the date of the **contract (exchange)**, not completion (**TCGA 1992 s.28**), so the disposal falls in **2025/26**. But a disposal of **UK residential property** on which CGT is due must be **reported and the tax paid within 60 days of completion**. Private Residence Relief applies only to a home the owner occupied, not to a flat she has never lived in."}],"crimlaw":[{"t":"actus-reus","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Gareth is staying overnight in a friend's empty flat. He falls asleep on the sofa with a lit cigarette, which drops onto a cushion. He wakes to find the cushion smouldering and realises it could set the flat alight. He moves into the bedroom and goes back to sleep without doing anything. The fire spreads and badly damages the flat.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Gareth's liability for arson?","o":["He is not guilty, because the fire started accidentally while he was asleep, so there was no voluntary act on his part.","He is not guilty, because the law imposes liability for a failure to act only where the defendant is under a contractual duty.","He may be guilty, because once aware of the danger he had created, he was under a duty to act and did nothing.","He is guilty only if a reasonable person in his position would have realised that the fire might spread through the flat.","He is not guilty, because his mens rea would have to coincide with the moment the cigarette fell onto the cushion."],"a":2,"e":"A defendant who **creates a dangerous situation**, even innocently, comes under a **duty to act** once he **becomes aware** of it; failing to take reasonable steps can then be the actus reus, and his recklessness at that point supplies the mens rea (R v Miller). The accidental start does not help him, because liability attaches to the later omission. Contract is only one source of a duty, and recklessness is judged **subjectively**, not by what a reasonable person would have realised."},{"t":"mens-rea","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"During a row in a car park, Hamid throws a heavy stone at Jonas intending to hit him. Jonas ducks and the stone smashes the window of a parked car behind him. Hamid says he did not think about the car at all.\n\nIs Hamid likely to be guilty of criminal damage to the car?","o":["Yes, because his intention to hit Jonas is transferred to the car under the doctrine of transferred malice.","Yes, because a reasonable person in Hamid's position would have seen an obvious risk of damaging the car.","Yes, because criminal damage is an offence of strict liability once damage is caused by an unlawful act of the defendant.","No, because transferred malice never applies where the actual victim differs from the intended victim.","No, unless he was reckless as to damaging property, as an intent to injure a person does not transfer to property."],"a":4,"e":"**Transferred malice** works only where the harm caused is of the **same type** as that intended; an intent to injure a person does not transfer to damage to property (R v Pembliton). So Hamid is guilty only if he was **subjectively reckless** as to damaging property, which on his account he was not. The reasonable-person test was abandoned (R v G), criminal damage is not strict liability, and transferred malice does apply where a different person of the same kind is hit."},{"t":"non-fatal-offences","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Over three weeks, Simone makes dozens of silent phone calls late at night to her former colleague Tara, sometimes breathing heavily. Tara becomes terrified that Simone is about to come round and attack her. A psychiatrist diagnoses Tara with a clinical anxiety disorder caused by the calls.\n\nWhich offence is Simone most likely to have committed?","o":["Assault occasioning actual bodily harm, because silent calls can be an assault and a recognised psychiatric illness is bodily harm.","Common assault only, because psychiatric harm of any kind can never amount to bodily harm under the 1861 Act.","No offence against the person, because an assault always requires spoken words or a physical gesture by the defendant.","Inflicting grievous bodily harm under s.20, because any diagnosed psychiatric illness is automatically serious harm.","Battery, because the calls caused Tara to suffer harm even though Simone never made physical contact with her."],"a":0,"e":"**Silent calls** can be an **assault** if they cause the victim to apprehend **immediate** unlawful force, and \"immediate\" includes fear of force in the near future (R v Ireland). A **recognised clinical psychiatric condition** counts as actual bodily harm, though mere fear or panic does not. So **s.47** fits. Battery needs physical contact, and psychiatric harm amounts to GBH only if it is really serious, which the facts do not show."},{"t":"theft","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Ruth organises her office's weekly lottery syndicate. Each Friday the six members give her £5 each so that she can buy their tickets. One week she spends the £30 on a meal out instead, intending to say that the numbers simply did not come up.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ruth's liability for theft of the £30?","o":["She is not guilty, because the members handed the money to her voluntarily, so ownership of it passed to her.","She is not guilty, because her obligation to buy the tickets was only a moral one arising between friends at work.","She is guilty only if she already intended to spend the money at the moment the members handed it to her.","She may be guilty, because she was legally obliged to deal with the money in a particular way, so it belongs to the members.","She is not guilty, because once the cash was mixed with her own money it no longer belonged to anyone else."],"a":3,"e":"Under **s.5(3) Theft Act 1968**, property received **on account of another** under a **legal obligation** to deal with it in a particular way is treated as **belonging to the other** (compare Davidge v Bunnett). Ruth received the money for the specific purpose of buying tickets, so spending it on herself is a dishonest appropriation of property belonging to the members. The voluntary handover does not defeat theft, the dishonest appropriation can occur later, and this is not a case like R v Hall where there was no obligation to use particular funds."},{"t":"robbery-burglary","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Kyle and Liam get into a fight with Marcus outside a takeaway over a spilt drink. Kyle punches Marcus, who runs off, leaving his rucksack on the pavement. Only after Marcus has gone does Kyle notice the rucksack and decide to take it. He walks away with it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Kyle's liability?","o":["Kyle is guilty of robbery, because he used force on Marcus only moments before he stole the rucksack from the pavement.","Kyle is guilty of theft and an assault offence, but not robbery, because the force was not used in order to steal.","Kyle is guilty of robbery, because appropriation is a continuing act and the force and theft formed one incident.","Kyle is guilty of an assault offence only, because Marcus abandoned the rucksack when he ran away.","Kyle is guilty of robbery, because force was used on the person who was in possession of the property at the time."],"a":1,"e":"Robbery requires force used **immediately before or at the time** of stealing **and in order to** steal. Where the defendant decides to take property only **after** the force has been used for another reason, the force is not used in order to steal, so there is **no robbery** (R v Vinall), though there is theft and an assault offence. The rucksack was not abandoned: Marcus fled, he did not give up ownership."},{"t":"criminal-damage","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"On a hot afternoon, Ines sees her neighbour's dog shut inside a car parked in the sun with the windows closed. The dog is panting heavily and collapsing. She cannot find the owner, so she breaks a side window with a stone to let the dog out. She is charged with criminal damage to the car.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Ines has a lawful excuse?","o":["No, because a dog is a living animal and so is not property that the defence of lawful excuse can protect.","No, because the defence of protecting property applies only where the defendant acts to protect property belonging to herself.","Yes, if she honestly believed the dog needed immediate protection and that breaking the window was reasonable.","Yes, but only if a reasonable person would also have believed that the dog was in immediate danger.","No, because she could have called the police or the RSPCA instead of choosing to damage the owner's car."],"a":2,"e":"Under **s.5(2)(b) Criminal Damage Act 1971**, the defendant has a lawful excuse if she damaged property **in order to protect property** (her own or **another's**) and **honestly believed** it was in **immediate need of protection** and the **means were reasonable**. By s.5(3) the beliefs need only be **honestly held**, not justified. A dog is property belonging to its owner. The other options wrongly limit the defence to the defendant's own property or apply an objective test to the defendant's beliefs."},{"t":"voluntary-manslaughter","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Nasser is told by his neighbour Ollie, in the street, that Ollie has been stealing from Nasser's elderly mother. Nasser is furious. He walks home, takes a hammer from his shed, sits in his kitchen for 40 minutes thinking about how Ollie has treated his family, then goes back to Ollie's house and kills him with the hammer. He is charged with murder.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Nasser can rely on loss of control?","o":["It is unlikely to succeed, because the evidence suggests he acted in a considered desire for revenge.","It will succeed, because since 2010 the loss of self-control need not be sudden and can follow a delay.","It will succeed if Nasser proves on the balance of probabilities that he lost his self-control.","It is excluded only because theft from a relative can never amount to a qualifying trigger.","It will succeed, because what Ollie said gave Nasser a justifiable sense of being seriously wronged."],"a":0,"e":"Although the loss of control **need not be sudden** (s.54(2) CJA 2009), the defence is **excluded** where the defendant acted in a **considered desire for revenge** (s.54(4)), and the time to fetch a weapon and reflect points to considered action rather than lost control (compare R v Jewell). The burden is on the **prosecution** to disprove the defence once raised; the defendant does not prove it. The trigger issue does not arise if there was no loss of self-control in the first place."},{"t":"involuntary-manslaughter","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Paula and Rob are the parents of a baby who develops a high fever and stops feeding. Despite the baby's obvious and worsening illness, they decide not to call a doctor because they are afraid social services will become involved. The baby dies two days later. Medical evidence shows prompt treatment would have saved the baby.\n\nWhich form of homicide is most appropriate?","o":["Unlawful act manslaughter, because their failure to call a doctor was an unlawful and dangerous omission.","Murder, because the baby's death was a natural and probable consequence of the parents' failure to act.","No homicide offence, because parents cannot be criminally liable for a mere failure to act towards their child.","Unlawful act manslaughter, because wilful neglect of a child is itself a criminal offence that can found it.","Gross negligence manslaughter, because they owed the baby a duty and their failure carried an obvious risk of death."],"a":4,"e":"**Unlawful act manslaughter cannot be based on an omission** (R v Lowe), so where parents simply fail to act the route is **gross negligence manslaughter**. Parents owe a **duty** to their child, the breach exposed the baby to a **serious and obvious risk of death**, the failure caused death, and a jury could find it **truly exceptionally bad**. Murder would need intent to kill or cause GBH, which is not shown."},{"t":"general-defences","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Unknown to Quinn, a colleague secretly adds a strong sedative drug to his coffee as a prank. Feeling oddly uninhibited, Quinn takes the opportunity to punch a co-worker he has long disliked, breaking his nose. Quinn admits he intended to hit the co-worker, but says he would never have done it if he had not been drugged.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the intoxication?","o":["It is a complete defence, because involuntary intoxication is a defence to any offence, whatever the defendant intended.","It is a defence to basic intent offences only, because the drug was administered without his knowledge.","It reduces his liability to common assault, because the drug lowered his inhibitions against violence.","It is no defence, because although his intoxication was involuntary, Quinn still formed the mens rea.","It is a defence only if Quinn proves he was so intoxicated that he did not know what he was doing."],"a":3,"e":"**Involuntary intoxication** can be a defence to any offence, but only where the defendant **did not form the mens rea**. If the defendant still formed the intent, it is **no defence**, however much the drug lowered his inhibitions: \"a drugged intent is still an intent\" (R v Kingston). Quinn admits he intended to hit, so the intoxication is relevant only to sentence."},{"t":"parties","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Sam and his friend Tom are walking home when Sam starts a fight with a stranger and kicks him repeatedly, causing serious injuries. Tom stands nearby throughout and watches, saying nothing. There is no evidence that Tom intended to encourage Sam, or that Sam was aware of or encouraged by Tom's presence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Tom's liability as an accessory?","o":["Tom is guilty, because by failing to intervene to stop the attack he aided Sam in committing the offence.","Tom is not guilty, because mere presence is not enough without actual encouragement and an intent to encourage.","Tom is guilty, because his voluntary presence at the scene of a crime is in itself enough to amount to abetting.","Tom is guilty, because he must have foreseen that Sam might cause the stranger serious injury.","Tom is not guilty, because an accessory must be present at the scene and take some part in the violence."],"a":1,"e":"**Mere presence** at the scene, even without trying to stop the crime, is **not** enough: the defendant must **actually encourage** the principal and **intend** to do so (R v Clarkson). Tom had no legal **right to control** Sam, so his failure to act cannot be aiding. Foresight alone is not the test after **Jogee**, and an accessory need not be present or use violence."},{"t":"attempts","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Vera buys a box of designer watches from a man in a pub for a fraction of their value, believing they are stolen. In fact the watches are genuine items which the seller had bought lawfully and is selling cheaply because he needs cash.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Vera's liability?","o":["She is guilty of attempted handling stolen goods, because she is judged on the facts as she believed them to be.","She is not guilty of any offence, because the goods were not stolen, so the offence was impossible to commit.","She is guilty of the full offence of handling stolen goods, because she had the mens rea for that offence.","She is not guilty, because impossibility is a defence to an attempt where the defendant's mistake is one of fact.","She is guilty of attempted handling only if a reasonable buyer would also have believed the goods were stolen."],"a":0,"e":"Under **s.1(2)-(3) Criminal Attempts Act 1981**, the defendant can be guilty of an attempt **even though the offence is impossible**, and is judged on the **facts as she believed them** (R v Shivpuri). Vera intended to handle stolen goods and did more than a merely preparatory act, so it is an attempt. The full offence fails because the goods were not stolen, and **factual impossibility is no defence**. Only where the intended act would not be a crime at all is there a defence."},{"t":"actus-reus","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Will offers Xena a lift home. During the drive he locks the doors, makes sexual threats and starts to touch her. Terrified, Xena opens the door and jumps out while the car is travelling at about 25 mph. She suffers a broken wrist and head injuries.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Will caused Xena's injuries?","o":["Will did not cause them, because Xena's free and deliberate decision to jump from the car broke the chain of causation.","Will caused them only if he actually foresaw at the time that Xena might jump from the moving car.","Will did not cause them, because a reasonable person in Xena's position would have waited for the car to stop.","Will caused them, because jumping was a reasonably foreseeable response and not so daft as to break the chain.","Will caused them only if jumping was the most sensible option that was available to Xena at the time."],"a":3,"e":"Where the victim is injured escaping from the defendant's threats, the defendant remains the legal cause if the victim's reaction was **reasonably foreseeable** and not **so daft or unexpected** that no reasonable person could have foreseen it (R v Roberts; R v Williams). The victim in fear need not choose the wisest option. The test is **objective**, so it does not matter whether Will himself foresaw her jumping."},{"t":"mens-rea","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"During an argument, Yusuf strikes Zane over the head with a bottle intending to kill him. Zane collapses and Yusuf, believing him dead, rolls him into a canal to hide the body. Medical evidence shows Zane was alive when he entered the water and died from drowning.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Yusuf's liability for murder?","o":["He is not guilty, because when he did the act that actually caused death he believed Zane was already dead.","He is not guilty, because the blow with the bottle, which was done with intent, did not cause Zane's death.","He is guilty, because the blow and the disposal can be treated as one series of acts during which he had the mens rea.","He is guilty of manslaughter only, because there was no coincidence of the actus reus and the mens rea.","He is guilty only if he foresaw that rolling Zane into the canal might cause his death by drowning."],"a":2,"e":"The actus reus and mens rea must **coincide**, but the courts treat a **series of acts** forming one transaction as a single continuing act (Thabo Meli; R v Church). Yusuf intended to kill when he struck Zane, and putting him into the water was part of the same plan, so the mens rea and the act causing death coincide. Arguments that the fatal act lacked mens rea, or that the first blow was not the cause, therefore fail."},{"t":"non-fatal-offences","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Amir and Ben, both adults, agree to settle a dispute with a fist fight in a pub car park. Ben punches Amir, breaking his nose. Ben is charged with assault occasioning actual bodily harm and says Amir consented to the fight.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Ben can rely on consent?","o":["Yes, because both men were adults who freely and knowingly agreed to accept the risk of being injured.","Yes, because consent is a defence to any non-fatal offence against the person short of grievous bodily harm.","No, because consent is never a defence to any offence that involves the application of force to another.","Yes, because an agreed fight between adults is a recognised exception in the same way as boxing.","No, because consent is no defence where actual bodily harm is caused and a street fight is not an exception."],"a":4,"e":"Consent is a defence to **assault and battery**, but where **actual bodily harm** or worse is intended or caused it is a defence only within a **recognised exception** in the public interest, such as properly conducted sports, surgery or tattooing (A-G's Reference (No 6 of 1980); R v Brown). An agreed **fist fight** in public is **not** an exception. Consent is a defence to some force (ordinary contact and battery), so the absolute option is wrong."},{"t":"theft","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Carla works on the till at a large supermarket. She takes £200 from the till to give to a homeless charity, believing that the supermarket makes too much profit and that most people would think she was doing the right thing. She knows the supermarket has not agreed to this.\n\nHow should the court approach whether Carla was dishonest?","o":["Carla is not dishonest, because she did not think that what she was doing was dishonest.","The court finds her actual belief as to the facts, then applies the standards of ordinary decent people.","Carla is not dishonest, because she believed that ordinary decent people would approve of what she did.","Carla is not dishonest, because she gained nothing for herself personally from taking the money.","Carla is dishonest only if she realised that ordinary decent people would regard her conduct as dishonest."],"a":1,"e":"Under the **Ivey** test (confirmed in R v Barton and Booth), the court first finds the defendant's **actual state of knowledge or belief as to the facts**, then asks whether her conduct was dishonest by the **standards of ordinary decent people**. The defendant's **own standards** are irrelevant, and the old **Ghosh** second limb no longer applies. Theft needs no gain for the defendant, and none of the s.2(1) beliefs (legal right, owner's consent, owner cannot be found) applies."},{"t":"robbery-burglary","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Dan enters a large electrical store during opening hours. He goes through a door marked 'Staff only' into the stockroom, intending to take a games console. A staff member sees him before he takes anything and he leaves empty-handed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Dan's liability for burglary?","o":["He is guilty of burglary under s.9(1)(a), as he entered part of a building as a trespasser with intent to steal.","He is not guilty of burglary, because he had permission to enter the store as a member of the public.","He is guilty of burglary under s.9(1)(b), because he entered as a trespasser and then attempted to steal.","He is not guilty of burglary, because he left the store without stealing anything at all.","He is guilty only of attempted burglary, because he was stopped before he could take the games console."],"a":0,"e":"A person who enters a **part of a building** from which he is excluded, such as a staff-only area, enters that part **as a trespasser** (R v Walkington). Dan had **intent to steal at the moment of entry**, so **s.9(1)(a)** burglary is complete on entry. Nothing need be stolen. s.9(1)(b) needs the defendant to steal or attempt to steal after entering, which the facts do not clearly show."},{"t":"criminal-damage","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"As a protest, Eddie writes slogans in coloured chalk across the pavement outside the town hall. The chalk would wash away in the next heavy rain, but the council does not want the slogans to stay visible, so it pays contractors to remove them with a pressure washer the same day.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Eddie has damaged property?","o":["He has not damaged the pavement, because the chalk would have washed away naturally without any lasting harm.","He has not damaged the pavement, because criminal damage requires a permanent physical change to the property.","He has damaged the pavement only if he intended the council to spend money on removing the chalk.","He may have damaged it, because damage includes temporary impairment that costs time or money to put right.","He has not damaged the pavement, because land cannot be damaged for the purposes of the 1971 Act."],"a":3,"e":"**Damage** is a question of fact and degree and includes **temporary** harm or impairment of **value or usefulness** where it costs **time, effort or money** to put right (Hardman v Chief Constable of Avon and Somerset: water-soluble paint removed by the council at a cost). Permanence is not required, and unlike theft, **land can be damaged**. Eddie's mens rea concerns damaging property, not the council's expense."},{"t":"murder","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Fergus is owed money by Gary. He waits for Gary outside his flat with a baseball bat, intending, as he later tells police, to 'break both his legs so he can't walk for months' but not to kill him. He hits Gary repeatedly on the legs and body. Gary dies from internal bleeding.\n\nWhich offence is Fergus most likely to be guilty of?","o":["Manslaughter only, because Fergus never intended that Gary should die from the attack.","Murder, but only if Fergus foresaw that Gary's death was a virtually certain result of the attack.","Unlawful act manslaughter, because the attack was an unlawful act that was objectively dangerous.","Manslaughter, because Fergus had not premeditated or planned to kill Gary when he waited for him.","Murder, because an intention to cause grievous bodily harm is sufficient mens rea for murder."],"a":4,"e":"The mens rea of murder (**malice aforethought**) is an intention to **kill** or to cause **grievous bodily harm**, meaning really serious harm (R v Vickers; R v Cunningham). Intending to break both legs is plainly an intention to cause GBH, so Fergus is guilty of **murder** even though he did not intend or foresee death. Woollin virtual certainty is needed only where intention is oblique, and malice aforethought needs no premeditation to kill."},{"t":"involuntary-manslaughter","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Harriet sells a bag of heroin to her friend Ian, who asks for it, and leaves immediately. Ian then prepares the drug and injects himself in his own flat. He dies of an overdose. Harriet owed Ian no special duty.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Harriet's liability for unlawful act manslaughter?","o":["She is guilty, because supplying a Class A drug is an unlawful act which sober and reasonable people would see as dangerous.","She is guilty, because it was reasonably foreseeable that Ian might take too much of the heroin and overdose.","She is not guilty, because Ian's free, deliberate and informed decision to inject himself broke the chain.","She is guilty of gross negligence manslaughter, because every supplier of drugs owes the user a duty of care.","She is not guilty, because supplying drugs is not a criminal offence capable of founding a manslaughter charge."],"a":2,"e":"Where an adult of sound mind **freely and knowingly self-administers** a drug, the supplier does **not cause** death (R v Kennedy (No 2)), so **unlawful act manslaughter** fails on causation even though supply is unlawful and dangerous. Foreseeability alone does not overcome a **free, deliberate and informed** act. GNM would need a **duty**, which the facts exclude; contrast a supplier who injects the victim (R v Cato)."},{"t":"fraud","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Jasmine runs a café and applies online for buildings insurance for the café premises. The application form asks whether the business has made any insurance claims in the last five years, and the policy terms require her to disclose this. She has made three, but leaves the answer blank so that she will get a cheaper premium. The insurer issues the policy without noticing the blank.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Jasmine's liability?","o":["She is not guilty of fraud, because she did not write anything untrue on the application form.","She is guilty of fraud by failing to disclose information she was under a legal duty to disclose.","She is not guilty of fraud, because the insurer was not actually deceived into issuing the policy by the blank.","She is guilty of fraud by abuse of position, because she was expected to safeguard the insurer's financial interests.","She is guilty of fraud only once she makes a claim under the policy and receives a payment."],"a":1,"e":"**s.3 Fraud Act 2006** is committed where the defendant **dishonestly fails to disclose** information she is under a **legal duty** to disclose, intending to make a **gain** or cause **loss**. As a **business** (non-consumer) applicant she had a legal duty to make a fair presentation of the risk (Insurance Act 2015), and the policy terms also required disclosure of past claims; a cheaper premium is a gain. (A consumer's duty is only not to misrepresent, so a consumer who gives a false answer would be charged under s.2.) Like s.2, s.3 is a **conduct crime**, so no one need be deceived and no claim need be made. s.4 is inapt because an applicant for insurance is not in a position of financial trust."},{"t":"general-defences","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Kieran decides to attack his former business partner Lewis, whom he blames for his bankruptcy. He buys a knife and then drinks most of a bottle of vodka 'to calm his nerves'. Very drunk, he goes to Lewis's house and stabs him, causing serious injuries. He is charged with wounding with intent to cause grievous bodily harm (s.18 OAPA 1861).\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of Kieran's intoxication?","o":["It is no defence, because he formed the intent before drinking and drank to give himself courage.","It can be a defence, because s.18 is a specific intent offence and he was very drunk when he stabbed Lewis.","It reduces the offence to s.20, because voluntary intoxication operates as a partial defence to s.18.","It is a complete defence if he was so drunk at the time of the stabbing that he did not know what he was doing.","It is relevant only if the vodka he drank was much stronger than Kieran realised when he drank it."],"a":0,"e":"A defendant who **forms the intent while sober** and then gets drunk to give himself **Dutch courage** cannot rely on intoxication, even for a **specific intent** offence (A-G for Northern Ireland v Gallagher). Normally voluntary intoxication can show the defendant lacked the intent for s.18, but not here. Underestimating a drink's strength would still be **voluntary** intoxication (R v Allen)."},{"t":"parties","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Megan wants money from her neighbour's garden shed. She tells her 9-year-old nephew Noah that the neighbour has said he can fetch a toolbox from the shed for her. Noah does so, and Megan sells the tools.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Megan's liability?","o":["Megan is not guilty of theft, because she never physically took the toolbox from the shed herself.","Megan is guilty of theft as an accessory, because she counselled Noah to commit the offence of theft.","Megan is not guilty of theft, because Noah, who took the toolbox, cannot himself be convicted of it.","Megan is guilty of theft as the principal, because Noah is an innocent agent through whom she acted.","Megan is guilty only of encouraging or assisting crime, because no principal offence was ever committed."],"a":3,"e":"A person who carries out the actus reus but is not guilty, for example because he is **under 10** or **lacks mens rea**, is an **innocent agent**, and the person who uses him is the **principal**. Noah is both under the age of criminal responsibility and unaware the taking was unlawful, so Megan commits theft as principal. Accessory liability is the wrong analysis where there is no guilty principal but the defendant has procured the act through an innocent agent."},{"t":"actus-reus","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Owen stabs Pria in the stomach during a robbery. In hospital, doctors tell her she needs a blood transfusion or she will die. Pria refuses because of her religious beliefs, and she dies of blood loss. The transfusion would almost certainly have saved her.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Owen caused Pria's death?","o":["Owen did not cause her death, because her refusal of treatment was unreasonable and broke the chain of causation.","Owen did not cause her death, because the thin skull rule applies only to a victim's physical conditions.","Owen caused her death only if he knew of her religious beliefs at the time he stabbed her.","Owen did not cause her death, because the hospital's failure to give a transfusion was the operating cause.","Owen caused her death, because he must take his victim as he finds her, including her religious beliefs."],"a":4,"e":"Under the **thin skull** rule, the defendant must **take his victim as he finds her**, which covers the **whole person**, including religious beliefs, not just physical frailty (R v Blaue). The victim's refusal of treatment does **not break the chain**, and the stab wound was still an **operating and substantial cause** of death. The defendant's knowledge of the victim's beliefs is irrelevant, and the hospital did nothing wrong in respecting her refusal."},{"t":"mens-rea","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Quentin is a pharmacist. A customer hands him a prescription for a prescription-only medicine. It is a skilful forgery and Quentin, who checks it carefully, honestly and reasonably believes it is genuine. He supplies the medicine. The relevant offence has been held by the courts to impose strict liability as to whether supply is in accordance with a genuine prescription, and the statute provides no due diligence defence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Quentin's liability?","o":["He is not guilty, because there is a presumption that mens rea is required for every criminal offence.","He is guilty, because no mens rea is needed as to whether the prescription was genuine.","He is not guilty, because his mistake about the prescription was both honest and reasonable.","He is guilty only if he was negligent in failing to spot that the prescription was forged.","He is not guilty, because he never intended to supply the medicine unlawfully to the customer."],"a":1,"e":"For a **strict liability** element, the prosecution need prove **no mens rea** as to that element, so an **honest and reasonable mistake** is no defence (Pharmacy Board v Storkwain). The **presumption of mens rea** (Sweet v Parsley) can be displaced, particularly for regulatory offences concerning public safety, and here the courts have already held the offence strict. Negligence is not required either, unless the statute provides a due diligence defence, which it does not."},{"t":"non-fatal-offences","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Ravi wants to frighten Sean, who owes him money. He slashes at Sean's forearm with a craft knife, intending to give him a small cut as a warning but not to cause him serious injury. The blade breaks the skin and causes a cut needing three stitches.\n\nWhich offence is Ravi most likely to be guilty of?","o":["Wounding with intent under s.18, because he intended to wound Sean when he slashed at his forearm.","Assault occasioning actual bodily harm only, because a cut needing stitches is not a wound in law.","Unlawful wounding under s.20, because he caused a wound and foresaw that some harm would result.","Wounding with intent under s.18, because he deliberately used a bladed weapon to cause the injury.","Battery only, because Ravi did not intend to cause Sean any serious injury."],"a":2,"e":"A **wound** is a break in **both layers of the skin**, so the cut qualifies. **s.18** needs an intent to cause **GBH** (or to resist arrest); an intent merely to wound is not enough. So the right offence is **s.20**, which requires only that the defendant **foresaw some harm**, which Ravi plainly did. Using a weapon is evidence of intent but does not itself make the offence s.18."},{"t":"theft","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Tilly takes her flatmate Uma's season ticket for their local football club without asking. She uses it to attend every remaining home match of the season, then puts it back in Uma's drawer the day after the final match, knowing it is now useless.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Tilly had an intention to permanently deprive?","o":["She did, because she meant to return the ticket only once all of its practical value had been used up.","She did not, because she always intended to return the ticket to Uma's drawer.","She did not, because borrowing property without permission can never amount to theft.","She did, but only because she knew that Uma would have refused her permission to use it.","She did not, because the ticket itself was returned to Uma in the same physical condition."],"a":0,"e":"Under **s.6 Theft Act 1968**, borrowing can amount to an intention to permanently deprive if it is for a period and in circumstances **equivalent to an outright taking**, meaning the thing is returned after its **goodness or virtue** has gone (R v Lloyd). A season ticket returned after the season is over is worthless, so this test is met. An intention to return the physical item does not help where its practical value has been used up."},{"t":"robbery-burglary","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Vince breaks into a house at night through a back window, intending to steal. In his jacket pocket he has a realistic toy pistol, which he brought in case anyone disturbed him. The house is empty, he never takes the toy out, and he leaves with a laptop.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Vince's liability?","o":["He is guilty only of burglary, because he never used, showed or threatened anyone with the toy pistol.","He is guilty only of burglary, because a toy pistol that cannot fire is not a firearm.","He is guilty of aggravated burglary only if someone in the house had been put in fear by the toy.","He is guilty of aggravated burglary, because he had an imitation firearm with him when he entered.","He is guilty only of burglary, because the article must be a weapon of offence made for causing injury."],"a":3,"e":"**Aggravated burglary (s.10 Theft Act 1968)** is committed where the defendant commits a burglary and **at the time** has with him a **firearm or imitation firearm**, weapon of offence or explosive. For a **s.9(1)(a)** burglary, the relevant time is **entry**, and Vince knowingly had the toy pistol with him then. An imitation firearm is anything that has the **appearance** of a firearm; it need not be used, shown or frighten anyone."},{"t":"murder","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Wyatt kicks his pregnant partner Xia in the stomach, intending to cause her serious harm. Their baby is born prematurely a week later, alive and breathing independently, but dies two months afterwards as a result of the injuries caused by the kick. Xia recovers.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Wyatt's liability for the baby's death?","o":["He is guilty of murder, because his intention to cause Xia serious harm transfers to the baby.","He cannot be guilty of any homicide offence, because the baby was a foetus when he kicked Xia.","He cannot be guilty of murder of the baby, but he may be guilty of unlawful act manslaughter.","He is guilty of murder, because the baby was born alive and later died as a result of his act.","He cannot be guilty of any homicide offence, because the baby died long after the kick."],"a":2,"e":"A foetus is not a **reasonable creature in being**, and the courts have held that malice cannot transfer from the mother to the foetus and then to the child born alive, so this is **not murder** (A-G's Reference (No 3 of 1994)). But because the child was **born alive** and died from the injuries, **unlawful act manslaughter** is possible. The year and a day rule was **abolished** in 1996."},{"t":"voluntary-manslaughter","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Yasmin has been drinking heavily all evening. At a party, a man she has just met taunts her, tells her that she is a failure and spits in her face. She loses her self-control and stabs him fatally with a kitchen knife. She is charged with murder and raises loss of control.\n\nHow should the jury approach the objective test?","o":["Whether a person of Yasmin's sex and age who had drunk the same amount as her might have reacted in the same way.","Whether Yasmin's reaction was understandable, taking her intoxication into account because it is one of her circumstances.","Whether Yasmin has proved on the balance of probabilities that a reasonable person would have done the same.","Only whether Yasmin genuinely lost her self-control, because the test for the defence is entirely subjective.","Whether a sober person of her sex and age, with normal tolerance and self-restraint, in her circumstances, might have done the same."],"a":4,"e":"Under **s.54(1)(c) CJA 2009**, the jury asks whether a person of the defendant's **sex and age**, with a **normal degree of tolerance and self-restraint**, in the defendant's **circumstances**, might have reacted in the same or a similar way. **Voluntary intoxication** is excluded where its only relevance is to reduce the defendant's self-restraint, so the comparison is with a **sober** person (R v Asmelash). Once the defence is raised, the **prosecution** must disprove it beyond reasonable doubt, and the test is partly objective, not entirely subjective."},{"t":"fraud","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Zoe climbs over a fence into a music festival that charges £150 for entry, intending not to pay. She watches the headline acts all evening. She speaks to no one and shows no ticket to anyone.\n\nWhich offence is Zoe most likely to have committed?","o":["Obtaining services dishonestly, because she obtained paid-for services by a dishonest act, intending not to pay.","Fraud by false representation, because by being inside the festival she impliedly represented that she had paid.","Theft of the entertainment she received, because services count as intangible property under the Theft Act.","No offence, because she did not deceive anyone or make any representation to the festival's staff.","Fraud by abuse of position, because she took advantage of the festival organiser's lack of security."],"a":0,"e":"**s.11 Fraud Act 2006** (obtaining services dishonestly) is committed where the defendant obtains services **by a dishonest act**, knowing they are to be paid for, without paying, intending not to pay. It needs **no representation** or deception, but it is a **result** crime, so the services must actually be obtained, as they were here. Climbing in silently is not a representation to anyone, so s.2 does not fit, and **services are not property** that can be stolen."},{"t":"general-defences","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Alfie, who is completely sober, is walking home late at night when a man runs towards him shouting and reaches into his jacket. Alfie genuinely believes the man is about to pull out a knife, so he punches him once, knocking him down. In fact the man was reaching for his phone to ask Alfie to call an ambulance for a friend. A reasonable person might not have made Alfie's mistake.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the test for self-defence?","o":["He cannot rely on self-defence, because his belief that the man was about to produce a knife was mistaken.","He is judged on the facts as he honestly believed them, even if unreasonably, and then on whether one punch was reasonable.","He can rely on self-defence only if his mistaken belief that the man had a knife was a reasonable one to hold.","He cannot rely on self-defence, because he struck the first blow before the man had attacked him in any way.","He is judged on the facts as they actually were, so he used force against someone who posed no threat."],"a":1,"e":"Stage one of self-defence is **subjective**: the defendant is judged on the facts as he **honestly believed** them, whether or not the belief was **mistaken or unreasonable** (s.76(3)-(4) CJIA 2008; R v Gladstone Williams). Reasonableness of the belief goes only to whether it was genuinely held. Stage two asks **objectively** whether the force was reasonable in the circumstances as the defendant believed them. A **pre-emptive strike** can be justified (Beckford v R), and Alfie's mistake was not caused by intoxication."},{"t":"parties","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Bea and Cole plan to burgle a house they believe is empty. Bea gives Cole a knife, telling him to use it to 'stop anyone who gets in the way, whatever it takes'. A resident disturbs Cole, who stabs and kills him, intending to cause serious harm. Bea was waiting in the car outside.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Bea's liability for the resident's death?","o":["Bea is not guilty of murder, because she was waiting in the car and was not present at the killing.","Bea is guilty of murder because she must have foreseen that Cole might use the knife on someone.","Bea is not guilty of murder, because her intention depended on someone disturbing Cole during the burglary.","Bea is guilty only of manslaughter, because she did not personally intend that anyone should be killed.","Bea is guilty of murder if she intended to assist or encourage Cole to cause at least serious harm if needed."],"a":4,"e":"After **R v Jogee**, an accessory must **intend to assist or encourage** the principal to commit the crime, with knowledge of any essential facts; for murder, that means intending to assist or encourage the causing of **death or GBH**. A **conditional intention** (if anyone resists) is enough. **Foresight** that the principal might use the weapon is only evidence of intention, not the test itself. An accessory need not be present, and need not personally intend to kill."},{"t":"attempts","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Donna walks along a row of parked cars at night, trying each door handle. Her plan is to take anything of value she finds inside any unlocked car. She opens the door of one car, but the police arrest her before she looks inside it. She is charged with attempted theft.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Donna's liability?","o":["She cannot be guilty, because she had no intention to steal any particular item from the car.","She cannot be guilty, because she had not yet looked inside the car, so her acts were merely preparatory.","She is guilty of the full offence of theft, because opening the car door was an appropriation of the car.","She can be guilty if charged with attempting to steal some or all of the car's contents.","She cannot be guilty, because a conditional intention to steal is never enough for an attempt."],"a":3,"e":"A **conditional intention** to steal whatever is worth taking is enough for an attempt, provided the charge is framed as attempted theft of **some or all of the contents** rather than specific items (A-G's References (Nos 1 and 2 of 1979)). Opening a car door to search it is very likely to be **more than merely preparatory**, because Donna had embarked on the crime proper. Simply opening the door is not an appropriation of the car itself."}],"crimprac":[{"t":"police-station-detention","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Ellis is arrested for driving while disqualified, a summary-only offence, and arrives at the police station at 08:00 on Tuesday. The custody officer authorises his detention at 08:20. At 06:00 on Wednesday the investigating officer asks a superintendent to extend Ellis's detention so that further enquiries can be made.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["A superintendent may extend detention to 20:00 on Wednesday, because the second review has already taken place.","A superintendent may extend detention to 20:20 on Wednesday, because the clock runs from authorisation of detention.","The police must apply to the magistrates for a warrant of further detention before 08:20 on Wednesday.","No extension is possible, so Ellis must be charged or released by 08:00 on Wednesday.","Ellis must be charged or released by 08:20 on Wednesday, unless a superintendent authorises an extension."],"a":3,"e":"Detention without charge is limited to **24 hours** from the **relevant time**, usually **arrival** at the station (PACE s.41). A superintendent's extension to 36 hours, and any warrant of further detention, is available only for an **indictable** offence, so for a **summary-only** offence no extension is possible. The 24 hours run from arrival at 08:00, not from authorisation at 08:20, which is the start point only for **reviews**."},{"t":"police-interview","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"You are attending the interview of your client Fiona, who is suspected of theft. Police disclosure has been minimal and you advise her, on the recording, not to answer a question about her bank account. The interviewing officer says this is obstruction and that he will have you removed from the interview.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether you can be removed?","o":["You cannot be removed for this, as advising a client not to answer a question is a proper part of your role.","You can be removed by the interviewing officer, because advising silence on a specific question obstructs the investigation.","You can be removed by the custody officer, because you intervened while the interview was being recorded.","You cannot be removed in any circumstances, because Fiona has an absolute right to have her solicitor present.","You can be removed, because that advice should have been given in private before the interview began."],"a":0,"e":"Under **Code C Note 6D**, the solicitor's only role is to **protect and advance the client's legal rights**, and this includes **advising the client not to reply** to particular questions. A solicitor may be removed only if their conduct **prevents or unreasonably obstructs** proper questions being put, for example by answering for the client, and only on the decision of a **superintendent** (or an inspector if none is readily available). The right to a solicitor is not absolute in this respect, but nothing you did justifies removal."},{"t":"identification","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Gus is arrested after a fight outside a nightclub in which a man was injured by a thrown bottle. A door supervisor says he saw Gus throw it. In interview, Gus admits that he threw the bottle but says he did so in self-defence because the man was attacking him. The officer in the case asks whether a video identification should be held.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["A video identification must be held, because Gus disputes the door supervisor's account of what happened.","An identification parade must be held, because the witness saw Gus at close range under good lighting.","A procedure must be held, because one is mandatory whenever a witness claims to have identified a suspect.","A confrontation should be held, because Gus will by now be known to the witness who saw the fight.","No procedure need be held, because Gus admits being present and the dispute is about what he did."],"a":4,"e":"Under **Code D para 3.12**, a procedure must normally be held where a witness may be able to identify a suspect who **disputes being the person seen**. It need not be held where it would **serve no useful purpose**, for example where the suspect **admits being at the scene** and gives an account, and the eyewitness sees nothing that **contradicts** it. Gus accepts he is the person who threw the bottle and disputes only **why** he did so, so identity is not in issue and no procedure is needed. Disagreeing with the witness's account of what happened is not a dispute about identity, and confrontation is only ever a last resort."},{"t":"bail","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Harry, aged 32, appears before the magistrates in October 2026 charged with theft of £400 of goods from his employer. He has three previous convictions for failing to surrender to bail, the most recent two years ago. The prosecution objects to bail on the ground that there are substantial grounds to believe he would fail to surrender. All parties agree that, if convicted, there is no real prospect of any custodial sentence other than a suspended sentence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court should deal with the objection?","o":["The court may refuse bail on that ground, because a suspended sentence is still a custodial sentence for this purpose.","The objection cannot be relied on, as there is no real prospect of custody other than a suspended sentence.","The court may refuse bail, because the no real prospect of custody rule applies only to summary-only offences.","The court must remand Harry in custody, because he has more than one previous conviction for failing to surrender.","The court may refuse bail on that ground, because the rule applies only after conviction while reports are prepared."],"a":1,"e":"Under **BA 1976 Sch 1 Part I para 1A**, as amended by the **Sentencing Act 2026 s.44** from **22 March 2026**, the main exceptions (fail to surrender, offending, interference), para 2A and para 6 **do not apply** where the defendant is **18 or over** and there is **no real prospect** of a **custodial sentence other than a suspended sentence**. A likely suspended sentence therefore no longer lets the prosecution rely on those exceptions, and the rule now applies **after conviction** too. Conditions can still be imposed to manage any risk."},{"t":"first-hearings","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Ivy, aged 40, has worked as a nursery assistant for 15 years and has no previous convictions. She is charged with theft of £120 from a colleague's handbag and intends to plead not guilty. She earns a modest wage and does not receive any benefits. She applies for a representation order in the magistrates' court.\n\nWhich interests of justice factors best support her application?","o":["That she is likely to lose her liberty, because theft is an imprisonable offence carrying up to seven years.","That the case involves a substantial question of law, because her dishonesty will be in issue at trial.","That she is likely to lose her livelihood and suffer serious damage to her reputation if convicted.","That she may be unable to understand the proceedings, because she has never been to court before.","That the interests of justice test is automatically satisfied for any either-way offence in either court."],"a":2,"e":"The **interests of justice** factors in **LASPO 2012 s.17(2)** include likely **loss of livelihood** and **serious damage to reputation**, the latter usually relevant where the defendant is of **good character** and the offence involves **dishonesty**. A conviction for theft would almost certainly end Ivy's career in childcare. Loss of liberty is unlikely for a first-time minor theft, dishonesty is a question of fact rather than law, and the test is automatically met only in the **Crown Court**. She must still pass the **means test** separately."},{"t":"case-management-disclosure","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Jack is being tried in the magistrates' court for assault. His defence is self-defence. His solicitor believes the police hold CCTV footage from a nearby shop which might show the complainant attacking first, but the prosecution has said there is no undisclosed material that meets the disclosure test. Jack has not served a defence statement.\n\nWhat should Jack's solicitor do to obtain an order for disclosure of the footage?","o":["Serve a defence statement, then apply to the court for an order for disclosure under s.8 CPIA 1996.","Apply at once under s.8 CPIA 1996, because a defence statement is only voluntary in the magistrates' court.","Ask the court to exclude the prosecution's evidence under s.78 PACE until the footage is disclosed.","Wait until the trial and then cross-examine the officer in the case about the existence of the footage.","Serve a defence witness notice naming the shop owner, which obliges the prosecution to disclose the footage."],"a":0,"e":"A defence statement is **voluntary** in the magistrates' court (**CPIA s.6**), but the defendant can apply for **specific disclosure under s.8** only **after serving a defence statement**. Serving one also triggers the prosecutor's **continuing duty** to review disclosure in light of the defence case (s.7A). s.78 is about excluding prosecution evidence, not obtaining undisclosed material, and a witness notice imposes no disclosure duty on the prosecution."},{"t":"hearsay-confessions","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Kofi is charged with robbery. The key prosecution witness, Lena, gave a full statement to police but has since emigrated to Australia. She is willing to help but cannot afford to travel back. The court has facilities for her to give evidence by live video link from Australia. The prosecution applies to read her statement under s.116 CJA 2003.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the defence's best argument against the application?","o":["Her statement is inadmissible, because hearsay can never be the main evidence relied on against a defendant.","A witness outside the UK can be dealt with only through the interests of justice gateway, not s.116.","Her statement is admissible under s.116 only if she is unwilling to give evidence because of fear.","The outside the UK condition is not met, because her attendance by live link is reasonably practicable.","Her statement is inadmissible under s.116, because she is neither dead nor unfit to be a witness."],"a":3,"e":"**s.116(2)(c)** allows first-hand hearsay where the maker is **outside the UK** and it is **not reasonably practicable to secure their attendance**, and the court should consider a **live link** first. If Lena can testify by video link, attendance is practicable, so the condition is not met. Hearsay can be the **sole or decisive** evidence with proper safeguards (R v Horncastle), and s.116 has five alternative reasons, of which fear is only one."},{"t":"bad-character","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Lewis is on trial for theft from a shop. Giving evidence in chief, he tells the jury: 'I've never been in trouble with the police in my life. I'm not that sort of person.' In fact he has two convictions for theft from shops five years ago, which the prosecution had not sought to adduce.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the prosecution's position?","o":["It may adduce them through gateway (g), because Lewis has attacked another person's character.","It cannot adduce them, because it did not give notice of the convictions before the trial began.","It may apply to adduce them through gateway (f), but only so far as necessary to correct the false impression.","It may adduce them, but Lewis can apply under s.101(3) to exclude them as unfair because they are old.","It may adduce them only through gateway (e), because they go to the question of his credibility."],"a":2,"e":"Under **s.101(1)(f) and s.105 CJA 2003**, where the defendant gives a **false or misleading impression** about himself, the prosecution may adduce evidence to **correct** it, going **no further than necessary**. The **s.101(3)** fairness exclusion applies only to gateways **(d) and (g)**, not (f), though s.78 PACE may be argued. Lewis has attacked no one, so (g) does not apply, and gateway (e) is available only to a **co-defendant**."},{"t":"trial-procedure","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"After a five-day Crown Court trial for assault, the jury of 12 retires at 11:00. At 12:50 the foreman sends a note saying they cannot all agree, but that 10 of them agree on a verdict. The prosecution asks the judge to give a majority direction at once.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the judge should do?","o":["Give a majority direction now, because a verdict of 10 to 2 is permitted where the jury has 12 members.","Discharge the jury, because the foreman has said that they cannot reach a unanimous verdict.","Accept a majority verdict of 10 to 2 immediately, because the note shows the jury cannot all agree.","Direct the jury that only a unanimous verdict can ever be accepted in a Crown Court trial for assault.","Decline to give a majority direction yet, because the jury has not deliberated for long enough."],"a":4,"e":"A jury must first try for a **unanimous** verdict. A **majority verdict** (11-1 or **10-2** with 12 jurors) may be accepted only after at least **2 hours** of deliberation under the Juries Act 1974 s.17, which in practice is **2 hours and 10 minutes**. The jury has been out only 1 hour 50 minutes, so a majority direction is premature. Discharging the jury this early would also be wrong."},{"t":"sentencing","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Kirsty, aged 29, is convicted by magistrates in October 2026 of theft from a shop committed in August 2026. At the time of the theft she was subject to a community order imposed for an earlier offence. The court decides that the custody threshold is passed and that the shortest appropriate term is 4 months. She is not in custody and there is no risk of harm to any particular person.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the court's approach to suspension?","o":["The court must suspend the sentence unless there are exceptional circumstances, because the term is 12 months or less.","The presumption does not apply, as she offended while subject to a supervision order, but the court may still suspend.","The court cannot suspend the sentence, because she committed the offence while subject to a community order.","The presumption applies because it turns on the date of the offence, which was after 22 March 2026.","The court must impose immediate custody, because offending during a community order is a statutory aggravating factor."],"a":1,"e":"From **22 March 2026**, for offenders **convicted on or after that date**, a custodial sentence of **12 months or less** on an adult **must be suspended** unless there are exceptional circumstances (**Sentencing Act 2026 s.1**). The presumption **does not apply** where, among other things, the offence was committed while the defendant was subject to a **supervision order** such as a community order, but the court keeps its ordinary **discretion** to suspend. The relevant date is the date of **conviction**, not the offence."},{"t":"appeals","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Lorna is tried in the magistrates' court for a public order offence. At the close of the prosecution case, the magistrates accept a defence submission that the offence requires proof that someone was actually caused distress, and acquit her. The CPS believes the magistrates misinterpreted the elements of the offence.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate way for the CPS to challenge the acquittal?","o":["Apply to the magistrates within 21 days to state a case for the opinion of the High Court.","Appeal to the Crown Court for a full rehearing within 15 business days of the acquittal.","Ask the Attorney General to refer the acquittal to the Court of Appeal on a point of law.","Appeal to the Court of Appeal, with leave, within 28 days of the date of the acquittal.","Apply for judicial review within 3 months, on the basis that the magistrates' decision was irrational."],"a":0,"e":"Either party may appeal by way of **case stated** to the **High Court** where the magistrates were **wrong in law** or acted in excess of jurisdiction (**MCA 1980 s.111**), applying within **21 days**; it is the main way the **prosecution** challenges a magistrates' **acquittal**. The prosecution has **no right of appeal to the Crown Court**. An Attorney General's reference on a point of law and appeals to the Court of Appeal concern trials on indictment in the Crown Court, and judicial review suits procedural unfairness rather than an error of law on the facts found."},{"t":"youth-court","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Max, aged 16, appears in the youth court charged with robbery of a mobile phone from another teenager, a grave crime. He has no previous convictions. On the prosecution's case, he pushed the victim and took the phone without any weapon. The prosecution invites the youth court to send the case to the Crown Court.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the test the youth court should apply?","o":["It must send the case, because robbery is an indictable-only offence when committed by an adult.","It should send the case if any detention is possible, because robbery carries life imprisonment for an adult.","It should keep the case in the youth court only if Max consents to being tried there.","It should send the case only if there is a real prospect of a sentence beyond the 2-year DTO maximum.","It must send the case if the prosecution considers that the youth court's powers are inadequate."],"a":3,"e":"For a **grave crime**, the youth court should send the case to the Crown Court only if there is a **real prospect** that the sentence needed is **long-term detention** under **Sentencing Code s.250** beyond the **2-year** DTO maximum, assessed on the prosecution's version of the facts. Otherwise the youth court keeps the case, as the court best equipped to deal with children. A child **never** has a right to elect, and robbery is not one of the offences that must be sent, such as homicide."},{"t":"police-station-detention","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Nadia is arrested for burglary of commercial premises and arrives at the police station at 09:00 on Monday. Her detention is authorised at 09:20. Reviews take place on time. At 07:00 on Tuesday a superintendent is asked to extend her detention. He is satisfied the investigation is being conducted diligently and expeditiously and that further detention is necessary to obtain evidence by questioning.\n\nUntil when can the superintendent authorise Nadia's detention without charge?","o":["09:00 on Tuesday","21:20 on Tuesday","21:00 on Tuesday","09:00 on Wednesday","09:00 on Friday"],"a":2,"e":"A **superintendent** may extend detention for an **indictable** offence (burglary is either way) up to **36 hours** from the **relevant time**, which here is arrival at **09:00 Monday**, so to **21:00 Tuesday** (PACE s.42). The extension must be given **after the second review** (due by 00:20 Tuesday) and **before the 24 hours expire** at 09:00 Tuesday, which 07:00 satisfies. The clock does not run from authorisation, and 96 hours requires warrants from the **magistrates**."},{"t":"police-interview","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Oscar, aged 34, has a learning disability and is arrested for criminal damage. His sister attends as his appropriate adult and you attend as his solicitor. Before the interview, his sister says she wants to be present while you take Oscar's instructions so that she can help him explain things.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["His sister must be present, because a vulnerable adult cannot consult a solicitor without an appropriate adult.","He may consult you privately without his sister, and what he says in front of her would not be privileged.","His sister may be present, and anything Oscar says will remain privileged because she is acting as his appropriate adult.","His sister should be replaced, because a relative cannot act as appropriate adult for a vulnerable adult.","You should act as appropriate adult yourself, so that the consultation remains privileged."],"a":1,"e":"A suspect may consult his solicitor **privately, without the appropriate adult** present (**Code C Note 1E**), and conversations with an appropriate adult are **not covered by legal privilege**. The appropriate adult must be present for the **interview**, but not for the legal consultation. A **relative** can act as appropriate adult for a vulnerable adult, but the suspect's **solicitor** cannot."},{"t":"identification","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Pete is arrested for a street robbery and disputes being the robber. The victim describes the robber as having a large spider's web tattoo on his neck. Pete has such a tattoo, and the police cannot find enough other people with similar tattoos to appear in a video identification. The identification officer asks what should be done.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate course?","o":["Hold a video identification with the tattoo concealed or replicated on all the images shown.","Hold a confrontation, because a video identification is not practicable without people with similar tattoos.","Show the victim a photograph of Pete's tattoo to confirm whether it matches the robber's tattoo.","Dispense with an identification procedure, because the distinctive tattoo is enough to identify Pete.","Hold a group identification, because it is always more suitable where a suspect has a distinctive feature."],"a":0,"e":"**Video identification** is the **normal first choice** under Code D, and **distinctive features** such as a tattoo can be **concealed or replicated** on all the images so the suspect does not stand out (Code D Annex A). **Confrontation** is a **last resort** only where nothing else is practicable. Showing the witness an image of the known suspect would breach Code D, and a procedure is still needed because identity is disputed."},{"t":"bail","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Rhiannon, aged 27, is charged in October 2026 with an either-way offence of supplying cannabis. The prosecution opposes bail on the ground that she would commit further offences on bail, pointing to two recent supply convictions; an immediate custodial sentence is a real prospect. Rhiannon is six months pregnant and is the primary carer for her disabled mother.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court should treat her pregnancy and caring responsibilities?","o":["They are irrelevant to the bail decision and can be raised only as personal mitigation if she is convicted.","They mean that bail must be granted, because a pregnant defendant cannot be remanded in custody.","They prevent the prosecution relying on the offending exception, because there is no real prospect of custody.","They are relevant only if she proves on the balance of probabilities that a remand would harm her mother.","The court must have regard to them in deciding whether there are substantial grounds to believe she would offend."],"a":4,"e":"From **22 March 2026**, **BA 1976 Sch 1 Part I para 9(ba)** requires the court, when deciding whether an exception is made out, to have regard to whether the defendant is **pregnant**, is the **primary carer** for another person, or has been a **victim of domestic abuse**. These are factors to weigh alongside the nature of the offence, the defendant's record and community ties; they do not oblige the court to grant bail. As immediate custody is a real prospect, the no real prospect rule does not apply, and the defendant bears no burden of proof here."},{"t":"allocation-sending","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Sian, aged 45, is charged with an either-way offence of fraud. She indicates a not guilty plea and the magistrates decide the case is suitable for summary trial. Before deciding whether to consent to summary trial, she asks for an indication of sentence. The court indicates that a non-custodial sentence would be more likely if she pleaded guilty now. She then pleads guilty.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the court's powers?","o":["The court may impose a suspended sentence order, because a suspended sentence is not immediate custody.","The court may commit her for sentence if, after reading a pre-sentence report, it decides its powers are inadequate.","The court cannot impose custody and cannot commit her for sentence on the basis its powers are inadequate.","The indication is not binding once a plea is entered, so the court may impose any sentence within its powers.","The court must give her a reduction of one quarter rather than one third, because she first indicated not guilty."],"a":2,"e":"Where the court gives an **indication of sentence** that a **non-custodial** sentence is more likely and the defendant then **pleads guilty**, the court **cannot impose a custodial sentence** (which includes a suspended sentence) and cannot **commit for sentence** on the ground that its powers are inadequate (**MCA 1980 ss.20-20A**). The indication is not binding only if the defendant does **not** plead guilty. The guilty plea reduction is a separate question."},{"t":"case-management-disclosure","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Tobias has been sent to the Crown Court for trial on a charge of burglary. The prosecution has served its case and initial disclosure. Tobias says that at the time of the burglary he was at his friend Usha's flat, and Usha will give evidence to confirm this.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Tobias's defence statement must contain about this?","o":["Only a general statement that he denies the offence, as he may keep the detail of his defence confidential until trial.","Details of the alibi only if Tobias himself, rather than Usha, will give evidence about it at trial.","Usha's signed witness statement, which must be served together with the defence statement.","The nature of his defence, including the alibi, and Usha's name, address and date of birth, so far as known.","Nothing about Usha, because alibi witnesses need only be named in the separate defence witness notice."],"a":3,"e":"In the Crown Court a **defence statement** is **compulsory** (CPIA s.5), served within **28 days** of initial disclosure. Under **s.6A** it must set out the **nature of the defence** and, for an **alibi**, the **name, address and date of birth** of each alibi witness, or as much as is known. Failure to do so risks **comment and adverse inferences** under s.11. A witness's statement need not be served with it, and a defence witness notice does not replace the alibi details."},{"t":"evidence-identification-silence","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Vic is arrested near a burgled house. His jacket has fresh blood on the sleeve. In interview he is asked to explain the blood but is not given a special warning. He answers 'no comment'. At trial, he does not give evidence and puts forward no explanation, simply putting the prosecution to proof.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether an adverse inference can be drawn from his failure to explain the blood in interview?","o":["An inference can be drawn under s.36, because he failed to account for a mark on his clothing after his arrest.","No inference can be drawn, as there was no special warning and he has relied on no fact in his defence.","An inference can be drawn under s.34, because he could reasonably have been expected to explain the blood.","An inference can be drawn under s.37, because he was arrested near the scene of the burglary.","No inference can be drawn, because inferences are never permitted where the suspect has had legal advice."],"a":1,"e":"An inference under **s.36** (failure to account for an object, substance or **mark**) requires a **special warning** in ordinary language; without one, no s.36 inference can be drawn. **s.34** applies only where the defendant later **relies on a fact** he failed to mention, and a defendant who simply **puts the prosecution to proof** relies on no fact. Silence on legal advice does **not automatically** prevent an inference."},{"t":"hearsay-confessions","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Wendy is stabbed in a pub car park. Seconds later, bleeding and screaming, she shouts to the landlord: 'Darren stabbed me!'. Darren is charged with wounding with intent. Wendy has recovered but refuses to give evidence, saying she does not want to get involved; she is not in fear. The prosecution wants the landlord to give evidence of what Wendy shouted.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the admissibility of Wendy's statement?","o":["It is admissible under s.116 CJA 2003, because Wendy is unavailable to give evidence at the trial.","It is inadmissible, because hearsay is never admissible where its maker is alive and present in the UK.","It is admissible only as a confession, because it was made immediately after the offence was committed.","It is admissible under s.117 CJA 2003, because the landlord heard it while acting in the course of his business.","It may be admitted as res gestae, if she was so emotionally overpowered that concoction can be disregarded."],"a":4,"e":"Wendy's words are **hearsay**, as they are an out-of-court statement relied on for their truth. A witness who is simply **unwilling** is not within any **s.116** category (fear requires actual fear and leave), but the **res gestae** rule preserved by **s.118** admits a statement made by someone so **emotionally overpowered** by an event that **concoction or distortion** can be disregarded. A confession is a statement adverse to its maker, and s.117 covers business documents, not overheard remarks."},{"t":"trial-procedure","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Xander is on trial for sexual assault of a child, Yara, aged 8. The defence questions whether Yara is able to give evidence at all, and if so, whether she can be sworn.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["She is competent if she can understand questions and give understandable answers, and will give unsworn evidence.","She is not competent, because children under 10 cannot give evidence in criminal proceedings.","She is competent only if she understands the importance of telling the truth on oath, and will then be sworn.","She is competent but not compellable, because she is a child under the age of 14.","Her competence must be proved by the prosecution beyond reasonable doubt before she can give evidence."],"a":0,"e":"Under **YJCEA 1999 s.53**, everyone is competent **whatever their age** unless they cannot **understand questions** and give **answers that can be understood**; the party calling the witness proves competence on the **balance of probabilities** (s.54). Under **s.55**, a witness can be sworn only if aged **14 or over** and they understand the solemnity of the occasion, so Yara gives **unsworn** evidence. A competent child is also **compellable** like any ordinary witness."},{"t":"sentencing","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Zach, aged 38, pleads guilty in the Crown Court and is sentenced in October 2026 for a fraud committed in 2025. After reductions, the judge decides the appropriate sentence is 2 years and 6 months' imprisonment. The judge is considering whether to suspend it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the judge's powers?","o":["The judge cannot suspend the sentence, because the maximum term that can be suspended is 2 years.","The judge must suspend the sentence unless there are exceptional circumstances, because it is 3 years or less.","The judge cannot suspend it, because the offence was committed before the Sentencing Act 2026 came into force.","The judge may suspend it, because up to 3 years can be suspended for offenders convicted since 22 March 2026.","The judge may suspend the sentence, but the operational period cannot be longer than 2 years."],"a":3,"e":"Under **Sentencing Act 2026 s.2**, the maximum term that can be suspended is **3 years** where the defendant is **convicted on or after 22 March 2026** (previously **2 years**); the date of the **offence** is irrelevant. Where the suspended term is **more than 2 years**, the **operational period** can be up to **3 years**. The **presumption** of suspension applies only to sentences of **12 months or less**, so here suspension is a matter of **discretion**."},{"t":"appeals","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Aaron is convicted after trial in the magistrates' court of common assault and receives a conditional discharge. He wants to appeal to the Crown Court against conviction, because he says the magistrates should have believed his account of self-defence.\n\nWhich of the following is the most important risk you should warn him about?","o":["He will need leave from the Crown Court, which is rarely granted for appeals that are only on the facts.","The Crown Court can impose any sentence up to the statutory maximum for the offence, as if he had been convicted on indictment.","If convicted again on rehearing, he can be given any sentence the magistrates could have imposed, including a heavier one.","He will lose his right to appeal by way of case stated if the Crown Court dismisses his appeal.","The prosecution may cross-appeal to the Crown Court against the leniency of the conditional discharge."],"a":2,"e":"An appeal from the magistrates to the Crown Court under **MCA 1980 s.108** is a **full rehearing**, needs **no leave** if brought within **15 business days** of sentence, and the Crown Court may impose **any sentence the magistrates could have imposed**, which can be **more severe** than the original (Senior Courts Act 1981 s.48). Its powers are capped at the **magistrates'** powers, not the indictment maximum. The prosecution has no right to appeal against a magistrates' sentence to the Crown Court."},{"t":"youth-court","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Bethan, aged 15, pleads not guilty in the youth court to theft, an imprisonable offence. She is found guilty after a trial. She has no previous findings of guilt. The court does not consider that custody, a hospital order or a discharge is appropriate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the court's sentencing options?","o":["The court must make a referral order, because she has no previous findings of guilt and the offence is imprisonable.","The court may make a referral order at its discretion, because she is a first-time offender in court.","A referral order is not available because she did not plead guilty, so the main community option is a YRO.","The court must impose a detention and training order, because theft is an imprisonable offence.","The court must remit her to the adult magistrates' court for sentence, because she was tried in the youth court."],"a":2,"e":"A **referral order** requires a **guilty plea** to at least one offence; the **compulsory** conditions also require an **imprisonable** offence, a guilty plea and no previous convictions. Because Bethan was convicted **after a not guilty plea**, a referral order is **not available at all**, and the main community option is a **youth rehabilitation order**. A DTO requires the custody threshold to be passed, which the court has rejected."},{"t":"police-station-detention","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Carl is arrested for burglary of a house and arrives at the police station at 14:00. Stolen jewellery has not been found. Carl asks for his partner to be told of his arrest. An inspector reasonably believes that if the partner is told, she will move the jewellery before police can search their home.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the right can be delayed?","o":["The right cannot be delayed at all, because the right to have someone informed of an arrest is absolute.","The inspector may delay it, because the offence is indictable and recovery of property would be hindered, for up to 36 hours.","Only a superintendent can authorise delay of this right, and then only for up to 24 hours from his arrival at the station.","The inspector may delay the right until Carl is charged, however long that takes.","The right can be delayed only if Carl is suspected of a summary-only offence."],"a":1,"e":"Under **PACE s.56** and **Code C Annex B**, an **inspector** or above may delay the right to have someone informed where the defendant is detained for an **indictable** offence, is **not yet charged**, and the officer has reasonable grounds to believe exercising the right **will**, among other things, **hinder the recovery of property** obtained by the offence. The maximum delay is **36 hours** from the **relevant time**. A **superintendent** is needed to delay **legal advice**, not this right."},{"t":"police-interview","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Dev and Eric are arrested together for an assault. You are the duty solicitor and see Dev first. He says Eric threw the only punch and he tried to pull Eric away. The custody officer then asks you to advise Eric as well, and mentions that Eric has told officers on arrest that Dev threw the punch. You have not yet spoken to Eric.\n\nWhat should you do?","o":["Act for both of them, provided you advise each of them to give a 'no comment' interview.","Act for Eric as well, but tell him what Dev said so that he can answer the allegation in interview.","Act for both, because the duty solicitor cannot refuse a request from the custody officer to advise a suspect.","Stop acting for Dev and act only for Eric, because Eric's account seems more likely to be true.","Decline to act for Eric, because the clients' interests conflict, and keep what Dev told you confidential."],"a":4,"e":"Under the **SRA Code of Conduct para 6.2**, a solicitor must not act for two clients whose interests **conflict**, as where co-suspects **blame each other**. You must also keep **Dev's information confidential** (para 6.3) and must not use it for Eric. Having already taken Dev's instructions, you should continue to act for Dev and decline Eric, who should get a separate solicitor; agreeing a joint no comment strategy does not remove the conflict."},{"t":"bail","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Faye is charged with assault occasioning actual bodily harm on her neighbour. At her first hearing the magistrates grant her bail with conditions. The prosecutor believes she will try to intimidate the neighbour and wants to appeal against the grant of bail.\n\nWhat must the prosecutor do?","o":["Give oral notice at the end of the hearing before Faye is released, and written notice within 2 hours.","Apply to the Crown Court within 2 business days, during which time Faye remains on conditional bail.","Ask the magistrates to reconsider their decision at the next hearing on the same information.","Give written notice of appeal within 48 hours, after which Faye will be arrested and held in custody.","Obtain a certificate of full argument from the magistrates and then appeal to the Crown Court."],"a":0,"e":"Under the **Bail (Amendment) Act 1993 s.1**, the prosecution may appeal against a grant of bail for an **imprisonable** offence by giving **oral notice** at the **end of the hearing, before the defendant is released**, and **written notice within 2 hours**. The defendant stays in custody until the Crown Court hears the appeal within **48 hours** (excluding weekends and bank holidays). Reconsideration under s.5B needs **new information**, and the certificate of full argument relates to a **defence** appeal against a refusal of bail."},{"t":"first-hearings","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Gabriel, aged 23, is charged with robbery, an indictable-only offence, and appears before the magistrates for the first time. He tells his solicitor he wants to plead guilty and wants the maximum possible reduction in sentence.\n\nWhich of the following is the best advice?","o":["He should plead guilty in the magistrates' court, which will then commit him to the Crown Court for sentence.","He should wait until the plea and trial preparation hearing, as the full one third reduction is available there.","He will receive a one third reduction whenever he pleads guilty, provided he does so before the trial date.","No plea will be taken, but he should indicate an intention to plead guilty now to keep the full one third reduction.","He should elect trial in the magistrates' court, so that he can plead guilty there and be sentenced at once."],"a":3,"e":"For an **indictable-only** offence, **no plea is taken** in the magistrates' court; the case is **sent forthwith** to the Crown Court under **CDA s.51**. The **first stage** of proceedings for the guilty plea guideline is normally the **first hearing in the magistrates' court**, so the defendant should **indicate** an intention to plead guilty there to keep the **one third** reduction. A plea first indicated at the PTPH attracts at most **one quarter**, and there is no election for an indictable-only offence."},{"t":"allocation-sending","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Hollie, aged 30, is charged with stealing a jumper worth £45 from a department store. She has no previous convictions. She denies the offence and tells her solicitor she would prefer a jury to decide her case.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["It is treated as a summary offence, but as an adult she can elect Crown Court trial and it will then be sent.","She must be tried in the magistrates' court, because low-value shoplifting is summary only with no right of election.","The magistrates will decide whether the case is suitable for summary trial, and she may elect only if they say it is.","She must be tried in the magistrates' court, because the Crime and Policing Act 2026 made all shop theft summary only.","She can elect Crown Court trial only if the value of the goods taken is over £200."],"a":0,"e":"Theft from a shop of goods worth **£200 or less** is treated as a **summary offence** under **MCA 1980 s.22A**, but an adult must be asked whether she wants to **elect Crown Court trial**, and if she does, the case is treated as **either way** and sent. Contrast **low-value criminal damage**, where there is **no** right to elect. The **Crime and Policing Act 2026** provision repealing s.22A was **not in force** at the 11 September 2026 cut-off, and it would have made shop theft either way, not summary."},{"t":"evidence-identification-silence","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Ian is charged with burglary. The only evidence is from his neighbour of ten years, Jo, who says she recognised him climbing out of a window across the street at dusk. Ian accepts that Jo knows him well but says she is mistaken and that he was elsewhere.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the judge should deal with Jo's evidence?","o":["No warning is needed, because a recognition by someone who knows the defendant well is not an identification.","Withdraw the case from the jury, because any identification made at dusk is poor and unsupported.","No warning is needed, because the defence case is in substance that Jo is lying about seeing him.","Exclude Jo's evidence, because no identification procedure was held before the trial.","Give a Turnbull warning, because the case depends on an identification said to be mistaken, and recognition can be wrong."],"a":4,"e":"The **Turnbull** guidelines apply wherever the case depends **wholly or substantially** on an identification the defence says is **mistaken**, and they cover **recognition** too, because mistakes are made even in recognising **friends and relatives**. The judge should warn of the special need for caution and point to the **ADVOKATE** circumstances, such as the light and distance. The case need be withdrawn only if the identification is **poor and unsupported**, and in an undisputed recognition case Code D usually requires no procedure."},{"t":"hearsay-confessions","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Kai is interviewed about a burglary. The court later finds that the officer told Kai he would get bail only if he admitted the offence, and excludes Kai's resulting confession under s.76(2)(b) PACE. In the confession, Kai had said the stolen laptop was hidden in his garage, where police then found it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the use the prosecution can make of the laptop?","o":["It cannot rely on the laptop at all, because it was discovered as a result of an inadmissible confession.","It can rely on the laptop being found in his garage, but not on the fact it was found because of what Kai said.","It can tell the jury that Kai led them to the laptop, because finding it proves the confession was true.","It can rely on the laptop only if it proves beyond reasonable doubt that the confession was reliable.","The laptop is admissible only if the defence agrees to it being adduced in evidence at trial."],"a":1,"e":"Under **PACE s.76(4)**, excluding a confession does **not** affect the admissibility of **facts discovered** as a result of it, so the finding of the laptop in Kai's garage is admissible. But the prosecution **cannot say** the laptop was found **because of** what Kai said, unless Kai himself raises it. The truth of the confession is irrelevant to exclusion under s.76(2), which turns on how it was obtained."},{"t":"bad-character","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Lucas is charged with possession of cocaine with intent to supply after police find drugs in his car. His defence is that the officers who searched the car planted the drugs to get him convicted. He has previous convictions for burglary and for perverting the course of justice.\n\nWhich of the following is the best advice to Lucas about the effect of running this defence?","o":["Alleging the drugs were planted is merely a denial of the offence, so it cannot make his convictions admissible.","His convictions will become admissible automatically, and the court has no power to exclude them as unfair.","His convictions can be admitted only through gateway (e), because the officers are prosecution witnesses.","He will attack the officers' character, opening gateway (g), though he can apply to exclude his convictions as unfair.","The prosecution can adduce his convictions only if the officers themselves have no previous convictions."],"a":3,"e":"Alleging that the **police fabricated or planted** evidence is an **attack on another person's character** (an imputation of an offence or reprehensible behaviour), which opens **gateway (g)** under **s.101(1)(g) and s.106 CJA 2003**. The defendant can apply under **s.101(3)** to exclude gateway (g) evidence if admitting it would have such an **adverse effect on fairness** that it ought not to be admitted. Gateway (e) is for **co-defendants** only, and a simple denial without an attack would not open (g)."},{"t":"trial-procedure","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Megan is to be tried at Cardiff Crown Court for theft. She is a first-language Welsh speaker and wants to give her evidence in Welsh. Her trial is listed for next month.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what should happen?","o":["The court should empanel a jury made up only of jurors who are able to speak and understand Welsh.","Megan must give her evidence in English, because the trial is taking place in the Crown Court.","Her solicitor should notify the court at the earliest opportunity, so translation can be arranged for jurors.","Megan may give evidence in Welsh only if she cannot speak English fluently enough to testify in it.","The whole trial must be conducted in Welsh once any party or witness chooses to speak it."],"a":2,"e":"Under the **Welsh Language Act 1993 s.22**, any party or witness in legal proceedings **in Wales** may **speak Welsh**, whether or not they can speak English. The defence should notify the court at the **earliest opportunity** so that case management directions can be made, such as listing before a Welsh-speaking judge and arranging **simultaneous translation**. There is **no power to select jurors** for their ability to speak Welsh, and the right is to speak Welsh, not to have the whole trial in Welsh."},{"t":"sentencing","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Nathan, aged 26, is convicted in June 2026 of a public order offence after a drunken fight outside a pub. He has a history of alcohol-related disorder in town centre pubs. The court decides that a community order is appropriate and wants to stop him from going into pubs and bars during the order.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the court's powers?","o":["The court cannot restrict his entry to pubs, because a community order may contain only unpaid work and rehabilitation activity requirements.","The court can restrict his entry to pubs only by imposing a suspended sentence order with requirements.","The court can achieve this only by a curfew, as no community requirement can stop him entering particular places.","The court may include such a requirement only if Nathan expressly consents to it at the hearing.","The court may include an exclusion requirement prohibiting him from entering specified pubs and bars for a set period."],"a":4,"e":"A community order may include an **exclusion requirement**, prohibiting the defendant from entering a specified place or area for a set period, so the court can ban Nathan from named pubs and bars (or a town-centre area). Any requirement must be **suitable** for the defendant and **proportionate** to the seriousness of the offence, and the order must include a **punitive** element (or a fine) unless that would be unjust. Exclusion does not need the defendant's consent, does not require a suspended sentence, and is not limited to unpaid work or rehabilitation activity; a curfew is only one option. The Sentencing Act 2026's specific ban on entering **drinking establishments** starts on **1 October 2026**, after the exam cut-off."},{"t":"sentencing","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Owen pleads guilty to assault occasioning actual bodily harm on the basis that he punched the victim once. The prosecution says he punched the victim and then kicked him repeatedly while he was on the ground, which would place the offence in a higher culpability category. The prosecution does not accept Owen's basis of plea.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what should happen?","o":["The judge must sentence on the prosecution's version, because Owen has pleaded guilty to the offence charged.","A Newton hearing, at which Owen must prove his version on the balance of probabilities.","A Newton hearing without a jury, where the prosecution must make the judge sure of its version.","A trial before a jury, to decide which version of the facts is correct before sentence is passed.","The judge must sentence on Owen's version, because the prosecution cannot challenge a basis of plea."],"a":2,"e":"Where the defendant pleads guilty on a **basis** that differs from the prosecution case in a way that would **materially affect sentence**, the judge must either accept the defendant's version or hold a **Newton hearing** without a jury. The **prosecution** must prove its version to the **criminal standard**, so that the judge is **sure**. If the defendant's version is rejected, the guilty plea reduction is normally **halved**. The defendant bears the burden only for **extraneous mitigation** within his own knowledge."},{"t":"youth-court","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Pippa, aged 15, appears in the youth court for theft. A local newspaper reporter attends the hearing and wants to publish Pippa's name and school in a story about shoplifting in the town.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The newspaper may publish Pippa's name unless the court makes a discretionary order prohibiting it.","Restrictions apply automatically, so nothing likely to identify Pippa may be published unless lifted.","The reporter must leave, because the press are not permitted to attend hearings in the youth court.","The newspaper may publish the name of Pippa's school, but not her own name.","Reporting restrictions apply only if Pippa's parents ask the youth court to impose them."],"a":1,"e":"In the youth court, **automatic reporting restrictions** under **CYPA 1933 s.49** prevent publication of anything likely to **identify** the child, such as name, address, school or photograph; the court can lift them only in limited cases. The youth court is closed to the public, but the **press may attend**. Restrictions in the **adult** courts are **discretionary** (YJCEA 1999 s.45)."}],"ethics":[{"t":"principles-competence","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Marcus, a solicitor, acts for Fenmore Ltd in a dispute with a customer about the quality of goods supplied under a contract. Without checking the file, he writes to the customer's solicitor stating that Fenmore Ltd \"has never received any complaint about this product line\". In fact the file contains three written complaints about the same product. Marcus did not know about them because he did not look.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Marcus's conduct?","o":["He has not breached the SRA Principles, because he did not know that the statement was untrue when he made it.","He has acted dishonestly, because the statement was objectively untrue and ordinary decent people would regard making it without checking as dishonest.","He may have failed to act with integrity by recklessly making a misleading statement, even though he was not dishonest.","He has breached only the duty to provide a competent service, which concerns his own client and not the other side.","He has breached no duty, because a solicitor owes no professional obligations to the other party in a dispute."],"a":2,"e":"**Integrity** is wider than honesty: recklessly making a misleading statement without checking it is a classic example of conduct lacking integrity, and it also engages the duty **not to mislead others** (para 1.4). It is not necessarily **dishonest**, because under the Ivey test the first stage asks what Marcus actually knew, and he did not know of the complaints (Wingate v SRA). The options saying there is no breach ignore the integrity standard and the fact that para 1.4 protects third parties, not just clients."},{"t":"principles-competence","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Ruth, a conveyancing solicitor, forgets to submit her client's Stamp Duty Land Tax return on time and HMRC charges a penalty. When the client asks why, Ruth tells him that HMRC's online system was down on the deadline day, which is untrue. Ruth believes that such \"white lies\" are common in practice and harmless, because her firm will pay the penalty anyway.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Ruth has acted dishonestly?","o":["She has acted dishonestly, because she knew the explanation was false and ordinary decent people would regard it as dishonest.","She has not acted dishonestly, because she genuinely believed that such explanations were acceptable practice and did not realise others would disagree.","She has not acted dishonestly, because the client will suffer no financial loss once the firm pays the penalty.","She has not acted dishonestly, because the underlying mistake was an honest oversight and so is a competence matter only.","She has acted dishonestly only if the client relied on the explanation and suffered some loss as a result."],"a":0,"e":"Under the **Ivey test**, the tribunal first establishes what Ruth **actually knew** (that the explanation was false) and then asks whether her conduct was dishonest by the **standards of ordinary decent people**. There is **no requirement** that she realised others would see it as dishonest, so her belief that white lies are acceptable is irrelevant (Ivey v Genting Casinos). The original missed deadline was a competence issue, but covering it up with a lie is dishonest, and loss or reliance is not needed. She should have been **honest and open** with the client (para 7.11)."},{"t":"duty-to-court","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Hassan acts for Corran Ltd, the defendant in a breach of contract claim in the High Court. While preparing the list of documents for standard disclosure, Corran Ltd's managing director shows Hassan an internal email in which an engineer admits the machinery supplied was defective. The email is plainly within the scope of disclosure. The managing director instructs Hassan to leave it out of the list.\n\nWhat should Hassan do?","o":["Advise that the email must be disclosed and, if Corran Ltd refuses, cease to act without explaining why.","Leave the email out of the list, because his duty of confidentiality to Corran Ltd overrides the disclosure rules.","Disclose the email to the claimant's solicitors himself, without Corran Ltd's consent, to avoid misleading the court.","Leave the email out of the list, provided he makes no positive statement to the court about the quality of the machinery.","Continue to act and follow the instructions, because compliance with disclosure is the client's responsibility rather than his."],"a":0,"e":"A solicitor must not **misuse or conceal evidence** (para 2.1) or be complicit in misleading the court (para 1.4), and a list of documents that omits a disclosable document would mislead. Hassan must advise that the disclosure duty overrides the client's wishes and, if the client refuses, **withdraw**. **Confidentiality** (para 6.3) still applies, so he must not reveal the email or his reasons himself; he simply ceases to act for professional reasons. Staying silent while serving a misleading list is not a neutral option."},{"t":"instructions-capacity-service","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Fallow & Co acts for Mr Lewis on the sale of his flat. Contracts are ready to exchange when Mr Lewis suffers a severe stroke. His doctors confirm that he now lacks capacity to make decisions about his property. He made no lasting or enduring power of attorney. His son asks the firm to exchange contracts on his father's behalf, saying it is what his father wanted.\n\nWhat is the firm's position?","o":["It may exchange on the son's instructions, because as next of kin he is authorised to act for a parent who lacks capacity.","Its authority has ended; it may exchange only on the instructions of someone with legal authority, such as a court-appointed deputy.","It may exchange, because Mr Lewis gave clear instructions to sell while he still had capacity and those instructions remain effective until the sale is complete.","It may exchange if a doctor confirms in writing that the sale would be in Mr Lewis's best interests.","It must exchange, because the buyer is entitled to rely on the agreed timetable for exchange."],"a":1,"e":"The retainer depends on a client able to instruct the solicitor, so when a client **loses capacity** and there is **no attorney or deputy**, the firm's **authority to act ends**. Only a properly authorised person, such as an attorney under a registered LPA or a **deputy appointed by the Court of Protection**, can give instructions for him (para 3.1). Being next of kin gives the son no authority, earlier instructions do not survive the loss of capacity for a step not yet taken, and a doctor's view cannot replace legal authority."},{"t":"conflicts-of-interest","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Mere & Co acts for Brightwell Ltd. The board instructs Leona, a partner, to draft a new service contract for the company's managing director, Sanjay. Sanjay then asks Leona to advise him personally on whether the notice period and bonus terms are fair to him, saying the company will not mind.\n\nWhat should Leona do?","o":["Advise both Brightwell Ltd and Sanjay, provided both give informed consent in writing.","Advise Sanjay, because as managing director he is the person who gives the firm its instructions.","Advise both, because a director's service contract is usually on standard terms.","Stop acting for Brightwell Ltd and act for Sanjay alone, because he is the individual most affected.","Decline to advise Sanjay on the contract and recommend that he takes separate legal advice."],"a":4,"e":"The company wants the best terms for itself and Sanjay wants the best terms for himself, so their interests in the **same matter conflict** (para 6.2). Neither exception applies: they do not have a **substantially common interest** in the terms and are not **competing for the same objective**, so written consent alone cannot allow Leona to act for both. The client is the **company**, not the director who passes on its instructions (para 8.1). Sanjay should be advised to take **separate advice**."},{"t":"conflicts-of-interest","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Penn Solicitors acts for Tom on his house purchase and for his lender, Northshire Bank, on its standard terms. The lender's instructions require the firm to report any incentive given by the seller. Tom tells the firm that the seller has privately agreed to pay him £15,000 after completion, which will not be mentioned to the lender, and instructs the firm to say nothing.\n\nWhat should the firm do?","o":["Continue to act for both, because the mortgage is on the lender's standard terms and so no conflict arises.","Continue to act for Tom only and tell Northshire Bank about the payment, because the duty to the lender overrides confidentiality.","Tell Northshire Bank about the payment and continue to act for both, because the lender is also the firm's client.","Continue to act for both, because the payment is a private matter between Tom and the seller.","Tell Tom the firm cannot continue unless the payment is disclosed to the lender and, if he refuses, stop acting for both."],"a":4,"e":"Acting for borrower and lender on standard terms is normally permitted, but a **conflict** arises once the firm learns the borrower is **misleading the lender**: the firm cannot both follow Tom's instructions and report to the lender as required. The information is **confidential** to Tom (para 6.3), so the firm cannot pass it on without consent, but it cannot act for the lender while withholding something material (para 6.4). The answer is to ask Tom to consent to disclosure and, if he refuses, **cease to act for both**; continuing would make the firm complicit in misleading the lender (para 1.4)."},{"t":"confidentiality-disclosure","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Dunmore Ltd, a manufacturer, is sued after a machine at its factory injured a visitor. After receiving the letter of claim, Dunmore Ltd's solicitors instructed an engineer to report on the cause of the accident for use in the expected proceedings. The claimant now seeks disclosure of several documents.\n\nWhich document is most likely to be protected by legal professional privilege?","o":["The machine's maintenance log, kept by Dunmore Ltd's staff for several years before the accident.","The engineer's report commissioned by the solicitors after the letter of claim, for use in the litigation.","Minutes of a Dunmore Ltd board meeting held before the accident discussing the cost of new safety guards.","An email from Dunmore Ltd's finance director to its auditors setting out the likely cost of the claim.","A letter from Dunmore Ltd to the visitor, sent the day after the accident, apologising for what happened."],"a":1,"e":"**Litigation privilege** protects confidential communications between a lawyer or client and **third parties**, such as experts, made when litigation is **in progress or reasonably in prospect** and for the **dominant purpose** of that litigation. The engineer's report fits exactly. The maintenance log and board minutes are pre-existing business records, the email to auditors is not a communication with a lawyer or for the dominant purpose of litigation, and a letter to the other party is not confidential. Those documents may be **confidential**, but confidentiality alone does not protect them from disclosure ordered by the court."},{"t":"firms-compliance-protections","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Three years ago Cobb & Partners, a firm of solicitors, closed down with no successor practice. Kestrel Ltd has just discovered that a shareholders' agreement drafted negligently by the firm in 2022 has caused it a loss of £400,000. No one at the firm acted dishonestly.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Kestrel Ltd's most likely route to recovery?","o":["An application to the SRA Compensation Fund, because the firm has closed and cannot pay.","A claim against the Solicitors Indemnity Fund, because the firm is no longer in practice.","A complaint to the Legal Ombudsman, which can award compensation for the full loss.","A negligence claim met by the run-off cover under the closed firm's indemnity insurance.","No recovery is available, because the firm closed before the claim was made."],"a":3,"e":"When a firm closes with no successor, its **professional indemnity insurance** provides **six years' run-off cover**, so a negligence claim made three years after closure is met by that insurer. The **Solicitors Indemnity Fund** deals only with claims arising **after** the six-year run-off period. The **Compensation Fund** is a discretionary fund of last resort for **dishonesty** or failure to account, not negligence, and the Legal Ombudsman's remedies are capped at **£50,000** and it deals with poor service."},{"t":"trust-fairness-undertakings","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Aled acts for Mrs Pryce on the sale of her house. Before completion he gives the buyer's solicitor a written undertaking to discharge \"all subsisting charges registered against the title\" on completion. He redeems the first mortgage and sends the balance of the proceeds to Mrs Pryce. He then discovers a second registered charge in favour of a finance company for £12,000, which he had overlooked. Mrs Pryce says she cannot repay the money.\n\nWhat is Aled's position?","o":["He is not bound in respect of the second charge, because he did not know about it when he gave the undertaking.","He remains bound to ensure the second charge is discharged, even if his firm has to fund the payment.","He is bound only if Mrs Pryce provides the money needed to redeem the second charge.","He is released from the undertaking if he promptly tells the buyer's solicitor what has happened.","His firm is bound by the undertaking, but he is not personally liable under it."],"a":1,"e":"A solicitor must **perform all undertakings** (para 1.3), and the obligation is **personal** to the solicitor as well as binding on the firm. It is no excuse that compliance has become **costly** or that the client cannot or will not pay, which is why undertakings should be limited to matters within the solicitor's control. An undertaking can be varied or released only with the **recipient's agreement**, not by simply notifying them, and Aled's lack of knowledge does not narrow wording that clearly covered all registered charges."},{"t":"client-money","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Grace, a solicitor, advises Holm Ltd on a new commercial lease and recommends that it insures the premises through a particular broker. Holm Ltd does so. The broker later pays Grace's firm a commission of £900 for the introduction. Holm Ltd has not been told about the commission.\n\nWhat should the firm do with the commission?","o":["Account to Holm Ltd for it, unless Holm Ltd agrees, once properly informed, that the firm may keep it.","Keep the commission, because it was paid by the broker rather than by Holm Ltd.","Keep the commission, provided it is recorded in the firm's accounts and shown to Holm Ltd on the firm's next bill.","Pay the commission to charity, because neither the firm nor the client is entitled to it.","Keep the commission, because it is less than the fees the firm charged Holm Ltd for the lease."],"a":0,"e":"Para 4.1 of the Code requires a solicitor to **properly account** to clients for any **financial benefit** received as a result of their instructions, **unless the client has agreed otherwise**. A broker's commission earned because Grace recommended the broker is such a benefit, so it belongs to Holm Ltd unless Holm Ltd, having been told about it, agrees the firm may keep it. Who paid it, how it is recorded and its size relative to the fees are all irrelevant, and there is no basis for giving it to charity."},{"t":"business-referrals-publicity","m":1,"p":"FLK1","q":"Claimline, a claims management company, offers to pass road traffic accident injury claims to Barr Solicitors. In return the firm would pay Claimline a \"monthly marketing fee\" of £3,000, which is calculated by reference to the number of claims it sends. Barr Solicitors proposes to tell each client about the arrangement in its client care letter.\n\nWhat is the best advice to Barr Solicitors?","o":["It may enter the arrangement, provided the fee-sharing agreement is made in writing and each client is told about the payment at the outset.","It may enter the arrangement, because a fee described as a marketing fee is not a referral fee.","It must not enter the arrangement, because personal injury referral fees are banned by statute whatever clients are told.","It may enter the arrangement, provided the firm's advice to each client remains independent of Claimline.","It may enter the arrangement, provided each client gives informed consent in writing to the payment."],"a":2,"e":"**Referral fees in personal injury claims are banned** by LASPO 2012 s.56, so disclosure, writing, independence and client consent cannot make the arrangement lawful. Calling the payment a \"marketing fee\" does not help: where it appears a referral fee has been paid, the SRA will **treat it as one unless the firm shows otherwise** (para 5.2), and a fee linked to the number of claims referred is plainly payment for referrals. The disclosure and written-agreement requirements of para 5.1 apply only where referral fees are lawful, such as in conveyancing."},{"t":"cooperation-reporting","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Kieran is a criminal defence solicitor. One Saturday evening he is involved in a scuffle in a pub and, after attending the police station voluntarily, accepts a simple caution for common assault. The incident had nothing to do with his work.\n\nWhat must Kieran do?","o":["Nothing, because the incident occurred in his private life and is unconnected with his practice.","Notify the SRA only if he is later charged with or convicted of an offence arising from the incident.","Disclose the caution to the SRA when he next renews his practising certificate.","Notify the SRA promptly that he has received a caution.","Tell his managing partner, who may then decide whether the SRA needs to know."],"a":3,"e":"Para 7.6 requires a solicitor to **notify the SRA promptly** if they are subject to any **criminal charge, conviction or caution**, subject to the Rehabilitation of Offenders Act 1974. A caution is expressly covered, so he need not wait for a charge or conviction, and the duty applies even though the conduct was outside practice. He could satisfy the duty by telling the firm's **COLP** on the understanding that the COLP will notify the SRA (para 7.12), but not by leaving it to a manager's discretion or waiting until renewal."},{"t":"complaints-legal-ombudsman","m":1,"p":"FLK2","q":"Whitby Solicitors acts for Mr Hale as executor of his late aunt's estate. Mrs Osei is the residuary beneficiary under the will but is not a client of the firm. She complained to the firm about two years of delay in administering the estate, and the firm sent its final response four weeks ago, giving details of the Legal Ombudsman and the six-month time limit.\n\nCan Mrs Osei take her complaint to the Legal Ombudsman?","o":["No, because only the executor, as the firm's client, may complain to the Legal Ombudsman about the firm's services to the estate.","No, because she must wait until eight weeks have passed since she complained to the firm.","Yes, because an estate's beneficiaries may complain about services provided to its personal representatives.","No, because complaints about delay by a solicitor must be made to the SRA instead.","Yes, but only if Mr Hale consents to her bringing the complaint."],"a":2,"e":"The Legal Ombudsman scheme allows **beneficiaries of an estate** to complain about services provided to the **personal representatives**, even though the beneficiary is not the firm's client. The eight-week wait applies only where the firm has **not** sent a final response, and here it has, so she has **six months** from that response. Delay is a **service** complaint for the Legal Ombudsman; the SRA deals with **misconduct**. No consent from the executor is required."},{"t":"principles-competence","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"The SRA investigates Darren, a solicitor in a commercial firm, after he created false board minutes to support a client's application for a loan. The SRA considers that his conduct was dishonest and that he should no longer be allowed to practise.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the sanctions available?","o":["The SRA may itself strike Darren off the roll, because a finding of dishonesty almost always leads to strike-off.","The SRA may itself impose an unlimited fine on Darren, because his conduct was dishonest.","The SRA may rebuke or fine him, but only the Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal can strike him off the roll.","The Legal Ombudsman may strike Darren off the roll if the lender complains about his conduct.","Only the High Court may sanction Darren, because solicitors are officers of the court."],"a":2,"e":"The SRA's own powers include a **rebuke**, a **fine up to £25,000** for individuals (unlimited only for economic crime matters such as money laundering failures) and conditions on a practising certificate. **Strike-off** and suspension, and unlimited fines generally, are reserved to the **Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal**, so the SRA must prosecute Darren there; a finding of dishonesty almost always leads to strike-off. The Legal Ombudsman deals with poor service, not discipline, and the High Court hears **appeals** from the SDT rather than acting at first instance."},{"t":"duty-to-court","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Jordan appears in the magistrates' court charged with shoplifting, represented by Asha, the duty solicitor. When asked, he gives the court his brother's name and date of birth. After the hearing he tells Asha the truth: he used his brother's details because he is on bail for another offence.\n\nWhat should Asha do?","o":["Continue to act, taking care not to refer to Jordan by name in court, because she has not herself misled the court.","Tell the court Jordan's true identity at the next hearing, because her duty to the court overrides confidentiality.","Continue to act, because she is under no duty to volunteer facts that are harmful to her client.","Cease to act and explain to the court that her client has given false details.","Ask Jordan to correct his details with the court and, if he refuses, cease to act without saying why."],"a":4,"e":"Giving the court a **false name or date of birth misleads the court**, and continuing to act would make Asha complicit (para 1.4). She must ask Jordan to correct the position and, if he will not, **withdraw**. Because of **confidentiality** (para 6.3) she must not tell the court the reason, only that she is withdrawing for professional reasons. The rule that a solicitor need not volunteer harmful facts does not help, because the court has already been actively misled."},{"t":"duty-to-court","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Owain acts for the defendant in a county court claim arising from a collision at a junction. During his investigations he identifies a passer-by who saw the collision and whose account would strongly support the claimant. The claimant's solicitors do not know about this witness, and Owain will not call her.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Owain's duty?","o":["He must tell the court about the witness, because he must draw its attention to matters likely to have a material effect on the outcome.","He must cease to act, because he cannot continue knowing of evidence that undermines his client's case.","He must tell the claimant's solicitors about the witness, but need not tell the court.","He may tell the court in his closing submissions that no independent witness saw the collision.","He need not tell the court or the claimant about the witness, provided he does not mislead the court about the evidence."],"a":4,"e":"A solicitor is **not required to volunteer facts** that harm the client, such as an unhelpful witness the other side has not found; the duty in para 2.7 to draw the court's attention to matters with a material effect concerns **law and procedural irregularities**, not facts. What Owain must never do is **mislead the court** (para 1.4), so telling it that no independent witness exists would be a breach. There is no reason for him to withdraw, as he can conduct the case properly without misleading anyone."},{"t":"instructions-capacity-service","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Bethan qualified six months ago and works in her firm's residential conveyancing team. A friend asks her to act for him in an unfair dismissal claim in the employment tribunal. Bethan has never done employment work, and no one at her firm practises employment law. Her friend says he is happy for her to \"learn on the job\".\n\nWhat should Bethan do?","o":["Accept the instructions, because a qualified solicitor may act in any area of law.","Accept the instructions, provided she warns her friend in writing that she is inexperienced in employment law.","Accept the instructions, provided she first completes a set number of hours of continuing professional development in employment law.","Decline or refer the work elsewhere, unless she can obtain the competence and supervision needed to do it properly.","Accept the instructions, because her friend's informed consent removes the need for her to be competent in the area."],"a":3,"e":"A solicitor must ensure the service provided is **competent** and delivered in a **timely** manner (para 3.2), so taking on work outside her competence without supervision would breach the Code. A warning to the client or the client's consent does not lower the standard, and there is **no set number of CPD hours** to satisfy, as continuing competence is based on reflection and addressing learning needs. The right course is to **decline or refer**, unless she can obtain proper expertise and **supervision**."},{"t":"conflicts-of-interest","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Mr Dyer, aged 90, wishes to transfer his house to his son, Paul, for much less than its market value. Paul, who manages his father's finances and drives him to all appointments, asks Ash & Co to act for both of them. They say the terms are agreed and they want to save money by using one firm.\n\nWhat is the best course of action for Ash & Co?","o":["Decline to act for both, as Mr Dyer is vulnerable and dependent on Paul, so he needs separate advice.","Act for both, because they have a substantially common interest and can both give written informed consent.","Act for both, because they are competing for the same objective and can both give written informed consent.","Act for Paul only, taking Mr Dyer's instructions through Paul because he manages his father's affairs.","Act for both, provided Paul agrees to pay the firm's fees for both clients."],"a":0,"e":"Even where clients appear to have a **substantially common interest**, a firm may act for both only if all give written informed consent, safeguards are in place where appropriate and it is **reasonable** to act (para 6.2). A very elderly client making a substantial transfer at an undervalue to the person on whom he depends is **vulnerable**, and the risk of **undue influence** means he needs independent advice, so acting for both is unlikely to be reasonable. The competing-objective exception does not fit, and taking Mr Dyer's instructions through Paul would breach the duty to check instructions directly with the client (para 3.1)."},{"t":"confidentiality-disclosure","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Three years ago a corporate team in the Manchester office of Gaunt LLP, a national firm, advised Corvus plc on a joint venture and obtained confidential information that is material to a new claim. A new client now wants Gaunt LLP's London litigation team to sue Corvus plc over the joint venture. Gaunt LLP has a long-standing, permanent information barrier system, and no one in the London team has had any involvement with Corvus plc. Corvus plc has refused to consent.\n\nMay Gaunt LLP act for the new client?","o":["Yes, if its measures mean there is no real risk of Corvus plc's information being disclosed.","No, because a firm may never act against a former client in a related matter without its consent.","Yes, because the duty of confidentiality to Corvus plc ended when the earlier retainer finished.","Yes, provided the new client gives informed written consent to the firm acting.","No, because the firm would have a conflict of interest between two current clients."],"a":0,"e":"Para 6.5 prevents a firm acting against a current or former client whose **material confidential information** it holds unless **either** effective measures mean there is **no real risk of disclosure**, **or** that client gives written informed consent. Consent is refused, so the question is whether the barrier is effective; an established, permanent barrier between different teams in different offices with no overlap is the strongest kind (Prince Jefri Bolkiah v KPMG). Confidentiality **survives the end of the retainer**, the new client's consent is irrelevant, and this is not a para 6.2 conflict between two current clients."},{"t":"confidentiality-disclosure","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Lark Solicitors acts for Mr Volkov on his cash purchase of a flat. The firm's money laundering reporting officer has made a suspicious activity report to the National Crime Agency about the source of his funds. Mr Volkov rings to ask why exchange has been delayed and whether there is any problem with his money.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's position?","o":["It must tell him about the report, because a solicitor must make the client aware of all information material to the matter.","It may tell him about the report, provided he agrees in writing to keep the information confidential.","It must tell him about the report after completion, when disclosure can no longer prejudice the transaction.","It must not tell him, because disclosure is prohibited by law to prevent crime, an exception to the duty of disclosure.","It may tell him about the report, because the information is confidential to him as the firm's client."],"a":3,"e":"The duty of disclosure (para 6.4) does not apply where disclosure is **prohibited by legal restrictions** imposed in the interests of national security or the **prevention of crime** (para 6.4(a)). Telling a client that a suspicious activity report has been made, where this is likely to prejudice an investigation, risks the offence of **tipping off** under POCA s.333A. Confidentiality and the client's own wishes cannot override that statutory prohibition, and waiting until after completion does not make disclosure lawful."},{"t":"ndas-slapps","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Dr Rhodes, a university academic, has published a report criticising working conditions in the supply chain of Tarnex plc. Tarnex plc instructs Jonah, a solicitor, to write to Dr Rhodes threatening to sue her personally for defamation and to seek substantial damages unless the report is withdrawn within 48 hours. Tarnex plc's director tells Jonah that it has no intention of actually bringing proceedings and simply wants the report taken down.\n\nWhat should Jonah do?","o":["Send the letter, because Tarnex plc is entitled to protect its reputation from criticism.","Refuse, because threatening a claim the client does not intend to bring, to force withdrawal, is an improper purpose.","Send the letter, provided it is marked \"without prejudice\" so that it cannot be published.","Send the letter, provided the 48-hour deadline is extended to 14 days.","Refuse to act for Tarnex plc on any matter involving the report, because defamation claims against academics are not permitted."],"a":1,"e":"The SRA's **SLAPPs warning notice** identifies threatening proceedings with **no intention** of bringing them, to close down publication, as an **improper purpose**, engaging Principles 1, 2 and 5 and paras 1.2 and 2.4. Marking the letter \"without prejudice\" would be a **misuse of labels**, since it is not a genuine settlement attempt, and a longer deadline does not cure the improper purpose. Defamation claims are not banned, so Jonah could act on a genuine, properly arguable complaint in measured terms; refusing all work for the client goes too far."},{"t":"trust-fairness-undertakings","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Nell acts for Ostler Ltd, which is buying the entire share capital of a private company. The seller's solicitor asks Nell in writing whether her client's bank funding is in place. Nell knows that the bank withdrew its offer yesterday and Ostler Ltd is seeking a new lender. Ostler Ltd instructs her to reply that \"funding is confirmed\".\n\nWhat should Nell do?","o":["Refuse to give that reply; she may decline to comment on her client's funding, but must not make a misleading statement.","Give the reply, because her duty is owed to her own client and not to the seller.","Tell the seller's solicitor that the bank has withdrawn its offer, because she must volunteer facts relevant to the transaction.","Give the reply, provided she adds that all correspondence is \"subject to contract\".","Give the reply, because it is her client's statement, which she is merely passing on."],"a":0,"e":"Para 1.4 prohibits **misleading** anyone, by a solicitor's own acts or by being **complicit** in the client's, so passing on a statement Nell knows is false is a breach. But there is **no duty to volunteer** weaknesses in the client's position, so with the client's instructions she can decline to answer, as long as she says nothing misleading. A \"subject to contract\" label affects whether a contract is formed, not whether a statement is misleading."},{"t":"client-money","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Rebecca practises as a freelance solicitor, drafting wills for individuals. She is not an authorised body and works alone. A client asks her to receive £20,000 from him and hold it until his will is signed, then pay it into a trust for his grandchildren.\n\nWhat should Rebecca do?","o":["Hold the money in a personal bank account labelled with the client's name, keeping a record of the receipt, until the will is signed.","Hold the money, provided the client agrees in writing to the arrangement.","Open a client account in her own name at a bank in England and Wales and hold the money there.","Decline, because a freelance solicitor may hold only limited sums on account of her own fees and disbursements.","Hold the money, provided she obtains an accountant's report at the end of the year."],"a":3,"e":"Para 4.3 provides that a solicitor does **not personally hold client money** except as the SRA's authorisation rules permit. A **freelance solicitor** may receive only limited payments, being money on account of their own **fees and unpaid disbursements**, and **cannot operate a client account**. Holding £20,000 to pay into a trust is outside those limits, so the client's written agreement, a labelled personal account or an accountant's report cannot make it permissible. The client should use an authorised firm or make the payment himself."},{"t":"business-referrals-publicity","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Ines is a partner in a residential conveyancing firm. She also owns a property survey company that is not regulated by the SRA or any other legal regulator. She would like her firm to recommend the survey company to buyer clients.\n\nOn what basis may the firm recommend the survey company?","o":["It may not recommend the survey company in any circumstances, because Ines has a financial interest in it.","Only if each client is told of Ines's interest and gives informed consent, knowing the company is not SRA-regulated.","It may recommend the survey company without further steps, provided the company carries its own professional insurance.","It may recommend the survey company without telling clients about Ines's interest, provided its prices are competitive.","It may recommend the survey company, provided it is presented to clients as part of the firm's conveyancing service."],"a":1,"e":"A referral to a **separate business** is permitted only with the client's **informed consent**, which includes understanding that it is **not regulated by the SRA** and that SRA protections do not apply (para 5.3). The firm must also tell clients of any **financial interest** it or its managers have in the referral (para 5.1(a)). Presenting the company as part of the firm would mislead clients about its regulated status (para 8.10(c)), and competitive prices or the company's own insurance do not replace disclosure and consent."},{"t":"cooperation-reporting","m":2,"p":"FLK2","q":"Freya, a solicitor in a property team, discovers that the firm's COLP, who is also the head of her department, has altered the date on a tenant's break notice so that it appears to have been served in time. She is worried about raising it within the firm.\n\nWhat should Freya do?","o":["Report the matter to the COLP, because telling the COLP always satisfies her own reporting duty.","Raise it with the COLP first and give him the opportunity to explain before deciding whether to report.","Report it promptly and directly to the SRA, as she cannot rely on the COLP to report his own conduct.","Investigate fully and gather all the evidence before deciding whether to report the matter.","Take no action unless the tenant or the landlord suffers a financial loss as a result."],"a":2,"e":"Altering a document to make it look as if it was served in time is likely to be **dishonest**, which is always capable of amounting to a **serious breach** that must be **reported promptly** (para 7.7). Telling the COLP satisfies a solicitor's duty only **on the understanding that the COLP will report** (para 7.12), which cannot be assumed where the COLP is the wrongdoer, so Freya must report **directly**. She need not finish her own investigation or wait for loss, and she is protected from detrimental treatment for reporting (para 7.9)."},{"t":"complaints-legal-ombudsman","m":2,"p":"FLK1","q":"Halden Solicitors acted for TechNova Ltd in recovering a commercial debt. TechNova Ltd has 35 employees and an annual turnover of £4 million. It is unhappy with delays in the matter, complained to the firm and has received the firm's final response.\n\nCan TechNova Ltd take its complaint to the Legal Ombudsman?","o":["Yes, because any client of a regulated law firm may complain to the Legal Ombudsman.","Yes, provided it refers the complaint within six months of the firm's final response.","No, because companies of any size are excluded from the Legal Ombudsman scheme.","Yes, but the Legal Ombudsman may only order the firm to reduce its fees.","No, because it is not a micro-enterprise and so is not eligible to complain."],"a":4,"e":"The Legal Ombudsman can consider complaints from **individuals** and from small organisations such as **micro-enterprises**, meaning fewer than **10 employees** and turnover or balance sheet of no more than **€2 million**, as well as small charities, clubs and trusts. With 35 employees and a £4 million turnover, TechNova Ltd is not eligible, so the six-month time limit is irrelevant. Companies are not excluded altogether, and where a complaint is accepted the remedies go beyond fee reductions, up to **£50,000**."},{"t":"principles-competence","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Owen, a solicitor at a commercial firm, posts a series of grossly offensive messages about a particular ethnic group on his personal social media account. His profile states that he is a solicitor. The posts are made in the evenings and have no connection with any client.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the SRA Principles apply?","o":["His conduct is not regulated, because the Principles apply only to work done for clients and not to a solicitor's private life.","It may breach the duty to uphold public trust and confidence in the profession, despite being outside practice.","His conduct is regulated only if it leads to a criminal conviction.","His conduct engages only the duty to act in the best interests of each client.","His conduct is a matter for the Legal Ombudsman rather than the SRA."],"a":1,"e":"The Principles can apply to conduct **outside practice** where it affects the trust the public places in the solicitor and the profession, which mainly engages **public trust and confidence** (Principle 2) and **integrity** (Principle 5). Grossly offensive public posts by someone identifying as a solicitor are a typical example, and they also run counter to encouraging **equality, diversity and inclusion**. No conviction is needed, the best-interests Principle concerns clients, and the Legal Ombudsman deals with service complaints rather than conduct."},{"t":"duty-to-court","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Petra is charged with burglary. At her first interview she told her solicitor, Aaron, that she was at a friend's house at the time. She now says she was at the cinema alone and that her earlier account was wrong because she was confused about the dates. She has never told Aaron that she committed the offence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Aaron's position?","o":["He may continue and present the cinema account after questioning her, as changed instructions are not an admission of guilt.","He must cease to act, because her change of account shows that she has lied to him.","He must tell the court about her earlier account, because otherwise the court will be misled about the true nature of her defence.","He may continue to act, but must not call Petra to give evidence about where she was at the time of the burglary.","He may continue to act, but only by putting the prosecution to proof without advancing any positive defence."],"a":0,"e":"A solicitor is only **knowingly** misleading the court if the client has actually told them that the defence is false. **Inconsistent instructions are not an admission**: Aaron may and should question Petra about the change, but it is for the court, not him, to decide whether she is guilty. Restrictions such as not calling the client and limiting the defence to putting the prosecution to proof apply where the client has **admitted guilt**, which Petra has not. Her earlier account is **confidential** and he has no duty to reveal it."},{"t":"instructions-capacity-service","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Morwen dismissed her solicitors, Brent & Co, in the middle of a commercial dispute and instructed a new firm. She has not paid Brent & Co's final bill of £6,000, which she disputes. Her new solicitors need the file urgently because a court hearing is listed for next week. Brent & Co proposes to keep the whole file until the bill is paid.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Brent & Co's position?","o":["It may keep the whole file until the bill is paid in full, because its right to retain the papers is unaffected by the forthcoming hearing.","It must hand over the file and waive its fees, because Morwen ended the retainer.","It generally has a lien, but should not use it to seriously prejudice the imminent hearing; the court may order the file released on terms.","It cannot charge Morwen for any work, because she ended the retainer before the case finished.","It may refuse to hand over the file, because a client cannot end the retainer while a bill is unpaid."],"a":2,"e":"A client may end the retainer **at any time**, though they may still have to pay for work done. On termination a solicitor generally has a **lien**, a right to keep the client's papers until the bill is paid, but should not use it where that would **seriously prejudice** the client or obstruct court proceedings, and the court can order the papers to be **handed over on terms**. The lien does not mean the firm loses its right to be paid, nor does it allow the firm to keep the file regardless of the hearing."},{"t":"instructions-capacity-service","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Mr Hughes, aged 72, had a stroke last year. He cannot speak, but he can understand what is said to him and communicates reliably using a letter board. He contacts Pike Solicitors to make a new will. His daughter telephones the firm and says that her father \"can't make decisions any more\" and that she will tell the firm what he wants.\n\nWhat should the firm do?","o":["Decline to act, because a person who cannot speak is unable to communicate a decision for the purposes of making a will.","Take instructions from the daughter, because she knows her father's wishes and can communicate them more easily.","Meet Mr Hughes, using his letter board and allowing extra time, and assess his capacity to make the will.","Decline to act until the Court of Protection has confirmed that Mr Hughes has capacity to make a will.","Act on Mr Hughes's instructions without considering capacity at all, because adults are presumed to have capacity."],"a":2,"e":"Under the Mental Capacity Act 2005, a person is not treated as unable to decide unless **all practicable steps** to help them have been taken, and a person who can **communicate by any means**, such as a letter board, is not unable to communicate. The firm must also make **reasonable adjustments** for his needs (para 3.4). Capacity is **presumed**, but the daughter's comment and his stroke give reason to assess it properly for this decision, so ignoring the issue is not best practice. Instructions must come from the client himself, not the daughter (para 3.1), and no court order is needed."},{"t":"conflicts-of-interest","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Yusuf, a solicitor, has acted for Delta Logistics Ltd for many years. Its managing director, impressed with his work, offers to arrange for the company to lend Yusuf £30,000 at a low rate of interest to help him buy into the partnership. She asks Yusuf to draw up the loan agreement for the company, and says the company is happy for him to do so.\n\nWhat should Yusuf do?","o":["Act for the company on the loan, because the company has given its informed consent.","Act for the company on the loan, provided the company's consent is given in writing.","Decline to act for the company on the loan and advise it to take independent legal advice.","Act for both the company and himself, because they have a substantially common interest in the loan.","Act for the company on the loan, provided the loan is on normal commercial terms."],"a":2,"e":"Where a client proposes to **lend money to the solicitor**, the solicitor's interest in favourable loan terms conflicts with the duty to protect the client, creating an **own interest conflict** (para 6.1). Unlike a client conflict, an own interest conflict has **no exceptions**, so the company's consent, in writing or otherwise, cannot allow Yusuf to act. The substantially common interest exception applies only to conflicts between clients. The company should be advised to take **independent advice**."},{"t":"confidentiality-disclosure","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Ottley Solicitors prepared the will of the late Mr Adeyemi, who died last month. His daughter is the sole executor and has not yet applied for a grant of probate. His son, who receives nothing under the will, asks the firm for a copy of the will and of the attendance notes explaining why he was left out.\n\nWhat should the firm do?","o":["Provide both documents, because the duty of confidentiality to a client comes to an end when the client dies.","Provide both documents, because the son is the deceased's next of kin.","Provide the attendance notes but not the will, because the will is not yet public.","Provide both documents once the son confirms in writing that he intends to challenge the will.","Refuse without the executor's consent, because confidentiality continues after the client's death."],"a":4,"e":"The duty of **confidentiality** (para 6.3) covers former clients and **continues after death**, and the right to waive it passes to the client's **personal representatives**. The firm should therefore not disclose the will or file notes without the **executor's consent**, unless the law requires it, for example under a court order. Being next of kin or intending to challenge the will gives the son no right to the documents."},{"t":"firms-compliance-protections","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Harker Legal, a small commercial firm, is applying to the SRA for authorisation. It proposes to appoint its senior partner, a solicitor, as its COLP and its practice manager, a qualified accountant who is not a lawyer, as its COFA.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the proposal?","o":["It is not permissible, because the COFA must be a solicitor or other lawyer.","It is not permissible, because a partner who takes management decisions may not also act as COLP.","It is permissible, and no SRA approval is needed because both people work within the firm.","It is not permissible, because the practice manager should be the COLP and the partner the COFA.","It is permissible, subject to SRA approval of both, because a COFA need not be a lawyer."],"a":4,"e":"The **COLP** must be a **lawyer** working in the firm, but the **COFA**, who is responsible for compliance with the Accounts Rules, **need not be a lawyer** and is often a finance director or practice manager. Both appointments require **SRA approval**. The rule preventing managers who can take significant decisions alone from acting as compliance officers in higher-risk firms was approved but **paused** and was not in force at the examinable date, so a manager may currently act as COLP or COFA."},{"t":"ndas-slapps","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Corwen Ltd is settling a claim of sexual harassment brought by a former employee, Lily, who has her own solicitor. Corwen Ltd asks its solicitor to include several terms in the settlement agreement.\n\nWhich of the following terms may the solicitor properly include?","o":["A warranty by Lily that she has not reported the harassment to any regulator or to the police before signing the agreement.","A clause requiring Lily to repay the whole settlement sum if she later makes a protected disclosure about the harassment.","A requirement that Lily sign the agreement within 24 hours, before she has time to consult her solicitor further.","A clause keeping the settlement terms confidential that expressly allows reports to the police, regulators and her advisers.","A term that Lily may not keep a copy of the signed agreement."],"a":3,"e":"NDAs are lawful for legitimate purposes, such as keeping **settlement terms** confidential, provided they are clear about what may still be disclosed. The SRA's **NDA warning notice** says solicitors must not use warranties or **clawback** clauses to deter **permitted disclosures**, such as reports to the police or regulators and **protected disclosures**, and must not apply oppressive time limits, discourage independent advice or refuse to let the other party keep a copy. Those terms would also engage paras 1.2 and 7.5 and, for a victim of crime, would be void under the Victims and Prisoners Act 2024 s.17."},{"t":"trust-fairness-undertakings","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Imran acts for a buyer of a commercial unit. To persuade the seller to grant a short extension of time, Imran gives the seller's solicitor a written undertaking to pay the seller's legal costs of £3,000 \"subject to receipt of the mortgage advance from Dalton Bank\". Dalton Bank later withdraws its mortgage offer, no advance is received, and the purchase does not proceed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Imran's position?","o":["He must pay the £3,000 from his firm's own money, because an undertaking must be performed whatever happens.","He must pay the £3,000 within a reasonable time, because no deadline was agreed.","He is in breach unless the seller's solicitor agrees to release him from the undertaking.","He is not in breach of the undertaking, because the condition on which it was given has not been met.","He must pay the £3,000 if his client agrees to reimburse the firm."],"a":3,"e":"An undertaking takes effect according to its terms, and one expressed to be **conditional** on receipt of funds is not triggered if the **condition is never met**. This is why good practice is to make undertakings to pay money **conditional on receipt** of the funds unless they are already held. The rules that an undertaking binds even when compliance becomes costly, and that only the recipient can release it, apply to an **unconditional** obligation; the reasonable-time rule applies only once the obligation has arisen."},{"t":"client-money","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Barlow LLP's COFA finds that several client ledgers in the commercial litigation department have been overdrawn over the past year. When she raises this at a partners' meeting, the partners say that dealing with client account is the cashier's job and that they are not responsible for her mistakes.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the partners' position?","o":["They are right, because responsibility for the Accounts Rules rests only with the person who makes the entries.","They are wrong, because all the firm's managers share responsibility for Accounts Rules compliance with the COFA.","They are right, because the COFA alone is responsible for compliance with the Accounts Rules, not the other partners.","They are wrong, but only the managing partner is responsible, because she supervises the cashier.","They are right, provided the firm's professional indemnity insurance covers any loss to clients."],"a":1,"e":"The **COFA** must take all reasonable steps to ensure compliance with the Accounts Rules and report serious breaches, but **all managers** are also responsible for the firm's compliance, and under the Code for Firms that responsibility is **joint and several** where managers share management responsibility. The Accounts Rules apply to the firm, its managers and employees, so responsibility cannot be delegated to a cashier. Any shortfall from the overdrawn ledgers must be **replaced immediately** from the firm's own money, whatever its insurance position."},{"t":"business-referrals-publicity","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Sorrel Solicitors wants to increase its residential conveyancing work and is considering several marketing ideas.\n\nWhich of the following proposals is permitted under the SRA Code of Conduct?","o":["Delivering leaflets advertising its fees, inclusive of VAT and disbursements, to every house in the town.","Telephoning homeowners whose houses have just been listed for sale online to offer its conveyancing services.","Sending text messages to people who have recently viewed a new housing development, offering its conveyancing services.","Visiting homes on a new estate to introduce the firm and offer its services in person to residents who are not clients.","Approaching bidders in person at a local property auction to offer its services to them."],"a":0,"e":"Para 8.9 bans **unsolicited approaches** to **members of the public**, other than current or former clients, to advertise legal services. Targeted telephone calls, texts, door-stepping and personal approaches at an auction are all caught. General **advertising**, such as leaflets delivered to every house, is not a targeted personal approach and is allowed, provided the information is **accurate and not misleading** about charges, including VAT and disbursements (para 8.8)."},{"t":"cooperation-reporting","m":3,"p":"FLK1","q":"Leah acts for the claimant in a commercial contract dispute. She discovers clear evidence that the defendant's solicitor, at another firm, signed a witness's name on a witness statement without the witness's knowledge and served it on Leah's firm.\n\nWhat should Leah do?","o":["Take no action, because the reporting duty applies only to people working in her own firm.","Report it promptly to the SRA, directly or via her COLP if confident the COLP will report it.","Raise the matter only with the court at trial, because it concerns evidence in the proceedings.","Wait until the proceedings have ended before reporting, so as not to prejudice her client's case.","Write to the defendant client to warn it about its solicitor's conduct."],"a":1,"e":"Para 7.7 requires a solicitor to **report promptly** to the SRA facts they reasonably believe are **capable of amounting to a serious breach** by **any** person the SRA regulates, including solicitors at other firms. Falsifying a witness's signature is likely to be **dishonest** and therefore serious. Leah may meet her duty by telling her firm's **COLP** if confident the COLP will pass it on (para 7.12), but she should not delay until the case ends, and contacting the opposing client directly is not the proper route."},{"t":"complaints-legal-ombudsman","m":3,"p":"FLK2","q":"Ewan is a beneficiary of an estate administered by Fenn Solicitors. In March 2025 he received the estate accounts and immediately noticed that the firm had wrongly deducted a fee from his share. He did nothing until May 2026, when he complained to the firm. The firm sent its final response in June 2026, giving details of the Legal Ombudsman and the six-month time limit. There are no exceptional circumstances.\n\nWhat is the Legal Ombudsman most likely to decide if Ewan refers the complaint to it now?","o":["It will accept the complaint, because it is brought within six months of the firm's final response.","It will accept the complaint, because it is brought within six years of the act complained of.","It will accept the complaint, because it is brought within three years of when Ewan knew of the problem.","It will not accept it, because more than a year passed after Ewan knew there was cause for complaint.","It will not accept the complaint, because beneficiaries of an estate may not complain to the Legal Ombudsman."],"a":3,"e":"Since **1 April 2023** a complaint must reach the Legal Ombudsman no later than **one year from the act or omission**, or **one year from when the complainant should reasonably have known** of the cause for complaint, as well as within **six months** of the firm's final response. Ewan knew of the problem in March 2025, so by the time he refers it more than a year has passed on both measures, and being within six months of the final response is not enough. The old six-year and three-year limits no longer apply, and beneficiaries are eligible to complain."}]};